&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) retreat; backoff</li><li>deviation, departure, digression</li></ol><p><i>отступи́ть</i> (otstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostate, recreant (person who renounces a faith)</li></ol><p><i>отступи́ть</i> (otstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apostasy, tergiversation (renunciation of set of beliefs)</li><li>defection</li><li>backsliding</li><li>recreancy</li><li>abjuration</li></ol><p><i>отсту́пник</i> (otstúpnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>monetary</i>) compensation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial) compensation</li></ol><p><i>отступи́ть</i> (otstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbuckle, to unzip, to unbutton, to unfasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a kickback</li></ol><p><i>отстегну́ть</i> (otstegnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack, absence</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>суть</i> (sutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be absent, to be lacking</li></ol><p>From <i>отсу́тствие</i> (otsútstvije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absent (being away from a place)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying</li></ol><p><i>отсуш(и́ть)</i> (otsuš(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count off, to count out</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count off, to count out</li></ol><p><i>отсчита́ть</i> (otsčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>count, counting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, to send off, to dispatch</li><li>to send back</li><li>to refer <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sending, dispatch</li><li>reference (<i>e.g. in a text</i>)</li></ol><p><i>отсыла́ть</i> (otsylátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour off, to separate by pouring out (bulk material, objects or pieces, not liquid)</li><li>to pour out (<i>thereby reducing the quantity; bulk material, objects or pieces, not liquid</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to give generously</li><li>to stop sleeping</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sleep for a while</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>отсы́пать</i> (otsýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпа́ть</i> (-sypátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to pour out, to spill out (<i>of a small quantity of a bulk material, objects, or pieces, not liquid</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rest after not sleeping for a while</li></ol><p><i>отсы́пать</i> (otsýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>отсыпа́ть</i> (otsypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпа́ть</i> (-sypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scree, rock fragments</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отсы́пать</i> (otsýpatʹ), with expected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become damp, to become wet</li></ol><p><i>отсыре́ть</i> (otsyrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become damp, to become wet</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сыре́ть</i> (syrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from here, hence</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>сюда</i> (sjuda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thaw out, to let thaw out <i>(transitive)</i></li><li>to thaw out <i>(intransitive)</i></li><li>to thaw <i>(out)</i>, to melt, to mellow, to relax</li></ol><p><i>отта́ять</i> (ottájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pushing back</li><li>repulsion</li><li>antagonization</li></ol><p><i>отта́лкивать</i> (ottálkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push back, to push away</li><li>to alienate, to antagonize</li></ol><p><i>оттолкну́ть</i> (ottolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to push off [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to proceed (from something as a starting point)</li></ol><p><i>отта́лкивать</i> (ottálkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>repulsive</li></ol><p>Compare typologically English <i>repulsive</i>, Polish <i>odpychający</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag away/aside, to pull away/aside, to drag/pull back</li></ol><p><i>оттаск-</i> (ottask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharpening, whetting</li><li>honing (e.g. skills)</li></ol><p><i>отта́чивать</i> (ottáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen, to whet</li><li>to perfect</li></ol><p><i>отточи́ть</i> (ottočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag away/aside, to pull away/aside, to drag/pull back</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thaw out, to let thaw out <i>(transitive)</i></li><li>to thaw out <i>(intransitive)</i></li><li>to thaw <i>(out)</i>, to melt, to mellow, to relax</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow outwards</li><li>to flow sideways</li></ol><p><i>отте́к-</i> (otték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shade (an artwork) (<i>creating a three-dimensional effect</i>)</li><li>to enhance, to set off (<i>by means of contrasting colors; usually of clothing or cosmetics</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to highlight</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>тени́ть</i> (tenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinge, nuance, shade, tint, hue (color or shade of color; tint; dye)</li><li>inflection, nuance</li><li>connotation</li></ol><p>Related to <i>оттени́ть</i> (ottenítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literary) relating to shade, tint, hue etc., containing a shade, tint, hue etc.</li></ol><p><i>отте́нок</i> (otténok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shade (an artwork) (<i>creating a three-dimensional effect</i>)</li><li>to enhance, to set off (<i>by means of contrasting colors; usually of clothing or cosmetics</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to highlight</li></ol><p><i>оттени́ть</i> (ottenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thaw (a warmth of weather, also figurative)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thaw</li></ol><p><i>о́ттепель</i> (óttepelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive back/off/away, to press back, to push aside, to shove back</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>тесни́ть</i> (tesnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive back/off/away, to press back, to push aside, to shove back</li></ol><p><i>оттесни́ть</i> (ottesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow outwards</li><li>to flow sideways</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impression, press, type</li><li>reprint</li></ol><p>Deverbal from the root <i>отти́ск-</i> (ottísk-) of <i>отти́снуть</i> (ottísnutʹ) (compare imperfective <i>отти́скивать</i> (ottískivatʹ)), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to push forcefully, to shove</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to crush, to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ти́скать</i> (tískatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push forcefully once, to shove once</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push forcefully, to shove</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crush once, to squeeze once</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crush, to squeeze</li><li>to imprint once, to stamp once</li><li>to imprint, to stamp</li></ol><p><i>отти́ск-</i> (ottísk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push forcefully once, to shove once</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push forcefully, to shove</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crush once, to squeeze once</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crush, to squeeze</li><li>to imprint once, to stamp once</li><li>to imprint, to stamp</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ти́снуть</i> (tísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, that's why (also <i>оттого́ и</i> (ottovó i))</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>от</i> (ot, “from, out of”) + <i>того́</i> (tovó), genitive singular of <i>тот</i> (tot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outflow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push back, to push away</li><li>to alienate, to antagonize</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to push off [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from somewhere’</small>]</li><li>to proceed (from something as a starting point)</li></ol><p><i>оттолкну́ть</i> (ottolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out (to cause to protrude)</li></ol><p><i>оттопы́рить</i> (ottopýritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out (to cause to protrude)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>топы́рить</i> (topýritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear away forcefully</li><li>to not accept; to reject</li></ol><p>Ultimately, descends from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъrgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear away forcefully</li><li>to not accept; to reject</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abruption</li><li>rejection</li></ol><p>From <i>отто́ргнуть</i> (ottórgnutʹ, “to tear away, to reject”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen, to whet</li><li>to perfect</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thence, from there</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>туда</i> (tuda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw off, to draw back, to pull aside</li><li>to weigh down</li><li>to delay, to procrastinate, to put off</li></ol><p><i>оттяг-</i> (ottjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delay, procrastination</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) guy, strut, brace, stay</li></ol><p><i>оттяг-</i> (ottjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw off, to draw back, to pull aside</li><li>to weigh down</li><li>to delay, to procrastinate, to put off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dim, to cloud, to fog</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тума́н</i> (tumán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become blunt (of instruments)</li><li>to grow stupid; to become mentally insensitive</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тупе́ть</i> (tupétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force to break a habit</li></ol><p><i>отучи́ть</i> (otučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlearn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>учити</i> (učiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>отуча́ть</i> (otučátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>отучи́ть</i> (otučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force to break a habit</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to finish teaching, to finish learning</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlearn (to break a habit)</li></ol><p><i>отучи́ть</i> (otučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to filter, to filter out</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>фильтрова́ть</i> (filʹtrovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to filter, to filter out</li></ol><p><i>отфильтрова́ть</i> (otfilʹtrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate, to cough up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ха́ркать</i> (xárkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) expectoration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate, to cough up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate, to cough up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ха́ркнуть</i> (xárknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to cut off in several snips</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chop off, to cut off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to perform (a dance) dashingly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get in trouble, to get beaten up</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chop off, to cut off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to perform (a dance) dashingly</li></ol><p>From <i>отхвати́ть</i> (otxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or <i>отхвата́ть</i> (otxvatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sup</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>хлеба́ть</i> (xlebátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a mouthful <i>(of)</i>, to take a sip</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>хлебну́ть</i> (xlebnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a mouthful <i>(of)</i>, to take a sip</li></ol><p>From <i>отхлебну́ть</i> (otxlebnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or <i>отхлеба́ть</i> (otxlebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure, sailing <i>(of a ship)</i></li><li>withdrawal, retirement, falling back</li><li>deviation, departure, break</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) waste, screenings, tailings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to go aside, to move away, to move aside</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to withdraw, to fall back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go away temporarily (with the intention to come back soon)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come around, to come back to normal (e.g. after a shock or disease)</li><li>to go out of fashion</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to recover (e.g. after a hangover or shock)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>requiescat (a prayer recited over the dying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) waste</li></ol><p><i>отхо́ды</i> (otxódy) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) comedown, hangover (the process of recovery of the body after consumption of alcohol and&#47;or drugs)</li></ol><p>From <i>отхо́д</i> (otxód, “withdrawal”) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waste products, waste, scrap, refuse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet, privy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;expressive) to fuck (to have sex with)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat, to hit; to fuck up</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat in a battle, game or competition</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to build, to finish building, to erect</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to perform any unusual or extraordinary act</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to work for someone somewhere</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>хуя́чить</i> (xujáčitʹ)</p><p>Same as 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish blooming</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to lose freshness or youth, to wither</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to lose color, to fade</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish blooming</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to lose freshness or youth, to wither</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to lose color, to fade</li></ol><p><i>отцвет-</i> (otcvet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unhitch, to unhook</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unfasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take off (an article of clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to untangle, to disentangle</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unhitch, to unhook</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unfasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take off (an article of clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to untangle, to disentangle</li></ol><p><i>отцепи́ть</i> (otcepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) patricide (killing of one's father)</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) patricide (murderer of own father)</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) father’s</li></ol><p><i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>father's, fatherly, paternal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatherhood</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despair, to be in despair</li><li>to not hope, to lose hope <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>отча́яться</i> (otčájatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to cast away; to unmoor; to sail away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave; to depart</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to kick back, to unwind</li></ol><p><i>отча́лить</i> (otčálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to cast away; to unmoor; to sail away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave; to depart</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ча́лить</i> (čálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>partially (to a partial degree)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>desperately (in a desperate manner)</li></ol><p><i>отча́янный</i> (otčájannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desperate</li><li>foolhardy, reckless</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rotten, frightful, awful</li></ol><p><i>отча́яние</i> (otčájanije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>отча́яние</i> (otčájanije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despair, to be in despair</li><li>to not hope, to lose hope <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ча́ять</i> (čájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative adverb</i>) why? (from what cause)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>от</i> (ot, “from, out of”) + <i>чего́</i> (čevó), genitive singular of <i>что</i> (što).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for some reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mint, to coin</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to chase, to caulk</li><li>to utter distinctly, to punctuate</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>чека́нить</i> (čekánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patronymic</li></ol><p>From <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc, “father”) (namely, the vocative form, <i>о́тче</i> (ótče)) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo). Doublet of <i>оте́чество</i> (otéčestvo) and <i>отцо́вство</i> (otcóvstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatherland, motherland, homeland, home country</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>отьчизна</i> (otĭčizna, “(fore)fathers, lineage, patrimony”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otьči(z)na</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Ukrainian <i>вітчи́зна</i> (vitčýzna) and Polish <i>ojczyzna</i>. By surface analysis, <i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) fatherly, paternal</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) native</li></ol><p><i>оте́ц</i> (otéc) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepfather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>о́тьчимъ</i> (ótĭčimŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;òtьčimъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deduction, money deducted</li><li>dismissal (of students from a school or a college)</li></ol><p><i>отчи́слить</i> (otčíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deduct</li><li>to assign, to allot</li><li>to remit, to transfer</li><li>to dismiss, to expel, to send down</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deduct</li><li>to assign, to allot</li><li>to remit, to transfer</li><li>to dismiss, to expel, to send down</li></ol><p><i>отчи́слить</i> (otčíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chew out, to bawl out, to reprimand</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to finish reading (something)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, to give a report</li><li>to give an account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chew out, to bawl out, to reprimand</li></ol><p><i>отчита́ть</i> (otčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report, to give a report</li><li>to give an account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate, to expropriate</li><li>to estrange</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to do something very strange</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>чуд-</i> (čud-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). The middle part is cognate with <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój, “another's, alien”), but lacking the Early Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-jь</i> suffix, suggesting that the term originated in Early Proto Slavic or before.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alienate, to expropriate</li><li>to estrange</li></ol><p><i>отчуди́ть</i> (otčudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alienation, exclusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estrangement, alienation, estrangement, detachment (the state)</li></ol><p><i>отчуждённый</i> (otčuždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detached, aloof</li><li>alienated, estranged</li><li>(<i>law</i>) expropriated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account, report</li><li>return</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otъčьtъ</i>, from <i>&#42;čisti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>distinctly, clearly, audibly, legibly</li></ol><p><i>отчётливый</i> (otčótlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinctness, clarity</li></ol><p><i>отчётливый</i> (otčótlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distinct, clear</li><li>intelligible</li></ol><p><i>отчёт</i> (otčót) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accountability (state of being accountable)</li><li>reporting</li></ol><p><i>отчётный</i> (otčótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) report, reported</li></ol><p><i>отчёт</i> (otčót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start back <i>(from)</i>, to shrink back <i>(from)</i>, to recoil <i>(from)</i></li><li>to forsake, to renounce</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>шата́ть</i> (šatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start back <i>(from)</i>, to shrink back <i>(from)</i>, to recoil <i>(from)</i></li><li>to forsake, to renounce</li></ol><p><i>отшатну́ться</i> (otšatnútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fling away, to throw off, to kick aside</li></ol><p><i>отшвырну́ть</i> (otšvyrnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fling away, to throw off, to kick aside</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>швырну́ть</i> (švyrnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermit, anchorite</li><li>recluse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) remoteness, apartness, usually in combination with <i>на</i> (na):</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow off (shirk or disregard a duty or person)</li><li>to dump someone (to end a romantic relationship with)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind, to polish, to file</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>шлифова́ть</i> (šlifovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spank, to smack, to slap</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to scold (someone)</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to walk some distance (usually in mud)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>шлёпать</i> (šljópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spank (to punish with slaps)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to scold (someone)</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to walk some distance or for some time (usually in mud)</li></ol><p><i>отшлёпать</i> (otšljópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh off (to respond to a negative situation lightheartedly, as though it were a joke)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh off (to respond to a negative situation lightheartedly, as though it were a joke)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renegade, misfit</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) dissenter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>одщепе́нецъ</i> (odščepénecʹ), from Polish <i>odszczepieniec</i>. By surface analysis, <i>отщепи́ть</i> (otščepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нец</i> (-énec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split off (a chip of wood)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-щепи́ть</i> (-ščepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to chip off</li></ol><p><i>отщепи́ть</i> (otščepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split off (a chip of wood)</li></ol><p><i>отщепи́ть</i> (otščepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to chip off</li></ol><p><i>отщепля́ть</i> (otščepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>от</i> (ot).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off; to leave alone; to stop annoying or pestering</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat off, to bite off</li></ol><p><i>отъед-</i> (otʺjed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>отъезжа́ть</i> (otʺjezžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to ride off, to depart (by vehicle)</li><li>only used in <i>отъезжа́ть наза́д</i> (otʺjezžátʹ nazád, “to back up”)</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a prolonged autumn hunt away from home</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat off, to bite off</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away, to ride off, to depart (by vehicle)</li><li>only used in <i>отъе́хать наза́д</i> (otʺjéxatʹ nazád, “to back up”)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol><p>From <i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorough, out-and-out, rank</li><li>inveterate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off; to leave alone; to stop annoying or pestering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win back</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win back money</li><li>to win again after a loss</li><li>to compensate [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for losses’</small>]</li><li>to take out one's anger [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>отыгра́ть</i> (otygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win back</li></ol><p><i>отыгра́ть</i> (otygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win back money</li><li>to win again after a loss</li><li>to compensate [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for losses’</small>]</li><li>to take out one's anger [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + instrumental) <small>‘on someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>оты́грывать</i> (otýgryvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to support, to protect (as a backup&#47;from behind)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тыл</i> (tyl) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) denominal</li></ol><p>From <i>от</i> (ot) +&lrm; <i>и́мя</i> (ímja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to track down</li><li>to look up, to search</li></ol><p><i>от-</i> (ot-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be found, to appear, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>отыска́ть</i> (otyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to track down</li><li>to look up, to search</li></ol><p><i>отыска́ть</i> (otyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be found, to appear, to turn up</li></ol><p><i>оты́скивать</i> (otýskivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden, to weigh down</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тяготи́ть</i> (tjagotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden, to weigh down</li></ol><p><i>отяготи́ть</i> (otjagotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aggravate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>отягчати</i> (otjagčati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;otęgъčati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) aggravating circumstance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow heavy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>тяжеле́ть</i> (tjaželétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edema, dropsy, hydrops, hypostasis</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o(b)tekъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydropic, edematous</li></ol><p><i>отёк</i> (otjók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>O</i>/<i>o</i>.</li><li>ow, oh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) peddler in villages and small fairs, selling sewing goods, textiles, books, prints, etc.</li></ol><p>Usually associated with Ancient Greek <i>Ἀθηναῖος</i> (Athēnaîos, “Athenian”); compare the nickname of ancient Bishop of Athens Hierotheos the Thesmothete – Ерофе́й-офе́ня. Less likely from Greek <i>αφέντης</i> (aféntis, “master, prince”) or <i>ὀθόνιον</i> (othónion), <i>ὀθόνη</i> (othóni) ("fine linen"). Also associated with Polish <i>ochweśnicki</i> (“related to an ochweśnik (a 19th-century dealer in devotional items from Skulsk)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offer, tender, quotation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Offerte</i>, ultimately from Late Latin <i>offerta</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;offerīre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) freaking awesomely, freaking amazingly, freaking well</li></ol><p><i>офиге́нный</i> (ofigénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) fucking awesomeness</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-, “around”) + <i>фиг</i> (fig, “screw you”) + <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ, “ness”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) freaking awesome, freaking amazing, freaking good</li></ol><p><i>офиге́ть</i> (ofigétʹ, “to be shocked”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, (nearly) to die of hunger, to cold, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mildly&#32;vulgar</i>) frick!, holy shit!</li><li>(mildly&#32;vulgar,&#32;used as an adverb) freaking, fricking</li></ol><p>From <i>фиг</i> (fig).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>office</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office</li></ol><p><i>о́фис</i> (ófis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;law enforcement) officer</li><li>(chess,&#32;informal) bishop (Russian abbreviation: С)</li></ol><p>Either directly from German <i>Offizier</i> from Latin <i>officiārius</i> (“officer”), or first via Polish <i>oficer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) officers (in military)</li></ol><p><i>офице́р</i> (oficér) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) officer; officer's, officers'</li></ol><p><i>офице́р</i> (oficér) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>officially</li></ol><p><i>официа́льный</i> (oficiálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>official</li><li>formal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waiter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>officiant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unofficial publication expressing the government's point of view</li><li>official government information concerning protocol</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deference, politeness</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) authorities, people close to power</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>offiziös</i>, from French <i>officieux</i> (“unofficial”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose one's mind, to go nuts</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get very surprised</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>фонаре́ть</i> (fonarétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put into shape, to form</li><li>to decorate, to arrange</li><li>to register, to legalize, to formalize, to put (documents, such as visas, passports, etc.) in order, to file</li><li>to hire, to register, to include into payroll or staff</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape</li><li>to legalize one's status, to do the formalities, to go through the formal procedure</li></ol><p><i>офо́рмить</i> (ofórmitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filing (<i>of documents</i>)</li><li>formatting</li><li>decoration (<i>e.g., of showcases</i>)</li><li>design</li></ol><p><i>офо́рмить</i> (ofórmitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put into shape, to form</li><li>to decorate, to arrange</li><li>to register, to legalize, to formalize, to put (documents, such as visas, passports, etc.) in order, to file</li><li>to hire, to register, to include into payroll or staff</li></ol><p><i>офо́рмить</i> (ofórmitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape</li><li>to legalize one's status, to do the formalities, to go through the formal procedure</li></ol><p><i>оформля́ть</i> (oformljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) ophrys (<i>Ophrys</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ophrys</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offside</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>offside</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ophthalmology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>официа́льный</i> (oficiálʹnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) something off-topic (especially online)</li></ol><p>From English <i>off-topic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offshore territory</li><li>offshore company</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>offshore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offshore (referring to offshore enterprises)</li></ol><p><i>офшо́р</i> (ofšór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oh, ah</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oxъ</i>, possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;o</i>, ultimately of onomatopoeic origin. Compare Latin <i>oh</i>, German <i>oh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become boorish, rude</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to describe, to characterize</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>характеризова́ть</i> (xarakterizovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, to moan</li></ol><p><i>ох</i> (ox) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slander, to backbite, to slag off, to find fault (with), to run down</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ха́ять</i> (xájatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coverage, scope</li><li>inclusion</li><li>(<i>military</i>) outflanking, envelopment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embrace</li><li>to include, to take in</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embrace, to include</li></ol><p><i>охвати́ть</i> (oxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to become impudent, brazen or insolent</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, to (nearly) die of hunger, cold, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking hell!, holy shit!, what the fuck!</li></ol><p>From <i>хер</i> (xer) by analogy with охуеть.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool down</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to lose interest (in something)</li></ol><p><i>охладе́ть</i> (oxladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool down</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to lose interest (in something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chill</li><li>to damp down</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хлад</i> (xlad) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool, to cool off, to chill, to quench</li><li>to damp down</li></ol><p><i>охлади́ть</i> (oxladítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coolant</li></ol><p>охлаждающий (oxlaždajuščij, “cooling”) + жидкость (židkostʹ, “liquid, fluid”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) cooling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to reward; to welcome with applause</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also plural</i>) residues from treatment of fibrous materials, in the form of flakes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become intoxicated, to drunk, to tipsy or high</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to become exhilarated</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хмеле́ть</i> (xmelétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ochna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ochna</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὄχνη</i> (ókhnē, “pear tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sigh, to moan</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о́хать</i> (óxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) ochodaeid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ochodaeidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smarten oneself up, to preen (to make oneself look pretty or handsome)</li></ol><p><i>охора́шивать</i> (oxorášivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, inclination, desire</li><li>hunt, hunting</li><li>shooting, game shooting, fowling</li><li>chase</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oxota</i>, derived from <i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ).</p><p>Came into use as taboo avoidance of an earlier word <i>лов</i> (lov); derived from Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li><li>to chase</li></ol><p><i>охо́та</i> (oxóta) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter</li><li>volunteer</li><li>lover <i>(of)</i>, enthusiast <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>охо́та</i> (oxóta) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter-gatherer</li></ol><p>Dvandva compound of <i>охо́тник</i> (oxótnik) +&lrm; <i>собира́тель</i> (sobirátelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>huntress</li><li>female lover (a person who loves something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hunting</li><li>hunter's</li></ol><p><i>охо́тник</i> (oxótnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with pleasure, willingly, readily, gladly</li></ol><p><i>охо́тный</i> (oxótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an extremist, obscurantist, black hundredist. Initially denoted a salesman at a market for game animals (охотный ряд), who were often called upon to quell student demonstrations and organize pogroms.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>willing</li><li>hunting</li></ol><p><i>охо́та</i> (oxóta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Okhotsk, Okhotsky District, Sea of Okhotsk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ochre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guarding, protection</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) guards</li><li>security</li></ol><p>Likely borrowing from Polish <i>ochrona</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safeguarding, protecting</li></ol><p><i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) a guardian, a defender</li><li>(dated,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) an ultraconservative who rejects modernisation</li></ol><p><i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ). Compare with Ukrainian <i>охорони́тель</i> (oxoronýtelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to safeguard, to defend, to preserve (e.g. the environment)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;historical) okhrana ("Department for Protecting the Public Security and Order", the secret police force of the Russian Empire operational from 1866 to 1917)</li></ol><p>From <i>охра́на</i> (oxrána, “guard”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>security guard, watchman (guard)</li><li>bodyguard</li></ol><p><i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) guarding, protection</li></ol><p><i>охра́на</i> (oxrána) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodyguards, security forces</li><li>(politics,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory) a Putinist who defends the actions of the Russian president Putin.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guarded, protected, secured</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to safeguard, to defend, to preserve (e.g. the environment)</li></ol><p><i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to become impudent, brazen, insolent</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, (nearly) to die of hunger, cold, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) what the hell!, holy shit!</li></ol><p>From <i>хрен</i> (xren).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become hoarse</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хри́пнуть</i> (xrípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ochre</li></ol><p><i>о́хр(а)</i> (óxr(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking great</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) extremely, super, very</li></ol><p><i>охуе́нный</i> (oxujénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking great, fucking amazing</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking huge</li></ol><p><i>охуе́ть</i> (oxujétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to become impudent, brazen or insolent</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, to (nearly) die of hunger, cold, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking hell!, holy shit!, what the fuck!</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “prick”) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) marvellous, wonderful, outstanding, splendid</li></ol><p><i>охуе́ть</i> (oxujétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>а́хуй</i> (áxuj)</li></ol><p>Formed from the verb <i>охуеть</i> (oxujetʹ, “to be shocked”), further from the noun <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “dick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch, to scrape</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocelot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evaluation, assessment (appraisal)</li></ol><p><i>оце́нивать</i> (océnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appraise, to price</li><li>to rate</li><li>to evaluate, to estimate</li></ol><p><i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appraise, to price</li><li>to evaluate, to estimate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appreciate (to value highly)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valuation, appraisal, feedback</li><li>estimation, appreciation</li><li>(<i>school</i>) mark, grade (where 5 = A, 1 = F)</li></ol><p><i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>value judgment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>estimated, evaluative, ratable</li></ol><p><i>оце́нка</i> (océnka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appraiser</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) connoisseur</li></ol><p><i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numbness; stupor; torpor</li><li>rigour</li></ol><p><i>оцепене́ть</i> (ocepenétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow torpid, to become rigid, to freeze</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>цепене́ть</i> (cepenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, to encircle, to cordon off</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surrounding, cordoning off</li></ol><p><i>оцепи́ть</i> (ocepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround, to encircle, to cordon off</li></ol><p><i>оцепи́ть</i> (ocepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Biblical expression) vinegar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ocьtъ</i>, from Latin <i>acētum</i>. Doublet of <i>ацето́н</i> (acetón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to zinc, to galvanize with zinc, to electroplate with zinc</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>цинкова́ть</i> (cinkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to zinc, to galvanize with zinc, to electroplate with zinc</li></ol><p><i>оцинкова́ть</i> (ocinkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth</li><li>pocket (an isolated group or area)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) breeding ground, hotbed</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source; Kazakh <i>ошақ</i> (oşaq), compare Kyrgyz <i>очок</i> (ocok), Uzbek <i>oʻchoq</i>, Turkish <i>ocak</i> (“stove”); from Old Turkic <i>očaq</i>, <i>očuq</i> (“stove, oven”). Ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;ōtčak</i> (“hearth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) witchcraft, sorcery</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) charm, allure</li><li>enchantment, fascination</li></ol><p><i>очарова́ть</i> (očarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charmed, taken</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charming, enchanting, adorable, lovely, fascinating</li></ol><p><i>очарова́ть</i> (očarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, to fascinate</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>ча́ры</i> (čáry) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a charming, sweet or adorable person (<i>also about animals, dolls, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>очарова́ть</i> (očarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́шка</i> (-áška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, to fascinate</li></ol><p><i>очарова́ть</i> (očarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyewitness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apparently, obviously</li></ol><p><i>очеви́дный</i> (očevídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evidence, obviousness, openness</li></ol><p><i>очеви́дный</i> (očevídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apparent, obvious (easily discovered or understood&#59; self-explanatory)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>о́ко</i> (óko, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid). Compare Czech <i>očividný</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very, really, much, very much</li></ol><p>Unknown:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>pleased to meet you</li></ol><p>Literally, “very pleasant”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole with hook attached used for catching things, crooked staff for fetching things; crane</li></ol><p>Cognate to Ukrainian <i>оче́па</i> (očépa). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>о́чеп</i> (óčep).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to make a tpyo, to make a tyop</li></ol><p><i>очепя́тка</i> (očepjátka) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); may be viewed as a humorous, self-descriptive alteration of <i>опеча́таться</i> (opečátatʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) a tpyo, a tyop</li></ol><p>A humorous, self-descriptive alteration of <i>опеча́тка</i> (opečátka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>next (<i>in an order or sequence</i>)</li><li>regular (<i>of a regular occurrence according to a schedule</i>)</li><li>(<i>indicating repetitiveness or perfunctoriness</i>) another, yet another, just another, another routine, one more, (<i>in the plural</i>) more, yet more, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>о́чередь</i> (óčeredʹ, “turn, order, rank”) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queue, line</li><li>turn, order, succession</li><li>phase (of a project)</li><li>salvo, volley, burst of fire</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>череда́</i> (čeredá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sketch, essay, feature story</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denigrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denigrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outline (line marking the boundary of an object figure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to become an idiot or a madman</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to hate, to feel aversion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outline, to draw a line around</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outline, to draw a line around</li></ol><p><i>очерти́ть</i> (očertítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sequence, order of priority</li></ol><p><i>очередно́й</i> (očerednój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectacle case</li></ol><p>From <i>очки́</i> (očkí, “glasses, spectacles”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, to cleanse, to purify, to purge</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear oneself</li><li>to clear</li><li>to become purer</li></ol><p><i>очи́стить</i> (očístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleaning, purification, clearing, refinement</li><li>peeling</li><li>(<i>military</i>) mopping-up</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) clearance</li></ol><p><i>очи́стить</i> (očístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cleaning, treatment</li></ol><p><i>очи́ст(ка)</i> (očíst(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonecrop, sedum (<i>Sedum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>очиток</i> (očytok), from <i>очисник</i> (očysnyk, “cleaner”). Further from <i>чи́стий</i> (čýstyj, “clean”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, to cleanse, to purify, to purge</li></ol><p><i>очи́стить</i> (očístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear oneself</li><li>to clear</li><li>to become purer</li></ol><p><i>очища́ть</i> (očiščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refinement, purification, rectification</li><li><i>(spiritual)</i> purification, cleansing, catharsis</li></ol><p><i>очи́стить</i> (očístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) four-eyes (person wearing glasses)</li></ol><p><i>очка́рь</i> (očkárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) four-eyes (person wearning glasses)</li></ol><p><i>очки́</i> (očkí) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bespectacled, four-eyed (wearing glasses)</li></ol><p><i>очки́</i> (očkí) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glasses, spectacles</li></ol><p>From <i>о́чи</i> (óči), plural of archaic <i>о́ко</i> (óko, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sports,&#32;games) point</li><li>spot, pip (<i>on cards or dice</i>)</li><li>hole, peephole</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) eye</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) asshole, anus</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) ability to stay calm and adequate while under pressure in a stressful situation</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) toilet (bowl)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) blackjack</li><li>key of the door, twenty-one in the lotto game</li></ol><p>From <i>о́ко</i> (óko, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó); originally a diminutive.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) to bud graft, to shield bud, to T-bud, to chip bud (to graft using a bud instead of a twig)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be afraid</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to panic, to be scared, to freak out, to show cowardice</li></ol><p><i>очко́</i> (očkó) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) four-eyes (person wearing glasses)</li></ol><p><i>очки́</i> (očkí, “glasses”) +&lrm; <i>-навт</i> (-navt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awaken</li><li>to come to, to gain consciousness</li><li>to come to one's senses</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѡчхнꙋтисѧ</i> (očxnutisę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oťьtnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>face to face</li><li>classroom (of education)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>о́ко</i> (óko) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crazy, insane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to act crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find oneself (suddenly) in a place</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be found (in a place)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>очꙋтитисꙗ</i> (očutitisja, “to find oneself, to come to be”), from Old East Slavic <i>очꙋтити</i> (očutiti, “to notice”) + <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oťutiti</i>. Doublet of <i>ощути́ть</i> (oščutítʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p><p>Unrelated to <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ), <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to come to oneself, to come to one's senses</li></ol><p>From a root related to <i>очну́ться</i> (očnútʹsja) + <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proscribed</i>) alternative form of <i>оче́чник</i> (očéšnik, očéčnik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crazy, crazed, woozy, zonked</li></ol><p><i>ошале́ть</i> (ošalétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шале́ть</i> (šalétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dumbfound, to strike dumb, to flabbergast, to bowl out</li><li>to beat, to bang</li></ol><p><i>ошара́шить</i> (ošarášitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dumbfound, to strike dumb, to flabbergast, to bowl out</li><li>to beat, to bang</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шара́х-</i> (šaráx-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to moor</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>швартова́ть</i> (švartovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar (e.g. on a dog or a cat)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шея</i> (šeja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare Bulgarian <i>нашийник</i> (našijnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stun, to stupefy</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шело́м</i> (šelóm, “helmet (archaic)”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Originally meant "to hit on the helmet" or "knock off the helmet".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stun, daze (the condition of being stunned or dazed)</li></ol><p><i>ошеломи́ть</i> (ošelomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stun, to stupefy</li></ol><p><i>ошеломи́ть</i> (ošelomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, stunning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>agape, stunned (with astonishment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a mistake, to err</li></ol><p><i>ошиби́ться</i> (ošibítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a mistake, to err</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistake, error, fault</li><li>bias</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>ошиба́ться</i> (ošibátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>erroneously, mistakenly (by mistake)</li></ol><p><i>оши́бочный</i> (ošíbočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erroneousness, incorrectness, fallaciousness, mistakenness</li></ol><p><i>оши́бочный</i> (ošíbočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erroneous, mistaken, fallacious</li></ol><p>From <i>оши́бка</i> (ošíbka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to lounge about</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to express disapproval by hissing or noise</li></ol><p>From <i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шикать</i> (šikatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a polar bear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piece, lump, shred</li><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) torn, worn bast shoe</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) pitiful, insignificant remnant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;obsъmetъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;ob-</i> + <i>&#42;sъ-</i> + the combining form of the ancestor of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”) + the ancestor of <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scald (one's hands, etc.) (to burn with boiling liquid or steam)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour boiling water over (<i>often specifically in order to kill something, e.g. an insect</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ошпа́рить</i> (ošpáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scald (one's hands, etc.) (to burn with boiling liquid or steam)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour boiling water over (<i>often specifically in order to kill something, e.g. an insect</i>)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>шпа́рить</i> (špáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fine, to penalize, to cite (issue citation)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>штрафова́ть</i> (štrafovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bristle, to tousle (hair or fur)</li></ol><p><i>ощети́нить</i> (oščetínitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bristle, to tousle (hair or fur)</li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>щети́на</i> (ščetína) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bristle up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to get angry</li></ol><p><i>ощети́нить</i> (oščetínitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel <i>(with fingers)</i></li></ol><p><i>о-</i> (o-) +&lrm; <i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel <i>(with fingers)</i></li></ol><p><i>ощу́пать</i> (oščúpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the use of the sense of touch, the act of groping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with one's sense of touch, by groping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tangibly, perceptibly</li></ol><p><i>ощути́мый</i> (oščutímyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tangible, perceptible, palpable</li></ol><p>From ощутить</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>palpable, perceptible, tangible</li><li>appreciable, noticeable, notable</li></ol><p><i>ощути́ть</i> (oščutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel, to sense, to perceive, to appreciate (to be aware of)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ощоутити</i> (oštutiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oťutiti</i>. Doublet of <i>очути́ться</i> (očutítʹsja), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be felt, to be perceived</li></ol><p><i>ощути́ть</i> (oščutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel, to sense, to perceive, to appreciate (to be aware of)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ощоущати</i> (oštuštati). Doublet of <i>очучать</i> (očučatʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be felt, to be perceived</li></ol><p><i>ощуща́ть</i> (oščuščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensation</li><li>feeling, perception, sense</li></ol><p><i>ощути́ть</i> (oščutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пара́граф</i> (parágraf, “paragraph”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Π</i> (P).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>пара́граф</i> (parágraf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>пиздец</i> (pizdec)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>пизда</i> (pizda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>посёлок городско́го ти́па</i> (posjólok gorodskóvo típa): townlet, urban-type settlement <i>(Russian administrative unit)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>про́шлого ме́сяца</i> (próšlovo mésjaca, “of last month, ult.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>проце́нтный пункт</i> (procéntnyj punkt)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>пу́нкты</i> (púnkty)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>про́шлого го́да</i> (próšlovo góda, “of last year”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>проце́нтный пункт</i> (procéntnyj punkt)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>пу́нкты</i> (púnkty)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>потому́ что</i> (potomú što, “because”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>про́шлого го́да</i> (próšlovo góda, “of last year”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>про́шлого ме́сяца</i> (próšlovo mésjaca, “of last month, ult.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>подполко́вник</i> (podpolkóvnik, “(military rank) lieutenant colonel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>по предъявле́нию</i> (po predʺjavléniju, “at sight, on presentation”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pas, step</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>near-, pseudo-</li><li>step-</li><li>A prefix forming nouns from verbal or adjectival stems</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pa-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, pub (<i>often in a foreign context</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pub</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) a public page (in a social network or a blog)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>public</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peahen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baboon (primate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pavian</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baboon (primate)</li></ol><p><i>павиа́н</i> (pavián) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pavilion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pavillon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacock, peafowl, pheasant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pfäulein</i>, a diminutive of <i>Pfau</i> from Old High German <i>pāwo</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pāwô</i>, itself a borrowing from Latin <i>pāvō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peacock</li></ol><p><i>павли́н</i> (pavlín) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pavlov (common Slavic surname)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pavlov, Pavlovo, Pavlovsk, Pavlovsky (referring to any of various settlements with these names in Russia, Ukraine, the Czech Republic, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>Па́влов</i> (Pávlov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Павловский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pavlovsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flood</li></ol><p><i>па́водок</i> (pávodok) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, freshet, surge (a flood resulting from heavy rain or a spring thaw)</li></ol><p><i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) fallen, like in a battle or in the line of duty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pagination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) pagoda</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great harm, absolute ruin, perdition, destruction; bane</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>пагоуба</i> (paguba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paguba</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>за́губа</i> (záguba), Polish <i>zguba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad, damaging habit (like addiction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pernicious, ruinous, baneful, fatal, baleful</li></ol><p><i>па́губа</i> (páguba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botanical,&#32;collective) windfall</li></ol><p>From <i>па́даль</i> (pádalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) carrion, offal (dead flesh&#59; carcasses)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) an objectionable person</li></ol><p><i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>cadāver</i> (akin to <i>cadō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) scavenger (animal feeding on decaying matter)</li></ol><p><i>па́даль</i> (pádalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to drop, to sink, to decline</li><li><i>(livestock)</i> to die, to perish</li><li>to account for, to constitute, to comprise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;padati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to faint, to swoon (to lose consciousness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) shooting star</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paddock (enclosed area at a racetrack where horses are prepared before a race and unsaddled afterwards)</li><li>similar area at a car racetrack where vehicles are prepared, media is located, etc.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>paddock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>падежь</i> (padežĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;padežь</i>. Doublet of <i>падёж</i> (padjóž), the inherited East Slavic form.</p><p>The sense of “grammatical case” is a calque of Latin <i>cāsus</i>, which is also derived from the verb for falling, <i>cadō</i> (and is itself a calque from Ancient Greek <i>πτῶσις</i> (ptôsis)). Compare Bulgarian <i>паде́ж</i> (padéž), Serbo-Croatian <i>padež</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fall, collapse, drop, sinking</li><li>(<i>government</i>) fall, downfall, overthrow</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) dip</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) incidence</li></ol><p><i>па́д-</i> (pád-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>juvenile&#32;slang</i>) synonym of <i>подъе́зд</i> (podʺjézd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Padishah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian پادشاه (pâdešâh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) bitch; vile, disgusting person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) crud, scum, creep, dirtbag, dirtball, scumbag, scuzzball, sleaze, sleazeball, slimeball</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>padonki (Internet riff-raff, see the plural form падо́нки)</li></ol><p>Deliberate misspelling of <i>подо́нок</i> (podónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) padonki (Internet riff-raff, a Russian-speaking Internet subculture that employs alternative spellings for comic effect, and which delights in the use of profanity and obscene subjects.)</li></ol><p>eye dialect spelling of <i>подо́нки</i> (podónki), a deliberate misspelling of Russian по- (po-, “a bit”) + дно (dno, “bottom”) + the diminutive suffix <b>-ок</b>, plural <b>-ки</b>; various unregulated spellings exist</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holly (genus <i>Ilex</i>)</li></ol><p><i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>дуб</i> (dub, “oak”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepdaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; Old East Slavic <i>дъчери</i> (dŭčeri) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica) or <i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; Old Church Slavonic <i>дъщери</i> (dŭšteri) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica), cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>падъштерица</i> (padŭšterica), Ukrainian <i>па́дчерка</i> (pádčerka), Belarusian <i>па́дчарка</i> (pádčarka), Czech <i>pastorkyně</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a person) that lost good qualities; that underwent moral decline, or character decay</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) (of a woman) who became a prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallen angel (angel who was exiled from heaven)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;East Siberia,&#32;Far East) A deep narrow valley, gorge, or riverbank, especially one that is foresty</li><li>honeydew (sweet, sticky substance deposited on leaves by insects)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>падать</i> (padatʹ, “to fall”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(cattle)</i> murrain, cattle plague, rinderpest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>падежь</i> (padežĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;padežь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž). Doublet of <i>паде́ж</i> (padéž), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) share, interest</li></ol><p><i>пай</i> (paj) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>page, pageboy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>page</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>паж</i> (paž, “page, pageboy”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groove, slot, mortise, rabbet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пазъ</i> (pazŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pazъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jigsaw puzzle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>puzzle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grooving plane, plough</li><li>cat's ear (<i>Hypochaeris</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p><i>паз</i> (paz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bosom</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sinus</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) axil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pazduxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who is obedient, reasonable, well-behaved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>share, interest</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Compare Turkish <i>pay</i>, Southern Altai <i>пай</i> (pay), Tatar <i>пай</i> (pay), Uzbek <i>пай</i> (pay).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldering</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) ration, slang form of <i>паёк</i> (pajók)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pack ice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) depot, entrepôt, goods shed, warehouse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Packhaus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small bag, parcel</li><li>(<i>networking</i>) fragment of data sent over a network, packet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Paket</i> and French <i>paquet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>паке́т</i> (pakét)</li></ol><p><i>паке́т</i> (pakét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) again, more</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>па́ки</i> (páki); cognate with <i>па́че</i> (páče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) time and time again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) regeneration, revival, rebirth, the age to come, the future; afterlife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pakistani</li></ol><p><i>Пакиста́н</i> (Pakistán) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pakistani</li></ol><p><i>Пакиста́н</i> (Pakistán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(paper production, forestry) sorting room</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Packkammer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) piece of oakum</li></ol><p><i>па́кля</i> (páklja) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oakum, tow (bundle of fibers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pack (<i>a bag for a trip, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packing</li></ol><p><i>пакова́ть</i> (pakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dirty</li><li>to harm, to damage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm, to damage</li></ol><p>From <i>па́кость</i> (pákostʹ, “orange”) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filth</li><li>dirty/mean trick</li><li>filthy language</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пакость</i> (pakostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pakostь</i> (“vicissitude”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>превра́тный</i> (prevrátnyj), <i>превра́тность</i> (prevrátnostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pact</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pactum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning</li><li>fire</li><li>grassland fire</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screamer (any bird in the taxonomic family Anhimidae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palanquin</li></ol><p>From Portuguese <i>palanquim</i> (“palanquin, palanquin-bearer”), from Odia <i>ପାଲଙ୍କି</i> (pālaṅki, “litter”), ultimately from Sanskrit <i>पल्यङ्क</i> (palyaṅka), variant of <i>पर्यङ्क</i> (paryaṅka, “bed, couch, litter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stole, tippet (fur or velvet women's scarf worn over the shoulders)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>palatine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stole, tippet</li><li>large-sized (<i>of a stole or scarf</i>)</li></ol><p><i>паланти́н</i> (palantín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lint-free carpet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, from Persian پلاس (pelâs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;plural only) palace</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) chamber, hall</li><li>(<i>healthcare</i>) ward</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) chamber, house</li><li><i>(state institute)</i> chamber, board</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>παλάτι</i> (paláti, “(emperor's) palace”), from Latin <i>palatium</i> (“(emperor's) palace”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>House of Lords</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>House of Commons</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;uncountable&#32;or&#32;countable) palatalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) palatalized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent (portable lodge)</li><li>booth, stall (a small shop)</li></ol><p><i>пала́та</i> (paláta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tent</li></ol><p><i>пала́тка</i> (palátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palazzo (palatial urban building in Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>palazzo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) executioner, hangman</li></ol><p>Often ascribed to Turkish <i>pala</i> (“sword”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač) or <i>&#42;palacı</i> (“sword bearer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backsword, single-edged broadsword (<i>used by heavy cavalry</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pałasz</i>, from Hungarian <i>pallos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(ecology,&#32;geography) palearctic</li></ol><p>From <i>Палеа́рктика</i> (Paleárktika, “Palearctic”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) bait, obvious, blatant</li></ol><p><i>па́лево</i> (pálevo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conflagration, fire</li><li>place where a fire has taken place</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) tricky or risky situation, affair having some likelihood to become ashamingly bait</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) failed situation</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) low-quality liquor, hooch, rotgut, such booze that makes a party fail</li></ol><p><i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-во</i> (-vo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straw-colored, pale yellow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleoanthropologist (<i>male or female</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleoanthropology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleontologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paleontologic</li></ol><p>From <i>палеонтоло́гия</i> (paleontológija, “paleontology”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>палеонтологи́ческой</i> (paleontologíčeskoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleontology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palestinian</li></ol><p><i>Палести́на</i> (Palestína) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Palestinian</li></ol><p><i>Палести́на</i> (Palestína) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finger or toe</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) (crank) pin, finger, cam</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pàlьcь</i> (“thumb, finger”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toe (each of the five digits on the end of the foot) (<i>lit.: finger/digit of the foot</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of Palekh</li></ol><p><i>Па́лех</i> (Pálex) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pali</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palimpsest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παλίμψηστος</i> (palímpsēstos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palindrome</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>παλίνδρομος</i> (palíndromos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front garden</li><li>paling, palisade</li></ol><p>From <i>палисад</i> (palisad) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palette</li></ol><p>From French <i>palette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to singe</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to destroy with fire, to scorch</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to burn (a bonfire, lantern, etc.; for the purposes of lighting)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to scorch, to be very hot (<i>of the sun, the day, etc.</i>)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figurative) to torment (<i>of feelings, experiences</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to fire, to shoot</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to talk very quickly</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to catch (someone) doing something illegal or disallowed, to catch red-handed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;colloquial) to use a sledgehammer to crack a nut</li></ol><p>Literally, “to shoot at sparrows with a cannon”. Likely calqued from a European source; cf. German <i>mit Kanonen auf Spatzen schießen</i> and Swedish <i>skjuta sparvar med kanoner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>mediopassive of <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ, “to put on fire”)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) mediopassive of <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ, “to make bait”), to be bait, to give oneself off</li></ol><p><i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) club, cudgel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;palica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull trout (<i>Salvelinus alpinus</i>, syn. <i>Salvelinus lepechini</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick, cane, club</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) instance of sex, a fuck</li><li>used in the expression</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;palъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) palladium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palladium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palliative care (specialized area of healthcare)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>palliative (reducing progression and relieving symptoms)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>palla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilgrim (traveler to a religious site)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>palmārius</i> (whence also Old French <i>paumier</i>, English <i>palmer</i>, Italian <i>palmiere</i>). By surface analysis, <i>па́льма</i> (pálʹma) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilgrimage</li></ol><p><i>пало́мник</i> (palómnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>па́лка</i> (pálka): (small) stick</li><li>(<i>music</i>) baton</li><li>(<i>music</i>) drumstick</li><li>wand</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) chopstick</li><li>bacillus</li><li>rod (eye cell)</li><li>palochka, the Caucasian Cyrillic letter Ӏ/ӏ</li></ol><p>From <i>па́лка</i> (pálka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking stick, leaf insect <i>(insect)</i></li></ol><p><i>па́лка</i> (pálka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extremely strict, to the point of enforcing by caning (<i>of a discipline or order enforcement</i>)</li></ol><p><i>па́лка</i> (pálka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halibut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Sami language, ultimately from Proto-Samic <i>&#42;pāltēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meat of halibut or turbot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) halibut, turbot</li></ol><p><i>па́лтус</i> (páltus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) deck</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a light rooflike cover on a boat or cart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;palubъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>луб</i> (lub), literally “bast cover”; cognate with Czech <i>paluba</i>, Polish <i>pałuba, pałub</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;nautical) deck</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) decked (having a deck)</li><li>(relational,&#32;aviation) carrier; carrier-based (capable of taking off from and landing on an aircraft carrier)</li></ol><p><i>па́луба</i> (páluba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;palьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба́</i> (-bá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm tree</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) palm oil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>palma</i> (“palm tree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palmyrene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Palmyrene</li></ol><p><i>Пальми́ра</i> (Palʹmíra) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пальми́рский</i> (palʹmírskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) palm (tree)</li></ol><p><i>па́льма</i> (pálʹma) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shoot once, to fire once</li></ol><p><i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>пальто́</i> (palʹtó): (small) overcoat</li></ol><p><i>пальто́</i> (palʹtó) +&lrm; <i>-и́шко</i> (-íško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overcoat, greatcoat, carrick</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>paletot</i>, from Spanish <i>paletoque</i> (“mantlet, short cape”), from Latin <i>palla</i> (“long outer garment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) finger, toe; digital (referring to the fingers or toes)</li></ol><p><i>па́лец</i> (pálec) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>па́лец</i> (pálec): (small) finger, toe</li></ol><p><i>па́лец</i> (pálec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) AA battery</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>па́льчиковая батаре́йка</i> (pálʹčikovaja bataréjka, literally “finger battery”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;diminutive) finger, toe, digit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fingerlike (elongated or oblong in shape)</li></ol><p><i>па́льчик</i> (pálʹčik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>singed, scorched</li><li>fake, counterfeit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pampas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disposable baby diaper/nappy</li></ol><p>First attested in 1992. From English <i>Pampers</i>, a genericized trademark brand of diapers, from English <i>pamper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lampoon, pamphlet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pamphleteer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memo, memorandum</li><li>instruction, written rules, instruction sheet</li></ol><p><i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a retentive memory, retentive</li></ol><p><i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monument, memorial, tombstone, statue</li></ol><p><i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>memorable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pamętьnъ</i>. Cognate with Polish pamiętny.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to remember, to recall</li></ol><p><i>па́мять</i> (pámjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pamętь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) Polish landowner, feudal lord, or gentleman</li><li>(title&#32;or&#32;form of address,&#32;Poland,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Czech Republic&#32;or&#32;Slovakia) sir, mister, gentleman, lord</li><li>(sometimes&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Polack, a Polish person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pan</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Пан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pan</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Πάν</i> (Pán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panama hat</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) swindle</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Панама)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panama (a country in Central America)</li><li>Panama City (the capital city of the country of Panama)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пана́ма</i> (panáma)</li><li>female Panamanian</li></ol><p><i>панама</i> (panama) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>Панама</i> (Panama) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Panamanian</li></ol><p><i>Пана́ма</i> (Panáma) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panacea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pangolin (mammal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pangram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panda (<i>Ailurus fulgens</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) pandeism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pandemic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) pandemic (a pandemic disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Punjabi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pundit (a Hindu scholar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>पण्डित</i> (paṇḍit), from Sanskrit <i>पण्डित</i> (paṇḍita, “scholar, learned man, teacher, philosopher”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ramp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pente douce</i> (“gentle slope”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panegyric, eulogy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panel</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) matrix</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sidewalk (U.S.), pavement (UK)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) panel</li></ol><p><i>пане́ль</i> (panɛ́lʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;Poland</i>) (Polish) young lady, miss; Polish young girl</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>panienka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panettone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>panettone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Poland,&#32;Czech Republic,&#32;Slovakia) pani, Mrs, madam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pani</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being buddy-buddies, (excessive) bonhomie, undue familiarity</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>panie bracie</i> + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo), from Polish <i>być za pan brat</i> (“to be like brothers”). Compare Ukrainian <i>панібра́тство</i> (panibrátstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panic, scare</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from French <i>panique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church chandelier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>πολυκάνδηλον</i> (polukándēlon, literally “many candle thing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to panic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarmist (one who causes others to become alarmed without cause)</li></ol><p><i>па́ника</i> (pánika) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarmism (production of needless alarms)</li></ol><p><i>паникёр</i> (panikjór) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to bread (roll in breadcrumbs)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>panieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral service</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παννυχίδα</i> (pannukhída), from Ancient Greek <i>παννυχίς</i> (pannukhís, “night-festival, vigil”). Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>νύξ</i> (núx), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) funeral service</li></ol><p><i>панихи́да</i> (panixída) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) panic</li><li>panicky</li></ol><p><i>па́н(ика)</i> (pán(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) punk</li><li>(music,&#32;usually in singular) punk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>punk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) punk rock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>punk rock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) wooden bowl</li></ol><p>Cognate with Bulgarian <i>паница</i> (panica, “bowl”), Serbo-Croatian <i>паница</i>/<i>panica</i> (“bowl”), Slovene <i>ponev</i> (“pan”), Slovak <i>panva</i> (“bowl, bassin”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pancake</li></ol><p>From Borrowed from English <i>pancake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pancreatitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a large) picture on a canvas, a decorative picture on a wall, ceiling, etc.</li><li>panel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>panneau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panorama</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panpsychism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pansexuality</li></ol><p><i>пансексуа́льный</i> (pansɛksuálʹnyj, “pansexual”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ity”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pansexual (having a sexual interest in all genders)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) boarding school</li><li>boardinghouse</li><li>board and lodging</li><li>(<i>tourism</i>) meals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pension</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>pendō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiday hotel, boarding house</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pensionnat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) long underwear, long johns, that is worn either under normal clothing in cold weather, or as a night dress.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pantalons</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) pantheism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantheist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantheon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panther</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantomime</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pantomime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) armored warrior (a warrior equipped with a cuirass or other body armor)</li><li>gar (a North or Central American fish of the family Lepisosteidae)</li></ol><p><i>па́нцирь</i> (páncirʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) coat of mail, hauberk</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) cuirass, armor (any type of armor protecting the torso)</li><li>shell (<i>of an arthropod or turtle</i>)</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) Pantsir missile system</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Low German <i>panser</i> or Polish <i>pancerz</i>, from Middle High German <i>panzier</i> (whence German <i>Panzer</i>). Both German forms stem from Old French <i>pancier</i>, from Latin <i>pantex</i>. The earlier form is <i>пансꙑрь</i> (pansyrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dad, daddy</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) male socket</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) pope</li><li>(archaic,&#32;childish,&#32;dialectal) bread, <i>especially</i> white bread</li><li>(archaic,&#32;childish,&#32;dialectal) food</li></ol><p>A Lallwort. Perhaps borrowed from French <i>papa</i>; obsolete variant <i>папа́</i> (papá) is certainly French.</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pāpa</i> (“pope”), from Koine Greek <i>πάπας</i> (pápas); originally a Lallwort. Doublet of поп (pop, “priest”).</p><p>A Lallwort; perhaps of native origin. Many other similar examples are present cross-linguistically; see the entry for English pap for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman Catholic pope</li></ol><p><i>па́па</i> (pápa, “pope”) +&lrm; <i>ри́мский</i> (rímskij, “Roman”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papaya (fruit or tree)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>papaya</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paparazzo, paparazzi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>paparazzi</i>, plural of <i>paparazzo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papakha, calpac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>papaq</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) same as (папа) dad</li><li>familiar term of address for an (elderly) man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dad</li></ol><p><i>папа́ша</i> (papáša) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portico of a church</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>папьрть</i> (papĭrtĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;papьrtь</i>, from <i>&#42;pa-</i> + <i>&#42;pьrtь</i> (“front”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>папръть</i> (paprŭtĭ), Bulgarian <i>папрът</i> (paprǎt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) patron, sponsor (<i>especially if well-connected; often of criminal bosses</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) sugar daddy</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>па́па</i> (pápa, “daddy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) dad’s, father’s</li></ol><p><i>па́па</i> (pápa) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papirosa <i>(strong Russian cigarette made with dark tobacco, no filter)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>papieros</i>, from <i>papier</i> (“paper”) fused with Spanish <i>cigarros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>папиро́са</i> (papirósa): cigarette (strong Russian type made with dark tobacco, no filter)</li></ol><p><i>папиро́са</i> (papirósa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cigarette (<i>with a cardboard holder</i>)</li></ol><p><i>папиро́са</i> (papirósa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigarette case</li></ol><p><i>папиро́са</i> (papirósa) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papyrus (a plant in the sedge family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daddy</li><li>folder (an organizer for storing papers)</li><li>file (things stored in a folder)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) folder (directory or virtual container in a file system)</li></ol><p><i>па́па</i> (pápa, “dad”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>From German <i>Pappe</i> (“cardboard”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fern</li></ol><p><i>па́пороть</i> (páporotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonstandard form of <i>папоротник</i> (paporotnik)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paportь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>па́па</i> (pápa): daddy</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>па́пка</i> (pápka): (small) folder</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>па́пка</i> (pápka): (small) file</li></ol><p><i>па́па</i> (pápa) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p><p><i>па́пка</i> (pápka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red pepper, paprika</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>Paprika</i>, from Serbo-Croatian <i>па̀прика</i>, from <i>па̏пар</i> (“pepper”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьpьrь</i>, from Latin <i>piper</i> (“pepper”), from Ancient Greek <i>πέπερι</i> (péperi), from Sanskrit <i>पिप्पलि</i> (pippali, “long pepper”). Doublet of <i>пе́рец</i> (pérec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>papal</li></ol><p><i>па́па</i> (pápa) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papuan</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) savage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papule</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papier-mâché</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>papier mâché</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam, vapor</li><li>fallow (fallow land)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;parъ</i>, <i>&#42;para</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair, couple</li><li>the other, second object associated with another to form a pair; the fellow of an object in a set of two</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) equal, match, equivalent</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) a few, not many</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) two, around two</li><li>two-hour lecture (<i>e.g. for a university or college class</i>)</li><li>(clothing,&#32;now&#32;rare) suit</li><li><i>(mark)</i> two <i>(out of five)</i>, poor; D mark, D grade</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>para</i>, from Middle High German <i>pâr</i> (German <i>Paar</i>), from Latin <i>pār</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a couple, two or three</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;geometry) parabola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Paraguayan</li></ol><p><i>Парагва́й</i> (Paragváj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) parahormone</li></ol><p><i>пара-</i> (para-, “para-”) +&lrm; <i>гормо́н</i> (gormón, “hormone”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parade</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>military</i>) military parade</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;linguistics) paradigm</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>paradīgma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>παράδειγμα</i> (parádeigma, “pattern”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) paradise</li><li>(poetic,&#32;archaic) Saint Petersburg</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;archaic) the upper gallery in a theatre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>paradis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) front door</li></ol><p><i>пара́д</i> (parád) +&lrm; <i>-ное</i> (-noje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parade</li><li>front, main (entrance)</li><li>gala, for show</li><li>formal, full (of dress, uniform)</li></ol><p><i>пара́д</i> (parád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paradox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paradoxicality</li></ol><p><i>парадокса́льный</i> (paradoksálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paradoxical</li></ol><p>From French <i>paradoxal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пара́д</i> (parád, “parade”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) parasite (organism)</li><li>parasite (person)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) bad person</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) filler word</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parasite</i>. Further from Latin <i>parasītus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>παράσιτος</i> (parásitos), from <i>παρά</i> (pará) + <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos). Compare typologically <i>нахле́бник</i> (naxlébnik) (&lt; <i>хлеб</i> (xleb)).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>cizopasník</i>, i.e. **чужепасник (akin to <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) and <i>пасти́</i> (pastí)), Slovene <i>zajedavec</i>, i.e. **заедавец, **заедалец (akin to <i>заеда́ть</i> (zajedátʹ), also note <i>zajedavsto</i> (“parasitism”), i.e. **заедавство, **заедальство). Also note now rare <i>чужея́д</i> (čužejád), <i>чужее́д</i> (čužejéd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to paralyze, to palsy</li><li>to petrify</li><li>to paralyze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paralysis, palsy</li></ol><p>First attested in 1659, first as <b>парали́ж</b>, most likely from Polish <i>paraliż</i> or Middle High German <i>раrаlis</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>paralysis</i> and Ancient Greek <i>παράλυσις</i> (parálusis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) parallelepiped, parallelopipedon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) parallelogram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) parallel (line)</li><li>parallel (line of latitude)</li><li>parallel (line of reasoning similar to another one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in parallel</li></ol><p><i>паралле́льный</i> (parallélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>parallel</li></ol><p><i>паралле́ль</i> (parallélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) paralogism (fallacious argument or illogical conclusion, especially one committed by mistake, or believed by the speaker to be logical)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>παραλογισμός</i> (paralogismós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parameter (various senses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paramecium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burqa, burka</li><li>paranja</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>пәрәнҗә</i> (päräncä), from Kazakh <i>паранжы</i> (paranjy), <i>паранжа</i> (paranja), from Uzbek <i>paranji</i>, from Tajik <i>фаранҷӣ</i> (faranji), from Persian فرجی (faraji), from Ottoman Turkish فراجه (ferace).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paranoiac</li></ol><p><i>парано́йя</i> (paranójja) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) paranoia</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>παράνοια</i> (paránoia, “madness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paranormal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parapet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parapet</i>, from Italian <i>parapetto</i>, from Italian <i>parare</i> (“to shield”) + Italian <i>petto</i>, from Latin <i>parō</i> + Latin <i>pectus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paraglider</li></ol><p>From French <i><b>para</b>chute</i> + <i><b>plan</b>eur</i>. Analyzable as a blend of <i>парашю́т</i> (parašút) +&lrm; <i>плани́ровать</i> (planírovatʹ) or a blend of <i>парашю́т</i> (parašút) +&lrm; <i>планёр</i> (planjór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>milling</i>) rind, millrind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) parataxis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) para-fascism</li></ol><p>Internationalism; compare English <i>para-fascism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paraphimosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paraffin</li><li>paraffin wax</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Paraffin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paraphrase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetaminophen, paracetamol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) close-stool in a prison</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bullshit, nonsense, lies, false rumors</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) toilet room (<i>especially a poorly cleaned or poorly equipped one</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Пара́ша</i> (Paráša), according to the popular name of the holiday of Saint Paraskeva of the Balkans – Параске́ва Грязни́ха (celebrated on 27 October); the name was given due to the autumn mud and bad weather.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Параша)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) The folk holiday of Saint Paraskeva of the Balkans, falling on October 17.</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;sarcastic) Russia.</li></ol><p>Derived from Параске́ва Грязни́ха (Paraskéva Grjazníxa), the holiday of Saint Paraskeva of the Balkans.</p><p>Influenced by <i>пара́ша</i> (paráša, “a prison toilet, after the rainy conditions from Paraskeva's feast day”) +&lrm; <i>Ра́ша</i> (Ráša, “Russia”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parachute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parachute</i>, from <i>para-</i> (“protection against”) (as in <i>parasol</i>) and <i>chute</i> (“fall”) (<&lt; Latin <i>cadō</i>).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>padák</i>, Macedonian <i>падобран</i> (padobran), Polish <i>spadochron</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>падобран</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parachutist, parachuter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parachute</li></ol><p><i>парашю́т</i> (parašút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sorry, excuse me</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pardon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pareidolia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) didactic text containing passages from the Scriptures</li><li>(linguistics,&#32;rare) proverb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παροιμία</i> (paroimía, “proverb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) didactic text containing passages from the Scriptures</li><li>(linguistics,&#32;rare) proverb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παροιμία</i> (paroimía, “proverb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow, lad, chap, guy, bloke, boy</li><li>boyfriend</li></ol><p>From <i>па́рубень</i> (párubenʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paorbьňь</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Парень)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paren River (in Kamchatka)</li><li>Paren, a small Koryak village in Kamchatka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>па́рень</i> (párenʹ)</li></ol><p><i>па́рень</i> (párenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bet, wager (an agreement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pari</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parisian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Paris, Parisian</li></ol><p><i>Пари́ж</i> (Paríž) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gonorrhea <i>(jocular, euphemism)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wig</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>perruque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friseur, barber, hairstylist, hairdresser</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Perückenmacher</i> (“wig-maker”), from French <i>perruque</i> (“wig”) + <i>Macher</i> (“maker”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbershop, beauty parlor</li></ol><p><i>парикма́хер</i> (parikmáxer) +&lrm; <i>-ская</i> (-skaja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam bath, steam room, sauna</li><li>room for steaming things (<i>in a factory</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka), with stress shift.</p><p><i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweating room</li></ol><p><i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to parry, to counter, to retort</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>parieren</i>. Further from Italian <i>parare</i>, from Latin <i>parō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Parität</i>, itself from Late Latin <i>paritas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steam, to stew</li><li>to steam out, to sweat out</li><li>to beat someone with a banny venik</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to vape</li><li>to plane, to glide</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pariti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a steam bath</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be exposed to heat</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) to sweat (an issue), to languish (over), to fuss (over), to agonise (over), to be nervous (about) (with various prepositions)</li></ol><p><i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pariah (member of an oppressed caste in India)</li><li>pariah (outcast and other senses)</li></ol><p>See <i>pariah</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>park (for recreation)</li><li>yard, depot</li><li>fleet, stock</li><li>depot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Park</i>, English <i>park</i> or French <i>parc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusement park</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusement park</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steaming</li><li>parka (long jacket)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parquet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parquet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a crossover, a CUV (an SUV-styled automobile unsuitable for off-road use)</li></ol><p><i>парке́т</i> (parkét) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik); from the colloquial expression <i>парке́тный внедоро́жник</i> (parkétnyj vnedoróžnik, literally “on a parquet floor”), meaning an SUV suitable only for use on paved roads.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parquet</li></ol><p><i>парке́т</i> (parkét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parking, parking lot, parking space</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>parking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to park (a car, a disk drive head)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to park (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>паркова́ть</i> (parkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parking (space or action)</li></ol><p><i>паркова́ть</i> (parkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) park</li></ol><p><i>парк</i> (park) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) parkour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parkourist</li></ol><p><i>парку́р</i> (parkúr) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parkour</li></ol><p><i>парку́р</i> (parkúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parkourist</li></ol><p><i>парку́р</i> (parkúr) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parliament</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parlement</i>, from Old French <i>parlement</i> (“parliament”), twice-borrowed from Middle English <i>parlement</i> (“discussion; parliament”), itself from Old French <i>parlement</i> (“discussion”), from Old French <i>parler</i> (“to speak”) + <i>-ment</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>parabolō</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parliamentarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parliament; parliamentarian, parliamentary</li></ol><p><i>парла́мент</i> (parláment) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parmesan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parmesan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam room <i>(in a bath)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot used for steaming</li><li>greenhouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) greenhouse</li></ol><p><i>парни́к</i> (parník) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greenhouse gas</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>greenhouse gas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greenhouse effect</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>greenhouse effect</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lad, boy</li></ol><p><i>па́рень</i> (párenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh, still warm with body heat (<i>of animal products</i>)</li><li>hot and close, warm and damp, sultry, muggy (<i>of air</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пар</i> (par) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artiodactyl (any ungulate mammal with an even number of toes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pair, twin</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) conjugate</li><li>(art,&#32;sleight) pair-horse</li><li>(rowing,&#32;relational) scull</li></ol><p><i>па́ра</i> (pára) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steamer (cooking appliance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>парова́я</i> (parovája) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam locomotive</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any locomotive</li></ol><p>From <i>пар</i> (par, “steam”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-воз</i> (-voz), combining form of <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ, “to transport”).</p><p>Coined in 1836 by Nikolay Gretsch.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>парово́з</i> (parovóz, “steam locomotive”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chain, procession (<i>of people, cars, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) method of smoking drugs involving blowing smoke from one person's mouth to another</li></ol><p><i>парово́з</i> (parovóz) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) locomotive</li></ol><p><i>парово́з</i> (parovóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>парово́з</i> (parovóz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steam</li><li>fallow</li></ol><p><i>пар</i> (par) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam boiler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to parody, to mimic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parody</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пар</i> (par)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carriage drawn by two horses</li></ol><p><i>пара</i> (para, “a pair”) +&lrm; <i>конь</i> (konʹ, “a stallion”) +&lrm; <i>ка</i> (ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paroxysm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>password</li></ol><p>From French <i>parole</i> (“promise, word”), most likely through Dutch <i>parool</i> (“password, promise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pormъ</i>. This is an instance in which the vowel reduction <i>о</i> (o) &rarr; <i>а</i> (a) is reflected in spelling: the expected spelling is <i>поро́м</i> (poróm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferryman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) paronym (a confusing word that sounds like another word)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>παρά</i> (pará, “near”) + <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”). Compare <i>πᾰρώνῠμον</i> (părṓnŭmon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>па̀ро-</i> (pàro-) +&lrm; <i>турби́на</i> (turbína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steamboat, steamer, steamship</li></ol><p>From <i>пар</i> (par, “steam”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ход</i> (&#42;xod), a deverbal agentive form of <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>парохо́д</i> (paroxód, “steamboat”)</li></ol><p><i>парохо́д</i> (paroxód) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steamboat</li></ol><p><i>парохо́д</i> (paroxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(steamship) line; steam navigation</li><li>shipping company</li></ol><p><i>парохо́д</i> (paroxód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>па́ра</i> (pára)</li></ol><p><i>па́ра</i> (pára) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>programming</i>) to parse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>parse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;painting) parsuna (17th-century icon-style Russian painted portrait)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Middle Russian <i>парсуна</i> (parsuna, “person; portrait; hypostasis”), also <i>порсуна</i> (porsuna) (att. mid-16th c.), from Old Ruthenian <i>парсуна</i> (parsuna), <i>порсуна</i> (porsuna), from Middle Polish <i>parsuna</i> (“person; hypostasis”), from Old Polish <i>parsuna</i> (“person; hypostasis”), from Latin <i>persona</i>. Doublet of <i>персо́на</i> (persóna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school desk</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from German <i>apart</i> (“individual, separate”) in 1665, further from French <i>à part</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) tattoo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party membership card, membership card of a political party</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йный биле́т</i> (partíjnyj bilét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Party Bureau, "partbyuro" (the leading elective body of the primary party organization of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union)</li><li>meeting of this body</li><li>a similar organization in any institute</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йное бюро́</i> (partíjnoje bjuró, “party bureau”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) the stalls, parquet, orchestra seats</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) informal party for young people, get-together</li></ol><p>Transcription of English <i>party</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party member (member of a political party)</li></ol><p><i>па́ртия</i> (pártija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guerrilla, partisan (armed insurgent or irregular fighting behind occupying enemy lines)</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) partisan (fervent supporter of an idea or a cause)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>partisan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>partisan</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) guerrilla</li></ol><p><i>партиза́н</i> (partizán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guerrilla warfare</li><li>partisanship</li></ol><p><i>партизан</i> (partizan) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) party affiliation</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) party spirit, partisanship</li><li>occurrence of party principles (<i>in literature or art</i>)</li></ol><p><i>парти́йный</i> (partíjnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;politics) party</li><li>loyal to the party line</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party member</li></ol><p>From <i>па́ртий-</i> (pártij-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) partita</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>partita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) partitive (partitive case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) score</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party (political group)</li><li>lot, batch, consignment, parcel</li><li>(<i>military</i>) detachment</li><li>(<i>music</i>) part</li><li>game, set, match</li><li>match (a candidate for matrimony)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>partia</i> or German <i>Partie</i>, from French <i>partie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йный комите́т</i> (partíjnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partner, associate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>partenaire</i> and adapted to the suffix <i>-ёр</i> (-jór), as if from a French <i>&#42;parteneur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) partner, affiliate, partnership</li></ol><p><i>партнёр</i> (partnjór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partnership</li><li>(economics,&#32;law) partnership</li></ol><p><i>партнёр</i> (partnjór) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party coordinator/organizer</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йный организа́тор</i> (partíjnyj organizátor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>party organization</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йная организа́ция</i> (partíjnaja organizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) party meeting, party gathering, party conference (gathering consisting of members of a political party)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>парти́йное собра́ние</i> (partíjnoje sobránije, “party meeting”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>па́ртия</i> (pártija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young Ukrainian man</li><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine) youth, guy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>па́рубок</i> (párubok). See also <i>ребёнок</i> (rebjónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sail</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>парусъ</i> (parusŭ), of uncertain origin. Either to be connected to Livonian <i>pūŗaz</i> (“sail”), Latvian <i>bura</i> (“sail”), Lithuanian <i>bùrė</i> (“sail”), or borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φᾶρος, φάρος</i> (phâros, pháros, “large cloth, spread”), for which latter compare <i>у́ксус</i> (úksus, “vinegar”) and <i>па́рдус</i> (párdus, “pard”), known Greek borrowings.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailcloth</li></ol><p>From <i>па́рус</i> (párus) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) canvas, sailcloth</li></ol><p>From <i>паруси́н(а)</i> (parusín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing ship (type of ship)</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sailfish</li><li>(<i>butterfly</i>) swallowtail</li></ol><p><i>па́рус</i> (párus) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing ship (type of ship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sail, sailing</li></ol><p><i>па́рус</i> (párus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) perfume, cologne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parfum</i>, possibly through intermediary German <i>Parfüm</i>, which better accounts for the transliteration.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfumer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>parfumeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfumery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parthian (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>favose, scabby</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mangy, lousy</li></ol><p><i>парша́</i> (paršá) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brocade (fabric)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پارچه (parça), from Persian پارچه (pârče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brocade (woven fabric)</li></ol><p><i>парча́</i> (parčá) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) favus (fungal skin disease)</li><li>(<i>phytopathology</i>) scab (fungal disease of plants)</li></ol><p>First attested 18th century. Cognate with <i>пе́рхоть</i> (pérxotʹ, “dandruff”) and ultimately derives from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrxъ</i>, but the exact etymology is unclear: the term might either be inherited from Old East Slavic or borrowed from Polish <i>parch</i>. Compare with Ukrainian <i>па́рша</i> (párša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoundrel</li></ol><p><i>парши́вый</i> (paršívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scabby, mangy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nasty, rotten, lousy</li></ol><p><i>парша́</i> (paršá, “mange”) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) guy, fellow, bloke, boyfriend</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>па́рень</i> (párenʹ); ultimately, from a diminutive of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paorbьňь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>volleyball</i>) set</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) pass (declining to play in one's turn)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) pass (the passing of the ball)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apiary</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pasěka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beekeeper, bee-master</li></ol><p><i>па́сека</i> (páseka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) apiary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) pronunciation spelling of <i>спаси́бо</i> (spasíbo); sank you, thank ya, thanksh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paskha (Easter dessert)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pascal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pascal</i>, named after French&#32;scientist and philosopher&#32;Blaise Pascal.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libel, libelous or defamatory piece of journalism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pasquill</i>, from Italian <i>pasquillo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a vile person, bastard, scoundrel, asshole</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) filth, abomination</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paskǫda</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>па́скуд</i> (páskud), <i>паску́да</i> (paskúda), Belarusian <i>по́скудзь</i> (póskudzʹ), Polish <i>paskuda</i>, Old East Slavic <i>паскудьнъ</i> (paskudĭnŭ, “poor”) (compare modern Russian <i>паску́дный</i> (paskúdnyj, “low, mean”)). By surface analysis, <i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>ску́д(ный)</i> (skúd(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting, repulsive, vile</li></ol><p><i>паску́да</i> (paskúda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filth, vileness</li></ol><p><i>паску́дный</i> (paskúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) or</p><p><i>паску́да</i> (paskúda) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, cloudy, overcast</li><li>gloomy, sullen</li></ol><p><i>па́смурь</i> (pásmurʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). See <i>хмурый</i> (xmuryj, “cloudy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(cards)</i> to pass</li><li>to surrender <i>(to)</i>, capitulate <i>(before)</i>, give way <i>(to)</i>, give in <i>(to)</i>, to give up, cave in, cry uncle</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to pass, to make a pass</li></ol><p><i>пас</i> (pas) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passport</li><li>ID card (<i>Russia</i>)</li><li>(product) certificate, manual</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>passeport</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) passport</li></ol><p><i>па́спорт</i> (pásport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arcade, passage (covered passage, usually with shops on both sides)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) passage</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) unexpected turn <i>(of events)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>passage</i>. Further from Latin <i>passus</i> (<~ <i>pandō</i> + <i>-tus</i>) + <i>-āre</i> + <i>-āticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passenger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Passagier</i>, from French <i>passager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) passenger, commuter</li></ol><p><i>пассажи́р</i> (passažír) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airliner (passenger aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade wind (steady wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>passaat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(combination) pliers</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pince à tige</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweating (the gentle heating of vegetables or flour in oil or butter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweating (the gentle heating of vegetables or flour in oil or butter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passive-aggressive (passive resistance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passiveness, passivity</li></ol><p><i>пасси́вный</i> (passívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a human with a lot of inner energy, strong will and hot temperament</li><li>the same as above, but set in a view of the controversial theory of ethnogenesis created by Lev Gumilev</li><li>the term from this theory used in a political context</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion, flame (object of passionate love or strong interest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paste</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pasta (<i>considered non-standard in this sense</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ink for a pen, ink refill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture</li></ol><p><i>паст-</i> (past-) +&lrm; <i>-бище</i> (-bišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pasture</li></ol><p><i>па́стбищ(е)</i> (pástbišč(e)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) congregation, flock, parish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastel</li><li>crayon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasteurization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pasteurize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsnip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pasternak</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>pastināca</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed <i>полево́й борщ</i> (polevój boršč), <i>попо́вник</i> (popóvnik), <i>козелки́</i> (kozelkí), <i>ствольё</i> (stvolʹjó), Slovene <i>rebrinec</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Пастернак)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Pasternak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graze, to pasture, to shepherd</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to follow, to tail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pastì</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastille (a soft flavoured&#47;flavored sweet&#47;candy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pastille</i> or Italian <i>pastiglia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graze (to eat grass from a pasture)</li></ol><p><i>пасти́</i> (pastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) pastor, shepherd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pastor</li></ol><p><i>па́стор</i> (pástor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herder, herdsman</li><li>shepherd</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пастухъ</i> (pastuxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pastuxъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пасти́</i> (pastí) +&lrm; <i>-ух</i> (-ux).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shepherd</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shepherd's</li></ol><p><i>пасту́х</i> (pastúx) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пасту́х</i> (pastúx): young shepherd</li><li>rail, railbird (<i>type of bird</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пасту́х</i> (pastúx) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пастушо́к</i> (pastušók), which is in turn a diminutive of <i>пасту́х</i> (pastúx): shepherd boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pastor; pastor's; pastoral (related to a pastor)</li></ol><p><i>па́стырь</i> (pástyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) pastor, shepherd</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пастꙑрь</i> (pastyrĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pastyřь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to fall</li><li>(of а decision,&#32;especially by lot) to fall on an option</li><li>(figurative,&#32;higher register) to fall, to die on the battlefield, to be killed in action</li><li>(figurative,&#32;formal,&#32;of historical states, governments) to fall, to lose power, to cease to exist, to be toppled</li><li>(figurative,&#32;formal,&#32;of historical cities, castles) to fall, to give way under siege</li><li>(figurative,&#32;formal) to stoop, to lower one's morals</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth, maw, jaws (of an animal, reptile or fish)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) mouth, piehole</li><li>throat</li><li>wide inlet on a device, structure or construction</li><li>trap, snare</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пасти</i> (pasti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pàsti</i> (“to fall”).</p><p>Attested since 1731. Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pastь</i>, from <i>&#42;pasti</i> (“to fall”) + <i>&#42;-tь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paskha (Easter dessert)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Пасха)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Easter</li><li>Passover</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πάσχα</i> (páskha, “Passover, Easter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Easter</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Passover</li><li>paschal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Latin <i>paschālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepson</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pasynъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>сын</i> (syn, “son”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solitaire, patience (card game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>patience</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patience (card game)</li></ol><p><i>пасья́нс</i> (pasʹjáns) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) rat (rodent)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>пацю́к</i> (pacjúk). Cognates include Belarusian <i>пацу́к</i> (pacúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chess,&#32;also&#32;figurative) stalemate</li><li><i>(sort of fruit jelly)</i> paste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pat</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>pâte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patent, licence</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>littera patēns</i> via German <i>Patent</i> and French <i>lettre patente</i>. First used in 1705 under Peter I.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patent</li></ol><p><i>пате́нт</i> (patént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to patent, to take out a patent <i>(for, on)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>пате́нт</i> (patént) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emotionality, pompousness, histrionics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) full of pathos, impassioned, passionate, emotional, pathetic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>pathét(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij); ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>παθητικός</i> (pathētikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) portable gramophone, phonograph</li></ol><p>From the French <i>Pathéphone</i> (trade mark) for a phonograph made by the Pathé Brothers Company.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) informal party for young people, get-together</li></ol><p>Transcription of English <i>party</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A male homosexual, particularly one who assumes the passive role in sex; a bottom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παθικός</i> (pathikós), cf. Latin <i>pathicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patina</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>patina</i>, from Latin <i>patina</i> (“dish, pan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pachinko</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese パチンコ (<i>pachinko</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pattypan squash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy</li></ol><p><i>па́тл(ы)</i> (pátl(y)) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hairy, shaggy, having long (usually unkempt) hair</li></ol><p><i>па́тлы</i> (pátly) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long, unkempt hair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;chess,&#32;also&#32;figurative) stalemate</li></ol><p><i>пат</i> (pat) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathogen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) pathogenesis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pathogenese</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>molasses; treacle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pathological (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>патоло́гия</i> (patológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>патологоанато́мия</i> (patologoanatómija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathoanatomy</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>патологи́ческая анато́мия</i> (patologíčeskaja anatómija, “pathological anatomy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) maltodextrin</li></ol><p><i>па́тока</i> (pátoka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;colloquial) alternative form of <i>портре́т</i> (portrét, “portrait”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patriarch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patriarchal</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>patriarchālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patriarchy</li><li>patriarchate</li></ol><p><i>патриарх</i> (patriarx) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) patriarchate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patriarch's</li></ol><p><i>патриа́рх</i> (patriárx) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patriot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>patriote</i>, from Late Latin <i>patriōta</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πατριώτης</i> (patriṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patriotism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>patriotisme</i>. By surface analysis, <i>патрио́т</i> (patriót) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm). Compare Ukrainian <i>патріоти́зм</i> (patriotýzm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patriotic</li></ol><p><i>патрио́т</i> (patriót) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrioticness (the quality or state of being patriotic or having patriotism)</li></ol><p>From <i>патриоти́чный</i> (patriotíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patriotic</li><li>characteristic of patriotism</li><li>imbued with patriotism</li></ol><p><i>патрио́т</i> (patriót) +&lrm; <i>-ичный</i> (-ičnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic&#32;or&#32;derogatory</i>) to feign being a patriot, especially in an ostentatious or self-indulgent manner</li></ol><p>From <i>патрио́т</i> (patriót) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartridge, shell, or round, consisting of the assembled bullet, casing, propellant, and primer</li><li>(<i>mechanical engineering</i>) chuck</li><li>lamp socket</li><li>tailor's pattern</li><li>patron, boss</li><li>patron saint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Patrone</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>patrōnus</i>, from Latin <i>pater</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>patron</i>. Further from Latin <i>patrōnus</i> (the same as above).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patronage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>patronage</i>. Further from Latin <i>patrōnus</i> + <i>-āticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) bandolier, cartridge/ammunition belt, ammunition bag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Patrontasche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jet, nipple, nozzle, socket, a short pipe</li></ol><p><i>па-</i> (pa-) +&lrm; <i>труба́</i> (trubá, “pipe”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrol, patrolling</li></ol><p><i>патрули́ровать</i> (patrulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to patrol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patrol</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>patrouille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patrol</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man on patrol, patrolman</li></ol><p><i>патру́ль</i> (patrúlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pattern (intelligible arrangement which has a mathematical, geometric, statistical etc. relationship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pattern</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>patrōnus</i> (whence also <i>патро́н</i> (patrón)), futher from Latin <i>pater</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ма́трица</i> (mátrica) &lt; Latin <i>mātrīx</i> &lt; <i>māter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) patch (piece of data intended to modify a computer file by replacing a part of it)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>patch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pause, interval; rest</li><li>(<i>music</i>) rest (pause of a specified length in a piece of music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pausa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mean, greedy person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>паукъ</i> (paukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paǫkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) wolf spider</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ant spider, zodariid</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>муравье́д</i> (muravʹjéd, “anteater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarantula</li></ol><p>From <i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca, “bird”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”). Literally, “bird-eater spider”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) lynx spider</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>ры́сь</i> (rýsʹ, “lynx”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jumping spider (Salticidae)</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>скаку́н</i> (skakún, literally “jumper, hopper”), literally “hopper-spider”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) paucal</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>paucālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arachnid</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz, “shape, image”) +&lrm; <i>-ное</i> (-noje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Arachnida</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiderlike, spidery</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz, “shape, image”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pauropod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pauropoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiderweb, cobweb</li></ol><p>This form arose by confusion between Old East Slavic <i>паучина</i> (paučina) (a form still found in dialects), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paǫčina</i>, and dialectal <i>пути́на</i> (putína), which is related to the word <i>пу́ты</i> (púty, “fetters”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>паути́на</i> (pautína, “web”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) knit scarf, etc. of very thin filaments</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) type of very thin silk stockings</li></ol><p><i>паути́на</i> (pautína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) cortinar, webcap (<i>Cortinarius</i> sp.)</li></ol><p><i>паути́на</i>&nbsp;f (pautína, “spiderweb, cobweb”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cobweb</li></ol><p><i>паути́на</i> (pautína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>пау́к</i> (paúk): spider</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spider; spider's, spidery</li></ol><p><i>паук</i> (pauk) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>пау́к</i> (paúk): spider</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiderling, spideret</li></ol><p><i>пау́к</i> (paúk, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>-онок</i> (-onok, forms terms for baby animals).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) powerbank</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>powerbank</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pathos, enthusiasm</li><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) pretentiousness, exaggerated drama</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>πάθος</i> (páthos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretentious, pompous</li></ol><p><i>па́фос</i> (páfos) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paxъ</i>, <i>&#42;paxa</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>паха́</i> (paxá), Bulgarian <i>пах</i> (pah), Czech <i>pach</i>, Polish <i>pacha</i></p><p>Vasmer proposes as well a connection with Sanskrit <i>पक्ष</i> (pakṣa, “(bird)wing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;criminal slang) big shot, don, underworld boss</li><li>(slang,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) father</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Stalin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plowman</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) farmer</li></ol><p><i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ, “to plow”) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plow/plough</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to work hard</li><li>(regional,&#32;Arkhangelsk) to sweep, to blow through (of weather), to carry with the wind</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;archaic) to flutter, to flap, to fly (in the wind)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;archaic,&#32;impersonal) to blow gently on all sides</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pāxàti</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) pachymeningitis</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>pachymeningitis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пахито́са</i> (paxitósa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pachychilid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pachychilidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baklava (<i>dessert</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>paxlava</i>, Armenian <i>փախլավա</i> (pʻaxlava) and Georgian <i>ფახლავა</i> (paxlava). Doublet of <i>баклава́</i> (baklavá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to have a smell [<i>with</i> adverb <small>‘in a particular way’</small> <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘of/like something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for there to be a smell [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something with a smell’</small>, <i>along with</i> adverb <small>‘in a particular way’</small> <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘of/like something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to smell, to give off a premonition [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of/like something bad’</small>]</li><li>to puff, to blow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plowing</li><li>plowed field</li></ol><p><i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arable</li></ol><p><i>па́хота</i> (páxota) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttermilk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragrant</li><li>strong-smelling, odorous</li></ol><p><i>па́х(нуть)</i> (páx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boy, kid, lad, fella (<i>referring to a boy or a young man; can be used as a term of address</i>)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) gang member</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>пацан</i> (pacan) during Soviet era, see more there.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomboy (girl who behaves in a typically boyish manner)</li></ol><p>From <i>паца́н</i> (pacán) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) boy, lad</li></ol><p><i>паца́н</i> (pacán) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patient</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>patiēns</i>, p.a.p. of <i>patior</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>терпи́ла</i> (terpíla) (a nomen patientis of <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) guy, lad</li></ol><p><i>паца́н</i> (pacán) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pacifism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pacifisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pacifist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) pronunciation spelling of <i>па́цик</i> (pácik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) more</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>паче</i> (pače, “more”), comparative of <i>пакы</i> (paky, “again, moreover”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;paky</i>. Compare <i>па́ки</i> (páki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bundle, pack, packet, batch, sheaf, package, parcel</li><li>tutu</li><li>(<i>military</i>) clip (ammunition)</li></ol><p>From <i>пак</i> (pak) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from German <i>Pack</i>. See also <i>пакова́ть</i> (pakovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty, to stain</li><li>to daub</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>paćkać</i> and <i>ciapka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) daub, scrawl; bungling</li></ol><p><i>па́чкать</i> (páčkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soiler</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) scribbler</li></ol><p><i>па́чк(ать)</i> (páčk(atʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pasha (title)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پاشا (Turkish <i>paşa</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Паша)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Pasha, of the male given names Па́вел (Pável) or Павло́ (Pavló)</li></ol><p><i>Па́вел</i> (Pável) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pashalik</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish پاشالق (paşalık).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ploughed field, arable land</li></ol><p><i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pâté</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>Pastete</i>, perhaps via Polish <i>pasztet</i>. Further from Latin <i>pasta</i> (whence also <i>па́ста</i> (pásta)), from Ancient Greek <i>παστά</i> (pastá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pâté</li></ol><p><i>паште́т</i> (paštét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pashto; one of the official languages of Afghanistan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Pashto پښتو (paẍto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pressed (caviar)</li></ol><p><i>па́юс</i> (pájus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blowtorch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldering iron</li></ol><p>From <i>пая́ть</i> (pajátʹ, “solder”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solderer (a person who solders)</li></ol><p><i>пая́(ть)</i> (pajá(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clown around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solder</li><li>(figurative,&#32;criminal slang) to try (usually through unfair, fraudulent, illegal means) to set the sentence of a defendant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>паꙗти</i> (pajati, “to forge”). Probably related to <i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ) (from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pojiti</i>) and thereby to Ukrainian <i>пая́ти</i> (pajáty), Old Church Slavonic <i>съпоити</i> (sŭpoiti, “to connect, to solder”), Serbo-Croatian <i>спо̀јити</i> (“to join, to solder”) (1sg. <i>спо̏jӣм</i>), Slovene <i>spojíti</i> (“to join, to solder”) (1sg. <i>spojím</i>), Czech <i>pojit</i> (“to join”) (also <i>spojit</i>), Polish <i>spoić</i> (“to join”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffoon, clown, jester</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>pagliaccio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ration, dietary allowance</li></ol><p><i>пай</i> (paj) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>посёлок городско́го ти́па</i> (posjólok gorodskóvo típa): townlet, urban-type settlement <i>(Russian administrative unit)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</li></ol><p><i>петь</i> (petʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евать</i> (-evatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěvьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving to sing</li><li>singing (<i>e.g. of a door or gate</i>)</li><li>prolonged and melodious, sonorous (<i>e.g. of speech or a voice</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пев-</i> (pev-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) singing, singer</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́вчий</i> (pévčij) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij) or <i>певе́ц</i> (pevéc) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>singing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chorister, choirboy</li></ol><p><i>пев-</i> (pev-) +&lrm; <i>-чий</i> (-čij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelduck (duck of genus <i>Tadorna</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пе́гий</i> (pégij) +&lrm; <i>-а́нка</i> (-ánka).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>shelduck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(originally&#32;Greek mythology,&#32;fantasy) pegasus (any winged horse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piebald, skewbald (esp. of horses)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пѣгъ</i> (pěgŭ). Cognate to Bulgarian <i>пега</i> (pega, “freckle”), Latin <i>pingō</i> (“I paint”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pegmatite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedagogue, teacher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedagogy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παιδαγωγική</i> (paidagōgikḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pedagogical</li></ol><p><i>педаго́гика</i> (pedagógika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to pedal</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to press, to drive, to push (continuously promote&#58; <i>agenda, issue, topic, viewpoint, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>педа́ль</i> (pedálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>педалюва́ти</i> (pedaljuváty), Polish <i>pedałować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedal, treadle</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>pedālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedant, prig</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pédant</i> (possibly via German <i>Pedant</i>), from Italian <i>pedante</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pedantically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pedantically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pedantic</li></ol><p><i>педа́нт</i> (pedánt) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) bedel, beadle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>pedellus</i>, from Middle English <i>bedel</i>. See <i>beadle</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pederast</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) pedophile</li><li>(<i>offensive</i>) faggot, homosexual man</li><li>(<i>offensive</i>) an effeminate boy or man</li><li>(by extension,&#32;offensive) asshole</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;right-wing usage) groomer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παιδεραστής</i> (paiderastḗs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pederastic</li></ol><p><i>педера́ст</i> (pederást, pedɛrást) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pederasty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>παιδεραστία</i> (paiderastía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pediatrician</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>педиатри́я</i> (pediatríja, “pediatrics”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pediatrics</li></ol><p>Borrowing from a European language; compare French <i>pédiatrie</i>, German <i>Pädiatrie</i>, Italian <i>pediatria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) pederast; male homosexual; faggot, fag (<i>US, Canada</i>); poof (<i>UK, Australia, New Zealand</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пед(ераст)</i> (ped(erast)) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedicure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pédicure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher's college, pedagogical institute</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>педагоги́ческий институ́т</i> (pedagogíčeskij institút).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedophile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedophilia/paedophilia (sexual feeling of desire directed towards children)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pädophilie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) + <i>φιλία</i> (philía, “love”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female pedophile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;vulgar) faggot</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) an unpleasant person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bottoms up!, cheers! (lit., <i>drink to the bottom&#33;</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pager <i>(device)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landscape</li><li>a landscape painting</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>paysage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) landscape</li></ol><p><i>пейза́ж</i> (pejzáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) paysan</li><li>farmer, peasant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>paysan</i> (“peasant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) paysan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>paysan</i> (“peasant”) + suffix <i>-ин</i> (-in), denoting people or nationality.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>alcoholic beverages</i>) pale ale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pale ale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paintball (sport where players shoot each other with gelatin capsules filled with paint-like dye)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>paintball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) paintball</li></ol><p><i>пейнтбо́л</i> (pɛjntból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch (dark, extremely viscous material made by distilling tar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>pek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pecan <i>(Carya illinoinensis)</i></li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) fisher <i>(Pekania pennanti)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bakery</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a computer or a laptop</li></ol><p><i>пе́карь</i> (pékarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja); sense 2 derives from the abbreviation ПК.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) baker</li></ol><p><i>пе́карь</i> (pékarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pekařь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пек-</i> (pek-, “to bake”) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beijinger (native or inhabitant of the city of Beijing, capital of China) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Пеки́н</i> (Pekín) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Beijing</li></ol><p><i>Пеки́н</i> (Pekín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorching heat</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heat (the height or stress of an action or condition)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) hell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пькъло</i> (pĭkŭlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьkъlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pelargonium</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Pelargonium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shroud</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelena</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearing angle, azimuth, bearing, bearing course</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelerine, cape</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pèlerine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fichu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pelican</li></ol><p><i>пелика́н</i> (pelikán) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pellagra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) pellet</li><li>(pharmaceutical,&#32;in the plural) pill</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) pellet (<i>animal feces</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>pellet</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pelmeni</li></ol><p><i>пельме́нь</i> (pelʹménʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) pelmen (a Russian dumpling resembling ravioli)</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) female genitalia; vagina or vulva</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Permic language, from Proto-Permic <i>&#42;pɛľńań</i>. The borrowing went through an intermediate dialectal form <i>пельмянь</i> (pelʹmjanʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пельме́нь</i> (pelʹménʹ, “(singular) pelmeni”): (a small) pelmeni dumpling, (a small) dumpling</li></ol><p><i>пельме́нь</i> (pelʹménʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any lichen species of the genus <i>Peltigera</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Peltigera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field pea (<i>Pisum sativum</i> subsp. <i>arvense</i> syn. <i>Pisum sativum</i> convar. <i>speciosum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>пелю́шка</i> (peljúška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peled (<i>fish</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nappy/diaper, swaddling cloth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumice</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>pumex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pumice</li></ol><p><i>пе́мз(а)</i> (pémz(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) foam, spume, scum, froth</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) lather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pencil case, pencil box (object purposed to contain stationery)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pennal</i>. Further derived from Latin <i>penna</i> (whence English <i>pen</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂r̥ ~ pth₂én-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>soccer</i>) penalty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>penalty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Ancient Rome) Penates (household gods)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) home</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Penātēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;literally&#32;or&#32;figuratively) a kick in the butt, a kick in the pants</li><li>(<i>soccer&#32;slang</i>) penalty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) seating pad (a piece of soft plastic used as a portable seat)</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) embroidery punch (a stylus-like tool, traditionally made of bone, used to open holes in fabric)</li><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) bodice, short cardigan, short jacket</li></ol><p>Attested 1930s, originally in the soccer slang sense. Probably derives from the first element in compound engineering terms (e.g. <i>пе́ндель-дина́мо</i> (péndelʹ-dinámo) or <i>пе́ндель-генера́тор</i> (péndelʹ-generátor)) deriving from German <i>Pendel</i> (“pendulum”), and chosen due to phonetic similarity to the obsolete 1930s pronunciation of <i>пе́нальти</i> (pénalʹti, “penalty kick”) (in modern Russian, the stress pattern has changed to <i>пена́льти</i> (penálʹti)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swing door</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pendeltür</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;derogatory) Alternative spelling of пиндос (pindos, “a Yank (American), (obsolete) a Pontic Greek”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Penza (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Пе́нза</i> (Pénza) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singing</li></ol><p><i>пе́ть</i> (pétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) penis</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>pēnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foamy</li></ol><p><i>пе́н(а)</i> (pén(a)) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foam</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penicillin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin forming on milk</li><li>meerschaum</li><li>sleeping mat</li><li>leaf warbler</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>пе́ние</i> (pénije) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pingos</i> (“chaffinch”), along with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finkiz</i> (compare English <i>finch</i>), Welsh <i>pinc</i> (“finch”), Ancient Greek <i>σπίγγος</i> (spíngos, “chaffinch”), Sanskrit <i>फिङ्गक</i> (phiṅgaka, “drongo, shrike”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) penny</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>penny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foam</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defoamer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expanded/exfoliated graphite</li></ol><p>from <i>пе́на</i> (péna, “foam”) + <i>графи́т</i> (grafít, “graphite”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyfoam, foamed plastic</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna, “foam”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пласт(ма́сса)</i> (plast(mássa), “plastic”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) styrofoam</li></ol><p><i>пенопла́ст</i> (penoplást) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>styrofoam (expanded polystyrene foam, such as is used in cups and packaging)</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полистиро́л</i> (polistiról).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyurethane foam, expanded polyurethane</li></ol><p><i>пе́на</i> (péna) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полиурета́н</i> (poliuretán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пе́нка</i> (pénka, “skin on milk”)</li><li>leaf warbler</li></ol><p><i>пе́нка</i> (pénka, “skin on milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>пе́нка</i> (pénka, “leaf warbler”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiffchaff (<i>Phylloscopus collybita</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́ночка</i> (pénočka, “leaf warbler”) +&lrm; <i>тенько́вка</i> (tenʹkóvka, “chiffchaff”). The latter word is said to arise from <i>тень-тинь-тянь-тень</i> (tenʹ-tinʹ-tjanʹ-tenʹ), the sound of falling drops of rain, which its song slightly resembles.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) penny</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pensioner, retiree</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pence</i>, plural for <i>penny</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>пенсионе́р</i> (pensionér).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pennsylvanian</li></ol><p><i>Пенсильва́ния</i> (Pensilʹvánija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pensioner, retiree</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pensionnaire</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>pēnsiō</i> (whence <i>пе́нсия</i> (pénsija)) + Latin <i>-arius</i>. Further from Latin <i>pendō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pension; pensionary</li></ol><p><i>пе́нсия</i> (pénsija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pension</li><li>retirement</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>pensio</i>. Further from Latin <i>pendō</i>. Doublet of <i>пансио́н</i> (pansión).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pince-nez</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pince-nez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentadecane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(tree) stump, stub</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;animate) blockhead</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Pentium processor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьнь</i> (pĭnĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pь̏ňь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пе́ние</i> (pénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemp fibre (for making into cloth or rope)</li></ol><p>Via Azerbaijani <i>bəng</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка́</i> (-ká), from Persian بنگ (bang, “hemp, haschich”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hemp fibre (for making into cloth or rope)</li></ol><p><i>пеньк(а́)</i> (penʹk(á)) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peignoir (a long outer garment for women)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>peignoir</i>. Further from Latin <i>pectinō</i>, from Latin <i>pecten</i> (akin to <i>pectō</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>дерю́га</i> (derjúga) (akin to <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)). For another descendant of PIE <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to pluck (wool, hair)”), <i>фехтова́ть</i> (fextovátʹ), note <i>дра́ться</i> (drátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine (payment for breaking the law)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) reproach</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>pena</i> (“fine”), from Latin <i>poena</i> (“penalty, punishment”), from Ancient Greek <i>ποινή</i> (poinḗ, “blood money, fine”). If so, doublet of <i>цена́</i> (cená, “price”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) name for Anglo-Saxon and Germanic pfennigs</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) (small) coin</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;in the plural) money</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěnędzь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reproach <i>(with)</i>, to blame</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>пе́ня</i> (pénja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foamy, bubbly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пень</i> (penʹ): (small) stump, stub</li></ol><p><i>пень</i> (penʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) ash, ashes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pepelъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;humorous&#32;or&#32;derogatory) whirlybird, flying machine (a helicopter or small fixed-wing aircraft)</li><li>(slang,&#32;humorous&#32;or&#32;derogatory) bucket of bolts, rattletrap (an automobile, often one which is old, strange, poorly designed, or homebuilt)</li></ol><p>From the term for spacecraft in <i>Kin-dza-dza!</i> (1986 science fiction film), from Georgian <i>პეპელა</i> (ṗeṗela, “butterfly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ashes, smoldering ruins</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hearth and home</li></ol><p><i>пе́пел</i> (pépel) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashtray</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́пел</i> (pépel, “ash”) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ash</li><li>ashen, ashy (the color of ash)</li></ol><p><i>пе́пел</i> (pépel) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Peperomia</i> (one of the 2 large genera of the Piperaceae family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyroclastic fall</li></ol><p><i>пе́п(е)л</i> (pép(e)l) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́д(ать)</i> (pád(atʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pepsi drink</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Pepsi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pepsi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pepsi</li></ol><p><i>пепси-ко́ла</i> (pepsi-kóla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) pepsin</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>πέψις</i> (pépsis), from Ancient Greek <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biochemistry,&#32;medicine) peptide</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Peptid</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πεπτός</i> (peptós, “digested”), from Ancient Greek <i>πέπτω</i> (péptō). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lane, by-street</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>переу́лок</i> (pereúlok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) merchandise of a high quality, of the first (i.e. the best) sort</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) someone who always/usually takes the first position in competitions, contests, etc.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) performer of the first roles</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) the first phase or the initial distillation of samogon (a Russian home-distilled illegal spirit)</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj, “first”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first sign, first indication, first example (an early or first sign of the onset of something)</li></ol><p>Literally, “first swallow (bird)”. Related to the idiom <i>пе́рвая ла́сточка весны́ не де́лает</i> (pérvaja lástočka vesný ne délajet, “one swallow does not a summer make”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first love (one's first feeling of romantic love)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firstborn</li><li>firstling</li></ol><p>Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>прьвьньць</i> (prĭvĭnĭcĭ), Bulgarian <i>първене́ц</i> (pǎrvenéc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first place, top ranking</li><li>priority</li><li>championship</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Compare Czech <i>prvenství</i>, Polish <i>pierwszeństwo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Protostomia.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>первичноро́тый</i> (pervičnorótyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prime, initial, primary</li><li>primordial, virgin</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>April fool (practical joke played on April Fools' Day)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primeval</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the first thing</li><li>ellipsis of <i>пе́рвое блю́до</i> (pérvoje bljúdo): starter (first course of a meal)</li><li>the first (in dates)</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj, “first”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) first person</li><li>the top person in an organization or government</li></ol><p>Rus. первый + лицо (in the sense of <i>person</i>, not <i>face</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augend (a quantity to which another is added)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savagery</li></ol><p><i>первозда́нный</i> (pervozdánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primordial</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зда́ние</i> (zdánije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin, primary source</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>исто́чник</i> (istóčnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) alternative form of <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first-former, first-grader (a pupil of the first year at school)</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first-class, first-rate, high-class</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>класс</i> (klass) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freshman, first-year student (<i>in a university or college</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) May Day</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>май</i> (maj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protomartyr</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initially, originally, at first, first (at the beginning)</li></ol><p><i>первонача́льный</i> (pervonačálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initial, original</li><li>elementary</li><li>primary</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нача́льный</i> (načálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fundamental principle</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>осно́ва</i> (osnóva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discoverer, pioneer</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>открыва́ть</i> (otkryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first and foremost, immediate</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>очередно́й</i> (očerednój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settler</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поселе́нец</i> (poselénec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) original / initial chaos, the beginning</li><li>root cause, underlying cause, first cause, prime cause</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>причи́на</i> (pričína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trailblazer, pioneer, pathfinder</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>проходи́ть</i> (proxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pioneer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high priest</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>свяще́нник</i> (svjaščénnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paramount</li></ol><p><i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сте́пень</i> (stépenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>chief, main</li><li>number one, top</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьрвъ</i> (pĭrvŭ, “first”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrvъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pírˀwas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂wós</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“before, formerly; through, throughout”).</p><p>Baltic cognates include Lithuanian <i>pi̇̀rmas</i> (“first; primary”), Latvian <i>pìrmaĩs</i>, Old Prussian <i>pirmas</i> (“first”).</p><p>Indo-European cognates include Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pū́rva, “foremost, first, previous”) and English <i>fore</i> and <i>prime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first, in the first place</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beebread</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parchment (material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parchment</li></ol><p>From <i>перга́мент</i> (pergáment) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glassine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;informal) to fart (noisily, as opposed to бздеть)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrděti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perd-</i>. Cognate to English <i>fart</i>, French <i>péter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) fart, farting</li></ol><p>From <i>перд(е́ть)</i> (perd(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) over, over again (describing an action of moving by overcoming one or more obstacles)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) across, trans- (describing an action of moving to a different place)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) too much (describing an action of resulting something excessive)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) extremely (describing an action of doing something much more extreme than an average action)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) re- (describing a repeating action)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an alternating or exchanging action</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing a consequence of a completed action that affects many entities of a group</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing a destructed state of an entity, usually by cutting or tearing with tools or tool-like implements</li><li>(used with verbs,&#32;colloquial,&#32;rare) describing a light action that is not done in full measure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;per-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overdo it, to go too far</li></ol><p><i>переборщи́ть</i> (pereborščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run <i>(across)</i>, to cross <i>(at a run [running])</i></li><li>to move, to shift</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defect <i>(to)</i>, to go over <i>(to)</i></li><li>to run too long</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run <i>(across)</i>, to cross <i>(at a run [running])</i></li><li>to move, to shift</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defect <i>(to)</i>, to go over <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush, dash</li><li>(<i>skating</i>) cornering step</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) defection</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) re-race (redoing of a race, when the outcome was unclear)</li></ol><p><i>перебежа́ть</i> (perebežátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defector, deserter, turncoat</li></ol><p><i>перебега́ть</i> (perebegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to shatter (<i>of all or many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be interrupted or disturbed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to survive with difficulty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live off [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a specified type of work’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>перебива́ть</i> (perebivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sort out, to look through</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to finger (strings of a string musical instrument)</li><li>to take in excess, to take too much</li><li>to recall, to turn over in one's mind</li><li>to be picky, to be choosy</li><li>to move (one's feet), to advance (slowly)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get over (an obstacle or to a place), to cross</li><li>to move (to some place)</li></ol><p><i>перебира́ть</i> (perebirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interrupt, to break</li><li>to kill, to slaughter, to slay (all)</li><li>to break (in two)</li><li>to break (all)</li><li>to pattern up, to beat into something different in shape and/or behaviour</li><li>(<i>literally&#32;and&#32;figuratively</i>) to outbid [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘which offer’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to shatter (<i>of all or many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be interrupted or disturbed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to survive with difficulty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to live off [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a specified type of work’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>переби́ть</i> (perebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoppage, interruption</li><li>irregularity, misfire</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) intermission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience, to suffer [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a disease or many diseases’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to experience [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘heartache, sorrow, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to grieve (<i>of the soul or heart</i>)</li><li>to hurt, to be painful (<i>of many or various body parts</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overcome [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a crush, an infatuation’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>переболе́ть</i> (perebolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience, to suffer [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a disease or many diseases’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to experience [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘heartache, sorrow, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overcome [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a crush, an infatuation’</small>]</li><li>to grieve (<i>of the soul or heart</i>)</li><li>to hurt, to be painful (<i>of many or various body parts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) running over the strings, fingering, arpeggio</li><li>excess, surplus</li><li>search, rundown, exhaustion, exhaustive search</li><li>(<i>church</i>) funeral bells</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sorting through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sorting out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) collecting of disassembled parts (<i>for the purpose of repair</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bulkhead, partition</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перебра́ть</i> (perebrátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>перебор-</i> (perebor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>перебер-</i> (pereber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome; to get the better of</li><li>to suppress (e.g. a strong feeling, addiction, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overdo it, to go too far</li></ol><p>Per Dal, from dialectal <i>борщить</i> (borščitʹ, “to pour a lot to no avail”), possibly from <i>борщ</i> (boršč, “borscht”)? Per Chernykh, from <i>перебо́рка</i> (perebórka, “sorting through”), through the intermediate sense "to be too picky".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrangle, squabble (minor (verbal) fight or argument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw over, to throw across</li><li>to transfer, to redeploy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shift <i>(onto)</i></li></ol><p><i>перебро́сить</i> (perebrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to hurry [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘through or to somewhere’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spread quickly (<i>e.g. of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to defect [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to the enemy’</small>]</li><li>to throw at each other [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quickly exchange [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘words, comments, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to play [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘cards, dominoes, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>перебра́сывать</i> (perebrásyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sort out, to look through</li><li>to finger (strings of a string musical instrument)</li><li>to take in excess, to take too much</li><li>to recall, to turn over in one's mind</li><li>to be picky, to be choosy</li><li>to move (one's feet), to advance (slowly)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get over (an obstacle or to a place), to cross</li><li>to move (to some place)</li></ol><p><i>перебра́ть</i> (perebrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw over, to throw across</li><li>to transfer, to redeploy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shift <i>(onto)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to hurry [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘through or to somewhere’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spread quickly (<i>e.g. of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to defect [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to the enemy’</small>]</li><li>to throw at each other [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quickly exchange [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘words, comments, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to play [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘cards, dominoes, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>перебро́сить</i> (perebrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfer, redeployment</li></ol><p><i>перебро́сить</i> (perebrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>быва́ть</i> (byvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossing, passing</li><li>mountain pass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to roll over</li><li>to cross, to get to the top</li></ol><p><i>перевали́ть</i> (perevalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll over</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to waddle</li></ol><p><i>перева́ливать</i> (pereválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to roll over</li><li>to cross, to get to the top</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to pass</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) <i>(about time)</i> to pass</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll over</li></ol><p><i>перевали́ть</i> (perevalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digestion</li></ol><p>From <i>перева́ривать</i> (perevárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overdo, to spoil by overdoing</li><li>to cook/boil again</li><li>to digest</li><li>to stand, to bear, to stomach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overdo, to spoil by overdoing</li><li>to cook/boil again</li><li>to digest (separate food in its passage through the alimentary canal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to digest, to process (think over and arrange methodically in the mind)</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (varítʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>перевари́ти</i> (perevarýty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport, to carry, to remove, to move</li><li>to take across, to put across</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pervezti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around</li><li>to overturn, to turn over</li><li>to turn (a page)</li><li>to turn inside out</li><li>to turn upside down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn over (<i>while searching, making a mess</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to transform, to radically change</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stun</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, to turn round</li><li>to capsize, to overturn</li></ol><p><i>переверну́ть</i> (perevernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overwind</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preponderance, advantage</li><li>excess weight <i>(of luggage)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang in a different place</li><li>to weigh again, to reweigh</li><li>to outweigh</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to take precedence, to be more significant</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to convey, to transfer, to move, to shift, to transmit, to switch</li><li>to translate, to interpret [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from a language’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into a language’</small>]</li><li>to remit (money)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfer <i>(to another place of work, study etc)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to an end, to become extinct</li></ol><p><i>перевести́</i> (perevestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang up (all or many things)</li><li>to hang (all or many people), to execute (all or many people) by hanging</li><li>to weigh (all or many things)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to weigh again, to reweigh</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang up in a different place</li><li>to weigh again, to reweigh</li><li>to outweigh</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to take precedence, to be more significant</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang up (all or many things)</li><li>to hang (all or many people), to execute (all or many people) by hanging</li><li>to weigh (all or many things)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to weigh again, to reweigh</li></ol><p><i>переве́сить</i> (perevésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>переве́шать</i> (perevéšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfer, transference</li><li>translation</li><li>remittance</li><li>money order</li><li>switching, shunting</li><li>conversion</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>translation studies</i>) sight translation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transferrer (someone who transfers)</li></ol><p><i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ, “to transfer”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to convey, to transfer, to move, to shift, to transmit, to switch</li><li>to translate, to interpret [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from a language’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘into a language’</small>]</li><li>to remit (money)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfer <i>(to another place of work, study etc)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to an end, to become extinct</li></ol><p><i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>translated, in translation</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) money order</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перево́д</i> (perevód) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>translating, translated</li><li>transferable (alienable), switchable, transfer</li></ol><p><i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>translatologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>translation studies</i>) translatology, translation studies</li></ol><p>From <i>перево́д</i> (perevód) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije), a calque of English <i>translatology</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;translation studies) translational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>translator</li><li>interpreter</li></ol><p><i>переводи́ть</i> (perevodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transportation</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>воз</i> (voz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport, to carry (by vehicle), to remove, to move</li><li>to take across, to put across</li><li>to transport, to carry (by vehicle) all, to transport, to carry too many</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conveyance, transportation</li></ol><p><i>перевози́ть</i> (perevozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transporter</li><li>ferryman, boatman</li><li>carrier</li><li>common sandpiper</li></ol><p><i>перевози́ть</i> (perevozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rearm</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вооружа́ть</i> (vooružátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rearmament, rearming</li></ol><p>From <i>перевооружи́ть</i> (perevooružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rearm</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вооружи́ть</i> (vooružítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reincarnated</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>воплоти́ться</i> (voplotítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reincarnated</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>воплоща́ться</i> (voploščátʹsja) or <i>перевоплоща́ть</i> (perevoploščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reincarnation (rebirth of a mental capacity in a physical life form)</li></ol><p>From <i>перевоплоти́ть</i> (perevoplotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>перевёртывать</i> (perevjórtyvatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>перевороти́ть</i> (perevorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, to turn round</li><li>to capsize, to overturn</li></ol><p><i>перевора́чивать</i> (perevoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somersault</li><li>revolution</li><li>coup d'état, revolt, revolution (the removal and replacement of a government)</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pervortъ</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>пре̏вра̄т</i> / <i>prȅvrāt</i>, Polish <i>przewrót</i>, Czech <i>převrat</i>; see also the adjective <i>превра́тный</i> (prevrátnyj), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвратьнъ</i> (prěvratĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>переверну́ть</i> (perevernútʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pervortiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>переверну́ться</i> (perevernútʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>перевороти́ть</i> (perevorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reeducation</li></ol><p><i>перевоспита́ть</i> (perevospitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reeducate</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>воспита́ть</i> (vospitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reeducate</li></ol><p><i>перевоспита́ть</i> (perevospitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muddle, to garble</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>врать</i> (vratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfulfil, exceed</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>выполня́ть</i> (vypolnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to cord</li><li>to tie up again, to cord again</li><li>to bandage</li><li>to knit again, to have knitted again</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandaging, dressing</li><li>marbled polecat</li></ol><p><i>перевяза́ть</i> (perevjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bandaging</li></ol><p>From <i>перевя́зка</i> (perevjázka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to cord</li><li>to tie up again, to cord again</li><li>to bandage</li><li>to knit again, to have knitted again</li></ol><p><i>перевяза́ть</i> (perevjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sash</li><li>crossbelt, shoulder belt, baldric</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) sling</li></ol><p><i>перевяза́ть</i> (perevjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around</li><li>to overturn, to turn over</li><li>to turn (a page)</li><li>to turn inside out</li><li>to turn upside down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn over (<i>while searching, making a mess</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to transform, to radically change</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stun</li><li>to overwind</li></ol><p><i>переверт-</i> (perevert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, to turn round</li><li>to capsize, to overturn</li></ol><p><i>перевёртывать</i> (perevjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unpleasant smell of (last night's) alcohol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) excess, extreme</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перегиба́ть</i> (peregibátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold, to bend</li><li>to go to extremes</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go too far; to overdo it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to lean over, to lean <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>перегиба́ть</i> (peregibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange glances, to look at each other</li></ol><p>From <i>перегляд-</i> (peregljad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange glances, to look at each other</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (somewhere else), to ferry</li><li>to distill</li><li>to surpass, to overtake</li><li>to outrun, to outdistance</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pergnojь</i>. Compare <i>перегни́ть</i> (peregnítʹ, “to rot completely”). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;per-</i>, <i>&#42;gnojь</i> and <i>&#42;gňiti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold, to bend</li><li>to go to extremes</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to lean over, to lean <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>перегну́ть</i> (peregnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk about something, to talk over, to discuss</li></ol><p><i>переговори́ть</i> (peregovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange remarks/words <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk, to have a talk, to speak <i>(to, with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discuss, to cover</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk down, to outtalk</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) negotiation, negotiations</li></ol><p><i>перегово́ры</i> (peregovóry) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negotiator</li></ol><p><i>перегово́ры</i> (peregovóry) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talks, negotiation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage (<i>of a journey</i>)</li><li>stretch, span (<i>of a railway line, between stations</i>)</li><li>driving (<i>of animals</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перегна́ть</i> (peregnátʹ),with the vocalism of the imperfective <i>перегоня́ть</i> (peregonjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (somewhere else), to ferry</li><li>to distill</li><li>to surpass, to overtake</li><li>to outrun, to outdistance</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to banish, to exile (many people)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to partition off</li><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>перегороди́ть</i> (peregorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out (<i>of a fire, fuse, bulb, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn to ashes, to burn to nothing</li><li>to fade (<i>also figuratively, of feelings, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перегоре́ть</i> (peregorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out (<i>of a fire, fuse, bulb, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn to ashes, to burn to nothing</li><li>to fade (<i>also figuratively, of feelings, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to partition off</li><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>городи́ть</i> (gorodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partition <i>(wall)</i></li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) septum</li></ol><p><i>перегороди́ть</i> (peregorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overheating</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перегрева́ть</i> (peregrevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overheating</li></ol><p><i>перегрева́ть</i> (peregrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overheat</li></ol><p><i>перегре́ть</i> (peregrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to overheat</li></ol><p><i>перегрева́ть</i> (peregrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overheat</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to overheat</li></ol><p><i>перегре́ть</i> (peregrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overload, to surcharge</li><li>to overload, to overwork</li><li>to overburden</li><li>to transship, to transfer</li><li>to reboot, to reload, to restart</li></ol><p><i>перегрузи́ть</i> (peregruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overload, surcharge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overload, to surcharge</li><li>to overload, to overwork</li><li>to overburden</li><li>to transship, to transfer</li><li>to reboot, to reload, to restart</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overload, surcharge</li><li>overload, overwork</li><li>transfer, transshipment</li></ol><p><i>перегрузи́ть</i> (peregruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regrouping</li></ol><p><i>перегруппирова́ть</i> (peregruppirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in front of, before</li><li>right/immediately before</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front, forepart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>передъ</i> (peredŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i>, whence also <i>пере-</i> (pere-), <i>перез</i> (perez). Doublet of <i>пред</i> (pred), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transmittable, transmissible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand over, to deliver, to give</li><li>to broadcast, to transmit</li><li>to reproduce, to render</li><li>to tell, to take a message</li></ol><p><i>переда́ть</i> (peredátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over to, to spread</li></ol><p><i>передава́ть</i> (peredavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) transmission, transfer</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gearing, drive, transmission</li></ol><p>Logically from <i>переда́ча</i> (peredáča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), but structurally as if from nonexistent <i>&#42;переда́тка</i> (&#42;peredátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) sender, messenger</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;radio,&#32;television) transmitter</li></ol><p><i>переда́ть</i> (peredátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand over, to deliver, to give</li><li>to broadcast, to transmit</li><li>to reproduce, to render</li><li>to tell, to take a message</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>передати</i> (peredati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdati</i>, from <i>&#42;per-</i> + <i>&#42;dati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ). Doublet of <i>преда́ть</i> (predátʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go over to, to spread</li></ol><p><i>переда́ть</i> (peredátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery</li><li>communication, handing over, passing, transfer, transmission</li><li>parcel</li><li>broadcast, broadcasting, relaying, telecast, transmission</li><li>television program</li><li>gear, gearing, drive, transmission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li><i>(time)</i> to shift, to move</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(to the other position)</i> to move, to transfer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move (everything or many things)</li></ol><p><i>передви́г-</i> (peredvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li>to move, to walk</li><li>to travel</li></ol><p><i>передвига́ть</i> (peredvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement, conveyance</li></ol><p><i>передви́г-</i> (peredvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peredvizhnik</li></ol><p><i>передвижно́й</i> (peredvižnój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>movable, mobile</li><li>traveling, mobile, itinerant</li></ol><p><i>передвигать</i> (peredvigatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li><i>(time)</i> to shift, to move</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(to the other position)</i> to move, to transfer</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li>to move, to walk</li><li>to travel</li></ol><p><i>передви́нуть</i> (peredvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repartition, redivision, redistribution</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) reprocessing or remelting of lower-grade metal into higher-grade metal</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) trouble, difficulty</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>передели́ть</i> (peredelítʹ, “to repartition, to redivide, to redistribute”).</p><p>Deverbal from <i>переде́лать</i> (peredélatʹ, “to redo”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make over, to alter (to change the form or structure of)</li><li>to do anew, to rework</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do in large quantities</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alteration, redo</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trouble, mess, fix, scrape</li></ol><p><i>переде́лать</i> (peredélatʹ, “to alter, to redo”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make over, to alter (to change the form or structure of)</li><li>to do anew, to rework</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do in large quantities</li></ol><p><i>переде́лать</i> (peredélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reprocessing or remelting of lower-grade metal into higher grade metal</li></ol><p><i>переде́л</i> (peredél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulletproof windscreen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>front, fore, foremost</li><li>first</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) anterior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пе́ред</i> (péred) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apron (clothing)</li></ol><p><i>пе́ред</i> (péred) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vestibule, hall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nosewheel leg <i>(jet fighter)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пе́ред</i> (péred): in front of, before</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading article, leader, editorial</li><li>(<i>military</i>) frontline</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Soviet Union</i>) pacemaker, model worker (especially winning distinctions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>progressive, leading, foremost, headmost, forward, advanced</li></ol><p><i>пе́ред</i> (péred) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overdose</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дозиро́вка</i> (doziróvka); probably a calque of English <i>overdose</i> or German <i>Überdosis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>articulated front part (usually with an axle and two wheels) of a two-axle horse carriage, a plow, a seeder etc.</li><li>(<i>military</i>) limber</li><li>front axle of a car, a tractor etc.</li><li>(<i>military jargon</i>) frontline</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) vulva</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́ред</i> (péred) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), attested no later than 1733 (note that Middle Russian <i>передъ</i> (pered, “front side of a single-axle carriage”) is attested in 16th c.). Doublet of <i>предок</i> (predok), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a break, to have a break, to have a rest, to take a breath (to have a short rest period in one's work)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to die off, to extinct, to die in masses (especially of animals or when likening people with animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic</li></ol><p><i>передразни́ть</i> (peredraznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дразни́ть</i> (draznítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fight (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дра́ться</i> (drátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scrape, fight</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trouble, mess</li></ol><p><i>передра́ть(ся)</i> (peredrátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change one's mind</li><li>to think over multiple things or many times</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change one's mind, to think better of it, to have second thoughts</li></ol><p><i>переду́мать</i> (peredúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пе́ред</i> (péred).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respite, breathing space</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дыша́ть</i> (dyšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>predah</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(in a card game) to sharp, to cheat, to swindle</li><li>to distort, to twist, to wrench, to misrepresent, to slant</li><li>to make wince, to make cringe, to make reel, to make squirm, to make flinch</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) to jerk, to toss off</li></ol><p><i>передёрг-</i> (peredjórg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(in a card game) to sharp, to cheat, to swindle</li><li>to distort, to twist, to wrench, to misrepresent, to slant</li><li>to make wince, to make cringe, to make reel, to make squirm, to make flinch</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to jerk off, have a jerk off</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overnutrition, overeating</li></ol><p><i>перееда́ть</i> (perejedátʹ, “to overeat”) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey, passage, crossing</li><li>crossing, highway crossing, crossroads</li><li>resettlement, removal, moving, leaving <i>(for)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (to change residence)</li><li>to cross, to run over, to drive over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (to change residence)</li><li>to cross, to run over, to drive over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait till (something) is over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experience</li><li>feeling, emotional experience</li><li>worry, anxiety</li></ol><p><i>пережива́ть</i> (pereživátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience, to go through</li><li>to endure, to suffer, to ache</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be upset, to worry</li><li>to survive, to outlive, to outlast, to self-survive</li></ol><p><i>пережи́ть</i> (perežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait till (something) is over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-жида́ть</i> (-židátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experience, what someone has lived/been/gone through</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relic</li><li>survival</li><li>remnant, leftover, vestige</li><li>anachronism (person or thing which seems to belong to a different time)</li><li>hangover, holdover</li><li>carryover</li></ol><p><i>пережи́ть</i> (perežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to survive</li><li>(by extension,&#32;transitive) to ride out, to go/get/live through; to be through, to get over <i>(a hardship)</i></li><li>[<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom?’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘на + by how much?’</small>] to outlive</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ). Cognate with Polish <i>przeżyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;computing</i>) to reload</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to reboot</li><li>(<i>also&#32;computing</i>) to reset</li></ol><p><i>перезагрузи́ть</i> (perezagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;computing</i>) to reload</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to reboot</li><li>(<i>also&#32;computing</i>) to reset</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>загрузи́ть</i> (zagruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;computing</i>) reboot, reload, reset</li></ol><p><i>перезагрузи́ть</i> (perezagruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rerecording</li><li>overwrite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reload <i>(weapon)</i></li><li>to recharge <i>(battery)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>заряди́ть</i> (zarjadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reload (one's firearm)</li><li>to recharge (oneself)</li></ol><p><i>перезаряди́ть</i> (perezarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reload <i>(weapon)</i></li><li>to recharge <i>(battery)</i></li></ol><p><i>перезаряди́ть</i> (perezarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring again</li><li>to call back, to ring back, to call again (<i>on the telephone</i>)</li><li>to ring (many or all bells)</li><li>to call (many or all people) (<i>on the telephone</i>)</li><li>to ring louder than anything or anyone else</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to alternately ring (different bells)</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;intransitive) to ring or chime with interruptions or alternately, to in different places</li></ol><p><i>перезвони́ть</i> (perezvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringing of bells</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перезвони́ть</i> (perezvonítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring again</li><li>to call back, to ring back, to call again (<i>on the telephone</i>)</li><li>to ring (many or all bells)</li><li>to call (many or all people) (<i>on the telephone</i>)</li><li>to ring louder than anything or anyone else</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquaint (with everybody or many people)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play again</li><li>to overdo, to overact</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to outplay, to get the better <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to change, to shift around</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play again</li><li>to overdo, to overact</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to get the better <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to change, to shift around</li></ol><p><i>переигра́ть</i> (pereigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reelect</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>избира́ть</i> (izbirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>re-election</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reelect</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>избра́ть</i> (izbrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overabundance</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>избы́ток</i> (izbýtok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to republish, to reprint, to reissue</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>издава́ть</i> (izdavátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reedition, reissue, reprinting, republication</li></ol><p><i>переизда́ть</i> (pereizdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to republish, to reprint, to reissue</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>изда́ть</i> (izdátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renaming</li></ol><p><i>переименова́ть</i> (pereimenovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rename, to give a new name</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>именова́ть</i> (imenovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rename, to give a new name</li></ol><p><i>переименова́ть</i> (pereimenovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reuse</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>испо́льзовать</i> (ispólʹzovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to get over, to get across</li><li>to pass on (to), to proceed (to), to turn (to)</li><li>to change, to switch (from/to), to convert (to)</li><li>to turn (into), to change, to convert, to transform (to), to go (into)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exceed, to go beyond (a limit)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overheat something</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to temper something excessively by heating</li></ol><p><i>перекали́ть</i> (perekalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rolling</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) sharp sound, crack (<i>of a shot</i>), rumble (<i>of thunder</i>)</li><li>shoal, shallow, sandbar (<i>especially of a river</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перекати́ть</i> (perekatítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roll (all or many things from one place to another)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take (all or many people) for a ride</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to resmooth by rolling (linen, felt, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smooth (all or many items of linen, felt, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumbleweed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nomad, vagabond, tramp</li></ol><p>From <i>перекати́ть</i> (perekatítʹ, “to roll”) +&lrm; <i>по́ле</i> (póle, “field”). Compare Belarusian <i>перакаці́-по́ле</i> (pjerakací-pólje), Ukrainian <i>перекоти́по́ле</i> (perekotýpóle).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll (<i>from one place to another</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll over</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll across</li></ol><p><i>перекати́ть</i> (perekatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll (<i>from one place to another</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roll (all or many things from one place to another)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take (all or many people) for a ride</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to resmooth by rolling (linen, felt, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smooth (all or many items of linen, felt, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>перекати́ть</i> (perekatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>переката́ть</i> (perekatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll over</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roll across</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to rumble (<i>of thunder</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перека́тывать</i> (perekátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump from one place to another, to siphon</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obtain (something) in one place and transfer it to another</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swing (all or many people) in turn on a swing</li><li>to toss (all or many people) up on one's hands</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump from one place to another, to siphon</li><li>to obtain (something) in one place and move it to another</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swing (all or many people) in turn on a swing</li><li>to toss (all or many people) up on one's hands</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to tilt, to heel (<i>of a boat</i>)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to cause to tilt, to rock (a boat)</li></ol><p><i>перекача́ть</i> (perekačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumping from one place to another, siphoning</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) obtaining (something) and transferring it from one place to another</li><li>swinging (all or many people on a swing)</li><li>tossing (all or many people) up on one's hand</li></ol><p><i>перекача́ть</i> (perekačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warp</li><li><i>(mostly impersonal)</i> <i>(face with anger, pain etc)</i> to twist, to contort, to distort</li></ol><p><i>перекоси́ть</i> (perekosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw over, to throw across</li><li>to throw <i>(one after another)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>переки́д-</i> (perekíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>перекида́ть</i> (perekidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap over</li><li>to spread (of a disease)</li><li>to throw (one to another)</li></ol><p><i>переки́дывать</i> (perekídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw over, to throw across</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap over</li><li>to spread (of a disease)</li><li>to throw (one to another)</li></ol><p><i>переки́нуть</i> (perekínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil too long</li></ol><p><i>перекипе́ть</i> (perekipétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil too long</li><li>to quiet down, to calm down, to cool off, to simmer down</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peroxide</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>(о́)кись</i> ((ó)kisʹ), with stress shift of a sort typical for derivatives ending in -ь; a calque of English <i>peroxide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossbeam, crosspiece, transom</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) horizontal bar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move, to put/place elsewhere</li><li>to shift <i>(onto)</i></li><li>to interlay <i>(with)</i></li><li>to set up/put in again</li><li>to arrange, to transpose</li><li>to put too much</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to trim (sails)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to one another</li><li>to have something in common <i>(with something)</i></li></ol><p><i>переклика́ть</i> (pereklikátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll call</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>switch</li></ol><p><i>переключа́ть</i> (pereključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to switch, to switch <i>(over to)</i></li><li>to switch <i>(over to)</i></li></ol><p><i>переключи́ть</i> (pereključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to switch, to switch over</li><li>to switch over <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>переключа́ть</i> (pereključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>switching, switchover, changeover</li></ol><p><i>переключи́ть</i> (pereključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to switch, to switch <i>(over to)</i></li><li>to switch <i>(over to)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to switch, to switch over</li><li>to switch over <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>переключи́ть</i> (pereključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reforge</li><li>to reshoe</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reforging</li><li>perekovka</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ко́вка</i> (kóvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reforge</li><li>to reshoe</li></ol><p><i>перекова́ть</i> (perekovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to reproach one another</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to warp; to wreck by warping</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to distort, to disfigure, to pervert, to ruin</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>корёжить</i> (korjóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sag, skewness, slant, tilt, warp</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) defect, flaw, fault</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mow (the whole part of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mow down, to kill</li><li>to warp</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;impersonal</i>) <i>(face with anger, pain etc)</i> to twist, to contort, to distort</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to mow, to cut”)</p><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to twist, to slant”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move on, to migrate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move, to get, to go <i>(somewhere)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кочева́ть</i> (kočevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move on, to migrate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move, to get, to go <i>(somewhere)</i></li></ol><p><i>перекочева́ть</i> (perekočevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a sign of the cross <i>(over)</i>, to cross</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rename <i>(as)</i></li><li>to cross, to intersect</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross oneself</li></ol><p><i>перекрести́ть</i> (perekrestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to intersect</li></ol><p><i>перекрести́ть</i> (perekrestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to intersect</li></ol><p><i>перекре́щивать</i> (perekréščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exceed</li><li>to stop up, to block</li><li>to cut off</li><li>to overlap</li><li>to re-cover</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to trump, to overtrump</li></ol><p><i>перекры́ть</i> (perekrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put the kibosh on, to put a lid on</li></ol><p>Literally, “to cut off the oxygen”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) floor, ceiling, overhead cover</li><li>overlapping</li><li>blocking, damming</li></ol><p><i>перекры́ть</i> (perekrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exceed</li><li>to stop up, to block</li><li>to cut off</li><li>to overlap</li><li>to re-cover</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to trump, to overtrump</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put the kibosh on, to put a lid on</li></ol><p>Literally, “to cut off the oxygen”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cross-</li></ol><p><i>перекрест(и́ть)</i> (perekrest(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossroad, road-crossing, carrefour, intersection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy what someone else wanted to buy, to outbuy, to outbid</li><li>to buy secondhand</li><li>to buy for resale</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy (a lot of things)</li><li>to bathe (all or many people)</li></ol><p><i>перекупи́ть</i> (perekupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy what someone else wanted to buy, to outbuy, to outbid</li><li>to buy secondhand</li><li>to buy for resale</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy (a lot of things)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secondhand dealer</li><li>reseller</li></ol><p><i>перекупи́ть</i> (perekupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smoko, break</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke (all or many kinds of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outsmoke</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to feel bad or damage one's health from smoking too much</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to take a smoking break</li></ol><p><i>перекури́ть</i> (perekurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke (all or many kinds of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outsmoke</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to feel bad or damage one's health from smoking too much</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to take a smoking break</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut/bite through, to bite off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a bite, to have a snack, to snatch a mouthful</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut/bite through, to bite off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a bite, to have a snack, to snatch a mouthful</li></ol><p><i>перекуси́ть</i> (perekusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift <i>(onto)</i></li><li>to arrange, to transpose</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in two</li><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p><i>переломи́ть</i> (perelomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb over [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>через</b> (čerez, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>переле́зть</i> (pereléztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb over [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>через</b> (čerez, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copse, coppice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(from one place to another or above something)</i></li><li>to fly too far, to overshoot <i>(the mark)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(from one place to another or above something)</i></li><li>to fly too far, to overshoot <i>(the mark)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overflow</li><li><i>(pouring)</i> too much</li><li><i>usually plural</i> play, tints, tinges <i>of colors</i></li><li><i>usually plural</i> modulations <i>of sound</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfusion (the act of pouring liquid from one vessel to another or the transfer of blood or blood products from one individual to another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood transfusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (from one container to another); to decant</li><li>to transfuse (blood)</li><li>to superfuse; to pour too much</li><li>to pour again</li></ol><p>From <i>перели́ть</i> (perelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>перелива́ти</i> (perelyváty), Belarusian <i>пераліва́ць</i> (pjeralivácʹ), Polish <i>przelewać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to chase one's tail; to mill the wind; to plough the sand; to talk empty, useless, unimportant talks</li></ol><p>Literally, “to pour from an empty (container) to an empty (one)”. Similar to Ukrainian <i>перелива́ти з пусто́го в поро́жнє</i> (perelyváty z pustóho v poróžnje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow</li><li>to overflow, to run over, to flow <i>(over)</i>, to run <i>(over)</i></li><li><i>(colors)</i> to play</li><li><i>(sounds)</i> to modulate</li></ol><p><i>перелива́ть</i> (perelivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, to leaf</li><li>to look <i>(through)</i></li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to page</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>листа́ть</i> (listátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over, to leaf</li><li>to look <i>(through)</i></li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to page</li></ol><p><i>перелиста́ть</i> (perelistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (from one container to another); to decant</li><li>to transfuse (blood)</li><li>to superfuse; to pour too much</li><li>to pour again</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow</li><li>to overflow, to run over, to flow <i>(over)</i>, to run <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>перели́ть</i> (perelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) arrangement (<i>for another instrument — action or result</i>), transposition (<i>into another key — action or result</i>)</li><li>setting (<i>to music, into verse, etc.</i>)</li><li>work set to music, into verse, etc.</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) written retelling of something recited, as an educational work</li></ol><p><i>переложи́ть</i> (pereložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move, to put/place elsewhere</li><li>to shift <i>(onto)</i></li><li>to interlay <i>(with)</i></li><li>to set up/put in again</li><li>to arrange, to transpose</li><li>to put too much</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>break, breaking, fracture</li><li>change, turn, turnaround, <i>(of a disease)</i> crisis, turning point</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perlomъ</i>. Literally, “a break across/though”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break (<i>multiple things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in two</li><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to overcome</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crucial, critical</li></ol><p><i>перело́м</i> (perelóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shovel over, to shovel across (to shovel from one place to another)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to sift through, to dig through, to pore over, to comb through, to trawl through (to look at, read or examine in large quantities)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to slog through, to crank through, to work through (to process, do or implement in large quantities)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>лопа́тить</i> (lopátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shovel over, to shovel across (to shovel from one place to another)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to sift through, to dig through, to pore over, to comb through, to trawl through (to look at, read or examine in large quantities)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to slog through, to crank through, to work through (to process, do or implement in large quantities)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-лопа́чивать</i> (-lopáčivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmigration (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>flight (<i>of an airplane</i>)</li><li>shot over the target</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>migratory (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) fast-flying</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) fleeting</li></ol><p><i>перелёт</i> (pereljót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice</li></ol><p><i>перемани́ть</i> (peremanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump over</li></ol><p><i>перемах(ну́ть)</i> (peremax(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to alternate (<i>with</i>), to intersperse (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перемежи́ть</i> (peremežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to alternate (<i>with</i>); to be interspersed (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перемежа́ть</i> (peremežátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;rare,&#32;transitive) to alternate (<i>with</i>), to intersperse (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>межи́ть</i> (mežítʹ, “to delineate the boundary of”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;rare,&#32;intransitive) to alternate (<i>with</i>), to be interspersed (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перемежи́ть</i> (peremežítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a qualitative change, shift, alternation, changeover (brought about either from within or from without)</li><li>(<i>education</i>) a recess (a break, intermission between lessons for rest or play, typically short)</li><li>a set of changeable clothing</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a course during a meal</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ме́на</i> (ména)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change</li><li>to change <i>(to, towards)</i>, to change one's attitude <i>(towards)</i></li></ol><p><i>перемени́ть</i> (peremenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>переме́на</i> (pereména)</li></ol><p><i>переме́на</i> (pereména) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alterable, alternate, transient, variable</li></ol><p><i>перемени́ть</i> (peremenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time-varying current (electric current which changes with time, e.g. alternating current)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change</li></ol><p><i>перемени́ть</i> (peremenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change</li><li>to change <i>(to, towards)</i>, to change one's attitude <i>(towards)</i></li></ol><p><i>переменя́ть</i> (peremenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>меси́ть</i> (mesítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift, to transfer</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li></ol><p><i>перемести́ть</i> (peremestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to agitate</li><li>to intermix, to intermingle, to mix (<i>together, with</i>), to blend (<i>with</i>)</li><li>to shuffle, to mix up, to confuse, to jumble up, to muddle up</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become mixed up, to become confused, to become jumbled</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mix together, to mingle (<i>of people or dissimilar objects</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перемеша́ть</i> (peremešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixing, stirring (<i>so as to obtain a homogeneous mixture</i>)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) mixing</li></ol><p><i>переме́шивать</i> (pereméšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to agitate</li><li>to intermix, to intermingle, to mix (<i>together, with</i>), to blend (<i>with</i>)</li><li>to shuffle, to mix up, to confuse, to jumble up, to muddle up</li><li>to knead</li></ol><p><i>перемеша́ть</i> (peremešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>перемеси́ть</i> (peremesítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become mixed up, to become confused, to become jumbled</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mix together, to mingle (<i>of people or dissimilar objects</i>)</li></ol><p><i>переме́шивать</i> (pereméšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift, to transfer</li></ol><p><i>перемести́ть</i> (peremestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li></ol><p><i>перемеща́ть</i> (peremeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement, relocation, shift, transference</li></ol><p><i>перемести́ть</i> (peremestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink at each other</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>мигну́ть</i> (mignútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift one's feet</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-мина́ть</i> (-minátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armistice, truce (formal agreement to end fighting)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>мир</i> (mir) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>primirje&#47;примирје</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect, to bridge (<i>two parts using a connector or jumper</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-мкнуть</i> (-mknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;ironic,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;Ukraine) victory, triumph (especially of Armed Forces of Ukraine, or of anything Ukrainian in general, often implying the opposite).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>перемо́га</i> (peremóha), analysable as a deverbal from <i>перемо́чь</i> (peremóčʹ) but the borrowing is recent.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind, to reel</li><li>to rewind, to re-reel</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rewinding, spooling</li></ol><p><i>перемота́ть</i> (peremotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;ironic,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory,&#32;Ukraine) synonym of <i>перемо́га</i> (peremóga)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>перемо́га</i> (peremóha), mocking the pronunciation of "г" as a "h" (which the closest equivalent in Russian is х (x)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;regional) to overcome, to conquer, to win</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>перемогти́</i> (peremohtý), Belarusian <i>перамагчы́</i> (pjeramahčý), Polish <i>przemóc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect, to bridge (<i>two parts using a connector or jumper</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial&#32;or&#32;folk poetic,&#32;transitive) to endure, to survive</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connection between parts, connector, jumper</li><li>watertight barrier</li><li>cofferdam, caisson</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) load-bearing door or window covering</li></ol><p><i>перемыка́ть</i> (peremykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>Compare Polish <i>przesmyk</i> (akin to <i>пресмыка́ться</i> (presmykátʹsja)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a resweeping</li><li>a sweeping (something) across (something else)</li><li>a rebasting (<i>sewing with large or loose stitches again</i>)</li><li>a basting of everything or a lot (<i>sewing everything or a lot of things with large or loose stitches</i>)</li><li>a throwing/casting/flinging (of something) across (something else)</li><li>a flinging/running/jumping (of oneself) across (something else)</li><li>a defection, a changing of sides, a tergiversation</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) snowdrift</li><li>(<i>fishing</i>) trotline</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) crossbeam serving as a connection between the walls of a hut, shed, barn etc.</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) double saddlebag (two tied bags thrown over a horse saddle)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перемета́ть</i> (peremetátʹ). The meaning covers the meanings of the other verbs <i>перемести́</i> (peremestí), <i>переметну́ть</i> (peremetnútʹ), <i>перемётывать</i> (peremjótyvatʹ), <i>переметну́ться</i> (peremetnútʹsja) and <i>перемётываться</i> (peremjótyvatʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>перено́сный</i> (perenósnyj) or <i>переносно́й</i> (perenosnój, “figurative, metaphorical”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redirect, redirecting, rerouting</li><li>redirection (setting a new direction)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-, “re-”) +&lrm; <i>направле́ние</i> (napravlénije, “direction, directing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overexert</li></ol><p><i>перенапря́г-</i> (perenaprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overexertion</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) surge, excessive voltage</li></ol><p><i>перенапря́г-</i> (perenaprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overexert</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>напря́чь</i> (naprjáčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overpopulation, surplus population</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>населе́ние</i> (naselénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realignment, changeover, reconfiguration, reset, resetting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry (all or many things) from one place to another</li><li>to wear (all or many items of clothing)</li><li>to carry (a child) too long in a pregnancy</li></ol><p><i>переноси́ть</i> (perenosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rehiring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfer, transference, transferral, moving, transporting</li><li>postponement</li><li>enduring, lasting</li></ol><p><i>перенести́</i> (perenestí) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfer, to carry, to transport</li><li><i>(word)</i> to carry over to the next line</li><li>to postpone, to put off</li><li>to extend</li><li>to bear, to endure, to stand, to survive, to withstand</li><li>to undergo</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to travel, to go <i>(very quickly)</i></li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to step</li></ol><p><i>перенести́</i> (perenestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to imitate</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying over, transfer</li><li>(grammar,&#32;poligraphy,&#32;informatics) word division, hyphenation</li><li>hyphen</li><li>slippage, transfer, carry-forward</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) snowdrift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tolerable, bearable</li><li>portable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfer, to carry, to transport (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>to carry over (a word) to the next line</li><li>to postpone, to put off</li><li>to extend (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to bear, to endure, to stand, to tolerate</li><li>to carry (all or many things) from one place to another</li><li>to wear (all or many items of clothing)</li><li>to carry (a child) too long in a pregnancy</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to travel, to go <i>(very quickly)</i></li><li>to be worn out, to be worn for too long</li><li>to stay for too long inside the mother's womb (of a fetus)</li></ol><p><i>переноси́ть</i> (perenosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nasal bridge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>перено́сный</i> (perenósnyj)</li></ol><p><i>перено́с</i> (perenós) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>figurative, metaphorical</li><li>portable</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) tolerable, acceptable, bearable</li></ol><p><i>перено́с</i> (perenós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrier, transferrer, transporter (a person, creature, or thing that carries something from one place to another)</li><li>(<i>epidemiology</i>) vector (carrier of a disease-causing agent)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) informer, intelligencer</li></ol><p>From <i>переноси́ть</i> (perenosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>переночева́ние</i> (perenočevánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend the night, to stay over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ночева́ть</i> (nočevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleepover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renumber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to renumber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to imitate</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reequipping, reequipment</li></ol><p><i>переобору́довать</i> (pereoborúdovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reequip</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>обору́довать</i> (oborúdovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>changing clothes</li><li>disguising</li></ol><p><i>переодева́ть</i> (pereodevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change someone's clothes</li><li>to disguise <i>(someone)</i></li><li>to change <i>(something)</i></li></ol><p><i>переоде́ть</i> (pereodétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change (clothing)</li></ol><p><i>переодева́ть</i> (pereodevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change someone's clothes</li><li>to disguise <i>(someone)</i></li><li>to change <i>(something)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>оде́ть</i> (odétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change (clothing)</li></ol><p><i>переоде́ть</i> (pereodétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reorientation</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ориента́ция</i> (orijentácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reorient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rethinking; reinterpretation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reequip, to replace old equipment of</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>оснасти́ть</i> (osnastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reequip, to replace old equipment of</li></ol><p><i>переоснасти́ть</i> (pereosnastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reequipping, replacing old equipment</li></ol><p><i>переоснасти́ть</i> (pereosnastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revalue, to reappraise</li><li>to overestimate, to overrate</li></ol><p><i>переоцени́ть</i> (pereocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revalue, to reappraise</li><li>to overestimate, to overrate</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>оцени́ть</i> (ocenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revaluation, reappraisal</li><li>overestimation</li></ol><p><i>переоцени́ть</i> (pereocenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>differential, difference, drop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall (<i>of many people or things, one after another</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall to (someone's) lot, to become (someone's) responsibility [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall (<i>of intermittent rain, snow, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to subside</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to pass by</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to lose weight, to get thin</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ)</p><p><i>перепад-</i> (perepad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solder again, to resolder</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to solder everything or many things</li><li>to drink (something) excessively, causing harm</li><li>to consistently drink all or most of (something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outdrink (all or many people), to drink (all or many people) under the table</li></ol><p><i>перепая́ть</i> (perepajátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>перепои́ть</i> (perepoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) skirmish, exchange of fire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squabble, wrangle, high words</li></ol><p><i>перепали́ть</i> (perepalítʹ, “to kill (all or many) by shooting”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall to (someone's) lot, to become (someone's) responsibility [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall (<i>of intermittent rain, snow, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to subside</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to pass by</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to lose weight, to get thin</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, to dirty (<i>all or many, or in many places</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>па́чкать</i> (páčkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solder again, to resolder</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to solder everything or many things</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пая́ть</i> (pajátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing all or many (things)</li><li>to sing again or otherwise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to surpass in singing</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) to rehash</li></ol><p><i>перепе́ть</i> (perepétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perpelъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hangover</li></ol><p>Play on the words <i>пе́репел</i> (pérepel, “quail”) and <i>перепи́л</i> (perepíl, “drank too much (alcohol)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) quail</li></ol><p><i>пе́репел</i> (pérepel) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparrowhawk</li><li>quailer</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́репел</i> (pérepel, “quail”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retranslation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing all or many (things)</li><li>to sing again or otherwise</li><li>to sing too much; to oversing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to surpass in singing, to outsing</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retype</li><li>to reprint</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reprinting; reprint</li></ol><p><i>перепеча́тать</i> (perepečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to retype</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reprint</li></ol><p><i>перепеча́тать</i> (perepečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink too much (liquid)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink too much alcohol, to overdrink</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink (liquid) longer than anyone else</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink alcohol longer than anyone else</li></ol><p><i>перепи́ть</i> (perepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rewrite, to retype</li><li>to copy</li><li>to make a list, to take a census <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reregister</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correspondence (reciprocal exchange of letters, emails, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>переписа́ть</i> (perepisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) census</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́репись</i> (pérepisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rewrite, to retype</li><li>to copy</li><li>to make a list, to take a census <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reregister</li></ol><p><i>переписа́ть</i> (perepisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in correspondence</li></ol><p><i>перепи́сывать</i> (perepísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>census</li><li>inventory</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>переписа́ть</i> (perepisátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink too much (liquid)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink too much alcohol, to overdrink</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink (liquid) longer than anyone else</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink alcohol longer than anyone else</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to melt (<i>entirely, or of all or many things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перепла́вить</i> (pereplávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to melt (<i>entirely, or of all or many things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>переплавля́ть</i> (pereplavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to replan, to reschedule</li><li>to remodel</li><li>to redevelop</li><li>to redraw, to lay out again</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>плани́ровать</i> (planírovatʹ)</p><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>планирова́ть</i> (planirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>replanning, rescheduling</li><li>remodeling</li><li>redeveloping</li><li>remodel</li><li>redevelopment</li></ol><p>From <i>переплани́ровать</i> (pereplanírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with expected stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interlace <i>(with)</i>, to interknit <i>(with)</i>, to intertwine <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(book)</i> to bind</li><li>to braid/plait again, to re-plait, to re-braid</li><li>to interweave <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interlace, to interweave</li><li>to become intertwined/interwoven</li></ol><p><i>переплести́</i> (pereplestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interlace <i>(with)</i>, to interknit <i>(with)</i>, to intertwine <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(book)</i> to bind</li><li>to braid/plait again, to re-plait, to re-braid</li><li>to interweave <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>переплет-</i> (pereplet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interlace, to interweave</li><li>to become intertwined/interwoven</li></ol><p><i>переплета́ть</i> (perepletátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textile</i>) weave</li><li>interweaving, interlacing</li><li>interweaving, intertwining</li><li>web, tangle</li></ol><p><i>переплет-</i> (pereplet-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim across</li><li>to sail across</li></ol><p><i>переплы́ть</i> (pereplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim across</li><li>to sail across</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit past or over something</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to spit further than someone; to outspit</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;vernacular,&#32;familiar) to outdo, to surpass, to top, to one-up</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>плю́нуть</i> (pljúnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binding (spine of a book)</li><li>difficult situation, jam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookbinder</li></ol><p><i>переплета́ть</i> (perepletátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;humorous,&#32;colloquial) a complicated expression or way of expressing ideas</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вы-</i> (vy-) +&lrm; <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retraining</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink (something) excessively, causing harm</li><li>to consistently drink all or most of (something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outdrink (all or many people), to drink (all or many people) under the table</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hangover</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перепои́ть</i> (perepoítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overflow</li></ol><p><i>перепо́лнить</i> (perepólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crowded, packed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfill, to overcrowd</li><li><i>(emotion)</i> to fill</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overfill, to overcrowd</li><li><i>(emotion)</i> to fill</li></ol><p><i>перепо́лнить</i> (perepólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To be overfilled; to fill up (as oneself)</li></ol><p><i>переполнять</i> (perepolnjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>ся</i> (sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commotion, flurry, ado, stir, fuss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane (enclosing or separating tissue)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>interdigital webbing</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perpona</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hymenopteran</li></ol><p>From <i>перепо́нчат(ый)</i> (perepónčat(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>крыл(о́)</i> (kryl(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>membranous, webbed</li></ol><p><i>перепо́нка</i> (perepónka) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage, crossing</li><li>crossing, ford</li><li>ferry, ferryboat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convey, to take across, to ferry over</li><li><i>(letters, parcels etc.)</i> to forward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to correct</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ford [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘a river’</small>] (<i>especially with difficulty</i>)</li><li>to cross [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘mountains, a swamp, or other difficult-to-cross place’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>перепра́вить</i> (pereprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfer, transshipment, transportation (to a place)</li></ol><p><i>перепра́вить</i> (pereprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convey, to take across, to ferry over</li><li><i>(letters, parcels etc.)</i> to forward</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to correct</li></ol><p><i>перепра́вить</i> (pereprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ford [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘a river’</small>] (<i>especially with difficulty</i>)</li><li>to cross [<i>with</i> <b>че́рез</b> (čérez, + accusative) <small>‘mountains, a swamp, or other difficult-to-cross place’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>переправля́ть</i> (perepravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try (all or many)</li><li>to try out, to test (all or many)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>про́бовать</i> (próbovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resell</li></ol><p><i>перепрода́ть</i> (pereprodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reseller</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>продаве́ц</i> (prodavéc) or <i>перепродава́ть</i> (pereprodavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resale</li></ol><p><i>перепрода́д-</i> (pereprodád-) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resell</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>прода́ть</i> (prodátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump over</li></ol><p><i>перепры́гнуть</i> (pereprýgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump over</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fright (only in expressions)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перепуга́ть</i> (perepugátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to frighten, to scare (badly)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get a fright, to get frightened, to panic</li></ol><p><i>перепуга́ть</i> (perepugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to confuse, to mix up, to muddle, to reverse</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get jumbled, to get mixed up</li><li>to get messed up</li></ol><p><i>перепу́тать</i> (perepútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entangle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to confuse</li></ol><p><i>перепу́тать</i> (perepútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get jumbled, to get mixed up</li><li>to get messed up</li></ol><p><i>перепу́тывать</i> (perepútyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process <i>(into)</i>, to work <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(to)</i></li><li>to remake</li><li>to exceed the fixed hours of work, to work overtime</li><li>to overwork</li><li>to recycle</li></ol><p><i>перерабо́тать</i> (pererabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) processing, reprocessing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process <i>(into)</i>, to work <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(to)</i></li><li>to remake</li><li>to exceed the fixed hours of work, to work overtime</li><li>to overwork</li><li>to recycle</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>processing, treatment</li><li>remaking, revision, revised version</li><li>overtime work</li></ol><p><i>перерабо́тать</i> (pererabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redistribution, reallocation, rearrangement</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>распределе́ние</i> (raspredelénije) or <i>перераспредели́ть</i> (pereraspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(over)</i>top, to outgrow, to outstrip</li><li>to develop <i>(into)</i>, to grow <i>(into)</i></li><li>to be too old <i>(for)</i>, to be past the stage <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>перерасти́</i> (pererastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to <i>(over)</i>top, to outgrow, to outstrip</li><li>to develop <i>(into)</i>, to grow <i>(into)</i></li><li>to be too old <i>(for)</i>, to be past the stage <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overspending</li><li>cost overrun</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>расхо́д</i> (rasxód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overspend</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>расхо́довать</i> (rasxódovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recalculation</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>расчёт</i> (rasčót)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reregistration</li></ol><p>From <i>перерегистр(и́ровать)</i> (pereregistr(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija) or from <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>регистра́ция</i> (registrácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting in two</li><li>cut point, place where something was cut in two</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) tub made of a barrel cut in half</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intersection, junction (<i>of paths or roads</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>перере́зать</i> (pererézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(all or many)</i> to kill, to slaughter</li><li>to cut</li><li>to cut off</li><li>to cut <i>(across)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>перере́зать</i> (pererézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>перереза́ть</i> (pererezátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>перере́зать</i> (pererézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to regenerate, to transform into a new person</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to regenerate, to transform into a new person</li></ol><p><i>перероди́ть</i> (pererodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebirth, regeneration</li><li>degeneration</li></ol><p><i>перероди́ть</i> (pererodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interruption, stop, break, intermission</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прерыва́ть</i> (preryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig across</li><li>to redig</li><li>to rummage (to search for something among many items by carelessly turning things over or pushing things aside)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up; to dig across</li><li>to dig again</li><li>to rummage</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a (small) change in one's seat</li><li>to transplant, to graft</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transplantation, grafting</li><li>change (of trains, planes), transfer, connection</li></ol><p><i>пересади́ть</i> (peresadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make someone change his seat</li><li>to transplant, to graft</li></ol><p><i>пересади́ть</i> (peresadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change seats, to move one's seat</li><li>to change (trains, buses, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ться</i> (sadítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intersect</li><li>to pierce into objects that are arranged in a line</li><li>to move across in a transverse direction</li><li>to move through any space vigorously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to divide into many parts</li></ol><p><i>пересе́чь</i> (pereséčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to intersect</li></ol><p><i>пересека́ть</i> (peresekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migrant, settler, immigrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migration, resettlement</li><li>move <i>(to another house)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) resettlement; migratory</li></ol><p><i>переселе́ние</i> (pereselénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to resettle</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to resettle, to migrate, to transmigrate</li><li>(religion,&#32;philosophy) to transmigrate</li></ol><p><i>пересели́ть</i> (pereselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to resettle</li></ol><p><i>пересели́ть</i> (pereselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to resettle, to migrate, to transmigrate</li><li>(religion,&#32;philosophy) to transmigrate</li></ol><p><i>переселя́ть</i> (pereseljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change seats, to move one's seat</li><li>to change (trains, buses, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intersection (in geometry)</li><li>overlap</li><li>crossing</li></ol><p><i>пересе́к-</i> (peresék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intersect</li><li>to pierce into objects that are arranged in a line</li><li>to move across in a transverse direction</li><li>to move through any space vigorously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to divide into many parts</li><li>to whip, to flog (all or many)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>се́чь</i> (séčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to intersect</li></ol><p><i>пересе́чь</i> (pereséčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, rugged (<i>of terrain</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outsit, to outstay</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit too long</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit and wait</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit out (e.g. a war)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause numbness in (a body part) by sitting too long</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit (<i>of all or many people</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ). Compare Czech <i>přesedět</i>, Kashubian <i>przesedzëc</i>, Silesian <i>przesiedzieć</i>, and Polish <i>przesiedzieć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outsit, to outstay</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit too long</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit and wait</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit out (e.g. a war)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause numbness in (a body part) by sitting too long</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit (<i>of all or many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пересиде́ть</i> (peresidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpower, to master, to subdue</li></ol><p><i>переси́лить</i> (peresílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpower, to master, to subdue</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-си́лить</i> (-sílitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retelling, narration</li><li>exposition, rendering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retell</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retell</li></ol><p><i>пересказа́ть</i> (pereskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump over, to vault (over)</li><li>to skip <i>(over)</i></li><li>to skip <i>(from to)</i>, to jump <i>(from to)</i></li></ol><p><i>перескок-</i> (pereskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump over, to vault <i>(over)</i></li><li>to skip <i>(over)</i></li><li>to skip <i>(from to)</i>, to jump <i>(from to)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to remit, to forward</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to review, to revise</li><li>to reconsider, to rethink</li><li>to go over (only in perfective aspect)</li></ol><p><i>пересмотре́ть</i> (peresmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to make fun of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mockingbird</li><li>mocker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconsideration, revision</li><li>review, retrial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to review, to revise</li><li>to reconsider, to rethink</li><li>to go over (only in perfective aspect)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oversalt</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to overdo</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to get too dry, to become parched</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to oversleep</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend the night, to stay over</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sleep <i>(with)</i>, to go to bed <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask again, to repeat a question, to ask to repeat</li></ol><p><i>переспроси́ть</i> (peresprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask again, to repeat a question, to ask to repeat</li><li>to ask many people</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>спроси́ть</i> (sprosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel with everyone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to cease, to quit</li></ol><p><i>переста́ть</i> (perestátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li>to rearrange</li><li>to transpose, to permute</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to shift</li><li>to rearrange</li><li>to transpose, to permute</li></ol><p><i>переста́вить</i> (perestávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shift, transposition</li><li>rearrangement</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) permutation</li><li>shift, transfer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overdo it, to act excessively, to be overzealous</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>стара́ться</i> (starátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to cease, to quit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perstati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuild, to reconstruct</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to tune, to attune</li><li><i>(a radion)</i> to switch over</li><li>to reorganize, to rearrange, to reform</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to re-form</li></ol><p><i>перестро́ить</i> (perestróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change lanes (when driving)</li><li>to change one's approach/ways/policy</li><li>(<i>radio</i>) to switch over <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to re-form</li></ol><p><i>перестра́ивать</i> (perestráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange of fire, skirmish, shoot-out, rumble</li></ol><p><i>перестреля́ть</i> (perestreljátʹ, “to shoot (many things)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot (<i>many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use up (<i>while shooting</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) perestroika</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) restructuring</li></ol><p><i>перестро́йка</i> (perestrójka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebuild, to reconstruct</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to tune, to attune</li><li><i>(a radion)</i> to switch over</li><li>to reorganize, to rearrange, to reform</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to re-form</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change lanes (when driving)</li><li>to change one's approach/ways/policy</li><li>(<i>radio</i>) to switch over <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to re-form</li></ol><p><i>перестро́ить</i> (perestróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tapping</li><li>taping sound</li><li>knocking out (a message), communicating through knocking</li><li>knocking repeatedly, or alternately on many objects</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>пересту́кать</i> (perestúkatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tap, to rap, to knock quickly</li><li>to knock out (a message)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tap, to rap, to knock quickly</li><li>to knock out (a message)</li><li>to knock repeatedly</li><li>to knock alternately (<i>on many things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пересту́кать</i> (perestúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step over, to overstep, to step</li><li>to transgress, to break</li><li>to move, to walk</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step over, to overstep, to step</li><li>to transgress, to break</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gossip, chitchat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recount, to count again</li><li>to convert <i>(to)</i> <i>(other units)</i></li><li>to count <i>(all)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recount, to count again</li><li>to convert <i>(to)</i> <i>(other units)</i></li></ol><p><i>пересчита́ть</i> (peresčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recount, recalculation</li><li>conversion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to remit, to forward</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sending, remittance, forwarding</li><li>transit prison</li></ol><p><i>пересыла́ть</i> (peresylátʹ, “to send, to remit, to forward”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour <i>(from; into)</i></li><li>to sprinkle <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(speech)</i> to intersperse <i>(with)</i></li><li>to pour too much, to put too much</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>пересы́пать</i> (peresýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, to get too dry, to become parched</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>-сыха́ть</i> (-syxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(specifically,&#32;geology,&#32;of a body of water) Existing only for certain seasons; intermittent.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag over, to carry over, to drag <i>(over)</i>, to lug <i>(across)</i>, to carry <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>перетаск-</i> (peretask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag over, to carry over, to drag <i>(over)</i>, to lug <i>(across)</i>, to carry <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill over, to overflow</li></ol><p><i>перете́к-</i> (pereték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li><li>to grind</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discuss</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endure</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to wait (patiently)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill over, to overflow</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li><li>to grind</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discuss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(bodybuilding,&#32;slang) the condition of having overtrained</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>перетрениро́вка</i> (peretreniróvka) and <i>перетрениро́ванность</i> (peretreniróvannostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to overwork</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>труди́ться</i> (trudítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to overwork</li></ol><p><i>перетруди́ться</i> (peretrudítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be frighten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to move, to trudge, to drag the feet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to travel (on a burdensome journey)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry (a burden)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal, to pinch</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to give off an unpleasant odour, to stink</li><li>(slang,&#32;impersonal) to enjoy, to revel</li><li>(slang,&#32;(especially so)) to ball out, to floss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;préy</i>, whence English <i>fare</i> and <i>ferry</i> as well as German <i>fahren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to travel (<i>usually far away</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shuffle, to trudge (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tug of war (rope-pulling competition)</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) tug of war (competition between two closely-matched forces)</li></ol><p>From <i>перетя́гивание</i> (peretjágivanije, “a pulling; an instance of the action of pulling”, from перетя́гивать (peretjágivatʹ, “to pull”)) +&lrm; <i>кана́т</i> (kanát, “rope”), literally “rope-pulling; pulling of rope”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull over</li><li>to stretch again</li><li>to pull in tight, to bind, to gird</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull, to draw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outbalance, to overbalance, to outweigh</li></ol><p><i>перетяг-</i> (peretjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pulling over, a hauling, a reupholstering</li></ol><p><i>перетяг-</i> (peretjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull over</li><li>to stretch again</li><li>to pull in tight, to bind, to gird</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull, to draw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to outbalance, to overbalance, to outweigh</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overpersuade, to change someone's mind</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince, to (over)persuade, to bring round</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>убежда́ть</i> (ubeždátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lane, alleyway, side street, bystreet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lane; alleyway</li></ol><p><i>переу́лок</i> (pereúlok, “lane”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repack</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>упакова́ть</i> (upakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overdo it, to act excessively, to be overzealous</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>усе́рдствовать</i> (usérdstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rearrange</li></ol><p><i>переустро́ить</i> (pereustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rearrange</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rearrangement, reconstruction, reorganization</li></ol><p><i>переустро́ить</i> (pereustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overwork</li><li>ergasthenia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rephrase; to paraphrase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interception</li><li>listening-in, wire tapping</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) capture</li><li>takeover</li><li>regrasp, change of grip</li><li>(<i>textile</i>) fastening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercept, to catch</li><li>to tap (into phone calls)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercept, to catch</li><li>to tap (into phone calls)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intercept, to catch</li><li>to tap (into phone calls)</li></ol><p>From <i>перехвати́ть</i> (perexvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>перехвата́ть</i> (perexvatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to outsmart, to outwit</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>хитри́ть</i> (xitrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage</li><li>crossing</li><li>(<i>military</i>) march</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) transition; conversion</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>хо́д</i> (xód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ad hominem (personal attack)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross, to get over, to get across</li><li>to pass on (to), to proceed (to), to turn (to)</li><li>to change, to switch (from/to), to convert (to)</li><li>to turn (into), to change, to convert, to transform (to), to go (into)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exceed, to go beyond (a limit)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adapter (device allowing more plugs at an outlet)</li></ol><p><i>переходи́ть</i> (perexodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transitional, transition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) transitive</li></ol><p><i>перехо́д</i> (perexód, “passage, crossing”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transitive verb (a verb that is accompanied by a direct object)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepper (including sweet peppers, chilis as well as black pepper)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьпьрьць</i> (pĭpĭrĭcĭ), from <i>пьпьрь</i> (pĭpĭrĭ, “pepper”) + <i>-ьць</i> (-ĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьpьrь</i>. Cognate with English <i>pepper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>list (register or roll of paper)</li></ol><p>From <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>че́сть</i> (čéstʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kʷit-tis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross <i>(out)</i>, to strike out</li><li>to cancel, to make null and void, to destroy <i>(the result of someone's actions)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>черкну́ть</i> (čerknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count <i>(all)</i>, to count again</li><li>to name <i>(all)</i></li><li>to reread, to read again</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>че́сть</i> (čéstʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kʷit-tis</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьsti</i> (“to read, to count”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enumeration, listing</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) transfer, transferring</li></ol><p><i>перечи́слить</i> (perečíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>listed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enumerate, to list (to specify each member of a sequence individually in incrementing order)</li><li>to transfer (funds, wages)</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to enroll (<i>in another location, category, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перечи́слить</i> (perečíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enumerate, to list (to specify each member of a sequence individually in incrementing order)</li><li>to transfer (funds, wages)</li></ol><p><i>перечи́слить</i> (perečíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to enroll (<i>in another location, category, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перечисля́ть</i> (perečisljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reread, to read again</li><li>to read too much</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perčitati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reread, to read again</li></ol><p><i>перечита́ть</i> (perečitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contradict [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to interfere with, to thwart [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to cut across, to cut crosswise [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perčiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;перёк</i> (&#42;perjók, “crosswise”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ); compare <i>поперёк</i> (poperjók, “crosswise”). Cognate with Polish <i>przeczyć</i> (“to contradict”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepper shaker (a small container designed to hold pepper and facilitate sprinkling it)</li></ol><p><i>пе́рец</i> (pérec) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross <i>(out)</i>, to strike out</li><li>to cancel, to make null and void</li></ol><p><i>перечеркну́ть</i> (perečerknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overstep, to step <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>перешагну́ть</i> (perešagnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overstep, to step <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>шагну́ть</i> (šagnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isthmus</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>ше́я</i> (šéja) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper to one another</li></ol><p><i>пере-</i> (pere-) +&lrm; <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perigee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) perihelion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>railing(s), handrail(s), banister(s)</li></ol><p>Suffixed derivative of <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пери́ла</i> (períla): (small) railings; bedrails</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perimeter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a feather bed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perina</i>. See <i>перо́</i> (peró, “feather”), <i>пери́ный</i> (perínyj, “of feather (adj.)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period (all senses)</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>περίοδος</i> (períodos), via French <i>période</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collectively,&#32;chiefly&#32;uncountable) periodicals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) periodic table</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) periodic table of elements</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>periodically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>periodic</li></ol><p><i>пери́од</i> (períod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periodicity</li></ol><p><i>периоди́чный</i> (periodíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>periodic</li></ol><p><i>пери́од</i> (períod) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peripeteia, peripetia, peripety</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) cirrocumulus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) cirrostratus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) pinnate</li><li>featherlike, plumose</li><li>(meteorology,&#32;relational) cirrus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peripheral, of/in the outlying districts, provincial</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) peripheral</li></ol><p><i>перифери́я</i> (periferíja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peripheral</li></ol><p><i>перифери́я</i> (periferíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periphery</li><li>outskirts, the outlying districts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) cambric muslin, percale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>percale</i>, from Persian پرگاله (pargâla, “rag”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) pearl</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) pearl</li><li>(<i>ironical</i>) malapropism, howler</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) Perl</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Perl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-of-pearl, nacre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Perlmutter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Argynnis</i>-genus fritillary.</li></ol><p>From <i>перламу́тр</i> (perlamútr, “mother-of-pearl”) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mother-of-pearl; nacreous</li><li>pearly, pearlescent</li></ol><p><i>перламу́тр</i> (perlamútr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pearl barley <i>(a meal)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) barley</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pearl barley</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;relational) pearl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permalloy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) permanganate</li></ol><p>From <i>пер-</i> (per-) +&lrm; <i>мангана́т</i> (manganát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) potassium permanganate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permanent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Perm</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) Permian</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Permic</li></ol><p><i>Пермь</i> (Permʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permutation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Permian (inhabitant of Perm)</li></ol><p><i>Пермь</i> (Permʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feathery (covered with feathers, usually of birds)</li></ol><p><i>перо́</i> (peró) +&lrm; <i>-на́тый</i> (-nátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feather, plume</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) feathers, down (<i>as a commodity or insulating material</i>)</li><li>nib, quill (writing implement)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) pen (a writer, a writer's individual style, or a metonymic reference to the process of writing)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) blade, vane, cutting edge (<i>of a tool or implement</i>)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) knife</li><li>(horticulture,&#32;colloquial) spear, scape (<i>of garlic, onion, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) fin (<i>of a marine creature</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) tail (<i>of a dog</i>)</li><li>(<i>cycling</i>) stay (chainstay or seatstay)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;però</i>; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird louse, feather louse</li></ol><p>From <i>перо́</i> (peró, “feather, plume”) +&lrm; <i>-е́д</i> (-éd, “-eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) featherwing beetle (Ptiliidae).</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>перо́</i> (peró, “feather”) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), literally “featherwinger”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pen(knife) (<i>originally "used for sharpening goose feathers, as used for writing"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>перо́</i> (peró) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perpendicular</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>пансио́н</i> (pansión), <i>пе́нсия</i> (pénsija), <i>пе́со</i> (péso), <i>пуд</i> (pud), <i>стипе́ндия</i> (stipéndija), <i>суспе́нзия</i> (suspénzija), and <i>фунт</i> (funt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(railway) platform</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>perron</i> or German <i>Perron</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Persian cat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) A contraction of персонаж</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>персъ</i> (persŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>Πέρσης</i> (Pérsēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;poetic,&#32;archaic) woman’s breasts, bosom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrsь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European, cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>пр̏са</i>/<i>pȑsa</i> (“breast”), Slovak <i>prs</i>, <i>prsia</i> (“bosom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian (<i>language</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Persian</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>Персида</i> (Persida) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peach (fruit or tree)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;offensive) buttocks, a woman's genitals</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>&#42;персикъ</i> (&#42;persik) (derivative персиковъ att. in 17th c.), likely from Middle Low German <i>përsik</i> (equal to Middle High German <i>pfërsich</i>, German <i>Pfersich</i>), from Latin <i>persicum</i> (Latin <i>mālum persicum</i> (fruit), Latin <i>mālus persicus</i> (tree)), see there. Compare Romanian <i>piersic</i>.</p><p>For semantic parallels compare <i>апельси́н</i> (apelʹsín). Also compare <i>гречи́ха</i> (grečíxa), <i>гре́цкий оре́х</i> (gréckij oréx), <i>рома́шка</i> (romáška), <i>ды́ня</i> (dýnja).</p><p>Sense 2 was coined on TikTok since 2024. Compare with the use of translingual <i>🍑</i> by English-speaking Internet users.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peach</li></ol><p><i>пе́рсик</i> (pérsik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Persian</li></ol><p><i>Пе́рсия</i> (Pérsija) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person, personage</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Polish <i>persona</i>, from Latin <i>persōna</i>. Doublet of <i>парсу́на</i> (parsúna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persona non grata</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>persona</i> (“person”) + <i>non</i> (“not”) + <i>grata</i> (from <i>gratus</i> (“acceptable”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>character, personage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>personnage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, personnel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bibliographic section describing the life of a person</li><li>biographical article (<i>in a dictionary, encyclopedia, or reference book</i>)</li><li>personalia (published materials devoted to the life of a person)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) persona</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>persōnālia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>personally (in a personal manner)</li></ol><p><i>персона́льный</i> (personálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>personal</li></ol><p>Latin <i>persōnālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>personal computer (small computer for use by one person at a time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to personify</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>personifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), from French <i>personnifier</i>; see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) persoonia (<i>Persoonia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Persoonia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perspective <i>(the appearance of depth in objects)</i></li><li>vista, prospect</li><li>prospect, outlook, look-out, aspects <i>(expected future)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property of being prospective, prospects</li></ol><p><i>перспекти́вный</i> (perspektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prospective, forward-looking</li><li>perspectival</li><li>promising</li></ol><p><i>перспекти́ва</i> (perspektíva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic) finger</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) destiny, a mystic and secret path or direction brought upon a man (most of the time by God)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrstъ</i>. Compare native <i>напёрсток</i> (napjórstok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(finger)</i> ring</li><li>seal ring, signet ring</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьрстьнь</i> (pĭrstĭnĭ), <i>перстень</i> (perstenĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrsty</i> (“finger ring; ring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) dust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьрсть</i> (pĭrstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrstь</i> (“dust, soil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Karelian cottage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьрть</i> (pĭrtĭ, “bath-house”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrtь</i> (“bath-house”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Peruvian</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>peruano</i> + <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) thunderbolt, lightning</li><li>(plural only,&#32;dated,&#32;poetic) the thunder of battle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>перунъ</i> (perunŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perunъ</i>. Cognate with the Slavic deity <i>Перу́н</i> (Perún).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Перун)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) Perun (god of thunder and lightning)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Перунъ</i> (Perunŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Perunъ</i>. Cognate with <i>перу́н</i> (perún, “thunderbolt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfect <i>(tense)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfectionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to cough</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandruff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrxotь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepper mill</li></ol><p>From <i>пере́ц</i> (peréc) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glove (<i>with separate sheaths for each finger</i>), gauntlet</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;slang) snakes alive, fifty-five in the lotto game</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>пьрстатица</i> (pĭrstatica), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrstъ</i> (“finger”). Related to <i>перст</i> (perst, “finger”). The sense of fifty-five in lotto comes from the two verses Вме́сто ва́ленок перча́тки &#47; Натя́нул себе́ на пя́тки. (Vmésto válenok perčátki &#47; Natjánul sebé na pjátki.) in the poem and 1975 film Вот какой рассе́янный, connecting <i>пя́тка</i> (pjátka) to <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pepper</li></ol><p><i>пе́рец</i> (pérec) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja): (little) song</li></ol><p><i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>songbook</li><li>(<i>chorus</i>) singer</li><li>songwriter</li></ol><p><i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) song</li></ol><p><i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arctic fox, polar fox</li><li>(minced oath,&#32;slang) curtains (the end for something or someone)</li></ol><p><i>пёс</i> (pjos) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p><p>Euphemistic form of <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc, a vulgar curse), influenced by the noun <i>песе́ц</i> (peséc, “arctic fox”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gudgeon (fish, <i>Gobio gobio</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piskorь</i>, folk-etymologically changed after the suffix +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>songwriter</li></ol><p><i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(church) chanting, canticle, motet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja): song</li><li>canto</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěsnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something very good, very cool</li><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) canticle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěsnь</i>, adapted to the common ending <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) peso</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>peso</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>pendō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand</li><li>quicksand</li><li>granulated sugar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěsъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>песо́к</i> (pesók, “sand”)</li></ol><p><i>песо́к</i> (pesók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stint, sandpiper</li></ol><p><i>песо́к</i> (pesók, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandbox (box with sand for children)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hourglass (clock)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sand, sandy</li><li>sand-coloured/sand-colored</li><li>(<i>baking</i>) short, shortcrust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěsъčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>песо́к</i> (pesók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pessimism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pessimisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pessimist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pessimiste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pessimistic</li></ol><p><i>пессими́ст</i> (pessimíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pessimistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle, stamper</li><li>pistil</li><li>(<i>childish</i>) pistol, gun</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Russian North, Ural) young edible horsetail (<i>Equisetum arvense</i>) shoot</li></ol><p>From <i>пест</i> (pest) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pesticide</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pestizid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;stilted) to nurse, to take care of</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;stilted) to educate, to raise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěstovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show/appear multicolored</li><li>to be multicolored or motley [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘with/due to something’</small>]</li><li>to become multicolored or motley</li><li>to look like a kaleidoscope/mosaic of, to abound in [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in/with multicolored things flickering in front of the eyes’</small>]</li><li>to abound, to be replete [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in/with repetitive things’</small>]</li><li>to be too motley or excessively multicolored</li></ol><p><i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motley, gayness; diversity of colours/colors</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lemming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) arctic fox, polar fox</li></ol><p><i>пес(е́)ц</i> (pes(é)c) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandstone, gritstone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandwort (<i>Arenaria</i> gen. et spp.)</li><li>sanderling</li><li>sand eel</li><li>gerbil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy</li></ol><p><i>песо́к</i> (pesók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grit, grain of sand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) petabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>пета-</i> (peta-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) petabit</li></ol><p>From <i>пета-</i> (peta-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pétanque, boules, bowls</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detonator, petard, squib</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>пе́тля</i> (pétlja): small loop, small noose</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>петля́</i> (petljá): small loop, small noose</li><li>eyelet, eye, pull strap, tab</li></ol><p><i>петля́</i> (petljá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) St. Petersburg</li></ol><p><i>Петербу́рг</i> (Peterbúrg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petersburger (resident or citizen of Saint Petersburg)</li></ol><p><i>Петербу́рг</i> (Peterbúrg) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) petit maître</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>petit-maître</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petition</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>petītiō</i> (“a request, petition, beseeching”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пети́ция</i> (petícija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttonhole, tab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Petliurite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loop (length of thread, line or rope, a shape produced by a curve)</li><li>noose</li><li>buttonhole, eye</li><li>stitch</li><li>hinge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;petьľa</i>, from an Old Germanic i-umlauted form of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fatilaz</i>: compare Old Norse <i>fetill</i>, Old High German <i>fezzil</i> (German <i>Fessel</i>), Old English <i>fetel</i> (English <i>fettle</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dodge</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>пе́тля</i> (pétlja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Peter's (<i>in collocations</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Петров)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Petrov, one of the most common Russian surnames</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Peter's</li></ol><p><i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Peter, Peter's (also referring to Peter the Great)</li></ol><p><i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Петровский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Petrovsky</li><li>a transliteration of the Macedonian surname <i>Петровски</i>&nbsp;m (Petrovski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petroglyph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Petrograd (name of Saint Petersburg between 1914 and 1924)</li></ol><p><i>Петрогра́д</i> (Petrográd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Petropavlovsk (a city in Kazakhstan)</li></ol><p><i>Петропа́влов(ск)</i> (Petropávlov(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) low-quality and tasteless humor, television shows and mass culture in general</li></ol><p>From <i>Петрося́н</i> (Petrosján) +&lrm; <i>-щи́на</i> (-ščína), after the comedian Yevgeny Petrosyan known for such humor.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parsley</li></ol><p><i>петру́шка</i> (petrúška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) parsley (herb)</li><li>foolishness, absurdity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pietruszka</i>, adapted from Czech <i>petržel</i> (see <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)), from Middle High German <i>petersîlje</i>, from Latin <i>petroselīnum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πετροσέλῑνον</i> (petrosélīnon, literally “rock celery”). See English <i>parsley</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Петрушка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive of the male given name Пётр (Pjotr)</li><li>Petrushka (a stock character of Russian folk puppetry), Punch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) root parsley</li></ol><p><i>петру́шка</i> (petrúška, “parsley”) +&lrm; <i>корнево́й</i> (kornevój, “root”, relational adjective)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bok choy (Chinese cabbage), <i>Brassica rapa</i> subsp. <i>chinensis</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>白菜</i> (báicài).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rooster, cock</li><li>(very&#32;rare,&#32;usually about children,&#32;easily confused with slang meaning) impulsive, aggressive person or group</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;prison slang,&#32;offensive) punk, prison bitch (<i>a passive homosexual male, or a man forced into a homosexual relationship and used as a sex slave or servant by another man, especially in the prison</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pětuxъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>петь</i> (petʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ух</i> (-ux), said to have been influenced by <i>Пе́тя</i> (Pétja), diminutive of <i>Пётр</i> (Pjotr). Related to <i>петь</i> (petʹ, “to sing”). Compare <i>пе́вень</i> (pévenʹ), Ukrainian <i>пі́вень</i> (pívenʹ), Belarusian <i>пе́вень</i> (pjévjenʹ), Bulgarian <i>пете́л</i> (petél), Macedonian <i>петел</i> (petel), Serbo-Croatian <i>pijétao</i>, <i>pijévac</i>, Slovene <i>petêlin</i>, <i>pẹ́təł</i>. Compare also Polish <i>piać</i> (“to crow”), which is cognate with <i>петь</i> (petʹ). Likewise Latin <i>canere</i> means both “to sing” and “to crow”. Also compare with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanô</i> (“rooster, cock”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanjō</i> (“hen”) (whence English <i>hen</i>) ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keh₂n-</i> (“to sing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cock, rooster</li></ol><p><i>пету́х</i> (petúx) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пету́х</i> (petúx): (young) rooster</li><li>rooster lollypop</li><li>fighting fish, betta</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) crested knit cap</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;offensive) (little) punk, bitch (<i>a passive homosexual male, or a man forced into a homosexual relationship, especially in prison</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing</li><li>to crow</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to snitch, to rat out, to crack, to start testifying</li></ol><p>From Late Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;poiH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only)</li></ol><p><i>пе́ть</i> (pétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infantry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěxъ</i>; <i>&#42;pěxota</i>, which is derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pēd-</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;ped-</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Russian <i>под</i> (pod, “floor, ground”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infantryman, foot soldier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) infantry</li></ol><p><i>пехо́та</i> (pexóta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sadden, to grieve</li></ol><p><i>печа́ль</i> (pečálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sad, to grieve</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to care, to take care</li></ol><p><i>печа́лить</i> (pečálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadness, grief, sorrow</li></ol><p>Related to <i>печь</i> (pečʹ, “to bake”). Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>печаль</i> (pečalĭ, “grief, concern, loathing”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pečalь</i>, from <i>&#42;peťi</i> (“to bake”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>го́ре</i> (góre) (akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sympathizer, someone who cares about others</li></ol><p>From <i>печа́литься</i> (pečálitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sadly, sorrowfully</li></ol><p><i>печа́льный</i> (pečálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, grieved, sorrowful, wistful, doleful, mournful</li><li>grievous</li></ol><p><i>печа́ль</i> (pečálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>printing</li><li>typing</li></ol><p><i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to print (to copy something on a surface, especially by machine)</li><li>to type (to use a typewriter or to enter text or commands into a computer using a keyboard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signet</li></ol><p><i>печа́ть</i> (pečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) printer, device that prints images and text on paper</li><li>printer (a person who works in printing)</li></ol><p><i>печа́ть</i> (pečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in print</li></ol><p><i>печа́тный</i> (pečátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>printed (relating to something written or published)</li><li>block (letter)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) printing</li></ol><p><i>печа́ть</i> (pečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) printing house</li></ol><p><i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>printing, print</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;collective) press (media)</li><li>stamp (device)</li><li>seal, stamp (imprint)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stamp (hallmark, feature or sign of some influence)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pečatь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pecheneg</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>печенѣгъ</i> (pečeněgŭ), of Turkic origin and related to Turkish <i>bacanak</i>. Compare Hungarian <i>besenyő</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liver</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>печень</i> (pečenĭ), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pečenь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>печёный</i> (pečónyj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>печі́нка</i> (pečínka), Polish <i>pieczeń</i> (“roast”), Czech <i>pečenka</i> (“roast”). Compare Lithuanian <i>kẽpenys</i> (“liver”), from <i>kèpti</i> (“bake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastry, cruller</li><li>(<i>UK</i>): biscuit; (<i>US</i>): cookie</li><li>cracker</li></ol><p>From <i>пе́чь</i> (péčʹ, “to bake”) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťì</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pektei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷeti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>, whence English <i>cook</i> and <i>biscuit</i> (in part).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пече́нье</i> (pečénʹje): cookie</li></ol><p><i>пече́нье</i> (pečénʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stove, oven</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) heater in an automobile</li></ol><p><i>печь</i> (pečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stovemaker, oven-maker</li></ol><p>From <i>печь</i> (pečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stove</li></ol><p><i>печь</i> (pečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) cave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>печера</i> (pečera, “cave”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťera</i>, from <i>&#42;peťь</i> (“oven”) + <i>&#42;-era</i>, from <i>&#42;peťi</i> (“to bake”) + <i>&#42;-tь</i>. Doublet of <i>пеще́ра</i> (peščéra), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Печора)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pechora (a river in the Komi Republic and the Nenets Autonomous Okrug, in northwest Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>печо́ра</i> (pečóra, “cave”), due to the fact that there are many caves downstream of the river.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small stove</li></ol><p><i>печо́ра</i> (pečóra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li>to scorch <i>(only in relation to the Sun or other stars)</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stove</li><li>oven</li><li>furnace</li><li>kiln</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>печи</i> (peči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťi</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;péktei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷeti</i>, from <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>.</p><p>See also English <i>cook</i>, <i>culinary</i>, <i>cuisine</i>, <i>concoct</i> and <i>peptic</i>; also Russian <i>пот</i> (pot, “sweat, perspiration”), Sanskrit <i>पचति</i> (pácati), Albanian <i>pjek</i>, Tocharian B <i>päk-</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō), Latin <i>coquō</i>.</p><p>From Old East Slavic <i>печь</i> (pečĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;péktis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷtis</i>, from <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>. Doublet of <i>пещь</i> (peščʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li><i>(in the sun)</i> to broil</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to take care <i>(of)</i>, to care <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>пе́чь</i> (péčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пе́чень</i> (péčenʹ): liver, especially as food</li><li>to be a pain in the neck is сидеть в печёнках. An example is "Твоё поведение мне сидит в печёнках." Your behavior is a pain in my neck. Or your behavior is getting on my nerves.</li></ol><p><i>пе́чень</i> (péčenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) liver; hepatic</li></ol><p><i>печёнка</i> (pečónka, “liver”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>baked (cooked by baking)</li></ol><p><i>пек-</i> (pek-) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) foot mail (the postal service)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pedestrian</li></ol><p><i>пешехо́д</i> (pešexód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestrian crossing (place for pedestrians to cross a street)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) diminutive of <i>пешком</i> (peškom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmounted</li><li>pedestrian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěšь</i>. Ultimately a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓds</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chess,&#32;animate&#32;or&#32;inanimate) pawn</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;animate) pawn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěšьka</i>, derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěšь</i> (“pedestrian, on foot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on foot, afoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icepick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пещера</i> (peštera, “cave”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťera</i>, from <i>&#42;peťь</i> (“oven”) + <i>&#42;-era</i>, from <i>&#42;peťi</i> (“to bake”) + <i>&#42;-tь</i>. Doublet of <i>печо́ра</i> (pečóra), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) corpus cavernosum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cavernous</li></ol><p><i>пеще́ра</i> (peščéra) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cave</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) wild, primitive</li></ol><p><i>пеще́ра</i> (peščéra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave cricket (Rhaphidophoridae)</li></ol><p><i>пеще́рный</i> (peščérnyj, “cave”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>кузне́чик</i> (kuznéčik, “bush-cricket”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caveman, troglodyte, cave-dweller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) oven, furnace (especially in religious contexts)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пещь</i> (peštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;peťь</i>. Doublet of <i>печь</i> (pečʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging) abbreviation of <i>пожалуйста</i> (požalujsta)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pi (the Greek letter Π (P)&#47;π (p))</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) pi</li><li>the English letter P/p; a pee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πεῖ</i> (peî), <i>πῖ</i> (pî).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>pee</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>пизда</i> (pizda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>пиздец</i> (pizdec)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piyāla, a small bowl</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پیاله (piyâle), from Persian پیاله (piyâle); compare to Ancient Greek <i>φιάλη</i> (phiálē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upright piano</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pianist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public relations</li><li>promotion, plug, hype</li></ol><p>First attested in 1988. Borrowed from English <i>PR</i> (“public relations”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public-relations manager, public-relations specialist</li></ol><p><i>пиа́р</i> (piár) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piastre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пи́во</i> (pívo): beer</li></ol><p><i>пи́во</i> (pívo) +&lrm; <i>-а́с</i> (-ás)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пи́во</i> (pívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewery</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>пивова́ренный заво́д</i> (pivovárennyj zavód).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пи́во</i> (pívo): beer</li></ol><p><i>пи́во</i> (pívo) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pub, beer parlour (a bar selling beers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beer</li></ol><p><i>пи́во</i> (pívo) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beer parlour, beershop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bar, pub</li></ol><p><i>пивна́я</i> (pivnája) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) portion of beer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пиво</i> (pivo, “drink, beverage; beer”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pivo</i> (“drink, beverage; beer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃iwom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewer</li></ol><p>From <i>пи́во</i> (pívo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вар</i> (-var), combining form of <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to brew”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brewing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brewery (place where beer is produced)</li></ol><p><i>пивова́р</i> (pivovár) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone habituated to drenching himself with beer, a beer fiend, beeraholic, suck-pint</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>пи́во</i> (pívo) +&lrm; <i>диноза́вр</i> (dinozávr). A meme spread in 2021, illustrated with dinosaurs holding beers, offering themselves for meatspace T-shirt designs.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) A dramatic change in a business's goals made to maintain functionality; a pivot.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пи́во</i> (pívo): beer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) brewski; beer (affectionately)</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) brewski; portion of beer (affectionately)</li></ol><p><i>пиво</i> (pivo) +&lrm; <i>-чан-</i> (-čan-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pygidium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pygmy (a member of various African peoples)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pygmaeī</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πυγμαῖος</i> (pugmaîos, “a member of a race of dwarves”), from <i>πυγμή</i> (pugmḗ, “fist (as small as a fist)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigment (any color in plant or animal cells)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>pigmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigmentation (coloration of tissue)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) alternative form of <i>пидор</i> (pidor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) alternative form of <i>пидорас</i> (pidoras)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coat, jacket (suit jacket for men)</li><li>sports jacket, sport jacket, sports coat, sport coat, lounge jacket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pea jacket</i>, from Dutch <i>pijjekker</i> or West Frisian <i>pijjakker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пиджа́к</i> (pidžák)</li></ol><p><i>пиджа́к</i> (pidžák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) pidgin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pidgin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) fucking asshole, motherfucker (a mean, dishonest or rude person)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) fag, faggot (a male homosexual)</li></ol><p>Shortened version of <i>пидора́с</i> (pidorás), a deliberate mispronunciation of <i>педера́ст</i> (pederást, pedɛrást). Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>παιδεραστής</i> (paiderastḗs).</p><p>Compare Macedonian <i>педер</i> (peder) and Serbo-Croatian <i>пе́дер</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) fag, faggot (a homosexual)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) motherfucker, asshole (a rude or highly unpleasant person)</li></ol><p>Deliberate mispronunciation of <i>педера́ст</i> (pederást, pedɛrást).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) faggoty</li></ol><p><i>пи́дор</i> (pídor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) piety, reverence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pietät</i>, from Latin <i>pietās</i> (whence also English <i>piety</i>, English <i>pity</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyjamas</li></ol><p>From English <i>pyjama</i>, from Hindi <i>पैजामा</i> (paijāmā) / Urdu پائِجامَہ (pāijāma), from Classical Persian پایجامه (pāyjāma), archaic form of پاجامه (pājāma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common tansy (<i>Tanacetum vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>piżmo</i> (“musk”), from Old Czech <i>pižmo</i> (“musk”), from Old High German <i>bisamo</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>bisamum</i>, from Hebrew <i>בֹּשֶׂם</i> (bṓśem, “perfume”). For one popular use of the plant is to burn it for fumigation; even bees are smoked with it for pacification.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) fop</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) unexperienced young man</li></ol><p>The sense “unexperienced young man” is the original one and is derived from French <i>pigeon</i> (“gullible person, gull; pigeon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) pussy, cunt, twat</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;extremely offensive,&#32;animate) woman, bitch, ho</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) bad situation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pīzdà</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pīˀsdāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;písdeh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>пиздобо́л</i> (pizdoból)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) face</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) mouth</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to hit, to beat</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to steal</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to drink (something alcoholic)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to say (something inappropriate or off-topic)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;intransitive) to fuck off (somewhere) (to go)</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) very good, fucking good</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “cunt, twat”) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to speak off the point, to bitch, to complain, to piss and moan</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lie, to bullshit</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) extreme degree of anything; fuckload, shitload, fuckton, shitton</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;sometimes humorous) clusterfuck; extremely undesirable situation</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking hell!, holy shit!, what the fuck!</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), attested since ca. 1973. Compare Ukrainian <i>пизде́ць</i> (pyzdécʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to beat the shit out of</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) liar, fibber, bullshitter</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>пизде́ть</i> (pizdétʹ, “to lie”) +&lrm; <i>балабо́л</i> (balaból, “flizz-talker”). By surface analysis, пизд- + -о- (originally a reflex of East Slavic liquid metathesis) + *bol-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lie, to tell lies</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to babble, to talk nonsense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lie, falsehood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) group of useless people</li></ol><p><i>пизда</i> (pizda, “pussy, cunt”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>братия</i> (bratija, “fraternity, brotherhood”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;offensive,&#32;vulgar) a gook (Asian person)</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “pussy, cunt”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar,&#32;slang) cuntlicker</li></ol><p>From <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “cunt”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лиз</i> (-liz), combining form of <i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ, “to lick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;informal) a man, a guy</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) asshole; hooligan</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) little shit, little fucker, shrimp, squirt (a hard worker)</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar,&#32;swear word) extreme degree of anything, slang form of <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc)</li></ol><p><i>пизда</i> (pizda) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a man who is overly concerned with his sex life; a man who is unsuccessfully trying to find sex partners</li></ol><p><i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) liar, fibber</li><li>shitass</li></ol><p>From <i>пизде́ть</i> (pizdétʹ, “to lie”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) fucker, fuckface, cuntface (a contemptible, unpleasant man)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) <i>(in conjunction with</i> мелкий<i>)</i> little shit, little fucker, shrimp, squirt (a small boy, a young son, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “cunt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) beating, kicking ass</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) telling off, scolding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) Young age, a period when a person is considered to be youth.</li></ol><p><i>пиздю́к</i> (pizdjúk) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;slang) about someone who is small, e.g. a young girl, young daughter, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “cunt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to travel from one place to another</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to walk or hike long distance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit, bullshit (deceitful statements, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>пизде́ть</i> (pizdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž), from <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “cunt, twat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pussy, cunt, twat diminutive of <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;humorous) poet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ποιητής</i> (poiētḗs). Doublet of <i>поэ́т</i> (poét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;humorous) poet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ποιητής</i> (poiētḗs). Doublet of <i>поэ́т</i> (poét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lance, pike</li><li>(card games,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;colloquial&#32;if&#32;in the singular) a spade</li><li>(pathology,&#32;rare) pica</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pika</i>, from French <i>pique</i>.</p><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>pīca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piquancy, poignancy</li><li>spice</li></ol><p><i>пика́нтный</i> (pikántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piquant, savoury, spicy, sexy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>pikant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickup truck (truck with an open cargo bed)</li><li>pickup (seduction technique, especially as practiced by pickup artists)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pickup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>пика́п</i> (pikáp): small pickup truck</li></ol><p><i>пика́п</i> (pikáp) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peep, to squeak</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually negative) to utter a peep, to cry out, to moan</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to object, to express one's opinion</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;video games) to peek (from behind cover)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Internet slang) to pick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pikati</i>, ultimately, per Vasmer, onomatopoeic; cognate with Polish <i>pikać</i> and Latvian <i>pīka</i> (“whiny child; gudgeon (<i>because the fish squeaks</i>)”).</p><p>From English <i>peek</i> +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p><p>From English <i>pick</i> +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) dive</li><li>(<i>textiles</i>) piqué</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) piqué</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>piqué</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) piqué (a kind of fabric)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickelhaube</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pickelhaube</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pickelhaube</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pickelhelm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) picket (soldiers or troops placed on a line forward of a position to warn against an enemy advance)</li><li>picket (group of protesters)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) piquet <i>(card game)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pikett</i>, from French <i>piquet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picketing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picketer</li><li>protester</li></ol><p><i>пике́т</i> (pikét) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) spades (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♠)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dive, diving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) piccolo</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>piccolo</i> (“small”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picnic (a meal eaten outdoors)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pique-nique</i>, English <i>picnic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peep, to squeak</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually negative) to utter a peep, to cry out, to moan</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to object, to express one's opinion</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;video games) to peek (from behind cover)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Internet slang) to pick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pìknǫti</i>.</p><p>From English <i>peek</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p><p>From English <i>pick</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen of spades</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spades</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) peak</li></ol><p><i>пи́ка</i> (píka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p><p><i>пик</i> (pik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of spades (the playing card belonging to the spades suit and featuring one pip)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picogram</li></ol><p>SI unit naming standard, equivalent to English <i>picogram</i>, Romanian <i>picogram</i>, French <i>picogramme</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pixel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pixel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pixel</li></ol><p><i>пи́ксель</i> (píkselʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pict</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pictogram (picture that represents a word or an idea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pila</i>, from Old High German <i>fil</i> (“file”). Compare modern German <i>Feile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pilea (<i>Pilea</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pilea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilgrim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawfly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sawyer</li></ol><p><i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) peeling, exfoliation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>peeling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw (cut with a saw)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nag, to chastise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appropriate (illegally, especially of public money, slang for the practice in which private contractors pay kickbacks to the various officials who award them projects)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to walk, to drive (especially of long distances)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to screw, to have sex with</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play awkwardly on a violin or similar instrument.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piliti</i>, whence cognate with Polish <i>pilić</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пила́</i> (pilá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawing</li><li>fret saw</li><li>fingernail file, nail file</li></ol><p><i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ, “to saw”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillar, pier, pylon, pier tower, tower</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>πῠλών</i> (pŭlṓn, “gateway”), an augmentative of <i>πῠ́λη</i> (pŭ́lē, “door-wing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawmill, powersaw bench</li></ol><p><i>пила́</i> (pilá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ра́ма</i> (ráma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) pilot</li><li>(dated,&#32;nautical) pilot</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pilot fish</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Ancient Greek <i>πηδόν</i> (pēdón) (via Italian <i>pilota</i> and others). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piloting, flying</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pilotage</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пило́т</i> (pilót) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pilot, to fly</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>pilotieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) or <i>пило́т</i> (pilót) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side cap, garrison cap, wedge cap, overseas cap</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) cunt, pussy <i>(euphemism for пизда)</i>; <i>(by extension)</i> woman.</li></ol><p><i>пило́т</i> (pilót) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trial, experimental, pilot</li></ol><p>English <i>pilot</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пила́</i> (pilá): a small saw; a small file (tool); a nail file</li><li>diminutive of <i>пи́лка</i> (pílka): a small saw; a small file (tool); a nail file</li></ol><p><i>пи́лка</i> (pílka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete&#32;or&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Poland) urgent</li><li>(obsolete&#32;or&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Poland) diligent, assiduous</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) saw, sawing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pilьnъ</i>.</p><p><i>пила́</i> (pilá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serrated</li></ol><p><i>пила́</i> (pilá) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawyer</li></ol><p><i>пила́</i> (pilá) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pill, bolus, globule, pilule</li></ol><p>Ultimately or directly from French <i>pilule</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut into pieces</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li></ol><p>Derived from an iterative of obsolete <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) (see <i>-пять</i> (-pjatʹ)), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pęti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ping pong, table tennis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penguin</li><li>Pingvin (Soviet anti-zero-G suit)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from French <i>pingouin</i>, from Dutch <i>pinguïn</i>, from English <i>penguin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) penguin</li></ol><p><i>пингви́н</i> (pingvín) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;derogatory) an American, a Yank</li><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;regional,&#32;obsolete) a Pontic Greek</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πῐ́νδος</i> (Pĭ́ndos).</p><p>The modern sense (“a Yank, an American”) originated in the late 1990s as military slang among Russian peacekeepers in Bosnia and Kosovo, firstly against American soldiers and then to any American. Earlier, Russian soldiers took it from Chechen militias, who used this term to refer to Russians, most likely from Balkan Muslims who joined the Chechens.</p><p>In modern Greek slang, the original word now refers to someone who is unsophisticated. The original subjects of this slur were either rural shepherds who lived along the Pindus Mountains between Albania and Greece (which was picked up by Balkans who joined the Chechens), or Pontic Greeks who lived along the Northern and Eastern shores of the Black Sea and the Sea of Azov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal,&#32;proscribed&#32;in modern usage) coat, jacket (a man's suit jacket)</li></ol><p>From <i>пиджа́к</i> (pidžák) contaminated by <i>спина́</i> (spiná, “back”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pinot Noir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kick</li></ol><p><i>пина́ть</i> (pinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tweezers, pincers (small pincer-like instrument, usually made of metal, used for handling small objects)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pincette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinscher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) pinyin, Pinyin</li></ol><p>From Mandarin 拼音 (<i>pīnyīn</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peony</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) pion</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Παιονία</i> (Paionía, “a place name in Macedon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pioneer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Young Pioneer camp (former type of summer camp in the Soviet Union and other socialist countries)</li></ol><p><i>пионерла́гер(ь)</i> (pionerláger(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Young Pioneer camp (former type of summer camp in the Soviet Union and other socialist countries)</li></ol><p><i>пионе́р</i> (pionér) +&lrm; <i>ла́герь</i> (lágerʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pioneer</li></ol><p><i>пионе́р</i> (pionér) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipa (musical instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 琵琶 (<i>pípá</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipette (small glass tube used for transferring liquid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;vulgar) extreme degree of anything, euphemism for <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;vulgar) hell!, euphemism for <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feather duster</li><li>(<i>business</i>) pompom (for cheerleading)</li><li>(animate,&#32;often in a figurative abusive meaning) euphemistic form of <i>педера́ст</i> (pederást); pederast</li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>PP (polypropylene)</i> + <i>duster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;childish) male or female genitalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) people (<i>sometimes used pejoratively</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>people</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast, banquet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пиръ</i> (pirŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pirъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piranha (fish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>piranha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyramid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Pyramid of Giza</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>пирами́да</i> (piramída, “pyramid”)</li><li>Russian pyramid (type of billiards played with 15 balls)</li><li>pyramid pack (triangular arrangement of billiard balls at the beginning of a game)</li><li>children's toy consisting of a set of multicolored rings, gradually diminishing in size, placed on a rod</li></ol><p><i>пирами́да</i> (piramída) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piranha (fish)</li><li>Project 865 Piranha (a type of Soviet and Russian midget submarine).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>piranha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pirat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pirated software, pirateware</li></ol><p><i>пира́т</i> (pirát) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pirate</li><li>pirated</li></ol><p><i>пира́т</i> (pirát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piracy</li></ol><p><i>пира́т</i> (pirát) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pyrenean</li><li>Iberian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) pyridine</li></ol><p>Internationalism; compare English <i>pyridine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pyro-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feast, to celebrate with feasting, to revel, to carouse</li></ol><p><i>пир</i> (pir) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pie; pirog</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) female genitalia; vagina or vulva</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пирогъ</i> (pirogŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pirogъ</i>, probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pirъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ogъ</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>pieróg</i>, Ukrainian <i>пиріг</i> (pyrih).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirogue</li></ol><p>From French <i>pirogue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) matters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pirozhki</li></ol><p><i>пирожо́к</i> (pirožók) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pieman</li></ol><p><i>пиро́г</i> (piróg) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small cake (a sweet dessert for one person)</li></ol><p>From <i>пиро́г</i> (piróg, “pie, pirog”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pie</li></ol><p><i>пиро́г</i> (piróg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пиро́г</i> (piróg): Russian pastry <i>(a small pastry baked with any sort of filling, from meats, fish, eggs, rice, cabbage or mushrooms to all kinds of fruit, cottage cheese, raisins or nuts)</i>, pirozhki</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) diminutive of <i>пиро́г</i> (piróg): female genitalia; vagina or vulva</li></ol><p><i>пиро́г</i> (piróg) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyroxene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soluble guncotton, collodion, pyroxylin, nitrocellulose.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyromaniac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyrotechnics (art and technology of fireworks)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyrophorus, pyrophore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pyrrhic</li></ol><p><i>Пирр</i> (Pirr, “Pyrrhus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrrhic victory (a costly victory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pier (raised platform built from the shore out over water)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>piers</i>. Further from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;petricus</i>, from Latin <i>petra</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πέτρα</i> (pétra, “stone”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piercing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>piercing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small, lively party with food served (<i>among a close circle of friends and relatives</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пир</i> (pir) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirouette (whirling or turning on the toes)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a complex, tricky movement, action or thought</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pirouette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revelry, feast</li></ol><p><i>пир</i> (pir) +&lrm; <i>-шество</i> (-šestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пир</i> (pir).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) scribbler</li></ol><p>From <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ака</i> (-aka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pointed writing implement used for carving letters in birchbark, stylus</li></ol><p><i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing, script, scripture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>писание</i> (pisanije). By surface analysis, <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Писание)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Scripture (a religion's foundational holy text&#59; most commonly, the Bible)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьsanьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) poor writing, hack writing, a written regulation or a document which is of no use or counterproductive</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) poorly written document, scribble</li></ol><p><i>писа́ние</i> (pisánije) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painted Easter egg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) written down, fixed in writing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) handwritten</li><li>written in color, decorated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very beautiful, as if painted or decorated</li><li>painted</li></ol><p><i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clerk; scribe, scrivener</li></ol><p><i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer</li></ol><p><i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) writer; writers', writer's</li></ol><p><i>писа́тель</i> (pisátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorship (the activity or craft of a writer)</li></ol><p><i>писа́тель</i> (pisátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to paint (a painting)</li><li>to urinate, to pee, to piss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>писати</i> (pisati, “to write; to paint”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьsati</i>. Related to Latin <i>pingō</i> (whence English <i>paint</i> and <i>picture</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>pissen</i>, from Old French <i>pissier</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;pissiāre</i>, of echoic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urinate (accidentally), to pee, to piss, to wet one's bed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) copyist, penman, scribe, scrivener, writer</li><li>(minced oath,&#32;slang) curtains (the end for something or someone)</li></ol><p><i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p><p>Euphemistic modification of <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc, a vulgar curse), based on <i>пи́сать</i> (písatʹ, “to piss”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peep, chirp, squeak, cheep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>песка́рь</i> (peskárʹ)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European *péyskos (fish). Whence Proto-Slavic root is <i>&#42;pīskàti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ořь</i>. Note also that Slovak apparently more recently formed <i>piskor</i> to mean the “common shrew” and the “mouthpiece of a pipe”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>squeaky</li></ol><p><i>писк</i> (pisk) +&lrm; <i>-ля́вый</i> (-ljávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give a squeak</li></ol><p><i>писк</i> (pisk) +&lrm; <i>-ну</i> (-nu) +&lrm; <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squeaker; cheeper</li></ol><p>From <i>пища́ть</i> (piščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;vulgar) extreme degree of anything, euphemism for <i>пизде́ц</i> (pizdéc)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) penis, doodle, wiener</li></ol><p><i>пиздец</i> (pizdec) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p><p>From пи́сать ("to pee").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urinal (fixture for standing urination)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pissoir</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pistol, handgun</li><li>spraying pistol, sprayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pistolet</i>. Ultimately from Czech <i>píšťala</i> (“whistle”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piščalь</i>, from <i>&#42;piskati, &#42;piščati</i> (“to squeak, whistle”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pīṣk-</i>. Compare <i>пища́ль</i> (piščálʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>submachine gun (short range machine gun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thompson submachine gun, Tommy gun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pistol, handgun</li></ol><p><i>пистоле́т</i> (pistolét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>percussion cap</li><li>(<i>music</i>) piston</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write something repeatedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;childish) male or female genitalia, doodle, pussy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>characters, letters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in writing</li></ol><p><i>пи́сьменный</i> (písʹmennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing, writing system, script, written language</li></ol><p><i>пи́сьменный</i> (písʹmennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>written</li></ol><p>From <i>письмо́</i> (pisʹmó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj); initial stress comes from the plural <i>пи́сьма</i> (písʹma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desk (table for writing and reading)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>письмо́</i> (pisʹmó)</li></ol><p><i>письме-</i> (pisʹme-) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>letter (written communication)</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) writing (action)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) writing system, script</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pisьmo</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>писмѧ</i> (pismę, “letter (<i>of the alphabet</i>); writing”) (genitive <i>писмене</i> (pismene)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain letter</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;sarcastic,&#32;derogatory) undesirable notification, such as speeding ticket, military summons, debt collection letter</li></ol><p>Literally, “letter of happiness”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) clerk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) mailman, postman, letter carrier, mail carrier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;childish) penis, doodle, wee-wee, willy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;childish) male or female genitalia, doodle, pussy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>питаха́йя</i> (pitaxájja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, nutrition, alimentation</li><li>power (<i>electrical supply</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nourishing, nutritious</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) feeding, feed</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) nutrient</li></ol><p><i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisturizer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, to nourish</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to supply</li><li>to feel, to experience (<i>in a prolonged or constant manner</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pitati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyt-</i> (“food, nutrition”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>питѣти</i> (pitěti) and Serbo-Croatian <i>pȉtati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abhor (to regard with horror or detestation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed (on), to live (on), to use as sustenance, to eat [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘food’</small>]</li><li>to use as a source of power</li></ol><p><i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragon fruit, pitaya/pitahaya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meant for drinking, especially alcohol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) an "australopith"; someone considered to be disgusting or stupid.</li></ol><p>Most likely a clipping of австралопите́к (avstralopiték, “australopith”). Also a direct borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>πῐ́θηκος</i> (pĭ́thēkos, “a monkey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inhabitant/resident of St. Petersburg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) Saint Petersburg</li></ol><p><i>Пи́тер</i> (Píter) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;ironic,&#32;Christianity) drinking, act of drinking (of wine), a drink</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>питиѥ</i> (pitije). Doublet of <i>питьё</i> (pitʹjó), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alumnus, former student</li><li>pet (in relation to a carer)</li></ol><p><i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ, “to feed, to nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeding-ground, nursery, a place where animals or plants are bred</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>python (constricting snake)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Πύθων</i> (Púthōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(programming,&#32;informal) Pythonist (a Python programmer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(programming,&#32;relational) Python</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пити</i> (piti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piti</i> (“to drink”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”). Related to Latin <i>pōtāre</i> (“to drink”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;colloquial) to pester, torment</li></ol><p>Literally, “to drink blood”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drinking; potable</li></ol><p><i>питьё</i> (pitʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) rat-a-tat-tat (the sound of gunshots)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push, to shove, to elbow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove <i>(into)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push, to shove, to elbow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>пиха́ть</i> (pixátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fir</li></ol><p>From German <i>Fichte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Pskov</i>) synonym of <i>письмо́</i> (pisʹmó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pizza</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>pizza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pizzeria (outlet that primarily sells pizza)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>pizzeria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overfeed (<i>usually forcibly or against someone's desires</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to overdose, to give too much medicine to (someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stuff, to cram (someone with useless or unnecessary knowledge or information)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to oversupply (useless or unnecessary knowledge or information)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, of unclear etymology, and not obviously related to <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ, “to feed”), <i>пи́ща</i> (píšča, “food”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdie, little bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пичу́жка</i> (pičúžka): birdie, little bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пичу́га</i> (pičúga): birdie, little bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typewriter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) writing, recording</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пища</i> (pišta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piťa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;dated) shepherd's pipe</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) harquebus</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) hand cannon, hand bombard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;piščalь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пища́ть</i> (piščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аль</i> (-alʹ). Likely a doublet of <i>пистолет</i> (pistolet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peep, to squeak (make a noise like a baby bird or mouse)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pīščàti</i> (“to squeak, to whistle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digestion</li></ol><p><i>пи́ща</i> (píšča) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>digestible</li></ol><p>From <i>пи́ща</i> (píšča) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gullet, oesophagus</li></ol><p>First attested in 1835. <i>пи́ща</i> (píšča) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ), calque of Ancient Greek <i>οἰσοφάγος</i> (oisophágos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>esophageal</li></ol><p><i>пищево́д</i> (piščevód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) food; alimentary, nutritional</li></ol><p><i>пи́ща</i> (píšča) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pika (mammal)</li><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) A bird in the genus <i>Certhia</i>, comprised of treecreepers.</li></ol><p><i>пища́ть</i> (piščátʹ, “to squeak”, verb) +&lrm; <i>-уха</i> (-uxa, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leech (blood-sucking annelid)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьjavъka</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>п'я́вка</i> (pʺjávka), Belarusian <i>п'я́ўка</i> (pʺjáwka), Czech <i>pijavka</i>, Polish <i>pijawka</i>, Slovene <i>pijavka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>square, plaza</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>пло́щадь</i> (plóščadʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;dialectal) swimming, swim, floating (only in combinations) (the condition of swimming or floating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming, floating</li><li>navigation, sailing</li><li>voyage, trip (<i>by ship</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пла́вание</i> (plávanije, “swimming, floating, sailing”)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swimming</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming pool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim, to float</li><li>to sail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plavati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i>. Cognate with English <i>float</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial) to sink like a stone; to be unable to remain afloat (<i>of an object in water</i>)</li><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial) to swim like a rock; be unable to swim (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to float/swim like an axe”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundry, smeltery</li></ol><p><i>пла́вить</i> (plávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to melt, to fuse</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to smelt</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to soften by heating</li><li>(regional,&#32;transitive) to float (timber) (to transport by floating)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plaviti</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>пла́вити</i> (plávyty), Serbo-Croatian <i>пла̏вити</i> (“to flood”), Slovene <i>рlаvíti</i> (“to float (timber), to sail”), Czech <i>plavit</i>, Slovak <i>рlаviť</i>, Polish <i>pławić</i>, Bulgarian <i>пла́вя</i> (plávja, “to rinse”). More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>pláuti</i> (“to wash, to rinse”), Sanskrit <i>प्लावयति</i> (plāvayati, “to cause to float, to flood”), Avestan <i>𐬟𐬭𐬁𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (frāuuaiie<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “to force to leave”), Ancient Greek <i>πλώω</i> (plṓō, “to swim”), English <i>flow</i>, Old Norse <i>flóа</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to melt</li><li>(imperfective only,&#32;intransitive) to float or swim downstream (<i>of timber, fish, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вить</i> (plávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fusion, fusing</li><li>melt <i>(process)</i></li><li>melt, tap, cast</li></ol><p><i>пла́вить</i> (plávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming trunks</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melting</li></ol><p>From <i>пла́вить</i> (plávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>пла́вный</i> (plávnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>пла́вно</i> (plávno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsh, meadowland, flooded areas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fin, flipper (appendage of a fish)</li><li>driftwood <i>uncountable</i></li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>smoothly (in a smooth manner)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вный</i> (plávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoothness (<i>of movement or speech</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вный</i> (plávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth, even</li><li>fluent, flowing</li><li>easy, light</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) liquid (the consonant r or l)</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buoyancy, floatability</li></ol><p><i>плаву́чий</i> (plavúčij, “buoyant, floating”) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>floating</li><li>buoyant</li></ol><p><i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plagiarism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Plagiat</i> or French <i>plagiat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plagiarist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Plagiator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) plug-in</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>plug-in</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) plasma (a state of matter consisting of partially ionized gas and electrons)</li><li>(biology,&#32;medicine) plasma (component of blood)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>πλάσμα</i> (plásma, “something formed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plasma</li></ol><p><i>пла́зма</i> (plázma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poster, placard, bill</li><li>broadsheet</li></ol><p>Borrowed in 1704 via German <i>Plakat</i>, from Dutch <i>plakkaat</i>, from French <i>placard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, to weep</li><li>to mourn, to weep</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плакати</i> (plakati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plakati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to complain [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) for there to be crying/weeping [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>cry</i> or <i>weep</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whining</li><li>piteous</li></ol><p><i>пла́кса</i> (pláksa) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetical</i>) to flame, to blaze</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to burn (with passion)</li></ol><p><i>пла́мен-</i> (plámen-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaming, fiery</li><li>ardent, flaming</li></ol><p><i>пла́мень</i> (plámenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) flame (<i>also figurative, referring to emotions</i>)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>пла́мя</i> (plámja), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. Compare Bulgarian <i>пла́мен</i> (plámen), Serbo-Croatian <i>пла̏ме̄н</i>, Slovene <i>plámen</i> (gen. sg. <i>plaména</i>)), Czech <i>plamen</i>, Slovak <i>plameň</i>, Polish <i>płomień</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame</li><li>blaze</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пламꙑ</i> (plamy, “flame”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polmy</i> (“flame”). Doublet of <i>по́лымя</i> (pólymja) and <i>по́ломя</i> (pólomja), the inherited East Slavic forms.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plan (set of intended actions), scheme</li><li>draft, plan, scheme, contrivance, road map</li><li>target, figure</li><li>plane</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) marijuana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>plan</i> from Latin <i>planus</i>, possibly via Polish <i>plan</i> or German <i>Plan</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>planum</i>.</p><p>Possibly derived from <i>плантация</i> (plantacija). Used at least since early 1970-s. The usage of "план" for "marijuana" was popularized by a song by Vysotsky, in his song "Вот раньше жизнь" ("That's Life Before") where he wrote: "Покуришь план, пойдешь на бан" (<i>You finish to smoke marijuana, and then go to the station</i>). Approximately the same year as the song of Vysotsky, the word was also used by Severny in his song "Анаша".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plan B</li></ol><p>Presumably borrowed from English <i>plan B</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) A sloping table on which the ore is washed.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Planherd</i>, from <i>Plan</i> (“plane”) +&lrm; <i>Herd</i> (“hearth”). First attested in 1798.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) rogue planet</li></ol><p><i>плане́та</i> (planéta, “planet”) +&lrm; <i>стра́нник</i> (stránnik, “wanderer”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planetarium (museum which displays images of space)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) planetary nebula</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>planetary</li></ol><p>From <i>плане́та</i> (planéta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planning</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) glide, gliding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plan, to project, to schedule</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to glide</li><li>to level, to lay out</li></ol><p><i>план</i> (plan) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p><p>From German <i>planieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planning</li><li>layout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plank, slat, lath</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) bar</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Medieval Latin <i>planca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plankton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planned economy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plan, planning</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) target; systematic, planned</li><li>routine</li></ol><p><i>план</i> (plan) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>planned, systematic</li></ol><p><i>плано-</i> (plano-) +&lrm; <i>-ме́рный</i> (-mérnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantain (fruit related to banana)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>plantain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planter (owner of a plantation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) planter (owner of a plantation)</li></ol><p><i>планта́тор</i> (plantátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantation (large farm)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>plantatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clipboard</li><li>(<i>cartography</i>) plane table, planchette</li><li>(<i>military</i>) plotting board, plot board, tactical display</li><li>(<i>military</i>) map case</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) tablet (tablet computer or graphics tablet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>planchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) glider (aircraft)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>planeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a short production meeting devoted to the work plan and its progress, a standup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layer, sheet, stratum</li><li>bed <i>(a deposit of ore, coal etc.)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пластъ</i> (plastŭ, “thin layer”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plastъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut in layers</li><li>to gut, to eviscerate (fish)</li></ol><p><i>пласт</i> (plast) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plastic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) layer, slice</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sculptor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Plastik</i>.</p><p><i>пласт</i> (plast) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plastic art</li><li>plastique</li><li>plasticity</li><li>grace, gracefulness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) plastic surgery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plastic</li></ol><p><i>пла́стик</i> (plástik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plasticine</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Italian <i>plastilina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пласти́на</i> (plastína): plate</li><li>record, disc</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) blade, lamina</li></ol><p><i>пласти́на</i> (plastína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Relating to the Scarabaeidae or the scarabs; scarabaeous.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) scarab; scarabaeid.</li></ol><p>From <i>пласти́нчатый</i> (plastínčatyj, “platen, made of plates”) +&lrm; <i>ус</i> (us, “antenna”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plasticizer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plastic art</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) plastique</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) plastic</li></ol><p>From French <i>plastique</i> +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;biology) plasticity</li><li>flexibility, limberness</li></ol><p><i>пласти́чный</i> (plastíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;biology) plastic</li><li>flexible or well-proportioned</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>plast(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plastic</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>пласти́ческая ма́сса</i> (plastíčeskaja mássa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plastic</li></ol><p><i>пластма́сса</i> (plastmássa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) layer, stratum</li><li>layered, stratified</li></ol><p><i>пласт</i> (plast) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) plaster, band-aid</li><li>(<i>naval</i>) patch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пластꙑрь</i> (plastyrĭ), from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;plastrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) kerchief, headscarf</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cloth, tablecloth, bedcover (<i>especially if undyed</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>платъ</i> (platŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;platъ</i>; cognate with Bulgarian <i>плат</i> (plat) and Polish <i>płat</i>; probably cognate with Russian <i>полотно́</i> (polotnó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fee, pay, salary, wages, fare, rent</li><li>card, cardboard, deck</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>плат</i> (plat) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), due to the original meaning of payments with textile (see Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;platiti</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane (deciduous tree, <i>Platanus</i>), sycamore, <i>Platanus occidentalis</i></li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>πλάτανος</i> (plátanos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) payer</li></ol><p>First attested in year 1665 as <i>плат<ruby>е<rt>ж</rt></ruby>щики</i> (plat<ruby>je<rt>ž</rt></ruby>ščiki, Npl). From <i>платеж</i> (platež, “payment”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik, “man”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payer</li></ol><p>First attested in year 1695. Likely from older <i>платещик</i> (plateščik) (<i>1672</i>), alternative spelling of <i>платежщик</i> (platežščik) (<i>1665</i>).</p><p>SRJa18 mentions <i>платейшик</i> (platejšik) but does not provide quote. See also <i>платейный</i> (platejnyj, “платье”, adj.), <i>плательный</i> (platelʹnyj, “платье”, adj.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(entomology,&#32;taxonomy) platygastrid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Platygastridae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard) payer</li></ol><p>First attested in 18th century (1782). From <i>плательщик</i> (platelʹščik) or <i>платить</i> (platitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>platinum</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Spanish <i>platina</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) platinum</li></ol><p><i>пла́тина</i> (plátina) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li><li>to pay off, to return</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;platiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay for (something)</li><li>to suffer harm</li></ol><p><i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a type of male clothing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>платьно</i> (platĭno). Doublet of <i>полотно́</i> (polotnó), which was inherited.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pay, chargeable (requiring payment&#59; operable or accessible on deposit of coins)</li></ol><p><i>пла́та</i> (pláta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plateau</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>plateau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handkerchief, kerchief</li><li>shawl, headscarf</li></ol><p><i>плат</i> (plat) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>platonic (not sexual in nature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>плато́к</i> (platók): (small) kerchief, headkerchief; handkerchief</li></ol><p><i>плато́к</i> (platók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>platform</li><li>goods truck, flatcar</li><li>platform sole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) platform</li><li>flatbed</li></ol><p><i>платфо́рм(а)</i> (platfórm(a)) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) clothes, clothing</li><li>dress, gown, frock</li></ol><p><i>плат</i> (plat) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>пла́ття</i> (pláttja), Belarusian <i>пла́цце</i> (pláccje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пла́тье</i> (plátʹje): (girl's) dress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) clothes, outerwear</li></ol><p><i>пла́тье</i> (plátʹje) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wardrobe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment (the act of paying)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;platežь</i>, from <i>&#42;platiti</i>. Morphologically, from <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solvency, ability to pay, paying capacity</li></ol><p><i>платёжеспосо́бный</i> (platjožesposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solvent, able to pay</li></ol><p><i>платёж</i> (platjóž) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) payment</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pay</li></ol><p><i>платёж</i> (platjóž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club moss, lycopod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plafond</li><li><i>for ceiling lamp</i> shade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>plafond</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block of wood</li><li>executioner's block</li></ol><p>Compare Czech <i>plocha</i> and Polish <i>płocha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parade ground</li></ol><p>First attested in 1705 as <i>пляц</i> (pljac). From German <i>Platz</i> via Polish <i>plac</i>, from French <i>place</i>, from Latin <i>platea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) bridgehead, parade ground, foothold</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) springboard, stepping stone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>place d'armes</i>, the first part is influenced by <i>плац</i> (plac), derived from German <i>Platz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placebo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placenta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often proscribed) reserved-seat ticket, seat reservation ticket (in a train)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reserved-seat ticket, seat reservation ticket (in a train)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Platzkarte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reserved-seat (train) ticket</li></ol><p><i>плацка́рта</i> (plackárta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weeping</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plačь</i>.</p><p>Cognate to <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ) and to Old Church Slavonic <i>плачь</i> (plačĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deplorable, lamentable, pitiable, plaintive, sorry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>плачевьнъ</i> (plačevĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>плач</i> (plač) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crying</li><li>whiny (<i>of a voice, sound or tone</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>die (device for cutting an external screw thread)</li><li>billet, board (flat piece of wood)</li><li>plate (small, flat piece of metal)</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) a type of traditional deadfall trap made of wood and used for small animals</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) solid color, solid printed area</li></ol><p><i>пла́ха</i> (pláxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>flat and wide side down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raincoat, overcoat</li><li>poncho, cloak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plaščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combination raincoat and tent</li></ol><p><i>плащ</i> (plašč) +&lrm; <i>пала́тка</i> (palátka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) shroud of Christ</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) shroud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) plebeian (one of the common people of ancient Rome)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) pleb, commoner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>plēbēius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) plebeian</li><li>vulgar, uncultured</li></ol><p>From <i>плебе́й</i> (plebéj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plebiscite (a referendum, especially one that concerns changes in sovereignty)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>plebiscitum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plebs (the common people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane, film, coat, pleura</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuspidor, spittoon (receptacle for spit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuspidor, spittoon (receptacle for spit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li><li>to spit upon, to not care for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pľьvati</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Bulgarian <i>плюя</i> (pljuja), Serbo-Croatian <i>пљу̏вати</i>/<i>pljȕvati</i>, Slovene <i>pljuvati</i>, Slovak <i>pľuť</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>πτύω</i> (ptúō), Gothic <i>𐍃𐍀𐌴𐌹𐍅𐌰𐌽</i> (speiwan), Sanskrit <i>ष्ठीवति</i> (ṣṭhīvati, “to spit”), Latin <i>spuō</i>, Old Armenian <i>թուք</i> (tʻukʻ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to loathe, to find disgusting/loathsome, to think awful/poor/miserable/lousy</li></ol><p><i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darnel, cockle (<i>Lolium</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative,&#32;in the plural) tares, weeds</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>плѣвелъ</i> (plěvelŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelvelъ</i>. Displaced <i>половелъ</i> (polovelŭ), the East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit, spittle</li><li>sputum</li><li>abuse, ribaldry</li></ol><p><i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleura</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleuritis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blanket, throw (a large piece of fabric, usually woolen, used for warmth while resting or traveling, as a bedcover, rug, etc.)</li><li>plaid (twilled woolen cloth with a tartan pattern&#59; a length of such material used as a piece of clothing, as formerly worn in the Scottish Highlands)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>plaid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(media) player (e.g. CD, DVD, mp3)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>player</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;music) playlist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>playlist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>playboy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>playboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) plectrum ((music) small piece of plastic, metal, ivory, etc for plucking the strings)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tribal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pedigree</li></ol><p><i>пле́мен-</i> (plémen-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribe</li><li>race</li><li>family, brood</li><li>generation</li><li>breed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>племѧ</i> (plemę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plemę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nephew</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>племенник</i> (plemennik), from <i>пле́мен-</i> (plémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), modified under the influence of the nominative form of <i>пле́мя</i> (plémja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nephew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nephew or (more commonly) niece</li></ol><p>From <i>племя́н(ник)</i> (plemján(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška) or from <i>племя́н(ница)</i> (plemján(nica)) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška); alternatively analyzable as <i>племя́ш</i> (plemjáš) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captivity, custody</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плѣнъ</i> (plěnŭ), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>плѣнъ</i> (plěnŭ, “booty”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelnъ</i>. Compare the inherited East Slavic doublet <i>поло́н</i> (polón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plenary (fully attended)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capturing, taking prisoner</li><li>conquering</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) captivation, fascination</li><li>captivity, dependence, subordination</li></ol><p><i>плени́ть</i> (plenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enchanting, captivating</li></ol><p><i>плени́ть</i> (plenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to capture, to take prisoner, to deprive of liberty</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to conquer (a land, a country)</li><li>to captivate, to fascinate, to charm</li></ol><p><i>плен</i> (plen) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captive, prisoner</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плѣньникъ</i> (plěnĭnikŭ), equivalent to <i>плен</i> (plen, “captivity”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) female captive (<i>under someone's irresistible influence</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пле́н(ник)</i> (plén(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>captive</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captive, prisoner</li></ol><p><i>плен</i> (plen) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plenum, plenary session, plenary meeting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to captivate, to fascinate, to charm</li></ol><p><i>плени́ть</i> (plenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) pleonasm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>pleonasmus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πλεονασμός</i> (pleonasmós), from <i>πλεονάζω</i> (pleonázō, “I am superfluous”), from <i>πλείων</i> (pleíōn, “more”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Gnosticism</i>) pleroma</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) plerome</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πλήρωμα</i> (plḗrōma, “a filling up, fullness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mildew, mold, must</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěsnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Plesetsk, Plesetsky District</li></ol><p><i>Плесе́ц(к)</i> (Pleséc(k)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Плесецкий)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Plesetsky, Plesetski, or Plesiecki</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splash, swash (the sound made by breaking or falling water)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) applause</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pleskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;and&#32;intransitive</i>) to splash</li><li>to flutter (<i>of flags or sails</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to applaud</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěskati</i>, <i>&#42;pleskati</i>, of onomatopoeic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lap, to swash</li><li>to splash</li><li>to splash about</li><li>to bathe, to splash around</li></ol><p><i>плеска́ть</i> (pleskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mouldy, mildewed</li></ol><p><i>пле́сневеть</i> (plésnevetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mold, to become moldy</li></ol><p><i>пле́сень</i> (plésenʹ, “mold”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to splash, to plash, to lap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to braid, to plait</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) to weave, to spin</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to chatter, to tell nonsense, to lie</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plestì</i>; see there for cognates.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag oneself along, to toil along, to trudge, to plod along, to plod on</li></ol><p><i>плести́</i> (plestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>пу́таться</i> (pútatʹsja) (&lt; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braid, braiding, wickerwork</li></ol><p><i>плет-</i> (plet-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wattle fence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip; lash</li></ol><p>Deverbal from плести&nbsp;impf (plesti)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wicker</li><li>wattled</li></ol><p><i>плет-</i> (plet-) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy) shoulder; humeral</li><li>(<i>transport&#32;slang</i>) hitchhiking, needing a ride, taking a ride (<i>for one segment of a truck or automobile's long-distance route</i>)</li></ol><p><i>плечо́</i> (plečó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coat hanger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>плечо́</i> (plečó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad-shouldered</li></ol><p><i>плечо́</i> (plečó) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) upper arm, brachium, humerus</li><li>(physics,&#32;engineering) arm (<i>as in lever arm or moment arm</i>)</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) leg (<i>of a polyphase electrical system</i>)</li><li>(<i>transport</i>) leg, haul, trip (a segment of a long-distance route)</li><li>(<i>archery</i>) limb (<i>of a bow</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pleťe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldness</li></ol><p><i>плеши́вый</i> (plešívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěšivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>плешь</i> (plešʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bald spot (e.g. on a head)</li><li>an empty spot (e.g. in a forest or field)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěšina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bald spot, bald patch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěšь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleiad, galaxy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) fire!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;rare) please</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>please</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirting board, baseboard (panel between floor and interior wall)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>plinthus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πλίνθος</i> (plínthos). Doublet of <i>плита́</i> (plitá). Compare French <i>plinthe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felt, velveteen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow wagtail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pliska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) felt, velveteen</li></ol><p><i>плис</i> (plis) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stove</li><li>hot plate</li><li>plate (shape)</li><li>slab</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плита</i> (plita), <i>плифа</i> (plifa), <i>пленьфа</i> (plenĭfa), <i>плинта</i> (plinta), a borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>πλίνθος</i>&nbsp;f (plínthos), with original reference to a four-sided stone ground in an ordinary Russian house set up as a fire-place; compare Latin <i>tēgula</i> and German <i>Kachel</i> for the semantic development. Doublet of <i>пли́нтус</i> (plíntus) and <i>пли́нфа</i> (plínfa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>плита́</i> (plitá): a (small, thin) slab; a (small) cooking range, stove</li><li>(countable&#32;or&#32;uncountable,&#32;collectively) tile, tiles</li><li>(countable&#32;or&#32;uncountable,&#32;collectively) paver block, paver blocks (<i>regularly shaped</i>)</li><li>bar, brick (a small, flattened, rectangular piece of some commodity)</li></ol><p><i>плита́</i> (plitá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of tiles</li><li>related to tiles</li></ol><p>From <i>пли́тка</i> (plítka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilaf, plov (dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian پلو (polow).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimmer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пловьць</i> (plovĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plovьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;pluti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit <i>(also figurative)</i></li><li>offspring</li><li>fetus, foetus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plodъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i>, whence English <i>full</i>, <i>plenty</i> and <i>poly-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to recreate, to spawn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propagate, to multiply</li></ol><p><i>плоди́ть</i> (plodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fruit</li></ol><p><i>плод</i> (plod) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A curculio, a weevil of the genus <i>Curculio</i>.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The genus <i>Curculio</i>.</li></ol><p><i>плод</i> (plod, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ, “to live, to reside (in)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear fruit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearing, fructification, fruitage</li></ol><p><i>плодоноси́ть</i> (plodonosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fertility (of the soil), fecundity</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>плод</i> (plod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fertile</li></ol><p><i>плодоро́дие</i> (plodoródije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to inseminate, to fertilize, to impregnate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fruitfully, productively</li></ol><p><i>плодотво́рный</i> (plodotvórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruitfulness (the state or quality of being fruitful)</li></ol><p><i>плодотво́рный</i> (plodotvórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fruitful, productive</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>плодотвори́ть</i> (plodotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curler, curling iron, hair roller (electrical)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) PlayStation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead, seal, stamp</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) filling, stopping, inlay, plug</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Plombe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(a tooth)</i> to stop, to fill</li><li>to seal</li></ol><p><i>пломба</i> (plomba) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip flask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flat, plane, level</li><li>trivial, tame, stale, trite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ploskъ</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“thin, flat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) flatworm</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij, “flat”) +&lrm; <i>че́рвь</i> (čérvʹ, “worm”), literally “flat worm”, a calque of translingual <i>Platyhelminthes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plateau, tableland</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pliers</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>губа́</i> (gubá) +&lrm; <i>-цы</i> (-cy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flat-bottomed</li></ol><p><i>пло́ск(ий)</i> (plósk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>до́нный</i> (dónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gripping tongs</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flatfoot (a condition in which the arch of the foot makes contact with the ground)</li></ol><p>From <i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>стопа́</i> (stopá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flat-footed (having flat feet)</li></ol><p><i>плоскосто́пие</i> (ploskostópije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) plane (flat surface extending infinitely in all directions)</li><li>flatness, flat</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) wing, flying surface</li><li>platitude</li><li>sheet</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) cucujid, flat bark beetle</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Cucujidae.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij, “flat, level”) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) A sea krait, a snake of the genus <i>Laticauda</i>.</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij, “flat”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хвост</i>&nbsp;m (xvost, “tail”, noun), literally “flat-tail”. A calque of translingual <i>Laticauda</i>&nbsp;f, from Latin <i>lātus</i> (“wide, broad”, adjective) +&lrm; Latin <i>cauda</i>&nbsp;f (“tail”), literally “broad-tail”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar) flatearther</li></ol><p><i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij, “flat”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Formed in analogy to <i>шароёб</i> (šarojób).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plъ̃tъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roach (<i>Rutilus rutilus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plotь</i>, <i>&#42;ploty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>плот</i> (plot, “raft”)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) bedrock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dam (structure placed across a flowing body of water)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>плаці́на</i> (placína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter</li></ol><p><i>плот</i> (plot) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carpenter</li></ol><p><i>пло́тник</i> (plótnik) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carpenter</li></ol><p><i>пло́тник</i> (plótnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tightly (in a tight manner)</li></ol><p><i>пло́тный</i> (plótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) density (amount of matter contained by a given volume&#59; the ratio of one quantity to that of another quantity)</li></ol><p><i>пло́тный</i> (plótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demography</i>) population density (average population per unit area&#59; especially, the average number of people who live on each square kilometer (or square mile) of land)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dense</li><li>compact, solid</li><li>thickset</li><li>close, thick</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, probably inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plъtьnъ</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plъtь</i> (whence Russian <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ, “flesh”)); compare Slovene <i>póltǝn</i> (“carnal, corporeal”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>). By surface analysis, <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carnivorous</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) lecherous, lustful, predatory (<i>of a smile, a look, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-яд</i> (-jad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); calque of Latin <i>carnivorus</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) adephagan</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Adephaga.</li></ol><p>From <i>плотоя́дный</i> (plotojádnyj, “carnivorous”) +&lrm; <i>жук</i> (žuk, “beetle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) carnal</li></ol><p><i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plotter (device)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>plotter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) foreskin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plъtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) baddie, a bad guy</li></ol><p>From <i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ш</i> (-íš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bad, badly (in a bad manner)</li><li>unwell</li></ol><p><i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad (not good)</li><li>ill, bad (ill, sick)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój): bad, miserable</li></ol><p>Derived form of <i>плох(о́й)</i> (plox(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a mistake, to blunder</li></ol><p><i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) oil lamp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>площка</i> (ploščka), probably composed as if <i>пло́ский</i> (plóskij) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), although others have suggested borrowing from Old High German <i>flasca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>area, ground, playground</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) (tennis) court</li><li>platform</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) landing</li></ol><p><i>пло́щадь</i> (plóščadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>square <i>(open area in a town or city)</i></li><li>space, living space</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) area</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>площадь</i> (ploščadĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ploščadь</i>, from <i>&#42;ploskъ</i> (“flat”) + <i>&#42;-ědь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough, plow (usually a modern-type metal plough with a mouldboard, as distinguished from wooden <i>соха́</i> (soxá) and simple wooden <i>ра́ло</i> (rálo))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плугъ</i> (plugŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plugъ</i>, borrowed from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;plōgaz</i> (“plough”), <i>&#42;plōguz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plough, plow</li></ol><p><i>плуг</i> (plug) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plough, plow</li></ol><p><i>плуг</i> (plug) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swindler, cheat, knave</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) rogue, crafty person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stray, to wander</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plǫtati</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>плу́таць</i> (plútacʹ), Polish <i>plątać</i> and Ukrainian <i>плу́тати</i> (plútaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trick</li></ol><p><i>плут</i> (plut) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plutocrat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plutocracy (government by the wealthy&#59; controlling class of the wealthy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) pluton, a large body of igneous rock solidified out of magma deep in the earth’s core.</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Плутон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Pluto (dwarf planet)</li><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) Pluto</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plutonium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim, to float</li><li>to sail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plyti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit</li><li>to spit upon, to not care for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pľьnǫti</i>; compare <i>&#42;pľьvati</i> (“to spit”), distantly related to Latvian <i>spļaũt</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>плю́нути</i> (pljúnuty), Belarusian <i>плю́нуць</i> (pljúnucʹ), Polish <i>plunąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pluralism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pluralisme</i>. Analyzable as Latin <i>plūrālis</i> + <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plus sign</li><li>plus</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) advantage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>plūs</i> (“more”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metatarsus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плесна</i> (plesna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pľusna</i>, an alternative form of <i>&#42;plesno</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pl(j)ousk-</i>. Pokorny compares Latin <i>plautus</i> (“flatfooted”), Latin <i>plaudo</i> (“to applaud”), Ancient Greek <i>πλαίσιον</i> (plaísion, “oblong case”), but a shared origin for all of these words is far from certain; possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleth₂-</i> (“flat”). Cognate with Latvian <i>plauksta</i>. Also compare <i>плюск</i> (pljusk, “flattened spot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plus</li><li>positive, above zero (<i>e.g. of temperature</i>)</li></ol><p><i>плюс</i> (pljus) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>плюсово́й</i> (pljusovój)</li></ol><p><i>плюс</i> (pljus) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flop <i>(down)</i>, to flop <i>(into)</i>, to plump <i>(into)</i>, to plop <i>(into)</i>, to drop <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>плюх</i> (pljux) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flop <i>(down)</i>, to flop <i>(into)</i>, to plump <i>(into)</i>, to plop <i>(into)</i>, to drop <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>плю́хаться</i> (pljúxatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plush</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>peluche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plush</li></ol><p><i>плюш</i> (pljuš) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sweet) bun, roll</li><li>slang form of <i>плюс</i> (pljus), advantage, benefit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) the @ sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivy (plant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľuščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press flat, to flatten by means of pressure</li><li>(slang,&#32;impersonal) to feel lack of energy, to be exhausted, usually after a bad trip</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pluščiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>плюск</i> (pljusk) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Javan spitting cobra (<i>Naja sputatrix</i>)</li></ol><p><i>плюю́щий</i> (pljujúščij, “spitting”) +&lrm; <i>инди́йский</i> (indíjskij, “Indian”) +&lrm; <i>ко́бра</i> (kóbra, “cobra”), literally “spitting Indian cobra”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beach</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>plage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beach</li></ol><p><i>пляж</i> (pljaž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dance (movements to music)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance, to esp. to do folk dancing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to caper, to hop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>плꙗсати</i> (pljasati, “to dance”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plęsati</i> (“to dance”). Gothic <i>𐍀𐌻𐌹𐌽𐍃𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (plinsjan) is a borrowing from some Slavic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance</li></ol><p><i>пляса́ть</i> (pljasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trifling, insignificant; frivolous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easy to do, not requiring much effort; nothing (about a deed, request, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very bad, unusable</li></ol><p><i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film, pellicle, tape</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reach <i>(of river)</i>, stretch <i>(of river or lake)</i></li><li>flipper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air rifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) air; pneumatic (relating to air or other gases)</li></ol><p>Probably from French <i>pneumatique</i> +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nail gun (gun which drives nails)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air pistol (pistol that propels a pellet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air gun (gun that propels a projective by compressed air)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pneumonia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Pneumocystis</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pneumocystis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick</li></ol><p>Derived from a semelfactive of obsolete <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) (see <i>-пять</i> (-pjatʹ)), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pęti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>used broadly to indicate action, typically involving movement, across a surface or across several things in a generally flat plane; across, on, along, down, up, all over, (all) around:</li><li>used to indicate the object&#47;field&#47;topic that something applies to; for, on, about, in, of (<i>or translated with an attributive noun followed by a noun</i>)</li><li>used to indicate accordance with, adherence to or judgement based on something; according to, from, by</li><li>used to indicate the reason for something, typically undesired&#47;undesirable (see usage notes on <i>из-за</i> (iz-za)); due to, on account of, out of, from, by</li><li>used with time (usually times of day or days of the week) to indicate regular repetition; on, in (the), every (<i>or translated with a plural noun</i>)</li><li>used with numbers when there is distribution among multiple objects or repetition across multiple instances in time (dative case is used with the numbers one, a thousand, a million, a billion, a trillion etc. and numbers ending in these words)</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>used with numbers when there is distribution among multiple objects or repetition across multiple instances in time (inanimate accusative case is used with numbers other than one, a thousand, a million, a billion, a trillion etc. or numbers ending in these words)</li><li>Used to indicate something serving as the boundary of the extent of some action.</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(literary,&#32;official) Used to indicate a temporal or spatial boundary; (immediately) after, upon, on</li><li>to the liking of</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> prepositional <i>or</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>used to indicate an object of rumination, yearning, mourning; over, for</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>по</i> (po), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;po</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ПО)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) software (encoded computer instructions)</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>програ́ммное обеспече́ние</i> (prográmmnoje obespečénije) or <i>програ́ммное обеспе́чение</i> (prográmmnoje obespéčenije).</p></html>

<h3>Russian (Capitalized: По)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Po (a river in Italy)</li><li>Poe (surname, especially of Edgar Allan Poe, American writer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) through connections, through knowing the right people, by means of cronyism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>generally (without reference to specific details)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apparently, seemingly, in all likelihood, most likely, it seems (from how things look)</li></ol><p>Literally, “according to all of what's visible”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>across the board (pertaining to all categories or things)</li><li>in all directions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>per pro., p.p. (<i>per procurationem</i>), by proxy</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>по дове́ренности</i> (po dovérennosti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in theory, in principle, technically, presumably</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ironically (evaluative (sentence-modifying))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) alternative letter-case form of <i>по КД</i> (po KD)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: по КД)</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) constantly (as soon as the cooldown period expires)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) constantly, as often as possible (as soon as the next opportunity arises)</li></ol><p>Literally, “on/at cooldown”, referring to the period in a game during which a player cannot use an item or ability after it has been used.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at least, at any rate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at least (at a lower limit)</li><li>at any rate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as (in line with, proportionally to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) because of, due to the fault of (someone)</li></ol><p>Literally, “by the grace (of)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in my opinion, in my view, to my mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>based (on) (a story), after</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>admittedly (by admission)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one at a time (individually)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in relation to, as regards, with respect to, vis-à-vis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in turn, one after another, taking turns, by turns</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mistakenly, by mistake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>about, regarding, concerning, in connection with</li></ol><p>Literally, “according/due to the cause/grounds/reason/occasion (of)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indicating "by reason" of something</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on someone's heels; right behind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time (simultaneously)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as compared with, as opposed to, in contrast with, against</li><li>versus, over against, beside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) in an essential manner; in essence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by default</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;in expressions) don't give a fuck, don't give a shit, don't care</li></ol><p>Literally, “up to the cock”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clockwise</li></ol><p>Literally, “by the hour hand”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) honestly, fairly, in an honest/fair way/manner</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) being honest, being frank (when saying or admitting something)</li></ol><p>Corruption of <i>по-че́стному</i> (po-čéstnomu) based on similarity with the dative singular of <i>чесно́к</i> (česnók, “garlic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;neologism) misspelling of <i>по чесноку́</i> (po česnokú)</li></ol><p>Corruption of <i>по чесноку́</i> (po česnokú) due to re-association with <i>по-че́стному</i> (po-čéstnomu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>perfective aspect</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) imperfective aspect</li><li>for a while, a little (see delimitative aspect)</li><li>added with a hyphen to certain adjectives to create adverbs, often (but not always) with the meaning "in the manner of behaviour, speech etc. of &#91;the adjective&#93;" typically changes the adjective ending to the dative masculine&#47;neuter singular ending, but the following additional changes also occur&#58; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) to <i>-ски</i> (-ski), <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój) to <i>-ски́</i> (-skí), <i>-ий</i> (-ij) to <i>-ьи</i> (-ʹi)</li><li>place names</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pо-</i>. See <i>по</i> (po, “up to”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the American manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in English</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) quietly, suddenly, without permission (compare: <i>French leave</i>)</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of англи́йский (anglíjskij), "English".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Arabic</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>ара́бский</i> (arábskij, “Arabic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the manner of a ram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Belarusian</li><li>the Belarusian way</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of белору́сский (belorússkij), "Belarusian".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in your opinion, according to your way of thinking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eveninglike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) apparently, seemingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in military fashion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a wolf-like manner</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>волк</i> (volk, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, “-ish, -ine”), then put under the по- (po-)...и construction used for languages, thus meaning "in wolfish".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>childishly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>де́тский</i> (détskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly, friendly</li><li>amicably, without a fight or quarrel</li><li>properly, well, seriously</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>до́брому</i> (dóbromu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as at home (comfortably, friendly, tasty, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simply, without ceremony</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дома́шнему</i> (domášnemu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently (than others or everything else)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a friendly way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a woman</li><li>like a woman</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>же́нский</i> (žénskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>according to local custom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as in winter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently, otherwise</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ино́му</i> (inómu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Spanish</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>испа́нский</i> (ispánskij, “Spanish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Italian</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>италья́нский</i> (italʹjánskij, “Italian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) into their language</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po, “in this way, like that”) +&lrm; <i>и́хний</i> (íxnij, “their [language]”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Chinese</li><li>the Chinese way, in the Chinese fashion</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>кита́йский</i> (kitájskij, “Chinese”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Korean</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>коре́йский</i> (koréjskij, “Korean”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as in summer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anyway, either way, in any way, in any case, by all means, definitely, for sure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a bear-like manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) in my opinion; methinks</li><li>(done) my way, the way <i>I</i> want it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by sea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the Moscow fashion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a man</li><li>like a man, manly</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>мужско́й</i> (mužskój) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>properly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>authoritatively, in a commanding manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as we would wish, as we would have it, the way we do it, our way</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) as we advise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (in) our language, the way we say/put it</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>на́шему</i> (nášemu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in German</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>неме́цкий</i> (neméckij, “German”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) in a language other than Russian</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) + <i>не-</i> (ne-) + <i>ру́сски</i> (rússki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a new way/fashion; anew</li><li>in the modern fashion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fatherly, in a fatherly manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fatherly, in a fatherly manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Portuguese</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>португа́льский</i> (portugálʹskij, “Portuguese”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as before, (still) as usual, still</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a friendly way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently; in various ways</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>childishly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>childishly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Russian</li><li>the Russian way</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) in plain Russian, plainly, frankly</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of ру́сский (rússkij), "Russian".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hoggishly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in one’s own way</li><li>according to one’s way of thinking</li><li>according to one’s way of doing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brutishly, in a brutish way, like a brute</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ско́тский</i> (skótskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a dog-like manner, like a dog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the manner of an old man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an old-fashioned way; like in the old days</li><li>the old way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Old French</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in [according to] your opinion, to your way of thinking, <i>(in a question)</i> do you think...?</li><li>as you wish, as you would have it, as you advise</li></ol><p>From <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>твоему́</i> (tvojemú), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Ukrainian</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of украинский, "Ukrainian".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Finnish</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>фи́нский</i> (fínskij, “Finnish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the navy way, navy-style</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>фло́тский</i> (flótskij, “navy, naval”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in French</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>францу́зский</i> (francúzskij, “French”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>boorishly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thriftily</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́йский</i> (xozjájskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>properly</li><li>thoroughly</li><li>in an amicable/friendly way</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>хоро́шему</i> (xoróšemu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Romani language, in Gypsy language</li><li>in a Romani way, in a Gypsy way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>humanly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>honestly, fairly, in an honest/fair way/manner</li><li>being honest, being frank (when saying or admitting something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the Chukchi manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(of ovens) without a chimney when a smoke goes directly into the house</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) illegally, without a permit; secretly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) severely; cruelly; mercilessly</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”), compare the usage in German <i>schwarz</i>, as in <i>schwarzarbeiten</i> (“to work illegally, lit.: to work black”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Swedish</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>шве́дский</i> (švédskij, “Swedish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Japanese</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>япо́нский</i> (japónskij, “Japanese”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow/turn crimson/purple, to redden, to flush</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>багрове́ть</i> (bagrovétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to talk loudly, to babble</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>база́рить</i> (bazáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>боя́ться</i> (bojátʹsja): to be rather afraid</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>боя́ться</i> (bojátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight, escape</li><li>sprout, shoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run (for a while), to have a run</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) running around</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) running errands</li></ol><p><i>побе́гать</i> (pobégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шки</i> (-úški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>побѣда</i> (poběda, “victory, defeat”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>побѣда</i> (poběda, “victory”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poběda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cemented carbide (alloy of tungsten carbide), pobedit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winner, victor, vanquisher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win, to be victorious over, to conquer, to vanquish, to defeat</li><li>to overcome</li><li>to beat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>побѣдити</i> (poběditi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poběditi</i>, from <i>&#42;běditi</i>. Related to the noun <i>беда́</i> (bedá). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>побије́дити</i>/<i>pobijéditi</i>, Bulgarian <i>победя́</i> (pobedjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>triumphantly, victoriously</li></ol><p><i>побе́дный</i> (pobédnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triumphal, triumphant, victorious</li></ol><p><i>побе́да</i> (pobéda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) pobedobesie: the ultranationalist scope, Soviet themes, and militarist form of celebrating the Victory Day.</li></ol><p>From <i>побе́да</i> (pobéda, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-бе́сие</i> (-bésije, “mania, madness”), compared to идолобе́сие (idolobésije, “idolomania, idolatry”). This word was coined in 2005.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perennial victor</li></ol><p><i>победоно́с(ный)</i> (pobedonós(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>victorious, triumphant</li></ol><p><i>побе́да</i> (pobéda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start running, to break into a run</li><li>to begin to flow (<i>of liquids</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win, to be victorious over, to conquer, to vanquish, to defeat</li><li>to overcome</li><li>to beat</li></ol><p><i>победи́ть</i> (pobedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defeated person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn white, (<i>intransitive</i>) to whiten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn gray</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become brighter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to dawn</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>беле́ть</i> (belétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whiten</li><li>to whitewash</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бели́ть</i> (belítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whitewashing</li><li>whitewash (<i>result</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) whitewash (<i>liquid</i>)</li></ol><p><i>побели́ть</i> (pobelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore, coast, seaboard, littoral</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep in reserve, to not use up</li><li>to protect and care for</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a talk, to have a chat</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бесе́довать</i> (besédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to madden, to rouse (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>беси́ть</i> (besítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, to bother</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>беспоко́ить</i> (bespokóitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg, live by begging</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) + <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) beggar, moocher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat, to give a beating (typically not badly)</li><li>to beat (a record)</li><li>to break</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thank</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>благодари́ть</i> (blagodarítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>благозву́чней</i> (blagozvúčnej)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) indulgence, leniency</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow pale, to turn white</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бледне́ть</i> (blednétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded (about plants or colors)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>блек(нуть)</i> (blek(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>бли́зкий</i> (blízkij): closer, nearer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) comparative degree of <i>бли́зко</i> (blízko): closer, nearer, more intimately</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearby (close to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whore, slut</li></ol><p>Related to <i>блядь</i> (bljadʹ, “whore”) or <i>блядова́ть</i> (bljadovátʹ, “to whore around”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded (about plants or colors)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>блёк(нуть)</i> (bljók(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade, to wither</li><li>to pale <i>(beside / before)</i>, to pale into insignificance</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>блёкнуть</i> (bljóknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>блесте́ть</i> (blestétʹ): to shine a little, to shine sometimes</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>блеск-</i> (blesk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ). Apparently formed from an underlying root блеск- of <i>блесте́ть</i> (blestétʹ); compare <i>блеск</i> (blesk, “brilliance, luster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beating (continuous striking of another person or animal)</li><li>battery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(great) battle; slaughter, carnage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chat (have a chat) <i>(with about)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>бо́льше</i> (bólʹše)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exaction, (high, backbreaking) fee, tax</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partisan (fervent supporter)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поборьникъ</i> (poborĭnikŭ), from <i>поборати</i> (poborati, “to help in a struggle, to protect”), from <i>борь</i> (borĭ, “struggle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight down, to overcome</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to beat</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-боро́ть</i> (-borótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to wrestle, to struggle, to strive for some time</li></ol><p><i>поборо́ть</i> (poborótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>side, collateral, accessory, incidental</li><li>natural (<i>of a child</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>byproduct, subproduct (secondary or additional product)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) to be afraid, to hesitate [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do, use or say something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>боя́ться</i> (bojátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>брань</i> (branʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood brother</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pobratimъ</i>, from <i>&#42;pobratiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fastidious/squeamish <i>(about)</i></li><li><i>(usually negative)</i> to disdain, to shrink <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бре́згать</i> (brézgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fastidious/squeamish <i>(about)</i></li><li><i>(usually negative)</i> to disdain, to shrink <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бре́зговать</i> (brézgovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plod, to start wandering</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave (<i>transitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>брить</i> (britʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave oneself (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>побри́ть</i> (pobrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw up <i>(leave in disorder)</i></li><li>to forsake, to desert, to abandon <i>(many things or persons)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clatter for a while or occasionally, to tinkle, to rattle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бря́кать</i> (brjákatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to clatter for a while or occasionally, to tinkle, to rattle</li></ol><p><i>побря́кать</i> (pobrjákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rattle (child's toy)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) small pendant or ornament that jingles when it moves</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) trinket, trifle</li></ol><p><i>побря́кать</i> (pobrjákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) inducing towards activity, compelling towards action</li></ol><p><i>побуди́ть</i> (pobudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to spend some time awakening</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wake up (all or many people, one by one)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;transitive) to awaken</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) to drive (an animal) from its hole or lair, to flush out</li><li>to induce, to prompt, to impel, to spur</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to induce, to prompt, to impel, to spur</li></ol><p><i>побуди́ть</i> (pobudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motive, inducement, incentive, motivation, urge, impulse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be at, to visit, to stay with</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>быва́ть</i> (byvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) short stay (somewhere)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) short home leave</li></ol><p><i>побыва́ть</i> (pobyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стрый</i> (býstryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>бы́стро</i> (býstro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>побе́гать</i> (pobégatʹ).</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бѣ́гать</i> (bě́gatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>побе́да</i> (pobéda).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>побежа́ть</i> (pobežátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>бѣжа́ть</i> (běžátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to train, to deign to teach (<i>someone to do something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́дить</i> (-váditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get in the habit of [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something, usually bad’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to frequent, to visit frequently</li></ol><p><i>пова́дить</i> (pováditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) habit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to train, to deign to teach (<i>someone to do something</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lead, to guide (<i>periodically</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive (<i>periodically</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to convey, to transport (<i>periodically</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пова́дить</i> (pováditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, to topple, to overthrow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(people)</i> to begin to throng; <i>(snow)</i> to begin to fall heavily, to begin to fall in thick flakes; <i>(smoke)</i> to begin to belch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ, “to cut, to throw, to fell”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall <i>(down)</i>, to collapse, to tumble down, to topple <i>(over, down)</i></li></ol><p><i>повали́ть</i> (povalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general, en masse</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) mass</li></ol><p><i>повал(и́ть)</i> (poval(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time rolling [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in flour, bread crumbs, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to drag [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘all or many’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in/through mud, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time rolling about or wallowing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time lying about (<i>of a person — idly or due to illness; of objects — due to being carelessly strewn</i>)</li></ol><p><i>поваля́ть</i> (povaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cook, chef</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>варъ</i> (varŭ, “boiled liquid”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;varъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table salt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>culinary</li></ol><p><i>по́вар</i> (póvar) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) kitchen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поварьнꙗ</i> (povarĭnja, “kitchen”), from <i>поваръ</i> (povarŭ, “cook”) +&lrm; <i>-ьнꙗ</i> (-ĭnja). By surface analysis, <i>по́вар</i> (póvar, “cook”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitchen boy, cook's helper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ladle (deep-bowled spoon with a long, usually curved, handle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to tell, to retell, to recount, to say</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>&#42;повѣдѣти</i> (&#42;pověděti); reshaped based on <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ).</p><p>Cognates include Polish <i>powiedzieć</i>, <i>powiadać</i>, Czech <i>povědět</i>, <i>povídat</i>, Slovak <i>povedať</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>povijedati</i>, Ukrainian <i>пові́дати</i> (povídaty), Belarusian <i>распавяда́ць</i> (raspavjadácʹ), Slovene <i>povedati</i>, Bulgarian <i>изповя́дам</i> (izpovjádam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conduct, behaviour</li></ol><p><i>повести́</i> (povestí) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>behavioral</li></ol><p>From <i>поведе́ние</i> (povedénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;rhetorical) alternative form of <i>пове́дывать</i> (povédyvatʹ) (<i>imperfective</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary&#32;or&#32;rhetorical) alternative form of <i>пове́дать</i> (povédatʹ) (<i>perfective</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вед-</i> (ved-) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to tell, to retell, to recount, to say</li></ol><p><i>пове́дать</i> (povédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convey, to carry (by vehicle), to deliver, to transport</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be lucky, to have luck, to work out for, to succeed, to be successful [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to command, to rule <i>(over)</i></li><li>to enjoin, to order, to command</li></ol><p><i>повеле́ть</i> (povelétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) command</li></ol><p><i>повеле́ть</i> (povelétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to command, to rule <i>(over)</i></li><li>to order, to enjoin, to command [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>веле́ть</i> (velétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereign, master (owner of an animal or slave)</li></ol><p><i>повеле́ть</i> (povelétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) imperative mood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperious, authoritative</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) imperative (relating to the imperative mood) (<i>no comparative or short forms</i>)</li></ol><p><i>повеле́ть</i> (povelétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry <i>(in church)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>венча́ть</i> (venčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be married <i>(in church)</i>, to get married <i>(in church)</i></li></ol><p><i>повенча́ть</i> (povenčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to throw down</li><li>to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>пове́ргнуть</i> (povérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to throw down</li><li>to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confidant</li><li>plenipotentiary, agent (the party who acts for another in a mandate contract (<i>догово́р поруче́ния</i> (dogovór poručénija)))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fallen, prostrate</li><li>toppled</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) defeated, conquered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entrust, to confide, to trust</li><li>to check, to verify</li><li>to believe, to trust [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to believe, to have faith [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checking up, check-up, verification</li><li>roll call</li><li>visiting, inspection (<i>of posts, guards</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пове́рить</i> (povéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn, to swing</li><li>to turn (to change direction of one's motion)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn, to swing, to change <i>intransitive</i></li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to take an unexpected turn</li></ol><p><i>поверну́ть</i> (povernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) surface tension (the effect on the surface of a liquid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) surface</li><li>shallow, superficial, perfunctory</li></ol><p><i>пове́рхность</i> (povérxnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surface, face</li></ol><p>From <i>пове́рх</i> (povérx) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the surface, over the top</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рху</i> (vérxu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legend, superstition, popular belief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scapegrace; playboy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to become merry/cheerful</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>веселе́ть</i> (veselétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to have fun</li></ol><p><i>повесели́ть</i> (poveselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to hang up</li><li>to hang (to execute by hanging)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sometimes with the verb omitted</i>) to be extremely tired</li></ol><p>Literally, “to hang (one's) tongue on (one's) shoulder”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang oneself</li></ol><p><i>пове́сить</i> (povésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narration, narrative</li></ol><p><i>повествова́ть</i> (povestvovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) narrative, declarative</li></ol><p><i>повествова́ть</i> (povestvovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrate, to relate, to recount, to give an account <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>по́весть</i> (póvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ствова́ть</i> (-stvovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to conduct, to direct, to drive</li><li>to move</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become the custom</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make friends (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>повести́</i> (povestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notice (formal written notification), invitation (written call for a meeting)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) subpoena, summons, writ</li><li>(<i>military</i>) call-up papers, draft papers (an order to report for military service)</li><li>agenda</li></ol><p>From <i>по́весть</i> (póvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agenda, order of the day (list of matters to be taken up)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrative, story, tale</li><li>novella</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>повѣсть</i> (pověstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pověstь</i>, from <i>&#42;povĕdtь</i>. Related to <i>пове́дать</i> (povédatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) plague, pestilence</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) air (obsolete&#58; mixture of gases making up the atmosphere of the Earth)</li><li>craze, fad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become decrepit</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ветша́ть</i> (vetšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging (<i>means of execution</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пове́сить</i> (povésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to blow</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be in the air, to be able to be sensed</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have an argument, fight, spat, falling out, etc., to have words</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вздо́рить</i> (vzdóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mature, to grow up</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>взросле́ть</i> (vzroslétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see, to get to see</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to visit</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вида́ть</i> (vidátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see, to meet, to see each other</li></ol><p><i>повида́ть</i> (povidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jam, marmalade, fruit butter, fruit paste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>powidło</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>визжа́ть</i> (vizžátʹ): to squeal a little, to squeal sometimes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>визг</i> (vizg) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeal for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>визжа́ть</i> (vizžátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dodder</li></ol><p>Derived from dated <i>пови́лый</i> (povílyj, “one that's winding around”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ка</i> (-íka). The name is associated with the feature of the dodder stem to twine around a host plant.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) confession, acknowledgement of guilt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duty, obligation</li></ol><p><i>пови́нный</i> (povínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guilty</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вина́</i> (viná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>повинѫти сѧ</i> (povinǫti sę), remodeled after class 2 verbs based on present tense forms. Compare Middle Russian <i>повину́ться</i> (povinútʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience, submission</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>повиновениѥ</i> (povinovenije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>повинѫти сѧ</i> (povinǫti sę). By surface analysis, <i>повинова́ть(ся)</i> (povinovátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to be suspended (in a certain position)</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) to cling [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘onto someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пови́снуть</i> (povísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang, to be suspended</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>висе́ть</i> (visétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to hang down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hang</li><li>to cling [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bindweed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwife (woman who assists other women in childbirth)</li></ol><p><i>пови́ть</i> (povítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-уха</i> (-uxa), <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ, “to wind, to twist, to weave”) +&lrm; <i>-уха</i> (-uxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entail, to bring about</li><li>to attract, to draw, to pull, to haul</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to incur</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to influence, to have an influence</li><li>to affect, to govern</li><li>to have effect, to have action</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>влия́ть</i> (vlijátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rein, bridle, bridle-rein</li><li>cause, ground, occasion</li></ol><p>Action noun of повести́ (povestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to walk, to take around</li><li>to drive (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>по́вод</i> (póvod): leash</li></ol><p><i>по́вод</i> (póvod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader of a blind person</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) guide</li></ol><p><i>поводи́ть</i> (povodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ы́рь</i> (-ýrʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to war, to wage war for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>воева́ть</i> (vojevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive, to take for drives</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fiddle, to busy oneself, to waste time</li><li>to mess around</li></ol><p><i>повози́ть</i> (povozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart, wagon, carriage</li></ol><p><i>повози́ть</i> (povozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cart, wagon, carriage</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cart driver, wagon driver</li></ol><p><i>пово́зка</i> (povózka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) driver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Volga region</li></ol><p><i>Пово́лжье</i> (Povólžʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn, to swing</li><li>to turn (to change direction of one's motion)</li></ol><p><i>повороти́ть</i> (povorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, to swing</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to do something quickly or hastily</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to change, to take shape in a certain way</li></ol><p><i>повора́чивать</i> (povoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turn (a change of direction or orientation)</li><li>curve</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) break (a significant change in circumstance, attitude, perception, or focus of attention)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;povortъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;transitive) to turn, to swing</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to turn (to change direction of one's motion)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to turn</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agile, nimble</li></ol><p><i>поворо́т</i> (povorót) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turning, rotary</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) swivel</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) decisive</li></ol><p><i>поворо́т</i> (povorót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure, to harm, to hurt, to damage, to cause damage</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вреди́ть</i> (vredítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure, to harm, to hurt, to damage, to cause damage</li></ol><p><i>повреди́ть</i> (povredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage</li><li>injury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wait (a little with something); to not proceed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daily routine, everyday life</li></ol><p><i>повседне́вный</i> (povsednévnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everyday, daily (appropriate for ordinary use, rather than for special occasions)</li><li>day-to-day (ordinary or mundane)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>днев-</i> (dnev-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere, in all places or parts</li><li>overall</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) ubiquitously</li></ol><p><i>повсеме́стный</i> (povseméstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ubiquity, omnipresence</li></ol><p><i>повсеме́стный</i> (povseméstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general, found everywhere, ubiquitous</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>все-</i> (vse-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ст</i> (mést) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebel, insurrectionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to meet, to run into</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>встреча́ть</i> (vstrečátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to meet, to run into [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <i>or</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>повстреча́ть</i> (povstrečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere, far and wide</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>всю́ду</i> (vsjúdu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition, repeat, rebroadcast, rerun</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) echo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition, revision (act or an instance of repeating or being repeated)</li></ol><p><i>повтори́ть</i> (povtorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repeat, to reiterate</li><li>to review (<i>a lesson</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to repeat</li></ol><p><i>повтори́ть</i> (povtorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>repeatedly, iteratively (done several times)</li></ol><p><i>повто́рный</i> (povtórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>repeated, recurring</li></ol><p><i>повтори́ть</i> (povtorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition, reiteration, recurrence</li></ol><p><i>повторя́емый</i> (povtorjájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repeat, to reiterate</li><li>to review <i>(lesson)</i></li></ol><p><i>повтори́ть</i> (povtorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to repeat</li></ol><p><i>повторя́ть</i> (povtorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to increase, to heighten, to boost, to elevate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to go up, to increase</li><li>to advance, to improve, to heighten</li><li>to soar, to boom, to jump, to mount</li></ol><p><i>повы́сить</i> (povýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to increase, to heighten, to boost, to elevate</li></ol><p><i>повы́сить</i> (povýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to go up, to increase</li><li>to advance, to improve, to heighten</li><li>to soar, to boom, to jump, to mount</li></ol><p><i>повыша́ть</i> (povyšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>высоко́</i> (vysokó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raise, promotion</li></ol><p><i>повы́сить</i> (povýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heightened, higher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to seize, to arrest, to bust, to nab, to pinch</li><li>to knit <i>(a little)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandage</li><li>band, armlet, fillet</li></ol><p><i>повяза́ть</i> (povjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie</li></ol><p><i>повяза́ть</i> (povjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>powiat (the second-level unit of local government and administration in Poland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>powiat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) pellet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;derogatory) scoundrel, bastard, scumbag, rascal</li></ol><p><i>пога́ный</i> (pogányj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toadstool, poisonous mushroom</li><li><i>Podiceps</i> (genus of waterbirds of the grebe family, whose meat has an unpleasant taste)</li></ol><p><i>пога́н(ец)</i> (pogán(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>пога́ный</i> (pogányj, “foul”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) heathen</li><li>foul, unclean</li><li>lousy, nasty, rotten</li><li><i>(mushroom)</i> poisonous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поганъ</i> (poganŭ, “pagan”), itself borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>pāgānus</i> (“pagan”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>крестья́нин</i> (krestʹjánin) and <i>крети́н</i> (kretín) (<&lt; Latin <i>Chrīstiānus</i>, akin to Ancient Greek <i>Χρῑστιᾱνός</i> (Khrīstiānós), whence <i>христиани́н</i> (xristianín)), also compare <i>дереве́нщина</i> (derevénščina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put out, to extinguish</li><li>to turn off (light)</li><li>to cancel (debt); to make invalid</li><li>to quench</li><li>to slake (lime)</li><li>to kill (a ball)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill, to murder, to waste; to beat</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гаси́ть</i> (gasítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out (of light, fire)</li><li>to die away</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>га́снуть</i> (gásnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) pogača</li></ol><p>From Bulgarian <i>пога́ча</i> (pogáča) or Serbo-Croatian <i>pògača</i> / <i>по̀гача</i>, Medieval Latin <i>focacea</i>, a derivative of <i>focus</i> (“hearth, fireplace”), in the meaning "hearth bread" (<i>panis focācius</i>). Compare with <i>пога́ч</i> (pogáč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paying off, clearing off</li></ol><p><i>погаси́ть</i> (pogasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perish, to die of unnatural causes, to be killed</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гиба́ть</i> (gibátʹ), from Old East Slavic <i>гꙑбати</i> (gybati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gybati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death, destruction, ruin, perdition</li><li><i>in expresions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perish, to die of unnatural causes, to be killed</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ги́бнуть</i> (gíbnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead, deceased</li><li>lost, ruined</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead person, deceased person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to iron, to press (clothes)</li><li>to pet (animals, etc.)</li><li>to stroke, to caress</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stroke, to pet</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare for a while [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘at someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>глазе́ть</i> (glazétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorber, absorbent</li></ol><p><i>поглоти́ть</i> (poglotítʹ, “to absorb”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow up, to devour</li><li>to absorb, to take up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поглътити</i> (poglŭtiti). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-глоти́ть</i> (-glotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow up, to devour</li><li>to absorb, to take up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поглъщати</i> (poglŭštati). By surface analysis, <i>поглоти́ть</i> (poglotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absorption (act or process of absorbing or sucking in anything)</li><li>intake, uptake, takeover, acquisition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preoccupation</li></ol><p><i>поглощённый</i> (pogloščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inwardly focused</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>глубо́кий</i> (glubókij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>глубоко́</i> (glubokó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get dumber, to become stupid or more stupid</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to become infatuated</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>глупе́ть</i> (glupétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, to glance</li><li>to look after, to take care of</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cast a glance (from time to time)</li><li>to look (after), to keep an eye (on)</li></ol><p><i>погляде́ть</i> (pogljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to lie</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start chasing, to start pursuing [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>погна́ть</i> (pognátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ). Compare Polish <i>pogiąć</i> and Silesian <i>pogiōńć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pogo stick</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pogo stick</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a conversation, to talk (used with с кем (s kem))</li><li>to talk (that lasts only a certain amount of time) (see по- (po-))</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saying, proverb, byword (concise, descriptive folk phrase)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) weather</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bad weather</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>погода</i> (pogoda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pogoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait a little (commonly used in the imperative), to hang on</li><li>to delay the start of something</li><li>to have a bad weather</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), from the original meaning of Old East Slavic <i>годъ</i> (godŭ, “time, stint”).</p><p>From <i>пого́да</i> (pogóda)<sup>?</sup></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>пого́да</i> (pogóda, “weather”)</li></ol><p><i>пого́да</i> (pogóda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) weather</li><li>yearly, annual</li></ol><p><i>пого́да</i> (pogóda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) soon, after some time (usually combined with <i>немно́го</i> (nemnógo) or <i>чуть</i> (čutʹ), e.g. немно́го погодя́ - a little bit later)</li></ol><p>From <i>погоди́ть</i> (pogodítʹ).</p><p>From <i>пого́дить</i> (pogóditʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>universally, to a man, without exception</li></ol><p><i>поголо́вный</i> (pogolóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general, total, universal, without exception</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) capitation; by head</li></ol><p><i>поголо́в(ье)</i> (pogolóv(ʹje)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>total number/head of livestock, population</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). For the meaning development compare with <i>cattle</i> ultimately from Latin <i>caput</i> (“head”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn blue</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn gay <i>(homosexual)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>голубе́ть</i> (golubétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder strap, shoulder board, shoulder mark (epaulette) (<i>military insignia</i>)</li><li>(<i>of a tank</i>) turret ring (<b>погон башни</b>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driver; drover (a person who drives animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chase, pursuit</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) the coat of arms of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, which depicts a knight on horseback galloping to chase an enemy.</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) the former coat of arms of Belarus.</li></ol><p>See also Belarusian <i>Паго́ня</i> (Pahónja) and Polish <i>pogoń</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) goad, cattle prod (something used to provoke movement somewhere or some action)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figuratively) constant supervision/control to ensure completion of work on time</li></ol><p><i>погоня́ть</i> (pogonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) goad, cattle prod (something used to provoke movement somewhere or some action)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) nickname, moniker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive on, to urge on, to hurry on</li><li>to drive <i>(for a time)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase for a while, to pursue for a while [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>погоня́ть</i> (pogonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be deprived of shelter or property as a result of fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to burn down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dry out, to become parched</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to burn (<i>of many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn for a while</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to fail</li></ol><p><i>погоре́ть</i> (pogorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has lost all in a fire</li></ol><p><i>погоре́ть</i> (pogorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) burned out, destroyed by a fire</li><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) of someone who lost all property in a fire.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be deprived of shelter or property as a result of fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to burn down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dry out, to become parched</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to burn (<i>of many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn for a while</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to fail</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get excited</li><li>to get impatient, to get angry, to lose one's temper</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>горячи́ться</i> (gorjačítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rural cemetery</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) village church located away from the settlement, with a graveyard, its own land and a house for the clergy</li><li>(historical,&#32;10th century AD) coaching inn for princes and important church officials</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) pogost, former administrative-territorial unit in Russia, up through the 18th century, consisting of several villages</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) pogost, a large village in the center of such an administrative-territorial unit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>погостъ</i> (pogostŭ). From Russian <i>гость</i> (gostʹ, “guest, visitor”), Russian <i>погости́ть</i> (pogostítʹ, “to stay for a while (at someone's house)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay for a while (<i>at someone's house</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гости́ть</i> (gostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frontier/border guard</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>грани́ца</i> (graníca) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frontier, boundary, border</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>грани́ца</i> (graníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a region along the country border</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-, “to”) +&lrm; <i>грани́ца</i> (graníca, “border, boundary”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border control</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>пограни́чный контро́ль</i> (pograníčnyj kontrólʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root cellar, vault</li><li>(<i>military</i>) magazine</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>погребъ</i> (pogrebŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pogrebъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) burial</li></ol><p><i>погреба́ть</i> (pogrebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury</li></ol><p><i>погреб-</i> (pogreb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial, interment</li></ol><p><i>погреб-</i> (pogreb-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rattle <i>(a baby's toy)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>греме́ть</i> (gremétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to row <i>(a little)</i></li><li>to bury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm oneself <i>(for a while)</i></li></ol><p><i>погре́ть</i> (pogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to err</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to unfairly accuse (someone)</li><li>to contradict (logic)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>греши́ть</i> (grešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) fault; delinquency; sin</li><li>mistake, error</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) error</li></ol><p>From <i>погреши́ть</i> (pogrešítʹ, “to err”) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>грози́ть</i> (grozítʹ). Compare Czech <i>pohrozit</i> and Polish <i>pogrozić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riot</li><li>pogrom, ethnic violence event</li><li>crushing defeat</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) mayhem, chaos, disorder</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>громи́ть</i> (gromítʹ, “to smash, to sack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>гро́мкий</i> (grómkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>гро́мко</i> (grómko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of a pogrom mob; rioter, vigilante</li></ol><p><i>погро́м</i> (pogróm) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, to submerge, to duck, to immerse, to plunge</li><li><i>(into a condition)</i> to immerse, to plunge, to absorb</li></ol><p><i>погрузи́ть</i> (pogruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to be submerged, (<i>intransitive</i>) to plunge (into)</li><li>to be immersed, to plunged, to absorbed (into a condition, thought, work, etc.)</li><li>to take cargo</li><li>to pile into (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>погружа́ть</i> (pogružátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immersion</li><li>submersion, submergence</li><li><i>(u-boat)</i> dive, diving</li><li><i>(ship)</i> sinking, settling</li></ol><p><i>погрузи́ть</i> (pogruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, to submerge, to duck, to immerse, to plunge</li><li><i>(into a condition)</i> to immerse, to plunge, to absorb</li><li>to load (to put a load on)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to be submerged, (<i>intransitive</i>) to plunge (into)</li><li>to be immersed, to plunged, to absorbed (into a condition, thought, work, etc.)</li><li>to take cargo</li><li>to pile into (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loading, shipment, lading, embarkation</li></ol><p><i>погрузи́ть</i> (pogruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loading</li></ol><p><i>погру́зка</i> (pogrúzka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to get stuck, to become mired, to bog down, to get bogged down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>погря́знуть</i> (pogrjáznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to get stuck, to become mired, to bog down, to get bogged down [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to get carried away by, to become completely absorbed in [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become involved [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in reprehensible deeds’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гря́знуть</i> (grjáznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wreck, to destroy, to ruin</li><li>to kill (make useless)</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>губи́ть</i> (gubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to go for a walk, to stroll for a while</li><li>to have a time-off, to have a free time for a while</li><li>to make merry, to enjoy oneself, to carouse for a while</li><li>to go with, to go out <i>with someone</i> for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thicken</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>густе́ть</i> (gustétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>under, below, beneath (movement to beneath a location)</li><li>under (beginning to fall under a condition, state or period of action)</li><li>towards, to</li><li>approaching, slightly less than (a quantitative characteristic, such as weight, age, etc.)</li><li>shortly before, towards, approaching (a time)</li><li>in imitation of, à la</li><li>for, suitable as</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> instrumental]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>under, below, beneath (being beneath a location)</li><li>under (being under a condition, state or period of action)</li><li>near, by</li><li>at (an angle)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) hearth, hearthstone</li><li>(dated,&#32;rare) floor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>henpecked, pussywhipped (submissive to or dominated by one's wife or other female partner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards evening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>henpecked, pussywhipped (submissive to or dominated by one's wife or other female partner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quietly, under one's breath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>open-air</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slightly drunk, tipsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) upwards (describing an action of moving from the bottom up)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) closer (describing an action of approaching to an entity)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) adding (describing an action of adding more entity)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) (describing a meaning that shows a finished action or an obtainment of a result)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) (describing an action of copying or following someone's action)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) (describing an existence of the certain action that is weak, invisible, hidden or incomplete)</li><li>takes nouns, produces nouns or adjectives: under-, sub- (referring to literal position under something, being under the influence of something, occupying a title or position below a superior, etc.)</li><li>takes adjectives, produces adverbs: completely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъ-</i>; see <i>&#42;podъ</i> for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waiter</li><li>supplier</li></ol><p><i>подава́ть</i> (podavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to proffer, to pass</li><li>to serve</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to pitch, to serve, to pass</li><li>to give alms</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дава́ть</i> (davátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (under pressure)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to yield, to give way</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make (for), to set out (for)</li></ol><p><i>подава́ть</i> (podavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suppress, to repress, to quell, to stifle</li><li>to depress, to overwhelm</li><li>to press (several times or continuously)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke (be unable to breathe because of obstruction of the windpipe)</li></ol><p><i>подави́ть</i> (podavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suppression, repression</li><li>(<i>military</i>) neutralization</li></ol><p><i>подави́ть</i> (podavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depression, dejection</li></ol><p><i>пода́вленный</i> (podávlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressed (<i>experiencing depression; of a person</i>)</li><li>depressed (<i>expressing depression; of a feeling, look, face, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li><li>suppressed (<i>feeling</i>)</li><li>muffled (<i>sound, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suppress, to repress, to quell, to stifle</li><li>to depress, to overwhelm</li></ol><p><i>подави́ть</i> (podavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overwhelming</li><li>depressing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so much the more, all the more, <i>(with negative)</i> even less so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gout, podagra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>да́льше</i> (dálʹše)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to present, to donate</li><li>to favour/favor, to bestow, to award</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дари́ть</i> (darítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present, gift</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дар</i> (dar) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пода́рок</i> (podárok, “gift, present”)</li></ol><p><i>пода́рок</i> (podárok) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gift, present</li></ol><p><i>пода́рок</i> (podárok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicant, petitioner, lodger (of an application)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pliant, pliable, complaisant</li></ol><p><i>пода́ться</i> (podátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to proffer, to pass</li><li>to serve</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to pitch, to serve, to pass</li><li>to give alms</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) tax, duty</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move (under pressure)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to yield, to give way</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make (for), to set out (for)</li></ol><p><i>пода́ть</i> (podátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) feed, feeding, supply</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) service, serve</li><li>presentation, portrayal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pittance, dole, charity, sop, tip (also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>пода́ча</i> (podáča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alms, charity, dole, handout (something given to the poor as charity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add slightly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>подбодря́ть</i> (podbodrjátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>подбодри́ть</i> (podbodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run up, to come running up to</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run up, to come running up to</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coltsfoot (<i>Tussilago farfara</i>)</li><li>butterbur or coltsfoot (<i>Petasites</i>)</li><li>bog rosemary (<i>Andromeda</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъbělъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch bolete (<i>Leccinum scabrum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>берёза</i> (berjóza) +&lrm; <i>-овик</i> (-ovik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to line (lining)</li><li>to resole (shoes)</li><li>to damage, to hurt (e.g. a person's eye)</li><li>to shoot down (bird, airplane)</li><li>to instigate, to abet (to assist or encourage in crime)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to assess, to sum up, to reckon up</li></ol><p><i>подби́ть</i> (podbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick up</li><li>to sort out, to select, to glean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъbirati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form</li><li>to steal up <i>(to)</i>, to approach stealthily</li><li>to find an approach <i>(to)</i></li><li>to aim <i>(for)</i></li><li>to tuck, to crouch</li><li>to straighten oneself up</li></ol><p><i>подбира́ть</i> (podbirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to line (lining)</li><li>to resole (shoes)</li><li>to damage, to hurt (e.g. a person's eye)</li><li>to shoot down (bird, airplane)</li><li>to instigate, to abet (to assist or encourage in crime)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to assess, to sum up, to reckon up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to encourage</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>бодри́ть</i> (bodrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to encourage</li></ol><p><i>подбодри́ть</i> (podbodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lining, backing</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подби́ть</i> (podbítʹ, “to line, to resole (<i>shoes</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection, assortment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъborъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection, matching</li><li>pickup</li><li>selection, set, <i>(in a newspaper)</i> related items/stories</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подобра́ть</i> (podobrátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>подбор-</i> (podbor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>подбер-</i> (podber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) chin</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>борода́</i> (borodá) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Compare Polish <i>podbródek</i>, Belarusian <i>падбаро́дак</i> (padbaródak), Serbo-Croatian <i>pòdbradak</i> / <i>по̀дбрадак</i>. Compare also Ukrainian <i>підборі́ддя</i> (pidboríddja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put one's arms akimbo</li></ol><p>From <i>подбоче́ниться</i> (podbočénitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ), from earlier <i>подбочи́ться</i> (podbočítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with one's arms akimbo, with one's hands on one's hips</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put one's arms akimbo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss up, to throw up</li><li>to throw <i>(under)</i></li><li>to put secretly/stealthily, to plant</li><li>to add</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give (someone) a lift (in a vehicle)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring up, to give some more</li></ol><p><i>подбро́сить</i> (podbrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw upwards</li><li>to flip</li><li>(automotive,&#32;colloquial) to give (someone) a lift (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basement, cellar, vault</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъvalъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) basement</li></ol><p><i>подва́л</i> (podvál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>подва́л</i> (podvál): small basement, small cellar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cellar bar (basement establishment selling alcoholic drinks for consumption on-site)</li></ol><p><i>подва́л</i> (podvál) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading</li><li>enumeration, summing up</li></ol><p><i>подвед-</i> (podved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>within the jurisdiction of, subordinate to, dependent</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домство</i> (védomstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li><li>to give a lift (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subject (to), to expose (to)</li></ol><p><i>подве́рг(нуть)</i> (podvérg(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undergo, to be exposed</li></ol><p><i>подверга́ть</i> (podvergátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subjected (to), exposed (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subject (to), to expose (to)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject to</li><li>liable to</li><li>prone to</li></ol><p>Formerly the past passive participle of <i>подве́ргнуть</i> (podvérgnutʹ), but now it functions only as an adjective.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten, to screw a little further</li><li>to tuck in, to tuck up</li><li>to turn up, to roll up</li><li>to sprain</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck up</li><li>to slip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up</li></ol><p><i>подверну́ть</i> (podvernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang up, to suspend</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging up, suspension</li><li>pendant</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) suspension</li></ol><p><i>подве́сить</i> (podvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suspended, pendant, pendulous, (<i>relational</i>) suspension</li></ol><p><i>подве́сить</i> (podvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead (to) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone or something’</small>]</li><li>to bring closer</li><li>to place <i>(under)</i></li><li>to substantiate <i>(with)</i>, to support <i>(with)</i>, to account <i>(for by)</i></li><li>to class, to group together, to present <i>(as)</i>, to depict <i>(as)</i></li><li>to drive <i>(at)</i>, to suggest</li><li>to let down, to do a bad turn</li><li>to pencil, to outline</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí). Compare with Czech <i>podvést</i> (“to cheat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leeward, lee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging</li><li>suspension</li></ol><p><i>подве́шивать</i> (podvéšivatʹ, “to hang up, to suspend”) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang up, to suspend</li></ol><p><i>подве́сить</i> (podvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>iliac</li><li>ileal</li></ol><p><i>подвздо́хи</i> (podvzdóxi, “ilia”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) from <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вздох</i> (vzdox).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exploit, feat, heroic deed</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) (difficult) labor, struggle</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) motion, progress, advance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>подвигъ</i> (podvigŭ), a deverbal of <i>подвигнѫти</i> (podvignǫti). Related to <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ) and <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push, to move</li><li>to advance, to push forward</li><li>to move for a while, to move a little</li></ol><p><i>подви́г-</i> (podvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to advance, to progress</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to advance <i>(in one's career)</i></li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move slightly</li></ol><p><i>подвига́ть</i> (podvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>подви́гать</i> (podvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;stilted</i>) to rouse, to induce, to encourage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>подвигнѫти</i> (podvignǫti). Doublet of <i>подвинуть</i> (podvinutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subspecies</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shift, (small) movement</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) progress, advancement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) ascetic, hermit</li><li>devotee, zealot, hero</li></ol><p><i>по́двиг</i> (pódvig) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) asceticism</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) self-sacrifice in service of a higher ideal</li></ol><p><i>подви́жник</i> (podvížnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mobile, travelling</li></ol><p><i>подвиг-</i> (podvig-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rail transport,&#32;collectively) rolling stock (railway vehicle, including main rail, subway, tram, and trolley)</li><li>(transport,&#32;collectively) motorized vehicles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motility</li><li>movability / moveability, mobility</li><li>agility</li><li>maneuverability</li><li>fluidity</li><li>mercuriality</li><li>removability</li></ol><p><i>подви́жный</i> (podvížnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mobile</li><li>lively, active</li><li>agile, nimble</li></ol><p><i>подви́гать</i> (podvígatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;transitive) to move, to cause to move</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to motivate to action, to impel</li></ol><p>Compare <i>подвига́ть</i> (podvigátʹ), <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ); this verb shows the effects of the Slavic progressive palatalization.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to rush (somewhere), to move forward</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to strive</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to labor, to act, to work (<i>expressing oneself in some area of activity</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;dated) to distinguish oneself in military matters, to fight bravely</li></ol><p><i>подвиза́ть</i> (podvizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move a little</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ). Doublet of <i>подви́гнуть</i> (podvígnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move</li><li>to advance, to progress</li></ol><p><i>подви́нуть</i> (podvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject <i>(to)</i>, dependent <i>(on)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supply, delivery</li><li>admission, intake</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подводи́ть</i> (podvodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dray, cart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring closer</li><li>to place <i>(under)</i></li><li>to substantiate <i>(with)</i>, to support <i>(with)</i>, to account <i>(for by)</i></li><li>to class, to group together, to present <i>(as)</i>, to depict <i>(as)</i></li><li>to drive <i>(at)</i>, to suggest</li><li>to let down, to do a bad turn</li><li>to pencil, to outline</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) submarine (undersea boat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>submariner</li><li>diver, frogman</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrofoil (wing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>underwater, submarine, subsea</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) drayage</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>подво́да</i> (podvóda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>UUV / underwater drone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) pitfall, catch (hidden difficulty or danger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li><li>to give a lift (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten, to screw a little further</li><li>to tuck in, to tuck up</li><li>to turn up, to roll up</li><li>to sprain</li></ol><p><i>подверт-</i> (podvert-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck up</li><li>to slip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn up</li></ol><p><i>подвора́чивать</i> (podvoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undercollar <i>(sewn under the collar of a soldier's tunic)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space between the gate and ground</li><li>board attached to the bottom of a gate</li><li>gateway</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) back street, dark corner</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>воро́та</i> (voróta) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), i.e., something under the gate.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmstead, courtyard; metochion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trick, catch</li></ol><p>Usually associated with <i>подво́д</i> (podvód), deverbal from <i>подводи́ть</i> (podvodítʹ, “to lead (somewhere)”). Compare the analogously formed <i>сва́ха</i> (sváxa) and <i>сват</i> (svat). Less reliably associated with <i>вохла́к</i> (voxlák) or <i>дво́хать</i> (dvóxatʹ, “to breathe”), Lithuanian <i>dvasià</i> (“soul, spirit”) as well as Old Norse <i>vasa-sk</i> (“to do something, to interfere”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to echo (someone's) howling [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only,&#32;intransitive) to howl softly, to whimper</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only,&#32;transitive) to call by howling (<i>of a wolf</i>)</li></ol><p><i>подвы́ть</i> (podvýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get slightly drunk, to have a couple/few</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вы́пить</i> (výpitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to echo (someone's) howling [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>выть</i> (vytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + instrumental) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to tie up</li><li>to tie on, to wear by tying</li><li>to knit more, to continue knitting</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tying up</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) garter</li></ol><p><i>подвяза́ть</i> (podvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>подвя́зкой</i> (podvjázkoj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + instrumental) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to tie up</li><li>to tie on, to wear by tying</li><li>to knit more, to continue knitting</li></ol><p><i>подвяза́ть</i> (podvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist</li><li>to turn</li><li>to roll</li><li>to tuck up (as with sleeves)</li></ol><p><i>подвёртывать</i> (podvjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harm somewhat</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>гад</i> (gad) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>tuck in, tuck under, bend under</li><li>to bend <i>(in / down / over)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) infraorbital; suborbital (below the eye socket)</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>глазни́чный</i> (glazníčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) subocular; infraocular (below the eye)</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>глазно́й</i> (glaznój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to watch furtively, to spy (<i>on</i>), to peep (<i>at</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to notice, to spot</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to watch furtively, to spy (<i>on</i>), to peep (<i>at</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to notice, to spot</li></ol><p><i>подгляде́ть</i> (podgljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite, to instigate, to abet (to assist or encourage in crime)</li></ol><p><i>подговори́ть</i> (podgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite, to instigate, to abet (to assist or encourage in crime)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment, alignment, calibration, adaptation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment, alignment, fitting, calibration, adaptation</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) intentional distortion of ideas, facts, data, etc. to hide contradictions, theft, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>подгон-</i> (podgon-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Derived from the present tense stem <i>подгон-</i> (podgon-) of <i>подогна́ть</i> (podognátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive / to urge on, to hurry (someone)</li><li>to fit, to adjust, to accommodate (to render fit or suitable)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn slightly</li></ol><p><i>подгоре́ть</i> (podgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slightly burnt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to train</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вить</i> (podgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to prepare [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подгота́вливать</i> (podgotávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preparatory, preparation</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вить</i> (podgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to train</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to prepare for</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вить</i> (podgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation</li><li>training</li><li>grounding, schooling</li><li>skill, knowledge</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вить</i> (podgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>readiness, preparedness</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вленный</i> (podgotóvlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to train</li></ol><p><i>подгото́вить</i> (podgotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to prepare [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подготовля́ть</i> (podgotovljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subgroup</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diaper, nappy</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) and <i>гу́зка</i> (gúzka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>подгу́зник</i> (podgúznik)</li></ol><p><i>подгу́зник</i> (podgúznik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike (<i>from below</i>), to kick</li><li>(<i>games</i>) to give away (a piece or card)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to booze</li></ol><p><i>подда́ть</i> (poddátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield to, to give in, to fall for, to cave in</li><li>to hold back (in a game), to intentionally let the opponent win or take the upper hand</li><li>to respond to (about a medical condition being responsive to a particular treatment)</li></ol><p><i>поддава́ть</i> (poddavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to say yes <i>(to)</i>, to assent <i>(to)</i>, to echo</li></ol><p><i>подда́кнуть</i> (poddáknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to say yes <i>(to)</i>, to assent <i>(to)</i>, to echo</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>да</i> (da) +&lrm; <i>-к-</i> (-k-) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>national (a person owing allegiance to a state)</li><li>citizen (a person who possesses citizenship of a state)</li><li>subject (legal member of a state)</li></ol><p>From <i>подда́ть</i> (poddátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анный</i> (-annyj).</p><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>poddany</i>, which is a calque of Latin <i>subditus</i>, probably mediated via Old Ruthenian <i>подданыи</i> (poddanyj). By surface analysis, <i>подда́ть</i> (poddátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анный</i> (-annyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizenship (state of being a citizen)</li><li>nationality (state of owing allegiance to a state)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>poddaństwo</i>, probably mediated via Old Ruthenian <i>подданьство</i> (poddanʹstvo). By surface analysis, <i>по́ддан(ный)</i> (póddan(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike <i>from below</i>, to kick</li><li>(<i>games</i>) to give away (a piece or card)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to booze, to bend the elbow</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield to, to give in, to fall for, to cave in</li><li>to hold back (in a game), to intentionally let the opponent win or take the upper hand</li></ol><p><i>подда́ть</i> (poddátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amenable</li><li>accessible, susceptible (open to the influence of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear, to put on [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to snag, to hook, to catch</li><li>to lift slightly (<i>from below</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat, to trick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tease, to needle</li></ol><p><i>подде́ть</i> (poddétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counterfeit, to falsify, to fake, to forge, to fabricate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterfeit, imitation; fake; forgery</li></ol><p><i>подде́лать</i> (poddélatʹ, “to counterfeit, to fake”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counterfeit, to falsify, to fake, to forge, to fabricate</li></ol><p><i>подде́лать</i> (poddélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false, counterfeit, fake, forged</li><li>imitation, artificial</li><li>spurious</li></ol><p><i>подде́л(ать)</i> (poddél(atʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maintenance, maintaining, support; keeping up</li></ol><p><i>поддержа́ть</i> (podderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support, to back up, to second</li><li>to maintain, to keep up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support, to back, to second, to uphold</li><li>to maintain, to keep up</li><li>to bear, to support</li></ol><p><i>поддержа́ть</i> (podderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be supported</li><li>to be maintained</li></ol><p><i>подде́рживать</i> (poddérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support (financial or other help)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear, to put on [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to snag, to hook, to catch</li><li>to lift slightly (<i>from below</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cheat, to trick</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tease, to needle</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pallet, underpan (a portable platform)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>дно</i> (dno)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taunt</li></ol><p><i>поддра́знивать</i> (poddráznivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of outerwear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to get (to another place)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дева́ться</i> (devátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affect, to have an effect on</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствовать</i> (déjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to do (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to share</li><li>to divide up</li><li>to deal well with someone (usually used with the negative particle)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be divided</li><li>to share</li></ol><p><i>подели́ть</i> (podelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (handmade) article</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a makeshift article</li><li>an odd job</li></ol><p><i>поде́лать</i> (podélatʹ, “to make, to do”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplice, stooge</li></ol><p><i>подели́ть</i> (podelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used, secondhand (that has or have previously been owned by someone else)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, to keep, to support</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get cheaper</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дешеве́ть</i> (deševétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subgenre</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>жанр</i> (žanr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry, to roast, to grill</li></ol><p><i>поджа́рить</i> (podžáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fry, to roast, to grill</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>жа́рить</i> (žáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lean, wiry, sinewy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck up</li><li>to tuck in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tighten</li><li>to put/exert pressure <i>(on)</i>, to press, to put the screws <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harvest further</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harvest what remains</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pancreas</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “sub”) +&lrm; <i>желу́дочный</i> (želúdočnyj, “gastric”) +&lrm; <i>железа́</i> (železá, “gland”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set on fire, to set fire (to), to ignite</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arsonist (one who has committed the act of arson, or illegally setting fire to property)</li></ol><p><i>поджига́ть</i> (podžigátʹ, “to set on fire”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmonger, militarist</li></ol><p>Literally, “arsonist / instigator of war”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set on fire, to set fire (to), to ignite</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wait</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lie in wait</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tuck up</li><li>to tuck in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tighten</li><li>to put/exert pressure <i>(on)</i>, to press, to put the screws <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harvest further</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harvest what remains</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arson, burning</li></ol><p>Related to <i>поджига́ть</i> (podžigátʹ), <i>подже́чь</i> (podžéčʹ), "to set on fire".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subheading, subhead</li><li>subheadline</li><li>subtitle (secondary title below or after a main title)</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>заголо́вок</i> (zagolóvok). Compare Ukrainian <i>підза́голо́вок</i> (pidzáholóvok), Belarusian <i>падзагало́вак</i> (padzahalóvak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to bore, to annoy, to bother slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to get tired slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>за-</i> (za-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>сolloquial</i>) clip on the back of the head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) client, defendant having a lawyer</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>защи́та</i> (zaščíta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), based on <i>под защи́той</i> (pod zaščítoj, “under the protection (<i>of</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vault, cave, dungeon, underground (large, naturally occurring cavity formed underground)</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) subway, metro, underground</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>underground, subterranean (below ground, under the earth, underground)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>podzol</li></ol><p><i>под</i> (pod, “under”) +&lrm; <i>зола́</i> (zolá, “ash”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spyglass</li></ol><p><i>подзо́рный</i> (podzórnyj, “observation”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>труба</i> (truba, “pipe”, noun), literally “observation pipe”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) observation, surveillance</li></ol><p>Related to <i>взор</i> (vzor, “look, glance, gaze”), with <i>под-</i> (pod-) and <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Belarusian <i>падзо́рны</i> (padzórny). Possibly also related to Old Church Slavonic <i>подзоръ</i> (podzorŭ), but likely not related to or at least not directly derived from <i>подзо́р</i> (podzór, “valance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to egg on, to provoke</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call up <i>(to)</i>, to beckon <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>пойди́</i> (pojdí)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) maybe, would, possibly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) just try</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to marvel <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>диви́ться</i> (divítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raised floor, podium, platform, stage, runway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>podium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henpecked, whipped husband</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>каблу́к</i> (kablúk) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a band under the shackle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drip adding to what is already present, to accumulate (drops of something)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ка́пать</i> (kápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out the earth under (something), to undermine, to sap</li><li>to dig (a passage or tunnel) under</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to undermine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig a bit, to dig a part of, to dig additionally</li><li>to drip adding to what is already present, to accumulate (drops of something)</li></ol><p><i>подкопа́ть</i> (podkopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>подка́пать</i> (podkápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ambush, waylay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed up, to fatten</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to add fertilizer <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>подкорми́ть</i> (podkormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>podcast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>podcast</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) podcaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>podcaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) podcast</li></ol><p>From <i>подка́ст</i> (podkást) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in general</i>) act of rolling up to or approaching</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) tackle</li><li>(<i>dating</i>) approach (for the purposes of flirting)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подкати́ть</i> (podkatítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(to)</i>, to drive <i>(to)</i></li><li>to roll up <i>(to)</i>, to drive up <i>(to)</i></li><li><i>(emotions)</i> to rise <i>(in)</i>, to fill, to seize</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(to)</i>, to drive <i>(to)</i></li><li>to roll up <i>(to)</i>, to drive up <i>(to)</i></li><li><i>(emotions)</i> to rise <i>(in)</i>, to fill, to seize</li><li><i>(figurative)</i> to hit on, to flirt with somebody</li></ol><p><i>подкати́ть</i> (podkatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pump up; to inflate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to let down; to disappoint</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pump up; to inflate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to let down; to disappoint</li></ol><p><i>подкача́ть</i> (podkačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to cut (grass, etc.) with a scythe</li><li>to mow at the base</li><li>to mow (<i>a little or additionally</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to knock down (<i>with a blow or shot</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cause (legs or knees) to buckle</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to deprive of health, to energy, to stamina, etc.</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to undermine, to undercut, to weaken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skew, to make askew (the direction of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squint slightly</li></ol><p><i>подкоси́ть</i> (podkosítʹ, “to cut with a scythe, to mow”) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>подкоси́ть</i> (podkosítʹ, “to skew, to squint slightly”) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss up, to throw up</li><li>to throw <i>(under)</i></li><li>to put secretly/stealthily, to plant <i>(a fake evidence)</i></li><li>to add</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a lift</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring up, to give some more</li><li>to add <i>(a card)</i> to be beaten</li></ol><p><i>подки́д-</i> (podkíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss up, to throw up</li><li>to throw <i>(under)</i></li><li>to put secretly/stealthily, to plant <i>(fake evidence)</i></li><li>to add</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give a lift</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring up, to give some more</li><li>to add <i>(a card)</i> to be beaten</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backing (material added behind or underneath)</li><li>lining, cushion</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) + a deverbal of <i>класть</i> (klastʹ, “to put, lay down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay <i>(under)</i>, to put <i>(under)</i></li><li>to line <i>(with)</i></li><li>to add, to put some more</li><li>to put/place furtively, to plant</li></ol><p><i>под</i> (pod) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect <i>(to)</i>, to link up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to enable, to turn on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get involved <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>подключи́ть</i> (podključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect <i>(to)</i>, to make a connection <i>(to)</i></li><li>to get involved <i>(in)</i>, to join in</li></ol><p><i>подключа́ть</i> (podključátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connecting, connection</li></ol><p><i>подключи́ть</i> (podključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect <i>(to)</i>, to link up <i>(with)</i></li><li>to enable, to turn on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get involved <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connect <i>(to)</i>, to make a connection <i>(to)</i></li><li>to get involved <i>(in)</i>, to join in</li></ol><p><i>подключи́ть</i> (podključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horseshoe</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) horseshoe vetch (<i>Hippocrepis</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъkova</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) someone who is grounded in something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>farriery</i>) to shoe (<i>a horse</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Hipposideridae.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Hipposideridae</i>, from the plural of <i>подковогу́бый</i> (podkovogúbyj), from <i>подковогу́б</i> (podkovogúb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>farriery</i>) to shoe (<i>horses</i>)</li></ol><p><i>подкова́ть</i> (podkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of dumplings in Mari El (a republic of Russia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Eastern Mari <i>подкогыльо</i> (podkogyĺo) or Western Mari <i>падкагыль</i> (padkagyĺ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subcutaneous, hypodermic</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “under, sub-”) +&lrm; <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) burn (witty insult), dig, jab</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подколо́ть</i> (podkolótʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joke, one-liner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to burn (make a witty insult), to dig (at), to jab (at)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under control</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) ruled (by a government)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>контро́ль</i> (kontrólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground passage, tunnel (<i>dug in the earth</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) intrigue</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подкопа́ть</i> (podkopátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out the earth under (something), to undermine, to sap</li><li>to dig (a passage or tunnel) under</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to undermine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig a bit, to dig a part of, to dig additionally</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed up, to fatten</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to add fertilizer <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additional fertilizing</li></ol><p><i>подкорми́ть</i> (podkormítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct or adjust slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>корректи́ровать</i> (korrektírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapwood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to cut (grass, etc.) with a scythe</li><li>to mow at the base</li><li>to mow (<i>a little or additionally</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to knock down (<i>with a blow or shot</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cause (legs or knees) to buckle</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to deprive of health, to energy, to stamina, etc.</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to undermine, to undercut, to weaken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skew, to make askew (the direction of something)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squint slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to mow”)</p><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to twist, to slant, to squint”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal up <i>(to)</i>, to sneak up</li></ol><p><i>подкра́сться</i> (podkrástʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch up (something with paint, dye or makeup)</li><li>to paint (something) more</li><li>to tint</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to beautify, to embellish</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal up <i>(to)</i>, to sneak up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>красть</i> (krastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch up (something with paint, dye or makeup)</li><li>to paint (something) more</li><li>to tint</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to beautify, to embellish</li></ol><p><i>подкра́сить</i> (podkrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support</li><li>to bolster <i>(with)</i>, to corroborate</li><li>to refresh, to fortify</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reinforce</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fortify oneself (<i>with food or drink</i>), to eat up, to drink up</li></ol><p><i>подкрепи́ть</i> (podkrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) reinforcement</li><li>refreshment (food)</li></ol><p><i>подкрепи́ть</i> (podkrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support</li><li>to bolster <i>(with)</i>, to corroborate</li><li>to refresh, to fortify</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reinforce</li></ol><p><i>подкрепи́ть</i> (podkrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fortify oneself (<i>with food or drink</i>), to eat up, to drink up</li></ol><p><i>подкрепля́ть</i> (podkrepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trick, to deceive</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>Кузьма́</i> (Kuzʹmá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bribery, subornation, graft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъkupъ</i>. Deverbal from <i>подкупа́ть</i> (podkupátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bribe, to suborn</li><li>to win, to prepossess</li><li>to buy extra</li></ol><p><i>подкупи́ть</i> (podkupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bribe, to suborn</li><li>to win, to prepossess</li><li>to buy extra</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet,&#32;video games) to lag slightly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust</li></ol><p><i>подла́дить</i> (podláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bark sometimes (like a dog)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ла́ять</i> (lájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply paint or varnish again</li><li>to embellish slightly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cover up flaws</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лакирова́ть</i> (lakirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) beside, near</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>подьлѣ</i> (podĭlě), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podьlě</i>. Cognates include Polish <i>podle</i>, Czech <i>podle</i>, Slovak <i>podľa</i>. Related to <i>во́зле</i> (vózle).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fishing</i>) fishing tool that is used under a frozen ice sheet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be subject (to), to be liable (to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) subject</li></ol><p>Nominalisation of <i>подлежа́щий</i> (podležáščij), present participle of <i>подлежа́ть</i> (podležátʹ). Calque of Latin <i>subiectum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undergrowth</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лес</i> (les) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly up <i>(to)</i>, to approach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run/rush up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly up <i>(to)</i>, to approach</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run/rush up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) wretch, scoundrel, rascal <i>(having a dishonest and low character)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podьlьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по́длый</i> (pódlyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour, to add (by pouring)</li></ol><p><i>подли́ть</i> (podlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add fuel to the fire (worsen a conflict)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravy</li><li>sauce</li></ol><p><i>подлива́ть</i> (podlivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suck-up, toady, lickspittle, wheedler, sycophant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, to insinuate, to suck up, to lick (someone's) boots, to wheedle, to curry favor with [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подли́за</i> (podlíza) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>original</li></ol><p><i>подлинный</i> (podlinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, truly</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) actually (<i>sentential adverb</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по́длинный</i> (pódlinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authenticity (the quality of being genuine or not corrupted from the original)</li></ol><p>From <i>по́длинный</i> (pódlinnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>original, authentic, genuine</li><li>true, real, pure</li></ol><p>Unidentified form; usually associated with <i>по́длинник</i> (pódlinnik, “long pole”) (see <i>длина́</i> (dliná, “length”)), on the supposed basis that during court punishment people were beaten with <i>"по́длинники"</i> ("pódlinniki", “long poles”) to obtain the truth; according to an alternative version, it is a derivative secondary adjective with the suffix <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) from the root <i>&#42;подьлинъ</i> (&#42;podĭlinŭ), a primary adjective from <i>подьлъ</i> (podĭlŭ, “lying at the bottom, on the bottom”)"; according to another version, it is derived from the root of the prepositional-case combination <i>по длине́</i> (po dliné), with an assumed primary meaning of "corresponding to the specified length".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour, to add (by pouring)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add fuel to the fire (worsen a conflict)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (someone) in the act, to trap/trick/set up [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘in something said or done’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что́бы</b> (štóby, + past-tense clause) <small>‘into doing something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgery</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подложи́ть</i> (podložítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;colloquial) submarine</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>подво́дная ло́дка</i> (podvódnaja lódka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay <i>(under)</i>, to put <i>(under)</i></li><li>to line <i>(with)</i></li><li>to add, to put some more</li><li>to put/place furtively, to plant</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom layer</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) substratum</li><li>(cinematography,&#32;photography) emulsion/film carrier, support</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) padding</li></ol><p><i>подлож(и́ть)</i> (podlož(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fake, false</li></ol><p><i>подло́г</i> (podlóg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armrest</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ло́коть</i> (lókotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meanness, baseness</li><li>mean/base/lowdown act/trick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podьlostь</i>, from <i>&#42;podьlъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по́длый</i> (pódlyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mean, low, dishonest, foul, dirty, bad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>podły</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podьlъ</i>. First attested in the XVII century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink (to blink with only one eye as a message, signal, or suggestion)</li></ol><p><i>подморгну́ть</i> (podmorgnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apprentice</li><li>journeyman</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ма́стер</i> (máster) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substitution (<i>secretly or unnoticed</i>)</li><li>relief (<i>worker</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substitute <i>(for)</i> (secretly or unnoticed)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to step in <i>(for)</i>, to stand in <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substitute <i>(for)</i> (secretly or unnoticed)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to step in <i>(for)</i>, to stand in <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>подмени́ть</i> (podmenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep</li><li>to baste on, to tack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notice</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to notice</li></ol><p><i>подме́тить</i> (podmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink (to blink with only one eye as a message, signal, or suggestion)</li></ol><p><i>подмигну́ть</i> (podmignútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink (to blink with only one eye as a message, signal, or suggestion)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>мигну́ть</i> (mignútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush (<i>underneath oneself</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to subjugate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-мина́ть</i> (-minátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>set theory</i>) subset</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>мно́жество</i> (mnóžestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) help, assistance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink (to blink with only one eye as a message, signal, or suggestion)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>моргну́ть</i> (morgnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>situated near Moscow</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>Москва́</i> (Moskvá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); for -моско́в-, compare <i>моско́вский</i> (moskóvskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scaffold, trestle</li><li>stage, platform</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash (an individual body part, e.g. the armpit)</li><li>to wash hastily, to in separate places</li><li>to wash away, to erode, to undermine (<i>with water</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to desire strongly, to be tempted</li></ol><p><i>подмы́ть</i> (podmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash (an individual body part, e.g. the armpit)</li><li>to wash hastily, to in separate places</li><li>to wash away, to erode, to undermine (<i>with water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit, underarm</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>мы́шка</i> (mýška, “"mouse", meaning "muscle" (see <i>мы́шца</i> (mýšca) for more)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush (<i>underneath oneself</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to subjugate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole (bottom of a shoe or boot)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to baste on, to tack</li></ol><p><i>подмета́ть</i> (podmetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to harm somewhat</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to egg on, to instigate, to goad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to egg on, to instigate, to goad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to partially wear out</li></ol><p><i>подноси́ть</i> (podnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subdemise, sublease, subrental, subtenancy, underlease</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heavens; celestial, heavenly</li><li>located on Earth, earthly</li></ol><p><i>поднебе́сье</i> (podnebésʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) heavenly heights, skies, heavens</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>не́бес-</i> (nébes-) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to carry (to)</li><li>to offer, to present</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hitherto</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>днесь</i> (dnesʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to elevate, to increase</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>heave</i> (akin to <i>have</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to go up, to get up</li><li>to ascend, to climb</li><li>to arise, to break out, to develop, to set out, to get agitated</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) to improve, to recover</li></ol><p><i>поднима́ть</i> (podnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowdown, ins and outs</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>подного́тная пра́вда</i> (podnogótnaja právda, literally “the truth from under the fingernails”), extracted by torture in ancient times.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the fingernail</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) secret, hidden</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) perfectly frank, brutally honest; giving the lowdown on something</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) meticulous, thorough (<i>search, examination</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>но́готь</i> (nógotʹ, “fingernail”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). The figurative senses refer to the medieval torture method of driving sharp objects under a suspect's fingernails to extract confessions.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot (of a mountain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step, footboard</li><li>trip, back heel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>подно́жие</i> (podnóžije)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tray, platter (object on which things are carried)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to carry (to)</li><li>to offer, to present</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to partially wear out</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toecap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving, offering</li><li>offering, gift</li></ol><p><i>подноси́ть</i> (podnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raising, rise, rising, lifting, elevation</li></ol><p><i>подня́ть</i> (podnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to elevate, to increase</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise, to go up, to get up</li><li>to ascend, to climb</li><li>to arise, to break out, to develop, to set out, to get agitated</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) to improve, to recover</li></ol><p><i>подня́ть</i> (podnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>под</i> (pod): under, towards, near, etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>под-</i> (pod-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, to befit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proper, correct, fitting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity</li><li>semblance, likeness, like</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>подобьѥ</i> (podobĭje), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podobьje</i>, ultimately derived from <i>&#42;doba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise (in like manner)</li></ol><p><i>подо́бный</i> (podóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>something like that, something like this, something similar, such a thing, that kind/sort of thing, stuff like that (<i>often translated as simply "that" or "this", meaning not necessarily the specific thing in question but rather anything resembling it</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>similar</li><li>like, such as</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podobьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>similarly, in a like manner (in a similar style or manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servility, obsequiousness, subservience</li></ol><p>From <i>подобостра́ст(ный)</i> (podobostrást(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subservient, menial, obsequious, servile (fawning)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>подобострастенъ</i> (podobostrastenŭ), from <i>подоба</i> (podoba, “manner”) + <i>страстенъ</i> (strastenŭ, “passionate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick up</li><li>to sort out, to select, to glean</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form</li><li>to steal up <i>(to)</i>, to approach stealthily</li><li>to find an approach <i>(to)</i></li><li>to aim <i>(for)</i></li><li>to tuck, to crouch</li><li>to straighten oneself up</li></ol><p><i>подобра́ть</i> (podobrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become kind</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>добре́ть</i> (dobrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive; to urge on, to hurry (someone)</li><li>to fit, to adjust, to accommodate (to render fit or suitable)</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>tuck in, tuck under, bend under</li><li>to bend <i>(in / down / over)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming up</li><li>reheating</li><li>rousing</li><li>intensification, exacerbation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подогрева́ть</i> (podogrevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to heat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stir up, to rouse</li></ol><p><i>подогре́ть</i> (podogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to heat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stir up, to rouse</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push up <i>(to)</i>, to move closer</li></ol><p><i>пододви́г-</i> (pododvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push up <i>(to)</i>, to move closer</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blanket cover, quilt cover, duvet cover, etc.; generally, an envelope of bedsheet fabric that encloses a blanket, duvet, or doona; historically, a type of sheet that attached to a blanket or duvet from below</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>одея́ло</i> (odejálo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait</li></ol><p>From <i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žьdati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call up <i>(to)</i>, to beckon <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>подзоръ</i> (podzorŭ), Bulgarian <i>подозрение</i> (podozrenie), Czech <i>podezření</i>, Polish <i>podejrzenie</i>. See <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) for more.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>suspiciō</i> (&lt; <i>sus-</i> + <i>speciō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspicion (act of suspecting something or someone, especially of something wrong)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>askance (of look&#58; with disapproval)</li><li>queerly, questionably, fishily</li></ol><p><i>подозри́тельный</i> (podozrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspiciousness</li></ol><p><i>подозри́тельный</i> (podozrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suspicious (arousing suspicion), suspect, shady</li><li>fishy, doubtful</li><li>suspicious, distrustful, mistrustful, querulous</li></ol><p>From <i>подозрева́ть</i> (podozrevátʹ) / <i>заподо́зрить</i> (zapodózritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk (to express milk from a mammal)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дои́ть</i> (doítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milking pail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, to come to, to walk to, to draw near [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k)]</li><li>to fit, to suit, to match, to be right [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти</i> (-jti)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windowsill</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>окно́</i> (oknó) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hem <i>(of a skirt)</i>, skirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>long, for hours <i>[days, months, etc]</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native of Podolia</li></ol><p><i>Подо́лье</i> (Podólʹje) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) rogue, bastard, rat, scum, scoundrel</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дно</i> (dno, “bottom”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok, “diminutive suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under wardship, under guardianship</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ward, charge (child or other person under guardianship)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>опе́ка</i> (opéka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hidden motive, underlying reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>experimental (acting as the subject of an experiment)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>о́пыт</i> (ópyt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) guinea pig (living experimental subject)</li></ol><p>Literally, “experimental rabbit”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow up, to blast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to undermine, to sap</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to explode, to blow up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be damaged, to deteriorate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) to suddenly start (doing something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) to be determined (to do something)</li></ol><p><i>подорва́ть</i> (podorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spotted eagle</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>орл</i> (orl, “eagle”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rise in price, to go up</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дорожа́ть</i> (dorožátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дорого́й</i> (dorogój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>до́рого</i> (dórogo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantain, ribwort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red-capped scaber stalk (<i>Leccinum aurantiacum</i>), orange birch bolete (<i>Leccinum versipelle</i>), or other similar red-, orange-, or white-capped mushroom in the <i>Leccinum</i> genus</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>оси́на</i> (osína, “aspen”) +&lrm; <i>-овик</i> (-ovik). Compare Ingrian <i>haapaopokka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send something (secretly)</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come/arrive/be in time <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(time)</i> to come, to arrive</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be mature/ripe</li></ol><p><i>подо-</i> (podo-) +&lrm; <i>спеть</i> (spetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infraspinate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ость</i> (ostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) subacute</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accountability (state of being accountable)</li></ol><p><i>подотчётный</i> (podotčótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accountable [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental)]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>отчёт</i> (otčót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die, to fall (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to peg out, to kick the bucket, to croak</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>до́хнуть</i> (dóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) income</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дохо́д</i> (doxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole (bottom of the foot or of the shoe)</li><li>foot <i>(of the mountain)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fall (under), come (under) (to become dependent on or influenced by) [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative)]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to fall (under), come (under) (to be under the jurisdiction of a law) [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>подпа́д-</i> (podpád-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harm somewhat</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>па́костить</i> (pákostitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burn mark</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пал-</i> (pal-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fall (under), come (under) (to become dependent on or influenced by) [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative)]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to fall (under), come (under) (to be under the jurisdiction of a law) [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative)]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>па́сть</i> (pástʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(choir)</i> supporting voice, harmonizing voice</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) yes-man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing the supporting part <i>(in a duet or choir)</i>, to sing backup</li><li>to sing along <i>(with)</i>, to harmonize <i>(with)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to echo</li></ol><p><i>подпе́ть</i> (podpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ) or <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prop up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing backing/supporting vocals</li><li>to sing along</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten by sawing</li><li>to saw at the base of</li></ol><p><i>подпили́ть</i> (podpilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten by sawing</li><li>to saw at the base of</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) US sympathizer</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пиндо́с</i> (pindós) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prop up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signing, subscription</li></ol><p><i>подписа́ть</i> (podpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>signed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign (one's own name)</li><li>to add to</li><li>to subscribe, to take out a subscription for someone</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ). Possibly a calque of Latin <i>subscrībere</i>. Compare German <i>unterschreiben</i>, English <i>underwrite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign, to put one's name on</li><li>to subscribe, to take out a subscription</li><li>to follow on social media</li></ol><p><i>подписа́ть</i> (podpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subscription</li><li><i>(charity)</i> collection</li><li>engagement, written undertaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subscription</li><li>by subscription</li><li>signed</li></ol><p><i>подписа́ть</i> (podpisátʹ, “to subscribe, to sign”) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subscriber, (<i>Internet</i>) follower</li></ol><p>From <i>подпи́ска</i> (podpíska, “subscription”) + <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign</li><li>to add to</li><li>to subscribe, to take out a subscription for someone</li></ol><p><i>подписа́ть</i> (podpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign, to put one's name on</li><li>to subscribe, to take out a subscription</li><li>to follow on social media</li></ol><p><i>подпи́сывать</i> (podpísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signature</li><li>caption, inscription</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supplement (<i>with substances or materials necessary for functioning</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to feed, to fuel, to supply additional support to</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intoxication</li></ol><p><i>подпи́ть</i> (podpítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplementation, recharge, replenishment</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) feed</li></ol><p><i>подпита́ть</i> (podpitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supplement (<i>with substances or materials necessary for functioning</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to feed, to fuel, to supply additional support to</li></ol><p><i>подпита́ть</i> (podpitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink (a certain amount of alcohol), to get somewhat drunk</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim or sail up to</li></ol><p><i>подплы́ть</i> (podplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim or sail up to</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cellar, basement</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пол</i> (pol)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl, to creep [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to crawl, to creep [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘up to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подползти́</i> (podpolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl, to creep [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to crawl, to creep [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘up to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) lieutenant colonel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lieutenant colonel</li></ol><p><i>подполко́вник</i> (podpolkóvnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) underground</li></ol><p><i>подпо́лье</i> (podpólʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground worker, fighter, etc. (someone engaged in clandestine activities)</li><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) closeted gay person</li></ol><p><i>подпо́лье</i> (podpólʹje) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prop, support</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) + a deverbal noun derived from <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ), with typical Indo-European nominal <i>o</i>-ablaut.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>подпо́ра</i> (podpóra): prop, support</li></ol><p><i>подпо́ра</i> (podpóra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spoil somewhat, to ruin somewhat</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird; to belt</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>по́яс</i> (pójas) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird; to belt</li></ol><p><i>подпоя́сать</i> (podpojásatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct</li><li><i>(clothes)</i> to straighten out</li><li>to touch up, to retouch</li><li><i>(beard, moustache)</i> to trim</li><li>to improve</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct</li><li><i>(clothes)</i> to straighten out</li><li>to touch up, to retouch</li><li><i>(beard, moustache)</i> to trim</li><li>to improve</li></ol><p><i>подпра́вить</i> (podprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer science</i>) subroutine, subprogram / subprogramme</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-, “under, sub-”) +&lrm; <i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma, “program”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subprocess</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “sub-”) +&lrm; <i>процесс</i> (process).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump up; to bounce up and down</li></ol><p><i>подпры́гнуть</i> (podprýgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subparagraph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow to approach [to come near]</li><li>to add some, to add a note <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow to approach [to come near]</li><li>to add some, to add a note <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make some money on the side</li><li>to work up</li></ol><p><i>подрабо́тать</i> (podrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make some money on the side</li><li>to work up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake, to tremble</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дро́гнуть</i> (drógnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitation (act of imitating)</li></ol><p><i>подража́ть</i> (podražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impersonator</li></ol><p><i>подражать</i> (podražatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imitate, to copy, to ape [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Probably literally, “to go the same path that someone has gone”, or more loosely translated as “to follow in someone's footsteps”, from Old Church Slavonic <i>дра́га</i> (drága, “road, path, way”) (see <i>доро́га</i> (doróga)). Over time, the meaning shifted to "to reproduce something or someone's actions".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsection</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>разде́л</i> (razdél)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) subdivision, sub-unit <i>(refers to subordinate units such as the battalions, companies or platoons of a rifle regiment, or the battalions or batteries of an artillery regiment, etc.)</i></li><li>division, subdivision, unit, department</li></ol><p><i>подраздели́ть</i> (podrazdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subdivide <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subdivide <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>подраздели́ть</i> (podrazdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subdivide <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>подразделя́ть</i> (podrazdeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease, to pester</li><li>to whet (appetite), to stimulate (curiosity)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дразни́ть</i> (draznítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imply, to mean, to have in mind</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>разуме́ть</i> (razumétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be implied/meant</li></ol><p><i>подразумева́ть</i> (podrazumevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up</li></ol><p><i>подрасти́</i> (podrastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to rip up, to tear up</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to wear out (shoes, etc.)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to pull</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to punish by pulling (hair, ears)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to run away</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to devour (<i>of animals</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to scuffle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дра́ться</i> (drátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to clip, to trim, to prune, to lop</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add, to cut some more</li><li>(<i>tennis</i>) to undercut, to chop, to slice <i>(the ball)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(car)</i> to cut in front <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>подре́зать</i> (podrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>подреза́ть</i> (podrezátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>подре́зать</i> (podrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;automotive) to have a drift, to go for a drift, to drift for a while (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дри́фтить</i> (dríftitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;automotive) to have a drift, to go for a drift, to drift for a while (in a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дрифтова́ть</i> (driftovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>подро́бный</i> (podróbnyj): more detailed</li><li>comparative degree of <i>подро́бно</i> (podróbno): at greater length, in greater detail, more minutely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in detail</li></ol><p><i>подро́бный</i> (podróbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detail</li><li>particularity</li></ol><p><i>подро́бный</i> (podróbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p><p>For the relation with <i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ), compare typologically <i>дета́ль</i> (detálʹ) (<&lt; Latin <i>de-</i> + <i>tāliō</i> (“to cut”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detailed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) subgenus</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>под-</i> (pod-, “sub-”) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod, “genus”). Cognate with Czech <i>podrod</i>, Polish <i>podrodzaj</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adolescent, teenage</li></ol><p>From <i>подро́сток</i> (podróstok) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) (ending-stressed) or <i>подро́сток</i> (podróstok) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teenager, juvenile, youth, adolescent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) to jerk off, to wank, to beat off (to masturbate)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) to tease, to humiliate, to punish; to train to exhaustion</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дрочи́ть</i> (dročítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girlfriend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make friends with, to establish a friendly relationship with</li></ol><p><i>подружи́ть</i> (podružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>подру́га</i> (podrúga)</li></ol><p><i>подру́га</i> (podrúga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>located under the sleeve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lightly fry, cook until brown</li><li>to put colour/color into</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elbow rest, hand rest, hand tool rest, tool rest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at hand, improvised, makeshift</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistant, mate, apprentice, helper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) blowing up, causing to explode</li><li>(the act of) undermining (of influence, government, domination)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подрыва́ть</i> (podryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow up, to blast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to undermine, to sap</li><li>to undermine, to sap</li></ol><p><i>под</i> (pod) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>подры́ть</i> (podrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to explode, to blow up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be damaged, to deteriorate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) to suddenly start (doing something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) to be determined (to do something)</li><li>to tunnel [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to undermine [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подрыва́ть</i> (podryvátʹ, “to blow up, to explode”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>подрыва́ть</i> (podryvátʹ, “to undermine”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blasting, demolition, blowing up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) subversive</li></ol><p><i>подры́в</i> (podrýv) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undermine, to sap</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tunnel [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to undermine [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подры́ть</i> (podrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>straight, in a row, in succession, running</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contract, outsourcing (transfer business)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>под</i> (pod) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) contract, outsourcing</li></ol><p><i>подря́д</i> (podrjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contractor</li></ol><p><i>подря́д</i> (podrjád) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>realia</i>) A monastic robe worn by Russian Orthodox nuns.</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ря́са</i> (rjása) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘next to someone else’</small>]</li><li>to plant additionally, to make an additional planting of (something)</li><li>to help [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘up into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘up onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘next to someone else’</small>]</li><li>to plant additionally, to make an additional planting of (something)</li><li>to help [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘up into something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘up onto something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подсади́ть</i> (podsadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a seat, to sit down [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘next to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get hooked on, to get addicted to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something, e.g. a drug’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ться</i> (sadítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sanctioned, under sanction</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>са́нкция</i> (sánkcija) +&lrm; <i>-онный</i> (-onnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>підсанкційний</i> (pidsankcijnyj), Belarusian <i>падсанкцы́йны</i> (padsankcýjny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illuminate, to highlight (<i>from below or from the side</i>)</li><li>to light slightly or additionally</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, lighting, illumination, booster light, accent light, micro-light</li><li>backlight</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) highlight</li></ol><p><i>подсвети́ть</i> (podsvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illuminate, to highlight (<i>from below or from the side</i>)</li><li>to light slightly or additionally</li></ol><p><i>подсвети́ть</i> (podsvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candlestick, sconce</li></ol><p><i>подсве́ч(ивать)</i> (podsvéč(ivatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piglet aged between four and ten months, gilt</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>свин-</i> (svin-) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) subfamily</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-, “under, sub-”) +&lrm; <i>семе́йство</i> (seméjstvo, “family”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a seat, to sit down [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘next to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get hooked on, to get addicted to [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something, e.g. a drug’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsystem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell (to help by telling, often discreetly), to prompt <i>(to)</i>, to suggest <i>(to)</i></li><li>to kibitz</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hint, tip</li><li>prompt, reminder</li><li>clue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prompter (someone who prompts)</li></ol><p><i>подсказа́ть</i> (podskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell (to help by telling, often discreetly), to prompt <i>(to)</i>, to suggest <i>(to)</i></li><li>to kibitz</li></ol><p><i>подсказа́ть</i> (podskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump up</li><li>to skyrocket (e.g. of prices)</li><li>to come running</li></ol><p>From <i>подскок-</i> (podskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ). подскок- is the underlying stem of perfective verb <i>подскочи́ть</i> (podskočítʹ), whose surface stem is affected by iotation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>поска́льзываться</i> (poskálʹzyvatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>подскользну́ться</i> (podskolʹznútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>поскользну́ться</i> (poskolʹznútʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump up</li><li>to skyrocket (e.g. of prices)</li><li>to come running</li></ol><p><i>под</i> (pod) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetener</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweeten, to sugar</li></ol><p><i>подсласти́ть</i> (podslastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a person) suspect, accused, allegedly guilty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a little half blind</li></ol><p>From a rethought word structure <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-слепова́тый</i> (-slepovátyj), where <i>под-</i> (pod-) is loosely attached. Further from a forgotten <i>подслéпый-</i> (podslépyj-) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj).</p><p>According to the restriction on the compatibility of morphemes within a word form: the dominant basic component cannot be combined with the dominant suffix, which requires stress to be placed to the right of the basic component.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eavesdrop, to overhear, to bug, to intercept</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eavesdropping (listening to private conversation)</li></ol><p><i>подслу́шивать</i> (podslúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eavesdrop, to overhear, to bug, to intercept</li></ol><p><i>подслу́шать</i> (podslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peep, to spy, to pry</li></ol><p><i>подсмотре́ть</i> (podsmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peep, to spy, to pry</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) snowdrop</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a corpse found when snow has thawed off of it</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding&#32;slang</i>) someone who goes to the gym only around April–May in order to looksmaxx but for summer; (<i>by extension</i>) anyone who does not follow a consistent fitness routine</li><li>(<i>business&#32;slang</i>) a company which has been registered but subsequently goes black and cannot be reached</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) storeroom, utility room</li></ol><p><i>подсо́б(ный)</i> (podsób(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ancillary (subordinate, secondary, auxiliary, accessory)</li><li>peripheral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(under)</i>, to shove <i>(under)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to palm off <i>(on / upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-со́вывать</i> (-sóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attaching, connecting, porting</li></ol><p><i>подсоединя́ть</i> (podsojedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subconsciousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>subliminally, subconsciously (in a manner that lacks conscious awareness)</li></ol><p><i>подсозна́тельный</i> (podsoznátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subconscious</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>созна́тельный</i> (soznátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunflower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sunflower</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sunflower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to dry up (<i>somewhat</i>)</li><li>to dry out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become thin, to wither up (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a bit of) help, support</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) latent, hidden, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>под спу́дом</i> (pod spúdom, “under a vessel, bucket (for grains)”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Matthew 5:15.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setup (to involve an unaware and unwilling person into some illegal or undesirable situation)</li><li>practical joke</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) support</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) the replacement set of horses for a carriage</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подста́вить</i> (podstávitʹ). Compare Polish <i>podstawa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to place [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘near something’</small>]</li><li>to hold up to, to offer to, to make accessible to (by bringing or turning something)</li><li>to substitute</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to expose, to leave vulnerable</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;criminology) to frame, to conspire to incriminate falsely a presumably innocent person</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “sub-, under”) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ, “to place”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, rest (object designed to be used to support something else)</li></ol><p><i>подста́вить</i> (podstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to place [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘near something’</small>]</li><li>to hold up to, to offer to, to make accessible to (by bringing or turning something)</li><li>to substitute</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to expose, to leave vulnerable</li></ol><p><i>подста́вить</i> (podstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ, “(<i>imperfectivizing suffix</i>)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>placed under</li><li>placed near</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) false</li></ol><p><i>подста́вить</i> (podstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>podstakannik, tea glass holder</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стака́н</i> (stakán) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substation</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hem a quilted lining under (something)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fasten, to clip (<i>something to something, usually from below or from the side</i>)</li><li>to whip on, to drive forward by whipping</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to spur, to motivate to action</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to intensify; to whet (one's appetite)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стегну́ть</i> (stegnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be on the watch <i>(for)</i>, to lie in wait <i>(for)</i>, to waylay, to catch</li></ol><p><i>подстере́г-</i> (podsterég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be on the watch <i>(for)</i>, to lie in wait <i>(for)</i>, to waylay, to catch</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стере́чь</i> (steréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underlay, spreading</li><li><i>(for cattle)</i> litter</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;animate) <i>(of a woman)</i> broad, whore</li></ol><p><i>подстила́ть</i> (podstilátʹ, “to underlay, to lay under”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build <i>(to)</i>, to add <i>(to)</i></li><li>to tune <i>(up)</i></li><li>to bring about by secret plotting, to contrive, to plot, to cook up</li></ol><p><i>подстро́ить</i> (podstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt <i>(to)</i>, to adjust <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>подстра́ивать</i> (podstráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subpage</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-, “sub-”) +&lrm; <i>страни́ца</i> (straníca, “page”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure; to insure; to stand by, to spot (in sports)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure; to insure; to stand by, to spot (in sports)</li></ol><p><i>подстрахова́ть</i> (podstraxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prompting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instigator (a person who provokes something)</li></ol><p><i>подстрека́ть</i> (podstrekátʹ, “to instigate”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incitement, instigation, subornation, setting on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite, to instigate, to set on, to abet</li><li>to stir up, to provoke, to excite</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strěkati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>подстрекну́ть</i> (podstreknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p><p>Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>стрѣкати</i> (strěkati, “to pierce”), Belarusian <i>стрiка́ць</i> (strikácʹ), Ukrainian <i>стрiка́ти</i> (strikáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite, to instigate, to set on, to abet</li><li>to stir up, to provoke, to excite</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стрекну́ть</i> (streknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to wound, to wing <i>(a bird)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-стрели́ть</i> (-strelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to clip, to trim, to prune, to lop</li></ol><p><i>подстри́г-</i> (podstríg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/have a haircut, to get/have one's hair cut</li></ol><p><i>подстрига́ть</i> (podstrigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to clip, to trim, to prune, to lop</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get/have a haircut, to get/have one's hair cut</li></ol><p><i>подстри́чь</i> (podstríčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build <i>(to)</i>, to add <i>(to)</i></li><li>to tune <i>(up)</i></li><li>to bring about by secret plotting, to contrive, to plot, to cook up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt <i>(to)</i>, to adjust <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>подстро́ить</i> (podstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under-the-line</li><li>subscript</li><li>bottom-of-the-page</li><li>verbatim, word-by-word</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>строка́</i> (stroká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approach (a way, passage, or avenue by which a place or buildings can be approached&#59; an access)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, to come up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to come <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>подступи́ть</i> (podstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) or <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come <i>(near)</i>, to approach, to get access <i>(to)</i>, to find a way of access <i>(to)</i></li><li>to approach, to set <i>(about)</i>, to find a way to deal/cope <i>(with)</i></li><li>to afford</li></ol><p><i>подступа́ть</i> (podstupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, to come up <i>(to)</i></li><li>to come <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come <i>(near)</i>, to approach, to get access <i>(to)</i>, to find a way of access <i>(to)</i></li><li>to approach, to set <i>(about)</i>, to find a way to deal/cope <i>(with)</i></li><li>to afford</li></ol><p><i>подступи́ть</i> (podstupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fasten, to clip (<i>something to something, usually from below or from the side</i>)</li><li>to whip on, to drive forward by whipping</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to spur, to motivate to action</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to intensify; to whet (one's appetite)</li><li>to hem a quilted lining under (something)</li></ol><p><i>подстегну́ть</i> (podstegnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>подстега́ть</i> (podstegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the accused, defendant, prisoner at the bar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognizance, jurisdiction, suability</li></ol><p><i>подсу́дный</i> (podsúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) subject to trial</li><li>(<i>law</i>) having jurisdiction</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) on trial, being tried in a court</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>суд</i> (sud) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(under)</i>, to shove <i>(under)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to palm off <i>(on / upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slight drying</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>су́шка</i> (súška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, to compute, to calculate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, to compute, to calculate</li></ol><p><i>подсчита́ть</i> (podsčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation, count</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send something (secretly)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour a bit more of</li><li>to pour stealthily (<i>e.g. poison</i>)</li><li>to increase the height of (something) by pouring</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ). Compare Czech <i>podsypat</i>, Kashubian <i>pòdsëpac</i>, Polish <i>podsypać</i>, and Silesian <i>podsypać</i>.</p><p><i>подсы́пать</i> (podsýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to dry up (<i>somewhat</i>)</li><li>to dry out</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become thin, to wither up (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-сыха́ть</i> (-syxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nudge, to push slightly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to encourage, to urge on</li></ol><p><i>подтолкну́ть</i> (podtolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drag (<i>somewhere</i>) in stages</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag somewhere</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drag somewhere in stages</li></ol><p>From <i>подтаск-</i> (podtask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or from <i>подтаска́ть</i> (podtaskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceptively shuffle (<i>cards</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) to manipulate/distort (<i>facts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тасова́ть</i> (tasovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceptive shuffling (<i>of cards</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) manipulation/distortion (<i>of facts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>подтасова́ть</i> (podtasovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceptively shuffle (<i>cards</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) to manipulate/distort (<i>facts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>подтасова́ть</i> (podtasovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slight nausea, queasiness</li></ol><p><i>подта́шнивать</i> (podtášnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to make slightly nauseated/nauseous, to make queasy</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тошни́ть</i> (tošnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confirm, to corroborate, to bear out, to endorse, to ratify, to verify</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove true, to be confirmed</li></ol><p><i>подтверди́ть</i> (podtverdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confirm, to corroborate, to bear out, to endorse, to ratify, to verify</li></ol><p><i>подтверди́ть</i> (podtverdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove true, to be confirmed</li></ol><p><i>подтвержда́ть</i> (podtverždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confirmation</li><li>corroboration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to leak</li></ol><p><i>подте́к-</i> (podték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subtext, implication, underlying theme/sense/idea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>wipe oneself</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow [<i>with</i> <b>под</b> (pod, + accusative) <small>‘under something’</small>]</li><li>to leak</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subtype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nudge, to push slightly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to encourage, to urge on</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poke fun (at) (<i>without harm intended</i>), to josh (with), to tease jokingly [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подтруни́ть</i> (podtrunítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poke fun (at) (<i>without harm intended</i>), to josh (with), to tease jokingly [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>труни́ть</i> (trunítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to blunt slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tightening <i>(of nuts, bolts, etc)</i></li><li>(<i>sports</i>) chin-up, pull-up</li></ol><p><i>подтя́гивать</i> (podtjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull (<i>to</i>), to pull up (<i>to</i>), to haul up (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to bring up, to move closer up (of troops, etc.)</li><li>to tighten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tuck in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to help catch up with the rest</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to join in singing, to join in a song, to sing along, to harmonize</li></ol><p><i>подтяг-</i> (podtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten one's belt</li><li>to pull oneself up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to improve one's standard, to catch up with the rest</li><li>to brace oneself up, to pull oneself together</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to move up, to move in</li></ol><p><i>подтя́гивать</i> (podtjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspender (for holding up clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) facelift</li></ol><p><i>подтяг-</i> (podtjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); compare imperfective <i>подтя́гивать</i> (podtjágivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspenders, braces, galluses (for trousers)</li></ol><p>Compare typologically French <i>soutien</i>, <i>soutien-gorge</i>, whence Bulgarian <i>сутиен</i> (sutien) (from Latin <i>sustineō</i>, distantly cognate via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i>, also note the correspondence of prefixes Latin <i>sub-</i>, Russian <i>под-</i> (pod-)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, sound, toned up, neat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull (<i>to</i>), to pull up (<i>to</i>), to haul up (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to bring up, to move closer up (of troops, etc.)</li><li>to tighten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tuck in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull up (improve something to a better standard)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to help catch up with the rest</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to join in singing, to join in a song, to sing along, to harmonize</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten one's belt</li><li>to pull oneself up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to improve one's standard, to catch up with the rest</li><li>to brace oneself up, to pull oneself together</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to move up, to move in</li></ol><p><i>подтяну́ть</i> (podtjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bruise</li><li>streak</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подте́чь</i> (podtéčʹ), with underlying stem подте́к-; compare imperfective <i>подтека́ть</i> (podtekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think (for a while), to have a think, to consider, to reflect, to ponder</li><li>to arrive at a thought, to conclude, to figure</li><li>to decide, to think (to do something), to arrive at an idea (with a verb in the infinitive)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to occur, to have a thought</li></ol><p>Either <i>поду́мать</i> (podúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) or <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ду́матся</i> (dúmatsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think about, to ponder, to contemplate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ду́рно</i> (dúrno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ду́рно</i> (dúrno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired slightly</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>уста́ть</i> (ustátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be drafty</li><li>to go quickly, to scamper</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to start blowing</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>поду́шка</i> (podúška)</li><li><i>(candy or drop)</i> cushion</li><li>pad <i>(of an animal foot)</i></li><li>ball (<i>of a finger or toe</i>)</li></ol><p><i>поду́шка</i> (podúška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pillow, cushion</li></ol><p><i>поду́шка</i> (podúška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow, cushion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airbag (protective system in automobiles)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) safety cushion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;informal,&#32;vulgar) whoopee cushion</li></ol><p><i>поду́шка</i> (podúška, “cushion”) +&lrm; <i>перде́ть</i> (perdétʹ, “to fart noisily”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulator, toady, lickspittle, bootlicker, sycophant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lickspittle; to fawn [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘over someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Related to <i>подхали́м</i> (podxalím).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subservience</li><li>adulation (flattery&#59; fulsome praise)</li></ol><p><i>подхали́м</i> (podxalím) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) picking up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) catching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joining in</li><li>log supported lengthwise in an adit in a mine</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>подхвати́ть</i> (podxvatítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (up), to pick up, to grasp, to snatch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch, to pick up (a disease)</li><li>to pick up (an expression, a song)</li><li>to join in, to take up, to catch up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch (up), to pick up, to grasp, to snatch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch, to pick up (a disease)</li><li>to pick up (an expression, a song)</li><li>to join in, to take up, to catch up</li></ol><p><i>подхвати́ть</i> (podxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approach</li><li>point of view</li><li>set <i>(a certain amount of repetitions of a physical exercise)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit (of an object, to match another object)</li><li>to come up (to), to approach, to go up (to)</li><li>to approach</li><li>to do (for); to suit [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to arrive, to come</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable, fitting, fit, appropriate</li><li>convenient</li></ol><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>conveniēns</i> (p.a.p. of <i>conveniō</i> &lt; <i>veniō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hook on, to pick up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pick up, to get</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hook on, to pick up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pick <i>(up)</i>, to get</li></ol><p><i>подцепи́ть</i> (podcepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at times, sometimes</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>под</i> (pod) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas).</p><p>Compare Belarusian <i>падча́с</i> (padčás), Polish <i>podczas</i>, and Ukrainian <i>під ча́с</i> (pid čás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underline, to underscore</li><li>to stress, to emphasize, to underscore</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>черкну́ть</i> (čerknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoop up (to grab by scooping), to scoop a little</li><li>to pick up (information), to learn</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>черпну́ть</i> (čerpnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoop up (to grab by scooping), to scoop a little</li><li>to pick up (information), to learn</li></ol><p><i>подчерпну́ть</i> (podčerpnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>submission, subjecting</li><li>subjection</li><li>subordination</li></ol><p><i>подчини́ть</i> (podčinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subordinate <i>(to)</i>, to place (under), to place under the command</li><li>to subdue</li><li>to dedicate</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to subordinate</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey, to submit to, to surrender, to be subordinate to</li></ol><p><i>подчини́ть</i> (podčinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subordinate <i>(to)</i>, to place (under), to place under the command</li><li>to subdue</li><li>to dedicate</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to subordinate</li></ol><p><i>подчини́ть</i> (podčinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey, to submit to, to surrender, to be subordinate to</li></ol><p><i>подчиня́ть</i> (podčinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subordination</li></ol><p><i>подчинённый</i> (podčinjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subordinate (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>subordinate (<i>of a role, position, nature, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subordinate, subject, slave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tidy up</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wholly, entirely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tidy up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) underline, emphasis</li><li>underline (text character)</li></ol><p><i>подчёркивать</i> (podčórkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underline, to underscore</li><li>to stress, to emphasize, to underscore</li></ol><p><i>подчеркну́ть</i> (podčerknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exaggeratedly, pronouncedly</li></ol><p><i>подчёркнутый</i> (podčórknutyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exaggerated (<i>courtesy, dignity, etc.</i>)</li><li>outlined vividly (<i>of eyes, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hem</li><li>to sew on</li><li>to sew a sole on, to sole (a shoe)</li><li>to file (documents, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>подши́ть</i> (podšítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing on</li><li>hemming</li><li>soling (<i>of a shoe</i>)</li><li>filing (<i>of documents</i>)</li><li>hem, lining</li><li>set of filed documents</li></ol><p><i>подшива́ть</i> (podšivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearing</li></ol><p>Att. since 1730s, <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>шип</i> (šip) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hem</li><li>to sew on</li><li>to sew a sole on, to sole (a shoe)</li><li>to file (documents, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slightly drunk, tipsy</li></ol><p>From French <i>chauffer</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>calefaciō</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>горячи́тельный напи́ток</i> (gorjačítelʹnyj napítok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;plural only) long underpants, long johns (<i>worn either under normal clothing in cold weather, or as a night dress</i>)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>штаны́</i> (štaný) +&lrm; <i>-ники</i> (-niki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh at, to mock, to make fun of</li><li>to play a joke on</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>шути́ть</i> (šutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh at, to mock, to make fun of</li><li>to play a joke on</li></ol><p><i>подшути́ть</i> (podšutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>под</i> (pod).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo) to gibe <i>(at)</i>, to taunt, to make fun of, to fuck <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo) to gibe <i>(at)</i>, to taunt, to make fun of, to fuck <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>подъеба́ть</i> (podʺjebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну</i> (-nu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section of multiapartment building with common entrance</li><li>public stairway in multiapartment building</li><li>(rare,&#32;obsolete) access by a vehicle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>подъѣздъ</i> (podŷjězdŭ). Action noun from <i>подъе́хать</i> (podʺjéxatʹ, “to approach”), from <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>під'їзд</i> (pidʺjizd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) approach</li></ol><p><i>подъе́зд</i> (podʺjézd) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive up (to)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in, to pay a short visit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get (round), to start a pick up line, to get on the right side (of)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive up (to)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drop in, to pay a short visit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get (round), to start a pick up line, to get on the right side (of)</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underskirt, slip</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ю́бка</i> (júbka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to lift, to raise, to pick up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>подъяти</i> (podŷjati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;podъjęti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) joke, one-liner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) joke, one-liner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) joker</li></ol><p><i>подъёб</i> (podʺjób) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo) to gibe <i>(at)</i>, to taunt, to make fun of, to fuck <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>подъеба́ть</i> (podʺjebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lifting, raise, raising</li><li>hoisting <i>(of a flag)</i></li><li>salvaging <i>(of a sunken ship)</i></li><li>ascent, rise, upgrade</li><li>climb <i>(of a plane)</i></li><li>slope, rise <i>(of a mountain)</i></li><li>raising, development, upsurge</li><li>enthusiasm, animation</li><li>instep <i>(of a foot)</i></li><li>reveille</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) height (of vowels)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>поднима́ть</i> (podnimátʹ). The combining form -ём is the normal deverbal equivalent of <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)/<i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ); compare archaic first singular present tense <i>емлю́</i> (jemljú) of <i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) lift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lift, elevator</li></ol><p><i>подъём</i> (podʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lifting</li></ol><p><i>подъём</i> (podʺjóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>подъе́зд</i> (podʺjézd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to accompany [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘on an instrument’</small>]</li><li>(theater,&#32;sports,&#32;card games) to assist, to help (someone) play [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to play along with (to help (someone) by assisting or going along with their actions) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ). Compare Polish <i>podegrać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) to accompany [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘on an instrument’</small>]</li><li>(theater,&#32;sports,&#32;card games) to assist, to help (someone) play [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to play along with (to help (someone) by assisting or going along with their actions) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>подыгра́ть</i> (podygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>поднима́ть</i> (podnimátʹ, “to raise, to elevate, to increase”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rise</li></ol><p><i>подыма́ть</i> (podymátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek out, to find, to try to find</li></ol><p><i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek out, to find, to try to find</li></ol><p><i>подыска́ть</i> (podyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sum up</li></ol><p><i>подыто́жить</i> (podytóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sum up</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ито́жить</i> (itóžitʹ) or from <i>под-</i> (pod-) +&lrm; <i>ито́г</i> (itóg) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die, to croak, to kick the bucket</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-дыха́ть</i> (-dyxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дыша́ть</i> (dyšátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) podyachy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dayworker (an employee paid on an irregular basis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull at (<i>multiple times</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twitch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;impersonal</i>) to cover, to coat (<i>with a thin layer</i>)</li><li>to envelop</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck for a while</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) (someone) doesn't give a fuck</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Compare Polish <i>pojebać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) thing, object</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нь</i> (-énʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) crap, shit (something worthless, unpleasant or hard to understand)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ебо́та</i> (jebóta) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), ultimately from еба́ть (jebátʹ, “to fuck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck for a while</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating up</li></ol><p><i>поеда́ть</i> (pojedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat up</li><li>to eat through</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-е́д-</i> (-éd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duel, rencounter</li><li>combat, fight, battle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dispute, quarrel, argument</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pojedynek</i> (“duel, rencounter”), from <i>po-</i> + <i>jeden</i> (“one”) + <i>-ek</i>. Cognate with <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj), <i>оди́н</i> (odín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) train, a line of connected railway cars</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) caravan, convoy, procession</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) a method of fishing, in which a fishing net is pulled by two side-by-side boats</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) a net used for this method of fishing</li></ol><p>According to Dal, from Russian <i>пое́здка</i> (pojézdka, “trip, journey”) and originally meaning a series of wagons following each other, a meaning preserved in <i>сва́дебный по́езд</i> (svádebnyj pójezd, “wedding train (procession)”). Cognates include Polish <i>pojazd</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go (by horse or vehicle), to ride, to drive</li><li>to come, to visit</li><li>to travel</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tour, journey, trip</li></ol><p><i>пое́здить</i> (pojézditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;rail transport) train, trolley, rail transport</li></ol><p><i>по́езд</i> (pójezd) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) because, inasmuch as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>поебсти́</i> (pojebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to have a bite (of something to eat), to try (eat)</li><li>to devour</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>поебсти́</i> (pojebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) crazy, mad, insane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Let's go!</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Let's do it!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go <i>(in a conveyance or on horseback)</i></li><li>to ride, to drive</li><li>to travel</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to go mad/insane, to lose one's mind</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be greedy</li></ol><p><i>жа́д(ный)</i> (žád(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>жа́дный</i> (žádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) sob story</li></ol><p><i>пожале́й</i> (požaléj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel sorry, to be sorry, to be sad (about something), to regret</li><li>to pity, to commiserate</li><li>to save, to spare (e.g. time, energy)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жале́ть</i> (žalétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grant (to); to bestow, to confer (on); to reward, to award (<i>stilted style</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to visit, to come to see (<i>stilted style or jocular</i>)</li><li>to favour/favor, to regard with favour/favor; to like</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жа́ловать</i> (žálovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain, to lament [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘(about) something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>maybe, perhaps</li><li>I suppose (hesitant agreement or acceptance after some thought)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>please</li><li>you're welcome, not at all, don't mention it, it's a pleasure (reply to thank you)</li><li>here you are (<i>when handing something over</i>)</li><li>please do (<i>when permitting a request</i>)</li></ol><p>Lexicalization of <i>пожа́луй</i> (požáluj) + <i>-ста</i> (-sta), an obsolete clitic that was used to add emphasis and to signify politeness, of obscure origin. The clitic is no longer used or known to most speakers, even as passive vocabulary, and has essentially disappeared from the language. Accordingly, to modern speakers, the ending of the word <i>пожалуйста</i> is merely a sequence of sounds of indeterminate meaning.</p><p>The letter <i>й</i>, despite still being used in the spelling, was dropped from the pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire, conflagration (occurrence of fire in a certain place)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;požarъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жар</i> (žar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where a fire occurred, burned area</li><li>augmentative of <i>пожа́р</i> (požár): large fire, conflagration</li></ol><p><i>пожа́р</i> (požár) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire brigade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>firefighting</i>) fire engine, fire truck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(low colloquial,&#32;firefighting) fireman, firefighter</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) one who has lost all in a fire</li></ol><p><i>пожа́р</i> (požár) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fire</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>firefighting</i>) fireman</li></ol><p><i>пожа́р</i> (požár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire hazard</li></ol><p><i>пожароопа́сный</i> (požaroopásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fire hazard; (fire) hazardous (hazardous due to the risk of fire)</li></ol><p><i>пожа́р</i> (požár) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>опа́сный</i> (opásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handshake</li><li>shrug</li></ol><p><i>пожа́ть</i> (požátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to press, to squeeze (now only used in set idioms below)</li><li>to reap, to crop</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew, to munch</li><li>to ruminate, to masticate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жева́ть</i> (ževátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish, desire (especially to someone else)</li><li>advice, recommendation, wish, demand, request</li></ol><p><i>пожела́ть</i> (poželátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish, to desire</li><li>to covet</li><li>to wish, to bear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘what’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жела́ть</i> (želátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become yellow</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>желте́ть</i> (želtétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act cutesy or mannered (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жема́ниться</i> (žemánitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry off (to force&#47;make a male relative (to) get married)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жени́ть</i> (ženítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry (as a couple).</li></ol><p><i>пожени́ть</i> (poženítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endowment, donation, contribution, dotation (a voluntary gift or contribution for a specific cause)</li></ol><p><i>поже́ртвовать</i> (požértvovatʹ, “to donate”) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to donate</li><li>to sacrifice, to give, to offer [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>же́ртвовать</i> (žértvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn up, to destroy by fire</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live <i>(in general)</i>, to get on, to get along</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жива́ть</i> (živátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to profit, to benefit at someone else's expense [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘from something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat, to avail oneself of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘some food’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>живи́ть</i> (živítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) life, for life, lifelong</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elderly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to press, to squeeze (now only used in set idioms below)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reap, to crop</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devourer</li></ol><p><i>пожира́ть</i> (požirátʹ, “to devour”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devour</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-жира́ть</i> (-žirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) belongings; things</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live for a while</li><li>to stay for a while</li><li>to have seen life</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) stubble field</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) meadow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пожьнѧ</i> (požĭnę), from <i>пожати</i> (požati, “to reap”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devour</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude) to eat, to grab</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жра́ть</i> (žrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold slightly (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>жури́ть</i> (žurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pose, posture, attitude</li><li>affectation, pretense</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pose</i>, from French <i>pose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>before last</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to divert, to entertain (to entertain or occupy in a pleasant manner)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>заба́вить</i> (zabávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care, to take care, to look after</li><li>to worry, to be anxious, to trouble</li></ol><p><i>позабо́тить</i> (pozabótitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to forget completely</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>забыва́ть</i> (zabyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to forget completely</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>забы́ть</i> (zabýtʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to envy, to be envious of [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>зави́довать</i> (zavídovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have breakfast, to breakfast, to lunch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>за́втракать</i> (závtrakatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>day before yesterday</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>вчера́</i> (včerá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to borrow</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>заи́мствовать</i> (zaímstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take possession of, to occupy (many things, one after the other)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to borrow [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘many things or from many people’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to entertain, to keep engaged (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>занима́ть</i> (zanimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study, to learn (<i>for a while</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a subject’</small>]</li><li>to be occupied (<i>for a while</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>to work out, to exercise</li></ol><p><i>позанима́ть</i> (pozanimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) three days ago</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>за</i> (za) +&lrm; <i>вчера́</i> (včerá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>before last</li></ol><p><i>поза-</i> (poza-) +&lrm; <i>про́шлый</i> (próšlyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) urgently, badly, desperately</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>заре́з</i> (zaréz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pozarikha</li></ol><p><i>Позари́ха</i> (Pozaríxa) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to ring periodically, to tinkle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to call (on the phone) periodically</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call</li><li>to ask, to invite</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permission, leave</li></ol><p><i>позво́лить</i> (pozvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow, to permit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to afford (often as <b>позволить себе</b>)</li><li>(imperative,&#32;as an introductory word) excuse (me), sorry, pardon (me) (used to express objection, disagreement or reminding of some fact)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pozwolić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allow, to permit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>позво́лить</i> (pozvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call (to contact by telephone) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to call [<i>with</i> locative adverb <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a location’</small>]</li><li>to cause to ring [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a bell, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ring (to make a ringing sound) (<i>a bell, a telephone, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertebra</li></ol><p>From <i>звено́</i> (zvenó, “link of a chain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) backbone, spinal column, vertebral column, spine (set of bones which connected make up the spine and spinal column)</li></ol><p><i>позвоно́к</i> (pozvonók) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertebrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vertebral</li></ol><p><i>позвоно́к</i> (pozvonók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clink intermittently, to jingle periodically</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>звя́кать</i> (zvjákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>по́здно</i> (pózdno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Late Latin</li></ol><p>From <i>по́здняя латы́нь</i> (pózdnjaja latýnʹ, “Late Latin”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>late Soviet</li></ol><p><i>поздне-</i> (pozdne-) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Late Middle Ages</li></ol><p>From <i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>пізньосередньові́чний</i> (piznʹoserednʹovíčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>late</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pozdьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>late</li></ol><p><i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rather late, belatedly</li></ol><p><i>позднова́тый</i> (pozdnovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rather late, belated</li></ol><p><i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij, “late”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Late Latin</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij, “late”) +&lrm; <i>латы́нь</i> (latýnʹ, “Latin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet, to salute, to say hello/hi</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>здоро́ваться</i> (zdoróvatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become healthier or stronger</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>здорове́ть</i> (zdorovétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) congratulation; congratulatory</li></ol><p><i>поздра́вить</i> (pozdrávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to congratulate, to felicitate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘for/about something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congratulation</li></ol><p><i>поздравля́ть</i> (pozdravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to congratulate, to felicitate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘for/about something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>здра́вый</i> (zdrávyj) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>поздоровля́ти</i> (pozdorovljáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>happy birthday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become green</li><li>to grow/turn green</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>зелене́ть</i> (zelenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>по́здний</i> (pózdnij): later, afterwards</li><li>comparative degree of <i>по́здно</i> (pózdno): later, afterwards</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pose, to posture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) positive</li><li>positive, positive information, feedback or mood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) positivism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>positivist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>positive (<i>usually in the sense "optimistic/high-spirited" or "favourable/favorable/beneficial"</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>positron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>positioning (measurement of a satellite's location, marketing a product in a certain niche, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>позициони́ровать</i> (pozicionírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to position</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively,&#32;with <i>себя́</i> (sebjá)) to position (oneself) (as a certain status)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>positionieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to position oneself [<i>with</i> <b>как</b> (kak) <small>‘as’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>позициони́ровать</i> (pozicionírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) position; positional</li></ol><p><i>пози́ция</i> (pozícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>position (usually in the sense "stance, attitude")</li><li>(<i>specialist</i>) item (of goods, equipment, a commodity etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(religious,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic,&#32;dated) to gild</li></ol><p>A borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>зла́то</i> (zláto) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to gild</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease a little</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>злить</i> (zlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cognitive</li><li>informative, educational</li></ol><p><i>познава́ть</i> (poznavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to cognize</li><li>to get to know, to learn</li><li>to experience, to get to know</li></ol><p><i>позна́ть</i> (poznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce, to acquaint, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>знако́мить</i> (znakómitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to make the acquaintance, to make someone’s acquaintance, to get acquainted</li><li>to familiarize oneself, to go into</li><li>to visit, to see</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>знако́миться</i> (znakómitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native or inhabitant of Poznań (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognition, perception</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) experience, knowledge</li></ol><p><i>позна́ть</i> (poznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Poznan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>poznański</i>. By surface analysis, <i>По́знань</i> (Póznanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to cognize</li><li>to get to know, to learn</li><li>to experience, to get to know</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poznati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gild</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>золоти́ть</i> (zolotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, disgrace, infamy, humiliation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pozorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disgrace, to defame</li></ol><p><i>позо́р</i> (pozór) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shame, disgrace</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sight, show, spectacle</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) theater</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>позорище</i> (pozorište). By surface analysis, <i>позо́р</i> (pozór) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče).</p><p>(<i>theater</i>): Compare typologically Irish <i>amharclann</i> (&lt; <i>amharc</i>), Ancient Greek <i>θέατρον</i> (théatron) (whence <i>теа́тр</i> (teátr)) (a verbal noun of <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shameless person</li></ol><p><i>позо́р</i> (pozór) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgraceful, shameful</li></ol><p><i>позо́р</i> (pozór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pillory</li></ol><p>Literally, “shameful pillar”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>позо́р</i> (pozór).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urge</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) call, appeal</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) + a deverbal combining form of <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ); compare <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>code name</li><li>call sign (broadcasting, military)</li><li>signature tune (TV, radio)</li></ol><p><i>позы́в</i> (pozýv) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call sign</li><li>signature <i>(tune)</i>, theme song</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) ship's number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn (from time to time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drifting snow, blowing snow</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) + <i>-зём-</i> (-zjóm-) (combining form of <i>земля́</i> (zemljá)) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poseur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacockery, swagger, foppery</li></ol><p>From <i>позёр</i> (pozjór, “poseur”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ): to play a little, to play sometimes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jeer, to scoff, to bully, to taunt (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>издева́ться</i> (izdevátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to show (interest, pity, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) to fuck over (to take advantage of materially or sexually)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capture, catch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be curious</li><li>to ask about something, to enquire</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>интересова́ть</i> (interesovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>search, quest</li><li>raid</li><li>Poisk (docking module of the International Space Station)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look for, to search (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) explosive ordnance disposal specialist</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) mining geologist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) search engine</li></ol><p>From <i>поиско́вый</i> (poiskóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) search, reconnaissance</li></ol><p>From <i>по́иск</i> (póisk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) (ending-stressed) or <i>по́иск</i> (póisk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) (stem-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(stilted,&#32;literary) truly, indeed, in truth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to water, to give to drink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поити</i> (poiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pojìti</i>. Derived from <i>&#42;pojь</i> (“drink”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>come on</li><li>let's go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nutritious drink for cattle, made with bran and flour</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) swill</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) any drink</li></ol><p><i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood plain, water meadow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to take</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) plus <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ, “to have”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flood plain, water meadow</li></ol><p><i>по́йма</i> (pójma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointer (type of dog)</li><li>(computing,&#32;colloquial) pointer (variable that stores the address of another variable), see указа́тель (ukazátelʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pointer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to walk</li><li>to start, to begin</li><li>to function, to work</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to back down, to back out, to backtrack, to backpedal, to reverse course, to fold, to compromise, to go back on one's word</li></ol><p>Literally, “to go onto the reverse (path, way etc.)”. Alteration of <i>пойти́ на попя́тный</i> (pojtí na popjátnyj). Overtook <i>пойти́ на попя́тный</i> (pojtí na popjátnyj) in usage in the second half of the 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;less common) alternative form of <i>пойти́ на попя́тную</i> (pojtí na popjátnuju)</li></ol><p><i>На попя́тный</i> (Na popjátnyj) is an ellipsis of <i>на попя́тный двор</i> (na popjátnyj dvor), which sees use from the 1820s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for the present, for the time being</li><li>for now (at the moment&#59; until later)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so long as, while</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) bye; so long; I’ll see you later; see you soon.</li></ol><p>Related to Ukrainian <i>поки</i> (poky), Belarusian <i>пакуль</i> (pakulʹ), Polish <i>póki</i>, from <i>по</i> (po) + <i>кꙑи</i> (kyi), in Russian with analogous formation after</p><p><i>дока</i> (doka), <i>пота</i> (pota) (Vasmer 1958).</p><p>(<i>Interjection</i>): Short of <i>прощай пока</i> (proščaj poka, “farewell so long”) or <i>пока до свидания</i> (poka do svidanija). Used since the early 20th century first as colloquial, thereafter as familiar. May be a calque from some other language, compare English <i>so long</i> (used since 19th century) and Swedish <i>hej så länge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>unless, until</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for the time being, in the meantime, as yet (up to the present)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>show, demonstration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testimony, evidence, statement</li><li>indication, reading, readout</li></ol><p><i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indicator, measure, index (sign, indication, token)</li><li>exponent, index</li><li>figure</li><li>rate, ratio</li><li>parameter, factor</li></ol><p><i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ, “to show, to point”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>index (mathematics&#58; raised suffix indicating a power)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>significant, revealing, indicative</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) demonstration</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) model</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) exponential</li></ol><p><i>показа́тель</i> (pokazátelʹ, “index, indicator, exponent”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>show trial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li><li>to display, to reveal, to achieve</li><li>to register, to read <i>(of measuring devices)</i></li><li>to point</li><li>to depose, to testify, to give evidence</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to teach someone a lesson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear, to come into view, to come in sight</li><li>to turn up, to show up</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem</li></ol><p><i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ostentatious, pretentious</li><li>flashy, garish, meretricious</li><li>specious</li><li>showy, ambitious</li><li>sporty</li><li>ostensible</li><li>affected</li><li>vain</li><li>tinsel, theatrical</li></ol><p><i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) window-dressing, ostentation, pretense, (fake) show, veneer, facade, façade</li></ol><p>From <i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ, “to show”) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa, pejorative noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li><li>to display, to reveal</li><li>to achieve <i>(some result)</i></li><li>to show, to register, to read</li><li>to point <i>(at)</i></li><li>to depose, to testify, to give evidence</li></ol><p><i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show oneself, to come in sight, to appear</li><li>to show up, to appear</li></ol><p><i>пока́зывать</i> (pokázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cripple, to maim, to mutilate</li><li>to pervert, to twist, to warp</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кале́чить</i> (kaléčitʹ, “to maim, cripple”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prick occasionally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) till, until</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>покамѣстъ</i> (pokaměst) (по ка мѣстъ att. since 1560s), from earlier <i>покамѣста</i> (pokaměsta) (по ка мѣста since 1509), presumably from Old Church Slavonic <i>по</i> (po) <i>каꙗ</i> (kaja) <i>мѣста</i> (města) (att. in Middle Russian since 1559), literally 'to which places' or 'until which places', which is a Slavonic analog of indigenous Middle Russian <i>по кои мѣста</i> (po koi města) attested since 1430s. The loss of final -а, according to Sobolevsky, is a regular phonological development throughout East Slavic.</p><p>The original spatial sense evolved into a temporal meaning ("until now", "for now", "presently") and later also developed a conjunctional function ("for the time being", "until"). The temporal meaning for Old East Slavic <i>мѣсто</i> (město) is attested already in the 14th c.</p><p>Dialectal variants with до- instead of по-, such as Russian <i>докамест</i> (dokamest), also exist (Middle Russian <i>до ка мѣста</i> (do ka města) att. in 1565). Also older <i>потамѣстъ</i> (potaměst), <i>посѧмѣстъ</i> (posjaměst). Compare <i>пока</i> (poka) and related phrases like по тѣхъ мѣстъ, до тѣхъ мѣстъ ('up to that time/place').</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) temporarily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punish, to chastise, to inflict penalty</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кара́ть</i> (karátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll for a while</li><li>to take for a drive</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go for a drive, to drive</li></ol><p><i>поката́ть</i> (pokatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, to push <i>(some object with wheels)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive <i>(quickly)</i></li><li><i>(a car etc.)</i> to bowl along</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to tear</li><li>to sweep, to move, to flow, to stream, to roll</li></ol><p><i>покати́ть</i> (pokatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) riding, rolling, going for a merry ride</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sloping, slanting, downhill</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>покат(и́ться)</i> (pokat(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to swing, to shake, to wobble; to dandle</li><li>to pump</li><li>to toss, to roll, to pitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lift up, to chair (to toss someone up, like a group of fans their champion)</li><li>to download (for some time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to train/pump muscle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rock slightly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rock (from time to time)</li></ol><p><i>пока́чивать</i> (pokáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake (slightly)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>качну́ть</i> (kačnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sway, to give a lurch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a turn for the worse</li></ol><p><i>покачну́ть</i> (pokačnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periodic coughing</li></ol><p><i>пока́шливать</i> (pokášlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough periodically</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ка́шл-</i> (kášl-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repentance, penitence</li><li>confession</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) + a form derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kàjati</i>. Compare <i>ка́яться</i> (kájatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>penitential</li></ol><p><i>покая́н(ие)</i> (pokaján(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repent (to feel sorrow or regret for what one has done or omitted to do)</li><li>to confess (to admit to the truth, particularly in the context of sins or crimes committed)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ка́яться</i> (kájatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get even</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pokémon <i>franchise</i></li><li>Pokémon <i>media</i> (i.e. manga, anime, video games, &c.)</li><li>A pocket monster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Pokémon</i>, ultimately from Japanese <i>ポケモン</i> (pokemon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) poker</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) four cards of a kind in a poker hand</li><li>(<i>soccer</i>) four goals scored by one player in a soccer match</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>poker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) poker</li></ol><p><i>по́кер</i> (póker) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poker,&#32;youth slang) poker face</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>poker face</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod a few times</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кива́ть</i> (kivátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to quit, to forsake</li><li>to abandon, to desert</li><li>to throw, to cast</li></ol><p><i>поки́д-</i> (pokíd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned (forsaken, deserted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to quit, to forsake</li><li>to abandon, to desert</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ, “to throw, to cast”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>almost exclusively in expressions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flexible, compliant, adaptable</li></ol><p>From <i>покла́д</i> (poklád, “deal”) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj), derived from <i>поклада́ться</i> (pokladátʹsja, “to agree”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) load</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) luggage, baggage, cargo</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) treasure</li><li>(dated,&#32;historical,&#32;law) contract for storage in prerevolutionary Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>клад-</i> (klad-) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;nonstandard,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;proscribed) to put, to lay, to lay down, to deposit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow (gesture made by bending forward at the waist)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) regards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поклонъ</i> (poklonŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poklonъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worship, adoration (an act of religious worship)</li><li>cult</li><li>idolatry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (to, before), to greet (to bend oneself as a gesture of respect or deference) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to give/send regards [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to cringe (before); to humiliate oneself (before); to humbly beg [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to worship</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admirer</li><li>fan, groupie</li><li>worshipper</li></ol><p><i>покло́н</i> (poklón) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) sycophancy, slavish worship</li></ol><p><i>покло́нник</i> (poklónnik, “admirer, worshipper”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worship [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>клоня́ть</i> (klonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from <i>покло́н</i> (poklón), perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to incline”); compare Russian <i>склон</i> (sklon), <i>склоне́ние</i> (sklonénije); per Vasmer, cognates include Danish <i>huld</i>, Old Norse <i>hallr</i>, Gothic <i>𐌷𐌿𐌻𐌸𐍃</i> (hulþs, “clement”), Latin <i>ausculto</i>, but see further <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vow, to swear (to make a vow)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кля́сться</i> (kljástʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;countable) slander, calumny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) living part of a house</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(on, upon)</i>, to repose <i>(on, upon)</i></li><li>to lie (<i>of the dead</i>)</li></ol><p><i>поко́ить</i> (pokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calm, rest, peace</li><li>quiet</li><li>old name of the letter <b>п</b> in the early Cyrillic alphabet</li><li>apartment, room, chamber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pokojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the deceased</li></ol><p>From <i>поко́й</i> (pokój) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>calmly, tranquilly</li></ol><p><i>поко́йный</i> (pokójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) calm, tranquil</li><li>late, deceased</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceased person</li></ol><p><i>поко́й</i> (pokój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to shake</li><li>to shake</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>колеба́ть</i> (kolebátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oscillate, to vibrate, to vacillate, to sway</li><li>to fluctuate, to vary</li><li>to hesitate, to waver</li></ol><p><i>поколеба́ть</i> (pokolebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation, age</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>коле́но</i> (koléno) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generational</li></ol><p><i>поколе́ние</i> (pokolénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat, to give a thrashing</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish off, to put an end to something, to do away with something, to be through with</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit suicide (to kill oneself)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig for a while</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to delve (into) for a while, to spend some time examining carefully</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ться</i> (kopátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conquest, subjugation</li></ol><p><i>покори́ть</i> (pokorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conqueror, subjugator</li></ol><p><i>покори́ть</i> (pokorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subdue, to conquer, to subjugate, to win (over)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pokoriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кори́ть</i> (korítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to submit (to), to resign oneself to</li></ol><p><i>покори́ть</i> (pokorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) most humbly (used in requests, expressions of thanks, apologies, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>поко́рнейший</i> (pokórnejšij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>поко́рный</i> (pokórnyj): most submissive, most obedient, most humble</li></ol><p><i>поко́рный</i> (pokórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>humbly, submissively, obediently</li></ol><p><i>поко́рный</i> (pokórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>submissiveness, obedience</li></ol><p><i>поко́рный</i> (pokórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>submissive <i>(to)</i>, obedient <i>(to)</i>, resigned</li></ol><p><i>покор-</i> (pokor-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to warp</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to ruin, to disfigure</li><li>to grate, to jar</li><li>(figurative,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to shock, to disgust</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>коро́бить</i> (koróbitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>make it snappy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;poetic) to submit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subdue, to conquer, to subjugate, to win (over)</li></ol><p><i>покори́ть</i> (pokorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to submit (to), to resign oneself to</li></ol><p><i>покоря́ть</i> (pokorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink to one side, to be lopsided</li><li>to squint, to look asquint, to look sideways, to cast a sidelong look</li></ol><p><i>покоси́ть</i> (pokosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p>Perhaps <i>покоси́ть</i> (pokosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mowing</li></ol><p><i>поко́с</i> (pokós) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink coffee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to colour/color, to paint, to dye, to stain</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(action of) painting</li><li>paint</li></ol><p><i>покра́сить</i> (pokrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reddening, redness</li><li>blushing</li></ol><p><i>покрасне́ть</i> (pokrasnétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redden, to grow red</li><li>to blush, to turn red in the face</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>красне́ть</i> (krasnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to strengthen, to grow stronger</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>крепча́ть</i> (krepčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>кре́пко</i> (krépko)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>кре́пкий</i> (krépkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to crook, to curve</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>криви́ть</i> (krivítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become crooked/bent/lopsided</li></ol><p><i>покриви́ть</i> (pokrivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hout <i>(at)</i>, to command around</li><li>to chide, to scold</li><li>to shriek, to yelp</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кри́кнуть</i> (kríknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry, to shout, to scream, to yell for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, covering</li><li>hearse cloth</li></ol><p>Ultimately deverbal of <i>покры́ть</i> (pokrýtʹ); compare <i>кров</i> (krov).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Покров)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pokrov (a city in Vladimir Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Pokrov (a city in Dnepropetrovsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>ellipsis of <i>Покро́в Пресвято́й Богоро́дицы</i> (Pokróv Presvjatój Bogoródicy, “Intercession of the Theotokos”)</li><li>Pokrov day, Intercession of the Theotokos (feast on 14 October)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patron, protector, sponsor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>покръвител҄ь</i> (pokrŭvitelʹĭ), from <i>покрꙑти</i> (pokryti, “to protect”) +&lrm; <i>-тел҄ь</i> (-telʹĭ, “-er”). By surface analysis, <i>покров-</i> (pokrov-) +&lrm; <i>-итель</i> (-itelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>patronisingly, condescendingly</li><li>protectively, overprotectively</li></ol><p><i>покрови́тельственный</i> (pokrovítelʹstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patronising, condescending</li><li>protective, overprotective</li></ol><p><i>покрови́тельство</i> (pokrovítelʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patronage, protection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to patronize (to provide patronage to); to sponsor; to protect [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pokrov (name of a city in Ukraine and several settlements in Russia, most notably in Vladimir Oblast)</li></ol><p><i>Покро́в</i> (Pokróv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut, style (of garments)</li><li>kind, breed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to turn, to rotate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play around <i>(with someone)</i>, to have an affair <i>(with someone)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedspread, bedcover (topmost covering of a bed)</li><li>veil</li></ol><p><i>покрыва́ть</i> (pokryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to drown out</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to mount (<i>an animal to mate</i>)</li><li>to extend (to possess a certain extent; to cover an amount of space)</li></ol><p><i>покры́ть</i> (pokrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover oneself, to get covered</li><li>to be covered <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>покрыва́ть</i> (pokryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, coating</li><li>coat, surface</li><li>discharge, payment</li><li>coverage <i>(of insurance etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>покры́ть</i> (pokrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to roof (<i>a house</i>) ; to thatch, to tile</li><li>to coat (<i>with paint</i>)</li><li>to cover, to trump (<i>in card games</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold, to rail (at), to swear (at)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover oneself, to get covered</li><li>to be covered <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>покры́ть</i> (pokrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) (tire-)cover, tire</li><li>covering</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as long as, while, whilst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buyer, purchaser, customer, client, shopper</li></ol><p><i>покупа́ть</i> (pokupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) purchasing power, buying power</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) purchasing</li></ol><p><i>покупа́ть</i> (pokupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy, to purchase</li><li>to bathe, to bath</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ, “to buy”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>купа́ть</i> (kupátʹ, “to bathe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li><li>to have a bathe, to bathe, to have/take a bath</li></ol><p><i>покупа́ть</i> (pokupátʹ, “to buy”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>покупа́ть</i> (pokupátʹ, “to bathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buying, purchasing, purchase (act of purchasing)</li><li>purchase, package (something bought)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pokupъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>покупа́ть</i> (pokupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) buyer, purchaser</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke <i>(a little, from time to time)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, to have a smoke <i>(tobacco)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite (many people or in many places)</li><li>to bite several times due to fear or excitement (<i>e.g. one's lips</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attempt</li><li>to encroach <i>(on, upon)</i></li><li>to make an attempt on someone's life</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>покоусити сѧ</i> (pokusiti sę, “to attempt”). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ): to nibble, to bite a little, to bite sometimes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>куса́ть</i> (kusátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to have, to take</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ку́шать</i> (kúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attempt</li><li>to encroach <i>(on, upon)</i></li><li>to make an attempt on someone's life</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>покоушати сѧ</i> (pokušati sę, “to attempt, to try”). By surface analysis, <i>покуси́ться</i> (pokusítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attempt (attack)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pokušenьje</i>, derived from <i>покуси́ться</i> (pokusítʹsja, “to attempt, make an attempt on someone's life”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor (bottom part of a room)</li><li>half</li><li>sex, gender</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъ</i>.</p><p>Related to <i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”), from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Пол)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the English male given name <i>Paul</i></li><li>a transliteration of the English surname <i>Paul</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>полу-</i> (polu-)</li><li>(takes ordinal adjective in genitive case,&#32;produces compound nouns) half past</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-liter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;euphemistic) a half-litre bottle of alcohol, usually vodka</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>литр</i> (litr, “liter”), with <i>литр</i> (litr) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases (at least in the more formal declension) based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past ten, ten-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>оди́ннадцатый</i> (odínnadcatyj, “eleventh”), with <i>оди́ннадцатый</i> (odínnadcatyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb elf</i> (“half past ten”, literally “half eleven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a point</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>очка́</i> (očká)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirt, tail, flap, lap <i>(of clothes)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pola</i>. Compare Polish <i>poła</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Polabian</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>Лаба</i> (Laba) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to define</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) (<i>transitive</i>) to apply, to impose</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to infer, to suppose, to guess, to believe, to think, to reckon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>полагати</i> (polagati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polagati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ).</p><p>The same as Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rely on, to pin one's hopes on, to bank on</li><li>to be due to, be entitled to</li><li>to be proper, to be in order, to be expected</li></ol><p><i>полага́ть</i> (polagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to an understanding <i>(with)</i>, to get on <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пола́зить</i> (polázitʹ)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ла́зать</i> (lázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb (up)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regale oneself, to treat oneself to, to feast on</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ла́комиться</i> (lákomitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to argue, to fight (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лая́ться</i> (lajátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spelt (<i>Triticum spelta</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Sometimes dubiously derived from a Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъlba</i> and considered an isogloss with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spiltaz</i> (“spelt”), Latin <i>puls</i> (“porridge of spelt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a bottle</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka, “bottle”), with <i>буты́лка</i> (butýlka) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a bucket</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>ведра́</i> (vedrá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a century</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek, “century”), with <i>век</i> (vek) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past seven, seven-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>восьмо́й</i> (vosʹmój, “eighth”), with <i>восьмо́й</i> (vosʹmój) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb acht</i> (“half past seven”, literally “half eight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past one, one-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój, “second”), with <i>второ́й</i> (vtorój) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb zwei</i> (“half past one”, literally “half two”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-year (period of half a year)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>го́да</i> (góda)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half of the city, half of the town (also in exaggeration)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod, “city, town”), with <i>го́род</i> (górod) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past eleven, eleven-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>двена́дцатый</i> (dvenádcatyj, “twelfth”), with <i>двена́дцатый</i> (dvenádcatyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb zwölf</i> (“half past eleven”, literally “half twelve”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past eight, eight-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>девя́тый</i> (devjátyj, “ninth”), with <i>девя́тый</i> (devjátyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb neun</i> (“half past eight”, literally “half nine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half the job</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>де́ло</i> (délo, “work, action”), with <i>де́ло</i> (délo) in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noon, midday</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) middle age</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) south</li></ol><p>From <i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past nine, nine-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>деся́тый</i> (desjátyj, “tenth”), with <i>деся́тый</i> (desjátyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb zehn</i> (“half past nine”, literally “half ten”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noon, noontime, midday</li><li>(regional,&#32;Southern Russia) place to stop herds at midday</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ, “day”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon snack, tea (light afternoon meal)</li></ol><p><i>по́лдень</i> (póldenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a day</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ, “day”), with <i>день</i> (denʹ) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halfway</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>доро́га</i> (doróga, “way”), with <i>доро́га</i> (doróga) placed in the genitive case. The oblique forms that exist have the normal endings of <i>доро́га</i> (doróga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half dozen</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>дю́жина</i> (djúžina, “dozen”), with <i>дю́жина</i> (djúžina) placed in the genitive case. Oblique cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field, ground</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) margins</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) brim of the hat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poľe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlefield</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field of vision (area that a person or an animal can see with its eyes without turning the head)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to hunt</li></ol><p>From <i>по́ле</i> (póle) +&lrm; <i>-евать</i> (-evatʹ). Compare Polish <i>polować</i>, Ukrainian <i>полюва́ти</i> (poljuváty), Belarusian <i>палява́ць</i> (paljavácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) field artillery (artillery, including guns, howitzers, mortars, rocket artillery, and recoilless guns, intended for direct support on the field of battle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become politically (more) leftist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bent, bentgrass</li></ol><p><i>поле</i> (pole) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love grass (<i>Eragrostis</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>по́ле</i> (póle, “field”) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka, diminutive suffix). Compare <i>полеви́ца</i> (polevíca), Belarusian <i>паляві́чка</i> (paljavíčka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) field</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) polevoy, a field spirit or sprite</li></ol><p><i>по́ле</i> (póle) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend to the ground (<i>of cereal grains or trees</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tilt, to lean (<i>of objects</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полег-</i> (poleg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to lessen, to abate</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;vernacular)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>легча́ть</i> (lexčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to lie down <i>(for a while)</i>, to have a lie-down</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ). Compare Czech <i>poležet</i>, Kashubian <i>pòleżec</i>, Silesian <i>poleżeć</i>, and Polish <i>poleżeć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>patent law</i>) utility model</li></ol><p>From <i>поле́зная</i> (poléznaja) + <i>моде́ль</i> (modɛ́lʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beneficially</li><li>usefully, helpfully</li></ol><p><i>поле́зный</i> (poléznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usability, usefulness, utility</li></ol><p>From <i>поле́зный</i> (poléznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful, helpful, good, beneficial (having a practical or beneficial use)</li><li>wholesome</li><li>usable</li></ol><p>From <i>по́льза</i> (pólʹza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). The form of this adjective is the regular outcome of the Proto-Slavic stem <i>&#42;polьdzьn-</i>; the first yer was strong due to the presence of another yer in the following syllable. The actual genitive plural <i>польз</i> (polʹz) of <i>по́льза</i> (pólʹza) is phonologically unexpected, and was formed by analogy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb (up, on to), to scramble</li><li>to get (into); to thrust the hand (into)</li><li>to thrust oneself (upon); to intrude (upon)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polemic, controversy</li><li>polemics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polemical</li><li>fervent, passionate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>polém(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be lazy</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-лени́ть</i> (-lenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), or directly from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лени́ться</i> (lenítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stack of firewood</li></ol><p><i>поле́но</i> (poléno, “firewood log”) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billet, log, chock (of wood), wood block (for use as firewood)</li><li>log-shaped cake, e.g. a Yule log</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>полѣно</i> (polěno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polěno</i>, related to <i>полѣти</i> (polěti, “burn, flame”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>поле́но</i> (poléno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshy woodlands</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polěsьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly, to be flying</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to die or be killed in battle (<i>of all or many people</i>)</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;colloquial) to lie down (<i>of all or many people</i>)</li><li>to bend to the ground (<i>of cereal grains or trees</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>полено</i> (poleno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a lifetime</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ, “life”), with <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, to crawl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pь̀lzati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>crawlingly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, to crawl</li><li>to move slowly</li><li>to spread <i>(of rumours)</i></li><li>to fray, to ravel out <i>(of fabric)</i></li><li>to slip, to collapse <i>(of soil)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crawler (someone or something that crawls)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) child or animal not yet able to walk; crawler</li><li>(<i>mechanical engineering</i>) crosshead</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) flat spot, wheel flat</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>полза́ть</i> (polzátʹ) (<i>ползти́</i> (polztí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) child or animal not yet able to walk; crawler</li><li>diminutive of <i>ползу́н</i> (polzún)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) slider (movable user interface element)</li></ol><p><i>ползу́н</i> (polzún) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crawling, creeping</li><li>creeping</li></ol><p><i>ползти́</i> (polztí) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering, irrigation, sprinkling</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>поли́ть</i> (polítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking&#32;or&#32;ceramics</i>) glaze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterer (device for watering)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterer (one who waters)</li></ol><p><i>полива́ть</i> (polivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour, to water</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лива́ть</i> (livátʹ).</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>паліва́ць</i> (palivácʹ) and Ukrainian <i>полива́ти</i> (polyváty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sling mud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour on/upon oneself</li></ol><p><i>полива́ть</i> (polivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyvinyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polyvinyl chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygamy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyglot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygon</li><li>firing range, ordnance yard</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>πολύγωνον</i> (polúgōnon), from <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) + <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía, “angle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygraph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polygraphic; printing</li></ol><p>From <i>полигра́ф</i> (poligráf, “polygraph”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygraphy, printing trades</li><li>printing arts</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Polygraphie</i>, from the Ancient Greek <i>πολυγραφία</i> (polugraphía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyeleos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πολῠέλεος</i> (polŭéleos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policlinic, outpatient clinic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>policlinique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) polymer</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) + <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part, share, portion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(organic chemistry,&#32;relational) polymer; polymeric, polymerous</li></ol><p>From <i>полиме́р</i> (polimér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>поліме́рний</i> (polimérnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polynomial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faded (that has lost some of its former color)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>линя́(ть)</i> (linjá(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fade, to lose color</li><li>to moult (to shed a covering of fur, feathers etc.)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>линя́ть</i> (linjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poliomyelitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(zoology,&#32;animate) polyp</li><li>(medicine,&#32;inanimate) polyp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>polype</i>, from Latin <i>polypus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πολύπους</i> (polúpous).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polypropylene</li></ol><p><i>поли-</i> (poli-) +&lrm; <i>пропиле́н</i> (propilén)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polished</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to polish, to shine, to buff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>polieren</i>, which is borrowed from Old French <i>polir</i> (see modern French <i>polir</i>), from Latin <i>polīre</i> (“to polish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polishing, buffing</li><li>shiny, polished surface</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) veneer (<i>of culture</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полирова́ть</i> (polirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policy</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) polis (<i>a Greek city-state</i>)</li><li>police</li></ol><p>Probably derived from Middle French <i>policie</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πόλις</i> (pólis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polysaccharide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polysyndeton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πολυσύνδετον</i> (polusúndeton). Compare with <i>многосою́зие</i> (mnogosojúzije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policeman (<i>usually in foreign, especially English-speaking countries</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>policeman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leaf through</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>листа́ть</i> (listátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polystyrene</li></ol><p><i>поли-</i> (poli-) +&lrm; <i>стиро́л</i> (stiról)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) polysulfide, polysulphide</li></ol><p><i>поли-</i> (poli-) +&lrm; <i>сульфи́д</i> (sulʹfíd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politburo, political bureau</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>полити́ческое бюро́</i> (politíčeskoje bjuró, “political bureau”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Политбюро)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Politburo of the Communist Party of the Soviet Union</li></ol><p>From <i>политбюро́</i> (politbjuró), a clipped compound of <i>полити́ческое бюро́</i> (politíčeskoje bjuró, literally “political bureau”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) polytheism</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from French <i>polythéisme</i>. Composed of Greek roots; see <i>polytheism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polytheist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) polytheistic</li></ol><p><i>политеи́ст</i> (politɛíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polytechnic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>polytechn(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political prisoner (a person imprisoned for their political views or political activism)</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>полити́ческий заключённый</i> (politíčeskij zaključónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) political prisoner (a person imprisoned for their political views or political activism)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческий зэк</i> (politíčeskij zɛk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politicization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to politicize</li></ol><p>From German <i>politisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politics</li><li>policy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>political-economic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) to act in a cunning way hiding one's true intentions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) political report or briefing on recent home and international events</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческая информа́ция</i> (politíčeskaja informácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>politically (in a political manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>political</li></ol><p><i>поли́т(ика)</i> (polít(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political convict</li></ol><p><i>полит-</i> (polit-) +&lrm; <i>каторжа́нин</i> (katoržánin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political correctness</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>полити́ческая корре́ктность</i> (politíčeskaja korréktnostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political scientist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political science</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Politologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politonym (a name referring to members of a political entity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) polytope</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Polytop</i>. By surface analysis, <i>поли-</i> (poli-, “poly-”) +&lrm; <i>-топ</i> (-top, “-tope”), from Ancient Greek <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “region; area”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;uncommon) polytope; polytopic</li></ol><p>From <i>полито́п</i> (politóp) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) polytope; polytopic</li></ol><p>From <i>полито́п</i> (politóp) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political department, political division</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческий отде́л</i> (politíčeskij otdél, “political department”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) political instructor</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческий рабо́тник</i> (politíčeskij rabótnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) political commissar; politruk</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческий руководи́тель</i> (politíčeskij rukovodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political council (governing body of a political party)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческий сове́т</i> (politíčeskij sovét, “political council”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political strategist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political technology</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческая техноло́гия</i> (politíčeskaja texnológija, “political technology”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polish, varnish, lacquer</li><li>purified ethanol varnish used for alcoholic consumption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political organization, political party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour, to water</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour on/upon oneself</li></ol><p><i>поли́ть</i> (polítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political economist</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>полити́ческий эконо́м</i> (politíčeskij ekonóm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>political economy</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>полити́ческая эконо́мия</i> (politíčeskaja ekonómija, “political economy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polyurethane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) polyurethane</li></ol><p><i>полиурета́н</i> (poliuretán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyphony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;offensive) <i>Hipo</i>, <i>Schutzmann</i> (collaborationist auxiliary policeman working for the Nazi German occupation forces during World War II)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cop (police officer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Polizei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) police</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policeman</li></ol><p><i>поли́ция</i> (polícija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>police</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>policja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpus delicti</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polyester (polymer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polyethylene, polythene</li></ol><p><i>поли-</i> (poli-) +&lrm; <i>этиле́н</i> (etilén)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) polyethylene</li></ol><p><i>полиэтиле́н</i> (polietilén) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) polyethylene terephthalate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) regiment</li><li>(literary,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) host, army</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) army, multitude (a great number)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пълъкъ</i> (pŭlŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъlkъ</i>, possibly from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulką</i> (“people”) (compare English <i>folk</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelf</li><li><i>(in a passenger railroad car)</i> berth</li><li>(<i>firearms</i>) flash pan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a kilo</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>кило́</i> (kiló, “kilo”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a kilometer</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>киломе́тр</i> (kilométr, “kilometer”), with <i>киломе́тр</i> (kilométr) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) colonel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pułkownik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) colonel</li></ol><p><i>полко́вник</i> (polkóvnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colonel's wife</li></ol><p><i>полко́вник</i> (polkóvnik) +&lrm; <i>-иха</i> (-ixa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander, military leader, warlord</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъlkovodьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>полк</i> (polk, “host, army”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вод(ить)</i> (vod(itʹ), “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) regiment; regimental</li></ol><p><i>полк</i> (polk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пол-ли́тра</i> (pol-lítra)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>литр</i> (litr, “litre”), with <i>литр</i> (litr) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a month</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac, “month”), with <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a metre</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>метр</i> (metr, “metre”), with <i>метр</i> (metr) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a million</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>миллио́н</i> (millión, “million”), with <i>миллио́н</i> (millión) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a minute</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>мину́та</i> (minúta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow stout, to put on weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause one to look fatter than one is</li><li>to cause one to gain weight</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to cause to swell, fill with, occupy, enrich</li></ol><p>Deadjectival, from <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “stout, chubby”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Newly-formed and not to be confused with the inherited combining perfective stem <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ), which differs in stress as well as meaning. The latter stem also exists as an archaic stand-alone imperfective meaning “to cause to swell, fill with”.</p><p>Directly or via Old Church Slavonic from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьlniti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Cognate to Ukrainian <i>по́внити</i> (póvnyty), Belarusian <i>по́ўніць</i> (pównicʹ) and parallel forms in most other Slavic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fully, in full</li><li>brim-full, full to the brim</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>okay, stop, that's it, enough (of this)</li></ol><p>From <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) (stem-stressed) or from <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-weight</li><li>heavyweight</li><li>strong, in full force</li><li>full, complete (<i>life, happiness, etc.</i>)</li><li>significant, credible</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>about a natural body of water, especially a river or waterfall</i>) wide, (full-)flowing (having a high abundance of water)</li><li>(<i>about a natural body of water, especially a river or waterfall</i>) high, deep, swollen (having a high water level)</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) pleophony, full-voicing, polnoglasie</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) pleophonic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-breasted, full-busted</li></ol><p><i>полн(ый)</i> (poln(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>груд(ь)</i> (grud(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plethoric, suffering from hyperaemia (excessive blood flow in the body)</li><li>healthy, blooming (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>active, full (<i>life, disclosure, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полнокро́вие</i> (polnokróvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full moon (phase of moon when in opposition to sun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) full-scale</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>масшта́б</i> (masštáb) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half size (of a dress, of shoes, etc.)</li><li>half a room (rented in a hotel)</li><li>half a digit (number)</li><li>half a number plate</li><li>half a number, issue (e.g. of a magazine or a newspaper)</li><li>half an item, turn, trick (e.g., in a circus)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>но́мер</i> (nómer, “size, (hotel) room”), with <i>но́мер</i> (nómer) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorization (formal sanction, permission or warrant)</li><li>authority, power</li></ol><p>From <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plenipotentiary</li></ol><p><i>полномо́ч(ие)</i> (polnomóč(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enjoying full rights, competent</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;vehicles) all-wheel drive, four-wheel drive</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>приво́д</i> (privód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, entirely, utterly, fully</li></ol><p>Instrumental case of <i>по́лность</i> (pólnostʹ), from <i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj, “full”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness, coverage</li><li>fullness, fulness</li><li>plenitude</li><li>entirety</li><li>integrality</li><li>integrity</li><li>fleshiness</li><li>obesity</li><li>fat, fatness, plumpness</li><li>embonpoint</li><li>amplitude (magnitude)</li><li>instep</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Expresses a demand to stop something; now, now, come on, hold on</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Expresses doubt, disagreement or objection; now, now, come on, hold on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of full value, valuable, full-fledged, complete, full</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a night</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ, “night”), with <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) midnight</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midnight</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) north</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъnoťь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>ночь</i> (nočʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having chubby cheeks</li></ol><p>From <i>полн(ый)</i> (poln(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>щека́</i> (ščeká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) full screen</li></ol><p><i>по́лный</i> (pólnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>экра́н</i> (ekrán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full, complete</li><li>absolute, perfect</li><li>exhaustive</li><li>stout, chubby</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьlnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pílˀnas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥h₁nós</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>plenus</i> → English <i>replenish</i>, Sanskrit <i>पूर्ण</i> (pūrṇa) and English <i>full</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) full speed ahead</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) polo (ball game)</li><li>polo shirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaff</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cuman</li></ol><p>Connected with Old East Slavic <i>половъ</i> (polovŭ, “pale yellow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Polovtsian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floormat (a mat that is laid on the floor)</li></ol><p><i>пол</i> (pol) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>полови́на</i> (polovína)</li><li>leaf (<i>of a door</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloqial</i>) half-size violin</li></ol><p><i>полови́на</i> (polovína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor plank, flooring strip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лов</i> (lov) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seasonal flooding (<i>from snowmelt, monsoons, etc.</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;poetic) flood (<i>e.g. of feelings</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>половодьє</i> (polovodʹje), attested since 1674, from <i>половодь</i> (polovodʹ) (since 16th c., akin to dialectal Russian <i>половодь</i> (polovodʹ)), from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ, “open from above, uncovered”), attested in a 14th-c. birchbark letter (No. 59) in "полои води" 'floodwaters.GEN.SG'.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>по́лая вода́</i> (pólaja vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse (sexual interaction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) floor, flooring (<i>the bottom or lower part of a room</i>)</li><li>(relational,&#32;biology,&#32;sex) sex (<i>as an action</i>); sexual, genital</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) waiter or servant in a Russian tavern</li></ol><p><i>пол</i> (pol, “floor”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój), from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ, “floor”).</p><p><i>пол</i> (pol, “sex, gender”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój), related to <i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual intercourse (sexual interaction)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sex organ</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>evolutionary theory</i>) sexual selection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis (male organ for copulation and urination)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straw-colored, wan, pale yellow (<i>of animal coat color</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polvъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>поло́ва</i> (polóva) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canopy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pologъ</i>. Composed of <i>по-</i> (po-) + a nominalized form of the verb <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ, “to lie”), with typical Indo-European nominal <i>o</i>-ablaut.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, gently sloping</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пологꙑи</i> (pologyi). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лог</i> (log) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), where <i>-ий</i> (-ij) is a variant of <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>поло́гий</i> (pológij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>position, location</li><li>situation</li><li>state, condition</li><li>standing</li><li>thesis</li><li>(<i>in the singular&#32;or&#32;in the plural</i>) regulations</li></ol><p><i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ, “to put, to lay”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Cognate with Macedonian <i>по́ложба</i> (póložba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>due</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let's assume, let's say, suppose (for example)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>positively (in various senses)</li></ol><p><i>положи́тельный</i> (položítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>positive, affirmative</li></ol><p>From <i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to lay, to lay down, to deposit (In a horizontal position)</li><li>to apply</li><li>to spend</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>положити</i>, Polish <i>położyć</i>, Czech <i>položit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rely on, to pin one's hopes on, to bank on</li></ol><p><i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) convicted career criminal (in prison)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) authoritative ruling or opinion on some matter; a rule that it is expected that the criminals</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) provisions which the prisoners have right to obtain from the prison administration</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) consensus, general opinion</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) synonym of <i>положение</i> (položenije)</li></ol><p><i>положение</i> (položenije) +&lrm; <i>-ня́к</i> (-nják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runner (smooth, flat surface, bent upward in the front, used for sliding on ice)</li><li>racer (snake)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelf</li><li>dray</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to rack, to cause to ache</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to break up, to fracture</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakdown, breakage</li></ol><p><i>полома́ть</i> (polomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>плен</i> (plen)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>плен</i> (plen), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>плѣнъ</i> (plěnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polonaise</li></ol><p>From French <i>polonaise</i> (“Polish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polonium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to take captive</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to conquer</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to captivate, to charm</li></ol><p><i>поло́н</i> (polón) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) captive</li></ol><p><i>поло́н</i> (polón) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slack-jawed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stripe, strip, streak</li><li>belt, zone</li><li>bar</li><li>field</li><li>lane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>striped, stripy</li></ol><p>From <i>полоса́</i> (polosá, “stripe”) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>полоса́</i> (polosá)</li></ol><p><i>полоса́</i> (polosá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slop bowl</li><li>finger bowl</li></ol><p><i>полоска́ть</i> (poloskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rinse</li><li>to gargle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polskati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slash (<i>once</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to whip (<i>once</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>полоса́</i> (polosá) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>поло́ска</i> (polóska, “small strip, stripe, lane, etc.”)</li></ol><p><i>поло́ска</i> (polóska) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavity (hollow area within the body)</li></ol><p><i>по́лый</i> (pólyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>towel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poltьnьce</i>. By surface analysis, <i>полотно́</i> (polotnó, “canvas”) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>платьньце</i> (platĭnĭce) and Sanskrit <i>पट</i> (paṭa, “cloth, garment”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>towel dryer</li></ol><p><i>полоте́нце</i> (poloténce, “towel”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ, “to dry”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>width, cloth</li><li>The different coloured/colored parts of a flag</li></ol><p><i>полотно́</i> (polotnó) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen</li><li>cloth, bunting</li><li>canvas</li><li>roadbed <i>(of a railroad)</i>; embankment</li><li>saw web, saw blade</li><li>panel</li><li>picture, painting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poltьno</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;polto-</i> (“cloth”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i>. Doublet of <i>платно́</i> (platnó), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) linen</li></ol><p><i>полотно́</i> (polotnó) +&lrm; <i>-я́ный</i> (-jányj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weed, to weed out, to pull up (weeds)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pèlti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) floor polisher</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) floor polisher</li></ol><p>From <i>пол</i> (pol, “floor”) +&lrm; <i>-тёр</i> (-tjór, “to rub, to scrub”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) fear, fright</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polxъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>поло́х</i> (polóx, “fear”), <i>по́лох</i> (pólox, “horror”), Russian Church Slavonic <i>плахъ</i> (plaxŭ, “fear”), Bulgarian <i>плах</i> (plah, “timid; fear”), Serbo-Croatian <i>пла̏х</i> (“fast, sharp”), Slovene <i>plȃh</i> (“timid”), Czech <i>plachý</i> (“timid”), Slovak <i>plachý</i> (“timid”), Polish <i>płochy</i> (“timid, frivolous”). Per Vasmer, probably related to Ancient Greek <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to excite”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌰</i> (usfilma, “frightened, horrified”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Polotsk</li><li>(<i>history</i>) Polochan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>по́лка</i> (pólka)</li></ol><p><i>по́лка</i> (pólka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worry, to frighten, to arouse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polšiti</i>. Compare <i>поло́х</i> (polóx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past twelve, twelve-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half past”) +&lrm; <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj, “first”), with <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb eins</i> (“half past twelve”, literally “half one”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a serving, half a portion</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>по́рция</i> (pórcija, “serving”), with <i>по́рция</i> (pórcija) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plenipotentiary</li></ol><p>Short for полномочный представитель</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halfway</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “way”), with <i>путь</i> (putʹ) placed in the genitive case. The oblique forms that exist have the normal endings of <i>путь</i> (putʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past four, four-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>пя́тый</i> (pjátyj, “fifth”), with <i>пя́тый</i> (pjátyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb fünf</i> (“half past four”, literally “half five”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past six, six-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>седьмо́й</i> (sedʹmój, “seventh”), with <i>седьмо́й</i> (sedʹmój) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb sieben</i> (“half past six”, literally “half seven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a word; (a few) word(s)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>полсло́ва</i> (polslóva): half a word; (a few) word(s)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>слове́чко</i> (slovéčko, “word”), with <i>слове́чко</i> (slovéčko) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>полсло́ва</i> (polslóva): half a word; (a few) word(s)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>словцо́</i> (slovcó, “word”), with <i>словцо́</i> (slovcó) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(about) fifty, half a hundred</li><li>(<i>radio communications</i>) fifty (avoiding confusion between <i>пятьдеся́т</i> (pjatʹdesját) and <i>шестьдеся́т</i> (šestʹdesját) on a noisy channel)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>со́тня</i> (sótnja, “hundred”), with <i>со́тня</i> (sótnja) in the genitive case. Oblique cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-time, part-time (work)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ста́вка</i> (stávka, “salary”), with <i>ста́вка</i> (stávka) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a glass</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>стака́н</i> (stakán, “glass”), with <i>стака́н</i> (stakán) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a century</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>столе́тия</i> (stolétija, “century”), with <i>столе́тие</i> (stolétije) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”) but may be indeclinable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half as much</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>сто́лько</i> (stólʹko, “so much”), with <i>сто́лько</i> (stólʹko) declined as if it were a noun and placed in the genitive case (by analogy with other words in <i>пол-</i> (pol-), which require that the following part be in the genitive case, most commonly ending in <i>-а</i> (-a)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a tablet</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>табле́тка</i> (tablétka, “tablet”), with <i>табле́тка</i> (tablétka) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Poltava</li></ol><p><i>Полта́ва</i> (Poltáva) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>полта́вский</i> (poltávskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Poltava</li></ol><p><i>Полта́ва</i> (Poltáva) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poltergeist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Poltergeist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fifty copecks</li><li>fifty-copeck piece</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fifty-ruble note or coin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) age of fifty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a fifty (<i>e.g. a 50 mm lens, a model 50 automobile, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полти́на</i> (poltína) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one and a half (1½), sesqui-</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;роlъ vъtora</i> (“half of the second”). By surface analysis, <i>пол</i> (pol, “half”) +&lrm; <i>второй</i> (vtoroj, “second”). Cognate to Polish <i>półtora</i>, Ukrainian <i>півтора́</i> (pivtorá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one hundred and fifty (150)</li></ol><p>From <i>полтора́</i> (poltorá, “one and a half”) + <i>сто</i> (sto, “hundred”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past two, two-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>тре́тий</i> (trétij, “third”), with <i>тре́тий</i> (trétij) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb drei</i> (“half past two”, literally “half three”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a thousand, five hundred</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača, “thousand”), with <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) in the genitive case. Oblique and plural cases based on <i>полу́-</i> (polú-, “half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(highly&#32;productive,&#32;takes nouns,&#32;produces compound nouns) semi-, demi-, half-</li><li>(highly&#32;productive,&#32;takes nouns in genitive case,&#32;produces compound nouns) semi-, demi-, half- paired with пол- form</li><li>(takes adjective,&#32;produces compound adjective) semi-, demi-, half-</li><li>(productive,&#32;takes verbs,&#32;produces compound verbs) incompletely</li><li>(takes adverb,&#32;produces compound adverb) incompletely</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъ</i> (“half”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) nibble (unit of memory equal to half a byte)</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>partly incoherent</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>бессвя́зный</i> (bessvjáznyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demigod, half-god</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>бог</i> (box)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low shoe</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>боти́нок</i> (botínok), calque of German <i>Halbschuh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semidelirious</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>бредо́вый</i> (bredóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>полведра́</i> (polvedrá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) light (not heavy)</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>возду́шный</i> (vozdúšnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) semivowel</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>гла́сный</i> (glásnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-year (period of half a year)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-naked</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>го́лый</i> (gólyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) semi-racer</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>го́ночный</i> (gónočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>parapublic</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>гражда́нский</i> (graždánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semiliteracy</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>гра́мотность</i> (grámotnostʹ, “literacy”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semiliterate</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>гра́мотный</i> (grámotnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midday gerbil (<i>Meriones meridianus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полу́денный</i> (polúdennyj, “midday”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>песча́нка</i> (pesčánka, “gerbil”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) noon</li></ol><p><i>полу́ден-</i> (polúden-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-childish</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>де́тский</i> (détskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-savage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be half-asleep</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>дрема́ть</i> (dremátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-forgotten</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>забы́тый</i> (zabýtyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semi-taboo</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>запре́т</i> (zaprét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) midfielder, halfback, half</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) short name, pet name (<i>the simplest diminutive form of a given name, such as Саша, Маша, Миша</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>и́мя</i> (ímja, “name”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semi-circle</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>кольцо́</i> (kolʹcó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overall (garment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-blood, half-breed</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semicircle</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>круг</i> (krug)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semicircular</li></ol><p><i>полукру́г</i> (polukrúg) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recline</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-moon, semilunar, crescent, lunate</li></ol><p><i>по́лу-</i> (pólu-) +&lrm; <i>лу́нный</i> (lúnnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a recumbent position, recumbently</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>лёжа</i> (ljóža)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-measure</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crescent (figure of the moon)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twilight, semi-darkness, shade</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>мрак</i> (mrak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>halfdead</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>мёртвый</i> (mjórtvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who does not sleep until all hours</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-lit</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>освещённый</i> (osveščónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peninsula</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>о́стров</i> (óstrov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peninsular</li></ol><p>From <i>полуо́стров</i> (poluóstrov) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semiaxis</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) OS/2</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>ось</i> (osʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half open, semiopen</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>откры́тый</i> (otkrýtyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic&#32;accentology</i>) semi-retractivity. <i>Clarification:</i> This stress is called “semi-retractivity” because it was assumed the leftward shift of the stress in the prefixed forms of the l-participle and aorist from the verbs of the Proto-Slavic accent paradigm b. In fact, the Proto-Slavic semi-retractivity is a consequence of the phonological prohibition on the rightward shift of stress from dominant roots to recessive suffixes in wordforms in which the stressed syllable was not the initial one.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>отметност</i> (otmetnost).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short coat</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half-”) +&lrm; <i>пальто́</i> (palʹtó, “coat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half board, demi-pension, modified American plan, bed and breakfast</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>пансио́н</i> (pansión)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-loop</li><li>immelman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-sad, half-mournful, half-doleful, half-wistful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-plane</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>пло́скость</i> (plóskostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semi-basement</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>подва́л</i> (podvál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-bow (a gesture)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>покло́н</i> (poklón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-closed</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>прикры́тый</i> (prikrýtyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semiconductor</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>проводни́к</i> (provodník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semitransparent, translucent</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>прозра́чный</i> (prozráčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-empty</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>пусто́й</i> (pustój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ramshackle, half-dilapidated, half-broken</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dilapidated, decayed, half-destroyed</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>разру́шенный</i> (razrúšennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) half-swastika, zwastika (the letter Z used as a distinguishing mark on Russian military vehicles during the 2022 invasion of Ukraine and as a symbol by supporters of Russian militarism)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half-”) +&lrm; <i>сва́стика</i> (svástika, “swastika”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-light</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>свет</i> (svet)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-death, near-death state</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>смерть</i> (smertʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-sleep, twilight sleep</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>сон</i> (son)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) halt</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>полстоле́тия</i> (polstolétija)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>столе́тие</i> (stolétije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemisphere</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>сфе́ра</i> (sféra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half raw; half baked; half done (of food)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>сыро́й</i> (syrój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) penumbra</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>тень</i> (tenʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) half tone, semitone</li><li>(<i>art</i>) halftone</li><li>undertone (of sound or color)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>тон</i> (ton)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;historical) 1.5 ton GAZ-AA or GAZ-MM truck (<i>manufactured 1932–1938 and 1938–1949 respectively</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small apartment consisting of a medium-sized room and a small room</li></ol><p><i>полу́тора</i> (polútora, “one and a half”, oblique case form) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an extremely tired, worn-out person resembling a corpse</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-, “half-”) +&lrm; <i>труп</i> (trup, “corpse”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-dark, semidarkness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-dark, dim, scantily lit</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-finished/semi-finished product</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>фабрикат</i> (fabrikat)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) semifinal</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>фина́л</i> (finál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half an hour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addressee, recipient</li></ol><p><i>получа́ть</i> (polučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, to get, to obtain, to accept</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come out, to arrive</li><li>to result</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to prove, to turn out</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to work out well, to manage, to come along</li></ol><p><i>получа́ть</i> (polučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demihuman, half human</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receipt</li><li>acquisition, getting</li><li>reception</li><li>obtainment</li></ol><p><i>получи́ть</i> (polučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive, to get</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come in, to arrive</li><li>to result</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to prove, to turn out</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to work out well, to manage, to come alone</li></ol><p><i>получи́ть</i> (polučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pay, sum paid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>лу́чше</i> (lúčše)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemisphere</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>шар</i> (šar) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-woollen</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>шерстяно́й</i> (šerstjanój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polushka</li></ol><p>From <i>полу́ха</i> (polúxa, “half (of a coin)”). By surface analysis, <i>пол</i> (pol, “half”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) half-shtof (an old unit of liquid measure equal to 0.615 litres, or ½ of a shtof, most often used for alcoholic beverages&#59; comparable to an English pint)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a half-shtof bottle or flask (<i>for vodka or other distilled liquor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>штоф</i> (štof)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short sheepskin coat, sheepskin jacket</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>шу́ба</i> (šúba) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-whisper</li></ol><p><i>полу-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>шёпот</i> (šópot)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often idiomatically in phrases to do with offering</i>) half (of) a kingdom, half (of) the kingdom</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>ца́рства</i> (cárstva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-price</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>цена́</i> (cená)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) brother soldier</li></ol><p><i>полк</i> (polk) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half an hour, half-hour</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>ча́са</i> (čása)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half an hour</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ча́сик</i> (čásik, “(short) hour”), with <i>ча́сик</i> (čásik) in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half a cup</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ча́шка</i> (čáška, “cup”), with <i>ча́шка</i> (čáška) placed in the genitive case.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past three, three-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>четвёртый</i> (četvjórtyj, “fourth”), with <i>четвёртый</i> (četvjórtyj) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb vier</i> (“half past three”, literally “half four”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horde (a large number of people)</li></ol><p><i>полк</i> (polk) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edible dormouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) half past five, five-thirty (hour of the day)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>шесто́й</i> (šestój, “sixth”), with <i>шесто́й</i> (šestój) in the genitive case, as with other words prefixed with <i>пол-</i> (pol-). Compare German <i>halb sechs</i> (“half past five”, literally “half six”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a half-shtof bottle or flask (<i>for vodka or other distilled liquor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пол-</i> (pol-) +&lrm; <i>штоф</i> (štof)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hollow</li><li>(dated,&#32;regional) naked</li><li>spilling over after spring ice breakup (<i>of water or a river</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ, “open from above, uncovered”), attested in a 14th-c. birchbark letter (No. 59) in "полои води" 'floodwaters.GEN.SG' (cf. Russian <i>половодье</i> (polovodʹje)). Modern meaning attested since 1636.</p><p>Related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poľe</i> (“field”) and <i>&#42;polnъ</i> (“flat, smooth, plain”), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) synonym of <i>пла́мя</i> (plámja)</li></ol><p>Via Middle Russian, then probably borrowed Old Pskovian <i>&#42;полꙑмѧ</i> (&#42;polymę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pòlmy</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>по́лымя</i> (pólymja).</p><p>Doublet of <i>пла́мя</i> (plámja) and <i>по́ломя</i> (pólomja), where the first one is an Old Church Slavonic word showing liquid metathesis characteristic of South Slavic area, and the second one normally inherited by pleophony of *TorT &gt; *ToroT type, while Old Pskovian dialect (Krivichs area) reflex of pleophony <i>&#42;TolT</i> &gt; <i>&#42;TolᵊT</i>, <i>&#42;TolᵒT</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormwood, mugwort, artemisia</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bitterness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пелынь</i> (pelynĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pelynь</i>&nbsp;f, <i>&#42;pelynъ</i>&nbsp;m. Compare Bulgarian <i>пели́н</i> (pelín), Czech <i>peluň</i>, Polish <i>piołun</i>. Per Vasmer, a connection with <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ, “to burn, scorch”) and <i>поле́но</i> (poléno, “(burning)log”) is possible. Compare typologically <i>го́рький</i> (górʹkij), <i>го́речь</i> (górečʹ), <b><i>горчи́ца</i> (gorčíca)</b> akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polynya</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>полыньѧ</i> (polynʹja), attested since 1639, from <i>полыи</i> (polyi, “open from above, uncovered; hollow”), whence also Russian <i>полый</i> (polyj, “hollow”), from Old East Slavic <i>полъ</i> (polŭ, “uncovered”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat”). By surface analysis, <i>по́лый</i> (pólyj) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja) +&lrm; <i>-ья́</i> (-ʹjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become bald</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>лысе́ть</i> (lysétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blaze</li><li><i>(war, conflict etc)</i> to blaze, to flare up, to explode, to break out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash, to blaze, to spit fire momentarily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relief, benefit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polьga</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>пільга</i> (pilʹha), Polish <i>ulga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, benefit, advantage, profit, usefulness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>польза</i> (polĭza), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polьdza</i>, from <i>&#42;po</i> + <i>&#42;lьga</i>. Doublet of dialectal польга (polʹga). Related to нельзя́ (nelʹzjá), <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, usage, utilization [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><li>enjoyment (of rights)</li></ol><p><i>по́льзовать</i> (pólʹzovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>user</li></ol><p><i>по́льзоваться</i> (pólʹzovatʹsja, “to use”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) user</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) custom, user-defined</li></ol><p><i>по́льзователь</i> (pólʹzovatelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to benefit</li><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to treat (<i>of a doctor and patient</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по́льза</i> (pólʹza) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to utilize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘health, good fortune, power, privilege, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>польѕевати сѧ</i> (polĭdzevati sę). By surface analysis, <i>по́льзовать</i> (pólʹzovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military slang,&#32;inanimate) a 60mm reduced-noise mortar or mortar round</li><li>(<i>music</i>) polka</li><li>polka (a haircut)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Polka</i>.</p><p>The military slang sense refers to the Polish LMP-2017 mortar.</p><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>polka</i> (“half-step dance”), variant of <i>půlka</i> (“a half”), diminutive of <i>půl</i> (“a half”) (Russian <i>пол-</i> (pol-)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polъ</i>, further origin uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Polish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polish language (short for <i>по́льский язы́к</i> (pólʹskij jazýk))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>польскъ</i> (polĭskŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, to adulate</li><li>to flatter, to please</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>льстить</i> (lʹstitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in love with, to grow fond of</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch the fancy <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>полюби́ть</i> (poljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admire, to feast one's eyes (upon) (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>любова́ться</i> (ljubovátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be curious, to ask</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>любопы́тствовать</i> (ljubopýtstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) poliudie (gathering tribute by the rulers of Kievan Rus' from vassal East Slavic tribes)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>полюдиѥ</i> (poljudije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) pole (either of the two points on the earth's surface around which it rotates)</li><li>(<i>algebra</i>) pole (a certain type of singularity of a complex-valued function of a complex variable)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) pole (a point of magnetic focus)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) pole (a fixed point relative to other points or lines)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>по́люс</i> (póljus), from Latin <i>polus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πόλος</i> (pólos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>по́ле</i> (póle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Killing Fields (sites in Cambodia where mass slaughter was carried out)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pole, Polish man</li><li>Polack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>Polak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadow, glade, clearing</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a lavish, celebratory meal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poľana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Polan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poľaninъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>поля́на</i> (poljána)</li></ol><p><i>поля́на</i> (poljána) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) polarization</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>polarisation</i>; alternatively analyzable as <i>поляриз(ова́ть)</i> (poljariz(ovátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) to polarize</li></ol><p>French <i>polariser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arctic fox (<i>Alopex lagopus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polar explorer, member of a polar expedition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aurora (an atmospheric phenomenon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polar</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>polāris</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) to Polonise</li></ol><p>From <i>поля́к</i> (polják, “Pole”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Polack</li></ol><p>From <i>поля́к</i> (polják) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vole, field mouse (mammal)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) myagrum (Brassicaceae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight, flying</li><li>Polyot (Soviet interim orbital carrier rocket)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;letъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;lekt-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lek-</i> (“jump”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flight</li></ol><p><i>полёт</i> (poljót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lipstick</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) pomade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pomade</i>, from French <i>pommade</i>, from Italian <i>pomata</i>, from Italian <i>pomo</i>, from Latin <i>pōmum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fondant, fudge</li></ol><p>from <i>помада</i> (pomada, “pomade”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anointing</li></ol><p><i>пома́зать</i> (pomázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anointed (a person who has been anointed, especially for religious reasons)</li><li>anointed sovereign</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>помазать</i> (pomazatʹ, “to oil, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oil, to grease, to lubricate</li><li>to anoint</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaving brush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint</li></ol><p><i>пома́зать</i> (pomázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little by little, gradually</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) so-so, tolerable</li></ol><p>From <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>мале́ньку</i> (malénʹku), perhaps an obsolete short dative form of <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hold one's tongue, to keep mum</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>молк-</i> (molk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ), based on the stem <i>молк-</i> (molk-) that underlies <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bit by bit, little by little</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beckon, to wave <i>(to)</i></li><li>to attract, to lure, to entice, to allure</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave, to flap, to fling, to brandish</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>маха́ть</i> (maxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wave, to whisk, to swing, to wag</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ме́дленнее</i> (médlenneje) a little slower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to linger <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ме́длить</i> (médlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomelo</li></ol><p><i>помести́</i> (pomestí) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pomelo</i>, from English <i>pomelo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ме́ньше</i> (ménʹše)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alter, to change</li><li>to exchange, to change</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to change</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to exchange, to trade</li></ol><p><i>поменя́ть</i> (pomenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitter orange</li><li>bitter orange tree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bitter orange</li></ol><p><i>помера́нец</i> (pomeránec) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to die</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мере́ть</i> (merétʹ).</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>померти</i> (pomerty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to seem <i>(to)</i>, to appear <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мере́щиться</i> (meréščitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try on</li><li>to measure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dark/dim</li><li>to fade, to wane</li><li>to pale <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ркнуть</i> (mérknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep (<i>for a while</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to snow strongly, to blizzard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) capacious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to put, to locate, to position, to set, to bestow, to stand</li><li>to invest</li><li>to lodge, to accommodate, to put up, to settle</li><li>to insert, to publish</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit</li><li>to stay, to take a seat</li></ol><p><i>помести́ть</i> (pomestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) estate, manor</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) land granted temporarily in exchange for military or civil service</li><li>occurring in a specific place</li></ol><p><i>поме́стье</i> (poméstʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сто</i> (mésto) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estate, manor, hall, property (a manor house)</li><li>land granted by the state, originally temporarily (e.g. for life), in exchange for military or civil service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hybrid, crossbreed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mixture</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>меси́ть</i> (mesítʹ). Compare <i>смесь</i> (smesʹ, “mixture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to date <i>(add the date)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, note</li></ol><p><i>поме́тить</i> (pométitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindrance, barrier (something which hinders)</li><li>(usually plural) (<i>radio</i>) noise, interference</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to date <i>(add the date)</i></li></ol><p><i>поме́тить</i> (pométitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dream for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mentally abnormal; crazy</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) obsessed (intensely preoccupied with or by a given topic or emotion&#59; driven by a specified obsession)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness, insanity</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) obsession, passion (<i>for something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>помеша́ть(ся)</i> (pomešátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb, to hinder, to impede</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stir; to mix, to mix up, to mingle, to blend, (something for some time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shuffle (playing cards)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go crazy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be obsessed with, to be a nut about [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ться</i> (mešátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir slowly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to put, to locate, to position, to set, to bestow, to stand</li><li>to invest</li><li>to lodge, to accommodate, to put up, to settle</li><li>to insert, to publish</li></ol><p><i>помести́ть</i> (pomestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be (in a location), to be located (<i>no perfective</i>)</li><li>to fit</li></ol><p><i>помеща́ть</i> (pomeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodgement, premise(s), room, apartment, quarters</li><li>investment, location, placement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poměščenьje</i>. Equivalent to <i>помести́ть</i> (pomestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landlord; a noble who owns landed property</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) serfholder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) landowner</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) manor; manorial</li></ol><p><i>поме́щик</i> (poméščik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>pomi d'oro</i>, plural of <i>pomo d'oro</i>, archaic form of <i>pomodoro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tomato</li></ol><p><i>помидо́р</i> (pomidór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tomato</li></ol><p><i>помидо́р</i> (pomidór) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>помидо́р</i> (pomidór)</li></ol><p><i>помидо́р</i> (pomidór) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pardon, mercy</li></ol><p><i>поми́ловать</i> (pomílovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show mercy <i>(to)</i>, to pardon, to grant pardon <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ми́ловать</i> (mílovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>besides, aside from, apart from, in spite of</li><li>beside</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>мимо</i> (mimo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer for the dead, remembrance of the dead in prayer</li><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) remembrance, wake; memorial</li></ol><p><i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to remember, to recall</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mention</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) to pray for the health living or the repose of the soul of the dead, to commemorate</li><li>to squeeze, to press, to wrinkle for a while</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wake, funeral repast</li></ol><p><i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-к</i> (-k)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commemoration, memorial</li></ol><p><i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овение</i> (-ovenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>continually, constantly</li></ol><p><i>помину́тный</i> (pominútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>occurring every minute</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) constant, continual</li><li>per minute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory</i>) to die</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-мира́ть</i> (-mirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's peace <i>(with)</i>, to be reconciled <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>помири́ть</i> (pomirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to recollect, to think of, to keep in mind</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) to think, to consider, to imagine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>помьнити</i> (pomĭniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pomьněti</i> or <i>&#42;pomьniti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;minḗˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn-eh₁-</i> (present stem <i>&#42;mn-eye-</i>), from the root <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, mind”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>помьнѣти</i> (pomĭněti), Polish <i>pomnieć</i>, Slovene <i>pomniti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to remember, to recollect</li></ol><p><i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to multiply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>помно́жить</i> (pomnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to multiply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мно́жить</i> (mnóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to aid, to assist materially [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with/using what’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to avail, to work (to have the desired effect)</li></ol><p>Compare to Serbo-Croatian <i>pomagati</i>, Polish <i>pomagać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>help!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swill, slops (mixture of solid and liquid food scraps)</li><li>swill, slops, lap (any disgusting or distasteful food, weak and distasteful drink)</li></ol><p>Related to Bulgarian <i>поми́я</i> (pomíja, “sewage, swill, slops”), Polish <i>pomyje</i> (“slops, swill”), Old Church Slavonic <i>помꙑѩ</i> (pomyję). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-мои</i> (-moi), combining form of Russian <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ, “to wash”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) garbage dump, rubbish heap</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dustbin, ashcan, garbage can</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slovenly place or dwelling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) junk (disorderly collection of unnecessary objects)</li></ol><p><i>помо́и</i> (pomói) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garbage, slops, swill</li></ol><p><i>помо́и</i> (pomói) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to proclaim as bridegroom or bride</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrothal, engagement</li></ol><p><i>помо́лвить</i> (pomólvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engaged, betrothed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray (to petition a higher being)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>моли́ться</i> (molítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become younger</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>молоде́ть</i> (molodétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep silent, to be silent (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coast-dweller <i>(inhabitant of White Sea and Barents Sea coast)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrinkle (to pucker or become uneven or irregular)</li><li>to wince, to make a wry face</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мо́рщиться</i> (mórščitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaboard, littoral region, coastal area</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pomorьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>море</i> (more) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Pomeranian (of Slavic tribe)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) coaster (a person who originates from or inhabits a coastal area)</li></ol><p><i>помо́рье</i> (pomórʹje) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dais, stage, platform, rostrum</li><li>scaffold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake (something) for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to aid, to assist materially [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘whom’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with/using what’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to avail, to work (to have the desired effect)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) belt, tape, or restraint to hold, support, or suspend something or someone (<i>also figurative</i>)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) suspenders (US), braces (Britain)</li><li>(historical,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) leading-string</li><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) a gathering of friends and neighbors to help in some project (<i>such as a quilting bee, a barn-raising bee</i>)</li><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) synonymous with по́мощь (pómoščʹ): help, assistance, aid, support</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;megʰ-</i>, whence English <i>might</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pomoťь</i>. Doublet of <i>по́мощь</i> (pómoščʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>associate, assistant, deputy</li><li>helper, aid</li><li>helpmate</li></ol><p><i>по́мощь</i> (pómoščʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Pronunciation from native <i>помо́чник</i> (pomóčnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, assistance, aid</li><li>relief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>помощь</i> (pomoštĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pomoťь</i>. Doublet of <i>помо́чь</i> (pomóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pump</li><li>pomp, state, ostentatious splendor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomposity</li></ol><p>From <i>помпе́зн(ый)</i> (pompézn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pompous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pompeian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pompom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to cover with gloom</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obscure</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to outshine, to surpass, to overshadow</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make sad, to sadden</li></ol><p><i>помрачи́ть</i> (pomračítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkening</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) exceeding, overshadowing</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) obscuring</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) saddening</li><li>darkness, gloom</li></ol><p><i>помрачи́ть</i> (pomračítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to cover with gloom</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to obscure</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to outshine, to surpass, to overshadow</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make sad, to sadden</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>помрачити</i> (pomračiti). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мрачи́ть</i> (mračítʹ). Doublet of <i>поморо́чить</i> (pomoróčitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become gloomy, to darken</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мрачне́ть</i> (mračnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play (some) music, to make (some) music (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>музици́ровать</i> (muzicírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cloud (the mind), to dull (the senses)</li><li>to incite to rebel</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be nauseated</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мути́ть</i> (mutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scurry, to dash, to race, to tear along</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мча́ться</i> (mčátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> accusative] to push around, to order around</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-мыкать</i> (-mykatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, design, intention</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>помыслить</i> (pomyslitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think <i>(about)</i>, to dream <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to wash oneself</li></ol><p><i>помы́ть</i> (pomýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) thought (especially in the plural), aspiration, inclination, intention</li></ol><p><i>помышля́ть</i> (pomyšljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think <i>(about)</i>, to dream <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-мышля́ть</i> (-myšljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to remember, to recall</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mention</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) to pray for the health living or the repose of the soul of the dead, to commemorate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pomęnǫti</i>. Related to Old Church Slavonic <i>помѧнѫти</i> (pomęnǫti), Ukrainian <i>пом'яну́ти</i> (pomʺjanúty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rumpled, crumpled, jammed, battered</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unkempt, sloppy, seedy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to crumple, to wrinkle, to fumble, to crease</li><li>to dent</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press, to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to crease, to wrinkle</li></ol><p><i>помя́ть</i> (pomjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, droppings, excrement (usually of animals)</li><li>litter, brood, farrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) clipping of <i>поня́тно</i> (ponjátno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) a quantity of, a lot of</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial,&#32;productive,&#32;used with verbs) to indicate pointless action</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>на-</i> (na-)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run in large numbers, to come running</li></ol><p><i>пона-</i> (pona-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to observe for a while, to watch for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>наблюда́ть</i> (nabljudátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up or invent in large quantities</li></ol><p><i>пона-</i> (pona-) +&lrm; <i>выду́мывать</i> (vydúmyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>наде́яться</i> (nadéjatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need, to be needed, to become necessary</li><li>to come in handy</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>на́доба</i> (nádoba) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism) immigrant, alien, nonresident</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>понае́хать</i> (ponajéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come in large numbers (usually in negative context)</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>нае́хать</i> (najéxatʹ, “to come in large numbers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in vain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;childish</i>) for fun, not seriously, in pretence</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>наро́чно</i> (naróšno, naróčno), modified colloquially.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) from/by hearsay</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>наслы́ш(аться)</i> (naslýš(atʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gradually build (a lot of something)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>настро́ить</i> (nastróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first, in the beginning, initially</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>against one’s will</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>нево́ле</i> (nevóle)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>понедѣльникъ</i> (ponedělĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ponedělьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-, “after”) +&lrm; <i>неде́ль-</i> (nedélʹ-, “Sunday; week”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), meaning a day after Sunday.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) inasmuch as, as, since, because</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пон҄еже</i> (ponʹježe), cognates include Bulgarian <i>поне́же</i> (ponéže).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bit by bit, little by little (small amount at a time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>понемно́гу</i> (ponemnógu)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry (for a while; on foot)</li><li>to bear; to suffer (loss)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to become pregnant</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush off <i>(after)</i>, to dash off <i>(after)</i>, to tear along <i>(after)</i></li></ol><p><i>понести́</i> (ponestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pony (small horse)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pony</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower, to reduce</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to demote</li></ol><p><i>пони́зить</i> (ponízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decrease, to go down, to drop (<i>e.g. of temperature, prices, a rate, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>понижа́ть</i> (ponižátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fall, lowering, drop, reduction</li><li>demotion</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) depression</li></ol><p><i>пони́зить</i> (ponízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low, reduced</li><li>depressed (<i>of a mood</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower, to reduce</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to demote</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ни́з(кий)</i> (níz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), a factitive construction.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decrease, to go down, to drop (<i>e.g. of temperature, prices, a rate, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пони́зить</i> (ponízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low</li><li>along the ground, close to the ground</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>низу</i> (nizu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, <i>(plants)</i> to wilt</li></ol><p><i>пони́кнуть</i> (poníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, <i>(plants)</i> to wilt</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comprehension, understanding</li><li>conception, sense, interpretation</li></ol><p><i>понима́ть</i> (ponimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, to comprehend, to see</li><li>to realize, to appreciate</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ). Related to <i>пойма́ть</i> (pojmátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>knowingly, consciously</li><li>intelligently</li></ol><p><i>понима́ющий</i> (ponimájuščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>understanding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) sexton, sacristan</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twaddler, babbler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarrhea</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) too much of something that is incapable to stop itself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry something (for a while)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wear clothes, to shoes (for a while)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to vilify, to criticize, to insult</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause (bad) diarrhea</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ).</p><p>Possibly from <i>поно́с</i> (ponós) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Unlikely from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ), as <i>по-</i> (po-) is almost always a perfectivizing prefix.</p><p><i>поно́с</i> (ponós) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel nostalgic for a while, to spend some time feeling nostalgic</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ностальги́ровать</i> (nostalʹgírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insult, offense, slander, vilification, obloquy, defamation</li><li>reproach, affront</li><li>shame, dishonor</li></ol><p><i>поноси́ть</i> (ponosítʹ, “to vilify, to criticize, to insult”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-worn, frayed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prematurely aging</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shabby, tacky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>like</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>нра́виться</i> (nrávitʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sea (especially in the name of certain seas known to the Greeks)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sea</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) victim of a rook</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) diverting one's attention during a theft</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) an act of showing off</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>ponte</i> (“punter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pickerel weed (<i>Pontederia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pontederia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pontic</li><li>Pontius</li></ol><p>Sense one is <i>Понт</i> (Pont, “Pontus”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p><p>Sense two is Latin <i>Pontius</i>, with -ius substituted by -ский (-skij) due to the analogous function as relational noun suffixes.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) to punt (to play against the bank, to bet against the bank)</li></ol><p>French <i>ponter</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pontiff</li><li>(sovereign) pontiff, the Pope</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pontifex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pontoon, ponton</li><li>pontoon bridge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ponton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to force (someone to do something)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ну́дить</i> (núditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to force (someone to do something)</li></ol><p><i>пону́дить</i> (ponúditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doughnut</li><li>(figurative,&#32;humorous) fat person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pączek</i>. Piecewise doublet of <i>пучо́к</i> (pučók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doughnutery (a doughnut shop)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) doughnut</li></ol><p>From <i>по́нчик</i> (pónčik, “doughnut”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) up to the present <i>(time)</i>, to this day</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ны́не</i> (nýne)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to sniff</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinch of snuff</li></ol><p><i>понюхать</i> (ponjuxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idea, concept, conception, notion</li><li>comprehension</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;slang) informal rules of the criminal underworld</li></ol><p><i>поня́ть</i> (ponjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conceptual</li><li>based not on law but on informal rules (slang, from the criminal slang meaning of "поня́тия" as the informal rules of the criminal underworld)</li></ol><p><i>поня́тие</i> (ponjátije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apt, quick-witted (mentally keen)</li></ol><p><i>поня́ть</i> (ponjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>understandably, comprehensibly</li><li>clearly, plainly</li></ol><p><i>поня́тный</i> (ponjátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obviously, of course, understandably, clearly, needless to say, unsurprisingly</li></ol><p>Literally, “(it's an) understandable affair/matter”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>understandable, intelligible, clear (capable of being understood)</li></ol><p><i>поня́ть</i> (ponjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness (at an official search or arrest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, to comprehend, to see</li><li>to realize, to appreciate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat lunch; to have lunch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>обе́дать</i> (obédatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>обеща́ть</i> (obeščátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate (with), to converse, to mix (with) (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>обща́ться</i> (obščátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пообе́дать</i> (poobédatʹ).</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>обѣ́дать</i> (obě́datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aloof (at or from a distance)</li><li>further off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>singly, one by one, one at a time</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>одино́чке</i> (odinóčke)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>осторо́жнее</i> (ostoróžneje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternately (succeeding by turns)</li><li>alternatively</li></ol><p><i>поочерёдный</i> (poočerjódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternating, done in turn</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>о́чередь</i> (óčeredʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encouragement, incentive, reward</li></ol><p><i>поощри́ть</i> (pooščrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encourage, to incentivise, to abet (to support, uphold, or aid)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поощрити</i> (pooštriti), featuring regressive palatalization across "р" (<i>стр</i> &gt; <i>щр</i>). Another example of palatalization across a liquid is found in the conjugation of <i>слать</i> (slatʹ). Doublet of <i>поостри́ть</i> (poostrítʹ, “to spend some time sharpening, to crack some jokes”), which does not feature such palatalization. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>подстрека́ть</i> (podstrekátʹ).</p><p>Also compare typologically:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encourage, to incentivise, to abet (to support, uphold, or aid)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поощрꙗти</i> (pooštrjati), featuring regressive palatalization across "р" (<i>стр</i> &gt; <i>щр</i>). Another example of palatalization across a liquid is found in the conjugation of <i>слать</i> (slatʹ). Compare native Russian <i>заостря́ть</i> (zaostrjátʹ, “to sharpen, to emphasize”), which does not feature such palatalization. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>о́стрый</i> (óstryj) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>подстрека́ть</i> (podstrekátʹ).</p><p>Also compare typologically:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) priest</li><li>pop, popular music</li><li>pop art</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>попъ</i> (popŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>πάπας</i> (pápas), variant of <i>πάππας</i> (páppas, “daddy, papa”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>pop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pop-up, especially an error window.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pop-up</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pop-it</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Pop-It</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;informal) rear, bottom, butt (buttocks)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unpleasant situation</li></ol><p><i>попада́(ть)</i> (popadá(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-во</i> (-vo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fiction</i>) accidental traveller</li></ol><p><i>попа́сть</i> (popástʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́нец</i> (-ánec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit</li></ol><p><i>попада́ть</i> (popadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit (a target)</li><li>to get (to), to come (upon), to fall (into), to find oneself (in), to hit (upon)</li><li>to fall one after another</li></ol><p><i>попа́д-</i> (popád-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get caught</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to come across, to chance upon, to meet</li></ol><p><i>попада́ть</i> (popadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) priestess, priest's wife or widow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пападïꙗ</i> (papadïja), Greek <i>παπαδιά</i> (papadiá), cognate with Ukrainian <i>попадя́</i> (popadjá), Belarusian <i>пападздзя́</i> (papadzdzjá) or <i>пападзьдзя́</i> (papadzʹdzjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn or scorch (for a while)</li><li>to burn or scorch (to some extent)</li><li><i>same as</i> спали́ть (spalítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit (a target)</li><li>to get (to), to come (upon), to fall (into), to find oneself (in), to hit (upon)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) The impersonal past with an interrogative, location or time adverb indicates something occurring at random.</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(simile,&#32;colloquial) to get into hot water</li></ol><p>Literally, “to get like a cock into the soup”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get caught</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to come across, to chance upon, to meet</li></ol><p><i>попа́сть</i> (popástʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smell slightly (of)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>па́хнуть</i> (páxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reproach <i>(with)</i>, to blame</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пеня́ть</i> (penjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternately, in turn</li></ol><p><i>попереме́нный</i> (popereménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternate</li><li>alternating (such that it alternates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to push forward, to barge (<i>through</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to pour out, to flow out, to erupt, to gush out, to burst forth</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to push/thrust/chuck/turn/drive out</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to haul</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dismiss, to discharge, to fire</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diameter</li></ol><p><i>поперёк</i> (poperjók, “across”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cross-</li><li>transverse, crosswise</li><li>lateral</li></ol><p>From <i>поперёк</i> (poperjók, “across”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>перха́ть</i> (perxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>across, athwart</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>across, crosswise</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;перёк</i> (&#42;perjók, “crosswise”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perkъ</i>. Underlying root found in Old East Slavic <i>перекъ</i> (perekŭ, “width, crossbeam”) as well as Belarusian <i>пе́рек</i> (pjérjek, “across”); Ukrainian <i>пере́ка</i> (peréka, “contradiction”); Church Slavonic <i>прѣкꙑ</i> (prěky, “across”); Bulgarian <i>пря́ко</i> (prjáko, “straight, across”); Serbo-Croatian <i>при̏јек</i> (“steep”); <i>при̏јекӣ</i> (“straight, nearest”); <i>при̏јеко</i> (“through”); Slovene <i>prek</i> (“across (<i>adverb</i>)”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), also <i>preko</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>); <i>prèk</i> (“across (<i>preposition</i>)”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>); Czech <i>příka</i> (“steepness”); <i>příč</i> (“width”); Slovak <i>priek</i> (“resistance”); Pannonian Rusyn <i>прейґ, прейґа</i> (prejg, prejga, “across, over”); Polish <i>w poprzek</i> (“across, crosswise”) or obsolete Polish <i>przeko</i> (“across”); Upper Sorbian <i>prěki</i> (“across”); Lower Sorbian <i>pŕeki</i> (“across”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) ass (the buttocks of a person)</li></ol><p><i>по́п(а)</i> (póp(a), “ass”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) trustee, guardian</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) warden, administrator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tutorial, custodial, supervisory</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) guardianship, trustee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) trusteeship, guardianship</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) board of guardians</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink slowly, to sip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take to drink, to drink occasionally, to take to the bottle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty style</i>) to trample</li><li>to defy, to flout, to scorn</li><li>to violate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write <i>(for some time, for a while)</i>, to do some writing</li><li>to paint, to spend some time painting</li><li>to pee, to pee-pee, to tinkle, to wee</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пи́сать</i> (písatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to write (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink (for a while or a little amount)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;informal,&#32;diminutive) rear end, bottom, buttocks</li><li>parrot</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) supervisor</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) monkey wrench, pipe wrench, adjustable spanner</li></ol><p><i>по́па</i> (pópa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Clipping of <i>попуга́й</i> (popugáj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>popcorn (a snack food made from corn kernels popped by dry heating)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>popcorn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) popcorn</li></ol><p><i>попко́рн</i> (popkórn) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim for a while, to float for a while</li><li>to sail for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пла́вать</i> (plávatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>float <i>(for angling)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry/weep <i>(for a while, a little)</i>, to shed a few tears, to have a cry</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay <i>(with)</i> <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ться</i> (platítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spit several times</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spit for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push off, to drag oneself along</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start swimming, to start floating</li><li>to start sailing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hesitate or be unsure</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang,&#32;figuratively) to lose one's nerve</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) son of a priest; clergyman</li></ol><p><i>поп</i> (pop) +&lrm; <i>-ович</i> (-ovič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a guerrilla movement on both sides of the middle part of Volga river and along the whole Medveditsa river. The movement was active during the Russian Civil War from February to May 1921.</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;religion) the Old Believer communities who recognize any form of ordained priesthood. See <i>Popovtsy</i>.</li></ol><p>Named after its leader Fedor Popov.</p><p>From <i>поп</i> (pop, “a priest”) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a bit later</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>по́зже</i> (pózže)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carousal, drinking party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two (equal parts)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) it’s all the same; it doesn’t matter</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>по</i> (po, “across; along”) +&lrm; <i>пола́м</i> (polám, “halves (dative)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>по́лзать</i> (pólzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) inclination, impulse</li><li>feeble effort</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>&#42;попльзновениѥ</i> (&#42;poplĭznovenije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>попльзнѫти сѧ</i> (poplĭznǫti sę, “to slip, to go wrong”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (start) creeping, crawling</li><li>to start moving slowly</li><li>to start spreading <i>(of rumours)</i></li><li>to start fraying, ravelling out <i>(of fabric)</i></li><li>to start slipping, collapsing <i>(of soil)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>replenishment</li><li>(<i>military</i>) reinforcement, fresh forces, additional staff</li></ol><p><i>попо́лнить</i> (popólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to replenish, to fill up, to supplement, to enrich, to widen, to enlarge</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reman, to reinforce, to replace, to beef up</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-по́лнить</i> (-pólnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to be replenished, <i>(knowledge)</i> to widen</li></ol><p><i>попо́лнить</i> (popólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to replenish, to fill up, to supplement, to enrich, to widen, to enlarge</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reman, to reinforce, to replace, to beef up</li></ol><p><i>попо́лнить</i> (popólnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to be replenished, <i>(knowledge)</i> to widen</li></ol><p><i>пополня́ть</i> (popolnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the afternoon; p.m.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>after midnight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to utilize for some time [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзоваться</i> (pólʹzovatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spoil</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become politically (more) right-wing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reparable, remediable</li></ol><p><i>попра́вить</i> (poprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to improve, to repair</li><li>to adjust, to put/set right</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get well, to recover, to be well again</li><li>to gain weight, to put on weight</li><li>to correct oneself</li><li><i>(business etc.)</i> (<i>intransitive</i>) to improve</li></ol><p><i>попра́вить</i> (poprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repairing, mending</li><li>correction, amendment</li><li>recovery (a return to normal health)</li></ol><p><i>попра́вить</i> (poprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>get well soon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>get well soon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to improve, to repair</li><li>to adjust, to put/set right</li></ol><p><i>попра́вить</i> (poprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get well, to recover, to be well again</li><li>to gain weight, to put on weight</li><li>to correct oneself</li><li><i>(business etc.)</i> (<i>intransitive</i>) to improve</li></ol><p><i>поправля́ть</i> (popravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice/practise, to exercise [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>], [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘on something’</small>] (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>практикова́ть</i> (praktikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to violate, to humiliate</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to trample</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>попьрати</i> (popĭrati), composed as if from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>прать</i> (pratʹ, “to crush, to stomp; to go against, to resist”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrati</i>; cognate with <i>попере́ть</i> (poperétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be chairman/president <i>(of)</i> (for a while)</li><li>to chair, to preside <i>(over)</i>, to be in the chair, to take the chair (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>председа́тельствовать</i> (predsedátelʹstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disapprovingly</i>): to reproach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for something done or not done in the past’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>попрёк</i> (poprjók) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add slightly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to welcome, to greet, to salute (<i>also figuratively, of a decision or proposal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>приве́тствовать</i> (privétstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field, arena (figurative sense)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>попьрище</i> (popĭrište). Equivalent to <i>попр-</i> (popr-) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test, to attempt, to try</li><li>to taste (to sample the flavor of something)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>про́бовать</i> (próbovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask for, to request [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘for something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘for something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li></ol><p><i>попроси́ть</i> (poprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simply, merely, just</li><li>simply (without ceremony or embarrassment, easily)</li><li>simply. quite simply, plainly (put simply/directly)</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>просто́й</i> (prostój). Compare Polish <i>po prostu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar, cadger, bum (person who begs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to beg, to panhandle</li></ol><p><i>попроша́йник</i> (poprošájnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say goodbye, to bid farewell</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>проща́ться</i> (proščátʹsja, “to say goodbye”), from <i>проща́ть</i> (proščátʹ) (see there for more). Cognate with <i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump, to spring, to leap</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гать</i> (prýgatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reproach, reproof, rebuke (mild rebuke, or an implied criticism)</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;прёк</i> (&#42;prjok, “crosswise”), from <i>&#42;перёк</i> (&#42;perjók); see <i>поперёк</i> (poperjók). Original meaning "objection".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>expressive</i>) pop music</li><li>low-grade profit-oriented music</li><li>any low-grade still popular thing</li><li>any contemporary work of art (literature, film etc.), that is created solely to make short-term profits</li><li>a cliche, an overused idea or concept</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parrot (bird)</li><li>parrot (person)</li><li>a showy and gaudily dressed person; popinjay</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) a jocular unit of length, presumably equal to the length of a parrot</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from Dutch <i>papegaai</i>. Attested since 1589.</p><p>The sense of a unit of length originates from the Soviet animated cartoon «Тридцать восемь попугаев» («Tridcatʹ vosemʹ popugajev») (1976), where animals measured the length of a boa constrictor by comparing him to a parrot.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>попуга́й</i> (popugáj), a parrotlet.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare a little</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>populism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>populist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>popularization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>popularity</li></ol><p><i>популя́рный</i> (populjárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>popular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) population</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>populatio</i>, from Latin <i>populus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) potpourri; medley</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pot-pourri</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to overlook, to acquiesce</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conniver, condoner</li></ol><p><i>попусти́ть</i> (popustítʹ, “to connive, overlook”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) connivance; permissiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>попусти́тель</i> (popustítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to overlook, to acquiesce, to condone</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in vain, uselessly</li></ol><p>From <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>пу́сту</i> (pústu), old short dative of <i>пусто́й</i> (pustój, “futile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>путеше́ствовать</i> (putešéstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a ride (a passing vehicle that someone uses to hitchhike)</li></ol><p><i>попу́тный</i> (popútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in passing, on one's way, incidentally, at the same time</li></ol><p><i>попу́тный</i> (popútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passing, following</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow traveller</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пут(ь)</i> (put(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attempt, to try</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пыта́ться</i> (pytátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attempt, try, effort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow smoke (<i>occasionally</i>)</li><li>to puff on [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a pipe, cigar, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p>Has the form and meaning of a diminutive verb, constructed of <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-пых-</i> (-pyx-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ), apparently based on a no-longer-existent verb (compare <i>пыхте́ть</i> (pyxtétʹ, “to puff, to pant”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) to push back, to cause to move back or back up</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;nonstandard) to expel, to oust</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to steal</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пя́тить</i> (pjátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move back (<i>usually slowly</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>пя́титься</i> (pjátitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) backwards, reverse</li></ol><p><i>попя́тить</i> (popjátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest's son, young son of a priest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>season</li><li>weather</li><li>period</li><li>pore</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work for some time, to do some work, to put in some work</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enslaver</li><li>conqueror, oppressor</li></ol><p><i>поработи́ть</i> (porabotítʹ, “to enslave”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enslave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поработити</i> (porabotiti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enslave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>порабощати</i> (poraboštati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enslavement, enthrallment, servitude, bondage (the act of enslaving or the state of being a slave)</li></ol><p><i>поработи́ть</i> (porabotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overtake, to come alongside</li><li>to become evenly</li></ol><p><i>поравня́ть</i> (poravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to oblige, to take care</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>раде́ть</i> (radétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gladden, to please, to rejoice, to make happy</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ра́довать</i> (rádovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice (be happy) for a while</li></ol><p><i>пора́довать</i> (porádovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to affect, to strike, to defeat (to infect or harm)</li><li>to amaze, to strike, to stagger, to startle</li></ol><p><i>порази́ть</i> (porazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be surprised/astonished/thunderstruck <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>поража́ть</i> (poražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>striking, impressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defeatist</li><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) A person, often part of a racial or social minority, that has been stripped of some or all of their rights.</li><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) A person arrested for doubting Soviet Union's victory over Nazi Germany.</li></ol><p>Coined by Russian writer Grigory Aleksinsky as a derivation of <i>пораже́ние</i> (poražénije, “defeat”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defeat</li><li>(<i>law</i>) deprivation</li><li>(<i>military</i>) striking, hitting</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) affection, disease</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poraženьje</i>. Derived from <i>порази́ть</i> (porazítʹ, “to strike, defeat”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije, “noun-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defeatism</li></ol><p>From <i>пораже́нец</i> (poražénec) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пораже́ние</i> (poražénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) afflicted, stricken</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend some time taking apart / disassembling</li><li>to spend some time analysing</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>разбира́ть</i> (razbirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strikingly</li></ol><p><i>порази́тельный</i> (porazítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, striking, startling, astonishing</li></ol><p><i>порази́ть</i> (porazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to affect, to strike, to defeat (to infect or harm)</li><li>to amaze, to strike, to stagger, to startle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poraziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be surprised/astonished/thunderstruck <i>(by)</i></li></ol><p><i>порази́ть</i> (porazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to think over, to give some thought <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>размы́слить</i> (razmýslitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ponder for a while, to meditate for a while [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘about/over someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>размышля́ть</i> (razmyšljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to wound, to injure, to hurt (<i>usually not severely</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ра́нить</i> (ránitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a bit earlier</li></ol><p>по- (<i>po-</i>) + раньше (<i>ránʹše</i>)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become overgrown</li></ol><p><i>порасти́</i> (porastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become overgrown</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear (rend)</li><li>to tear apart</li><li>to tear out</li><li>to break off (a relationship)</li><li>to break [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off, to be torn</li></ol><p><i>порва́ть</i> (porvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) kerb, curb</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ребро́</i> (rebró) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an act of sexual intercourse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pornography, porno</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thin, to thin out, to get thin</li><li>to be depleted, to thin out</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>реде́ть</i> (redétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting</li><li>a pattern carved on wood or metal; carving</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leek (vegetable)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Porrei</i>, from French <i>porrée</i>, from Latin <i>porrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recommend, to advise</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рекомендова́ть</i> (rekomendovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to decide, to settle <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to finish off, to do away <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>porous</li></ol><p>From <i>по́ра</i> (póra) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>по́ристий</i> (pórystyj), Belarusian <i>по́рысты</i> (pórysty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censure, animadversion (criticism, a critical remark)</li><li>blame, reprobation</li><li>reproof, reproval, reprehension</li><li>reflection, reflexion</li><li>dispraise</li></ol><p><i>порица́ть</i> (poricátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>condemnatory, disapproving</li></ol><p>From <i>порица́ть</i> (poricátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blame, to reproach, to censure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>порицати</i> (poricati). By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-рица́ть</i> (-ricátʹ). Related to <i>речь</i> (rečʹ), <i>-река́ть</i> (-rekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unstitching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) whipping, flogging</li></ol><p><i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female porn star, adult actress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male porn star, porn actor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pornbot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pornographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pornographic</li></ol><p><i>порногра́фия</i> (pornográfija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pornography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porn shoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) pornography, porno</li></ol><p><i>по́рн(о)</i> (pórn(o)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) porn</li></ol><p><i>порну́ха</i> (pornúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>equally (in equal parts)</li></ol><p>Adapted from <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshold, doorstep</li><li>rapid (<i>turbulent flow section in a river</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porgъ</i> (“threshold”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;parˀgas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;porgʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) threshold, doorstep</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rapid</li></ol><p><i>поро́г</i> (poróg) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) breed, kind, race, species, sort (all animals or plants of the same species or subspecies)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) rock, layer</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) form (Arabic verb conjugation type or class)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Bulgarian <i>порода</i> (poroda), Carpathian Rusyn <i>порода</i> (poroda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoroughbred, pedigree, pure-bred</li><li><i>(person)</i> of <i>(high)</i> breeding</li></ol><p><i>поро́да</i> (poróda) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth</li><li>to beget, to cause, to entail, to generate</li><li>to engender, to give rise</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make related</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>родни́ть</i> (rodnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth</li><li>to beget, to cause, to entail, to generate</li><li>to engender, to give rise</li></ol><p><i>породи́ть</i> (porodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>result, outcome, consequence</li><li>product, child</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(river)</i> full of rapids</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty (devoid of content)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>порожьнъ</i> (porožĭnŭ), from earlier пороздьнъ (porozdĭnŭ), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porzdьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>пра́здный</i> (prázdnyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vehicle without cargo</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idle talk, babble</li></ol><p><i>поро́жний</i> (poróžnij) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without a burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apart, separately</li></ol><p><i>по́-</i> (pó-) +&lrm; <i>рознь</i> (roznʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn pink</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>розове́ть</i> (rozovétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at times, now and then</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vice (bad or undesirable habit)</li><li>flaw, defect, blemish</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) mangonel, catapult</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>порокъ</i> (porokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porokъ</i>. Related to <i>порица́ть</i> (poricátʹ).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>порокъ</i> (porokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foam rubber (expanded polyurethane, often used as a padding material, e.g. in furniture)</li></ol><p>Coined in the 1960s after the Norwegian company Porolon A/S, which sold foam rubber to the USSR.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1956 spelling of <i>паро́м</i> (paróm, “ferry”).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pormъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Northern Russia</i>) cattle, bull</li><li>(<i>Pskov</i>) boar</li><li>(<i>Siberia</i>) uncastrated domestic deer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoots, young growth, verdure</li><li>shrub</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) youth, young people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine&#32;or&#32;humorous) young pig, piglet</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) pig (a dirty, unscrupulous, or mean person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поросѧ</i> (porosę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porsę</i> (“piglet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piglet, young pig</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porsę</i> (“piglet”) + <i>-о́нок&#47;-ёнок</i> (-ónok&#47;-jónok, “diminutive suffix for baby animals”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>prȃse</i>, Czech <i>prase</i>, Slovak <i>prasa</i>, Polish <i>prosię</i>, Slovene <i>prašič</i>, Bulgarian <i>прасе</i> (prase).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rip (a garment) at the seams, to unstitch</li><li>to flog, to flagellate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>in expressions</i>)</li><li>to fuck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пороти</i> (poroti, “cut, rip”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come undone, to rip, to get unstitched/unpicked</li><li>(mildly&#32;vulgar,&#32;euphemistic) to do the deed, to meddle</li></ol><p><i>пороть</i> (porotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gunpowder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>порохъ</i> (poroxŭ, “dust”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porxъ</i>. Doublet of <i>прах</i> (prax).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gunpowder</li></ol><p><i>по́рох</i> (pórox) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defame, to discredit, to smear</li></ol><p><i>поро́к</i> (porók) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depravity, viciousness</li><li>fallaciousness</li></ol><p><i>поро́чный</i> (poróčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immoral, vicious, depraved, wanton</li><li>faulty, defective, flawed, fallacious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>порочныи</i> (poročnyi), from <i>порокъ</i> (porokŭ) +&lrm; <i>-ныи</i> (-nyi). By surface analysis, <i>поро́к</i> (porók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circular definition</li><li>begging the question, circular argument, petitio principii</li><li>vicious circle, Catch-22, self-sustained trouble</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>circulus vitiōsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freshly-fallen snow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to snow lightly</li><li>to fall lightly (<i>of snow</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poršiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по́рох</i> (pórox) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) powder; powdered</li><li>powderlike</li></ol><p><i>порошо́к</i> (porošók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powder-like, powdery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>powder</li></ol><p><i>по́рох</i> (pórox) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at times, now and then</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>port, harbor</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) port</li></ol><p>First dated 1702, from English <i>port</i> or from German <i>Port</i>, finally from Latin <i>portus</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>port</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Port Said (city in Egypt)</li></ol><p><i>По̀рт-Саи́д</i> (Pòrt-Saíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portal</li><li>gantry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>portable</li></ol><p>From French <i>portatif</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>портати́вний</i> (portatývnyj), Belarusian <i>партаты́ўны</i> (partatýwny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>port wine</li><li>(<i>Soviet context</i>) tonic wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Portwein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter, stout (a strong, dark ale, originally favored by porters)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portico</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>porticus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spoil, to ruin, to mar, to damage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to corrupt, to spoil, to deflower (virgins)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пъртити</i> (pŭrtiti, “spend”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъrtiti</i>. Cognate to <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ), <i>портно́й</i> (portnój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be off, to deteriorate, to go bad</li><li>to spoil</li></ol><p><i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pants, trousers</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пъртъ</i> (pŭrtŭ, “piece of cloth (sg)”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъrtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallet, purse, porte-monnaie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>porte-monnaie</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>portō</i> (whence multiple descendants, compare close related English <i>portfolio</i>, Russian <i>портфе́ль</i> (portfélʹ), Russian <i>портфо́лио</i> (portfólio)) + Latin <i>monēta</i> (whence English <i>money</i>, English <i>mint</i>, Russian <i>моне́та</i> (monéta)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailorbird</li></ol><p><i>портно́й</i> (portnój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sartorial, tailor's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailor, clothier (person who makes, repairs, or alters clothing as a profession)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tailor</li></ol><p><i>портно́й</i> (portnój) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>портня́га</i> (portnjága): tailor</li></ol><p><i>портня́га</i> (portnjága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sartorial, tailor's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) port</li></ol><p><i>порт</i> (port) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portrait, picture</li><li>portrayal (of), picture (of), description (of)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Porträt</i>, from French <i>portrait</i>. Further from Latin <i>prōtrahō</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>drawing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portraitist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigarette case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>porte-cigares</i> or <i>porte-cigare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Portuguese person</li></ol><p><i>Португа́лия</i> (Portugálija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Portuguese</li></ol><p><i>Португа́лия</i> (Portugálija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swordbelt, knife belt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>porte-épée</i> (“swordbelt”). Ultimately from Latin <i>portō</i> (whence multiple descendants, compare close related English <i>portfolio</i>, Russian <i>портфе́ль</i> (portfélʹ), Russian <i>портфо́лио</i> (portfólio)) + Latin <i>spatha</i> (whence English <i>spade</i>, Russian <i>шпа́га</i> (špága)), further from Ancient Greek <i>σπάθη</i> (spáthē).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefcase</li><li>portfolio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>portefeuille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portfolio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>portfolio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porter, doorman, receptionist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>portier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain, door curtain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>portière</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) footwrap, portyanki (a strip of cloth worn around the feet)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;slang,&#32;figurative) wall of text</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;Russia or Russians) a person trusting the government, believing any propaganda, following leaders</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to axe, to fell, to chop, to cut (<i>with an axe</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mocking, taunt, jeer, insult, humiliation</li><li>desecration</li></ol><p><i>поруга́ть</i> (porugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quarrel <i>(with)</i>, to break <i>(off) (with)</i></li></ol><p><i>поруга́ть</i> (porugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bail, surety, guarantee, responsibility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to guide, to direct, to manage, to run (to conduct or direct with authority) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>руководи́ть</i> (rukovodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charge, to commission, to entrust, to instruct</li></ol><p><i>поручи́ть</i> (poručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) to vouch [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>поруча́ть</i> (poručátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assignment, commission, errand</li><li>(economics,&#32;financial) order</li></ol><p><i>поручи́ть</i> (poručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handrail</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) lieutenant, first lieutenant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>porucznik</i>, from Old Czech <i>poručník</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>lieutenant Rzhevsky (a fictional young cavalry officer, coarse and vulgar, who figures in numerous popular Russian jokes that poke fun at social sophistication and decorum)</li></ol><p>The surname Ржевский is the adjective of Ржев (Rzhev), a small but very old town situated on the Volga River between Moscow and Minsk.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guarantor</li></ol><p><i>поручи́ть</i> (poručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bail, surety</li><li>guarantee, pledge</li><li>reference (statement that vouches for someone)</li></ol><p><i>поручитель</i> (poručitelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charge, to commission, to entrust, to instruct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warrant, to guarantee, to certify, to answer for, to vouch for [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to assure, to promise</li><li>to bet, to be sure</li></ol><p><i>поручи́ть</i> (poručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to tear down</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disrupt, to frustrate, to ruin</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) Porphyry.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal mantle (<i>garment</i>)</li><li>(<i>phycology</i>) red alga</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) porphyry</li></ol><p>From <i>порфи́р</i> (porfír) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flit, to flutter, to fly about</li><li>(ironical,&#32;deprecative) to hop, to move <i>(from place to place)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrxati</i>/<i>&#42;pъrxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flit, to flap one's wings <i>(once)</i>, to fly quickly, to dart</li></ol><p>From <i>порха́ть</i> (porxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portion</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>porcja</i>, from Latin <i>portio</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>по́рція</i> (pórcija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoiling</li><li>damage</li><li>corruption</li><li>evil curse, hex</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ, “to spoil, damage”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piston; sucker, plunger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish, to angle (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>рыба́чить</i> (rybáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gust (a strong, abrupt rush of wind)</li><li>fit (sudden outburst of emotion)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>порыва́ть</i> (poryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break <i>(off)</i> <i>(with)</i>, to desert</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to endeavour</li></ol><p><i>порыва́ть</i> (poryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>impulsively, impetuously</li></ol><p><i>поры́вистый</i> (porývistyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impulsive, impetuous</li><li>jerky (<i>movement</i>)</li><li>gusty (<i>wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>поры́в</i> (porýv) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig <i>(in)</i></li><li>to burrow <i>(in)</i>, to rake <i>(over, through)</i></li><li>to rummage <i>(in)</i>, to ransack</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ры́ться</i> (rýtʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ordinal number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ordinal</li><li>serial</li></ol><p><i>поря́док</i> (porjádok) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>index number, serial number</li><li>atomic number (number of protons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rather, pretty, a great deal; properly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(proper)</i> order</li><li>order, sequence</li><li>order, procedure, manner, way, form</li><li>order, regime, system</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) customs, usages, observances</li><li>(<i>military</i>) order, array</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) order</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porędъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ряд</i> (rjad) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>order</i> (<&lt; Latin <i>ōrdō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decently, honestly, respectably</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fairly well</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quite a bit</li></ol><p><i>поря́дочный</i> (porjádošnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decency, honesty, integrity</li></ol><p><i>поря́дочный</i> (porjádošnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decent, honest, respectable</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) decent, sizable, considerable</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) utter, out-and-out</li></ol><p><i>поря́док</i> (porjádok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) trading quarter <i>(situated outside city wall)</i></li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) suburb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat, to set</li><li>to plant</li><li>to put in jail</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planting</li><li>embarkation</li><li>(trains,&#32;airplanes) boarding</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) landing</li></ol><p><i>посади́ть</i> (posadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a mayor in some East Slavic cities, towns, and oblasts.</li><li>(historical,&#32;specifically) the mayor or leader of Novgorod and Pskov.</li></ol><p>Old Church Slavonic <i>посадити</i> (posaditi) + <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Can be analysed as <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Named because originally, the posadniks were appointed by the Prince of Kievan Rus as his governors, but were later elected by veche councils.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) planting</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) boarding</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) landing, alighting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>поса́дочный тало́н</i> (posádočnyj talón); boarding pass</li></ol><p><i>поса́дка</i> (posádka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boarding pass (a document to board a plane, ship, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>planted, seated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;dialectal) to greet</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сала́мкаться</i> (salámkatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snort, to sniff</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сопе́ть</i> (sopétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sparkle slightly, to glint (<i>periodically</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сверка́ть</i> (sverkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lighten, to become lighter</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>светле́ть</i> (svetlétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>посвисте́ть</i> (posvistétʹ)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>свиста́ть</i> (svistátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>свиста́ть</i> (svistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devote, to dedicate (various senses)</li><li>to initiate (into), to let (into)</li><li>to ordain (into), to consecrate (into)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be initiated or ordained into (a rank, position, title, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>посвяти́ть</i> (posvjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devote, to dedicate (various senses)</li><li>to initiate (into), to let (into)</li><li>to ordain (into), to consecrate (into)</li></ol><p><i>посвяти́ть</i> (posvjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be initiated or ordained into (a rank, position, title, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>посвяща́ть</i> (posvjaščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dedication, devotion</li><li><i>(a secret)</i> initiation <i>(into)</i></li><li>consecration <i>(into)</i>, initiation <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>посвяти́ть</i> (posvjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sowing</li><li>crops</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) plating, seeding, inoculation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>посе́ять</i> (poséjatʹ), but structurally as if from nonexistent imperfective <i>&#42;посева́ть</i> (&#42;posevátʹ); compare prefixed imperfectives in <i>-сева́ть</i> (-sevátʹ), e.g. imperfective <i>обсева́ть</i> (obsevátʹ) to perfective <i>обсе́ять</i> (obséjatʹ), imperfective <i>засева́ть</i> (zasevátʹ) to perfective <i>засе́ять</i> (zaséjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(period of) sowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sowing</li></ol><p><i>посе́в</i> (posév) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become gray, to turn gray</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>седе́ть</i> (sedétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement</li><li>colony</li><li>location</li><li>accommodation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to lodge</li><li>to inspire, to engender</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to take up one's residence</li></ol><p><i>посели́ть</i> (poselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) village, settlement</li></ol><p><i>посёлок</i> (posjólok) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) rustic (person from a rural area)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to lodge</li><li>to inspire, to engender</li></ol><p><i>посели́ть</i> (poselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to take up one's residence/quarters, to make one's home</li></ol><p><i>поселя́ть</i> (poseljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, thence <i>(office terminology, dated)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coat with silver, to silver-plate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>серебри́ть</i> (serebrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become silvery</li></ol><p><i>посеребри́ть</i> (poserebrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the middle, halfway along</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the middle</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>середи́не</i> (seredíne)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become gray</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to become gloomy</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to fade (<i>e.g. of talent</i>), to become commonplace</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сере́ть</i> (serétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get serious, to become serious</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>серьёзнеть</i> (serʹjóznetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visitor, caller</li></ol><p><i>посети́ть</i> (posetítʹ, “to visit”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call on</li><li>to attend <i>(lectures)</i></li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>посѣтити</i> (posětiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posětiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, to complain <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>се́товать</i> (sétovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendance (frequency of one's presence)</li></ol><p><i>посеща́емый</i> (poseščájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to visit, to call on</li><li>to attend <i>(lectures)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>посѣщати</i> (posěštati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visit, call</li><li>attendance</li></ol><p><i>посети́ть</i> (posetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow, to drill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose, to squander</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to spread (an idea, fear etc.)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (village) gathering, sit-round gathering</li></ol><p>From <i>посидеть</i> (posidetʹ). Compare Polish <i>posiadówka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>within one's strength, abilities, powers (of a job, task)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning blue</li></ol><p><i>посине́ть</i> (posinétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn/become blue</li><li><i>(skin)</i> to turn blue/livid</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сине́ть</i> (sinétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gallop away</li><li>to hop away (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip and fall, to especially on ice</li></ol><p><i>поскользну́ться</i> (poskolʹznútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip and fall, especially on ice</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>inasmuch as, as</li><li>since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) the male hemp plant</li></ol><p><i>по́скон(ь)</i> (póskon(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the male hemp plant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poskonь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>скоре́е</i> (skoréje)</li><li>quick!; hurry!; hurry up!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch for a while, to scrape for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>скрести́</i> (skrestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeak, to creak <i>(faintly)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sparing with, to be stingy with, to skimp on, to scrimp on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘praise, love, money, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>скупи́ться</i> (skupítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>посло́вица</i> (poslóvica, “proverb”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indulgence, leniency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, envoy, ambassador</li></ol><p><i>посла́ние</i> (poslánije) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>message, missive</li><li>(literature,&#32;religion) epistle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posъlanьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, envoy, minister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch</li><li>to swear <i>(at someone)</i>, to tell someone to get lost</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posъlati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;informal,&#32;euphemistic) to send (someone) packing, to tell (someone) to F off</li></ol><p>Literally, “to send onto the three letters”. Euphemism of <i>посла́ть на́ хуй</i> (poslátʹ ná xuj, “to tell (someone) to fuck off”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after (in time)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>later, afterwards</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poslě</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>AD (anno Domini); CE (Common Era)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>по́сле Рождества́ Христо́ва</i> (pósle Roždestvá Xristóva, “after Christ's birth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Anno Domini (in the year of our Lord)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when pigs fly ("never", expressed by an idiom describing an event that cannot possibly ever occur)</li></ol><p>Literally, “after a rain on Thursday”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards, thereafter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>post-, after-</li></ol><p>From <i>по́сле</i> (pósle, “after”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postnuptial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) aftertaste (a taste of something that persists when it is no longer present)</li></ol><p>From <i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>вкус</i> (vkus) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>post-war</li></ol><p><i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;humorous) China's final warning, empty threat</li></ol><p>Literally, “final Chinese warning”, in reference to the People's Republic of China issuing over 900 unfulfilled "final warnings" to the United States between 1958 and 1964, due its military jets patrolling the Taiwan Strait.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last, latest, recent</li><li>final, ultimate, definitive</li><li>latter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) worst</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>послѣдьнъ</i> (poslědĭnŭ), from <i>послѣди</i> (poslědi) + <i>-ьнъ</i> (-ĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poslědьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower</li></ol><p><i>после́довать</i> (poslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ); cognate with Bulgarian <i>последовател</i> (posledovatel)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consistently (in a consistent manner)</li><li>in series (about connection)</li><li>in sequence (in programming)</li></ol><p><i>после́довательный</i> (poslédovatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consistency, logicality</li><li>succession, sequence, series</li></ol><p><i>после́довательный</i> (poslédovatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fibonacci sequence (sequence of numbers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consistent, logical</li><li>successive</li><li>step-by-step</li><li>sequential</li></ol><p><i>после́довать</i> (poslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow (move continually behind someone or something)</li><li>to follow (in sequence), to be next</li><li>to follow (logically, consequentially), to be a consequence</li><li>to follow (to be guided by something, act in accordance with something) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consequence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the following</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following, subsequent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>day after tomorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>за́втра</i> (závtra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) postposition</li></ol><p>From <i>после</i> (posle) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ), by analogy with <i>предло́г</i> (predlóg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon (part of the day between noon and evening)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postprandial, after-lunch, after-dinner</li></ol><p><i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>обе́д</i> (obéd) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in three days</li></ol><p>From <i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>послеза́втра</i> (poslezávtra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postrevolutionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afterword, epilogue, postamble</li></ol><p>From <i>после-</i> (posle-) +&lrm; <i>сло́в(о)</i> (slóv(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) post-Soviet (after the dissolution of the Soviet Union)</li><li>(geopolitics,&#32;international relations) post-Soviet (relating to the countries which emerged from the dissolution of the Soviet Union, especially in the context of Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>после-</i> (posle-, “after-”) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proverb, saying, adage (<i>communicating a lesson or important truth</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poslovica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informatics</i>) word-by-word, word-serial</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve (for some time)</li><li>to serve, to act, to work <i>(as)</i>, to be</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li><li><i>(in a monastery)</i> work of penance</li></ol><p><i>послу́шать</i> (poslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to listen, to listen to (someone/something)</li><li>to attend</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to auscultate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey, to take (someone's) advice, to listen to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>послу́шать</i> (poslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religious</i>) novice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obediently</li></ol><p><i>послу́шный</i> (poslúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obedient, dutiful, amenable, agreeable (willing to comply with)</li></ol><p><i>послу́шать</i> (poslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>oboediēns</i> (whence English <i>obedient</i>) (<&lt; <i>audiō</i>), Ancient Greek <i>ὑπήκοος</i> (hupḗkoos) (<&lt; <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō)), Dutch <i>gehoorzaam</i> (<&lt; <i>horen</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be heard</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be felt, to be smelled</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;perfective only) to be misheard</li></ol><p><i>послы́шать</i> (poslýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>после</i> (posle).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>после́дний</i> (poslédnij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chuckle</li><li>to laugh, to poke fun [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘at someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>посмея́ться</i> (posmejátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>posthumously (after death)</li></ol><p><i>посме́ртный</i> (posmértnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>posthumous</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>смерть</i> (smertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, to make bold</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сметь</i> (smetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laughing stock</li></ol><p><i>посмеши́ть</i> (posmešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh, to chuckle</li><li>to laugh <i>(at)</i>, to mock <i>(at)</i>, to make fun, to scoff</li><li>to joke, to say in jest</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>смея́ться</i> (smejátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look</li><li>to see, to watch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to search, to look for</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>allowance, benefit, pension, dole; gratuity</li><li>handbook, manual, tutorial, textbook</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, ultimately a univerbation corresponding to <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>себе́</i> (sebé). See also <i>посо́бник</i> (posóbnik, “accomplice, abettor”), <i>подсо́бный</i> (podsóbnyj, “supplementary”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dole, unemployment benefit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplice, abettor</li></ol><p><i>пособѝть</i> (posobìtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to counsel</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сове́товать</i> (sovétovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult</li><li>to confer, to deliberate, to exchange views</li></ol><p><i>посове́товать</i> (posovétovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confer for a while (<i>e.g. of a jury</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>совеща́ться</i> (soveščátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abet (to support, uphold, or aid) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to assist, to promote, to facilitate [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>соде́йствовать</i> (sodéjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambassador; legate</li><li>salting (adding salt to food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>посълъ</i> (posŭlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posъlъ</i>. Probably originally deverbal from the ancestor of <i>посла́ть</i> (poslátʹ), or alternatively from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;po-</i> + the deverbal form of <i>&#42;sъlati</i>.</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сол(ить)</i> (sol(itʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt</li><li>to pickle, to corn</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>соли́ть</i> (solítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become tipsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) embassy</li><li>ambassadorial</li></ol><p>From <i>посо́л</i> (posól) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij) or (based on the presence of -ь-) from <i>посо́ль(ство)</i> (posólʹ(stvo)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embassy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posъlьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>посо́л</i> (posól, “ambassador”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compete, to contend, to emulate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>соревнова́ться</i> (sorevnovátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, stick</li><li>crosier</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>посохъ</i> (posoxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posoxъ</i>. Akin to <i>соха́</i> (soxá, “wooden plough”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sympathize <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сочу́вствовать</i> (sočúvstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>по́сох</i> (pósox)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one last drink for the road, one for the road (usually in expressions with <i>на</i> (na) - <i>на посошо́к</i> (na posošók))</li></ol><p><i>по́сох</i> (pósox) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep, to have some sleep</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen</li><li>to make it, to be on time, etc.</li></ol><p><i>поспе́ть</i> (pospétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen</li><li>to make it, to be on time, etc.</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спеть</i> (spetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) to hurry, to hasten</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to hasten, to make haste, to be in a hurry</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hastily (in a hasty manner)</li></ol><p><i>поспе́шный</i> (pospéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurry, haste</li><li>rashness</li></ol><p><i>поспе́шный</i> (pospéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prompt, hasty, hurried, rash, thoughtless</li></ol><p><i>поспеш(и́ть)</i> (pospeš(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) common, general (<i>in Poland, Lithuania, Ukraine</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) common, commoner, plebeian (<i>in Poland, Lithuania, Ukraine</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>pospolity</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute, to argue (for some time)</li><li>to discuss, to debate (for some time)</li><li>to bet, to wager</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спо́рить</i> (spóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promote, to further, to favour/favor, to be conducive to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to assist [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бствовать</i> (sposóbstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>поспе́шный</i> (pospéšnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame, to disgrace, to dishonor</li><li>to expose, to debunk</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>срами́ть</i> (sramítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame, to disgrace, to dishonor</li><li>to expose, to debunk</li></ol><p><i>посрами́ть</i> (posramítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have a shit, to take a shit (<i>referring to the whole act, usually in an organised and intentional manner</i>)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;impersonal,&#32;usually in the infinitive even when translated with a finite verb) for (someone) to not give a shit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>not give a shit</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the middle of, in the midst of</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>среди</i> (sredi)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>посереди́не</i> (poseredíne)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>посереди́не</i> (poseredíne)</li></ol><p>Old Church Slavonic variant of <i>посереди́не</i> (poseredíne).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mediator, intermediary, negotiator</li><li>agent, broker, middleman</li><li>umpire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) intermediary, mediator, mediation</li></ol><p><i>посре́дник</i> (posrédnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mediation, intermediation, medium, agency</li></ol><p><i>посре́дник</i> (posrédnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indirectly</li><li>mediocre(ly), average(ly), middling(ly)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) average (mark), three (out of five)</li></ol><p><i>посре́дственный</i> (posrédstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mediocrity</li></ol><p><i>посре́дственный</i> (posrédstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mediocre</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) passable</li></ol><p><i>посре́дство</i> (posrédstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>by means of, with the help of, per, via</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to piss, to urinate</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;impersonal,&#32;usually in the infinitive even when translated with a finite verb) for (someone) to not give a shit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroil <i>(with)</i>, to cause to quarrel <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ссо́рить</i> (ssóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel, to fall out <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>поссо́рить</i> (possóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fast (period of restricted eating)</li><li>post, office, appointment, position, job</li><li>picket, outpost, guard post, station</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) post, message (online forums)</li></ol><p>Old Russian and pre-Reform <i>постъ</i> (post), as it were Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;postъ</i>, a loan from Old High German <i>fasta</i> (“fast”) (8th century, a feminine noun from the root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fastāną</i>), presumably first in this sense in Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (fastan), gloss of <i>νηστεία</i> (nēsteía).</p><p>From the French <i>poste</i>, ca. 1700.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>post</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>post- (later)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pair of millstones or rollers used for grinding grain, one of which is stationary and the other rotates</li><li>manner of keeping the body in a given position, setting</li><li>a mill (or loom?) for weaving handicrafts</li><li>cloth generated on a loom</li><li>a piece or roll of canvas</li><li>method of fishing</li><li>delivery</li><li>stand (for holding objects up)</li><li>storage container for spirits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place, to set, to stand, to station (In a vertical position)</li><li>to apply, to put on (a compress)</li><li>to put on stage, to stage, to produce, to present (a play)</li><li>(<i>gambling</i>) to wager, to place a bet, to stake</li><li>to raise, to put (a question)</li><li>to deliver, to supply, to furnish, to market, to purvey</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery, supply, shipping</li></ol><p><i>поста́вить</i> (postávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to supply</li></ol><p><i>поста́вить</i> (postávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplier, provider, purveyor; caterer</li></ol><p><i>поста́вить</i> (postávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щи́к</i> (-ščík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestal, base</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>постановля́ть</i> (postanovljátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>постанови́ть</i> (postanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) staged event, faked situation, prearranged performance</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>постано́вка</i> (postanóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decree, to enact, to decide, to resolve</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>станови́ть</i> (stanovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) staging, production</li><li>performance, play</li><li>raising, erection</li><li>organization, arrangement, setting</li><li>fitting, placement, insertion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resolution, decision</li><li>decree, enactment</li></ol><p><i>постанови́ть</i> (postanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decree, to enact, to decide, to resolve</li></ol><p><i>постанови́ть</i> (postanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;theater) production, staging</li></ol><p><i>постано́вка</i> (postanóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage manager, director, producer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>стона́ть</i> (stonátʹ): to groan a little, to groan sometimes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стона́ть</i> (stonátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to attempt</li><li>to endeavour/endeavor, to make an effort</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стара́ться</i> (starátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, to age, to advance in age</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>старе́ть</i> (starétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to lay, to make (<i>bed</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed (<i>a piece of furniture to sleep on</i>)</li><li>(<i>singular only</i>) bedding (<i>bedclothes</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posteľь</i>. Analyzable as deverbal from <i>постели́ть</i> (postelítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sex scene (segment in a movie in which characters have sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bed</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) bedroom</li></ol><p><i>посте́ль</i> (postélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) <i>same as</i> <i>посте́ль</i> (postélʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gradually, step by step, by and by, progressively</li></ol><p><i>постепе́нный</i> (postepénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gradual, progressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>poster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be afraid, to be ashamed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to be afraid of, to be ashamed of [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стесня́ться</i> (stesnjátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend</li><li>to befall, to happen (to) (<i>esp. of misfortune</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пости́гнуть</i> (postígnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend</li><li>to befall, to happen (to) (<i>esp. of misfortune</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;postignǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-сти́гнуть</i> (-stígnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding, comprehension, comprehending, grasp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comprehensible</li></ol><p>Related to постига́ть (postigátʹ), пости́гнуть (postígnutʹ), пости́чь (postíčʹ, “comprehend, grasp, befall”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postindustrial</li></ol><p><i>пост-</i> (post-) +&lrm; <i>индустриа́льный</i> (industriálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, to wash cloth or clothes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to post</li><li>(archaic,&#32;dialectal) to observe (a fast)</li></ol><p><i>пост</i> (post, “post”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p><p><i>пост</i> (post, “fast”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Compare with Old Church Slavonic <i>постити</i> (postiti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fast (abstain from food or drink)</li></ol><p><i>пост</i> (post) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend</li><li>to befall, to happen (to) (<i>esp. of misfortune</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-стичь</i> (-stičʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) post-Keynesian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>post-Keynesian</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postcommunist</li></ol><p><i>пост-</i> (post-) +&lrm; <i>коммунисти́ческий</i> (kommunistíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to lay, to make (<i>bed</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стлать</i> (stlatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(art,&#32;philosophy) postmodernism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postmodernist</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>postmodernist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>postmodern</li></ol><p><i>постмодерни́ст</i> (postmodɛrníst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Christianity</i>) Lenten oil (any vegetable oil or, rarely, fish oil)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;modern usage) sunflower oil</li></ol><p>Literally, “Lenten oil”; during Lent, Orthodox Christians cannot consume butter, and therefore substitute it with vegetable oil or (formerly and more rarely) fish oil.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leanness (of meat)</li><li>(humorous,&#32;colloquial) glumness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piety, sanctimoniousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lenten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lean</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pious, hypocritical, glum</li></ol><p><i>пост</i> (post) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postprocessing</li></ol><p><i>пост-</i> (post-) +&lrm; <i>об-</i> (ob-) +&lrm; <i>работ</i> (rabot, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on duty</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) sentry, guard</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) duty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sentry, guard, man on duty</li></ol><p><i>пост</i> (post) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Used in a main clause where <i>поско́льку</i> (poskólʹku, “insofar as, inasmuch as”) occurs in a subordinate clause.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) partly, a little bit, up to a point; half-heartedly; so-so</li></ol><p>From the conjunction <i>посто́льку поско́льку</i> (postólʹku poskólʹku, “insofar as, inasmuch as”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step aside, to make way</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сторони́ться</i> (storonítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange, outside, foreign</li><li>unauthorized</li><li>accessory, secondary</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger, outsider, foreigner</li><li>unauthorized person</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>сторо́н</i> (storón) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodger</li></ol><p><i>постоя́ть</i> (postojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coaching (referring to staying in a hotel or the like, especially in pre-Revolutionary Russia)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́льный</i> (-jálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) constant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Planck's constant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>constantly, always</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>постоя́ть</i> (postojátʹ), from <i>по-</i> (po-) + <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stojati</i> (“to stand”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constant, permanent</li><li>continual, continuous</li><li>direct <i>(current)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;technical) constant current (direct current without any variation in magnitude)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constancy, permanency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand <i>(for a while)</i></li><li>to stand up [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperative</i>) expresses a demand to stop or stop moving</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) post-punk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>post-punk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post-truth</li></ol><p>From <i>пост-</i> (post-) and <i>пра́вда</i> (právda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>injured, damaged, suffering, stricken</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) victim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to come to harm</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shoot (<i>many things</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot (<i>for a while</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scamp, imp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) monastic tonsure (rite of the adoption of monasticism, accompanied by shaving the top of the head)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>постри́чь</i> (postríčʹ), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim, to clip, to crop (<i>usually of hair</i>)</li><li>to tonsure</li></ol><p><i>постриг-</i> (postrig-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trim, to clip, to crop (<i>usually of hair</i>)</li><li>to tonsure</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, building (process of constructing)</li><li>plotting</li><li>structure</li><li>scheme, construct, reasoning</li></ol><p><i>постро́ить</i> (postróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>стро́гий</i> (strógij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>стро́го</i> (strógo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build</li><li>to upbuild</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a house for oneself</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to draw up, to form, to assume formation</li></ol><p><i>постро́ить</i> (postróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, structure (anything constructed)</li><li>building, construction (process)</li></ol><p><i>постро́ить</i> (postróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociology</i>) post-Sovietism (the social system of the post-Soviet countries, combining elements of Sovietism, Westernism, feudalism and&#47;or fundamentalism)</li></ol><p>Coined by Russian philosopher and writer Alexander Zinoviev in the 1970s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) post-Soviet (after the dissolution of the Soviet Union)</li><li>(<i>geopolitics</i>) post-Soviet (relating to countries which were formerly part of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>пост-</i> (post-) +&lrm; <i>сове́тский</i> (sovétskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>posttraumatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) post-traumatic stress disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tapping, rapping</li></ol><p><i>посту́кивать</i> (postúkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tap on, to rap on</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) translational (<i>motion</i>)</li><li>forward (<i>motion</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ongoing</li></ol><p><i>поступа́ть</i> (postupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act, to behave, to do things (in a certain way)</li><li>to treat (with), to deal (with), to handle</li><li>to enter, to join, to matriculate</li><li>to arrive, to come in, to be received, to be forthcoming</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abdicate, to forgo, to waive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act, to behave, to do things (in a certain way)</li><li>to treat (with), to deal (with), to handle</li><li>to enter, to join, to matriculate</li><li>to arrive, to come in, to be received, to be forthcoming</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abdicate, to forgo, to waive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrance, entering, joining, admission, intake</li><li>receipt, arrival</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) proceeds, earnings</li></ol><p><i>поступи́ть</i> (postupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act, action, behaviour, conduct, deed</li></ol><p><i>поступи́ть</i> (postupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Parallel with Bulgarian <i>постъпка</i> (postǎpka), Macedonian <i>постапка</i> (postapka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step, tread</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li><li>to rap, to tap</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li></ol><p><i>постуча́ть</i> (postučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>after the fact, ex post facto, post factum</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>post factum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(grammar,&#32;in the narrow sense) a suffix which follows an inflectional ending (<i>for example, -ся, -то, -либо, -нибудь in Russian</i>)</li><li>(grammar,&#32;in the wide sense) suffix, postfix (<i>of any variety</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>постыди́ть</i> (postydítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to annoy, to disgust [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>посты́лый</i> (postýlyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) undesired, disliked</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disgusting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disliked person, undesired person</li></ol><p><i>посты́ть</i> (postýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>посты́ть</i> (postýtʹ)</li></ol><p><i>посты́ть</i> (postýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time cooling down</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to become disgusting, to disgust</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>стыть</i> (stytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tableware, dishes, dishware</li><li>(glass, plastic or metal) container, in which food products or drinks are sold, such as jar, bottle, etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posǫda</i>. For more see <i>су́дно</i> (súdno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nautical,&#32;derogatory) tub, rustbucket (slow or poorly-maintained vessel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tableware, dishes, dishware</li></ol><p><i>посу́да</i> (posúda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishwasher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dishwashing</li></ol><p><i>посудомо́йка</i> (posudomójka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) scullery maid (a woman who washes dishes)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;colloquial) dishwasher (a machine for washing dishes)</li></ol><p><i>посу́да</i> (posúda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мо́йка</i> (mójka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishwasher</li></ol><p><i>посу́да</i> (posúda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мо́йщик</i> (mójščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) promise</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сули́ть</i> (sulítʹ). Possibly akin to <i>слать</i> (slatʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>prōmissum</i>, Ancient Greek <i>ἐπαγγελία</i> (epangelía) (<&lt; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos), whence <i>а́нгел</i> (ángel)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to promise</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сули́ть</i> (sulítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to have luck, to succeed in [<i>with</i> perfective infinitive]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>счастли́вый</i> (sčastlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate</li><li>to count</li><li>to think, to opine, to consider</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle accounts (with), to consider, to take into consideration, to reckon (with)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to be considered / reputed (someone), to be, to pass (for)</li></ol><p><i>посчита́ть</i> (posčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to count time to time</li></ol><p><i>посчита́ть</i> (posčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drive forward; premise; message</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li><li>to dispatch</li><li>to swear <i>(at someone)</i>, to tell someone to get lost</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posylàti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;informal,&#32;euphemistic) to send (someone) packing, to tell (someone) to F off</li></ol><p>Literally, “to send onto the three letters”. Euphemism of <i>посыла́ть на́ хуй</i> (posylátʹ ná xuj, “to tell (someone) to fuck off”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispatching, mailing, sending</li><li>package, parcel</li><li>premise, sumption</li></ol><p><i>посыла́ть</i> (posylátʹ, “to send, to dispatch”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, commissionaire</li><li>errand boy</li></ol><p><i>посыла́ть</i> (posylátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour (of friable/solid objects, such as sands, not liquids; sometimes used figuratively)</li><li>to sprinkle, to strew</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to start falling (of precipitation like rain or snow)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to start saying words frequently</li><li>to start saying quickly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to dash quickly (for many entities)</li><li>to scatter (on a surface)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>посы́пать</i> (posýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to fall down, to fall down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour down, to rain</li></ol><p><i>посы́пать</i> (posýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>посыпа́ть</i> (posypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streusels, sprinkles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encroachment, infringement (intrusion upon another's possessions or rights)</li></ol><p><i>посяга́ть</i> (posjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encroach on, to infringe on</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>сяга́ть</i> (sjagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encroach on, to infringe on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small settlement, village</li><li>town</li></ol><p>Formed of <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Cognate with Belarusian <i>пасёлак</i> (pasjólak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urban-type settlement</li><li>workers’ town; factory housing estate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>посе́в</i> (posév).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>посети́ть</i> (posetítʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>посеща́ть</i> (poseščátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>посѣти́ть</i> (posětítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweat, perspiration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>потъ</i> (potŭ, “sweat”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potъ</i> (“sweat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to hide, to conceal</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to conceal for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden, secret</li></ol><p><i>потаи́ть</i> (potaítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to connive, to indulge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) accessory, conniver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a dance, to dance <i>(for a while)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>танцева́ть</i> (tancevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shabby, worn, threadbare</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unkempt, seedy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry, to pull, to drag for some time</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry, to pull, to steal all or many things</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) strumpet, slut, prostitute</li></ol><p>Female form of <i>потаску́н</i> (potaskún).</p><p>For the relation with <i>таска́ться</i> (taskátʹsja), <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ), compare typologically French <i>traînée</i> (&lt; <i>traîner</i>). Also compare <i>волоки́та</i> (volokíta) related to <i>волочи́ться</i> (voločítʹsja), <i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>потаску́ха</i> (potaskúxa)</li></ol><p><i>потаску́ха</i> (potaskúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brawl, fracas, ruckus (fight)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conniver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start pulling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start carrying (unwillingly or something heavy)</li><li>to start stealing</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trail along</li><li>to drag oneself along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trail along <i>(behind)</i>, to follow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go, to drag oneself <i>(to a place)</i></li></ol><p><i>потащи́ть</i> (potaščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hidden, secret</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;mythology) beast, monster</li></ol><p>Unclear. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>потво́ра</i> (potvóra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>потво́р</i> (potvór)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>потво́ра</i> (potvóra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) connivance; indulgence</li></ol><p><i>потворить</i> (potvoritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indulge, to connive (at), to pander</li></ol><p><i>потво́рство</i> (potvórstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>darkened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to darken, to become dark</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>темне́ть</i> (temnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perspiration, sweating (process of perspiring)</li></ol><p><i>поте́ть</i> (potétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potential (currently unrealized ability)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) potential</li></ol><p>Derived from Late Latin <i>potentialis</i>, likely through German <i>Potenzial</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>potentially</li></ol><p><i>потенциа́льный</i> (potɛnciálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>potential</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potency (especially sexual)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>potentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming</li></ol><p><i>потепле́ть</i> (poteplétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become warmer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) injured, wronged</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) victim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be patient, to keep one's patience</li><li>to suffer</li><li>to suffer, to stand, to tolerate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss (something lost)</li><li>casualty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lost</li><li>embarrassed, perplexed</li><li>hopeless, ruined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose, to waste</li><li>to shed</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose one's head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lose face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get lost, to disappear</li><li>to lose one's way</li><li>to lose one's presence of mind, to get flustered</li></ol><p><i>потеря́ть</i> (poterjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to crowd</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тесни́ть</i> (tesnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make room</li><li>to sit or stand closer, to bunch up</li></ol><p><i>потесни́ть</i> (potesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweat, to perspire</li><li>to become misty, to steam up</li><li>to sweat over, to toil over</li></ol><p><i>пот</i> (pot) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fun, amusement</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>поте́шить</i> (potéšitʹ, “to amuse, to entertain”). Compare Ukrainian <i>поті́ха</i> (potíxa), Belarusian <i>паце́ха</i> (pacjéxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to flow</li><li>to start to leak, to leak</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to entertain</li></ol><p><i>поте́шить</i> (potéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse oneself, to make fun of</li></ol><p><i>потеша́ть</i> (potešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to entertain</li><li>to comfort, to console</li><li>to please</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>те́шить</i> (téšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse oneself, to have fun</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to jeer, to mock</li></ol><p><i>поте́шить</i> (potéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amusing, comical, funny</li></ol><p>From поте́ха (potéxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ποτήριον</i> (potḗrion), diminutive of <i>ποτήρ</i> (potḗr, “cup”), from <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “I drink”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>потихо́ньку</i> (potixónʹku); silently, quietly; slowly; gently; OK; so-so</li></ol><p><i>потихо́ньку</i> (potixónʹku) +&lrm; <i>-ечку</i> (-ečku)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>OK, so-so (in answer to the question, "как дела?" ("How are you?"), the response "потихоньку"—"OK" or "I'm getting along okay"—implies that things could be better and could be worse)</li><li>silently</li><li>gently</li><li>on the sly</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-оньку</i> (-onʹku)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potlatch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinook Jargon <i>potlatch</i> (“gift”), from Nootka <i>p̕ačiƛ</i> (“to give in ceremony”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(excessive) sweatiness, hyperhidrosis</li></ol><p><i>потли́вый</i> (potlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweaty</li><li>sweating easily</li></ol><p><i>пот(е́ть)</i> (pot(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddle blanket</li></ol><p><i>пот</i> (pot) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweaty</li></ol><p><i>пот</i> (pot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream</li><li>torrent</li><li>flow</li><li>assembly line production</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) thread</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a form of punishment in medieval Russia when a criminal was exiled and property was confiscated, later the criminal was enslaved and property given away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potokъ</i>. Morphologically <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ток</i> (tok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk <i>(with about)</i>, to have a <i>(short)</i> talk <i>(with about)</i></li><li>to discuss <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceiling</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) upper limit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) limit in someone's development</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) roof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potolъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;tьlo</i> (“ground, bottom”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-o-</i> (“ground”), from <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear, carry”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>तल</i> (tala, “surface, bottom”), Latin <i>tellūs</i> (“ground, land”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ceiling</li></ol><p><i>потоло́к</i> (potolók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, to crush, to grind (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on weight</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>толсте́ть</i> (tolstétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards, afterward, after that</li><li>then</li><li>later</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>по</i> (po, “after, upon”) +&lrm; <i>том</i> (tom, “that”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>descendant, offspring, progeny, scion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potomъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hereditary, ancestral</li><li>by birth</li></ol><p><i>пото́мство</i> (potómstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>posterity</li><li>descendants</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potomьstvo</i>. Parsable as <i>пото́м</i> (potóm) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, because of that, consequently</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>по</i> (po, “according to”) + <i>тому́</i> (tomú), dative singular of <i>тот</i> (tot, “that”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) because, since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, so</li></ol><p><i>потому́</i> (potomú) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person who answers questions</li></ol><p><i>потому́</i> (potomú) +&lrm; <i>-чка</i> (-čka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to go down</li><li>to drown</li><li>to be lost</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тону́ть</i> (tonútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, deluge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>потопъ</i> (potopŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potopъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stomp/stamp [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘one's feet’</small>]</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;low colloquial,&#32;humorous) to leave, to go away</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;regional&#32;or&#32;dated) to sink; to drown; to be lost</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>то́пать</i> (tópatʹ)</p><p><i>потоп-</i> (potop-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat for a while</li><li>to sink</li><li>to ruin, to destroy</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to heat”)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to sink”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to trample</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge</li></ol><p><i>потопта́ть</i> (potoptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to hasten, to precipitate, to rush</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>торопи́ть</i> (toropítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to be in a hurry, to hasten</li></ol><p><i>поторопи́ть</i> (potoropítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot-au-feu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pot-au-feu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly line production; streamlined</li></ol><p><i>пото́к</i> (potók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down, to spoil, to damage <i>(grass, crops, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li><li>to waste</li><li>to fritter away (time)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) need</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тре́ба</i> (tréba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consumer (person purchasing goods)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) consumer</li></ol><p>From <i>потреби́тель</i> (potrebítelʹ, “consumer”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consume, to use</li></ol><p>From <i>потре́ба</i> (potréba) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consumption (the act of consuming something)</li></ol><p><i>потреби́ть</i> (potrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consume, to use</li></ol><p><i>потреби́ть</i> (potrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>necessarily, obligatorily</li></ol><p><i>потре́бный</i> (potrébnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, want</li><li>requirement</li></ol><p><i>потре́бный</i> (potrébnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary, required, needed</li></ol><p><i>потре́ба</i> (potréba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Czech <i>potřebný</i> and Polish <i>potrzebny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demand, to request</li><li>to need, to call for</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need, to require</li><li>to take (time, effort, money, etc. for something)</li></ol><p><i>потре́бовать</i> (potrébovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>трево́жить</i> (trevóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull about, to blow about, to flutter, to tousle</li><li>to pat</li><li>to beat up, to maul, to pummel</li><li>to give a hard life/time</li><li>to mention too often, to make too many references <i>(to)</i>, to hackney</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to chap</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тре́скаться</i> (tréskatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crackling</li><li>crackle</li></ol><p><i>потре́скивать</i> (potréskivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crackle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тре́ск-</i> (trésk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тро́гать</i> (trógatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) giblets, bowels, viscera (internal organs of the body, especially animals)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potroxa</i>. According to Vasmer, related to Bulgarian <i>троха</i> (troha), Serbo-Croatian <i>troha</i>, Czech <i>trocha</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disemboweller</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) ripper, as in Jack the Ripper</li></ol><p><i>потроши́ть</i> (potrošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disembowel, to eviscerate, to gut</li><li>to paunch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rifle, to ransack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potrošiti</i>, from <i>&#42;trošiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, to labor, to toil</li><li>to bother (doing something)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>труди́ться</i> (trudítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, to brandish [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to shake, to rock</li><li>to amaze, to astound, to shock, to astonish</li></ol><p><i>потрясти́</i> (potrjastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stunningly, breathtakingly, amazingly, astonishingly</li><li>fantastically, sensationally, shockingly, strikingly</li><li>appallingly, horribly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>awesome!, super!</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>потряса́ющий</i> (potrjasájuščij, “terrific, fabulous”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awesome, stunning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shock</li></ol><p><i>потряс-</i> (potrjas-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incredible, awesome, zingy, super, tremendous, cool, killer, super-duper, jim-dandy</li></ol><p><i>потряса́ть</i> (potrjasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, to brandish</li><li>to shake, to rock</li><li>to amaze, to astound, to shock, to astonish</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shocked, astonished, dumbfounded, speechless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threadbare, shabby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) intense muscle contraction to eject something from the body, such as during childbirth</li><li>(usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;colloquial) strenuous, painful effort or attempt (usually unsuccessful)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;potǫga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower (one's eyes, gaze, etc.) (so as not to look at someone)</li><li>to dull a little, to blunt a little</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ту́пить</i> (túpitʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look down, to cast down one's eyes, to drop one's eyes</li></ol><p><i>поту́пить</i> (potúpitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lower (one's eyes, gaze, etc.) (so as not to look at someone)</li></ol><p><i>поту́пить</i> (potúpitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look down, to cast down one's eyes, to drop one's eyes</li></ol><p><i>потупля́ть</i> (potupljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to have fun together, to hang out</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>туси́ть</i> (tusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dim, to dull, to lose its lustre</li><li>to pale</li><li>to wane</li><li>to pale <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тускне́ть</i> (tusknétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supernatural, otherworldly</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>ту</i> (tu) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to die out (<i>of light, fire, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extinct (of volcanoes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extinct volcano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish, to smother</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to turn off (source of light)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to braise, to stew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to regale, to treat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>почьщивати</i> (počĭštivati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) back of the head, occiput</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to steal, to nick</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ты́рить</i> (týritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rap, to peck</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тю́кать</i> (tjúkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to compete, to contend, to go toe-to-toe [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>потяга́ть</i> (potjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke, to pull <i>(at)</i>, to draw <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тяг-</i> (tjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stretch (of a person, to take a stretch)</li></ol><p><i>потя́гивать</i> (potjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) stretching</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тяг-</i> (tjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ушки</i> (-uški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, to draw, to haul, to drag</li><li>to weigh</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stretch (of a person, to take a stretch)</li><li>to reach [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for something/someone’</small>]</li><li>to follow in succession</li></ol><p><i>потяну́ть</i> (potjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streak</li></ol><p>Deverbal from the underlying stem <i>поте́к-</i> (poték-) of <i>поте́чь</i> (potéčʹ, “to begin to flow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Potemkin village</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shabby, threadbare</li><li>(long form only,&#32;colloquial) emaciated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reduce a little, to cause to go down a little [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>уба́вить</i> (ubávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to diminish a little, to go down a little, to get a little less</li></ol><p><i>поуба́вить</i> (poubávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;Internet slang) immigrant from Russia, especially during the 2020s Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Formed similarly to понае́х (derogatory term for an immigrant to Russia, especially to its capital city Moscow), but with prefix <i>у-</i> (u-) the neologism is getting the opposite meaning (an immigrant <i>from</i> Russia). See уе́хать.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dine; to have dinner; to have supper (<i>refers to the 3rd meal of the day, served around 7 to 8 p.m.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>у́жинать</i> (úžinatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>умно́</i> (umnó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become wiser, to wisen</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>умне́ть</i> (umnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to act smart, to be a smartarse/smartass (for a while)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to overanalyse things, to overcomplicate things (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>у́мничать</i> (úmničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend some time practising, to practise for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>упражня́ться</i> (upražnjátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>About a general area</i>) To calm down; to quiet down, to die down [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative)].</li><li>To sucede, to subside.</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>утихнуть</i> (utixnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in the morning</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>утру́</i> (utrú)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take part, to participate, to collaborate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>уча́ствовать</i> (učástvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to teach, to instruct</li><li>to preach <i>(at)</i>, to lecture, to give a lecture</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lecture, sermon, lesson, homily</li></ol><p><i>поучи́ть</i> (poučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>instructive, didactic</li></ol><p><i>поучи́ть</i> (poučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach for a while</li><li>to study for a while</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study for a while</li><li>to learn for a while</li></ol><p><i>поучи́ть</i> (poučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mildly&#32;vulgar</i>) don't-give-a-damn attitude</li></ol><p>From <i>по́фиг</i> (pófig, “<i>(someone)</i> does not care”, predicative) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproval) one who doesn't care</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>фиг</i> (fig) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flex one's muscles</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flex (flaunt one's superiority or show off)</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>фле́ксить</i> (fléksitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flirt (to play at courtship)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>флирт</i> (flirt) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show off; to swank</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to boast; to brag; to put on airs</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to aggressively push or promote (something) in an artificial manner in order to generate forced hype, especially online</li></ol><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>форси́ть</i> (forsítʹ).</p><p>From <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>фо́рсить</i> (fórsitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) clipping of <i>по́хуй</i> (póxuj); don't give a fuck, don't give a shit, don't care</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>похуй</i> (poxuj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bawdy, raunchy, coarse, foul</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bawdry; obscenity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compliment, to praise</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>похвали́ть</i> (poxvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy, commendable, laudable</li><li>laudatory</li></ol><p><i>похвала́</i> (poxvalá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to praise, to compliment</li></ol><p><i>похвали́ть</i> (poxvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>похваля́ть</i> (poxvaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>похва́статься</i> (poxvástatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хва́стать</i> (xvástatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хва́статься</i> (xvástatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to cross out, to X out</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to destroy, to kill off, to put an end to</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to neglect, to forget about</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хер</i> (xer, “old name for the Cyrillic letter <i>Х</i> (X)”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abductor (kidnapper)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abduct, to kidnap</li><li>to purloin, to steal</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-хи́тить</i> (-xítitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abduct, to kidnap</li><li>to purloin, to steal</li></ol><p><i>похи́тить</i> (poxítitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft, kidnapping, abduction, hijacking</li></ol><p><i>похи́тить</i> (poxítitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хле́стче</i> (xléstče)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slap</li><li>to give a clap</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bother (to do something)</li><li>to spend some time bustling about</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хлопота́ть</i> (xlopotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pat, to clap, to tap</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) soup, pottage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take the hair of the dog that bit you, to freshen the nip (to take a shot in the morning to help make one's hangover go away)</li></ol><p><i>похме́лье</i> (poxmélʹje) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hangover</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хмель</i> (xmelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hangover</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drunk</li></ol><p><i>похме́лье</i> (poxmélʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a hair of the dog that bit you, to freshen the nip (take a shot in the morning to help make one's hangover go away)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) campaign, march</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) cruise</li><li>trip, walking tour, hike</li><li>tour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poxodъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solicit <i>(for)</i>, to apply <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хода́тайствовать</i> (xodátajstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look like, to resemble [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to walk <i>(for a while)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gait, walk (manner of walking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) march, marching, route</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) field, camp</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) field, mobile</li></ol><p><i>похо́д</i> (poxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gait, walk (manner of walking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) looks like, it seems that, apparently</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>похо́же</i> (poxóže), influenced by <i>по хо́ду де́ла</i> (po xódu déla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>похо́д</i> (poxód).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) on the move, hastily</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) at the same time, by the way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adventure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>it seems, apparently, it looks (as if)</li></ol><p><i>похо́жий</i> (poxóžij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity, likeness, resemblance</li></ol><p><i>похо́жий</i> (poxóžij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alike; similar [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooling (<i>of the weather</i>), cold snap, cold spell</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) cooling, worsening (<i>of relations between people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>похолода́ть</i> (poxolodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to become colder (<i>of the weather</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to become cold or colder</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to suffer from the cold, to shiver, to get chilled</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>холода́ть</i> (xolodátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow cold</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>холоде́ть</i> (xolodétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury, to inter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to conceal, to lay away</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хорони́ть</i> (xoronítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an official letter informing of a person killed in action, kiyi, death notification</li></ol><p><i>похоро́нный</i> (poxorónnyj, “funeral (relative adjective)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) funeral; funerary</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) somber, sad</li></ol><p><i>по́хороны</i> (póxorony) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial, funeral</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more beautiful, to grow prettier</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хороше́ть</i> (xorošétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lasciviousness, salacity, lewdness, lust</li></ol><p><i>похотли́вый</i> (poxotlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lustful, lewd, lascivious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poxotьlivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по́хоть</i> (póxotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;poxotь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ, “to want”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to exchange a triple kiss (<i>as Easter salutation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>христо́соваться</i> (xristósovatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow thin, to lose weight</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>худе́ть</i> (xudétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) indifference, disdain</li></ol><p><i>похуй</i> (poxuj, “who gives a damn?”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) someone showing indifference or disdain</li></ol><p><i>похуй</i> (poxuj) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) alternative form of <i>по́ хую</i> (pó xuju)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>по́ хую</i> (pó xuju). Compare with Polish <i>po chuj</i> and Ukrainian <i>по́хуй</i> (póxuj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) stupid person, jerk, putz</li><li>(slang,&#32;less common) penis, putz</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>פּאָץ</i> (pots, “penis, fool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch slightly, to mar slightly</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss, to give a kiss</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>целова́ть</i> (celovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol><p><i>поцелова́ть</i> (pocelovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kiss</li><li>kissing sound</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>поцелова́ть</i> (pocelovátʹ), based on the present stem (compare first singular <i>поцелу́ю</i> (pocelúju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) an ultranationalist, an extreme nationalist</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>поц</i> (poc, “fool”) +&lrm; <i>патрио́т</i> (patriót, “patriot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>почему́</i> (počemú); y (why)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maize ear</li><li>(<i>textile</i>) cop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to begin</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to begin to consume</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;počęti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-чать</i> (-čatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more often</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чаще</i> (čašče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth, soil, ground (mixture of sand and organic material)</li><li>basis, base, ground</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>подъшьва</i> (podŭšĭva, “sole (bottom); foundation”), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šiti</i> (“to sew”). Doublet of <i>подо́шва</i> (podóšva, “sole, (mountain) foot”).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>solum</i> (see also English <i>soil</i>) related to <i>solea</i> (whence English <i>sole</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) pochvennichestvo (a movement in the Russian Empire)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) soil</li></ol><p><i>по́чва</i> (póčva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) why (due to what, on what grounds)</li><li>(<i>indicative</i>) why (due to what, on what grounds)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>по</i> (po, “according to”) + <i>чему́</i> (čemú), dative singular of <i>что</i> (što).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for some reason, somewhy</li></ol><p>From <i>почему́</i> (počemú) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person, often a child, who asks a lot of questions</li></ol><p>From <i>почему́</i> (počemú, “why”) +&lrm; <i>-чка</i> (-čka). The word was inspired by a well-known Russian children's book titled <i>Что я ви́дел</i> (Što ja vídel, “What I saw”), which tells the story of a highly inquisitive boy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handwriting, hand (style of penmanship)</li><li><i>(individual manner of doing something)</i> hand, style</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blackened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn/become/grow black, to blacken</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>черне́ть</i> (černétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get, to draw, to derive</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>черпну́ть</i> (čerpnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li><li>(<i>textiles</i>) to card, to comb</li><li>to scratch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to give oneself the trouble</li></ol><p><i>почеса́ть</i> (počesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honour/honor, laurels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(often in plural) honour/honor, laurels</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to consider [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘as something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по́-</i> (pó-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ, “honor”)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ, “to consider”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be considered as [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>поче́сть</i> (počéstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) prurigo (simplex), pruritus</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) scrapie (a degenerative prion disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) scratch (an act of scratching the skin to alleviate an itch or irritation)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ушки</i> (-uški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>renal</li></ol><p><i>по́чка</i> (póčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) bedchamber</li></ol><p><i>почива́ть</i> (počivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to sleep, to rest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;počivati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest on one's laurels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiative</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beginning, first sale of the day</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>почина́ть</i> (počinátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to begin</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to begin to consume</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-чина́ть</i> (-činátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repair, to mend, to fix</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repair, mending, fixing</li></ol><p><i>почини́ть</i> (počinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, to scour</li><li>to peel</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) almost, nearly, nigh on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) it seems, seemingly, apparently</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honouring/honoring, respect, esteem</li><li>reverence, worship</li></ol><p><i>почита́ть</i> (počitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admirer, worshipper</li></ol><p><i>почти́ть</i> (počtítʹ, “to honor, revere”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read (for a while)</li><li>to esteem, to respect, to honour/honor</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to consider [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘as something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;počitati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ).</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be considered as [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>почита́ть</i> (počitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) to rest, to repose, to sleep</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary,&#32;poetic) to die, to pass away (<i>perfective only</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;počiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest on one's laurels</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kidney</li><li>bud</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>почька</i> (počĭka). Vasmer suggests derivation from печь (pečʹ, “bake”); compare <i>пе́чень</i> (péčenʹ, “liver”).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>почька</i> (počĭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъťьka</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>по́чки</i> (póčky), Polish <i>pestka</i>, Czech <i>pecka</i>, Slovene <i>реčkà</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bud, to gemmate, to propagate by gemmation</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to multiply</li></ol><p><i>по́чка</i> (póčka) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mail <i>(U.S.)</i>, post <i>(GB)</i></li><li>post office</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>poczta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mailman, postman, letter carrier, mail carrier (post office employee)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>postillon</i>, ultimately from Italian <i>postiglione</i>, influenced by <i>по́чта</i> (póčta), which is borrowed from Polish <i>poczta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post office (place)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Postamt</i>, influenced by <i>по́чта</i> (póčta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mailman, postman, letter carrier, mail carrier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respect, esteem, consideration, deference</li></ol><p><i>почт-</i> (počt-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respectable, honorable, eminent</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) considerable</li></ol><p><i>почте́ние</i> (počténije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost, nearly</li></ol><p>Imperative of <i>поче́сть</i> (počéstʹ, “count, consider”). Literally, “consider it so”, when something is almost true.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost everywhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>almost everywhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>expressive</i>) almost</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>respectfully</li></ol><p><i>почти́тельный</i> (počtítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respectful, deferential</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) respectable, considerable</li></ol><p><i>почт-</i> (počt-) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honor, to revere</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чтить</i> (čtitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) postmaster (in pre-revolutionary Russia and in speaking of other countries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Postmeister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) why (what for)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) why (due to what cause)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>что</i> (što).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postage stamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mail; postal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) post office</li></ol><p><i>по́чта</i> (póčta) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>postcode (sequence of letters and&#47;or numbers added to a postal address)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mailbox, letterbox (box into which mail is put)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) secret facility, military base</li></ol><p>In the sense of “secret facility”, from the Soviet-era rule of keeping the names of such facilities secret by addressing mail to a post office box number without a name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel (something)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act strange (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чуда́чить</i> (čudáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem <i>(to)</i>, to appear <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чу́диться</i> (čúditʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell</li><li>to feel, to sense</li><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) to hear</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how much?</li><li>how much does it cost?</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>how</li></ol><p><i>по</i> (po) +&lrm; <i>чём</i> (čom)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ): to scratch a bit or sometimes</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honour/honor, esteem, respect</li></ol><p>Analyzable as deverbal from <i>поче́сть</i> (počéstʹ) (compare past adverbial participle <i>почётши</i> (počótši)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honorable (worthy of respect)</li></ol><p><i>почёт</i> (počót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time stepping or pacing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to walk with measured steps, to pace</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шага́ть</i> (šagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>step-by-step, stepwise</li><li>turn-based</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шаг</i> (šag) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search for a while, to fumble around (for something)</li><li>to share something, especially digitally</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ша́рить</i> (šáritʹ)</p><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ша́рить</i> (šáritʹ), further derived from English <i>share</i> + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шатну́ть</i> (šatnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stagger, to shake, to lean on one side</li><li>to be shaken</li></ol><p><i>пошатну́ть</i> (pošatnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шата́ть</i> (šatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stagger, to reel, to sway on one's feet</li></ol><p><i>поша́тывать</i> (pošátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to move, to stir, to budge</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шевели́ть</i> (ševelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move, to stir, to budge</li></ol><p><i>пошевели́ть</i> (poševelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper for some time</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing</li><li>sewing many things</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>поши́ть</i> (pošítʹ), but constructed as if derived from a corresponding non-existent imperfective <i>&#42;пошива́ть</i> (&#42;pošivátʹ) (compare <i>обши́вка</i> (obšívka) from <i>обшива́ть</i> (obšivátʹ), <i>подши́вка</i> (podšívka) from <i>подшива́ть</i> (podšivátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend time sewing</li><li>to sew (many things)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck off!, go fuck yourself! (to a woman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck off!, go fuck yourselves! (to a group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) duty (tax&#59; tariff)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) duty (tax; tariff)</li></ol><p><i>по́шлина</i> (póšlina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>commonly, tritely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) vulgarity</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) vulgarism</li><li>(<i>aesthetic</i>) poshlost</li></ol><p>From <i>по́шлый</i> (póšlyj, “vulgar”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vulgar, commonplace, trivial, banal, trite</li><li>erotic, sexual, bawdy, pertaining to sexuality or sexual activity</li><li>(rare,&#32;by extension) pertaining to defecation, urination or farting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пошьлъ</i> (pošĭlŭ, “ancient, primordial, former, usual”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>обихо́дный</i> (obixódnyj), <i>расхо́жий</i> (rasxóžij) (<… <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vulgar person</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shallow person</li></ol><p><i>по́шлый</i> (póšlyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) something very vulgar, low-grade or trivial</li></ol><p><i>по́шлый</i> (póšlyj) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low&#32;colloquial</i>) why</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to joke, to jest</li><li>to trifle, to play (with)</li><li>to make fun (of)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>шути́ть</i> (šutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>begone!, go away! (<i>to a man</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fuck off!, go fuck yourself! (to a man)</li></ol><p>Literally, “go to the dick!” Past tense of verbs is often used to give rather rude commands. Compare Ukrainian <i>пішо́в на́ хуй</i> (pišóv ná xuj)!.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercy, quarter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spare, to have mercy (on)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щади́ть</i> (ščadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tickle</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щекота́ть</i> (ščekotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch (for some time), to have a pinch</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щипа́ть</i> (ščipátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tingling, prickling (sensation)</li><li>pinching (act)</li></ol><p><i>пощи́пывать</i> (poščípyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch (for some time), to have a pinch</li></ol><p><i>пощипа́ть</i> (poščipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel, to touch; to grope</li><li>to probe</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to snap, to click, etc. for a while [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a whip, one's heels, one's fingers, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to crack (nuts) for a while</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack (someone)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щёлкать</i> (ščólkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slap in the face, box on the ear</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>щека́</i> (ščeká, “cheek”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetry</li><li>charm, elegance, romantic appeal of something</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>poezja</i> (“poetry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem (large poem)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>poète</i>, from Latin <i>poēta</i>, itself from Ancient Greek <i>ποιητής</i> (poiētḗs). Doublet of <i>пии́т</i> (piít) and <i>пии́та</i> (piíta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phased, stage-by-stage, step-by-step</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>эта́п</i> (etáp) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literature,&#32;philosophy) poetics (theory of poetry)</li><li>poetic manner/style</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poetic</li></ol><p><i>поэ́т</i> (poét) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, therefore, that's why</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>по</i> (po, “according to”) + <i>э́тому</i> (étomu), dative of <i>э́то</i> (éto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth&#32;slang</i>) to use (for a while), to spend some time using</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ю́зать</i> (júzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>singing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear, to show up, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ться</i> (javítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, emergence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear, to show up, to emerge</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>явля́ться</i> (javljátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belt, girdle, band, waistband, cummerbund</li><li>zone, region</li><li>flange, collar, hoop</li><li>boom (of an arch)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pȍjāsъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asteroid belt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarification, explanation, elucidation</li></ol><p><i>поясни́ть</i> (pojasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clarify, to explain, to illustrate</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-ясни́ть</i> (-jasnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waist, loins, small of the back</li></ol><p><i>по́яс</i> (pójas) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lumbar (referring to the small of the back or the loins)</li></ol><p><i>поясни́ца</i> (pojasníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to the belt (attire); in reference to the belt or position on body thereof. Also spelled «поясный».</li></ol><p><i>пояс</i> (pojas) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>поясной</i> (pojasnoj)</li></ol><p><i>пояс</i> (pojas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clarify, to explain, to illustrate</li></ol><p><i>поясни́ть</i> (pojasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>по́яс</i> (pójas): small belt</li></ol><p><i>по́яс</i> (pójas) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cringe slightly</li></ol><p><i>поёжиться</i> (pojóžitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shiver, to shudder</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ёжиться</i> (jóžitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave mockingly/mischievously/frivolously/cynically, to make jabs towards others (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ёрничать</i> (jórničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>по́езд</i> (pójezd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пара́графы</i> (parágrafy, “paragraphs”)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) P (pawn, plural form of <i>п²</i> (p²))</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>пара́графы</i> (parágrafy).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>пе́шки</i> (péški).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) censored spelling of <i>пипе́ц</i> (pipéc)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) abbreviation of <i>приве́т</i> (privét); hi</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(typically in the form <i>и пр.</i> (i pr.)) abbreviation of <i>про́чее</i> (próčeje); other stuff, other things</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пра́порщик</i> (práporščik, “(military rank) ensign”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>great (<i>when used with ancestors and descendants</i>)</li><li>ur-, proto- (primitive, original)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pra-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) great-grandmother</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>ба́бка</i> (bábka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandmother</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>ба́бушка</i> (bábuška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>води́тельские права́</i> (vodítelʹskije pravá): driver's license</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human rights (the basic rights and freedoms that all humans should be guaranteed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth (conformity to fact or reality&#59; true facts)</li><li>justice</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) law, code</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>sure enough, indeed, actually, as a matter of fact, in point of fact</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) is that so?</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>though, however, but</li><li>admittedly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>правьда</i> (pravĭda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravьda</i>, derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravъ</i> (compare <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj)) plus an unclear suffix.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>пра́вда</i> (právda, “truth”), Belarusian <i>пра́ўда</i> (práwda, “truth”), Old Church Slavonic <i>правьда</i> (pravĭda, “justice, truth”), Bulgarian <i>пра́вда</i> (právda, “justice, truth, livestock capital”), Serbo-Croatian <i>пра̑вда</i> (“justice, truth, controversial matter”), Slovene <i>prȃvda</i> (“regulation, law, court case”), Czech <i>pravda</i> (“truth”), Slovak <i>pravda</i> (“truth”), Polish <i>prawda</i> (“truth”), Upper Sorbian <i>prawda</i> (“truth”), Lower Sorbian <i>pšawda</i> (“truth”). Compare derived adjective <i>пра́ведный</i> (právednyj).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Правда)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pravda ("The Truth") was a leading newspaper of the Soviet Union and an official organ of the Central Committee of the Communist Party between 1912 and 1991. Now defunct.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grand truth, raw and hard truth</li></ol><p><i>пра́вда</i> (právda, “truth”) + <i>ма́тка</i> (mátka, “mother (rare)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly (in accordance with the facts)</li></ol><p><i>правди́вый</i> (pravdívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truthfulness, uprightness, veracity</li></ol><p><i>правди́вый</i> (pravdívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, truthful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravьdivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truthseeker</li></ol><p><i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>truth-loving</li></ol><p><i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of truth</li></ol><p><i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plausibility, credibility, verisimilitude, probability, likelihood</li></ol><p><i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бие</i> (podóbije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>plausibly</li></ol><p>From <i>правдоподо́бный</i> (pravdopodóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o). Compare Ukrainian <i>правдоподі́бно</i> (pravdopodíbno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plausible, verisimilar, probable, likely</li></ol><p>From <i>правдоподо́бие</i> (pravdopodóbije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>правдоподі́бний</i> (pravdopodíbnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) just/righteous person</li><li>pious person, one who follows one's religion scrupulously</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>правьдьникъ</i> (pravĭdĭnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piousness</li><li>righteousness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravьdьnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пра́ведный</i> (právednyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pious, religious</li><li>just, righteous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravьdьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become politically (more) right-wing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rule</li><li>rule, regulations, law, canon</li><li>rule, method, algorithm</li><li>rule, principle, habit</li><li>straightener, reversing rod</li><li>strickle <i>(for sharpening scythes)</i></li><li>(<i>hunters jargon</i>) tail, brush</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) helm, rudder</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>правило</i> (pravilo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravidlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) rule of thumb</li></ol><p>Direct calque of English <i>rule of thumb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>пра́вильный</i> (právilʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>пра́вильно</i> (právilʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>correctly</li></ol><p><i>пра́вильный</i> (právilʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rightness, correctness</li><li>regularity</li></ol><p><i>пра́вильный</i> (právilʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right, correct</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) regular</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) regular</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravidlьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пра́вило</i> (právilo, “rule”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regular verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) regular polygon (polygon which is both equiangular and equilateral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler (a person who rules or governs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>правитель</i> (pravitelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) government; governmental</li></ol><p><i>прави́тельство</i> (pravítelʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government, administration, cabinet</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to govern, to rule</li><li>to drive, to steer</li><li>to correct</li><li>to strop, to set</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to edit, i.e. to change the content of a file, a website or the code of software</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>правити</i> (praviti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;praviti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proof-reading, correcting, correction (act of correcting)</li><li>trueing, adjustment</li><li>edit (change of a text, or a document)</li></ol><p><i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governing, ruling (period of time when a monarch has been tooking his or her throne)</li><li>government, administration</li><li>board (committee), board of directors</li></ol><p><i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>правле́ние</i> (pravlénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandson</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) great-grandchildren</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandchildren</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-granddaughter</li></ol><p><i>пра́внук</i> (právnuk) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right; claim</li><li>(<i>law</i>) law, right</li><li>justice</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) driving licence</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indeed, really</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>право</i> (pravo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravo</i>. Calque of German <i>Recht</i> and French <i>droit</i>, based on <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj, “right, correct; straight”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>пра́во</i> (právo), Belarusian <i>пра́ва</i> (práva), Old Church Slavonic <i>право</i> (pravo), Bulgarian <i>пра́во</i> (právo), Serbo-Croatian <i>пра̏во</i>, Slovene <i>prȃvo</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>právo</i>, Slovak <i>právo</i>, Polish <i>prawo</i>.</p><p><i>пра́вый</i> (právyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) right bank</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj) +&lrm; <i>бе́рег</i> (béreg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jurist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>правове́дение</i> (pravovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law studies, jurisprudence</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) law studies</li></ol><p><i>правове́д(ение)</i> (pravovéd(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devout, faithful (to a religion)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;ironic) devout to any doctrine or ideology</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muslim (muʾmin)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) believer, Orthodox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legal, lawful, rightful</li><li>based on law, constitutional</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human rights activist, activist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) human rights</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>защи́та</i> (zaščíta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legitimacy</li></ol><p><i>правоме́рный</i> (pravomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legal, lawful</li><li>legitimate</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), calque of German <i>rechtmäßig</i> or German <i>gesetzmäßig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>competent</li><li>authorized</li><li>(literary,&#32;jurisprudence) recognized</li><li>(<i>law</i>) available, valid</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;relational) related to one's rights</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) violation of the law, offence</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>наруше́ние</i> (narušénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) lawbreaker, delinquent, offender</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo, “law, right”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, “to violate”) +&lrm; <i>нару́шить</i> (narúšitʹ, “er”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) jural relation, legal relation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law enforcement, law enforcement agency (various government agencies involved in the prevention of crime and the apprehension of criminals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) law enforcement</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spelling, orthography (act, practice, ability, or subject of forming words with letters, or of reading the letters of words)</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj, “correct”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije), or their equivalents in Old Church Slavonic, calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθογραφία</i> (orthographía), from <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “correct”) and <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “I write”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law and order</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́во</i> (právo, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поря́док</i> (porjádok, “order”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) successor, assignee</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прее́мник</i> (prejémnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law enforcement</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>примене́ние</i> (primenénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) law enforcement</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right-wing radical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right-wing radicalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right-handed</li></ol><p><i>пра́вый</i> (právyj, “right”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) Orthodoxy</li><li>the Eastern Orthodox Church of Christianity</li><li>the Russian Orthodox Church</li><li>(<i>paganism</i>) Slavic neopaganism, Rodnovery</li></ol><p>Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθοδοξία</i> (orthodoxía, “correctness in doctrine and belief”). From <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj, “right, correct”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́ва</i> (sláva, “glory, glorification; (general) opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p><p>Sense 4, used by some Slavic neopagan groups, was derived from their belief that Orthodox Christianity in Russia emerged from a period where paganism and Christianity coexisted, making Slavic neopaganism the "true" Orthodoxy that supposedly preceded the Christian counterpart.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orthodox Church (the Eastern body of Christendom)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Orthodox</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) an Orthodox (<i>an Orthodox Christian person</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>православьнъ</i> (pravoslavĭnŭ), in turn a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ὀρθόδοξος</i> (orthódoxos). By surface analysis, <i>пра́вый</i> (právyj, “right, correct”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сла́ва</i> (sláva, “glory, glorification; (general) opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legal awareness, sense of justice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legal capacity</li></ol><p><i>правоспосо́бный</i> (pravosposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) possessing legal capacity</li></ol><p><i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) Old East Slavic</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>восточнославя́нский</i> (vostočnoslavjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>justice (the civil power dealing with law)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right, rightfulness, correctness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right-wing extremist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right-hander (one who is right-handed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right, right-hand</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) starboard</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) right-wing</li><li>right, correct</li><li>righteous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nominalized,&#32;politics) rightist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pravъ</i>. Compare <i>пра́вда</i> (právda). Cognate with Latin <i>prōvincia</i> (“territory, dominion, office, duty, province”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frawjô</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paganism</i>) "rightness", the universal order according to some Slavic neopagan groups.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) Proto-Germanic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Proto-Germanic</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>герма́нский</i> (germánskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pragmatism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pragmatist</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πραγματικός</i> (pragmatikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pragmatic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>pragmat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pragmatic, practical</li><li>pragmatic (<i>in reference to the philosophy or theory of pragmatism</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>pragmat(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandfather</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>дед</i> (ded)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) great-grandfather</li><li>ancient, old</li></ol><p><i>пра́дед</i> (práded) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandfather</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>де́душка</i> (déduška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Praguer, Praguian</li></ol><p><i>Пра́га</i> (Prága) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Prague</li></ol><p><i>Пра́га</i> (Prága) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Proto-West Germanic</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-, “proto-”) +&lrm; <i>за̀падногерма́нский</i> (zàpadnogermánskij, “West Germanic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) festival, festivities, celebration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>праздьньство</i> (prazdĭnĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>праздный</i> (prazdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiday</li><li>festival, a feast</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) joy, pleasure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>праздьникъ</i> (prazdĭnikŭ, “holiday”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porzdьnikъ</i> (“skip, gap in a sequence”), derived from <i>&#42;porzdьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пра́здник</i> (prázdnik)</li></ol><p><i>пра́здник</i> (prázdnik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>festively</li></ol><p><i>пра́здничный</i> (prázdničnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) holiday</li><li>festive, festal (having the atmosphere, decoration, or attitude of a festival, holiday, or celebration)</li></ol><p><i>пра́здник</i> (prázdnik) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celebration, celebrating, commemoration, commemorating</li></ol><p><i>пра́здновать</i> (prázdnovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to celebrate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>праздьновати</i> (prazdĭnovati). By surface analysis, <i>праздный</i> (prazdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble, to talk nonsense, to talk needlessly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idleness, indolence</li></ol><p><i>пра́здный</i> (prázdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, loafer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idle (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) idler, loafer</li></ol><p><i>пра́здный</i> (prázdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>шата́ющийся</i> (šatájuščijsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) empty</li><li>idle</li><li>useless, unnecessary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>праздьнъ</i> (prazdĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porzdьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>поро́жний</i> (poróžnij), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praseodymium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proto-Indo-European (person)</li></ol><p>From <i>праиндоевропе́(йский)</i> (praindojevropé(jskij)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Proto-Indo-European</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>И́ндия</i> (Índija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>европе́йский</i> (jevropéjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Proto-Italic (hypothetical prehistoric Indo-European language, ancestor of all Italic languages)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proto-Italic language (short for праитали́йский язы́к)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>итали́йский</i> (italíjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privacy (state of being private)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>privacy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;genetics) primer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>primer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primary election (preliminary election to select a candidate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>primaries</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a practical person, a practical worker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practice</li><li>internship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intern, probationer, trainee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practise something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practise, to exercise</li></ol><p><i>практикова́ть</i> (praktikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>academia</i>) workshop (course, type of activity), (US) practicum</li><li>(<i>academia</i>) workshop (place where such courses or activities are conducted)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>prācticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>practically, in a practical manner</li><li>practically, virtually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>practical</li><li>applied</li></ol><p><i>пра́кт(ика)</i> (prákt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>practically, economically</li></ol><p><i>практи́чный</i> (praktíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>practicality</li></ol><p><i>практи́чный</i> (praktíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>practical (having skills or knowledge that are practical)</li><li>usable</li></ol><p><i>пра́кт(ика)</i> (prákt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supercontinent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) prank (practical joke or mischievous trick)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>prank</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) prankster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>prankster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) ancestor, forefather (one from whom a person is descended)</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-, “great”) +&lrm; <i>отец</i> (otec, “father”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;obsolete) regimental colours/colors</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) praporshchik</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прапоръ</i> (praporŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) ensign (military rank); praporshchik</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́пор</i> (prápor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik), from Old Church Slavonic <i>прапоръ</i> (praporŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-grandmother</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>праба́бушка</i> (prabábuška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-grandson</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) great-great-grandchildren</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-granddaughter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-grandfather</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>пра́дед</i> (práded)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-grandfather</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>праде́душка</i> (pradéduška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-great-grandmother</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>прапраба́бушка</i> (praprabábuška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-great-great-grandfather</li></ol><p><i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>прапра́дед</i> (prapráded)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Urheimat, cradleland, original homeland</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-, “ur-”) +&lrm; <i>ро́дина</i> (ródina, “motherland, homeland”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progenitor, primogenitor, forefather, ancestor</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>роди́тель</i> (rodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Proto-Slavic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) one who purchases cattle/meat/fish for resale; cattle-dealer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urform (the (real or idealised) version of a transmissible cultural element, such as a word, language, religion, ideology, folktale, melody, meme, etc., which is taken to be the original form from which all other versions have deviated)</li></ol><p>From <i>пра-</i> (pra-) +&lrm; <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dust, earth</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>) ashes, remains</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прахъ</i> (praxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porxъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pers-</i>. Doublet of <i>по́рох</i> (pórox), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) work, labour</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old Polish <i>praca</i> (via Polish <i>praca</i>, Old Ruthenian <i>пра́ца</i> (práca), Belarusian <i>пра́ца</i> (práca), or Ukrainian <i>пра́ця</i> (prácja)), from Old Czech <i>prácě</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porťa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laundry (place or room where laundering is done)</li></ol><p>From <i>пра́чка</i> (práčka, “laundress, washerwoman”) +&lrm; <i>-ная</i> (-naja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washerwoman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrakъ</i> / <i>&#42;pьraka</i> + <i>&#42;-ьka</i>, from <i>&#42;pьrati</i> + <i>&#42;-akъ</i> / <i>&#42;-aka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>прать</i> (pratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чка</i> (-čka). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>перачка</i> (peračka), Polish <i>praczka</i>. Root cognate with Czech <i>pradlena</i> (<~ <i>prádlo</i> <~ <i>prát</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) sling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>праща</i> (prašta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;porťa</i>, from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to beat”). Displaced Old East Slavic <i>пороча</i> (poroča).</p><p>Cognate with Polish <i>proca</i>, Bulgarian <i>пра́шка</i> (práška), Slovene <i>prača</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>пра̏ћа</i> / <i>prȁća</i>. Compare Slovak <i>prak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) someone who uses a sling, slinger</li></ol><p>From <i>пращ(а́)</i> (prašč(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancestor, forefather (one from whom a person is descended)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пращуръ</i> (praščurŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;praščurъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) protolanguage</li><li>parent language, ancestor language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) over, across</li><li>(<i>used with adjectives, may be accompanied by reduplication</i>) most, very (<i>superlative degree</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пре-</i> (pre-), <i>прѣ-</i> (prě-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;per-</i>. Compare native <i>пере-</i> (pere-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preamble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very large, huge</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>большо́й</i> (bolʹšój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stay, sojourn (abode, holiday or temporary residence)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣбꙑваниѥ</i> (prěbyvanije). By surface analysis, <i>пребыва́ть</i> (prebyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be (at some place), to stay, to remain</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be (in some state or condition), to stay, to remain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣбꙑвати</i> (prěbyvati). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>быва́ть</i> (byvátʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>перебува́ти</i> (perebuváty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be (at some place), to stay, to remain</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be (in some state or condition), to stay, to remain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣбꙑти</i> (prěbyti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domination</li></ol><p><i>превали́ровать</i> (prevalírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to prevail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;folk poetry) great, significant</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) outstanding (standing out in a certain quality)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>вели́кий</i> (velíkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preventive, preventative</li><li>preemptive (<i>of a war</i>)</li></ol><p>French <i>préventif</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excel, to surpass</li><li>to surpass, to exceed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвъзити</i> (prěvŭziti), remodeled after native <i>взойти́</i> (vzojtí). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>взойти́</i> (vzojtí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very tasty</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>вку́сный</i> (vkúsnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To prioritize</li></ol><p><i>превозвы́сить</i> (prevozvýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exaltation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome</li></ol><p><i>превозмог-</i> (prevozmog-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overcome</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>возмо́чь</i> (vozmóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extol, to praise</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвъзнести</i> (prěvŭznesti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>вознести́</i> (voznestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>вознести́ до небе́с</i> (voznestí do nebés)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to extol, to praise</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвъзносити</i> (prěvŭznositi). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>возноси́ть</i> (voznosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>возноси́ть до небе́с</i> (voznosítʹ do nebés)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Excellency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excel, to surpass</li><li>to surpass, to exceed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвъсходити</i> (prěvŭsxoditi). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>восходи́ть</i> (vosxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) superlative degree, superlative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superbly, fine, great, admirably</li></ol><p><i>превосхо́дный</i> (prevosxódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent, splendid, superior (possessing or displaying splendor)</li><li>first-class, first-rate</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) superlative</li></ol><p><i>превосходи́ть</i> (prevosxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superiority</li><li>excellence</li></ol><p><i>превосходи́ть</i> (prevosxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) transformative (pertaining to the oblique case answering the question <i>в кого́</i> (v kovó, “[turn] into whom, [become] who?”), <i>во что</i> (vo što, “[turn] into what, [become] what?”))</li></ol><p><i>преврати́ть</i> (prevratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn into, to change</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвратити</i> (prěvratiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pervortiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ, “to turn”). Doublet of <i>перевороти́ть</i> (perevorotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn into, to change into</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвратити сѧ</i> (prěvratiti sę). Doublet with native <i>перевороти́ться</i> (perevorotítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wrongly (incorrectly&#59; by error)</li></ol><p><i>превра́тный</i> (prevrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vicissitude, changeability (a change, especially in one's life or fortunes)</li></ol><p><i>превра́тный</i> (prevrátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perverse, awry (wrong or distorted&#59; amiss)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвратьнъ</i> (prěvratĭnŭ). Compare also Czech <i>převratný</i>, Polish <i>przewrotny</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn into, to change</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвращати</i> (prěvraštati). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn into, to change into <i>(intransitive)</i></li></ol><p><i>превраща́ть</i> (prevraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change, transformation, conversion, transmutation, metamorphosis</li></ol><p><i>преврати́ть</i> (prevratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exceed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exceed</li></ol><p><i>превы́сить</i> (prevýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣвꙑше</i> (prěvyše).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exceeding, excess</li></ol><p><i>превы́сить</i> (prevýsitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very stupid</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>глу́пый</i> (glúpyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, barrier, obstacle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-гради́ть</i> (-gradítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to block, to obstruct</li></ol><p><i>прегради́ть</i> (pregradítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sin, transgression</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣгрѣшениѥ</i> (prěgrěšenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) in front of, before</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъ</i> (prědŭ). Doublet of <i>пе́ред</i> (péred), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) before, fore-, pre-</li><li>cis- (in place names)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъ-</i> (prědŭ-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betray</li><li>to expose, to subject, to commit, to hand over</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдавати</i> (prědavati). Doublet of <i>передава́ть</i> (peredavátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>предава́ть</i> (predavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handing over, committing</li><li>legend; tradition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣданиѥ</i> (prědanije). By surface analysis, <i>предать</i> (predatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>пре́данный</i> (prédannyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>пре́данно</i> (prédanno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>пре́данный</i> (prédannyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>пре́данно</i> (prédanno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>devotedly</li></ol><p><i>пре́данный</i> (prédannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loyalty, devotion</li><li>attachment (dependence, especially a strong one)</li></ol><p><i>пре́данный</i> (prédannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devoted, faithful, loyal [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li><li>devoted, faithful, loyal, staunch (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traitor, betrayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдатель</i> (prědatelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>преда́ть</i> (predátʹ, “to betray”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>traitorous, treacherous</li><li>treacherous (dangerous, insidious)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) revealing, informative</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unreliable</li></ol><p><i>преда́тель</i> (predátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betrayal, treason, treachery</li></ol><p><i>преда́тель</i> (predátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betray</li><li>to expose, to subject, to commit, to hand over, to put</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдати</i> (prědati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdati</i>, from <i>&#42;per-</i> + <i>&#42;dati</i>. Doublet of <i>переда́ть</i> (peredátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give oneself up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>преда́ть</i> (predátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dressing-room <i>(in a bathhouse)</i></li><li>anteroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) preventive-detention cell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>preliminarily (in a preliminary manner)</li></ol><p><i>предвари́тельный</i> (predvarítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preliminary, advance</li></ol><p><i>предвари́ть</i> (predvarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forestall, to anticipate</li><li>to premise <i>(with)</i>, to begin <i>(by)</i></li><li>to tell beforehand <i>(about)</i>, (fore)to warn <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъварити</i> (prědŭvariti). Synchoronically analyzable as <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;variti</i> (“to overtake”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forestall, to anticipate</li><li>to premise <i>(with)</i>, to begin <i>(by)</i></li><li>to tell beforehand <i>(about)</i>, (fore)to warn <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъварꙗти</i> (prědŭvarjati). Synchoronically analyzable as <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>вар-</i> (var-) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prelude to spring, pre-spring, late winter (period from February 15 to March 28)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presage, foretoken, portent, omen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdъvěstьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-, “front”) +&lrm; <i>весть</i> (vestʹ, “message”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbinger, herald, precursor, forerunner</li></ol><p>From <i>предвест(и́ть)</i> (predvest(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare Ukrainian <i>передві́сник</i> (peredvísnyk), Belarusian <i>прадве́снік</i> (pradvjésnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forebode, to presage, to portend, to betoken, to foretoken, to foreshadow, to herald, to augur</li></ol><p>From the <i>предвещ-</i> (predvešč-) stem of <i>предвести́ть</i> (predvestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>передвіща́ти</i> (peredviščáty), Belarusian <i>прадвяшча́ць</i> (pradvjaščácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bias, prejudice</li></ol><p><i>предвзя́тый</i> (predvzjátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biased, prejudiced</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>взя́тый</i> (vzjátyj). Compare Polish <i>przedwzięty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foresight, prediction, prevision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foresee, to foreknow, to see coming</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be expected/foreseen</li></ol><p><i>предви́деть</i> (predvídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) to look forward <i>(to)</i>, to anticipate <i>(with pleasure)</i></li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>вкуси́ть</i> (vkusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look forward <i>(to)</i>, to anticipate <i>(with pleasure)</i></li></ol><p><i>предвкуси́ть</i> (predvkusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anticipation, foretaste</li></ol><p><i>предвкуси́ть</i> (predvkusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, ringleader</li></ol><p><i>предводи́ть</i> (predvodítʹ, “(dated) be the leader, be in charge”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to head, to be at head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to head, to be at head</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pre-war</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to anticipate, to forestall</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>восхи́тить</i> (vosxítitʹ) (stem-stressed variant) or from <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>восхити́ть</i> (vosxitítʹ) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to anticipate, to forestall</li></ol><p><i>предвосхи́тить</i> (predvosxítitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preelection, election</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>вы́борный</i> (výbornyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foothill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near the sofa</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>дива́н</i> (diván) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limit, boundary, margin</li><li>border</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) precincts</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) limit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдѣлъ</i> (prědělŭ). Doublet of <i>переде́л</i> (peredél), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, utterly</li></ol><p><i>преде́льный</i> (predélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) limit, maximum</li><li>utmost, extreme</li></ol><p><i>преде́л</i> (predél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omen, augury, presage (warning of a future event)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pro-, fore-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;mathematics,&#32;philosophy) predicate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) predicative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predicative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prologue, foreword, preface, introduction</li></ol><p>From <i>преди-</i> (predi-) +&lrm; <i>сло́в(о)</i> (slóv(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pre-COVID</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) pre-COVID</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) slat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer, to proffer</li><li>to propose, to suggest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдълагати</i> (prědŭlagati). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretext, excuse, pretense</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) preposition</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>предложи́ть</i> (predložítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offer</li><li>suggestion, proposal (<i>also: marriage proposal</i>), proposition</li><li>(<i>commerce</i>) supply</li><li>(<i>parliamentary action</i>) motion</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) sentence, clause</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>предложи́ть</i> (predložítʹ), <i>предложи́ть</i> (predložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije); the grammar sense is a semantic loan from Latin <i>prōpositiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>предложе́ние</i> (predložénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to offer, to proffer</li><li>to propose, to suggest, to proposition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъложити</i> (prědŭložiti). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) prepositional</li></ol><p>From <i>предло́г</i> (predlóg, “preposition”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prepositional case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suburb, outskirts, faubourg (an outlying part of a city or town)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>przedmieście</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>место</i> (mesto) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object</li><li>subject, topic</li><li><i>(academic)</i> subject</li><li><i>(commercial)</i> article, commodity, item</li><li>piece of work</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>przedmiot</i> (“subject; object”), itself a calque of Latin <i>objectum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subject</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) object</li><li>material, physical</li></ol><p><i>предме́т</i> (predmét, “object, subject”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appropriate</li><li>to assign</li><li>to design (for a specific purpose)</li><li>to intend (for), to destine (for / to); to mean (for); to set aside (for), to earmark (for)</li><li>to consign; to reserve</li></ol><p><i>предназна́чить</i> (prednaznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be intended</li></ol><p><i>предназнача́ть</i> (prednaznačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destination, predestination, purpose, designation</li></ol><p><i>предназна́чить</i> (prednaznáčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meant (<i>for, to be</i>), intended (<i>for, to be</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appropriate</li><li>to assign</li><li>to design (for a specific purpose)</li><li>to intend (for), to destine (for / to); to mean (for); to set aside (for), to earmark (for)</li><li>to consign; to reserve</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>назна́чить</i> (naznáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>intentionally, premeditatedly (in an intentional manner, on purpose)</li></ol><p><i>преднаме́ренный</i> (prednamérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premeditated, intentional, deliberate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>before New Year</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>нового́дний</i> (novogódnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) alternative form of <i>пред</i> (pred): in front of, before</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefather, ancestor</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) parent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъкъ</i> (prědŭkŭ); By surface analysis, <i>пред</i> (pred) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdъkъ</i>. Doublet of <i>передок</i> (peredok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prepayment, advance payment (part of a contractually due sum)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predetermine, to predestine, to foreordain</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>определи́ть</i> (opredelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predetermine, to predestine, to foreordain</li></ol><p><i>предопредели́ть</i> (predopredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very annoying</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>доса́дный</i> (dosádnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let have <i>(to)</i>, to leave <i>(to)</i></li><li>to give <i>(to)</i>, to render <i>(to)</i>, to grant <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъставити</i> (prědŭstaviti), from <i>прѣдъ-</i> (prědŭ-) +&lrm; <i>ставити</i> (staviti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be allowed, to be permitted</li><li>to be provided</li></ol><p><i>предоста́вить</i> (predostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assignment, allotment, placing at someone's disposal</li></ol><p><i>предоста́вить</i> (predostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let have <i>(to)</i>, to leave <i>(to)</i></li><li>to give <i>(to)</i>, to render <i>(to)</i>, to grant <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>предоста́вить</i> (predostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be allowed, to be permitted</li><li>to be provided</li></ol><p><i>предоставля́ть</i> (predostavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) plenty <i>(of)</i>, more than enough</li></ol><p><i>предоста́точный</i> (predostátočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more than enough</li></ol><p><i>предо-</i> (predo-) +&lrm; <i>ста́точный</i> (státočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn, to caution, to admonish, to put on one's guard</li></ol><p><i>предостере́г-</i> (predosterég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>admonitory, cautioning, warning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admonition, warning, caution, caveat</li></ol><p><i>предостере́г-</i> (predosterég-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn, to caution, to admonish, to put on one's guard</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>остере́чь</i> (osteréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supraspinate</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>ость</i> (ostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caution, precaution</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>осторо́жность</i> (ostoróžnostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reprehensible, despicable</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>предо-</i> (predo-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avert, to stave off, to prevent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдотъвратити</i> (prědotŭvratiti). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>отврати́ть</i> (otvratítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avert, to stave off, to prevent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдотъвращати</i> (prědotŭvraštati). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>отвраща́ть</i> (otvraščátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prevention, staving off</li></ol><p><i>предотврати́ть</i> (predotvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safety device, preventer, protector, safeguard</li><li>safety (on a gun)</li></ol><p><i>предохрани́ть</i> (predoxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) safety, protection</li></ol><p><i>предохрани́ть</i> (predoxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect from harm</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>охрани́ть</i> (oxranítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect from harm</li></ol><p><i>предохрани́ть</i> (predoxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulation, injunction (law or administrative rule)</li><li>prescription (written order for the administration of a medicine)</li></ol><p><i>предписа́ть</i> (predpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prescribe</li><li>to direct, to order [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prescribe</li><li>to direct, to order [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>предписа́ть</i> (predpisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forearm</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>плечо́</i> (plečó) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarsus</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>плюсна́</i> (pljusná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suspectedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assumed</li><li>estimated</li><li>alleged, supposed, suppositional, suppositious</li><li>expected, anticipated</li><li>prospective</li><li>implied</li><li>presumed, putative</li><li>presumptive, reputed</li><li>probable</li><li>conjecturable</li><li>would-be</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assume, to suppose</li><li>to presume, to imply</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be planned</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be assumed, to be supposed; to be presumed, to be implied</li></ol><p><i>предполага́ть</i> (predpolagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assumption</li><li>hypothesis, supposition, guess, speculation, conjecture, surmise</li><li>presumption</li><li>presupposition</li><li>sumption</li></ol><p><i>предположи́ть</i> (predpoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>supposedly</li></ol><p><i>предположи́тельный</i> (predpoložítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conjectural, presumable, hypothetical, estimated</li><li>a priori ((logic) based on hypothesis rather than experiment.)</li></ol><p><i>предположи́ть</i> (predpoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assume, to suppose</li><li>to presume, to imply</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preflight</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>полёт</i> (poljót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>penultimate</li><li>last but one, next to last</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>после́дний</i> (poslédnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prerequisite, precondition (something that must be gained in order to gain something else)</li><li>supposition, presupposition, premise, background</li></ol><p><i>предпосыла́ть</i> (predposylátʹ, “to precede (as an explanation)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prefer</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>поче́сть</i> (počéstʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prefer</li></ol><p><i>предпо(че́сть)</i> (predpo(čéstʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preference, predilection (a state being preferred) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>предпочт-</i> (predpočt-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>preferably</li></ol><p><i>предпочти́тельный</i> (predpočtítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preferable</li><li>preferred</li></ol><p><i>предпочти́-</i> (predpočtí-) +&lrm; <i>-те́льный</i> (-télʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preholiday</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>пра́здничный</i> (prázdničnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antepenultimate, antepenult (two before the last)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) alternative form of <i>предпринима́ть</i> (predprinimátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>приима́ть</i> (priimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initiative, enterprise (willingness to take the initiative, especially in business)</li></ol><p><i>предприи́мчивый</i> (predpriímčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enterprising, businesslike</li></ol><p><i>предприима́ть</i> (predpriimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industrialist, businessman, entrepreneur</li></ol><p><i>предпринима́ть</i> (predprinimátʹ, “to undertake”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) business; entrepreneurial</li></ol><p><i>предпринима́тель</i> (predprinimátelʹ, “entrepreneur”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrepreneurship, enterprise</li></ol><p><i>предпринима́тель</i> (predprinimátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undertake, to endeavour</li></ol><p>From <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>принимать</i> (prinimatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undertake, to endeavour</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enterprise, business, undertaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predispose [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>располага́ть</i> (raspolagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predisposition</li><li>aptitude (natural ability to acquire knowledge or skill)</li></ol><p><i>предрасполо́женный</i> (predraspolóžennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone, predisposed [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predispose [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>расположи́ть</i> (raspoložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) predawn</li></ol><p><i>предрассве́тье</i> (predrassvétʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predawn</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>рассве́т</i> (rassvét) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prejudice, superstition</li></ol><p>Calqued from French <i>préjugé</i> in the 18th century. By surface analysis, <i>пред</i> (pred, “pre-”) +&lrm; <i>рассу́док</i> (rassúdok, “judgement, reason”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to foretell, to predict, to prognosticate</li></ol><p><i>предре́к-</i> (predrék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to foretell, to predict, to prognosticate</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prejudge, to predetermine, to predestine</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>реша́ть</i> (rešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prejudge, to predetermine, to predestine</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chairman, president</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-, “pre-”) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ, “to sit”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chairman, president</li></ol><p><i>председа́тель</i> (predsedátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chairmanship, presidency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be chairman/president <i>(of)</i></li><li>to chair, to preside <i>(over)</i>, to be in the chair, to take the chair</li></ol><p><i>председа́тельство</i> (predsedátelʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chairperson, chair, chairman, moderator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auricle, atrium (chamber of the heart)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prediction; forecast; prophecy</li><li>prognostication</li><li>forecasting; prognosis</li></ol><p><i>предсказа́ть</i> (predskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell, to predict, to forecast, to prophesy</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predictability</li></ol><p><i>предсказу́емый</i> (predskazújemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predictable, foreseeable</li></ol><p><i>предсказа́ть</i> (predskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́емый</i> (-újemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foretell, to predict, to forecast, to prophesy</li></ol><p><i>предсказа́ть</i> (predskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) death</li><li>dying (<i>words, wish, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>смерть</i> (smertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>предста́ть</i> (predstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>representative</li><li>specimen</li></ol><p>From <i>предста́вить</i> (predstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ). Compare Czech <i>představitel</i> and Polish <i>przedstawiciel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;law) representative</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) imposing, dignified, impressive</li></ol><p><i>предста́вить</i> (predstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>representative</li></ol><p><i>представи́тель</i> (predstavítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>representation, representative office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to offer</li><li>to produce, to show</li><li>to introduce, to present</li><li>to imagine, to picture, to fancy, to conceive, to envision</li><li>to perform, to act</li><li>to represent, to embody, to depict, to envision, to figure, to realize</li><li>to represent, to be</li><li>to recommend, to put forward</li></ol><p>Probably a calque of German <i>vorstellen</i>, possibly via Polish <i>przedstawić</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occur, to present itself; to offer, to arise</li><li>to introduce oneself</li><li>to seem</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pretend (to be), to pass oneself off (as)</li></ol><p><i>предста́вить</i> (predstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>introduction (a means, such as a personal letter, of presenting one person to another)</li><li>presentation, submission, introduction</li><li>performance, show</li><li>idea, notion, conception</li></ol><p><i>предста́вить</i> (predstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to offer</li><li>to produce, to show</li><li>to introduce, to present</li><li>to imagine, to picture, to fancy, to conceive</li><li>to perform, to act</li><li>to represent, to embody, to depict, to envision, to figure, to realize</li><li>to represent, to be</li><li>to recommend, to put forward</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъставлꙗти</i> (prědŭstavljati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to occur, to present itself; to offer, to arise</li><li>to introduce oneself</li><li>to seem</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pretend (to be), to pass oneself off (as)</li></ol><p><i>представля́ть</i> (predstavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) lying ahead</li></ol><p><i>предстоя́ть</i> (predstojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie ahead, to be at hand, to be in the offing</li><li>to have to do something in the future</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъстоꙗти</i> (prědŭstojati). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forthcoming, coming, imminent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) predusk</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>су́мерки</i> (súmerki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) forerunner, precursor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдътеча</i> (prědŭteča). By surface analysis, <i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prejudice, bias</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>убежде́ние</i> (ubeždénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>предубежде́ние</i> (predubeždénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prejudiced, biased [<i>with</i> <b>про́тив</b> (prótiv, + genitive) <small>‘towards someone/something’</small>] (<i>of a person: experiencing feelings of prejudice or bias towards someone or something</i>)</li><li>prejudiced, biased (<i>of an attitude, a look, etc.; expressing feelings of prejudice or bias</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>уве́домить</i> (uvédomitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn</li></ol><p><i>предуве́домить</i> (preduvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divine, to foresee, to foretell</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>угада́ть</i> (ugadátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divine, to foresee, to foretell</li></ol><p><i>предугада́ть</i> (predugadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>premeditation</li></ol><p>From <i>предумы́шленный</i> (predumýšlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premeditated, aforethought</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attentiveness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preventive, precautionary</li><li>warning</li><li>obliging, attentive, courteous</li></ol><p><i>предупреди́ть</i> (predupredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn, to let know beforehand</li><li>to forestall, to anticipate</li><li>to avert, to prevent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warn, to let know beforehand</li><li>to forestall, to anticipate</li><li>to avert, to prevent</li></ol><p><i>предупреди́ть</i> (predupredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notice, warning, notification</li><li>prevention</li></ol><p><i>предупреждать</i> (predupreždatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very disagreeable</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>дурно́й</i> (durnój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foresee, to envisage, to anticipate</li><li>to provide (for), to stipulate (for)</li></ol><p><i>предусмотре́ть</i> (predusmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provided, specified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foresee, to envisage, to anticipate</li><li>to provide (for), to stipulate (for)</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>усмотре́ть</i> (usmotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>prudently, with foresight</li></ol><p><i>предусмотри́тельный</i> (predusmotrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence, foresight</li></ol><p><i>предусмотри́тельный</i> (predusmotrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provident, prudent, precautionary, far-sighted</li></ol><p><i>предусмотре́ть</i> (predusmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pre-installed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premorning</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>утренний</i> (utrennij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreboding, foreshadowing, presentiment, hunch (a premonition; a feeling that something, often of undesirable nature, is going to happen)</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a presentiment, to have a foreboding (of, about)</li></ol><p><i>предчу́вствие</i> (predčúvstvije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predecessor, precursor, forerunner</li><li>progenitor</li><li>ancestor (an earlier type)</li><li>forebear</li><li>foregoer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perdъšьdstvьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to precede, to forego, to forerun, to antedate</li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>ше́ствовать</i> (šéstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>previous, former</li><li>preceding</li><li>prior, anterior</li><li>foregoing</li><li>antecedent (earlier in time or order)</li><li>precedent</li><li>precursory</li><li>aforegoing</li><li>aforetime</li><li>precedential</li><li>anteceding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) complaint, issue</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>предъяви́ть</i> (predʺjavítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearer, presenter</li></ol><p><i>предъяви́ть</i> (predʺjavítʹ, “to present, to produce, to show (to authorities)”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to produce, to show</li><li>to make <i>(a demand, request)</i>, to assert <i>(rights)</i></li><li>to bring <i>(a lawsuit)</i>, to press, to prefer <i>(an accusation, charge)</i></li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>producing, presentation</li></ol><p><i>предъяви́ть</i> (predʺjavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to produce, to show</li><li>to make <i>(a demand, request)</i>, to assert <i>(rights)</i></li><li>to bring <i>(a lawsuit)</i>, to press, to prefer <i>(an accusation, charge)</i></li></ol><p><i>пред-</i> (pred-) +&lrm; <i>явля́ть</i> (javljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>previous</li><li>antecedent, early (earlier in time or order)</li></ol><p>From <i>предыдти́</i> (predydtí, “go past”) +&lrm; <i>-ущий</i> (-uščij, “(present participle ending)”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣдъити</i> (prědŷiti). See English <i>preterite</i> for a similar semantic development.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>events preceding, events leading up, backstory, prehistory</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) history</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pre-ejaculate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>successor</li><li>heir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>succession, continuity</li></ol><p><i>прее́мственный</i> (prejémstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successive (following one after another)</li><li>inherited by right of succession</li></ol><p><i>прее́мство</i> (prejémstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) succession (transfer of power)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) succession (transfer by inheritance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Church Slavonic <i>преемство</i> (prejemstvo), from <i>преѧти</i> (prejęti, “to follow in succession”) + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>previously, before, formerly</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before, previously to</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣжде</i> (prěžde).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first of all</li><li>above all</li><li>in the first place</li><li>to begin with</li><li>primarily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>previous to, before</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>prematurely, in an untimely manner</li></ol><p><i>преждевре́менный</i> (preždevrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premature, untimely</li></ol><p><i>пре́жде</i> (préžde) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мен-</i> (vrémen-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>former, previous</li></ol><p>. By surface analysis, <i>пред</i> (pred, “before, in front of”) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donative, gift, present</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presentation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to officially present</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to present, to give as a gift</li></ol><p>French <i>présenter</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condom (flexible sleeve worn on the penis)</li></ol><p>From French <i>préservatif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>president (the head of state of a republic, the primary leader of a corporation, male or female)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>président</i>, from Latin <i>praesidens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) president; presidential</li></ol><p><i>президе́нт</i> (prezidént) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presidency</li></ol><p><i>президе́нт</i> (prezidént, “president”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presidium</li><li>head of a committee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>praesidium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>презервати́в</i> (prezervatív)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>президе́нт</i> (prezidént)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to despise, to hold in contempt</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scorn, to disdain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣзирати</i> (prězirati) (whence also Bulgarian <i>презирам</i> (preziram)). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-зира́ть</i> (-zirátʹ). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrěti</i> for more.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>dēspiciō</i> (whence English <i>despise</i>) (&lt; <i>dē-</i> + <i>speciō</i>). Also compare <i>ненави́деть</i> (nenavídetʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contempt, scorn, disdain</li><li>defiance</li></ol><p><i>презре́ть</i> (prezrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemptible, despicable, despised, held in contempt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to despise, to hold in contempt</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scorn, to disdain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣзьрѣти</i> (prězĭrěti). Equivalent to <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>contemptuously (in a disrespectful or discourteous manner)</li></ol><p><i>презри́тельный</i> (prezrítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemptuous, scornful, disdainful (showing contempt)</li></ol><p><i>презре́ть</i> (prezrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) presumption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>predominantly (in a predominant manner)</li><li>mostly</li></ol><p><i>преиму́щественный</i> (preimúščestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primary</li><li>(<i>law</i>) preferential</li></ol><p><i>преиму́щество</i> (preimúščestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advantage (any condition, circumstance, opportunity, or means, particularly favorable to success)</li><li>preference</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>иму́щество</i> (imúščestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hell, nether world</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-, “over, across”) + <i>испо́дний</i> (ispódnij, “underneath”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to fill to the limit</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fill (with a feeling)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>испо́лнить</i> (ispólnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to fill to the limit</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fill (with a feeling)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>исполня́ть</i> (ispolnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price list</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Preiskurant</i>, from French <i>prix courant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>перейти́</i> (perejtí)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precariat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admiration, worship</li></ol><p><i>преклони́ть</i> (preklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;literary) to bend down, to incline, to bow (<i>e.g. one's head</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (<i>to, before</i>)</li><li>to kneel (<i>before</i>)</li><li>to admire, to worship, to revere</li></ol><p><i>преклони́ть</i> (preklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, advanced (<i>in age</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>преклон(и́ть)</i> (preklon(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;literary) to bend down, to incline, to bow (<i>e.g. one's head</i>)</li></ol><p><i>преклони́ть</i> (preklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (<i>to, before</i>)</li><li>to kneel (<i>before</i>)</li><li>to admire, to worship, to revere</li></ol><p><i>преклоня́ть</i> (preklonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) precondition, objection</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣкословиѥ</i> (prěkoslovije), from <i>прѣко</i> (prěko, “against”) (compare Serbo-Croatian <i>при̏jеко</i> (“across, through, against”), Russian <i>поперёк</i> (poperjók, “across, crosswise”)) + <i>слово</i> (slovo, “word”) + <i>-иѥ</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contradict, to cross</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>прекра́сный</i> (prekrásnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>прекра́сно</i> (prekrásno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beautifully, great, gorgeously, well, nicely</li></ol><p><i>прекра́сный</i> (prekrásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful</li><li>fine, splendid, excellent, nice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣкрасьнъ</i> (prěkrasĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) the fair sex, the weaker sex, women collectively</li></ol><p>Literally, “fair sex”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to cease, to end, to discontinue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣкратити</i> (prěkratiti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to end, to cease, to stop</li></ol><p><i>прекрати́ть</i> (prekratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop, to cease, to end, to discontinue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣкращати</i> (prěkraštati). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to end, to cease, to stop</li></ol><p><i>прекраща́ть</i> (prekraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cessation (a ceasing or discontinuance)</li></ol><p><i>прекрати́ть</i> (prekratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armistice (formal agreement to end fighting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prelate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prelate</li></ol><p><i>прела́т</i> (prelát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>преле́стный</i> (preléstnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>преле́стно</i> (preléstno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>charmingly, delightfully, lovely</li></ol><p><i>преле́стный</i> (preléstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charming, delightful, lovely</li></ol><p><i>пре́лесть</i> (prélestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom quark, beauty quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm, fascination</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) delights</li><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) spiritual delusion, deception, conceit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣльсть</i> (prělĭstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perlьstь</i> (“allure”). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>лесть</i> (lestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preliminary things, preliminaries</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) to refract</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to break in two, to fracture</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to reinterpret subjectively</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to overcome</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to betray [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) refraction</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) breaking in two, fracturing</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) subjective reinterpretation</li></ol><p><i>преломи́ть</i> (prelomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) to refract</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to break in two, to fracture</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to reinterpret subjectively</li></ol><p><i>преломи́ть</i> (prelomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seducer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seducer</li></ol><p>From <i>прельсти́тель</i> (prelʹstítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, to tempt, to entice, to seduce, to captivate, to lure, to attract, to dazzle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣльстити</i> (prělĭstiti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>льстить</i> (lʹstitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, to tempt, to entice, to seduce, to captivate, to lure, to attract, to dazzle</li></ol><p><i>прельсти́ть</i> (prelʹstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adulterous</li></ol><p><i>прелюбоде́й</i> (preljubodéj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit adultery, to adulterate, to fornicate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;biblical</i>) adultery (sexual intercourse by a married person with someone other than their spouse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) prelude (short piece of music)</li><li>foreplay (the acts at the beginning of a sexual encounter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very easy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very light (not heavy)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>лёгкий</i> (ljóxkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very small</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ма́ленький</i> (málenʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very dear, sweet</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ми́ленький</i> (mílenʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very nice</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail (to do something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rewarding, bonus payment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>premium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>premium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonus, premium, prize, reward (something of value given in return for an act)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisdom</li><li>subtlety, intricacy</li></ol><p><i>прему́дрый</i> (premúdryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(very) wise; sage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣмѫдръ</i> (prěmǫdrŭ), also analysed as <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj). Compare Macedonian <i>пре́мудар</i> (prémudar) and Bulgarian <i>премъ́дър</i> (premǎ́dǎr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>премье́р-мини́стр</i> (premʹjér-minístr): prime minister (chief member of the cabinet and head of the government)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prime minister</li></ol><p>From French <i>premier ministre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>premiere (first showing of a film, play or other form of entertainment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>première</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negligible, neglected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect, to disregard, to disdain, to slight</li><li>to scorn, to ignore, to despise</li></ol><p><i>пренебре́г-</i> (prenebrég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglect, disregard</li><li>scorn, disdain (feeling of contempt or scorn)</li></ol><p><i>пренебре́г-</i> (prenebrég-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>contemptuously, scornfully, disdainfully</li></ol><p><i>пренебрежи́тельный</i> (prenebrežítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorn, disdain, disparagement</li></ol><p><i>пренебрежи́тельный</i> (prenebrežítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disparaging, contemptuous, disdainful</li></ol><p><i>пренебре́г-</i> (prenebrég-) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect, to disregard, to disdain, to slight</li><li>to scorn, to ignore, to despise</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>небре́чь</i> (nebréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) most unpleasant</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>неприя́тный</i> (neprijátnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>пренесно́сный</i> (prenesnósnyj): the most insufferable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very intolerable</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>несно́сный</i> (nesnósnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debate, discussion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пьрѣниѥ</i> (pĭrěnije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrěnьje</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrěti</i>. Analysable as <i>пре́ть</i> (prétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ния</i> (-énija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predominance, prevalence</li></ol><p><i>преоблада́ть</i> (preobladátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prevail [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘over someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to predominate, to dominate</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>облада́ть</i> (obladátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prevailing, predominant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform, to transfigure</li></ol><p><i>преобрази́ть</i> (preobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be transformed, to look different</li></ol><p><i>преобража́ть</i> (preobražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transformation</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) Transfiguration</li></ol><p><i>преобрази́ть</i> (preobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;Christianity) Transfiguration</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Preobrazheniye, Preobrazhensky, Preobrazhenskaya, Preobrazhenskoye (any of various settlements in Russia with these names)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Preobrazhensky (surname of several notable Russians and Soviets)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Preobrazhensky Regiment</li></ol><p><i>преображе́ние</i> (preobražénije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Преображенский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Preobrazhensky</li></ol><p>From <i>Преображение</i> (Preobraženije, “Transfiguration of Jesus”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform, to transfigure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣобразити</i> (prěobraziti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>образи́ть</i> (obrazítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be transformed, to look different</li></ol><p><i>преобрази́ть</i> (preobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reform</li><li>reformation</li><li>transition</li><li>transformation, conversion</li><li>conversion, translation</li></ol><p><i>преобразова́ть</i> (preobrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reformer, reorganizer</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) transformer, converter</li></ol><p><i>преобразова́ть</i> (preobrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform <i>(into)</i>, to turn <i>(into)</i>, to reform <i>(into)</i>, to reorganize <i>(into, as)</i></li><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics) to transform <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to convert <i>(to)</i>, to translate <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣобразовати</i> (prěobrazovati). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to transform</li><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to reform, to undergo radical change</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be transfigured, to change shape or appearance</li></ol><p><i>преобразова́ть</i> (preobrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform <i>(into)</i>, to turn <i>(into)</i>, to reform <i>(into)</i>, to reorganize <i>(into, as)</i></li><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics) to transform <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to convert <i>(to)</i>, to translate <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>преобразова́ть</i> (preobrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to transform</li><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to reform, to undergo radical change</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be transfigured, to change shape or appearance</li></ol><p><i>преобразо́вывать</i> (preobrazóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gigantic, colossal, enormous</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>огро́мный</i> (ogrómnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get over, to overcome</li><li>to traverse</li><li>to negotiate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣодолѣвати</i> (prěodolěvati). By surface analysis, <i>пре</i> (pre) +&lrm; <i>одоле-</i> (odole-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overcoming</li></ol><p><i>преодоле́ть</i> (preodolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get over, to overcome</li><li>to traverse</li><li>to negotiate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣодолѣти</i> (prěodolěti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>одоле́ть</i> (odolétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surmountable</li></ol><p>Constructed as if the present passive participle of <i>преодоле́ть</i> (preodolétʹ, “to overcome, to surmount”) (but this verb is perfective and has no present tense).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Right Reverend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) a prepared specimen; a specimen mounted on a microscope slide</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) medicine, drug, preparation (a prepared medicinal substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Präparat</i>, from Latin <i>praeparātiō</i>. Compare to English <i>preparation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laboratory assistant, demonstrator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punctuation</li></ol><p><i>препина́ть</i> (prepinátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altercation, bickering, argument, dispute, squabbling</li></ol><p><i>препира́ть(ся)</i> (prepirátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) teacher</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>преподава́тель</i> (prepodavátelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, instruction</li></ol><p><i>преподава́ть</i> (prepodavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, lecturer, instructor, professor (a person who teaches)</li></ol><p><i>преподава́ть</i> (prepodavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>teacher's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) teaching</li></ol><p><i>преподава́тель</i> (prepodavátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, to lecture</li></ol><p><i>препода́ть</i> (prepodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give</li><li>to teach</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>пода́ть</i> (podátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to make a present</li><li>to communicate</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>поднести́</i> (podnestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to present, to make a present</li><li>to communicate</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>подноси́ть</i> (podnosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reverend, saint</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣподобьнъ</i> (prěpodobĭnŭ). Compare Ukrainian <i>преподо́бний</i> (prepodóbnyj), Belarusian <i>прападо́бны</i> (prapadóbny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) a reaching of the midpoint of something; prime, mid-life, etc.</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) a dividing into halves</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣполовление</i> (prěpolovlenije), from <i>прѣполовити</i> (prěpoloviti).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Преполовение)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) ellipsis of <i>Преполове́ние Пятидеся́тницы</i> (Prepolovénije Pjatidesjátnicy); Mid-Pentecost</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣполовление</i> (prěpolovlenije), from <i>прѣполовити</i> (prěpoloviti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) obstacle, hindrance, impediment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣпона</i> (prěpona), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perpona</i>. Cognate with <i>пина́ть</i> (pinátʹ, “to kick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very apt, very quickwitted</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>поня́тливый</i> (ponjátlivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to entrust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to entrust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escort, to convoy</li><li>to send (documents etc.)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to spend (time)</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escort, to convoy</li><li>to send (documents etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>провожда́ть</i> (provoždátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) pre-production</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pre-production</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very repulsive</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>проти́вный</i> (protívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preputial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) prepuce (foreskin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>praeputium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstacle, impediment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder, to prevent, to prohibit, to impede, to put obstacles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coitus interruptus (sexual intercourse interrupted by withdrawal of the penis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abort, to cut short, to discontinue or suspend abruptly</li><li>to interrupt (a conversation, act, etc.)</li><li>to break off, to sever</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a break, to adjourn, (US) to recess</li><li>to be aborted, to be cut short, to be discontinued or suspended abruptly</li><li>to be interrupted (of a conversation, act, etc.)</li><li>to break up, to be cut off (of a voice, line, connection, etc.)</li><li>to be broken off, to be severed</li></ol><p><i>прерва́ть</i> (prervátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) questionable, arguable</li></ol><p><i>пререка́(ться)</i> (prereká(tʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bicker, to quarrel</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-рек-</i> (-rek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prairie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>prairie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prerogative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>praerogativa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interruption, cutting off, breaking off</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) interrupt</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) interrupt handler</li></ol><p>From <i>прерыва́ть</i> (preryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abort, to cut short, to discontinue or suspend abruptly</li><li>to interrupt (a conversation, act, etc.)</li><li>to break off, to sever</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a break, to adjourn, (US) to recess</li><li>to be aborted, to be cut short, to be discontinued or suspended abruptly</li><li>to be interrupted (of a conversation, act, etc.)</li><li>to break up, to be cut off (of a voice, line, connection, etc.)</li><li>to be broken off, to be severed</li></ol><p><i>прерыва́ть</i> (preryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that interrupts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intermittent</li><li>irregular</li><li>broken (<i>line</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прерыв(а́ть)</i> (preryv(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Presbyterian</li></ol><p>From <i>пресвите́рия</i> (presvitɛ́rija) +&lrm; <i>-а́нин</i> (-ánin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Presbyterianism (form of Protestantism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>most holy</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>пресвяти́й</i> (presvjatýj). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suppress, to stop</li></ol><p><i>пресе́к-</i> (presék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preclusion, prevention, constraint, restraint, restriction, suppression</li></ol><p><i>пресе́к-</i> (presék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suppress, to stop</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exceedingly badly, in a nasty or foul manner</li></ol><p><i>прескве́рный</i> (preskvérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>скве́рный</i> (skvérnyj); most (or exceedingly) bad, nasty, foul</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>скве́рный</i> (skvérnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pursuit, chase, prosecution, persecution</li></ol><p><i>пресле́довать</i> (preslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosecutor, persecutor, pursuer</li></ol><p><i>пресле́довать</i> (preslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue, to chase</li><li>to persecute</li><li>to torment, to victimize</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to prosecute</li><li>to strive (for); to pursue</li><li>to haunt</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) proverbial, notorious</li><li>ill-famed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, to crawl</li><li>to grovel <i>(before)</i>, to bow and scrape <i>(before)</i>, to fawn <i>(on)</i>, to lick the boots <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣсмꙑкати сѧ</i> (prěsmykati sę, “to creep, to crawl”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smykati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>смыка́ть</i> (smykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reptile</li></ol><p>From пресмыка́ться (presmykátʹsja, “to grovel, to crawl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable&#32;or&#32;uncountable</i>) fresh water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freshwater (living in fresh water)</li></ol><p><i>пре́сный</i> (présnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet, fresh (water)</li><li>unleavened (bread)</li><li>tasteless (food)</li><li>stale, insipid, vapid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прѣснъ</i> (prěsnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prěsnъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;prḗˀskas</i>. Cognate with English <i>fresh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extremely calmly, extremely tranquilly</li></ol><p><i>преспоко́йный</i> (prespokójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extremely calm, extremely tranquil</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>споко́йный</i> (spokójnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press (device used to apply pressure)</li><li>abdominal muscles, abs, prelum, prelum abdominale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>press</i>. Further from Latin <i>pressō</i> (frequentative of <i>premō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press conference (question and answer session with members of television, print and other media)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paperweight (small, decorative, somewhat weighty object)</li><li>blotter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>presse-papier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press release (official written media statement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>press release</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press secretary</li></ol><p>First attested in 1973. Borrowed from English <i>press secretary</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press-center</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>media</i>) press</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>presse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) defensive pressure, e.g.:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>basketball</i>) press (e.g. full-court press)</li><li>(<i>soccer</i>) pressing</li><li>(<i>field hockey or ice hockey</i>) pressure</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) pressure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pressing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to compress, to apply pressure to (<i>using a press</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pressure</li></ol><p><i>пресс</i> (press) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;religious</i>) alternative form of <i>перестава́ть</i> (perestavátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>преста́ть</i> (prestátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aged, advanced in years</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>старе́(ть)</i> (staré(tʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;religious</i>) alternative form of <i>переста́ть</i> (perestátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ста́ть</i> (státʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prestige</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>prestige</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prestigiousness, prestige</li></ol><p><i>прести́жный</i> (prestížnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prestigious, prestige</li></ol><p><i>прести́ж</i> (prestíž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) presto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>presto</i> (“quickly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) altar, communion table (the table used as the place of offering in the celebration of the Eucharist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣстолъ</i> (prěstolŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perstolъ</i>. Note prefixless Polish <i>stolec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heir to the throne</li></ol><p><i>престо́л</i> (prestól) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>насле́дник</i> (naslédnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>too strange, very strange, extremely strange, most strange</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-, “most”) +&lrm; <i>стра́нный</i> (stránnyj, “strange”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very scary</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>стра́шный</i> (strášnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to infringe, to violate (laws, regulations, norms, etc.)</li><li>to overstep, to transgress, to trespass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣстѫпати</i> (prěstǫpati), compare native <i>переступа́ть</i> (perestupátʹ). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to infringe, to violate (laws, regulations, norms, etc.)</li><li>to overstep, to transgress, to trespass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣстѫпити</i> (prěstǫpiti), compare native <i>переступи́ть</i> (perestupítʹ). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crime, criminal act, offense</li><li>specifically felony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣстѫплениѥ</i> (prěstǫplenije). By surface analysis, <i>преступи́ть</i> (prestupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminal, delinquent, offender</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣстѫпьникъ</i> (prěstǫpĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>преступи́ть</i> (prestupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminality (state of being criminal)</li><li>crime (general crime)</li></ol><p><i>престу́пный</i> (prestúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>criminal, felonious</li></ol><p><i>преступи́ть</i> (prestupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) transubstantiation</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>trānsubstantiātiō</i>; <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>существо́</i> (suščestvó) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be satiated, have had a surfeit, be surfeited</li></ol><p><i>пресыща́ть</i> (presyščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satiety</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) change, transformation</li></ol><p><i>претвори́ть</i> (pretvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implement</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;literary) to turn <i>(into)</i>, to change <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣтворити</i> (prětvoriti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ). Doublet of native dialectal <i>перетвори́ть</i> (peretvorítʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>перетворити</i> (peretvoryty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be realized/implemented</li></ol><p><i>претвори́ть</i> (pretvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implement</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;literary) to turn <i>(into)</i>, to change <i>(into)</i>, to convert <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣтварꙗти</i> (prětvarjati). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>тво́р-</i> (tvór-) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ). Doublet of native dialectal <i>перетворя́ть</i> (peretvorjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be realized/implemented</li></ol><p><i>претворя́ть</i> (pretvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claimant (to, upon), aspirant (to), applicant (one who applies)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to claim, to apply, to pretend, to aspire, to have pretensions</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>praetendō</i>, + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claim, pretension, title (demand for one's rights, or for someone/something one thinks they have the right to).</li><li>complaint, dissatisfaction.</li><li>(<i>law</i>) formal complaint of a creditor/purchaser/customer expressing protest to a debtor/supplier/contractor on account of a contract violation.</li><li>pretense (an attempt to impress others by showing off some positive attributes).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretentiousness</li></ol><p><i>претенцио́зн(ый)</i> (pretenciózn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretentious, affected</li><li>ambitious (showy)</li><li>grandiose</li><li>orotund</li><li>brassy</li><li>rococo</li><li>artsy-fartsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, to endure, to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>to undergo</li></ol><p><i>претерпе́ть</i> (preterpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer, to endure, to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>to undergo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣтрьпѣти</i> (prětrĭpěti). By surface analysis, <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ). Doublet of native <i>перетерпе́ть</i> (pereterpétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to forbid, to ban</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to hinder, to oppose</li><li>to sicken, to disgust</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прѣтити</i> (prětiti, “to threaten”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pertiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) impediment, obstacle (used almost exclusively in idiomatic expression <i>ка́мень преткнове́ния</i> (kámenʹ pretknovénija))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superthin</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sweat</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) <i>(of skin)</i> to become intertriginous, to become wet and irritated in folds</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exaggeration</li></ol><p><i>преувели́чить</i> (preuvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exaggerated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exaggerate, to overstate</li></ol><p><i>преувели́чить</i> (preuvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exaggerate, to overstate</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>увели́чить</i> (uvelíčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very surprising</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>удиви́тельный</i> (udivítelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to downplay</li><li>to underestimate</li><li>to mince (to suppress or weaken the force of something or someone)</li></ol><p><i>преуме́ньшить</i> (preuménʹšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to downplay</li><li>to underestimate</li><li>to mince (to suppress or weaken the force of something or someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very stubborn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed, to be successful, to prosper</li><li>to flourish, to thrive, to prosper</li></ol><p><i>преуспе́ть</i> (preuspétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flourishing, forehanded, going, prosperous, successful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to succeed, to be successful, to prosper</li><li>to flourish, to thrive, to prosper</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>успе́ть</i> (uspétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) success, prosperity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prefect</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>praefectus</i> (“put in charge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prefecture (administrative division)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>praefectūra</i>, from <i>praefectus</i> (compare <i>префе́кт</i> (prefékt)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preference (a card game)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) preference for, predilection</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>préférence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preference</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prefix (letters at the beginning of a word)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) prefixation</li></ol><p>From <i>пре́фикс</i> (préfiks) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(litery,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>переходи́ть</i> (perexodítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>temporary, transitory, transient, short-lived</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>прехоро́ший</i> (prexoróšij): very good, very pretty</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>хоро́шенький</i> (xoróšenʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very good</li></ol><p>From <i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very brave</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>хра́брый</i> (xrábryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precedent (past act used as example)</li><li>legal authority</li><li>(<i>law</i>) precedent, example, case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>praecēdēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;astronomy) precession</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very clean</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very pure</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) immaculate</li></ol><p><i>пре-</i> (pre-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>with, when, during (in the time and/or presence (of))</li><li>at, by</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pri</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;prei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;préy</i>, whence English <i>for</i> and <i>per</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for all that, despite all (of) that/this</li></ol><p>Literally, “with all that”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by means of, with the help of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all things being equal, all else being equal, ceteris paribus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at death's door</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at that (in addition), (and) at the same time</li><li>in the process, in doing so</li><li>on top of that, withal, for good measure</li></ol><p>при (pri) + э́том (étom)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) arrival</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) attachment, on</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) adding to</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) slightly, partially</li><li>(<i>place names</i>) by, near, next to</li></ol><p>See <i>при</i> (pri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>approx., approximately</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>приблизи́тельно</i> (priblizítelʹno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic&#32;colloquial,&#32;vulgar,&#32;derogatory) calling card</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add (to perform the arithmetical operation of addition)</li><li>to increase speed</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to rise, to swell, to wax</li></ol><p><i>приба́вить</i> (pribávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, increment</li><li>raise, rise</li><li>allowance</li><li>annex (addition, an extension)</li></ol><p><i>приба́вить</i> (pribávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, raise, rise</li><li>addition, augmentation</li><li>addendum, supplement, appendix</li></ol><p><i>приба́вить</i> (pribávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add (to perform the arithmetical operation of addition)</li><li>to begin to move more quickly</li></ol><p><i>приба́вить</i> (pribávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to rise, to swell, to wax</li></ol><p><i>прибавля́ть</i> (pribavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) citizen/dweller of one of the Baltic states (usually of Estonia, Latvia or Lithuania)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>балт</i> (balt, “Balt”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Baltic (referring to the Baltic States, not the Baltic Sea)</li></ol><p><i>Приба́лт(ика)</i> (Pribált(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humorous saying</li><li>(<i>folklore</i>) nursery rhyme, short entertaining story for children, fable</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ба́(ять)</i> (bá(jatʹ), “to tell”) +&lrm; <i>-у́тка</i> (-útka)</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>iocus</i> (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yek-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resort, to have recourse, to fall back <i>(upon)</i></li><li>to come running</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resort, to have recourse, to fall back <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бегнуть</i> (begnutʹ), from <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ, “to run”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act poorer than one really is</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to act modest, to downplay one's abilities or achievements, to sell oneself short (such as to appear humble or arouse sympathy)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj, “poor”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act poorer than one really is</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to act modest, to downplay one's abilities or achievements, to sell oneself short (such as to appear humble or arouse sympathy)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бе́дный</i> (bédnyj, “poor”) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come running</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge, resort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to reserve</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nail down (to attach with a nail)</li><li>to move, to drive somewhere (<i>of the wind or water</i>)</li><li>to press to the ground (<i>by an impact or by wind or water</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill</li></ol><p><i>приби́ть</i> (pribítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be nailed, to be attached with nails</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend down (<i>from wind, rain, etc.</i>)</li><li>to get close, to be brought close (<i>from wind, rain, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to join [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a group, a family, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прибива́ть</i> (pribivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in order, to clean up, to tidy <i>(up)</i></li><li>to put away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pribirati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nail down (to attach with a nail)</li><li>to move, to drive somewhere (<i>of the wind or water</i>)</li><li>to press to the ground (<i>by an impact or by wind or water</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be nailed, to be attached with nails</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend down (<i>from wind, rain, etc.</i>)</li><li>to get close, to be brought close (<i>from wind, rain, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to join [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a group, a family, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приби́ть</i> (pribítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring nearer</li></ol><p><i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw near, to approach</li><li>to near</li><li>to approximate</li></ol><p><i>приближа́ть</i> (približátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approach (the act of drawing near)</li><li>approximation</li></ol><p><i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>приближа́ть</i> (približátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close (<i>of people</i>)</li><li>approximate, rough</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person in attendance, retainer</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) retinue</li></ol><p>Has the appearance of a past passive participle of <i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ), but the actual past passive participle of this verb is <i>прибли́женный</i> (priblížennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>approximate, approximately, roughly</li></ol><p><i>приблизи́тельный</i> (priblizítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>approximate, rough, general</li></ol><p><i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring nearer</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>близ</i> (bliz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw near, to approach</li><li>to near</li><li>to approximate</li></ol><p><i>прибли́зить</i> (priblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stray</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surf, breakers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to become a little ill, to come down with something</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>palustral; palustrine (pertaining to or located in marshes)</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>боло́тный</i> (bolótnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>device, appliance, tool, instrument, gadget</li><li>set, apparatus (<i>collection of compactly collapsible accessories for a particular purpose</i>)</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;colloquial) male genitals</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;priborъ</i>, related to <i>прибра́ть</i> (pribrátʹ, “to put in order, tidy up”), as <i>сборка</i> (sborka) is related to <i>собрать</i> (sobratʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) instrument</li></ol><p><i>прибо́р</i> (pribór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument making</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in order, to clean up, to tidy <i>(up)</i></li><li>to put away</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coastal</li><li>riverside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coast, shore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive</li><li>to increase, to rise, to grow</li><li>to rise, to swell (<i>water</i>), to wax (<i>moon</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прибы́ть</i> (pribýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rise, increase, growth, increment</li><li>profit, gain, benefit, increment, return, earning</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) runner, riser, head</li></ol><p><i>прибы́ть</i> (pribýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profitability</li></ol><p><i>при́быльный</i> (príbylʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profitable, lucrative</li></ol><p><i>при́быль</i> (príbylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival (act of arriving or something that has arrived)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive</li><li>to increase, to rise, to grow</li><li>to rise, to swell <i>(water)</i>, to wax <i>(moon)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ). Compare Czech <i>přibýt</i> and Polish <i>przybyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>приве́т</i> (privét); hi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bait, lure (anything which allures)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halt <i>(during travel)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, to rest</li><li><i>(ship)</i> to come alongside</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come, to appear</li></ol><p><i>привали́ть</i> (privalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean, to rest</li><li><i>(ship)</i> to come alongside</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come, to appear</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) private chat or account</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privat-docentship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privatization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to privatize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>privatisieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privacy</li></ol><p><i>прива́тный</i> (privátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>private (individual, not public, or secret)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) unofficial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>prywatny</i>, from Latin <i>prīvātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing, leading</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) reduction</li></ol><p><i>привед-</i> (prived-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring, to fetch (to transport toward someone&#47;somewhere by vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fastidious, fussy person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>picky, choosy, hard to please, fastidious, squeamish, excessively particular</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вере́дливый</i> (verédlivyj, “weak, effeminate”), from <i>ве́ред</i> (véred, “boil, abscess”) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj), where <i>ве́ред</i> (véred) originally meant, and still dialectally means, "pain, harm"; compare doublet <i>вред</i> (vred). Sense development "too sensitive to pain" → "weak, effeminate" → "squeamish, picky".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastidious, fussy person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adherent, follower, partisan, stickler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adherence, devotion</li></ol><p><i>приве́рженный</i> (privéržennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>committed, devoted, loyal, faithful [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Russian Church Slavonic <i>приверженый</i> (priverženyj), originally past passive participle of <i>&#42;привергнꙋти</i> (&#42;privergnuti); compare Russian Church Slavonic <i>привергнꙋтисѧ</i> (privergnutisę, “to be committed to”), Old Church Slavonic <i>приврѣшти</i> (privrěšti, “to strike”), and Russian <i>отве́рженный</i> (otvéržennyj), originally past passive participle of <i>отве́ргнуть</i> (otvérgnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendage (a part that is joined to something larger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (someone with)</li><li>to lead (of a road)</li><li>to result, to lead, to bring</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to reduce</li><li>to quote, to cite, to adduce, to list</li><li>to bring, to put, to set (into condition)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to happen [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for there to be the occasion [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have the occasion to</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>привести́</i> (privestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) hello, hi</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a greeting, a hello, a hi</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>привѣтъ</i> (privětŭ, “greeting; appeal; dictum, word; intention, will”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;privětъ</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>приві́т</i> (pryvít) and Belarusian <i>прыве́т</i> (pryvjét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) diminutive of <i>приве́т</i> (privét); hi, hey</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) diminutive of <i>приве́т</i> (privét); a hi, a hey</li></ol><p><i>приве́т</i> (privét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>friendly (in a friendly manner)</li></ol><p><i>приве́тливый</i> (privétlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendliness, affability</li></ol><p><i>приве́тливый</i> (privétlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affable, neighborly, communicable, forthcoming</li></ol><p><i>приве́т</i> (privét) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salutatory, welcoming</li></ol><p><i>приве́тствие</i> (privétstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greeting, salutation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>привет</i> (privet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet, to hail, to welcome</li><li>to salute</li></ol><p><i>приве́т</i> (privét) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) hi</li></ol><p><i>приве́т</i> (privét) +&lrm; <i>-у́ли</i> (plural of <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to greet; to welcome; to make someone welcome</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give shelter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;privěťati</i> (“to greet”), from <i>&#42;věťati</i> (“to say”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to inoculate, to vaccinate <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>botany</i>) to engraft <i>(upon)</i>, to inoculate <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(a habit or property)</i> to inculcate, to impart, to cultivate</li></ol><p><i>приви́ть</i> (privítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) inoculation, immunization, vaccination</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vaccine</li><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) grafting</li></ol><p><i>привив(ать)</i> (priviv(atʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-к(а)</i> (-k(a))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghost, spectre, apparition, spook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appear <i>(in a dream or vision)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>privileged</li></ol><p>Constructed as a past passive participle of <i>&#42;привилегирова́ть</i> (&#42;privilegirovátʹ, “to privilege”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privilege (a peculiar benefit, advantage, or favor)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>privilegium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw on</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>винти́ть</i> (vintítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw on</li></ol><p><i>привинти́ть</i> (privintítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to inoculate, to vaccinate <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>botany</i>) to engraft <i>(upon)</i>, to inoculate <i>(with)</i></li><li><i>(a habit or property)</i> to inculcate, to impart, to cultivate</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smack, touch, aftertaste</li><li>shade, tinge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attractiveness, appeal</li></ol><p><i>привлека́тельный</i> (privlekátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attractive, winning, alluring</li></ol><p><i>привлека́ть</i> (privlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>притяга́тельный</i> (pritjagátelʹnyj), Latin <i>attractīvus</i> (whence English <i>attractive</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attract, to draw (arouse interest)</li></ol><p><i>привле́чь</i> (privléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effort to attract</li><li>involvement</li><li>impleading, institution</li></ol><p><i>привле́к-</i> (privlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attract, to draw (arouse interest)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>привлѣщи</i> (privlěšti). By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ). Doublet of <i>приволо́чь</i> (privolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce <i>(into)</i>, to add <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>внести́</i> (vnestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to introduce <i>(into)</i>, to add <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вноси́ть</i> (vnosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technology</i>) drive, driving gear</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) drive</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) ellipsis of <i>приводна́я радиоста́нция or приводно́й радиомая́к</i> (privodnája radiostáncija or privodnój radiomaják); non-directional beacon (NDB)</li><li>bringing, leading</li><li>(<i>law</i>) forcible delivery (<i>of a witness, defendant, etc. to court</i>)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) arrest</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>приводи́ть</i> (privodítʹ), since the 19th century. Perhaps a calque of German <i>Antrieb</i>.</p><p>Deverbal from <i>приводи́ть</i> (privodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (<i>someone with</i>)</li><li>to lead (<i>of a road</i>)</li><li>to result, to lead, to bring</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to reduce</li><li>to quote, to cite, to adduce, to list</li><li>to bring, to put, to set (<i>into condition</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse (to puzzle, to bewilder)</li><li>to abash (to make ashamed, to embarrass)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to happen [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for there to be the occasion [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have the occasion to</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>приводи́ть</i> (privodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alighting/landing on water, splashdown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drive</li></ol><p><i>при́вод</i> (prívod) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój) or <i>приво́д</i> (privód) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing</li><li>import</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>во́з</i> (vóz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, to fetch by vehicle</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>located near the train station</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вокза́л</i> (vokzál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Volga region, area near the Volga river</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>Во́лга</i> (Vólga) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ). Compare Czech <i>přivléci</i>, Kashubian <i>przëwléc</i>, Silesian <i>prziwlyc</i>, and Polish <i>przywlec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open space; expanse</li><li>freedom, ease; comfort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free; unrestrained; carefree</li><li>vast (of a location)</li></ol><p><i>приво́лье</i> (privólʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorkeeper, gatekeeper</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>врата́</i> (vratá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to half-rise</li></ol><p><i>привста́ть</i> (privstátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to half-rise</li><li>to rise, stand up</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вста́ть</i> (vstátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptation</li><li>acquired tolerance, addiction, habituation</li></ol><p><i>привыка́ть</i> (privykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get used (to), to get accustomed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>привы́кнуть</i> (privýknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get used (to), to get accustomed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;privyknǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́кнуть</i> (-výknutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accustomed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habit, custom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;privyčьka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>привы́кнуть</i> (privýknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>customarily, usually, habitually</li><li>in a practiced manner</li></ol><p><i>привы́чный</i> (privýčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>habitual, customary, usual</li><li>accustomed (to)</li></ol><p><i>привы́кнуть</i> (privýknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attachment, affection</li></ol><p><i>привя́занный</i> (privjázannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to attach, to fasten, to tether</li><li>to win over, to attach</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/get/be attached <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attach oneself <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to pester</li></ol><p><i>привяза́ть</i> (privjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tying, attaching</li><li>linkage, affixment</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) binding</li></ol><p><i>привяза́ть</i> (privjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to attach, to fasten, to tether</li><li>to win over, to attach</li></ol><p><i>привяза́ть</i> (privjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/get/be attached <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attach oneself <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to pester</li></ol><p><i>привя́зывать</i> (privjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leash, lead</li><li>tether, harness, bridle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;endearing) hi</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>приве́т</i> (privét, “hi”) +&lrm; <i>мя́у</i> (mjáu, “meow”). Recorded since at least the 2000s. Had a boost in popularity in 2019 when it was used by singer Olga Buzova in episode 11 of YouTube game show <i>Comment Out</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>приве́т</i> (privét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>пригиба́ть</i> (prigibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) adverbial</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>глаго́л</i> (glagól) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), a calque of Latin <i>adverbiālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adverbial case (grammatical case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth</li><li>to slick back (hair)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth</li><li>to slick back (hair)</li></ol><p><i>пригла́дить</i> (prigláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invite</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гласи́ть</i> (glasítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invite</li></ol><p><i>пригласи́ть</i> (priglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invitation (act of inviting)</li><li>invitation (document containing an invitation)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>пригласи́ть</i> (priglasítʹ), <i>пригласи́ть</i> (priglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to deaden, to damp down</li><li>to ease, to relieve, to mitigate</li><li>to dim, to subdue, to moderate, to soften</li></ol><p><i>приглуши́ть</i> (priglušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muffle, to deaden, to damp down</li><li>to ease, to relieve, to mitigate</li><li>to dim, to subdue, to moderate, to soften</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>глуши́ть</i> (glušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>muffled</li><li>subdued</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look after, to watch [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to choose, to find</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look hard [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘at something’</small>]</li><li>to have an eye</li><li>to get accustomed/used [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to bore [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пригляде́ть</i> (prigljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look after, to watch [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to choose, to find</li></ol><p><i>пригляде́ть</i> (prigljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look hard [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘at something’</small>]</li><li>to get accustomed/used [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to bore [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пригля́дывать</i> (prigljádyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to catch/take someone's fancy</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (someone or something here)</li><li>to adjust</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend down, to bow</li></ol><p><i>пригну́ть</i> (prignútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sentence (to), to convict, to damn</li><li>to accompany something with words</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to talk into doing something</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to repeat, to say again and again, to keep saying/repeating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sentence, verdict</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) judgement, conviction (<i>of opinion</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) word accompaniment</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) decision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sentence (to), to convict, to damn</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come in handy, to prove useful</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>годи́ться</i> (godítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fitness, suitability</li></ol><p><i>приго́дный</i> (prigódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable (for), fit (for), good (for) (appropriate for a certain occasion)</li><li>applicable, eligible</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>го́дный</i> (gódnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come in handy, to prove useful</li></ol><p><i>пригоди́ться</i> (prigodítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) beautiful, good-looking</li><li>(of weather) clear, bright, sunny</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>го́жий</i> (góžij), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;goditi</i>. Parallel with Belarusian <i>прыго́жы</i> (pryhóžy). Cognate with Czech <i>hezký</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>згодан</i>. Compare typologically <i>хоро́шенький</i> (xoróšenʹkij) (see more). Also compare English <i>handsome</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (someone or something here)</li><li>to adjust</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be burnt</li></ol><p><i>пригоре́ть</i> (prigorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slightly burnt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be burnt slightly</li><li>to stick to the (hot) surface as a result of burning</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suburb</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>го́род</i> (górod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suburban</li><li>local</li></ol><p><i>при́город</i> (prígorod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hillock, knoll</li></ol><p>Compare Polish <i>pagórek</i>. Analyzable as <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гора́</i> (gorá) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow of the hand, cupped hand</li><li>handful, fistful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to start being sad, to start grieving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to make ready</li><li>to cook</li></ol><p><i>пригото́вить</i> (prigotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, to prepare oneself [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пригота́вливать</i> (prigotávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to make ready</li><li>to cook</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, to prepare oneself</li></ol><p><i>пригото́вить</i> (prigotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation, arrangement, preparative</li></ol><p><i>пригото́вить</i> (prigotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, to make ready</li><li>to cook</li></ol><p><i>пригото́вить</i> (prigotóvitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, to prepare oneself [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘for what’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приготовля́ть</i> (prigotovljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frontier, border</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>грани́ца</i> (graníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warm up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shelter</li></ol><p><i>пригре́ть</i> (prigrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to warm up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shelter</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>грози́ть</i> (grozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a sip, to taste</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>губа́</i> (gubá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to impart; to attach</li><li>to increase, to strengthen</li></ol><p><i>прида́ть</i> (pridátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(against)</i>, to press down <i>(against)</i>, to weigh down <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(against)</i>, to press down <i>(against)</i>, to weigh down <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>придави́ть</i> (pridavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving, conferring, imparting, communication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dowry, dot, portion</li><li>layette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendage, addition, supplement</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) appendix</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hypophysis</li></ol><p><i>прида́ть</i> (pridátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) subordinate clause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>additional, supplementary</li></ol><p>From <i>прида́ток</i> (pridátok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, to impart; to attach</li><li>to increase, to strengthen</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving, imparting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(up)</i>, to draw up</li></ol><p><i>придви́г-</i> (pridvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move up, to draw near</li></ol><p><i>придвига́ть</i> (pridvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(up)</i>, to draw up</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move up, to draw near</li></ol><p><i>придви́нуть</i> (pridvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) court (of a king, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtier (person in attendance at a royal court)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>двор</i> (dvor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) side chapel, side altar (of a church)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приде́лать</i> (pridélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, to hold back</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, to hold back</li></ol><p><i>придержа́ть</i> (prideržátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold <i>(to)</i>, to keep <i>(to)</i></li><li>to stick, to adhere [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приде́рживать</i> (pridérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find fault with, to carp on, to nag, to pick on (to criticize excessively) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>придра́ться</i> (pridrátʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take things too literally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nitpick, quibble</li></ol><p><i>придир(а́ться)</i> (pridir(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a hypercritical or faultfinding manner</li><li>meticulously, rigorously</li></ol><p><i>приди́рчивый</i> (pridírčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypercritical, persnickety, nitpicky, faultfinding, quibbling</li></ol><p><i>придир-</i> (pridir-) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Transnistrian</li><li>(near) Dniester river, (of) Dniester region</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roadside, wayside</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>доро́га</i> (doróga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find fault with, to carp on, to nag, to pick on (to criticize excessively) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ, “to tear off, fleece”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1956 spelling of <i>прийти́</i> (prijtí).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прийти́сь</i> (prijtísʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up, to think out, to devise, to contrive, to invent</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idea, device</li></ol><p><i>приду́мать</i> (pridúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up, to think up, to devise, to contrive, to invent</li></ol><p><i>приду́мать</i> (pridúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play dumb (to fool&#47;mess&#47;screw around by feigning ignorance)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>дури́ть</i> (durítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupidish</li></ol><p><i>приду́р(о)к</i> (pridúr(o)k) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moron, imbecile</li><li>arsehole (inconsiderate or mean-spirited person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolishness, whim, whimsy, freak, caprice, fancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>приду́риваться</i> (pridúrivatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>дури́ть</i> (durítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to choke, to strangle</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>души́ть</i> (dušítʹ, “to strangle, to smother”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) aspiration (burst of air that follows the release of some consonants)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-, “adding to”) +&lrm; <i>дыха́ние</i> (dyxánije, “breathing”), a calque of Latin <i>aspīrātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) aspirate, aspirated</li></ol><p><i>приды́хать</i> (pridýxatʹ, “to exhale strongly”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspirate (linguistics&#58; sound produced)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to bother, to pester, to harass or to find fault</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pall on, to become boring [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ед-</i> (jed-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival (act of arriving or something that has arrived by car, truck, bus, or train)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive, to come (by vehicle or horse)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nonresident</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>visitant, guest, entrant, newcomer, alien, nonresident</li></ol><p>From приезжа́ть (prijezžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptability, admissability</li></ol><p><i>прие́млемый</i> (prijémlemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acceptable (capable, worthy or sure of being accepted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pall on, to become boring [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Exclamation used to indicate the impossibility of further continuation of a process; that's it, here we go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive, to come (by vehicle or horse)</li><li><i>(past tense, plural only)</i> to hit rock bottom</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(to)</i>, to clasp <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press hard, to put in a tight situation</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself (to), to nestle up (to), to snuggle up (to), to cuddle up (to)</li></ol><p><i>прижа́ть</i> (prižátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn (<i>by touching something hot</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to cauterize</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to slightly burn</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a poor non-serf (nobleman, intellectual, merchant) who lived in a rich person's house at the owner's expense and did not have any particular responsibilities/occupation</li><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) a person living at someone else's expense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) a person living at someone else's expense; dependent, parasite, leech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a poor female non-serf (noblewoman, intellectual, merchant) who lived in a rich person's house at the owner's expense, did not have any particular responsibilities/occupation and entertained and kept the owner company</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;disapproving) a female living at someone else's expense; dependent, parasite, leech</li></ol><p><i>прижива́л</i> (priživál) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) impoverished nobleman, merchant, or intellectual who lived in a rich person's house and entertained them and kept them company</li><li>toady, sycophant</li></ol><p><i>прижива́ть(ся)</i> (priživátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get accustomed <i>(to a place)</i>, to get acclimated/acclimatized</li><li>to take root, to strike root</li></ol><p><i>прижи́ться</i> (prižítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cauterization, searing</li></ol><p><i>прижига́ть</i> (prižigátʹ, “to cauterize, to sear”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn (<i>by touching something hot</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to cauterize</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to slightly burn</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) occurring during someone's life, not posthumous</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press <i>(to)</i>, to clasp <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself (to), to nestle up (to), to snuggle up (to), to cuddle up (to)</li></ol><p><i>прижима́ть</i> (prižimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get accustomed <i>(to a place)</i>, to get acclimated/acclimatized</li><li>to take root, to strike root</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prize</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Middle Low German <i>pris</i>, from Old French <i>pris</i>, from Latin <i>pretium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become thoughtful/pensive, to give a thought, to consider</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>заду́маться</i> (zadúmatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become thoughtful/pensive, to give a thought, to consider</li></ol><p><i>призаду́маться</i> (prizadúmatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocation, calling</li><li>mission</li></ol><p><i>призва́ть</i> (prizvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>summoned</li><li>urged to, obliged to (+ abstract infinitive)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conscript</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to appeal, to summon</li><li>to call upon <i>(to)</i>, to urge <i>(to)</i></li><li>to draft, to call out, to call up</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overtone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stocky, squat, heavyset, thickset</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landing, touchdown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to land, to touch down</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to land, to touch down</li></ol><p><i>приземли́ться</i> (prizemlítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) prism</li><li>(<i>optics</i>) prism</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>πρίσμᾰ</i> (prísmă, “anything sawn; sawdust; (<i>Koine, geometry</i>) prism”), further from <i>πρίζω</i> (prízō) (a variant of <i>πρίω</i> (príō, “to saw”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize (to acknowledge the existence or legality of something)</li><li>to acknowledge, to see, to admit, to own</li><li>to find, to consider, to declare, to pronounce</li></ol><p><i>призна́ть</i> (priznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess, to admit</li></ol><p><i>признава́ть</i> (priznavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign, indication, tell</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) characteristic, feature</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-к</i> (-k).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>signum</i> (whence English <i>sign</i>, and many more), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (most likely), whence also Latin <i>sciō</i>. Also note <i>просека́ть</i> (prosekátʹ), <i>усека́ть</i> (usekátʹ) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i>), <i>заруби́ть себе́ на носу́</i> (zarubítʹ sebé na nosú).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acknowledgement, recognition</li><li>confession, admission, concession</li><li>declaration <i>(e.g., of love)</i></li></ol><p><i>призна́ть</i> (priznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acknowledged, recognized, avowed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>призна́ние</i> (priznánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appreciation (a just valuation or estimate of merit, worth, weight, etc.&#59; recognition of excellence)</li><li>gratitude, thankfulness</li><li>acknowledgment, credit</li></ol><p><i>призна́тельный</i> (priznátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grateful, appreciative, thankful</li></ol><p><i>призна́ть</i> (priznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p><p>Compare typogically Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þankaz</i> (whence English <i>thank</i>, akin to <i>&#42;þankijaną</i>, whence English <i>think</i>, further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teng-</i>, whence also Latin <i>tongeō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize (to acknowledge the existence or legality of something)</li><li>to acknowledge, to see, to admit, to own</li><li>to find, to consider, to declare, to pronounce</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess, to admit</li></ol><p><i>призна́ть</i> (priznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prize</li></ol><p><i>приз</i> (priz) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ghost, spectre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>призракъ</i> (prizrakŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spectral, phantasmal, shadowy, ghostly</li><li>unreal, illusory</li></ol><p><i>при́зрак</i> (prízrak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to support by charity</li></ol><p><i>призре́ть</i> (prizrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, charity</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) benevolence, mercy</li></ol><p><i>призре́ть</i> (prizrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to watch over, to supervise</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to support by charity</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>call, appeal</li><li>slogan</li><li>conscription, draft</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>призыва́ть</i> (prizyvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summoner, caller (<i>of something or for something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>призыва́ть</i> (prizyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, to appeal, to summon</li><li>to call upon <i>(to)</i>, to urge <i>(to)</i></li><li>to draft, to call out, to call up</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calling, appealing, summoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) draftee, recruit, conscript</li></ol><p><i>призы́в</i> (prizýv) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) draft, conscription</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draftee, conscripted person</li></ol><p><i>призы́в</i> (prizýv) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prize-winner, awardee</li></ol><p><i>приз</i> (priz) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) alternative form of <i>принима́ть</i> (prinimátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>има́ть</i> (imátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come (to), to arrive (at)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prijьti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get confused</li><li>to abash (to lose self-possession&#59; to become ashamed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather one's wits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be necessary, to be required [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have to</i> or <i>must</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>to fall, to occur [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on a specific date’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be (<i>in a certain state</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a certain state’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>to have a certain time of it</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>to come from, to be constituted by (<i>of a quantity</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a source’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>account for</i> with the object of <i>на</i> (na) as the subject)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be due [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘from someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘some amount’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be related [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to suit, to fit (<i>usually in specific expressions</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘with respect to something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Prefix <i>при-</i> (pri-, “indicating action or motion towards a terminal point”) + <i>идти́</i> (idtí, “to walk or go”) + <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ, “reflexive”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order (oral), command, orders (written)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) board, department, office, chancellery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, instruction</li></ol><p><i>приказа́ть</i> (prikazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to command, to give orders, to direct</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;literary) to die</li><li>(figurative,&#32;ironical,&#32;humorous,&#32;of a non-living thing) to cease to exist</li></ol><p>Literally, “to command to live long”. Popular etymology: a dying person, blessing the crowd of relatives, bequeathed (ordered) them to live long.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) shop clerk, shop assistant, salesman (hired worker in a shop in pre-revolutionary Russia)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) steward (managing landowner’s manor in pre-revolutionary Russia)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;dated) someone who blindly follows and obeys his master</li></ol><p><i>приказа́ть</i> (prikazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shop clerk, shop assistant, salesman (hired worker in a shop in pre-revolutionary Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to command, to give orders, to direct</li></ol><p><i>приказа́ть</i> (prikazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pin, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stab to death</li></ol><p><i>приколо́ть</i> (prikolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to joke around; to tease; to pull someone's leg</li></ol><p><i>прика́лывать</i> (prikályvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish off</li></ol><p><i>прико́нчить</i> (prikónčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appropriate; to use something as property when it belongs to someone else; to steal</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>карма́н</i> (karmán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (make physical contact with)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>каса́ться</i> (kasátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to press down by rolling, to roll smooth</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to arrive, to come rolling in</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to roll (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to arrive, to come rolling in</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to press down by rolling, to roll smooth</li></ol><p><i>прикати́ть</i> (prikatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>приката́ть</i> (prikatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic spelling of <i>прика́зчик</i> (prikázčik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>прика́щик</i> (prikáščik).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prequel</li></ol><p>First attested in 1993 [Yandex News]. From English <i>prequel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) threads, outfit, clothing, attire</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прики́нуть</i> (prikínutʹ, “to try on”), based on the stem <i>прики́д-</i> (prikíd-); compare imperfective <i>прики́дывать</i> (prikídyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) estimate</li></ol><p><i>прики́д-</i> (prikíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to estimate, to make a rough calculation, to ballpark</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gauge, to size up, to weigh up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw in, to add</li></ol><p><i>прики́д-</i> (prikíd-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pretend to be, to feign, to act</li></ol><p><i>прики́дывать</i> (prikídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to estimate, to make a rough calculation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gauge, to size up, to weigh up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw in, to add</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperative) to get, to imagine, to believe, to think (about), to guess what; to fully appraise (a situation)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p><p>Compare typologically <i>наброса́ть</i> (nabrosátʹ), <i>набро́сок</i> (nabrósok) (&lt; <i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pretend to be, to feign, to act</li></ol><p><i>прики́нуть</i> (prikínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stick <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a strong liking <i>(to)</i>, to take a fancy <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>прикипе́ть</i> (prikipétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stick <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take a strong liking <i>(to)</i>, to take a fancy <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кипе́ть</i> (kipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rifle) stock, butt</li><li>trimmings</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прикла́дывать</i> (prikládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>applied, practical</li></ol><p><i>прикла́д(ывать)</i> (priklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (next to)</li><li>to attach, to enclose, to join</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put oneself <i>(to)</i>, to press oneself <i>(against)</i></li><li>to take aim</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kiss</li></ol><p><i>прикла́дывать</i> (prikládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to glue, to paste</li></ol><p><i>прикле́ить</i> (prikléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere, to be glued, to be pasted</li></ol><p><i>прикле́ивать</i> (prikléivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to glue, to paste</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere, to be glued, to be pasted</li></ol><p><i>прикле́ить</i> (prikléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean (something) slightly</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean (something) slightly</li></ol><p><i>приклони́ть</i> (priklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to happen, to occur</li></ol><p><i>приключи́ться</i> (priključítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adventure (that which happens without design)</li></ol><p><i>приключи́ться</i> (priključítʹsja, “to happen”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adventure, (<i>movie, novel</i>)</li></ol><p><i>приключе́ние</i> (priključénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>action movie (a type of motion picture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to happen, to occur</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ключи́ть</i> (-ključítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chain</li><li>to rivet</li><li>to pin down</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chain</li><li>to rivet</li><li>to pin down</li></ol><p><i>прикова́ть</i> (prikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>popular speech</i>) not bad!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joke</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) storage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) punch line, point</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pin, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stab to death</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to joke around; to tease; to pull someone's leg</li></ol><p><i>приколо́ть</i> (prikolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) funny, amusing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) curious, odd</li></ol><p><i>прико́л</i> (prikól) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish off</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ко́нчить</i> (kónčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a nap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прикосновеньѥ</i> (prikosnovenĭje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (make physical contact with)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>косну́ться</i> (kosnútʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to attach <i>(also figurative)</i></li><li>to register</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten</li><li>to register</li></ol><p><i>прикрепи́ть</i> (prikrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attachment (process or means by which something is physically attached)</li><li>insertion</li></ol><p><i>прикрепи́ть</i> (prikrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, to attach <i>(also figurative)</i></li><li>to register</li></ol><p><i>прикрепи́ть</i> (prikrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten</li><li>to register</li></ol><p><i>прикрепля́ть</i> (prikrepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout <i>(at)</i>, to raise one's voice</li></ol><p><i>прикри́кнуть</i> (prikríknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout <i>(at)</i>, to raise one's voice</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кри́кнуть</i> (kríknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spin on, to tie on, to attach by spinning or tying</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to screw in, to tighten by screwing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn down (a lantern, lamp, etc.) (to reduce the power of by turning a screw or lever)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shorten (a wick) by twisting</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spin on, to tie on, to attach by spinning or tying</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to screw in, to tighten by screwing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn down (a lantern, lamp, etc.) (to reduce the power of by turning a screw or lever)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shorten (a wick) by twisting</li></ol><p><i>прикрути́ть</i> (prikrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to screen, to close / shut softly</li><li>to protect, to shelter, to cover, to shield</li><li>to cover up, to conceal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to liquidate, to close down</li></ol><p><i>прикры́ть</i> (prikrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover <i>(oneself)</i> <i>(with)</i></li><li>to use as a cover, to take refuge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to close down</li></ol><p><i>прикрыва́ть</i> (prikryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, escort</li><li>screen, cloak, covering, disguise, false front</li></ol><p><i>прикры́ть</i> (prikrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, to screen, to close / shut softly</li><li>to protect, to shelter, to cover, to shield</li><li>to cover up, to conceal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to liquidate, to close down</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover <i>(oneself)</i> <i>(with)</i></li><li>to use as a cover, to take refuge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to close down</li></ol><p><i>прикры́ть</i> (prikrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) widow (cards played face down in some card games)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy <i>(some more)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigarette lighter (of an automobile)</li><li>jumper cable</li></ol><p>From <i>прику́ривать</i> (prikúrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light a cigarette</li><li>to get a light from someone's cigarette</li><li>to jumpstart an automobile (with jumper cables)</li></ol><p><i>прикури́ть</i> (prikurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light a cigarette</li><li>to get a light from someone's cigarette</li><li>to jumpstart an automobile (with jumper cables)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li></ol><p><i>прикуси́ть</i> (prikusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>прилага́тельное</i> (prilagátelʹnoje, “adjective”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter, desk, shopboard, stall, stand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjective</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>и́мя прилага́тельное</i> (ímja prilagátelʹnoje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjective</li><li>(rare,&#32;proscribed) adjectival</li></ol><p>From <i>прилага́ть</i> (prilagátʹ, “to append, to adject”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective suffix), a calque of Latin <i>adjectīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply, to use</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack, to smite, to whack</li><li>to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be attached, to be supplemented</li></ol><p><i>прилага́ть</i> (prilagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attach, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to fit (clothing)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt oneself, to accommodate oneself</li></ol><p><i>прила́дить</i> (priláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attach, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to fit (clothing)</li></ol><p><i>прила́дить</i> (priláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt oneself, to accommodate oneself</li></ol><p><i>прила́живать</i> (priláživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caress (touch, kiss or stroke lovingly)</li><li>to fondle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to be nice to someone, to show a friendly disposition</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ласка́ть</i> (laskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjoin, to border</li><li>to fit snugly on, to fit closely</li><li>to cling to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjacent, adjoining (lying next to, close, or contiguous&#59; neighboring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diligence, industry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to be diligent</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to be inclined to</li><li>alternative form of <i>прилега́ть</i> (prilegátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) adjoining, contiguous</li><li>adjacent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently (in a diligent manner)</li></ol><p><i>приле́жный</i> (priléžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diligence, industry</li></ol><p><i>приле́жный</i> (priléžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diligent, industrious, assiduous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прилежьнъ</i> (priležĭnŭ). By surface analysis, (<i>archaic, literrary</i>) <i>прилежа́ть</i> (priležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stick, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a nickname’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stick [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прилепи́ть</i> (prilepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stick, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a nickname’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прилепи́ть</i> (prilepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stick [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прилепля́ть</i> (prilepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to land; to touch down</li><li>to descend to soil (flying).</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to arrive swiftly, to abruptly (sometimes unexpectedly)</li></ol><p><i>прилете́ть</i> (priletétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly (here), to arrive (by flying)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down (<i>for a short period</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tide</li><li>high tide</li><li>flood, flow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rush</li><li>congestion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) (f) remora, suckerfish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>m</i> or <i>f</i>) bore, obsessive person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere, to cleave</li></ol><p><i>прили́пнуть</i> (prilípnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere, to cleave</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ли́пнуть</i> (lípnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stipule</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to befit, to behove</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decency, propriety, decorum, convenances</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decently, properly</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) quite, rather</li></ol><p><i>прили́чный</i> (prilíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decent, proper, respectable</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) suitable, in accordance [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) quite significant, rather large</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;priličьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>прили́чие</i> (prilíčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendix, supplement (text added to the end of a book or an article)</li><li>apposition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) appositive</li><li>application (<i>general sense</i>)</li><li>(computing,&#32;calque) application, app</li></ol><p><i>приложи́ть</i> (priložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (next to)</li><li>to enclose, to join, to attach</li><li>to apply, to use</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack, to whack</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put oneself <i>(to)</i>, to press oneself <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kiss</li></ol><p><i>приложи́ть</i> (priložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;computing) application, app</li></ol><p><i>прилож(е́ние)</i> (prilož(énije)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon landing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to land on the surface of the Moon</li></ol><p><i>прилуни́ть</i> (prilunítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cling <i>(to)</i>, to stick <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>льнуть</i> (lʹnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival by air (act of arriving or something that has arrived, by airplane)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;euphemistic) airstrike, hit (<i>by a single missile, bomb, drone, or shell</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tonic, unison interval</li><li>(music,&#32;ballet) principal female soloist</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Italian <i>prima</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>opera</i>) prima donna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>prima donna</i> (“first lady”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) live-in son-in-law (man who marries and lives with his wife's family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lure, to entice, to allure, to decoy</li></ol><p><i>примани́ть</i> (primanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lure, to entice, to allure, to decoy</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мани́ть</i> (manítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bait, lure (anything which allures)</li></ol><p><i>примани́ть</i> (primanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bait, decoy</li></ol><p><i>прима́нка</i> (primánka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primate</li><li>primacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten a little</li><li>to apply lotion (or other wet substance) to</li></ol><p><i>примочи́ть</i> (primočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) to pave with a second layer</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to place/perch in a hard-to-reach spot</li></ol><p><i>примости́ть</i> (primostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become commonplace by frequent usage</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мелька́ть</i> (melʹkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>application, use, usage (the manner or the amount of use)</li></ol><p><i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicability, suitability</li></ol><p><i>примени́мый</i> (primenímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>applicable, suitable</li></ol><p><i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with reference, as applied [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply, to use, to employ</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt oneself, to conform, to adjust</li></ol><p><i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply, to use, to employ</li></ol><p><i>примени́ть</i> (primenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adapt oneself, to conform, to adjust</li></ol><p><i>применя́ть</i> (primenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>example, instance</li><li>sample math problem, math exercise, math task (<i>in a math book</i>)</li><li>praxis</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>приме́рить</i> (priméritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>примеря́ть</i> (primerjátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>приме́рить</i> (priméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>примеря́ться</i> (primerjátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>приме́ривать</i> (primérivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try on (clothes)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try (<i>to do something</i>)</li><li>to adapt, to adjust [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приме́рить</i> (priméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trying on</li><li>fitting (<i>of clothes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>приме́рить</i> (priméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>about, approximately</li><li>exemplarily</li></ol><p><i>приме́рный</i> (primérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>approximate, rough</li><li>exemplary; (<i>relational</i>) model</li></ol><p><i>приме́р</i> (primér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fitting room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>приме́рить</i> (priméritʹ)</li><li>to try on (clothes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try (<i>to do something</i>)</li><li>to adapt, to adjust [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>приме́риться</i> (priméritʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>примеря́ть</i> (primerjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>приме́рять</i> (primérjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admixture, tinge, dash</li><li>touch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omen, sign, token</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to notice, to take note</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conspicuous, prominent, noticeable</li><li>perceptible, visible</li></ol><p>From <i>приме́т-</i> (primét-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or <i>приме́та</i> (priméta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>note, notice, annotation, comment (marginal comment or explanation)</li></ol><p><i>примеча́ть</i> (primečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>notably</li></ol><p><i>примеча́тельный</i> (primečátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>notable, noteworthy, remarkable</li></ol><p><i>примеча́ть</i> (primečátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to notice, to take note</li></ol><p><i>приме́тить</i> (primétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix in</li><li>to add by kneading</li></ol><p><i>перемеша́ть</i> (peremešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>примеси́ть</i> (primesítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconciliation (the reestablishment of friendly relations&#59; conciliation or rapprochement)</li></ol><p><i>примири́ть</i> (primirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a conciliatory manner</li></ol><p><i>примири́тельный</i> (primirítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conciliatory, reconciliatory</li><li>pacifying</li><li>arbitration</li></ol><p><i>примири́ть</i> (primirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li><li>to conciliate</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reconciled <i>(with someone)</i>, to make up <i>(with someone)</i></li><li>to reconcile oneself</li></ol><p><i>примири́ть</i> (primirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconcile</li><li>to conciliate</li></ol><p><i>примири́ть</i> (primirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reconciled <i>(with someone)</i>, to make up <i>(with someone)</i></li><li>to reconcile oneself</li></ol><p><i>примиря́ть</i> (primirjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) primitive object; anything simple or undeveloped</li><li>(<i>art</i>) primitive work of art, artwork from a primitive culture</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) primitive (basic graphical element)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>primitif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primitiveness</li></ol><p><i>примити́вный</i> (primitívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>primitive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>примири́тельная ка́мера</i> (primirítelʹnaja kámera): industrial arbitration board</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to affix, to join together, to close</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to join ranks, to side with someone; to stand next to</li><li>(transitive,&#32;dated) to include amongst</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-мкнуть</i> (-mknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become silent</li></ol><p><i>примо́лк(нуть)</i> (primólk(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become silent</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лкнуть</i> (mólknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coastal, maritime, seaside, littoral</li></ol><p><i>примо́рье</i> (primórʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaside, seaboard</li></ol><p><i>при</i> (pri, “near”) + <i>мо́ре</i> (móre, “sea”) + <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Приморье)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>informal name given to the Russian Primorsky Krai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) to pave with a second layer</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to place/perch in a hard-to-reach spot</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мости́ть</i> (mostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find room [a place] for oneself, to perch oneself</li></ol><p><i>примости́ть</i> (primostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten a little</li><li>to apply lotion (or other wet substance) to</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moistening, soaking</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) medicinal lotion</li><li>(figurative,&#32;slang) gadget</li></ol><p><i>примочи́ть</i> (primočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primrose</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>Primula</i> (named by botanist Carl von Linnaeus), from Latin <i>prīmula</i>. Compare Polish <i>prymula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Primus stove, Primus</li></ol><p>From the trademark 'Primus'.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come tearing along, to be in no minute</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>мча́ться</i> (mčátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>junction</li></ol><p><i>примыка́ть</i> (primykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, to side <i>(with)</i></li><li>to adjoin, to border, to abut</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to adjoin</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjacent, adjoining (lying next to, close, or contiguous&#59; neighboring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crumpled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>приме́р</i> (primér).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to belong (to)</li><li>to pertain</li></ol><p><i>при</i> (pri) +&lrm; <i>надлежа́ть</i> (nadležátʹ, “to do as required”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belonging, membership, affiliation</li><li>accessory, article, implement</li><li>characteristic, attribute, property</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) possession</li></ol><p>From <i>принадлежа́ть</i> (prinadležátʹ, “to belong”) +&lrm; <i>-ность</i> (-nostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress smartly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress smartly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing, fetching (the act by which something is brought)</li><li>bearing, yielding (the act by which something is produced)</li><li>offering (the act by which something is offered)</li></ol><p><i>принести́</i> (prinestí) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (by foot), to fetch (transitive) to transport toward someone/somewhere)</li><li>to yield, to bear</li><li>to offer <i>(apologies, thanks, etc.)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí). Compare Polish <i>przynieść</i> and Ukrainian <i>принести́</i> (prynestý)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate, to degrade</li><li>to belittle, to downplay</li></ol><p><i>прини́зить</i> (prinízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humiliated, submissive (<i>of a look, position, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate, to degrade</li><li>to belittle, to downplay</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ни́зить</i> (-nízitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself <i>(against)</i>, to press oneself close <i>(to)</i>, to nestle <i>(against, to)</i>, to nestle close <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>прини́кнуть</i> (priníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press oneself <i>(against)</i>, to press oneself close <i>(to)</i>, to nestle <i>(against, to)</i>, to nestle close <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><li>to admit, to accept</li><li>to receive</li><li>to assume</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to take at face value</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start doing something</li><li>to take root</li></ol><p><i>принима́ть</i> (prinimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get used to, to get accustomed to</li><li>to learn how to do something, to get the hang of it</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>норови́ть</i> (norovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring (by foot), to fetch</li><li>to yield, to bear</li><li>to offer (<i>apologies, thanks, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift</li></ol><p><i>приноси́ть</i> (prinosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) printer (device that prints images and text on paper)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forced labor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced, coercive</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) forced (by mechanical means)</li></ol><p><i>прину́дить</i> (prinúditʹ, “to force, to compel”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to compel, to coerce</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prinuditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to compel, to coerce</li></ol><p><i>прину́дить</i> (prinúditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compulsion, coercion, constraint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>принужде́ние</i> (prinuždénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forced, constrained, obliged (<i>to do something; of a person</i>)</li><li>forced (<i>laugh, manner, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince, crown prince (a son or other male family member of a king or a non-Russian emperor)</li><li>(rare,&#32;historical) prince (the holder of a princely title, especially in the Kingdom of France)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Prinz</i>, from French <i>prince</i>, from Latin <i>prī̆nceps</i>. Further analyzable as <i>prīmus</i> + <i>-ceps</i> (“catcher”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>самоде́ржец</i> (samodéržec) (<~+ <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>principle (fundamental assumption)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>prīncipium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in principle, in essence</li><li>fundamentally</li><li>on principle, on purpose</li></ol><p><i>принципиа́льный</i> (principiálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adherence to one's principles</li></ol><p><i>принципиа́льный</i> (principiálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of) principle</li><li>fundamental</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniff (<i>so as to recognize a smell</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become accustomed to the smell [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniff (<i>so as to recognize a smell</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become accustomed to the smell [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приню́хаться</i> (prinjúxatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all things considered (all in all)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptance, adoption (an agreeing to terms or proposals)</li><li>reception (act of receiving something)</li></ol><p><i>приня́ть</i> (prinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assumption (the act of assuming, or taking to or upon one's self)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accepted</li><li>adopted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><li>to admit, to accept</li><li>to receive</li><li>to assume</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to arrest, to detain, to take in</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ). Compare Czech <i>přijmout</i> and Polish <i>przyjąć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to take at face value</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set about, to start doing [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘task’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to take in hand, to deal with [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘person’</small>]</li><li>to take root</li></ol><p><i>приня́ть</i> (prinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hug a little, to embrace somewhat</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>обнима́ть</i> (obnimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hug a little, to embrace somewhat</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>обня́ть</i> (obnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cheer up somewhat</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ободри́ть</i> (obodrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cheer up somewhat</li></ol><p><i>приободри́ть</i> (priobodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cheer up somewhat</li></ol><p><i>приободри́ть</i> (priobodrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cheer up somewhat</li></ol><p><i>приободря́ть</i> (priobodrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire, to gain</li><li>to purchase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>приобрѣсти</i> (priobrěsti). By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>обрести́</i> (obrestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquisitor</li></ol><p><i>приобрета́ть</i> (priobretátʹ, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er, -or”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquire, to gain</li><li>to purchase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>приобрѣтати</i> (priobrětati). By surface analysis, <i>приобрет-</i> (priobret-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquisition, gain, acquiring (act of gaining)</li></ol><p>From <i>приобрет-</i> (priobret-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije) or borrowed directly from Old Church Slavonic <i>приобрѣтениѥ</i> (priobrětenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accustom, to familiarize <i>(with)</i></li><li>to join <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to administer the sacrament <i>(to)</i>, to communicate</li></ol><p><i>приобщи́ть</i> (priobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join</li><li>to become familiar <i>(with)</i>, to familiarize oneself <i>(with)</i>, to learn to use, to enjoy</li><li>to communicate</li></ol><p><i>приобща́ть</i> (priobščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familiarization, communion, attaching, inclusion, introduction</li></ol><p><i>приобщи́ть</i> (priobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accustom, to familiarize <i>(with)</i></li><li>to join <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to administer the sacrament <i>(to)</i>, to communicate</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-общи́ть</i> (-obščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join</li><li>to become familiar <i>(with)</i>, to familiarize oneself <i>(with)</i>, to learn to use, to enjoy</li><li>to communicate</li></ol><p><i>приобщи́ть</i> (priobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priority</li><li>right of way (a right to proceed first in traffic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Priorität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) priority</li></ol><p><i>приорите́тный</i> (prioritétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) priority</li><li>high-priority, paramount, most important</li></ol><p><i>приорите́т</i> (prioritét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to overshadow slightly</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to cover with a shadow</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>осени́ть</i> (osenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspend, to stop, to call a halt <i>(to)</i>, to check</li></ol><p><i>приостанови́ть</i> (priostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(temporarily)</i> to halt, to come to a halt, to pause</li></ol><p><i>приостана́вливать</i> (priostanávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspend, to stop, to call a halt <i>(to)</i>, to check</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>останови́ть</i> (ostanovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(temporarily)</i> to halt, to come to a halt, to pause</li></ol><p><i>приостанови́ть</i> (priostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspension, interruption, delay, letup (temporary or conditional delay, interruption or discontinuation)</li></ol><p><i>приостанови́ть</i> (priostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspension (temporary or conditional delay, interruption or discontinuation)</li></ol><p><i>приостанови́ть</i> (priostanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open slightly, to set ajar</li></ol><p><i>приоткры́ть</i> (priotkrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open a bit, to become ajar (of a door)</li></ol><p><i>приоткрыва́ть</i> (priotkryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ajar (slightly turned or opened)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open slightly, to set ajar</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>откры́ть</i> (otkrýtʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open a bit, to become ajar (of a door)</li></ol><p><i>приоткры́ть</i> (priotkrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down <i>(to)</i>, to press oneself <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to limp <i>(slightly)</i></li></ol><p><i>припа́д-</i> (pripád-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seizure</li><li>fit, paroxysm</li><li>attack (medicine&#58; sudden onset of a disease)</li><li>bout, turn</li><li>access</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) chance, luck, accident</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) favor</li></ol><p>From <i>припада́ть</i> (pripadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Sense&nbsp;6 and sense&nbsp;7 are semantic loans from Polish <i>przypadek</i>, which eventually fell out of use due to higher use of the other meanings. Compare Czech <i>případek</i>, Kashubian <i>przëpôdk</i>, Polish <i>przypadek</i>, and Silesian <i>przipŏdek</i>. Further compare Hungarian <i>eset</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epileptic, seizure (related to seizures)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) epileptic (of a person; suffering or prone to seizures)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;as a noun) psycho, hysterical, nutty</li><li>(with <i>как</i> (kak)) constantly, incessantly</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) spontaneous (of a person)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) fortunate, lucky (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>припа́док</i> (pripádok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poultice, fomentation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to park</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>паркова́ть</i> (parkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to park (<i>intransitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>припаркова́ть</i> (priparkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to park</li></ol><p><i>припаркова́ть</i> (priparkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) supply, provision</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) provisions, victuals</li></ol><p>Deverbal noun from <i>припасти́</i> (pripastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay in store, to lay up, to prepare, to provide</li></ol><p><i>припасти́</i> (pripastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay in store, to lay up, to prepare, to provide</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пасти́</i> (pastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down <i>(to)</i>, to press oneself <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplies</li></ol><p>Deverbal noun from <i>припасти́</i> (pripastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) refrain, chorus, burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing along</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) happily; in clover; as a prince</li></ol><p>Literally, “singing along”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whim, whimsy, freak, caprice, fancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roast with fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to harass, to offend</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to be very hot (<i>of the sun, fire, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>припек-</i> (pripek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure (a door, etc.), to close tightly so it can't be opened</li><li>to press firmly [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘against someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to be necessary, to be required [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have to</i> or <i>must</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) to carry, to take</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) to come</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) to come (<i>uninvited or inappropriately</i>)</li></ol><p><i>припере́ть</i> (priperétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to roast with fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to harass, to offend</li><li>(rare,&#32;figurative,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to be very hot (<i>of the sun, fire, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure (a door, etc.), to close tightly so it can't be opened</li><li>to press firmly [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘against someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to be necessary, to be required [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have to</i> or <i>must</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>(rude,&#32;low colloquial) to carry, to take</li><li>(rude,&#32;low colloquial) to come</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;low colloquial) to come (<i>uninvited or inappropriately</i>)</li></ol><p><i>припира́ть</i> (pripirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add (<i>by writing</i>)</li><li>to attach, to register</li><li>to arrogate, to ascribe, to attribute, to impute</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a member of, to register for [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘an account, a service, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приписа́ть</i> (pripisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition, postscript</li><li>registration</li><li>doctoring, distortions, doctored records</li></ol><p><i>приписа́ть</i> (pripisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attribution, ascription (act of ascribing a quality to someone or something)</li></ol><p><i>припи́сывать</i> (pripísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add (<i>by writing</i>)</li><li>to attach, to register</li><li>to arrogate, to ascribe, to attribute, to impute</li></ol><p><i>приписа́ть</i> (pripisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a member of, to register for [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘an account, a service, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>припи́сывать</i> (pripísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plait in, to weave in, to weave together</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to bring up, to drag in (<i>e.g. an irrelevant subject</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to invent, to come up with (<i>a fanciful detail for one's story</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litter, offspring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim up, to come swimming, to sail up</li></ol><p><i>приплы́ть</i> (priplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim up, to come swimming, to sail up</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance up and down</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пляса́ть</i> (pljasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to lift (a little)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>поднима́ть</i> (podnimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise oneself, to lift oneself (a little)</li><li>to sit up</li></ol><p><i>приподнима́ть</i> (pripodnimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elevated (<i>language, style</i>)</li><li>exultant (<i>mood</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, to lift (a little)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>подня́ть</i> (podnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise oneself, to lift oneself (a little)</li><li>to sit up</li></ol><p><i>приподня́ть</i> (pripodnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be a bit late</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>по́здно</i> (pózdno, “late”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get by crawling [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приползти́</i> (pripolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get by crawling [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to recall</li><li>to get even, to revenge</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ, “to remember, to recall (dated)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remember, to recall</li><li>to get even, to revenge</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ, “to remember; (<i>in the negative</i>) to lose control”). Compare Polish <i>przypomnieć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seasoning, relish, condiment, flavouring/flavoring (something used to enhance flavor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season, to flavor</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to spice up (a story or speech with jokes, anecdotes, etc.)</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) to adjust the printing pressure to obtain the correct impression</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season, to flavor</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to spice up (a story or speech with jokes, anecdotes, etc.)</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) to adjust the printing pressure to obtain the correct impression</li></ol><p><i>припра́вить</i> (priprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to secrete</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lay up, to store up, to put by</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to secrete</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lay up, to store up, to put by</li></ol><p><i>припря́тать</i> (priprjátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be slightly swollen</li></ol><p><i>припу́х(нуть)</i> (pripúx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be slightly swollen</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extra earning, extra job, perk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equate, to put/place on the same footing, to give the same status [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to/as someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>to level</li></ol><p><i>приравня́ть</i> (priravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>прировня́ть</i> (prirovnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to equate to, to have the same status as</li><li>to be like</li></ol><p><i>прира́внивать</i> (prirávnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equate, to put/place on the same footing, to give the same status [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to/as someone/something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>равня́ть</i> (ravnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to equate to, to have the same status as</li><li>to be like</li></ol><p><i>приравня́ть</i> (priravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adhere to , to grow to</li><li>to grow, to increase, to accrue</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to increase (something), to cause to grow</li><li>to cultivate, to nurture (emotions, knowledge, etc.)</li><li>to unite, to join (something to something else)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, growth</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) increment</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) augment</li></ol><p><i>прирасти́ть</i> (prirastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to increase (something), to cause to grow</li><li>to cultivate, to nurture (emotions, knowledge, etc.)</li><li>to unite, to join (something to something else)</li></ol><p><i>прирасти́ть</i> (prirastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to level</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ровня́ть</i> (rovnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nature</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;priroda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nature; natural</li><li>born, innate, inherent</li><li>native, indigenous</li></ol><p><i>приро́да</i> (priróda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural gas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) conservation, nature protection</li></ol><p><i>приро́да</i> (priróda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>охра́на</i> (oxrána) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>environmental management, wildlife management, use of natural resources</li></ol><p><i>приро́да</i> (priróda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>по́льзование</i> (pólʹzovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innate, inborn</li><li>born (<i>leader, poet, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, increment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame</li></ol><p><i>приручи́ть</i> (priručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame, to become tame</li></ol><p><i>прируча́ть</i> (priručátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taming</li></ol><p><i>приручи́ть</i> (priručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-ручи́ть</i> (-ručítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame, to become tame</li></ol><p><i>приручи́ть</i> (priručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additive, amendment (that which is added)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down</li></ol><p><i>приса́живать</i> (prisáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); pairing with <i>присе́сть</i>&nbsp;pf (priséstʹ) corresponds to pairing between <i>сади́ться</i>&nbsp;impf (sadítʹsja) and <i>сесть</i>&nbsp;pf (sestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appropriate; to claim or use something as property especially when it belongs to someone else</li><li>to give, to confer <i>(on)</i>, to award <i>(to)</i></li><li>to assign</li></ol><p><i>присво́ить</i> (prisvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give a whistle</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сви́стнуть</i> (svístnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle</li><li>to sibilate</li></ol><p><i>присви́стнуть</i> (prisvístnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appropriation, assumption, grab, acquisition</li><li>awarding, conferment</li></ol><p><i>присво́ить</i> (prisvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appropriate; to claim or use something as property especially when it belongs to someone else</li><li>to give, to confer <i>(on)</i>, to award <i>(to)</i></li><li>to assign</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-сво́ить</i> (-svóitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squat</li></ol><p>Analyzable as deverbal from <i>присе́сть</i> (priséstʹ) (compare imperfective equivalent <i>приседа́ть</i> (prisedátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crouching, squatting</li><li>(<i>exercise</i>) squat</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) curtsey</li></ol><p><i>приседа́ть</i> (prisedátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squat</li><li>to cower</li><li>to curtsy</li></ol><p><i>присе́д-</i> (priséd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle (somewhere else)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle (somewhere else)</li></ol><p><i>присели́ть</i> (priselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down</li><li>to squat</li><li>to cower</li><li>to curtsy</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, in, from, characteristic of, or pertaining to Prysyvashshia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(story-teller's) prelude, introduction</li><li>(interesting or funny) saying, expression; proverb, adage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come riding</li><li>to come jumping</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quickly arrive (to a place)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lamentable, deplorable, grievous, regrettable, sorrowful, tragic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send (here, to this place)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean <i>(against)</i>, to rest <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-слони́ть</i> (-slonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean against</li></ol><p><i>прислони́ть</i> (prislonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean <i>(against)</i>, to rest <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>прислони́ть</i> (prislonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean against</li></ol><p><i>прислоня́ть</i> (prislonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female servant, maidservant</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) servants, domestics, household servants m or f</li><li>(<i>military, collective</i>) crew, gunners, gun detachment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prisluga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to provide services to (<i>out of courtesy, politeness, or trying to curry favor</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to perform the duties of a servant</li></ol><p><i>прислужи́ть</i> (prislužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) to provide services to (<i>out of courtesy, politeness, or trying to curry favor</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(dated,&#32;rare) to perform the duties of a servant</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) servant</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fawner, menial, servitor, underling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to (carefully), to lend an ear</li><li>to listen to, to heed, to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to (carefully), to lend an ear</li><li>to listen to, to heed, to pay attention to</li></ol><p><i>прислу́шаться</i> (prislúšatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep an eye on (to watch and pay attention to) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to find, to look for, to seek</li></ol><p><i>присмотре́ть</i> (prismotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look closely/attentively <i>(at)</i>, to watch</li><li>to get accustomed/used <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>присма́тривать</i> (prismátrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, tending, looking after, superintendence, supervision</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep an eye on (to watch and pay attention to) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to find, to look for, to seek</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look closely/attentively <i>(at)</i>, to watch</li><li>to get accustomed/used <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>присмотре́ть</i> (prismotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be dreamed [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>dream</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for one to dream [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>что</b> (što) <small>‘that...’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>dream</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сни́ться</i> (snítʹsja). Compare Czech <i>přisnít</i>, Kashubian <i>przësnic</i>, Silesian <i>prziśnić</i>, and Polish <i>przyśnić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) always, ever</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>присьно</i> (prisĭno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>associates</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrue</li></ol><p><i>присовокупля́ть</i> (prisovokupljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joining, connection, connecting</li><li>annexation</li><li>accession, joining (a coming to)</li></ol><p><i>присоедини́ть</i> (prisojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conjunctive</li></ol><p><i>присоедини́ть</i> (prisojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, to link, to add, to connect, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to annex (territory)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a group, organization, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to accede [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to a treaty, protocol, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to associate oneself [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘with a group, organization, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to endorse, to go along with [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a proposal, suggestion, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>присоедини́ть</i> (prisojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join, to link, to add, to connect, to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to annex (territory)</li></ol><p><i>присоедини́ть</i> (prisojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a group, organization, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to accede [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to a treaty, protocol, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to associate oneself [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘with a group, organization, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to endorse, to go along with [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘a proposal, suggestion, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>присоединя́ть</i> (prisojedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sucker (organ or device)</li><li>suction cup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplice, stooge, henchman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to urgently need to do</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>спи́ца</i> (spíca, “spoke, knitting needle”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ); sense development by way of "to prick".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptation</li></ol><p><i>приспоса́бливать</i> (prisposáblivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit, to adapt, to adjust, to accommodate</li><li>to convert</li></ol><p><i>приспосо́бить</i> (prisposóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust oneself, to adapt oneself, to accommodate oneself</li></ol><p><i>приспоса́бливать</i> (prisposáblivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit, to adapt, to adjust, to accommodate</li><li>to convert</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>спо́соб</i> (spósob) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adjust oneself, to adapt oneself, to accommodate oneself</li></ol><p><i>приспосо́бить</i> (prisposóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) opportunist, timeserver, temporizer</li></ol><p><i>приспособле́ние</i> (prisposoblénije) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptation, accommodation, adjustment</li><li>device, contrivance, contraption, appliance, gadget</li></ol><p><i>приспосо́бить</i> (prisposóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adaptability</li></ol><p><i>приспособля́емый</i> (prisposobljájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>приспоса́бливать</i> (prisposáblivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>приспосо́бить</i> (prisposóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>приспоса́бливаться</i> (prisposáblivatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>приспособля́ть</i> (prisposobljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bailiff (officer of the court)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) the local police chief or constable in pre-Revolutionary Russia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) bailiff, in Moscow in historical times</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) in animals, the shape of the connection between the head or neck and the breast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bailiff</li></ol><p><i>при́став</i> (prístav) +&lrm; <i>исполни́тель</i> (ispolnítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to pester</li><li>to hit on, to accost</li><li>to harass</li></ol><p><i>приста́ть</i> (pristátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against, to)</i>, to lean <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appoint to look <i>(after)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistic morphology,&#32;lexicography,&#32;grammar) prefix</li><li>video game console</li></ol><p><i>приста́вить</i> (pristávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against, to)</i>, to lean <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appoint to look <i>(after)</i></li></ol><p><i>приста́вить</i> (pristávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) prefixal</li></ol><p><i>приста́вка</i> (pristávka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fixedly, intently</li></ol><p><i>при́стальный</i> (prístalʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intent (look)</li><li>rapt, steadfast (attention)</li></ol><p><i>приста́ть</i> (pristátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-альный</i> (-alʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge, haven, shelter, asylum</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pristanišče</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wharf, pier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick, to adhere</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to pester</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buckle up, to zip up, to button up, to fasten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seemly, proper, not indecent or obscene</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a building, room, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto (another) building’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put, to place, to set (something somewhere)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to place into formation</li></ol><p><i>пристро́ить</i> (pristróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get a place, to be settled, to be fixed up</li><li>to join up <i>(with)</i>, to form up <i>(with)</i>, to join formation <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пристра́ивать</i> (pristráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addiction, predilection (for), propensity</li><li>passion (for), weakness (for)</li><li>bias (towards), partiality (to/for)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>страсть</i> (strastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make passionate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to addict, to cause to be addicted [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пристра́стие</i> (pristrástije) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to develop passion (for), to take (to), to conceive a liking (for) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to get addicted [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пристрасти́ть</i> (pristrastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biased, partial</li><li>addicted</li></ol><p><i>пристра́стие</i> (pristrástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to threaten, to intimidate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make passionate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘about something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to addict, to cause to be addicted [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>страща́ть</i> (straščátʹ)</p><p><i>пристрасти́ть</i> (pristrastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to develop passion (for), to take (to), to conceive a liking (for) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to get addicted [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пристраща́ть</i> (pristraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to threaten, to intimidate</li></ol><p><i>пристраща́ть</i> (pristraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot <i>(down)</i></li><li>to calibrate, to align (a firearm)</li><li>to fire test shots at (a target, landmark, etc.) to adjust a gun's sights</li></ol><p><i>пристрели́ть</i> (pristrelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>пристреля́ть</i> (pristreljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot <i>(down)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-стрели́ть</i> (-strelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to zero, to calibrate, to align (a firearm)</li><li>to fire test shots at (a target, landmark, etc.) to adjust a gun's sights</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a building, room, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto (another) building’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put, to place, to set (something somewhere)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to place into formation</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to help someone get a job in a certain institution or group</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get a place, to be settled, to be fixed up</li><li>to join up <i>(with)</i>, to form up <i>(with)</i>, to join formation <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пристро́ить</i> (pristróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annex (addition or extension to a building)</li><li>ell (addition or extension to a building)</li><li>outbuilding</li><li>enlargement</li></ol><p><i>пристро́ить</i> (pristróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assault, onset, onslaught, storm</li><li>fit, attack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) access</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, to proceed, to start</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set about, to start, to begin</li><li>to proceed</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foothold</li><li>step (of a stairway)</li><li>stepping ladder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) foothold</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ступе́нь</i> (stupénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame, put to shame, make ashamed of <i>something</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>стыди́ть</i> (stydítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ashamed, abashed (<i>experiencing shame; of a person</i>)</li><li>ashamed, abashed, hangdog (<i>expressing shame; of a look, tone, face, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buckle up, to zip up, to button up, to fasten</li></ol><p><i>пристегну́ть</i> (pristegnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sentence <i>(to)</i>, to condemn <i>(to)</i></li><li>to award, to adjudge, to confer <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sentence <i>(to)</i>, to condemn <i>(to)</i></li><li>to award, to adjudge, to confer <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>присуди́ть</i> (prisudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>award (decision of arbitrators)</li><li>conferment</li><li>adjudgment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(stilted,&#32;dated) public; office, related to hours of attendance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presence, attendance (the fact or condition of being present)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) the duties of government service</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a government office or bureau</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) one of the renderings of <i>dasein</i></li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>суть</i> (sutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije); compare Serbo-Croatian <i>присутност</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li></ol><p><i>прису́тствие</i> (prisútstvije) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present (in the immediate vicinity)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inherent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>происходит</i> (proisxodit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add on</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add on</li></ol><p><i>присчита́ть</i> (prisčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send (here, to this place)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to pour some more</li><li>to sprinkle <i>(with)</i>, to powder '<i>(with)</i>, to dust <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>присы́пать</i> (prisýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath (solemn pledge), oath of allegiance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prisęga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear, to take one's oath, to swear an oath [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘about something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>сяга́ть</i> (sjagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear, to take one's oath, to swear an oath [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘about something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>присяга́ть</i> (prisjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oath</li><li>oath-bound, sworn under an oath</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juror</li></ol><p><i>прися́га</i> (prisjága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to conceal</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lurk, to hide, to conceal oneself, to keep quiet</li></ol><p><i>притаи́ть</i> (pritaítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance, to make dancing movements</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move the feet restlessly</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>танцева́ть</i> (tancevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring, to drag, to haul</li></ol><p><i>притаск-</i> (pritask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring, to drag, to haul</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vestibule (<i>in a church</i>)</li><li>a method and application by which a door or window is attached to a building</li><li>a leavened mass from which bread is going to be made</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, to shut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shut, to close</li><li>to pretend, to feign, to dissemble, to simulate, to sham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mock, feigned</li><li>sham, pretended, assumed</li><li>fictive</li><li>simulated</li><li>affected</li><li>artificial (false, misleading)</li><li>hypocritical</li><li>shoddy</li><li>seeming</li><li>bastard</li><li>(relational,&#32;architecture) rabbet, window rabbet</li></ol><p><i>притвор(и́ться)</i> (pritvor(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretense, dissimulation, sham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretender, feigner, simulant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close, to shut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, to close</li><li>to pretend, to feign, to dissemble, to simulate, to sham</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pretender, feigner</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) spider beetle (beetle of the subfamily Ptininae)</li></ol><p>From <i>притворя́ться</i> (pritvorjátʹsja, “to pretend”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow.</li><li>To be carried somewhere by the air.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get somewhere.</li></ol><p>From <i>при</i> (pri) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppression</li></ol><p>From <i>притесни́ть</i> (pritesnítʹ, “to oppress”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to oppress</li></ol><p>From <i>притесни́ть</i> (pritesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) point to which something is attached</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) highest point in the sky to which the sun rises</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sentry post</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow quiet, to hush</li><li>to abate (of wind, noise, pain, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>прити́хнуть</i> (pritíxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow quiet, to hush</li><li>to abate (of wind, noise, pain, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ти́хнуть</i> (tíxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pin, to fasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to place hastily or haphazardly, to stick (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lean [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘against someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to perch, to settle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crash (somewhere), to stay temporarily</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nestle, to be squeezed in (<i>somewhere awkward or cramped</i>)</li></ol><p><i>приткну́ть</i> (pritknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflow, afflux</li><li>tributary, confluent (<i>river</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>притолока</i> (pritoloka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) lintel</li></ol><p>Modification of earlier <i>при́толка</i> (prítolka, “lintel”) under the influence of <i>потоло́к</i> (potolók, “ceiling”), from <i>при́-</i> (prí-) +&lrm; <i>тол-</i> (tol-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in addition, furthermore, besides</li></ol><p><i>при</i> (pri) +&lrm; <i>том</i> (tom)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorderly house, den, nest (hideout)</li><li>haunt, hangout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply the brake, to slow down, to brake (slightly)</li></ol><p><i>притормози́ть</i> (pritormozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to apply the brake, to slow down, to brake (slightly)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>тормози́ть</i> (tormozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>saccharine, syrupy</li><li>sentimental, cloying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li></ol><p><i>притра́г-</i> (pritrág-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to touch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>тро́нуть</i> (trónutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set down haphazardly, to stick (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) + dialectal <i>тули́ть</i> (tulítʹ, “to hide”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuliti</i>. Also found are <i>вту́лка</i> (vtúlka, “plug”), dialectal <i>втули́ть</i> (vtulítʹ, “to drive (something) in”), dialectal <i>зату́лка</i> (zatúlka, “plug”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тули́ти</i> (tulýty, “to press, to put (<i>against</i>)”), <i>втули́ти</i> (vtulýty, “to put in”), Belarusian <i>тулі́ць</i> (tulícʹ, “to shelter”), Church Slavonic <i>затулити</i> (zatuliti, “to hide”), Bulgarian <i>ту́ля</i> (túlja), Serbo-Croatian <i>ту́лити</i> (“to extinguish”) (1sg. <i>ту̑лӣм</i>), Slovene <i>túliti</i> (“to squeeze”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>) (1sg. Slovene <i>túlim</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>)), Czech <i>toulit</i> (“to press, to squeeze”) (also <i>tulit</i>), Slovak <i>túliť sa</i> (“to press oneself against”), Polish <i>tulić</i> (“to soothe”), Upper Sorbian <i>tulić</i> (“to press, to bend”), Lower Sorbian <i>tuliś</i> (“to bend; to tame; to hide”). Further connections doubtful.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle, to take up a position (<i>somewhere secluded or uncomfortable</i>)</li></ol><p><i>притули́ть</i> (pritulítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set down haphazardly, to stick (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>притули́ть</i> (pritulítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to settle, to take up a position (<i>somewhere secluded or uncomfortable</i>)</li></ol><p><i>притуля́ть</i> (prituljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dull somewhat, to blunt somewhat</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to dull, to blunt (feelings, one's mind, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dull somewhat, to blunt somewhat</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to dull, to blunt (feelings, one's mind, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>притупи́ть</i> (pritupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parable, fable</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) an unexpected event, occurrence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>притъча</i> (pritŭča, “incident”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pritъča</i> (“incident, tale”). The meaning "tale" is likely a semantic loan from Old Church Slavonic <i>притъча</i> (pritŭča, “tale, parable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) talk of the town (a topic or person discussed by many people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>притъча въ ѩзꙑцѣхъ</i> (pritŭča vŭ języcěxŭ) "a fable/parable in/among the people", following its semantics and grammar. It's used several times in the Old Testament.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pin, to fasten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to place hastily or haphazardly, to stick (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lean [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘against someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to perch, to settle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crash (somewhere), to stay temporarily</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nestle, to be squeezed in (<i>somewhere awkward or cramped</i>)</li></ol><p><i>притыка́ть</i> (pritykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attractiveness</li></ol><p><i>притяга́тельный</i> (pritjagátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attractive, inviting, magnetic</li></ol><p><i>притяга́ть</i> (pritjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>привлека́тельный</i> (privlekátelʹnyj), Latin <i>attractīvus</i> (whence English <i>attractive</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) alternative form of <i>притя́гивать</i> (pritjágivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>притяг-</i> (pritjag-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to pull, to attract</li><li>to summon</li></ol><p><i>притяг-</i> (pritjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to take (something) far, to misapply, (intransitively) to reach (to bend the reality of something to make it applicable to a certain situation)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to pull by the ears”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) possessive pronoun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) possessive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possessive case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attraction (tendency to attract)</li><li>gravitation, gravity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretension, claim</li></ol><p><i>притяза́ть</i> (pritjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to pull, to attract</li><li>to summon</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to take (something) far, to misapply, (intransitively) to reach (to bend the reality of something to make it applicable to a certain situation)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to pull by the ears”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embellish, to decorate (in addition or in a small amount)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to embellish, to sugarcoat, to minimise, to gloss over</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>укра́сить</i> (ukrásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embellish, to decorate (in addition or in a small amount)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to embellish, to sugarcoat, to minimise, to gloss over (to omit or add details to make something appear better than it actually is)</li></ol><p><i>приукра́сить</i> (priukrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become despondent, to get depressed</li></ol><p><i>приуны́ть</i> (priunýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become despondent, to get depressed</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>уны́ть</i> (unýtʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to time <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>приуро́чить</i> (priuróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to time <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>уро́к</i> (urók) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) homestead, farmstead</li><li>located on a homestead or farmstead</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>уса́дьба</i> (usádʹba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to train <i>(to)</i>, to school <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>приучи́ть</i> (priučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to train, to teach, to inure, to accustom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;priučiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ironic) privatization</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>прихвати́ть</i> (prixvatítʹ, “to seize, to grab”) +&lrm; <i>приватиза́ция</i> (privatizácija, “privatization”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up, to seize up, to grab, to borrow, to grip</li><li>to fasten, to tie up</li><li>to nip, to damage, to touch (of frost)</li><li>to tack</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tack weld (a small joint used to position and secure the pieces being welded)</li><li>potholder, oven glove</li></ol><p><i>прихва́тывать, прихвати́ть</i> (prixvátyvatʹ, prixvatítʹ); <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch up, to seize up, to grab, to borrow, to grip</li><li>to fasten, to tie up</li><li>to nip, to damage, to touch (of frost)</li><li>to tack</li></ol><p><i>прихвати́ть</i> (prixvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) henchman; stooge; lackey; lickspittle; toady</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanger-on, sponger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitism (about people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a sip</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>хлебну́ть</i> (xlebnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam (a door)</li><li>to bang, to slap</li><li>to catch (one's finger in a door)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>хло́пнуть</i> (xlópnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slam (a door)</li><li>to bang, to slap</li><li>to catch (one's finger in a door)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to clap in rhythm</li></ol><p><i>прихло́п(нуть)</i> (prixlóp(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sip</li></ol><p><i>прихлебну́ть</i> (prixlebnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrival, coming; trip</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) receipt, credit, income</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) parish, congregation</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) onset of a drug, immediately preceding the buzz</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>приходи́ть</i> (prixodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come (to), to arrive (at) (place, conclusion, state of abandonment, anger, insanity)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-, “indicating action or motion towards a terminal point”) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to walk or go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather one's wits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be necessary, to be required [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>have to</i> or <i>must</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>to fall, to occur [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on a specific date’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be (<i>in a certain state</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> adverb <small>‘in a certain state’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>to have a certain time of it</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>to come from, to be constituted by (<i>of a quantity</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘a source’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>account for</i> with the object of <i>на</i> (na) as the subject)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be due [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘from someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘some amount’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be related [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to suit, to fit (<i>usually in specific expressions</i>) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘with respect to something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>приходи́ть</i> (prixodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parish, congregation</li></ol><p><i>прихо́д</i> (prixód) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parishioner, congregant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vestibule, hall, antechamber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smarten oneself up, to preen (to make oneself look pretty or handsome)</li></ol><p><i>прихора́шивать</i> (prixorášivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), ultimately from <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smarten oneself up, to preen (to make oneself look pretty or handsome)</li></ol><p><i>прихороши́ть</i> (prixorošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), ultimately from <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whimsical, capricious, fastidious (difficult to please)</li><li>fanciful, intricate</li></ol><p><i>при́хоть</i> (príxotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whim, caprice, whimsy, fancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limp, to hobble</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>хрома́ть</i> (xromátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight <i>(device used in aiming a firearm)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim, to take aim</li></ol><p><i>прице́литься</i> (pricélitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim, to take aim</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>це́лить</i> (célitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) sighting, aiming</li><li>well-aimed, precision, pinpoint</li></ol><p><i>прице́л</i> (pricél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trailer (vehicle towed behind another)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прицепи́ть</i> (pricepítʹ). Cognate with Polish <i>przyczepa</i>.</p><p>Compare Czech <i>přívěs</i> (akin to <i>приве́сить</i> (privésitʹ)), Slovene <i>priklopnik</i> (akin to <i>приклепа́ть</i> (priklepátʹ)), Macedonian <i>приколка</i> (prikolka), Serbo-Croatian <i>приколица</i>, Slovene <i>prikolica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to hitch, to hook, to couple [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a railroad car, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pin, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a brooch, piece of paper, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hook onto [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to cling to, to latch onto [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to pester [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to find fault [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to linger (<i>of a disease</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прицепи́ть</i> (pricepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>to hitch, to hook, to couple [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a railroad car, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pin, to fasten [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a brooch, piece of paper, etc.’</small> <i>and</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘onto someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прицепи́ть</i> (pricepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hook onto [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to cling to, to latch onto [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to pester [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to find fault [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to linger (<i>of a disease</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прицепля́ть</i> (pricepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>прича́стие</i> (pričástije, “participle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pier, mooring, berth, quay, dock, wharf, marina</li><li>Prichal (module of the International Space Station)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to moor, to land</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tie up (at), to moor (to)</li></ol><p><i>прича́лить</i> (pričálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to moor, to land</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tie up (at), to moor (to)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ча́лить</i> (čálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) participle</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) communion; Eucharist (ceremony, sacrament, or substances)</li></ol><p>From <i>часть</i> (častʹ, “part”). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>μετοχή</i> (metokhḗ) or Latin <i>participium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>present participle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>past participle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to give Communion to</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to connect (someone or something) to (something), to bring (someone) into communication with (something), to accustom, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>части́ть</i> (častítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to take Communion</li></ol><p><i>причасти́ть</i> (pričastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participation, involvement, complicity</li></ol><p><i>прича́стный</i> (pričástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>participating, concerned, involved, privy</li><li>participial</li></ol><p><i>прича́стие</i> (pričástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to give Communion to</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to connect (someone or something) to (something), to bring (someone) into communication with (something), to accustom, to familiarize</li></ol><p><i>причасти́ть</i> (pričastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to take Communion</li></ol><p><i>причаща́ть</i> (pričaščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Communion; communication</li></ol><p><i>причасти́ть</i> (pričastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Black Sea coast (<i>attributive</i>), Black Sea coastal, Pontic littoral</li></ol><p>From <i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>черномо́рский</i> (černomórskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do someone's hair, to brush someone's hair, to comb someone's hair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to edit, to polish, to touch up, to smooth out, to doctor</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb one's hair</li></ol><p><i>причеса́ть</i> (pričesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) hairstyle</li></ol><p><i>причеса́ть</i> (pričesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to add; to attach (when counting or calculating)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cause (source or reason of an event or action)</li><li>reason</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pričina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) infliction, civil injury, injurious action, tort, trespass, wrongdoing</li></ol><p><i>причини́ть</i> (pričinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause, to do</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causal</li></ol><p><i>причи́нный</i> (pričínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сле́дствие</i> (slédstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>causality (agency of cause)</li></ol><p><i>причи́нный</i> (pričínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causal, causative</li></ol><p><i>причи́на</i> (pričína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause, to do</li></ol><p><i>причини́ть</i> (pričinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add on <i>(to)</i></li><li>to reckon <i>(among, in)</i>, to number <i>(among)</i>, to rank <i>(among, with)</i></li><li>to attach <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to add on <i>(to)</i></li><li>to reckon <i>(among, in)</i>, to number <i>(among)</i>, to rank <i>(among, with)</i></li><li>to attach <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>причи́слить</i> (pričíslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamentation, wail</li></ol><p>From <i>причита́ть</i> (pričitátʹ, “to lament, wail”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament <i>(for, over)</i>, to bewail, to keen <i>(over)</i></li><li>to add; to attach (when counting or calculating)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be due</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>причита́ть</i> (pričitátʹ): to lament <i>(for, over)</i>, to bewail, to keen <i>(over)</i></li><li>alternative form of <i>причита́ть</i> (pričitátʹ): to add; to attach (when counting or calculating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smack one's lips (<i>usually out of pleasure or surprise</i>)</li></ol><p><i>причмо́кнуть</i> (pričmóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smack one's lips once (<i>usually out of pleasure or surprise</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>чмо́кнуть</i> (čmóknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the clergy of a parish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whim, whimsy, freak, caprice, fancy</li><li>oddity, vagary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fancifully, whimsically, capriciously</li><li>bizarrely, fantastically</li></ol><p><i>причу́дливый</i> (pričúdlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fancy, complicated</li><li>cute, quaint</li><li>bizarre, weird, peculiar (strangely unconventional)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) whimsical</li></ol><p><i>причу́да</i> (pričúda) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>moreover, and, at that, with</li></ol><p><i>при</i> (pri) +&lrm; <i>чём</i> (čom)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haircut, coiffure, hairdo, hair dress, hair style</li></ol><p>Deverbative of <i>причеса́ть</i> (pričesátʹ, “to comb, to brush”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do someone's hair, to brush someone's hair, to comb someone's hair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to edit, to polish, to touch up, to smooth out, to doctor</li></ol><p><i>причеса́ть</i> (pričesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb one's hair</li></ol><p><i>причёсывать</i> (pričósyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to moor</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>швартова́ть</i> (švartovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newcomer, stranger</li><li>space alien, extraterrestrial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пришьльць</i> (prišĭlĭcĭ), <i>пришельць</i> (prišelĭcĭ). By surface analysis, <i>прише́л</i> (prišél) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sojourn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Advent (coming of the Christ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пришьствиѥ</i> (prišĭstvije), <i>пришествиѥ</i> (prišestvije). By surface analysis, <i>прише́д</i> (prišéd) +&lrm; <i>-ствие</i> (-stvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to strike down, to strike dead</li></ol><p><i>пришиби́ть</i> (prišibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to strike down, to strike dead</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;perfective only) to depress, to dispirit</li></ol><p><i>при</i> (pri) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crushed, dejected, crestfallen (<i>of a person or one's mood</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew on; to nail on</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill, to murder, to waste</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to falsely accuse of a crime, to pin an accusation</li></ol><p><i>приши́ть</i> (prišítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew on; to nail on</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to kill, to murder, to waste</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to falsely accuse of a crime, to pin an accusation</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alien, foreign, nonlocal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pin, to fasten (<i>with pin, needle, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pin, to fasten (<i>with pin, needle, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>jam, pinch (<i>a finger, a toe</i>)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>щеми́ть</i> (ščemítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes peg, clothespin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squint (an expression in which the eyes are partly closed)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прищу́риться</i> (priščúritʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to squint</li></ol><p><i>прищу́рить</i> (priščúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squint</li></ol><p><i>прищу́ривать</i> (priščúrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to squint</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>щу́рить</i> (ščúritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squint</li></ol><p><i>прищу́рить</i> (priščúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) catch, seize, apprehend, nail</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) catch unawares, catch red-handed</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) give a dressing-down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge, shelter</li><li>asylum, orphanage</li><li>flophouse, doss-house</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prijǫtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shelter, to give refuge</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>прию́т</i> (prijút) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shelter oneself, to take cover</li><li>to make oneself comfortable</li></ol><p><i>приюти́ть</i> (prijutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) friendly, amiable</li></ol><p><i>прия́знь</i> (prijáznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship, friendliness, goodwill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>приꙗзнь</i> (prijaznĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prijàznь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;priH-</i> (“to love”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend, chum</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buddy, pal, mate (informal address)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>приꙗтель</i> (prijatelĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prijateľь</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>प्रिय</i> (priya, “dear, beloved”) and English <i>friend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly</li></ol><p><i>прия́тель</i> (prijátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casual friendship, acquaintanceship, amicable relations</li></ol><p><i>прия́тель</i> (prijátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceptance, receiving, taking</li></ol><p><i>прия́ть</i> (prijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pleasantly, pleasingly, agreeably</li></ol><p><i>прия́тный</i> (prijátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>pleased to meet you, nice to meet you</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bon appétit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasantness</li><li>amenity</li><li>gratefulness</li><li>jocundity</li><li>prettiness</li></ol><p><i>прия́тный</i> (prijátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, pleasing, agreeable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>приѩтьнъ</i> (prijętĭnŭ, “acceptable, pleasant”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>приѩтъ</i> (prijętŭ, “received, accepted”) + <i>-ьнъ</i> (-ĭnŭ). Equivalent to <i>прия́т(ый)</i> (priját(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>прийня́тний</i> (pryjnjátnyj, “acceptable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) alternative form of <i>приня́ть</i> (prinjátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>при-</i> (pri-) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to bother, to pester, to harass or to find fault</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reception</li><li>acceptance, admission</li><li>consultation</li><li>engagement</li><li>(<i>military</i>) enlistment</li><li>taking</li><li>dose</li><li>movement, draught</li><li>sitting</li><li>device, trick, gimmick</li><li>method, way</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>amateur radio</i>) over, do you copy?</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>takeover, taking over</li><li>formal acceptance <i>(of a completed construction project, a consignment of goods, etc)</i></li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>приня́ть</i> (prinjátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>приём-</i> (prijóm-), derived from the obsolete present tense stem <i>прие́м-</i> (prijém-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reception room, waiting room</li><li>reception, reception desk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receiver, receiving set</li><li><i>(electrical)</i> transducer</li><li>detector <i>(radiation)</i></li><li>collector, receptacle, reservoir</li><li>container, vessel, flask, tank</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) pitot tube</li><li><i>(gunnery)</i> ammunition feeder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitot tube <i>(airplanes)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reception, waiting (<i>of a room, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) receiving, consulting</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) office (<i>of hours</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) entrance (<i>of an examination</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) foster (<i>of a parent or child</i>)</li></ol><p><i>приём</i> (prijóm) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acceptance and delivery (<i>of a package, document, shipment, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>приём</i> (prijóm) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сда́точный</i> (sdátočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>приём</i> (prijóm)</li></ol><p><i>приём</i> (prijóm) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>about, concerning</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>про</i> (pro), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pro</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pra</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pró</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: ПРО)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missile defense</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>противораке́тная оборо́на</i> (protivorakétnaja oboróna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> про (pro),&lrm; себя́ (sebjá).</li><li>silently, to oneself (<i>of reading</i>)</li><li>inwardly (<i>of thinking</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) through, past (describing an action of passing through a space or an object)</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action of covering a whole object</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action of passing by an entity and moving further from an entity</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action that entails completeness, exhaustiveness, or accuracy</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing a momentary action with a short duration</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action that lasts for a certain period of time</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) describing an action that describes losses, expenses, wasting, or forgetting</li><li>(<i>used with nouns</i>) describing a state of being that is located between two entities or among more than two entities</li><li>(<i>used with adjectives or nouns</i>) describing an initial appearance or insufficiency of characteristics</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pro-</i> prefix, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to analyse</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>анализи́ровать</i> (analizírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assist (help)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ассисти́ровать</i> (assistírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, test</li><li>assay, sample</li><li>fineness (<i>of precious metal</i>)</li><li>hallmark, assay stamp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>próba</i> or German <i>Probe</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>proba</i>, from Latin <i>probō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-bʰH-wó-s</i> (“being in front”), from <i>&#42;pro-</i> (“forward”) + <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to be”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) literary debut, artistic debut (someone's first literary or other creative work)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a warm-up, a trial run</li></ol><p>Literally, “nib test” or “quill test”; in reference to testing an ink pen nib or quill on a scrap of paper before starting to write.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive) to blab, to spill the beans; to blurt out (something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shake completely, to shake thoroughly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shake (<i>for a specified quantity of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>проболта́ть</i> (proboltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blab, to spill the beans</li></ol><p><i>проба́лтывать</i> (probáltyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say or sing in a deep voice, to say or sing in a bass voice</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>баси́ть</i> (basítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>run <i>(distance)</i></li><li>(<i>sports</i>) run, race</li><li>total distance passed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run past, to run through</li><li>to run a certain distance or for some time</li><li>to skim, to scan, to look through</li><li>to spend some time running</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run past, to run through</li><li>to run a certain distance or for some time</li><li>to skim, to scan, to look through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run a little</li><li>to travel along the runway (<i>of a plane</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slide (<i>over something; of the hands or fingers</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пробежа́ть</i> (probežátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jog, run (a single exercise)</li></ol><p><i>пробега́ть</i> (probegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space, blank, gap</li><li>flaw, defect</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бел(ый)</i> (bel(yj))</p><p>Compare another natively formed Czech <i>mezerník</i> (&lt; <i>mezera</i>, see <i>промежу́ток</i> (promežútok), <i>межа́</i> (mežá), <i>ме́жду</i> (méždu)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punch out</li><li>to pierce, to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>to break <i>(through)</i></li><li>to make <i>(a road, a tunnel)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to issue a check/chit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pay to the cashier</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press <i>(for)</i>, to push <i>(for)</i>, to struggle <i>(for)</i>, to win, to bring off, to put across</li></ol><p><i>проби́ть</i> (probítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight/force/make one's way through, to break/win/strike through</li><li>to shoot, to show, to push up (<i>of plants</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пробива́ть</i> (probivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to feel strong coldness of the air or wind</li><li>to scold</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's way, to thread one's way, to pick one's way, to edge through</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test tube, test glass, vial</li></ol><p>From <i>проби́ровать</i> (probírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;in general) to test, to try, to tertiate, to examine, to sample, to assess</li><li>(<i>now&#32;technical</i>) to extract a sample from</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>probieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to punch out</li><li>to pierce, to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>to break <i>(through)</i></li><li>to make <i>(a road, a tunnel)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to issue a check/chit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pay to the cashier</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press <i>(for)</i>, to push <i>(for)</i>, to struggle <i>(for)</i>, to win, to bring off, to put across</li><li>to strike, to sound</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;probiti</i>, from <i>&#42;pro-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;biti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight/force/make one's way through, to break/win/strike through</li><li>to shoot, to show, to push up (<i>of plants</i>)</li></ol><p><i>проби́ть</i> (probítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plug</li><li>cork</li><li>congestion</li><li>traffic jam</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fuse (<i>device preventing overloading of a circuit</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traffic jam</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cork; corky</li></ol><p><i>про́бка</i> (próbka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>problem (difficulty)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>problema</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πρόβλημα</i> (próblēma, “anything thrown forward, hindrance, obstacle, anything projecting, a headland, promontory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>range of problems, agenda, subject matter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>problematic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) problem, problematic</li></ol><p><i>пробле́ма</i> (probléma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flash</li><li>gleam, ray</li><li>glimpse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flashing</li></ol><p>From <i>про́блеск</i> (próblesk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) (stem-stressed) or <i>про́блеск</i> (próblesk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency vehicle lighting, flashing light, flashing beacon (emergency vehicle lighting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whore, slut, prostitute</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>блѧдь</i> (blędĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;blędь</i> (“error”), from <i>&#42;blęsti</i> (“to talk nonsense, err”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sample, trial, test</li><li>hallmarked</li></ol><p><i>про́ба</i> (próba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test, to attempt, to try</li><li>to taste (to sample the flavor of something), to feel</li></ol><p>From <i>про́ба</i> (próba), from Polish <i>próba</i> or German <i>Probe</i>, from Middle Latin <i>proba</i>, from <i>probō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-bʰH-wó-s</i> (“being in front”), from <i>&#42;pro-</i> (“forward”) + <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to be”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, breach, leak, rupture (in a ship, aircraft, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пробо́й</i> (probój) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), from <i>проби́ть</i> (probítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(for a lock)</i> holdfast, clamp, hasp</li><li>rupture, breakdown, punch-through, runaway, puncture, disruption, burst</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>проби́ть</i> (probítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to chatter away, to spend time chatting or talking idly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive) to blab, to spill the beans; to blurt out (something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive,&#32;perfective only) to talk for a while about (something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shake completely, to shake thoroughly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to shake (<i>for a specified quantity of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to spend (some time) (<i>somewhere; usually idly or in vain</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blab, to spill the beans</li></ol><p><i>проболта́ть</i> (proboltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parting <i>(of hair)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, to murmur, to babble</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бормота́ть</i> (bormotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) corkscrew (implement for opening bottles sealed by a cork)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to feel strong coldness of the air or wind</li><li>to scold</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to part (hair)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's way, to thread one's way, to pick one's way, to edge through</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare with Belarusian <i>прабрацца</i> (prabracca), Ukrainian <i>пробратися</i> (probratysja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to mutter</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бубни́ть</i> (bubnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;transitive,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to arouse, to awaken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend some time trying to awaken (someone)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to engender (a feeling)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;intransitive) to awaken, to wake up</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to come to life (<i>about nature, humanity, etc.</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) to arise (<i>of a feeling</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пробуди́ть</i> (probudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to waken, to rouse, to wake up, to awake</li><li>to rouse, to excite</li></ol><p><i>пробуди́ть</i> (probudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awake, to wake up</li><li>to be aroused, to be awakened</li></ol><p><i>пробужда́ть</i> (probuždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awakening, waking</li><li>arousal (the act of arousing or the state of being aroused)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to rumble (to make a low growling or rumbling stomach noise)</li><li>to grumble, to complain</li><li>to mutter, to mumble</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>бурча́ть</i> (burčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to stay (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>пробы́ть</i> (probýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay (somewhere)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) Internet provider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>provider</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collapse</li><li>hole, depression</li><li>failure, flop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to botch up, to ruin, to mess up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only,&#32;imperative) to get lost</li></ol><p><i>провали́ть</i> (provalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall through, to come down</li><li>to fail, to flunk (on an exam)</li></ol><p><i>прова́ливать</i> (proválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to botch up, to ruin, to mess up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall through, to come down</li><li>to fail, to flunk (on an exam)</li></ol><p><i>провали́ть</i> (provalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Provençal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to see, to call <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out, to learn <i>(of, about)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, building, laying (<i>of roads, cables, pipes, etc.</i>)</li><li>organization, holding (<i>of a meeting, event, activity</i>)</li><li>carrying out, realization, implementation</li><li>adoption (<i>of a resolution, law</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to see, to call <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out, to learn <i>(of, about)</i></li></ol><p><i>прове́дать</i> (provédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport, to convey</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proved, proven, reliable, tried and true, tried and tested</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to check, to control, to verify, to test, to audit</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>check, examination, test, verification</li><li>inspection</li></ol><p><i>прове́рить</i> (provéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn with difficulty</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to quickly do or arrange</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) test, verification, check, checking, checkup</li></ol><p><i>прове́рка</i> (provérka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn with difficulty</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn with difficulty</li></ol><p><i>проверте́ть</i> (provertétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to check, to control, to verify, to test, to audit</li></ol><p><i>прове́рить</i> (provéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) to test, to vet (to check for hidden flaws)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to check for lice”. Compare French <i>chercher des poux</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do a checkup (usually medical)</li><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial) to check information about oneself</li></ol><p><i>проверя́ть</i> (proverjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to conduct, to guide, to steer, to take</li><li>to lay, to construct, to build, to install <i>a road, a pipe, a wire etc.</i></li><li>to carry out, to carry through, to realize, to put into practice, to conduct</li><li>to pass over, to run over</li><li>to spend, to pass <i>(time)</i></li><li>to book, to register, to enter</li><li>to draw <i>a line</i></li><li>to cheat, to take in, to trick, to fool</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вести</i> (vesti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to air, to ventilate</li></ol><p><i>прове́трить</i> (provétritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to air, to ventilate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́трить</i> (-vétritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interact, to act reciprocally, to cooperate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>взаимоде́йствовать</i> (vzaimodéjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) food, provisions, victuals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Proviant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foresight, foreknowledge</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) providence; Providence</li></ol><p><i>прови́деть</i> (provídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p><p><i>прови́деть</i> (provídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to foresee</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seer, prophet</li></ol><p><i>прови́деть</i> (provídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) provisions, food</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>prowizja</i> or directly from German <i>Provision</i>; ultimately from Latin <i>prōvīsiō</i> (“foresight, diligence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be at fault, to commit an offence <i>(in)</i>, to be guilty <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вини́ть</i> (vinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duty</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fault, offence, misconduct, misdeed, misdemeanour</li></ol><p>From <i>провини́ться</i> (provinítʹsja, “to commit an offence”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a native or resident of the province, rather than capital</li><li>naively simple-minded person, behavior and habits, indicating a provincial way of life; country bumpkin</li><li>(<i>Roman Catholicism</i>) head of the province of the Catholic order</li></ol><p>From <i>провинция</i> (provincija) +&lrm; <i>-ал</i> (-al).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provincial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>province (Ancient Rome)</li><li>province (administrative region)</li><li>area located far from the capital or large urban centers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wire, lead, conductor</li><li>leading, conducting, steering</li><li>denoting, marking, indicating (<i>by means of a long line or track</i>)</li><li>construction (<i>of canals, roads, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately deverbal from <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ), with stress shift betraying an early derivation.</p><p>Deverbal from <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conductivity, conduction</li></ol><p><i>проводи́мый</i> (provodímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend <i>(time)</i>, to pass</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) to conduct</li><li>to conduct, to lead, to guide</li><li>to carry out, to carry through, to realize, to put into practice, to conduct</li><li>to put through, to get through</li><li>to draw (a line)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to lay road, to construct</li><li>to develop, to pursue</li><li>(<i>bookkeeping</i>) to enter</li><li>to cheat, to deceive</li><li>to escort, to accompany, to see off</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be carried out; to be held, to be conducted; to be in progress</li></ol><p><i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conducting, steering</li><li>construction, building, laying (<i>of roads or communication</i>)</li><li>installation (<i>of wiring or canalization</i>)</li><li>wires, wiring</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) posting</li></ol><p><i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide <i>(person)</i></li><li>conductor, guard, train attendant <i>(on a train)</i></li><li>conductor <i>(physical, electrical)</i></li></ol><p><i>про́вод</i> (próvod) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conductive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pro-war (in favour of a military solution to a political problem)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вое́нный</i> (vojénnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort, guide, chaperone</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>провожа́ть</i> (provožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj), but if so, with a very unusual use of <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj) found only in this word and words derived from <i>вожа́тый</i> (vožátyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escort, to accompany, to see off</li></ol><p><i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>провожа́ть</i> (provožátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of transporting; lift</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) harbinger</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>провозві́сник</i> (provozvísnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proclaim, to promulgate, to declare, to enunciate (solemnly or officially)</li><li>to acclaim (to declare by acclamations)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proclaim, to promulgate, to declare, to enunciate (solemnly or officially)</li><li>to acclaim (to declare by acclamations)</li></ol><p><i>провозгласи́ть</i> (provozglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclamation, enunciation</li></ol><p><i>провозгласи́ть</i> (provozglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport, to convey</li><li>to carry around <i>(for a certain time)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provocateur, provoker (one who engages in provocative behavior)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>provocator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provocative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provocation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>provocatio</i>, borrowed or through the intermediate of another language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>про́волока</i> (próvoloka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wire (thin thread of metal)</li><li>red tape, drag, delay</li></ol><p>From <i>волочи́ть</i> (voločítʹ, “to draw, pull”).</p><p>Related to <i>волоки́та</i> (volokíta, “fuss and bother, red tape”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>про́волока</i> (próvoloka): a little piece of wire</li><li>delay, protraction</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dressing-down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wire</li></ol><p><i>про́волока</i> (próvoloka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crank</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn with difficulty</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to quickly do or arrange</li></ol><p><i>провороти́ть</i> (provorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) to do multiple or cumbersome tasks very quickly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nimbly, dexterously, adroitly</li><li>quickly, briskly</li></ol><p><i>прово́рный</i> (provórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, swift, brisk, expeditious</li><li>nimble, deft, agile, adroit, dexterous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;provorьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>прово́рить</i> (provóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to crank</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to turn with difficulty</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to quickly do or arrange</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agility, nimbleness, legerity, lightness</li><li>quickness, adroitness, deftness</li><li>dexterity</li><li>promptness, promptitude</li><li>fleetness</li></ol><p><i>прово́рить</i> (provóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, to grumble</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ворча́ть</i> (vorčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke</li></ol><p>From German <i>provozieren</i>, itself derived from Latin <i>provocare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drill a hole in, to perforate</li><li>to twist (something) through (something) (<i>esp. meat through a meat grinder</i>)</li><li>to crank</li><li>to turn with difficulty</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to quickly do or arrange</li></ol><p><i>проверну́ть</i> (provernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) computer program, app</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glade <i>(in a forest)</i></li><li>gap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buckling, flexure, caving</li><li>concession; giving in to someone's demands</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend (<i>under the action of gravity or pressure</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend (<i>under the action of gravity or pressure</i>)</li><li>to straighten and arch one's back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flatter [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прогиба́ть</i> (progibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingestion, swallowing</li></ol><p><i>прогла́тывать</i> (proglátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije, verbal abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li><li>to swallow, to bolt, to gulp down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(accept without protest)</i> to swallow, to pocket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swallow, to mutter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(book)</i> to devour</li></ol><p><i>проглоти́ть</i> (proglotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li><li>to swallow, to bolt, to gulp down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(accept without protest)</i> to swallow, to pocket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swallow, to mutter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(book)</i> to devour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look <i>(through)</i>, to skim</li><li>to overlook, to miss</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look <i>(through)</i>, to skim</li><li>to show, to appear, to peep out</li><li>to be discernible</li></ol><p><i>прогляд-</i> (progljad-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>прогляде́ть</i> (progljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to appear, to peep out</li><li>to be discernible</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-гляну́ть</i> (-gljanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (cattle)</li><li>to chase away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause to disappear, to banish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fire</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гнев</i> (gnev) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot to the core</li></ol><p><i>прогни́ть</i> (prognítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot to the core</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гнить</i> (gnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forecast, projection</li><li>prognosis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>prognose</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πρόγνωσις</i> (prógnōsis, “foreknowledge, perceiving beforehand, prediction”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forecasting, prognostication</li></ol><p><i>прогнози́ровать</i> (prognozírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predict, to foretell, to prognosticate</li></ol><p><i>прогно́з</i> (prognóz) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prognostic</li><li>anticipated, expected, predictable</li></ol><p><i>прогно́з</i> (prognóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bend (<i>under the action of gravity or pressure</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bend (<i>under the action of gravity or pressure</i>)</li><li>to straighten and arch one's back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flatter [<i>with</i> <b>пе́ред</b> (péred, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>прогну́ть</i> (prognútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, to utter</li><li>to talk (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>проговори́ть</i> (progovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let out a secret, to blab <i>(out)</i>, to let the cat out of the bag</li></ol><p><i>прогова́ривать</i> (progovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, to utter</li><li>to talk (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let out a secret, to blab <i>(out)</i>, to let the cat out of the bag, to spill the beans</li></ol><p><i>проговори́ть</i> (progovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel/get/grow hungry</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>голода́ть</i> (golodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vote (assert a formalised choice)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>голосова́ть</i> (golosovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driving (of animals)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) purlin, bearer, baulk</li><li>stairwell, well shaft</li><li>run-through</li><li>pass, passage</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a collective letter written by members of a criminal community (often in a prison) addressed to another criminal community (in other cells of the same prison or in another prison)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прогна́ть</i> (prognátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive (cattle)</li><li>to chase away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause to disappear, to banish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive for a while</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to distill for a while</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chase for a while, to pursue for a while</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn up, to burn completely (<i>of wood</i>)</li><li>to be damaged by fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to go bankrupt</li></ol><p><i>прогоре́ть</i> (progorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burn up, to burn completely (<i>of wood</i>)</li><li>to be damaged by fire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to go bankrupt</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma)</li><li>program (leaflet listing information about a play, game or other activity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>program/programme</li><li>(<i>academic</i>) syllabus, curriculum</li><li>program, agenda</li><li>(television,&#32;radio) program, broadcast, show</li><li>(television,&#32;radio) channel</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>πρόγραμμα</i> (prógramma). Further from Ancient Greek <i>προγράφω</i> (prográphō), from <i>προ-</i> (pro-) + Ancient Greek <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>add-on (extension of core application)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) programmer (a device that installs a software program into some other machine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>programming</li></ol><p><i>программи́ровать</i> (programmírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to program</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>programmieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) programmer (one who designs software)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma): (small) programme; app, applet; TV guide, theatre programme</li></ol><p><i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) software (encoded computer instructions)</li></ol><p>From <i>програ́ммное</i> (prográmmnoje, “program”) +&lrm; <i>обеспече́ние</i> (obespečénije, “supply”) (or <i>обеспе́чение</i> (obespéčenije, “supply”); the position of the accent has no effect on the meaning).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) program</li></ol><p><i>програ́мма</i> (prográmma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pronounce with a uvular/guttural <i>р</i> (r), as in French</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>грасси́ровать</i> (grassírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming-up, heat, heating, heating-through, warming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm, to heat, to warm up</li></ol><p><i>прогре́ть</i> (progrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thunder</li><li>to resound, to ring out</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to roar, to speak loudly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to roar past (while driving)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>греме́ть</i> (gremétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progress (a moving forward)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>progressus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>progressively</li></ol><p><i>прогресси́вный</i> (progressívnyj, progrɛssívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progressiveness</li></ol><p><i>прогресси́вный</i> (progressívnyj, progrɛssívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>progressive</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>prōgressīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to progress (<i>also of an illness</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прогре́сс</i> (progréss, progrɛ́ss) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progression (mathematical sequence)</li><li>sequence <i><b>Прогрессия</b> — это последовательное повторение мелодической фразы или гармонического оборота на другой высоте.</i><dl><dd><i><b>Progressija</b> — eto posledovatelʹnoje povtorenije melodičeskoj frazy ili garmoničeskovo oborota na drugoj vysote.</i></dd><dd>A <b>sequence</b> is the restatement of a motif or harmonic passage at another pitch.</dd></dl></li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prōgressiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm, to heat, to warm up</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hum, to buzz (to make a droning sound)</li><li>to sound (<i>of a horn, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гуде́ть</i> (gudétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unexcused absence, truancy, shirking</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) absenteeism</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прогуля́ть</i> (proguljátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk (children, pets)</li><li>to miss (class), to wag (to not go to school), to play hooky</li></ol><p><i>прогуля́ть</i> (proguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a walk/stroll, to promenade</li><li>to stroll, to saunter, to ramble</li></ol><p><i>прогу́ливать</i> (progúlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walk, outing (trip made by walking)</li><li>trip, ride, tour</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гулять</i> (guljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pleasure, leisure</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) walk, promenade</li><li>leisurely, walking</li></ol><p><i>прогу́лка</i> (progúlka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truant</li></ol><p><i>прогуля́ть</i> (proguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to stroll (for some time or some distance)</li><li>to walk (children, pets for some time or some distance)</li><li>to miss (class), to wag (to not go to school)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a walk/stroll, to promenade</li><li>to stroll, to saunter, to ramble</li></ol><p><i>прогуля́ть</i> (proguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) continuation (used in the context of, e.g., online self-publishing, in reference to posting of the next piece of a work in progress).</li></ol><p>Abbreviated продолжение</p><p>Russian computer slang for "production server"</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li><li>to sell out, to betray</li></ol><p><i>прода́ть</i> (prodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell (intransitive)</li><li>to sell oneself, to betray</li></ol><p><i>продава́ть</i> (prodavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seller, salesman, vendor</li><li>shop assistant (shop employee)</li></ol><p><i>продава́ть</i> (prodavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush/break through, to squeeze/press through</li><li><i>(seat, sofa etc)</i> to cause to sink [sag down]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press <i>(for)</i>, to ram, to win, to bring off, to put across, to push <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush/break through, to squeeze/press through</li><li><i>(seat, sofa etc)</i> to cause to sink [sag down]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to press <i>(for)</i>, to ram, to win, to bring off, to put across, to push <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>продави́ть</i> (prodavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sale, selling (exchange of goods or services for currency or credit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>продажа</i> (prodaža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prodaďa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability to sell</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) selling out</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) venality</li></ol><p><i>прода́жный</i> (prodážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>venal (willing to take bribes)</li><li>sale, selling</li></ol><p><i>прода́жа</i> (prodáža) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production the process of creating a creative product (chiefly in the film, television and music industries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>production</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прода́кшен</i> (prodákšen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li><li>to sell out, to betray</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell (intransitive)</li><li>to sell oneself, to betray</li></ol><p><i>прода́ть</i> (prodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>or</i> to push ahead (on, forward, further)</li><li>to promote, to advance, to further (to encourage growth)</li><li>to spend some time moving (something)</li></ol><p><i>продви́г-</i> (prodvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гать</i> (dvígatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advance, to move/push forward/on/further, to gain/make ground, to make headway</li><li>to advance, to make progress/headway</li></ol><p><i>продвига́ть</i> (prodvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advancement</li><li>(<i>military</i>) advance, progress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advanced (at or close to state of the art)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>or</i> to push ahead (on, forward, further)</li><li>to promote, to advance, to further (to encourage growth)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advance, to move/push forward/on/further, to gain/make ground, to make headway</li><li>to advance, to make progress/headway</li></ol><p><i>продви́нуть</i> (prodvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declaim, to recite</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>деклами́ровать</i> (deklamírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (a hole), to break through (a hole)</li><li>to do (a trick)</li><li>to carry out (a task; work)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prank, trick</li></ol><p><i>проде́лать</i> (prodélatʹ, “to do (a trick)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (a hole), to break through (a hole)</li><li>to do (a trick)</li><li>to carry out (a task; work)</li></ol><p><i>проде́лать</i> (prodélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demonstrate, to exhibit, to show</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>демонстри́ровать</i> (demonstrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, to hold (<i>an amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold out, to stand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(food)</i> to keep <i>(fresh)</i></li></ol><p><i>продержа́ть</i> (proderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to parade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sell too cheap, to make a bad bargain</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal&#32;and&#32;figurative</i>) to dictate <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>диктова́ть</i> (diktovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hole, to tear holes <i>(in)</i>, to wear out</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear [be worn] into holes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze/make/force one's way <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>продира́ть</i> (prodirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ration card</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>продово́льственная ка́рта</i> (prodovólʹstvennaja kárta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extend, to prolong, to elongate, to make longer</li><li>to renew, to extend (e.g. subscriptions, books in a library)</li></ol><p>From <i>продлѣти</i> (prodlěti) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ), variant of <i>продли́ть</i> (prodlítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prolongation, extension</li></ol><p><i>продли́ть</i> (prodlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>продлева́ть</i> (prodlevátʹ)</li></ol><p>First attested in Kuranty in year 1646 as <i>про<b>д</b>ливаю<b>т</b></i> (pro<b>d</b>livaju<b>t</b>, 3pl). From <i>продли́ть</i> (prodlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extend, to prolong, to elongate, to make longer</li><li>to renew, to extend (e.g. subscriptions, books in a library)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>длить</i> (dlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to last <i>(for some time)</i>, to draw out</li></ol><p><i>продли́ть</i> (prodlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) alternative form of <i>продлить</i> (prodlitʹ)</li></ol><p>Attested in year 1638.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>prodnalog</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>продово́льственный нало́г</i> (prodovólʹstvennyj nalóg).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) food</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) rations</li></ol><p><i>продово́льствие</i> (prodovólʹstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food (supply); foodstuffs, provisions, rations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) food, supplies (organized supplies for a group of person like an army)</li></ol><p><i>продово́льствовать</i> (prodovólʹstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) To supply (to provide food, to feed in an organized manner).</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дово́льство</i> (dovólʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat through</li><li>to screw up, to fail</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>долба́ть</i> (dolbátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elongated, oblong</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neuroanatomy</i>) medulla oblongata</li></ol><p>From <i>продолгова́тый</i> (prodolgovátyj, “elongated, oblong”) +&lrm; <i>мозг</i> (mozg, “part; lobe”), a calque of Latin <i>medulla oblongāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continuator, continuer, successor</li></ol><p><i>продолжа́ть</i> (prodolžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to continue, to proceed (with), to go on</li><li>to keep on</li></ol><p><i>продо́лжить</i> (prodólžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to last, to continue/be continued, to go on, to endure, to lengthen</li></ol><p><i>продолжа́ть</i> (prodolžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continuation (act or state of continuing)</li></ol><p><i>продо́лжить</i> (prodólžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duration; continuance</li></ol><p><i>продолжи́тельный</i> (prodolžítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lifespan, lifetime (the typical length of time that any particular organism can be expected to live)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li><li>prolonged, extended</li><li>protracted (<i>of sound</i>)</li></ol><p><i>продо́лжить</i> (prodólžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to continue, to proceed (with), to go on</li><li>to keep on</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), cognates include Ukrainian <i>продо́вжити</i> (prodóvžyty), Belarusian <i>прадо́ўжыць</i> (pradówžycʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to last, to continue/be continued, to go on, to endure, to lengthen</li></ol><p><i>продо́лжить</i> (prodólžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>longitudinal</li><li>lengthwise</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дол-</i> (dol-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Expected form based on <i>длина́</i> (dliná) would be &#42;проде́льный; form with -о- influenced by <i>до́лгий</i> (dólgij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Russian and Soviet military policy of confiscation of grain and other agricultural products in 1916-1921</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;figurative) any act of violent expropriation made by military or para-military groups in rural area</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>продово́льственная развёрстка</i> (prodovólʹstvennaja razvjórstka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hole, to tear holes <i>(in)</i>, to wear out</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дра́ть</i> (drátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear [be worn] into holes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze/make/force one's way <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>продра́ть</i> (prodrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be cold, to become frozen, to feel chilly; to freeze, to numb (by coldness), to shiver</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дро́гнуть</i> (drógnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to duplicate</li><li>to dub (a film)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дубли́ровать</i> (dublírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean by blowing, to blow clean</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause to catch a cold from the wind</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to lose (<i>in sports or gambling</i>)</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to blow through</li></ol><p><i>проду́ть</i> (prodútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>product (produced by a process or action)</li><li>product (commodity for sale)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) foods, edibles, produce</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>productivity (state of being productive)</li></ol><p><i>продукти́вный</i> (produktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>productive; effective, efficient; fruitful</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>productivus</i> via French <i>productif</i> or German <i>produktiv</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) food</li></ol><p><i>проду́кт</i> (prodúkt) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foods, edibles</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production, product(s), commodity, output</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(well)</i> thought-out, considered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over, to think through, to consider carefully</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think over, to think through, to consider carefully</li></ol><p><i>проду́мать</i> (prodúmatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean by blowing, to blow clean</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause to catch a cold from the wind</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to blow (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to lose (<i>in sports or gambling</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only) to run (<i>a specified distance</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to generate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>produzieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(film) producer (male or female)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>producer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck (<i>for a certain period of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to botch, to mess up, to fuck up</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to miss (<i>an opportunity, target</i>)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lose (<i>an item, a game, a war</i>)</li><li>(military slang,&#32;vulgar) to fail to guard; to negligently allow something to be stolen or destroyed</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) + <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journey</li><li>passage, thoroughfare</li><li>lane, passage</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>проезжа́ть</i> (projezžátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) travel; serving for travel</li></ol><p><i>прое́зд</i> (projézd) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through/by (by horse or vehicle), to ride through/by, to drive through/by</li><li>to drive past/along/across</li><li>to travel a certain distance</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive, to ride (<i>for a short distance; for pleasure</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mock</li></ol><p><i>проезжа́ть</i> (projezžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carriageway (part of a road that carries traffic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passing by, through (traveller)</li><li>fit, used for traffic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>project (planned endeavor)</li><li>design</li><li>draft</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Projekt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designing, projecting, projection, planning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to design, to project, to plan</li><li>to plan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planner, designer, developer</li></ol><p><i>проекти́ровать</i> (proektírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>planned, designed</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) project, design, planning</li></ol><p>From <i>прое́кт</i> (proékt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>projector</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Projektor</i>. Doublet of <i>проже́ктор</i> (prožéktor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>projection; (graphical) view, perspective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) Used to express the desire to change topic or that a topic has already been resolved &#47; is in the past; forget it, never mind, doesn't matter, let's move on / drop it, whatever, we're past/beyond it/that (now)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through/by (<i>by horse or vehicle</i>), to ride through/by, to drive through/by</li><li>to drive past/along/across</li><li>to travel (a certain distance)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive, to ride (<i>for a short distance; for pleasure</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mock</li></ol><p><i>прое́хать</i> (projéxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to project (onto)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>projizieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait for [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> (<i>dated</i>) dative <small>‘an amount of time’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ждать</i> (ždatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew/masticate well</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>жева́ть</i> (ževátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) project (same as прое́кт (proékt), прое́кт (proékt), the modern term)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly in the plural) plan(s) for the future</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>projet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a larger) spotlight, floodlight, searchlight; projector (=проектор)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>projecteur</i>. Doublet of <i>прое́ктор</i> (proéktor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn a hole in (something)</li><li>to burn clean</li><li>to warm strongly</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to burn (to cause a painful, burning sensation)</li><li>to burn (e.g. a lamp, a candle) (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seasoned, experienced</li><li>inveterate, hardened (<i>of a criminal, scoundrel</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residing, inhabitation, residence</li></ol><p><i>прожива́ть</i> (proživátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to live, to reside, to dwell</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spend, to run through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>жива́ть</i> (živátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn a hole in (something)</li><li>to burn clean</li><li>to warm strongly</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to burn (to cause a painful, burning sensation)</li><li>to burn (e.g. a lamp, a candle) (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) admixture of Jewish blood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streak, vein</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>жи́лка</i> (žílka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsistence, living</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsistence, living</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) subsistence</li></ol><p><i>прожи́ток</i> (prožítok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living wage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live, to outlast</li><li>to reside (for some time)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spend, to run through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gluttonous, voracious</li></ol><p>From <i>прожо́ра</i> (prožóra, “glutton”, found in Dal) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj), from <i>прожира́ть</i> (prožirátʹ, “to devour”); compare <i>обжо́ра</i> (obžóra, “glutton”), <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ, “to devour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew/masticate well</li></ol><p><i>прожева́ть</i> (proževátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prose</li><li>prosaic aspect, monotony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prose writer, prosaist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prose</li><li>prosaic, matter-of-fact, prosy</li></ol><p><i>про́за</i> (próza) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name someone/something as something, to make a name or a nickname (to someone or something); to nickname</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resound, to ring out (to make a loud sound)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>звене́ть</i> (zvenétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nickname, sobriquet, byname, cognomen, soubriquet</li><li>alias (another name&#59; an assumed name)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) surname</li></ol><p><i>прозва́ть</i> (prozvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound</li><li>to be heard (of sound, voice)</li><li>to be discernible (of emotion, mood in a voice or words)</li><li>to sound (of impression)</li><li>to be known widely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn (for a while), to spend some time yawning</li><li>to miss (a train, a stop, a chance, an opportunity, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>зева́ть</i> (zevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interstice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acumen, sagacity, astuteness</li></ol><p><i>прозорли́вый</i> (prozorlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insightful, shrewd, perspicacious</li></ol><p><i>прозор-</i> (prozor-) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transparence, transparency, limpidity, pellucidity</li></ol><p><i>прозра́чный</i> (prozráčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transparent, clear, see-through, lucent</li><li>limpid, liquid</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) obvious</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>зрак</i> (zrak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover one's sight</li><li>to begin to see clearly</li></ol><p><i>прозре́ть</i> (prozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recovery of sight</li><li>enlightenment, insight, epiphany</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prozrenьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recover one's sight</li><li>to begin to see clearly</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nickname, to name</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vegetate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>прозѧбати</i> (prozębati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zębnǫti</i> (“to germinate”). Unrelated to <i>зя́бнуть</i> (zjábnutʹ, “to freeze”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ignore, to disregard (to deliberately pay no attention to), to igg</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>игнори́ровать</i> (ignorírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to ignore (to not respond)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to ignore (to mute a user, hiding their content)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>игно́рить</i> (ignóritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loser (person who fails to win)</li><li>underdog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lost (as in "lost battle" or "lost game")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to play, to play through, to play over</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gramophone, record player</li><li>(media) player (e.g. CD, DVD, mp3)</li></ol><p><i>прои́грывать</i> (proígryvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to play, to play through, to play over</li></ol><p><i>проигра́ть</i> (proigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss, losing (<i>in a game or war</i>)</li><li>loss, what is lost, the lost sum</li><li>(<i>music</i>) musical part of a song without singing</li></ol><p>From <i>проиграть</i> (proigratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ыш</i> (-yš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation</li><li>work of art, work (a literary, artistic, or intellectual production)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) product</li></ol><p><i>произвед-</i> (proizved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work of art (product of the fine arts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to carry out, to execute, to effect</li><li>to give birth</li><li>to produce</li><li>to promote</li><li>to cause, to produce, to create</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacturer (one who manufactures)</li><li>stud (male animal used for breeding)</li></ol><p><i>производи́ть</i> (proizvodítʹ, “to manufacture, to produce”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>productivity (state of being productive)</li><li>efficiency</li><li>performance</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) reproduction</li></ol><p><i>производи́тельный</i> (proizvodítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) productive</li></ol><p>From <i>производи́ть</i> (proizvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to carry out, to execute, to effect</li><li>to give birth</li><li>to promote</li><li>to cause, to produce, to create</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) derivative (value)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>derivative</li></ol><p><i>производ(и́ть)</i> (proizvod(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production worker (worker directly involved in the production process)</li></ol><p><i>произво́дствен(ный)</i> (proizvódstven(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manufacturing, industrial</li></ol><p><i>произво́дство</i> (proizvódstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production, manufacture</li><li>plant, works, factory</li></ol><p><i>производи́ть</i> (proizvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>free will</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) power abuse; arbitrary will, behaviour or rule</li><li>lawlessness</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>произволъ</i> (proizvolŭ). Can also be analysed as <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>arbitrarily, randomly</li><li>ad hoc (on the spur of the moment)</li></ol><p><i>произво́льный</i> (proizvólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arbitrary, random, any given (determined by impulse)</li></ol><p><i>произво́л</i> (proizvól) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronouncing, utterance</li></ol><p><i>произнес-</i> (proiznes-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pronounce, to articulate</li><li>to deliver, to utter</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>изнести́</i> (iznestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pronounceable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pronounce, to articulate</li><li>to deliver, to utter</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>износи́ть</i> (iznosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronunciation</li><li>articulation</li></ol><p><i>произноси́ть</i> (proiznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take place, to happen, to occur</li><li>to derive, to be descended, to descend, to originate</li><li>to spring, to arise, to result, to take place</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>изойти́</i> (izojtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growing, sprouting</li></ol><p><i>произраста́ть</i> (proizrastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to spring up, to sprout</li></ol><p><i>произраст-</i> (proizrast-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to spring up, to sprout</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>из-</i> (iz-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to illustrate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>иллюстри́ровать</i> (illjustrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to index (to arrange an index)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>индекси́ровать</i> (indɛksírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify, to advertise (to give public notice of&#59; to announce publicly)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>информи́ровать</i> (informírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intrigues, scheming, underhand plotting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to result <i>(from)</i>, to spring <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>происте́к-</i> (proisték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to result <i>(from)</i>, to spring <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>исте́чь</i> (istéčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take place, to happen, to occur</li><li>to derive, to be descended, to descend, to originate</li><li>to spring, to arise, to result, to take place</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>исходи́ть</i> (isxodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what's happening, what's going on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ongoing, occurring, happening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin</li><li>provenance</li><li>lineage</li><li>birth, extraction, parentage, descent,</li></ol><p><i>происходи́ть</i> (proisxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>what has happened, incident</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incident, event, occurrence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) scoundrel, swindler, rogue, cheat</li></ol><p><i>пройд-</i> (projd-) +&lrm; <i>-о́ха</i> (-óxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass, to go, to walk</li><li>to pass by</li><li><i>(time)</i> to pass, to go by, to elapse, to slip by</li><li>to be over</li><li><i>(meeting, gathering)</i> to go off, to be held</li><li>to study, to cover (in a course)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (go for a) walk, to stroll</li><li>to go over; to scrutinize; to analyze.</li></ol><p><i>пройти́</i> (projtí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, benefit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прокъ</i> (prokŭ, “rest, remnant”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokъ</i> (“rest, remnant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leprosy</li><li>leprous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prank, trick, mischief, roguery</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) leprosy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>проказа</i> (prokaza, “harm, loss, trick, malintent”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokaza</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>проказа</i> (prokaza, “leprosy”), from Old Church Slavonic <i>проказа</i> (prokaza, “leprosy”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokaza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прока́зничать</i> (prokázničatʹ)</li><li>to afflict with leprosy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>прока́за</i> (prokáza) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p><p>Perhaps from <i>прока́за</i> (prokáza) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mischiefmaking</li></ol><p><i>прока́зливый</i> (prokázlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mischievous</li></ol><p>From <i>прока́з(а)</i> (prokáz(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rogue; prankster, mischiefmaker</li></ol><p><i>прока́за</i> (prokáza) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play pranks, to make mischief</li><li>to horse around</li></ol><p><i>прока́за</i> (prokáza) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, to puncture, to prick, to perforate, to punch</li><li>to run a knife <i>(through)</i>, to stab</li></ol><p><i>проколо́ть</i> (prokolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say with a mispronounced <i>р</i> (r) or <i>л</i> (l) sound</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>карта́вить</i> (kartávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hire, rental (<i>of goods</i>)</li><li>rolling</li><li>rolled metal</li><li>distribution <i>(of films)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out with a roller</li><li>to roll smooth (a road or path)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to subject to a rolling process</li><li>to transport (<i>for a period of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figure skating</i>) to perform (a figure skating program)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend (money) on a trip</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll (somewhere)</li><li>to entertain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blackball, to vote against, to reject (a candidate at an election)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to criticize sharply and publicly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to quickly drive by/past</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to reject (a candidate at an election); to punish</li></ol><p>Possibly an idiom from the play by Ivan Turgenev “A Month in the Country”. Literally, “to take a ride on black horses”. Perhaps it has something to do with the blackball (obsolete voting with white and black balls). Contamination is also possible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go for a ride</li></ol><p><i>прокати́ть</i> (prokatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>for rent</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rental</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) rolled (<i>metal</i>)</li><li>(relational,&#32;metallurgy) rolling</li></ol><p><i>прока́т</i> (prokát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll (somewhere)</li><li>to entertain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blackball, to vote against, to reject (a candidate at an election)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to criticize sharply and publicly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to quickly drive by/past</li><li>to smooth out with a roller</li><li>to roll smooth (a road or path)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to subject to a rolling process</li><li>to transport (<i>for a period of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figure skating</i>) to perform (a figure skating program)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend (money) on a trip</li></ol><p><i>прокати́ть</i> (prokatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>проката́ть</i> (prokatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump (through/over)</li><li>to flush</li><li>to pump, to swing, etc. (describes the verb <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) that lasts for a certain period of time)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to level up something</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pump (through/over)</li><li>to flush</li><li>to pump, to swing, etc. (describes the verb <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ) that lasts for a certain period of time)</li></ol><p><i>прокача́ть</i> (prokačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear one's throat by coughing</li></ol><p><i>прока́шливать</i> (prokášlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear one's throat by coughing</li></ol><p><i>прока́шлять</i> (prokášljatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sour (e.g. a milk product)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ки́снуть</i> (kísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying, building, construction (<i>of roads, pipes, communications, etc.</i>)</li><li>packing, lining, gasket</li><li>menstrual pad</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a fraudulent business that steals money from customers</li><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) the white-red-white flag of Belarus, formerly the national flag, used to protest Alexander Lukashenko's government</li><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) the white-blue-white flag used in Russia to protest the 2022 invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>прокла́д(ывать)</i> (proklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Senses 5 and 6 refer to the protest flags' similar appearance to a menstrual pad.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(a road, pipe etc.)</i> to build, to construct, to lay</li><li>to clear</li><li>to map</li><li>to interlay <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>про</i> (pro) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclamation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>proclamatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse, to damn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curse, to damn</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>клясть</i> (kljastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) damnation, condemnation, excommunication</li><li>anathema</li><li>curse, imprecation, malediction</li><li>extreme and imminent misfortune; continuous state of trouble</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>dammit!, damnation! (expressing strong negative emotions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>damned, cursed</li><li>(<i>rude</i>) damned, damn (<i>used as an expletive</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>прокля́сть</i> (prokljástʹ, “to curse, damn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an intractable question which defies solution; an issue which is difficult to investigate or inquire into</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> проклятый (prokljatyj),&lrm; вопрос (vopros).</li></ol><p>From прокля́тый&nbsp;m (prokljátyj, “cursed, damned”) + вопро́с&nbsp;m (voprós, “question”). The phrase appeared in an 1858 translation by Mikhail Larionovitch Mikhailov of a German-language poem by Heinrich Heine and saw use in Russian literary and academic circles subsequently.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прокля́тие</i> (prokljátije)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прокля́тие</i> (prokljátije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puncture, pinhole</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blunder, boner, goof, failure, washout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, to puncture, to prick, to perforate, to punch</li><li>to run a knife <i>(through)</i>, to stab</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comment (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>комменти́ровать</i> (kommentírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to act as a consultant</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>консульти́ровать</i> (konsulʹtírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>проконсульти́ровать</i> (prokonsulʹtírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control, to check</li><li>to monitor, to supervise</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>контроли́ровать</i> (kontrolírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed (e.g. a family)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>корми́ть</i> (kormítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to adjust</li><li>to proofread</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>корректи́ровать</i> (korrektírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneak (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to arise imperceptibly, to sneak in (<i>of thoughts or feelings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прокра́сться</i> (prokrástʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneak (<i>somewhere</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to arise imperceptibly, to sneak in (<i>of thoughts or feelings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>красть</i> (krastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pro-Kremlin (supporting the policies and actions of the government of the Russian Federation)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>прокремлі́вський</i> (prokremlívsʹkyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout, to cry out, to yell</li><li>to shout <i>(for a certain time)</i>, to scream</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to trumpet, to tout</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>крича́ть</i> (kričátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over</li><li>to play back, to replay</li><li>to scroll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put across, to put through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get turned over, to make a quick gain</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning</li><li>scroll, scrolling</li></ol><p><i>прокрути́ть</i> (prokrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn over</li><li>to play back, to replay</li><li>to scroll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put across, to put through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get turned over, to make a quick gain</li></ol><p><i>прокрути́ть</i> (prokrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proxemics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>proxemics</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) proxy, representative</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) proxy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>proxy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) procurator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>prōcūrātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) Prosecutor's Office, prosecution</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) procuratorate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill with tobacco smoke, to smoke up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend on smoking</li></ol><p><i>прокури́ть</i> (prokurítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill with tobacco smoke, to smoke up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend on smoking</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) prosecutor, district attorney (a lawyer who decides whether to charge a person with a crime)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>public prosecutor's</li></ol><p><i>прокуро́р</i> (prokurór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) pro-lifer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pro-lifer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through, to penetrate, to pierce, to make a hole in</li><li>to fracture (one's skull)</li></ol><p>From <i>проломи́ть</i> (prolomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or <i>пролома́ть</i> (prolomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to run</li></ol><p><i>проле́г-</i> (prolég-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) prolegomenon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>προλεγόμενον</i> (prolegómenon, “that which is said beforehand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie <i>(for a certain time)</i></li><li>to remain <i>(for a certain time)</i></li><li>to get a bedsore <i>(on)</i></li><li>to cause to sink/sag down <i>(by lying on it)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лежа́ть</i> (ležátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) pressure ulcer, bedsore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get through, to wriggle through, to climb through, to squeeze through</li><li>to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to worm oneself <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>проле́зть</i> (proléztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get through, to wriggle through, to climb through, to squeeze through</li><li>to go <i>(through)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to worm oneself <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble, to murmur</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лепета́ть</i> (lepetátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to squills.</li><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) Pertaining to or characteristic of the subfamily Scilloideae.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;taxonomy) Any plant of the subfamily Scilloideae.</li></ol><p>From <i>проле́ска</i> (proléska, “squill”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of terrain that is characterized by forest plots interleaved with fields and meadows</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proletariat (working class or lower class)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>prolétariat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proletarianization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proletarian, worker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>prōlētārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proletarian, (<i>relational</i>) proletariat</li></ol><p><i>пролета́рий</i> (proletárij) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(by, past, through)</i>, to fly <i>(over)</i></li><li>to overfly, to overshoot</li><li>to cover, to pass rapidly</li><li><i>(time)</i> to fly by</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be passed over <i>(for)</i>, to miss the mark, to miss the boat</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly <i>(by, past, through)</i>, to fly <i>(over)</i></li><li>to overfly, to overshoot</li><li>to cover, to pass rapidly</li><li><i>(time)</i> to fly by</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be passed over <i>(for)</i>, to miss the mark, to miss the boat</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to run</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strait, channel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to shed <i>(light, tears, blood)</i></li></ol><p><i>проли́ть</i> (prolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shed light on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative)]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li></ol><p><i>пролива́ть</i> (prolivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pouring, torrential (<i>of rain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пролива́ть</i> (prolivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leaf through, to flip through, to scroll through (a book, manuscript, text, etc.)</li><li>to skim (without carefully reading) (a book, manuscript, text, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>листа́ть</i> (listátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leaf through, to flip through, to scroll through (a book, manuscript, text, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skim (without carefully reading) (a book, manuscript, text, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пролиста́ть</i> (prolistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to shed <i>(light, tears, blood)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li></ol><p><i>проли́ть</i> (prolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) synaxarion, synaxarium</li><li>prologue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(a road, pipe etc.)</i> to build, to construct, to lay</li><li>to clear</li><li>to map</li><li>to interlay <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breach, gap, break</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through/penetrate in stages</li><li>to spend time breaking</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through, to penetrate, to pierce, to make a hole in</li><li>to fracture (one's skull)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bald spot (e.g. on a head)</li><li>an empty spot (e.g. in a forest or field)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie <i>(a certain time)</i>, to spend a certain time somewhere lying</li></ol><p><i>пролежа́ть</i> (proležátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight, flyby</li><li>migration (of bird)</li><li>aperture, bay, aisle (open space between anything)</li><li>gap, span (distance between adjacent members in any structure or device)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) space between any two balusters of a flight of stairs</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) section of railway track between stations or stops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear, to plaster, to anoint, to grease, to oil (<i>properly, thoroughly; deep into</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss (the target)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oiled, impregnated with oil</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) greasy, oily (dirty and stained with grease or oil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(when shooting or throwing)</i> miss</li><li>blunder, slip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss <i>(one's aim)</i>, to miss the mark, to fail to hit, to miscue</li><li>to miss the mark, to be wide of the mark</li></ol><p><i>промахну́ться</i> (promaxnútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss <i>(one's aim)</i>, to miss the mark, to fail to hit, to miscue</li><li>to miss the mark, to be wide of the mark</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) blunder, mistake</li></ol><p><i>прома́х</i> (promáx) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procrastination, protraction, delay (act of postponing, delaying or putting off)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) between, among</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>меж</i> (mež)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) perineum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gap, interval, period, space, stretch, span (a distance in space)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>межу́ток</i> (mežútok). Cognate with Czech <i>mezera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intermediate</li></ol><p><i>промежу́ток</i> (promežútok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glimpse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flash, to fly by, to flit by</li><li>to be perceptible</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мелькну́ть</i> (melʹknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange <i>(for)</i>, to trade <i>(for)</i>, to barter <i>(for)</i>, to truck <i>(for)</i></li><li>to change <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>променя́ть</i> (promenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange <i>(for)</i>, to trade <i>(for)</i>, to barter <i>(for)</i>, to truck <i>(for)</i></li><li>to change <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>survey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze through, to be frozen solid</li><li>to be chilled through</li></ol><p><i>промёрз(нуть)</i> (promjórz(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promethium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industrial zone</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>промы́шленная з́она</i> (promýšlennaja źona).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>per mille, per mil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promiscuity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promotional video</li></ol><p>Possibly from English <i>promo video</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piercingly cold and damp</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мо́зглый</i> (mózglyj, “rotten; damp (<i>of weather</i>)”), from <i>мзга</i> (mzga, “rot, mold, damp weather”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъzga</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>μύδος</i> (múdos, “dampness, rot”), Old Irish <i>mosach</i> (“unclean”), Low German <i>mussig</i> (“dirty”), Latvian <i>mudas</i> (“rotten algae”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravine, scour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wet/soaked, to be soaking, to be sopping wet</li><li>to let water <i>(through)</i>, to not be impervious to water, to not be waterproof</li><li>to blot</li></ol><p><i>промо́кнуть</i> (promóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>промокну́ть</i> (promoknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) blotting paper (absorbent paper used to dry ink)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get wet/soaked, to be soaking, to be sopping wet</li><li>to let water <i>(through)</i>, to not be impervious to water, to not be waterproof</li><li>to blot</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мо́кнуть</i> (móknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to say, to declare</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лвить</i> (mólvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep silent, to say nothing</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;commerce,&#32;marketing) promoter (one who promotes)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>promoter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;commerce,&#32;marketing) promotion (dissemination of positive information)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>promotion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;proscribed</i>) alternative form of <i>промо́ушен</i> (promóušen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to drench, to soak</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мочить</i> (močitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industrial-financial plan of an enterprise, drawn up for a certain period of time, setting economic production and fiscal management goals</li></ol><p>From <i>промы́шленно-</i> (promýšlenno-, “industrial”) +&lrm; <i>фина́нсовый</i> (finánsovyj, “financial”) +&lrm; <i>план</i> (plan, “plan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear/ sweep <i>(by, past, through)</i></li><li><i>(time)</i> to fly by, to frit by</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мча́ться</i> (mčátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablution, abstersion, douche, irrigation, lavement</li></ol><p><i>промыва́ть</i> (promyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain-washing, brainwash</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>brain-washing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash <i>(well/properly/thoroughly)</i></li><li>to rinse, to bathe, to irrigate</li><li>to jig</li><li>to wash out, to wash away, to make <i>(a hole, ravine, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>промы́ть</i> (promýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ablution, sluice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obtainment of resources (<i>usually wild animals, birds, fish</i>); hunting, fishing</li><li>small-scale handicraft production (<i>pottery, forging, etc.</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) obtainment of something through ill-gotten means</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) industrial mining enterprise</li><li>providence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to get, to obtain</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to worry about (someone or something)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commercial</li><li>producer's</li><li>sold commercially</li></ol><p><i>про́мысел</i> (prómysel) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash <i>(well/properly/thoroughly)</i></li><li>to rinse, to bathe, to irrigate</li><li>to jig</li><li>to wash out, to wash away, to make <i>(a hole, ravine, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moo</li><li>to bellow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mumble</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мыча́ть</i> (myčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industrialist (person involved in the ownership or management of an industrial enterprise)</li><li>professional hunter and fur trader</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) handicraftsman</li></ol><p>From <i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>industry</li></ol><p><i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>industrial</li></ol><p>From the retroflexion of <i>промы́сленный</i> (promýslennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production piece, industrial standard</li><li>(<i>law</i>) industrial design (<i>as an object of intellectual property law</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Industrial Revolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a living <i>(by)</i>, to support oneself <i>(with)</i></li><li>to hunt</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to worry about</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-мышля́ть</i> (-myšljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mumble</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мя́млить</i> (mjámlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freeze through, to be frozen solid</li><li>to be chilled through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>мёрзнуть</i> (mjórznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry <i>(by, past, through)</i>, to smuggle, to sneak</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial)</li><li>to carry with one <i>(for a length of time)</i></li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to cause (bad) diarrhea</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush by/past/through, to fly</li></ol><p><i>пронести́</i> (pronestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, to stab, to impale</li><li>to run through</li></ol><p><i>пронзи́ть</i> (pronzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shrilly, piercingly, stridently</li><li>poignantly, stirringly</li></ol><p><i>пронзи́тельный</i> (pronzítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piercing, penetrating</li><li>shrill, strident</li></ol><p><i>пронзи́ть</i> (pronzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, to stab, to impale</li><li>to run through</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>проньзити</i> (pronĭziti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pronьziti</i>, from <i>&#42;pro-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;nьziti</i>. Related to <i>вонзи́ть</i> (vonzítʹ), <i>низа́ть</i> (nizátʹ) and <i>нож</i> (nož).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce <i>(through)</i>, to transpierce</li><li>to penetrate, to permeate</li><li>to run <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>низа́ть</i> (nizátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce <i>(through)</i>, to transpierce</li><li>to penetrate, to permeate</li><li>to run <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>прониза́ть</i> (pronizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raw, bitter (wind, cold)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) piercing, sharp (look, glance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penetration (act of penetrating)</li></ol><p><i>проника́ть</i> (pronikátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate</li><li>to permeate</li><li>to infiltrate</li><li>to perforate</li></ol><p><i>прони́кнуть</i> (proníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imbued <i>(with)</i>, to imbue one's mind <i>(with)</i>, to be filled <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>проника́ть</i> (pronikátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penetration (act of penetrating), infiltration</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) insight</li><li>sincerity, heartfeltness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with emotion, with conviction, sincerely</li></ol><p><i>проникнове́нны</i> (proniknovénny) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>penetrating, piercing</li><li>heartfelt, sincere</li></ol><p><i>проникнове́н(ие)</i> (proniknovén(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imbued, inspired, full</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to penetrate</li><li>to permeate</li><li>to infiltrate</li><li>to perforate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imbued <i>(with)</i>, to imbue one's mind <i>(with)</i>, to be filled <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>прони́кнуть</i> (proníknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to penetrate (<i>of the cold, the wind, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to impress, to make a big impression on</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permeability, penetrability, perviousness, pellucidity</li></ol><p><i>проница́емый</i> (pronicájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perspicacity, insight, acumen (the human faculty or power to mentally grasp or understand clearly)</li></ol><p><i>проница́тельный</i> (pronicátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perspicacious, acute, shrewd, astute</li></ol><p><i>проница́ть</i> (pronicátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to penetrate, to permeate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to guess, to understand, to comprehend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>проницати</i> (pronicati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nicati</i> (“to arise”). Related to <i>проника́ть</i> (pronikátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry (something through something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to cause (bad) diarrhea</li><li>to carry, to have</li><li>to wear out</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot/sweep past, to rush/scud past</li><li>to fly by, to blow over</li><li>to spread <i>(quickly)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to number, to enumerate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>нумерова́ть</i> (numerovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slicker</li></ol><p>Most likely borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пронꙑръ</i> (pronyrŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniff out; to get wind of (to hear about&#59; to learn of, especially with respect to facts intended to have been kept confidential or secret)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ню́хать</i> (njúxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniff out; to get wind of (to hear about&#59; to learn of, especially with respect to facts intended to have been kept confidential or secret)</li></ol><p><i>проню́хать</i> (pronjúxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nurse, to fuss over (for a while)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ня́нчить</i> (njánčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to penetrate (<i>of the cold, the wind, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to impress, to make a big impression on</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prototype</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to operate on (someone)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>опери́ровать</i> (operírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to complain, to whinge, to say "ох" for a while</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>о́хать</i> (óxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propaganda</li><li>promotion, advocacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promotion</li></ol><p><i>пропаганди́ровать</i> (propagandírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propagandize, to advocate</li><li>to popularize, to promote</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propagandist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) propaganda</li><li>propagandistic</li></ol><p><i>пропаганди́ст</i> (propagandíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) propagandist</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>пропаганди́ст</i> (propagandíst) +&lrm; <i>гандо́н</i> (gandón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get lost, to be done</li><li>to disappear, to vanish</li><li>to lose, to fail</li><li>to perish, to die</li><li>to be wasted</li><li>to fall over some time, to fall for a while</li></ol><p><i>пропад-</i> (propad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>[прожечь]</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to weed (a garden, etc.)</li><li>to pull up (weeds)</li></ol><p><i>прополо́ть</i> (propolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) propane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>проппа́нт</i> (proppánt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>precipice, abyss, chasm</li><li>lots, tons</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get lost, to be done</li><li>to disappear, to vanish</li><li>to lose, to fail</li><li>to perish, to die</li><li>to be wasted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;propastь</i>.</p><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ), from Proto-Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough, to plow, to cultivate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work <i>(through)</i>, to slog away <i>(at)</i>, to plough/plow one's way <i>(through)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough, to plow, to cultivate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to work <i>(through)</i>, to slog away <i>(at)</i>, to plough/plow one's way <i>(through)</i></li><li>to smell of, to become permeated with the smell of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stink as a result of having spoiled</li></ol><p><i>пропаха́ть</i> (propaxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>пропа́хнуть</i> (propáxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell of, to become permeated with the smell of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start to stink due to having spoiled</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>па́хнуть</i> (páxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propeller, airscrew, propulsive screw (mechanical device used to propel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) propeller, airscrew</li></ol><p><i>пропе́ллер</i> (propéller) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing</li><li>to sing <i>(for some time)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend/squander on drink</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ruin through drinking</li></ol><p><i>пропи́ть</i> (propítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw cut, cut, saw kerf, kerf, saw notch, sawcut, slit</li><li>propyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to prescribe (medicine)</li><li>to register (tenants, documentation)</li><li>to record (information)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propiska, residence permit, (mandatory) registration (<i>USSR</i>)</li><li>the stamp in the Soviet <i>internal passport</i> (serving as a personal ID) with the registered address.</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>), place where one lives (as stated in the stamp)</li></ol><p><i>прописа́ть</i> (propisátʹ, “to register”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital letter (upper-case letter A B C (Latin) or А Б В (Cyrillic), etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conventional, trivial, commonplace, common</li><li>upper-case, capital (letter)</li><li>cursive (letter)</li></ol><p><i>про́пись</i> (própisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to prescribe (medicine)</li><li>to register (tenants, documentation)</li><li>to record (information)</li></ol><p><i>прописа́ть</i> (propisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prescription</li><li>(dated,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) cursive sample sheet</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) cliche, platitude</li><li>handwriting among printed text</li><li>font imitating handwriting, such as true italics</li><li>(<i>painting</i>) preliminary drawing on canvas, used to guide the finished painting</li></ol><p><i>прописа́ть</i> (propisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional) subsistence (something that is required to stay alive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsistence</li></ol><p><i>пропита́ть</i> (propitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, to nourish</li><li>to impregnate <i>(with)</i>, to saturate <i>(with)</i>, to soak <i>(in)</i>, to steep <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imbued, to be saturated, to be soaked</li><li>to be impregnated</li></ol><p><i>пропита́ть</i> (propitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to impregnate <i>(with)</i>, to saturate <i>(with)</i>, to soak <i>(in)</i>, to steep <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>пропита́ть</i> (propitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imbued, to be saturated, to be soaked</li><li>to be impregnated</li></ol><p><i>пропи́тывать</i> (propítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend/squander on drink</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ruin through drinking</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim by, to float by, to sail by</li><li>to swim (a certain distance or time), to sail (a certain distance or time)</li></ol><p><i>проплы́ть</i> (proplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim by, to float by, to sail by</li><li>to swim (a certain distance or time), to sail (a certain distance or time)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) to preach, to sermonize</li><li>to propagate, to advocate</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пове́дать</i> (povédatʹ), derived from <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preacher</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>проповѣдьникъ</i> (propovědĭnikŭ, “herald, messenger”). By surface analysis, <i>пропове́дать</i> (propovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) preaching; preacher's</li><li>preachy, predicant, homiletic</li></ol><p><i>пропове́дник</i> (propovédnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preaching</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) to preach, to sermonize</li><li>to propagate, to advocate</li></ol><p><i>пропове́дать</i> (propovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sermon, homily</li><li>propagation (of ideas), advocacy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>проповѣдь</i> (propovědĭ) or <i>пропове́дать</i> (propovédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;propovědь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) proposition (<i>generally</i>)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) proposition</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prōpositiō</i> (“proposition”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunk, drunkard (person who is habitually drunk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, to crawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crawl/creep along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(rumor etc)</i> to spread</li><li>to spend time crawling</li></ol><p><i>проползти́</i> (propolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>по́лзать</i> (pólzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, to crawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crawl/creep along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(rumor etc)</i> to spread</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propolis, bee glue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weeding</li></ol><p><i>прополо́ть</i> (propolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weed (a garden, etc.)</li><li>to pull up (weeds)</li><li>to weed (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>поло́ть</i> (polótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>proportionally, pro rata (in proportion)</li></ol><p><i>пропорциона́льный</i> (proporcionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proportionality (the property of being proportional)</li></ol><p><i>пропорциона́льный</i> (proporcionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proportional, proportionate</li><li>well-proportioned</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>proportionālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proportion</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prōportiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) propofol</li></ol><p>Possible borrowed from English <i>propofol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proppant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>proppant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entry permit, pass</li><li>omission</li><li>gap, blank</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>пропуска́ть</i> (propuskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) passing, transmission</li></ol><p><i>пропуска́ть</i> (propuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let pass in, to let pass through, to admit</li><li>to run through, to pass through</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li><li>to miss, to fail to attend, to let slip</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to turn a deaf ear (to something), to ignore</li></ol><p>Literally, “to let go past one's ears”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) throughput</li><li>access, pass</li></ol><p><i>про́пуск</i> (própusk) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let pass in, to let pass through, to admit</li><li>to run through, to pass through</li><li>to omit, to leave out</li><li>to miss, to skip, to fail to attend, to let slip</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to turn a deaf ear (to something)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to let go past one's ears”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel (for some time)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>путеше́ствовать</i> (putešéstvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) pro-Putin</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreman, leadman, ganger (leader of a work crew)</li></ol><p>Possibly, acronym of <i>производи́тель рабо́т</i> (proizvodítelʹ rabót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to master (<i>in detail</i>), to study (<i>in detail</i>), to work through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discuss</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare (a role, scene, etc.) for execution</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish the details of (a painting or sculpture)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to think through, to work out the details of</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to chew out, to criticize harshly</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to work (<i>for a while, for some time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прорабо́тать</i> (prorabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to master (<i>in detail</i>), to study (<i>in detail</i>), to work through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to discuss</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare (a role, scene, etc.) for execution</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to finish the details of (a painting or sculpture)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to think through, to work out the details of</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to chew out, to criticize harshly</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to work (<i>for a while, for some time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) study, examination</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) criticism, slating, talking-to, tongue-lashing</li><li>work on the details, elaboration</li></ol><p><i>прорабо́тать</i> (prorabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to germinate, to sprout, to shoot</li></ol><p><i>прорасти́</i> (prorastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to germinate, to sprout, to shoot</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) large amount</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) chasm, abyss</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) new channel dug by a river</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) river whirlpool, deep place in a river</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) dank place, pit in a swamp, ravine with water</li></ol><p>From <i>прорва́ть</i> (prorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), with stress shift. Compare Ukrainian <i>прі́рва</i> (prírva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break through</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burst open</li><li>to force one’s way</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burst open, to break</li><li>to force one's way through, to break through</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tear (e.g. of clothing)</li></ol><p><i>прорва́ть</i> (prorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) to thin out</li><li>to resample down, subsample, open</li></ol><p><i>прореди́ть</i> (proredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut, slit, kerfing, perforation, tear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>проре́зать</i> (prorézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cut through (<i>of teeth</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прореза́ть</i> (prorezátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>проре́зать</i> (prorézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прореза́ть</i> (prorezátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>проре́зать</i> (prorézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut, opening, slit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prorector, vice-chancellor (<i>in a university</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rent, tear, slit, hole</li><li>lapse, gap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to review, to give feedback on</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>рецензи́ровать</i> (recenzírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophecy, inspired prediction</li></ol><p><i>прорица́ть</i> (proricátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diviner</li><li>augur (diviner who foretells events by unusual occurrences)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prorokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>проро́к</i> (prorók).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to utter, to breathe, to shed</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-рони́ть</i> (-ronítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pro-Russian</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prophetic, oracular</li></ol><p><i>проро́к</i> (prorók) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophecy</li></ol><p><i>проро́к</i> (prorók) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prophesy</li></ol><p><i>проро́к</i> (prorók) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut through</li><li>to cut (a passage, swath, etc.) through trees</li></ol><p><i>проруби́ть</i> (prorubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut through (<i>with an axe</i>)</li><li>to cut (a passage, swath, etc.) through trees</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice hole, hole in the ice (usually artificial)</li></ol><p><i>проруби́ть</i> (prorubítʹ, “to cut through (with an ax or pickaxe)”) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blunder, failure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>break, bursting</li><li>breakthrough, breach</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>прорыва́ть</i> (proryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through</li><li>to tear</li><li>to burst open</li><li>to force one’s way</li><li>to dig through, to dig across</li><li>to burrow through, to burrow across</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>проры́ть</i> (prorýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to burst open, to break</li><li>to force one's way through, to break through</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tear (e.g. of clothing)</li></ol><p><i>прорыва́ть</i> (proryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig through, to dig across</li><li>to burrow through, to burrow across</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to growl, to snarl (to make a low growling or rumbling animal noise)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to speak rudely, to growl</li><li>to growl, to snarl (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>рыча́ть</i> (ryčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) rope-making machine</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) an unpleasant, awkward or embarrassing situation</li></ol><p>Either <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сак</i> (sak) or deverbal from <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to percolate, to leak, to ooze, to seep out, to exudate, to soak, to trickle <i>(through)</i>, to filter</li><li>to filter <i>(into)</i>, to infiltrate <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>просочи́ться</i> (prosočítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clear space, ray of light <i>(in the clouds)</i></li><li>ray of hope</li><li><i>(on shoulder strap)</i> space</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) gap</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) bay, aperture, opening</li><li>transparency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enlightener</li><li>educator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>educational, enlightening</li></ol><p><i>просвети́ть</i> (prosvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельский</i> (-telʹskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine light through (something) (<i>e.g. using an X-ray or backlight</i>)</li><li>to shine for an amount of time (<i>e.g. of the sun</i>)</li><li>to enlighten, to educate, to illuminate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>просвѣтити</i> (prosvětiti). By surface analysis, <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To acquire knowledge or become cultured.</li><li>To attain enlightenment; to enlighten oneself.</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>ся</i> (sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enlightenment</li><li>clarification</li></ol><p><i>просветли́ть</i> (prosvetlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear up</li><li><i>(person)</i> to brighten up</li><li><i>(conscience, mind etc)</i> to get/become lucid</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>светле́ть</i> (svetlétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clarify</li><li>to elucidate</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) or perhaps from <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>светли́ть</i> (svetlítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clarify</li><li>to elucidate</li></ol><p><i>просветли́ть</i> (prosvetlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright</li><li>serene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to X-ray</li><li>to be translucent, to be transparent</li><li>to appear through, to show trough, to be seen through, to be visible through</li></ol><p><i>просвети́ть</i> (prosvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlighten, to educate</li></ol><p><i>просвети́ть</i> (prosvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enlightenment; education</li></ol><p><i>просвети́ть</i> (prosvetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) enlightenment; enlightened</li></ol><p><i>просвеще́ние</i> (prosveščénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enlightened, educated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosphora (a holy bread; a small loaf of leavened bread used for communion in the Russian Orthodox Church. It usually consists of two layers, symbolizing the dual nature of Christ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman who bakes prosphora</li></ol><p><i>просви́р(а)</i> (prosvír(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to make a short whistling sound, to whistle for a short time</li><li>to whistle (a musical piece)</li><li>to whizz, to whistle while flying by</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fritter away, to waste</li><li>to whistle (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to penetrate, to blow through (<i>of strong winds</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>свиста́ть</i> (svistátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to make a short whistling sound, to whistle for a short time</li><li>to whistle (a musical piece)</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to whizz, to whistle while flying by</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fritter away, to waste</li><li>to whistle (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle (a musical piece)</li><li>to whizz, to whistle while flying by</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fritter away, to waste</li></ol><p>From <i>просвисте́ть</i> (prosvistétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>просвиста́ть</i> (prosvistátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>graying hair or fur</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) + a combining form of <i>седе́ть</i> (sedétʹ, “to turn gray”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glade, clearing</li><li>cleared path in a forest</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сек-</i> (sek-) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a). Doublet of <i>просе́кко</i> (prosékko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prosecco (grape and wine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Prosecco</i>. Doublet of <i>про́сека</i> (próseka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signal, to indicate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to warn</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сигнализи́ровать</i> (signalizírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to signal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to honk (a horn)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сигна́лить</i> (signálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit (for a while)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be imprisoned, to do time (for some time)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit (for some time), to sit up (for some time), to spend one's time in a sitting position, to spend one's time under confinement, to stay (for some time)</li></ol><p>From <i>просид(е́ть)</i> (prosid(étʹ)) [и → и́; д → ж] + <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) January</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prosìnьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>про́синь</i> (prósinʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue tint or admixture with blue; patches of blue in the color of something</li><li>the blue of the sky passing between the clouds</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>синь</i> (sinʹ), second element from <i>си́ний</i> (sínij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say hoarsely</li><li>to be hoarse for a while</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сипе́ть</i> (sipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask for, to request [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘for something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘for something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что́бы</b> (štóby, + past-tense clause) <small>‘for someone to do something’</small>]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to be a beggar</li><li>to invite, to call [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to make a fuss, to stand up (for), to speak up, to petition, to plead [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>просити</i> (prositi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prositi</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;práśīˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preḱ-</i>. Distantly related to English <i>pray</i> and <i>precarious</i>.</p><p>Indo-European cognates include Persian پرسیدن (porsidan), Northern Kurdish <i>pirsîn</i>, Sanskrit <i>पृच्छति</i> (pṛccháti), Armenian <i>հարց</i> (harcʻ), Latin <i>posco</i> and Old English <i>frignan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li><li>to long <i>(for)</i>, to yearn <i>(for)</i>, to thirst <i>(for)</i></li><li>to ask <i>(+ to passive)</i>, to cry out <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(sun)</i> to come out, to begin to shine, to shine</li><li>to brighten <i>(with)</i>, to light up <i>(with)</i>, to beam <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to dart, to dash, to fly, to shoot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(past)</i>, to leave <i>(behind)</i>, to cross, to make it <i>(across)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss, to overshoot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall <i>(through, between)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip in, to sneak in, to slip <i>(into)</i>, to sneak <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage to become <i>(someone)</i>, to manage to get <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make it <i>(through)</i>, to manage, to cope <i>(with)</i>, to hurdle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip by, to pass quickly</li></ol><p><i>проскок-</i> (proskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, to sneak, to slip</li><li>to slip out, to be discernible</li></ol><p><i>проскользну́ть</i> (proskolʹznútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to decline</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>склоня́ть</i> (sklonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, to sneak, to slip</li><li>to slip out, to be discernible</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to dart, to dash, to fly, to shoot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go <i>(past)</i>, to leave <i>(behind)</i>, to cross, to make it <i>(across)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss, to overshoot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall <i>(through, between)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip in, to sneak in, to slip <i>(into)</i>, to sneak <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage to become <i>(someone)</i>, to manage to get <i>(somewhere)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make it <i>(through)</i>, to manage, to cope <i>(with)</i>, to hurdle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip by, to pass quickly</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>скочи́ть</i> (skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creak, to screech, to squeak</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>скрипе́ть</i> (skripétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify, to sing the praises of</li><li>to make famous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander, to defame</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сла́вить</i> (slávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become famous <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) to become notorious <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>просла́вить</i> (proslávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glorification, apotheosis (exaltation&#59; crediting someone with extraordinary power or status)</li><li>blazon</li></ol><p><i>просла́вить</i> (proslávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famous, illustrious, celebrated, renowned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прославля́ть</i> (proslavljátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>просла́вить</i> (proslávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glorify, to sing the praises of</li><li>to make famous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slander, to defame</li></ol><p><i>просла́вить</i> (proslávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spy <i>(on)</i>, to track</li><li>to trail, to follow</li><li>to trace, to retrace (to track the development of)</li><li>to observe, to see to (e.g. "to see to it that..."), to make sure</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proceed, to go/pass in state</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spy <i>(on)</i>, to track</li><li>to trail, to follow</li><li>to trace, to retrace (to track the development of)</li><li>to observe, to see to (it), to make sure</li></ol><p><i>проследи́ть</i> (prosledítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shed a few tears, to have tears in one's eyes</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>слези́ться</i> (slezítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layer</li><li>stratum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>for a certain time</i> to serve, to work, to be in the services</li><li>to be in use</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to listen to from start to finish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fail to hear, to miss (someone's words), to not catch (what is said)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audition (performance by an aspiring performer)</li><li>listening (act of listening)</li></ol><p><i>прослу́шивать</i> (proslúšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear, to listen to</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss, to not to catch <i>(what was said)</i></li><li>to audition (<i>of actors or performers</i>)</li><li>to tap, to listen in</li></ol><p><i>прослу́шать</i> (proslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eavesdropping, bugging, (phone) interception</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bug, wire (electronic listening device)</li></ol><p><i>прослу́шать</i> (proslúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass (for), to have the reputation (for), to be said / reputed</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>слыть</i> (slytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to learn, to hear from rumors [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>про</b> (pro, + accusative) <small>‘about something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look over, to look through, to glance over, to run over</li><li>to view, to watch</li><li>to check, to review, to go <i>(over)</i></li><li><i>(file)</i> to view, <i>(directory, web page)</i> to browse</li></ol><p><i>просмотре́ть</i> (prosmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be visible, to be in/within view</li><li>to be noticeable/discernible/perceptible</li></ol><p><i>просма́тривать</i> (prosmátrivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viewing, browsing, examination, review, revision</li><li>oversight</li><li>lookup, scan</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>просмотре́ть</i> (prosmotrétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look over, to look through, to glance over, to run over</li><li>to view, to watch</li><li>to check, to review, to go <i>(over)</i></li><li><i>(file)</i> to view, <i>(directory, web page)</i> to browse</li><li>to overlook, to miss</li><li>to neglect, to fail to take proper care <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awake, woken up (conscious)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to awake, to wake up</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet (grain)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>просо</i> (proso), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;proso</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>про́са</i> (prósa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/push through/in, to force through, to shove through/in, to thrust through/in, to pass through/in</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-со́вывать</i> (-sóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;poetry) prosodic</li></ol><p><i>просо́дия</i> (prosódija, “prosody”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “-ic”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;poetry) prosody</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>προσῳδία</i> (prosōidía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to percolate, to leak, to ooze, to seep out, to exudate, to soak, to trickle <i>(through)</i>, to filter</li><li>to filter <i>(into)</i>, to infiltrate <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сочи́ться</i> (sočítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oversleep, to sleep in</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prospekt; avenue (broad street)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) prospectus, outline</li><li>booklet, folder, advertisement</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Prospekt</i>, from Latin <i>prōspectus</i> (“view, sight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to conjugate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>спряга́ть</i> (sprjagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let expire</li></ol><p><i>просро́чить</i> (prosróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let expire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delinquency, delay, missing of a deadline (<i>especially for payment of a debt</i>)</li></ol><p><i>просро́чить</i> (prosróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) clipping of <i>про́сто</i> (prósto); just</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter (data or information)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enter (data or information)</li></ol><p><i>проста́вить</i> (prostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to stay (for some time)</li><li>to stand idle</li><li>to stand, to remain (for buildings)</li></ol><p><i>простоя́ть</i> (prostojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simpleton (simple person lacking common sense)</li></ol><p>From <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostate, prostate gland</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prostata</i>, itself from Ancient Greek <i>προστάτης</i> (prostátēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the part of a wall between a window and a door, or between two windows</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) partition, bulkhead</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стена́</i> (stená) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>просто́й</i> (prostój, “simple”)</li></ol><p><i>прост(о́й)</i> (prost(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend, to hold/reach <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prosterti</i> (first singular <i>&#42;prostьrǫ</i>), composed of <i>&#42;pro-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;sterti</i>, cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्तृणाति</i> (stṛṇā́ti, “to sprinkle, throw”), <i>स्तृणोति</i> (stṛṇṓti), <i>स्तरति</i> (stárati), Avestan <i>𐬯𐬙𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (stara<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “to stretch out”), Latvian <i>stirinât</i> (“to move, shake”), Ancient Greek <i>στόρνῡμι</i> (stórnūmi, “to spread”), <i>στορέννῡμι</i> (storénnūmi), <i>στρώννῡμι</i> (strṓnnūmi), Latin <i>sternō</i> (“to spread out”). Past passive participle <i>&#42;prostьrtъ</i> is an exact cognate of Avestan <i>𐬟𐬭𐬀𐬯𐬙𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬀</i> (frastər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>ta), and base form <i>&#42;stьrtъ</i> is an exact cognate of Sanskrit <i>स्तृत</i> (stṛtá), Ancient Greek <i>στρωτός</i> (strōtós), Latin <i>strātus</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>прострѣти</i> (prostrěti) (first singular <i>простьрѫ</i> (prostĭrǫ)), Serbo-Croatian <i>про̀стриjети</i> (first singular <i>про̏стриjе̄м</i>), Slovene <i>prostrẹ́ti</i> (first singular <i>prostrèm</i>), Czech <i>prostříti</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to reach, to range</li></ol><p><i>простере́ть</i> (prosterétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simpleton</li><li>(<i>Harry Potter universe</i>) Muggle</li></ol><p>From <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) basic, simple, modest</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simple, easygoing</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>sorry</li><li>excuse me</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (Lord) forgive me (for saying this) (used to express recognition of the rudeness or abruptness of something being said)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (Lord) forgive me (for saying this) (used to express an ironic or sarcastic attitude to something being said)</li></ol><p>Literally, “forgive (me) Lord”.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: прости Господи)</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>прости́ го́споди</i> (prostí góspodi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stimulate (to encourage into action)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стимули́ровать</i> (stimulírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend, to hold/reach <i>(out)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wash</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-стира́ть</i> (-stirátʹ), imperfective equivalent of <i>-стере́ть</i> (-sterétʹ) (see <i>простере́ть</i> (prosterétʹ)); compare <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ), imperfective equivalent of <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ) in prefixed verbs.</p><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to stretch, to reach, to range</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wash oneself well</li></ol><p><i>простира́ть</i> (prostirátʹ, “to stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>простира́ть</i> (prostirátʹ, “to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wash</li></ol><p><i>простира́ть</i> (prostirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>excusably, forgivably</li></ol><p><i>прости́тельный</i> (prostítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excusable, pardonable, forgivable, venial</li></ol><p><i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whore, prostitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitution (having sex for profit)</li></ol><p>From French <i>prostitution</i>, itself from Latin <i>prostitutio</i>. Attested since the 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excuse, to forgive, to pardon</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>простити</i> (prostiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prostiti</i>. Cognate to простой.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say goodbye <i>(to)</i>, to take (one's) leave (of), to bid adieu, to bid farewell</li></ol><p><i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>simply, plainly, just</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just like that; for no reason</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simply, just</li></ol><p><i>про́сто</i> (prósto) +&lrm; <i>на</i> (na) +&lrm; <i>про́сто</i> (prósto)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rustic, naive, unsophisticated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupid</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat mediocre, not great</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simpleness, naïveté, innocence, simple-heartedness, open-heartedness</li></ol><p>From <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>artlessly, simply</li></ol><p><i>простоду́шный</i> (prostodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>openhearted, simple-hearted, simple-minded, artless, unsophisticated</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elementary substance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>number theory</i>) prime number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, ordinary</li><li>basic</li><li>(<i>number theory</i>) prime</li><li>(<i>classification</i>) simple, simplex</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standstill, stoppage, idle time, downtime</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>простъ</i> (prostŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prostъ</i>.</p><p>From <i>проста́ивать</i> (prostáivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>просто́й, как три копе́йки</i> (prostój, kak tri kopéjki)</li></ol><p>Literally, “simple, like five kopecks”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dumb as a rock, simple, dumb, unintelligent (about a person)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) simple, easy, uncomplicated (about an affair)</li></ol><p>Literally, “simple, like three kopecks”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>просто́й, как три копе́йки</i> (prostój, kak tri kopéjki)</li></ol><p>Literally, “simple, like three roubles”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostokvasha, (thick) soured milk, sour clotted milk, amasi (sour milk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commoner</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>простълюдинъ</i> (prostŭljudinŭ), from <i>про́стъ</i> (próstŭ, “simple, ordinary”) and <i>людинъ</i> (ljudinŭ, “free man”); see <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) and <i>лю́ди</i> (ljúdi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan, to moan <i>(for a certain time)</i></li><li>to utter a groan/moan</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стона́ть</i> (stonátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open space, expanse</li><li>freedom</li><li>scope, range</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prostorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) An informally spoken idiom that is considered to be non-standard, and associated with the colloquial speech of uneducated urban dwellers. Should not be confused with dialects (<i>говоры</i>), which in Russian are more associated with rural areas.</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój, “ordinary”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>речь</i> (rečʹ, “speech”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;relational) colloquial, popular</li></ol><p><i>просторе́чие</i> (prostoréčije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>spaciously</li><li>loosely, in a loose-fitting manner</li></ol><p><i>просто́рный</i> (prostórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spaciousness, roominess</li></ol><p><i>просто́рный</i> (prostórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spacious, roomy</li><li>loose (not fitting tightly)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prostorьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>просто́р</i> (prostór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Good-natured and sincere; heartfelt.</li></ol><p><i>просто</i> (prosto) +&lrm; <i>сердце</i> (serdce) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simplicity</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dupe, ninny, duffer, klutz</li></ol><p><i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Фи́ля</i> (Fílja), diminutive of <i>Фили́пп</i> (Filípp).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to stay (<i>for some time</i>)</li><li>to stand idle</li><li>to stand, to remain (<i>of buildings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) extensive, vast</li><li>diffuse, verbose, wordy</li></ol><p>Based on the same root as <i>простра́нство</i> (prostránstvo, “space”) + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare also <i>стра́нствие</i> (stránstvije, “wandering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spatial</li></ol><p><i>простра́нство</i> (prostránstvo, “space”) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space (intervening contents of a volume)</li><li>spacing, space, gap, distance between two points</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>пространьство</i> (prostranĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prostornьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>страна́</i> (straná, “country, region”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) namespace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel (for some time)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стра́нствовать</i> (stránstvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prostration (exhaustion, typically from heat or anxiety)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>prōstrātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch</li></ol><p><i>прострочи́ть</i> (prostročítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot through, to pierce with an arrow or bullet</li><li>to rake/sweep with fire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to test-fire (a weapon)</li><li>to spend (some time) shooting</li></ol><p><i>прострели́ть</i> (prostrelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>простреля́ть</i> (prostreljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot through, to pierce with an arrow or bullet</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-стрели́ть</i> (-strelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to test-fire (a weapon)</li><li>to spend (some time) shooting</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take some time building (something), to build (something) over a period of time</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend money building (something)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend money (often too much money) building</li></ol><p><i>простро́ить</i> (prostróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>строчи́ть</i> (stročítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold, common cold, chill (illness)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cold sore, oral herpes</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>студь</i> (studʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studъ</i> (“cold”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let catch cold, to let take/catch a chill</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>студи́ть</i> (studítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch a cold</li></ol><p><i>простуди́ть</i> (prostudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let catch cold, to let take/catch a chill</li></ol><p><i>простуди́ть</i> (prostudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch a cold</li></ol><p><i>простужа́ть</i> (prostužátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become faintly visible</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to exude, to ooze</li><li>to appear (from under of something)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exude, to ooze</li><li>to become faintly visible</li><li>to appear (from under of something)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdemeanour, offense, misdeed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get cold</li><li>to catch a cold</li></ol><p>From <i>просты́ть</i> (prostýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ) or from <i>просты́нуть</i> (prostýnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>просты́ть</i> (prostýtʹ)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сты́нуть</i> (stýnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>простыня́</i> (prostynjá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheet, bedsheet</li><li>(figurative,&#32;chiefly&#32;Internet slang) wall of text</li></ol><p><i>простой</i> (prostoj) +&lrm; <i>-ыня́</i> (-ynjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get cold</li><li>to catch a cold</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>стыть</i> (stytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/push through/in, to force through, to shove through/in, to thrust through/in, to pass through/in</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry, to make dry</li></ol><p><i>просуши́ть</i> (prosušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry, to make dry</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying</li></ol><p><i>просуши́ть</i> (prosušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exist <i>(for a certain time)</i>, to last</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>существова́ть</i> (suščestvovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosphora</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>προσφορά</i> (prosphorá, “offering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to count, to calculate, to work out</li><li>to estimate, to calculate, to consider</li><li>to spend some time calculating</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make an error in counting, to go wrong counting</li><li>to miscalculate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to count, to calculate, to work out</li><li>to estimate, to calculate, to consider</li><li>to spend some time calculating</li></ol><p><i>просчита́ть</i> (prosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make an error in counting, to count wrongly</li><li>to miscalculate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checking</li><li>error, miscalculation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oversleep, to sleep in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss <i>(an opportunity, a chance etc.)</i></li><li>to spill <i>(something solid)</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпа́ть</i> (-sypátʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>просы́пать</i> (prosýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake up, to awaken</li><li>to spill</li></ol><p><i>просыпа́ть</i> (prosypátʹ, “to awaken”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>просы́пать</i> (prosýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>просыпа́ть</i> (prosypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja, “to spill”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>request, entreaty, plea</li><li>petition, application</li><li>(formal,&#32;as an introductory word) it is requested, we ask, please</li></ol><p><i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba). Attested since the mid-16th.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>country road/track, dirt road</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) country road, dirt road</li></ol><p><i>просёлок</i> (prosjólok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protagonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protactinium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thawed patch, glade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push (something) forward, to push (something) through or in</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to push through (an action, decision, matter, case, etc.) (to speed up the implementation of)</li></ol><p><i>протолкну́ть</i> (protolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break through, to smash through (usually with a battering ram)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>таран</i> (taran, “battering ram”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull through, to drag, to trail</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push through</li></ol><p><i>протаск-</i> (protask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull through, to drag, to trail</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protégé</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>protégé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosthesis (artificial replacement for a body part)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prothesis - in phonetics and etymology, a sound at the beginning of the word added for ease of pronunciation: Rus. восемь, восьмой, восемнадцать, восемьдесят &lt; Old East Slavic осьмь, осьмой</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosthetics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protein</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>protéine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;botany) proteaceous (relating to the Proteaceae)</li></ol><p>From <i>протея</i> (proteja, “protea”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluxion, passage, percolation, passing</li></ol><p><i>протека́ть</i> (protekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow, to run</li><li>to leak, to ooze</li><li>to be leaky</li><li>to elapse, to fly</li><li>to progress</li></ol><p><i>проте́к-</i> (proték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>tread</i> of a car tire</li><li>state exercising a protectorate</li><li>(<i>history</i>) protector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protectorate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;politics) protectionism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>protectionnisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nepotism, favoritism</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>prōtectio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) A proteolytic microorganism, a microorganism capable of proteolysis.</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from English <i>proteolytic</i> (adjective).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear through, to wear into holes, to fray, to rub a hole <i>(in)</i></li><li>to wipe, to rub</li><li>to rub through, to grate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push one's way in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push one's way in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protest, remonstrance (formal objection)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Рrоtеst</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Protestant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Protestantism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;religion) Protestant</li></ol><p><i>протеста́нт</i> (protestánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Protestantism</li></ol><p>From <i>протеста́нт</i> (protestánt) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>тести́ровать</i> (tɛstírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) protest</li><li>protesting</li></ol><p><i>проте́ст</i> (protést) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protest, to object, to remonstrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leakage; an oozing out</li></ol><p><i>протек-</i> (protek-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow, to run</li><li>to leak, to ooze</li><li>to be leaky</li><li>to elapse, to fly</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against</li><li>opposite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>противъ</i> (protivŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;protivъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aback (said of sails pressed backward)</li><li>upwind, windward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anticlockwise, counter-clockwise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baking pan, baking tray, sheet pan</li></ol><p>Distortion of German <i>Bratpfanne</i>. Influenced by <i>про́тив</i> (prótiv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose, to object, to resist, to stand up <i>(against)</i>, to set one's face (against)</li></ol><p><i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy, adversary</li><li>opponent, antagonist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;protivьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>disgustingly</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) against</li></ol><p><i>проти́вный</i> (protívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting, repulsive, nasty</li><li>contrary, adverse</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) naughty, mischievous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;protivьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>counter, anti-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confrontation, opposition, resistance</li><li>antagonism</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>боро́ться</i> (borótʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expostulate, to fight, to resist</li></ol><p><i>противобо́рство</i> (protivobórstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counterbalance, counterpoise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antiviral (medicine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) air defense (the use of systems, equipment, and personnel to prevent or reduce the impact of an attack by enemy aircraft or missiles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) antiaircraft (intended to attack aircraft)</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-, “anti-”) +&lrm; <i>во́здух</i> (vózdux, “air”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gas mask, breathing mask, respirator</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>газ</i> (gaz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthelmintic, vermicidal (destructive to parasitic intestinal worms)</li></ol><p>From <i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глист</i> (glist) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counteraction, opposition, resistance (action)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counteract, to oppose</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствовать</i> (déjstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bucking, opposed, counteracting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unnatural, perverted</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) illegal</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contraceptive</li></ol><p><i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зача́ток</i> (začátok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticough</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>ка́шель</i> (kášelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) anti-personnel</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>пехо́тный</i> (pexótnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fire prevention</li></ol><p>From <i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>пожа́р</i> (požár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraindication</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>показа́ние</i> (pokazánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) contraindicated, not recommended</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>пока́занный</i> (pokázannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrast, to compare, to oppose</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrast, opposition</li></ol><p><i>противополо́жный</i> (protivopolóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ). Compare with Czech <i>protiklad</i> (“opposite, contrast (difference)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li><li>contrary, opposed</li></ol><p><i>противоположи́ть</i> (protivopoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare with Czech <i>protikladný</i> (“opposite, contrary, contradictory, antagonistic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the opposite sex (the other gender to which one is referring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose</li><li>to contrast <i>(with)</i>, to set off <i>(against)</i>, to compare <i>(to)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrast, contrasting, contraposition, opposition</li></ol><p><i>противопоста́вить</i> (protivopostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppose</li><li>to contrast <i>(with)</i>, to set off <i>(against)</i>, to compare <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>противопоста́вить</i> (protivopostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) wrongful, unlawful, illegal</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missile defense (antimissile defense system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antimissile</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>раке́та</i> (rakéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inconsistency</li></ol><p><i>противоречи́вый</i> (protivorečívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contradictory, conflicting, discrepant (that is itself a contradiction)</li></ol><p><i>противоре́чие</i> (protivoréčije) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contradiction (act of contradicting)</li></ol><p><i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>речь</i> (rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contradict</li><li>to contrary, to be at variance, to conflict</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>речь</i> (rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confrontation, face-off</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) opposition</li></ol><p><i>противостоя́ть</i> (protivostojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counter, to contradict, to oppose, to confront, to withstand, to resist [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-, “counter-”) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ, “stand”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>протистояти</i> (protystojaty). Note the structural similarity to Germanic languages: English <i>withstand</i>, German <i>widerstehen</i>, Dutch <i>weerstaan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) antitank</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>танк</i> (tank) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) Czech hedgehog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anticellulite</li></ol><p><i>противо-</i> (protivo-) +&lrm; <i>целлюли́тный</i> (celljulítnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antidote (remedy to counteract a poison)</li></ol><p><i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>яд</i> (jad) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear through, to wear into holes, to fray, to rub a hole <i>(in)</i></li><li>to wipe, to rub</li><li>to rub through, to grate</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push/shove through</li></ol><p><i>проти́снуть</i> (protísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to force/push/shoulder/elbow one's way <i>(through)</i>, to squeeze <i>(oneself)</i> through</li></ol><p><i>проти́скивать</i> (protískivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push/shove through</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ти́снуть</i> (tísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to force/push/shoulder/elbow one's way <i>(through)</i>, to squeeze <i>(oneself)</i> through</li></ol><p><i>проти́снуть</i> (protísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce <i>(through)</i>, to transfix, to pink, to spit, to skewer</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>proto- (first)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archpriest (religious title&#47;rank, Orthodox church)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duct</li><li>channel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>channel (<i>of a river or brook</i>); (artificial) canal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minutes, record</li><li>protocol</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>коллаге́н</i> (kollagén), <i>колла́ж</i> (kolláž), and <i>колло́ид</i> (kollóid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record-keeping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) protocol</li></ol><p><i>протоко́л</i> (protokól) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to push (something) forward, to push (something) through or in (<i>using several actions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push (something) forward, to push (something) through or in</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to push through (an action, decision, matter, case, etc.) (to speed up the implementation of)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) protologism</li></ol><p>Coined by Mikhail Epstein in 2003, from Ancient Greek <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm), by analogy with <i>прототи́п</i> (prototíp) and <i>неологи́зм</i> (neologízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) proton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) protoplasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protoplanet</li></ol><p>From <i>прото-</i> (proto-) +&lrm; <i>плане́та</i> (planéta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>protoplanetary</li></ol><p>From <i>прото-</i> (proto-) +&lrm; <i>плане́т(а)</i> (planét(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to clear (a path or road) (<i>by frequent walking or traveling</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to blaze (a trail, path, road, etc.), to make easier for others to follow</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>тори́ть</i> (torítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to clear (a path or road) (<i>by frequent walking or traveling</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to blaze (a trail, path, road, etc.), to make easier for others to follow</li></ol><p><i>протори́ть</i> (protorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Prototaxites</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Prototaxites</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prototype</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>πρωτότυπος</i> (prōtótupos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowing, not standing (<i>of water</i>)</li><li>with flowing water</li><li>(relational,&#32;technical) flow</li></ol><p><i>прото́к</i> (protók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sober <i>(up)</i>, to become/grow sober</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>трезве́ть</i> (trezvétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to go bad, to spoil (of food etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to go bad, to spoil (of food etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ту́хнуть</i> (túxnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul, rotten, putrid, bad (of food), tainted, addled, rotten (of eggs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce <i>(through)</i>, to transfix, to pink, to spit, to skewer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make many (holes) in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pierce in several places</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend</li><li>to hold / stretch / reach out; to extend</li><li>to delay, to drag out</li><li>to drawl, to prolong, to sustain</li><li>to hold out, to live a while longer, to last (survive)</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p><i>протяг-</i> (protjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out, to reach out</li><li>to extend, to reach, to stretch</li></ol><p><i>протя́гивать</i> (protjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extent, stretch, length</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a prolonged or slow manner (<i>sound, speech, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>протя́жный</i> (protjážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prolonged, slow (<i>sound, speech, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>протяг-</i> (protjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extent, length</li></ol><p><i>протяжённый</i> (protjažónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long, extended</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) detailed, verbose</li></ol><p><i>протяг-</i> (protjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ённый</i> (-jónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend</li><li>to hold / stretch / reach out; to extend</li><li>to delay, to drag out</li><li>to drawl, to prolong, to sustain</li><li>to hold out, to live a while longer, to outlast (survive)</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out, to reach out</li><li>to extend, to reach, to stretch</li></ol><p><i>протяну́ть</i> (protjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lane, alleyway, pass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to teach (someone) a lesson</li></ol><p><i>проучи́ть</i> (proučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to teach (<i>for some time</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to teach (someone) a lesson</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ). Compare with Serbo-Croatian <i>проучити</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to study (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>проучи́ть</i> (proučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignoramus (totally ignorant person)</li><li>layman, outsider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>profānus</i> (“unholy”), further from Latin <i>fānum</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>без царя́ в голове́</i> (bez carjá v golové), <i>бо́жья и́скра</i> (bóžʹja ískra), <i>без бо́жьей и́скры</i> (bez bóžʹjej ískry).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profanation, desecration, blasphemy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>profanatio</i>. Also derivable synchronically from <i>профан(и́ровать)</i> (profan(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to profane</li></ol><p>From German <i>profanieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), or directly from French <i>profaner</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>профессиона́льная деформа́ция</i> (professionálʹnaja deformácija): professional deformation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>professional, pro</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>professionalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>professional deformation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>professionally</li></ol><p><i>профессиона́льный</i> (professionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>professional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profession, occupation, trade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Profession</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>professor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>professorial</li></ol><p><i>профе́ссор</i> (proféssor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faculty (scholarly staff at colleges or universities)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pro</li></ol><p>1973 or earlier. Borrowed from German <i>Profi</i>, shortening of <i>Professional</i>, from English <i>professional</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prevention, preventive measures, precautions, prophylaxis</li><li><i>(technical, usually television and radio)</i> short form of профилактические работы; maintenance, technical difficulties</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prophylactic, preventative</li></ol><p><i>профилакт-</i> (profilakt-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profile (<i>various senses</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) profile</li><li>main, core</li></ol><p><i>про́филь</i> (prófilʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(also&#32;figurative,&#32;physics) to filter</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>фильтр</i> (filʹtr) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finance, to fund</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>финанси́ровать</i> (finansírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) profit, gain, interest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economics,&#32;finance,&#32;fitness) surplus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade-union committee</li></ol><p>Short for профсоюзный комитет</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade union boss</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>профсою́зный организа́тор</i> (profsojúznyj organizátor, “trade union organizer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>профессиона́льная ориента́ция</i> (professionálʹnaja orijentácija): career guidance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade union</li></ol><p>shortening of <i>профессиона́льный</i> (professionálʹnyj, “professional”) + <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz, “union”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade unionist m</li></ol><p><i>профсою́з</i> (profsojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trade-union</li></ol><p><i>профсою́з</i> (profsojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>профессиона́льный станда́рт</i> (professionálʹnyj standárt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to let go to waste, to miss the opportunities that life gives you</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squander, to fritter away</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to (go for a) walk, to stroll</li></ol><p><i>проха́живать</i> (proxáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scoundrel, rascal, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>профо́с</i> (profós), from German <i>Profoss</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>praepositus</i> (whence also English <i>provost</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swindler, fraudster, crook, rogue</li></ol><p>Per Trubachyov, apparently a modification of <i>&#42;прохине́й</i> (&#42;proxinéj), from <i>про-</i> (pro-) + a derivative of <i>хи́нить</i> (xínitʹ, “to blaspheme, to scorn, to scold”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coolness (the state of being cool)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;proxolda</i>. By surface analysis, <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>хлад</i> (xlad) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get cooler, to cool off</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;proxolditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coolly</li></ol><p><i>прохла́дный</i> (proxládnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cool (<i>temperature</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>хла́дный</i> (xládnyj, “cold”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage, pass, aisle</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>проходи́ть</i> (proxodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) rascal, rogue, scoundrel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permeability</li><li>ability to carry traffic (<i>of a surface</i>)</li><li>ability to travel cross-country (<i>of a vehicle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>проходи́мый</i> (proxodímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passable, practicable, permeable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass, to go, to walk</li><li>to pass by</li><li><i>(time)</i> to pass, to go by, to elapse, to slip by</li><li>to be over</li><li><i>(meeting, gathering)</i> to go off, to be held</li><li>to study</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>control post, pass-through (checkpoint in a building)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) passed pawn, passer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>through, pass-through</li><li>(giving right to pass somewhere) access</li><li>(work of art) run-of-the-mill, commonplace, routine, undistinguished, unimaginative</li><li>anadromous (of migratory fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passing, passage</li><li>(<i>video games</i>) walkthrough</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passing, in transit</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passer-by (a person who is passing by)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosperity (prosperity)</li></ol><p><i>процвета́ть</i> (procvetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper, to thrive, to flourish</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>цвета́ть</i> (cvetátʹ). Second element related to <i>цвести</i> (cvesti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to filter, to strain</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>цеди́ть</i> (cedítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procedure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>procédure</i>, from Latin <i>procedere</i> (“to go forward, proceed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) procedure; procedural</li></ol><p><i>процеду́ра</i> (procedúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to filter, to strain</li></ol><p><i>процеди́ть</i> (procedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>percent</li><li>percentage (fraction or share)</li><li>(finance,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) interest, rate; interest income</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Prozent</i>, ultimately from New Latin <i>per centum</i>. First attested 1724.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) percentage</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) (bank) interest</li></ol><p><i>проце́нт</i> (procént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) usurer, pawnbroker</li></ol><p><i>проце́нт</i> (procént) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>process</li><li>trial, legal proceedings, lawsuit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procession</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>processor (device which processes)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>processor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>processual, (<i>relational</i>) process</li><li>(<i>law</i>) judicial</li><li>(<i>law</i>) legal</li><li>(<i>law</i>) procedural</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quote, to cite</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>цити́ровать</i> (citírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) to check, to skip a move</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to check (general sense)</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>че́кать</i> (čékatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw (a line)</li><li>to spend some (specific amount of) time carousing</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ, “to draw”)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ, “to misbehave,to carouse”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish reading, to read through</li><li>to recite</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>other</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прочии</i> (pročii, “other”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>про̏чӣ</i> (“other”). Per Vasmer, apparently an original comparative of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokъ</i> (whence Russian <i>прок</i> (prok)), originally an adjective meaning "ahead". See Russian <i>прочь</i> (pročʹ) and <i>прок</i> (prok) for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean out, to clean the inside of</li><li>to clear (one's throat)</li><li>to blow (one's nose)</li><li>to clear (a passage, a path)</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to clean (<i>for a specified amount of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read, to read through</li><li>to recite</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read through</li><li>to recite</li></ol><p><i>прочита́ть</i> (pročitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intend <i>(for)</i></li><li>to expect <i>(to become)</i>, to consider as the most likely candidate <i>(for)</i></li><li>to expect (to have, to)</li></ol><p><i>прок</i> (prok) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean out, to clean the inside of</li><li>to clear (one's throat)</li><li>to blow (one's nose)</li><li>to clear (a passage, a path)</li></ol><p><i>прочи́стить</i> (pročístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>durably, enduringly</li></ol><p><i>про́чный</i> (próčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>durability, solidity, firmness, strength, fastness</li></ol><p><i>про́чный</i> (próčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>durable, strong, firm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;prokъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>прок</i> (prok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading, perusal</li><li>reading, interpretation</li></ol><p><i>прочт-</i> (pročt-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of emotion, heartfelt (<i>of words, a tone of voice, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel deeply/acutely/keenly</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>away, aside</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>be off, get out</li><li>begone, away with you</li><li>beat it, scat</li><li>hence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прочь</i> (pročĭ, “away”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pročь</i>. Cognate with Old East Slavic <i>проче</i> (proče, “except”), Ukrainian <i>пріч</i> (prič, “away”), <i>проча́нин</i> (pročányn, “pilgrim”), Belarusian <i>проч</i> (proč, “away”), Old Church Slavonic <i>прочь</i> (pročĭ, “rest, remaining”), <i>проче</i> (proče, “therefore”), Bulgarian <i>про́чее</i> (próčee, “therefore”), Slovene <i>pròč</i> (“away”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Polish <i>oprócz</i> (“except”); also, less clearly, with Czech <i>pryč</i> (“away, out”), Slovak <i>preč</i> (“away”), Polish <i>precz</i> (“away”), Upper Sorbian <i>preč</i> (“away”), Lower Sorbian <i>pšec</i> (“away”) (also <i>pšejc</i>). Per Vasmer, apparently an original comparative of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prokъ</i> (whence Russian <i>прок</i> (prok)), originally an adjective meaning "ahead". See Russian <i>про́чий</i> (próčij) and <i>прок</i> (prok) for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to step</li><li>to walk for some time</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шага́ть</i> (šagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to take a stroll, to take a walk</li></ol><p>From <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>швы́рнуть</i> (švýrnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the past</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) past tense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>past</li><li>last</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to whisper</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шелесте́ть</i> (šelestétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petition, application (a verbal or written request)</li></ol><p><i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper (to talk in a quiet voice)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break through (a door, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to affect, to come over (<i>someone, of a sweat, a chill, a pain, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to penetrate (<i>of the cold or wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>прошиби́ть</i> (prošibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break through (a door, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to affect, to come over (<i>someone, of a sweat, a chill, a pain, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to penetrate (<i>of the cold or wind</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch, to sew</li><li>to pierce (through)</li><li>to riddle (<i>with bullets, bombs, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to broach</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to modify/flash/upgrade firmware <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>проши́ть</i> (prošítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insert (clothing)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) firmware</li></ol><p><i>прошива́ть</i> (prošivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiss</li><li><i>(cat)</i> to spit, <i>(snake, goose)</i> to hiss</li><li>to sizzle, to fizz, to sputter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to scold maliciously, to nag, to give the rough edge of one's tongue</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шипе́ть</i> (šipétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch, to sew</li><li>to pierce (through)</li><li>to riddle (<i>with bullets, bombs, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) last year; last year's</li></ol><p><i>про́шлый</i> (próšlyj, “last”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) Used in comparisons, to indicate that something is as much use as a chocolate teapot, i.e. completely useless.</li></ol><p>Ultimately a calque of French <i>neiges d'antan</i>. Compare German <i>Schnee von gestern</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the past</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last, past</li><li>former</li></ol><p>Compare <i>прошёл</i> (prošól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rude</i>) prostitute, slut, skank</li><li>(<i>rude</i>) snake (sly woman)</li><li>(<i>often ironically or jokingly</i>) girl, woman</li><li>(<i>rude</i>) faggot</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>шмон</i> (šmon) +&lrm; <i>манда́</i> (mandá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>farewell, adieu, good-bye (said to wish a fond farewell&#59; good-bye) (<i>informal to one person</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>farewell, adieu, good-bye (said to wish a fond farewell&#59; good-bye) (<i>formal or to a group of people</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) farewell</li></ol><p><i>проща́ть</i> (proščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parting, leave-taking, farewell</li></ol><p><i>проща́ть</i> (proščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>проща́ние</i> (proščánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forgive, to pardon, to excuse</li><li>to remit</li><li>to condone, to overlook</li><li>(<i>imperative&#32;only</i>) to farewell</li></ol><p>From <i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say goodbye, to bid farewell</li></ol><p><i>проща́ть</i> (proščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>просто́й</i> (prostój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>про́сто</i> (prósto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;hedge) in layman's terms; phrased simply, without jargon.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) upstart, rogue, rascal</li></ol><p>Akin to Russian <i>щель</i> (ščelʹ), i.e. one, who can get in (go through) any crack. Compare typologically <i>пройдо́ха</i> (projdóxa), <i>проходи́мец</i> (proxodímec), <i>проны́ра</i> (pronýra), <i>прола́за</i> (proláza).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgiveness, pardon (forgiveness for an offence)</li></ol><p><i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>проще́ние</i> (proščénije)</li></ol><p><i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel by touching</li><li>to probe</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to test the waters</li></ol><p>Literally, “to test the soil”. Compare French <i>tâter le terrain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel by touching</li><li>to probe</li></ol><p><i>прощу́пать</i> (proščúpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to test the waters</li></ol><p>Literally, “to test the soil”. Compare French <i>tâter le terrain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) developer (liquid used in chemical film processing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to display, to evince, to manifest, to reveal</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) to develop</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manifestation, display, demonstration</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) development</li></ol><p><i>прояви́ть</i> (projavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to display, to evince, to manifest, to reveal</li><li><i>(photograph)</i> to develop</li></ol><p><i>прояви́ть</i> (projavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become apparent</li></ol><p><i>проявля́ть</i> (projavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(of an image,&#32;view) clearing</li><li>(<i>weather</i>) clearing up</li><li>clarification</li></ol><p><i>проясни́ть</i> (projasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear up (make easy to see)</li><li>to clear up, to clarify (by expressing ideas into words)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to clear up (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p><p><i>про-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear <i>(become better seen)</i></li><li><i>(face)</i> to brighten <i>(up)</i></li><li><i>(weather)</i> to clear <i>(up)</i></li><li>to clear <i>(up)</i>, to clarify, to become clearer</li></ol><p><i>проясни́ть</i> (projasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear up (make easy to see)</li><li>to clear up, to clarify (by expressing ideas into words)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to become clear, to become bright (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>проясни́ть</i> (projasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear <i>(become better seen)</i></li><li><i>(face)</i> to brighten <i>(up)</i></li><li><i>(weather)</i> to clear <i>(up)</i></li><li>to clear <i>(up)</i>, to clarify, to become clearer</li></ol><p><i>проясня́ть</i> (projasnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening, aperture (<i>in a wall, e.g. for a door or window</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>пронима́ть</i> (pronimátʹ) / <i>проня́ть</i> (pronjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>пра́порщик</i> (práporščik, “(military rank) ensign”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>whoa (<i>when commanding a horse or imitative thereof</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pond</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прудъ</i> (prudŭ, “stream, pressure, dam”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prǫdъ</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>prąd</i> (“flow, current”), Slovak <i>prúd</i> (“flow, current”) and Slovene <i>prod</i> (“gravel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пруд</i> (prud)</li></ol><p><i>пруд</i> (prud) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dam up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (device made of flexible material)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prǫžina</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>пружи́на</i> (pružýna), Bulgarian <i>пръжи́на</i> (prǎžína), Slovene <i>prožína</i>, Czech <i>pružina</i>, Polish <i>sprężyna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be elastic, to be springy, to spring back, to yield</li><li>to tense (one's muscles)</li></ol><p><i>пружи́на</i> (pružína) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пружи́на</i> (pružína)</li></ol><p><i>пружи́на</i> (pružína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>прусса́к</i> (prussák, “Prussian”)</li><li>cockroach, especially red one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prussian</li><li>alternative form of <i>пруса́к</i> (prusák, “cockroach”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Prussian, Old Prussian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, switch</li><li>rod</li><li>wicker</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prǫtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin/short switch, small twig</li></ol><p><i>прут</i> (prut) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>прут</i> (prut); small stick, twig</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) bar, stick, rod</li></ol><p><i>прут</i> (prut, “twig, rog”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) proof coining/technology</li><li>proof (US unit of alcohol strength)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) proof, confirmation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>proof</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to prove</li></ol><p><i>пруф</i> (pruf) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used to describe or encourage jumping and skipping.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump, to spring, to leap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prygati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prew-</i> (“to jump”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>प्रवते</i> (pravate, “to jump”), Persian پریدن, English <i>frolic</i>, and German <i>fröhlich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump, to spring, to leap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prygnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jump, leap, bound</li><li>dive</li></ol><p><i>пры́гать</i> (prýgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high jump (athletics event)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst into laughter</li><li>to dart, to dash</li><li>to spray to sprinkle [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to drizzle, to fall in small drops (<i>of rain</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pryskati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst into laughter</li><li>to dart, to dash</li><li>to spray to sprinkle [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to gush, to spurt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prysnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пры́с(кать)</i> (prýs(katʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agile, nimble, prompt, quick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prytъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agileness</li></ol><p>From <i>пры́ткий</i> (prýtkij, “agile”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) agility, quickness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimple, spot, pustule</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прꙑщь</i> (pryščĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pryščь</i>. Cognate to <i>пры́скать</i> (prýskatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pimply; spotty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small pimple, spot</li></ol><p><i>прыщ</i> (pryšč) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to jump</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prędati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinning mill</li></ol><p><i>пряд-</i> (prjad-) +&lrm; <i>-и́льня</i> (-ílʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>прядь</i> (prjadʹ, “lock (<i>of hair</i>), strand”)</li></ol><p><i>прядь</i> (prjadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li>strand</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prędь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(belt) buckle, clasp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distaff</li></ol><p><i>пря́сть</i> (prjástʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>пря́мо</i> (prjámo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straight line</li><li>straight (a part of a racecourse or running track that is not curved)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rectum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) direct speech (the writing of speech)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;inanimate) straight path</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;animate) direct, frank person</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) across country</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) directly, straight</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) straightforwardly</li></ol><p>Instrumental case of <i>прями́к</i> (prjamík, “straight path”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to straighten</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to travel forward on a straight path</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become straight, to straighten up</li></ol><p><i>прями́ть</i> (prjamítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>straight</li><li>openly, blankly, frankly</li><li>real, really, truly</li><li>exactly, downright</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) really, actually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight</li><li>perpendicular, right, orthogonal, normal, rectangular</li><li>direct</li><li>straightforward</li><li>through (<i>of a train</i>)</li><li>downright</li><li>outspoken, frank</li><li>live (of a broadcast)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>прѧмъ</i> (pręmŭ, “straight, correct, honest, simple”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pręmъ</i>, regional variant of <i>&#42;prěmъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right angle (angle of 90 degrees)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straightness, rectilinearity</li><li>straightforwardness, frankness (in a way that lacks sensitivity)</li></ol><p><i>прямолине́йный</i> (prjamolinéjnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rectilinear</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) straightforward</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ли́ния</i> (línija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straightforwardness, plain dealing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) rectangle (quadrilateral)</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “angle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right-angled</li><li>rectangular</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “angle”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right triangle (a triangle having a right angle as one of its interior angles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rectangular; only used in <i>Пика́ссо прямоуго́льчатый</i> (Pikásso prjamougólʹčatyj) and <i>Пикассо́ прямоуго́льчатый</i> (Pikassó prjamougólʹčatyj, “triggerfish”)</li></ol><p><i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gingerbread, spice cake</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) carrot (any motivational tool&#59; an incentive to do something)</li></ol><p><i>пря́(ный)</i> (prjá(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пря́ник</i> (prjánik): (small) pryanik, gingerbread</li></ol><p><i>пря́ник</i> (prjánik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gingerbread man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) spice (plant matter used to season or flavour/flavor food)</li><li>spiciness</li></ol><p><i>пря́ный</i> (prjányj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to jump</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prędnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spicy (containing spice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьпьрѧнъ</i> (pĭpĭręnŭ, “peppery”), <i>пьпьръ</i> (pĭpĭrŭ, “pepper”) +&lrm; <i>-ѧнъ</i> (-jęnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьpьrěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin (to make yarn)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pręsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceal, to hide</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prętati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, to conceal oneself, to hide away (intransitive)</li></ol><p>From <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide and seek (a game)</li></ol><p>From <i>пря́таться</i> (prjátatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>приобрести́</i> (priobrestí).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пре́сный</i> (présnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psalmist, psalm writer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;religion) psalm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>псалъмъ</i> (psalŭmŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ψαλμός</i> (psalmós). Doublet of <i>пса́льма</i> (psálʹma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;rare) psalter</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>псалътꙑрь</i> (psalŭtyrĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>псалътꙑр҄ь</i> (psalŭtyrʹĭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ψαλτήριον</i> (psaltḗrion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) psalter</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>псалътꙑрь</i> (psalŭtyrĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>псалътꙑр҄ь</i> (psalŭtyrʹĭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ψαλτήριον</i> (psaltḗrion).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kennel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kennel</li></ol><p><i>псарь</i> (psarʹ, “keeper of hunting dogs”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of hunting dogs, huntsman (one who manages the hounds during a hunt)</li></ol><p>From <i>пёс</i> (pjos) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>pseudo-</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false, lying”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) pseudocode</li><li>(computing,&#32;informal) bytecode, p-code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pseudofolk</li></ol><p><i>псевдо-</i> (psevdo-) +&lrm; <i>наро́д</i> (naród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscience</li></ol><p><i>псевдо-</i> (psevdo-) +&lrm; <i>нау́ка</i> (naúka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudonym, alias, pen name, stage name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetastry (inferior poetry)</li></ol><p><i>псевдо-</i> (psevdo-) +&lrm; <i>поэ́зия</i> (poézija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoscorpion</li></ol><p>From <i>псевдо-</i> (psevdo-) +&lrm; <i>скорпио́н</i> (skorpión).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pseudorandom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pseudoscience</i>) pseudoscientist</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pseudoscience</i>) pseudoscientific</li></ol><p><i>псевдо-</i> (psevdo-) +&lrm; <i>учёный</i> (učónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pseudoephedrine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psi (the Greek letter Ψ&#47;ψ)</li><li>psi (the old Cyrillic letter Ѱ&#47;ѱ, used in Church Slavonic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ψεῖ</i> (pseî).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogmeat, dog flesh</li><li>smell of a dog, doggy odor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>пёс</i> (pjos); a (large) dog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьsina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пёс</i> (pjos, “a dog”) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пси́на</i> (psína): little dog</li></ol><p><i>пси́на</i> (psína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) psycho, lunatic, psychopath (a person who is psychotic or otherwise insane).</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) nut (a person who acts in a bizarre or dangerous manner)</li><li>(slang,&#32;in the plural) state of being angry; madness; being in a rash (often used when speaking about children)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a military attack when the soldiers are marching towards the enemy without using weapon</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>психическая атака</i> (psixičeskaja ataka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychiatric hospital, mental asylum</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>психиатри́ческая больни́ца</i> (psixiatríčeskaja bolʹníca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychiatrist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asylum (mental asylum)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychiatric clinic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>psychiatric</li></ol><p><i>психиатри́я</i> (psixiatríja) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) psychiatry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psyche, mind, mentality</li><li>psychics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mentally</li><li>psychically</li></ol><p><i>психи́ческий</i> (psixíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mental, psychic</li></ol><p><i>пси́х(ика)</i> (psíx(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>psycho-</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ψυχή</i> (psukhḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychoanalysis</li></ol><p><i>психо-</i> (psixo-) +&lrm; <i>ана́лиз</i> (análiz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychoanalyst</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become hysterical, to freak out</li></ol><p><i>псих</i> (psix) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychogeography</li></ol><p><i>психо-</i> (psixo-) +&lrm; <i>геогра́фия</i> (geográfija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"psychosotherapy" (seems to be a protologism). This is a pseudoscientific medical discipline allegedly created by supporters of неврозотерапия that purports to replace medically incurable neuroses with more easily treatable psychoses. The concept is discredited.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>psychologically (in a psychological manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>psychological</li></ol><p><i>психоло́гия</i> (psixológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychopath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychopathy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abnormal psychology (academic study)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;psychology) psychosomatic (of physical effects with mental causes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychotherapist (who practices psychotherapy, male or female)</li></ol><p>From <i>психо-</i> (psixo-) +&lrm; <i>терапе́вт</i> (terapévt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychotherapy, psychotherapeutics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>psychotechnical</li></ol><p><i>психо-</i> (psixo-) +&lrm; <i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) psychotic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) psychotropic, psychoactive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>psychotrope</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychophysics</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Psychophysik</i> or French <i>psychophysique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychophysiologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>психофизиоло́гия</i> (psixofiziológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>psychophysiological</li></ol><p><i>психофизиоло́гия</i> (psixofiziológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychophysiology</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>psychophysiology</i> or French <i>psychophysiologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>psychophysical</li></ol><p><i>психофи́зика</i> (psixofízika) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) mental hospital</li></ol><p><i>псих(о́з)</i> (psix(óz)) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) citizen of Pskov</li></ol><p><i>Псков</i> (Pskov) +&lrm; <i>-итянин</i> (-itjanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Pskov</li></ol><p><i>Псков</i> (Pskov) +&lrm; <i>-ич</i> (-ič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pskov</li></ol><p><i>Псков</i> (Pskov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) bird</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъtakъ</i>, derived from <i>&#42;pъta</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to fly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small bird</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъtakъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пта́шка</i> (ptáška): birdie, little bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пта́ха</i> (ptáxa): birdie, little bird</li></ol><p><i>пта́ха</i> (ptáxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chick, nestling, fledgling, baby bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pterodactyl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pъtica</i>. Beware <b>false</b> cognate Greek <i>πτηνό</i> (ptinó, “bird”) and related forms in all languages from Ancient Greek <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;però</i> and true Slavic cognates of the Greek word, like Russian <i>перо́</i> (peró, “feather”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjutant (bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poultry farmer, poultry breeder</li></ol><p>From <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca, “bird”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poultry farming</li></ol><p>From <i>птицево́д</i> (pticevód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star-of-Bethlehem (<i>Ornithogalum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca, “bird”) +&lrm; <i>мле́ко</i> (mléko, “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), a calque of translingual <i>Ornithogalum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poultry factory, industrial poultry farm</li></ol><p><i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>фа́брика</i> (fábrika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poultry farm, hennery</li></ol><p><i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>фе́рма</i> (férma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bird</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) poultry</li></ol><p><i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language of the birds</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) Greek (incomprehensible speech or text overloaded with special terminology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca): birdie, little bird, birdling</li></ol><p><i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aviary (confinement for keeping birds)</li><li>poultry-man</li></ol><p><i>пти́ца</i> (ptíca) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hen’s teeth (something extremely rare)</li><li>(<i>also capitalised</i>) "Ptichye moloko", (name of) popular chocolate candies, also known in the West under the Polish name <i>ptasie mleczko</i> (“birds’ milk”), and in Germany as <i>Vogelmilch</i> (“birds’ milk”).</li></ol><p>Literally, “birds’ milk”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пти́чий</i> (ptíčij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ptolemaic</li></ol><p><i>Птолеме́й</i> (Ptoleméj) +&lrm; <i>-евский</i> (-evskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audience (a group of people seeing or hearing a performance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publication (what is published)</li><li>publishing</li><li>notice, advertisement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish, to issue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to publish one's work/paper</li><li>to be published</li></ol><p><i>публикова́ть</i> (publikovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publicist, commentator, columnist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publicizing, opinion journalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>journalistic</li></ol><p><i>публици́ст</i> (publicíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, overtly, publicly, candidly, frankly</li></ol><p><i>публи́чный</i> (publíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>publicity</li></ol><p><i>публи́чный</i> (publíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>public</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house of prostitution, brothel; parlor house</li></ol><p>Literally, “public house”, calque of French <i>maison publique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarecrow</li><li>bugaboo, bugbear</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fright</li></ol><p><i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimidated, cowed</li><li>not so easily scared</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten suddenly, to startle</li><li>to worry, to make anxious</li><li>to warn, to caution, to alert</li><li>to intimidate, to threaten, to browbeat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puďati</i>, dialectal <i>пужа́ть</i> (pužátʹ), cognate with Ukrainian <i>пужати</i> (pužaty), Belarusian <i>пужаць</i> (pužacʹ). Presence of evolution Proto-Slavic /ď/→Russian /г/ testifies to the borrowing from the Old Novgorodian or Old Pskovian dialects where this transition was regular, compare dialectal (Pskov) <i>рога́ть</i> (rogátʹ) 'give birth'. For further details see Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frightened (of), to be startled (of), to take fright (at); to shy (at) [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to be afraid [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pop gun</li></ol><p>Reinterpretation of the word <i>пугу́ч</i> (pugúč), compare onomatopoeic <i>пу́гу́</i> (púgú, “the sound made by an eagle owl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scary, frightening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timid, fearful</li></ol><p><i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to scare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>button (knob or small disc serving as a fastener)</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>пѫгъвь</i> (pǫgŭvĭ) + <i>-ица</i> (-ica), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫgy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) button (knob or small disc serving as a fastener)</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́говица</i> (púgovica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пу́говица</i> (púgovica): (small) button (on clothing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pood (an obsolete Russian unit of mass equal to 40 фунт (funt), or 16.3804815 kg (36.1127 pounds))</li><li>pood (in modern kettlebells, rounded off to 16 kg (35.2739 pounds))</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) percent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫdъ</i>, from Late Latin <i>pondo</i>, from Classical <i>pondus</i>. Doublet of <i>фунт</i> (funt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poodle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pudel</i>. Known at least since the end of 18th century. In 1807 Derzhavin wrote a poem Милорду, моему пуделю ("To Milord, my poodle").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pudding</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pudding</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>likewise&#32;obsolete</i>) synonym of <i>пудо́вка</i> (pudóvka)</li></ol><p><i>пуд</i> (pud) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of measure, when for volume then equal to a <i>четвери́к</i> (četverík) and when for weight then equal to a <i>пуд</i> (pud)</li><li>a container in which the measure of said volume fits</li><li>a kettlebell, originally of the said weight</li></ol><p><i>пуд</i> (pud) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Pudozh</li></ol><p><i>Пу́дож</i> (Púdož) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>powder</li><li>face powder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>poudre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compact (small powder container)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powdered</li></ol><p><i>пу́дрить</i> (púdritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) to powder</li></ol><p><i>пу́др(а)</i> (púdr(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to try to deceive or mislead, to bullshit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) potbellied, paunchy</li><li>(<i>of a vessel, jar, etc.</i>) paunchy</li></ol><p><i>пу́зо</i> (púzo) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) belly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puzo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bubble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to billow</li></ol><p><i>пузы́рь</i> (puzýrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Lentibulariaceae.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bubble</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) blister</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bladder</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bottle (<i>usually of an alcoholic beverage</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́зо</i> (púzo) +&lrm; <i>-ы́рь</i> (-ýrʹ).</p><p>For the <i>пу́зо — пузы́рь</i> connection compare typologically Latin <i>venter</i> — <i>vēsīca</i>. Also compare Macedonian <i>мев</i> (mev, “abdomen, belly; bellows”) and Macedonian <i>меур</i> (meur, “bubble; blister; bludder”), Ancient Greek <i>φῦσα</i> (phûsa, “bellows; bladder;...”), English <i>belly</i> — <i>bellows</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bubble</li></ol><p><i>пузырёк</i> (puzyrjók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пузы́рь</i> (puzýrʹ): (small) bubble; vial, phial; vesicle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nip, miniature bottle (<i>of hard liquor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пузы́рь</i> (puzýrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wisp, bunch, bundle, tuft</li><li>poot, fart</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>поукъ</i> (pukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫkъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pank-</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>пук</i> (puk), Czech <i>puk</i>, Polish <i>pęk</i>.</p><p>Further cognates, per Vasmer, are Persian پنگ (pang, “date palm cluster”) and Latin <i>pānus</i> (“millet ear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fart (<i>mild or childish</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fart (<i>mild or childish</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pukjai (a kind of soup, similar to the South Korean doenjangjjigae, but usually eaten among Soviet Koreans)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>북자이</i> (bukjai) (in today's South Korea, 북장). Essentially from the Soviet Korean dialect (a variety of the Hamgyeong-do dialect).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economy</i>) pool</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) pool (billiards)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pool</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bullet</li></ol><p><i>пу́ля</i> (púlja) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine gun</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́ля</i> (púlja) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-мёт</i> (-mjót), combining form of <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”).</p><p>Attested since the 1880s, at the time to denote Maxim guns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) machine gun</li></ol><p><i>пулемёт</i> (pulemjót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gunner, machine-gunner</li></ol><p><i>пулемёт</i> (pulemjót) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulletproof</li></ol><p><i>пу́ля</i> (púlja) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>не-</i> (ne-) +&lrm; <i>пробива́ть</i> (probivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pullover (sweater)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pullover</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>atomizer, sprayer (instrument for reducing a liquid to spray)</li></ol><p>Most likely borrowed from French <i>pulvérisateur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>болок</i> (bolok)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami <i>пуӆӆьк</i> (pul̥l̥’k). Doublet of <i>болок, болк</i> (bolok, bolk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bulgogi, a Korean dish of shredded beef with vegetables.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>불고기</i> (bulgogi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulmonologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulmonology, pulmology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physiology,&#32;also&#32;figuratively) pulse (regular beat caused by the heart)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) pulsar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pulsar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulsation, throbbing, pulse</li><li>ripple</li></ol><p><i>пульс(и́ровать)</i> (pulʹs(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throbbing</li></ol><p><i>пульси́ровать</i> (pulʹsírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pulsate</li><li>to beat, to throb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;astronomy,&#32;physics) pulsing, pulsating</li><li>throbbing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulsejet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lectern, music stand</li><li>console, (control) panel, benchboard</li><li>remote control</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pult</i>, from Latin <i>pulpitum</i> (“platform, scaffold”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remote control (device used to operate an appliance from a short distance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>strictly</i>) bullet (projectile)</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) cartridge (assembled package of bullet, primer and casing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>пуля</i> (pulja, “bullet, arquebus projectile”), att. since 1620s. As suggested by Chernykh and proven by Zoltan, a back-formation from <i>пулька</i> (pulʹka, “bullet; projectile in general”) (att. 1580s), itself a dissimilated borrowing from Old Ruthenian <i>кулька</i> (kulʹka) (att. 1550s), from Middle Polish <i>kulka</i>, from Old Polish <i>kulka</i> (att. 1497), from <i>kula</i> (att. 1461), from Middle High German <i>kūle</i>, a rare variant of <i>kugele</i>, itself of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) cougar, puma, mountain lion (<i>Puma concolor</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) point</li><li>point, station</li><li>paragraph, item</li></ol><p>Through Polish <i>punkt</i> and/or German <i>Punkt</i> from Latin <i>pūnctum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dotted line</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>punctually, on the dot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punctuality (state of being punctual)</li></ol><p><i>пунктуа́льный</i> (punktuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>punctual, precise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) punctuation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crimson</li></ol><p>Through Polish <i>pąsowy</i> (“light red”), from French <i>ponceau</i> (“poppy colour/color”), a derivation of <i>paon</i> (“peacock”), from Latin <i>pāvō</i> (“peacock”). Compare Russian <i>павли́н</i> (pavlín, “peacock”), Bulgarian / Macedonian / Serbo-Croatian <i>паун</i> (paun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punch (a hot alcoholic beverage made from wine, rum, fruit juice, sugar or honey, and hot water and spices)</li><li>punch (an alcoholic beverage mixed in a large bowl and generally containing a mixture of fruit juice and some other beverage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Punsch</i>, from English <i>punch</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) punch (A beverage)</li></ol><p><i>пунш</i> (punš) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) belly button, navel, umbilicus</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) YouTube poop, edited videos designed in a humorous and surreal manner.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пупъ</i> (pupŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫpъ</i> (“bud, navel”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>poop</i>, short for YouTube poop.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) center of the world</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>nombril du monde</i>, Polish <i>pępek świata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umbilical cord (cord between foetus and placenta)</li></ol><p><i>пуп</i> (pup, “navel”) +&lrm; <i>-ови́на</i> (-ovína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) navel, belly button, umbilicus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫpъkъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пуп</i> (pup) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) navelwort (<i>Omphalodes</i> spp.)</li></ol><p><i>пупо́к</i> (pupók, “belly button, navel”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), calque of translingual <i>Omphalodes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doll, puppet (shaped as a human)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) little child; kid; baby</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Puppe</i>. Further from Latin <i>pūpus</i> (via <i>pūpa</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пупс</i> (pups) doll (<i>baby doll</i>)</li><li>(humorous,&#32;way of address) sweetie, cutie, cutie pie, pupsik</li></ol><p><i>пупс</i> (pups) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pimple</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) protrusion, bump</li></ol><p><i>пупы́рь</i> (pupýrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ышек</i> (-yšek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) blister</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́п</i> (púp) +&lrm; <i>-ы́рь</i> (-ýrʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blizzard, snowstorm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nonsense</li><li>Purga (Soviet and Russian icebreaker)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Finnic language, from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;purku</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purist</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>pūrus</i> (whence ultimately also English <i>pure</i>), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewH-</i> (whence also Latin <i>putus</i>, Latin <i>pius</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Puritan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Puritan</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>pūrus</i> (whence ultimately also English <i>pure</i>), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewH-</i> (whence also Latin <i>putus</i>, Latin <i>pius</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tyrian purple (<i>dye or dyed cloth</i>)</li><li>crimson</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Purpur</i>, from Latin <i>purpura</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra); cognate with English <i>purple</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amaranth (color&#47;dye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) Tyrian purple (<i>relating to the dye</i>)</li><li>crimson, purple</li><li>magenta (<i>e.g. in the CMYK color model</i>)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) purpuric</li></ol><p><i>пу́рпур</i> (púrpur, “Tyrian purple, crimson”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); cognate with English <i>purple</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>startup (act)</li><li>launch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li><i>same as</i> пусть (pustʹ)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>same as</i> пусть (pustʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, to relinquish</li><li>to set going, to set in motion, to set in operation, to start, to set working</li><li>to let in</li><li>to launch, to throw</li><li>to sprout, to put out, to put forth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пускати</i> (puskati), back-formation from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puščati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unaddressed, to leave to chance, to leave (something) to take/run its course</li></ol><p>Literally, “to leave to self-flow”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start, to set out</li><li>to start</li><li>to go <i>(into)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to engage in a reprehensible activity without restraint, to indulge in vices (e.g. drinking, dissoluteness, etc.), to break bad</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to not scruple to use any means, to not be squeamish about any means one uses</li></ol><p>From the professional jargon of bell ringers, where <i>тя́жкая</i> (tjážkaja, literally “heavy”) refers to a bell. Thus, the phrase originally meant “to start ringing all the bells”, later reinterpreted by association with the expression <i>тя́жкий грех</i> (tjážkij grex, “grave sin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) starting, launching, launch</li></ol><p><i>пуск</i> (pusk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kestrel (any small falcon of genus <i>Falco</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shallow, frivolous person (m or f)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become empty</li><li>to become deserted</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let <i>(go)</i>, to allow, to permit, to release</li><li>to set going, to set in motion, to set in operation, to start, to set working</li><li>to let in</li><li>to launch, to throw</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) to sprout, to put out, to put forth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unaddressed, to leave to chance, to leave (something) to take/run its course</li></ol><p>Literally, “to leave to self-flow”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to derail, to circle the drain, to go downhill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start, to set out</li><li>to start</li><li>to go <i>(into)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to engage in a reprehensible activity without restraint, to indulge in vices (e.g. drinking, dissoluteness, etc.), to break bad</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to not scruple to use any means, to not be squeamish about any means one uses</li></ol><p>From the professional jargon of bell ringers, where <i>тя́жкая</i> (tjážkaja, literally “heavy”) refers to a bell. Thus, the phrase originally meant “to start ringing all the bells”, later reinterpreted by association with the expression <i>тя́жкий грех</i> (tjážkij grex, “grave sin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialect</i>) an empty, abandoned housing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an empty manner, emptily</li><li>in an idle manner</li><li>in a shallow or vain manner (<i>of one's personality</i>)</li><li>futilely</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat empty, emptyish</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/stand empty, to be tenantless, to be uninhabited, to lie fallow</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) empty set</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty, void, hollow</li><li>idle (<i>of talk</i>)</li><li>shallow (<i>of one's personality</i>)</li><li>futile, frivolous</li><li>vain, ungrounded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>поустъ</i> (pustŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) something devoid of any verbal significance or meaning; meaningless statements</li></ol><p>Literally, “empty sound”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twaddler, babbler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meaningless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prattler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empty rhetoric, twaddle, verbiage</li></ol><p><i>пуст-</i> (pust-, “empty”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble, to talk nonsense, to talk needlessly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>void of meaning, nonsensical</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emptiness, void</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barren flower, unfertile flower</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) useless person</li></ol><p><i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasteland; uninhabited, abandoned or uncultivated area</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustošь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) desert</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) deserted, empty (without people)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) secluded, hermitlike (living in isolation, in a remote, unpopulated area)</li></ol><p><i>пусты́ня</i> (pustýnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermitage</li><li>a small monastery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desert, wilderness</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) unpopulated area</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustyni</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacant lot</li><li>wasteland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) pacifier, dummy</li><li>(<i>inanimate&#32;or&#32;animate</i>) somthing vain, lacking its crucial part, a placebo</li><li>(animate,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine) shallow person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let + subject + conjugated verb</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) let</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>though, even (if), although</li></ol><p>From пуска́ть (puskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trifling, trivial</li></ol><p><i>пустя́к</i> (pustják) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trifling, trivial</li></ol><p><i>пустя́к</i> (pustják) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>пустя́к</i> (pustják, “trifle”)</li></ol><p><i>пустя́к</i> (pustják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) prostitute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>puttana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion, hash, muddiness, mess, muss, tangle, maze, snarl, jumble, mishmash, mingle-mangle, imbroglio, embroilment, disorder, mull, bewilderment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пута́на</i> (putána)</li></ol><p>From <i>пута́на</i> (putána) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tangled, interwoven</li><li>inconsistent, illogical, unclear, confused, difficult to understand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) frivolous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupid</li></ol><p><i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tangle</li><li>to mix up (with), to confuse (with)</li><li>to confuse</li><li>to get mixed up, to confuse</li><li>to involve in, to embroil</li><li><i>(horses)</i> to hobble, to fetter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫtati</i>. Compare Polish <i>pętać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tangled</li><li>to be misled, to get confused, to get mixed up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to interfere <i>(in)</i>, to meddle <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to keep company <i>(with)</i>, to get mixed up <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sleep <i>(with)</i>, to go to bed <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide, guide book</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guiding, leading</li></ol><p><i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вод-</i> (vod-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) path, road</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) track</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) direction; directional</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) travel, journey</li></ol><p><i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overpass</li><li>underpass</li><li><i>railroad</i> overbridge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveller, voyager (one who travels)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>travels, journey</li></ol><p><i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ше́ствие</i> (šéstvije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) traveller, voyager (one who travels)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel</li></ol><p><i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ше́ствовать</i> (šéstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to travel, go on travels, voyage</li></ol><p>From <i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ше́ствововать</i> (šéstvovovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Puting (a political rally to support Vladimir Putin)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin) +&lrm; <i>ми́тинг</i> (míting, “meeting, rally”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;neologism) putinization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Putinism (the political policies of Vladimir Putin)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) (partisan) joking characterization of Vladimir Putin supporters as if they are having a disease called <i>"путинизм"</i>, sounding much like some officially recognized diseases, ex. aneurysm</li></ol><p><i>Путин</i> (Putin) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Vladimir Putin</li></ol><p><i>Пу́тин</i> (Pútin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;often&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics,&#32;especially&#32;Germany,&#32;Austria) Putin apologist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Putinversteher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) Putlerite (Vladimir Putin's supporter)</li></ol><p><i>Пу́тлер</i> (Pútler) +&lrm; <i>ов</i> (ov) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a belt that attaches stirrups to the saddle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveler, wayfarer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫtьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>пѫтьникъ</i> (pǫtĭnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reasonable, good</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) worthwhile, good, decent, usable</li></ol><p><i>пут(ь)</i> (put(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mandarin, Putonghua</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 普通話／普通话 (<i>pǔtōnghuà</i>, literally “common speech”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coup d'état, armed uprising, putsch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Putsch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hobble (of horses)</li><li>fetters, chains</li><li>trammels</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way, path, road</li><li>track, line</li><li>means</li><li>trip, journey</li><li>route</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>путь</i> (putĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫtь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;pántis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;póntoh₁s</i> (whence also Latin <i>pōns</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πόντος</i> (póntos), English <i>find</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tourist voucher</li><li>waybill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reasonable, sensible</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) good, decent, usable, worthwhile</li></ol><p><i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>by means of, through, via</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>properly</li></ol><p>Instrumental of <i>путь</i> (putʹ).</p><p>(<i>by means of</i>): Compare typologically Polish <i>drogą</i> (instrumental singular of <i>droga</i>), English <i>via</i> (&lt; ablative singular of Latin <i>via</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puukko (a type of sharp, pointed knife of Nordic heritage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>puukko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ottoman, pouf (low, padded stool)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pouf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пуф</i> (puf)</li></ol><p><i>пуф</i> (puf) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down (feathers)</li><li>fluff</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>sound of a shot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пухъ</i> (puxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пу́хлый</i> (púxlyj, “chubby, plump”)</li></ol><p><i>пу́хлый</i> (púxlyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having thick lips</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́хл(ый)</i> (púxl(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>губ(а́)</i> (gub(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-faced</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́хл(ый)</i> (púxl(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лиц(о́)</i> (lic(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having chubby cheeks</li></ol><p>From <i>пу́хл(ый)</i> (púxl(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>щека́</i> (ščeká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump, chubby</li><li>swollen, puffy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puxlъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пух</i> (pux, “fluff, originally breath”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj); compare <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump, chubby</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puxnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down jacket</li><li>down bed</li></ol><p><i>пух</i> (pux) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>powder puff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) down (soft feathers); downy</li></ol><p><i>пух</i> (pux) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goggle-eyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulf, abyss</li><li>eddy</li><li>the deep</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫčina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to swell, to inflate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to open wide, to bulge, to goggle (the eyes)</li><li>to burst from the inside</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pǫ̀čiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bundle, bunch, sheaf (any collection of things bound together)</li><li>fascicle, wisp</li></ol><p>From <i>пук</i> (puk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók). Piecewise doublet of <i>по́нчик</i> (pónčik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) cannon fodder, disposable infantry</li></ol><p>Approximate calque of French <i>chair à canon</i> (literally “meat for the cannon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gun, cannon</li></ol><p><i>пу́шка</i> (púška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fuzz, fluff</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tiny snowflake</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) cinch (something easy)</li></ol><p><i>пух</i> (pux) +&lrm; <i>-и́нка</i> (-ínka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>furry (covered with fur), fluffy, light, airy, fuzzy</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mascot (person in a furry animal suit)</li></ol><p>Related to пух (pux, “down, fluff”); ultimately from Old East Slavic <i>пухъ</i> (puxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;puxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) gun, cannon</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piece, handgun</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hoax</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) something evoking strong emotions</li></ol><p>Common Slavic. Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пушка</i> (puška) / <i>пушька</i> (pušĭka). Further etymology is disputed:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gun-howitzer (an artillery piece that combines characteristics of a gun and a howitzer, allowing fire both with a flat trajectory or with steep angle of elevation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) cannoneer, artillery (<i>in the Tsardom of Russia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пушка́рь</i> (puškárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) cannoneer (15th–17th century artilleryman)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) cannon founder, cannon maker</li></ol><p><i>пу́шка</i> (púška, “cannon”) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>all literature and art connected with the life and works of Pushkin, research about him, and all publications of his work</li></ol><p><i>Пу́шкин</i> (Púškin) +&lrm; <i>-иана</i> (-iana)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pushkinism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Ukraine) The demolition of statues of the Russian writer Alexander Pushkin in Ukraine.</li></ol><p><i>Пу́шкин</i> (Púškin, “Pushkin”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́д(ать)</i> (pád(atʹ), “to fall”), modelled on <i>снегопа́д</i> (snegopád, “snowfall”), <i>водопа́д</i> (vodopád, “waterfall”) or <i>листопа́д</i> (listopád, “fall of leaves”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pushkin (Russian surname, especially referring to Alexander Pushkin, Russian poet, novelist and playwright); Pushkin's, Pushkinian</li></ol><p><i>Пу́шкин</i> (Púškin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down</li><li>fluff</li><li>bloom (on fruit)</li></ol><p><i>пух</i> (pux) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lime, slaked lime (calcium hydroxide)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) pronunciation spelling of <i>пу́што</i> (púšto); cuz, coz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) contraction of <i>потому́ что</i> (potomú što); cuz, coz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pashto; one of the official languages of Afghanistan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Pushtu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pashto, Pashtun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick forest</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>пуща</i> (pušča, “thick forest”), from Old East Slavic <i>пуща</i> (pušča, “desolate place”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pušča</i> from <i>&#42;pȗstъ</i> (“desolate”). By surface analysis, <i>пусто́й</i> (pustój) +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja). Cognate with Polish <i>puszcza</i>, Ukrainian <i>пу́ща</i> (púšča), Belarusian <i>пу́шча</i> (púšča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) more than, worse than</li></ol><p>Obsolete comparative of <i>пусто́й</i> (pustój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) greater</li></ol><p>Adjective formed from the comparative <i>пу́ще</i> (púšče, “greater”); compare <i>бо́льший</i> (bólʹšij) and <i>бо́льше</i> (bólʹše).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pu'er</li></ol><p>From <i>Пуэ́р</i> (Puér), a region in the Chinese Province of Yunnan, ultimately from Mandarin <i>普洱</i> (Pǔ'ěr)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Puerto Rican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) pkhachich</li></ol><p>From West Circassian <i>пхъэкӏыч</i> (pꭓɛkʼəč) and East Circassian <i>пхъэцӏыч</i> (pꭓɛcʼəč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьčela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey bee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьčelinъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beekeeper</li></ol><p>From <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá, “bee”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apiculture, beekeeping (keeping and maintenance of bees)</li></ol><p><i>пчелово́д</i> (pčelovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee-eater (Meropidae, Accipitridae, Old-World insectivorous birds)</li><li>sand wasp, bee wolf (Bembicinae, species of wasp that kills bees)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee-eater</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bulgarian <i>пчелоя́д</i> (pčelojád).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apiary (a place where bees and hives are kept)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;бьчельникъ</i> (&#42;bĭčelĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьčelьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá): (small) bee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;бьчелъка</i> (&#42;bĭčelŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bьčelъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>пчела́</i> (pčelá, “bee”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang) Polack</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. A reference to the common Polish consonant cluster /pʂ/, as in Polish <i>przy</i>. Compare the synonymous Czech <i>pšoun</i>, <i>pšonec</i>, <i>pšonek</i> for similar formations.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;of the Polish language) to speak with a lot of /pʂ/ sounds</li></ol><p>From <i>пше́к</i> (pšék) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьшеница</i> (pĭšenica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьšenica</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peys-</i>, whence also <i>пиха́ть</i> (pixátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>trīticum</i> (&lt; <i>trītus</i>, p.p.p of <i>terō</i>), Latin <i>secāle</i> (<? Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wheat, wheaten</li></ol><p><i>пшени́ца</i> (pšeníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang) female Polack</li></ol><p>From <i>пшек</i> (pšek) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (handheld) spray</li></ol><p><i>пши́кать</i> (pšíkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet porridge (breakfast cereal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) millet</li></ol><p><i>пшено́</i> (pšenó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wad (soft plug; also in firearm charges)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wad hook, gun worm</li></ol><p><i>пыжо́вый</i> (pyžóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), the former the relative adjective from <i>пыж</i> (pyž, “wad”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li><li>ardour, zeal, blaze (great warmth of feeling; fervor; passion)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pylъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blaze, to flame, to be aflame</li><li>to glow</li><li>to burn <i>(with a feeling)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ablaze, ignescent, glowing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dusty plasma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance to dust and moisture</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вла́га</i> (vlága) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емость</i> (nepronicájemostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dust</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ, “dust”) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dustproof, dust-tight</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vacuum cleaner</li></ol><p>From <i>пыль</i> (pylʹ, “dust”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-сос</i> (-sos), combining form of <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ, “to suck”). Calque of German <i>Staubsauger</i>. First attested in 1907. Calqued also as <i>пылевса́сыватель</i> (pylevsásyvatelʹ) (1914).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vacuum, hoover</li></ol><p><i>пылесо́с</i> (pylesós) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speck of dust</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́нка</i> (-ínka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise dust, to fill the air with dust</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become covered in dust, to get dusty</li><li>(figurative,&#32;imperfective only) to gather dust (to lie unused for a long period of time)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to raise dust, to produce dust (<i>of dry land, a dry road, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пыли́ть</i> (pylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ardent, fervent, fiery, impassioned, fervid</li><li>torrid</li><li>perfervid</li><li>passionate</li><li>spunky</li><li>mettlesome</li><li>impetuous</li><li>ablaze, glowing</li><li>flaming</li><li>vivid</li><li>hot</li></ol><p><i>пыл</i> (pyl) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pylъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>пыл</i> (pyl), Polish <i>pył</i>, Ukrainian <i>пил</i> (pyl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) anther (pollen-bearing part of the stamen)</li><li>duster, smock (a light, loose-fitting long coat)</li><li>dustcover (a protective hood or cover to protect joints or assemblies from dust, sand, dirt, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dustish</li></ol><p><i>пы́льн(ый)</i> (pýlʹn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dustiness</li></ol><p><i>пыльный</i> (pylʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusty</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pollen</li></ol><p><i>пыль</i> (pylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ца</i> (-ca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch grass, dog grass, quitch grass (<i>Elymus repens</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pyrьjь</i>, further from <i>&#42;pyrъ</i> (“type of wheatgrass”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьjь</i> (<i>substantivation suffix</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to stab, to jab</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torture (to intentionally inflict unnecessary pain or suffering on helpless victims)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) to ask, to inquire</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try, to attempt, to check</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pȳtàti</i> (“to ask, examine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attempt, to try</li><li>to assay</li><li>to seek</li><li>to endeavour</li></ol><p><i>пыта́ть</i> (pytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torture, torment (intentional infliction of pain or suffering)</li></ol><p><i>пыта́ть</i> (pytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inquisitive, curious, searching, keen</li></ol><p><i>пыта́ть</i> (pytátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torture chamber, torture room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) torture</li></ol><p><i>пы́тка</i> (pýtka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blaze, to burn brightly, to glow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to radiate [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘heat, fire, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to project, to radiate, to be bursting with [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘health, happiness, malice, etc.’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to emit smoke, to puff [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘on something, e.g. a cigarette or cigar’</small>]</li><li>(slang,&#32;transitive) to huff, to inhale (drugs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pyxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blaze once, to burn brightly (once) (<i>of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flash (<i>of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to puff once</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to smoke a spliff, to smoke a joint</li></ol><p><i>пы́хать</i> (pýxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p><p><i>пы́хать</i> (pýxatʹ, “to inhale (drugs)”) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to puff, to pant</li></ol><p>Related to <i>пух</i> (pux, “fuzz, down”) (originally "breath") and <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ, “to swell”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) doughnut, puff, bun (deep-fried piece of dough or batter)</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) dumpling (a plump person, especially a woman or a girl)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splendor, pomp</li></ol><p><i>пы́шный</i> (pýšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fluffy, luxuriant</li><li>magnificent, splendid, sumptuous (suggesting abundance and great expense, and having a flavour/flavor of debauchery)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>piédestal</i>, itself from Italian <i>piedestallo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Piedmontese</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piedmontese (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>play (theatrical performance)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) piece (small instrumental composition)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pièce</i>, from Late Latin <i>pettia</i>, from Gaulish <i>&#42;pettyā</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kʷezdis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pewter (alloy of tin, copper and antimony)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>pewter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) loving to drink alcohol, alcoholic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj, “drunk”)</li></ol><p><i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get intoxicated/tipsy/inebriated</li><li>to get intoxicated <i>(with joy, success etc)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk</li></ol><p><i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunkard, drunk</li><li>A card game, in which players throw a card from the bottom of their deck and whoever has the biggest card takes everything currently laying on the table.</li></ol><p><i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carousal</li><li>drinking bout, spree, binge</li></ol><p><i>пьяне́ть</i> (pʹjanétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat drunk, tipsy</li></ol><p><i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunkenness, heavy drinking</li></ol><p><i>пьяне́ть</i> (pʹjanétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be drunk often</li></ol><p>From <i>пья́нство</i> (pʹjánstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drunk, drunkard (person who is habitually drunk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пьянчу́га</i> (pʹjančúga, “drunk, drunkard”): drunk, drunkard</li></ol><p><i>пьянчу́га</i> (pʹjančúga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drunk</li><li>related to foods with alcoholic drinks</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) drunk person, drunk, drunkard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пиꙗнъ</i> (pijanŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьjanъ</i>, passive participle of <i>&#42;pijati</i>, iterative aspect of <i>&#42;piti</i> (“to drink”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective,&#32;derogatory) drunks, drunkards</li><li>(<i>abusive</i>) drunkenness</li></ol><p><i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter П, п.</li><li>The Roman letter P, p.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncommon spelling of <i>пѐйл-э́ль</i> (pèjl-élʹ, “pale ale”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pair</i>. Further from Latin <i>pār</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>music stand, lectern; desk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>pupitre</i>. Further from Latin <i>pulpitum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purée</li><li>mashed potato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>purée</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>pūrus</i> (whence ultimately also English <i>pure</i>, Russian <i>пури́зм</i> (purízm)), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewH-</i> (whence also Latin <i>putus</i>, Latin <i>pius</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пюре́</i> (pjurɛ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geometer moth (Geometridae)</li></ol><p>Thought to be from <i>пядь</i> (pjadʹ, “span; Russian unit”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica, feminine agent suffix), drawing a parallel to English <i>geometer moth</i> and translingual <i>Geometra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) An ennomine moth.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Ennominae.</li></ol><p>From <i>пяде́ница</i> (pjadénica, “geometer moth”) +&lrm; <i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj, “variegated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) span (Old Russian unit of length corresponding to the distance between the spaced ends of the thumb and index fingers, seven inches, 17.78 cm)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) small area of land</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) hand</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sole of the foot, foot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) span (Old Russian unit of length corresponding to the distance between the spaced ends of the thumb and index fingers, seven inches, 17.78 cm)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) small area of land</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) hand</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sole of the foot, foot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пядь</i> (pjadĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pędь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch tight, to put on (clothing) with difficulty</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>):</li></ol><p>From <i>пя́ло</i> (pjálo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pędlo</i> (“embroidery hoop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stare, to gaze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embroidery hoop, tambour frame</li></ol><p>From <i>пя́лить</i> (pjálitʹ, “to stretch tight, tauten”) +&lrm; <i>-цы</i> (-cy), a back-formation from <i>пя́ло</i> (pjálo, “frame onto which leather is stretched”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pędlo</i>, from <i>&#42;pęti</i> (“to stretch”) + <i>&#42;-dlo</i>.</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>п'я́льці</i> (pʺjálʹci), <i>п'я́льця</i> (pʺjálʹcja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) metacarpus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) heel; foot</li><li>wide foot of a support, mainstay, pier, or buttress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pęta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five-kopeck coin</li><li>five roubles, five dollars, etc.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) muzzle, mug (face); schnozzle, conk, honker, schnoz, snoot (nose)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пята́к</i> (pjaták): five-kopeck coin</li><li>pig's snout</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small patch of land</li></ol><p><i>пята́к</i> (pjaták) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Пятачок)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piglet (from Winnie-the-Pooh)</li></ol><p>From <i>пятачо́к</i> (pjatačók, “snout of a pig”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fifth (one of five equal parts of a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>all five fingers, palm with five fingers, five</li></ol><p><i>пя́теро</i> (pjátero) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five (in a group together), fivesome, five of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) fifteen-copeck coin</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “five”) +&lrm; <i>алты́н</i> (altýn, “an altyn, a three-copeck coin”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentathlete</li></ol><p>From <i>пятибо́рье</i> (pjatibórʹje, “pentathlon”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentathlon</li></ol><p>From <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>боро́ть(ся)</i> (borótʹ(sja), “to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) pentahedron</li></ol><p>From <i>пяти-</i> (pjati-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “five-side-er”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fifty-year period</li><li>fiftieth anniversary, fiftieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>пяти́десяти</i> (pjatídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fifty-year</li><li>fifty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>пяти́десяти</i> (pjatídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentacontagon</li></ol><p><i>пятьдеся́т</i> (pjatʹdesját) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiftieth</li></ol><p><i>пяти́десяти</i> (pjatídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) five-day</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having five stars (e.g. star system; decorations)</li><li>five-star (hotel)</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having five stars</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five-year period, half-decade</li><li>fifth anniversary, fifth birthday</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period of five years</li><li>five-year plan (<i>for the national economy of the USSR</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пятиле́тие</i> (pjatilétije) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) five-year</li><li>five-year-old</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five-year plan (<i>for the national economy of the USSR</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) five minutes</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>мину́т(а)</i> (minút(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>five-hundredth, 500th</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a deserter, a shirker, a traitor</li><li>(military slang,&#32;dated) medical supplies</li></ol><p><i>пятисо́т</i> (pjatisót) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p><p>Modeled on <i>двухсо́тый</i> (dvuxsótyj) and <i>трёхсо́тый</i> (trjoxsótyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push back, to cause to move back or back up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stick out (chest, abdomen, lip, etc.)</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;intransitive</i>) to move back, to back up</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>пята́</i> (pjatá) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move back (<i>usually slowly</i>)</li></ol><p><i>пя́тить</i> (pjátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentagon</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pentagonal (shaped like a pentagon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) five-storey building</li></ol><p><i>пятиэта́жный</i> (pjatietážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>five-story</li></ol><p><i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) heel</li></ol><p><i>пята́</i> (pjatá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) pentadecahedron</li></ol><p><i>пятна́дцать</i> (pjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fifteen-year period</li><li>fifteenth anniversary, fifteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>пятна́дцать</i> (pjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fifteen-year</li><li>fifteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>пятна́дцать</i> (pjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pentadecagon</li></ol><p><i>пятна́дцать</i> (pjatnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifteen (15)</li></ol><p>From <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>15×, fifteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tag (<i>in a game</i>)</li><li>to disgrace, to defame, to dishonor</li></ol><p><i>пятно́</i> (pjatnó) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tag (children's game)</li><li>15 puzzle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speckling, spottiness</li></ol><p><i>пятни́стый</i> (pjatnístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spotty, spotted, dappled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friday</li></ol><p>From <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica), since Friday is the fifth day of the week (counting from Monday). Compare Ukrainian <i>п'я́тниця</i> (pʺjátnycja), Old East Slavic пятъкъ, Old Church Slavonic <i>пѧтъкъ</i> (pętŭkŭ), Bulgarian <i>пе́тък</i> (pétǎk) and <i>пе́тка</i> (pétka), and Serbo-Croatian <i>пе́так</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spot, mark</li><li>blot, blemish, stain</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) blemish, stain (a defamatory fact)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пятьно</i> (pjatĭno, “brand, sign”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pętьno</i> (“trace, sign”), from <i>&#42;pęta</i> (“heel”).</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stopa</i> (“foot; footstep, footprint, trace”); Ancient Greek <i>πτέρνη</i> (ptérnē, “heel; hoof; heel of a shoe; footstep; …”); Latin <i>vestīgium</i> (“footprint, track; trace, vestige, mark, sign; sole of the foot; horseshoe; …”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пятно́</i> (pjatnó): (small) speck, spot, blot, blemish, stain</li></ol><p><i>пятно́</i> (pjatnó) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>five (a set or group with five elements)</li></ol><p>From <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achilles tendon (strong tendon in the calf of the leg)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heel; calcaneal</li></ol><p><i>пя́тка</i> (pjátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пѧтъ</i> (pętŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pętъ</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pętъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;penktas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;penkʷtós</i>..</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five (5)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;pénkʷe</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пѧть</i> (pętĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pętь</i>.</p><p>Cognate with English <i>five</i>, German <i>fünf</i>, Italian <i>cinque</i>, French <i>cinq</i>, Greek <i>πέντε</i> (pénte), Persian پَنج (panj), Sanskrit <i>पञ्च</i> (pañca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) <i>(mark)</i> excellent!, five <i>(out of five)</i>, excellent; A mark, A grade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty (50)</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ), literally “five tens”. <b>Де́сят</b> was the Old East Slavic genitive plural, which is required by numbers <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “5”) and up.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-eight (58)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-two (52)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-nine (59)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-one (51)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-five (55)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-seven (57)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-three (53)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-four (54)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty-six (56)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five hundred (500)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>5×, five times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>50×, fifty times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(mark)</i> five <i>(out of five)</i>, excellent; A mark, A grade</li><li>the figure five written out</li><li>fiver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>пёрнуть</i> (pjórnutʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) wood drill, wood bit</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;perъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to fart (noisily, as opposed to бздеть)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>перо́</i> (peró): (small) feather, plume, a plumelet</li></ol><p><i>перо́</i> (peró) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li><li>male dog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьsъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dog</li></ol><p><i>пёс</i> (pjos) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>пёс</i> (pjos): small dog, doggie</li></ol><p><i>пёс</i> (pjos) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj, “variegated, multicolored”)</li></ol><p><i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>variegated, motley</li><li>multicoloured/multicolored</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) mixed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) florid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>пьстръ</i> (pĭstrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьstrъ</i> (“variegated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пёс</i> (pjos).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пе́на</i> (péna).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пе́сня</i> (pésnja).</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pěsnь</i>, adapted to the common ending <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пету́х</i> (petúx).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>петь</i> (petʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>пигме́й</i> (pigméj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river</li><li>rouble (<i>singular or plural</i>)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>река́</i> (reká).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>рубль</i> (rublʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave, serf, bondslave (<i>in common sense and about male person, not female separately</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>рабъ</i> (rabŭ) via Old East Slavic <i>рабъ</i> (rabŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbъ</i>. Displaced Old East Slavic <i>робъ</i> (robŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) rabbi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) rhabdomyolysis (rapid breakdown of skeletal muscle tissue)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chainlink</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>сетка Рабица</i> (setka Rabica), after the inventor’s name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Rabelaisian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaveowner</li></ol><p><i>раб</i> (rab) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>владе́лец</i> (vladélec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servility, cringing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>servile, obsequious, fawning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bow and scrape, to kowtow (to behave in a servile, obsequious manner)</li></ol><p>From <i>раболе́пство</i> (rabolépstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work</li><li>labor, toil</li><li>assignment, task</li><li>job, occupation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>робота</i> (robota), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbòta</i>. By surface analysis, <i>роби́ть</i> (robítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>работа</i> (rabota), whence spelling. Doublet of <i>ро́бот</i> (róbot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘on a task’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘as an occupation or role’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with a tool or device’</small>]</li><li>to function</li><li>to labor, to toil</li><li>to be open (for business)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbotati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) to work hard, to work like a dog</li></ol><p>Literally, “to work like papa Carlo”. The expression originated after the character of hardworking woodcarver Carlo in the popular children’s motion picture "Приключе́ния Бурати́но" (The Adventures of Buratino), 1975, based on the novel "The Little Gold Key or the Adventures of Buratino" by Aleksey Tolstoy, which, in turn, is an adaptation of "The Adventures of Pinocchio" by Carlo Collodi.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be working (<i>typically in a certain condition</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>labouring, in service, timer, working</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta)</li></ol><p><i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker, blue-collar worker, employee</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbotьnikъ</i>, from <i>&#42;orbotьnъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maid, housemaid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbotьnica</i>, from <i>&#42;orbotьnъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рабо́т(ник)</i> (rabót(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>employer (person or entity which employs others)</li></ol><p>From <i>работ-</i> (rabot-, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ, “give”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”).</p><p>Alternatively <i>работ-</i> (rabot-, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>датель</i> (datelʹ, “giver”) (<i>synchronically only found in compounds</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave trade</li></ol><p><i>раб</i> (rab) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>торго́вля</i> (torgóvlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capacity for work, efficiency</li><li>functionality, operability</li></ol><p><i>работоспосо́бный</i> (rabotosposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able-bodied, able to work</li><li>efficient at work (able to work well and efficiently)</li><li>functioning (able to fulfill one's purpose, of an institution or organization)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) operating as expected (<i>of a machine or product</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;approvingly) hard worker; plodder, slogger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbotęga</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ, “to work”) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) industrious, hardworking</li></ol><p>From <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́щий</i> (-jáščij); has the form of a present active participle.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta): nasty work, nasty job</li></ol><p><i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labour aristocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) workstation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Workers’ and Peasants’</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) worker (a person who performs physical labor for hire, especially in industry, construction, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) worker; worker's (relating to people who perform physical labor for hire, especially in industry, construction, etc.)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) work, working</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) labor</li><li>working, functioning (of a device, machine, etc.); viable, feasible (of a plan, proposal, etc.)</li><li>preliminary, working, draft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orboťь</i>, from <i>&#42;orbota</i> + <i>&#42;-jь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workday, working day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working class (social class doing physical work)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workpeople</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slavishly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) slave; slavish, servile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbьskъ</i>, from <i>&#42;orbъ</i> (“slave, servant”) + <i>&#42;-ьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>раб</i> (rab) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery, thralldom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbьstvo</i>, from <i>&#42;orbъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i>. Also analyzable as <i>раб</i> (rab) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) workers' faculty, rabfak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>раб</i> (rab).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) rabbi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>rabbi</i>, and from Koine Greek <i>ῥαββί</i> (rhabbí), from (post-biblical) Hebrew <i>רַבִּי</i> (rabbi, “my master”), from <i>רַב</i> (rav, “master [of]”) +&lrm; <i>־י</i> (-i, “me”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equality, parity (fact of being equal)</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plain (an expanse of land with relatively low relief)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orvьnina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plain, plains (flat expanse of land)</li><li>flat (<i>of terrain</i>), low (<i>of relief</i>)</li></ol><p><i>равни́на</i> (ravnína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>equally, as well, as well as</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isapostolicity</li></ol><p>From <i>равноапо́стольный</i> (ravnoapóstolʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>isapostolic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) isosceles (having two sides of equal length, used especially of an isosceles triangle)</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бедро́</i> (bedró) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, equilibrium (condition of a system in which competing influences are balanced)</li><li>harmony</li><li>parity</li></ol><p>Ultimately a calque of Latin <i>aequilibrium</i>, possibly via German <i>Gleichgewicht</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves, “weight”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) equilibrium, balance</li><li>balanced</li></ol><p><i>равнове́сие</i> (ravnovésije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equinox (intersection of the ecliptic with the celestial equator)</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indifference, unconcern, disregard</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Literally, “equanimity”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>indifferently</li></ol><p><i>равноду́шный</i> (ravnodúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indifferent, apathetic (void of feeling)</li></ol><p><i>равноду́шие</i> (ravnodúšije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equivalent, equipollent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equivalent, equal in significance</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>знак</i> (znak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>evenly, uniformly</li></ol><p><i>равноме́рный</i> (ravnomérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>even, uniform</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equality (equal treatment of people irrespective of social or cultural differences)</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal in rights, possessing/enjoying equal rights</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пра́во</i> (právo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equipotent, equally strong</li><li>equal <i>(to)</i>, tantamount <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Ultimately a calque of Latin <i>aequipollens</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>си́ла</i> (síla, “strength,power”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) equilateral</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) equilateral triangle (a triangle having all three sides equal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal in value, equivalent</li></ol><p><i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal (the same in all respects)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>равьнъ</i> (ravĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orvьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equalize</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to equate [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to compete, to match</li><li>to be equal, to be equivalent, to amount</li><li>to emulate, to follow the example</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to dress, to align oneself</li></ol><p><i>равня́ть</i> (ravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ragout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ragoût</i>. Further from Latin <i>regustō</i>, from Latin <i>re-</i> + <i>gustō</i>, akin to Latin <i>gustus</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵéwstus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵews-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a boor, a hillbilly.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;offensive) a person from western Ukraine.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;offensive) a supporter of the Euromaidan protests.</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;offensive,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;by extension) a Ukrainian nationalist, especially one seen as against Russian interests.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>рагу́ль</i> (rahúlʹ), used since the 1960's among Russians in Lviv, and spread all over western Ukraine. Various etymologies suggest that this term was originally from рог (rog, “a horn”) or рога́тка (rogátka, “a slingshot, a turnpike”). The latter referred to barriers that kept outsiders from cities before they were annexed into the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic.</p><p>Sense 3 was popularised by pro-Russian opponents of the Euromaidan protests, who perceive supporters to be mostly from western Ukraine.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) rad (non-SI unit of absorbed radiation dose)</li><li>radian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radъ</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>радиан</i> (radian).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rada, Rada (a parliamentary body in a number of Slavic countries)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ра́да</i> (ráda), from Old Polish <i>rada</i>, from Old Czech <i>rada</i>, from Middle High German <i>rāt</i>, from Old High German <i>rāt</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;rād</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рада)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rada (parliament of Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>radar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) radio</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) walkie-talkie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to oblige, to take care</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rajah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>राजा</i> (rājā) / Urdu راجا (rājā), from Sanskrit <i>राजन्</i> (rājan, “king, prince”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for the sake of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>for God's sake, please (<i>expressing an urgent or heartfelt request</i>)</li><li>by all means, please, feel free, of course, go ahead (<i>giving permission or expressing indifference</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radial</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>radial</i> or French <i>radial</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heatsink</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) radiator</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) grille</li></ol><p>From German <i>Radiator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radiation</li></ol><p><i>радиа́ция</i> (radiácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ionizing radiation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>radiatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) diligent</li><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) zealous</li></ol><p><i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radio</li><li>radio-equipped</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) radical</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) radical</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) radical (person with radical opinions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radicalization (the process or result of radicalizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radicalism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>radicalisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>radically (in various senses)</li></ol><p><i>радика́льный</i> (radikálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) radiculitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio, tuner</li><li>Romeo (<i>R</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Radio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>radio- (radiation&#59; radio (broadcasting))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radioactivity</li></ol><p><i>радиоакти́вный</i> (radioaktívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radioactive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>radioaktiv</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio set</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>аппара́т</i> (apparát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio equipment</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>аппарату́ра</i> (apparatúra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio broadcasting</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ние</i> (veščánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio wave</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио-</i> (rádio-) +&lrm; <i>волна́</i> (volná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiogram, wireless (message), radio-telegram, aerogram</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́мма</i> (-grámma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio jargon (numerous code expressions used in amateur radio communications to save time)</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>жарго́н</i> (žargón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) radiosonde</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from French <i>radiosonde</i>. Form influenced by <i>зонд</i> (zond). By surface analysis, <i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зонд</i> (zond).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio interferometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio interferometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radiocommunications</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>коммуника́ция</i> (kommunikácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiogram (combination radio and record player)</li><li>allseed flax (<i>Radiola linoides</i>, syn. <i>Linum radiola</i>)</li></ol><p>Originally a trademark of the RCA Corporation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiologist, radiation therapist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radar</li></ol><p>From a word related to English <i>radiolocation</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radar gunsight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>navigation</i>) radio beacon</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>мая́к</i> (maják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio silence, absence of radio signals</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ние</i> (molčánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio transmitter</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>переда́тчик</i> (peredátčik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio broadcast</li></ol><p><i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>переда́ча</i> (peredáča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio, receiver, receiving set, radio, radio set</li><li>wireless receiver, wireless</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио-</i> (rádio-) +&lrm; <i>приёмник</i> (prijómnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radio wave transparent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio industry</li></ol><p><i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>промы́шленность</i> (promýšlennostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio communication/contact</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио</i> (rádio) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>связь</i> (svjazʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio station, broadcasting station</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио-</i> (rádio-) +&lrm; <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cordless telephone</li><li>radiotelephone</li></ol><p><i>ра́дио-</i> (rádio-) +&lrm; <i>телефо́н</i> (telefón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radio engineering</li></ol><p><i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radio frequency</li></ol><p><i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>частота́</i> (častotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>radio-electronic</li></ol><p><i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>электро́нный</i> (elektrónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to radio (to send a message by radio)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radio operator, radioman</li><li>telegraphist, telegrapher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radio operator(s)</li></ol><p><i>ради́ст</i> (radíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) radius (line segment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>radius</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>poloměr</i> (i.e. <i>**полуме́рник</i>, akin to <i>ме́ра</i> (méra)), Slovene <i>polmer</i>, Macedonian <i>полупречник</i> (poluprečnik), Serbo-Croatian <i>полупречник</i>, Polish <i>promień</i> (note Belarusian <i>пра́мень</i> (prámjenʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gladden, to please, to rejoice, to make happy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, to be glad, to be happy</li></ol><p><i>ра́довать</i> (rádovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radon</li></ol><p><i>радо́н</i> (radón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>joyfully, gladly, joyously</li></ol><p><i>ра́достный</i> (rádostnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful, glad, joyous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radostьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ра́дость</i> (rádostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, delight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>радость</i> (radostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) rainbow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radǫga</i>. The first element more likely derives from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;radъ</i> (“glad, dear, willing”) than folk etymology’s Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rajь</i>. Compare Russian <i>весёлка</i> (vesjólka, “rainbow”) (<i>dialectal</i>) related to <i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj, “merry, cheerful, happy, convivial, gleeful, lighthearted”), Belarusian <i>вясёлка</i> (vjasjólka, “rainbow”). The second element from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dǫga</i> would be cognate with Lithuanian <i>dangùs</i> (“sky, heaven”) and perhaps further Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tunglą</i> (“heavenly body”) together from a tentative Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;denǵʰ-</i>.</p><p>Slavic cognates include Ukrainian <i>ра́йдуга</i> (rájduha).</p><p>A borrowing from Alanic, compare Ossetian <i>ӕрдын</i> (ærdyn, “bow; rainbow”), Classical Persian درونه (durōna, “rainbow”) is also possible.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) iris (colored part of the eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rainbow</li><li>cheerful</li><li>optimistic, bright, roseate, rosy</li></ol><p><i>радуга</i> (raduga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keel-billed toucan (<i>Ramphastos sulfuratus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ра́дужный</i> (rádužnyj, “rainbow”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>тука́н</i> (tukán, “toucan”), literally “rainbow toucan”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cordiality, kindliness, hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;радоду́шие</i> (&#42;radodúšije) by haplology, from <i>рад</i> (rad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>душа́</i> (dušá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cordially (in a friendly manner)</li></ol><p><i>раду́шный</i> (radúšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cordial, kindly, hearty</li><li>hospitable</li></ol><p><i>раду́шие</i> (radúšije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ра́дио</i> (rádio, “radio”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>радиовеща́ние</i> (radioveščánije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rage, passion, frenzy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (an instance or occurrence)</li><li>one (<i>in counting</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) once, one day, once upon a time</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) if, since; as long as</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>разъ</i> (razŭ, “strike”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;razъ</i>. Cognate to <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ), <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ). For the semantic shift, compare Polish <i>raz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once and for all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) asunder, apart</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) dis-, di-, un-</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) enlarged action</li><li>(<i>used with verbs</i>) with reflexives, giving free rein to</li><li>(used with verbs,&#32;in the negative) not quite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>роз-</i> (roz-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz-</i>. The spelling "раз-" was reinforced by Old Church Slavonic <i>раз-</i> (raz-), as well as vowel reduction, which is also reflected in Belarusian <i>раз-</i> (raz-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to dissect, to anatomize</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>анатоми́ровать</i> (anatomírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dilute, to thin</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dilution (action)</li></ol><p><i>разба́вить</i> (razbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dilute</li></ol><p><i>разба́вить</i> (razbávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squandering (<i>of something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разбаза́ривать</i> (razbazárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander</li></ol><p><i>разбаза́рить</i> (razbazáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>база́рить</i> (bazáritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reveal, to give away (a secret)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake up, to mix, to stir</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to loosen</li></ol><p><i>разболта́ть</i> (razboltátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) unban, unbanning</li></ol><p>From <i>разба́нить</i> (razbánitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to unban</li></ol><p><i>разба́нить</i> (razbánitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to unban</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ба́нить</i> (bánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running start</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter</li><li>to take a run, to run up <i>(before jumping, diving, etc)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start running strenuously</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start running and not stop</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter</li><li>to take a run, to run up <i>(before jumping, diving, etc)</i></li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch (a wound), causing pain</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disturb, to stir up, to agitate (one's heart or soul, etc.), causing one to relive painful experiences</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>береди́ть</i> (beredítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to crash, to crush, to smash, to shatter</li><li>to defeat, to beat, to smash</li><li>to divide, to break (down)</li><li>to lay out <i>(park)</i>, to mark out</li><li>to pitch <i>(a tent)</i>, to set up <i>(a tent or a camp)</i></li><li><i>a body part</i> to knock, to hurt badly, to break, to fracture, to damage severely</li><li><i>polygraphy</i> to space out</li></ol><p><i>разби́ть</i> (razbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to crash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break up, to split, to divide</li><li>to collapse, to die (e.g. of dreams)</li><li>to get seriously hurt</li></ol><p><i>разбива́ть</i> (razbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splitting, dividing, partitioning, decomposition, fragmentation</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) partition</li><li>(<i>telecommunications</i>) splitting</li><li>(<i>information technology</i>) decomposition</li></ol><p><i>разби́ть</i> (razbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing, examination, investigation (proceeding at which discussions are heard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismantle, to disassemble, to take apart, to deconstruct</li><li>to analyse, to parse</li><li>to agitate, to disturb, to trouble</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal with, to sort out</li><li>to understand, to see into, to work with [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to figure out, to work out</li></ol><p><i>разбира́ть</i> (razbirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) broken heart (a feeling of grief or loss, especially after a failed romantic relationship)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) jaded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to crash, to crush, to smash, to shatter</li><li>to defeat, to beat, to smash</li><li>to divide, to break down</li><li>to lay out (park), to mark out</li><li>to pitch (a tent), to set up (a tent or a camp)</li><li>to knock, to hurt badly, to break, to fracture, to damage severely (a body part)</li><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) to space out</li><li>(<i>billiard</i>) to make the first shot</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to crash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break up, to split, to divide</li><li>to collapse, to die</li><li>to get seriously hurt</li></ol><p><i>разби́ть</i> (razbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unblock, to release, to unlock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;humorous,&#32;puristic) menu</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) breakdown (of facts, values, etc.), detailed analysis</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>блюдо</i> (bljudo) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow rich</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>богате́ть</i> (bogatétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery, brigandage</li><li>(<i>law</i>) armed robbery, aggravated robbery (<i>robbery with the threat or use of dangerous or deadly force</i>)</li><li>(<i>billiards</i>) break shot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>разбои</i> (razboi), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzbojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brigand, highwayman, robber</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rogue, wretch</li></ol><p><i>разбо́й</i> (razbój) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) female rogue, wretch</li></ol><p><i>разбо́й(ник)</i> (razbój(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob; to maraud; to plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>разбо́йник</i> (razbójnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) robber; robber's</li><li>predatory, piratical</li></ol><p><i>разбо́йник</i> (razbójnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall ill (badly or continuously)</li><li>to start to hurt/ache (of a body part)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>боле́ть</i> (bolétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstable, unsteady</li><li>unruly, disorganized</li><li>soft, loose (<i>of movements or gait</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reveal, to give away (a secret)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shake up, to mix, to stir</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to loosen</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>болта́ть</i> (boltátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy by bombing</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бомби́ть</i> (bombítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>analysis, review, critique, investigation, examination, inquiry</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) parsing</li><li>(<i>law</i>) trial, hearing</li><li>selectivity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzborъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorting out</li><li>disassembling, dismantling, taking apart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) showdown</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разобра́ть</i> (razobrátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>разбор-</i> (razbor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>разбер-</i> (razber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legible, readable</li><li>scrupulous, fastidious</li><li>particular, choosy</li></ol><p><i>разбо́р</i> (razbór) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter, to throw about, to strew</li></ol><p><i>разброса́ть</i> (razbrosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander off in different directions (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разбрести́сь</i> (razbrestísʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander off in different directions (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>брести́</i> (brestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) dispersal</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) dispersed location</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) disorder, disarray, confusion</li></ol><p>Constructed as if deverbal from nonexistent <i>&#42;разброди́ть</i> (&#42;razbrodítʹ); compare <i>разбрести́сь</i> (razbrestísʹ, “to wander off in different directions”), and the relationship between simplex concrete verb <i>брести́</i> (brestí, “to wander, to stroll”) and its abstract verb equivalent <i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispersion, spread</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разброса́ть</i> (razbrosátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter, to throw about, to strew</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>броса́ть</i> (brosátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle, to spray, to splatter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake up, to awake, to waken</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>буди́ть</i> (budítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;nonstandard) to take one's shoes off</li></ol><p>From a rebracketing of <i>обу́ться</i> (obútʹsja) as <i>о</i>- + <i>буться</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to distend</li><li>to grow excessively/immoderately, to inflate</li></ol><p><i>разбу́хнуть</i> (razbúxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, to distend</li><li>to grow excessively/immoderately, to inflate</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бу́хнуть</i> (búxnutʹ, “to swell”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to rage (<i>harder and harder</i>), to go wild (<i>of a storm, a drunk person, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>бушева́ть</i> (buševátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disintegration, breakdown</li><li>disorganization, disorder</li><li>open-air bazaar</li><li><i>(wheel)</i> camber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull down</li><li>to disorganize, to mess up</li></ol><p><i>развали́ть</i> (razvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tumble down, to collapse</li><li>to go/fall to pieces, to break down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sprawl, to lounge</li></ol><p><i>разва́ливать</i> (razválivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruins, ruin (construction withered by time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sprawling; broad</li></ol><p><i>развал-</i> (razval-) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull down</li><li>to disorganize, to mess up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tumble down, to collapse</li><li>to go/fall to pieces, to break down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sprawl, to lounge</li></ol><p><i>развали́ть</i> (razvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clunker, wreck, jalopy (anything in poor condition or of poor quality—often an old, dilapidated car or building)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) perhaps (<i>often used in rhetorical questions</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) only</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) unless</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>развѣ</i> (razvě, “only, besides”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzvě</i>.</p><p>Displaced Old East Slavic <i>розвѣ</i> (rozvě)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) except perhaps/maybe, aside from</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) only, only if, not unless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wave (<i>e.g. a flag</i>)</li><li>to cause to flutter or blow about</li></ol><p><i>разве́ять</i> (razvéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flutter, to fly, to stream</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to scatter, to disperse, to dissipate</li></ol><p><i>развева́ть</i> (razvevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out <i>(about)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reconnoitre, to reconnoiter</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) to prospect, to explore</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligence (information about the enemy)</li></ol><p><i>разве́дывательные да́нные</i> (razvédyvatelʹnyje dánnyje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeding, rearing, cultivation</li><li>swinging open, opening</li></ol><p><i>развед-</i> (razved-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconnaissance, intelligence</li><li>scouting, exploration</li></ol><p><i>разве́дать</i> (razvédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligence service; intelligence (a department, agency or unit designed to gather information, usually secret, about the enemy or about hostile activities)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>разве́дывательная слу́жба</i> (razvédyvatelʹnaja slúžba, “intelligence service”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) scout</li><li>intelligence officer</li><li>(military,&#32;inanimate) reconnaissance plane or ship</li><li>prospector</li></ol><p><i>разве́дать</i> (razvédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a female scout</li><li>a female prospector</li></ol><p>разведать + -чица</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconnaissance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reconnaissance, intelligence</li></ol><p><i>разве́дывать</i> (razvédyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out <i>(about)</i></li><li>(<i>military</i>) to reconnoitre, to reconnoiter</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) to prospect, to explore</li></ol><p><i>разве́дать</i> (razvédatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) divorcée</li></ol><p><i>разводи́ться</i> (razvodítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ёнка</i> (-jónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divorcee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to transport (multiple people or things to their respective destinations)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to make impassable</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scatter, to disperse</li><li>to dispel</li><li>to cast away, to lay aside, to put out of mind</li></ol><p><i>разве́ять</i> (razvéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to scatter, to disperse, to dissipate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disappear, to be dispelled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be distracted from one's cares, to have fun</li></ol><p><i>разве́ивать</i> (razvéivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unroll, to unwind, to unfold, to unwrap</li><li>to show, to display</li><li>to start <i>(up)</i></li><li>to put on a wide scale, to widen, to broaden, to expand</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to deploy, to extend</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to expand <i>(into)</i></li><li>to turn, to swing about, to swing around, to slew <i>(about)</i>, to make a U-turn</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around, to swing about / around; to make a U-turn (vehicle); to slew (about)</li><li>to be deployed (of troops)</li><li>to come unrolled / unfolded / unwrapped</li><li>to spread, to expand</li><li>to display oneself</li></ol><p><i>разверну́ть</i> (razvernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to brighten up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>весели́ть</i> (veselítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cheer up, to become cheerful</li></ol><p><i>развесели́ть</i> (razveselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;ironic) klyukva; ignorant stereotypes and tall tales, especially in Western depictions of Russia</li></ol><p>Literally, “highbush cranberry”. Attested early 20th century. Derives from a 19th century joke about a French teller of tall tales who claims to have rested in the shade of a cranberry plant during a supposed visit to Russia; in reality, cranberries in the wild are tiny dwarf plants.</p><p>Nabokov pointed out that the expression literally translates to “highbush cranberry” (<i>Viburnum opulus</i>) in English, but in Russian (unlike English) it appears absurd because Russian uses entirely different terms for tall shrubs in the genus <i>Viburnum</i> (<i>кали́на</i> (kalína)) and dwarf plants in the subgenus <i>Oxycoccus</i> (<i>клю́ква</i> (kljúkva)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spread-out, sprawling; spreading (<i>of a tree</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разве́сить</i> (razvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh out</li><li>to hang <i>(in different places)</i></li><li><i>(boughs)</i> to spread, to stretch out</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take along, to bring; to conduct</li><li>to part, to move apart, to pull apart, to separate</li><li>to dilute, to mix</li><li>to dissolve</li><li>to start</li><li>to divorce</li><li>to mount, to relieve, to post (sentinels)</li><li>to rear, to breed (animals)</li><li>to plant, to cultivate, to grow (plants)</li><li>to make, to plant, to lay out (a park, garden, etc.)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to cheat, to swindle</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be divorced (from), to divorce, to obtain a divorce (with) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to grow in number, to breed</li></ol><p><i>развести́</i> (razvestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to branch out, to cause to ramify</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to divide into separate industries</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-ветви́ть</i> (-vetvítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to branch out, to ramify</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ветви́ться</i> (vetvítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bifurcation; branching; ramification</li></ol><p><i>разветви́ть</i> (razvetvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to branch out, to cause to ramify</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to divide into separate industries</li></ol><p><i>разветви́ть</i> (razvetvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to branch out, to ramify, <i>(road)</i> to fork, to divide</li></ol><p><i>разветви́ться</i> (razvetvítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extensive, far-flung</li><li>ramified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang <i>(in different places)</i></li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh out</li><li>to hang <i>(in different places)</i></li><li><i>(boughs)</i> to spread, to stretch out</li></ol><p>From <i>разве́сить</i> (razvésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ); in the meaning "to hang (something) out", also from <i>разве́шать</i> (razvéšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scatter, to disperse</li><li>to dispel</li><li>to cast away, to lay aside, to put out of mind</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wave (<i>e.g. a flag</i>)</li><li>to cause to flutter or blow about</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ять</i> (véjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to scatter, to disperse, to dissipate</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to disappear, to be dispelled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be distracted from one's cares, to have fun</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to flutter, to blow about</li></ol><p><i>разве́ять</i> (razvéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to develop, to evolve, to advance</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to untwist, to unwind</li></ol><p><i>разви́ть</i> (razvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to develop (to progress)</li><li>to untwist; to come unwound; to come uncurled, to lose its curl (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol><p><i>развива́ть</i> (razvivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>developing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fork, bifurcation</li><li>forked crown, furcation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fork (<i>in a road</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ви́лка</i> (vílka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>development, growth, progress</li><li>maturity</li><li>continuation</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Entwicklung</i>, which itself is a calque of French <i>développement</i>. By surface analysis, <i>разви́ть</i> (razvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>developed, mature, cultivated, advanced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>development, maturity</li></ol><p>From <i>развито́й</i> (razvitój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), with stress as in the short masculine singular form <i>ра́звит</i> (rázvit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>developed (mature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to develop, to evolve, to advance</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to untwist, to unwind</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to develop (to progress)</li><li>to untwist; to come unwound; to come uncurled, to lose its curl (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разви́ть</i> (razvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) entertainment, entertaining</li></ol><p><i>развлека́ть</i> (razvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain, to amuse</li><li>to divert</li></ol><p><i>развле́к-</i> (razvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have fun, to have a good time, to amuse oneself</li></ol><p><i>развлека́ть</i> (razvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entertainment, amusement (entertaining activity or process of being entertained)</li><li>recreation, pastime (entertaining activity)</li></ol><p><i>развле́чь</i> (razvléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain, to amuse</li><li>to divert</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ), a calque of French <i>distraire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have fun, to have a good time, to amuse oneself</li></ol><p><i>развле́чь</i> (razvléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divorce</li><li>opening, raising, swinging open</li><li>(<i>military</i>) relief, mounting, posting</li><li>breeding</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) free designs, broad pattern</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) stains</li><li>(slang,&#32;informal) scam, cheating</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) hoaxing; making a practical joke</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разводи́ть</i> (razvodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take along, to bring; to conduct</li><li>to part, to move apart, to pull apart, to separate</li><li>to dilute, to mix</li><li>to dissolve</li><li>to start</li><li>to divorce</li><li>to mount, to relieve, to post (sentinels)</li><li>to rear, to breed (animals)</li><li>to plant, to cultivate, to grow (plants)</li><li>to make, to plant, to lay out (a park, garden, etc.)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to cheat, to swindle</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be divorced (from), to divorce, to obtain a divorce (with) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to grow in number, to breed</li></ol><p><i>разводи́ть</i> (razvodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing, setting</li><li>saw set</li><li>interconnections, wiring layout</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) con, humbug (hoax, prank, or jest)</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-вод-</i> (-vod-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to transport (multiple people or things to their respective destinations)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to make impassable</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to excite, to agitate</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>волнова́ть</i> (volnovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get excited/agitated</li></ol><p><i>разволнова́ть</i> (razvolnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unroll, to unwind, to unfold, to unwrap</li><li>to develop</li><li>to show, to display</li><li>to start <i>(up)</i></li><li>to put on a wide scale, to widen, to broaden, to expand</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to deploy, to extend</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to expand <i>(into)</i></li><li>to turn, to swing about, to swing around, to slew <i>(about)</i>, to make a U-turn</li><li>to play havoc <i>(among, with)</i>, to turn upside down</li></ol><p><i>развороти́ть</i> (razvorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn around, to swing about / around; to make a U-turn (vehicle); to slew (about)</li><li>to be deployed (of troops) = развёртываться (razvjórtyvatʹsja)</li><li>to come unrolled / unfolded / unwrapped = развёртываться (razvjórtyvatʹsja)</li><li>to spread, to expand</li><li>to display oneself</li></ol><p><i>развора́чивать</i> (razvoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal (in parts or regularly), to sack, to embezzle, to plunder</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ворова́ть</i> (vorovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal (in parts or regularly), to sack, to embezzle, to plunder</li></ol><p><i>разворова́ть</i> (razvorovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>U-turn</li><li>centerfold (single sheet of paper that forms the middle two pages of a magazine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wreak havoc <i>(of)</i>, to play havoc <i>(among, with)</i>, to turn upside down</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debauch, debauchery, depravity, dissipation, lechery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debaucher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to debauch, to corrupt</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libertine, debauchee, rake, profligate</li></ol><p><i>разврати́ть</i> (razvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fornicate, to whore around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissolute, lewd, lecherous</li></ol><p><i>разврати́ть</i> (razvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to debauch, to corrupt</li></ol><p><i>разврати́ть</i> (razvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption, depravation</li></ol><p><i>разврати́ть</i> (razvratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie, to unbind, to undo, to unleash</li><li>to liberate <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to start</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to untie, to come untied</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to disassociate, to free oneself [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something burdensome or interfering’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>развяза́ть</i> (razvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The conclusion or resolution of a plot.</li><li>outcome, issue, upshot</li><li>traffic interchange, intersection</li><li>(<i>electrotechnology</i>) isolating device, cross coupling, decoupling, uncoupler</li></ol><p><i>развяза́ть</i> (razvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudence, insolence, cheekiness, undue familiarity, swagger, abandon, forwardness</li><li>unceremoniousness, familiarity</li></ol><p><i>развя́зный</i> (razvjáznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheeky, impudent, insolent</li><li>unceremonious, familiar</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) overly relaxed</li></ol><p><i>развяза́ть</i> (razvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untying</li></ol><p><i>развя́зывать</i> (razvjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie, to unbind, to undo, to unleash</li><li>to liberate <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to start</li></ol><p><i>развяза́ть</i> (razvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to untie, to come untied</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to disassociate, to free oneself [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from someone/something burdensome or interfering’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>развя́зывать</i> (razvjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extensive, large-scale, full-scale, all-out</li><li>detailed</li><li>(<i>military</i>) deployed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apportionment, allotment, assessment</li><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) making-up, make-up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unrolling, unwinding, unfolding, unwrapping</li><li>development, expansion</li><li>(<i>military</i>) deployment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unroll, to unwind, to unfold, to unwrap</li><li>to show, to display</li><li>to start <i>(up)</i></li><li>to put on a wide scale, to widen, to broaden, to expand</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to deploy, to extend</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to expand <i>(into)</i></li><li>to turn, to swing about, to swing around, to slew <i>(about)</i>, to make a U-turn</li></ol><p><i>разверну́ть</i> (razvernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become undone, to become unrolled/unfolded</li><li>to develop</li><li>(<i>military</i>) (or other services / institutions) to deploy</li></ol><p><i>развёртывать</i> (razvjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>разве</i> (razve).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unravel</li><li>to guess, to divine</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solution, answer</li></ol><p><i>разгада́ть</i> (razgadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unravel</li><li>to guess, to divine</li></ol><p><i>разгада́ть</i> (razgadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height <i>(of)</i>, high point <i>(of)</i></li><li>scoring, erosion (<i>of a weapon barrel</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to straighten, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to straighten, to straighten up, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>разгиба́ть</i> (razgibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slob</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, formed like <i>разгуля́й</i> (razguljáj, “fun place”), <i>растрепа́й</i> (rastrepáj, “ragamuffin”), etc.; possibly based in <i>гиль</i> (gilʹ, “confusion”). Per Trubachev, however, quoting Popov, derived from Tatar <i>уразгильды</i> (urazgilʹdı), <i>уразгильдей</i> (urazgilʹdey), also <i>разгильдей</i> (razgilʹdey), <i>разгильдяй</i> (razgilʹdyay).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heedlessness, slovenliness</li></ol><p><i>разгильдя́й</i> (razgilʹdjáj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expatiate, to spout, to rant (to speak tediously and at length)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth with a hot iron</li><li>to straighten out the folds/wrinkles/etc. of</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth with a hot iron</li><li>to straighten out the folds/wrinkles/etc. of</li></ol><p><i>разгла́дить</i> (razgláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal, to disclose (<i>something secret or unseemly, to all or many</i>)</li><li>to announce</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гласи́ть</i> (glasítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal, to disclose (<i>something secret or unseemly, to all or many</i>)</li><li>to announce</li></ol><p><i>разгласи́ть</i> (razglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disclosure, divulgation</li></ol><p>From <i>разгласи́ть</i> (razglasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make out, to discern, to descry</li><li>to detect, to discern</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, scrutinizing</li></ol><p><i>разгля́дывать</i> (razgljádyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, to view, to scrutinize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, irate (<i>experiencing anger; of a person</i>)</li><li>angry, irate (<i>expressing anger; of a look, words, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger, to enrage, to incense</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гнев</i> (gnev) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get angry</li></ol><p><i>разгне́вать</i> (razgnévatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to speak, to talk, to converse (with)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be on speaking terms (with)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dissuade (someone)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to get (someone) talking</li></ol><p><i>разговори́ть</i> (razgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal,&#32;only&#32;imperfective) to want/be in the mood to talk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk eagerly</li></ol><p><i>разгова́ривать</i> (razgovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) breaking fast</li></ol><p><i>разгове́ть</i> (razgovétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije), with the root word made of раз- (raz-) + гове́ть (govétʹ, “to fast”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to break one's fast</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гове́ть</i> (govétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to break one's fast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk, conversation</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rumours, talk</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разговори́ть</i> (razgovorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get (someone) talking; engage (others) to chat; draw in collocutors to make conversation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dissuade</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause an interlocutor to speak more fluently in language; make a conversant more engaged in the conversation, able to converse more easily</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;perfective) to enter into a conversation <i>(with)</i>, to find plenty to talk about <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak more eagerly, to warm to one's topic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become more fluent <i>(in a language)</i>, to speak more easily/fluently</li></ol><p><i>разговори́ть</i> (razgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phrasebook</li></ol><p>From <i>разгово́р</i> (razgovór) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colloquial (relating to oral communication language)</li><li>conversational, spoken</li></ol><p><i>разгово́р</i> (razgovór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, loquacious</li></ol><p><i>разговори́ть</i> (razgovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>разгово́р</i> (razgovór, “conversation, talking, gossip”)</li></ol><p><i>разгово́р</i> (razgovór) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispersal, dispersion</li><li>acceleration</li><li>gap, spacing</li><li>shim, wedge</li><li>(computing,&#32;informal) overclocking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disperse, to scatter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to banish, to dismiss (many people)</li><li>to disband (an organization)</li><li>to cause (a feeling) to pass</li><li>to speed up (something)</li><li>to race (a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>разгоня́ть</i> (razgonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to partition off, fence off</li></ol><p><i>разгороди́ть</i> (razgorodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flame up, to flare up, to get very hot</li><li>to flare up, to run high</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flame up, to flare up, to get very hot</li><li>to flare up, to run high</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to excite</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>горячи́ть</i> (gorjačítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get hot</li><li>to be flushed <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>разгорячи́ть</i> (razgorjačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ransack, to plunder, to pillage, to loot</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гра́бить</i> (grábitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plundering, pillaging, ransacking, looting</li></ol><p><i>разгра́бить</i> (razgrábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delimitation, demarcation</li><li>differentiation, discrimination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shovel, to rake (<i>to the side</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разгреб-</i> (razgreb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shovel, to rake (<i>to the side</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>грести́</i> (grestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, defeat, downfall, rout</li><li>discord, chaos, mayhem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smash up, to raid, to sack, to loot</li><li>to rout, to smash, to pound, to defeat, to destroy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to attack, to pan, to inveigh <i>(against)</i>, to fulminate <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>громи́ть</i> (gromítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to discharge</li><li>to relieve</li></ol><p><i>разгрузи́ть</i> (razgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload, to discharge</li><li>to relieve</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unloading</li><li>relief</li></ol><p><i>разгрузи́ть</i> (razgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revelry, debauch, bacchanalia, saturnalia</li><li>(wild) outburst, spree</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гул</i> (gul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to stroll about/around, to walk about/around</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to walk around unhindered, to be on the loose</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to spread around, to circulate (a rumor, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to blow</li><li>to keep from falling asleep, to wake up</li><li>to cast away, to lay aside, to put out of mind</li></ol><p><i>разгуля́ть</i> (razguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give oneself free rein</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to binge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become very powerful (<i>of a blizzard, a wave, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stop wanting to sleep</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become clearer, to clear up (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разгу́ливать</i> (razgúlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep from falling asleep, to wake up</li><li>to cast away, to lay aside, to put out of mind</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>гуля́ть</i> (guljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to give oneself free rein</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to binge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become very powerful (<i>of a blizzard, a wave, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stop wanting to sleep</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become clearer, to clear up (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разгуля́ть</i> (razguljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand out, to distribute, to give out, to pass out</li></ol><p><i>разда́ть</i> (razdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, to ring out (usually unexpectedly), to go off (e.g. telephone, gunshot)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to give way, to make way; to move aside</li><li>to expand, to become wide; to gain weight</li></ol><p><i>раздава́ть</i> (razdavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, to squash</li><li>to overwhelm</li><li>to suppress</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squash</li></ol><p><i>раздави́ть</i> (razdavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) distribution, dispensing, dispensation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hand out, to distribute</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, to ring out (usually unexpectedly), to go off (e.g. telephone, gunshot)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to give way, to make way; to move aside</li><li>to expand, to become wide; to gain weight</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution, dispensing, dispensation</li><li>expansion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bisect, to divide into two, to bifurcate</li><li>to make appear double</li></ol><p><i>раздвои́ть</i> (razdvoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move apart</li><li>to slide apart</li><li>to pull/draw apart</li><li>to extend, to expand</li></ol><p><i>раздвиг-</i> (razdvig-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to expand</li><li>to make way (<i>of a crowd</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to part (<i>of a curtain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раздвига́ть</i> (razdvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move apart</li><li>to slide apart</li><li>to pull/draw apart</li><li>to extend, to expand</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to expand</li><li>to make way (<i>of a crowd</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to part (<i>of a curtain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раздви́нуть</i> (razdvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bifurcation; dichotomy; a splitting in two</li></ol><p><i>раздвои́ть</i> (razdvoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bifurcation</li></ol><p><i>раздво́енный</i> (razdvójennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forked, bifurcated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bisect, to divide into two, to bifurcate</li><li>to make appear double</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>двои́ть</i> (dvoítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) changing room, locker room</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cloakroom, checkroom</li></ol><p><i>раздева́ть</i> (razdevátʹ, “to undress”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloakroom</li></ol><p><i>раздева́ть</i> (razdevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undress (to remove someone’s clothing)</li></ol><p><i>разде́ть</i> (razdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off one’s clothes, to undress, to disrobe, to get undressed</li><li>to strip (down to one’s underwear)</li><li>to take off one’s coat</li></ol><p><i>раздева́ть</i> (razdevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, section</li><li>column (newspaper column), rubric</li><li>subarea</li><li>category</li><li>division, partition, allotment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process, to prepare</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to treat the surface of, to apply a treatment or finish to</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do (something) expertly or dazzlingly</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to strongly criticize</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) to beat violently, to damage</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to evade (something unpleasant)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pay off, to settle accounts with</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat severely, to punish</li></ol><p><i>разде́лать</i> (razdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, separation</li><li>discrimination, differentiation</li></ol><p><i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) division of labour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dividing, separating (of things that divide or separate)</li></ol><p><i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) partitive (partitive case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide</li><li>to separate</li><li>to share (to have in common)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide <i>(into)</i></li><li>to separate, to part</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to divide</li></ol><p><i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>processing; dressing (<i>of meat</i>); handling; cutting</li><li>something processed</li><li>finishing (<i>of a surface, of furniture</i>)</li><li>fire-retardant brickwork around a stovepipe</li><li>(<i>cycling</i>) individual time trial</li></ol><p><i>разде́лать</i> (razdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting board or chopping board (a sturdy board, upon which one chops and prepares food)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chopping; carving</li></ol><p><i>разде́лка</i> (razdélka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to process, to prepare</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to treat the surface of, to apply a treatment or finish to</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do (something) expertly or dazzlingly</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to strongly criticize</li><li>(figurative,&#32;low colloquial) to beat violently, to damage</li></ol><p><i>разде́лать</i> (razdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to evade (something unpleasant)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pay off, to settle accounts with (someone)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat severely, to punish</li></ol><p><i>разде́лывать</i> (razdélyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, distinctly</li><li>clearly (<i>of speech</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разде́льный</i> (razdélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separate, distinct (followed by "from": not together with)</li></ol><p><i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide</li><li>to separate</li><li>to share (to have in common)</li></ol><p><i>раздели́ть</i> (razdelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide <i>(into)</i></li><li>to separate, to part</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to divide</li></ol><p><i>разделя́ть</i> (razdeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undress (to remove someone’s clothing)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>деть</i> (detʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undress (oneself), to get undressed</li></ol><p><i>разде́ть</i> (razdétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear up</li><li>to tear <i>(apart)</i>, to rend, to split</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gain weight, to get fat</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>добре́ть</i> (dobrétʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>роздобрі́ти</i> (rozdobríty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to procure, to get</li></ol><p><i>раздобы́ть</i> (razdobýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to procure, to get</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>добы́ть</i> (dobýtʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;derogatory) no-account, goof</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to break using multiple blows</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to destroy by using sloppily</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to scold, to criticize</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>долба́ть</i> (dolbátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expanse</li><li>freedom, liberty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free; carefree</li><li>vast (of a location)</li></ol><p><i>раздо́лье</i> (razdólʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord, contention, dissension</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>раздоръ</i> (razdorŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzdorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annoyed, vexed (troubled, irritated by something unwanted or unliked)</li><li>dissatisfied</li><li>sick, mad</li><li>discontented</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vex, to annoy, to anger</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>доса́довать</i> (dosádovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to irritate</li><li>to annoy, to vex, to get on the nerves of</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́ть</i> (razdražítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become irritated</li><li>to get irritated/annoyed, to chafe</li></ol><p><i>раздража́ть</i> (razdražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>annoying, irritating, vexing</li><li>exasperating</li><li>irritative</li><li>provoking</li><li>jarring</li><li>grating</li><li>provocative</li><li>acrid</li><li>sore</li><li>trying, nerve-racking</li><li>disgruntling</li><li>niggling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irritation, annoyance</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́ть</i> (razdražítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irritant</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́ть</i> (razdražítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irritability</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́тельный</i> (razdražítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irritable</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́ть</i> (razdražítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to irritate</li><li>to annoy, to vex, to get on nerves</li></ol><p>Parallel to and possibly borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>раздражити</i> (razdražiti). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>дра́жити</i> (“to pester, to anger”) (1sg. <i>дра̑жим</i>), Slovene <i>drážiti</i> (“to pester”), Old Czech <i>drážiti</i>, Slovak <i>dráżiť</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dražiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become irritated</li><li>to get irritated/annoyed, to chafe</li></ol><p><i>раздражи́ть</i> (razdražítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with irritation, in an irritated manner</li></ol><p><i>раздражённый</i> (razdražónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>irritated, annoyed, angry, exasperated (experiencing or expressing a feeling of irritation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease, to pester</li><li>to whet (appetite), to stimulate (curiosity)</li></ol><p><i>раздразни́ть</i> (razdraznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tease, to pester</li><li>to whet (appetite), to stimulate (curiosity)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дразни́ть</i> (draznítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) discord, quarrel, strife</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) confusion, mess, turmoil</li></ol><p>From a verb раздирать, from раз- + драть.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li><li>to smash, to shatter</li><li>to fragment, to split up</li><li>to reduce (<i>to smaller units</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дроби́ть</i> (drobítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up into small parts, to be crushed</li><li>to break (<i>of waves</i>)</li><li>to be refracted (<i>of rays of light</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figurative) to disintegrate</li></ol><p><i>раздроби́ть</i> (razdrobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragmentation</li></ol><p><i>раздро́бленный</i> (razdróblennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragmented, crushed, broken, splintered, shattered</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush</li><li>to smash, to shatter</li><li>to fragment, to split up</li><li>to reduce (<i>to smaller units</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раздроби́ть</i> (razdrobítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up into small parts, to be crushed</li><li>to break (<i>of waves</i>)</li><li>to be refracted (<i>of rays of light</i>)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figurative) to disintegrate</li></ol><p><i>раздробля́ть</i> (razdrobljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>раздро́бленность</i> (razdróblennostʹ, “fragmentation”)</li></ol><p><i>раздроблённый</i> (razdrobljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>раздро́бленный</i> (razdróblennyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fan, to blow</li><li>to inflate, to blow <i>(out)</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to swell</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blow up, to exaggerate</li><li>to fan, to boost</li><li>to blow about, to flutter</li></ol><p><i>разду́ть</i> (razdútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell up</li><li>to bulge</li></ol><p><i>раздува́ть</i> (razduvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change one's mind, to decide not</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ду́мать</i> (dúmatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think <i>(about)</i>, to muse <i>(over)</i>, to ponder <i>(over)</i>, to consider, to ruminate <i>(on)</i>, to brood <i>(over)</i>, to deliberate</li><li>to hesitate</li><li>to change one's mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meditation, thought, reflection, reflexion</li><li>afterthought, recollection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start to grok, to get to grasp</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дупло́</i> (dupló, “hole, cavity”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), “getting all holes”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grok, to grasp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fan, to blow</li><li>to inflate, to blow <i>(out)</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to swell</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blow up, to exaggerate</li><li>to fan, to boost</li><li>to blow about, to flutter</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell up</li><li>to bulge</li></ol><p><i>разду́ть</i> (razdútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to open <i>(wide)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz(ъ)zěvati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>разе́-</i> (razé-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demote</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>жа́ловать</i> (žálovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undo, to open, to unclench, to let down, to release</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to kindle</li><li>to inflame, to stir up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rouse</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rich, to cash in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to obtain [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>разжи́ться</i> (razžítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindling</li><li>incitement</li></ol><p><i>разжига́ть</i> (razžigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fomenter, firestarter</li></ol><p><i>разжига́ть</i> (razžigátʹ, “to light, to kindle; to inflame, to stir up”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to kindle</li><li>to inflame, to stir up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rouse</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undo, to open, to unclench, to let down, to release</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rich, to cash in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to obtain [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to arouse, to fire up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>задо́р</i> (zadór) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gawk, gaper, scatterbrain (a distracted, inattentive, clumsy person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz(ъ)zěva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to open <i>(wide)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz(ъ)zinǫti</i>, from <i>&#42;orz-</i> + <i>&#42;zinǫti</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>разіну́ць</i> (razinúcʹ), Serbo-Croatian <i>razinuti</i>, Bulgarian <i>раззи́на</i> (razzína), Macedonian <i>раззине</i> (razzine).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gawk, gaper, scatterbrain (a distracted, inattentive, clumsy person)</li></ol><p><i>рази́нуть</i> (razínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>striking</li></ol><p><i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to hit, to smite</li><li>to strike down, to defeat, to crush</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to reek (of), to stink (of)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raziti</i>. Cognate to <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to decompose, (<i>mathematics</i>) to factorize, (<i>physics</i>) <i>(force)</i> to resolve</li><li>to demoralize, to corrupt</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decompose, (<i>mathematics</i>) to be factorized</li><li>to decompose, to rot, to decay</li><li>to get corrupted/demoralized</li></ol><p><i>разлага́ть</i> (razlagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorder</li><li>discord, dissension</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disorganized, disorderly, disordered</li><li>defective, out-of-order</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break apart, to break into pieces</li><li>to destroy</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;low colloquial) to ache severely</li></ol><p>From <i>разлома́ть</i> (razlomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>разломи́ть</i> (razlomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start barking intensely</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to start arguing, to start fighting</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лая́ться</i> (lajátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unglue, to unstick</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to force apart, to open with difficulty (one's eyes, one's lips, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unglue, to unstick</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to force apart, to open with difficulty (one's eyes, one's lips, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>разлепи́ть</i> (razlepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to scatter <i>(in the air)</i></li><li>to come apart, to be blown apart</li><li><i>(news, rumour etc)</i> to spread, to be spread about/around, to become known</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be ruined/shattered/squashed/upset</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to scatter <i>(in the air)</i></li><li>to come apart, to be blown apart</li><li><i>(news, rumour etc)</i> to spread, to be spread about/around, to become known</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be ruined/shattered/squashed/upset</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, overflow</li><li>bottling</li><li>spill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pouring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pouring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to pour out, to bottle</li></ol><p><i>разли́ть</i> (razlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to overflow</li><li>to spread</li><li>to pour out one's song</li></ol><p><i>разлива́ть</i> (razlivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to pour out, to bottle</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill</li><li>to overflow</li><li>to spread</li></ol><p><i>разли́ть</i> (razlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, to tell apart, to tell the difference between two or more entities</li><li>to differ</li><li>to discriminate</li><li>to recognize</li></ol><p><i>различи́ть</i> (različítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;io) to differ [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in a specified dimension or quantity, e.g. length, volume, age’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>различа́ть</i> (različátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>differentiation, recognition</li></ol><p><i>различи́ть</i> (različítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference (quality of being different, used in a comparison)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perceptible, discernible, distinguishable</li></ol><p><i>различи́ть</i> (različítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, to discern</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-личи́ть</i> (-ličítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>different (not the same)</li><li>diverse, various</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>различь</i> (različĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzličьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decomposition, decay</li><li>dissolution, dissipation</li><li>corruption</li><li>dissociation</li><li>analysis (chemistry&#58; process of breaking down a substance or the result of this process)</li><li>putrefaction</li></ol><p><i>разложи́ть</i> (razložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (<i>in separate locations</i>), to put away</li><li>to lay out, to display</li><li>to lay flat (<i>e.g. a newspaper or cot</i>)</li><li>to spread out (cards)</li><li>to distribute, to apportion, to allocate</li><li>to build and light (a fire)</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to unharness</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to crash an aircraft</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to decompose</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to factorize</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) <i>(force)</i> to resolve</li><li>to demoralize, to corrupt</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unpack</li><li>to unfold, to spread out, to open up, to open out</li><li>to decompose, (<i>mathematics</i>) to be factorized</li><li>to decompose, to rot, to decay</li><li>to get corrupted/demoralized</li></ol><p><i>разложи́ть</i> (razložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking, break-up</li><li>break</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) fault, fracture, rupture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break apart, to break into pieces</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break apart, to break into pieces</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;low colloquial) to ache severely</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation</li><li>parting, separation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to part, to sever</li></ol><p><i>разлучи́ть</i> (razlučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, part</li></ol><p><i>разлуча́ть</i> (razlučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to part, to sever</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ, “to unite (originally, to separate)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall out of love (with) someone, to stop loving</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pad out, to spin out</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gruel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ditherer, wishy-washy person</li></ol><p><i>разма́зать</i> (razmázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pad out, to spin out</li></ol><p><i>разма́зать</i> (razmázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind, mill</li></ol><p><i>размоло́ть</i> (razmolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unwind, to unreel</li></ol><p><i>размота́ть</i> (razmotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swing</li><li>sweep (a revolving motion)</li><li>wingspan</li><li>scope, range, sweep, scale (<i>of activity</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>размахну́ть</i> (razmaxnútʹ, “to spread, to open wide”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sway, to swing</li><li>to dangle</li><li>to brandish, to flourish [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a weapon’</small>]</li><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) to cause to swing?</li></ol><p><i>размахну́ть</i> (razmaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swing one's arm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do things in a big way</li></ol><p><i>разма́хивать</i> (razmáxivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread (one's hands)</li><li>to open (a door) wide</li><li>to brandish [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a weapon’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swing one's arm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do things in a big way</li></ol><p><i>размахну́ть</i> (razmaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soak, to steep</li></ol><p><i>размочи́ть</i> (razmočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sprawling, wide, broad</li><li>sweeping, swinging (<i>motion</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разма́х(нуть)</i> (razmáx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division, delineation, demarcation</li><li>separation, disengagement</li></ol><p><i>размежева́ть</i> (razmeževátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide up</li><li>to separate, to delineate</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>межева́ть</i> (meževátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide up</li><li>to separate, to delineate</li></ol><p><i>размежева́ть</i> (razmeževátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, to pulverize, to grind</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мелкий</i> (melkij) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchanging, changing, breaking (<i>of money or other valuable items for smaller units</i>)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) exchange</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разменя́ть</i> (razmenjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change <i>(money for lesser units)</i></li><li>to exchange</li><li>to live to certain age</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) to exchange</li></ol><p><i>разменя́ть</i> (razmenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, to break <i>(money for lesser units)</i></li><li>to exchange</li><li>to live to certain age</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) to exchange</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>меня́ть</i> (menjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size, dimension, measure, amount</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) meter, metre</li><li>(<i>music</i>) time, measure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzměrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measured</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure off, to measure</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dimension, dimensionality</li></ol><p><i>разме́рный</i> (razmérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dimensional, mensural</li></ol><p><i>разме́р</i> (razmér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure off, to measure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>разме́рить</i> (razméritʹ)</li></ol><p><i>разме́рить</i> (razméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep clean</li><li>to sweep away</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мести́</i> (mestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to put, to stow</li><li>to accommodate, to put up, to house, to quarter, to billet</li><li>to deploy, to station</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) to invest, to place</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take seats</li><li>to be quartered, to be housed, to be accommodated</li></ol><p><i>размести́ть</i> (razmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep clean</li><li>to sweep away</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to scatter</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread apart</li></ol><p><i>размет-</i> (razmet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to mark out</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to mark up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ме́тить</i> (métitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) markup</li></ol><p><i>разме́тить</i> (razmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark</li><li>to mark out</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to mark up</li></ol><p><i>разме́тить</i> (razmétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge into dreams</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мечта́ть</i> (mečtátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to mix</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to mix</li></ol><p><i>размеша́ть</i> (razmešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to put, to stow</li><li>to accommodate, to put up, to house, to quarter, to billet</li><li>to deploy, to station</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) to invest, to place</li></ol><p><i>размести́ть</i> (razmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take seats</li><li>to be quartered, to be housed, to be accommodated</li></ol><p><i>размеща́ть</i> (razmeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placing, placement</li><li>accommodation</li><li>deployment, stationing</li><li>placement, layout; distribution pattern</li><li>(<i>finances</i>) placement</li></ol><p><i>размести́ть</i> (razmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead</li><li>to mash</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch (one's legs, muscles, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-мина́ть</i> (-minátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) to warm up, limber up, loosen up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch one's legs</li></ol><p><i>размина́ть</i> (razminátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demine (to remove explosive mines from)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мини́ровать</i> (minírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) warm-up, limbering-up</li></ol><p><i>размина́ть</i> (razminátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss, to fail to meet, to get separated</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мину́ть</i> (minútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to reproduce, to breed, to spawn, to propagate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to multiply</li></ol><p><i>размножа́ть</i> (razmnožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproduction (<i>the act of reproducing new individuals biologically</i>), breeding, proliferation</li><li>propagation, duplication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to reproduce, to breed, to spawn, to propagate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to multiply</li></ol><p><i>размно́жить</i> (razmnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get soaked, to get sodden</li></ol><p><i>размо́кнуть</i> (razmóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiff, spat, quarrel, disagreement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defrost, to unfreeze</li></ol><p><i>разморо́зить</i> (razmorózitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defrost, to unfreeze</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unwind, to unreel</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unwinding, unreeling</li></ol><p><i>размота́ть</i> (razmotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing away, erosion</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) blurring</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dilution</li></ol><p><i>размыва́ть</i> (razmyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erode, to wash away, to destroy with a stream of water</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to blur</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to reduce (a role, stake, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>размы́ть</i> (razmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off</li><li>to open, break</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to open</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;rare) to disclose</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мы́кать</i> (mýkatʹ)</p><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;razmykati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ponder, to muse, to meditate, to think (over), to reflect</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blur</li></ol><p><i>размы́ть</i> (razmýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>washed out</li><li>blurry, fuzzy, dim; smudgy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erode, to wash away, to destroy with a stream of water</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to blur</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to reduce (a role, stake, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection, thought, musing</li></ol><p>From <i>размышля́ть</i> (razmyšljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ponder, to muse, to meditate, to think (over), to reflect [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘about/over someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to mellow, to melt, to disarm (<i>of emotion</i>)</li></ol><p><i>размягчи́ть</i> (razmjaxčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to mellow, to melt, to disarm (<i>of emotion</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мягчи́ть</i> (mjaxčítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to grow soft</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(person)</i> to be softened, to melt</li></ol><p><i>размя́кнуть</i> (razmjáknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to grow pulpy</li><li>to grow flabby, to become soft</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мя́кнуть</i> (mjáknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knead</li><li>to mash</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch (one's legs, muscles, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>мя́ть</i> (mjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) to warm up, limber up, loosen up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch one's legs</li></ol><p><i>размя́ть</i> (razmjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li><li>to scatter</li><li>to spread apart</li></ol><p><i>размета́ть</i> (razmetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution (in a planned manner)</li><li>distribution list</li><li>distribution instructions</li><li>voucher, order</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>наряди́ть</i> (narjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in <i>(shoes)</i></li></ol><p><i>разноси́ть</i> (raznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become comfortable <i>(clothes, shoes)</i></li></ol><p><i>разна́шивать</i> (raznášivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to convey, to deliver</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(news, rumor etc)</i> to spread around</li><li>to enter, to book</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to cause to swell</li><li>to smash, to destroy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scatter, to disperse</li><li>to give a rating/dressing-down, to dress down, to blow up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li><li>to resound</li></ol><p><i>разнести́</i> (raznestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unpick or to string (pearls, beads, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disjoint, to dismantle, to separate, to part, to pull apart</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to vary, to differ</li></ol><p><i>ра́знить</i> (ráznitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference (quality of being different, can be used to refer to the difference itself without talking about its size)</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica). Compare Belarusian <i>ро́зніца</i> (róznica), Czech <i>různice</i>, Kashubian <i>różnica</i>, Polish <i>różnica</i>, Silesian <i>rōżnica</i>, and Ukrainian <i>різни́ця</i> (riznýcja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disagreement, inconsistency, contradiction</li><li>broken things, scrap</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety, kind, form, sort</li><li>manner, nature</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) breed, species</li></ol><p><i>разнови́дный</i> (raznovídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), from <i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) and <i>вид</i> (vid).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference, disagreement, discord</li><li>discrepancy</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multidirectional</li><li>in different directions</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>напра́вленный</i> (naprávlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract verb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diversity, variety (quality of being diverse&#59; difference)</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diversify</li></ol><p><i>разнообра́зие</i> (raznoobrázije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diverse, manifold, various, varied</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of different races, of different tribes</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>племён-</i> (plemjón-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterogeneous, diverse, variegated</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying, delivery</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rating, dressing-down, blowing-up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to convey, to deliver</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(news, rumor etc)</i> to spread around</li><li>to enter, to book</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;colloquial) to cause to swell</li><li>to smash, to destroy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scatter, to disperse</li><li>to give a rating/dressing-down, to dress down, to blow up</li><li>to break in (shoes)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li><li>to resound</li><li>to become comfortable <i>(clothes, shoes)</i></li></ol><p><i>разноси́ть</i> (raznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) scalene</li><li>many-sided, versatile</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) remainder, the result of substraction</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peddler, hawker, delivery man</li><li>messenger</li><li>(<i>animate&#32;or&#32;inanimate</i>) carrier (e.g. of a disease)</li></ol><p><i>разноси́ть</i> (raznosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>variously characteristic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>multicolored, motley</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raznochinetz, raznochinets <i>(Historical: an intellectual of 18th–19th-century Russia who did not belong to the gentry)</i>.</li></ol><p>ра́зный (ráznyj) <i>(different, various)</i> + чин (čin) <i>(rank)</i> + -ец (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) variant reading, textual discrepancy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disagreement, difference in opinion</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>чте́ние</i> (čténije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of a single animal) having different types of fur colours&#47;colors</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) variegated, diverse, motley, mixed</li></ol><p><i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>шерсть</i> (šerstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) polyphagan</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Polyphaga.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Polyphaga</i>, as <i>разноя́дный</i> (raznojádnyj, “polyphagous”) +&lrm; <i>жук</i> (žuk, “beetle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profligacy</li></ol><p><i>разну́зданный</i> (raznúzdannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbridled</li><li>orgiastic (relating to an orgy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>different, diverse, unlike</li><li>various, varied, miscellaneous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>разьнъ</i> (razĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>ро́зный</i> (róznyj). See also <i>рознь</i> (roznʹ), <i>раз</i> (raz), <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disjoint, to dismantle, to separate, to part, to pull apart</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>раз-</i> (raz-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to disrobe, to divest, to unclothe, to undress</li><li>to disclose, to expose, to unmask, to lay bare, to denounce</li></ol><p><i>разоблачи́ть</i> (razoblačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disrobe, to divest oneself, to undress oneself</li></ol><p><i>разоблача́ть</i> (razoblačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exposure, exposing, disclosure, disclosing, unmasking, denunciation</li></ol><p><i>разоблачи́ть</i> (razoblačítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) to disrobe, to divest, to unclothe, to undress</li><li>to disclose, to expose, to unmask, to lay bare, to denounce</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>облачи́ть</i> (oblačítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismantle, to disassemble, to take apart, to deconstruct</li><li>to analyse, to parse</li><li>to take, buy up, snap up</li><li>to make out, read</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal with, to sort out</li><li>to figure out, to work out</li><li>to understand, to see into, to work with [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to disconnect, to uncouple</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to estrange, to alienate</li></ol><p><i>разобщи́ть</i> (razobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to disconnect, to uncouple</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to estrange, to alienate</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-общи́ть</i> (-obščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disunity</li><li>estrangement</li><li>isolation</li></ol><p><i>разобщённый</i> (razobščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valid for a single occasion</li><li>one-time, one-shot, nonrecurrent</li><li>single</li><li>disposable</li></ol><p><i>раз</i> (raz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disperse, to scatter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to banish, to dismiss (many people)</li><li>to disband (an organization)</li><li>to cause (a feeling) to pass</li><li>to speed up (something)</li><li>to race (a vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>разогна́ть</i> (razognátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to straighten, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>гнуть</i> (gnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to straighten, to straighten up, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>разогну́ть</i> (razognútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming-up, initial heating, firing <i>(of furnace)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up, to heat up</li><li>to reheat</li></ol><p>разо- + греть</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up, to heat up</li><li>to reheat</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress (oneself) very smartly</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>одева́ться</i> (odevátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very smartly dressed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress (someone) very smartly</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>оде́ть</i> (odétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress (oneself) very smartly</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>оде́ться</i> (odétʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear up</li><li>to tear <i>(apart)</i>, to rend, to split</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke to anger, to get a rise out of someone</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>злить</i> (zlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get angry <i>(with someone, at something)</i></li></ol><p><i>разозли́ть</i> (razozlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to disperse (in different directions), to spread</li><li>to break up (<i>of a crowd, meeting, etc.</i>)</li><li>to part, to separate, to break up (<i>of two or three persons</i>), to get divorced, to drift apart</li><li>to be sold out (well), to sell well (<i>of a product</i>)</li><li>to disagree, to differ (<i>of opinions</i>)</li><li>to diverge, to branch off</li><li>to miss one another, to fail to meet</li><li>to be spent, to go (<i>of money</i>)</li><li>to separate, to not close (<i>of clothes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>раз</i> (raz)</li></ol><p><i>раз</i> (raz) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the same time, simultaneously</li><li>suddenly, all of a sudden</li><li>at once</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to disjoin, to disconnect, to break, to open</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to languish</li></ol><p><i>разомле́ть</i> (razomlétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to languish</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>млеть</i> (mletʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear apart/asunder, to tear up</li><li>to sever, to break (ties), to rend</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to burst, to explode</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to tear, to burst</li><li>to burst, to go off, to explode</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to struggle to do (cope with) everything at once</li></ol><p><i>разорва́ть</i> (razorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(financial, economical) ruin, bankruptcy</li><li>devastation, ravage</li></ol><p><i>разори́ть</i> (razorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to ravage</li><li>to ruin, to bring to ruin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>разорити</i> (razoriti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzoriti</i>, from <i>&#42;orz-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;oriti</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>разорити</i> (razoriti), Serbo-Croatian <i>разо̀рити</i>, Old East Slavic <i>разорь</i> (razorĭ), Old Church Slavonic <i>орити</i> (oriti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruin oneself, to come to ruin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend money <i>(on)</i>, to fork out money <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>разори́ть</i> (razorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disarm</li></ol><p><i>разоружи́ть</i> (razoružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disarmament (the reduction of military forces and armaments)</li></ol><p><i>разоружи́ть</i> (razoružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disarm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to ravage</li><li>to ruin, to bring to ruin</li></ol><p><i>разори́ть</i> (razorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruin oneself, to come to ruin</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend money <i>(on)</i>, to fork out money <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to shout, to clamor, to make a din, to drone on, to drivel on, to nag <i>(at)</i>, to carp <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>разоря́ть</i> (razorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send <i>(about, round)</i>, to distribute, to circulate</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>сла́ть</i> (slátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread <i>(out)</i>, to lay</li></ol><p><i>разо-</i> (razo-) +&lrm; <i>стлать</i> (stlatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fawn <i>(upon)</i>, to bow and scrape <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>разостла́ть</i> (razostlátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disappointment (emotion)</li></ol><p><i>разочарова́ть</i> (razočarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with disappointment, disappointedly</li></ol><p><i>разочаро́ванный</i> (razočaróvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disappointed (<i>in someone or something</i>)</li><li>pessimistic</li><li>sad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappoint, to underwhelm, to disillusion, to disenchant</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>очарова́ть</i> (očarovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disappointed <i>(in someone; with something)</i></li></ol><p><i>разочарова́ть</i> (razočarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappoint, to underwhelm, to disillusion, to disenchant</li></ol><p><i>разочарова́ть</i> (razočarovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disappointed <i>(in someone; with something)</i></li></ol><p><i>разочаро́вывать</i> (razočaróvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dev (software developer)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>разрабо́тчик</i> (razrabótčik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work out, to elaborate, to develop, to design, to engineer, to devise</li><li>to exploit, to work up (<i>mining</i>)</li><li>to cultivate, to till</li><li>to exhaust, to use up</li></ol><p><i>разрабо́тать</i> (razrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to work with a zest, to get involved with one's work</li><li>to begin to work/run smoothly</li></ol><p><i>разраба́тывать</i> (razrabátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work out, to elaborate, to develop, to design, to engineer, to devise</li><li>to exploit, to work up (<i>mining</i>)</li><li>to cultivate, to till</li><li>to exhaust, to use up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin to work with a zest, to get involved with one's work</li><li>to begin to work/run smoothly</li></ol><p><i>разрабо́тать</i> (razrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>development, designing, elaboration</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) cultivation</li><li>mine workings</li></ol><p><i>разрабо́тать</i> (razrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>developer (a person who creates or improves certain classes of products)</li><li>designer, design group</li></ol><p><i>разрабо́тать</i> (razrabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(war, storm etc.)</i> to break out, to burst out</li><li>to burst <i>(into)</i>, to burst <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p><i>разрази́ться</i> (razrazítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(war, storm etc.)</i> to break out, to burst out</li><li>to burst <i>(into)</i>, to burst <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzraziti</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>разрази́ть</i> (razrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), or directly from <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expansion, spreading, sprawl</li></ol><p><i>разраста́ть(ся)</i> (razrastátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to expand, to spread <i>(out)</i>, to grow thick, to bush out</li></ol><p><i>разрасти́сь</i> (razrastísʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to expand, to spread <i>(out)</i>, to grow thick, to bush out</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thin out, weed out</li><li><i>(air)</i> to rarefy</li></ol><p><i>разреди́ть</i> (razredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparse (not dense)</li><li>thin (<i>of ice, etc.</i>)</li><li>rarefied (<i>of a gas, of the air</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut</li><li>section (cutting)</li><li>open-pit mine, strip mine</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разре́зать</i> (razrézatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to bisect</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>разре́зать</i> (razrézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to permit, to allow [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to solve, to resolve</li><li>to authorize</li><li>to settle (a problem)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>реша́ть</i> (rešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be solved</li><li>to be settled/resolved</li><li>to be allowed</li></ol><p><i>разреша́ть</i> (razrešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>permissive, approbative (giving authorization or approval)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>permission, authorization, approval</li><li>solution</li><li>settlement</li><li>(computing,&#32;photography) resolution (of a screen or picture)</li></ol><p><i>разреши́ть</i> (razrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solvable</li></ol><p><i>разреши́ть</i> (razrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to permit, to allow, to let [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to authorize</li><li>to solve, to settle, to resolve</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be solved</li><li>to be settled/resolved</li><li>to be allowed</li></ol><p><i>разреши́ть</i> (razrešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humuhumunukunukuapuaa (either of two species of triggerfish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw all over, to cover with drawings</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw all over, to cover with drawings</li></ol><p><i>разрисова́ть</i> (razrisovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>odd, incomplete</li><li>separate, uncoordinated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up (<i>a set of things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разро́знить</i> (razróznitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up (<i>a set of things</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>рознь</i> (roznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) or perhaps from <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ро́знить</i> (róznitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut up, to chop up</li></ol><p><i>разруби́ть</i> (razrubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut up, to chop up</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to end a friendship after having argued ("to argue oneself out")</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break into a long and vicious argument or scolding</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>руга́ться</i> (rugátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become rosy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become rosy</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>румя́нить</i> (rumjánitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruin, devastation</li></ol><p>Possibly a borrowing from Polish <i>rozruch</i>, according to Krylov and Sitnikov.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to demolish, to wreck</li><li>to ruin</li><li>to frustrate, to blast, to blight, to wreck</li></ol><p><i>разру́шить</i> (razrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to ruin, to collapse</li><li>to fail, to fall to the ground</li></ol><p><i>разруша́ть</i> (razrušátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, demolition, devastation (the act of destroying)</li></ol><p><i>разру́шить</i> (razrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruined; destroyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destroyer, depredator</li></ol><p><i>разру́шить</i> (razrúšitʹ, “to destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructive</li></ol><p><i>разру́шить</i> (razrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to demolish, to wreck</li><li>to ruin</li><li>to frustrate, to blast, to blight, to wreck</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to ruin, to collapse</li><li>to fail, to fall to the ground</li></ol><p><i>разру́шить</i> (razrúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupture</li><li>explosion</li><li>break, gap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rend, to sever</li><li>to dig, to excavate</li><li>to jumble</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>разры́ть</i> (razrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to tear, to burst</li><li>to burst, to go off, to explode</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to struggle to do (cope with) everything at once</li></ol><p><i>разрыва́ть</i> (razryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) discontinuous</li></ol><p><i>разры́в</i> (razrýv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst into tears/sobs</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>рыда́ть</i> (rydátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aerator-fluffer</li><li>detergent</li><li>baking powder</li></ol><p><i>разрыхли́ть</i> (razryxlítʹ, “to make friable; to loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loosen</li><li>to make light, to hoe</li></ol><p><i>разрыхли́ть</i> (razryxlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>category, class</li><li>discharge</li><li>unloading</li><li>numerical place, digit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discharge (battery)</li><li>to ease (tensions)</li><li>to dress someone (in a fancy way)</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) to increase distance between letters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be discharged</li><li>to run out of charge, to discharge</li><li>to ease (of tensions)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dress oneself (in a fancy way)</li></ol><p><i>разряди́ть</i> (razrjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electricity</i>) discharge</li><li>discharge, unloading (<i>of weapon</i>)</li><li>relaxation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>diplomacy</i>) détente</li></ol><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) spacing</li></ol><p><i>разряди́ть</i> (razrjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) number of digits (<i>in a given numeric value written in decimal or other positional notation</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) bitness, bit width, word size, word length (the size in bits of the natural unit of data processed by a given CPU or transferred over a given bus)</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) bit depth, resolution (<i>of digitized data, in bits</i>)</li></ol><p><i>разря́дный</i> (razrjádnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discharge</li><li>to defuse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dress up, dress out</li><li>(<i>poligraphy</i>) to space out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be discharged</li><li>to run out of charge, to discharge</li><li>to ease (of tensions)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dress oneself (in a fancy way)</li></ol><p><i>разряжа́ть</i> (razrjažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissuade</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissuade</li></ol><p><i>разубеди́ть</i> (razubedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take someone's shoes off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to deprive of, to take away</li></ol><p><i>разу́ть</i> (razútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take one's shoes off</li></ol><p><i>разува́ть</i> (razuvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reassure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reassure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out, to make inquiries <i>(about)</i>, to try to find out</li></ol><p><i>разузна́ть</i> (razuznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find out, to make inquiries <i>(about)</i>, to try to find out</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>узна́ть</i> (uznátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, to decorate</li><li>to beautify, to embellish (a song, performance, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>укра́сить</i> (ukrásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, to decorate</li><li>to beautify, to embellish (a song, performance, etc.)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to embellish (a story)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat to the point of leaving bruises or abrasions, to beat black and blue</li></ol><p><i>разукра́сить</i> (razukrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reason, mind, intellect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>разоумъ</i> (razumŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzumъ</i>. Displaced native <i>&#42;розум</i> (&#42;rozum).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding, comprehension</li></ol><p><i>разуме́ть</i> (razumétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) to understand (to be aware of the meaning of)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>разумѣти</i> (razuměti), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>разоумѣти</i> (razuměti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzuměti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be meant / understood; to be taken to mean</li></ol><p><i>разуме́ть</i> (razumétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reasonably, judiciously, wisely, rationally</li><li>cleverly, sensibly</li></ol><p><i>разу́мный</i> (razúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasonableness, rationality, sanity</li></ol><p><i>разу́мный</i> (razúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rational</li><li>reasonable, sensible</li><li>clever, wise</li></ol><p><i>ра́зум</i> (rázum) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligent design</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ра́зум</i> (rázum).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>разуме́ть</i> (razumétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take someone's shoes off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to deprive of, to take away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzuti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-уть</i> (-utʹ). Compare with Polish <i>rozzuć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take one's shoes off</li></ol><p><i>разу́ть</i> (razútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn, to prepare to perform</li></ol><p><i>разучи́ть</i> (razučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose the skill <i>(of)</i>, to forget <i>(how to)</i></li></ol><p><i>разу́чивать</i> (razúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn, to prepare to perform</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose the skill <i>(of)</i>, to forget <i>(how to)</i></li></ol><p><i>разучи́ть</i> (razučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar) a irresponsible, unreliable person;</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to break, to crash, to crush, to smash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to defeat, to beat, to smash</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to knock, to hurt badly, to break, to fracture, to damage severely (a body part)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to kick ass</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to corrode</li></ol><p><i>разъе́д-</i> (razʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to part, to disjoin</li><li>to disconnect, to break, to cut off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, to part, to disjoin</li><li>to disconnect, to break, to cut off</li></ol><p><i>разъедини́ть</i> (razʺjedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, part</li><li>(<i>electrical egineering</i>) to get disconnected</li></ol><p><i>разъединя́ть</i> (razʺjedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li><li>travelling</li><li>moving into separate homes</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) passing track, double track section, halt</li><li>(<i>military</i>) mounted patrol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to drive about, to ride about, to travel about</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, to leave</li><li>to disperse</li><li>to pass by one another (on a narrow road, etc.)</li><li>to pass by and miss one another, to fail to meet</li><li>to separate, to break up (of a couple)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall apart</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn smooth (<i>of a road; from frequent use</i>)</li><li>worn out (<i>of a road; from excessive use</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat away, to corrode</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to depart, to leave</li><li>to disperse</li><li>to pass by one another (on a narrow road, etc.)</li><li>to pass by and miss one another, to fail to meet</li><li>to separate, to break up (of a couple)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall apart</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>разыгра́ть</i> (razygrátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>разы́грывать</i> (razýgryvatʹ).</li></ol><p><i>разъигра́ть</i> (razʺigrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infuriate, to enrage, to rouse to fury</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>яри́ть</i> (jarítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to infuriate, to enrage, to rouse to fury</li></ol><p><i>разъяри́ть</i> (razʺjarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, mad, furious, enraged</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarification, explanation, elucidation, interpretation</li></ol><p><i>разъясни́ть</i> (razʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>explanatory</li></ol><p><i>разъясни́ть</i> (razʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain, to interpret</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to clear up, to become clear (<i>of the weather</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>-я́снить</i> (-jásnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clarified; to clarify oneself</li></ol><p><i>разъясни́ть</i> (razʺjasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain, to interpret</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) bollocking, dressing-down (severe verbal reprimand)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разъёбывать</i> (razʺjóbyvatʹ). Compare with Ukrainian <i>розйоб</i> (rozjob).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socket, slot, connector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play, to perform</li><li>to raffle off, to draw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play a trick, to play a practical joke, to pull one's leg</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become frolicsome</li><li><i>(pianist, actor etc)</i> to warm up</li><li><i>(wind, sea etc)</i> to rise, to break</li><li>to run high</li><li>to unfold, to develop, to take place</li></ol><p><i>разыгра́ть</i> (razygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play, to perform</li><li>to raffle off, to draw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play a trick, to play a practical joke, to pull one's leg</li></ol><p><i>разыгра́ть</i> (razygrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become frolicsome</li><li><i>(pianist, actor etc)</i> to warm up</li><li><i>(wind, sea etc)</i> to rise, to break</li><li>to run high</li><li>to unfold, to develop, to take place</li></ol><p><i>разы́грывать</i> (razýgryvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search, to look (for)</li></ol><p><i>разыска́ть</i> (razyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>searching, (<i>relational</i>) investigation</li></ol><p><i>разыска́ть</i> (razyskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paradise, heaven</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rajь</i>, borrowed from Iranian, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;raHíš</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;raHíš</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;reh₁ís</i> (“wealth, goods”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) district executive committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>райо́нный исполни́тельный комите́т</i> (rajónnyj ispolnítelʹnyj komitét, “district executive committee”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) district committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>райо́нный комите́т</i> (rajónnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>district</li><li>region, area</li></ol><p>First attested in 1865. Borrowed from French <i>rayon</i>, from Middle French <i>royon</i>, <i>rayon</i>, from Old French <i>raie</i>, <i>ree</i> (“honeycomb”), from Frankish <i>&#42;hrātu</i> (“honeycomb”). Not cognate with English <i>region</i>, of which it is a false cognate.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division into districts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) district; regional</li></ol><p><i>райо́н</i> (rajón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) paradise-crow (<i>Lycocorax</i> sp.)</li></ol><p><i>ра́йский</i> (rájskij, “paradisiacal”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>воро́на</i> (voróna, “crow”, noun), literally “crow of paradise, paradise-crow”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) bird of paradise (Paradisaeidae)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Райская Птица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Apus (a constellation of the southern sky)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickets in the Garden of Eden</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a place of abundance and prosperity</li></ol><p>Literally, “paradise's/paradisaical thickets”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paradisiacal, paradisiac, Edenic</li></ol><p><i>рай</i> (raj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital/centre of a district/region</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>райо́нный центр</i> (rajónnyj centr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rayah, the non-Muslim population in the Ottoman Empire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish رعایا (<i>reaya</i>), from Arabic رَعِيَّة (raʕiyya, “citizens, flock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crawfish, crayfish</li><li>(astronomy,&#32;astrology) Cancer</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cancer (disease of uncontrolled cellular proliferation)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rakъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рак)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Cancer (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of a crab)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Cancer (zodiac sign for the crab, covering June 22 — July 22)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrine (a massive tomb holding the relics of a saint’s body)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рака</i> (raka, “monument, shrine”). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>ра́ка</i> (ráka, “casket with relics”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ра̏ка</i> (“grave crypt”), Slovene <i>ráka</i> (“crypt”), from Gothic <i>𐌰𐍂𐌺𐌰</i> (arka, “chest, coffer”), from Latin <i>arca</i> (“chest, coffer, coffin”); or directly from the Latin. Compare <i>ра́ковина</i> (rákovina, “shell”), derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orky</i> (“shell”) (genitive <i>&#42;orkъve</i>), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arkō</i> (“chest, coffer”), from Latin <i>arca</i> (“chest, coffer, coffin”) as above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) doctor blade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rakel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket (vehicle propelled by a rocket engine)</li><li>missile (self-propelled military projectile with an adjustable trajectory)</li><li>flare, signal rocket</li><li><i>Raketa</i> hydrofoil</li><li>(<i>poker</i>) one of a pair of pocket aces</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rakete</i> or Dutch <i>raket</i>; first attested 1696.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>launch vehicle, carrier rocket</li></ol><p><i>раке́та</i> (rakéta) +&lrm; <i>носите́ль</i> (nositélʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(tennis,&#32;badminton) racket, racquet</li><li>(<i>table tennis</i>) paddle</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;animate) tennis player</li><li>diminutive of <i>раке́та</i> (rakéta); a small rocket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>raquette</i>.</p><p><i>раке́та</i> (rakéta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) space rocket</li></ol><p><i>раке́тный</i> (rakétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>косми́ческий</i> (kosmíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;military) rocket weapon(s) (<i>includes all types of rockets, from rocket-propelled grenades, to guided anti-tank missiles, to rocket mortar and artillery projectiles, to intercontinental ballistic missiles</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) missile</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rocket</li></ol><p><i>раке́та</i> (rakéta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) jetpack</li></ol><p>From <i>раке́тный</i> (rakétnyj, “rocket”) +&lrm; <i>ра́нец</i> (ránec, “backpack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sallow, willow (<i>Salix fragilis</i>, <i>Salix caprea</i>) (tree or wood)</li><li>broom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orkyta</i>, which Pokorny connects to Ancient Greek <i>ἄρκευθος</i> (árkeuthos, “juniper”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erkʷ-</i> (“bend”). Also compare Latvian <i>ērkšķis</i> (“thorn bush”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steel ruler used in textile printing machines to remove excess paint from the surface of the printing shaft</li><li>spatula for leveling self-leveling concrete</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>racle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shell (hard calcareous external covering of mollusks)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pinna, auricle, helix (the external ear)</li><li>sink, washbowl, basin, bowl</li><li>vesicle</li><li>bandstand</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) blister, cavity, bubble, flaw, blowhole</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>раковина</i> (rakovina, “mother of pearl”). Per Vasmer, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orky</i> (“shell”) (genitive <i>&#42;orkъve</i>) + <i>&#42;-ina</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arkō</i> (“chest, coffer”), from Latin <i>arca</i> (“chest, coffer, coffin”). Cognate with <i>ра́ка</i> (ráka, “shrine of a saint, originally coffin”), from the same Latin source, and with Slovene <i>rákəv</i> (“coffin, crypt”), Czech <i>rakev</i> (“coffin”), <i>rakvice</i> (“shell”), Slovak <i>rakev</i> (“box”), Polabian <i>rakåí</i> (“box”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crawfish farming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crawfish, crayfish</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cancer (medical condition); cancerous</li></ol><p><i>рак</i> (rak) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ass-backwards (<i>moving backwards, rear end first; or hopelessly misguided</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) doggy style</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar,&#32;often&#32;figurative) bent over (<i>ready to be sexually penetrated</i>)</li></ol><p>Instrumental singular of <i>рак</i> (rak, “crayfish”); from the observation that crayfish, when threatened, may try to escape by walking or swimming backwards.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>гусько́м</i> (gusʹkóm) (&lt; <i>гусь</i> (gusʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crustacean (arthropod of the subphylum Crustacea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) eurypterid</li></ol><p><i>рак</i> (rak, “crawfish”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>скорпио́н</i> (skorpión, “scorpion”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(painting,&#32;cinematography,&#32;photography) angle, foreshortening</li><li>angle, viewpoint, perspective</li></ol><p>From French <i>raccourci</i> (“shortcut; foreshortening”). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>re-</i> + <i>ad-</i> + Latin <i>curtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seashell</li></ol><p><i>раку́шка</i> (rakúška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ра́ковина</i> (rákovina): shell, seashell</li><li>a cup, a rigid concave protective covering for the male genitalia, predominantly used during sports activities</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism,&#32;by extension) a woman's genitals</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́к(овина)</i> (rák(ovina)) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška) (with stress shift) or from <i>ра́к(овина)</i> (rák(ovina)) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška) (without stress shift).</p><p>Sense 3 was coined on TikTok in 2024.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) European roller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rally (motor racing event)</li><li>(US,&#32;neologism) rally (public gathering for a cause)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rally</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ard (a simple wooden plough&#47;plow)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ordlo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂érh₃trom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frame (structural elements of a building, bicycle, or other constructed object)</li><li>frame (<i>of a photo, picture, etc.</i>)</li><li>window frame</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a person with excessive muscle mass; a muscleman; a bodybuilder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rama</i>, from Middle High German <i>ram</i>, <i>rame</i>, or directly borrowed from German <i>Rahmen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Islam,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) Ramadan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic رَمَضَان (ramaḍān).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рамадан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>рамада́н</i> (ramadán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Islam,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) Ramadan</li></ol><p>From Turkic, from Persian رمضان (ramazân), from Arabic رَمَضَان (ramaḍān).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рамазан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>рамаза́н</i> (ramazán)</li><li>a male given name, Ramazan, from Arabic, of Muslim usage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartilage lichen (<i>Ramalina</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ramalina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rambutan (fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay <i>rambutan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frame</li><li>framework</li><li>limits</li><li>metal detector (walk-through)</li></ol><p><i>ра́ма</i> (ráma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ра́мка</i> (rámka, “small frame”)</li></ol><p><i>ра́мка</i> (rámka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) footlights</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) low barrier along the proscenium, hiding the lights</li><li>(theater,&#32;dated) low barrier separating a box from the auditorium</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) railing</li><li>(military,&#32;historical) embrasure, crenel</li><li>(<i>technical&#32;or&#32;skateboarding</i>) ramp</li><li>apparatus for centralized distribution to consumers of compressed gas cylinders</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rampe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to wrangle, to cample, to row</li></ol><p><i>рамс</i> (rams, “the rams card game”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ramen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ラーメン</i> (rāmen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) shoulder</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;collective) shoulders</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ormę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, injury</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) suffering, heartache</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rank, grade, degree, class</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rang</i> or German <i>Rang</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) spar (linear object used as a mast, sprit, yard, boom, pole or gaff)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>rondhout</i>, first recorded as <i>ронго́ут</i> (rongóut).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;ironic</i>) rendezvous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rendez-vous</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) indigoberry (<i>Randia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Randia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;colloquial) random</li></ol><p>From English <i>random</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earlier, already (prior to some time)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>previous to [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, wounding, injury, injuring</li></ol><p><i>ра́нить</i> (ránitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wounded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wounded, injured</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) wounded person, injured person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ranenъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rennet, pippin (variety of apple)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) ranetka (sexually precocious girl)</li></ol><p>From <i>ране́та</i> (ranéta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from French <i>reinette</i> (“little queen”).</p><p><i>ра́но</i> (ráno, “early”) +&lrm; <i>-е́тка</i> (-étka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backpack, satchel (<i>usually a blocky, hard-sided one, e.g. a traditional backpack worn by schoolchildren, or the backpack of a 19th or early 20th century soldier</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ranzen</i> (“satchel”), assimilated to the suffix <i>-ец</i> (-ec). First attested in 1764.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to rank (to rate&#47;sort&#47;list things according to certain criteria)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ranger</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragile, vulnerable (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wound, to injure</li><li>to hurt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raniti</i>, from <i>&#42;rana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ра́на</i> (rána): (small) wound</li></ol><p><i>ра́на</i> (rána) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Early Middle Ages</li></ol><p>From <i>ра́нний</i> (ránnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>ранньосередньові́чний</i> (rannʹoserednʹovíčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early</li></ol><p><i>ра́но</i> (ráno) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ranъ</i> + <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sooner or later, eventually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rentier (an individual who receives an income from his or her assets and investments)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rentier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ranch (<i>usually in the North American context</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>rancho</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(almost always in the phrase <i>в таку́ю <b>рань</b></i> (v takúju <b>ranʹ</b>)) early morning</li></ol><p><i>ра́но</i> (ráno) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earlier, before, in the past</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technical pen, rapidograph</li></ol><p>From Rapidograph, the brand name of a technical pen introduced by Rotring in the 1950s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) rapier (a European sword with a straight, narrow blade)</li><li>(<i>fencing</i>) foil (a very thin sword used in fencing competitions)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Rapier</i>, or directly from French <i>rapière</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rapport</i>, from <i>rapporter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report</li></ol><p>French <i>rapporter</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapeseed (<i>Brassica napus</i>)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Raps</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rapeseed</li></ol><p><i>рапс</i> (raps) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rarity, curiosity (rare or unusual object)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rarität</i>, from Latin <i>raritas</i> ≃ <i>rarus</i> (“rare”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>раз-</i> (raz-) (<i>before voiceless consonants</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race (group of people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rasse</i>, French <i>race</i> or Italian <i>razza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rasam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tamil <i>ரசம்</i> (racam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>racisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>racist</li></ol><p><i>раси́ст</i> (rasíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) storyboard</li></ol><p><i>раскадрова́ть</i> (raskadrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to repent <i>(of)</i></li><li>to be remorseful <i>(of)</i>, to regret</li></ol><p><i>раска́яться</i> (raskájatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make burning hot, to bring to a great heat, to incandesce</li></ol><p><i>раскали́ть</i> (raskalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make burning hot, to bring to a great heat, to incandesce</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кали́ть</i> (kalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave, to break <i>(sugar)</i>, to crack <i>(nuts)</i>, to chop <i>(firewood)</i></li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to divide, to split up</li></ol><p><i>расколо́ть</i> (raskolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleave, to split, to crack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start talking, to tell/reveal/disclose the truth, to blab, to let it out, to spill the beans, to spill</li></ol><p><i>раска́лывать</i> (raskályvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat (in an extreme way)</li></ol><p><i>раскали́ть</i> (raskalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, to get hot</li></ol><p><i>раскаля́ть</i> (raskaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burning hot, scorching, red-hot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out, to grub up, to grub out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig out, to dig up, to unearth</li><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) to excavate</li></ol><p><i>раскопа́ть</i> (raskopátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reverberation, peal, roll (a heavy, reverberatory sound)</li><li>roar</li><li>growl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(out)</i></li><li>to make slippery (by sliding on)</li><li>to roll apart, <i>(a log building)</i> to take apart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rolling, resounding</li></ol><p><i>раска́т</i> (raskát) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accelerate by rolling</li><li>to roll apart, <i>(a log building)</i> to take apart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll <i>(out)</i></li><li>to make slippery (by sliding on)</li><li>to roll apart, <i>(a log building)</i> to take apart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive <i>(about, around)</i>, to ride <i>(about, around)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>раската́ть</i> (raskatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>раскати́ть</i> (raskatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to swing, to rock</li><li>to loosen, to shake loose, to undermine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move, to stir up</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock the boat (to disturb the status quo)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to rock the boat”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swing, to rock</li><li>to rock oneself to and fro, to sway</li><li>to become rickety/shaky/unsteady</li><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) to be sluggish, to mark time, to barely drag one's feet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bestir/move oneself, to start moving</li></ol><p><i>раскача́ть</i> (raskačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to swing, to rock</li><li>to loosen, to shake loose, to undermine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to move, to stir up, to motivate</li></ol><p><i>раскача́ть</i> (raskačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock the boat (to disturb the status quo); to stir the pot</li></ol><p>Literally, “to rock the boat”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to swing, to rock</li><li>to rock oneself to and fro, to sway</li><li>to become rickety/shaky/unsteady</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to be sluggish, to mark time, to barely drag one's feet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bestir/move oneself, to start moving</li></ol><p><i>раска́чивать</i> (raskáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rocking, swinging</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swaying</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) motivation (<i>action or result</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раскача́ть</i> (raskačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repentance, remorse (feeling of regret or remorse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to repent <i>(of)</i></li><li>to be remorseful <i>(of)</i>, to regret</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ка́яться</i> (kájatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quartering, billeting (the act of providing housing for military personnel), accommodation (lodging)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle accounts <i>(with)</i>, to reckon <i>(with)</i>, to square up <i>(with)</i>, to settle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get even; to get revenge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw in different directions</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>branchy; spreading (of a tree); sprawling</li></ol><p><i>раскид(ываться)</i> (raskid(yvatʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw in different directions</li><li>to stretch out, to spread</li><li>to spread, to deal</li><li><i>(a tent, a camp</i>to ) to pitch, to set up</li></ol><p><i>раскида́ть</i> (raskidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread out, to stretch out, to stretch far away</li><li>to sprawl</li></ol><p><i>раски́дывать</i> (raskídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out, to spread</li><li>to spread, to deal</li><li>to pitch, to set up (a tent, a camp)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread out, to stretch out, to stretch far away</li><li>to sprawl</li></ol><p><i>раски́нуть</i> (raskínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become soft/limp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be slack/loose/flabby/droopy/drooping, to be out of sorts, to feel seedy/weak/spent/wasted, to become maudlin, to lose heart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become sentimental/tearful/maudlin/mawkish/lachrymose</li></ol><p><i>раски́снуть</i> (raskísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become soft/limp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be slack/loose/flabby/droopy/drooping, to be out of sorts, to feel seedy/weak/spent/wasted, to become maudlin, to lose heart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become sentimental/tearful/maudlin/mawkish/lachrymose</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ки́снуть</i> (kísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) spread, layout, deal</li><li>state of affairs/things, the lie of the land, the lay of the land, ball game</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) distribution, apportionment, allocation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) share</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) recipe</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) laying out, spreading; laying flat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) layout (<i>e.g. of a keyboard</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) building and lighting (<i>of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) ratio of margin width to margin height (<i>on a page</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раскла́д(ывать)</i> (rasklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folding bed, camp bed, cot</li><li>pop-up book</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clamshell phone, flip phone</li></ol><p><i>раскла́дывать(ся)</i> (raskládyvatʹ(sja), “to unfold”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (<i>in separate locations</i>), to put away</li><li>to lay out, to display</li><li>to lay flat (<i>e.g. a newspaper or cot</i>)</li><li>to spread out (cards)</li><li>to distribute, to apportion, to allocate</li><li>to build and light (a fire)</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to unharness</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-кла́дывать</i> (-kládyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to unpack</li><li>to unfold, to spread out, to open up, to open out</li></ol><p><i>раскла́дывать</i> (raskládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (<i>e.g. of a gentleman or performer</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for some purpose’</small>]</li><li>to greet by bowing [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to say goodbye, to bid farewell [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>раскла́няться</i> (rasklánjatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bow (<i>e.g. of a gentleman or performer</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for some purpose’</small>]</li><li>to greet by bowing [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to say goodbye, to bid farewell [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кла́няться</i> (klánjatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relaxedness, uninhibitedness</li></ol><p><i>раско́ванный</i> (raskóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relaxed, uninhibited</li><li>unshod (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>unshackled, unchained</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove a horseshoe from a horse</li><li>to unfetter</li><li>to reforge</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove a horseshoe from a horse</li><li>to unfetter</li><li>to reforge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>split, dissidence</li><li>cleavage</li><li>disunity</li><li>schism, dissent</li></ol><p>Action noun of <i>расколо́ть</i> (raskolótʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disenchant (to remove a spell or magic enchantment from)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>колдова́ть</i> (koldovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disenchant (to remove a spell or magic enchantment from)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave, to break <i>(sugar)</i>, to crack <i>(nuts)</i>, to chop <i>(firewood)</i></li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to divide, to split up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make to talk</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cleave, to split, to crack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to start talking, to tell/reveal/disclose the truth, to blab, to let it out, to spill the beans, to spill</li></ol><p><i>расколо́ть</i> (raskolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;religion) Raskolnik</li></ol><p>From <i>раско́л</i> (raskól) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig out, to grub up, to grub out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dig out, to dig up, to unearth</li><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) to excavate</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) excavation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-fed, corpulent, fat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scold, to berate, to lambast, to chide</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кости́ть</i> (kostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slant-eyed</li><li>suffering from strabismus, cross-eyed</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slanted, inclined, oblique</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>косо́й</i> (kosój) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be cough up (money), to shell out (money)</li></ol><p>From <i>коше́ль</i> (košélʹ, “purse (dated)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cough up (money), to shell out (money), to fork out</li></ol><p>From <i>коше́ль</i> (košélʹ, “purse (dated)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal (in parts or regularly), to sack, to embezzle, to plunder</li></ol><p><i>раскра́д-</i> (raskrád-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (fabric to specific lengths and shapes to make clothes)</li></ol><p><i>раскрои́ть</i> (raskroítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to colour/color</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coloring, coloration (action or result)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) coloring book</li></ol><p><i>раскра́сить</i> (raskrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get red in the face, to redden, to flush, to blush</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>красне́ть</i> (krasnétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal (in parts or regularly), to sack, to embezzle, to plunder</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>красть</i> (krastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint, to colour/color</li></ol><p><i>раскра́сить</i> (raskrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (fabric to specific lengths and shapes to make clothes)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>крои́ть</i> (kroítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(pattern)</i> cutting</li><li>cut, cutout, layout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unwind, to unroll, to unspin</li><li>to promote, to market, to hype</li><li>to persuade someone into doing something (often with deceit), to hustle someone into doing something/out of their money, etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>разкрꙋтити</i> (razkrutiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzkrǫtiti</i>. Morphologically <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to unwind, to untwist, to unscrew</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to spin (<i>strongly</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed in business, to become well-known (<i>through business</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раскрути́ть</i> (raskrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>untwisting, untwining, undoing</li><li>unwinding</li><li>spin, spinning, rotation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) publicity campaign, buildup, hype, promotion</li></ol><p><i>раскрути́ть</i> (raskrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unwind, to unroll, to unspin</li><li>to promote, to market, to hype</li><li>to persuade someone into doing something (often with deceit), to hustle someone into doing something/out of their money, etc.</li></ol><p><i>раскрути́ть</i> (raskrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to unwind, to untwist, to unscrew</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to spin (<i>strongly</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed in business, to become well-known (<i>through business</i>)</li></ol><p><i>раскру́чивать</i> (raskrúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to uncover, to expose, to bare</li><li>to disclose, to reveal, to discover</li></ol><p><i>раскры́ть</i> (raskrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to uncover oneself</li><li><i>(crime, cheat etc.)</i> to come out, to come to light</li><li>to reveal/manifest oneself</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to show one's cards/hand, to lay one's cards on the table</li></ol><p><i>раскрыва́ть</i> (raskryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening</li><li>disclosure</li><li>solving, exposure</li><li>discovery</li></ol><p><i>раскры́ть</i> (raskrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>open</li><li>exposed</li><li>detected, uncovered, disclosed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to uncover, to expose, to bare</li><li>to disclose, to reveal, to discover</li><li>to solve <i>(a murder)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>to uncover oneself</li><li><i>(crime, cheat etc.)</i> to come out, to come to light</li><li>to reveal/manifest oneself</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to show one's cards/hand, to lay one's cards on the table</li></ol><p><i>раскры́ть</i> (raskrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) dekulakization (dispossession of kulaks, i.e. better-off peasants, of their resources, often killing them in the process)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;ironic) forcible seizure of ill-gotten assets, illegal confiscation</li></ol><p><i>раскула́чивать</i> (raskuláčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to dekulakize, to dispossess (a kulak) of his/her property and/or rights</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disassemble (a car, structure, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>раскула́чить</i> (raskuláčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to dekulakize, to dispossess (a kulak) of his/her property and/or rights</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disassemble (a car, structure, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>кула́к</i> (kulák) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to guess, to get to the core, to get to the heart, to get to the bottom <i>(of)</i>, to see through (someone), to get (someone's) measure</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite through</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to guess, to get to the core, to get to the heart, to get to the bottom <i>(of)</i>, to see through (someone), to get (someone's) measure</li></ol><p><i>раскуси́ть</i> (raskusítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>racially (relating to race)</li></ol><p><i>ра́совый</i> (rásovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>racial (of a race)</li></ol><p><i>ра́са</i> (rása) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>распиздяйство</i> (raspizdjajstvo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disintegration, breakup, collapse</li><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) decay, dissociation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disintegrate, to fall apart, to fall to pieces, to come apart, to come asunder</li><li>to break up <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to dissociate</li><li>to break up, to collapse</li></ol><p><i>распад-</i> (raspad-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unpack, undo, unwrap, unbox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steam</li><li>to soften by steaming</li><li>to stew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause to sweat</li></ol><p><i>распа́рить</i> (raspáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steam</li><li>to soften by steaming</li><li>to stew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cause to sweat</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unstitch, to tear at the seams</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rip open (skin, a body part, etc.) with a sharp object</li></ol><p><i>распоро́ть</i> (rasporótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disintegrate, to fall apart, to fall to pieces, to come apart, to come asunder</li><li>to break up <i>(into)</i></li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to dissociate</li><li>to break up, to collapse</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough up, to till</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw open, to fling open; to open wide</li><li>to plough up, to till</li></ol><p><i>распахну́ть</i> (raspaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>распаха́ть</i> (raspaxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly/swing/sweep open</li><li>to throw open one's coat</li></ol><p><i>распа́хивать</i> (raspáxivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw open, to fling open; to open wide</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пахну́ть</i> (paxnútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly/swing/sweep open</li><li>to throw open one's coat</li></ol><p><i>распахну́ть</i> (raspaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) liturgical chant</li><li>prolonged pronunciation, approaching singing</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>распева́ть</i> (raspevátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend time singing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sing loudly</li><li>to rehearse (a song)</li></ol><p><i>распе́ть</i> (raspétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst open, to cause to burst</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overflow, to fill up, to fill to the brim, to be brimming over <i>(with)</i>, to swell <i>(with)</i>, to bloat <i>(with)</i></li><li>to hold apart; to strut off</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehearse (a song)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unseal (to break the seal of (something) in order to open it)</li><li>to print (using a printer)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>печа́тать</i> (pečátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsealing</li><li>printing out</li><li>printout, listing</li></ol><p><i>распеча́тать</i> (raspečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unseal (to break the seal of (something) in order to open it)</li><li>to print (using a printer)</li></ol><p><i>распеча́тать</i> (raspečátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink (usually alcohol) with someone else</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to take one's time drinking (something)</li></ol><p><i>распи́ть</i> (raspítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot-house</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) irresponsible, unreliable person; slob</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пизда́</i> (pizdá, “a cunt, a pussy”) +&lrm; <i>-яй</i> (-jaj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) slovenliness</li></ol><p><i>распиздя́й</i> (raspizdjáj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where a tree beam was sawed</li><li>the act of sawing (e.g. of a tree trunk)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) partition of money or a misuse of budget, e.g. by bribes</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>пилить</i> (pilitʹ, “to saw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw to pieces</li><li>(figurative,&#32;slang) to embezzle (money, e.g. from a contract) by intentionally padding costs</li></ol><p><i>распили́ть</i> (raspilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw to pieces</li><li>(figurative,&#32;slang) to embezzle (money, e.g. from a contract) by intentionally padding costs</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify</li><li>to kick away</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-пина́ть</i> (-pinátʹ)</p><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пина́ть</i> (pinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst open, to cause to burst</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to overflow, to fill up, to fill to the brim, to be brimming over <i>(with)</i>, to swell <i>(with)</i>, to bloat <i>(with)</i></li><li>to hold apart; to strut off</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schedule (<i>time-based plan of events</i>)</li><li>timetable (<i>trains, lessons, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>расписа́ть</i> (raspisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to enter (numerical values, dates, or names)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘a document’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to register one's marriage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to admit openly, to acknowledge [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>расписа́ть</i> (raspisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receipt (written acknowledgement)</li><li>promissory note</li></ol><p><i>расписа́ть</i> (raspisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painted</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) multicolored, motley</li></ol><p><i>расписа́ть</i> (raspisátʹ, “to paint”) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) to enter (numerical values, dates, or names)</li></ol><p><i>расписа́ть</i> (raspisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sign [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘a document’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to register one's marriage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to admit openly, to acknowledge [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>распи́сывать</i> (raspísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink (usually alcohol) with someone else</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt <i>(down)</i>, to found, to fuse</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пла́вить</i> (plávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to melt</li></ol><p><i>распла́вить</i> (rasplávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt <i>(down)</i>, to found, to fuse</li></ol><p><i>распла́вить</i> (rasplávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to melt</li></ol><p><i>расплавля́ть</i> (rasplavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst into tears</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пла́кать</i> (plákatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut into layers</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread flat</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пласта́ть</i> (plastátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie flat</li></ol><p><i>распласта́ть</i> (rasplastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut into layers</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread flat</li></ol><p><i>распласта́ть</i> (rasplastátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie flat</li></ol><p><i>распла́стывать</i> (rasplástyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment, pay</li><li>reckoning</li><li>payoff</li><li>penalty</li><li>satisfaction</li><li>atonement (reconciliation of God and mankind)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘for something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to pay off [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘a debt, loan, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to get even with, to take revenge on [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pay for, to receive punishment for [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘a mistake, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘for something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to pay off [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘a debt, loan, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to get even with, to take revenge on [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pay for, to receive punishment for [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘a mistake, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>расплати́ться</i> (rasplatítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to break off (<i>with, esp. after a quarrel</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be done (<i>with</i>), to see the back (<i>of</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плева́ть</i> (plevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spill (liquid) out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плеска́ть</i> (pleskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spill (liquid) out (<i>once</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плесну́ть</i> (plesnútʹ) or from <i>расплеска́ть</i> (raspleskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untwine, to untwist, to unweave, to undo, to unbraid, to unplait</li></ol><p><i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untwine, to untwist, to unweave, to undo, to unbraid, to unplait</li></ol><p><i>расплет-</i> (rasplet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce, to recreate, to spawn</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плоди́ть</i> (plodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propagate, to multiply</li></ol><p><i>расплоди́ть</i> (rasplodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li><li>to be blurred, to become fuzzy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run to fat, to grow obese</li><li>to sail away <i>(from one another)</i></li><li>to spread</li></ol><p><i>расплы́ться</i> (rasplýtʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blurred, indistinct, fuzzy</li><li>vague, indistinct</li></ol><p><i>расплыв(а́ться)</i> (rasplyv(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run</li><li>to be blurred, to become fuzzy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run to fat, to grow obese</li><li>to sail away <i>(from one another)</i></li><li>to spread</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плыть</i> (plytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatten, to crush</li></ol><p><i>расплю́щить</i> (raspljúščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatten, to crush</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>плю́щить</i> (pljúščitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spill (liquid) out</li></ol><p><i>расплеска́ть</i> (raspleskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) dissimilation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recognition, discerning, discernment</li></ol><p><i>распознава́ть</i> (raspoznavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to discern</li></ol><p><i>распозна́ть</i> (raspoznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize, to discern</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>позна́ть</i> (poznátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispose, to have available</li><li>to place, to dispose, to arrange, to set</li><li>to gain, to win over</li><li>to dispose, to be favourable, to be conducive</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle oneself, to make oneself comfortable</li><li>to camp, to set up a camp, to take up a position</li></ol><p><i>располага́ть</i> (raspolagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li><li>to spread, to run, to blur</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread, to sprawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear <i>(apart)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run to fat, to grow obese</li></ol><p><i>расползти́сь</i> (raspolztísʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li><li>to spread, to run, to blur</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread, to sprawl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tear <i>(apart)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run to fat, to grow obese</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placement, arrangement, placing</li><li>situation, location, disposition</li><li>favour/favor, liking</li><li>inclination (to, for); disposition (to, for), propensity</li></ol><p><i>расположи́ть</i> (raspoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frame of mind, state of mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposed towards, sympathetic to [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>inclined [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘towards something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to dispose, to arrange, to set</li><li>to gain, to win over</li><li>to dispose, to be favourable, to be conducive</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>положи́ть</i> (položítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle oneself, to make oneself comfortable</li><li>to camp, to set up a camp, to take up a position</li></ol><p><i>расположи́ть</i> (raspoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>располо́женный</i> (raspolóžennyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unstitch, to tear at the seams</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rip open (skin, a body part, etc.) with a sharp object</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) administrator, manager, steward (one who administers affairs)</li><li>master of ceremonies, host</li></ol><p><i>распоряди́ться</i> (rasporjadítʹsja, “to guide, make arrangements”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to decree [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘for (something unspecified) to be done’</small> <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘for an action to take place’</small>]</li><li>to arrange for, to organize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, e.g. an event’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ряди́ть</i> (rjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, routine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to decree [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘for (something unspecified) to be done’</small> <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘for an action to take place’</small>]</li><li>to arrange for, to organize [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something, e.g. an event’</small>]</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to manage, to administer, to direct [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘money, resources, one's life, etc.’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>распоряди́ться</i> (rasporjadítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disposal, direction</li><li>order, instruction, direction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie one's belt, to remove one's belt, to ungirdle oneself</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to throw aside all restraint, to put aside all shame and decorum, to let oneself go</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-поя́сать</i> (-pojásatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to untie one's belt, to remove one's belt, to ungirdle oneself</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to throw aside all restraint, to put aside all shame and decorum, to let oneself go</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-поя́сывать</i> (-pojásyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>massacre, carnage</li><li><i>(savage)</i> punishment (of), harsh treatment (of), reprisals (against), violence</li><li>short work</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out</li><li>to straighten, to stretch</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat violently, to punish</li><li>to get rid of (someone) by destroying them or rendering them harmless</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to deal with (something), to handle (something), to manage (something)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to eat up (something) without leaving a trace</li><li>to get smoothed out, to even out</li><li>to become straight, to straighten out</li></ol><p><i>распра́вить</i> (rasprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out</li><li>to straighten, to stretch</li></ol><p><i>распра́вить</i> (rasprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat violently, to punish</li><li>to get rid of (someone) by destroying them or rendering them harmless</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to deal with (something), to handle (something), to manage (something)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to eat up (something) without leaving a trace</li><li>to get smoothed out, to even out</li><li>to become straight, to straighten out</li></ol><p><i>расправля́ть</i> (raspravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution (act of distribution or being distributed)</li><li>allocation (<i>e.g. of resources</i>)</li><li>assignment (<i>e.g. of young specialists by educational institutions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>распредели́ть</i> (raspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;inanimate) distributor (various senses)</li><li>distribution center</li></ol><p><i>распредели́ть</i> (raspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) distribution</li><li>distributive</li></ol><p><i>распредели́ть</i> (raspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distribute, to apportion, to allocate (to divide and distribute portions of a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li></ol><p><i>распредели́ть</i> (raspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distribute, to apportion, to allocate (to divide and distribute portions of a whole)</li></ol><p><i>распредели́ть</i> (raspredelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread</li></ol><p><i>распределя́ть</i> (raspredeljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(technical,&#32;statistics) distributed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell (everything to many people), to sell out</li></ol><p><i>распрода́ть</i> (rasprodátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearance sale, fire sale, sale (sale of goods at reduced prices)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell (everything to many people), to sell out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>прода́ть</i> (prodátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>простере́ть</i> (prosterétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to extend</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>простира́ть</i> (prostirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spread, expansion</li><li>distribution, circulation</li><li>dissemination, propagation, spreading, distribution</li><li>prevalence, ubiquitousness</li></ol><p><i>распространи́ть</i> (rasprostranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disseminator, propagator, purveyor</li><li>dealer, distributor</li></ol><p><i>распространи́ть</i> (rasprostranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proliferate, to spread, to diffuse, to distribute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to extend</li><li>to apply</li><li>to enlarge, to expatiate, to dilate</li></ol><p><i>распространи́ть</i> (rasprostranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to proliferate, to spread, to diffuse, to distribute</li></ol><p><i>распространи́ть</i> (rasprostranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to extend</li><li>to apply</li><li>to enlarge, to expatiate, to dilate</li></ol><p><i>распространя́ть</i> (rasprostranjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prevalence</li></ol><p><i>распространённый</i> (rasprostranjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>widespread, prevalent, common</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bid farewell, to say goodbye</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>проща́ться</i> (proščátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discord, strife, skirmish (violent conflict)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>распьрꙗ</i> (raspĭrja), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrěti</i> (“to quarrel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unharness, to unbridle</li></ol><p><i>распря́г-</i> (rasprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>прями́ть</i> (prjamítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten up, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>распрями́ть</i> (rasprjamítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>распрями́ть</i> (rasprjamítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straighten up, to unbend</li></ol><p><i>распрямля́ть</i> (rasprjamljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unharness, to unbridle</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-прячь</i> (-prjačʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пуга́ть</i> (pugátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismiss</li><li>to disband</li><li>to loosen</li><li>to let out</li><li>to dissolve, to melt</li><li>to unknit</li><li>to untuck, to let out</li><li>to allow to get out of hand, to let get out of hand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set afloat, to spread</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(plants)</i> to open, to blossom out</li><li>to become loose, to unloosen, to slacken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become undisciplined, to get out of hand</li><li>to dissolve, to melt</li><li><i>(woolen cloth)</i> to get/come unravelled</li></ol><p><i>распуска́ть</i> (raspuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismiss</li><li>to disband</li><li>to loosen</li><li>to let out</li><li>to dissolve, to melt</li><li>to unknit</li><li>to untuck, to let out</li><li>to allow to get out of hand, to let get out of hand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set afloat, to spread</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(plants)</i> to open, to blossom out</li><li>to become loose, to unloosen, to slacken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become undisciplined, to get out of hand</li><li>to dissolve, to melt</li><li><i>(woolen cloth)</i> to get/come unravelled</li></ol><p><i>распусти́ть</i> (raspustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud season, the time when roads turn impassable (twice a year in spring and autumn)</li><li>impassable roads, flooded roads</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “road”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica, suffix forming abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libertine (someone loose in morals)</li></ol><p>From <i>распу́тный</i> (raspútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fornicate, to whore around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissolute, libertine, profligate, dissipated, immoral, lecherous, wanton, rakish, reprobate, abandoned, wicked, self-abandoned, given to sin, lickerish, Cyprian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzpustьnъ</i>, equivalent to <i>распусти́ть</i> (raspustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lechery, profligacy, lasciviousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crossroads, parting of the ways</li><li>intersection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пу́хнуть</i> (púxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laxity, lack of discipline</li><li>licentiousness, libertinism, dissoluteness</li></ol><p><i>распу́щенный</i> (raspúščennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spraying, atomizing</li><li>spray</li><li>grinding into dust, pulverizing, pulverization</li><li>dissipating, dissipation</li></ol><p><i>распыли́ть</i> (raspylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spray; pulveriser</li></ol><p><i>распыли́ть</i> (raspylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spray, to atomize</li><li>to grind into dust, to pulverize</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dissipate</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to distract</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>пыли́ть</i> (pylítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spray, to atomize</li><li>to grind into dust, to pulverize</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dissipate</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to distract</li></ol><p><i>распыли́ть</i> (raspylítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pulverized, atomized, powdered, turned to dust</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dispersed, scattered, fragmented</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide/to split into five parts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide/to split into five parts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crucifixion, crucifix</li><li>cross</li></ol><p><i>распя́ть</i> (raspjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crucify</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-пять</i> (-pjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seedlings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat, to offer seats</li><li>to separate, to seat separately</li><li>to transplant, to plant out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nursery, seed-plot (place for cultivation of young plants)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hotbed, breeding-ground</li></ol><p><i>расса́да</i> (rassáda) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat, to offer seats</li><li>to separate, to seat separately</li><li>to transplant, to plant out</li></ol><p><i>рассади́ть</i> (rassadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take one's seats</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sprawl</li></ol><p><i>расса́живать</i> (rassáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to dawn</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-свести́</i> (-svestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawn, daybreak</li><li>Rassvet (component of the International Space Station)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>разсвѣтъ</i> (razsvět), from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;розсвѣтъ</i> (&#42;rozsvětŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzsvětъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to dawn</li></ol><p><i>рассвет-</i> (rassvet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) daybreak</li></ol><p><i>рассве́т</i> (rassvét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow furious, to begin to see red</li><li>to become fierce, to become ferocious, to become cruel</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>свирепе́ть</i> (svirepétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to split</li></ol><p><i>рассе́сться</i> (rasséstʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unsaddle</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>седла́ть</i> (sedlátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissipate, to disseminate, to scatter, to diffuse, to disperse</li><li>to sow (seeds) in various places</li></ol><p><i>рассе́ять</i> (rasséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disperse, to scatter</li><li><i>(darkness, clouds etc)</i> to disperse, to dissipate, <i>(smoke, fog)</i> to clear away</li><li><i>(fears, doubts)</i> to blow over</li><li>to divert/distract oneself</li></ol><p><i>рассе́ивать</i> (rasséivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to cleave</li><li>to pass <i>(through)</i>, to cleave</li><li>to cut, to slash</li></ol><p><i>рассе́к-</i> (rassék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declassify</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>секре́т</i> (sekrét) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>declassification</li></ol><p><i>рассекре́чивать</i> (rassekréčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declassify</li></ol><p><i>рассекре́тить</i> (rassekrétitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settling, settlement</li><li>separation, settling apart</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle <i>(in a new place)</i></li><li>to separate, to settle apart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle <i>(in a new place)</i></li><li>to separate, to settle separately</li></ol><p><i>рассели́ть</i> (rasselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle <i>(in a new place)</i></li><li>to separate, to settle apart</li></ol><p><i>рассели́ть</i> (rasselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle <i>(in a new place)</i></li><li>to separate, to settle separately</li></ol><p><i>расселя́ть</i> (rasseljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry, to anger, to rile</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>серди́ть</i> (serdítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get angry</li></ol><p><i>рассерди́ть</i> (rasserdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry, angered (<i>experiencing anger; of a person</i>)</li><li>angry (<i>expressing anger; of a look, tone, face, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take one's seats</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sprawl</li><li>to crack, to split</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, to cleave</li><li>to pass <i>(through)</i>, to cleave</li><li>to cut, to slash</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispersion</li></ol><p><i>рассе́ять</i> (rasséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>absently, absent-mindedly</li></ol><p><i>рассе́янный</i> (rasséjannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispersion</li><li>absent-mindedness</li></ol><p><i>рассе́янный</i> (rasséjannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scattered, dispersed</li><li>absent-minded, forgetful, clumsy (having one's attention scattered)</li><li>(<i>of a light</i>) soft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissipate, to disseminate, to scatter, to diffuse, to disperse</li><li>to sow (seeds) in various places</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disperse, to scatter</li><li><i>(darkness, clouds etc)</i> to disperse, to dissipate, <i>(smoke, fog)</i> to clear away</li><li><i>(fears, doubts)</i> to blow over</li><li>to divert/distract oneself</li></ol><p><i>рассе́ять</i> (rasséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>story, tale, narrative</li><li>short story</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сказ</i> (skaz), synchronically deverbal from <i>рассказать</i> (rasskazatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell</li><li>to relate, to narrate</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz): little story, little tale</li></ol><p><i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz, “story”)</li></ol><p><i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrator, storyteller</li></ol><p><i>расска́з</i> (rasskáz) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell</li><li>to relate, to narrate, to recount</li></ol><p><i>рассказа́ть</i> (rasskazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be told</li></ol><p><i>расска́зывать</i> (rasskázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to relax</li><li>to weaken, to enfeeble, to unnerve</li></ol><p>From <i>раз-</i> (raz-) +&lrm; <i>слабе́ть</i> (slabétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to relax</li></ol><p><i>рассла́бить</i> (rasslábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakening</li><li>relaxation, relaxing</li></ol><p><i>рассла́бить</i> (rasslábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relaxedness, relaxation, lack of stress</li><li>limpness, slackness, flaccidity</li><li>enervation, lack of strength and energy</li></ol><p><i>рассла́бленный</i> (rassláblennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relaxed, at ease</li><li>limp, flaccid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) paralytic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to relax</li><li>to weaken, to enfeeble, to unnerve</li></ol><p><i>рассла́бить</i> (rasslábitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to relax</li></ol><p><i>расслабля́ть</i> (rasslabljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) state of content relaxation; state of being chill</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>рассла́бить</i> (rasslábitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigation (the act of investigating)</li></ol><p><i>рассле́довать</i> (rasslédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigator</li></ol><p><i>рассле́довать</i> (rasslédovatʹ, “to investigate”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to investigate, to look <i>(into)</i>, to hold an inquiry <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exfoliation, stratification</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>слой</i> (sloj) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear successfully, to catch</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at, to peruse, to examine</li><li>to regard, to consider</li></ol><p><i>рассмотре́ть</i> (rassmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai) (whence <i>скепти́ческий</i> (skeptíčeskij)) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speḱ-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (someone) laugh</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>смеши́ть</i> (smešítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burst out laughing, to start laughing</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>смея́ться</i> (smejátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination (the process of examining)</li><li>consideration (the process of considering)</li></ol><p><i>рассмотре́ть</i> (rassmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at, to peruse, to examine</li><li>to regard, to consider</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brine; pickle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzsolъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сол</i> (sol). Cognate with Polish <i>rosół</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rassolnik</li></ol><p><i>рассо́л</i> (rassól, “brine”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resolve, to dissolve</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to disperse, to scatter</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to question, to make inquiries <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>расспроси́ть</i> (rassprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>questioning, making inquiries</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to question, to make inquiries <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>спроси́ть</i> (sprosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>credit</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parting (farewell)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to part, to separate</li><li>to leave (homeland, home)</li><li>to give up</li><li>to break up</li></ol><p><i>расста́ться</i> (rasstátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to arrange</li><li>to move apart</li><li><i>(a dress etc.)</i> to let out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place, to arrange</li><li>to move apart</li><li><i>(a dress etc.)</i> to let out</li></ol><p><i>расста́вить</i> (rasstávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement (act of arranging)</li><li>ranking</li><li>collocation</li><li>disposal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to part, to separate</li><li>to leave (homeland, home)</li><li>to give up</li><li>to break up</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) rasstegai (type of pirozhok with an open filling)</li><li>an old Russian festive dress (sarafan)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>расстега́ть</i> (rasstegátʹ, “to unfasten”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbuckle, to unzip, to unbutton, to unfasten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread <i>(out)</i>, to lay</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fawn <i>(upon)</i>, to bow and scrape <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>расстели́ть</i> (rasstelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread <i>(out)</i>, to lay</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-стила́ть</i> (-stilátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fawn <i>(upon)</i>, to bow and scrape <i>(before)</i></li></ol><p><i>расстила́ть</i> (rasstilátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to unlock, to free (<i>something held in place by a latch, stop, pin, arresting device, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to loosen (<i>a locked nut or screw</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сто́порить</i> (stóporitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance, interval, length, range, space</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>distance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disorder, to disturb, to unsettle</li><li>to upset, to disrupt, to derange</li><li>to frustrate, to derange, to thwart</li></ol><p><i>расстро́ить</i> (rasstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall into confusion</li></ol><p><i>расстра́ивать</i> (rasstráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooting, execution <i>(by a firing squad)</i></li><li>shooting down, fusillade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to execute (by shooting)</li><li>to expose to heavy (artillery or gun-) fire</li></ol><p><i>расстреля́ть</i> (rasstreljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot someone dead, to execute (by shooting)</li><li>to expose to heavy (artillery or gun-) fire</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upset, sad, downcast</li><li>(<i>of a musical instrument</i>) untuned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disorder, to disturb, to unsettle</li><li>to upset, to disrupt, to derange</li><li>to frustrate, to derange, to thwart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get upset</li></ol><p><i>расстро́ить</i> (rasstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upset, disappointment, frustration, vexation (emotions)</li><li>Something that causes the above.</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) upset, disturbance, disruption, imbalance</li><li>disorder</li><li>(<i>music</i>) The state of an instrument being out of tune.</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) The state of a receiver being out of tune.</li><li>(<i>healthcare</i>) A disease where an organ is malfunctioning.</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) A state of inner turmoil.</li></ol><p><i>расстро́ить</i> (rasstróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(pathology,&#32;psychology) autism spectrum disorder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) personality disorder (one of a class of mental disorders)</li></ol><p><i>расстройство</i> (rasstrojstvo, “disorder”) +&lrm; <i>личность</i> (ličnostʹ, “personality”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give way, to step aside</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open, to part</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give way, to step aside</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open, to part</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undocking (the process of disconnecting one spacecraft from another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unbuckle, to unzip, to unbutton, to unfasten</li></ol><p><i>расстегну́ть</i> (rasstegnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judiciousness, reasonableness, judgement, sobriety</li></ol><p><i>рассуди́тельный</i> (rassudítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>level-headed, rational, reasonable</li></ol><p><i>рассуди́ть</i> (rassudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to argue, to reason</li><li>to debate, to discuss [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘(about) someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to judge</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reason, sense, senses</li><li>judgment, mind</li></ol><p><i>рассуди́ть</i> (rassudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to argue, to reason</li><li>to debate, to discuss [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘(about) someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рассуди́ть</i> (rassudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasoning (action of the verb 'to reason')</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) judgement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk slowly or for too long with unnecessary details</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to do something very slowly (lingering or procrastinating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to compute</li><li>to dismiss, to pay off; to sack; to discharge, to fire</li><li>to number off, to order to number</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle accounts <i>(with)</i>, to reckon <i>(with)</i>, to square up <i>(with)</i>, to settle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get even, to get back some of one's own</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to number <i>(off)</i></li><li>to pay <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>рассчита́ть</i> (rassčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate, to compute</li><li>to dismiss, to pay off; to sack; to discharge, to fire</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to count / reckon (on); to calculate (on, upon), to depend (on, upon), to rely</li><li>to number off, to order according to number</li></ol><p><i>рассчита́ть</i> (rassčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle accounts <i>(with)</i>, to reckon <i>(with)</i>, to square up <i>(with)</i>, to settle up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get even, to get back some of one's own</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to number <i>(off)</i></li><li>to pay <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>рассчи́тывать</i> (rassčítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send <i>(about, round)</i>, to distribute, to circulate</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution, delivery, mailing, sending</li><li>newsletter</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) courier, runner, delivery boy</li></ol><p><i>рассыл(а́ть)</i> (rassyl(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) distribution, sending</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, errand boy (a male employed to run errands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spill, to strew, to scatter</li><li>to pour out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>рассы́пать</i> (rassýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumble, to fall to pieces; to disintegrate</li></ol><p><i>ра́ссыпать</i> (rássypatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>рассыпа́ть</i> (rassypátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crumbly, friable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unsaddle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mildly&#32;vulgar</i>) expresses that the speaker reckons a result consequential and has no emotional investment herefore, usually following a demonstrative pronoun in the clause, hell if I care</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push away, aside, or apart (all or many people)</li><li>to shake awake</li></ol><p>From <i>растолка́ть</i> (rastolkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or <i>растолк(ну́ть)</i> (rastolk(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to kindle</li><li>to melt, to thaw</li></ol><p><i>растопи́ть</i> (rastopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample, to stamp <i>(on)</i>, to crush</li><li>to crush, to stamp out, to ride roughshod <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>растопта́ть</i> (rastoptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take away <i>(bit by bit)</i>, to remove <i>(part by part)</i>, to pilfer</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take away <i>(bit by bit)</i>, to remove <i>(part by part)</i>, to pilfer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag/pull apart</li></ol><p><i>растаск</i> (rastask) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take away <i>(bit by bit)</i>, to remove <i>(part by part)</i>, to pilfer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag/pull apart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt, to thaw</li><li>to melt away, to wane, to dwindle</li><li>to melt <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) solution</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) mortar, grout</li><li>span, extent of opening</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orztvorъ</i>. Deverbal from <i>растворя́ть, раствори́ть</i> (rastvorjátʹ, rastvorítʹ, “to dissolve”).</p><p>Deverbal from <i>растворя́ть, раствори́ть</i> (rastvorjátʹ, rastvorítʹ, “to open, to spread open”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissolution</li></ol><p><i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soluble, dissoluble, dissolvable</li></ol><p><i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instant coffee (beverage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) dissolvent, solvent, thinner, dilutant</li></ol><p><i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to open suddenly (e.g. a window)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to spread (e.g. legs of a compass or person)</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;transitive) to dissolve</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knead (dough or clay)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-твори́ть</i> (-tvorítʹ)</p><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open (<i>of doors, flaps, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dissolve</li></ol><p><i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ, “to open”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ, “to dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to dissolve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dissolve</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to open (<i>of doors, flaps, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>растворя́ть</i> (rastvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to run</li><li>to spread about</li></ol><p><i>расте́к-</i> (rasték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) plant (living organism)</li></ol><p><i>раст-</i> (rast-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant growing, agricultural cultivation</li><li>agricultural science</li></ol><p><i>расте́ние</i> (rasténije) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind</li><li>to spread</li><li>to rub, to massage</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ). Compare Czech <i>rozetřít</i>, Kashubian <i>rozetrzéc</i>, Silesian <i>rozetrzić</i>, and Polish <i>rozetrzeć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment; laceration; tearing to pieces</li></ol><p><i>растерза́ть</i> (rasterzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear to pieces</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>терза́ть</i> (terzátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear to pieces</li></ol><p><i>растерза́ть</i> (rasterzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an absentminded person, loser (of things), a heedless person, muddler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perplexedly (in a confused or puzzled manner)</li></ol><p><i>расте́рянный</i> (rastérjannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perplexity (quality of being confused or puzzled)</li></ol><p><i>расте́рянный</i> (rastérjannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>embarrassed, abashed (<i>of a person; experiencing embarrassment</i>)</li><li>embarrassed, abashed (<i>of a look, glance, voice, etc.; expressing embarrassment</i>)</li><li>confused, bewildered (<i>of a person; experiencing confusion</i>)</li><li>confused, bewildered (<i>of a look, glance, voice, etc.; expressing confusion</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose little by little</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get lost (of multiple objects, especially if they are many or they are lost one after another)</li><li>to lose one's head; to become confused, to mixed up flustered; to be bewildered</li></ol><p><i>растеря́ть</i> (rasterjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an absent-minded person, muddler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to run</li><li>to spread about</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to increase</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orsti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind</li><li>to spread</li><li>to rub, to massage</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ). Compare Czech <i>rozetírat</i>, Kashubian <i>rozcerac</i>, Silesian <i>rozciyrać</i>, and Polish <i>rozcierać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become powdered, to turn into powder, to become triturated</li><li>to rub oneself</li></ol><p><i>растира́ть</i> (rastirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetable oil (oil produced from plants)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vegetation, verdure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hair (especially on the face)</li></ol><p><i>расти́тельный</i> (rastítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vegetation, vegetable (<i>relational</i>), vegetal; vegetative</li></ol><p><i>расти́ть</i> (rastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow, to raise, to cultivate</li><li>to breed, to rear, to raise</li><li>to bring up</li><li>to train, to prepare, to form</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orstiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deflower (a virgin by rape or seduction)</li><li>to corrupt morally</li></ol><p><i>растли́ть</i> (rastlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deflowering (<i>of a virgin by rape or seduction</i>)</li><li>moral corruption (<i>action or result</i>)</li></ol><p><i>растли́ть</i> (rastlítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deflower (a virgin by rape or seduction)</li><li>to corrupt morally</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тлить</i> (tlitʹ, “to rot, to spoil (<i>transitive</i>)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push away, aside, or apart (all or many people)</li><li>to shake awake</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain <i>(something to someone)</i>, to make someone see/understand something</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain <i>(something to someone)</i>, to make someone see/understand something</li></ol><p><i>растолкова́ть</i> (rastolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, to crush, to grind (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, to kindle</li><li>to melt, to thaw</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting, kindling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kindling wood</li></ol><p><i>растопи́ть</i> (rastopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample, to stamp <i>(on)</i>, to crush</li><li>to crush, to stamp out, to ride roughshod <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread wide apart</li></ol><p><i>растопы́рить</i> (rastopýritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread wide apart</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>топы́рить</i> (topýritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cancel, to dissolve, to annul, to abrogate</li></ol><p>Ultimately descends from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъrgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cancel, to dissolve, to annul, to abrogate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancellation, rescission, breaking off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divorce</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightness</li><li>alacrity (promptness)</li></ol><p><i>расторо́пный</i> (rastorópnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prompt, efficient, and quick-witted</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>торопи́ть</i> (toropítʹ, “to hurry”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>розторо́пний</i> (roztorópnyj), Polish <i>roztropny</i> (“clever”) and more distantly Lithuanian <i>tar̃pti</i> (“to succeed, to prosper”) (1sg. <i>tarpstù</i>), <i>tarpà</i> (“prosperity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk thistle (<i>Silybum</i>-genus plants)</li></ol><p>Cognate to Ukrainian <i>росторо́пша</i> (rostorópša), <i>расторо́пша</i> (rastorópša), <i>розтро́пта</i> (roztrópta), <i>розторо́пша</i> (roztorópša), Belarusian <i>растаро́пша</i> (rastarópša, “milk thistle”), and Ukrainian <i>росто́паш</i> (rostópaš), <i>ростопаст</i> (rostopast), <i>ростокащ</i> (rostokašč), <i>росто́хвасть</i> (rostóxvastʹ), <i>ростропаск</i> (rostropask, “greater celandine, <i>Chelidonium majus</i>”).</p><p>Old Polish <i>ostropest</i> (“milk thistle”) is a borrowing from Old Czech <i>ostropezd</i> (“cotton thistle”), while Slovene <i>ostropes</i>, <i>ostropec</i> (“milk thistle”) is probably from Slovak <i>ostropes</i>, <i>ostropestrec</i>.</p><p>The original compound is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profligate (an overly wasteful individual)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander, to waste, to blow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spending</li><li>waste</li><li>embezzlement, peculation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander, to waste, to fritter away</li><li>to illegally spend or use (someone else's money or property)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who wastes someone else's money or property</li></ol><p><i>растра́т(ить)</i> (rastrát(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander, to waste, to fritter away</li><li>to illegally spend or use (someone else's money or property)</li></ol><p><i>растра́тить</i> (rastrátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to very strongly alarm</li><li>to disturb very much, to upset strongly</li></ol><p><i>растрево́жить</i> (rastrevóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to very strongly alarm</li><li>to disturb very much, to upset strongly</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>трево́жить</i> (trevóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread the word, to gossip, to divulge information</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>трезво́нить</i> (trezvónitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disarrange</li><li><i>(book etc)</i> to tatter, to deface</li><li>to tear to pieces</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to blab, to blabber, to give away</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to chap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to touch, to affect deeply</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тро́гать</i> (trógatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funnel-shaped extension</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>flare (<i>of a skirt, bell, jeans, etc.</i>)</li><li>bell (<i>of a trumpet</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ochrea, ocrea</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) + the root of <i>труба́</i> (trubá, “pipe”). Compare Polish <i>roztrąb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruffled, disheveled (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li>unkempt, slovenly</li><li>ragged, tattered (<i>from use</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>growing, rising, increasing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch <i>(out)</i></li><li>to wear out, to strain (<i>clothes</i>)</li><li>to strain, to sprain</li><li>to stretch out, to spread out</li><li>to protract, to prolong, to spin out, to drag out</li></ol><p><i>растяг-</i> (rastjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to lengthen out</li><li>to be worn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch oneself, to sprawl</li><li>to stretch out, to spread</li><li>to drag on</li></ol><p><i>растя́гивать</i> (rastjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expansion, stretching</li><li>sprain (act or result of spraining)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretching, extension, lengthening out</li><li>(sports,&#32;ballet) stretch, flexibility</li><li>device for stretching</li><li>(military,&#32;colloquial) tripwire; mine or grenade triggered by a tripwire</li></ol><p><i>растяг-</i> (rastjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stretched, prolonged, unnecessarily long</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch <i>(out)</i></li><li><i>(clothes)</i> to wear out, to strain</li><li>to strain, to sprain</li><li>to stretch out, to spread out</li><li>to protract, to prolong, to spin out, to drag out</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, to lengthen out</li><li>to be worn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stretch oneself, to sprawl</li><li>to stretch out, to spread</li><li>to drag on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go sprawling, to measure one's length</li></ol><p><i>растяну́ть</i> (rastjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gawk, gaper, scatterbrain, (a distracted, inattentive, clumsy person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to forbeat, to fuck up</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>фига́чить</i> (figáčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disband, to dissolve (an organization or group)</li><li>to decouple, to uncouple (a train)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>формирова́ть</i> (formirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disband, to dissolve (an organization or group)</li><li>to decouple, to uncouple (a train)</li></ol><p><i>расформирова́ть</i> (rasformirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk/strut about</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise strongly, to commend</li></ol><p><i>расхвали́ть</i> (rasxvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise strongly, to commend</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief that stole on many occasions, or on many locations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunder, to steal, to embezzle</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-хи́тить</i> (-xítitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunder, to steal, to embezzle</li></ol><p><i>расхи́тить</i> (rasxítitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose (<i>of a door, etc.</i>)</li><li>shaky, wobbly, unsteady</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) undisciplined, disorganized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to eat together (liquid food)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to disentangle, to fix or face consequences, to face the music</li></ol><p><i>расхлеба́ть</i> (rasxlebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expense, expenditure</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) charges, expenses, exps, outlay</li><li>debit</li><li>consumption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to disperse (in different directions), to spread</li><li>to break up (<i>of a crowd, meeting, etc.</i>)</li><li>to part, to separate, to break up (<i>of two or three persons</i>), to get divorced, to drift apart</li><li>to be sold out (well), to sell well (<i>of a product</i>)</li><li>to disagree, to differ (<i>of opinions</i>)</li><li>to diverge, to branch off</li><li>to miss one another, to fail to meet</li><li>to be spent, to go (<i>of money</i>)</li><li>to separate, to not close (<i>of clothes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>разойти́сь</i> (razojtísʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) expense</li><li>expendable, consumable</li></ol><p><i>расхо́д</i> (rasxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expense, expenditure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) consumption</li></ol><p><i>расхо́довать</i> (rasxódovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li><li>to consume, to use up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spend, to lay out money</li><li>to be used <i>(up)</i>, to be spent/consumed</li></ol><p><i>расхо́довать</i> (rasxódovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flow meter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divergence</li><li>discrepancy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(goods)</i> in demand, popular</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in everyday use</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) common, widespread, commonplace, stock, stereotyped</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>расход(и́ться)</i> (rasxod(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To make someone cold, indifferent; to make someone disappointed in something</li><li>To moderate the strength and vividness of feelings in someone.</li></ol><p><i>расхолоди́ть</i> (rasxolodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stop wanting, to lose desire (for) [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) for one to stop wanting or lose desire (for) [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>расхоте́ть</i> (rasxotétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to burst out laughing, to roar with laughter</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>хохота́ть</i> (xoxotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blossom, to bloom (have or open into blossoms)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to flourish, to thrive, to prosper</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flowering, blooming, blossoming</li><li>boom (period of prosperity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blossom, to bloom (have or open into blossoms)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to flourish, to thrive, to prosper</li></ol><p><i>расцвет-</i> (rascvet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) colours/colors, colouration/coloration, colouring/coloring</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss fervently, to kiss over and over, to smother with kisses</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>целова́ть</i> (celovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss fervently, to kiss over and over, to smother with kisses</li></ol><p><i>расцелова́ть</i> (rascelovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to estimate, to value, to assess</li><li>to rate <i>(as)</i>, to consider, to regard <i>(as)</i></li></ol><p><i>расцени́ть</i> (rascenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to estimate, to value, to assess</li><li>to rate <i>(as)</i>, to consider, to regard <i>(as)</i></li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price, pricing, rate, tariff</li></ol><p><i>расцени́ть</i> (rascenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncoupling, disengagement</li></ol><p><i>расцепи́ть</i> (rascepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li><li>to card (flax or wool)</li><li>to scratch raw, to irritate by scratching</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat, to defeat</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb one's hair</li></ol><p><i>расчеса́ть</i> (rasčesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) alternative form of <i>рассчита́ть</i> (rassčitátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) alternative form of <i>рассчита́ться</i> (rassčitátʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>расче́сть</i> (rasčéstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear, to clear away</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearing (<i>of land, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>расчи́стить</i> (rasčístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clear, to clear away</li></ol><p><i>расчи́стить</i> (rasčístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dismemberment, dissection</li><li>division</li></ol><p><i>расчлени́ть</i> (rasčlenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismember</li><li>to divide, to break up</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to break up, to open out, to break down</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismember</li><li>to divide, to break up</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to break up, to open out, to break down</li></ol><p><i>расчлени́ть</i> (rasčlenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be deeply moved (<i>emotionally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb (toothed implement for grooming the hair)</li></ol><p><i>расчеса́ть</i> (rasčesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li><li>to card (flax or wool)</li><li>to scratch raw, to irritate by scratching</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat, to defeat</li></ol><p><i>расчеса́ть</i> (rasčesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb one's hair</li></ol><p><i>расчёсывать</i> (rasčósyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation (mathematical operation)</li><li>estimation, expectation, intention, expectation, assumption, calculation (of a future event)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) payment, settlement of accounts (compensation for purchased goods/services received)</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) calculated amount for a termination payment / redundancy payout / severance package</li><li>settlement (process of determining a payment)</li><li>consideration, account</li><li>(<i>military</i>) gun crew, gunners (group of soldiers operating an artillery piece, machine gun, etc.)</li><li>(finance,&#32;in the plural) payment (acts of transferring money)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzčьtъ</i>, from <i>&#42;čisti</i>; synchronically analyzable as a deverbal form of <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-читать</i> (-čitatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prudent, calculating</li><li>self-seeking, calculating</li><li>economical, thrifty</li></ol><p><i>расчёт</i> (rasčót) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) calculation</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pay, payment</li><li>estimated</li></ol><p><i>расчёт</i> (rasčót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to shake, to sway, to make loose, to unsteady, to shaky</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to undermine, to weaken, to destabilize</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>шата́ть</i> (šatátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rock, to shake, to sway, to make loose, to unsteady, to shaky</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to undermine, to weaken, to destabilize</li></ol><p><i>расшата́ть</i> (rasšatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widening, expansion (act of widening)</li><li>(<i>file</i>) extension</li></ol><p><i>расши́рить</i> (rasšíritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) extended reality</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>extended reality</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expanded, enlarged, broadened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to widen, to broaden</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to increase</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to broaden, to enlarge, to extend, to expand</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>расширити</i> (rasširiti), asynchronically analyzable as <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>ши́рить</i> (šíritʹ, “to widen”). See <i>ширь</i> (širʹ, “breadth, width”) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to widen, to broaden</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to broaden, to enlarge, to extend, to expand</li></ol><p><i>расши́рить</i> (rasšíritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to widen, to broaden</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to increase</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to broaden, to enlarge, to extend, to expand</li></ol><p><i>расши́рить</i> (rasšíritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to widen, to broaden</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to broaden, to enlarge, to extend, to expand</li></ol><p><i>расширя́ть</i> (rasširjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroider</li><li>to unseam, to rip, to undo</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decipher, to decode, to decrypt</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ), or <i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>шифр</i> (šifr) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deciphering, decoding, decryption</li></ol><p><i>расшифрова́ть</i> (rasšifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decipherer</li></ol><p><i>расшифров(а́ть)</i> (rasšifrov(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decipher, to decode, to decrypt</li></ol><p><i>расшифрова́ть</i> (rasšifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlace</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>шнур</i> (šnur) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unlace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleft, crevice, fissure (narrow crack in rock or ice)</li><li>crack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščelina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave</li></ol><p><i>рас-</i> (ras-) +&lrm; <i>-щепи́ть</i> (-ščepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>splitting, splintering</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) breaking up, decomposition</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) splitting, fission</li></ol><p><i>расщепи́ть</i> (rasščepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave, to fission</li></ol><p><i>расщепи́ть</i> (rasščepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, to cleave, to fission</li></ol><p><i>расщепля́ть</i> (rasščepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ratatouille</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ratatouille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ratification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ratify</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>ratifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) warrior, soldier</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ortьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рать</i> (ratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literary) military, combat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ortьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рать</i> (ratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to fight <i>(for)</i>, to stand up <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to declaim <i>(against)</i>, to inveigh <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ortovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рать</i> (ratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) administrative body of a city or township</li><li>town hall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ратуша</i> (ratuša), from Polish <i>ratusz</i>, from Middle High German <i>rāthūs</i> (whence also German <i>Rathaus</i>); ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēdaz</i> + Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūsą</i>. Polish /ʃ/ replaced Middle High German <i>s</i> because at the time, this letter was pronounced as apicoalveolar /s̺/ (with a weak hushing sound, similar to Castilian Spanish) and was distinct from the letter written <i>z</i>, pronounced as laminoalveolar /s/ (with a strong hissing sound, similar to English).</p><p>Compare parallel Russian <i>Дом Сове́тов</i> (Dom Sovétov, “House of Soviets”) (Дом Советов), <i>дом сове́та</i> (dom sovéta) from <i>дом</i> (dom) + <i>сове́т</i> (sovét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) army, host, array</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) battle, war</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ortь</i>. Related to <i>реть</i> (retĭ, “quarrel, competition”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ऋति</i> (ṛti, “assault”), Sanskrit <i>ऋतीयते</i> (ṛtīyate, “to quarrel, to hate”), Sanskrit <i>समरण</i> (samaraṇa, “war, strife”), Ancient Greek <i>ἔρῐς</i> (érĭs, “strife, quarrel”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ernustuz</i> (see there for further descendants)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Rauvolfia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Rauvolfia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) round</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>round</i>. Further from Latin <i>rotundus</i> (from <i>rotō</i> or from <i>rota</i>). Doublet of <i>ро́ндо</i> (róndo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rout (fashionable assembly or large evening party)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raf coffee (raph coffee) (a coffee drink made from espresso, vanilla sugar or syrup, and cream that is mixed and whipped with steam from an espresso machine in a pitcher)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) minibus, van <i>(manufactured by RAF works in Riga)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubed sugar, sugar cubes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>raffinade</i>, from <i>raffiner</i> (“to refine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purified, refined</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) pure, genuine</li><li>(literary,&#32;derogatory) overly refined, recherché</li><li>refined, exquisite, sophisticated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refine, to purify</li></ol><p>French <i>raffiner</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkish delight, rahat lokum, lokum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راحت لوقوم (rahat lokum), vulgar form of راحت الحلقوم (râhatü'l-hulkûm), itself from Arabic رَاحَة الْحُلْقُوم (rāḥat al-ḥulqūm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rachitis (rickets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;disapprovingly) long edifying speech</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>oracja</i>, from Latin <i>oratio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;philosophy</i>) ratiocination (reasoning, conscious deliberate inference)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>ratiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diet, the food and beverage a person or animal consumes</li><li>ration, food allowance</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ratiō</i>. Doublet of <i>резо́н</i> (rezón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) rational person, rationalist</li><li>rationality</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) rationale (Catholic vestment)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ratiōnālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rationalization (process or result of rationalizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rationalize (to make something rational)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rationalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rationalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rationally</li><li>sensibly, in a worthwhile manner</li><li>logically</li></ol><p><i>рациона́льный</i> (racionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rationality</li></ol><p><i>рациона́льный</i> (racionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rational</li><li>sensible, worthwhile</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) logical</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) rational</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ratiōnālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portable radio transmitter, walkie-talkie</li></ol><p>Short for радиостанция</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crayfish, crawfish</li></ol><p><i>рак</i> (rak) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to desire, to want</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to be diligent, to strive</li><li>(video games&#32;slang,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to play unskillfully</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;by extension) to do anything unskillfully</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;račiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) rush</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rush</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;politics,&#32;derogatory) Ruscism (ideology, militarist Russian nationalism)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Ра́ша</i> (Ráša, “Russia (derogatory)”) +&lrm; <i>фаши́зм</i> (fašízm, “fascism”), identical to Ukrainian <i>раши́зм</i> (rašýzm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;derogatory) rashist, russist (a Russian nationalist, especially one engaged in war against Ukraine or with supremacist views)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;derogatory,&#32;specifically) a Russian soldier involved during the 2022 invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>Ра́ша</i> (Ráša, “Russia”) (<i>derogatory</i>) +&lrm; <i>фаши́ст</i> (fašíst, “fascist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;usually&#32;Ukraine,&#32;derogatory,&#32;attributive) Ruscist, supportive of aggression of the Russian Federation</li></ol><p><i>раши́ст</i> (rašíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), blend of <i>Ра́ша</i> (Ráša) +&lrm; <i>фаши́стский</i> (fašístskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grill, grid, gridiron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rasp (<i>coarse file</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Raspel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>райо́н</i> (rajón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear</li><li>to jerk, to tug</li><li>to start with a jerk, to get off with a jerk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash, to rush, to race, to scurry, to spurt; <i>(car)</i> to speed, to tear off, to zip, to zoom</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take flight, to take to one's heels, to whip off, to whip away, to dash off</li><li>to blow up, to blast</li></ol><p><i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rush, to dash, to dart</li></ol><p><i>рвану́ть</i> (rvanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torn</li><li>jagged</li><li>uneven</li></ol><p><i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear (rend)</li><li>to tear apart</li><li>to tear out</li><li>to break</li><li>to break up, to break off [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>to pick, to pluck <i>flowers or fruits</i></li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to vomit, to puke, to feel sick (<i>impersonal, the person vomiting is the object</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръвати</i> (rŭvati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъvàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) to run for it; to run for one's life; to get out of Dodge</li></ol><p>Literally, “to tear claws”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to burst, to be torn</li><li>to strive, to long, to be dying to (to do something)</li></ol><p><i>рва́ть</i> (rvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) self-seeker, grabber</li></ol><p><i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fervor, zeal, ardor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>рьвьньѥ, рьвениѥ</i> (rĭvĭnĭje, rĭvenije), from <i>&#42;рьвьнь</i> (&#42;rĭvĭnĭ, “zeal”) +&lrm; <i>-ьѥ</i> (-ĭje). Cognate with Russian <i>ре́вность</i> (révnostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vomit (the regurgitated contents of the stomach)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pondweed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sago pondweed (<i>Stuckenia pectinata</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рде́ст</i> (rdést, “pondweed”) +&lrm; <i>гребе́нчатый</i> (grebénčatyj, “pectinate”), A calque of translingual <i>Stuckenia pectinata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) to redden, to blush</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръдѣти сѧ</i> (rŭděti sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъděti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) red</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>D (musical note)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>re-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rehabilitation</li></ol><p><i>реабилита́ция</i> (reabilitácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rehabilitation</li><li>exoneration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehabilitate, to exonerate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>réhabiliter</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reagent, agent (active power or cause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reacting</li></ol><p><i>реаги́ровать</i> (reagírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to react, to respond [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to something/someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agent, reagent (active power or cause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket artillery <i>(artillery consisting of ballistic missiles and rocket launchers such as the Katyushas, as well as cross-country combat vehicles outfitted with multiple rocket launchers)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket launcher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jet efflux</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reactive</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) jet, impulse, pulse; jet-propelled</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rocket</li></ol><p>From <i>реакти́в</i> (reaktív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reaction engine</li><li>jet engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailpipe (of a jet plane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) jetpack</li></ol><p>From <i>реакти́вный</i> (reaktívnyj, “jet-propelled”) +&lrm; <i>ра́нец</i> (ránec, “backpack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jet (airplane)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermoset</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reactor (a device which uses energy to produce heat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reactionary</li></ol><p><i>реа́кция</i> (reákcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reaction</li><li>response</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) reaction, extreme conservatism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) real (former unit of currency of Spain and Spain's colonies)</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) composing frame, rack</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Internet) real world, real life, reality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realization (the act of making real)</li><li>implementation (the process of moving an idea from concept to reality)</li><li>implementation (a result of implementing something)</li></ol><p>Compare Czech <i>realizace</i> and Polish <i>realizacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to realize, to bring about, to see fulfilled</li><li>to sell, to convert into cash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to materialize, to appear, to be realized</li></ol><p><i>реализова́ть</i> (realizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implement, to carry out, to realize</li><li>to sell, to realize</li></ol><p><i>реализова́ть</i> (realizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to materialize, to appear, to be realized</li></ol><p><i>реализо́вывать</i> (realizóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>realistic</li></ol><p><i>реали́ст</i> (realíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>realistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality show (a program of reality television)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>reality show</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>actual, <i>(plural)</i> realia, reality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, in reality</li><li>objectively</li><li>specifically, factually</li></ol><p><i>реа́льный</i> (reálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality</li></ol><p><i>реа́льный</i> (reálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>real (that has physical existence)</li><li>realistic</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>reālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) resuscitation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) resuscitation department, intensive care unit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) resuscitation ambulance</li><li>revival, effort to breathe life</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reanimate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) rebab, rawap</li></ol><p>From Arabic ربابة.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ribbed; ribby</li></ol><p><i>ребро́</i> (rebró) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) rib</li><li>edge, verge, arc</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) edge</li><li>(<i>graph theory</i>) edge</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) roentgenizdat; music illicitly reproduced on X-ray film</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ребро</i> (rebro), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rebrò</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;rébra</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rebʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebutia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Rebutia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young men, boys, friends, comrades</li></ol><p>From the plural form of <i>ребёнок</i> (rebjónok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta): (small) children, kids</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) group of children, kids</li></ol><p><i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta) +&lrm; <i>-и́шки</i> (-íški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta) children, kids; lads, boys</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) children, kids</li></ol><p><i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) diminutive of <i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta); guys</li></ol><p><i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta) +&lrm; <i>-ушки</i> (-uški)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childish, infantile (behaving immaturely)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childishness, puerility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) children's, child's</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) childish</li></ol><p>From <i>ребя́та</i> (rebjáta) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), with unexpected iotation (compare <i>щеня́чий</i> (ščenjáčij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural де́ти, less commonly ребя́та</i>) child, kid, baby: children, kids, babies</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) (<i>plural ребя́та</i>) guys, fellows, friends, comrades</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>робѧ</i> (robę), diminutive of <i>робъ</i> (robŭ, “slave”) from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbę</i>. Compare with <i>па́робок</i> (párobok, “lad”) (<i>dialectal</i>). The relationship with "slave" could arise from the meaning "orphan" because orphans carried out the hard work in the household.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ребёнок</i> (rebjónok): (little) baby, child, kid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revenge, retaliation</li><li>rematch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>revanche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revanchism (political policy of endeavouring to regain lost territory)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>revanchisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhubarb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راوند (<i>ravent</i>), from Persian راوند (râvand).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtsey</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) servility</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>révérence</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reverse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, to bellow, to howl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wail, bawl</li></ol><p>Infinitive modified by analogy with 1sg. <i>реву́</i> (revú), from Old East Slavic <i>рюти</i> (rjuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;řuti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historiography</i>) revisionism (advocacy of a revision of some accepted theory, doctrine or a view of historical events)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revisionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) audit</li></ol><p><i>реви́зия</i> (revízija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audit (independent review)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) inspection, examination</li><li>revision (<i>of a theory or treaty</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) census of the male population (<i>for calculating poll taxes</i>)</li><li>tax roll, tax register</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rewizja</i>, from Latin <i>revīsiō</i> (“review”); original meaning "census of the male population". From the time of Peter I.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditor, inspector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revolutionary committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>революцио́нный комите́т</i> (revoljuciónnyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rheumatism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rheumatismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a jealous man</li></ol><p><i>ревни́вый</i> (revnívyj, “jealous”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>jealously</li></ol><p><i>ревни́вый</i> (revnívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jealous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rьvьnivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ревнова́ть</i> (revnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) zealot, strict adherent, ardent advocate</li></ol><p>Constructed as if <i>&#42;ревни́ть</i> (&#42;revnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ); compare <i>ревнова́ть</i> (revnovátʹ, “to be jealous, (<i>dated</i>) to be zealous”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be jealous of</li><li>(dated,&#32;intransitive) to envy</li><li>(dated,&#32;intransitive) to be zealous [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘about something’</small>]</li><li>(dated,&#32;intransitive) to compete [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ревн-</i> (revn-) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>zealous, eager</li></ol><p><i>ре́вность</i> (révnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jealousy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рьвьность</i> (rĭvĭnostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rьvьnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ре́вный</i> (révnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>firearms</i>) revolver (a handgun with a revolving chamber)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>revolver</i> (used since the middle of 1830s).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revolutionary, revolutionist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>révolutionnaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>revolutionary (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>револю́ция</i> (revoljúcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revolution (political upheaval)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rewolucja</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>revolutio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>револю́ция</i> (revoljúcija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revue (form of theatrical&#59; any entertainment featuring skits, dances, and songs)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>revue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(history&#32;or&#32;dated,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) regalia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rēgālia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) rugby</li></ol><p>From English <i>rugby</i> via French <i>rugby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rugby player</li></ol><p>From <i>ре́гби</i> (régbi, “rugby”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>регбі́ст</i> (rehbíst) and Belarusian <i>рэгбі́ст</i> (rehbíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) reggae</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>reggae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regeneration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>regenerātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regent</li><li>precentor (director of a church choir)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Regent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regency</li></ol><p><i>ре́гент</i> (régent) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>region</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regional (relating to a specific region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>register</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) registry</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) case</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>registrum</i>. Doublet of <i>рее́стр</i> (rejéstr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) registrar, recordkeeper, file clerk</li><li>(animate,&#32;medicine) hospital receptionist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) recorder, recording device</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;computing,&#32;engineering) logger</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;technical) display, monitor</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) file, folder</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Medieval Latin <i>registrator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>registry, registration office, reception, front desk (especially in a clinic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Registratur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) registration</li></ol><p><i>регистра́ция</i> (registrácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>registration (the act of signing up or registering for something)</li><li>check-in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>registry, registering</li></ol><p><i>регистри́ровать</i> (registrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register, to log, to record</li><li>to enroll</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register oneself, to check in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to register one's marriage</li></ol><p><i>регистри́ровать</i> (registrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order, regulations</li><li>time limit <i>(for speeches)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regulate</li></ol><p>Probably <i>регла́мент</i> (regláment) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regress, to retrogress</li></ol><p><i>регре́сс</i> (regréss, regrɛ́ss) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) regression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulation, adjustment</li></ol><p><i>регули́ровать</i> (regulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to regulate, to control</li><li>to adjust, to tune up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment, readjustment (small change)</li><li>control, regulation</li><li>alignment, aligning</li><li>tuning, tune-up</li></ol><p><i>регули́ровать</i> (regulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traffic guard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjustable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>regularly</li></ol><p><i>регуля́рный</i> (reguljárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regularity</li></ol><p><i>регуля́рный</i> (reguljárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regular (repeating with constant frequency)</li><li>recurrent (<i>event</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) regular (formally organized, professional)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulator, control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulation, control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>editorial office</li><li>editor</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>реда́кция</i> (redákcija).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>реда́ктор</i> (redáktor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>editing</li></ol><p><i>редакти́ровать</i> (redaktírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to edit</li><li>to edit, to lead the publication of (<i>as an editor</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to edit</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old French <i>redacter</i> or Latin <i>redactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) editor (person who edits)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;computing) editor, processor; editing program</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rédacteur</i> (“editor”) (compare Czech <i>redaktor</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) editor; editor's (related to someone who edits, or to software that helps someone edit)</li><li>editorial</li></ol><p><i>реда́ктор</i> (redáktor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>editorial, (<i>relational</i>) drafting, (<i>relational</i>) editing</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>Redaktion</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). By surface analysis, <i>реда́кция</i> (redákcija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>editorship, redaction</li><li>editorial staff, editorial board</li><li>editorial office</li><li>version, edition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thin, to thin out, to get thin</li><li>to be depleted, to thin out</li></ol><p><i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) radish</li></ol><p><i>ре́дька</i> (rédʹka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(European small) radish</li></ol><p>Derived from Italian <i>radice</i>, but via one or more intermediaries, judging by the first vowel. Further from Latin <i>rādīx</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radish (plant or vegetable)</li><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) turd, asshole, twit</li></ol><p>From <i>реди́с</i> (redís, “radish”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>The slang sense was popularized by the Soviet film <i>Gentlemen of Fortune</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rare, unfrequent</li><li>rare, exceptional, extraordinary</li><li>thin, sparse</li><li>scarce</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rědъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rarely, seldom</li><li>sparsely</li></ol><p><i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>editorial board</li></ol><p>Short for редакционная коллегия</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rare, unique, uncommon</li></ol><p><i>ре́дкость</i> (rédkostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rarity, uncommon thing; scarcity</li><li>curio</li></ol><p><i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reduction drive</li><li>reduction gear</li><li>pressure regulator</li></ol><p>Literally, “reducer”, constructed according to Latin derivation rules from Latin <i>redūcō</i>. Probably borrowed from another European language; compare Spanish <i>reductor</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>rèduktor</i>/<i>redȕktor</i> with the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reduce (in volume, number, etc.)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reduce (a complex process to a simpler one)</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;biology) to reduce (decrease in size or complexity or completely die off due to loss of function)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to reduce (an unstressed vowel to a weaker articulation)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to reduce (undergo chemical reduction, i.e. reversal of oxidation)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to reduce (subject to reduction, i.e. pressure reduction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>reduzieren</i>, further from Latin <i>redūcere</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;redьky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>register, registry</li><li>roll (an official or public document)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rejestr</i>. Doublet of <i>реги́стр</i> (regístr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ре́дкий</i> (rédkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ре́дко</i> (rédko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) regime (mode of rule or management)</li><li>routine</li><li>conditions, rate</li><li>mode (of operation, e.g. standby mode)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>régime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep schedule</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) regime, state</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) regimen, mode, method</li><li>operating according to a regimen</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) defense industry, military industry</li><li>strictly regimented</li></ol><p><i>режи́м</i> (režím) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>direction, stage direction, production (<i>of a film, spectacle</i>)</li><li>the art of direction</li><li>(collective,&#32;dated) directors</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) director</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>régisseur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) film director; (film) directorial</li></ol><p><i>режиссёр</i> (režissjór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cutting</li><li>sharp (<i>pain</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut</li><li>slice</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) a cutting method</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) in Kyivan Rus, the profit or percentage of money given on loan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězъ</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>řez</i>, Polish <i>rzeź</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) rezana, a unit of currency in ancient Russia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѣзана</i> (rězana). Cognates with Ukrainian <i>рі́зана</i> (rízana), Belarusian <i>рэ́зана</i> (rézana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chive (<i>Allium schoenoprasum</i>)</li><li>chive (herb)</li></ol><p>From <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анец</i> (-anec), a calque of German <i>Schnittlauch</i>, or, properly, a loan rendering of it, of about the same age as the semi-calque <i>шнитт-лу́к</i> (šnitt-lúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to cut once with a choppy movement</li></ol><p><i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut up, to cut open, to slice, to carve</li><li>to slaughter, to knife, to kill</li><li><i>(on wood or metal)</i> to carve, to cut, to engrave</li><li>to cut off, to slice, to chop</li><li><i>(on a test)</i> to give a failing grade</li><li><i>(cause pain)</i> to hurt, to cut into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to operate, to cut open</li><li>to irritate, to offend <i>(eyes)</i>, to grate on <i>(ears)</i></li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to pass close, to shave</li><li>to speak bluntly</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>рѣзати</i> (rězati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;rḗˀźtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₁ǵ-</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>рѣзати</i> (rězati), Ukrainian <i>різати</i> (rizaty), Bulgarian <i>режа</i> (reža), Serbo-Croatian <i>резати</i>, Slovene <i>rezati</i>, Czech <i>řezat</i>, Slovak <i>rezať</i>, Polish <i>rzezać</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>rězać</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>rězaś</i>, and Ancient Greek <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cut, to be cut</li><li>to cut through (<i>of teeth</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fight with knives</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play with passion [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘a game’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gambol, to frisk, to romp</li></ol><p><i>ре́звый</i> (rézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, swiftly, sprightly, with agility</li></ol><p><i>ре́звый</i> (rézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friskiness</li></ol><p>From <i>ре́звый</i> (rézvyj, “frisky”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frisky, frolicsome, playful, high-spirited</li><li>fast (<i>horse</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězvъ</i>. Akin to <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically: Russian <i>как ужа́ленный</i> (kak užálennyj) (&lt; <i>ужа́лить</i> (užálitʹ)), <i>как ошпа́ренный</i> (kak ošpárennyj) (&lt; <i>ошпа́рить</i> (ošpáritʹ)), <i>зада́ть стрекача́</i> (zadátʹ strekačá) akin to <i>стрека́ть</i> (strekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mignonette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>résédа</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reserve, reserves (that which is reserved, or kept back, as for future use)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) reserve</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>réserve</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refuge</li><li>reservation</li><li>reserve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservation (<i>in various senses</i>)</li><li>indigenous reservation</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>reservātiō</i> (“reservation, saving”), probably by way of English <i>reservation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservation, booking</li><li>redundancy</li></ol><p><i>резерви́ровать</i> (rezervírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reserve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) reservist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reserve</li></ol><p><i>резе́рв</i> (rezérv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reservoir, vessel, tank; basin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>réservoir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rutherfordium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter, cutting tool, chisel, draw knife</li><li>incisor <i>(tooth)</i></li><li>drill bit, tool bit</li><li>plow colter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рез(ать)</i> (rez(atʹ), “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Резец)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Caelum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;formal</i>) resident</li><li>(<i>espionage</i>) fixed-post spy; resident spy, resident, "rezident", chief of a country's intelligence operations in another country</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>espionage</i>) a base of espionage operations within a foreign country; an intelligence service station in a foreign country; a <i>rezidentura</i></li><li>(<i>healthcare</i>) residency (<i>in the context of American, European, or other foreign medical training terminology</i>)</li></ol><p><i>резиде́нт</i> (rezidént) +&lrm; <i>-у́ра</i> (-úra)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) residence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubber (pliable material derived from the sap of the rubber tree)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tire/tyre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>resina</i>; cf. French <i>résine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eraser (thing used to erase something written or drawn)</li><li>elastic band; scrunchie (<i>for hair</i>); garter (<i>for stockings</i>)</li><li>gum (<i>for chewing</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) condom, rubber</li></ol><p><i>рези́на</i> (rezína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubber duck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rubber</li></ol><p><i>рези́на</i> (rezína) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) resistor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) cutter (device for cutting)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp, keen</li><li>sharp, abrupt (offensive, critical, or acrimonious)</li><li>biting, piercing</li><li>harsh, shrill, glaring</li><li>acute, sharp, pungent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sharply, harshly, abruptly</li><li>bluntly</li></ol><p><i>ре́зкий</i> (rézkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harshness</li><li>acuity</li><li>abruptness</li><li>sharpness (<i>of an image</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ре́зкий</i> (rézkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut, carved</li></ol><p><i>рез-</i> (rez-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>massacre, bloodbath, slaughter (intentional mass killing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a vicious, bloody fight (<i>with knives or other cold steel weapons</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resolution <i>(of an assembly, etc)</i></li><li>instructions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reason</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>raison</i>, from Latin <i>ratiōnem</i>. Doublet of <i>рацио́н</i> (ración).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) resonance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) resonance, impact, stir (the evocation of strong emotions or mental associations in response)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>résonance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resonant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reasonably</li></ol><p><i>резо́нный</i> (rezónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reasonable, logical</li></ol><p><i>резо́н</i> (rezón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect, result, return, outcome</li></ol><p>From French <i>résultat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>basketball</i>) assist (statistical recognition of making a pass)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potency, effectiveness, fecundity</li></ol><p><i>результати́вный</i> (rezulʹtatívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective, efficacious</li></ol><p>Formed as if from German <i>resultativ</i> (“resultative”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), but with the meaning based on <i>результа́т</i> (rezulʹtát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhesus factor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ре́зко</i> (rézko)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ре́зкий</i> (rézkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter (person that cuts)</li></ol><p><i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) sharp pain, colic</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) carving</li></ol><p><i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carving, fretwork</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) thread, threading (<i>as on a screw</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rězьba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summary</li><li>curriculum vitae, CV, resume</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>résumé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sum up, to summarize, to recapitulate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>resümieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reincarnation</li></ol><p><i>реинкарн(и́ровать)</i> (reinkarn(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reincarnate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>réincarner</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yard, a crossways-mast on a ship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>ra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;countable) a rave party (a disco party with electronic music)</li><li>(music,&#32;uncountable) rave (term for electronic dance music, primarily techno and house)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rave</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;law enforcement) raid</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) roads, roadstead, anchorage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>raid</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>rede</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) commerce raiding</li><li>(<i>business</i>) corporate raids</li><li>(business,&#32;derogatory) illegal takeovers, e.g. through fraud, bribery, or intimidation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lath; strip of wood</li><li>measuring rod</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>reja</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Dutch <i>ra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Rhenish (pertaining to the river Rhine)</li></ol><p>From <i>Рейн</i> (Rejn) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight (a specific journey made by an aircraft)</li><li>trip, voyage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>reis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trip, voyage, flight</li><li>fixed-route, scheduled (<i>of a bus or airplane</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рейс</i> (rejs) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruling pen, drawing pen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reißfeder</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>T-square</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reißschiene</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rating, ranking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rating</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>ratus</i>, from Latin <i>reor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rating, ratings, ranking</li></ol><p><i>ре́йтинг</i> (rɛ́jting, réjting) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jodhpurs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reithose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Reich, a German empire; especially the Third Reich (Nazi Germany)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive) a country whose policies are perceived to be fascist or oppressive; a dystopia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reich</i> (“empire, realm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Reichstag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reichstag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river, stream</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѣка</i> (rěka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěka</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃royHkeh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃reyH-</i>, whence English <i>runlet</i> and <i>rivulet</i> and Spanish <i>río</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>requiem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) properties, props</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) details</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confiscation, requisition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advertising, publicity</li><li>advertisement, ad, commercial (advertisement in a common media format)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>réclame</i> (“advertisement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advertise, to publicize</li><li>to boost, to push, to play up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advertiser</li><li>(dated,&#32;derogatory) self-advertiser, publicity seeker</li></ol><p><i>рекла́ма</i> (rekláma) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рекла́ма</i> (rekláma): (small) advertising</li></ol><p><i>рекла́м(а)</i> (reklám(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) advertising, publicity; promotional</li></ol><p><i>рекла́ма</i> (rekláma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advertiser (one who advertises)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconnaissance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rekognoszierung</i> (“reconnaissance”), from Latin <i>recognosco</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>recombinant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>recommendatory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recommendation, character reference</li><li>advice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recommend, to advise [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to introduce oneself</li><li>to be advisable/recommended [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рекомендова́ть</i> (rekomendovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reconstruct</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>rekonstruieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reconstruction (act of restoring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record (most extreme known value of some achievement)</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>recordor</i>, further from <i>re-</i> + <i>cor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recorder (judge)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) record, record-breaking</li></ol><p><i>реко́рд</i> (rekórd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record holder, record breaker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>recordman</i>, a <i>faux anglicisme</i> borrowed into other European languages. Attested in Russian since at least 1915, according to Google Books.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) recruit, draftee, conscript (male or female)</li></ol><p>Derived from French <i>recruter</i> via Polish <i>rekrut</i> or German <i>Rekrut</i> (when the stress in Russian was on the second syllable).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chancellor, president, rector (of a university)</li><li>(<i>church</i>) rector</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rektor</i>, from Latin <i>rēctor</i>, from <i>regō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) recursion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relaxation</li><li>the process of establishing equilibrium in a physical system</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>relaxātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relay, switch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>relais</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relevance (property or state of being relevant, pertinency)</li></ol><p><i>релева́нтный</i> (relevántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) relevant (serving to distinguish linguistic units)</li></ol><p>English <i>relevant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). From Latin <i>relevāns, relevāntem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious studies scholar, specialist in religious studies, religiologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>религиове́дение</i> (religiovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious studies, religiology</li></ol><p><i>рели́гия</i> (relígija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) religious studies; religiological</li></ol><p><i>религиове́д(ение)</i> (religiovéd(enije)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religiousness, religiosity; piety, piousness</li></ol><p><i>религио́зный</i> (religióznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>religious</li></ol><p>From German <i>religiös</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>religia</i>, from Latin <i>religio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release (distribution of a new product, software, movie, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>release</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion, Catholic or Protestant) reliquary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>reliquārium</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relic, cherished object, treasure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) relict</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>relictus</i> (“abandoned”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) relict</li></ol><p><i>рели́кт</i> (relíkt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floodmeadow, floodplain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ridge, crest</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sawhorse, trestle</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) groyne</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chicken ladder</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) swing, a hanging seat for children’s play</li><li>(<i>in the plural or singular</i>) gallows</li></ol><p>Unknown. Cognate to Ukrainian <i>ре́лі</i> (réli, “swing”). Lithuanian <i>rėklės</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;pl (“trapdoor; rod scaffolding for drying; <i>Söller</i>, مِحراب”), <i>rėklai</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (“garret, <i>Lucht</i>”), Old Prussian <i>riklis</i> (glossed as Middle Low German <i>suller</i>), may point to a Baltic term, otherwise connected with Dutch <i>rek</i> and <i>ra</i>. A Germanic cognate of German <i>Riegel</i> or English <i>reel</i> may also have been borrowed through some kind of specialist exchange.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relief (difference of elevations on a surface)</li><li>(<i>sculpture</i>) relief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>relief</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convex, protruding</li><li>embossed, relief</li><li>expressive, clear</li></ol><p><i>релье́ф</i> (relʹjéf) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>railroad</i>) rail (the metal bar that makes the track for a railroad)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rails</i>. Further from Latin <i>rēgula</i>, from Latin <i>regō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rail transport,&#32;archaic,&#32;colloquial,&#32;often proscribed) rail (the metal bar that makes the track for a railroad) (<i>also used in professional speech, such as in the following example</i>)</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) pre-programmed channels/directions of movement for a character in a computer game</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) railgun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) relativistic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>релятиви́ст</i> (reljativíst, “relativist”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) war report (report on a wartime incident, especially concerning one's own troops)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>relation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remark, note</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) stage direction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>remarque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap, thong, sling</li><li>belt, waist-belt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;remy</i> (genitive <i>&#42;remene</i>). Cognate to Old East Slavic <i>ремыкъ</i> (remykŭ), Old Church Slavonic <i>ремень</i> (remenĭ), Ukrainian <i>ремінь</i> (reminʹ); Bulgarian <i>ремен</i> (remen), <i>ремик</i> (remik), <i>ремък</i> (remǎk); Serbo-Croatian <i>ре̏ме̑н</i> (genitive <i>ремена</i>), <i>рѐмик</i>; Slovenian <i>rémen</i>, <i>jérmen</i>; Czech <i>řemen</i>, Slovak <i>remeň</i>; Polish <i>rzemień</i>; Upper Sorbian <i>rjemjeń</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>rjemjeń</i>.</p><p>Despite the similarity to German <i>Riemen</i> (Old High German <i>riomo</i>), Vasmer concludes that the claim of some earlier dictionaries that the Slavic word is a loan of the Germanic one is phonologically impossible; see <i>Riemen</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat belt, safety belt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artisan, craftsman</li></ol><p>From <i>реме́сленный</i> (reméslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) craft, trade</li><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) profession, occupation; occupational</li><li>artisanal</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) uncreative, stereotyped, according to a template</li></ol><p><i>ремесло́</i> (remesló) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade, craft (skilled practice)</li><li>handicraft</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) profession, occupation</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) uncreative work, unimaginative work, work according to a template</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;remeslo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>реме́нь</i> (reménʹ)</li><li>small strap, thong, wristlet (<i>of a watch</i>)</li></ol><p><i>реме́нь</i> (reménʹ) +&lrm; <i>-шо́к</i> (-šók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) remix</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>remix</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remonstrance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repair, overhaul</li><li>maintenance</li><li>mending, renovation, refurbishment</li><li>refit</li><li>remount service</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>remonte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repair, to refit, to recondition, to overhaul, to renovate, to refurbish</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) to resupply (the cavalry) with horses</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(figurative,&#32;humorous) to recuperate</li></ol><p><i>ремонти́ровать</i> (remontírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repairer, repairman</li></ol><p><i>ремо́нт</i> (remónt) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) repair</li></ol><p><i>ремо́нт</i> (remónt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) belt</li></ol><p><i>реме́нь</i> (reménʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renegade, turncoat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Renaissance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>Renaissance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhenium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reputation, fame</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>renommée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) wine cellar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rent, rente</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>profitability, profitableness</li></ol><p><i>рента́бельный</i> (rentábelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>profitable, paying</li></ol><p>French <i>rentable</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) X-ray (short wavelength electromagnetic radiation or photograph made with X-rays)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) röntgen (roentgen) (a unit of exposure to ionizing radiation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Röntgen</i>, eponym for Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) roentgenizdat (improvised gramophone recordings made from X-ray film, used to smuggle prohibited music into the Soviet Union).</li></ol><p>From <i>рентге́н</i> (rentgén, “X-ray”) + a clipping of <i>изда́тельство</i> (izdátelʹstvo, “publishing house”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roentgenium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) X-ray; radiographic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) roentgen (a unit of exposure to ionizing radiation)</li></ol><p><i>рентге́н</i> (rentgén) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>X-ray</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>X-ray radiation, X-radiation, X-rays</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>X-ray, radiograph, roentgenogram (photograph made with X-rays)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiography</li></ol><p><i>рентгено-</i> (rentgeno-) +&lrm; <i>граф</i> (graf) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) radiodiagnosis</li></ol><p>From <i>рентге́н</i> (rentgén, “X-ray”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>диагно́стика</i> (diagnóstika, “diagnostics, diagnosis”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>X-ray electrocardiographic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rent, annuity; rental</li></ol><p><i>ре́нта</i> (rénta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reorganization</li></ol><p>From <i>ре-</i> (re-) +&lrm; <i>организа́ция</i> (organizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reorganize</li></ol><p>French <i>réorganiser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) head</li><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) reputation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěpa</i>.</p><p>(<i>head</i>): Compare typologically <i>ты́ква</i> (týkva), <i>ты́ковка</i> (týkovka), <i>коча́н</i> (kočán), German <i>Birne</i>, <i>Rübe</i>, Italian <i>zucca</i>, Swedish <i>knopp</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>репута́ция</i> (reputácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reparation (for war transgressions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burdock</li><li>bur</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěpьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burdock</li></ol><p>From <i>репе́й</i> (repéj, “burdock”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geodesy,&#32;patent law) benchmark</li><li>registration target, registration point</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) frame, reference</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>repère</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repertoire, repertory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>répertoire</i>. Further from Latin <i>repertōrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) repertoire, repertory; repertorial</li></ol><p><i>репертуа́р</i> (repertuár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rehearse</li><li>to coach</li></ol><p>From German <i>repetieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(private) tutor, coach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rehearsal</li></ol><p><i>репети́ция</i> (repetícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rehearsal (practicing of performance to test or improve it)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>repetycja</i> or German <i>Repetition</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>repetītiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailhead, dock (sacrum and coccyx, the last part of the spine before the tail in animals such as horses, lambs, dogs, and cats)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ре́па</i> (répa): (small) turnip</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) knot or sealing at the end of a rope cable to prevent it from unraveling</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) female tutor</li></ol><p><i>ре́па</i> (répa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>реп(ети́тор)</i> (rep(etítor)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remark</li><li>retort, rejoinder</li><li><i>theatral</i> cue</li><li>replica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;genetics) replication</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>replicātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) turnip</li></ol><p><i>ре́па</i> (répa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) turnip</li></ol><p><i>ре́па</i> (répa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) repository</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>repositōrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>media</i>) report</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>reportage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>news reporter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) repost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>repost</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;intransitive) to repost</li></ol><p><i>репо́ст</i> (repóst) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repressiveness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>repressive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>repressiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subject to repression</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>репре́ссия</i> (représsija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repression, reprisal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>circus</i>) gag</li><li>(<i>music</i>) reprise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reproductive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>reproductif</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loudspeaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) <i>(photographic)</i> reproduction</li><li>(physiology,&#32;agriculture) reproduction</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>reprōductiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>репроду́кция</i> (reprodúkcija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reptile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reputation, image, fame (what someone is known for)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>reputātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulbous, turnip-shaped (<i>usually of onions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ре́па</i> (répa) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reception, front desk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>reception</i>. Doublet of <i>реце́пция</i> (recépcija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ресе́пшен</i> (resɛ́pšen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>reception</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rescript</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Reskript</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѣсьнъ</i> (rěsĭnŭ) + <i>-ица</i> (-ica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ręsьnica</i>, from <i>&#42;ręsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resovietization</li></ol><p><i>ре-</i> (re-) +&lrm; <i>советиза́ция</i> (sovetizácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) respect, kudos, props</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>respect</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>respectable</li><li>dignified, well-bred, polished</li><li>reputable</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>respectable</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respirator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) respirator, gas mask, breather</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) respiratory</li></ol><p><i>респира́тор</i> (respirátor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>Perhaps from English <i>respirator(y)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sociology</i>) respondent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>respondent</i>, from <i>to respond</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>respondēre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>republic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rēspūblica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>republican</li><li>Republican (member of the Republican Party in the United States)</li></ol><p><i>респу́блика</i> (respúblika) +&lrm; <i>-а́нец</i> (-ánec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>republican</li></ol><p><i>респу́блика</i> (respúblika) +&lrm; <i>-ан-</i> (-an-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf spring, spring (device made of flexible material)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ressort</i> (“spring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restorer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restoration (the process of bringing an object back to its original state)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, to refurbish (<i>e.g. a building</i>)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to restore, to bring back (<i>e.g. an institution, custom, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>restaurieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) restitution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>restitūtiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restaurant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>restaurant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) restaurant</li></ol><p><i>рестора́н</i> (restorán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рестора́н</i> (restorán): (small) restaurant</li></ol><p><i>рестора́н</i> (restorán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restructuring, restructure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resource (something that one uses to achieve an objective)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ressource</i>, further from Latin <i>resurgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) resource</li></ol><p><i>ресу́рс</i> (resúrs) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resource-efficient</li></ol><p>From <i>ресу́рс</i> (resúrs) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>ресурсоефекти́вний</i> (resursoefektývnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;offensive) retard (a stupid, slow-to-learn person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>retard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to retweet</li></ol><p>English <i>retweet</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) zealous, diligent</li><li>lively, frisky, high-spirited</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) ardent, passionate</li></ol><p>From obsolete or dialectal <i>рети́ться</i> (retítʹsja, “to fuss, to be zealous”) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj), from Old East Slavic <i>ретити</i> (retiti, “to impel, to stir”), from Old East Slavic <i>реть</i> (retĭ, “zeal, competition, strife”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>реть</i> (retĭ, “competition, strife”), Old Church Slavonic <i>ретити</i> (retiti, “to strive”), Ukrainian <i>рети́тися</i> (retýtysja, “to fight”), and Russian <i>рать</i> (ratʹ, “army, battle”). Further cognate with Sanskrit <i>ऋति</i>&nbsp;f (ṛtí, “attack, quarrel”), Ancient Greek <i>ἔρις</i> (éris, “dispute, competition”), Ancient Greek <i>ἐρίζω</i> (erízō, “to argue”), Ancient Greek <i>ἐρέθω</i> (eréthō, “to excite, to irritate”), Ancient Greek <i>ὄρνῡμι</i> (órnūmi, “to excite”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retina (thin layer of cells at the back of the eyeball)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) to retreat, to retire, to withdraw, to go away</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>se retirer</i> (“to retreat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(art,&#32;fashion,&#32;style) retro</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(art,&#32;fashion,&#32;style) retro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rétro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>retro-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vintage car</li></ol><p>From <i>ретро-</i> (retro-) +&lrm; <i>автомоби́ль</i> (avtomobílʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retrograde</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>retrogradus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) retrospective review</li><li>(<i>art</i>) retrospective</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rétrospective</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>retrōspectus</i>, p.p.p. of <i>retrōspiciō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retouch</li></ol><p>From <i>ретуши́ровать</i> (retušírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retouch, retouching</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract, synopsis, summary (an abrigement or summary)</li><li>report, paper, essay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Referat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>referendum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reader, reviewer</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) referent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) referee (umpire&#59; judge&#59; the supervisor of a game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>referee</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refinancing</li></ol><p><i>рефинанси́ровать</i> (refinansírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refinance</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>refinance</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflex</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>reflexus</i>, through German <i>Reflex</i> according to Max Vasmer.</p><p>Attested since the early 19th century, but only became common in the early 20th century.</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>odruch</i> (&lt; <i>od-</i> + <i>ruch</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) to react/respond with a reflex</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reflect (to think, to ponder) [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental), <b>по по́воду</b> (po póvodu, + genitive), etc.]</li></ol><p><i>рефле́кс</i> (refléks) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) reflection, consideration, meditation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) to react/respond with a reflex</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to reflect (to think, to ponder) [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental), <b>по по́воду</b> (po póvodu, + genitive), etc.]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>reflektieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reform</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reformer (someone who reforms)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) reformer (someone involved in the Reformation)</li></ol><p><i>реформ(и́ровать)</i> (reform(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reform</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) reformer; reformer's</li><li>reformatory</li></ol><p><i>реформа́тор</i> (reformátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reforming</li></ol><p><i>реформи́ровать</i> (reformírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reform</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>reformieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reformist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refractor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;poetry) refrain, burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refrigerator (appliance that refrigerates food)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from or related to <i>рёха</i> (rjóxa, “dupe, ninny”) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); compare Bulgarian <i>ре́хам се</i> (réham se, “to wander”). Further connections are unclear. Per Trubachyov, however, from Russian <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reviewer, referee (an expert reviewing the work prior to publishing), reader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reviewing</li></ol><p><i>рецензи́ровать</i> (recenzírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to review, to give feedback on</li></ol><p><i>реце́нзия</i> (recénzija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>review, critique, notice</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>recensio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recipe</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prescription</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rezept</i>, from Latin <i>receptus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) receptor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recipe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rezeptur</i>, from Latin <i>receptūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perception (<i>detection by the senses</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;law) reception (<i>of a foreign doctrine, cultural phenomenon, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) reception, front desk (<i>in a hotel or other establishment</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rezeption</i>, from Latin <i>receptiō</i>. Doublet of <i>ресе́пшен</i> (resépšen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recession (a period of reduced economic activity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) relapse</li><li>(<i>law</i>) repeated commission <i>(of an offence)</i></li><li>recurrence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repeat offender, reoffender, recidivist</li><li>(habitual) jailbird, gaol-bird</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) speech; vocal</li><li>(relational,&#32;computing) voice</li></ol><p><i>речь</i> (rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eloquent, glib, voluble, loquacious</li></ol><p><i>речь</i> (rečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) recitative</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>recitativo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small river</li></ol><p><i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river-transport worker</li></ol><p><i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) river; fluvial, riverine</li></ol><p><i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river terminal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>река́</i> (reká): small river, rivulet</li></ol><p><i>река́</i> (reká) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, language (spoken)</li><li>discourse, talk, conversation</li><li>word</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěčь</i> (“speech”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(spoken) motto, slogan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) fixer, solver (<i>of problems</i>)</li></ol><p><i>реша́ть</i> (rešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solve, to settle</li><li>to decide, to resolve, to make up one's mind, to determine</li></ol><p><i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up one's mind (to), to decide (to, on), to determine (to), to resolve (to)</li><li>to bring oneself (to); to dare, to risk; to venture</li></ol><p><i>реша́ть</i> (rešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decisive, deciding, critical, crucial, vital, climacteric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decision, solution, answer</li><li>solving, deciding, determining</li><li>(<i>law</i>) resolution, judgment, decree, verdict</li></ol><p>From <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije). Cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>рјеше́ње</i> and Macedonian <i>решение</i> (rešenie).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lath (thin, narrow strip of wood for sheathing a roof)</li><li>slat or bar in a grill, grate, lattice, etc.</li></ol><p><i>решето́</i> (rešetó) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover completely in holes, to pierce in many places, to riddle</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to strengthen by nailing laths to</li></ol><p><i>решето́</i> (rešetó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p><p><i>реше́т(ник)</i> (rešét(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(technical,&#32;collective) material for sheathing roofs; laths</li><li>master sieve maker</li></ol><p><i>решето́</i> (rešetó) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>решето</i> (rešeto) or <i>рѣшето</i> (rěšeto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rešeto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>решётчатый</i> (rešótčatyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resolution, resoluteness, determination</li></ol><p><i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>resolutely, firmly</li><li>decisively</li><li>definitely</li><li>absolutely</li></ol><p><i>реши́тельный</i> (rešítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resolution, resoluteness, decision</li></ol><p><i>реши́тельный</i> (rešítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decisive</li><li>critical</li><li>firm, absolute, resolute</li><li>definite, determined</li></ol><p>From <i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj), a calque of French <i>résolu</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>resolūtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solve, to settle</li><li>to decide, to resolve, to make up one's mind, to determine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѣшити</i> (rěšiti, “to untie, absolve”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěšiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyḱ-</i> (“to twist, bend”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>ріши́ти</i> (rišýty), Belarusian <i>рашы́ць</i> (rašýcʹ), Serbo-Croatian <i>решити</i>, Czech <i>řešit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make up one's mind (to), to decide (to, on), to determine (to), to resolve (to)</li><li>to bring oneself (to); to dare, to risk; to venture</li></ol><p><i>реши́ть</i> (rešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) sieve</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tails (the side of a coin <i>not</i> bearing a coat of arms)</li></ol><p>From <i>решётка</i> (rešótka, “tails (since 1730s, now obsolete); lattice, grid”), from <i>решето́</i> (rešetó, “sieve”), likely referring to the design of 5 kopeck copper coins in the 1720s (Fig. 2), or perhaps to the earlier silver kopeck coins.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decided, fixed, not subject to change</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lattice</li><li>grid</li><li>bars, grating (of cage, prison etc.)</li><li>hash sign</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tails of a coin</li></ol><p><i>решето́</i> (rešetó, “big sieve”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lattice</li><li>latticed, slatted</li></ol><p><i>решётка</i> (rešótka) +&lrm; <i>-атый</i> (-atyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>рей</i> (rej)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рея)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Rhea</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Rhea, moon of Saturn</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ῥέα</i> (Rhéa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soar, to fly, to hover</li><li>to flutter, to fly (of a flag)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѣяти</i> (rějati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rějati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) rust (<i>on iron</i>)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rust (<i>on plants</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръжа</i> (rŭža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъďa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rust</li></ol><p>From <i>ржа́вый</i> (ržávyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ), or from <i>ржа́вый</i> (ržávyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ) for the end-stressed version.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rust</li><li>smut (fungal disease of plants)</li></ol><p>From <i>ржа́вый</i> (ržávyj) +&lrm; <i>-чина</i> (-čina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rust, related to rust</li><li>rust-colored</li></ol><p><i>ржа́вчина</i> (ržávčina, “rust”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, “adjectival suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) rust fungus</li></ol><p>From <i>ржа́вчинный</i> (ržávčinnyj, “rust”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>гриб</i> (grib, “fungus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rusty (affected by rust)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръжавъ</i> (rŭžavŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъďavъ</i>, from <i>&#42;rъďa</i> + <i>&#42;-avъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ржа</i> (rža) +&lrm; <i>-а́вый</i> (-ávyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) something very funny</li></ol><p><i>ржа́ть</i> (ržátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighing</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) guffawing</li></ol><p><i>ржать</i> (ržatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rye</li></ol><p><i>рожь</i> (rožʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ано́й</i> (-anój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rye bread</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neigh</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;low colloquial) to laugh</li></ol><p>Inherited Old East Slavic <i>ръзати</i> (rŭzati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъzati</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewǵ-</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>ržát</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) funny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Rzhev</li></ol><p><i>Ржев</i> (Ržev) +&lrm; <i>-итянин</i> (-itjanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;text messaging,&#32;humorous) LOL</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) the ruble, seen as a weakened currency</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>ржу</i> (ržu, “[I am] laughing”) +&lrm; <i>рубль</i> (rublʹ, “ruble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) rial (currency of Iran, Oman, Yemen, and Saudi Arabia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ribose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) ribozyme</li></ol><p>Internationalism of English <i>ribozyme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) ribonucleic acid, RNA</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ribonucleic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) riboflavin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshing barn, rickyard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>riihi</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рига)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Riga (the capital city of Latvia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) longitudinal girder/beam, crossbar</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) latch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Riegel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>рие́лтор</i> (riéltor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>realtor (dealer in real estate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>realtor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riel (currency of Cambodia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Khmer <i>រៀល</i> (riəl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rigan</li></ol><p><i>Ри́га</i> (Ríga) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Rigan (related to Riga, capital of Latvia)</li></ol><p><i>Ри́га</i> (Ríga) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chasuble</li><li>(<i>on icons</i>) riza, ornament</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;poetic) robe, raiment, garments</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;riza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) sacristy, vestry</li></ol><p><i>ри́за</i> (ríza) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) sexton, sacristan</li></ol><p><i>ри́зница</i> (ríznica) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>risotto (dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>risotto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ricotta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>ricotta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ricochet, rebound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) person pulling a rickshaw</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) rickshaw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anilingus (form of oral sex)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman numeral (a numeral represented by letters)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Rome, Roman</li></ol><p><i>Рим</i> (Rim) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman law</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) ring (place where some sports take place)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ring</i>. Doublet of <i>рым</i> (rym) and <i>ры́нок</i> (rýnok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) ringgit (currency of Malaysia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay <i>ringgit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinecanthus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinoplasty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to dash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rìnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, to dash, to dart</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to plunge (into)</li></ol><p><i>ри́нуть</i> (rínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of fabric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rice, paddy (plants)</li><li>rice (food)</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from Middle Low German <i>rīs</i>, from Late Latin <i>risus</i>, from Latin <i>orīza</i>, <i>oryza</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὄρυζα</i> (óruza), <i>ὄρυζον</i> (óruzon); see there for more. Slowly displaced earlier сарацинское пшено during 18th-19th cc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recycling (the practice of sorting and collecting waste materials for new use)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>recycling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a grain of rice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>risk (possible, usually negative, outcome)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>risque</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groove in the form of a line, which signifies a border between parts of a product (for example on a pill)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to risk, to venture, to adventure (to run the risk of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>risky, dodgy</li><li>risque</li></ol><p>Constructed as if the past passive participle of <i>рискова́ть</i> (riskovátʹ, “to risk”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to risk, to venture, to adventure (to run the risk of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing (act of producing a picture)</li></ol><p><i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice cooker (kitchen appliance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to design</li><li>to paint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rysować</i>, from Middle High German <i>rīʒen</i>, whence also German <i>reißen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take shape, to emerge, to appear, to stand out (against), to become visible (to have one's outlines become visible against a background)</li><li>to be pictured, to appear (in one's imagination)</li><li>to show off (to act in an unnatural way, attempting to arouse interest in oneself)</li></ol><p><i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Java sparrow (<i>Padda oryzivora</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) posing, showing off, pretentiousness, acting</li><li>(slang,&#32;anime community,&#32;video games) drawing, graphics, animation style</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) plotting</li></ol><p><i>рис</i> (ris) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p><p><i>рисова́ть</i> (risovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rice</li></ol><p><i>рис</i> (ris) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing</li><li>pattern</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rysunek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) retail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>retail</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhythm (various senses)</li><li>(<i>physiology</i>) heartbeat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rhythmus</i>. Doublet of <i>рифма</i> (rifma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhythmic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literature,&#32;linguistics) rhythmics, rhythm system</li><li>rhythmics, theory of rhythm</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) eurhythmics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhythmic</li></ol><p><i>ритм</i> (ritm) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhythmic (with a rhythm, e.g. music)</li><li>rhythmic (having a sense of rhythm, e.g. an actor)</li><li>measured (systematic, without long breaks, e.g. work)</li></ol><p><i>ритм</i> (ritm) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhyton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhetoric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhetorical</li></ol><p><i>рито́р(ика)</i> (ritór(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ritual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ritual, ritualistic</li><li>associated with funeral, undertaking</li></ol><p><i>ритуа́л</i> (rituál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reef</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhyme (sameness of sound of part of some words)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) poetaster</li></ol><p><i>ри́фма</i> (rífma) +&lrm; <i>-а́ч</i> (-áč)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetry</i>) rhymed, rhyming (having rhyme)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetaster</li></ol><p><i>ри́фма</i> (rífma) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetastress (female poetaster)</li></ol><p><i>рифмоплёт</i> (rifmopljót) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetasterism</li></ol><p><i>рифмоплёт</i> (rifmopljót) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>рие́лтор</i> (riéltor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>рие́лтор</i> (riéltor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rho (the Greek letter Ρ&#47;ρ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ῥῶ</i> (rhô).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overalls</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be timid or shy</li><li>to funk, to quail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Robinia</i>, false acacia, black locust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to do</li><li>(regional&#32;or&#32;low colloquial,&#32;rare) to work</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>робити</i> (robiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shy, timid, bashful (lacking in courage or confidence)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>робъ</i> (robŭ, “serf”) + <i>-кий</i> (-kij), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbъ</i> (“slave”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃órbʰos</i> (“orphan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shyly (in a shy manner)</li></ol><p><i>ро́бкий</i> (róbkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-driving car, robotic car, robo-car</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shyness, timidity</li></ol><p><i>ро́б(кий)</i> (rób(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robot (mechanical or virtual, artificial agent)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) bot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>robot</i>, from both the Czech and the Slovak <i>robota</i> (“worker”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orbota</i> (“hard work, slavery”) derived from <i>&#42;orbъ</i> (“slave”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃erbʰ-</i> (“to change or evolve status”), the predecessor to <i>&#42;h₃órbʰos</i> (“orphan”). Doublet of <i>рабо́та</i> (rabóta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery robot</li></ol><p><i>ро́бот</i> (róbot, “robot”) +&lrm; <i>доста́вщик</i> (dostávščik, “deliveryman”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robovac</li></ol><p><i>ро́бот</i> (róbot, “robot”) +&lrm; <i>пылесо́с</i> (pylesós, “vacuum cleaner”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robotaxi</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, moat, trench, fosse (defensive ditch)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ровъ</i> (rovŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rovъ</i> (“ditch, pitch”), from <i>&#42;ryti</i> (“to dig”) (whence <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contemporary, age-mate (one who is the same age as another)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>rovesník</i> or Polish <i>rówieśnik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roving (a fluffy bundle of fibers used to make yarn)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Karelian <i>raunivo</i>. Compare Finnish <i>raunio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) roving (a fluffy bundle of fibers used to make yarn)</li></ol><p>From <i>ро́вница</i> (róvnica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>smoothly</li><li>evenly, regularly, uniformly</li><li>sharp, exactly, precisely</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) as if, as though, like (expresses an uncertain assumption)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absolutely, at all, completely, quite</li></ol><p><i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ровно)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rivne (a city, the administrative centre of Rivne Oblast, Ukraine).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a miskrada of the raion of the same name of Rivne Oblast, Ukraine.</li><li>an oblast in northwestern Ukraine, established in November 1939.</li><li>an urban hromada of Rivne Oblast, Ukraine, established in 2020.</li><li>a raion of Rivne Oblast, Ukraine, established in 1940.</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>even, level, flat, smooth</li><li>straight</li><li>equal, balanced</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orvьnъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>ро́ўны</i> (równy). Doublet of <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) very flat and smooth</li></ol><p>Literally, “flat as a tablecloth”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>match, equal, peer, compeer (attribute equaling or exceeding)</li><li>coordinate</li></ol><p>From <i>ро́в(ный)</i> (róv(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja) (stem-stressed) or from <i>ро́в(ный)</i> (róv(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to level, to even</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) absolutely, right at, sharp, precisely, on the dot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ров</i> (rov).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horn</li><li>antler</li><li>bugle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rogъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;rágas</i>. The irregular plural <i>рога́</i> (rogá) is an old dual form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornucopia, horn of plenty</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>cornūcōpia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>croissant, kifli (a small crescent-shaped bread roll or shortcrust pastry, sometimes with a filling)</li><li>(<i>video games&#32;slang</i>) roguelike</li></ol><p>From <i>рог</i> (rog, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-а́л</i> (-ál) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Compare Polish <i>rogalik</i>.</p><p>Alteration of English <i>roguelike</i>, influenced by the noun <i>рога́лик</i> (rogálik, “crescent-shaped bread roll or pastry”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) bear spear, boar-spear</li></ol><p>Possibly <i>рог</i> (rog, “a horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forked/Y-shaped stick</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) catapult, slingshot</li><li>obstacle, hurdle</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) fourhorn sculpin</li></ol><p><i>рога́тый</i> (rogátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horned</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cuckolded</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) devil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rogatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stag, hart</li><li>stag beetle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rogačь</i>, from <i>&#42;rogъ</i> (“horn”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ačь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рог</i> (rog, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cornea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horn (substance or instrument)</li><li>horny (made of horn)</li></ol><p><i>рог</i> (rog) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an anarchist guerrilla movement in Siberia, Altai and North-Eastern Kazakhstan during the Russian Civil War</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matting, burlap (coarse fabric)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рогожа</i> (rogoža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rogoža</i>, from <i>&#42;rogozъ</i> (“reed, rush”). By surface analysis, <i>рого́з</i> (rogóz) +&lrm; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) burlap (coarse fabric)</li></ol><p><i>рого́ж(а)</i> (rogóž(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bulrush, cattail (any of several wetland herbs, <i>Typha</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rogozъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) lesser bulrush (<i>Typha angustifolia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рого́з</i> (rogóz, “<i>Typha</i>”) +&lrm; <i>узколи́стный</i> (uzkolístnyj, “angustifolious”), a calque of translingual <i>Typha angustifolia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckold (man married to an unfaithful wife)</li></ol><p><i>рог</i> (rog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>рог</i> (rog).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generation, birth, origin, stock, family, race, tribe, lineage</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) genus</li><li>sort, kind, genre, style, branch</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) gender, class</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>родъ</i> (rodŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rodъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;radás</i>. Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wréh₂ds</i> (“root”), whence English <i>root</i>. Cognates include Latvian <i>rads</i>, Ukrainian <i>рід</i> (rid) and Polish <i>ród</i> amongst others.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maternity home, maternity hospital</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>роди́льный дом</i> (rodílʹnyj dom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rhodium; rhodic</li></ol><p><i>ро́дий</i> (ródij) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhodium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maternity, birthing, birth, delivery, puerperal</li></ol><p><i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>роди́мый</i> (rodímyj); darling, dear (especially about a male)</li></ol><p><i>роди́мый</i> (rodímyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthmark, mole (dark spot on the skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>darling, dear</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homeland, motherland, native land</li><li>(endearing,&#32;usually capitalized) Russia as the Motherland</li><li>(endearing,&#32;dated,&#32;usually capitalized) the Soviet Union</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rodina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) birthmark, mole, melanism, nevus (naevus) (dark spot on the skin)</li></ol><p><i>ро́дина</i> (ródina) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parent</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) father</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>родитель</i> (roditelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “give birth”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) genitive (relating to the case of possession)</li></ol><p>From <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Calque of Latin <i>genetīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genitive case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parent; parental</li></ol><p><i>роди́тель</i> (rodítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parental control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth</li><li>to give rise to</li><li>to bear, to yield (<i>of land</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rodìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born</li><li>to arise, to come into being</li><li>to sprout, to grow, to germinate, to yield at harvest</li></ol><p><i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born with a silver spoon in one's mouth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relation, relative, kindred, kinsfolk, kin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (water source)</li><li>source, origin</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make related</li><li>to bring closer together</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be in common <i>(between)</i>, to create an affinity <i>(between)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>родня́</i> (rodnjá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>родни́к</i> (rodník): spring (water source)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fontanelle</li></ol><p><i>родни́к</i> (rodník) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;paganism) a Rodnover; a Slavic neopagan.</li></ol><p>From роднове́рие (rodnovérije), made of <i>род</i> (rod, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “faith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;paganism) Slavic neopaganism; Rodnovery</li></ol><p>From <i>род</i> (rod, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “faith”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije, suffix forming abstract nouns). Compare Ukrainian <i>ріднові́р'я</i> (ridnovírʺja).</p><p>The earliest usage of this term was in 1964 by the Ukrainian emigree Lev Sylenko. The standard, non-compound form of this word was adopted in 1997 by the Union of Slavic Native Belief Communities, while the portmanteau itself was popularised in 1999 by the <i>volkhv</i> Veleslav.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Родноверие)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>роднове́рие</i> (rodnovérije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>native, childhood, home (relating to where one was born or grew up&#59; relating to one's homeland)</li><li>biological, own, closely or directly related by blood</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dear</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) original, OEM, first-party (of a part or accessory&#58; originally belonging to a device or item in question&#59; or produced by the same company which made a device or item in question and intended to be used with it)</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother tongue, native language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relatives, kinsfolk, kin, the loved ones</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) relative(s), relation(s), folk, kin, kinfolk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wellborn, highborn</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ови́тый</i> (-ovítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ancestral, patrimonial</li><li>tribal</li><li>generic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) gender</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) childbirth, birth</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwifery, obstetrics</li><li>organization that deals with the care of women during pregnancy, childbirth, and the recuperative period following delivery.</li></ol><p><i>ро́ды</i> (ródy) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вспоможе́ние</i> (vspomožénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhododendron (flowering shrub in the genus Rhododendron)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Rhododendron camtschaticum</i></li></ol><p><i>рододе́ндрон</i> (rododéndron, “rhododendron”) +&lrm; <i>камча́тский</i> (kamčátskij, “Kamchatkan”), a calque of translingual <i>Rhododendron camtschaticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by birth, by origin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Rhodomyrtus</i></li></ol><p>Partial calque of translingual <i>Rhodomyrtus</i>, building on Russian <i>мирт</i> (mirt, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patriarch</li><li>apical ancestor (common ancestor)</li><li>stirps, stirpes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogy</li><li>pedigree, lineage</li></ol><p>From <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogy, pedigree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) genealogy</li></ol><p><i>родосло́в(ие)</i> (rodoslóv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative, relation, kinsman (someone in the same family; someone connected by blood, marriage, or adoption)</li></ol><p><i>ро́дственный</i> (ródstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relation, affinity (family relationship through marriage of a relative)</li><li>likeness, connection, tie</li><li>closeness; familiarity, intimacy</li></ol><p><i>ро́дственный</i> (ródstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related</li><li>kindred</li><li>akin, cognate, kin, consanguineous (of the same kin&#59; related by blood)</li><li>consanguine</li><li>sister</li><li>allied</li><li>relational</li><li>congenerous</li><li>agnate</li><li>parental</li></ol><p><i>родство́</i> (rodstvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relationship, kinship, affinity (family relationship through marriage of a relative)</li><li>kin</li><li>consanguinity, cognation</li><li>connection, connexion</li><li>alliance</li><li>blood</li><li>propinquity</li><li>cousinage</li></ol><p><i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery, childbirth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarming (<i>of bees</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рои́ть</i> (roítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) face, mug, ugly mug, puss</li><li>erysipelas</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďa</i>, related to <i>&#42;rodъ</i>. Compare Old East Slavic <i>рожаи</i> (rožai, “appearance, face”).</p><p>A pan-European formation in medical symptomatology after the appearance of the rose-blossom, compare the translations of <i>erysipelas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth, to bear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ражати</i> (ražati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raďati</i>. Doublet of <i>рожда́ть</i> (roždátʹ), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthrate</li></ol><p><i>рожда́емый</i> (roždájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth</li><li>to give rise to</li><li>to bear, to yield (<i>of land</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>раждати</i> (raždati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raďati</i>. Doublet of <i>рожа́ть</i> (rožátʹ), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born (to come into existence through birth)</li></ol><p><i>рожда́ть</i> (roždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li></ol><p><i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>рожде́ние</i> (roždénije, “birth”)</li></ol><p><i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Christmas</li></ol><p><i>Рождество́</i> (Roždestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рождественский)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rozhdestvensky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) birth; nativity</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Рождество́</i> (Roždestvó)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďьstvo</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рождество)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas (<i>lit.</i>, the Birth)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>Рождество́ Христо́во</i> (Roždestvó Xristóvo).</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>рождьство</i> (roždĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďьstvo</i>. Displaced inherited Old East Slavic <i>рожьство</i> (rožĭstvo), which is still found in dialects (see <i>рожество́</i> (rožestvó) and <i>рожство́</i> (rožstvó)).</p><p>Morphologically, see <i>род</i> (rod), <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ) + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman in childbirth</li><li>woman recently confined (gave birth)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďanica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-еница</i> (-enica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Christmas</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) human face</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a kind of plant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рожьство</i> (rožĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďьstvo</i> (“birth; birthday; Christmas”). Compare рожство́ (rožstvó). Doublet of Рождество́ (Roždestvó), a Church Slavic loanword.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>ро́жа</i> (róža, “ugly mug, ugly face”)</li></ol><p><i>ро́жа</i> (róža) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) little horn, prong</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) antenna, feeler, horn</li><li>ear trumpet</li><li>(<i>music</i>) horn (any of several musical wind instruments), clarion, siren; bugle</li><li>(<i>military</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) rifle magazine</li><li>cone (<i>of ice-cream</i>)</li><li>gas burner</li><li>feeding bottle</li><li>shoehorn</li><li>lamp socket</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) St. John's bread, carob bean</li></ol><p><i>рог</i> (rog) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) pointed stake (placed at an angle in a defensive fortification)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trouble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oržьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) Christmas</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рожьство</i> (rožĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roďьstvo</i>. Compare рожество́ (rožestvó). Doublet of Рождество́ (Roždestvó), a Church Slavic loanword.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) rye (grass&#59; grain)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) stand or field of rye</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръжь</i> (rŭžĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъžь</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>raž</i>, Bulgarian <i>ръж</i> (rǎž), Latvian <i>rudzi</i>, German <i>Roggen</i>, English <i>rye</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Stressed equivalent of <i>раз-</i> (raz-). Found primarily in deverbal nouns and past passive participles.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz-</i>. Doublet of <i>раз-</i> (raz-), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose (shrub, flower)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) rose-coloured/colored complexion on the skin</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Роза)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Roza, equivalent to English Rose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosarium, rose garden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch rod, cane (for whipping)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rozga</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;razgā́ˀ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)resg-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>restis</i>, Old Armenian <i>երագազ</i> (eragaz).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>плеть</i> (pletʹ) akin to <i>плести́</i> (plestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brief rest, breather, respite.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socket, outlet (opening for a plug)</li><li>female jack or socket for headphones</li><li>(architecture,&#32;art) rosette</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) rosette</li><li>jam dish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rosette</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosette (ornamental imitation of a rose)</li><li>rosette (thin, cookie-like, deep-fried Scandinavian pastry, made using an iron, which resembles a rose blossom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rosette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rosetta Stone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindling, ignition</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>разже́чь</i> (razžéčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosemary (<i>Salvia rosmarinus</i>, syn. <i>Rosmarinus officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rosmarin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retail (sale of goods directly to the consumer)</li></ol><p><i>рознь</i> (roznʹ, “difference”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica), suggesting that retail sales sell products at different quantites directly to the customer.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>retail</li></ol><p><i>ро́зница</i> (róznica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) separate, different</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>ра́зный</i> (ráznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) discord, disagreement</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orz-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pinkish, light pink</li></ol><p><i>ро́за</i> (róza) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;nominalized) gay woman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn pink</li><li>to show pink <i>(imperfective only)</i></li></ol><p><i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pink and green</li></ol><p><i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj, “pink”) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj, “green”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rosy-cheeked</li></ol><p><i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>щека́</i> (ščeká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pink</li><li>rosy</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rose; rosaceous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lesbian, homosexual (related to female homosexuality)</li><li>magenta</li></ol><p><i>ро́за</i> (róza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>лило́вый</i> (lilóvyj) (akin to <i>ли́лия</i> (lílija)), <i>фиоле́товый</i> (fiolétovyj) (akin to <i>фиа́лка</i> (fiálka)). Also note <i>цвет</i> (cvet), <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ро́за</i> (róza): (small) rose; a symbol or a decoration in the shape of a rose</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a sharded bottleneck</li></ol><p><i>ро́за</i> (róza) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draw (procedure by which the result of a lottery is determined)</li><li>prank, practical joke, hoax</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>search, investigation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>разыскно́й</i> (razysknój)</li></ol><p><i>ро́зыск</i> (rózysk) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>beekeeping</i>) to collect (bees) into a swarm</li></ol><p>From <i>рой</i> (roj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>рої́ти</i> (rojíty), Belarusian <i>раі́ць</i> (raícʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(bees etc.)</i> to swarm</li><li><i>(thoughts)</i> to crowd</li></ol><p><i>рои́ть</i> (roítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рои</i> (roi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rojь</i> (“stream, current”). Compare cognate Old East Slavic <i>нарои</i> (naroi, “assault, onset”), Macedonian <i>порој</i> (poroj), Czech <i>zdroj</i> (“source, resource”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, doom</li><li>(<i>usually in singular</i>) rock music</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rokъ</i> (Modern Russian <i>срок</i> (srok)). Related to <i>речь</i> (rečʹ).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>rock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) rock group (musical group specializing in rock music)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>rock group</i>; <i>рок</i> (rok) was also borrowed from English, <i>гру́ппа</i> (grúppa) from other European languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) rock-’n’-roll (genre of Russian dancing that emulates the energetic American rock-’n’-roll dance-style of the 1950s)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rock 'n' roll</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) belt road, lateral road</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rocade</i>. Doublet of <i>рокиро́вка</i> (rokiróvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rocker (rock music performer or fan)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) biker (person whose life centers on motorcycles)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rocker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) castling; a move where the castle (rook) and king swap places</li><li>(business,&#32;government,&#32;organizations) reshuffle, swap of roles or positions</li></ol><p>From <i>рокировать</i> (rokirovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Doublet of <i>рока́да</i> (rokáda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fatal, deadly, ruinous</li><li>fateful</li></ol><p><i>рок</i> (rok) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>fātālis</i> (whence <i>фата́льный</i> (fatálʹnyj)) &lt; <i>fātum</i>, akin to Latin <i>for</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;art) rococo (style of baroque architecture)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(architecture,&#32;art) rococo (style of baroque architecture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roar, low rumble, murmur</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рокотъ</i> (rokotŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rokotъ</i>. Probably related to <i>&#42;reťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roar, to roll, to murmur</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рокотати</i> (rokotati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rokotati</i>. Probably related to <i>&#42;reťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roquefort</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>roquefort</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotating cylinder, shaft, roller</li><li>roll (<i>of paper</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rolle</i> (“roll, role”), in turn borrowed from Old French <i>rolle</i> (whence French <i>rôle</i>), from Latin <i>rotulus</i> (“paper scroll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) roleplayer, roleplaying fan</li></ol><p><i>ролево́й</i> (rolevój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) role</li><li>role-playing</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) roll (<i>of paper</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cylindrical shaft, roller</li></ol><p><i>роль</i> (rolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p><p><i>рол</i> (rol) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roller, roll (that which rolls)</li><li>bowl</li><li>pulley, block</li><li>sheave</li><li>trundle</li><li>caster</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) in-line skates</li><li>video clip</li></ol><p><i>рол</i> (rol) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in-line skates</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) roller</li></ol><p><i>ро́лик</i> (rólik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roller skate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) RORO, roll-on roll-off</li></ol><p>Attested 1973. Coined from a clipping of English <i>roll-on roll-off</i>, with the ending influenced by nautical terms such as <i>ба́лкер</i> (bálker) and <i>та́нкер</i> (tánker); displaced earlier terms <i>ро-ро</i> (ro-ro) (which was perceived as awkward by Russian speakers) and <i>ро́ллер</i> (róller) (which could be confused with <i>моторо́ллер</i> (motoróller)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll (makizushi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>role, part</li><li>roll (<i>of paper</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rôle</i>, from Latin <i>rotulus</i> (“paper scroll (for actors)”).</p><p>Apparently borrowed from German <i>Rolle</i> (“roll, role”), in turn borrowed from Old French <i>rolle</i> (whence French <i>rôle</i>), from Latin <i>rotulus</i> (“paper scroll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romaji</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ローマ字</i> (Rōma-ji, “romaji”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novel (work of prose fiction)</li><li>romance, love affair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>roman</i>, from Old French <i>romanz</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Роман)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Roman</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rōmānus</i>, from <i>Rōma</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>Арка́дий</i> (Arkádij), <i>Ли́дия</i> (Lídija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romanization (putting text into the Latin (Roman) alphabet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>novelist</li><li>specialist in the Romance languages</li><li>specialist in Roman law</li></ol><p><i>рома́н</i> (román) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romance studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romance, a love song <i>(of a sentimental nature)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>romance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Romanesque</li><li>Roman</li><li>romance</li><li>Latin (of descendants from ancient Romans)</li><li>Romanic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romanticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romantic</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) romanticist</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) romantic meeting</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>romantique</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romance, romantic appeal, romanticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>romantic</li></ol><p><i>рома́нт(ика)</i> (románt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>romantic</li></ol><p><i>рома́нт(ика)</i> (románt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camomile</li><li>oxeye daisy</li><li>mayweed</li></ol><p><i>романъ</i> (roman, “camomile”) +&lrm; <i>-ашка</i> (-aška), from New Latin <i>anthemis romana</i>. Compare Czech <i>rmenec</i>, Polish <i>rumianek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>camomile, chamomile</li></ol><p><i>рома́шка</i> (romáška) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) rhombus, diamond</li><li>(<i>military</i>) diamond formation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ромб</i> (romb): small rhombus</li></ol><p><i>ромб</i> (romb) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhombic</li></ol><p><i>ромб</i> (romb) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhombicosidodecahedron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rum</li></ol><p><i>ром</i> (rom) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) romulea (<i>Romulea</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Romulea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ballet</i>) rond de jambe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>rond de jambe</i> (literally “circle of the leg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ronin (<i>masterless samurai</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>浪人</i> (ろうにん, rōnin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>РОНО́</i> (RONÓ)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: РОНО)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>district department of education</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>Райо́нный Отде́л Наро́дного Образова́ния</i> (Rajónnyj Otdél Naródnovo Obrazovánija).</p></html>

<h3>Russian (Capitalized: Роно)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>РОНО́</i> (RONÓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop (something), usually unintentionally</li></ol><p>From <i>ронить</i> (ronitʹ), from Old East Slavic <i>ронити</i> (roniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;roniti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>ро̀нити</i>, Polish <i>ronić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murmur, grumble, growl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ръпътъ</i> (rŭpŭtŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъpъtъ</i>, onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murmur, to grumble</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ръпътати</i> (rŭpŭtati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъpъtati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Stressed equivalent of <i>рас-</i> (ras-). Found primarily in deverbal nouns.</li><li>Russian (used in the names of companies or organizations)</li></ol><p>Shortening of Россия (Rossija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dew</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rosà</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;rasā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rṓs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the National Guard of Russia</li></ol><p><i>Росгва́рдия</i> (Rosgvárdija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec) or acronym of <i>росси́йский гварде́ец</i> (rossíjskij gvardéjec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>роса́</i> (rosá): a singe drop of dew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dewy</li></ol><p><i>роса́</i> (rosá) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(euphemistic,&#32;Internet slang) to kill oneself, to take one's life, to commit suicide</li></ol><p><i>Роскомнадзо́р</i> (Roskomnadzór) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), due to Roskomnadzor allegedly removing suicide-related materials from Runet.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>luxurious</li><li>magnificent</li></ol><p><i>ро́скошь</i> (róskošʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luxury, opulence</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orzkošь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tall (of a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolverine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>росома́ха</i> (rosomáxa), <i>россома́ха</i> (rossomáxa), attested from early 17th c.; <i>росома́ка</i> (rosomáka), <i>россома́ка</i> (rossomáka), <i>росома́къ</i>&nbsp;m (rosomák), attested from middle 16th c. Further etymology unclear.</p><p>Per Trubachev, possibly a taboo metathesis from <i>соромаха</i>, still present in some Ukrainian dialects, from hypothetical Proto-Slavic form <i>&#42;sormaxa</i>, therefore cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harmô</i>, Latvian <i>sermulis</i> and Lithuanian <i>šarmuõ</i>, <i>šermuõ</i>, <i>šermuonė̃lis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paintings; mural</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) signature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissolution, dissolving</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) dissolution, dismissal</li><li>type of long trailer for transporting logs, I-beams, etc.</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) similar type of sled carriage for transporting logs</li><li>(<i>papermaking</i>) pulp slurry</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>распуска́ть</i> (raspuskátʹ), with stress shift and corresponding expected change from <i>рас-</i> (ras-) to <i>ро́с-</i> (rós-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) Russian (person)</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from Byzantine Greek <i>Ῥῶς</i> (Rhôs, “Rus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Russian (concerning the country, nationality)</li></ol><p><i>Росси́я</i> (Rossíja) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Russian-American</li></ol><p><i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>америка́нский</i> (amerikánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian citizen, national, or resident of Russia <i>(any ethnicity)</i></li></ol><p><i>Россия</i> (Rossija) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) crossroads</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dilemma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) placer, placer/alluvial deposit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scattering</li><li>bulk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>росси́йский</i> (rossíjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growth (in a living organism)</li><li>growth, increase, rise (in a quantity, price, etc.)</li><li>height, stature</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ростъ</i> (rostŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orstъ</i>. Synchronically derivable as deverbal from <i>расти́</i> (rastí, “to grow”); note that stem-stressed forms of this verb begin with <i>рос-</i> (ros-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roast beef</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>roast beef</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thaw</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thaw</li></ol><p><i>ро́степель</i> (róstepelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seedcorn maggot (<i>Delia platura</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ростко́вый</i> (rostkóvyj, “of a sprout or shoot of a plant”) +&lrm; <i>му́ха</i> (múxa, “fly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) growth, increase</li></ol><p><i>рост</i> (rost) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Rostov, Rostov-na-Donu</li></ol><p><i>Росто́в</i> (Rostóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Rostov or Rostov-on-Don</li></ol><p><i>Росто́в</i> (Rostóv) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usurer, moneylender</li></ol><p>From <i>ростово́й</i> (rostovój) +&lrm; <i>-щи́к</i> (-ščík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprout; shoot</li></ol><p>From <i>рост</i> (rost) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>росто́к</i> (rostók, “sprout, shoot”)</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>рост</i> (rost, “growth”)</li></ol><p><i>росто́к</i> (rostók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flourish</li></ol><p>From <i>рос-</i> (ros-) + a deverbal noun derived from <i>черка́ть</i> (čerkátʹ, “to scratch, to leave a mark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sundew</li></ol><p><i>роса́</i> (rosá, “dew”) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) company</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>rotte</i> (“detachment, unit, crowd”), from Old French <i>rote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amur sleeper (<i>Perccottus glenii</i>)</li><li>(rare,&#32;derogatory) belly-god, a term of abuse for someone who eats a great quantity of food in one sitting</li></ol><p><i>рот</i> (rot, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án, augmentative suffix) by its voracious appearance and behaviour.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bigmouthed (having a large mouth)</li></ol><p>From <i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>polygraphic</i>) rotary printing press</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rotary</li></ol><p>From <i>рота́ция</i> (rotácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotation (a regular variation in a sequence)</li><li>rotary press</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rotātiō</i>, or taken through an intermediate language. Further from Latin <i>rotō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rottweiler (type of dog)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rottweiler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roti (type of Indian flatbread)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>रोटी</i> (roṭī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рот</i> (rot, “mouth”)</li></ol><p><i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) captain (in the tsarist army)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rittmeister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) company</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) company commander</li></ol><p><i>ро́та</i> (róta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mouth; oral</li></ol><p><i>рот</i> (rot) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onlooker, bystander</li><li>gawk, gaper, scatterbrain, (a distracted, inattentive, clumsy person)</li></ol><p><i>рот</i> (rot, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>зия́ть</i> (zijátʹ, “to gape”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to rubberneck, to gawk, to goggle</li></ol><p><i>ротозе́й</i> (rotozéj) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rotor</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) curl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roaming (<i>using a mobile phone outside of its original registering zone</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>roaming</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to joke, to jest</li><li>to laugh</li><li>to laugh at (над кем, над чем)</li><li>to laugh because (с кого, с чего)</li><li>to mock, to deride</li></ol><p><i>ро́фл</i> (rófl) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) from English ROFL, abbreviation for (<i>rolling on the floor, laughing</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dawdle, dawdler, a sluggish or low-energy person, weakling</li><li>hand pallet truck, pallet jack, which serves mainly to move cargo on pallets</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove (a medium-sized collection of trees)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oršča</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;orstja</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Russian <i>рост</i> (rost, “growth”) and <i>расти́</i> (rastí, “to grow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ро́ща</i> (róšča, “copse, grove”)</li></ol><p><i>ро́ща</i> (róšča) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grand piano</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>royal</i>. Further from Latin <i>rēgālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swarming (of bees)</li></ol><p><i>рои́ть</i> (roítʹ, “to swarm”) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ре́ки</i> (réki, “rivers”)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>р.</i> (r.), abbreviation of <i>река́</i> (reká, “river”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mercury, mercurial, mercuric</li></ol><p><i>ртуть</i> (rtutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercury (Hg)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рътуть</i> (rŭtutĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rьtǫtь</i>, possibly related to Lithuanian <i>risti</i> (“to roll”),.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rwanda (language)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Руанда)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rwanda (a country in East Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Rwandan</li></ol><p><i>Руа́нда</i> (Ruánda) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rouble (<i>singular or plural</i>)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>рубль</i> (rublʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane (a tool)</li><li>jointer</li><li>jack plane</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Low German <i>rûbank</i>, equivalent to German <i>Raubank</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to axe, to fell, to chop, to cut with an axe (<i>one time</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to not mince words, to speak the truth, to speak one's mind</li></ol><p>From <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ) or <i>руба́ть</i> (rubátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shirt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫbaxa</i>, from <i>&#42;rǫbъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>рубъ</i> (rub, “a piece of cloth”) +&lrm; <i>-аха</i> (-axa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>руба́шка</i> (rubáška, “shirt”)</li><li>diminutive of <i>руба́ха</i> (rubáxa, “shirt”)</li></ol><p><i>руба́шка</i> (rubáška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>button-up shirt</li><li>chemise</li><li>back (of a playing card)</li><li>dust jacket, dustcover (of a book)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fetal membranes, amniotic sac</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) jacket</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) harness, straitjacket</li></ol><p><i>руба́ха</i> (rubáxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boundary, border, borderline, frontier (the line or frontier area separating regions)</li><li>line</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>рубежь</i> (rubežĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫbežь</i>, from <i>&#42;rǫbiti</i> (“to chop, hem”) + <i>&#42;-ežь</i>. Doublet of <i>рубёж</i> (rubjóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofing felt, asphalt roll roofing (rolled cardboard impregnated with bitumen and covered with a protective layer of bitumen and talc or sand aggregate, used as a flexible roofing and waterproofing material)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Ruberoid</i>, an American brand of asphalt roll roofing introduced in 1892, from English <i>rubber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scar, wake, cicatrice</li><li>weal, wale</li><li>hem, seam</li><li>paunch, rumen, the first compartment of the stomach of a ruminant</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) tripe, chitterlings</li></ol><p><i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rubidium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubidium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brutal fight (a physical confrontation between people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electricity</i>) knife switch; cutout</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) nose; face; mouth; snout; schnozzle</li></ol><p><i>руби́ль(ный)</i> (rubílʹ(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chopping, chiping</li><li>acting to chop up into parts</li></ol><p><i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruby (type of gem)</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>rubīnus</i> &lt; <i>rubeus</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (whence also <i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij), <i>ру́дый</i> (rúdyj), <i>ру́сый</i> (rúsyj)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>я́хонт</i> (jáxont) (<&lt; Ancient Greek <i>ὑάκινθος</i> (huákinthos), whence also <i>гиаци́нт</i> (giacínt)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рубин)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Rubin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ruby</li></ol><p><i>руби́н</i> (rubín) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to axe, to fell, to chop, to cut (<i>with an axe</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to not mince words, to speak the truth, to speak one's mind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫbiti</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>руба́ць</i> (rubácʹ), Bulgarian <i>ръ́бя</i> (rǎ́bja), Ukrainian <i>руби́ти</i> (rubýty), Czech <i>roubit</i>, Polish <i>rąbać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting, felling (<i>of trees</i>)</li><li>hewing, chopping</li><li>mincing, chopping</li><li>slashing</li><li>deckhouse, deck cabin</li></ol><p><i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>roef</i> (“cabin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chopped, diced</li><li>cut, ground up</li><li>prepared from chopped or ground meat or fish</li><li>inflicted with a cutting weapon</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) intermittent and sharp (<i>of a sound</i>)</li><li>made of logs, timbered</li></ol><p><i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рубль</i> (rublʹ, “ruble”): (<i>colloquial</i>) ruble</li></ol><p><i>рубль</i> (rublʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) ruble</li></ol><p><i>рубль</i> (rublʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruble, rouble (a monetary unit of Russia and Belarus equal to 100 kopecks)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рубль</i> (rublĭ). By surface analysis, <i>руб-</i> (rub-) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ). Literally, “a cut” (of a grivna bullion). False cognate of Sanskrit <i>रूप्य</i> (rūpya, “silver”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) ruble</li></ol><p><i>рублёвый</i> (rubljóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рублёвка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rublyovka, a prestigious residential area in Moscow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ruble</li><li>one-ruble</li></ol><p><i>рубль</i> (rublʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to axe, to chop (<i>one time</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) alternative spelling of <i>реба́б</i> (rebáb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heading, caption</li><li>column</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rubryka</i>, from Latin <i>rubrica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) cutting (of wood e.g. with an axe)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫbežь</i>. Doublet of <i>рубе́ж</i> (rubéž), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. By surface analysis, <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abuse, swearing (insulting speech)</li><li>quarrel, argument</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abusive</li></ol><p><i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curse, oath, swear word (vulgar epithet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reprimand, to abuse, to scold, to rail, to curse out, to swear</li><li>to criticize severely, to tear to pieces, to lash</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call (bad) names, to curse, to swear, to use bad language</li><li>to quarrel, to fight [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) to swear like a sailor, to swear like a trooper, to curse like a pagan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) abuse; swearing</li></ol><p><i>руга́ть</i> (rugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ore</li><li>(dated,&#32;dialectal) blood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ruda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudbeckia (<i>Rudbeckia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Rudbeckia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vestige</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ore mine, pit</li></ol><p>From <i>руда́</i> (rudá, “ore”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mine</li></ol><p><i>рудни́к</i> (rudník) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ore</li></ol><p><i>руда́</i> (rudá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>ру́дый</i> (rúdyj, “red, redhaired, redheaded”)</li></ol><p>Etymology</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рудъ</i> (rudŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rudъ</i>. Compare to Ukrainian <i>руди́й</i> (rudýj), Czech <i>rudý</i>, Polish <i>rudy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) red, blood-red</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) redhaired, redheaded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рудъ</i> (rudŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rudъ</i>. Compare to Ukrainian <i>руди́й</i> (rudýj), Czech <i>rudý</i>, Polish <i>rudy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;Slavic Native Faith) September</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gunsmith</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gun, rifle</li></ol><p><i>ружьё</i> (ružʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ружьё</i> (ružʹjó): (small) rifle, shotgun</li></ol><p><i>ружьё</i> (ružʹjó, “rifle, shotgun”) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shotgun, musket, rifle</li><li>a device resembling such a firearm</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) weapon, arm</li></ol><p>Inherited from a colloquial form of Old East Slavic <i>оружие</i> (oružije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orǫžьje</i>, from <i>&#42;orǫga</i> / <i>&#42;orǫžь</i>. Doublet of <i>ору́жие</i> (orúžije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ruin (construction withered by time or calamity)</li><li>feeble man weakened by old age or disease</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>ruīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li><li>arm</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>рѫка</i> (rǫka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫka</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ránkāˀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hand in hand (in an inextricably linked fashion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeve</li><li>distributary</li><li>hose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫkavъ</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>manica</i> (&lt; <i>manus</i>), German <i>Ärmel</i> (&lt; <i>Arm</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mitten, mitt</li><li>gauntlet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫkavica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glove, mitten</li></ol><p><i>рукави́ца</i> (rukavíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sleeve</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) hose</li></ol><p><i>рука́в</i> (rukáv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-armed</li><li>efficient, skillful</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hands up!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arugula (herb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masturbator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) masturbation, onanism</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>блуд</i> (blud) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Literally, “fornication of the hand”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to indulge in masturbation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arm wrestling</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бор(о́ться)</i> (bor(ótʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>directed, operated, guided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader, chief, manager, director, instructor, teacher</li></ol><p><i>руководи́ть</i> (rukovodítʹ, “to conduct, to lead, to direct (with authority)”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to guide, to direct, to manage, to run (to conduct or direct with authority) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”)</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>handle</i> (&lt; <i>hand</i>); English <i>manage</i> (&lt; Latin <i>manus</i> (whence also English <i>manipulate</i>, Russian <i>манипули́ровать</i> (manipulírovatʹ))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guidance, instruction, direction</li><li>leadership (a group of leaders)</li><li>textbook, guide</li></ol><p><i>руководи́ть</i> (rukovodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be guided by, to follow (e.g. some rules)</li></ol><p><i>руково́дство</i> (rukovódstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guiding, leading</li><li>senior, supervisory</li><li>chief</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needlework, fancywork, embroidery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needlewoman</li></ol><p><i>рукоде́лие</i> (rukodélije) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do needlework/fancywork</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handmade, handcrafted</li></ol><p><i>рукоде́лие</i> (rukodélije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;vulgar) bungler, lummox, klutz (an incompetent and clumsy worker)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand, arm”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “arse”); a nominalization from the phrase <i>ру́ки из жо́пы расту́т</i> (rúki iz žópy rastút), referring to the fanciful notion of an incompetent worker's hands or arms growing from his arse.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a stone's throw away, around the corner, very close</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>within reach</i>, French <i>à portée de main</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chiroptera (of bat family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washstand</li><li>lavabo for secular use</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aye-aye (nocturnal quadruped)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand-to-hand fight, man to man fight (fighting without weapons)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hand-to-hand</li></ol><p>From <i>рукопа́шь</i> (rukopášʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from <i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пашь</i> (pašʹ, “a waving, a flapping”), from <i>паха́ть</i> (paxátʹ, “to wave, to flap”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) manuscript</li><li>handwritten</li><li>cursive, chirographic</li><li>typewritten</li></ol><p>From <i>ру́копись</i> (rúkopisʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manuscript</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) clapping, applauding</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) applause</li></ol><p>From <i>рукоплеска́ть</i> (rukopleskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to applaud, to clap</li></ol><p>From <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плеска́ть</i> (pleskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handshake (grasping of hands by two people)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) handshake</li></ol><p>From <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пожа́тие</i> (požátije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) one who has not stained himself with unworthy, vile or immoral acts, someone you can shake hands with</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пожа́ть</i> (požátʹ, “to shake (as in "shake hands")”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) to ordain</li></ol><p><i>руко-</i> (ruko-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) ordination</li></ol><p><i>рукоположи́ть</i> (rukopoložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) to ordain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assault and battery, beating (by hands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to employ physical violence, to hit, to beat (with the hands)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) man-made, artificial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle, grip, hilt (part of an object which is held in the hand when used or moved)</li></ol><p><i>рукоя́ть</i> (rukojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle</li><li>hilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rǫko(j)ętь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steering, rudder</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmsman</li></ol><p><i>руль</i> (rulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(food)</i> roll</li><li>Swiss roll</li><li>boned gammon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>roulette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tape measure (<i>retractable, e.g. for carpentry</i>)</li><li>roulette (game of chance)</li></ol><p><i>руле́т</i> (rulét) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), French <i>roulette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to taxi</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to steer the wheel</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) to be in charge, to be in the driver's seat</li></ol><p><i>руль</i> (rulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll (that which is rolled up)</li><li>reel</li><li>bolt (roll of fabric)</li><li>web (long sheet fed from a roll)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French (compare <i>rouleau</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudder</li><li>helm</li><li>steering wheel</li><li>handlebars</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>roer</i>, via an intermediate form <i>&#42;рурь</i> (&#42;rurʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elevator <i>(of airplane wings)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxiing</li><li>taxiway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) taxiway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(aviation,&#32;relational) taxiing</li></ol><p><i>рулёк(ка)</i> (ruljók(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) <i>(compass)</i> point</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rumba</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) tiller</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>roerpen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romanian (male person from Romanian)</li></ol><p>Directly from Romanian <i>rumân</i>, archaic form of <i>român</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Romanian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romanian language</li></ol><p><i>Румы́ния</i> (Rumýnija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blush, blusher, rouge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colour/color, flush, blush</li></ol><p><i>румя́на</i> (rumjána) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rouge</li></ol><p><i>румя́н(а)</i> (rumján(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruddy, rosy, pink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>румꙗнъ</i> (rumjanŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ruměnъ</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;rudměnъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rune</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from German <i>Rune</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kirundi, Rundi (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a large storage chest with a flat lid that could be used as a bench</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) locker</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) seabag, duffel bag</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) foreroom</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing, Nogai <i>орындыкъ</i> (orındık”, “chair”), Kazakh <i>орындық</i> (oryndyq, “chair”), Kyrgyz <i>орундук</i> (orunduk, “chair”), Tatar <i>урындык</i> (urındıq, “chair”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Руне́т</i> (Runɛ́t)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Рунет)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) The Russian-language websites on the Internet, taken as a whole, Runet</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij) +&lrm; <i>интерне́т</i> (intɛrnɛ́t).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a user of Russian-language sites on the Internet</li></ol><p><i>Руне́т</i> (Runɛ́t) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleece, wool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;runo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>रुपया</i> (rupyā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>megaphone</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) megaphone, mouthpiece (spokesman, also collective, as a newspaper, magazine, TV channel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>roeper</i>, from <i>roepen</i> (“to shout”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a representative of the Rus tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European hare (<i>Lepus europaeus</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) Russian, Russki (someone who really looks Russian or who has characteristic Russian features)</li></ol><p>From <i>ру́сый</i> (rúsyj, “dark blond, blond-brown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) rusalka; a Slavic female water spirit associated with death by drowning and with Green Week traditions occurring around the time of Pentecost</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) nymph, mermaid (<i>in the context of Western fantasy and mythology</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>русалиꙗ</i> (rusalija), Old Church Slavonic <i>роусалиѩ</i> (rusaliję) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Byzantine Greek <i>ῥουσάλια</i> (rhousália), from Latin <i>rōsālia</i> (“festival of the roses”). Compare with Bulgarian руса́лия (rusálija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rusalka</li></ol><p><i>руса́лка</i> (rusálka) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>руса́лка</i> (rusálka): rusalka</li></ol><p><i>руса́лка</i> (rusálka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rusalka</li></ol><p><i>руса́лка</i> (rusálka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Russify, to assimilate and become like a Russian</li><li>to become fair-haired</li></ol><p><i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p><p><i>ру́сый</i> (rúsyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russism, Russicism, Russianism (a word or other feature originating in the Russian language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rusyn (male member of a people living in the eastern Carpathian Mountains&#59; an inhabitant of the ancient Rus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Rusyn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rusyn language</li></ol><p>From <i>руси́н</i> (rusín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russificator (That which does Russification)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) Localization of software into the Russian language.</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) A specific program, mod, or user that translates a movie, game, or similar artistic work into the Russian language.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Russificator</i>. Can be analysed as <i>русифика́ция</i> (rusifikácija) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>russification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Russify (to make or become more Russian)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to localize for Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be Russified (become Russian)</li></ol><p><i>русифици́ровать</i> (rusificírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an inhabitant of ancient Rus, Rusyn</li></ol><p><i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ич</i> (-ič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riverbed</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) direction, way, manner, path, vein, course (of development of a situation)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) blood vessel</li></ol><p>Presumably related to Lithuanian <i>rusė́ti</i> (“to flow”), <i>rusnóti</i> (“to flow slowly”). Compare the name of the Lithuanian river <i>Raũsvė</i>, and <i>Rusni̇̀s</i>, a right distributary of the Neman.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) Russia</li><li>(collective,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive,&#32;by extension) Russians, Russkis</li></ol><p><i>рус-</i> (rus-) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja) or <i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p><p>This slur was coined during the Second Chechen War by the Chechen militant Shamil Basayev in analogy of the name Чечня́ (Čečnjá), as the -ня suffix was seen by him as derogatory for referring Chechens as a mob.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a person who looks very much like a Russian</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a Russian chauvinist</li></ol><p>Related to ру́сский (rússkij, “Russian”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>русопя́т</i> (rusopját)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) slaughter, destruction, or murder of ethnic Russians</li><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) one who murders ethnic Russians</li></ol><p><i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij, “a Russian”) +&lrm; <i>рез</i> (rez, “cut, one who cuts, murderer”).</p><p>Attested since 2007 by the nationalist writer Konstantin Krylov referring to people who murder ethnic Russians, as a bogeyman in Russian nationalist discourse. Popularized during the 2022 invasion of Ukraine by Ukrainian Internet users as an Internet meme against Russian soldiers, in mockery towards pro-invasion supporters.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophilia (a strong interest in the country, culture, or people of Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Russophilic (referring to Russophilia or liking or love of Russia and Russian people)</li></ol><p><i>русофи́л</i> (rusofíl) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophilia</li></ol><p><i>русофи́л</i> (rusofíl) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophobe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophobia (the fear of Russia or Russians)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Russophobic (referring to Russophobia or fear or hatred of Russia and Russian people)</li></ol><p><i>русофо́бия</i> (rusofóbija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russenorsk</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Norwegian Bokmål <i>russenorsk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>руси́зм</i> (rusízm)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russism, Russicism, Russianism (a word or other feature originating in the Russian language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> русский (russkij),&lrm; па́лка (pálka).</li><li>Russian bar (gymnastics-style beam held by two porters)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>по-ру́сски</i> (po-rússki)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) Russian, related to Russian ethnicity, language, or culture</li><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) Russian, related to the Russian state</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Rus', related to the Rus' people and Kievan Rus'</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) a Russian, especially an ethnic Russian person (for Russian national or resident, see <i>россия́нин</i> (rossijánin))</li><li>(animate,&#32;informal) a Russian (a citizen or resident of Russia)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) the Russian language (short for ру́сский язы́к (rússkij jazýk))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>русьскъ</i> (rusĭskŭ). By surface analysis, <i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ, “Rus”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russian Sign Language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) The Russian march, a nationalist and far-right rally held in some cities across Russia on Unity Day.</li></ol><p>From ру́сский (rússkij, “Russian”) марш (marš, “march, rally”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The community of Russia as its own world, along with countries influenced by Russia; the Russosphere.</li></ol><p>From ру́сский (rússkij, “Russian”) мир (mir, “world”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Russo-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Russian-English</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Russophone, Russian-speaking</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russophone, Russian speaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Russianness (quality of being Russian)</li></ol><p>From <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Russian-speaking, Russophone (Russian-speaking)</li><li>written or spoken in Russian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) rustication, rustic masonry</li></ol><p><i>рустова́ть</i> (rustovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair(-haired), blond(e), light brown (of hair), dirty blond</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>русъ</i> (rusŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rusъ</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;rudsъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rowdʰ-s-o-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;collective) Russians, Ruthenians (people of Kievan Rus)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Русь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rus</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Russia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Kievan Rus (a medieval principality in Eastern Europe centred around Kyiv; at its maximum extent covering much of modern Ukraine, Belarus and western Russia and considered an early predecessor of each country)</li><li>An umbrella term for the territory of modern Russia, Ukraine and Belarus, inhabited by the East Slavic Christian Orthodox people. <i>Used until the 20th century</i>.</li><li>name of a St. Petersburg daily newspaper published from 1903 to 1908</li><li>name of a special forces unit (ОСНАЗ) of the Russian Ministry of Internal Affairs (formed 1994)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>Русь</i> (Rusĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rue (<i>Ruta</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>rūta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;uncommon,&#32;neologism) the Russian-speaking community on Twitter.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij, “Russian”) +&lrm; <i>Тви́ттер</i> (Tvítter, “Twitter”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruthenium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>routine</li><li>rut, groove</li><li>resistance to change, conservatism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>routine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>routine, habitual, conventional</li></ol><p><i>рути́на</i> (rutína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rue (<i>Ruta</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ру́та</i> (rúta) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofer (a daredevil who performs stunts on tops of skyscrapers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>roofer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofing (a way of travel which consists in clambering over the roofs of skyscrapers)</li></ol><p><i>ру́фер</i> (rúfer) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofing (a way of travel which consists in clambering over the roofs of skyscrapers)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>roofing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate,&#32;uncountable) junk, lumber, cruft, old junk (anything old or of inferior quality)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;animate) an old and fragile person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collapse, to crash</li><li>to fall through, to crash, to fail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ruxnǫti</i>, from <i>&#42;rȗxъ</i> (“movement”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guarantee, warrant, representation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warrant, to guarantee, to certify, to answer for, to vouch for [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to assure, to promise</li><li>to bet, to be sure</li></ol><p><i>-ручи́ть</i> (-ručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brook, stream, creek</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ručьjь</i>. Akin to Ukrainian <i>руча́й</i> (ručáj), Czech <i>ručej</i>, Polish <i>ruczaj</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caddis fly</li></ol><p><i>руче́й</i> (ručéj, “brook”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare typologically Slovak <i>potočník</i> (&lt; <i>potok</i>, akin to <i>пото́к</i> (potók)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>руче́й</i> (ručéj): small brook, rivulet</li><li>rucheyok (a traditional Russian game)</li></ol><p><i>руче́й</i> (ručéj) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>рука́</i> (ruká): large hand, paw (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-и́ща</i> (-íšča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рука́</i> (ruká): small hand</li><li>handle, crank</li><li>knob</li><li>grip</li><li>chair arm</li><li>lever (<i>for controlling a mechanical device</i>)</li><li>pen (writing instrument)</li><li>pen holder</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench hammer</li><li>hand brake</li><li>stick shift, manual transmission</li><li>ruchnik (traditional embroidered towel or cloth, used for ceremonies or holy sites)</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hand cannon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hand; manual</li><li>handmade</li><li>tame, domesticated</li></ol><p><i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>mānsuēs</i>, <i>mānsuētus</i> (<&lt; <i>mānus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portable rocket launcher; rocket-propelled grenade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рука́</i> (ruká), diminutive of <i>ру́чка</i> (rúčka): (small) hand, (small) arm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Staraya Russa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raze, to tear down</li><li>to husk (grain)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рꙋшити</i> (rušiti, “to destroy”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rušiti</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ру́шити</i> (rúšyty, “to move, to wiggle”), also <i>руша́ти</i> (rušáty), Belarusian <i>ру́шыць</i> (rúšycʹ, “to move, to wiggle”), Old Church Slavonic <i>роушити</i> (rušiti, “to destroy”), Bulgarian <i>ру́ша</i> (rúša, “to destroy”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ру̏шити</i> (“to destroy”), Slovene <i>rúšiti</i> (“to shake, to demolish”) (1sg. <i>rȗšim</i>), Czech <i>rušit</i> (“to disturb, to break the silence”), Slovak <i>rušiť</i> (“to move”), Polish <i>ruszyć</i> (“to move”), Lower Sorbian <i>rušowaś</i> (“to rage, to make noise”).</p><p>More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>raũsti</i> (“to dig”) (1sg. <i>rausiù</i>, 1sg. past <i>rausiaũ</i>), Latvian <i>ràust</i> (“to rake, to sweep”) (1sg. <i>ràušu</i>, 1sg. past <i>ràusu</i>), Lithuanian <i>rausis</i> (“cave”), <i>rūsỹs</i> (“cellar”), also <i>rū́sas</i>, Latvian <i>ruzga</i> (“fidgety person”), <i>rušinât</i> (“to dig”), <i>rusums</i> (“steep bank”), Old Norse <i>rúst</i> (“ruin, ruined wall”), <i>ryskja</i> (“to tear”), dialectal German <i>ruscheln</i> (“to work hastily”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall in, to collapse</li><li>to fall to the ground, to collapse</li></ol><p><i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ruschia (<i>Ruschia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ruschia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Belarus,&#32;Ukraine) towel</li><li>rushnyk, rushnik, ruchnik (traditional embroidered towel or cloth, used for ceremonies or holy sites)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>рушни́к</i> (rušnýk). Doublet of <i>ручни́к</i> (ručník).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) name of the Cyrillic letter Р, р, called <i>эр</i> (er) in modern Russian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>рьци</i> (rĭci), the imperative singular form of <i>рещи</i> (rešti, “to speak, to say”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) fish (as a living animal)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) fish (as food)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;dominoes) blocked game; block</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;publishing,&#32;music) placeholder, lorem ipsum</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ryba</i>, further etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angelfish (marine fish of the family Pomacanthidae)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a large whale or fish</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) a legendary large whale or fish, which according to legend is the mother of all fish</li></ol><p>Literally, “fish-whale”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) clownfish</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>кло́ун</i> (klóun, “clown”), likely a calque of English <i>clownfish</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilot fish (a fish, <i>Naucrates ductor</i>, that often follows large vessels or sharks)</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>ло́цман</i> (lócman)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sawfish (Pristidae spp.)</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>пила́</i> (pilá, “saw”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anglerfish (fish of the bony fish order Lophiiformes)</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>уди́льщик</i> (udílʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>From <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak). Compare Polish <i>rybak</i>. Also compare Bulgarian <i>рибар</i> (ribar), Czech <i>rybář</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>рибар</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>рибич</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to fish, to angle</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>риба́лити</i> (rybályty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(feminine,&#32;inanimate) fishing trip</li><li>(masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine,&#32;animate,&#32;rare) someone who fishes</li><li>(feminine,&#32;animate,&#32;dialectal) a bird that eats fish</li></ol><p>From <i>рыба́лить</i> (rybálitʹ, “to fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) fisherman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rybařь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ) (ending-stressed variant) or from <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ) (stem-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fisherman's</li><li>(relational,&#32;fishing) fishing</li></ol><p><i>рыба́к</i> (rybák) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>рыба́цкий</i> (rybáckij)</li></ol><p><i>рыба́к</i> (rybák) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish, to angle</li></ol><p><i>рыба́к</i> (rybák, “fisherman”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish, piscine</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cold-blooded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rybьjь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> ры́бий (rýbij),&lrm; жир (žir).</li><li>fish oil, cod liver oil (nutritional supplement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) big fish</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;nautical) ribband, diagonal; batten</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba): small fish, fishy</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish shop, fishmonger's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish pie, traditional fish pie, rybnik</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fish</li><li>fish-filled, fishful (abounding in fish, full of fish)</li></ol><p><i>ры́ба</i> (rýba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piscicultural</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish farming, pisciculture</li></ol><p>From <i>рыбово́д</i> (rybovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Compare Belarusian <i>рыбаво́дства</i> (rybavódstva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisher (one who fishes)</li></ol><p>From <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ, “to catch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fishing, piscatory, piscatorial</li></ol><p><i>рыболо́в</i> (rybolóv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishery, fishing</li></ol><p><i>рыболо́в</i> (rybolóv) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-shaped</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who catches or processes fish on industrial scale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a fish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ры́ба</i> (rýba)</li></ol><p><i>рыбёха</i> (rybjóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ры́бий</i> (rýbij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jerk (quick, often unpleasant tug or shake)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) dash, burst, spurt</li><li>leap (a leap in technological development)</li><li>snatch (Olympic (<i>weightlifting</i>) discipline)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) swede, rutabaga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to belch loudly</li><li>to vomit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rygati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobbing, weeping</li></ol><p><i>рыда́ть</i> (rydátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sob, to blubber, to weep (to make noises while crying)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рꙑдати</i> (rydati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rydati</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>रोदिति</i> (roditi), <i>रोदति</i> (rodati).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auburn, ginger (<i>hair, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij) +&lrm; <i>-ева́тый</i> (-evátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-haired</li></ol><p><i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij): red, sorrel; ginger (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij): circus clown</li></ol><p><i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red, sorrel</li><li>(<i>of hair</i>) red, ginger</li><li>(<i>of a person or other thing that has hair</i>) red-haired, red-headed, ginger</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circus clown</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рꙑжии</i> (ryžii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ryďь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rūdʰ-yo-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saffron milk cap (<i>Lactarius deliciosus</i>)</li><li>camelina (<i>Camelina sativa</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;animate) person with red hair</li></ol><p><i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>рижика</i> (rižika), Czech <i>ryzec</i>, Macedonian <i>рујница</i> (rujnica), Polish <i>rydz</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>rujnica</i>, Slovak <i>rýdzik</i>. Compare Spanish <i>robellón</i> (cognate via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>бе́лый гриб</i> (bélyj grib).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>officialese</i>) An annual congress for palm-builders.</li><li>(<i>music</i>) The second part of a traditional opera.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snout (<i>of pigs, fish, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) mug (ugly human face)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рыло</i> (rylo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rydlo</i> (“spade, snout”). Related to Polish <i>ryj</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>Rüssel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ры́ло</i> (rýlo, “snout, mug (human face)”)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) stigma</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spout (<i>of a vessel</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ры́ло</i> (rýlo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an armor-bearer or bodyguard of a Muscovite tsar</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) three triple strikes of a ship's bell marking noon in Russian naval tradition</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) a ship's bell</li></ol><p>From a mondegreen of English <i>“ring the bell”</i>, a British officer's command interpreted as <i>«ры́нду бей»</i> («rýndu bej», “strike the rynda”) by Russian sailors.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marketplace, market</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rynek</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hringaz</i> (“ring”). Further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)krengʰ-</i> (“ring, circle”) (akin to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krongʰ-o-</i>, whence Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krǫgъ</i>, whence Russian <i>круг</i> (krug)), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>. Doublet of <i>ранг</i> (rang) and <i>ринг</i> (ring).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) labour market</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ры́нок</i> (rýnok, “market”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>market economy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;jargon) a supporter of the market, the market economy; advocate of market economy</li></ol><p>From <i>ры́ночный</i> (rýnočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) market</li></ol><p><i>ры́нок</i> (rýnok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrash (about), to struggle</li><li>to bother (to do something, usually futile or no longer necessary)</li></ol><p>Reflexive of <i>ры́пать</i> (rýpatʹ, “to spring, to fling (oneself)”), which, in its turn, is from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rypati</i> (“to burrow; to tear”). Cognate with Czech <i>rýpat</i>, Polish <i>rypać</i>, Bulgarian <i>ри́пам</i> (rípam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trotter (a type of horse)</li></ol><p><i>рыси́ть</i> (rysítʹ, “to trot”) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prowl, to scour <i>(about)</i></li><li>to rummage <i>(in)</i>, to ransack</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to gripe, to yaw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рискати</i> (riskati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;riskati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>trotting (namely of horses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lynx</li><li>trot (horse gait)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rysь</i>, distantly related to English <i>lynx</i>.</p><p>Related to <i>риста́ть</i> (ristátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pothole</li><li>pit</li></ol><p>From <i>рытва</i> (rytva) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), from <i>рыть</i> (rytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тва</i> (-tva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li><li>to burrow, to mine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ryti</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rūti</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)rewH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig <i>(in)</i></li><li>to burrow <i>(in)</i>, to rake <i>(over, through)</i></li><li>to rummage <i>(in)</i>, to ransack</li></ol><p><i>ры́ть</i> (rýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digging</li></ol><p><i>рыть</i> (rytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тьё</i> (-tʹjó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friable, crumbly, loose</li><li>flabby, podgy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ryxlъ</i>. Related to <i>ру́шить</i> (rúšitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knight's, knights'</li><li>knightly, chivalrous</li></ol><p><i>ры́царь</i> (rýcarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) knighthood (knights collectively, in feudal Europe)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) chivalry</li></ol><p><i>ры́царь</i> (rýcarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knight (a medieval Western European nobleman serving as an armored and mounted warrior&#59; a person on whom knighthood has been conferred)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) knight, cavalier, gallant (a brave and chivalrous man)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rycerz</i>. The form is influenced by <i>царь</i> (carʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lever (rigid thing turning about an axis, used for transmitting and modifying force and motion)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>rycząg</i> (“pin on the front axle of a cart”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lever</li></ol><p><i>рыча́г</i> (ryčág) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рыча́г</i> (ryčág)</li></ol><p><i>рыча́г</i> (ryčág) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growl, grumble, snarl (a low thundering, rumbling or growling sound)</li></ol><p><i>рыча́ть</i> (ryčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble, to growl, to snarl (to make a low growling or rumbling animal noise)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ryčati</i>, <i>&#42;rykati</i>. Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>zealously</li></ol><p><i>рья́ный</i> (rʹjányj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>zealous</li><li>mettlesome</li></ol><p>Formed from the same root as <i>ри́нуть</i> (rínutʹ) and <i>ре́ять</i> (réjatʹ), analogously to <i>пья́ный</i> (pʹjányj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) ragtime (music genre)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ragtime</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racket (blackmail)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>racket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racketeer (criminal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>racketeer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) rap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapper (performer of rap)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rapper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rap (speak lyrics in the style of rap music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>rap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) deliberate misspelling of <i>русский</i> (russkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>backpack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rucksack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рюкза́к</i> (rjukzák, “backpack, rucksack”)</li></ol><p><i>рюкза́к</i> (rjukzák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any of several types of glasses primarily used for drinking liquor, such as a wine glass or shot glass and especially a rocks glass.</li><li>shot (a quantity of liquor)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Römer</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>рю́мка</i> (rjúmka): (small) wine glass, shot glass; shot (a quantity of liquor)</li></ol><p><i>рю́мка</i> (rjúmka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Estonia,&#32;military,&#32;colloquial) platoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Estonian <i>rühm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruff, ruche</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ruche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain ash, rowan</li><li>rowanberry</li><li>spot, blot, blemish, stain (<i>of skin</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rębina</i>.</p><p><i>рябо́й</i> (rjabój) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ряби́на</i> (rjabína): rowanberry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ря́бина</i> (rjábina): small discolored skin patch (<i>from disease</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fieldfare (<i>Turdus pilaris</i>)</li><li><i>Sorbaria</i> (false spirea) (genus of flowering plants)</li></ol><p><i>ряби́на</i> (rjabína) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rowan vodka</li></ol><p><i>рябиновый</i> (rjabinovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rowan, rowanberry</li></ol><p><i>ряби́на</i> (rjabína) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intr</i>) to ripple</li><li>to speckle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be speckled</li><li>to pockmark</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to have spots in the eyes</li></ol><p><i>рябь</i> (rjabʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitted, pocked, pockmarked</li><li>speckled</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rębъ</i> (“speckled, spotted”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁erbʰ-</i>, see also Ancient Greek <i>ὀρφνός</i> (orphnós, “dark brown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazel grouse (bird)</li><li>fritillary (plant)</li></ol><p><i>рябо́й</i> (rjabój) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ripple</li><li>speckling, spotting</li><li>dazzle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bellow, to roar</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bellow, to roar</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>row, line, tier</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the plural) ranks (the body or membership of an organization&#59; the soldiers of an army)</li><li>series</li><li>a number of, several</li><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) degree of backness or frontness</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) contract, agreement, treaty (<i>in medieval Kievan Rus' law</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>рѧдъ</i> (rędŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rędъ</i>. Cognate with Latvian <i>rinda</i> (“row, line”), Old Norse <i>greida</i> (“unfold, arrange”), Persian رده (rade, “row, category”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress up <i>(as)</i>, get up <i>(as)</i></li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to ordain <i>(that)</i>, to lay down the law</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to contract</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ręditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) row, line</li><li>ordinary, common, average, routine</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rank and file</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) private (rank)</li></ol><p><i>ряд</i> (rjad) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alongside, beside, side by side, nearby</li><li>alongside, side by side</li><li>near, beside, next to</li><li>heel (command for a dog)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) near, nearby, alongside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hydraulic&#32;engineering,&#32;historical) crib pier (a wooden frame sunk into the ground, usually filled with dry, viscous, greasy clay or cobblestone&#59; used to support dams, embankments, and sometimes bridges)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ryazhenka <i>(baked fermented milk)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ryazan</li><li>from Ryazan</li></ol><p><i>Ряза́нь</i> (Rjazánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cassock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>ῥάσον</i> (rháson), from Latin <i>rāsus</i> (p.p.p. of <i>rādō</i>).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>дерю́га</i> (derjúga) (akin to <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duckweed</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ря́са</i> (rjása, “cassock”)</li></ol><p><i>ряса́</i> (rjasá, “swampy terrain”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>ря́са</i> (rjása, “cassock”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rude</i>) face (usually broad or fat); mug</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;rude) face (usually fat); mug</li></ol><p><i>ря́ха</i> (rjáxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rib, costal</li></ol><p>From <i>ребро́</i> (rebró) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roar, bellow (long, loud, deep shout)</li><li>howl (loud cry)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>реве́ть</i> (revétʹ, “to roar”). Cognates include Belarusian <i>роў</i> (row), Ukrainian <i>рев</i> (rev).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crybaby</li></ol><p><i>рёва</i> (rjóva) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) red, reddish-yellow (of cattle hair color)</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>рьдьръ</i> (rĭdĭrŭ) (Russian), via vowel assimilation from <i>&#42;ръдьръ</i> (&#42;rŭdĭrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rъdrъ</i>; cognate with Old East Slavic <i>ръдръ</i> (rŭdrŭ). <i>рьдьръ</i> (rĭdĭrŭ) + <i>-ый</i> (-yj) developed to <i>&#42;ре́дрый</i> (&#42;rédryj), then to the modern form <i>рёдрый</i> (rjódryj) by diphthongization (see History of the Russian language § The yo vowel on Wikipedia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rösti (a kind of potato pancake in Switzerland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Rösti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ре́дко</i> (rédko).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ре́дька</i> (rédʹka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>река́</i> (reká).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ресни́ца</i> (resníca).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ре́чь</i> (réčʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> instrumental]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>with</li><li>with (<i>in certain time expressions</i>)</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>from, off, from off, from below (<i>with abstract nouns; nouns entailing a flat, open area; and special exceptions</i>)</li><li>because of, since (<i>expressing a cause of motivation</i>)</li><li>from (<i>indicating a duration of time</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘to an ending time’</small>]</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>for, about (<i>indicating approximate time or number</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>centi- (0.01)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i>, see there for more.</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>санти-</i> (santi-).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: С)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>се́вер</i> (séver, “north”)</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) abbreviation of <i>слон</i> (slon, “bishop”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Happy New Year</li></ol><p>The verb <i>поздравля́ть</i> (pozdravljátʹ, “to congratulate”) is implied.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>God be with you!, God speed! God go with you, speed!</li><li>God be your help!, success attend you!, good luck!, here goes!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from side to side</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) extempore</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, from the interjections <i>бух</i> (bux) and <i>бара́х</i> (baráx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tête-à-tête, privately</li></ol><p>Literally, “from eye to eye”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>happy birthday</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>поздравля́ю с днём рожде́ния</i> (pozdravljáju s dnjom roždénija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>since when</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a little over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>a friendly remark made to someone who's just got out of the shower or sauna</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>поздравля́ю с лёгким па́ром</i> (pozdravljáju s ljóxkim párom, literally “I congratulate [you] for the light steam”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from place to place</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>God is with us</li></ol><p>Interpretation of Biblical Hebrew <i>עִמָּנוּאֵל</i> ('imanu'éll) (Isaiah 7:14, 8:10) and Koine Greek <i>μεθ ἡμῶν ὁ Θεός</i> (meth hēmôn ho Theós) (Matthew 1:23). Compare German <i>Gott mit uns</i> and Ukrainian <i>з нами Бог</i> (z namy Boh).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;with verbs встать, поставить, перевернуть) (to turn) on its head, upside down, (to turn) from a normal position upside down</li></ol><p>Literally, “from the feet to the head”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from scratch, from the ground up</li><li>anew, all over again, back to square one, back to the drawing board</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the one hand</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first sight, at a glance, at a glimpse, at first blush</li><li>prima facie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>red-handed (in the act of wrongdoing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>via, by dint of (by (means of)&#59; using (a medium))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>happy holiday! (a generic phrase used on various types of national or public holidays)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>happy arrival!, welcome!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>of course, with pleasure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the start, from the very beginning, from the outset, from the first, from the get-go; all along</li><li>initially</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>since that time, since then</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>с тех по́р как</i> (s tex pór kak); since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>difficultly, with difficulty (being difficult to do the action)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with pleasure (willingly, without argument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>about something being successfully done</i>) immediately, instantly, right away, on the spot, off the bat, off the top, off the cuff, on the first attempt</li><li>on the way, along the way, in stride, on the move, on the go (in the course of moving somewhere)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>с</i> (s, “off (of)”) +&lrm; <i>хо́ду</i> (xódu, “course, motion”, in the partitive case).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from now on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>съезд</i> (sʺjezd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censored spelling of <i>суици́д</i> (suicíd)</li><li>filter-avoidance spelling of <i>суици́д</i> (suicíd)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>сука</i> (suka)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>сука</i> (suka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censored spelling of <i>секс</i> (seks)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censored spelling of <i>сиська</i> (sisʹka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) (describing an action moving from a single position or many positions into another single position)</li><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) down (describing a downward action)</li><li>(<i>with verbs</i>) off, from off (describing an action of removing oneself to another location)</li><li>added to nouns, forms adverbs with the approximate meaning "from the location of &#91;the noun&#93;"</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱom</i>. See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> for more information. The /n/ persists in <i>снять</i> (snjatʹ) (originally a prefixed derivative of <i>ять</i> (jatʹ, “to take”)), from which other words in <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ) were produced by rebracketing. See also <i>с</i> (s), Old East Slavic <i>съ</i> (sŭ), and compare the analogous loss of <i>-n-</i> in <i>в-</i> (v-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>страни́ца</i> (straníca, “page”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>село́</i> (seló, “village”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>сре́дний род</i> (srédnij rod, “neuter gender”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ го́да</i> (sevó góda, “of this year, ultimo”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ ме́сяца</i> (sevó mésjaca, “of this month, inst.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ числа́</i> (sevó čislá, “of this date”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ го́да</i> (sevó góda, “of this year, ultimo”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ ме́сяца</i> (sevó mésjaca, “of this month, inst.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ числа́</i> (sevó čislá, “of this date”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сре́дняя шко́ла</i> (srédnjaja škóla): secondary school (equivalent to high school)</li><li>initialism of <i>се́верной широты</i> (sévernoj široty)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ го́да</i> (sevó góda, “of this year, ultimo”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ ме́сяца</i> (sevó mésjaca, “of this month, inst.”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>сего́ числа́</i> (sevó čislá, “of this date”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male Sami</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Sami language; compare Kildin Sami <i>са̄ммь</i> (sāmm’).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sami (member of an indigenous Finno-Ugric people of Lapland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Sami language; compare Kildin Sami <i>са̄ммь</i> (sāmm’) and Northern Sami <i>sámi</i>. Possibly related to Russian <i>самое́д</i> (samojéd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sabantuy, plow party; the name of a Tatar national holiday.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) party, gettogether</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>Сабантуй</i> (Sabantuy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;jargonism) subway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>subway</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subwoofer</li></ol><p>Borrowed no later than 1995 from English <i>subwoofer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Any plant of the genus <i>Comarum</i>.</li></ol><p><i>са́бля</i> (sáblja, “saber, cutlass”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, nominative suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) subject, topic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>subject</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of raisins made of sultana grapes, treated before drying with an alkaline solution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Sabine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sabre-toothed</li></ol><p><i>са́бля</i> (sáblja) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sabre-toothed tiger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saber, cutlass</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) rider, cavalryman (<i>as a counting unit of military strength, see also штык</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>саблꙗ</i> (sablja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sabľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dereliction (premeditated non-execution, very slow or careless execution, of one's duties)</li><li>sabotage (deliberate action of subversion, obstruction, disruption, destruction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sabotage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saboteur</li></ol><p>From <i>сабота́ж</i> (sabotáž, “sabotage”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sabotage</li></ol><p>German <i>sabotieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shroud (dress for the dead)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σάβανον</i> (sábanon, “linen cloth or towel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savanna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>savant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stiff-tailed duck, Oxyurinae tribe duck</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) long-tailed duck, <i>Clangula hyemalis</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Savoy cabbage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Savoie</li></ol><p><i>Саво́й(я)</i> (Savój(ja)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) A horse of a light chestnut colour</li></ol><p><i>савра́с</i> (savrás) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light chestnut (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A horse of a light chestnut colour</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saga</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сага)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saga (a prefecture of Japan)</li><li>Saga (the capital city of Saga Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>佐賀</i> (さが, Saga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow case</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a horse archer's archery equipment, consisting of a bow in its bow case and arrows in a quiver</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language. Ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>ᠰᠠᠭᠠᠳᠠᠭ</i> (saɣadag, “quiver”); see Azerbaijani <i>sadaq</i> and Bashkir <i>һаҙаҡ</i> (haźaq) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agitate</li><li>to persuade</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>агити́ровать</i> (agitírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sagittal (in the direction from dorsal to ventral)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sago</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sago</i>, from Dutch <i>sago</i>, from Malay <i>sagu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) to aggro (to cause (a non-player character) to become aggressive towards the player's character)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;by extension) to provoke; to deliberately anger; to pick a fight</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>а́грить</i> (ágritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to get angry</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>а́гриться</i> (ágritʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden, orchard</li><li>(botany,&#32;zoology) garden (institution that collects plants or animals)</li><li>kindergarten</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sadъ</i>. Related to English <i>set</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to hit once, to punch once</li></ol><p><i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadism (enjoyment of inflicting pain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sadisme</i>. By surface analysis, <i>Сад</i> (Sad) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сад</i> (sad) (small) garden, orchard</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kindergarten</li></ol><p><i>сад</i> (sad) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadist (one who derives pleasure through cruelty or pain to others)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sadistic</li></ol><p><i>сади́ст</i> (sadíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or let someone sit down, to seat</li><li>to plant (e.g. trees or flowers)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to imprison, to put to jail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;saditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down, to sit up</li><li>to take, to board, to get on</li><li>to sit down to</li><li>to land, to alight</li><li>to be imprisoned</li><li>to shrink (about clothes after washing)</li></ol><p><i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to be sore (<i>from scrapes, abrasions, burns, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hurt (the heart, soul, etc.), to sadden, to cause emotional distress to</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to hurt (<i>in the heart, etc.</i>), to experience emotional distress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sadьniti</i>, from <i>&#42;sadьno</i>. By surface analysis, <i>садно́</i> (sadnó) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) needle bush</li></ol><p>Literally, “Garden acacia”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardener, groundskeeper</li></ol><p><i>сад</i> (sad) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horticulturist (a gardener, a person interested or practising horticulture)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardening, horticulture (the science of caring for gardens)</li></ol><p><i>садово́д</i> (sadovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garden; cultivated</li></ol><p><i>сад</i> (sad) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish pond (artificial pond for keeping and breeding fish)</li><li>coop (room for keeping, breeding or fattening birds and small animals)</li><li>name for a series of traps for animals and game</li><li>diminutive of <i>сад</i> (sad): small garden</li></ol><p><i>сад</i> (sad, “fish pond (<i>obsolete, dialectal, per Dal</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p><p><i>сад</i> (sad, “garden”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sadomasochism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military slang</i>) Arab ally; Arab friend (a soldier in the Baathist Syrian Arab Armed Forces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from South Levantine Arabic صديق (ṣadīq), from Arabic صَدِيق (ṣadīq, “friend”), in reference to the alliance between Russia and Baathist Syria.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scientology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;saďa</i>, cognate with Bulgarian <i>сажди</i> (saždi), Chakavian Serbo-Croatian <i>sađa</i> and <i>saže</i>, Slovene <i>saje</i>, Slovak <i>sadza</i>, Polish <i>sadza</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Old Norse <i>sót</i> (“soot”), English <i>soot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seat, to set</li><li>to plant</li><li>to land (an aircraft)</li><li>to put in jail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sad'ati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapling, young plant, seedling</li></ol><p><i>сажа́ть</i> (sažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енец</i> (-enec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sazhen, old Russian unit of length equal to 3 arshins or 2.134 meters or 7 feet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѧжень</i> (sęženĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sęženь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;se(n)g-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to seat (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>сажа́ть</i> (sažátʹ), iterative of <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>сажа́ть</i> (sažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ) or from <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit down (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>сади́ться</i> (sadítʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>са́живать</i> (sáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sazan, wild carp (<i>Cyprinus carpio</i>)</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Compare Ottoman Turkish سازان (sazan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sazan (<i>Cyprinus carpio</i>)</li></ol><p><i>саза́н</i> (sazán) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Far East,&#32;especially&#32;Kamchatka) A food cache</li><li>(Central Siberia,&#32;Amur) An aranga, a burial scaffold (traditionally used by some native tribes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saiga</li><li>Saiga (family of Russian semi-automatic rifles)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saiga (antelope)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chagatai سایغاق (saiɣak, “a kind of chamois”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coalfish, saithe (<i>Pollachius virens</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Norse <i>seiðr</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saidaz</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;soytós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow case</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a horse archer's archery equipment, consisting of a bow with its bow case and arrows in a quiver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic language. Possibly ultimately from Mongolic; see Azerbaijani <i>sadaq</i> and Bashkir <i>һаҙаҡ</i> (haźaq) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread roll, bun</li><li>Arctic cod, polar cod</li></ol><p>The sense of a bread is borrowed from Estonian <i>sai</i>, the sense of a fish variating <i>са́йда</i> (sájda), both applying the suffix +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pacific saury, mackerel pike (<i>Cololabis saira</i>)</li><li>saury (any fish of the <i>Cololabis</i> genus)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>佐伊羅<rp>(</rp><rt>さいら</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>saira</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pacific saury, mackerel pike (<i>Cololabis saira</i>)</li></ol><p><i>са́йр(а)</i> (sájr(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>website, site</li><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) site</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>site</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) travel bag</li><li>small bag-shaped net stretched over a half-hoop, like a fishing tackle</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) wide woman's coat</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Saka (member a group of nomadic Iranian peoples who historically inhabited the northern and eastern Eurasian Steppe and the Tarim Basin, related to the Scythians)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sac</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Persian (Sakā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpetbag (generally made from leather or heavy cloth)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sac de voyage</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>саквая́ж</i> (sakvajáž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sake, Japanese rice wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>酒</i> (さけ, saké).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a traditional village house in the Caucasus and Crimea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>სახლი</i> (saxli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacral</li><li>sacred</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>sacral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacramental</li><li>sacred, cherished</li><li>traditional, customary</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>sacramentālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saxon (member of an ancient Germanic tribe)</li><li>seax</li><li>(<i>music</i>) sax</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>саксофо́н</i> (saksofón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saxaul (any of three species of the amaranth genus <i>Haloxylon</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultiamately Mongolic, compare Mongolian <i>заг</i> (zag), Kalmyk <i>за</i> (za).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saxophone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saxophonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sakura</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sake, Japanese rice wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>酒</i> (さけ, saké).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) noob, rookie, greenhorn (m or f)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(small) sledge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salak (fruit)</li></ol><p>From Malay <i>salak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) Baltic herring (<i>Clupea harengus membras</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) noob, rookie, greenhorn (m or f)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Finnic language, from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;salakka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Baltic herring (<i>Clupea harengus membras</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сала́к(а)</i> (salák(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) salaam (greeting in a Muslim context)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic سَلَام (salām, “peace”). Doublet of <i>шало́м</i> (šalóm).</p><p>See the corresponding lemma form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>саля́м але́йкум</i> (saljám aléjkum)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) RIP, done for, goodbye</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salamander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>salamandra</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σαλαμάνδρα</i> (salamándra), from Persian سمندر (samandar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) salamander</li></ol><p><i>салама́ндр(а)</i> (salamándr(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;dialectal) to greet</li></ol><p>Derived from салам (salam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salad (<i>dish</i>)</li><li>lettuce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>salade</i>, from Italian <i>salata</i> (“salad, something salted”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salad bowl</li></ol><p>From <i>сала́т</i> (salát) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salad bowl</li></ol><p><i>сала́т</i> (salát) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lime green</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lettuce</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) salad</li></ol><p><i>сала́т</i> (salát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lime green</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) salad</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lettuce</li></ol><p><i>сала́т</i> (salát) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grease, to smear</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to soil with grease</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit (a runaway player) with the hand or a ball (<i>in some games</i>)</li></ol><p><i>са́ло</i> (sálo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suet, fat, lard</li><li>tallow</li><li>a lubricant made of minerals or plants</li><li>icy slush that flows down the river before the river freeze</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) a place in a game of lapta where the ball is hit and thrown</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сало</i> (salo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sadlo</i>.</p><p>For <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) relation, compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>στέαρ</i> (stéar) (whence <i>стеари́н</i> (stearín), <i>стеато-</i> (steato-)) <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Ukrainian</li></ol><p>From <i>са́ло</i> (sálo, “lard”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”). Referring to the usage of lard in Ukrainian cuisine.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salon (fashionable shop or customer service), hairdressing salon</li><li>show, exhibition</li><li>art gallery</li><li>lounge, waiting room</li><li>cabin, passenger compartment</li><li>(reception with performing poets, musicians etc) salon</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) saloon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>salon</i> (“reception room”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Resembling salo.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of warm clothing</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, borrowed from French <i>salope</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tallow boilery</li></ol><p><i>са́ло</i> (sálo, “fat”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to melt down”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tallow boilery</li></ol><p><i>са́ло</i> (sálo, “fat”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to melt down”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saloon (<i>a tavern in the Western United States</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>napkin, serviette</li><li>doily</li><li>(wet) wipe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Salvette</i>, from Italian <i>salvietta</i> and French <i>serviette</i>, compare Serbo-Croatian <i>salveta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doily</li><li>antimacassar (cover for furniture)</li><li>tidy</li></ol><p><i>салфе́тка</i> (salfétka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Salvadoran</li></ol><p><i>Сальвадо́р</i> (Salʹvadór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>accounting</i>) balance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>saldo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) omentum</li><li>rubber washer, seal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) animal fat, lard</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) tallow</li><li>containing a high percentage of fat (<i>e.g. of an animal breed</i>)</li><li>left by fat (<i>e.g. of a spot or mark</i>)</li><li>greasy</li><li>obscene</li><li>grossly cynical</li></ol><p><i>са́ло</i> (sálo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>French <i>sale</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salsa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>salsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) saltation (sudden change from one generation to the next)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) saltation (transport of loose particles by a fluid, such as wind or water)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>saltātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somersault</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>salto</i> (“jump”). Further from Latin <i>saltus</i> (<i>saliō</i> + <i>-tus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salto mortale (a dangerous and daring jump with possibly lethal outcome)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>salto</i> (“leap”) + <i>mortale</i> (“deadly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>са́ло</i> (sálo)</li></ol><p><i>са́ло</i> (sálo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fireworks</li><li>salute</li><li>Salyut (Soviet space station)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) hello</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) bye</li></ol><p>First attested in 1720. Via German <i>Salut</i> or directly from French <i>salut</i>, from Latin <i>salūs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) salaam alaikum (<i>Muslim greeting</i>)</li></ol><p>From Arabic السَّلَام عَلَيْكُم (as-salām ʕalaykum, “peace be upon you”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salami</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>salami</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) salat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic صَلَاةَ (ṣalāta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(definitive pronoun,&#32;emphatic) self, -self</li><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;numeral,&#32;emphatic) alone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>самъ</i> (samŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;samъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sāmas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;somHós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not oneself (not acting or feeling as is typical for oneself)</li></ol><p>Literally, “not one's own self”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adobe (unburnt brick)</li><li>air brick</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language. See Turkish <i>saman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) adobe</li></ol><p><i>сама́н</i> (samán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samarium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samaritan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Samarkand</li></ol><p><i>Самарка́нд</i> (Samarkánd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Samara</li></ol><p><i>Сама́ра</i> (Samára) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) samba (Brazilian ballroom dance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>samba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sambo (martial art)</li><li>Zambo (Afro-Indian)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>самооборо́на&#47;самозащи́та без ору́жия</i> (samooboróna&#47;samozaščíta bez orúžija, “self-defense without weapons”).</p><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>zambo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sambuk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sambuk</li></ol><p><i>самбу́к</i> (sambúk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) macho</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;samьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Soviet Union) samizdat (underground publishing, a samizdat publication)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>самоизда́тельство</i> (samoizdátelʹstvo), from <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>изда́тельство</i> (izdátelʹstvo, “publishing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) female, dam, bitch, doe, hen, she, jenny (an individual belonging to the sex that can give birth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summit (gathering of leaders)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>summit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by itself, naturally (without outside intervention)</li><li>of course, obviously, naturally, it goes without saying (that), needless to say</li></ol><p>Literally, “itself by/with itself”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>auto-, self- (regarding oneself)</li></ol><p>Prefixed version of <i>сам</i> (sam, “self”); see there for further information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>originality, <i>(unique)</i> identity, distinctive character</li></ol><p><i>самобы́тный</i> (samobýtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>original</li><li>distinctive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>быт</i> (byt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samovar (a large coal- or woodfired metal urn with a spigot for boiling water for tea)</li><li>A person without legs or hands; a basket case.</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) + a deverbal form of <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to boil”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>самова́р</i> (samovár)</li></ol><p><i>самова́р</i> (samovár) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>самова́р</i> (samovár)</li></ol><p><i>самова́р</i> (samovár) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) samovar</li></ol><p><i>самова́р</i> (samovár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>самова́р</i> (samovár)</li></ol><p><i>самова́р</i> (samovár) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) autocracy, absolute rule (form of government)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>власть</i> (vlastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narcissism; self-love</li></ol><p><i>самовлюблённый</i> (samovljubljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narcissistic</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>влюблённый</i> (vljubljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insubordination, willfulness</li><li>high-handedness, overbearingness, imperiousness</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;military) AWOL (absent without official leave)</li></ol><p><i>самово́льный</i> (samovólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>self-willedly, wilfully, arbitrarily</li></ol><p><i>самово́льный</i> (samovólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>willful, insubordinate</li><li>unauthorized</li></ol><p><i>самово́л(ие)</i> (samovól(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boast, brag</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>восхвале́ние</i> (vosxvalénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-pickup, an option to get items ordered online at a designated pickup point instead of having it delivered to the residence.</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>вы́воз</i> (vývoz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to commit suicide, to self-delete</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>выпи́ливаться</i> (vypílivatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to commit suicide, to self-delete</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>вы́пилиться</i> (výpilitʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-expression</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>выраже́ние</i> (vyražénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>homebrew, samogon, moonshine</li><li>any home-made distilled alcoholic beverage</li></ol><p><i>сам</i> (sam, “self”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>гон</i> (gon, “distill”), from <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ, “to distill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) moonshine, samogon</li></ol><p><i>самого́н</i> (samogón) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the process of making samogon</li></ol><p><i>самого́н</i> (samogón, “samogon”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>варе́ние</i> (varénije, “process of cooking by boiling”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonshiner, someone who makes or distributes samogon</li></ol><p><i>самого́н</i> (samogón) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-moving</li><li>self-propelling, self-propelled</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>дви́жущийся</i> (dvížuščijsja, “moving”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improvised explosive device</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>homemade, improvised, do-it-yourself</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>дел-</i> (del-) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocracy</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autocratic</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>αὐτοκρατής</i> (autokratḗs, “sovereign”, literally “self-power”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocrat (ruler with absolute power)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) the tsar or emperor of Russia</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>prī̆nceps</i> (<+ <i>-ceps</i> (“catcher”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amateur cultural activity</li><li>initiative, spontaneous action</li><li>unauthorized activity</li></ol><p><i>самоде́ятельный</i> (samodéjatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amateur (theatre, etc.)</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) active</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>де́ятельный</i> (déjatelʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Samoyed (a person belonging to a Samoyedic ethnic group)</li></ol><p>From <i>самодин</i> (samodin, used as an autonym by some Nganasan people when speaking Russian), a dialectal variant of <i>самое́д</i> (samojéd, “Samoyed”), ultimately probably from Sami <i>&#42;saam-edne</i> (“land of the people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Samoyedic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smug, self-satisfied</li><li>complacent</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>дово́льный</i> (dovólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complacency, self-satisfaction, self-righteousness, smugness</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>дово́льство</i> (dovólʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-sufficiency (condition of being self-sufficient)</li></ol><p><i>самодоста́точный</i> (samodostátočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-sufficient (able to provide for oneself independently of others)</li><li>self-reliant</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>доста́точный</i> (dostátočnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a petty tyrant, a wilful person, despot</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) + <i>дури́ть</i> (durítʹ, “to behave like a fool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at most, at the outside</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;sometimes&#32;offensive) (<i>in the narrow sense</i>) a Nenets</li><li>(dated,&#32;sometimes&#32;offensive) (<i>in the wide sense</i>) a Samoyed (a member of any Samoyedic ethnic group)</li><li>a Samoyed (dog)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a self-critical, self-deprecating, excessively introspective person</li></ol><p>Probably from Sami <i>&#42;saam-edne</i> (“land of the people”); compare to Finnish <i>Suomi</i> (“Finn”).</p><p>Russian folk etymology derives the term from <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”), literally “self-eater”.</p><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”), literally “self-eater”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) excessive self-criticism, self-disparagement, self-torment</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo), based on a combining form of <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;puristic) car, automobile</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ), a purist semi-Calque of Russian <i>автомобиль</i> (avtomobilʹ). Compare Russian самолёт.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selflessness, self-forgetfulness</li><li>self-consciousness</li><li>absorption, reverie</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>забве́ние</i> (zabvénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>selflessly, self-forgetfully</li></ol><p><i>самозабве́нный</i> (samozabvénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selfless, self-forgetful</li><li>all-consuming</li></ol><p><i>самозабве́н(ие)</i> (samozabvén(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-employed</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>за́нятый</i> (zánjatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-defense, self-protection</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>защи́та</i> (zaščíta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impostor, pretender, impersonator</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>званец</i> (zvanec, “caller”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-proclaimed, self-styled</li><li>uninvited</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>зва́ный</i> (zványj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-identification</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) self-realization</li><li>self-determination</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>идентифика́ция</i> (idɛntifikácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-isolation</li><li>lockdown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kick scooter</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>-кат</i> (-kat), combining forms of <i>сам</i> (sam, “self”) and <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ, “to roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) kick scooter</li></ol><p><i>самока́т</i> (samokát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-control</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>контро́ль</i> (kontrólʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-criticism</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>кри́тика</i> (krítika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rollup, rollie (hand-rolled cigarette)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) oneself, personally, in person, by oneself</li></ol><p><i>самоли́чный</i> (samolíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in-person</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>ли́чный</i> (líčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambitious (possessing, or controlled by, ambition)</li></ol><p>From <i>самолю́бивый</i> (samoljúbivyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-esteem, self-respect, pride</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narcissism; self-admiration</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>любова́ние</i> (ljubovánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airplane, aircraft</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ). Cognate with Belarusian <i>самалёт</i> (samaljót) and Polish <i>samolot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air tanker (aircraft used to deliver liquids (or powders) from the air)</li></ol><p><i>самолёт</i> (samoljót) +&lrm; <i>запра́вщик</i> (zaprávščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;military) buzz bomb, cruise missile (replaced by the more modern term крылатая ракета)</li></ol><p><i>самолёт</i> (samoljót) +&lrm; <i>снаря́д</i> (snarjád)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>самолёт</i> (samoljót, “airplane”)</li></ol><p>From <i>самолёт</i> (samoljót) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) airplane, aircraft</li></ol><p><i>самолёт</i> (samoljót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aircraft construction</li></ol><p>From <i>самолёт</i> (samoljót) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>строе́ние</i> (strojénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conceit, self-importance</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>мне́ние</i> (mnénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, presumptuous, assuming, assumptive</li><li>overweening (over-confident)</li><li>self-confident, confident, self-assured, self-sufficient</li><li>presuming</li><li>reliant</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>наде́ять(ся)</i> (nadéjatʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-янный</i> (-jannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonym (a name used by a group or category of people to refer to themselves or their language)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>назва́ние</i> (nazvánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-possession, self-control, self-command, self-mastery, composure</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>облада́ние</i> (obladánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-deception, self-deceit (fooling oneself)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>обма́н</i> (obmán)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-deception</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>обольще́ние</i> (obolʹščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-defense</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>оборо́на</i> (oboróna, “defense”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-study (learning outside of the context of a school)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ние</i> (obrazovánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-service</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>обслу́живание</i> (obslúživanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-incrimination; self-perjury</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>огово́р</i> (ogovór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-limitation</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>ограниче́ние</i> (ograničénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-determination</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>определе́ние</i> (opredelénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-organizing, self-organization</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>организа́ция</i> (organizácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selflessness, dedication, disregard of self</li></ol><p><i>самоотве́рженный</i> (samootvéržennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selfless</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>отве́рженный</i> (otvéržennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commitment, dedication, devotion</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>отда́ча</i> (otdáča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renunciation, self-denial, (self-)abnegation</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>отрече́ние</i> (otrečénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-esteem (confidence in one's own worth)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>оце́нка</i> (océnka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-perception</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>ощуще́ние</i> (oščuščénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autocannibalism (the act of eating oneself)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>поеда́ние</i> (pojedánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-sacrifice (giving up of one's own benefit)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>поже́ртвование</i> (požértvovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-knowledge, self-comprehension</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>позна́ние</i> (poznánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-proclaimed</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>провозглашённый</i> (provozglašónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spontaneous</li></ol><p><i>сам</i> (sam) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>произво́льный</i> (proizvólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinning wheel with a flyer mechanism; flyer wheel</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>пря́лка</i> (prjálka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) self-development</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>разви́тие</i> (razvítije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-realization, self-actualization</li><li>self-fulfilment</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>реализа́ция</i> (realizácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-promotion, self-advertisement</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>рекла́ма</i> (rekláma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-reference</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>рефере́нция</i> (referéncija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nugget (small chunk or clump)</li><li>gifted person</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod, “origin”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold nugget</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dump truck (a heavy truck, for carrying loose, bulk cargo, having a hinged bed that allows the contents to be unloaded onto the ground)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>свали́ть</i> (svalítʹ, “to dump”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-improvement</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>соверше́нствование</i> (soveršénstvovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-immolation</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>сожже́ние</i> (sožžénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-awareness (state of being self-aware)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>созна́ние</i> (soznánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-preservation</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>сохране́ние</i> (soxranénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>independently, solo</li><li>by oneself, without assistance, on one's own, self-sufficiently</li></ol><p><i>самостоя́тельный</i> (samostojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>independence (state of having sufficient means for a comfortable livelihood)</li></ol><p><i>самостоя́тельный</i> (samostojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>independent, self-dependent, free</li><li>self-reliant, self-sufficient</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>самостійний</i> (samostijnyj). May be a calque from some other European language (see e.g. German <i>selbstständig</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) crossbow</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) self-inflicted wound (by using firearms)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a person (usually a soldier) with a self-inflicted wound</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illegal building (<i>not compliant with laws or building codes; especially DIY</i>)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>стро́й</i> (strój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selfness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lynching, vigilantism, mob law</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>суд</i> (sud)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unauthorized settler</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a <i>samosel</i> (a civilian resident of the Chernobyl Exclusion Zone who refused to evacuate or who returned against government orders)</li></ol></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>сели́ться</i> (selítʹsja), literally “self-settler”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) dildo</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self”) and <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ, “to poke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drift, gravity flow</li><li>free run, something that happens without a plan or control (spontaneously)</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>-тёк</i> (-tjók), combining form of <i>течь</i> (tečʹ, “to flow”) (compare 1sg. present <i>теку́</i> (tekú)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) suicidal</li></ol><p><i>самоуби́йство</i> (samoubíjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suicide</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo).</p><p>Compare Czech <i>sebevražda</i>, Polish <i>samobójstwo</i>, Slovene <i>samomor</i>, Ukrainian <i>самогу́бство</i> (samohúbstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suicide victim, suicide (<i>person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca).</p><p>Compare Bulgarian <i>самоуби́ец</i> (samoubíec), Czech <i>sebevrah</i>, Polish <i>samobójca</i>, Slovene <i>samomorilec</i>, Ukrainian <i>самогу́бец</i> (samohúbec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-respect (knowledge of one's own worth)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>уваже́ние</i> (uvažénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>self-confidently</li></ol><p><i>самоуве́ренный</i> (samouvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance, cockiness</li></ol><p><i>самоуве́ренный</i> (samouvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, cocky</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>уве́ренный</i> (uvérennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-satisfaction (contentment with one's own accomplishments or situation)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>удовлетворе́ние</i> (udovletvorénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) self-destruction</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>уничтоже́ние</i> (uničtožénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonomy, self-administration, self-government</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>управле́ние</i> (upravlénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-driving car</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arbitrary</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>упра́ва</i> (upráva) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;law) arbitrariness (broad-based crime in Russian law encompassing any "actions contrary to the order presented by a law")</li></ol><p><i>самоупра́в(ный)</i> (samoupráv(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep oneself aloof, to get out of, to distance oneself from, to dodge</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>устраня́ть</i> (ustranjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-assertion</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>утвержде́ние</i> (utverždénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-teaching book</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>учитель</i> (učitelʹ, “teacher”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-taught person, autodidact, automath</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boastfulness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car, automobile</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>хо́д</i> (xód, “walking, movement”), a calque of French <i>automobile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-propelled gun</li><li>self-propelled chassis</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-propelled artillery, self-propelled artillery mount</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-propelled artillery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-propelled artillery, self-propelled artillery mount</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-propelled</li></ol><p><i>самохо́д</i> (samoxód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gem, gemstone, semi-precious stone</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet, “color”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end in itself</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>цель</i> (celʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-censorship</li></ol><p>From <i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>цензу́ра</i> (cenzúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-valuable</li></ol><p>From <i>сам</i> (sam) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>це́нный</i> (cénnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(state of) health, feeling (well or bad)</li></ol><p><i>само-</i> (samo-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствие</i> (čúvstvije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sampan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samsa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samsara</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>संसार</i> (saṃsāra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simoom (hot, dry wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic سَمُوم (samūm, “hot wind”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samurai (feudal Japanese soldier)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>侍<rp>(</rp><rt>さむらい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>samurai</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box (<i>Buxus</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian شمشاد (šemšâd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сам</i> (sam).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>definitive</i>) the very</li><li>the most (superlative degree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;samъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сам</i> (sam) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, really, actually, without doubt, certainly, indisputably</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typical, genuine, idiosyncratic, quirky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious title/order/rank, dignity</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>санъ</i> (sanŭ) (u-stem), probably of Oghur origin. Cognate with Turkish <i>san</i> (“reputation”), Turkmen <i>san</i> (“account”), Kazakh <i>санау</i> (sanau, “count”), Tatar <i>san</i> (“number”) and akin to dial. Turkish <i>sаnаmаk</i> (“to take into account”), Uzbek <i>sanamoq</i> (“to count”) from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;sā(j)-</i> (“to count, to determine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanatorium, sanitarium, health resort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sanatorium</li></ol><p><i>санато́рий</i> (sanatórij) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanguine person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sangria (alcoholic drink)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>sangría</i>, derivative of <i>sangre</i> (“blood”), from Latin <i>sanguis</i>, due to its color. Compare Ukrainian <i>сангрі́я</i> (sanhríja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandalwood, sandal</li><li>santal</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Sanskrit <i>चन्दन</i> (candana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandal (type of footwear)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сэ́ндвич</i> (sɛ́ndvič)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sledge, sleigh, sled (a vehicle on runners, used for conveying loads over the snow or ice)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) luge (a racing sled)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сани</i> (sani), plural of <i>сань</i> (sanĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sanь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orderly, hospital attendant, male nurse, corpsman, paramedic, aidman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanitation (the policy and practice)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;military</i>) ambulance (emergency vehicle, usually for transporting sick or wounded soldiers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sanitary, sanitization</li><li>medical</li></ol><p><i>санитари́я</i> (sanitaríja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>са́ни</i> (sáni): (small) sledge, sled, sleigh, kicksled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) St. Petersburg</li></ol><p><i>Са́нкт-Петербу́рг</i> (Sánkt-Peterbúrg) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petersburger (resident or citizen of Saint Petersburg)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>authorized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sanction, to authorize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanction (approval, by an authority, that makes something valid)</li><li>sanction (penalty, coercive measure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) San Marino</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sledge, sleigh, sled, luge</li></ol><p>From <i>са́н(и)</i> (sán(i)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dignified, dignitary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high official, dignitary</li></ol><p><i>сано́в(ный)</i> (sanóv(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distinguished, high-ranking</li><li>dignified</li></ol><p><i>сан</i> (san) +&lrm; <i>-о́в</i> (-óv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sanskrit (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;accentological) Sanskrit addiction</li></ol><p>From <i>санскри́т</i> (sanskrít) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зави́симость</i> (zavísimostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sanskrit</li></ol><p><i>санскри́т</i> (sanskrít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Santali (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumber (one who fits, etc., pipes for water, gas or drainage)</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>санитарный те́хник</i> (sanitarnyj téxnik, “sanitary technician”). Compare Ukrainian <i>санте́хнік</i> (santéxnik), Belarusian <i>сантэ́хнік</i> (santéxnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumbing</li><li>sanitary engineering</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>санита́рная те́хника</i> (sanitárnaja téxnika, “sanitary engineering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(excessive) sentimentality, sentimentalism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sentiments</i>, plural of <i>sentiment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centimeter/centimetre <i>(SI unit of measure)</i></li><li>tape measure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centimeters per second, cm/s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet and bathroom</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>санита́рный у́зел</i> (sanitárnyj úzel).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>San Franciscan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) infirmary (short for санитарная часть)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glanders, equinia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) sap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sape</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Zapatista (a member of the Liberation Army of the South, a group formed and led by Emiliano Zapata that took part in the Mexican Revolution)</li><li>Zapatista (a member of the Zapatista Army of National Liberation, an indigenous leftist group that formed in the jungles of Chiapas in 1983 and coordinated an uprising on January&nbsp;1, 1994, the day NAFTA took effect)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>zapatista</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;relational) Zapatist:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>relating to the Zapatistas</li><li>relating to Zapatismo, the political philosophy of the Zapatistas</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>са̀пати́ст</i> (sàpatíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сапогъ</i> (sapogŭ). Obscure etymology:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сапо́г</i> (sapóg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cobbler, shoemaker (person who repairs shoes)</li><li>bungler, botcher</li></ol><p>From <i>сапо́г</i> (sapóg, “boot”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boot</li></ol><p><i>сапо́г</i> (sapóg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boot</li><li>columbine</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>сапо́г</i> (sapóg, “boot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zapotec</li></ol><p>From Spanish <i>zapoteco</i>, from Classical Nahuatl <i>Tzapotēcah</i>, plural of <i>Tzapotēcatl</i> (“inhabitant of the place of sapote”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peregrine falcon</li></ol><p>According to several sources, borrowed from Kalmyk in the late 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) breather, crankcase ventilation valve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapphire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sapphire</li></ol><p><i>сапфи́р</i> (sapfír) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sapeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sapper, military engineer</li></ol><p><i>сапёр</i> (sapjór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarabande</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shed, storage building (small wooden construction)</li><li>uncomfortable, untidy room, pigsty</li><li>(<i>Kazakhstan</i>) palace</li></ol><p>Via Turkic, from Persian سرای (sarây).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сара́й</i> (saráj): small shed</li></ol><p><i>сара́й</i> (saráj) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martagon (<i>Lilium martagon</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>сарана</i> (sarana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) locust, grasshopper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;sārïg</i> (“yellow”), compare Karakhanid (sarïğča, “locust”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(young) locust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Saratov</li></ol><p><i>Сара́тов</i> (Sarátov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarafan (a traditional long, trapeze-shaped Russian pinafore worn by women and girls)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Persian سراپا (sarâpâ, “from head to foot; honourable/honorable clothing”) via Turkic <i>särapa(i)</i> or Ancient Greek <i>σάραπις</i> (sárapis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) grapevine, bush telegraph, rumor mill (an informal person-to-person means of circulating news or gossip)</li><li>(<i>marketing</i>) word of mouth</li></ol><p>Literally, “sarafan radio”, originally in reference to gossiping village women.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sarafan</li></ol><p><i>сарафа́н</i> (sarafán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saracen</li></ol><p>Borrowed in 18th century (no later than 1782) from some Western European derivative of Late Latin <i>Saracenus</i>, from Koine Greek <i>Σαρακηνός</i> (Sarakēnós), further origin uncertain (see there).</p><p>Replaced earlier forms <i>срацин</i> (sracin), <i>срацинин</i> (sracinin), <i>срачинин</i> (sračinin), and <i>сорочинин</i> (soročinin) from Old Church Slavonic <i>срацинъ</i> (sracinŭ), a hypercorrected (-ra- instead of -ara-) form of the same ethnonym.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garfish, needlefish (a fish from the Belonidae family&#59; most commonly, <i>Belone belone</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>σαργάνι, σαργάνιον</i> (sargáni, sargánion) (cf. modern Greek <i>ζαργάνα</i> (zargána)), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>σαργός</i> (sargós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sausage, wiener, polony, saveloy (a thick variety of sausage)</li></ol><p>From <i>сарде́ль</i> (sardélʹ, “sardine, sausage”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Polish <i>sardela</i> (“anchovy”), from Italian <i>sardella</i> (“sardine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sardine, pilchard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sardine</li></ol><p><i>сарди́н(а)</i> (sardín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sardonic (scornfully mocking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sari (<i>the traditional dress of women in the Indian subcontinent</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>साड़ी</i> (sāṛī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sarcastic (containing sarcasm)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcophagus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Sarmatian</li><li>RS-28 Sarmat (Russian intercontinental ballistic missile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sarov (closed city in Russia, 120 miles from Nizhny Novgorod&#59; a center of nuclear research)</li></ol><p><i>Саро́в</i> (Saróv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sarpinka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sarpinka</li></ol><p><i>сарпи́нк(а)</i> (sarpínk(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective) rabble, scum (workers, common sailors, etc.)</li></ol><p>Per Trubachyov, from Old East Slavic <i>сара</i> (sara, “sailor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common buzzard</li><li>buzzard (any member of the <i>Buteo</i> family)</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, a Turkic borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;siarïg</i> (“yellow, white”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sasanian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sashimi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>刺身</i> (sashimi, literally “sticking into; slicing + flesh, meat”), from the way the flesh of the fish is sliced.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sassafras (a tree native to Eastern United States and Asia with mitten-shaped leaves and red, aromatic heartwood)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>sassafras</i>, from Spanish <i>sasafrás</i>, of Latin origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mythology,&#32;religion) Satan, devil</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) jerk</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сатана)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satan, Prince of Darkness</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>Сатана</i> (Satana), from Ancient Greek <i>Σατάν</i> (Satán), from Hebrew <i>שָׂטָן</i> (Sātān, “adversary, accuser”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run wild, to go crazy</li></ol><p><i>сатана́</i> (sataná) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satanism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>satanic</li></ol><p><i>сатана́</i> (sataná) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satanist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satay (dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malay or Indonesian <i>sate</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satellite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sateen, satin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) satyr</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) satyrid</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Satyridae.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>satyrida</i>, a back-formation of translingual <i>Satyridae</i> (now obsolete, reclassified as Satyrinae).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satirist (male or female)</li></ol><p><i>сати́ра</i> (satíra) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>satirical</li></ol><p><i>сати́ра</i> (satíra) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satrap, a governor of a province in ancient Persia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) an absolute ruler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>satrapēs</i> (“governor”), from Ancient Greek <i>σατράπης</i> (satrápēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturnalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Saudi (relating to Saudi Arabia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnish <i>sauna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soundtrack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>soundtrack</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safari</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swahili <i>safari</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Morocco leather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) Morocco leather</li></ol><p><i>сафья́н</i> (safʹján) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Morocco leather</li></ol><p><i>сафья́н</i> (safʹján) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sakha (person, people or language)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sakha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>саха́</i> (saqá).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Саха)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sakha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>Саха</i> (Saqa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sakhalin</li></ol><p><i>Сахали́н</i> (Saxalín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sugar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saccharide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) saccharin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>containing sugar or sugarlike</li></ol><p><i>са́хар</i> (sáxar) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton candy, candy floss, fairy floss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sugar beet (type of beet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sugar bowl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sugar</li><li>rich in sugar</li><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) sugary</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sensual</li><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) sweet, pleasant, nice</li></ol><p><i>са́хар</i> (sáxar) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sugar cane (species of grass whose sap is a source of sugar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sucrose, saccharose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>са́хар</i> (sáxar)</li></ol><p><i>са́хар</i> (sáxar) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>herpetology</i>) Sahara frog (<i>Pelophylax saharicus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>саха́рский</i> (saxárskij, “Saharan”) +&lrm; <i>лягу́шка</i> (ljagúška, “frog”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Saharan, relating to the Sahara.</li></ol><p><i>Саха́ра</i> (Saxára, “Sahara”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satsebeli (a Georgian sauce)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>საწებელი</i> (sac̣ebeli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satsivi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>საცივი</i> (sacivi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skive off, to goof off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) butterfly net, hand net, net (for butterflies, insects or small fish)</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) loafer, idler, shirker</li></ol><p>From <i>сак</i> (sak) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sachet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sachet</i>, further from Latin <i>saccus</i> + <i>-ittus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sashimi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>刺身</i> (sashimi, literally “sticking into; slicing + flesh, meat”), from the way the flesh of the fish is sliced.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off <i>(from)</i>, to deduct, to rebate, to discount</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off <i>(from)</i>, to deduct, to rebate, to discount</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-бавля́ть</i> (-bavljátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid (of), to shake off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, harmony</li></ol><p><i>сбаланси́рованный</i> (sbalansírovannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>balanced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to balance, to keep one's balance</li><li>to balance, to equipoise</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>баланси́ровать</i> (balansírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run down (from)</li><li>to run away, to make off, to flee, to break out</li><li>to escape</li><li>to elope</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(to a place)</i> and get back, to run <i>(for)</i>, to go and get</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come running (<i>of many people</i>)</li><li>to get used to running together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run down (from)</li><li>to run away, to make off, to flee, to break out</li><li>to escape</li><li>to elope</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come running (<i>of many people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savings bank (financial institution)</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>сберега́тельный банк</i> (sberegátelʹnyj bank).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passbook, bankbook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>savings</li></ol><p><i>сберега́ть</i> (sberegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to preserve</li><li>to spare</li></ol><p><i>сберег-</i> (sbereg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) savings</li><li>economy, saving</li></ol><p><i>сбере́г-</i> (sberég-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to preserve</li><li>to spare</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savings bank (financial institution)</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>сберега́тельная ка́сса</i> (sberegátelʹnaja kássa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>сберега́тельная кни́жка</i> (sberegátelʹnaja knížka): passbook (booklet used to record bank transactions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whisk, beater</li></ol><p><i>сбива́ть</i> (sbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock down, to shake down, to cause to fall</li><li>to knock down, to beat down</li><li>to put out</li><li>to tread down</li><li>to knock together</li><li>to churn</li><li><i>(eggs, cream)</i> to whisk, to beat, to whip</li></ol><p><i>сбить</i> (sbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-вать</i> (-vatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flounder, to fluff, to get lost (also figuratively)</li><li>to be errant, to stray</li><li>to go astray</li><li>to assemble</li><li>to felt</li></ol><p><i>сбива́ть</i> (sbivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confusing, inconsistent (of speech or a report)</li></ol><p><i>сби(ва́ться)</i> (sbi(vátʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering, errant, wayward</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) sbiten (a hot winter honey-based Russian traditional drink&#47;decoction with spices and jam)</li></ol><p><i>сбить</i> (sbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>down at heel, worn down (<i>of boots</i>)</li><li>battered</li><li>whipped, whisked</li><li><i>in expressions only</i> (<i>of build</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock down</li><li>to cause to fall, to shake down</li><li>to put out</li><li>to tread down</li><li>to knock together</li><li>to churn</li><li><i>(eggs, cream)</i> to whisk, to beat, to whip</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get off</li><li>to be aberrant</li><li>to go afield</li><li>to go astray</li><li>to be errant, to stray</li></ol><p><i>сбить</i> (sbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw/bring together</li></ol><p><i>сбли́зить</i> (sblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach <i>(each other)</i></li><li>to approach, to close in</li><li>to draw closer together</li><li>to become good/close friends <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сближа́ть</i> (sbližátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convergence</li></ol><p><i>сбли́зить</i> (sblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw/bring together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>близ</i> (bliz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach <i>(each other)</i></li><li>to approach, to close in</li><li>to draw closer together</li><li>to become good/close friends <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сбли́зить</i> (sblízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail, to misfunction</li></ol><p><i>сбой</i> (sboj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>failure, fault, error</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) losing the step, falling out of step</li></ol><p>Related to <i>сбива́ть</i> (sbivátʹ, “to knock down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>askance (sideways, obliquely)</li><li>from one side, on one side</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бок</i> (bok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to just happen to say something, to say something silly (or wrong), to blurt out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection, accumulation, gathering, harvest, picking</li><li>levy, tax, duty, receipts</li><li>(<i>military</i>) muster, assembly</li><li>assembly, meeting, get-together</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съборъ</i> (sŭborŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъborъ</i>. Doublet of <i>собо́р</i> (sobór), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assemblage, gathering of people</li><li>mob</li></ol><p><i>сбор</i> (sbor, “collection, gathering”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly (<i>e.g. of devices or machines</i>)</li><li>pleat</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>собра́ть</i> (sobrátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>сбор-</i> (sbor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>сбер-</i> (sber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combined team, national sports team</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>book</i>) collection</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) storage tank, receptacle</li></ol><p><i>сбор</i> (sbor) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>collēctiō</i>, whence <i>колле́кция</i> (kollékcija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>collapsible</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) collection</li><li>combined</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly, rallying</li></ol><p><i>сбор</i> (sbor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assembly (<i>of machines or devices</i>)</li><li>(relational,&#32;colloquial) assembly (gathering of people)</li></ol><p><i>сбо́рка</i> (sbórka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collector</li></ol><p><i>сбор</i> (sbor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw down, to drop</li><li><i>(a government)</i> to throw off</li><li><i>(skin, leaves)</i> to shed</li><li><i>(clothes, shoes)</i> to throw off</li></ol><p><i>сбро́сить</i> (sbrósitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave off</li></ol><p><i>сбрить</i> (sbritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) scum, mob, riffraff, rabble, varletry, ragtag and bobtail</li></ol><p>From сбреда́ться (sbredátʹsja, “to converge in one place”), then <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>броди́ть</i> (brodítʹ, “to wander”), and ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brodìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water escape</li><li>dumping, discharge</li><li>reduction, dropping</li><li>(<i>computer science</i>) reset, resetting, clearance, nulling, nullification, zeroing</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) fault</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) payload dropper, drop device (<i>for a dropper drone</i>)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>сбро́сить</i> (sbrósitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw down, to drop</li><li><i>(a government)</i> to throw off</li><li><i>(skin, leaves)</i> to shed</li><li><i>(clothes, shoes)</i> to throw off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>бро́сить</i> (brósitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse tack (equipment and accessories worn by horses for harnessing or riding)</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Polish <i>zbroja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell, to market</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>сбыть</i> (sbytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come true, to be fulfilled</li></ol><p><i>сбыва́ть</i> (sbyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sale</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>сбыть</i> (sbytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sales</li></ol><p><i>сбыт</i> (sbyt) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) feasible, possible (<i>of a business deal</i>)</li></ol><p>Formed as if from <i>сбы́ток</i> (sbýtok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); <i>сбы́ток</i> (sbýtok) is attested only in Dal's dictionary, as a synonym of <i>сбыт</i> (sbyt, “sale”), which does not have the right meaning. (The lemma is derived from <i>сбы́ться</i> (sbýtʹsja, “to be fulfilled”), not <i>сбыть</i> (sbytʹ, “to sell”).)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell, to market</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get rid of</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come true, to be fulfilled</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-, “perfective prefix”) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ, “to be”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja, “reflexive particle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wedding</li></ol><p><i>сва́дьба</i> (svádʹba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding</li><li>participants in the wedding ceremony as a whole</li><li>wedding anniversary (in the phrases (<i>relational</i>) + <i>сва́дьба</i>)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сватьба</i> (svatĭba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svatьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сват</i> (svat) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Swaziland</li></ol><p><i>Свазиле́нд</i> (Svazilénd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>svaika (traditional Russian throwing game)</li><li>throwing spike (<i>for use in a game of svaika</i>)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) marlinspike</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) awl</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) rod, shaft (penis)</li></ol><p><i>сва́я</i> (svája) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knock down, to knock over, to fell</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drop, to throw down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dump, to carelessly drop (many things in one place)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to get rid of (something unpleasant)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to overthrow</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to deprive of strength, to weaken, to overcome (<i>of sleep, an illness, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to shift the blame for (something to someone), to pass the buck</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to go away, to scram, to beat it, to bolt; to emigrate (temporarily or permanently)</li></ol><p><i>свали́ть</i> (svalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li><li>to be ill in bed</li><li>to collapse</li><li>to mat, to form a mat, to tangle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knock down, to knock over, to fell</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drop, to throw down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dump, to carelessly drop (many things in one place)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to get rid of (something unpleasant)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to overthrow</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to deprive of strength, to weaken, to overcome (<i>of sleep, an illness, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;transitive) to shift the blame for (something to someone), to pass the buck</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to go away, to scram; to emigrate (temporarily or permanently)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вали́ть</i> (valítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li><li>to be ill in bed</li><li>to collapse</li></ol><p><i>свали́ть</i> (svalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dump, junkyard</li><li>dumping</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scuffle, brawl</li></ol><p><i>свали́ть</i> (svalítʹ, “to dump”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quarrel, squabble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svãrъ</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;swer-</i> (“to resound, enunciate, pronounce; to swear”). Cognate with Old Norse <i>swara</i> (“answer”), English <i>answer</i>, <i>swear</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whip up, to throw together, to cook something quickly.</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>варга́нить</i> (vargánitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weld</li></ol><p><i>свари́ть</i> (svarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cook, to boil</li><li>to smelt</li><li>to weld</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сварить</i> (svaritĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvariti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become boiled/cooking</li><li>to weld</li></ol><p><i>свари́ть</i> (svarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welding, weld</li></ol><p><i>свари́ть</i> (svarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peevish, shrewish, grumpy, cantankerous</li></ol><p><i>свар(и́ть)</i> (svar(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welded</li></ol><p><i>свари́ть</i> (svarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) welding</li></ol><p><i>сва́рка</i> (svárka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>welder (one who welds)</li></ol><p><i>свари́ть</i> (svarítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swastika, fylfot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>स्वस्तिक</i> (svastika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) swastika</li></ol><p><i>сва́стика</i> (svástika) +&lrm; <i>-о́н</i> (-ón)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matchmaker, go-between, <i>feminine:</i> <i>сва́ха</i> (sváxa), (<i>colloquial</i>) <i>сва́тья</i> (svátʹja)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) co-father-in-law (father of one’s son-in-law or of one’s daughter-in-law), <i>feminine:</i> <i>сва́тья</i> (svátʹja)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propose as a wife or as a husband (as in matchmaking); to match; to marry off</li></ol><p>From <i>сват</i> (svat). Compare Ukrainian <i>сва́тати</i> (svátaty), Belarusian <i>сва́таць</i> (svátacʹ), Polish <i>swatać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propose oneself as a wife or as a husband, to woo, to court</li></ol><p><i>сва́тать</i> (svátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matchmaking</li><li>courtship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) female matchmaker, go-between</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) co-mother-in-law (the female parent of one of the spouses in relation to parents of another spouse)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile (building foundation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) information, intelligence</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) notice, knowledge</li><li>reduction</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) squaring (of accounts)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) contraction, cramp</li><li>(<i>music</i>) mixing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣдѣниѥ</i> (svěděnije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣдѣниѥ</i> (sŭvěděnije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>versed</li><li>adept</li><li>aware (having knowledge of something)</li><li>conversant</li><li>experienced</li><li>well-grounded</li><li>well-accepted</li><li>well-acquainted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flay (to strip the skin off, to skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freshly dug</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freshly shaved</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freshly squeezed, fresh-pressed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh-frozen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>freshly starched</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>све́жий</i> (svéžij, “fresh”)</li></ol><p><i>све́жий</i> (svéžij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freshness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to freshen, to become fresh</li><li>(<i>of wind</i>) to freshen, to become stronger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh (new or clean)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svěžь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(perfective only,&#32;transitive) to transport, to drive, to convey</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;perfective only,&#32;transitive) to transport there and back, to drive there and back</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transport down, to drive down</li><li>to transport away, to carry away (<i>by vehicle</i>)</li><li>to gather together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) sand wave</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) snowdrift</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Swede (a member of an ancient North Germanic tribe that inhabited what is now central Sweden)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Swede (a person from Sweden)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>све́ять</i> (svéjatʹ).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣи</i>&nbsp;pl (svěi), from Old Swedish <i>swēar</i> and Old Norse <i>svíar</i>. Doublet of <i>швед</i> (šved).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nonstandard&#32;or&#32;dialectal,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>свёкла</i> (svjókla)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cold borsht, usually without meat, usually eaten in the summer</li></ol><p><i>свёкла</i> (svjókla) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beet</li></ol><p><i>свёкла</i> (svjókla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mother of one's husband; mother-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свекрꙑ</i> (svekry), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svekry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ukraine&#32;or&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;sometimes&#32;disrespectful</i>) woman's mother-in-law (<i>husband's mother only</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to itch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svьrběti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bunias, sea rocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сверби́га</i> (sverbíga)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol><p><i>све́ргнуть</i> (svérgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overthrow, to throw down, to dethrone</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-ве́ргнуть</i> (-vérgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sverdlovsk</li></ol><p><i>Свердло́в(ск)</i> (Sverdlóv(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) citizen of Sverdlovsk</li></ol><p><i>Свердло́вск</i> (Sverdlóvsk) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dethronement (the act of being dethroned)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare, to check, to verify [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘against something/someone else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check, to confer, to consult</li></ol><p><i>све́рить</i> (svéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verification, collation</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) revision</li></ol><p><i>све́рить</i> (svéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glitter, sparkle, scintillation, glisten, glister</li><li>flash, flashing (burst of light)</li><li>glint</li><li>flare</li><li>glance</li><li>twinkle</li><li>coruscation</li><li>lambency</li></ol><p><i>сверка́ть</i> (sverkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sparkle, to twinkle</li><li>to glitter</li><li>to glare</li></ol><p>Possibly inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svьrkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ablaze (on fire)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sparkle, to twinkle</li><li>to glitter</li><li>to glare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber beetle, lymexylonid (<i>Lymexylon navale</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drill, to perforate, to bore</li></ol><p><i>сверло́</i> (sverló) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drill, drill bit, auger, bore (carpenter's tool)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свьрдьло</i> (svĭrdĭlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svьrdьlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to displace, to remove</li><li>to roll up</li><li>to curtail, to reduce, to cut down</li><li>to wind up, to bring to a close</li><li>to coagulate</li><li>to turn, to make a turn</li><li>to strip <i>(a screw, thread)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curl up, to roll up, to coil up</li><li>to lessen, to contract</li><li><i>(milk, cream)</i> to turn, to curdle, <i>(blood)</i> to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>сверну́ть</i> (svernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) to lay out (a page)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>верста́ть</i> (verstátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contemporary</li><li>coeval</li><li>age-mate (one who is the same age as another)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съвьрстьникъ</i> (sŭvĭrstĭnikŭ). Compare Old East Slavic <i>съвьрстьнъ</i> (sŭvĭrstĭnŭ, “of the same age”), <i>съвьрсть</i> (sŭvĭrstĭ, “of the same age; coeval, age-mate”), from <i>вьрста</i> (vĭrsta, “age”). Compare Slovene <i>vȓst</i> (“row, type, age”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>) (genitive <i>vrstȋ</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>)), Sanskrit <i>वृत्ति</i>&nbsp;f (vṛttí, “method, essence, nature, gender”). Morphologically <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>верста́</i> (verstá) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above, over, beyond</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>верх</i> (verx, “top, highest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>moreover, besides, additionally (by way of addition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>extra-, over-, super-, ultra-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ultra-long-range</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superpower</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>super-task (extremely difficult or important task)</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>зада́ча</i> (zadáča)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supersonic</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) superacid</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>superacid</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>кислота́</i> (kislotá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superreliable</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) supernova</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) supernova</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suprasociety</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>о́бщество</i> (óbščestvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idiosyncratic, quirky, avant-garde, offbeat, eccentric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superpatriotism</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>патриоти́зм</i> (patriotízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superpopular</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>популя́рный</i> (populjárnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) superprofit, excess profit</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>superprofit</i>; from <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>при́быль</i> (príbylʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) superconductor</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>проводни́к</i> (provodník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) supercluster</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>скопле́ние</i> (skoplénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extremely urgent</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superhard</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superfluidity (frictionless flow of a liquid)</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>теку́чий</i> (tekúčij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supertoxic</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>токси́чный</i> (toksíčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superthin</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above, on top</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over, above</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рху</i> (vérxu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overtime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) overtime</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>уро́к</i> (urók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supersuccessful</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>успе́шный</i> (uspéšnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A very cold temperature.</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-, “super”) +&lrm; <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod, “cold”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) overvalued idea (an unreasonable and sustained belief with an underlying affective component)</li></ol><p><i>сверхценный</i> (sverxcennyj, “overvalued”) +&lrm; <i>идея</i> (ideja, “idea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) superman, overman</li><li>strong personality whose actions and desires do not obey normal restrictions</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) great man</li><li>(journalistic,&#32;Nazism) übermensch, Aryan (member of a proposed German master race)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Übermensch</i>; from <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superhuman</li></ol><p>From <i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>челове́ческий</i> (čelovéčeskij) or <i>сверхчелове́к</i> (sverxčelovék) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supernumerary</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>штат</i> (štat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supernatural</li><li>extraordinary</li></ol><p><i>сверх-</i> (sverx-) +&lrm; <i>есте́ственный</i> (jestéstvennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;entomology) The Grylloidea.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;ornithology) The Locustellidae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>сверчко́вый</i> (sverčkóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cricket (insect)</li><li>grass warbler (<i>Locustella</i>) (bird)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свьрчькъ</i> (svĭrčĭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svьrčь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, to occur</li><li>to come true</li></ol><p><i>сверша́ть</i> (sveršátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>achievement, fulfilment, performance</li></ol><p><i>сверши́ть</i> (sveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fait accompli; done deal</li></ol><p>From <i>сверши́вшийся</i> (sveršívšijsja) +&lrm; <i>факт</i> (fakt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to manage, to control, to direct, to do, to accomplish</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>верши́ть</i> (veršítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, to occur</li><li>to come true</li></ol><p><i>сверши́ть</i> (sveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare, to check, to verify [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘against something/someone else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>све́рить</i> (svéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check (with), to confer (with), to consult (with)</li></ol><p><i>сверя́ть</i> (sverjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protruding or hanging part of something; overhang</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let hang, to let down, to lower</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ве́сить</i> (vésitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean over</li><li>to hang over, to overhang</li></ol><p><i>све́сить</i> (svésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to take (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li><li>to bring together, to throw together</li><li>to reduce (<i>to</i>), to bring (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to remove</li><li>to trace (<i>a picture</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive someone crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get even</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil down <i>(to)</i>, to come <i>(to)</i></li><li>to come off (<i>of a transfer picture</i>)</li></ol><p><i>свести́</i> (svestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light</li><li>day</li><li>radiance</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) lights, lighting</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;colloquial) power, electricity</li><li>world, earth, universe:</li><li>society, people, aristocracy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣтъ</i> (světŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;světъ</i> (“light; world”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple of someone's eye (favourite&#47;favorite, a particular preference, or a loved one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to dawn</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as a term of endearment for a person.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>свет</i> (svet); (little) light</li></ol><p><i>све́т</i> (svét) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavenly body, celestial body, lamp</li><li>luminary, notability</li></ol><p><i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp, light fixture</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣтильникъ</i> (světilĭnikŭ). By surface analysis, <i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ, “to shine, lamp, beam, light”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik). Compare Ukrainian <i>світи́льник</i> (svitýlʹnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) lamp</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) wick</li></ol><p><i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine</li><li>to give someone light</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have a chance</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look at, to face (to be presented or confronted with&#59; to have in prospect)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;světìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to glow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to attract (unwanted) attention</li></ol><p><i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lighten, to become lighter</li><li>to be visible (<i>of a light-coloured/colored object</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brightly</li><li>warmly, with kindness</li></ol><p><i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light blue</li></ol><p><i>све́тл(ый)</i> (svétl(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>голубо́й</i> (golubój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light green</li></ol><p><i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tan (colour)</li></ol><p><i>све́тл(ый)</i> (svétl(yj), “light”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кори́чневый</i> (koríčnevyj, “brown”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light gray</li></ol><p><i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́рый</i> (séryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair-haired</li></ol><p><i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light-skinned</li></ol><p><i>све́тл(ый)</i> (svétl(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ко́ж(а)</i> (kóž(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walleye (Sander vitreus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light (having light), bright</li><li>jolly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;světьlъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>alcoholic beverages</i>) pale ale</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>pale ale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glowworm; firefly, lightning bug</li></ol><p><i>све́тлый</i> (svétlyj, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>светля́к</i> (svetlják)</li></ol><p><i>светля́к</i> (svetlják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retroreflector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) light</li><li>luminous</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light year (astronomical distance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) light-emitting diode, LED</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дио́д</i> (diód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;technology) light-emitting diode, LED</li></ol><p><i>светодио́д</i> (svetodiód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lightproof, light-tight</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>непроница́емый</i> (nepronicájemyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) luminiferous, luminous, light-bringing</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ, “to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;physics) luminiferous aether</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting/illuminating engineering, lighting technology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traffic light</li></ol><p>From <i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; Ancient Greek <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “carrying”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) lamp, luminary, light</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣтꙑчь</i> (světyčĭ). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;světъ</i> (“light”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) society, high-society</li><li>secular, worldly, lay</li></ol><p><i>свет</i> (svet) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>luminous, lambent, lucent</li><li>luminescent</li><li>radiant</li><li>fluorescent</li><li>phosphorescent</li><li>candescent</li><li>agleam</li><li>alight (lit.&#58; on fire, switched on)</li><li>irradiant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) upper room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candle (a light source, usually of wax)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) candela (unit of luminous intensit)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) spark plug / sparking plug</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) suppository</li><li>(<i>ball games</i>) lob</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѣча</i> (svěča, “candle”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svěťa</i> (“candle”), from <i>&#42;světъ</i> (“light”) + <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spark plug</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>свечно́й</i> (svečnój, “candle (<i>relational</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>свеча́</i> (svečá) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glow, luminescence, fluorescence</li></ol><p><i>свети́ть</i> (svetítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>свеча́</i> (svečá)</li></ol><p>From <i>свеч(а́)</i> (sveč(á)) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>све́чка</i> (svéčka)</li></ol><p><i>све́чка</i> (svéčka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>свеча́</i> (svečá): a small candle</li></ol><p><i>свеча́</i> (svečá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candlemaker, chandler</li><li>someone who carries a burning candle (e.g. in a traditional wedding)</li><li>candlestick</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>свѣчьникъ</i> (svěčĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svěťьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>свеча́</i> (svečá, “candle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) candle</li></ol><p><i>свеча́</i> (svečá) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let hang, to let down, to lower</li></ol><p><i>све́сить</i> (svésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean over</li><li>to hang over, to overhang</li></ol><p><i>све́шивать</i> (svéšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind, to twist, to twine</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to swaddle <i>(a child)</i></li></ol><p><i>свить</i> (svitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coil, to roll up</li></ol><p><i>свива́ть</i> (svivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appointment; date</li><li>visit (with a prisoner, patient, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>сви́деться</i> (svídetʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije); see <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) for more on the first component.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) dated form of <i>свида́ние</i> (svidánije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyewitness, witness (one who has a personal knowledge of something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣдѣтель</i> (sŭvědětelĭ). Originally contained the archaic root вѣд- (věd-, “to know”); after regular dissimilation ѣ-ѣ &gt; и-ѣ reanalysed as containing the root вид- (vid-, “to see”), and equivalent to <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) witness, eyewitness</li></ol><p><i>свиде́тель</i> (svidételʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>government certificate</li><li>witness (conceptual)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣдѣтельство</i> (sŭvědětelĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>свиде́тель</i> (svidételʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to testify, to witness, to be evidence (of)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣдѣтельствовати</i> (sŭvědětelĭstvovati). By surface analysis, <i>свиде́тельство</i> (svidételʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) politically conscious (<i>used in a satirical context in relation to Ukrainian nationalists</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>свідомий</i> (svidomyj, “aware, conscious”), from Polish <i>świadomy</i>. It may also be influenced by the word содо́мия (sodómija, “sodomy, homosexuality, perversion”). Compare the semantic development of English <i>woke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigsty, pigpen (shelter where pigs are kept)</li></ol><p><i>свина́р(ня)</i> (svinár(nja)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigsty</li></ol><p><i>свина́рь</i> (svinárʹ, “worker caring for pigs”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead (the metal, the chemical element)</li><li>bullet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свиньць</i> (svinĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svinьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pork</li></ol><p><i>свин(ья)</i> (svin(ʹja)) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá); (little) pig, piggy</li><li>guinea pig</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) mumps (<i>contagious disease</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pig</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pork</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свинꙑ́и</i> (svinýi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svinъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;swīˀnas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-iHnos</i>, from <i>&#42;suH-</i> (“pig, hog, swine”) + <i>&#42;-iHnos</i>.</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>сви(нья́)</i> (svi(nʹjá)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swine flu, swine influenza</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) pig dog, scoundrel</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;Ukraine) a Russian person</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;Ukraine) a Russian soldier who takes part in the 2022 invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá, “pig”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka, “dog”). Compare German <i>Schweinehund</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig farm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>…</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>свин</i> (svin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swinishness, piggishness, piggery, rudeness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dirtiness, flagrant untidiness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) meanness, mean/base nature</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mean/low-down trick</li></ol><p><i>свинья́</i> (svinʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to screw together</li><li>to unscrew</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave, to depart, to go (away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bind, to tie up</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>винти́ть</i> (vintítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) pig</li></ol><p>Attested no later than 1839.</p><p>Possibly a blend of сви́нья (svínʹja) and Latin <i>-tus</i>, from seminary jargon influenced by Latin <i>stultus</i> (“foolish, stupid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lead (metal); leaden</li></ol><p><i>свине́ц</i> (svinéc) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig, hog, sow, swine (mammal of genus <i>Sus</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svinьja</i>. Compare German <i>Schwein</i>, Danish <i>svin</i>, and English <i>swine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to litter, to soil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipe, svirel (musical instrument)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свирѣль</i> (svirělĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svirělь</i>, from <i>&#42;svirati</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ělь</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swer-</i> (“to buzz, hum”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>svirel musician</li></ol><p>From <i>свире́ль</i> (svirélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow furious, to begin to see red</li><li>to become fierce, to become ferocious, to become cruel</li></ol><p><i>свире́пый</i> (svirépyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ferociously, fiercely</li></ol><p><i>свире́пый</i> (svirépyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferocity, savagery, atrociousness</li></ol><p><i>свире́пый</i> (svirépyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) ferocity, savagery, atrociousness</li></ol><p>From <i>свире́пый</i> (svirépyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to rage</li></ol><p><i>свире́пство</i> (svirépstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodthirsty, ferocious, savage</li><li>fierce, furious</li><li>wicked, atrocious, savage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sverěpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) waxwing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>свире́пый</i> (svirépyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>свирепе́ть</i> (svirepétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to hang down, to dangle</li><li>to slouch</li></ol><p><i>сви́снуть</i> (svísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to hang down, to dangle</li><li>to slouch</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ви́снуть</i> (vísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whistle (act of whistling)</li></ol><p>See the verb <i>свистать</i> (svistatʹ, “to whistle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle, to make a whistling sound (<i>without the aid of a whistle</i>)</li><li>to call someone by whistling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat strongly with liquid</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svistati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сви́стнуть</i> (svístnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>all hands on deck (order)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle, to make a whistling sound (<i>with or without the aid of a whistle</i>)</li><li>to sing, to pipe (<i>of birds</i>)</li><li>to whine (<i>of a bullet</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whistle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack, to slap</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to sneak, to pinch, to steal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweysd-</i> (“to hiss”), of imitative origin; see also Ancient Greek <i>σίζω</i> (sízō, “I hiss”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whistle (device used to make a whistling sound, act of whistling)</li></ol><p><i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustle, mad rush</li></ol><p><i>свист</i> (svist, “whistle”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пля́ска</i> (pljáska, “dance”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin whistle, pennywhistle, Irish whistle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whistler</li></ol><p>From <i>свисте́ть</i> (svistétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suite, escort, retinue, entourage</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) suite</li></ol><p>Via Polish <i>świta</i> or German <i>Suite</i> from French <i>suite</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweater (knitted jacket)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sweater</i> with some phonetic changes resulting from a hypercorrect pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll, scroll</li></ol><p><i>свить</i> (svitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind</li><li>to twist, to weave</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coil, to roll up</li></ol><p><i>сви́ть</i> (svítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to dislocate, to put out of joint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go astray</li></ol><p><i>свихну́ть</i> (svixnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom, liberty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свобода́</i> (svobodá), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svobodà</i> (“freedom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom of speech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>freely (without interference or restriction)</li><li>freely, easily (without difficulties)</li></ol><p><i>свобо́дный</i> (svobódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free (not subject to limitations, not subject to rigid control)</li><li>free, unrestricted, free access</li><li>free, vacant, unoccupied, empty, spare</li><li>loose, spacious, loose-fitting</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) free, not bound to other elements, free valence.</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;software&#32;and&#32;artworks</i>) libre</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svobodьnъ</i>. Parsable as <i>свобо́да</i> (svobóda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vault, arch</li><li>(<i>law</i>) code</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvodъ</i>. Synchronically analyzable as deverbal from <i>своди́ть</i> (svodítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead, to take (<i>from somewhere</i>)</li><li>to bring together, to throw together</li><li>to reduce (<i>to</i>), to bring (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to remove</li><li>to trace (<i>a picture</i>)</li><li>to cramp</li><li>to take (<i>somewhere</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive someone crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get even</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil down <i>(to)</i>, to come <i>(to)</i></li><li>to come off (<i>of a transfer picture</i>)</li></ol><p><i>своди́ть</i> (svodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>summary, report</li><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) revise</li></ol><p><i>своди́ть</i> (svodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimp, procurer of women</li></ol><p><i>своди́ть</i> (svodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procuress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to procure, to pander, to pimp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>summary</li><li>step-</li></ol><p><i>своди́ть</i> (svodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepbrother</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willfulness, stubbornness</li><li>arbitrariness</li></ol><p><i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>во́ля</i> (vólja) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>arbitrarily, capriciously, high-handedly</li></ol><p><i>своево́льный</i> (svojevólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>about a person</i>) headstrong, self-willed, wilful, capricious, whimsical, opinionated, high-handed, stubborn</li><li>(<i>about an act</i>) arbitrary (arbitrarily or capriciously done)</li></ol><p><i>свое</i> (svoje) +&lrm; <i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in time</li></ol><p><i>своевре́менный</i> (svojevrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timeliness</li></ol><p><i>своевре́менный</i> (svojevrémennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timely, well-timed; opportune</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(a) kind of, (a) sort of, some kind of, some sort of, somewhat of</li><li>Used other than idiomatically&#58; see <i>своего́</i> (svojevó) and <i>ро́да</i> (róda)&#59; usually means "of its genus".</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wayward, fickle, headstrong</li></ol><p><i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>нрав</i> (nrav) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <b>antonymous</b> Latin <i>mōrigerus</i> (<i>mōs</i> + <i>gero</i>, i.e. **нравоносный, **нравосносящий, сносящий чужой нрав, see also <i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ), <i>сно́сный</i> (snósnyj), <i>несно́сный</i> (nesnósnyj), <i>выноси́ть</i> (vynosítʹ), <i>невыноси́мый</i> (nevynosímyj) (akin to <i>нести́</i> (nestí), <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ))).</p><p>Compare <i>своеобы́чный</i> (svojeobýčnyj) (akin to <i>обы́чай</i> (obýčaj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>originality, peculiarity, singularity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>originally, idiosyncratically (in a new, different, creative manner)</li></ol><p><i>своеобра́зный</i> (svojeobráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>original, idiosyncratic, singular, one of a kind (fresh, new, different, creative)</li><li>reminiscent [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) handwritten</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) handmade</li></ol><p><i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transport down, to drive down</li><li>to transport away, to carry away (<i>by vehicle</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather together</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to transport there and back, to drive there and back</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive-possessive pronoun</i>) one's own; my, his, her, its, our, your, their <i>(always refers to the subject of the clause)</i></li><li>allied, friendly</li><li>peculiar</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свои</i> (svoi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svojь</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin suus, Ancient Greek <i>ἕ</i> (hé), Sanskrit <i><b>स्व</b>तह्</i> (<b>sva</b>taḥ), and perhaps Old English <i><b>se</b>lf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>characteristically</li></ol><p><i>сво́йственный</i> (svójstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inherent, typical (of, for), peculiar (to)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>своиствьнъ</i> (svoistvĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>сво́йство</i> (svójstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property, virtue, quality (attribute or abstract quality associated with an object)</li><li>kind</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) characteristics</li><li>relationship by marriage</li><li>affinity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>своиство</i> (svoistvo), which is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἰδίωμα</i> (idíōma). By surface analysis, <i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p><p><i>свой</i> (svoj) + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) villain, scoundrel</li></ol><p><i>сво́лочь</i> (svóločʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́га</i> (-úga)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag, to trail</li><li>to draw</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;collective) riffraff, rabble</li><li>asshole, scoundrel, drag</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>воло́чь</i> (volóčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pack (a number of hounds or dogs)</li><li>gang</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up</li><li>to curtail, to reduce, to cut down</li><li>to wind up, to bring to a close</li><li>to turn, to make a turn</li><li>to displace, to remove</li></ol><p><i>свороти́ть</i> (svorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curl up, to roll up, to coil up</li><li>to lessen, to contract</li><li><i>(milk, cream)</i> to turn, to curdle, <i>(blood)</i> to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>свора́чивать</i> (svoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) itch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>своробъ</i> (svorobŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svorbъ</i>. Slavic cognates include Czech <i>svrab</i>. Compare with the modern Russian <i>свербе́ние</i> (sverbénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, to snaffle, to thieve</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ворова́ть</i> (vorovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to displace, to remove</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) brother-in-law (husband of wife’s sister)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) relative</li><li>(<i>billiards</i>) cue ball</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svojakъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) sister-in-law (wife's sister)</li></ol><p><i>своя́к</i> (svoják) +&lrm; <i>-еница</i> (-enica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get used to, to get accustomed to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvyknǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-вы́кнуть</i> (-výknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>haughtily, with disdain</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) from on high</li></ol><p>From <i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>высока́</i> (vysoká), apparently an obsolete short-form genitive of <i>высо́кий</i> (vysókij, “high”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above, on top</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) from the above, from God</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above (more)</li></ol><p>From выше, comparative degree of высоко, with с- prefix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constrained (<i>by something</i>)</li><li>halting (<i>of speech</i>)</li><li>connected [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with/to something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) combined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, to bind</li><li>to tie together</li><li>to connect, to join</li><li>to knit, to crochet</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate, to get in contact [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get involved [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>связа́ть</i> (svjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signalman, signaller; postal / telecommunications worker, communication man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binding, tying together</li><li>bunch, sheaf, bundle</li><li>rope team (group of people moving one after the other, as in the mountains, connected together by a safety line)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ligament</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) copula; connective, linking verb (grammar)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) connective</li></ol><p><i>связа́ть</i> (svjazátʹ, “to tie”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coherently, cohesively</li></ol><p><i>свя́зный</i> (svjáznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) communications</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) liaison</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, liaison</li></ol><p><i>связь</i> (svjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coherent, cohesive</li></ol><p><i>связь</i> (svjazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>connecting</li></ol><p>Has the form of a present active participle of a nonexistent verb &#42;связова́ть (compare <i>свя́зывать</i> (svjázyvatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tying up, tying/binding together</li><li>linking, linkage, connecting</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) binding</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) combining, binding, fixation</li></ol><p><i>свя́зывать</i> (svjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie together, to bind</li><li>to connect, to join</li></ol><p><i>связа́ть</i> (svjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate, to get in contact [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get involved [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>свя́зывать</i> (svjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie, relation, bond, contact</li><li>link, connection, liaison</li><li>(<i>military</i>) signal</li><li>communication</li></ol><p><i>связа́ть</i> (svjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>свя́то</i> (svjáto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holiness</li></ol><p><i>святе́йший</i> (svjatéjšij, “holiest”) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) church (in Orthodox Christianity)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) sanctuary (a revered place)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) holy place (in general)</li></ol><p><i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-и́лище</i> (-ílišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prelate</li><li>saint who was an archbishop before being canonized.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to the highest individuals in Russian Orthodox Church hierarchy, or a saint who was an archbishop in life.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmastide; Yuletide; Christmas season</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свꙗтокъ</i> (svjatokŭ) from Old Church Slavonic <i>свѧтъкъ</i> (svętŭkŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>свѧтъ</i> (svętŭ) (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svętъ</i>) + Old Church Slavonic <i>-ъкъ</i> (-ŭkŭ) (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>). Compare Carpathian Rusyn <i>святкы</i> (svjatkŷ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>piously, with awe</li></ol><p><i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holy, sacred (dedicated to a religious purpose)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saint</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѧтъ</i> (svętŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svętъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwen-</i> (“holy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Holy Russian (often relating to the Russian Empire, with respect to the Russian Orthodox Church)</li></ol><p>From <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij, “Russian”), based on <i>Свята́я Русь</i> (Svjatája Rusʹ, “Holy Russia, Holy Rus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness, sanctity</li></ol><p><i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrilege (desecration, profanation, misuse or violation of something sacred)</li></ol><p>From <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тать</i> (tatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Christmastide; Yuletide</li></ol><p>From <i>свя́тки</i> (svjátki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>menologium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrine</li><li>sacred object, something considered sacred, a sanctity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svętyni</i>. By surface analysis, <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest, clergyman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свѧщеникъ</i> (svęščenikŭ) / <i>свѧщеньникъ</i> (svęščenĭnikŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleric, churchman, clergyman, ecclesiastic, minister (of church)</li></ol><p><i>свяще́нный</i> (svjaščénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>служи́тель</i> (služítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness, sanctity</li></ol><p><i>свяще́нный</i> (svjaščénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacred, holy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>свѧщеньнъ</i> (svęštenĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>святи́ть</i> (svjatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj). Doublet of <i>свячёный</i> (svjačónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy synod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) priesthood (the role or office of a priest)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) priesthood (priests, collectively)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) divine power, grace</li></ol><p><i>свяще́н(ник)</i> (svjaščén(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(botany,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) beet (the root plant <i>Beta vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сеѵклъ</i> (sevklŭ), via Byzantine Greek from Ancient Greek <i>σεῦκλον</i> (seûklon), as is Bulgarian <i>цвекло́</i> (cvekló), Macedonian <i>цвекло</i> (cveklo), Serbo-Croatian <i>ци̏кла</i> / <i>cȉkla</i>, Slovak / Czech <i>cvikla</i>, Polish <i>ćwikła</i>, also Hungarian <i>cékla</i> and Romanian <i>sfeclă</i>. Hither belongs also the taxonomic <i>Beta vulgaris</i> var. <i>cicla</i> and Arabic سِلْق (silq).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the father of one's husband; father-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>свекръ</i> (svekrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svekrъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱuros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>свёкор</i> (svjókor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>package, bundle, parcel</li><li>roll (that which is rolled up)</li><li>clot</li></ol><p><i>сверт-</i> (svert-) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rolling up</li><li>curtailment</li><li>coagulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to displace, to remove</li><li>to roll up, to furl</li><li>to curtail, to reduce, to cut down</li><li>to wind up, to bring to a close</li><li>to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>сверт-</i> (svert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curl up, to roll up, to coil up</li><li>to lessen, to contract</li><li><i>(milk, cream)</i> to turn, to curdle, <i>(blood)</i> to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>свёртывать</i> (svjórtyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>свет</i> (svet).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, crimp, fold</li><li>crook (<i>of the arm</i>)</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>сгиба́ть</i> (sgibátʹ, “to bend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve, to crook</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li><li>to bend <i>(down)</i>, to bow <i>(down)</i>, to stoop <i>(down)</i></li><li>to bend, to be flexible</li></ol><p><i>сгиба́ть</i> (sgibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to disappear, to vanish</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ги́нуть</i> (gínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out</li><li>to smooth over, to smooth away</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out</li><li>to smooth over, to smooth away</li></ol><p><i>сгла́дить</i> (sgláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to smooth things out, to smooth things over, to take the edge off things</li></ol><p>Literally, “to round the angles”. Compare French <i>arrondir les angles</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil eye, hex, jinx, whammy (an evil spell)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put the evil eye <i>(on, upon)</i>, to bewitch, to jinx, to put a hex/jinx <i>(on)</i>, to put the whammy on, to put off <i>(by too much praise)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li><li>to gulp down</li></ol><p><i>сглотну́ть</i> (sglotnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swallow</li><li>to gulp down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>глотну́ть</i> (glotnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, to go bad, to putrefy, to decompose, to decay</li><li><i>teeth</i> to become carious</li><li><i>water</i> to stagnate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>гнить</i> (gnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to terms, to come to an agreement</li><li>to plot, to gang up</li></ol><p><i>сговори́ться</i> (sgovorítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collusion, deal, conspiracy, scheme</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) betrothal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to terms, to come to an agreement</li><li>to plot, to gang up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accommodating, tractable, amenable, pliable, appeasable, manageable (willing to respond to persuasion or suggestions)</li></ol><p><i>сговори́ть</i> (sgovorítʹ, “to persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to be useful, to come in handy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>годи́ться</i> (godítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away</li><li>to drive together (cattle, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose (weight)</li><li>to remove (from the surface of)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to move quickly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to cause to go somewhere quickly and come back</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combustion</li></ol><p><i>сгора́ть</i> (sgorátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out, to burn down (intransitive)</li></ol><p><i>сгоре́ть</i> (sgorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hunch</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>го́рбить</i> (górbitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hunch over</li></ol><p><i>сго́рбить</i> (sgórbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hunched, stooped, hunchbacked, hunched-over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn out (to finish burning out), to burn down</li><li>to be burned, be used up</li><li>to burn (<i>intransitive</i>)</li><li>to burn oneself out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rashly, in the heat of the moment, without reflection</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a temper</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>горяча́</i> (gorjačá), the second element apparently an obsolete short-form genitive of <i>горя́чий</i> (gorjáčij, “passionate, hot-tempered”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rake up, to rake together, to shovel up, to shovel in</li><li>to shovel <i>(off, from)</i></li><li>to grab</li></ol><p><i>сгреб-</i> (sgreb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rake up, to rake together, to shovel up, to shovel in</li><li>to shovel <i>(off, from)</i></li><li>to grab</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>грести́</i> (grestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crowd, to bunch</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>гру́да</i> (grúda) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (root-stressed) or <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>гру́да</i> (grúda) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload</li></ol><p><i>сгрузи́ть</i> (sgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unload</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>грузи́ть</i> (gruzítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unloading</li></ol><p><i>сгрузи́ть</i> (sgruzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to group, to classify</li><li>to collect, to concentrate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>группирова́ть</i> (gruppirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to group together, to be grouped, to cluster</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to assume the correct posture (<i>preparatory to a jump, somersault, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сгруппирова́ть</i> (sgruppirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to group, to classify</li><li>to collect, to concentrate</li></ol><p><i>сгруппирова́ть</i> (sgruppirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to group together, to be grouped, to cluster</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to assume the correct posture (<i>preparatory to a jump, somersault, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сгруппиро́вывать</i> (sgruppiróvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wreck, to destroy, to ruin</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>губи́ть</i> (gubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to condense</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curdle, to clot, to thicken, to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>сгусти́ть</i> (sgustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clot</li></ol><p><i>сгусти́ть</i> (sgustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to condense</li></ol><p><i>сгусти́ть</i> (sgustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to curdle, to clot, to thicken, to coagulate</li></ol><p><i>сгуща́ть</i> (sguščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickening, concentration (the act or process of reducing the volume of a liquid)</li><li>condensation, concretion</li></ol><p><i>сгусти́ть</i> (sgustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) condensed milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to season, to flavor</li><li>to fertilize (plants, soil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deliver, to hand in, to hand over</li><li>to return, to turn in</li><li><i>(a fortress, city etc.)</i> to surrender, to yield</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) to deal</li><li>to take/pass <i>(an exam)</i></li><li>to be weakened, to be in a reduced state</li></ol><p><i>сдать</i> (sdatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surrender, to yield</li><li>to give up, to give in</li><li>to give in, to give way</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to seem</li></ol><p><i>сдава́ть</i> (sdavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a constrained voice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crushed, squeezed, compressed</li><li>muffled; stifled (<i>of a sound or the voice</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>сдави́ть</i> (sdavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) delivery</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) surrender</li></ol><p>Logically from <i>сда́ча</i> (sdáča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), but structurally as if from nonexistent <i>&#42;сда́тка</i> (&#42;sdátka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare <i>переда́точный</i> (peredátočnyj) from <i>переда́ча</i> (peredáča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deliver, to hand in, to hand over</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to return, to turn in</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to surrender, to yield</li><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive,&#32;card games) to deal</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to take/pass, to usually about an exam</li><li>to be weakened, to be in a reduced state</li><li>to move back a bit (like while driving)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surrender, to yield</li><li>to give up, to give in</li><li>to give in, to give way</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;in the past tense,&#32;negative constructions) to be necessary, to be needed [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>] (can be used with an interrogative, or the interrogative can be dropped, and the question can be converted into a sentence with the meaning "is not necessary", "is not needed", "screw" etc.; also, often used with <i>э́то</i> (éto))</li></ol><p><i>сдать</i> (sdatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivery (of the final result of a project)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) fighting back, hitting back the offender</li><li>surrender</li><li>change (money given back when a customer hands over more than the exact price of an item)</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) deal</li><li>letting out, leasing out (property; usually followed by <i>внаём</i> (vnajóm))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shift, displacement</li><li>change for the better, progress, improvement</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) displacement, dislocation</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) shear</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>сдви́нуть</i> (sdvínutʹ), based on the underlying stem (compare imperfective <i>сдвига́ть</i> (sdvigátʹ)). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> and <i>&#42;dvigati</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make (<i>someone obstinate or lazy</i>) move, to make someone take actions</li><li>to move objects together</li></ol><p><i>сдви́г-</i> (sdvíg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to budge</li><li>to come/draw together</li><li>to move forward, to get going, to improve</li></ol><p><i>сдвига́ть</i> (sdvigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>displaced, shifted, offset</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crazy, balmy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make (someone obstinate or lazy) move, to make someone take actions</li><li>to move objects together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дви́нуть</i> (dvínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to budge</li><li>to come/draw together</li><li>to move forward, to get going, to improve</li></ol><p><i>сдви́нуть</i> (sdvínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>do-it-yourself</li></ol><p>Verb form <i>сде́лай</i> (sdélaj, “do”, singular imperative) + pronoun <i>сам</i> (sam, “self”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, to do</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>де́лать</i> (délatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>pretend, affect (to make a false display of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propose (to ask for one's hand in marriage) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, to get, to grow, to turn</li><li>to happen (with, to), to be going on</li></ol><p><i>сде́лать</i> (sdélatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deal, bargain</li><li>transaction (the act of conducting or carrying out business, negotiations, plans, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>сде́лать</i> (sdélatʹ, “to make, to do”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with restraint, with reserve, reservedly</li></ol><p><i>сде́ржанный</i> (sdéržannyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restraint, self-restraint</li><li>reserve, moderateness</li></ol><p><i>сде́ржанный</i> (sdéržannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>restrained, reserved (held back, limited, kept in check or under control)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold in, to hold back, to keep back, to restrain, to hold in check, to contain, to deter</li><li>to keep (promise, word)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control/restrain oneself, to hold oneself in check</li></ol><p><i>сдержа́ть</i> (sderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deterrence</li></ol><p><i>сде́рживать</i> (sdérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold in, to hold back, to keep back, to restrain, to hold in check, to contain, to deter</li><li>to keep (promise, word)</li></ol><p>From <i>сдержа́ть</i> (sderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to control/restrain oneself, to hold oneself in check</li></ol><p><i>сде́рживать</i> (sdérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip, to strip off, to scratch off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to charge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to copy, to crib, to plagiarize</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) <i>сдо́ба</i>, relished, seasoned; shortened</li></ol><p><i>сдо́ба</i> (sdóba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to season, to flavor</li><li>to fertilize (plants, soil)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die <i>(of livestock, cattle; battery)</i></li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) to croak (to die)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>до́хнуть</i> (dóxnutʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>здо́хнути</i> (zdóxnuty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to leave, to retreat, to run away after being scared by something</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дри́стнуть</i> (drístnutʹ, “to have diarrhoea”). See <i>дриста́ть</i> (dristátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (people) friends, to cause (people) to become friends</li></ol><p><i>сдружи́ть</i> (sdružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become friends, to make friends (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сдружа́ть</i> (sdružátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (people) friends, to cause (people) to become friends</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дружи́ть</i> (družítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become friends, to make friends (<i>with</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сдружи́ть</i> (sdružítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away, to blow off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crib <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>сдуть</i> (sdutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to get stupid, to become foolish</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дуре́ть</i> (durétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupidly, foolishly</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>дур-</i> (dur-) +&lrm; <i>-у</i> (-u), as if the partitive case of a hypothetical noun <i>&#42;дур</i> (&#42;dur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow away, to blow off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crib <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ). Compare with Ukrainian <i>зду́ти</i> (zdúty, “to blow away”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(animals)</i> to die, to fall</li><li><i>(people)</i> (<i>rude</i>) to die, to peg out, to kick the bucket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be dying</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьz-dyxati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull off</li></ol><p><i>сдёрг-</i> (sdjorg-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>дёрнуть</i> (djórnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>session, sitting, show, showing (duration of a movie, performance etc.), séance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>séance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>keep on</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>себѣ</i> (sebě) or inherited from Old East Slavic <i>собѣ</i> (sobě); both from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sebě</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>auto- (regarding oneself)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prime cost, cost price, net cost</li></ol><p><i>себе-</i> (sebe-) +&lrm; <i>сто́имость</i> (stóimostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cebuano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(reflexive pronoun)</i> myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves (no nominative case)</li><li><i>(reflexive pronoun)</i> oneself</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Perhaps from the blending of Old East Slavic <i>себе</i> (sebe) and <i>сѧ</i> (sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sebe</i> and <i>&#42;sę</i>, respectively.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) selfie</li></ol><p><i>себя́</i> (sebjá, “oneself”) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška), calque of English <i>selfie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>себе́</i> (sebé, “keep on”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sowing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěvъ</i>. See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěti</i> for more information. Compare Ukrainian <i>сів</i> (siv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sevan trout (<i>Salmo ischchan</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sevastopol</li></ol><p><i>Севасто́поль</i> (Sevastópolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>north</li><li>North (<i>northern part of any region</i>)</li><li>Arctic (<i>northern parts of Russia</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěverъ</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>šiaurė</i>, Latin <i>caurus</i> and English <i>shower</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Север)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Severus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aurora borealis, northern lights (an atmospheric phenomenon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>north, northern</li><li>northerly</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arctic Circle (one of the five major circles of latitude)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>northeast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northeast, northeastern</li><li>northeasterly</li></ol><p><i>северо-восто́к</i> (severo-vostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>northwest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northwest, northwestern, northwesterly</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>за́пад</i> (západ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) North Caucasus</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>Кавка́з</i> (Kavkáz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>North American</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>америка́нский</i> (amerikánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) North Atlantic</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>атланти́ческий</i> (atlantíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>North Korean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>North Macedonian (of, or relating to North Macedonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>northerner</li></ol><p><i>се́вер</i> (séver) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) crop rotation (farming practice)</li></ol><p>From <i>сев</i> (sev) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>оборо́т</i> (oborót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sevruga, starry sturgeon</li></ol><p>From Turkic, such as Tatar <i>söyrök</i> (“sturgeon”) or Kumyk (süyrik), both from Old Turkic <i>&#42;sivrüg</i> (“sharpened, pointed”), referring to the snout. See also Turkish <i>sivri</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sevruga</li></ol><p><i>севрю́га</i> (sevrjúga) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>segment</li><li>class (of vehicles)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>segmentum</i>, from Latin <i>secō</i> (“to cut”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>today</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>today, this day</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сего дьне</i> (sego dĭne), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;se dьne</i> / <i>&#42;sego dьne</i>, from <i>*sego</i>, genitive of <i>&#42;sь</i> (“this”) + <i>&#42;dьnь</i> (“day”). Synchronically analyzable as a univerbation of <i>сего́</i> (sevó) +&lrm; <i>дня́</i> (dnjá).</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>сёння</i> (sjónnja) and Ukrainian <i>сього́дні</i> (sʹohódni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) today's, of this day</li><li>today's, present</li></ol><p>From <i>сего́дня</i> (sevódnja) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) seat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ischium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sedan</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Седан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sedan (a town in France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) saddle</li><li>(automotive,&#32;relational) fifth wheel</li></ol><p><i>седло́</i> (sedló) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(hair)</i> to become gray, to turn gray</li></ol><p><i>седо́й</i> (sedój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gray hair</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) great age, old age</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saddle, to put on a halter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddle (seat on an animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>седъло</i> (sedŭlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sedъlo</i>. Cognate to <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) saddleback, saddle, col</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ecclesiastic,&#32;rare,&#32;obsolete) number seven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) week</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sedmica</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>се́дмица</i> (sédmica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>седоволо́сый</i> (sedovolósyj)</li></ol><p><i>седо́й</i> (sedój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>влас</i> (vlas) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gray-haired</li></ol><p><i>седо́й</i> (sedój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grey-crowned goldfinch (<i>Carduelis caniceps</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>седоголо́вый</i> (sedogolóvyj, “having gray hair on the head”) +&lrm; <i>щего́л</i> (ščegól, “goldfinch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey, grey-haired, grey-headed (having grey hair)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) very old, distant, ancient</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѣдъ</i> (sědŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;śědъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passenger in a horse-drawn carriage</li><li>horse rider</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>сѣд(ѣти)</i> (sěd(jěti)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok), by surface analysis, <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) sedum (<i>Sedum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Sedum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seventh part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>седмъ</i> (sedmŭ), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>седмъ</i> (sedmŭ), compared dialectal <i>сёмый</i> (sjómyj). From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sedmъ</i> (“seventh”), from earlier <i>&#42;sebdmъ</i> &lt; <i>&#42;septmъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;septmas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septmós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>season (quarter of a year)</li><li>(<i>television</i>) season (a group of episodes of a television or radio program)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>saison</i>, from Latin <i>satiōnem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seasonal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) season</li></ol><p><i>сезо́н</i> (sezón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic,&#32;literary,&#32;humorous) this, this here</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic,&#32;literary,&#32;humorous) this one, this one here</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сь</i> (sĭ, “this”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sь</i> (“this”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;śis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱís</i>. Doublet of <i>сий</i> (sij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. Also compare to Ukrainian <i>цей</i> (cej).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieidi, among the Sami people, a special site where thanks and offerings are given to the spirits</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Northern Sami <i>sieidi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sale (sale of goods at reduced prices)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seiner (type of fishing boat)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>seiner</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seismic</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>σεισμός</i> (seismós) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seismograph (instrument that detects and records earthquakes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seismology (the study of the vibration of the Earth's interior)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthquake protector</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safe (box in which valuables can be locked for safekeeping)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>safe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now (presently, at present, at the moment)</li><li>now (very recently in the past)</li><li>now (very shortly)</li><li>immediately, right away, at first glance</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually with a rushed pronunciation such as <i>щас</i> (ščas) or <i>ща</i> (šča)) hold on, one sec, wait</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>сей</i> (sej, “this”, now archaic) + <i>час</i> (čas, “hour; time”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) of now, current</li></ol><p><i>сейча́с</i> (sejčás) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) coco-de-mer (<i>Lodoicea maldivica</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сейше́льский</i> (sejšélʹskij, “Seychellois”) +&lrm; <i>па́льма</i> (pálʹma, “palm tree”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Seychellois</li></ol><p><i>Сейше́лы</i> (Sejšély) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sec, second</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) seka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) pruning shears and/or loppers; secateurs.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sécateur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cleaver</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bill, billhook (<i>cutting tool</i>)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) a tusker; an adult male boar, hog or seal with large lower tusks or canine teeth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѣкачь</i> (sěkačĭ), from <i>сѣкати</i> (sěkati, “to cut down, to chop off”) + <i>-ачь</i> (-ačĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) sequestration</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) sequestrum</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) sequester, across-the-board cut</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sekwestr</i>, from Latin <i>sequestrum</i> (“sequester”), from <i>sequester</i> (“intermediary”); see English <i>sequester</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) redwood, sequoia (tree)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sequoia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) clitoris</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sь̀kyľь</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Novgorodian <i>сѣкꙑле</i> (sěkyle) (document № 955 in the birch bark records of Novgorod); and Ukrainian <i>си́кель</i> (sýkelʹ), Ukrainian <i>се́кель</i> (sékelʹ), and Ukrainian <i>се́кіль</i> (sékilʹ), all of which are also archaic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any kind of battle-axe on a long haft and with a broad half-moon blade</li><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) axe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>секꙑра</i> (sekyra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sekyra</i>, related to <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) second-hand shop (<i>almost exclusively of shops specialising in clothing</i>)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>секонд-хе́нд</i> (sɛkond-xɛ́nd).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) second-hand good (used item, chiefly clothing or footwear, sold as such)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) second-hand shop (shop that specialises in selling second-hand goods)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>postpositive</i>) used, secondhand, pre-owned</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>second-hand</i>. Compare German <i>secondhand</i> and Romanian <i>second-hand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret</li><li>(<i>military</i>) ambush</li><li>secretion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sēcrētus</i>, from <i>sēcernō</i> (“separate, part, reject”).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sēcrētiō</i>, from <i>sēcernō</i> (“separate, part, reject”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secretariat (the office or department of a government secretary)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the wife of a secretary</li></ol><p>From <i>секрета́рь</i> (sekretárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secretary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sekretarz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secretary (type of desk)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>secrétaire</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be secretive</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk (secretly) to someone in such a way so that nobody else could hear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly (in secret)</li></ol><p><i>секре́тный</i> (sekrétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secrecy</li></ol><p><i>секре́тный</i> (sekrétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret</li><li>clandestine (secret; covert, as a clandestine organization)</li><li>covert</li></ol><p><i>секре́т</i> (sekrét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) sex (sexual intercourse)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sex bomb, bombshell (someone who is very attractive)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>sex bomb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sex shop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;colloquial,&#32;obsolete) sexy babe, sexy female</li></ol><p><i>сексапи́л</i> (sɛksapíl, “sex appeal”) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sexy, lush (beautiful, having sexual appeal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sex appeal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sexdecillion (10<sup>51</sup>, or 10<sup>96</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sexy (having sexual appeal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sexy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sexist</li></ol><p><i>секси́ст</i> (sɛksíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexologist (<i>male or female</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sixth (musical interval)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sextant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sextans</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sextillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>21</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexual orientation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sexually (in a sexual manner, with respect to intercourse)</li><li>sexy</li></ol><p><i>сексуа́льный</i> (sɛksuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) sexual violence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sexuality (concern with or interest in sexual activity)</li></ol><p><i>сексуа́льный</i> (sɛksuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sexual</li><li>sexy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>sexualis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sect, a religious group professing beliefs outside the accepted norm</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a cult, a group, often isolated from others, professing narrow, idiosyncratic views or interests</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sectarian, cultist</li></ol><p><i>се́кта</i> (sékta) +&lrm; <i>-а́нт</i> (-ánt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sector</li><li>sphere, branch, department</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sector</i>, from Latin <i>secō</i> (“to cut”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>сѣщи</i> (sěšti, “to cut, hack, chop off”) and Old English <i>saga</i> (English <i>saw</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaza Strip (a region in Palestine between Egypt and Israel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secularism</li></ol><p>From English <i>secularism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second (unit of time)</li><li>A second (unit of angle)</li><li>A second (musical interval)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a short amount of time</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>secunda</i>, feminine singular of <i>secundus</i>; ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second (attendant of a duel or boxing match standing in for a contestant)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) mediator, intermediary, middleman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) second (unit of time)</li><li>lasting one second, momentary</li></ol><p><i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stopwatch (timepiece)</li></ol><p><i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мер</i> (mer). Parallel with Polish <i>sekundomierz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda, “second (<i>of time</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wait a moment</li><li>stop!</li><li>(<i>now&#32;rare</i>) right (especially as first words in the sentence)</li></ol><p>Accusative case of <i>секу́ндочка</i> (sekúndočka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jiffy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section</li><li>wall unit</li><li>breakup group</li><li>hobby group, hobby circle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) celeb, star, celebrity</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>селе́брити</i> (selébriti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Seleucid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drake, male duck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>селезень</i> (selezenĭ), cognate to Ukrainian <i>се́лезень</i> (sélezenʹ) and dialectal Serbo-Croatian <i>slezen</i>, <i>slezenjak</i>, <i>slezenka</i>, <i>slezinka</i>, <i>slezenjača</i>, <i>sljez</i>, <i>šjez</i> (“drake”). The earlier history is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spleen, milt (organ)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;selzenь</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Belarusian <i>селязёнка</i> (sjeljazjónka), Ukrainian <i>селезі́нка</i> (selezínka), Serbo-Croatian <i>слезина</i>, Czech <i>slezina</i>, Polish <i>śledziona</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>selectively</li></ol><p><i>селекти́вный</i> (selektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selective</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>selektiv</i> or French <i>sélectif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selector, selector switch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or related to a selector</li></ol><p><i>селектор</i> (selektor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breeder (someone involved in breeding)</li></ol><p><i>селекцио́н-</i> (selekción-) +&lrm; <i>-е́р</i> (-ér)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selection (process or act of selecting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selenium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, colony; village, hamlet</li></ol><p>From <i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) selenium</li></ol><p><i>селе́н</i> (selén) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selenology</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>Selenologie</i>; see also <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) saltpetre (potassium nitrate, sodium nitrate)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>селитра</i> (selitra) (since 1596), from Old Ruthenian <i>селитра</i> (selitra) (since 1565, whence Ukrainian <i>селітра</i> (selitra)), from earlier <i>салитра</i> (salitra) (att. in 1517 in Skaryna's Proverbs). Further origin unclear but akin to Middle Polish <i>saletra</i> (whence Old Ruthenian <i>салетра</i> (saletra) and Belarusian <i>салетра</i> (saljetra)), Old Polish <i>sanletra</i>, Old Czech <i>salnitr</i>, <i>sanitr</i>, <i>sanytr</i>, Bavarian <i>Salitter</i>, Middle High German <i>saliter</i>, <i>salniter</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>са̀литра</i>, Spanish <i>salitre</i>, Catalan <i>salnitre</i>, Latin <i>sal nitrum</i> (which is from <i>sal</i> and <i>nitrum</i>, see there for further etymology).</p><p>Displaced earlier Middle Russian <i>ямчуга</i> (jamčuga) during the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to lodge</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to take up one's residence</li></ol><p><i>сели́ть</i> (selítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>село</i> (selo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;selo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>selfie (photographic self-portrait)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>selfie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-harm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>self-harm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landslide, mudslide, mudflow (a geological disaster)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic سَيْل (sayl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tropical rainforest (typically in South America); Amazon rainforest, selva</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>selva</i> or Portuguese <i>selva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) herring</li></ol><p><i>сельдь</i> (selʹdʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>selderij</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) celery</li></ol><p><i>сельдере́й</i> (selʹderéj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herring (<i>Clupea</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сельдь</i> (selĭdĭ) (1497), from earlier <i>&#42;сьлдь</i> (&#42;sĭldĭ), from Old Norse <i>síld</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>селядзе́ц</i> (sjeljadzjéc), Ukrainian <i>оселе́дець</i> (oselédecʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dressed herring salad ("herring under fur coat")</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) herring</li></ol><p><i>сельдь</i> (selʹdʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) a village shop, store</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rural cooperative (organization or store)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>се́льское потреби́тельское о́бщество</i> (sélʹskoje potrebítelʹskoje óbščestvo, “rural consumer association”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electrical engineering</i>) selsyn, synchro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>selsyn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) village</li><li>rural, country (relating to less-populated, non-urban areas)</li><li>agricultural</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сельскъіи</i> (selĭskyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sèlьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>село́</i> (seló, “village”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, adjective-forming suffix). Cognate with Belarusian <i>се́льскі</i> (sjélʹski) and Ukrainian <i>сільськи́й</i> (silʹsʹkýj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agriculture (the art or science of cultivating the ground)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agricultural, farming</li></ol><p>From <i>се́льский</i> (sélʹskij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хозя́йство</i> (xozjájstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj), based on <i>се́льское хозя́йство</i> (sélʹskoje xozjájstvo, “agriculture”, literally “agricultural enterprise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village Soviet, village council</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>се́льский сове́т</i> (sélʹskij sovét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agricultural enterprise</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сельскохозя́йственное предприя́тие</i> (selʹskoxozjájstvennoje predprijátije, “agricultural enterprise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) agricultural products</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сельскохозя́йственная проду́кция</i> (selʹskoxozjájstvennaja prodúkcija, “agricultural products”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agricultural producer, grower</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сельскохозя́йственный производи́тель</i> (selʹskoxozjájstvennyj proizvodítelʹ, “agricultural producer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>село́</i> (seló)</li></ol><p><i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>countryman (a person living in a rural area)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fellow-villager</li></ol><p><i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustic (person from a rural area)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;seľaninъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>село́</i> (seló) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) herring (<i>Clupea</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сельдь</i> (selʹdʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>селёдка</i> (seljódka); herring</li></ol><p><i>селёдка</i> (seljódka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) elongated plate used for fish, especially herring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) herring</li></ol><p><i>селёдка</i> (seljódka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) semantics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semantic (relating to semantics or the meanings of words)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>sémant(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semaphore, signal post</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) semaphore</li></ol><p><i>семафо́р</i> (semafór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>семья́</i> (semʹjá): (little) family</li><li>pejorative of <i>семья́</i> (semʹjá, “family”)</li></ol><p><i>семья́</i> (semʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>family, domestic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěmьjьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>семья́</i> (semʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth (as a symbol for home or family life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family, household</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) family</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěmьjьstvo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>семья́</i> (semʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mince <i>(along)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seed</li><li>seminal, spermatic</li></ol><p><i>се́мен-</i> (sémen-) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) seminal vesicle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) seed lobe, cotyledon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective number</i>) seven (in a group together), seven of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semester, term (half of school year)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>semestre</i>, from Latin <i>sēmēstris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a problem simple to solve, a task, or challenge that is considered very easy, trivial, or insignificant for someone to handle, a child's play, nothing, a breeze, a cinch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>се́мя</i> (sémja): seed</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) sunflower seeds</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) child's play, something very easy to do</li></ol><p><i>се́мя</i> (sémja, “seed”) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko, “(usually endearing diminutive suffix)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly intelligent, rocket scientist</li></ol><p>Literally, “of seven feet in the forehead”, based on the idea that one's intelligence is proportional to the size of one's forehead.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a group of seven powerful Russian business oligarchs who played an important role in the political and economical life of Russia between 1996 and 2000</li></ol><p>From <i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>банки́р</i> (bankír) +&lrm; <i>-щина</i> (-ščina), by analogy with <i>семибоя́рщина</i> (semibojárščina). Literally means "the group of seven bankers".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>athletics</i>) heptathlon</li></ol><p>From <i>семь</i> (semʹ, “sveen”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>боро́ть(ся)</i> (borótʹ(sja), “to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) heptahedron</li></ol><p>From <i>семи-</i> (semi-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “seven-side-er”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seventy-year period</li><li>seventieth anniversary, seventieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>семи́десяти</i> (semídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seventy-year</li><li>seventy-year-old</li></ol><p><i>семи́десяти</i> (semídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seven-day</li></ol><p><i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seven-year period</li><li>seventh anniversary, seventh birthday</li></ol><p><i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seven-year period</li><li>seven-year plan</li><li>seven-year school, grade school (ages 7–13)</li><li>seven-year-old child, child of seven</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seven-year</li><li>seven-year-old</li></ol><p><i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seminar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) seminar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sēminārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seminary (theological school)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sēminārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semiotics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>семистру́нная гита́ра</i> (semistrúnnaja gitára): seven-string guitar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seven-string (of a guitar)</li></ol><p><i>семи</i> (semi) +&lrm; <i>стру́нный</i> (strúnnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Semite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Semitic</li></ol><p><i>семи́т</i> (semít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heptagon</li></ol><p><i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-copeck coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seed (colloq.; especially edible sunflower seed)</li></ol><p><i>се́мя</i> (sémja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seventeen-year period</li><li>seventeenth anniversary, seventeenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>семна́дцать</i> (semnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seventeen-year</li><li>seventeen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>семна́дцать</i> (semnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heptadecagon</li></ol><p><i>семна́дцать</i> (semnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventeenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventeen (17)</li></ol><p>From <i>семь</i> (semʹ, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>17×, seventeen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sample</li></ol><p>First attested in 1993. Borrowed from English <i>sample</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sampler</li></ol><p>First attested in 1989. Borrowed from English <i>sampler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sampler, sampling</li></ol><p><i>се́мплер</i> (sɛ́mpler) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sampling</li></ol><p>First attested in 1993. Borrowed from English <i>sampling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;neologism) to sample</li></ol><p>First attested in 1992. From <i>семпл</i> (sɛmpl) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven (7)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;septḿ̥</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>семь</i> (semĭ), <i>седмь</i> (sedmĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sedmь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septḿ̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seven Wonders of the World</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy (70)</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>семь</i> (semʹ) +&lrm; <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ), literally “seven tens”. <b>Де́сят</b> was the Old East Slavic genitive plural, which is required by numbers <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “5”) and up.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-eight (78)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-two (72)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-nine (79)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-one (71)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-five (75)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-seven (77)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-three (73)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-four (74)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy-six (76)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven hundred (700)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>7×, seven times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>70×, seventy times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѣмиꙗ</i> (sěmija, “family, household; husband, wife”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěmьja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>family man</li></ol><p><i>семья́</i> (semʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seed (<i>also figurative</i>)</li><li>semen</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сѣмѧ</i> (sěmę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěmę</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sḗˀmen</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;séh₁mn̥</i>, whence English <i>seed</i> and <i>semen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ejaculatory</li></ol><p><i>се́мя</i> (sémja) +&lrm; <i>выбра́сывающий</i> (vybrásyvajuščij), present active participle of <i>выбра́сывать</i> (vybrásyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ejaculatory duct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seminiferous</li></ol><p><i>се́мя</i> (sémja) +&lrm; <i>вынося́щий</i> (vynosjáščij), present active participle of <i>выноси́ть</i> (vynosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vas deferens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) seed lobe, cotyledon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) ejaculation</li></ol><p><i>се́мя</i> (sémja) +&lrm; <i>изверже́ние</i> (izveržénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>achene (small dry fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(digit)</i> seven</li><li>group of seven</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) seven</li><li>No 7 bus, tram, etc</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>senātus</i>, from <i>senex</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>senātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senate, senatorial</li></ol><p>From <i>сена́т</i> (senát, “senate”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saint Bernard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>saint-bernard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) tundra</li></ol><p>Uncertain. From a non-Slavic language. First attested in 1810 in Kamchatka. Later attested through the whole Northern Siberia and Arkhangelsk-area.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) the spirit of tundra</li></ol><p>From <i>сендуха</i> (senduxa) +&lrm; <i>-ной</i> (-noj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Senegalese</li></ol><p><i>Сенега́л</i> (Senegál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seneschal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sénéchal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in older log houses</i>) hall, vestibule, entrance hall (a room between the porch and the habitable part of a house where people leave their boots and overcoats&#59; especially in rustic homes. May also be used for storage purposes, also as a room for pets and even livestock and humans)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) railroad carriage platform, tambour</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>сень</i> (senʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěnь</i>. Compare to Belarusian <i>сені</i> (sjeni) and Ukrainian <i>сіни</i> (siny)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) hayloft</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) hay seller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any heath or ringlet butterfly of the genus <i>Coenonympha</i>.</li></ol><p><i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hay</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) vestibule, lobby, entrance hall</li></ol><p><i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p><p><i>се́ни</i> (séni) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěno</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;śáina</i>. Compare to Lithuanian <i>šienas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hayloft</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вал</i> (val).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) A psocopteran, an insect of the order Psocoptera.</li></ol><p><i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haymaking</li><li>hayfield</li></ol><p>From <i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-кос</i> (-kos), combining form <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ, “to mow, to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvestman, daddy longlegs (arachnid)</li></ol><p><i>сеноко́с</i> (senokós) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mower</li></ol><p><i>се́но</i> (séno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensational</li></ol><p><i>сенса́ция</i> (sensácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensation (widespread excitement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensei (a martial arts instructor)</li><li>a Japanese (language) teacher.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>先生<rp>(</rp><rt>せんせい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>sensei</i>, “teacher; elder”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensor (device that perceives touch)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) person with skin sensitivity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sensor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) touch</li><li>sensory</li></ol><p><i>се́нсор</i> (sénsor, sɛ́nsor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p>From <i>се́нсор</i> (sénsor, sɛ́nsor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>сентя́брь</i> (sentjábrʹ) or <i>сентя́бря</i> (sentjábrja, “September”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maxim, dictum, wise sentence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sententia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сенте́нция</i> (senténcija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sentimentality</li></ol><p><i>сентимента́льный</i> (sentimentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sentimental</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>sentimental</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>September</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;septḿ̥</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сентѧбрь</i> (sentębrĭ), <i>семтѧбрь</i> (semtębrĭ), <i>септѧбрь</i> (septębrĭ), <i>септѧбръ</i> (septębrŭ), <i>септѧмбрь</i> (septęmbrĭ), via Old Church Slavonic <i>септѧбр҄ь</i> (septębrʹĭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>Σεπτέμβριος</i> (Septémbrios) from Latin <i>september</i> (“seventh month”), from <i>septem</i> (“seven”).</p><p>See also <i>седьмо́й</i> (sedʹmój), Ukrainian <i>ве́ресень</i> (véresenʹ, “September”), Old East Slavic <i>врѣсень</i> (vrěsenĭ, “September”), <i>рюѥнъ</i> (rjujenŭ), <i>рюинъ</i> (rjuinŭ, “September”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) September</li></ol><p><i>сентя́брь</i> (sentjábrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>се́ни</i> (séni)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canopy (in church)</li><li>(poetic,&#32;obsolete) foliage, umbrage</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) shade, shelter</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сѣни</i> (sěni, “shade, shelter”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) seigneur</li><li>señor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>seigneur</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>señor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>señora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>señorita (courtesy title for an unmarried woman in the Hispanophone country)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>señorita</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сеньо́р</i> (senʹór)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separatism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separatist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separatist (advocating or seeking the separation of a country or territory)</li></ol><p><i>сепарати́ст</i> (separatíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>septendecillion (10<sup>54</sup>, or 10<sup>102</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>septendecillion (10<sup>54</sup>, or 10<sup>102</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>septet (group of seven musicians performing together&#59; composition for such a group)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;septḿ̥</td></tr></table></p><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>septem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>septillion (1,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>24</sup>)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;septḿ̥</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seventh (musical interval)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;septḿ̥</td></tr></table></p><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>septem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic</i>) vicious circle in a definition</li></ol><p>A neologism found in Stanisław Lem.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulphur, sulfur</li><li>brimstone</li><li>earwax, cerumen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) seraph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old East Slavic and Old Church Slavonic <i>серафимъ</i> (serafimŭ), from Koine Greek <i>σεραφείμ</i> (serapheím), from Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׂרָפִים</i> (śərāp̄îm), the plural form of <i>שָׂרָף</i> (śārāp̄, “burning one”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Серафим)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Serafim</li></ol><p>Same as <i>серафим</i> (serafim)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>серафим</i> (serafim).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any requiem shark of the genus <i>Carcharhinus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “gray”) +&lrm; <i>аку́ла</i> (akúla, “shark”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) spotted flycatcher (<i>Muscicapa striata</i>)</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “gray”) +&lrm; <i>мухоло́вка</i> (muxolóvka, “flycatcher”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any vole in the genus <i>Microtus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “grey”) +&lrm; <i>полёвка</i> (poljóvka, “vole”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serb, Serbian (person of Serbian descent)</li><li>Sorb, Sorbian, Wend (person of Wendish descent)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrbъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Serb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Serbo-Croatian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Serbian</li></ol><p><i>Се́рбия</i> (Sérbija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Serbo-Croatian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serbo-Croatian language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Serbo-Croatian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serbo-Croatian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) server</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>се́рвер</i> (sérver) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>furniture</i>) sideboard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saveloy (a seasoned pork sausage, normally purchased ready cooked)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) server (a computer or a program which provides services to other programs or users)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>server</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, set</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>service</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set (the table)</li><li>to serve (food, drinks, etc.)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) to serve</li></ol><p>French <i>servir</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p><p>English <i>serve</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting (the table)</li><li>table setting</li><li>table decoration during a meal</li></ol><p><i>сервирова́ть</i> (servirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, hospitality</li><li>quality service</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) service center</li><li>(computing,&#32;informatics) service, tools</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>service</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) service, servicing</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) service, utility</li></ol><p><i>се́рвис</i> (sérvis, sɛ́rvis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce): (small) heart; a heart symbol/character</li></ol><p><i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inner part (<i>of a machine</i>), core (<i>of an electromagnet</i>)</li><li>bittercress</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cardiologist</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) heart-disease sufferer</li></ol><p><i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coalroot bittercress, <i>Cardamine bulbifera</i></li></ol><p><i>серде́чник</i> (serdéčnik, “bittercress”) +&lrm; <i>лу́ковичный</i> (lúkovičnyj, “bulbous”), a calque of translingual <i>Cardamine bulbifera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cordially, heartily</li></ol><p><i>серде́чный</i> (serdéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cardiovascular</li></ol><p><i>серде́чный</i> (serdéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сосу́дистый</i> (sosúdistyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindness, sincerity, cordiality</li></ol><p><i>серде́чный</i> (serdéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heart</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) cordial, heartfelt</li><li>cardiac</li><li>amorous</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) love</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrdьčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>angrily, in a cross manner</li><li>scowlingly</li></ol><p><i>серди́тый</i> (serdítyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrditъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>сярдзі́ты</i> (sjardzíty), Ukrainian <i>серди́тий</i> (serdýtyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make angry, to anger, to rile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrditi (sę)</i>. Compare сердце (The heart was understood to be the seat of anger).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry</li></ol><p><i>серди́ть</i> (serdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenderhearted, compassionate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) heart</li><li>temper</li><li>anger</li><li>darling, love, sweetheart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сьрдьце</i> (sĭrdĭce), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrdьce</i> (“heart”), distantly related to English <i>heart</i> and <i>core</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heartbeat</li><li>heart palpitation</li></ol><p><i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heart-shaped</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>core, pith, heart</li><li>kernel (central part of a nut)</li></ol><p><i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ви́на</i> (-vína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) core, kernel</li></ol><p><i>сердцеви́на</i> (serdcevína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a very attractive man, a heartthrob, a womanizer</li></ol><p><i>сердце</i> (serdce, “heart”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silverization</li></ol><p><i>серебри́ть</i> (serebrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silvery green</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silver-grey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silvery, silver (color/colour)</li></ol><p><i>серебро́</i> (serebró) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coat with silver, to silver-plate</li></ol><p><i>серебро́</i> (serebró) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become silvery</li></ol><p><i>серебри́ть</i> (serebrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrebro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siver coin</li></ol><p><i>серебр(о́)</i> (serebr(ó), “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>серебряный</i> (serebrjanyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silver</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) melodious, sonorous, high-pitched</li></ol><p><i>серебро́</i> (serebró) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low&#32;colloquial</i>) Wednesday</li><li>(<i>low&#32;colloquial</i>) middle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>середа</i> (sereda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serda</i>. Doublet of <i>среда́</i> (sredá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle (centre, midpoint)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serdina</i>. Parsable as <i>середа́</i> (seredá) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) middle, located in the middle</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) average, mediocre</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wishy-washy, half-hearted</li></ol><p><i>середи́на</i> (seredína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serdьňь</i>. Doublet of <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij), which was borrowed from Old Church Slavonic. By surface analysis, <i>середа́</i> (seredá) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Russia&#32;or&#32;Soviet Union,&#32;historical) a peasant owing agricultural means but not exploiting others, in the Lenin-Stalin terminology someone between a poor peasant - <i>бедня́к</i> (bednják) and a rich exploiter, a kulak - <i>кула́к</i> (kulák)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>середня́к</i> (serednják)</li></ol><p><i>середня́к</i> (serednják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serenade (serenade)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “gray”)</li><li>dull, murky</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become gray</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to become gloomy</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to fade (<i>e.g. of talent</i>), to become commonplace</li><li>(third person only,&#32;imperfective only) to appear (<i>of gray things</i>)</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) sergeant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sergent</i> (“sergeant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sergeant (military rank)</li></ol><p><i>сержа́нт</i> (seržánt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>series (television program)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serial, (<i>relational</i>) series</li></ol><p><i>се́рия</i> (sérija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serial killer (person who commits multiple murders)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>series (a number of related things coming in sequence)</li><li>(<i>television</i>) episode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>seriēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamois, gemsa</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сьрна</i> (sĭrna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrna</i>, cognate to Latvian <i>sirna</i>, Latin <i>cervus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) sulfuric acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sulfur; sulfuric</li></ol><p><i>се́ра</i> (séra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(humorous,&#32;colloquial) of unknown color</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “grey”) + <i>бу́рый</i> (búryj, “brown”) + <i>мали́новый</i> (malínovyj, “crimson, raspberry”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue-gray; gray and light blue</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “gray”) +&lrm; <i>голубо́й</i> (golubój, “light blue”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey-green</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj, “grey”) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj, “green”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slightly gray</li><li>somewhat unremarkable</li><li>somewhat uneducated</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) hydrogen sulfide (H<sub>2</sub>S)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) serodiagnosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serological</li></ol><p><i>сероло́гия</i> (serológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) serology</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>Serologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dullness, grayness, drabness</li></ol><p><i>се́рый</i> (séryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serotonin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle</li><li>crescent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrpъ</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>срьпъ</i> (srĭpŭ, “sickle”), Bulgarian <i>сърп</i> (sǎrp), Czech <i>srp</i>, Polish <i>sierp</i>, Yiddish <i>סערפּ</i> (serp); and with Latvian <i>sirpis</i>, Greek <i>ἅρπη</i> (árpi), Latin <i>sarpō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) hammer and sickle (Soviet symbol of industry and agriculture)</li></ol><p>Literally, “sickle and hammer”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) streamers, confetti</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) winding mountain road</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>serpentin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) August</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>серпьнъ</i> (serpĭnŭ, “August”). By surface analysis, <i>серп</i> (serp) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ) with серп (serp, “sickle”) referring to harvesting. Cognates include Czech <i>srpen</i> (“August”), Polish <i>sierpień</i>, and Serbo-Croatian <i>srpanj</i> (“July”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serpopard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw-wort (<i>Serratula</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>From <i>серп</i> (serp) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Serpukhov</li></ol><p><i>Се́рпухов</i> (Sérpuxov) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certification (right)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certification (verbal nominal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to certify</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry</li></ol><p>From a root related to <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce, “heart”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey, gray</li><li>ashen (<i>of a face</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dull, dreary</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unremarkable, mundane, mediocre</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dim, ignorant, uneducated</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) not quite legal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) wolf</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) cop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;śěrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>éminence grise, power broker (person having the ability to influence important decisions, power behind the throne)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earring</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>серьга</i> (serĭga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) seriousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj): most serious</li></ol><p><i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get serious, to become serious</li></ol><p><i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>seriously, gravely, earnestly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>indeed, really</li></ol><p><i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seriousness, earnestness</li><li>gravity, severity</li></ol><p><i>серьёзный</i> (serʹjóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>serious, earnest</li><li>serious, grave, important</li><li>serious, strong, formidable</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sérieux</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) middle</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) guts, innards</li></ol><p><i>середа́</i> (seredá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>серьга́</i> (serʹgá): earring</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) ament</li></ol><p><i>серьга́</i> (serʹgá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sotho (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>session, sitting</li><li>(<i>law</i>) term</li><li>examinations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) nurse</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swésōr</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сестра</i> (sestra), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sestra</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swésōr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) sister’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sisterly</li></ol><p>From <i>сестра́</i> (sestrá) +&lrm; <i>-инский</i> (-inskij), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sisterhood</li></ol><p><i>сестра́</i> (sestrá) +&lrm; <i>-инство</i> (-instvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сестра́</i> (sestrá): sister, sis; little sister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сестра́</i> (sestrá): sis, sister</li></ol><p><i>сестра́</i> (sestrá) +&lrm; <i>-и́чка</i> (-íčka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сестра́</i> (sestrá): sis, little sister</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be imprisoned</li><li>to become flat, to die (<i>of an electrical battery or accumulator</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сѣсти</i> (sěsti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěsti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sḗˀstei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tennis</i>) set</li><li>(<i>poker</i>) three of a kind</li><li>set, combination, collection (for example, of photos, cosmetics, and other items)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>set</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сет)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Set (an ancient Egyptian god)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound card</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) network</li><li>(relational,&#32;electrotechnology) power, mains</li></ol><p><i>сеть</i> (setʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net, netting</li><li>mesh, gauze</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) net</li><li>string bag, shopping net</li><li>luggage rack (<i>in a train</i>)</li><li>grid, graticule</li><li>scale</li><li>reticulum, bonnet, the second compartment of the stomach of a ruminant</li></ol><p><i>сеть</i> (setʹ, “net”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complaining, lamentation</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) complaint</li></ol><p><i>се́товать</i> (sétovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lament, to complain <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѣтовати</i> (sětovati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sětovati</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>се̏та</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>се́тка</i> (sétka, “grid, mesh, network”)</li></ol><p><i>се́тка</i> (sétka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setter (breed of dog)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>setter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retina (thin layer of cells at the back of the eyeball)</li></ol><p><i>се́тчатый</i> (sétčatyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mesh; reticulate, retecious, retiary</li></ol><p><i>сеть</i> (setʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net (used for catching fish), mesh</li><li>grid</li><li>network, system</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sětь</i> (“net, snare”). Cognate with Upper Sorbian <i>syć</i>, Polish <i>sieć</i>, Czech <i>síť</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>сѣть</i> (sětĭ), Lower Sorbian <i>seś</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internet user</li></ol><p><i>сеть</i> (setʹ, “the Net; Internet”) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) battle</li></ol><p><i>сечь</i> (sečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>сі́ча</i> (síča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section, cut, sectional view</li></ol><p><i>сечь</i> (sečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) February</li><li>(archaic,&#32;Ukraine) January</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěťi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>сі́чень</i> (síčenʹ, “January”), Polish <i>styczeń</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>siječanj</i>, and Macedonian <i>сечко</i> (sečko, “February”). Related to сечь (sečʹ, “to cut, chop, hew”), referring to the cutting and chopping of wood at this time of year.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chopping knife, cleaver</li><li>chopped straw, chaff</li><li>ground cereal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut (up), to chop, to hew, to slash to pieces; to cleave</li><li>to flog, to whip, to lash (to whip or scourge someone or something as punishment)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to catch, to latch on</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to know something <i>(about)</i>, to know the ropes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěťi</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>sjȅći</i>, <i>сје̏ћи</i>, Slovene <i>sẹ́či</i>, Bulgarian <i>сека́</i> (seká), Belarusian <i>сячы́</i> (sjačý), Ukrainian <i>сі́кти</i> (síkty), Slovak <i>siecť</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>syc</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>sec</i>, Polabian <i>sect</i>, Czech <i>síct</i> and Polish <i>siec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seeder, sowing machine</li></ol><p>From <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seedling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sower</li><li>disseminator</li></ol><p><i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose, to squander</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to spread (an idea, fear etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take pity [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘on whom’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жаль</i> (žalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction (reduction in size or activity)</li><li>compression, pressing</li><li>1K17 Szhatie (Soviet self-propelled laser vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>сжать</i> (sžatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>air, etc.</i>) condensed, compressed</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) compressed</li><li>clenched</li><li>concise, brief, succinct</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to squeeze</li><li>to hold tight</li><li>to narrow (down)</li><li>to reap, to crop</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contract, to shrink</li><li>to be clenched</li><li>to be compressed (of gas or liquid)</li></ol><p><i>сжа́ть</i> (sžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn</li><li>to burn up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, incineration (the act of burning, incinerating)</li></ol><p><i>сжига́ть</i> (sžigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn down, to burn out (to cause to be consumed by fire)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-жига́ть</i> (-žigátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquefied natural gas</li></ol><p>Calque composed of сжиженный (sžižennyj, “liquified”) + природный (prirodnyj, “natural”) + газ (gaz, “gas”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compressibility</li></ol><p>From <i>сжима́ем(ый)</i> (sžimájem(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to squeeze</li><li>to hold tight</li><li>to narrow (down)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink, to contract</li><li>to compress</li><li>to contract, to clench</li></ol><p><i>сжима́ть</i> (sžimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reap, to crop</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oust</li><li>to force out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accustom himself</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(from) behind</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>зад</i> (zad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) behind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) B (musical note)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) ti (musical note)</li><li>(<i>English language</i>) C, c (letter of the Latin alphabet)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Си)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a transliteration of the Mandarin surname <i>習</i>&nbsp;/ <i>习</i> (Xí)</li><li>A particular high-level programming language from which many others are derived.</li></ol><p>From Mandarin 習／习 (<i>Xí</i>).</p><p>From English <i>C</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) B major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) B minor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) B-flat major</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siamese cat (mammal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Siamese</li></ol><p><i>Сиа́м</i> (Siám) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Xi'an</li></ol><p>From <i>Сиа́нь</i> (Siánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) sea bass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sea bass</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) anthrax</li></ol><p>Literally, “Siberian disease”, because in prerevolutionary Russia anthrax was predominantly spread in Siberia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Siberian</li></ol><p><i>Сиби́рь</i> (Sibírʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberian salamander (Salamandrella keyserlingii)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a resident of Siberia, a Siberian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;rare) sibling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sibling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaborgium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;singular only) Siberian punk (usually before mid-1990s)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>сибирский</i> (sibirskij) + <i>панк</i> (pank).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) north wind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěverъ</i>, with dialectal development of &#42;ě.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) north wind</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) cold weather brought by the north wind</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) northern slope of a mountain</li></ol><p><i>си́вер</i> (síver) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a grey horse</li></ol><p>From <i>си́вый</i> (sívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Compare typologically <i>бурёнка</i> (burjónka) (&lt; <i>бу́рый</i> (búryj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw vodka</li></ol><p><i>си́вый</i> (sívyj) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea lion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) raw vodka (сивуха)</li></ol><p><i>сиву́ха</i> (sivúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gray, grey (of ash)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sivъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A whitefish of the family Coregoninae, including:</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>сигъ</i> (sig) attested in 1496, borrowed from Old Novgorodian <i>сиге</i> (sige). Ultimately from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;siika</i>, of unknown further origin. Compare also Swedish <i>sik</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>sik</i> and Latvian <i>sīga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cig (cigarette)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>сигаре́та</i> (sigaréta, “cigarette”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сига)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shiga (a prefecture of Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>滋賀</i> (しが, Shiga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a leap</li></ol><p><i>сига́ть</i> (sigátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigar (rolled bundle of tobacco made for smoking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cigare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cigarette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cigarette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сигаре́та</i> (sigaréta)</li></ol><p><i>сигаре́та</i> (sigaréta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cigarette</li></ol><p><i>сигаре́та</i> (sigaréta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сига́ра</i> (sigára) (<i>colloquial</i>) cigar, cigarette</li></ol><p><i>сига́ра</i> (sigára) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cigar</li></ol><p><i>сига́ра</i> (sigára) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cigarlike</li></ol><p><i>сига́ра</i> (sigára) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jump, to leap</li></ol><p>Probably ikanye for <i>сяга́ть</i> (sjagátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Coregonus</i> (a genus under the Salmonidae family)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigillography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>синкли́т</i> (sinklít)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σύγκλητος</i> (súnklētos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сигкли́т</i> (sigklít).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigma (the Greek letter Σ&#47;σ&#47;ς)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) sigma (standard deviation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σῖγμα</i> (sîgma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) fansign (photograph of a person with an inscription)</li></ol><p>From English <i>sign</i> + <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarm (instance of an alarm ringing or clanging)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signalling, alarm (system), warning (system) (mechanical contrivance for awaking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to signal, to indicate</li><li>to warn</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>signalisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to signal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to honk (a horn)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to indicate desire to turn left or right using turn signal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to indicate hazard on the road using high-beam</li></ol><p><i>сигна́л</i> (signál) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) signal</li></ol><p><i>сигна́л</i> (signál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coregoninae (a subfamily that includes whitefishes and ciscoes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сиг</i> (sig).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female caretaker, carer, (sick)-nurse (one who takes care of a person)</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ, “to look after”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting</li><li>seat</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lazybones, idler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be imprisoned, to do time</li><li>to stay at home (with), to look after [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to stay (<i>in a physical location</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stay (<i>in a virtual location by using something computer-based</i>)</li><li>to use a tool or be occupied with, to sit at (<i>usually in a sitting position</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘an activity’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѣдѣти</i> (sěděti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěděti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sēdḗˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be willing to sit, to stay (in one place)</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sidonian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cider</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cider</i>, from Latin <i>sicera</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σίκερα</i> (síkera), of Semitic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sitting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sedentary, sitting, sit-down, with seats</li><li>sessile</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)</li></ol><p><i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cig (cigarette)</li></ol><p><i>сига</i> (siga) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Syzygium</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Syzygium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sisyphean, relating to Sisyphus</li></ol><p><i>Сизи́ф</i> (Sizíf, “Sisyphus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сизифов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sisyphus's</li></ol><p><i>Сизи́ф</i> (Sizíf, “Sisyphus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sisyphean labour, Sisyphean task</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat dove-coloured/colored, bluish grey</li></ol><p><i>си́з(ый)</i> (síz(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) European roller</li></ol><p><i>си́зый</i> (sízyj, “blue-gray”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>воро́на</i> (voróna, “crow”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having dove-colored wings</li></ol><p><i>си́з(ый)</i> (síz(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>крыл(о́)</i> (kryl(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dove-coloured/colored, warm grey, bluish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сизъ</i> (sizŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sizъ</i>.</p><p>Possibly cognate to <i>си́вый</i> (sívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycamore (<i>Platanus occidentalis</i> and <i>Acer pseudoplatanus</i>)</li><li>water elm (<i>Planera aquatica</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycamore (<i>Platanus occidentalis</i> and <i>Acer pseudoplatanus</i>)</li><li>water elm (<i>Planera aquatica</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sequel</li></ol><p>First attested in 1992. Borrowed from English <i>sequel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycomore (<i>Ficus sycomorus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycomore (<i>Ficus sycomorus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>συκοφάντης</i> (sykofántis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength</li><li>force</li><li>power, might</li><li>vigor</li><li>intensity</li><li>efficacy</li><li>energy</li><li>volume</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sila</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;séiˀlāˀ</i>. Cognates include Lithuanian <i>si̇́ela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willpower (strength of will)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strong man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;си́лачь</i> (&#42;sílačĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sìlačь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Silesian (relating to Silesia, a region spanning parts of Poland, Germany and the Czech Republic)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Silesian language</li></ol><p><i>Силе́зия</i> (Silézija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) silica gel (a granular, porous form of silica made synthetically from sodium silicate and used as a desiccant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silicosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silicone</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) breast implant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silicone</li></ol><p><i>силико́н</i> (silikón) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try, to make efforts</li></ol><p><i>-си́лить</i> (-sílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) silicon</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>silicium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) by force, forcibly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) syllogism (argument whose conclusion is supported by two premises, of which one contains the term that is the predicate of the conclusion, and the other contains the term that is the subject of the conclusion)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>συλλογισμός</i> (sullogismós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Perm) alternative form of <i>сило́к</i> (silók)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sidlo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) silovik, securocrat</li><li>law enforcement officer, intelligence officer, security service agent, soldier (someone belonging to a nation's law enforcement, security, or military forces)</li><li>(<i>boxing</i>) slugger</li></ol><p><i>силово́й</i> (silovój, “force-based, using troops”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) strength-based</li><li>(relational,&#32;physics) force</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) power (energy-producing)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) force-based, using troops</li></ol><p><i>си́ла</i> (síla) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare, loose, clap-net</li></ol><p><i>сило</i> (silo) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), further from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sidlo</i>, akin to <i>сеть</i> (setʹ). The relation with <i>си́ла</i> (síla) (mentioned by Dahl) is secondary (folk etymology).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) silage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>silos</i> (“silos”), plural of <i>silo</i> (“silo”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silhouette</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>silhouette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>си́льно</i> (sílʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>си́льно</i> (sílʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strongly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) intensively, actively</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) impressively, convincingly</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to a significant degree, very much, hard, badly</li></ol><p><i>си́льный</i> (sílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) strong interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) strong nuclear interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;sometimes&#32;ironic) the great and the good, the powers that be</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, powerful</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) irregular</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;silьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>си́ла</i> (síla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symbiosis (relationship of mutual benefit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symbol</li><li>(computing,&#32;typography) character, glyph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σύμβολον</i> (súmbolon). Occurrence of /v/ indicates a relatively late borrowing.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) creed (basic tenets of faith)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to symbolize</li></ol><p>Ultimately from New Latin <i>symbolizāre</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symbolism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>symbolisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symbolism, symbolics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) Symbolist (proponent of Symbolism)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>symboliste</i>, influenced by <i>си́мвол</i> (símvol).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>symbolic</li></ol><p><i>си́мвол</i> (símvol) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>symbolic</li></ol><p><i>си́мвол</i> (símvol) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>си́мвол</i> (símvol).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) SIM card</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>си́м-карта</i> (sím-karta)+&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>symmetric, symmetrical</li></ol><p><i>симметри́я</i> (simmetríja) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symmetry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>symmetria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>συμμετρία</i> (summetría), from <i>σύμμετρος</i> (súmmetros, “symmetrical”), from <i>σύν</i> (sún, “with”) + <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simony</li></ol><p>Entered via French <i>simonie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) simp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>simp</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in sympathy <i>(with)</i>, to sympathize <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>sympathisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>attractively, pleasantly</li></ol><p>From <i>симпати́чный</i> (simpatíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nice, attractive, pleasant, pretty, handsome, cute</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>sympathique</i>, from <i>симпатия</i> (simpatija) +&lrm; <i>-ичный</i> (-ičnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favour/favor; attraction; sympathy</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) sweetheart; darling; flame (<i>a person who is liked or desired</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sympathie</i> or Polish <i>sympatia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleasant person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>symposium (conference)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>симпати́чный</i> (simpatíčnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) See симпатичный.</li></ol><p>From симпотичный, misspelled variant of симпатичный.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) symptom</li><li>indication, sign, symptom</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>σύμπτωμα</i> (súmptōma, “a happening, accident, symptom of disease”), from stem of <i>συμπίπτω</i> (sumpíptō, “Ι befall”), from <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) + <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō, “I fall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bush sunflower (<i>Simsia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Simsia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to simulate</li><li>to feign an illness or disability, especially in clinical or legal context; to malinger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>simulieren</i>, from Latin <i>simulō</i> and German <i>-ieren</i>, with the Russian suffix <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). Morphologically симул- + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malingerer</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the oblique stem of Latin <i>simulāns</i>, perhaps via obsolete German <i>Simulant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simulative, simulator, simulated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simulation</li><li>malingering</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>simulātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) symphony</li><li>symphonic</li></ol><p><i>симфо́ния</i> (simfónija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) symphony</li><li>concordance (type of alphabetical index)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>symphōnia</i> or Ancient Greek <i>σῠμφωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (sŭmphōnĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) symphorock, symphonic rock</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>симфо́ния</i> (simfónija) +&lrm; <i>ро́к</i> (rók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synagogue</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>сѵнаго́га</i> (sünagóga), from Ancient Greek <i>σῠνᾰγωγή</i> (sŭnăgōgḗ, “assembly, gathering”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) synapse (junction between neurons)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>σύναψις</i> (súnapsis, “conjunction”), from <i>συνάπτω</i> (sunáptō, “to clasp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sinhalese</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinhalese (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Singaporean</li></ol><p><i>Сингапу́р</i> (Singapúr) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) single</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>single</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) singularity</li></ol><p><i>сингуля́рный</i> (singuljárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) singular, unique</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>singulāris</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syndicate, combine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolidation of several enterprises into syndicates</li></ol><p>From <i>синдици́ровать</i> (sindicírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije), from синдика́т (sindikát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) syndrome (complex of symptoms)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>συνδρομή</i> (sundromḗ, “concurrence of symptoms, concourse”), from <i>σύνδρομος</i> (súndromos, “running together”), from <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) and <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “running, course”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) Asperger's syndrome (autism-related developmental disorder)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;medical genetics) Turner syndrome</li></ol><p>Named after American&#32;endocrinologist&#32;Henry Turner, who first described the condition in 1938.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tourette syndrome</li></ol><p>Named after French&#32;physician&#32;Georges Gilles de la Tourette.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;medical genetics) Turner syndrome (<i>literally, “Shereshevsky-Turner syndrome”</i>)</li></ol><p>Named after American&#32;endocrinologist&#32;Henry Turner, who first described the condition in 1938, however, the condition was already discovered already by the Soviet endocrinologist Nikolai Shereshevsky in 1925 before Turner's discovery.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) acquired immune deficiency syndrome, AIDS</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sindhi <i>(language)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue green</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj). Cognates with Ukrainian <i>си́ньо-зеле́ний</i> (sýnʹo-zelényj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue colour/color, blue</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) clear sky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dark) bluish</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij) +&lrm; <i>-ева́тый</i> (-evátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blue-eyed</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i></li></ol><p>From <i>синегно́йный</i> (sinegnójnyj, “deep-blue pus”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>па́лочка</i> (páločka, “bacillus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;ornithology</i>) (deep) blue-headed</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij, “deep blue”) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) synecdoche (certain figure of speech)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) sinecure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sinekure</i>, from Latin <i>sine</i> (“without”) + <i>cūra</i> (“care”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cinematography</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cinema, movie theatre</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) cinematograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>си́ний</i> (sínij, “deep blue, indigo”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine) eggplant, aubergine</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) blue-eyed</li></ol><p><i>си́н(ий)</i> (sín(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́к(о)</i> (ók(o)) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synergy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>συνεργεῖα</i> (sunergeîa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(prosody,&#32;phonetics) synaeresis (contraction of two vowels into a diphthong or a long vowel)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>συναίρεσις</i> (sunaíresis, “unification”). Doublet of <i>синере́зис</i> (sinerézis, “synaeresis; separating out of the liquid from a gel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) synaeresis (separating out of the liquid from a gel)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>συναίρεσις</i> (sunaíresis, “unification”). Doublet of <i>синере́за</i> (sineréza, “synaeresis; contraction of two vowels into a diphthong or a long vowel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn/become blue</li><li><i>(skin)</i> to turn blue/livid</li><li>to appear/show blue</li></ol><p><i>си́ний</i> (sínij) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>film</i>) movie fan; cinephile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cinéphile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>синизе́са</i> (sinizésa, “synizesis”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>синизе́са</i> (sinizésa, “synizesis”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(prosody,&#32;phonetics,&#32;poetry) synizesis (the fusion of two syllables into one without the formation of a recognized diphthong, especially to fit a certain poetic meter)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>συνίζησις</i> (sunízēsis, “a sitting together”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep blue</li><li>indigo</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) dead (<i>sense transferred from the bluish color of a dead person's face</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) drunken, drunk (<i>also acts as noun</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) being a thief belonging to the Russian mafia (<i>from the blue color of the tattoos that cover his body</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;siňь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue whale (<i>Balaenoptera musculus</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “blue whale”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tit, titmouse, tomtit (any bird of the family Paridae)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sinica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shinkansen (a Japanese high-speed train)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>新幹線</i> (shinkansen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) synclite (specially summoned council or assembly of elders in classical Greece)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) synclite (senate in the context of the Byzantine Empire, or one of the states in its sphere of influence which adopted similar institutions)</li><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) synod</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) meeting; gathering</li></ol><p>Phonologically adapted borrowing of Ancient Greek <i>σύγκλητος</i> (súnklētos), displacing earlier <i>сигкли́т</i> (sigklít).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>синкли́т</i> (sinklít).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syncope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syncretism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sinningia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Sinningia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synoglyph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σύνοδος</i> (súnodos, “assembly, meeting”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synodal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>synodal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinology, Chinese studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) synonymous</li></ol><p>From <i>сино́ним</i> (sinónim, “synonym”) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>синоними́я</i> (sinonimíja, “synonymy”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synopsis</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>σύνοψις</i> (súnopsis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch of meteorology concerned with weather forecasting</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>συνοπτικός</i> (sunoptikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinophobia (<i>the fear of China or the Chinese</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syntagma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguiistics</i>) syntax (set of rules that govern how words are combined)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>syntactic, syntactical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synthesis (formation of something complex or coherent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) synthesizer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to synthesize</li></ol><p><i>си́нтез</i> (síntɛz) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synthetics (<i>materials, products, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>synthetic</i> or German <i>Synthetik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synthetic</li></ol><p><i>синте́т(ика)</i> (sintɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shinto (religion)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) sine</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sinus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) sinusitis</li></ol><p><i>си́нус</i> (sínus) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinusoid, sine wave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) sinusoidal</li></ol><p>From <i>синусо́ида</i> (sinusóida) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synchronization</li></ol><p><i>синхрониз(и́ровать)</i> (sinxroniz(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to synchronize</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from German <i>synchronisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simultaneous interpreter, conference interpreter</li></ol><p>From <i>синхро́нный</i> (sinxrónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synchrony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>synchronie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>synchronously, in a synchronous manner</li></ol><p><i>синхро́нный</i> (sinxrónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) synchronized swimming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synchronous (at the same time)</li></ol><p><i>синхрони́я</i> (sinxroníja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>translation studies</i>) simultaneous interpreting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) synchrotron</li></ol><p>Internationalism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) synchrotronic</li></ol><p>From <i>синхротро́н</i> (sinxrotrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) synchrophasotron (type of synchrotron, a type of particle accelerator, especially one operating at the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research in Dubna from 1957 to 2003)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue colour</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) clear sky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bluing (blue pigment used for coloring clothes when washing)</li><li>blueprint (paper-based reproduction usually of a technical drawing), diazo print, ammonia print</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drunkenness, drinking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Italy</i>) signore, signor, Mr</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>signor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cyanosis</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) Jacob’s ladder (<i>Polemonium</i> spp.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) bruise (medical: mark on the skin)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Echium</i>, a genus of flowering plant in the family Boraginaceae</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a hard drinking person, drunkard</li></ol><p>From <i>синий</i> (sinij) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>си́ний</i> (sínij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zionism</li></ol><p>From <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm), a calque of German <i>Zionismus</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>сіоні́зм</i> (sionízm), Belarusian <i>сіяні́зм</i> (sijanízm), Polish <i>syjonizm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zionist (advocate of Zionism)</li></ol><p>From <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist), a calque of German <i>Zionist</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>сіоні́ст</i> (sioníst), Belarusian <i>сіяні́ст</i> (sijaníst), Polish <i>syjonista</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;uncommon) Zionistic:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>relating to Zionism</li><li>relating to Zionists</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>сиони́ст</i> (sioníst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij). Compare Ukrainian <i>сіоністи́чний</i> (sionistýčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zionist; Zionistic:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>relating to Zionism</li><li>relating to Zionists</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>сиони́ст</i> (sioníst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Ukrainian <i>сіоні́стський</i> (sionístsʹkyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy) Zion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сионовъ</i> (sionovŭ). By surface analysis, <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>сіо́новий</i> (siónovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zion</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zionism</li></ol><p>From <i>Сио́н</i> (Sión) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij). Compare Ukrainian <i>сіо́нський</i> (siónsʹkyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li><li>husky voice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak hoarsely</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be hoarse</li><li>to hiss (<i>from heat</i>)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>husky (a voice)</li></ol><p><i>си́плый</i> (síplyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>muffled and hoarse (<i>of a voice</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сип(е́ть)</i> (sip(étʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become husky/hoarse</li></ol><p>Compare <i>си́плый</i> (síplyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sire (in medieval France and England)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mythology,&#32;animate) siren</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) siren (device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air raid siren, civil defense siren (attack on a ground target by military aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lilac (<i>color</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сире́нь</i> (sirénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lilac (<i>Syringa</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) that is, in other words, i.e.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Syrian (relating to Syria)</li></ol><p>From <i>Си́рий-</i> (Sírij-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sirocco (hot and humid southeast to southwest wind)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>scirocco</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syrup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sirop</i>. Related to <i>щербет</i> (ščerbet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sirota</i>, from <i>&#42;sirъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ota</i>. By surface analysis, <i>си́рый</i> (síryj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become an orphan, to be orphaned</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to become deserted (<i>of a location</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сирота́</i> (sirotá) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сирота́</i> (sirotá, “orphan”)</li></ol><p><i>сирота́</i> (sirotá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) orphan; orphan's</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) poor, miserable</li><li>(relational,&#32;dated) orphan care</li></ol><p><i>сирота́</i> (sirotá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphanhood</li></ol><p><i>сирота́</i> (sirotá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>сиро́ты</i> (siróty, “orphans”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) orphaned, lonely, deserted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сиръ</i> (sirŭ), <i>сирꙑи</i> (siryi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sirъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сири́ец</i> (siríjec).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сири́йский</i> (siríjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admin, sysop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) diminutive of <i>си́ська</i> (sísʹka): titty</li></ol><p><i>си́ська</i> (sísʹka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>system</li><li>(<i>martial arts</i>) systema (Russian martial art)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>systēma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σύστημᾰ</i> (sústēmă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Braille</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>systematizer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>systematization (act of systematizing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to systematize, to methodize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>systematisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>systematization</li><li>(life sciences,&#32;uncountable) taxonomy (the science of finding, describing, classifying and naming organisms)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>systematically (in an organized manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>systematic, methodical</li><li>regular</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>systematisch</i> + <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) system</li></ol><p><i>систе́ма</i> (sistéma) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) systole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) boob</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beer can</li></ol><p>Possibly, inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sisa</i> with -ка (-ka) suffixed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crystalline glass, glassceramic, pyroceramic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitar</li></ol><p>From Hindi <i>सितार</i> (sitār) or Urdu سِتار (sitār), both from Persian سه‌تار.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chintz, calico, printed cotton (fabric)</li></ol><p>From Dutch <i>sits</i> +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), from Hindi <i>छींट</i> (chī̃ṭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(London) city</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;English-speaking countries</i>) city, downtown (business part of a city)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>city</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush (<i>Juncus</i> of any kind or in general)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) white bread particularly from wheat</li><li>someone who works with sieves</li></ol><p>Already in the XI<sup>st</sup> century Old East Slavic <i>ситникъ</i> (sitnikŭ) and ситие (sitije), also сѣтникъ (sětnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sitъ</i>. Depending on the etymology of that word, the bread-name is either the same word or rather <i>си́то</i> (síto, “sieve”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik) because of being a bread made of sifted starch.</p><p><i>си́то</i> (síto) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сито</i> (sito), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sito</i> (“sieve”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citron (a French sweet citrus kind of lemonade popularized in Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>citron</i> (“lemon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ad hoc (for this particular purpose)</li></ol><p><i>ситуацио́нный</i> (situaciónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) situation; situational</li></ol><p><i>ситуа́ция</i> (situácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>situation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sytuacja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) printed cotton, calico, chintz</li></ol><p><i>си́тец</i> (sítec) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) syphilis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syphilitic, a person who has syphilis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siphon, siphon trap (a bent pipe or tube with one end lower than the other)</li><li>soda siphon</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) siphon (organ found in aquatic mollusks)</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) siphon (ancient weapon used by the Byzantine navy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicilian (native or inhabitant of Sicily, Italy) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, this very minute, instantly, at once</li><li>just a moment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, this very second, instantly, at once</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>momentary, of the moment</li><li>immediate, instant</li></ol><p><i>сию́ мину́ту</i> (sijú minútu) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radiance, twinkle, shine</li></ol><p><i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Excellency</li></ol><p><i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to beam</li><li><i>(facial expression)</i> to shine, to beam, to be radiant</li><li>to shine, to be resplendent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sijati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shining, sheeny</li><li>radiant, refulgent</li><li>alight ((figuratively) lit&#59; on fire, burning)</li><li>aglitter</li><li>fulgent</li><li>lambent</li><li>auroral</li><li>aglare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) splinter</li></ol><p>Cognate with Lithuanian <i>skabarga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Southern Russia</i>) splinter</li></ol><p>From <i>ска́ба</i> (skába) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Belarusian and Ukrainian <i>ска́бка</i> (skábka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indecent, unseemly</li></ol><p>From French <i>scabreuse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(let's) say, (let's) assume</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tale</li><li>lay</li><li>skaz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legend; saga; story</li></ol><p><i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, to tell</li></ol><p>Perfective form of archaic Russian <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kazati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affect [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to manifest itself in, to show [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘an effect’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pretend to be (<i>by reporting</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary genre</i>) tale; fairy tale</li><li>lies, fabrication</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a report (information describing events)</li></ol><p><i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>ска́зка</i> (skázka, “tale; lie”)</li></ol><p><i>ска́зка</i> (skázka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storyteller, writer of fairy tales or fables</li><li>artist, composer or director who creates works derived from fairy tales</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) liar</li></ol><p><i>ска́зка</i> (skázka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magical</li><li>fairy-tale (<i>character, plot</i>)</li><li>fabulous, fantastical, unbelievable</li></ol><p><i>ска́зка</i> (skázka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) predicate</li></ol><p>Nominalised neuter passive participle of <i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ) or <i>ска́зывать</i> (skázyvatʹ), older alternative form of <i>ска́зываемый</i> (skázyvajemyj). Calque of Latin <i>praedicātum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to utter, to narrate</li></ol><p><i>сказа́ть</i> (skazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to affect [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to manifest itself in, to show [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <i>or</i> instrumental <small>‘an effect’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pretend to be (<i>by reporting</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ска́зывать</i> (skázyvatʹ, “to tell, to say (<i>poetic</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) gallop</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jump rope, skipping rope</li></ol><p><i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ, “to hop, to jump”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ride (on) a horse</li><li>to hop, to jump (about)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skakati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racehorse</li></ol><p><i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́н</i> (-ún)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, cliff, crag (mass of projecting rock)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skala</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ayers Rock (giant rock in Australia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rocky</li></ol><p><i>скала́</i> (skalá) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show (one's teeth)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skaliti</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ска́лити</i> (skályty), Belarusian <i>скаліць</i> (skalicʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bared (<i>of teeth</i>)</li><li>to bare one's teeth (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to grin, to smile</li></ol><p><i>ска́лить</i> (skálitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rolling pin</li><li>laundry mangle</li><li><i>(technical)</i> ram, plunger, plunger piston</li></ol><p><i>скать</i> (skatʹ, “to twist (thread), to roll out (dough)”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock climber</li></ol><p>From <i>скала́</i> (skalá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-лаз</i> (-laz), combining form of <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ, “to climb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock climbing</li></ol><p><i>скала</i> (skala, “rock, cliff, crag”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>-лаз</i> (-laz) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female rock climber</li></ol><p><i>скалола́з</i> (skaloláz) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skald</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from German <i>Skalde</i> or directly from Old Norse <i>skald</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rock</li><li>rocky</li></ol><p><i>скала́</i> (skalá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) scalp (part of head where the hair grows)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scalp</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scalpel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>scalpellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scalping</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angelfish (<i>Pterophyllum</i>, a freshwater fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>скаме́йка</i> (skaméjka), diminutive of <i>скамья́</i> (skamʹjá): (small) bench</li></ol><p><i>скаме́йка</i> (skaméjka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench</li></ol><p><i>скамья́</i> (skamʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench (long seat in a park or garden)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скамиꙗ</i> (skamija), from Byzantine Greek <i>σκαμνία</i> (skamnía), plural of <i>σκαμνίον</i> (skamníon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) dock (part of a courtroom where the accused sits)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>banc des accusés</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) scanned image file</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scandal</li><li>fracas, brawl, donnybrook (a noisy disorderly quarrel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Skandal</i> or French <i>scandale</i>. Further from Latin <i>scandalum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σκάνδαλον</i> (skándalon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to damage someone's reputation through scandal</li></ol><p>From <i>сканда́л</i> (skandál, “scandal”) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brawl, to make a row</li></ol><p><i>сканда́л</i> (skandál) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scandalous</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quarrelsome, rowdy</li></ol><p><i>сканда́л</i> (skandál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) scandalous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scandium</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>scandium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scandinavian</li></ol><p><i>Скандина́в(ия)</i> (Skandináv(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chant; to scan; to cry out</li></ol><p>German <i>skandieren</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) scanner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scanner</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>scandō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scan, scanning</li></ol><p><i>скани́ровать</i> (skanírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scan (create a digital copy of an image using a scanner)</li></ol><p>English <i>scan</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filigree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accumulate, to amass, to store <i>(up)</i>, <i>(money)</i> to save</li></ol><p><i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accumulate, to pile up</li><li>to gather, to crowd</li></ol><p><i>ска́пливать</i> (skáplivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) scarabaeine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Scarabaeinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarab</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>scarabaeus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belongings; things</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>skarb</i> (“treasure”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stingy, niggardly</li><li>mean</li></ol><p>From <i>ска́ред</i> (skáred) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skarędъ</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>скарѧдь</i> (skarędĭ), Czech <i>škaredý</i> and Polish <i>szkaradny</i>.</p><p>Vasmer also suspects relation with Sanskrit <i>अवस्कर</i> (avaskara, “trash, ordure”), Ancient Greek <i>σκῶρ</i> (skôr, “dung”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarną</i> (“filth, dung”) (whence English <i>skarn</i> and <i>sharn</i>), which would make it derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóḱr̥</i> (“excrement, dung”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) scarlet fever, scarlatina (streptococcal infection)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slope, incline, ramp, pitch</li><li>rolling down</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) tire/tyre</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) a set of wheelsets (<i>e.g. a pair of wheelsets for one bogie</i>)</li><li>ray, skate (a marine fish of the order Batoidea with a flat body, large wing-like fins, and a whip-like tail)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъkatъ</i>. Verbal noun from <i>скати́ть</i> (skatítʹ).</p><p>Borrowed from Norwegian Nynorsk <i>skate</i>&nbsp;f (according to Vasmer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up</li><li>(<i>school</i>), (<i>colloquial</i>) to crib, to copy</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablecloth (a cloth used to cover and protect a table, especially for a dining table)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Three hypotheses exist:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good riddance! (used to indicate that one welcomes the departure of someone or something)</li></ol><p>Literally, “let the road be like a tablecloth”.</p><p>Initially, the expression used as the literal wishes for a good and incidentless journey. A 'tablecloth-like road' meant a smooth and clean road, while the quality of roads was usually poor in the Russian State. However eventually, скатертью дорога is almost completely lost its well-wishing message, getting closed by its meaning to what is said in English as 'good riddance'. Such a shift in the meaning was in particular noticed by Alexei N. Tolstoi at the beginning of the 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll down</li><li>to come down <i>(to)</i>, to slide <i>(into)</i>, to slip <i>(into)</i>, to sink <i>(into)</i>, to end up</li></ol><p><i>скати́ть</i> (skatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up</li><li>(school,&#32;colloquial) to crib, to copy</li><li>to roll down</li></ol><p>From <i>ската́ть</i> (skatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) or from <i>скати́ть</i> (skatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll down</li><li>to come down <i>(to)</i>, to slide <i>(into)</i>, to slip <i>(into)</i>, to sink <i>(into)</i>, to end up</li><li>to roll up</li><li>to pill</li></ol><p><i>ска́тывать</i> (skátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;regional,&#32;Krasnoyarsk) to twist (thread), to roll out (dough)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съкати</i> (sŭkati, “to twist”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ска́терть</i> (skátertʹ, “tablecloth”): (small) tablecloth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scout (member of the scout movement)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard diving suit</li><li>space suit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκάφη</i> (skáphē, “boat, ship”) plus <i>ἀνδρός</i> (andrós), the genitive of <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”). Compare French <i>scaphandre</i>, English <i>scaphander</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) space suit, atmospheric diving suit</li></ol><p><i>скафа́ндр</i> (skafándr) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to download</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кача́ть</i> (kačátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>download (file transfer to the local computer)</li></ol><p><i>ска́чивать</i> (skáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to download.</li></ol><p><i>скача́ть</i> (skačátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallop</li><li>(<i>often in the plural form</i>) horse races</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) download (file transfer to the local computer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jump, bound, leap; surge</li></ol><p><i>скака́ть</i> (skakátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mow <i>(down)</i></li><li>to squint</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to bevel, to slope</li></ol><p><i>скоси́ть</i> (skosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chink, crack, slit, gap, interstice, rift</li><li>pore, hole, aperture</li><li>borehole, drill hole</li><li>oil well</li></ol><p>Related to <i>сквозь</i> (skvozʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niggard, miser</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public garden, square, park</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>square</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сквер</i> (skver): (small) public garden, square, park</li></ol><p><i>сквер</i> (skver) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;religion) filth, pollution</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скврьна</i> (skvrĭna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skvьrna</i>. The inherited form would be <i>&#42;сквёрна</i> (&#42;skvjórna). Related to Czech <i>skvrna</i>, Slovak <i>škvrna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly, bad</li></ol><p><i>скве́рный</i> (skvérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foul language (profane words)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скврьнословиѥ</i> (skvrĭnoslovije). By surface analysis, <i>скве́рный</i> (skvérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad, nasty, foul</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скврьнъ</i> (skvrĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>скве́рна</i> (skvérna) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) squirt, female ejaculate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>to show [be seen] through</li><li>to show, to be discernible</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) <i>(cloth)</i> to be transparent, to be threadbare</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>сквозь</i> (skvozʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>through</li><li>prevailing</li><li>end-to-end</li></ol><p><i>сквозь</i> (skvozʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draught (a current of air)</li></ol><p>From <i>сквозь</i> (skvozʹ) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сквозня́к</i> (skvoznják)</li></ol><p><i>сквозня́к</i> (skvoznják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>through</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сквозѣ</i> (skvozě, “through, across”), a locative case of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skvozъ</i> (“aperture”). Related to <i>сква́жина</i> (skvážina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skvorьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;skvьrь-</i> (“scream”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdhouse, nestbox</li></ol><p><i>скворе́ц</i> (skvoréc, “starling”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdhouse, nestbox</li></ol><p><i>скворе́ц</i> (skvoréc) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squatter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>squatter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squash (sport)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>squash</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skateboard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>skateboard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skeleton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scepsis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sceptic, disbeliever (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skepticism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>skeptically, cynically</li></ol><p><i>скепти́ческий</i> (skeptíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skeptical</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) skepticism</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>skept(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) sketch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discount</li></ol><p><i>скид-</i> (skid-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a sale</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) chipping in (money)</li></ol><p><i>скида́ть</i> (skidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-он</i> (-on)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) discount</li></ol><p><i>ски́дка</i> (skídka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw down, to throw off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(clothes, shoes)</i> to take off, to throw off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw off, to dethrone</li><li>to throw together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock off, to discount</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to send by Internet or SMS</li></ol><p><i>скид-</i> (skid-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chip in, to pitch in, to club together (to collectively contribute towards the cost of something)</li></ol><p><i>ски́дывать</i> (skídyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin (<i>a set of resources that modifies the appearance and/or layout of the graphical user interface of a computer program</i>)</li><li>skinhead</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>skin</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>скинхе́д</i> (skinxéd, skinxɛ́d).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw down, to throw off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(clothes, shoes)</i> to take off, to throw off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw off, to dethrone</li><li>to throw together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock off, to discount</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to send by Internet or SMS</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ки́нуть</i> (kínutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chip in, to pitch in, to club together (to collectively contribute towards the cost of something)</li></ol><p><i>ски́нуть</i> (skínutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skinhead (adherent to white-supremacism or anti-immigrant principles)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>skinhead</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sceptre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκῆπτρον</i> (skêptron).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turpentine</li></ol><p>Compare Belarusian <i>шкіпіна́р</i> (škipinár), <i>шпікіна́р</i> (špikinár), <i>шпігіна́р</i> (špihinár), Ukrainian <i>скипида́р</i> (skypydár), <i>шпиґина́р</i> (špygynár), <i>шпигина́р</i> (špyhynár). More distantly connected to Polish <i>śpikonarda</i>, <i>szpikanarda</i>, <i>śpikanarda</i>, <i>szpikanard</i>, English <i>spikenard</i>, Italian <i>spigonardo</i>, all ultimately from Medieval Latin <i>spīca nardī</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hay) stack, rick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sour (e.g. a milk product)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become sad or depressed</li></ol><p><i>ски́снуть</i> (skísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn sour (e.g. a milk product)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become sad or depressed</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ки́снуть</i> (kísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skete (in Eastern Christianity, a small hermitage at some distance from the main monastery)</li><li>remote monastery among Old Believers</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκήτη</i> (skḗtē), which could be from <i>Σκήτις</i> (Skḗtis), <i>Σκήτη</i> (Skḗtē), <i>Σκήτες</i> (Skḗtes, “Scetis valley”), a region in northern Egypt, from Coptic <i>Ϣⲓϩⲏⲧ</i> (Šihēt, “measure of the hearts”), or otherwise via aphesis from <i>ἀσκητής</i> (askētḗs, “monk, hermit”), from <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō, “to exercise”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer, vagabond</li></ol><p><i>скита́ть(ся)</i> (skitátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering, roaming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, to stray</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скꙑтати сѧ</i> (skytati sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skytati (sę)</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>скита́тися</i> (skytátysja), Bulgarian <i>ски́там се</i> (skítam se), Serbo-Croatian <i>скитати</i>, Slovene <i>skítati sе</i>. More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>kutė́ti</i> (“to shake”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skundijaną</i> (“to accelerate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Scyth, Scythian</li><li>wild, rough man</li><li>(astronautics,&#32;weaponry) Skif-DM (Soviet orbital weapons platform)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) skiff (boat)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Σκύθης</i> (Skúthēs).</p><p>From English <i>skiff</i>, from Middle French <i>esquif</i>, from Old Italian <i>schifo</i> (“small boat”), from Lombardic <i>skif</i> (“boat”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skipą</i> (“boat, ship”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scythian</li></ol><p><i>скиф</i> (skif) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ски́фский</i> (skífskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>скиф</i> (skif).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) abbreviation of <i>склоне́ние</i> (sklonénije, “declension”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>склад</i> (sklad, “warehouse”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supply, stock</li><li>warehouse, depot</li><li>(<i>military</i>) dump</li><li>way, manner</li><li>build, body type, physique</li><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;except&#32;in fixed expressions</i>) syllable (in this sense, the plurals have the endings accented, as in the non-nominative/accusative plurals of <i>слог</i> (slog, “syllable”))</li><li>(music,&#32;informal) harmony</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъkladъ</i>, from <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i> and <i>&#42;klasti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warehousing, storing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crease, buckling, crimp, fold, plica, ply</li></ol><p><i>скла́д(ывать)</i> (sklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well, smoothly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in rhyme</li></ol><p><i>скла́дный</i> (skládnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>collapsible, folding</li></ol><p><i>скла́д(ывать)</i> (sklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) well-built, well-made, well-proportioned (<i>of a person or object</i>)</li><li>fluent, smooth (<i>speech, writing</i>)</li><li>harmonious, coherent</li></ol><p><i>склад</i> (sklad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>скла́дка</i> (skládka, “crease, fold”)</li></ol><p><i>скла́дка</i> (skládka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warehouse</li></ol><p><i>склад</i> (sklad) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection of money or products for a common cause (<i>action or result</i>)</li><li>party or soiree arranged using funds so collected</li></ol><p><i>скла́д(ывать)</i> (sklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-чина</i> (-čina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay together, to put together, to pile up, to heap, to stack</li><li>to pack up</li><li>to add, to sum up</li><li>to make, to assemble, to put together</li><li><i>(song, rhyme)</i> to compose, to make up</li><li>to fold</li><li>to take off, to put down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>класть</i> (klastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to club together (with), to pool (with), to pool one's money / resources</li><li>to consist, to (be) form(ed), to develop, to turn out, to take shape; to arise (<i>of a situation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>скла́дывать</i> (skládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gluing, pasting</li><li>adhesion</li><li>agglutination (act of uniting by glue or other tenacious substance)</li><li>conglutination</li></ol><p><i>скле́ивать</i> (skléivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue together, to paste together, to stick together</li><li>to mend</li></ol><p><i>скле́ить</i> (skléitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue together, to paste together, to stick together</li><li>to mend</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кле́ить</i> (kléitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kick the bucket, to die</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial vault, crypt, ossuary</li><li>vault (a secure, enclosed area)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>склеп</i> (sklep, “vault, crypt”), borrowed from Polish <i>sklep</i> (“vault, cellar, crypt”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъklepъ</i>. Doublet of <i>склёп</i> (skljop), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sclera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκληρός</i> (sklērós, “hard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) sclerosis</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) memory loss, especially in old age</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sclérose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slimy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squabble, row</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slope</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъklonъ</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) declension</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) declination</li></ol><p><i>склони́ть</i> (sklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to incline, to decline</li><li>to convince someone to do something</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline, to bend, to stoop</li><li>to be inclined (to)</li><li>to yield (to) (to give up under pressure)</li></ol><p><i>склони́ть</i> (sklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propensity, proclivity</li><li>tendency, inclination, disposition</li><li>leaning, penchant</li><li>predilection</li><li>taste</li><li>addiction</li><li>bent</li><li>aptitude</li><li>predisposition</li><li>appetite</li></ol><p><i>скло́нный</i> (sklónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined</li><li>addicted (being dependent on something)</li></ol><p><i>склон</i> (sklon) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) declinable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to incline, to decline</li><li>to convince someone to do something</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to decline a noun or adjective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incline, to bend, to stoop</li><li>to be inclined (to)</li><li>to yield (to) (to give up under pressure)</li></ol><p><i>склоня́ть</i> (sklonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) argumentative, quarrelsome person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) squabblesomeness, quarrelsomeness</li></ol><p><i>скло́чный</i> (sklóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quarrelsome, squabblesome, prone to fights, intrigues or gossip</li></ol><p>From <i>скло́ка</i> (sklóka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vial, flask, bottle, glass (any kind of small glass vessel)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) bell, half an hour</li><li>(nautical,&#32;obsolete,&#32;in the plural) watch glass, hourglass</li></ol><p>From the obsolete form <i>сткля́нка</i> (stkljánka), from Old East Slavic <i>стьклянъ</i> (stĭkljanŭ); cognate with <i>стекло́</i> (stekló, “glass vessel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) riveting</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъklepъ</i>. Doublet of <i>скле́п</i> (sklép), a Polish loanword.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cramp, crampon, staple, bracket, shackle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skoba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>carpentry</i>) shaving/drawing knife, spokeshave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skobľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>typography</i>) parenthesis, bracket</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) bracket</li></ol><p><i>скоба́</i> (skobá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, to scrub</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skobľiti</i> (“to carve, to delve with a tool”). By surface analysis, <i>ско́бель</i> (skóbelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ско́бка</i> (skóbka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) awkwardness, inhibition</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) stiffness of movement</li></ol><p><i>ско́ванный</i> (skóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constrained</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge, to hammer out</li><li>to forge together</li><li>to chain</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hold, to fix</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;with ice) to lock, to beset</li><li>to fetter</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying pan (long-handled, shallow pan used for frying food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сковорода</i> (skovoroda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skovorda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сковорода́</i> (skovorodá): frying pan</li></ol><p><i>сковорода́</i> (skovorodá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frying pan</li></ol><p><i>сковорода́</i> (skovorodá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forge, to hammer out</li><li>to forge together</li><li>to chain</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to hold, to fix</li><li><i>(with ice)</i> to lock</li><li>to fetter</li></ol><p><i>скова́ть</i> (skovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gallop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock together, to knit together</li><li>to cobble together (money)</li></ol><p><i>сколоти́ть</i> (skolotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) scoliosis</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>σκολιός</i> (skoliós), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any wasp of the genus <i>Scolia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Scolia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock together, to knit together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>колоти́ть</i> (kolotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) how much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sliding, slipping, slippage</li></ol><p><i>скользи́ть</i> (skolʹzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide, to slip</li><li>to glide</li><li>to float</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ско́льз(кий)</i> (skólʹz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery, dangerous (<i>of a surface</i>)</li><li>slimy</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ко́льзкий</i> (&#42;kólʹzkij, “slippery”) (<i>ко́льзко</i> (kólʹzko) is attested), from Old East Slavic <i>кользъкъ</i> (kolĭzŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlzъkъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>сковзки́й</i> (skovzkýj, “slippery”) and <i>ко́взати</i> (kóvzaty, “to slip”). Additionally cognate with Polish <i>kiełzać</i> (“to stumble”). Per Vasmer, probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlz-</i>, of unknown further origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a slippery manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide, to slip</li><li>to glide</li><li>to float</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to sneak (<i>into, by</i>)</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to graze [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘someone/something’</small>] (<i>of a bullet</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скълъзнути</i> (skŭlŭznuti). By surface analysis, <i>скользи́ть</i> (skolʹzítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sliding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>how much, how many, so many (obsolete form of сколько, used as adjective or without a following noun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>how much, how many</li><li>so much, so many</li><li>how long</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how long, how much, so much</li><li>as</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) + Old East Slavic <i>колико</i> (koliko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;koliko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>any number, as many/much as one wants</li><li>ad lib (to whatever extent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, a few; any, anything (in a question)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>some, a few; any (in a question)</li><li>a little, to (at least) some extent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command, to order</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кома́ндовать</i> (komándovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to combine, to arrange</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scheme, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>комбини́ровать</i> (kombinírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crumple</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash off, to race through</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ко́мкать</i> (kómkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) skomorokh (a medieval East Slavic actor)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) buffoon, mountebank</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скоморохъ</i> (skomoroxŭ), of unknown origin. Possibly from Ancient Greek <i>σκώμμαρχος</i> (skṓmmarkhos, “joke leader”) (σκῶμμα (skômma)+ἀρχός (arkhós)) or from Italian <i>scaramuccia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fool around</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compromise</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>компромети́ровать</i> (komprometírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to construct, to build, to structure, to design, to form</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>конструи́ровать</i> (konstruírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abashed, confounded, disconcerted (<i>experiencing embarrassment or confusion; of a person</i>)</li><li>abashed, confounded, disconcerted (<i>expressing embarrassment or confusion; of a look, tone, face, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disconcert, to discomfit, to embarrass, to put in an awkward or embarrassing position</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>конфу́зить</i> (konfúzitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disconcerted, to be embarrassed, to get into an embarrassing situation</li></ol><p><i>сконфу́зить</i> (skonfúzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, (<i>military</i>) to mass</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>концентри́ровать</i> (koncentrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to amass, to get together, to mass</li><li>to concentrate <i>(on)</i>, to focus <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>сконцентри́ровать</i> (skoncentrírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) end, ending</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) death</li></ol><p><i>сконча́ть</i> (skončátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to end, to finish</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>конча́ть</i> (končátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) to pass away (to die, to stop living)</li></ol><p><i>сконча́ть</i> (skončátʹ, “to end, to finish”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coordinate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>координи́ровать</i> (koordinírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) group, accumulation; stock, savings</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) osprey</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eunuch</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) cold, passionless man</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) skopets, a member of a Russian religious eunuch movement practising self-castration</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скопьць</i> (skopĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skopьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;skopiti</i> (“to castrate”). By surface analysis, <i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsimony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to copy, to make a copy</li><li>to imitate, to mimic, to copy</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>копи́ровать</i> (kopírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accumulate, to amass, to store <i>(up)</i>, <i>(money)</i> to save</li><li>to emasculate, to castrate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>копи́ть</i> (kopítʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skopiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accumulate, to pile up</li><li>to gather, to crowd</li></ol><p><i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crowd, throng</li><li>assemblage (large collection of anything in one place)</li></ol><p>From <i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cluster</li><li>accumulation, aggregation, collection, assemblage</li><li>congestion</li><li>gathering</li><li>conglomeration, aggregate</li><li>congeries</li><li>agglomeration</li><li>congregation</li><li>concourse</li></ol><p><i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ска́пливать</i> (skáplivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>скопи́ть</i> (skopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ска́пливаться</i> (skáplivatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>скопля́ть</i> (skopljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a crowd, all together, en masse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;collective) raw animal skins</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скора</i> (skora), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skora</i>. Doublet of <i>шку́ра</i> (škúra, “pelt”) (a borrowing from Polish); related to <i>кора́</i> (korá, “bark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ambulance (emergency vehicle)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>ско́рая по́мощь</i> (skóraja pómoščʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambulance (emergency vehicle)</li><li>emergency medical service</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grieve <i>(about, over)</i>, to mourn <i>(for, over)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skъrběti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>скорбь</i> (skorbʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sorrowfully, mournfully</li></ol><p><i>ско́рбный</i> (skórbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sorrowful, mournful, doleful</li></ol><p><i>скорбь</i> (skorbʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scurvy</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>Skorbut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrow, grief</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скърбь</i> (skŭrbĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skъrbь</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>скръбь</i> (skrŭbĭ), Bulgarian <i>скръб</i> (skrǎb), Serbo-Croatian <i>скрб</i> (“care”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj): faster, quicker</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ско́ро</i> (skóro): sooner, rather</li><li>more exactly, more likely</li><li>be quick!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most likely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ско́ро</i> (skóro)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shell (of an egg or animal), hull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скорлупа</i> (skorlupa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skorupa</i>, from <i>&#42;skora</i> (“bast, skin”) + лупить, "peel".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>скорлупа́</i> (skorlupá, “shell”)</li></ol><p><i>скорлупа́</i> (skorlupá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>soon</li><li>speedily, quickly</li></ol><p><i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue-twister (phrase which is difficult to say)</li><li>rapid speech, sputter</li></ol><p><i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj, “fast, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ, “to speak”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(agriculture,&#32;dialectal) harrow (device)</li><li>chive (<i>Allium schoenoprasum</i>)</li><li>chive (herb)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skorda</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>σκόροδον</i> (skórodon), <i>σκόρδον</i> (skórdon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skorodite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) to harrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hasty, hurried (prone to quick actions and decisions)</li></ol><p>From <i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Compare cognate <i>вспы́льчивый</i> (vspýlʹčivyj), <i>запа́льчивый</i> (zapálʹčivyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) skoropis (an obsolete form of Russian handwriting used in the 15th–18th centuries)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Greek minuscule or cursive (<i>Late Antiquity and Byzantine era</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scrawl, chicken scratch (hasty, often illegible handwriting)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) shorthand</li></ol><p><i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precocious</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speed, high-speed</li><li>fast</li></ol><p><i>ско́рость</i> (skórostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed</li><li>rate</li><li>velocity</li></ol><p><i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed of light</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loose-leaf binder; folder</li></ol><p><i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сшива́тель</i> (sšivátelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to while away, to pass</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>корота́ть</i> (korotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>briefness, fleetingness, brevity, short-term nature, fast pace, transience</li></ol><p><i>скороте́чный</i> (skorotéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) brief, fleeting, ephemeral, short-lived</li></ol><p>From <i>ско́р</i> (skór) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-тек</i> (-tek) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), based on a combining form of <i>течь</i> (tečʹ, “to flow”) (compare 1sg. present <i>теку́</i> (tekú)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpion</li></ol><p>Via a European language from Latin <i>scorpio</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Скорпион)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Scorpius (constellation of the zodiac containing the red giant Antares, in the shape of a scorpion)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Scorpio (zodiac sign for the scorpion, covering October 23 – November 21)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpionfly</li></ol><p><i>скорпио́н</i> (skorpión, “scorpion”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correct, to adjust</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>корректи́ровать</i> (korrektírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ко́рчить</i> (kórčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to writhe, to squirm</li></ol><p><i>ско́рчить</i> (skórčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fast, quick, rapid, speedy, swift</li><li>forthcoming, upcoming</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) express</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скоръ</i> (skorŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skorъ</i>. Related to Russian <i>я́щерица</i> (jáščerica, “lizard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any weevil of the genus <i>Otiorhynchus</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mow <i>(down)</i></li><li>to squint</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to bevel, to slope</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>коси́ть</i> (kosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become lop-sided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to strike off (debt), to knock off (price)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кости́ть</i> (kostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) cattle, livestock</li><li>brute, beast (in an abusive sense)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skotъ</i>, of disputed origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) cattle</li><li>brute</li><li>dolt, boor (m or f)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>скот</i> (skot) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cattle, livestock</li></ol><p><i>скот</i> (skot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abattoir (public slaughterhouse)</li></ol><p><i>скот</i> (skot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бо́йня</i> (bójnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cattle breeding</li></ol><p><i>скот</i> (skot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вод</i> (vod) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cattle, livestock</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) brutish, bestial</li></ol><p><i>скот</i> (skot) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of the Scoti tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adhesive tape</li><li>Scotch (whiskey)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Scotch tape</i>, but the meaning has expanded beyond the corresponding meaning in English.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>Scotch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to mitigate (something painful)</li><li>to brighten, to make more attractive (something dull or boring)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to mitigate (something painful)</li><li>to brighten, to make more attractive (something dull or boring)</li></ol><p><i>скра́сить</i> (skrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scraping knife</li></ol><p><i>скреб-</i> (skreb-) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scraper, scrubber, flesher</li><li>long-handled ice scraper</li><li>large scoop with a cutting edge for excavating soil, etc.</li><li>device in a conveyor for moving bulk cargo</li></ol><p><i>скреб-</i> (skreb-) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gritting/grinding sound</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grate, to grind</li><li>to gnash [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘one's teeth’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скрьжьтати</i> (skrĭžĭtati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skrьgъtati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raspy (rough, raw)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brace, clamp, fastener, bond</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) bond (something that unites or holds together)</li><li>(<i>specialist</i>) a place to fasten something</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) conjunction</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) confirmatory signature</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>скрепи́ть</i> (skrepítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scraper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scraper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten <i>(together)</i>, to pin <i>(together)</i>, to tie, to clamp, to bolt, to mortar</li><li>to strengthen</li><li>to seal, to sign</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крепи́ть</i> (krepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paper clip</li><li>staple</li></ol><p><i>скрепи́ть</i> (skrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten <i>(together)</i>, to pin <i>(together)</i>, to tie, to clamp, to bolt, to mortar</li><li>to strengthen</li><li>to seal, to sign</li></ol><p><i>скрепи́ть</i> (skrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, to scrub, to scratch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skreti</i>, cognates include Ukrainian <i>скребти́</i> (skrebtý), Belarusian <i>скрэ́бці</i> (skrébci).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a scratching noise (<i>of a small animal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>скрести́</i> (skrestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) to cross, to crossbreed</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крести́ть</i> (krestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interbreed; to cross</li><li>to clash; to cross</li></ol><p><i>скрести́ть</i> (skrestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) interbreeding, crossing</li></ol><p><i>скре́щивать</i> (skréščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) to cross, to crossbreed</li></ol><p><i>скрести́ть</i> (skrestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interbreed; to cross</li><li>to clash; to cross</li></ol><p><i>скре́щивать</i> (skréščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to distort</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>криви́ть</i> (krivítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become crooked/bent/lopsided</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a face</li></ol><p><i>скриви́ть</i> (skrivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) table, tablet <i>(with sacred text inscribed upon it)</i></li><li>(<i>literary</i>) the tables <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>church</i>) pectoral <i>(of a bishop)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скрижаль</i> (skrižalĭ), which is possibly borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>скрижаль</i> (skrižalĭ), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skrižalь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) screamer, jumpscare</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>screamer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>software</i>) screensaver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>screensaver</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) screenshot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>screenshot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeak, creak, crunch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skripъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) violinist, fiddler (person who plays violin)</li></ol><p><i>скри́пка</i> (skrípka, “violin, fiddle”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ч</i> (-áč)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creak, to screech, to squeak (to make a prolonged sharp grating or squeaking sound)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skripěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lactarius vellereus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) violin</li></ol><p><i>скри́пка</i> (skrípka) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violin</li></ol><p>From <i>скрипе́ть</i> (skripétʹ, “to creak”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Czech <i>skřipky</i>, Polish <i>skrzypce</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creak, to screech, to squeak (to make a prolonged sharp grating or squeaking sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) script (procedure or program)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creaky, squeaky</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) harsh, unpleasant, rasping (<i>sound, voice, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>скрипе́ть</i> (skripétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out (fabric to specific lengths and shapes to make clothes)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крои́ть</i> (kroítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) modest person, nice nelly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be excessively modest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decently, modestly, discreetly</li><li>humbly</li><li>moderately, slightly</li></ol><p><i>скро́мный</i> (skrómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modesty, humility (the quality of being modest)</li></ol><p><i>скро́мный</i> (skrómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, humble</li><li>modest, frugal</li><li>modest, unassuming, unpretentious</li><li>modest, prudish</li></ol><p>First attested in the 17th century. Borrowed from Polish <i>skromny</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъkromьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) (gas) scrubber, washer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scrubber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>scrupulously</li></ol><p><i>скрупулёзный</i> (skrupuljóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrupulousness, scrupulosity</li></ol><p><i>скрупулёзный</i> (skrupuljóznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scrupulous, meticulous</li></ol><p>French <i>scrupuleux</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Latin <i>scrūpulōsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to roll</li><li>to bind, to tie up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring into submission, to subdue, to overmaster</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(disease)</i> to overpower, to attack, to seize</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крути́ть</i> (krutítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electricity</i>) splice (<i>especially rat-tail splice</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) handmade cigarette</li></ol><p><i>скрути́ть</i> (skrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist, to roll</li><li>to bind, to tie up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring into submission, to subdue, to overmaster</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(disease)</i> to overpower, to attack, to seize</li></ol><p><i>скрути́ть</i> (skrutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, to conceal</li><li>to dissemble, to keep back</li><li>to keep secret</li></ol><p><i>скрыть</i> (skrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear; to hide from; to go into hiding</li><li>to escape; to steal away; to sneak away; to slip away</li></ol><p><i>скрыва́ть</i> (skryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) trunk, chest, coffer</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) mill race</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrophysics</i>) dark matter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment, hiding</li></ol><p><i>скрыть</i> (skrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly (in secret)</li></ol><p><i>скры́тный</i> (skrýtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secretiveness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) secrecy</li></ol><p><i>скры́тный</i> (skrýtnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reserved, reticent, secretive</li><li>surreptitious, furtive</li></ol><p><i>скрыть</i> (skrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in secret</li><li>invisibly</li></ol><p><i>скры́тый</i> (skrýtyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concealed, hidden</li><li>latent</li><li>secret</li><li>covert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, to conceal</li><li>to dissemble, to keep back</li><li>to keep secret</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>съкрꙑти</i> (sŭkryti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъkryti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to hide from, to go into hiding</li><li>to escape, to steal away</li></ol><p><i>скрыть</i> (skrytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crook, to double up</li></ol><p><i>скрю́чить</i> (skrjúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crook, to double up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>крю́чить</i> (krjúčitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scribble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scratch, to scrape</li></ol><p>Cognates include Ukrainian <i>скря́бати</i> (skrjábaty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>niggard, miser, cheapskate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skręga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great skua</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Faroese <i>skúgvur</i>, from Old Norse <i>skúfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scuba</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scuba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scuba diving</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scuba diving</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scant, scanty, meagre [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>poor, pathetic, wretched</li><li>poor, infertile</li></ol><p>Constructed as if from <i>скуда́</i> (skudá, “poverty”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Old East Slavic <i>скудъ</i> (skudŭ), Old Church Slavonic <i>скѫдъ</i> (skǫdŭ). From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skǫdьnъ</i>; see also pre-first-palatalization <i>&#42;skęd-</i>, whence <i>щади́ть</i> (ščadítʹ, “to spare, to have mercy on”). Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬯𐬗𐬀𐬥𐬛𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬥𐬙𐬌</i> (scandaiieinti, “they destroy”), <i>𐬯𐬐𐬆𐬥𐬛𐬀</i> (skənda, “broken; illness”), Latin <i>scandula</i> (“shingles”) (also <i>scindula</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) scarcity</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) poverty</li></ol><p><i>ску́д(ный)</i> (skúd(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boredom, ennui (state of being bored)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skuka</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>ску́чити</i>, <i>ску̑чи̑м</i> (“to press, put in a predicament”), Slovene <i>skúčati</i> (“to whine, to squeal”), Czech <i>skučet</i>, <i>skoukat</i> (“to howl, to whine”), Slovak <i>skučаť</i> (“to whine, squeal, whistle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheekbone</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a curved corner of a ship's hull (<i>between bow and sides, or between stern and sides</i>); a bilge between the bottom and side plating</li><li>(<i>nautical&#32;slang</i>) a side of a ship’s bow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with high cheekbones</li></ol><p><i>скула́</i> (skulá) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to whine, to whimper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sculptor (a person who sculpts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) sculpture (art of sculpting)</li><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) sculpture (collective, the works of a sculptor)</li><li>sculpture (a sculpted piece)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sculptural</li><li>sculpted</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) statuesque</li></ol><p><i>скульпту́ра</i> (skulʹptúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mackerel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>σκουμπριά</i> (skoumpriá), plural of <i>σκουμπρί</i> (skoumprí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) to think, to ponder, to ruminate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have knowledge in some affair; to understand</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>куме́кать</i> (kumékatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skunk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>skunks</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy up, to corner (e.g. shares)</li></ol><p><i>скупи́ть</i> (skupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skinflint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miser</li></ol><p><i>скупо́й</i> (skupój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy up, to corner (e.g. shares)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>купи́ть</i> (kupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sparing with, to be stingy with, to skimp on, to scrimp on [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘praise, love, money, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to buy up</li></ol><p><i>скупо́й</i> (skupój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p><p><i>скупи́ть</i> (skupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buying up, buying off</li></ol><p><i>скупи́ть</i> (skupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sparingly, stingily</li></ol><p><i>скупо́й</i> (skupój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avaricious, stingy, frugal, mean, parsimonious, miserly</li><li>scanty, poor</li><li>taciturn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miser</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скупъ</i> (skupŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skǫpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinginess, niggardliness, miserliness, avarice (excessive or inordinate desire of gain)</li></ol><p><i>скупо́й</i> (skupój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parliament in various countries of the former Yugoslavia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian <i>ску̏пштина</i> / skȕpština. Cognate with Macedonian <i>скупштина</i> (skupština), Slovene <i>skupščina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to become worse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scooter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>scooter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory) fatbeard, dad bod, couch potato (an unattractive and unambitious middle-aged man, often overweight, balding, with bad skin, unkempt facial hair, no fashion sense, poor hygiene, and spending much of his free time in front of a screen)</li></ol><p>Attested c. 2018; originally from a Russian imageboard meme; clipping of the surname of the 2ch moderator <i>Алексей Скуфьин</i> (Aleksej Skufʹin), who was doxxed and mocked for his appearance after posting an angry video message of himself during a heated argument with another user.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>expressive</i>) alternative form of <i>скуф</i> (skuf)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dad bod, couch potato</li></ol><p><i>ску́ф</i> (skúf) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skufia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>скуфиꙗ</i> (skufija), from Ancient Greek <i>σκούφια</i> (skoúphia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bored</li><li>to long for, to miss [<i>with</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bored</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ску́чный</i> (skúšnyj, skúčnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ску́чно</i> (skúšno, skúčno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tediously, boringly (in a tedious manner)</li></ol><p><i>ску́чный</i> (skúšnyj, skúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dullish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, boring, tedious, tiresome (inciting boredom)</li><li>bored, listless</li></ol><p><i>ску́ка</i> (skúka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to have, to take</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to swallow, to pocket</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat up, to buy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat up, to ruin</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ку́шать</i> (kúšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>сле́дующим о́бразом</i> (slédujuščim óbrazom): as follows</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) weakling</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) wimp, coward</li></ol><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj, “weak, loose”)</li></ol><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, to grow weak/feeble, to slack off, to slacken</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) slack</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) weak point, weak spot</li></ol><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj, “weak”) +&lrm; <i>-ина́</i> (-iná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to weaken, to become weak or weaker</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slacken, to become slack or slacker</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) + <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>faintly, feebly, weakly</li><li>badly, poorly</li></ol><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of drinks) mildly alcoholic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) weak interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the weakest link (the part of a system that is most likely to fail or cause problems)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) weak nuclear interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barely viable</li></ol><p>From <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жизнь</i> (žiznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakness, feebleness</li><li>weak point</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slabostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychiatry</i>) dementia</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stupidity, idiocy</li></ol><p><i>сла́бый</i> (slábyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imbecile, weakheaded, weakminded, feeble-minded, soft in the head</li></ol><p><i>слабоу́мие</i> (slaboúmije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble</li><li>faint</li><li>infirm</li><li>delicate</li><li>flabby</li><li>poor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slabъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glory</li><li>fame, renown</li><li>repute, reputation</li><li>rumour, rumor</li><li>Slava (Soviet and Russian watches)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>слава</i> (slava), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slàva</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Слава)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Борисла́в (Borisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given names Бронисла́в (Bronisláv) or Бронісла́в (Bronisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Брячесла́в (Brjačesláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Венцесла́в (Vencesláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Владисла́в (Vladisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Всесла́в (Vsesláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given names Вышесла́в (Vyšesláv) or Вишесла́в (Višesláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given names Вячесла́в (Vjačesláv) or В’ячесла́в (V’jačesláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Градисла́в (Gradisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given names Изясла́в (Izjasláv) or Ізясла́в (Izjasláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Милосла́в (Milosláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Миросла́в (Mirosláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Мстисла́в (Mstisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Ростисла́в (Rostisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Святосла́в (Svjatosláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given names Станисла́в (Stanisláv) or Станісла́в (Stanisláv)</li><li>a diminutive, Slava, of the male given name Яросла́в (Jarosláv)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>any Slavic given name ending in -сла́в</i> (-sláv) + <i>-а</i> (-a); in all cases, <i>-сла́в</i> (-sláv) derives from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slàva</i> (“glory, fame”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>"glory to Rus", "glory to Russia" (a greeting used by Russian nationalists and pan-Slavists)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thank God!; thank goodness!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paganism</i>) "glory to the [Slavic] race", a greeting used by Slavic neopagans and nationalists.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavic studies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to praise, to glorify, to laud, to extol</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slaviti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be famous <i>(for)</i>, to be famed <i>(for)</i>, to be renowned <i>(for)</i>, to have a reputation <i>(for)</i></li><li><i>(imperative)</i> to glory <i>(to)</i>, all hail</li></ol><p><i>сла́вить</i> (slávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) famously, well</li><li>(predicative,&#32;impersonal) it is nice</li></ol><p><i>сла́вный</i> (slávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famous, renowned</li><li>glorious</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nice</li><li>capital, dandy</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сла́вьнъ</i> (slávĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slavьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) glorification, adulation (<i>especially of God</i>), doxology</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) immoderate praise, flattery</li></ol><p><i>сла́ва</i> (sláva) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavism (a Slavic idiom or phrase)</li></ol><p><i>славяни́н</i> (slavjanín, “a Slav”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slav (person of Slavic origins)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>словѣнинъ</i> (slověninŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slověninъ</i> (“Slav”). Further etymology is disputed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humanities</i>) Slavic studies, Slavistics</li></ol><p>From <i>славя́н-</i> (slavján-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavophile</li></ol><p><i>славя́не</i> (slavjáne, “the Slavs”) +&lrm; <i>-фил</i> (-fil, “-phile”), used since early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Slavophile, Slavophilism; Slavophilic</li></ol><p><i>славянофи́л</i> (slavjanofíl, “Slavophile”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij, “-ic”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slavophilism</li></ol><p><i>славянофи́л</i> (slavjanofíl, “Slavophile”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo, “-ism”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Slav, Slavic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slověnьskъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) summand, augend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compose</li><li>to put/lay down <i>(from)</i></li><li>to lay down</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сълагати</i> (sŭlagati). By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-лага́ть</i> (-lagátʹ). Compare it's neutral counterpart <i>скла́дывать</i> (skládyvatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be formed</li></ol><p><i>слага́ть</i> (slagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in fixed expressions</i>) ability to cope, ability to manage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make, to produce</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to arrange, to manage, to settle</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;colloquial) to overcome, to defeat</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;colloquial) to handle, to cope with, to deal with</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sweeten</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to malt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak sweetly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to flatter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to be sweet-tasting</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p><p><i>сла́д(кий)</i> (slád(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet (having a pleasant taste)</li><li>sugary</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сладъкъ</i> (sladŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soldъkъ</i>. See also the dialectal Russian <i>соло́дкий</i> (solódkij), a doublet.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>sweet dreams</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sweetly (in a sweet or pleasant manner)</li></ol><p><i>сла́дкий</i> (sládkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat sweet, sweetish</li><li>sweet-smelling, fragrant</li><li>falsely sentimental</li><li>excessively courteous</li></ol><p><i>сла́дкий</i> (sládkij) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dessert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a person with a sweet tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mellifluent, mellifluous, sweet (having a pleasant sound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet, delightful</li></ol><p><i>сла́дость</i> (sládostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedonism, licentiousness, lasciviousness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voluptuary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voluptuous</li><li>sensual</li></ol><p><i>сладостра́стие</i> (sladostrástije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetness</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) sweets, sweetmeats, candies</li></ol><p><i>сла́д(кий)</i> (slád(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coherence, cohesiveness, orderliness</li></ol><p><i>сла́женный</i> (slážennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coherent, cohesive, orderly, well-organized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to make, to produce</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to arrange, to manage, to settle</li></ol><p><i>сла́дить</i> (sláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to climb down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ла́зить</i> (lázitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slide (transparent image for projecting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>slide</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slalom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break into pieces</li></ol><p><i>сломи́ть</i> (slomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shale, schist, slate (sedimentary rock)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) slides, thongs, flip-flops (plastic or rubber footwear)</li></ol><p>From the town of <i>Сла́нцы</i> (Sláncy), a manufacturing center for such footwear during the Soviet period.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweets</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>сласть</i> (slastʹ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>сласть</i> (slastĭ, “pleasure, sweetness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;also&#32;figuratively) to sweeten</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have a sweet or sweetish taste</li></ol><p>From <i>сласть</i> (slastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ), or directly inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solstiti</i>, in which case it's a doublet of <i>солости́ть</i> (solostítʹ, “to have a sweetish taste (regional)”), inherited from Old East Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voluptuary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>libidinous, voluptuous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;now&#32;rare) sweetness</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) pleasure, delight</li><li>(<i>plural only</i>) sweets, candy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сласть</i> (slastĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solstь</i>. The native Old East Slavic <i>&#42;солость</i> (&#42;solostĭ) is unattested, but is the source of <i>солости́ть</i> (solostítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a person with a sweet tooth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, to dispatch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сълати</i> (sŭlati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъlati</i>, from either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₁-</i> (“favourable/favorable”) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₂-</i> (“mercy, comfort”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mushy; sickly; sugary; schmaltzy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>сла́дкий</i> (sládkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>сла́дко</i> (sládko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>слобода́</i> (slobodá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the left; from the left</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ле́во</i> (lévo) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long thick pole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get seriously ill</li></ol><p><i>слег-</i> (sleg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lightly, slightly</li><li>in passing</li><li>easily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track, trail</li><li>footprint, print</li><li>sole of a sock or stocking</li><li>trace, sign, vestige</li><li>mark, print, legacy</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>слѣдъ</i> (slědŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slědъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sláidus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sleydʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consequently; therefore</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following, next</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>сле́довательно</i> (slédovatelʹno).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>сле́дующий</i> (slédujuščij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;law enforcement) investigator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch, to follow, to spy</li><li>to look after (<i>e.g. health</i>)</li><li>to keep an eye on</li><li>to leave traces or footprints behind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slěditi</i>. Equivalent to <i>след</i> (sled) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>investigative committee</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сле́дственный комите́т</i> (slédstvennyj komitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement</li><li>compliance</li></ol><p><i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law enforcement</i>) investigator, detective</li></ol><p><i>сле́довать</i> (slédovatʹ, “to follow”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consequently; therefore</li><li>so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow (move continually behind someone or something) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘behind whom/what’</small>]</li><li>to follow (in sequence), to be next [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘behind whom/what’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to follow (logically, consequentially), to be a consequence, to ensue</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be necessary, proper, expected or worthwhile (used in constructions meaning "should(n't)", "must(n't)" etc., &#91;<i>with</i> dative <small>‘who’</small>&#93;)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to follow (to be guided by something, act in accordance with something) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(official,&#32;of trains, buses etc.) to follow a route, to be bound (for), to travel, to go</li></ol><p><i>след</i> (sled) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p><p>(<i>to be necessary, proper, expected or worthwhile</i>): For the relation with the derived term <i>пресле́довать</i> (preslédovatʹ), compare typologically <i>пови́нность</i> (povínnostʹ) (<…~ <i>вина́</i> (viná) <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i>).</p><p>(<i>to go</i>): Compare typologically English <i>march</i> (akin to <i>mark</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(immediately) after, (right) behind [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hunting</i>) tracker</li><li>someone who searches historical materials for traces of past events</li></ol><p><i>след</i> (sled) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пыт</i> (-pyt), combining form of <i>пыта́ть</i> (pytátʹ, “(<i>originally</i>) to examine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) investigation; investigative, investigatory</li></ol><p><i>сле́дствие</i> (slédstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect, consequence</li><li>investigation, inquest</li><li>trial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the following</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>сле́дующий</i> (slédujuščij, “next, following”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following</li><li>next</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as follows, thuswise, this way, in the following way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tracking, following, tracing</li></ol><p><i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadowing</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>слега́</i> (slegá): small pole</li></ol><p>From <i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with unexpected iotation.</p><p><i>слега́</i> (slegá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear (a drop of liquid from the eyes)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сльза</i> (slĭza), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slьza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come down, to get down <i>(from)</i>, to dismount <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(from a train, bus etc)</i> to get out <i>(of)</i>, to get off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peel off, to come off</li></ol><p><i>слезть</i> (sleztʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>слеза́</i> (slezá): tear, teardrop</li></ol><p><i>слеза́</i> (slezá) +&lrm; <i>-и́нка</i> (-ínka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to water</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>слеза́</i> (slezá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given to crying; schmaltzy; tearful, lachrymose</li></ol><p><i>слеза́</i> (slezá) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lachrymatory, watering, causing tears (from eyes)</li></ol><p><i>слезоточи́ть</i> (slezotočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear gas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear up, to shed tears</li></ol><p><i>слеза́</i> (slezá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “to shed”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come down, to get down <i>(from)</i>, to dismount <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(from a train, bus etc)</i> to get out <i>(of)</i>, to get off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peel off, to come off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лезть</i> (leztʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) slang</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>slang</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) slang</li></ol><p><i>сленг</i> (slɛ́ng) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsefly, gadfly (a blood-sucking fly in the family Tabanidae)</li></ol><p><i>слепо́й</i> (slepój, “blind”) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ), referring to the fact that some horsefly species appear to go blind while in the process of sucking blood and can be easily removed, compare also Ukrainian <i>сліпи́й о́від</i> (slipýj óvid), German <i>Blindbremse</i>, Norwegian Nynorsk <i>blinding</i> (“deer fly”) and dialectal Swedish <i>blinning</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blind man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slěpь̀cь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>слепо́й</i> (slepój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to model, to sculpture, to sculpt, to fashion, to shape</li><li>to build, to make (cells, nest, etc.)</li><li>to stick together</li><li>to blind, to dazzle</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лепи́ть</i> (lepítʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slěpiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>слепо́й</i> (slepój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/go blind, to lose one's sight</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>слепо́й</i> (slepój) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>blindly</li></ol><p><i>слепо́й</i> (slepój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deafblind</li></ol><p>слепо́й (slepój) + -о (-o) + глухо́й (gluxój) + -о (-o) + немо́й (nemój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blind snake (Scolecophidia spp.)</li></ol><p><i>слепо́й</i> (slepój, “blind”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>зме́йка</i>&nbsp;f (zméjka, “snakeling, snakelet”), literally “blind snakeling”. Compare English <i>blind snake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind (unable to see)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) blind man, blind person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slěpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mould, cast</li></ol><p><i>слепи́ть</i> (slepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blindness (condition of being blind)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blinding, dazzling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) metal workshop, locksmithery</li></ol><p><i>сле́сарь</i> (slésarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locksmith</li><li>metalworker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlosser</i> (“locksmith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly down <i>(from)</i>, to fly away <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall down <i>(from)</i>, to fall <i>(off)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose, to tumble <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peel <i>(off)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go, to be gone</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to crash</li><li>to fly there and back</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be there and back <i>(again)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лета́ть</i> (letátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly down <i>(from)</i>, to fly away <i>(from)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall down <i>(from)</i>, to fall <i>(off)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose, to tumble <i>(from)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to peel <i>(off)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go, to be gone</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get seriously ill</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>typography</i>) slash ("&#47;" symbol)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>slash</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discharge, drain, outlet</li><li>weathering, wash</li><li>leak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum (fruit or tree)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>слива</i> (sliva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sliva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out, to pour off, to empty, to drain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to merge, to fuse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to uncover some information about</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) to have a piss</li></ol><p><i>слить</i> (slitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow together, to interflow</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to merge, to fuse, to blend</li></ol><p><i>слива́ть</i> (slivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cream (milk fat)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) elite</li></ol><p>From <i>слива́ть</i> (slivátʹ, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-лить</i> (-litʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;liti</i>, + <i>-ка</i> (-ka) in plural.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plum</li></ol><p><i>сли́ва</i> (slíva) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cream, creamy</li><li>cream-colored</li></ol><p><i>сли́вки</i> (slívki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick off</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to destroy, to eliminate (<i>of fire or water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лиза́ть</i> (lizátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mucus; mucous, mucic</li></ol><p><i>слизь</i> (slizʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slug (gastropod)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slizь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) mucous membrane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mucous (relating to mucus)</li><li>slimy, pituitous</li></ol><p>From <i>слизь</i> (slizʹ) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick off (<i>once</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to destroy, to eliminate (<i>of fire or water; once</i>)</li><li>to be covered with slime</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лизну́ть</i> (liznútʹ) or from <i>слиза́ть</i> (slizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p><p><i>слизь</i> (slizʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) slug</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a slimeball</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a weak-willed, worthless person</li></ol><p>From <i>сли́зень</i> (slízenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lick off</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to destroy, to eliminate (<i>of fire or water</i>)</li></ol><p><i>слиза́ть</i> (slizátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mucus</li><li>slime</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slizь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fade</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skip off, to skip out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>линя́ть</i> (linjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick together, to stick to each other</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick together, to stick to each other</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ли́пнуть</i> (lípnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>conjointly</li><li>(written) in one word (with no spaces or hyphens)</li></ol><p><i>сли́тный</i> (slítnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conjoint, joint, cohesive</li><li>written together (without spaces)</li></ol><p><i>слить</i> (slitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ingot, bar (e.g. of gold), bullion</li></ol><p><i>слить</i> (slitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out, to pour off, to empty, to drain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to merge, to fuse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to uncover some information about</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) to have a piss</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow together, to interflow</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to merge, to fuse, to blend</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to drop out, to dismiss</li></ol><p><i>слить</i> (slitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>too (more than enough), too much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merger (the act or process of merging)</li><li>fusion (merging of elements into a union)</li><li>confluence (point where two rivers or streams meet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sloboda (a large village, settlement, or suburb, usually populated by non-serf (free) peasants, and initially granted freedom from taxes and forced labor duties)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svoboda</i>, cognate with свобо́да (svobóda, “freedom”), in reference to such settlements' initial freedom from taxes and forced labor duties.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slovak (native of Slovakia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>слова́рь</i> (slovárʹ, “dictionary”)</li></ol><p><i>слова́рь</i> (slovárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dictionary</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) vocabulary</li></ol><p><i>слова́рь</i> (slovárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dictionary, wordbook</li><li>vocabulary, glossary</li><li>lexicon</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Slovak, Slovakian</li></ol><p><i>слова́к</i> (slovák) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Slovene (person from Slovenia)</li></ol><p><i>Слове́ния</i> (Slovénija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Slovenian</li></ol><p><i>Слове́ния</i> (Slovénija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) words, text, verbiage</li></ol><p>Archaic plural of <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) (originally an <i>s</i>-stem).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>literature</li><li>lore, folklore</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) philology</li></ol><p><i>слове́сный</i> (slovésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>verbal, oral</li><li>philological</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>словесьнъ</i> (slovesĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo): word</li></ol><p><i>словцо́</i> (slovcó) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Slovincian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Slovio language (a constructed language based on the Pan-Slavic "esperanto" that speakers of Slavic languages easily slip into to communicate with speakers of other Slavic languages)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wordlist</li></ol><p>From <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as if, like (<i>for expressing conditional, imaginary, or apparent things or actions</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as if, like</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сло́вно</i> (slóvno)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сло́вно</i> (slóvno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word, term</li><li>speech, address</li><li>promise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>слово</i> (slovo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slȍvo</i> (“word”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>word for word (in exactly the same words)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rambling, yammering, verbiage, verbal gymnastics; empty, vapid talk, idle chatter</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>блуд</i> (blud, “lechery”) or <i>блуди́ть</i> (bludítʹ, “to whore around”) + <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) "slovoyers", ironical term for the honorific Russian suffix <i>-с</i> (-s) (an abbreviated form of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ), or <i>sir</i>) that was formerly used to emphasize the speaker’s politeness or servility before a person of higher social status.</li></ol><p>From the words <i>«сло́во ― еръ ― съ»</i> («slóvo ― jer ― s») from a rote memorization exercise in school spelling classes; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) and <i>ер</i> (jer) were the old names of the Cyrillic letters с and ъ respectively. Compare to the English ampersand.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) (word) inflection</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>измене́ние</i> (izmenénije, “change”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type foundry</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лить</i> (litʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in short, in a word</li></ol><p>From <i>слово</i> (slovo) +&lrm; <i>-ом</i> (-om).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) word formation (formation of new words)</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ние</i> (obrazovánije, “formation”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) word-formative, word-formational, derivational</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ть</i> (obrazovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, loquacious, garrulous</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>охо́та</i> (oxóta) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument, dispute</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>преть</i> (pretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) word combination, phrase</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сочета́ние</i> (sočetánije, “combination”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) wordform, inflected form</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma, “form”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo): word</li></ol><p><i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-цо́</i> (-có)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) syllable</li><li>style, manner of writing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сълогъ</i> (sŭlogŭ). By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) + the root of <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slogan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>slogan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) syllable; syllabic</li></ol><p>From <i>слог</i> (slog) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) syllabification, syllabication</li></ol><p><i>слог</i> (slog) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>деле́ние</i> (delénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adding, composing</li><li>composition</li><li>laying down, resignation</li><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) addition</li><li>constitution, build</li></ol><p><i>сложи́ть</i> (složítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-established, mature</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay together, to put together, to pile up, to heap, to stack</li><li>to pack up</li><li>to add, to sum up</li><li>to make, to assemble, to put together</li><li><i>song, rhyme</i> to compose, to make up</li><li>to fold</li><li>to compose</li><li>to take off, to put down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ). See also Serbo-Croatian <i>složiti</i> and Czech <i>složit</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chip in; to club together (with), to pool (with), to pool one's money / resources</li><li>to (be) form(ed), to develop, to turn out, to take shape; to arise (<i>of a situation, relationship</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сложи́ть</i> (složítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>сло́жно</i> (slóžno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a complicated manner</li></ol><p><i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemical compound</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complexity, complicacy, complication, difficulty</li></ol><p>From <i>сло́жный</i> (slóžnyj, “complicated”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complex, compound</li><li>complicated, intricate</li></ol><p><i>слож(и́ть)</i> (slož(ítʹ), “to pile up; to fold”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) ester</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a certain type of body</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) nimbostratus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) stratocumulus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>layered</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) stratus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to layer, to arrange in layers</li></ol><p><i>слой</i> (sloj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layer</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) stratum</li><li>coat, coating (of paint, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъlojь</i>. Cognate with <i>слить</i> (slitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>layer (single thickness of some material covering a surface)</li><li>a puff pastry bun</li></ol><p>From <i>слои́ть</i> (sloítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulling down, demolition, destruction</li><li>fracture, break</li><li><i>(mental)</i> collapse, breakdown</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to fracture</li><li>to demolish, to pull down (a house)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to destroy, to break down, to ruin</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лома́ть</i> (lomátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, to break up, to fracture, to split</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break down (of a device, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to collapse, to fall apart</li><li>to crack, to break (of the voice)</li></ol><p><i>слома́ть</i> (slomátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to crush <i>(a person or an abstract noun—not a physical object)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ломи́ть</i> (lomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) bishop</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually about children) one who walks loudly or heavily; stomper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slonъ</i>, possibly a deverbative from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sloniti sę</i> (“to lean against”), relating to а medieval story of а sleeping elephant who leaned against a tree. According to some other sources, it is related to the Turkish <i>arslan</i> (“lion”).</p><p>Vovin (2011) proposes that Slavic <i>slonъ</i> reflects Old Chinese <i>象</i> (&#42;ljaŋʔ /⁠*slaŋ⁠/, “elephant”), an etymology previously suggested by Ivanov (1977: 156–57) albeit with an incorrect Old Chinese reconstruction *<i>sðaŋ</i>. As Vovin notes, contact between Slavic and Old Chinese is out of the question, so the solution might arise from an intermediary source. Ivanov (1977:154) believes that the Chuvash forms <i>слон</i> (slon), <i>сӑлан</i> (sălan, “elephant”) are Russian loans, with the latter being called into question by Vovin on phonetic grounds. According to him, Russian /o/ (phonetically a diphthong [uo] with a mid-high syllabic element [o]) is unlikely to be borrowed as Chuvash low vowel /a/. The reverse, namely the borrowing of Bulgar slightly labialised /a/ as Slavic /o/ is more than likely. Chuvash <i>сӑлан</i> (sălan, “elephant”) is exactly the expected outcome of the Old Chinese <i>*slaŋ</i> with the insertion of ⟨ă⟩ breaking the OC initial cluster /sl-/ and typical Bulgar shift of Proto-Turkic *ŋ to /n/. The presence of this word in Chuvash places proto-Bulgar speakers in the vicinity of Northern China no later than first century BCE, because approximately after that date the initial clusters in Old Chinese underwent the process of simplification.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) elephantiasis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivory paper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivory</li></ol><p>Literally, “elephant's bone”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elephant; elephantine, elephant's</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) ivory</li></ol><p><i>слон</i> (slon, “elephant”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heffalump</li><li>(<i>humorous</i>) clumsy, awkward person</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>слон</i> (slon, “elephant”) + <i>гиппопота́м</i> (gippopotám, “hippo”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to linger, to loaf, to loiter about</li></ol><p><i>-слони́ть</i> (-slonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant calf, baby elephant</li></ol><p><i>слон</i> (slon) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to devour, to gobble</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ло́пать</i> (lópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant</li><li>clothes valet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sluga</i>. Cognates include Irish <i>slua</i>, Lithuanian <i>slaugà</i> (“servitude”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>employee</li><li>office worker, white-collar worker</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used <i>(for, to)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service</li><li>employment, job</li><li>office, work, post</li><li>duty</li><li>divine service</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;služьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба</i> (-ba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) service book, ordinal, missal</li></ol><p><i>слу́жба</i> (slúžba) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) office</li><li>official</li><li>secondary, subordinate, subservient</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) relational</li></ol><p><i>слу́жба</i> (slúžba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) service, ministration</li></ol><p><i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>служе́ние</i> (služénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant</li><li>servant</li><li>acolyte</li><li>warden</li><li>page</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>слоужитель</i> (služitelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to serve, to act, to work <i>(as)</i>, to be</li><li>to be <i>(something)</i>, to serve <i>(as)</i></li><li>to be used <i>(for)</i>, to serve <i>(for)</i>, to do <i>(for)</i></li><li>to be in use, to do one's duty</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to serve, to officiate</li><li>to sit up and beg (<i>of a dog</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>служи́ти</i> (služíti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;služìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fleece someone <i>(of a certain amount)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лупи́ть</i> (lupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing, ear</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) rumor, hearsay, <i>diminutive</i>: <i>слушо́к</i> (slušók)</li><li>news, sign</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>слухъ</i> (sluxŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sluxъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>, whence Sanskrit <i>श्रुति</i> (śruti), English <i>listen</i>, and <i>loud</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gossip (idle talk), rumors</li><li>talk, buzz</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hearing; auditory</li></ol><p><i>слух</i> (slux) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by any chance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>case</li><li>occurrence, event</li><li>occasion</li><li>opportunity, chance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъlučajь</i>, from <i>&#42;lučiti</i>. Compare <i>случи́ться</i> (slučítʹsja), <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accidentally, by accident</li><li>randomly, casually</li><li>by chance, unpredictably</li><li>by any chance</li></ol><p><i>случа́йный</i> (slučájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>random number (any of a sequence of random numbers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chance</li><li>fortuity, fortuitousness</li></ol><p><i>случа́йный</i> (slučájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accidental, casual, fortuitous, chance</li><li>random, unpredictable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъlučajьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>слу́чай</i> (slúčaj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;impersonal</i>) to happen, to occur, to come about [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>случи́ться</i> (slučítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something that happened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;impersonal</i>) to happen, to occur, to come about [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ, “to allow, originally accomplish”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Not related to <i>случи́ть</i> (slučítʹ, “to bring together, to mate animals”) (see <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coupling, pairing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>listening (act of listening)</li><li>hearing (proceeding at which discussions are heard)</li></ol><p><i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>listener, hearer, audience (someone who listens)</li><li>student</li></ol><p><i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ, “to hear, listen”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to listen, to listen to (someone/something)</li><li>to attend</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to auscultate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slušati</i>, from an earlier <i>&#42;sluxěti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>, whence English <i>listen</i> and Sanskrit <i>श्रोषति</i> (śroṣati, “to listen, obey”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obey, to take (someone's) advice, to listen to</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to obey, to be under control (<i>of a mechanism or body part</i>)</li></ol><p><i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>, whence English <i>listen</i> and possibly <i>slave</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) at your service, milord</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) at your service, milady</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>слу́шаю</i> (slúšaju) +&lrm; <i>-с</i> (-s), which is an abbreviated form of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ)/<i>суда́рыня</i> (sudárynja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass (for), to have the reputation (for), to be said / reputed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slyti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i> (“to hear”). Compare Ancient Greek <i>κλύω</i> (klúō, “to hear, listen”), <i>κλυτός</i> (klutós, “famous”), Latin <i>inclitus</i> (“renowned”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hear of, to hear from</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>слыха́ть</i> (slyxátʹ), iterative of <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>слыха́ть</i> (slyxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to smell, to feel (a smell)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slỳšati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be heard</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be felt, to be smelled</li></ol><p><i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audibility</li></ol><p><i>слы́шимый</i> (slýšimyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>audible (being able to be heard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>audibly</li></ol><p><i>слы́шный</i> (slýšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>audible (able to be heard)</li></ol><p><i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>слы́шишь</i> (slýšišʹ)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey!</li></ol><p>Literally, “do you [familiar] hear”. Imperative form of слышать, as well.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>слы́шите</i> (slýšite)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>слеш</i> (slɛš)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mica (hydrous aluminosilicate minerals)</li></ol><p>Compare Czech <i>slída</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mica</li></ol><p><i>слюда́</i> (sljudá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saliva, spittle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) saliva, drool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slina</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leyH-</i> (“to smear”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>слина</i> (slina), Bulgarian <i>слю́на</i> (sljúna), Czech <i>slina</i>, Polish <i>ślina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>слюна́</i> (sljuná)</li></ol><p><i>слюна́</i> (sljuná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>слю́ни</i> (sljúni, “slobber, drool”)</li></ol><p><i>слю́ни</i> (sljúni) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salivary gland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salivary</li><li>salival</li></ol><p><i>слюна́</i> (sljuná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) sissy, driveller, sniveller</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bib (item of clothing for babies)</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>слюня́вить</i> (sljunjávitʹ, “to slobber, to drool”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slushily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) slush, mire, crud, mud</li></ol><p><i>сля́коть</i> (sljákotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slush, mire, crud, mud</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slękъtь</i>. Compare Polish <i>śląkwa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw together, to slap together (to make something carelessly, hurriedly)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ля́пать</i> (ljápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tear (water from the eyes); lacrimal</li><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) teary, tearful</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) plaintive, sorrowful, tearful</li></ol><p><i>слеза́</i> (slezá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convent, rally, meeting, congress, gathering</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>зліт</i> (zlit), Belarusian <i>злёт</i> (zljot)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>след</i> (sled).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>слепо́й</i> (slepój).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cm, centimeter</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>сантиме́тр</i> (santimétr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>cf., v., q.v., refer to, compare with</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>смотри́</i> (smotrí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cm/s, centimeters per second</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>сантиме́тров в секу́нду</i> (santimétrov v sekúndu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) heat; dryness in the mouth</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) thirst</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) soot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smaga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear, to grease, to oil, to lubricate, to dub</li><li>to smear, to wipe off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grease someone's palm</li><li><i>(a question, a problem)</i> to slur</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) to blur</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bash, to dot</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearing, greasing, oiling, lubrication</li><li>lubricant</li></ol><p><i>сма́зать</i> (smázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pretty, cute</li></ol><p><i>сма́зать</i> (smázatʹ, “to oil, to lubricate”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) smearing, greasing, oiling, lubrication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearing, greasing, oiling, lubrication</li><li>slurring</li></ol><p><i>сма́зывать</i> (smázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear, to grease, to oil, to lubricate, to dub</li><li>to smear, to wipe off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grease someone's palm</li><li><i>(a question, a problem)</i> to slur</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) to blur</li></ol><p><i>сма́зать</i> (smázatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smiley (representation of a smiling face&#59; sequence of keyboard characters)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>сма́йлик</i> (smájlik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) smiley</li></ol><p>First attested in 1997 [RNC]. From English <i>smiley</i> +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gusto, pleasant taste</li><li>(<i>figuratively&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) zest, relish, gusto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>smak</i> (“taste”), from Old High German <i>gismac</i> (“taste”) or Middle High German <i>gesmac(h)</i> (“taste”); compare German <i>Geschmack</i> (“taste”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to relish, to savor</li></ol><p><i>смак</i> (smak) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease, lard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smalt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>smalto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manoeuvre, to maneuver, to make evolutions</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>маневри́ровать</i> (manevrírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entice, to lure</li></ol><p><i>смани́ть</i> (smanítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) emerald</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic,&#32;relational) emerald</li></ol><p><i>смара́гд</i> (smarágd) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smartphone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to contrive</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мастери́ть</i> (masterítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind completely, to wind up (a spool of rope, a fishing line, etc.)</li><li>to unwind</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) (<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>смота́ть</i> (smotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to leave, to scram, to skedaddle, to vamoose</li></ol><p><i>сма́тывать</i> (smátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisk <i>(away, off)</i>, to flap <i>(away, off)</i>, to flick away, to brush <i>(off)</i></li><li>to look like, to resemble, to smack <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p><i>смахну́ть</i> (smaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisk <i>(away, off)</i>, to flap <i>(away, off)</i>, to flick away, to brush <i>(off)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>махну́ть</i> (maxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten, to wet</li></ol><p><i>смочи́ть</i> (smočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) deliciously, appetizingly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strongly, expressively</li></ol><p><i>сма́чный</i> (smáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) delicious, appetizing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong, expressive, precise</li></ol><p><i>смак</i> (smak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjoined, contiguous</li><li>related</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>межа́</i> (mežá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>keen-witted, sharp-witted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keenness of wit, sagacity</li></ol><p><i>смека́ть</i> (smekátʹ, “to understand, to get”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;intransitive) to understand, to get it, to catch on, to realize</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мека́ть</i> (mekátʹ, “to understand, to think (<i>dialectal</i>)”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mětiti</i>. The transition /t'/ to /k/ instead of the expected /ч/ indicates that the source of the word was the Old Novgorod or Old Pskov dialects, where this transition was regular, compare the dialectal (Pskov) <i>сустрека́ть</i> (sustrekátʹ) 'to meet'.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;intransitive) to understand, to get it, to catch on, to realize</li></ol><p><i>смека́ть</i> (smekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>смёл</i> (smjol)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow bolder</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>сме́лый</i> (smélyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>courageously, daringly, audaciously, boldly, bravely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boldness, courage; audacity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmělostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сме́лый</i> (smélyj, “bold, courageous”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>courageous, daring, audacious, bold, brave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съмѣлъ</i> (sŭmělŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmělъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>сметь</i> (smetʹ, “to dare”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daredevil, a brave man</li></ol><p>From <i>сме́лый</i> (smélyj). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change, replacement</li><li>shift</li><li>(<i>military</i>) relief</li><li>supersession</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) successors</li><li>Smena (Soviet 35 mm film camera)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ме́на</i> (ména)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, to replace</li><li>to succeed (to come after)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-мени́ть</i> (-menítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, to change [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘into someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to alternate, to take turns</li><li>to be relieved [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from a work shift’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>смени́ть</i> (smenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shift (work, etc.)</li><li>replaceable, removable</li></ol><p><i>сме́на</i> (sména) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) Smenovekhovist, Smenovekhovtsy</li></ol><p>From <i>сме́на</i> (sména, “change, replacement”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ха</i> (véxa, “milestone, landmark”) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p><p>Formed shortly after the publication of the magazine <i>Smena Vekh</i> (“Change of Signposts”) in Prague in 1921.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who alternates shifts with another (<i>e.g. on the same machine</i>)</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) understudy</li></ol><p><i>сме́на</i> (sména) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to change, to replace</li><li>to succeed (to come after)</li></ol><p><i>смени́ть</i> (smenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn, to change [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘into someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to alternate, to take turns</li><li>to be relieved [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘from a work shift’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to exchange, to swap</li></ol><p><i>сменя́ть</i> (smenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a low-class peasant, smerd</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) peasant; serf; slave</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>смръдъ</i> (smrŭdŭ, “someone who stinks”), related to <i>смерде́ть</i> (smerdétʹ, “to stink”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>смерд</i> (smerd), Belarusian <i>смерд</i> (smjerd) or Belarusian <i>смердзь</i> (smjerdzʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stink, to smell bad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>смьрдѣти</i> (smĭrděti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smьrdě̀ti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;smirˀdḗˀtei</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to measure</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dark (<i>referring to the day</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сме́ркнуться</i> (smérknutʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become dark (<i>referring to the day</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ркнуть</i> (mérknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mortally, fatally</li><li>grievously (<i>insulted</i>)</li><li>utterly (<i>bored, exhausted, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>смерте́льный</i> (smertélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortal, deadly, lethal, fatal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) death; death-defying</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Polish <i>śmiertelny</i>, probably via Old Ruthenian <i>смерте́льный</i> (smertélʹnyj), ultimately from Old Czech <i>smrtedlný</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death penalty, capital punishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person sentenced to death</li><li>suicide bomber, kamikaze (someone who performs a suicide attack)</li></ol><p>From <i>смерть</i> (smertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortality</li><li>death rate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmьrtьnostь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortal (susceptible to death)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) death</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortal (person susceptible to death)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmьrtьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>death-bearing, deadly, mortal, fatal</li></ol><p><i>смерть</i> (smertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) murder</li></ol><p><i>сме́рть</i> (smértʹ) +&lrm; <i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) Death</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) an ugly woman, a hag</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective) rags; ugly, shabby things (<i>usually of clothing</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;used as an intensifier) dying to, dying for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съмьрть</i> (sŭmĭrtĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmьrtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) tornado, twister, whirlwind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>смьрьчь</i> (smĭrĭčĭ, “cloud”). Cognates include <i>сморка́ть</i> (smorkátʹ, “to blow one's nose”), <i>морок</i> (morok, “darkness, cloud, fog”) and <i>мрак</i> (mrak, “darkness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>сме́рить</i> (sméritʹ)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>сме́рить</i> (sméritʹ), due to having the same pronunciation in the infinitive.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plumbing</i>) mixer</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) commingler, stirrer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brush off, to sweep off, to wipe off</li><li>to carry away (by wind, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to destroy, to dispel, to eliminate</li><li>to collect into one place (by raking or sweeping)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to displace, to move</li><li>to remove <i>(from position)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move, to heave</li></ol><p><i>смести́ть</i> (smestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blend, mix, mixture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account, estimate, budget (reckoning, calculation)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;съмѣта</i> (&#42;sŭměta); see <i>сме́тить</i> (smétitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour cream; smetana</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmetana</i>, cognate with Slovene <i>smetana</i>, Slovak <i>smotana</i>, Bulgarian <i>сметана</i> (smetana). According to Vasmer, the Slavic words are unrelated to German <i>Schmand, Schmant</i> (“sour milk”). However, other scholars (Jacob Grimm, Václav Machek) consider <i>Schmand, Schmant</i> to be a loanword from Slavic. See <i>Schmand</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smetannik (a type of sponge cake made with smetana)</li><li>a vessel for smetana</li></ol><p><i>смета́н(а)</i> (smetán(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel for smetana</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) smetana</li></ol><p><i>смета́н(а)</i> (smetán(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brush off, to sweep off, to wipe off</li><li>to carry away (by wind, etc.)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to destroy, to dispel, to eliminate</li><li>to stack, to rick</li><li>to baste, to tack <i>(together)</i></li></ol><p><i>смет-</i> (smet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to throw”)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мета́ть</i> (metátʹ, “to baste, to tack”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>keen-witted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare, to make bold, to be so bold as to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmě̀ti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;usually&#32;uncountable) garbage, junk, rubbish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmetьje</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>сміття́</i> (smittjá) or <i>смі́ття</i> (smíttja), Belarusian <i>сме́цце</i> (smjéccje) or dialectal <i>смяццё</i> (smjaccjó), Polish <i>śmiecie</i>, Czech <i>smetí</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>smeće</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laugh, laughter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;směxъ</i>. Cognate with <i>смея́ться</i> (smejátʹsja, “to laugh at”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) laugh, laughter</li></ol><p><i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ridiculous, ludicrous</li><li>small, insignificant</li></ol><p><i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lulz, funny stuff, jokes</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>смешки́</i> (smeškí, “laughs, fun”) +&lrm; <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “penis, cock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abashed (embarrassed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mixed, hybrid</li><li>composite</li><li>miscellaneous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mix, to mingle, to blend</li><li>to mix up the order of</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mess up</li><li>to confuse, to mix up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>меша́ть</i> (mešátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mix, to blend (in), to mingle, to intermingle with [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to merge</li><li>to get mixed up, to be jumbled</li><li>to become confused</li></ol><p><i>смеша́ть</i> (smešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixing, mixture; blending, merging</li></ol><p>From archaic <i>смеси́ть</i> (smesítʹ, “to mix”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije); see. The same verb produces other derivatives that are still in use, such as <i>смеси́тель</i> (smesítelʹ, “mixer”). See also <i>смеша́ть</i> (smešátʹ), the normal-register synonym of <i>смеси́ть</i> (smesítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixing, a mixing</li></ol><p><i>сме́шивать</i> (sméšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix, to mix up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mess up</li><li>to confuse, to mix up</li></ol><p><i>смеша́ть</i> (smešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mix, to blend (in), to mingle, to intermingle with [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>сме́шивать</i> (sméšivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (someone) laugh</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make an unpleasant impression (on someone)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;směšiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone to laugh, giggly (<i>of a person or mood</i>)</li></ol><p><i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>смешно́й</i> (smešnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>смешно́</i> (smešnó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>смешно́й</i> (smešnój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>смешно́</i> (smešnó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a funny manner/way, comically</li><li>(predicative,&#32;impersonal) it is nice</li></ol><p><i>смешно́й</i> (smešnój) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>funny, comic, droll</li><li>laughable, ridiculous, ludicrous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;směšьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chuckle, short laugh</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) jokes, jeers</li></ol><p><i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to displace, to move</li><li>to remove <i>(from position)</i></li></ol><p><i>смести́ть</i> (smestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift, to move, to heave</li></ol><p><i>смеща́ть</i> (smeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>displacement, shift</li></ol><p><i>смести́ть</i> (smestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh</li><li>to laugh at (над кем, над чем)</li><li>to mock, to deride</li></ol><p><i>-смея́ть</i> (-smejátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smьjati</i> + <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh in one's sleeve (to laugh in secret or to oneself, usually gloatingly)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to laugh in one's fist”. Compare Polish <i>śmiać się w kułak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to crumple, to wrinkle, to fumble, to crease</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-мина́ть</i> (-minátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humility, meekness; humbleness; resignation (state of uncomplaining)</li></ol><p><i>смири́ть</i> (smirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abjectly (with great shame, desperately&#59; in an abject fashion)</li></ol><p><i>смире́нный</i> (smirénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humility, meekness</li></ol><p><i>смире́нный</i> (smirénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humble, meek, submissive</li><li>abject (showing resignation)</li></ol><p><i>смири́ть</i> (smirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straitjacket (a jacket-like garment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble, to tame</li><li>to suppress, to restrain (an emotion)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съмѣрити</i> (sŭměriti, “mitigate”). After regular assimilation <i>ѣ</i>…<i>и́</i> &gt; <i>и</i>…<i>и́</i> reanalysed as a derivative <i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become reconciled [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to/with someone’</small>]</li><li>to give in</li></ol><p><i>смири́ть</i> (smirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>змѵ́рьна</i> (zmǘrĭna), from Ancient Greek <i>σμύρνα</i> (smúrna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>сми́рный</i> (smírnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>сми́рно</i> (smírno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quietly (in a quiet manner)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) attention! Used as a command to bring soldiers to the attention position.</li></ol><p><i>сми́рный</i> (smírnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quiet, mild, meek</li></ol><p>From <i>смир(и́ть)</i> (smir(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble, to tame</li><li>to suppress, to restrain (an emotion)</li></ol><p><i>смири́ть</i> (smirítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become reconciled [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘to/with someone’</small>]</li><li>to give in</li></ol><p><i>смиря́ть</i> (smirjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>smog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to model, to design, to fashion, to shape</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;engineering) to simulate</li><li>(<i>art</i>) to model, to sculpture</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>модели́ровать</i> (modɛlírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;folkloric) dragon (mythical creature)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig</li><li>fig tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smoky</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuxedo</li><li>dinner jacket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>smoking</i>, from English <i>smoking jacket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smokъtati</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>смокта́ти</i> (smoktáty), Belarusian <i>смакта́ць</i> (smaktácʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resin, pitch, tar, coal tar (viscous liquid of plant origin)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>смола</i> (smola), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smola</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Smolensk</li></ol><p><i>Смоле́н(ск)</i> (Smolén(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resinous</li></ol><p><i>смол(а́)</i> (smol(á)) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resin, to tar, to pitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smoke, puff</li></ol><p><i>смола</i> (smola) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(sound, noise)</i> to cease, to stop</li><li>to grow silent, to fall into silence, to fall silent</li></ol><p><i>смо́лкнуть</i> (smólknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(sound, noise)</i> to cease, to stop</li><li>to grow silent, to fall into silence, to fall silent</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лкнуть</i> (mólknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar works</li></ol><p><i>смола́</i> (smolá, “tar”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from a young age</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tar works</li></ol><p><i>смола́</i> (smolá, “tar”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кури́ть</i> (kurítʹ, “to distill”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thresh, to flail</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>молоти́ть</i> (molotítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind, to mill (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to talk nonsense</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>моло́ть</i> (molótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/remain silent, to say nothing, to hold one's tongue</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) resin, tar</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) resinous, tarry</li></ol><p><i>смола́</i> (smolá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Smolensk</li></ol><p><i>Смоле́нск</i> (Smolénsk) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) girl studying at the Smolny Institute</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) lady's smock</li></ol><p><i>смоля́н(ин)</i> (smolján(in)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p><i>Смо́ль(ный институ́т)</i> (Smólʹ(nyj institút)) +&lrm; <i>-я́нка</i> (-jánka)</p><p><i>смоляно́й</i> (smoljanój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tar, pitch</li><li>tarry (resembling tar)</li><li>tarred (covered with tar)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) jet-black, pitch-black, glossy black</li></ol><p><i>смола́</i> (smolá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assemble (to put together)</li><li>to mount, to arrange (cinema)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>монти́ровать</i> (montírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow (one's nose)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>смъркати</i> (smŭrkati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smъrkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow one's nose</li></ol><p><i>сморка́ть</i> (smorkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) stink, stench</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>смородъ</i> (smorodŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smordъ</i>; cognate with Ukrainian <i>смо́рід</i> (smórid), Belarusian <i>смо́рад</i> (smórad). Doublet of <i>смрад</i> (smrad), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>currant (berry and plant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smordina</i>, by surface analysis, (<i>obsolete</i>) <i>смо́род</i> (smórod, “stench”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), because of the smell of its leaves. For the semantics compare German <i>Stinkbaum</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Смородина)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) the Smorodina River (in medieval Russian folklore, a river of fiery sulphur that separates the worlds of the living and the dead)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smordina</i>, by surface analysis, (<i>obsolete</i>) <i>смо́род</i> (smórod, “stench”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) currant (berry and plant)</li></ol><p><i>сморо́дина</i> (smoródina) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) currant (berry and plant)</li></ol><p><i>сморо́дина</i> (smoródina) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to say (something ridiculous, nonsensical or stupid); to blurt</li></ol><p>Often thought to come from <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>моро́зить</i> (morózitʹ, “to freeze”), with the meaning "to make someone freeze from the stupid words spoken".</p><p>Alternatively, derived from <i>с-</i> (s-) + <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós, “stupid”) (cf. <i>moron</i>) + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) in seminarian argot.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) morel (any of several fungi in the genus <i>Morchella</i>, the upper part of which is covered with a reticulated and pitted hymenium)</li></ol><p><i>сморч(о́)к</i> (smorč(ó)k) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mycology</i>) morel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) wimp, nebbish (frail, nondescript man)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) shortie, runt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) act of blowing one's nose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smъrčьkъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>змо́ршок</i> (zmóršok), Belarusian <i>смарчок</i> (smarčók), Slovak <i>smrčok</i>, Macedonian <i>смрчак</i> (smrčak), Serbo-Croatian <i>смр́чак</i>, Slovene <i>smȓčək</i>, obsolete Czech <i>smrček</i>. Other languages have derivatives from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smъržь</i>, dialectal Ukrainian <i>сморж</i> (smorž) (gen. sg. <i>сморжа́</i> (smoržá)), <i>смарж</i> (smarž), <i>смордж</i> (smordž). also <i>сморжі́й</i> (smoržíj) (gen. sg. <i>сморжія́</i> (smoržijá)), <i>сморч</i> (smorč) (gen. sg. <i>сморча́</i> (smorčá)); Czech <i>smrž</i>; Slovak <i>smrž</i>, Polish <i>smarz</i>, also <i>smardz</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>smorž</i>, also <i>smorža</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>smarž</i>. Bulgarian has <i>смръ́чка</i> (smrǎ́čka), <i>смръ́чкула</i> (smrǎ́čkula), also Lower Sorbian <i>smargula</i> (<i>smaržel</i> after German <i>Morchel</i>), Czech and Slovak also <i>smrh</i>, <i>smrha</i>; sometimes an original <i>&#42;smъrkъ</i> or <i>&#42;smъrgъ</i> is suggested. Which form is earlier is unclear, but Vasmer suggests a possible relationship with <i>сморга́ть</i> (smorgátʹ, “to blink”), HJP with <i>&#42;smьrkъ</i> (“spruce”).</p><p><i>сморка́ть</i> (smorkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrinkled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wrinkle</li></ol><p><i>смо́рщить</i> (smórščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wrinkle (to become wrinkled)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrivel</li></ol><p><i>смо́рщивать</i> (smórščivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrinkle (one's nose)</li><li>to wrinkle (something)</li><li>to purse (one's lips)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мо́рщить</i> (mórščitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wrinkle (to become wrinkled)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrivel</li></ol><p><i>смо́рщить</i> (smórščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wind completely, to wind up (a spool of rope, a fishing line, etc.)</li><li>to unwind</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) (<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to leave, to scram, to skedaddle, to vamoose</li></ol><p><i>смота́ть</i> (smotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>went</i> (simple past of <i>go</i>, simple past and past participle of <i>wend</i>, akin to <i>wind</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) review, inspection</li><li>parade, show</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, to gaze</li><li>to see, to watch</li><li>to view, to review</li><li>to examine, to inspect</li><li>to mind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmotriti</i>. Compare <i>&#42;motriti</i> (“to look at, to watch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn a blind eye (to ignore or deliberately overlook)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to look/watch through one's fingers”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at oneself</li><li>to be acceptable to the eye, to look (well or bad)</li><li>to produce an effect</li></ol><p><i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traditional Russian wedding ceremony, where the groom, groom's family and the matchmaker could see and evaluate the prospective bride</li></ol><p>Has the form of the plural of <i>&#42;смотри́на</i> (&#42;smotrína), from <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervisor, inspector, keeper, custodian</li></ol><p><i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) inspection</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) observation, lookout</li></ol><p><i>смотр</i> (smotr) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with an interrogative</i>) depending on, it depends on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten, to wet</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мочи́ть</i> (močítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be able, to manage</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мочь</i> (močʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stench, stink</li></ol><p>Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>смрадъ</i> (smradŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smȏrdъ</i>. Displaced the native East Slavic form <i>смо́род</i> (smórod) from Old East Slavic <i>смородъ</i> (smorodŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>смерде́ть</i> (smerdétʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>смрад</i> (smrad) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stinking</li></ol><p>From <i>смрад</i> (smrad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>эсэмэ́ска</i> (esɛmɛ́ska)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dark-skinned, swarthy (<i>of complexion or skin colour/color</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;smǫglъ</i>, the past participle of <i>&#42;smǫgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dark-complexioned woman or girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be too clever, to complicate things unnecessarily</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мудри́ть</i> (mudrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) smoothie (drink)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>smoothie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) troubles, disturbance, revolt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) discord</li><li>turmoil, anxiety, distress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъmǫta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse, to embarrass</li><li>to daunt, to dismay, to discomfort</li><li>to disturb, to trouble, to stir up</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съмѫтити</i> (sŭmǫtiti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be confused, to be embarrassed</li></ol><p><i>смути́ть</i> (smutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vaguely, unclearly</li></ol><p><i>сму́тный</i> (smútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disturbed by troubles or revolts; chaotic</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) nebulous, indistinct, hazy, vague</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anxious, troubled, worried</li></ol><p><i>сму́та</i> (smúta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troublemaker, rabble-rouser</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revolt, to rebel, to incite a revolt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confuse, to embarrass</li><li>to daunt, to dismay, to discomfort</li><li>to disturb, to trouble, to stir up</li></ol><p><i>смути́ть</i> (smutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be confused, to be embarrassed</li></ol><p><i>смуща́ть</i> (smuščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abashment, confusion, embarrassment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съмѫщениѥ</i> (sŭmǫštenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>confusedly, embarrassedly</li></ol><p><i>смущённый</i> (smuščónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>embarrassed, confused (experiencing embarrassment or confusion) (<i>usually of a person</i>)</li><li>embarrassed, confused (expressing embarrassment or confusion) (<i>look, etc; usually inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash off, to flush</li><li>to wash away, to wash down</li><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to wash away, to redeem, to atone</li></ol><p><i>смыть</i> (smytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to link <i>(up)</i>, to close in, (military,&#32;sports) to close</li><li>to close</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-мыка́ть</i> (-mykátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to close ranks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close <i>(up)</i>, to close down <i>(upon)</i>, to close in <i>(on)</i>, (military,&#32;sports) to close</li><li>to close</li></ol><p><i>смыка́ть</i> (smykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense, meaning</li><li>respect</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) use</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>смы́слить</i> (smýslitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand</li><li>to be knowledgeable <i>(about)</i>, to know something <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semantic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) sense</li></ol><p><i>смысл</i> (smysl) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash off, to flush</li><li>to wash away, to wash down</li><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to wash away, to redeem, to atone</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip away, to sneak off</li></ol><p><i>смыть</i> (smytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiddlestick, bow <i>(rod used for playing stringed instruments)</i></li></ol><p>Cognate to Belarusian <i>смык</i> (smyk), Ukrainian <i>смичо́к</i> (smyčók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smart, intelligent</li></ol><p><i>смы́слить</i> (smýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to extenuate</li><li>to allay, to assuage, to mitigate, to alleviate</li><li>to tone down <i>light, colors</i></li><li>to relax <i>harshness</i></li><li>to mollify</li><li>to palatalize</li></ol><p><i>смягчи́ть</i> (smjaxčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to become soft(er)</li><li>to relent, to soften, to grow lenient</li><li>to ease <i>(off)</i>, to lessen, <i>(wind)</i> to relent</li><li><i>(weather)</i> to grow mild(er)</li></ol><p><i>смягча́ть</i> (smjaxčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) extenuating circumstance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mitigation, palliation</li><li>softening</li><li>alleviation, relief, assuagement</li><li>abatement</li><li>mollification</li><li>palatalization</li><li>give</li><li>easing off</li></ol><p><i>смягчи́ть</i> (smjaxčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, extenuate</li><li>to allay, to assuage, to mitigate, to alleviate</li><li>to tone down <i>light, colors</i></li><li>to relax <i>harshness</i></li><li>to mollify</li><li>to palatalize</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мя́гкий</i> (mjáxkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, to become soft(er)</li><li>to relent, to soften, to grow lenient</li><li>to ease <i>(off)</i>, to lessen, <i>(wind)</i> to relent</li><li><i>(weather)</i> to grow mild(er)</li></ol><p><i>смягчи́ть</i> (smjaxčítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>softened, mitigated, moderated, reduced, attenuated; softer, more moderate</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) softened (<i>of a formerly hard consonant</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion, disarray, tizzy</li><li>turmoil, agitation</li><li>tumult</li><li>commotion, whirl</li><li>perturbation</li><li>dither</li><li>alarm (sudden surprise with fear or terror)</li><li>distraction</li></ol><p>From verbal noun of Old East Slavic <i>смꙗсти</i> (smjasti), from <i>мꙗсти</i> (mjasti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;męsti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not smooth, wrinkled, wrinkly</li><li>crushed, trampled (<i>of plants</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rumple, to crumple, to wrinkle, to fumble, to crease</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>мять</i> (mjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield to pressure</li><li>to get rumpled, to crease, to wrinkle</li></ol><p><i>смя́ть</i> (smjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stack, to rick</li><li>to baste, to tack <i>(together)</i></li></ol><p><i>смета́ть</i> (smetátʹ, “to stack (hay)”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>смета́ть</i> (smetátʹ, “to baste”) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сметь</i> (smetʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сме́х</i> (sméx).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>смея́ться</i> (smejátʹsja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide, to supply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъnabъděti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>на-</i> (na-) +&lrm; <i>бдеть</i> (bdetʹ). Compare Old East Slavic <i>набъдѣти</i> (nabŭděti, “to care for, to protect”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide, to supply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>снабди́ть</i> (snabdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supply, provision (act of supplying)</li></ol><p><i>снабди́ть</i> (snabdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug, medicament, potion (a small portion or dose of a liquid which is medicinal, poisonous, or magical)</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>на́доба</i> (nádoba) +&lrm; <i>-ье</i> (-ʹje). Compare dialectal <i>снадобля́ть</i> (snadobljátʹ, “"to prepare, to provide with"”) and <i>пона́добиться</i> (ponádobitʹsja, “to be needed, to come in handy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sniper</li><li>sharpshooter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sniper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sniper</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) sharpshooter</li></ol><p><i>сна́йпер</i> (snájper) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the outside, from the outside, outwardly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;weaponry) shell, missile, projectile</li><li>apparatus</li><li>tool, equipment</li><li>tackle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip, to fit out</li><li><i>(an expedition, a search party, etc.)</i> to send, to dispatch</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>наряди́ть</i> (narjadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shell, missile</li></ol><p><i>снаря́д</i> (snarjád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip, to fit out</li><li><i>(an expedition, a search party etc.)</i> to send, to dispatch</li></ol><p><i>снаряди́ть</i> (snarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outfit, equipment, tackle</li></ol><p><i>снаряди́ть</i> (snarjadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tackle</li><li>(nautical,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) rope, rigging</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>снасть</i> (snastĭ, “tool, weapon, armor”). Probably related to dialectal <i>сна́дить</i> (snáditʹ, “to put together, to fasten together”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at first</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out</li></ol><p><i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) snow, the white electrical noise on a TV set when there is no TV signal</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sněgъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;snáigas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snóygʷʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bullfinch</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>снег</i> (sneg). Compare typologically Serbo-Croatian <i>зимница</i>.</p><p>Also compare typologically <i>зя́блик</i> (zjáblik) (Macedonian <i>снегар</i> (snegar)), Also see <i>зиморо́док</i> (zimoródok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowman (figure made of snow)</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snow; snowy</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow scooter</li></ol><p>From <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-кат</i> (-kat), combining form of <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ, “to drive, to ride”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowfall</li></ol><p>First attested in 1899. From <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>па́д(ать)</i> (pád(atʹ), “to fall”); possibly a calque of German <i>Schneefall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowshoe</li></ol><p>From <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ступ</i> (-stup, combining form of <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ, “to tread”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow plow (vehicle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snow removal (<i>off of roads</i>)</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>убо́рка</i> (ubórka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowmobile</li></ol><p>From <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ход</i> (-xod), combining form of <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ, “to walk, to go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) food</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сънѣдь</i> (sŭnědĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) February</li></ol><p>From <i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”). Related to Belarusian <i>снежань</i> (snježanʹ, “December”), Macedonian <i>снежник</i> (snežnik, “December”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowflake</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-инка</i> (-inka, “diminutive suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snow</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) to cover with snow</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) snowball fight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowman (figure made of snow)</li></ol><p>Literally, “snow woman”; <i>ба́ба</i> (bába) in contemporary usage is pejorative in the sense of "woman".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blizzard, snowstorm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow patch, snowfield (accumulated snow which remains unmelted for part of the warm season or year-round)</li><li>ground which occasionally remains frozen year-round</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) snowberry (<i>Symphoricarpos</i>)</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snow; snowy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sněžьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abominable snowman, yeti, sasquatch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow globe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowball (ball of snow)</li><li>diminutive of <i>снег</i> (sneg): (a bit of) snow; light snowfall</li><li>snezhok (fermented milk drink)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) snow, cocaine</li></ol><p><i>снег</i> (sneg) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry down</li><li>to carry to</li><li>to pull down, to demolish</li><li>to blow off, to blow away (<i>of the wind</i>)</li><li>to carry away (<i>of water</i>)</li><li>to tolerate, to endure</li><li>to cut off</li><li>to discard (a card)</li><li>to lay eggs</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, to reduce</li></ol><p><i>сни́зить</i> (snízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to descend</li><li>to drop, to fall</li></ol><p><i>снижа́ть</i> (snižátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reduction, decrease (act, process, or result of reducing)</li><li>lowering</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) descent</li></ol><p><i>сни́зить</i> (snízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring down, to reduce</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-ни́зить</i> (-nízitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to descend</li><li>to drop, to fall</li></ol><p><i>сни́зить</i> (snízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condescend, to deign [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to an action’</small>]</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>condescendre</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-, “con-”) +&lrm; <i>низойти́</i> (nizojtí, “descend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from below, underarm</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ни́з</i> (níz)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sneaker</li><li>Snickers-brand chocolate bar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sneakers</i>.</p><p>From Snickers.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to droop, to wilt, to relent</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ни́кнуть</i> (níknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off, to take away, to take down</li><li>to take, to make</li><li>to photograph, to film, to x-ray, to radiograph</li><li>to rent (e.g. an apartment)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have one's photograph taken</li><li>to act in a film</li></ol><p><i>снима́ть</i> (snimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photo, photograph, picture, snapshot</li></ol><p><i>снима́ть</i> (snimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get, to win, to gain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сънискати</i> (sŭniskati). Compare native <i>сыскать</i> (syskatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>graciously, indulgently, leniently</li><li>in a condescending manner, arrogantly</li><li>contemptuously (<i>in a disrespectful or discourteous manner</i>)</li></ol><p><i>снисходи́тельный</i> (snisxodítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condescension</li><li>leniency</li><li>indulgence, forgiveness</li></ol><p><i>снисходи́тельный</i> (snisxodítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indulgent, lenient (tolerant&#59; not strict)</li><li>condescending</li></ol><p><i>снисходи́ть</i> (snisxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to condescend, to deign [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘to an action’</small>]</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>condescendre</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-, “con-”) +&lrm; <i>нисходи́ть</i> (nisxodítʹ, “descend”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indulgence, leniency</li><li>condescension</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be dreamed [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>dream</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for one to dream [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>что</b> (što) <small>‘that...’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>dream</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>сн-</i> (sn-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snob (person who seeks to be a member of the upper classes)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>snob</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snobbishness, snobbery</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>snobisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anew</li><li>again</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>но́вый</i> (nóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again and again (repeatedly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move to and fro, to scurry about, to whisk, to dash about, to skirr, to fuss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;snovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream, vision (in a dream)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amazing, flabbergasting, stunning, rip-snorting, smashing</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-, “off from”) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “leg, foot”) +&lrm; <i>сшиба́ть</i> (sšibátʹ, “to knock down off”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj); literally, “knocking one off one's feet”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaf (<i>bundle of grain or straw</i>)</li><li>shaft (<i>of light</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;snopъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) reaper-binder, (sheaf) binder (a machine used in harvesting that ties cut stalks of grain into a bundle)</li></ol><p><i>сноп</i> (snop, “sheaf”) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ, “to bind”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skill, proficiency, dexterity</li></ol><p><i>снорови́ть</i> (snorovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulling down; demolition; drift</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ, “to pull down”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry down</li><li>to carry to</li><li>to pull down, to demolish, to raze</li><li>to blow off, to blow away (<i>of the wind</i>)</li><li>to carry away (<i>of water</i>)</li><li>to tolerate, to endure</li><li>to cut off</li><li>to discard (a card)</li><li>to carry somewhere and bring back</li><li>to wear out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footnote</li></ol><p><i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supportable, tolerable, passable</li></ol><p><i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soporific, somnifacient (sleep inducing agent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soporific, somnolent, hypnic</li><li>(too) boring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowboard (a board with the ability to glide on snow)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>snowboard</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) snowboarding</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>snowboarding</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man's daughter-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;snъxa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snusós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) A man (свёкор) who takes sexual advantage of his daughter-in-law (сноха).</li></ol><p>From <i>сноха́</i> (snoxá) +&lrm; <i>-а́ч</i> (-áč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sexual relations and cohabitation between a man and his daughter-in-law</li></ol><p><i>сноха́ч</i> (snoxáč) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to copulate, to have sex, to make love</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dealings, intercourse</li><li>intercourse (sexual intercourse)</li></ol><p><i>сноси́ть</i> (snosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>snus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking off, removal</li><li>withdrawal, removal</li><li>gathering, harvest, reaping</li><li>ending</li><li><i>in expressions</i></li><li>(Hegelian philosophy) sublation</li></ol><p><i>снять</i> (snjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skim, skimmed: only used in <i><b>снято́е</b> молоко́</i> (<b>snjatóje</b> molokó, “<b>skim</b> milk”)</li></ol><p><i>снять</i> (snjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-то́й</i> (-tój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off, to take away, to take down</li><li>to take, to make</li><li>to photograph, to film</li><li>to rent (e.g. an apartment)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъnęti</i>, from <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i> (see <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i>) + <i>&#42;ęti</i>. Reanalyzed as <i>с-</i> (s-) + <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ), from which other words in <i>-нять</i> (-njatʹ) were created, such as <i>наня́ть</i> (nanjátʹ), <i>обня́ть</i> (obnjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have one's photograph taken</li><li>to act in a film</li></ol><p><i>снять</i> (snjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>снег</i> (sneg).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>снежи́нка</i> (snežínka).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>с</i> (s): with, off, from, etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>like stink, at full pelt</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>à toutes jambes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from one's part, as far as one is concerned</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty</li><li>reluctantly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>с-</i> (s-) (<i>used before certain consonant clusters</i>)</li><li>with, con-, co-</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i> (see <i>с-</i> (s-) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> for more information). The yer sound -ъ- was treated as strong when another yer followed in the next syllable, and was vocalized to -o-, whereas the yer in the next syllable was deleted, producing a consonant cluster.</p><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съ-</i> (sŭ-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i>; see above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coauthor (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>co-authorship, joint authorship</li><li>co-designership</li></ol><p><i>соа́втор</i> (soávtor) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog</li><li>hound</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;figuratively) mongrel, cur, bastard (a detestable person)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) fox (a clever, capable person)</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) @ (at sign)</li><li>(<i>computing&#32;slang</i>) watchdog timer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>собака</i> (sobaka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sobaka</i>, derived from Middle Iranian <i>&#42;sabāka-</i>, from West Iranian *<i>spaka</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;cwā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i>; compare Zoroastrian Dari (sabah), Old Median <i>σπάκα</i> (spáka) [the source of Old Armenian <i>ասպակ</i> (aspak, “dog”)], Avestan <i>𐬯𐬞𐬀𐬐𐬀</i> (spaka, “dog-like”). Cognates include Ukrainian <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka), Belarusian <i>саба́ка</i> (sabáka), Polish <i>sobaka</i> (<i>dialectal</i>), Kashubian <i>sobaka</i> (“bitch (female dog); dissolute man”), Sanskrit <i>शुनक</i> (śunaka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog meat</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>собачатина</i> (sobačatyna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>соба́чка</i> (sobáčka): little doggy</li></ol><p><i>соба́чка</i> (sobáčka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dog; dog's, canine</li></ol><p><i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) beastly cold weather, brass monkey weather, bust ass cold weather</li></ol><p>Literally, “dog's cold”. Compare German <i>Hundekälte</i>.</p><p>Compare also French <i>temps de chien</i>, Dutch <i>hondenweer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>doggy</li><li>at (@ sign)</li></ol><li>trigger, self-locking latch, turnpiece</li></ol><p><i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog hunter (a person who is engaged in the extermination of stray dogs)</li></ol><p><i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka): nasty little dog, stray dog</li></ol><p><i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka) +&lrm; <i>-о́нка</i> (-ónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog's life</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>dog's life</i>, French <i>vie de chien</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ape, to monkey (imitate)</li><li>to act the ape/monkey</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>обезья́нничать</i> (obezʹjánničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) social security</li><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) social security agency</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>социа́льное обеспе́чение</i> (sociálʹnoje obespéčenije, “social security”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speaking partner, other person (with whom one is talking), interlocutor</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>бесе́да</i> (beséda) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interview (a formal meeting for the assessment of a candidate or applicant)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) talk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picking</li><li>collecting, gathering</li><li>collection, accumulation, aggregation, assemblage</li><li>compilation</li></ol><p><i>собира́ть</i> (sobirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije). Compare <i>собра́ние</i> (sobránije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collector, gatherer</li></ol><p><i>собира́ть</i> (sobirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) collective numeral (a numeral which signifies that several persons or things are taken together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>collecting (serving for collecting something)</li><li>generalized, collective</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) collective</li></ol><p><i>собира́ть</i> (sobirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to collect, to harvest</li><li>to assemble</li><li>to convoke</li><li><i>(for a journey)</i> to equip, to prepare</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) to build, to compile</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to gather, to assemble</li><li>to be going (somewhere), to be heading (off) (somewhere)</li><li>to be about to (do something), to be going to (do something), to intend (to do something), to mean (to do something)</li></ol><p><i>собира́ть</i> (sobirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temptation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съблазнъ</i> (sŭblaznŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъblaznъ</i>, from <i>&#42;blaznь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seducer</li></ol><p><i>соблазни́ть</i> (soblaznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seductive</li><li>alluring, enticing</li><li>provocative</li><li>tempting</li></ol><p><i>соблазни́ть</i> (soblaznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seduce</li></ol><p>From <i>собла́зн</i> (soblázn, “temptation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seduce</li></ol><p><i>соблазни́ть</i> (soblaznítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comply, to obey, to observe, to follow, to stick <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съблюдати</i> (sŭbljudati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъbľudati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>соблюд-</i> (sobljud-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>observance (of laws, customs, etc.), upholding</li></ol><p><i>соблюд-</i> (sobljud-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comply, to obey, to observe, to follow, to stick <i>(to)</i></li><li>to guard, to keep</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съблюсти</i> (sŭbljusti). Equivalent <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>блюсти́</i> (bljustí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condolence (comfort, support or sympathy)</li></ol><p><i>соболе́зновать</i> (soboléznovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to sympathize with, to express condolences to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>боле́знь</i> (boléznʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>condoleō</i> (&lt; <i>con-</i> + <i>doleō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sable</li></ol><p><i>со́боль</i> (sóbolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sable</li></ol><p><i>со́боль</i> (sóbolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-иный</i> (-inyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sable (a small carnivorous mammal)</li><li>sable (the fur of this animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>соболь</i> (sobolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sobolь</i>. Further, compare Middle Persian <i>smwl</i> (*samōr).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Соболь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sobol</li></ol><p>From <i>со́боль</i> (sóbolʹ, “sable (bird)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cathedral, catholicon (a large church, usually the current or former seat of a bishop)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) council, diet (formal assembly)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съборъ</i> (sŭborŭ) (a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συναγωγή</i> (sunagōgḗ)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъborъ</i>. Doublet of <i>сбор</i> (sbor), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;literary) the principle of joint discussion and solution of important national issues at a council of people's representatives; consensus-driven decision making</li><li>(<i>Russian philosophy</i>) sobornost (the free spiritual unity of religious and secular communities)</li><li>(dated,&#32;religion) catholicity, universality</li></ol><p><i>собо́рный</i> (sobórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cathedral</li><li>conciliar, catholic</li></ol><p><i>собо́р</i> (sobór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Church) minister</li></ol><p><i>собо́р</i> (sobór) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, gathering</li><li>assembly</li><li>collection, set</li><li>collected works</li></ol><p><i>собра́ть</i> (sobrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discipline, self-discipline, focus, organization</li></ol><p><i>со́бранный</i> (sóbrannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(person)</i> precise, accurate, self-disciplined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative, old-fashion spelling of собрание (see)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow, mate, colleague</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>брат</i> (brat). Compare Latin <i>confrāter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to collect</li><li>to assemble</li><li>to convoke</li><li><i>(for a journey)</i> to equip, to prepare</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to assemble <i>mutually reflexive</i></li><li>to be about, to make up one's mind, to be going, to intend, to be on the point</li><li>to prepare, to be ready to start, to set out</li></ol><p><i>собра́ть</i> (sobrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proprietor, owner</li><li>someone possessive, someone who seeks to control</li></ol><p>Compound of собство (sobstvo, “propriety”) and -ник (-nik, “-or”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a matter of fact, in fact</li></ol><p><i>со́бственный</i> (sóbstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>actually, as a matter of fact, in fact</li><li>strictly speaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>himself/herself/themselves, in the flesh, in person (used to emphasise that someone encountered is a given person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with one's own hands; personally, single-handedly</li></ol><p><i>собственнору́чный</i> (sobstvennorúčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handwritten; written in one's own hand</li></ol><p>From <i>со́бственный</i> (sóbstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>property</li><li>ownership</li></ol><p><i>со́бственный</i> (sóbstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one's own, peculiar, proper</li><li>(<i>linear algebra</i>) eigen-</li></ol><p>Constructed from Old East Slavic <i>собьство</i> (sobĭstvo, “quality, essence”) + <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) property, characteristics</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>собьство</i> (sobĭstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drinking companion, booze buddy</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>буты́л(ка)</i> (butýl(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>event, occurrence, incident</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). From the verb Old East Slavic <i>събꙑтися</i> (sŭbytisja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Soviet (used in the names of companies or organizations)</li><li>council (used in names of groups, not always Soviet-related)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl</li><li>night person, night owl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sova</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put</li><li>to slip, to give</li><li>to poke, to stick in</li><li>to butt in</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>совати</i> (sovati, “throw”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sovati</i> (“throw, push”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory,&#32;figuratively) to stick one's nose in (to be nosy&#59; to meddle or interfere in the affairs of another)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>fourrer son nez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try to get <i>(somewhere)</i>, to try to enter <i>(a place)</i>, to poke one's head in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn <i>(to)</i>, to go <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to butt <i>(in)</i>, to poke one's nose <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>сова́ть</i> (sovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Soviet power</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сове́тская вла́сть</i> (sovétskaja vlástʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) Sovdepia, (originally) RSFSR, the Soviet Russia, later USSR, Soviet Union and modern Russia, the old Soviet system in general, sovok</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>сове́т наро́дных депута́тов</i> (sovét naródnyx deputátov).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person from Sovega area (north of Soligalich)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accomplish, to perform, to commit, to perpetrate, to make</li></ol><p><i>соверши́ть</i> (soveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, to take place</li></ol><p><i>соверша́ть</i> (soveršátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplishment, fulfilment, perpetration</li><li>execution</li></ol><p><i>соверши́ть</i> (soveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>соверше́нный</i> (soveršénnyj): most perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>соверше́нн(ый)</i> (soveršénn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perfectly</li><li>absolutely, quite, totally, utterly</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нный</i> (soveršénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age, full age, legal age, age of majority (time of life when one attains full person rights)</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нный</i> (soveršénnyj, “complete”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto, “summer, year”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of age, adult</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нный</i> (soveršénnyj, “complete”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto, “summer, year”) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absolute, complete, perfect</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) perfect, perfective</li></ol><p><i>соверши́ть</i> (soveršítʹ, “to accomplish, to effect”) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) perfective aspect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perfection, consummation, fineness, polish</li></ol><p><i>совершенный</i> (soveršennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>development, improvement</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нствовать</i> (soveršénstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to better, to develop, to improve, to perfect, to refine</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нство</i> (soveršénstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perfect oneself, to improve one's skills, to upgrade one's skills</li></ol><p><i>соверше́нствовать</i> (soveršénstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accomplish, to perform, to commit, to perpetrate, to make</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>съврьшити</i> (sŭvrĭšiti), from <i>съ-</i> (sŭ-) + <i>връшити</i> (vrŭšiti) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wers-</i>. Also related to <i>верх</i> (verx, “top, summit”) and to Old East Slavic <i>вьрхъ</i> (vĭrxŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrxъ</i>. Literally, “to top out”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen, to take place</li></ol><p><i>соверши́ть</i> (soveršítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to appeal to one's conscience, shame, reproach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscientious</li></ol><p><i>со́весть</i> (sóvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>со́вестливый</i> (sóvestlivyj)</li></ol><p><i>со́весть</i> (sóvestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conscience (moral sense)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣсть</i> (sŭvěstĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advice</li><li>counsel</li><li>council, board</li><li>(<i>Soviet Union</i>) soviet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвѣтъ</i> (sŭvětŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvětъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Soviet Union,&#32;politics) Sovietization</li></ol><p><i>сове́т</i> (sovét) +&lrm; <i>-иза́ция</i> (-izácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advisor, counselor, councillor</li></ol><p><i>сове́т</i> (sovét) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, to counsel, to recommend [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult</li><li>to confer, to deliberate, to exchange views</li></ol><p><i>сове́товать</i> (sovétovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soviet power</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soviet Socialist Republic (SSR)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Soviet</li></ol><p>From <i>сове́т</i> (sovét, “advice, council, Soviet”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adviser, advisor (one who advises)</li><li>guide</li><li>consultant</li></ol><p><i>сове́т</i> (sovét) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conference, meeting (gathering among business people to discuss their business)</li><li>discussion</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъvěťanьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>advisory (able to give advice)</li><li>deliberative</li><li>consultative, consultive</li></ol><p><i>совеща́ть(ся)</i> (soveščátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult (with), to confer (with), to hold a consultation</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) owl</li></ol><p><i>сова́</i> (sová) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) owlet, little owl</li><li>(<i>bird</i>) scops owl</li><li>(<i>moth</i>) noctuid, owlet moth</li></ol><p><i>сова́</i> (sová) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Совка)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) Soviet Union</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) Russia</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>сово́к</i> (sovók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shovel (tool for moving portions of material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) tankie, obsessive Soviet Union supporter, person showing extreme communist nostalgia</li></ol><p>From <i>сов(о́)к</i> (sov(ó)k, “Soviet”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>дро́чер</i> (dróčer, “wanker”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to control, to get the better <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>владе́ть</i> (vladétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coowner (co-owner) (someone who owns something together with one or more other people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compatibility</li></ol><p><i>совмести́мый</i> (sovmestímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compatible</li></ol><p><i>совмест(и́ть)</i> (sovmest(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work for more than one employer, two jobs at a time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to combine</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>вмести́ть</i> (vmestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to combine, to join</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to coincide</li></ol><p><i>совмести́ть</i> (sovmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>jointly (together)</li></ol><p><i>совме́стный</i> (sovméstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint venture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joint, shared (done by two or more people or organisations working together)</li><li>common, conjoint, united</li><li>compatible, simultaneous</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>вме́сте</i> (vméste) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to combine</li></ol><p><i>совмести́ть</i> (sovmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to combine, to join</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to coincide</li></ol><p><i>совмеща́ть</i> (sovmeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combination</li><li>superposition</li></ol><p><i>совмести́ть</i> (sovmestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) sovnarkhoz (one of the regional economic councils of the Soviet Union, created in 1957)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>сове́т наро́дного хозя́йства</i> (sovét naródnovo xozjájstva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoop, dustpan</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;derogatory) Soviet Union, sovok, sovdep, sovdepia/sovdepiya</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory) person supporting Soviet ideology, morals or habits, sovok</li></ol><p>From <i>сова́ть</i> (sovátʹ, “to put; to slip, to give; to poke in”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p><p>From <i>сове́т</i> (sovét) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók), possibly inspired by the above homonym.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coition, coupling, sexual intercourse (sexual intercourse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to get connected, to get linked</li><li>to copulate, to have sex, to make love</li></ol><p><i>совокупля́ть</i> (sovokupljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>totality, the aggregate, the sum total</li></ol><p><i>совоку́пный</i> (sovokúpnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combined, total, aggregate (a result of combining or adding of different parts together)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>сово́к</i> (sovók): small scoop</li></ol><p>From <i>сово́к</i> (sovók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coincide</li><li>to concur</li><li>to match, to agree</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>coïncider</i>. By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пада́ть</i> (padátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coincidence</li></ol><p><i>совпа́д-</i> (sovpád-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coincide</li><li>to concur</li><li>to match, to agree</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>coïncider</i>. By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>в-</i> (v-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to move aside, to deflect</li><li>to incite to behave badly, to corrupt</li><li>to seduce, to pervert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвратити</i> (sŭvratiti). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-врати́ть</i> (-vratítʹ, “to turn”). Doublet of <i>свороти́ть</i> (svorotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie</li><li>to make a mistake</li><li>to be inaccurate</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>врать</i> (vratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to move aside, to deflect</li><li>to incite to behave badly, to corrupt</li><li>to seduce, to pervert</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съвращати</i> (sŭvraštati). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>враща́ть</i> (vraščátʹ, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>совреме́нный</i> (sovreménnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>совреме́нно</i> (sovreménno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contemporary (someone living (or something existing) at the same time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a contemporary manner</li><li>in a modern manner</li><li>in an up-to-date, present-day manner</li></ol><p><i>совреме́нный</i> (sovreménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the present (times), modernity, contemporaneity</li></ol><p><i>совреме́нный</i> (sovreménnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemporary</li><li>modern</li><li>up-to-date, present-day</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>вре́мя</i> (vrémja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare Bulgarian <i>съвреме́нен</i> (sǎvreménen) and Serbo-Croatian <i>савремен</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quite, entirely, totally</li><li>at all</li></ol><p>Univerbation of the preposition <i>со</i> (so, “with”) + <i>всем</i> (vsem), the instrumental case of <i>весь</i> (vesʹ, “all”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovkhoz, state farm</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>сове́тское хозя́йство</i> (sovétskoje xozjájstvo, “Soviet farm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sovkhoz, state farm</li></ol><p><i>совхо́з</i> (sovxóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owlet</li></ol><p><i>сова́</i> (sová) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>со́весть</i> (sóvestʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сове́т</i> (sovét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сове́т</i> (sovét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) I agree, agreed</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>согла́сен</i> (soglásen), <i>согла́сна</i> (soglásna) or <i>согла́сны</i> (soglásny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consent, assent</li><li>agreement</li><li>accord, accordance, concord, harmony</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) consonant</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Bulgarian <i>съгла́сие</i> (sǎglásie).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree (to), to consent, to assent</li><li>to agree (with), to concur, to concede, to admit</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>гласи́ть</i> (glasítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consonant (letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>according to, as to</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>harmoniously</li><li>in agreement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready (for); willing (to)</li><li>agreeable</li><li>harmonious, concordant</li><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) consonantic, consonantal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) consonant</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>глас</i> (glas, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix). The phonetic sense is a semantic loan from Ancient Greek <i>σῠ́μφωνον</i> (sŭ́mphōnon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concordance (agreement)</li></ol><p><i>согласова́ть</i> (soglasovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concordance (agreement)</li><li>coherence</li><li>consistency</li></ol><p><i>согласо́ванный</i> (soglasóvannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coordinated, concerted, consensual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to co-ordinate, to adjust, to agree, to match</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree <i>(with)</i>, to accord with, to be concordant with</li></ol><p><i>согласова́ть</i> (soglasovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to co-ordinate, to adjust, to agree, to match</li></ol><p><i>согласова́ть</i> (soglasovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree <i>(with)</i>, to accord with, to be concordant with</li></ol><p><i>согласо́вывать</i> (soglasóvyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>согла́сие</i> (soglásije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree (to), to consent, to assent</li><li>to agree (with), to concur, to concede, to admit</li></ol><p><i>согласи́ться</i> (soglasítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, understanding, consent</li><li>agreement, contract (legally binding contract enforceable in a court of law)</li><li>covenant, compact, concordat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(youth slang,&#32;Internet) I agree, agreed</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>согла́сен</i> (soglásen), <i>согла́сна</i> (soglásna) or <i>согла́сны</i> (soglásny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;derogatory) eavesdropper, voyeur, spy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съглѧдатаи</i> (sŭględatai). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тай</i> (-átaj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive away</li><li>to drive together (cattle, etc.)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose (weight)</li><li>to remove (from the surface of)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent</li><li>stooped</li><li>bowed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, to curve, to crook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bent, to be curved, (<i>intransitive</i>) to bend</li></ol><p><i>согну́ть</i> (sognútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow citizen</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>граждани́н</i> (graždanín)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up</li></ol><p><i>согре́ть</i> (sogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm <i>(oneself)</i>, to get warm</li></ol><p><i>согрева́ть</i> (sogrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>греть</i> (gretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm <i>(oneself)</i>, to get warm</li></ol><p><i>согре́ть</i> (sogrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sin (to commit a sin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat raw flesh or raw fish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soda (sodium carbonate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to make, to create, to produce, to execute</li></ol><p><i>соде́ять</i> (sodéjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistance, aid</li><li>promotion</li><li>contribution</li><li>agency</li><li>instrumentality</li><li>abetment</li><li>subservience</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abet (to support, uphold, or aid) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to assist, to promote, to facilitate [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съдѣиствовати</i> (sŭděistvovati). By surface analysis, <i>соде́йство</i> (sodéjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeping</li><li>upkeep, maintenance</li><li>content, substance</li><li>contents</li><li>table of contents</li><li>custody</li></ol><p><i>содержа́ть</i> (soderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kept woman (woman supported financially by a lover)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner (of an establishment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>substantial, substantive, full of substance, rich in content, meaty</li></ol><p><i>содержа́ть</i> (soderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contain, to hold</li><li>to maintain, to keep (in a certain state)</li><li>to support, to keep, to provide with all necessities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contain, to hold</li></ol><p><i>содержа́ть</i> (soderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contents</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to make, to create, to produce, to execute</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съдѣꙗти</i> (sŭdějati). Equivalent to <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>де́ять</i> (déjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soju, a Korean distilled alcoholic beverage, similar to sake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>소주</i> (soju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) debauchery, fornication</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hubbub, commotion, disorder, uproar, row</li></ol><p>From <i>Содо́м</i> (Sodóm, “Sodom”), from Ancient Greek <i>Σόδομα</i> (Sódoma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sodomise (to perform anal sex upon a person, especially if against his or her will)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sodomite, sodomist</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a male homosexual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sodomy</li><li>buggery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sodomie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip, to strip off, to scratch off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to charge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to copy, to crib, to plagiarize</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shudder, shiver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shudder</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>дро́гнуть</i> (drógnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shudder</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>содрога́ться</i> (sodrogátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>community</li><li>concord</li><li>cooperation</li><li>collaboration</li><li>commonwealth</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>друг</i> (drug) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) soy</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) soy (characteristic of a soy boy; weak, unmasculine, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>со́я</i> (sója) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soy sauce</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joining, junction</li><li>connection</li><li>combination, union</li><li>joint, junction</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) compound</li><li>(<i>military</i>) formation <i>(refers to a corps, a division, or a brigade; also informally, an army)</i></li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) combination</li></ol><p><i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connector, joiner</li></ol><p><i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ, “to connect”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conjunctive, conjoining, connecting</li></ol><p><i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite, to join</li><li>to connect, to put through</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to combine</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>еди́н</i> (jedín) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite</li><li>to connect, to join</li><li>to get connected, to get linked, to connect</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to combine</li></ol><p><i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite, to join</li><li>to connect, to put through</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to combine</li></ol><p><i>соедини́ть</i> (sojedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unite</li><li>to connect, to join</li><li>to get connected, to get linked, to connect</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to combine</li></ol><p><i>соединя́ть</i> (sojedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united</li><li>joint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regret</li><li>pity</li></ol><p><i>сожале́ть</i> (sožalétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pity, to be sorry <i>(for someone)</i></li><li>to regret, to deplore, to be sorry <i>(that)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, burning up</li></ol><p><i>сожг-</i> (sožg-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roommate</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-, “co-”) +&lrm; <i>жиле́ц</i> (žiléc, “tenant”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roommate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cohabitant (<i>of the opposite sex</i>)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cohabitation (<i>of two lovers</i>); common-law marriage</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) living under one roof; living together as roommates</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) coexistence as neighbors</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) cohabitation (<i>e.g. of a president and a prime minister from different parties</i>)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live <i>(with)</i>, to lodge <i>(with)</i>, to live together</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cohabit, to shack up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) cohabitation</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>житие́</i> (žitijé).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сожи́тие</i> (sožítije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to eat</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to gorge, to scarf down <i>(overeat or eat greedily, immodestly)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;of an insect) to sting, to bite</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contact someone by telephone, to have a call</li></ol><p><i>созвони́ться</i> (sozvonítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call <i>(together)</i>, to summon, to invite</li><li>to call, to convene, to convoke</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>звать</i> (zvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constellation</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of Latin <i>constellātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Canis Major</i>, the Greater Dog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canis Minor, the Lesser Dog (constellation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) instance of getting in contact via a call; phone call, conference call, voice call, video call, check-up, scheduled talk, etc.</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>созвони́ться</i> (sozvonítʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contact someone by telephone, to have a call</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(prosody,&#32;music) consonance</li></ol><p>From <i>со</i> (so) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk, “sound”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>sazvučje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmonious, assonant</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) in accordance [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>созву́ч(ие)</i> (sozvúč(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li><li>to found, to originate</li><li>to set up, to establish</li></ol><p><i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to form, to be created</li></ol><p><i>создава́ть</i> (sozdavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation (act of creation)</li><li>creature</li></ol><p><i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>created, generated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creator (one who creates)</li></ol><p><i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ, “to create”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li><li>to found, to originate</li><li>to set up, to establish</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to build, to erect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съзьдати</i> (sŭzĭdati, “to build, to erect”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъzьdati</i>, from <i>&#42;zьdati</i>. The modern conjugation is analogical after <i>дать</i> (datʹ), replacing the old present stem <i>созижд</i>-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to form, to be created</li></ol><p><i>созда́ть</i> (sozdátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contemplation</li></ol><p><i>созерца́ть</i> (sozercátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) to examine, to see, to behold, to observe, to gaze at</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to contemplate, to meditate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съзрьцати</i> (sŭzrĭcati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zьrcati</i>. Related to <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ), <i>-зирать</i> (-ziratʹ). Compare other verbs from the same root <i>зе́риться</i> (zéritʹsja), <i>поза́рится</i> (pozáritsja), <i>зы́ркать</i> (zýrkatʹ), <i>зы́рить</i> (zýritʹ). Compare native dialectal <i>зе́ркать</i> (zérkatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation (act of creation)</li></ol><p><i>созида́ть</i> (sozidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty style</i>) creator</li></ol><p><i>созида́ть</i> (sozidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creative, constructive</li></ol><p><i>созида́ть</i> (sozidátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to create</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to build, to erect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съзидати</i> (sŭzidati). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-зида́ть</i> (-zidátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to realize, to see</li><li>to be conscious (of)</li><li>to acknowledge, to admit, to understand</li></ol><p><i>созна́ть</i> (soznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess</li></ol><p><i>сознава́ть</i> (soznavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consciousness (awareness)</li><li>realization, perception, awareness</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) confession</li></ol><p><i>созна́ть</i> (soznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consciously (in a conscious manner&#59; knowingly, volitionally)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awareness, consciousness</li></ol><p><i>созна́тельный</i> (soznátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conscious, conscientious</li><li>deliberate</li></ol><p><i>созна́ть</i> (soznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to realize, to see</li><li>to (be) conscious (of)</li><li>to acknowledge, to admit, to understand</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>зна́ть</i> (znátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confess</li></ol><p><i>созна́ть</i> (soznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ripening (fruit, etc.)</li><li>maturing, maturation</li></ol><p><i>созрева́ть</i> (sozrevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen, to mature</li><li><i>(person)</i> to mature</li><li>to mature, to take shape</li></ol><p><i>созре́ть</i> (sozrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen, to mature</li><li><i>(person)</i> to mature</li><li>to mature, to take shape</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convocation, calling together</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call <i>(together)</i>, to summon, to invite</li><li>to call, to convene, to convoke</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-зыва́ть</i> (-zyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;ironic) assent, approbation, permission, pleasure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to deign</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>изво́лить</i> (izvólitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to deign</li></ol><p><i>соизво́лить</i> (soizvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commensurable (able to be measured using a common standard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) namesake (person with the same name as another)</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-, “con-, co-”) +&lrm; <i>имен-</i> (imen-, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) competition</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) gaining, winning, obtaining</li><li>presentation of a work in order to receive a title or money</li><li>desire to receive a title or money for a work so presented</li></ol><p><i>соиска́ть</i> (soiskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>applicant (one who applies)</li><li>competitor, contestant</li></ol><p><i>соиска́ть</i> (soiskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asylum seeker (one who seeks asylum)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to compete, to vie</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to gain, to win, to obtain</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) coitus, copulation, sexual intercourse</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>coitus</i>. Composed of <i>сой(тись)</i> (soj(tisʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije), with full -и- in the middle as in e.g. <i>наи́тие</i> (naítije) from <i>найти́</i> (najtí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian jay (<i>Garrulus glandarius</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sojьka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>со́я</i> (sója, “jay (<i>dialectal</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>со́я</i> (sója), Belarusian <i>со́я</i> (sója), Bulgarian <i>со́йка</i> (sójka), Serbo-Croatian <i>со̑јка</i>, Slovene <i>sȏjka</i> (“type of jackdaw”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>sojka</i> (“jay”), Slovak <i>sojka</i> (“jay”), Polish <i>soja</i> (also <i>sójka</i>), Upper Sorbian <i>soja</i>. Per Vasmer, presumably related to Russian <i>сия́ть</i> (sijátʹ, “to shine”), based on the colorful feathers.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Kazakhstan,&#32;offensive) Kazakh individual, who calls to speak and learn the Kazakh language</li></ol><p>From Kazakh <i>сөйле</i> (söile, “speak!”) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik), the word comes from the Kazakh phrase <i>қазақша сөйле</i> (qazaqşa söile, “speak Kazakh!”), often used by native Kazakhs to encourage the use of Kazakhstan’s national language instead of Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to come down, to descend, to get off</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to come off, to disappear, to go from</li><li>to pass by</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to pass for, to be taken for</li><li>to drift</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be acceptable or sufficient</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go crazy, to lose one's mind</li></ol><p>Literally meaning <i>to go out of [one's] mind</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to come together</li><li>to converge, to coalesce</li><li>to become friends; to become close or sexually intimate</li><li>to match, to tally, to harmonize, to agree</li></ol><p><i>сойти́</i> (sojtí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice</li><li>sap</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) cum, semen</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сокъ</i> (sokŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sȍkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sakás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sokʷós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellmate</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>ка́мера</i> (kámera) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;US,&#32;Japan,&#32;Canada,&#32;Australia) soccer, association football</li></ol><p>From American English <i>soccer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeezer (for making juice out of fruits or vegetables), juicer</li></ol><p><i>сок</i> (sok, “juice”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>выжима́ть</i> (vyžimátʹ, “to squeeze out”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falcon</li><li>Sokol (series of Soviet and Russian soft-body pressure suits)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) battering ram</li><li>hawk (plaster's tool), mortarboard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sokolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) falcon</li></ol><p><i>со́кол</i> (sókol) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyas, baby falcon, falcon chick</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bold and daring young man.</li></ol><p><i>сокол</i> (sokol) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten, to curtail, to abridge, to abbreviate</li><li>to reduce, to cut, to curtail, to retrench</li><li>to dismiss, to discharge; to lay off</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to cancel, to abbreviate by cancellation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съкратити</i> (sŭkratiti). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Compare the Belarusian <i>скараці́ць</i> (skaracícʹ) and Ukrainian <i>скороти́ти</i> (skorotýty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to decrease, to shorten</li></ol><p><i>сократи́ть</i> (sokratítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten, to curtail, to abridge, to abbreviate</li><li>to reduce, to cut, to curtail, to retrench</li><li>to dismiss, to discharge; to lay off</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to cancel, to abbreviate by cancellation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съкращати</i> (sŭkraštati). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>кра́ткий</i> (krátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to decrease, to shorten</li></ol><p><i>сокраща́ть</i> (sokraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation</li><li>reduction, curtailment</li><li>abridgment</li><li>contraction, short form</li><li>downsizing</li></ol><p><i>сокраща́ть</i> (sokraščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an abbreviated form</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shorter, shortened</li><li>contracted, abbreviated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) secret, concealed, innermost, inner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съкръвеньнъ</i> (sŭkrŭvenĭnŭ, “hidden, concealed”), from <i>съкръвенъ</i> (sŭkrŭvenŭ, “hidden, concealed”) + <i>-ьнъ</i> (-ĭnŭ). Related to <i>сокры́ть</i> (sokrýtʹ) and <i>скры́ть</i> (skrýtʹ). Doublet of <i>скры́тый</i> (skrýtyj, “hidden, concealed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure (collection of valuable things)</li></ol><p>From <i>сокры́ть</i> (sokrýtʹ, “to hide, cover”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče).</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huzdą</i> (whence English <i>hoard</i>) &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury (place where treasure is stored safely, also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>сокро́вище</i> (sokróvišče) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to distress, to grieve</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съкроушати</i> (sŭkrušati). Equivalent to <i>сокруш-</i> (sokruš-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distressed, to grieve <i>(for, over)</i></li></ol><p><i>сокруша́ть</i> (sokrušátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Note the same sense evolution with cognate Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrewwaną</i> (whence English <i>rue</i> (“to repent or regret a past action or event”)).</p><p>Also compare typologically Czech <i>truchlit</i> (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;truxlъ</i>, whence also <i>трухля́вый</i> (truxljávyj), akin to <i>труха́</i> (truxá)); Latin <i>doleō</i> (“to hurt, to suffer; <b>to be sorry, to grieve for</b>”), <i>condoleō</i> (akin to <i>dolō</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shattering, destructive, crushing</li></ol><p><i>сокруши́ть</i> (sokrušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smash, to shatter</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to distress, to grieve</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съкроушити</i> (sŭkrušiti). Equivalent to <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>круши́ть</i> (krušítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>regretfully, with grief</li></ol><p><i>сокрушённый</i> (sokrušónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, grieving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment</li><li>hiding (the act of hiding something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow student, classmate (e.g. at a university)</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>курс</i> (kurs) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сок</i> (sok).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, to tell lies</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier (male or female)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>soldato</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>солда́т</i> (soldát) (small) soldier; toy soldier</li></ol><p><i>солда́т</i> (soldát) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) soldiery</li></ol><p><i>солдат</i> (soldat) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soldier’s</li></ol><p><i>солда́т</i> (soldát) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a rude and uncultured man from the military</li><li>(sarcastic,&#32;derogatory) a military officer whose interests and experience are strongly limited by his professional knowledge</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;figuratively) a rude or aggressive man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltern</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltern</li></ol><p><i>соль</i> (solʹ, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to boil, to brew”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) salt</li></ol><p><i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salting, curing (preserving food with salt); pickling (instance of processing something in brine or in vinegar)</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) pickle</li></ol><p>From <i>соли́ть</i> (solítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solenoid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>соле́ние</i> (solénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>jointly, in unity</li></ol><p><i>солида́рный</i> (solidárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solidarity</li></ol><p><i>солида́рный</i> (solidárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united, as one, solidary</li><li>in full agreement [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>solidar(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>solidly, firmly</li><li>sizeably</li></ol><p><i>соли́дный</i> (solídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solidity, reliability</li><li>portliness</li><li>stoutness</li></ol><p><i>соли́дный</i> (solídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid (large, massive)</li><li>reliable, respectable</li><li>considerable</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) middle-aged</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) massive, stout (of a person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed ultimately from Latin <i>solidus</i>. Doublet of <i>со́льдо</i> (sólʹdo) and <i>су</i> (su).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltery</li></ol><p><i>соли́ть</i> (solítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) solipsism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Solipzismus</i> or English <i>solipsism</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>sōlus</i> (“alone”) + <i>ipse</i> (“self”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solo (to perform solo)</li></ol><p><i>со́ло</i> (sólo) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soloist, solo performer</li><li>lead vocalist, principal performer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>soliste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tapeworm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics) soliton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>soliton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to salt</li><li>to pickle, to corn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soliti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solar flare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Any hummingbird of the genus <i>Phaethornis</i>.</li></ol><p><i>со́лнечный</i> (sólnečnyj, “solar, sunny”) +&lrm; <i>коли́бри</i> (kolíbri, “hummingbird”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) solar eclipse (when the Moon passes between the Earth and the sun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunglasses (tinted glasses worn to protect the eyes from the sun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sun; solar</li><li>sunny</li></ol><p><i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>, whence English <i>sun</i> and <i>solar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) solar wind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronautics</i>) solar sail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) (a place in) hot sunlight, in the heat of the sun</li></ol><p>From <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пёк</i> (pjok), combining form of <i>печь</i> (pečʹ, “to beat down (<i>of the sun's rays</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сълньце</i> (sŭlnĭce), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъlnьce</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>, whence English <i>sun</i>, <i>solar</i> and <i>helium</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Солнце)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Sun, Sol, Old Sol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) solstice</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) heliotrope</li></ol><p>From <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-воро́т</i> (-vorót), combining form of <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ, “to bring back”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunglasses (tinted glasses worn to protect the eyes from the sun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sunproof</li></ol><p><i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>защи́тный</i> (zaščítnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(formal,&#32;poetic) sunlike, haloed, with an aureole, glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>лицо́</i> (licó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place warmed by the sun</li></ol><p>From <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>пёк</i> (pjok), combining form of <i>печь</i> (pečʹ, “to beat down (<i>of the sun</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) solstice (point where the earth stands at the extreme of its ellipsis around the sun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce): (small) sun</li><li>ray of sunshine, darling, sweetheart, baby (form of address)</li></ol><p><i>со́лнце</i> (sólnce) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>solo</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;dance) solo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>solo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightingale</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>соловии</i> (solovii), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solvьjь</i>, from <i>&#42;solvъ</i> (“cream-colored”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьjь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;saluo-</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ря́бчик</i> (rjábčik) (akin to <i>рябо́й</i> (rjabój)), <i>сиза́рь</i> (sizárʹ) (akin to <i>си́зый</i> (sízyj), also note <i>го́лубь</i> (gólubʹ) (akin to <i>голубо́й</i> (golubój), the opposite direction of the meaning development)), <i>пестру́шка</i> (pestrúška) (akin to <i>пёстрый</i> (pjóstryj)), <i>бурёнка</i> (burjónka) (akin to <i>бу́рый</i> (búryj)), etc.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Соловей)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Solovey</li></ol><p>From <i>солове́й</i> (solovéj, “nightingale (bird)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>солове́й</i> (solovéj): nightingale</li></ol><p>From <i>солове́й</i> (solovéj) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Solovki, Solovetsky Islands (archipelago in the White Sea)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Solovetsky District (district containing the Solovetsky Islands)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Solovetsky Monastery (monastery on the Solovetsky Islands)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Solovetsky, Solovetskaya, Solovetskoye (any of various Russian settlements with these names)</li></ol><p><i>Соловки́</i> (Solovkí) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale yellow with light tail and mane (<i>of a horse</i>)</li><li>dull, inexpressive (<i>from intoxication or sickness; of a look or gaze</i>)</li><li>sluggish, drowsy (<i>from fatigue or intoxication; of a man or animal</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pale yellow horse with light tail and mane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solvъ</i>. Cognate with Russian Old Church Slavonic <i>славоочиѥ</i> (slavoočije, “blue-gray (<i>of eyes</i>)”), and more distantly with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salwaz</i> (“gray, muddy”) (whence English <i>sallow</i>). Ultimately related to Russian <i>солове́й</i> (solovéj, “nightingale”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) nightingale</li></ol><p><i>солове́й</i> (solovéj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malt (sprouted grain used in brewing)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>солодъ</i> (solodŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soldъ</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>сла̑д</i> and Polish <i>słód</i>.</p><p>See also <i>сла́дкий</i> (sládkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malthouse</li></ol><p><i>солоди́ть</i> (solodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to malt</li></ol><p><i>со́лод</i> (sólod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquorice (<i>Glycyrrhiza</i>, a plant)</li></ol><p><i>со́лод</i> (sólod) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Western Russia) sweet (having a pleasant taste)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soldъkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;salˀdus</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂l-</i>. Doublet of <i>сла́дкий</i> (sládkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malthouse</li></ol><p><i>со́лод</i> (sólod) +&lrm; <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) malt (sprouted grain used in brewing)</li></ol><p><i>со́лод</i> (sólod) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solma</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱolh₂mos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) straw</li><li>straw-colored, pale yellow</li></ol><p><i>соло́ма</i> (solóma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a single straw</li></ol><p><i>соло́ма</i> (solóma) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>соло́мина</i> (solómina): straw</li><li>drinking straw, straw</li></ol><p><i>соло́мина</i> (solómina) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>соло́ма</i> (solóma)</li><li>straws, shreds</li><li>matchwood</li></ol><p><i>соло́ма</i> (solóma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain weasel (<i>Mustela altaica</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solonetz</li></ol><p>From <i>солон-</i> (solon-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc), the irregular short stem <i>солон-</i> (solon-) of <i>солёный</i> (soljónyj) which appears as masculine singular <i>со́лон</i> (sólon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corned beef (a smoke cured and salted beef)</li><li>brined meat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solnina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt cellar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt shaker, salt cellar (a small container designed to hold salt and facilitate sprinkling)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brackish (slightly salty)</li></ol><p>From <i>солон-</i> (solon-) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj). This term is built off of the irregular short stem <i>солон-</i> (solon-) of <i>солёный</i> (soljónyj); compare short masculine singular <i>со́лон</i> (sólon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt marsh</li><li>solonchak (pale or grey soil type found in arid to subhumid, poorly drained conditions)</li></ol><p>From <i>солоне́ц</i> (solonéc) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;dated) to have a sweet or sweetish taste</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>солостити</i> (solostiti), either an Old Russian derivative of <i>&#42;солость</i> (&#42;solostĭ) (from <i>&#42;solstь</i>) + <i>-ити</i> (-iti), or directly inherited from <i>&#42;solstiti</i>, in which case it is a doublet of <i>сласти́ть</i> (slastítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) point</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) punch line (e.g. in stand-up or in a joke)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) psychoactive bath salts (mephedrone, or any similar stimulant designer drug)</li><li><i>(musical note)</i> G</li><li>sol <i>(unit of Peruvian currency)</i></li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;solь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;séh₂l-</i>, <i>&#42;sḗh₂l</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सलिल</i> (salila), Ancient Greek <i>ἅλς</i> (háls), Armenian <i>աղ</i> (aġ), Latin <i>sāl</i>, and Old English <i>sealt</i> (English <i>salt</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sol(ve)</i> in the hymn for St. John the Baptist whence all note names were taken.</p><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>sol</i> (“sun”), itself from Latin <i>sol</i> (“sun”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solo</li></ol><p><i>со́ло</i> (sólo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) solfeggio, solfège</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>solfeggio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>соль</i> (solʹ, “salt”)</li></ol><p><i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ца́</i> (-cá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) hydrochloric acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solyanka, a soup with tomatoes and sausages</li></ol><p>From archaic <i>селя́нка</i> (seljánka) with influence from <i>соль</i> (solʹ, “salt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) salt (related to salt deposits, excavation, processing or use)</li></ol><p><i>соль</i> (solʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solarium</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>sōlārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) solar oil (a grade of fuel oil used by some diesel engines installed on heavy machinery, rail locomotives, and ships)</li></ol><p><i>соля́ровый</i> (soljárovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); suffixed clipping of <i>соля́ровое ма́сло</i> (soljárovoje máslo), from German <i>Solaröl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salty (tasting of salt)</li></ol><p>From <i>соли́ть</i> (solítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catfish, sheatfish</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) som, so'm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;somъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Kyrgyz <i>сом</i> (som), Uzbek <i>сўм</i> (soʻm)/<i>soʻm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Somali (a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Somali (<i>language</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Сомали́</i> (Somalí) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somatology (the study of the physical nature of human beings)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, Ancient Greek <i>σῶμα, σώματος</i> (sôma, sṓmatos) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>соматоло́гия</i> (somatológija, “somatology”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sombrero</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>sombrero</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>umbra</i> (possibly via <i>umbrō</i>) + <i>-ārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sommelier (wine steward)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sommelier</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>sagma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to link <i>(up)</i>, to close in, (military,&#32;sports) to close</li><li>to close</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-мкну́ть</i> (-mknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to close ranks</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close <i>(up)</i>, to close down <i>(upon)</i>, to close in <i>(on)</i>, (military,&#32;sports) to close</li><li>to close</li></ol><p><i>сомкну́ть</i> (somknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>somnambulism, sleepwalking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>somnus</i> + <i>ambulō</i> + <i>-ismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>мнить</i> (mnitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt</li><li>question</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>doubtfully, dubiously</li></ol><p><i>сомни́тельный</i> (somnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertainty, doubtfulness</li></ol><p><i>сомни́тельный</i> (somnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doubtful, dubious, questionable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siluridae (a biological family that includes catfishes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) somoni (currency of Tajikistan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tajik <i>сомонӣ</i> (somoni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siluriformes (a biological order that includes catfishes)</li></ol><p><i>сом</i> (som) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep</li><li>dream (imaginary events seen in the mind while sleeping)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ̀nъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;supnós</i> (whence also <i>гипно́з</i> (gipnóz)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sonar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sonata</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>sonata</i>, from Latin <i>sonare</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sonatina</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sonata form</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sonata</li><li>sonata-form</li></ol><p><i>сона́та</i> (sonáta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sonnet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>sonetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>соне́т</i> (sonét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleepiness (property of being sleepy)</li></ol><p><i>сонли́вый</i> (sonlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleepy</li></ol><p><i>сон</i> (son) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>host, crowd</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съньмъ</i> (sŭnĭmŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъnьmъ</i>. Identical to <i>съём</i> (sʺjom), <i>сойм</i> (sojm), <i>суём</i> (sujóm) and <i>сейм</i> (sejm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>book of dream interpretations, dream dictionary</li><li>char (any of the several species of fishes of the genus <i>Salvelinus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>сон</i> (son, “dream”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sleepily, drowsily</li></ol><p><i>со́нный</i> (sónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeping, sleepy</li></ol><p><i>сон</i> (son) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сон</i> (son).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sleepyhead</li><li>dormouse</li></ol><p>From <i>сон</i> (son, “sleep”), by analogy with <i>Со́ня</i> (Sónja, “Sonya”), a diminutive of <i>Со́фья</i> (Sófʹja).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Соня)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Sonya, of the female given names Со́фья (Sófʹja), Софи́я (Sofíja), or Софі́я (Sofíja)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Со́фья</i> (Sófʹja), <i>Софи́я</i> (Sofíja), or Ukrainian <i>Софі́я</i> (Sofíja) + <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormouse, loir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;Internet) community (an online or Internet one, often referring to a formally created group, in some uses more nebulously)</li><li>(slang,&#32;Internet) message (in an online chat or messenger application)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>соо́бщество</i> (soóbščestvo).</p><p>Clipping of <i>сообще́ние</i> (soobščénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;joking) ability to think or understand</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Sverdlovsk Oblast) children's game or puzzle</li></ol><p><i>сообража́ть</i> (soobražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to ponder (over), to think (over / out); to weigh, to weigh the pros and the cons</li><li>to understand, to grasp, to guess, to figure out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare (food), to cook</li></ol><p><i>сообрази́ть</i> (soobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consideration</li><li>reason</li><li>grasp, understanding</li></ol><p><i>сообрази́ть</i> (soobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acumen, gumption, quick wit</li></ol><p><i>сообрази́тельный</i> (soobrazítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick-witted, astute, clever</li></ol><p><i>сообрази́ть</i> (soobrazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, to ponder (over), to think (over / out); to weigh, to weigh the pros and the cons</li><li>to understand, to grasp, to guess, to figure out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare (food), to cook, to whip up, to fix</li><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial) to drink alcohol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>according to, in accordance (with), in conformity with [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>сообра́зный</i> (soobráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commensurate (with), consistent (with), appropriate (for), proportionate (to), in line (with) [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together, conjointly, jointly (at the same time, in the same place)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate, to inform, to report, to tell, to say, to let know</li><li>to impart</li></ol><p><i>сообщи́ть</i> (soobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be reported</li><li>to be communicated, to communicate</li><li>to be in communication <i>(with)</i>, to communicate <i>(with)</i></li><li>to be imparted <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>сообща́ть</i> (soobščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>report, communication, message</li><li>statement, announcement, information</li><li>connection, line (of transportation), route</li></ol><p><i>сообщи́ть</i> (soobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>community</li><li>company</li></ol><p><i>сообщи́ть</i> (soobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to communicate, to report, to let know</li><li>to impart</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>о́бщий</i> (óbščij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be imparted <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>сообщи́ть</i> (soobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplice (an associate in the commission of a crime)</li></ol><p><i>сообщи́ть</i> (soobščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, to construct, to erect</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>оруд</i> (orud) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, to construct, to erect</li></ol><p><i>сооруди́ть</i> (soorudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, building, erection (<i>action</i>)</li><li>building, structure</li></ol><p><i>сооруди́ть</i> (soorudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accordingly, correspondingly (in proportion)</li><li>properly, the way it should be done</li><li>respectively</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>according to, in accordance with</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correspondence, conformity (agreement of situations or objects with an expected outcome) [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correspond, to conform, to agree, to comply, to be in line [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with/to what’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corresponding, respective</li><li>appropriate, suitable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compatriot, fellow countryman</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>оте́чество</i> (otéčestvo) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correlation</li></ol><p><i>соотнести́</i> (sootnestí) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correlate <i>(with)</i>, to bring into correlation <i>(with)</i>, to compare <i>(to, with)</i></li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>отнести́</i> (otnestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correlate <i>(with)</i>, to bring into correlation <i>(with)</i>, to compare <i>(to, with)</i></li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>относи́ть</i> (otnosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to correspond, to relate, to sort with</li></ol><p><i>соотноси́ть</i> (sootnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>corresponding, relating, sorting with</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correspondence (agreement of situations or objects with an expected outcome)</li><li>correlation, ratio</li></ol><p><i>соотноси́ть</i> (sootnosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheezing</li><li>wheeze, wheezing sound</li></ol><p><i>сопе́ть</i> (sopétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empathy</li><li>compassion</li></ol><p><i>сопережива́ть</i> (sopereživátʹ, “empathize”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to empathize</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>пережива́ть</i> (pereživátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rival, adversary, opponent</li><li>antagonist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>супьрьникъ</i> (supĭrĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫpьrьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compete <i>(with in)</i>, to rival <i>(in)</i>, to vie <i>(with in)</i></li></ol><p><i>сопе́рник</i> (sopérnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rivalry, competitiveness, competition</li></ol><p><i>сопе́рничать</i> (sopérničatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wheeze (to breathe heavily and noisily through the nose)</li><li>to sniffle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сопѣти</i> (sopěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sopěti</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>сопі́ти</i> (sopíty), Belarusian <i>сапці́</i> (sapcí) or <i>со́пці</i> (sópci).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knoll, hill, mound</li><li>volcano</li></ol><p>From <i>соп</i> (sop, “mound (<i>dialectal</i>)”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpъ</i>, related to <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ, “to pour, to strew”). Per Vasmer, meaning "volcano" presumably influenced by <i>сопе́ть</i> (sopétʹ, “to wheeze”); Czech <i>sopka</i> (“volcano”), Slovak <i>sopka</i> (“volcano”) are borrowings from Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribemate, fellow tribesman, congener</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>пле́мя</i> (plémja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) snivel, snot (mucus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;literally) snot-nosed, snotty (with snot running from one's nose)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) young and inexperienced, wet behind the ears</li></ol><p><i>со́пли</i> (sópli) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nozzle (inlet or outlet pipe)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>соп(еть)</i> (sop(etʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>nozzle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (a piece of) snivel, snot (mucus)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) ping-pong ball flying in close to vertical direction, difficult or impossible to catch</li><li>brat, worthless, lousy person; wimp, spineless person, weakling</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sopъľa</i>. Cognate with Czech <i>sopel</i>.</p><p>For the cognation with Russian <i>сопе́ть</i> (sopétʹ), compare typologically English <i>snivel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) brat, snot, punk (a selfish, spoiled, or unruly child or teenager)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparable (able to be compared)</li><li>commensurable (able to be measured using a common standard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to match up, to compare, to contrast (with)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>поста́вить</i> (postávitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comparison, contrasting</li><li>juxtaposition</li><li>collation, matching</li></ol><p><i>сопоста́вить</i> (sopostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to match up, to compare, to contrast (with)</li></ol><p><i>сопоста́вить</i> (sopostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) soprano (pitch)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>soprano</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adjoining, contiguous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>co-chair, co-chairman, co-chairperson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot due to high temperature or humidity</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sweat</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>преть</i> (pretʹ, “to rot; to sweat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to be contiguous <i>(to)</i>, to adjoin</li><li>to have in common</li><li>to be in contact <i>(with)</i>, to have to do <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>прикаса́ться</i> (prikasátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>osculant</li><li>contiguous (connected, touching, abutting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contiguity; contact; touch</li><li>contiguity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>прикосну́ться</i> (prikosnútʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) involvement, participation, implication (<i>in</i>)</li></ol><p><i>соприча́стный</i> (sopričástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>involved, implicated (<i>in conjunction with someone else</i>)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>прича́стный</i> (pričástnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accompanying (<i>document, form, etc.</i>)</li><li>escorting (<i>vehicle, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сопроводи́ть</i> (soprovodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accompany</li><li>to attend, to escort, to convoy</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>проводи́ть</i> (provodítʹ, “to escort, to accompany”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accompany</li><li>to attend, to escort, to convoy</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>провождать</i> (provoždatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant, escort</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) maintainer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accompaniment (that which accompanies)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) accompaniment (that which gives support or adds to the background)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(film) coproducer (male or female)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>продю́сер</i> (prodjúsɛr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) (electrical) resistance</li><li>resistor</li></ol><p>From <i>сопротивля́ться</i> (soprotivljátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije). The sense "electrical resistance" is a semantic loan from German <i>Widerstand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resistance</li></ol><p><i>сопротивля́ть(ся)</i> (soprotivljátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-емый</i> (-emyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resist, to oppose [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>про́тив</i> (prótiv) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer hardware</i>) coprocessor</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>coprocessor</i>. By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>проце́ссор</i> (procéssor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to join, to couple, to conjugate, to mate</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to interface</li></ol><p><i>сопря́чь</i> (soprjáčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coupling, linking</li><li>junction</li><li>interface</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) conjugation</li></ol><p><i>сопря́г-</i> (soprjág-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to join, to couple, to conjugate, to mate</li><li>(<i>informatics</i>) to interface</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-пря́чь</i> (-prjáčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accompany, to attend</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concomitant, attendant, accompanying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trash, rubbish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sorъ</i>, possibly from the root of Russian <i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to defecate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coworker</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тник</i> (rabótnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commensurate, proportionate, proportioned, balanced</li><li>adequate (equal to some requirement)</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>разме́р</i> (razmér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comrade in arms</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>рать</i> (ratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prankster, mischiefmaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick, to pluck (<i>flowers or fruits</i>)</li><li>to tear off</li><li>to ruin, to destroy, to frustrate, to derange (e.g. a plan, work)</li><li>to wrench off (thread)</li><li>to vent (upon)</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break loose, to come unhinged, to come off</li><li>to fall down (from a cliff)</li><li>(of screw threads) to lose integrity, to become broken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to end with a failure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall off the wagon, to lapse back into an old habit or addiction</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart off, to dash off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose one's temper</li></ol><p><i>сорва́ть</i> (sorvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) madcap, romp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) daredevil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorghum (cereal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>sorgo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competition, contest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compete, to contend, to emulate</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>ревнова́ть</i> (revnovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) sorry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sorry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to orient, to orientate (oneself), to get one's bearings (to familiarize with a situation or circumstance)</li></ol><p><i>сориенти́ровать</i> (sorijentírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speck, mote, piece of debris</li></ol><p><i>сор</i> (sor) +&lrm; <i>-и́нка</i> (-ínka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to litter (drop or throw trash without properly disposing of it)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to waste something (e.g. money) recklessly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>growing wild, drowning out cultivated crops</li><li>contaminated, unclean</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>со́р</i> (sór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) weed (unwanted plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relative, kinsman</li><li>fellow countryman, compatriot</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>ро́дич</i> (ródič)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty (40)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сорокъ</i> (sorokŭ, “a bunch of 40 sable pelts; forty”), displaced <i>четꙑредесѧте</i> (četyredesęte, “forty”) (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četyre desęte</i> (“forty”)).</p><p>Further etymology is unclear. In the past regarded as borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>σαράκοντα</i> (sarákonta, “40”), but this etymology is dubious for phonetic and semantic reasons. The older meaning is a bunch of sable pelts. May be related to соро́чка (soróčka); compare Old Norse <i>serkr</i> (“shirt; 200 furs”), archaic Slovak <i>meru</i> (“40”) from Hungarian <i>mérő</i> (“sack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-eight (48)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-two (42)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-nine (49)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-one (41)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-five (45)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-seven (47)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-three (43)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-four (44)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty-six (46)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian magpie (<i>Pica pica</i>) (Russians associate the magpie with unpleasant squawking, complaining, and especially thievery)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;s(v)orka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hexoctahedron, tetracontaoctahedron</li></ol><p><i>сорока</i> (soroka, genitive) +&lrm; <i>восемь</i> (vosemʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forty-year period</li><li>fortieth anniversary, fortieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>сорока́</i> (soroká, genitive) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) forty-year</li><li>forty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>сорока́</i> (soroká, genitive) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetracontagon</li></ol><p><i>сорока́</i> (soroká, genitive) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortieth</li></ol><p><i>со́рок</i> (sórok) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centipede</li></ol><p><i>со́рок</i> (sórok, “forty”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>но́жка</i> (nóžka, “leg (diminutive)”) or <i>со́рок</i> (sórok, “forty”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “leg”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) shame, disgrace</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>соромъ</i> (soromŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sormъ</i>. Doublet of <i>срам</i> (sram), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) someone allegedly affiliated with George Soros</li></ol><p><i>Со́рос</i> (Sóros) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) shirt, alternative form of <i>соро́чка</i> (soróčka)</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>срачи́ца</i> (sračíca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemise, shift (historical undergarment), nightdress, nightgown</li><li>(rare,&#32;dated) shirt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сорочька</i> (soročĭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sorka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) sorry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sorry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort, kind, variety (type, race, category)</li><li>quality, grade</li><li>brand</li><li>cultivar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sorte</i>, from Latin <i>sors, sortis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) john, toilet</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a position of loss in checkers (draughts) when a player has no valid move remaining (in Russian it is called a loss by being locked in the toilet [запереть в сортире], see #1)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sortir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assort; to sort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assortment, sorting, grading, sizing</li><li>separator, sorter</li></ol><p><i>сортирова́ть</i> (sortirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sorting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorter</li></ol><p><i>сортирова́ть</i> (sortirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) sorry</li></ol><p>From <i>со́ри</i> (sóri, “sorry”) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án, augmentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sucking</li><li>suction (the process of creating an imbalance in pressure to draw matter from one place to another)</li></ol><p><i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) to perform fellatio</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съсати</i> (sŭsati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъsàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol><p><i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сусѣдъ</i> (susědŭ, “neighbor”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫsědъ</i>, deverbative of <i>&#42;sъsěděti</i> (“sit together”). The prefix <i>су-</i> (su-) was replaced with productive verbal <i>со-</i> (so-); compare dialectal <i>сусе́д</i> (suséd). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighboring country</li></ol><p><i>сосе́д</i> (soséd) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neighboring, next to</li></ol><p><i>сосе́д</i> (soséd) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neighbor</li><li>neighbor's, neighbors'</li></ol><p><i>сосе́д</i> (soséd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighborhood, vicinity (close proximity, particularly in reference to home)</li></ol><p><i>сосе́д</i> (soséd) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be someone's neighbour</li><li>to be near <i>(someone or something)</i>, to be close <i>(to)</i></li><li>to be combined, to be there along <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сосе́дство</i> (sosédstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>сосна́</i> (sosná, “pine”)</li></ol><p><i>сосна́</i> (sosná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cooked) sausage, wiener, frankfurter, hot dog</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p><p>From French <i>saucisse</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Ultimately from Latin <i>sāl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saucisson</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) small sausage</li></ol><p><i>соси́ска</i> (sosíska) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) teat, nipple (rubber nipple attached to a bottle to feed an infant)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) pacifier, dummy</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;vulgar) cocksucker</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;vulgar) inexperienced, stupid girl or twink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъsъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump off <i>(of)</i>, to jump down <i>(from)</i>, to spring down <i>(from)</i></li><li>to come off</li></ol><p><i>соскок-</i> (soskok-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide down, to slide off, to glide down, to slip <i>(off)</i></li></ol><p><i>соскользну́ть</i> (soskolʹznútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slide down, to slide off, to glide down, to slip <i>(off)</i></li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump off <i>(of)</i>, to jump down <i>(from)</i>, to spring down <i>(from)</i></li><li>to come off</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-скочи́ть</i> (-skočítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape away; to scratch off</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>скрести́</i> (skrestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become bored, to start feeling boredom</li><li>to miss, to start missing someone or something</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>-ску́чить</i> (-skúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) subjunctive mood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subjunctive</li></ol><p><i>сослага́ть</i> (soslagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to add together, to combine, to connect</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to banish, to exile</li><li>to make someone refer to</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refer to, to allude to, to cite, to quote [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>сосла́ть</i> (soslátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) social estate</li><li>social class, social rank (<i>with strong connotations of cultural caste</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съсловие</i> (sŭslovije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъslovьje</i>, itself a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σύγκλητος</i> (súnklētos) / <i>σύλλογος</i> (súllogos). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) social estate (related to the division of society into hereditary social classes)</li><li>based on social rank, class-based</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) caste</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) privileged-class, high-class</li></ol><p><i>сосло́вие</i> (soslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colleague (fellow member of a profession)</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve (well or badly), to do (a service) to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to perform (a service or favor) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine, pine tree</li><li>pine wood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сосна</i> (sosna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sosna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pine</li></ol><p><i>сосна́</i> (sosná) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>co-owner</li></ol><p>Compound of со- (so-, “co-”) and собственник (sobstvennik, “owner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nipple</li></ol><p><i>сос(ать)</i> (sos(atʹ), “to suck”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сосо́к</i> (sosók); a (small) nipple</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;botany) papilla</li></ol><p><i>сосо́к</i> (sosók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) endearing diminutive of <i>со́ска</i> (sóska): pacifier</li><li>(animate,&#32;vulgar,&#32;derogatory) endearing diminutive of <i>со́ска</i> (sóska): chick, gal</li></ol><p><i>со́ска</i> (sóska) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to fix, to focus</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́чить</i> (sosredotóčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be concentrated</li></ol><p><i>сосредота́чивать</i> (sosredotáčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentration, focus</li><li>centralization</li><li>assembly</li><li>mass</li><li>build-up</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́чить</i> (sosredotóčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with concentration</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́ченный</i> (sosredotóčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concentration</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́ченный</i> (sosredotóčennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concentrated</li><li>focused</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to fix, to focus</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́чить</i> (sosredotóčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be concentrated</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́чивать</i> (sosredotóčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to focus (transitive)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-, “con-”) +&lrm; <i>средото́чие</i> (sredotóčije, “center, focus”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ, “-ate, -ize”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to concentrate, to focus (intransitive)</li></ol><p><i>сосредото́чить</i> (sosredotóčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composition, structure, membership, staff, body</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) composition, solution, mixture</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) train, stock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съставъ</i> (sŭstavŭ), which itself is a calque of Ancient Greek <i>σύστημα</i> (sústēma), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, with, together) + <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compiler, writer, author</li></ol><p>From <i>соста́вить</i> (sostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to constitute, to make up, to form</li><li>to amount to, to sum up to</li><li>to compose, to construct, to draw up, to prepare</li><li>to place objects on top of each other to form a stack</li><li>to move things from one place to another</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съставити</i> (sŭstaviti). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съставитисѧ</i> (sŭstavitisę). By surface analysis, <i>соста́вить</i> (sostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making, compiling, working out, drawing up</li></ol><p><i>соста́вить</i> (sostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to constitute, to make up, to form</li><li>to amount to, to sum up to</li><li>to compose, to construct, to draw up, to prepare</li><li>to stack (to place objects on top of each other to form a stack)</li><li>to transfer, to place (to move things from one place to another)</li></ol><p><i>соста́вить</i> (sostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form</li></ol><p><i>составля́ть</i> (sostavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>component, constituent</li></ol><p>Substantivized feminine of <i>составля́ющий</i> (sostavljájuščij), present active participle of <i>составля́ть</i> (sostavljátʹ, “to constitute, to compose, to put together”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>composite, compound</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) component, constituent</li></ol><p><i>соста́вить</i> (sostávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make old, to make look old</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, to age</li></ol><p><i>соста́рить</i> (sostáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, status, condition</li><li>fortune, capital, bankroll</li></ol><p><i>состоя́ть</i> (sostojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, affluence, prosperity</li><li>(<i>finance</i>) solvency</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) soundness, well-foundedness (<i>of an argument</i>)</li></ol><p><i>состоя́тельный</i> (sostojátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affluent, wealthy, prosperous, well-off, well-to-do</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) reasoned, persuasive, well-founded</li></ol><p><i>состоя́ть</i> (sostojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consist (of)</li><li>to be a member of, to occupy a position in, to be in a position, to belong to</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take place, to come about</li><li>to establish oneself</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compassion, sympathy</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>страда́ние</i> (stradánije, “suffering”), perhaps a calque of Latin <i>compassiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sympathize, to empathize, to feel for</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack jokes, to quip, to be witty</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>остри́ть</i> (ostrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to build</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to arrange, to organise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull a facial expression, to grimace</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cook, to cook food</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to concoct, to cook up</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>стря́пать</i> (strjápatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competition, contest (action of competing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competitiveness</li></ol><p><i>состяза́тельный</i> (sostjazátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) competition</li><li>competitive</li></ol><p><i>состяза́ться</i> (sostjazátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compete, to vie, to contend</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old Church Slavonic <i>сътѧѕати сѧ</i> (sŭtędzati sę), <i>сътѧзати сѧ</i> (sŭtęzati sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tędzati</i>, featuring 3rd palatalization. Compare native <i>тяга́ться</i> (tjagátʹsja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęgati</i> without 3rd palatalization.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel (container)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vessel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съсудъ</i> (sŭsudŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъsǫdъ</i>. Related to Old Church Slavonic <i>съсѫдъ</i> (sŭsǫdŭ), Bulgarian <i>съсъ̀д</i> (sǎsǎ̀d).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small blood vessel, a capillary</li></ol><p><i>сосуд</i> (sosud) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;anatomy) vascular</li></ol><p><i>сосу́д</i> (sosúd) +&lrm; <i>-истый</i> (-istyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>icicle <i>(a spear-shape of ice)</i></li></ol><p><i>сосу́ля</i> (sosúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) icicle</li></ol><p>From <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́ля</i> (-úlja), supposedly due to children's habit of sucking icicles.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sucker (of baby)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coexistence</li></ol><p><i>сосуществова́ть</i> (sosuščestvovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coexist</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>существова́ть</i> (suščestvovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, to calculate, to compute</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to square accounts <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get even</li></ol><p><i>сосчита́ть</i> (sosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, to calculate</li></ol><p><i>сосчита́ть</i> (sosčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to square accounts <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get even</li></ol><p><i>сосчи́тывать</i> (sosčítyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>со́сенка</i> (sósenka)</li></ol><p><i>сосна́</i> (sosná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сосе́дство</i> (sosédstvo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hexagonal cell (<i>as in a honeycomb</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) cell, compartment</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) cell (area covered by a single tower in a cellular network)</li></ol><p>Analogical singular of <i>со́ты</i> (sóty, “honeycomb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hundredth part</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) very small part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation, making</li></ol><p><i>сотвори́ть</i> (sotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to create</li><li>to do, to make</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сътворити</i> (sŭtvoriti). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>create</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saute pan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cell phone</li></ol><p>From <i>со́товый телефо́н</i> (sótovyj telefón, “cell phone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) area, one hundred square metres</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Ural,&#32;Kazakhstan,&#32;Kyrgyzstan) phone</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>Clipping of <i>сотовый</i> (sotovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>ткать</i> (tkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) centurion</li><li>sotnik <i>(Cossack lieutenant)</i></li></ol><p><i>со́т</i> (sót) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hundred</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) sotnia, company (Cossack military unit)</li></ol><p><i>со́тый</i> (sótyj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colleague, associate</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) honeycomb</li><li>(relational,&#32;telephony) cell; cellular</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellular telephone, mobile phone, cell phone</li></ol><p><i>со́ты</i> (sóty) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cell phone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eating companion</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>тра́пеза</i> (trápeza) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) collaborator, colleague, assistant</li><li>employee, associate</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) staff</li><li>(<i>journalism</i>) contributor, author (a person who regularly writes articles for a newspaper or a journal, or writes entries for a multi-part work, e.g. an encyclopedia)</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-, “co”) +&lrm; <i>труд</i> (trud, “work, labor”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Typologically compare French <i>collaborateur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cooperate, to collaborate</li><li>to contribute <i>(to a newspaper etc.)</i>, to work on a newspaper</li></ol><p><i>сотру́дник</i> (sotrúdnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). But see <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooperation</li><li>collaboration, cooperation (with), partnership</li><li>contribution</li></ol><p><i>сотру́дничать</i> (sotrúdničatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сътрѧсати</i> (sŭtręsati). By surface analysis, <i>сотрясти́</i> (sotrjastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Doublet of <i>стряса́ть</i> (strjasátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, to tremble</li></ol><p><i>сотряса́ть</i> (sotrjasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concussion, shaking; commotion</li><li>percussion, tremor, jolt, shock</li></ol><p><i>сотряс-</i> (sotrjas-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сътрѧсти</i> (sŭtręsti). By surface analysis, <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí). Doublet of <i>стрясти́</i> (strjastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, to tremble</li></ol><p><i>сотрясти́</i> (sotrjastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) local militiaman</li></ol><p>Most likely derived from сто (sto, “hundred”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeycomb, honeycombs</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъtъ</i> (which also inherited into Belarusian <i>со́ты</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (sóty), Slovene <i>sat</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>сат</i> (sat), and borrowed into Albanian <i>sotinë</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a hundred</li></ol><p>From сто (sto). For derivation, compare торопыга (toropyga), барыга (baryga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundredth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) soul music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>soul</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauce</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sḗh₂l</td></tr></table></p><p>Borrowed from French <i>sauce</i>. Further from Latin <i>salsus</i> (akin to <i>sāl</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḗh₂l</i> (whence also Russian <i>соль</i> (solʹ), Ancient Greek <i>ἅλς</i> (háls), English <i>salt</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravy boat</li></ol><p><i>со́ус</i> (sóus) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saucer (dish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>participating</li><li>accessory (assisting a crime)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complicity (the state of being complicit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accomplice (an associate in the commission of a crime)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>classmate</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>учени́к</i> (učeník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cofounder</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>учреди́тель</i> (učredítelʹ). Apparently not derived from the verb <i>соучреди́ть</i> (součredítʹ, “to cofound”), which is later and must be a back-formation from this word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sofa whose back and sides have the same height; a canapé</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sofa</i> or Italian <i>sofà</i>, ultimately from Arabic صُفَّة (ṣuffa, “a long seat made of stone or brick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sophist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sophistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage lighting instrument (<i>e.g. spotlight, floodlight</i>)</li><li>metal rack for hanging light fixtures above a stage, raised and lowered by an electric actuator</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) soffit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>soffitto</i> (“ceiling”). Further from Latin <i>suffīgō</i> (<i>sub-</i> + Latin <i>fīgō</i>), compare Latin <i>suffīxus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sophora (<i>Sophora</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Sophora</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softball (game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>softball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) software (encoded computer instructions)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>software</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sokha, Russian ard (a type of wooden plough&#47;plow with high, horizontal draft-poles and usually two metal-tipped shares, originating in Northern Russia in medieval times and widely used in Russia and nearby countries until the early 20th century)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soxa</i>, akin to Bulgarian <i>соха́</i> (sohá, “forked rod”), Serbo-Croatian <i>соха</i>/<i>soha</i>, Slovak <i>socha</i> (“statue”). Non-Slavic cognates include Sanskrit <i>शाखा</i> (śākhā, “branch”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐍉𐌷𐌰</i> (hōha, “plough”), Armenian <i>ցախ</i> (cʻax, “branch”), Lithuanian <i>šakà</i> (“branch”), Old Irish <i>géc</i> (“branch”) and Persian شاخ (šâx, “branch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boughy, branching</li><li>with/having branching antlers</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elk (<i>British</i>), moose (<i>U.S., Canada</i>)</li></ol><p><i>соха́</i> (soxá, “sokha, Russian ard”) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj), referring to a Russian ard's construction.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry</li><li>to wither, to fade</li><li>to have a crush on <i>(someone)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съхнути</i> (sŭxnuti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъxnǫti</i> (“to dry, wither”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coowner</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>хозя́ин</i> (xozjáin, “owner”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preservation, conservation (the act of preserving, conserving)</li><li>saving (e.g. a computer file)</li><li>safe-keeping</li></ol><p><i>сохрани́ть</i> (soxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) to save (to write a file to a disk)</li><li>to preserve, to conserve</li><li>to retain</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain intact, to last out</li><li>to be well preserved (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>сохрани́ть</i> (soxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safety</li><li>integrity</li></ol><p><i>сохра́нный</i> (soxránnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) subject to safekeeping, guarded</li><li>safe</li><li>whole, intact</li></ol><p><i>сохрани́ть</i> (soxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) to save (to write a file to a disk)</li><li>to preserve, to conserve</li></ol><p><i>сохрани́ть</i> (soxranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain intact, to last out</li><li>to be well preserved (of a person)</li></ol><p><i>сохраня́ть</i> (soxranjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) inflorescence, blossom/flower cluster</li></ol><p>From <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Ukrainian <i>суцві́ття</i> (sucvíttja), Belarusian <i>сукве́цце</i> (sukvjéccje).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social democrat</li></ol><p><i>социа́л(ьный)</i> (sociál(ʹnyj)) +&lrm; <i>демокра́т</i> (demokrát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>social democratic</li></ol><p><i>социа́л-демокра́т</i> (sociál-demokrát) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social chauvinism</li></ol><p>From <i>социали́зм</i> (socialízm) + <i>шовини́зм</i> (šovinízm).</p><p>The concept of social chauvinism as a kind of opportunism was first used by Vladimir Ulyanov-Lenin in the pamphlet <i>Dead Chauvinism and Living Socialism (1914)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socialization</li><li>conversion to socialism or state ownership</li></ol><p><i>социализ(и́ровать)</i> (socializ(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to socialize (<i>especially to socialist society</i>)</li><li>to convert to socialism or state ownership</li></ol><p><i>социали́з(м)</i> (socialíz(m)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socialism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>socialisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socialist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>socialist, socialistic</li></ol><p>From <i>социали́ст</i> (socialíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social network (a network of personal or business contacts on the Internet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>socially</li></ol><p><i>социа́льный</i> (sociálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sociopolitical</li></ol><p><i>социа́льный</i> (sociálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полити́ческий</i> (politíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>socio-psychological, psychosocial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>socio-economic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social media</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>social</li><li>subsidized, welfare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Socism</li></ol><p>Apparently, the word first appears in the first novel <i>Yawning Heights</i> (1976) by the Soviet philosopher Alexander Zinoviev.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Socinian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>socio-cultural</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociolect (a language used by a social group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociolinguistics</li></ol><p>From <i>социо-</i> (socio-, “socio-”) +&lrm; <i>лингви́стика</i> (lingvístika, “linguistics”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sociolinguistic (pertaining to sociolinguistics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sociological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociology (study of society, human social interactions, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sociopath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>society, social stratum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>socium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social security, social welfare</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>социа́льное обеспе́чение</i> (sociálʹnoje obespéčenije) or <i>социа́льное обеспече́ние</i> (sociálʹnoje obespečénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socialist realism</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>социалисти́ческий реали́зм</i> (socialistíčeskij realízm, “socialist realism”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social network (a network of personal or business contacts on the Internet)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>социа́льная сеть</i> (sociálʹnaja setʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christmas Eve (evening before Christmas Day)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compatible, associative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combination (one or more elements selected without regard of the order)</li><li>conjunction</li></ol><p><i>сочета́ть</i> (sočetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>associative, combinative, combinatorial</li></ol><p><i>сочета́ть</i> (sočetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to combine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to unite</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to associate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съчетати</i> (sŭčetati). Equivalent to <i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>чета́</i> (četá) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to combine</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to unite</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to associate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to marry</li></ol><p><i>сочета́ть</i> (sočetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hemp seed oil; poppy seed oil</li><li>kutia (sweet grain pudding, traditionally served in Polish, Ukrainian, Lithuanian, Belarusian and Russian cultures)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сочиво</i> (sočivo, “lentil seed”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sočivo</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>сочиво́</i> (sočivó, “soup”), Serbo-Croatian <i>со̏чиво</i> (“lentils, legumes”), Slovene <i>sočȋvo</i> (“lentils, legumes”). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sokъ</i> (“juice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>composition, essay</li><li>opus</li></ol><p><i>сочини́ть</i> (sočinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) writer, author</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) storyteller, inventor</li></ol><p><i>сочини́ть</i> (sočinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) writing (<i>of literature, poems, etc.</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) inventing, fantasizing</li></ol><p><i>сочини́ть</i> (sočinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write, to compose (text, music)</li><li>to make up (an unreal story or excuse), to invent, to fabricate, to lie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write, to compose (text, music)</li><li>to make up (an unreal story or excuse), to invent, to fabricate, to lie</li></ol><p><i>сочини́ть</i> (sočinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-fetched, unnatural, contrived</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emit <i>(a liquid)</i></li><li>to get a tree sap</li></ol><p><i>сок</i> (sok) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ooze <i>(out)</i>, to exude, to trickle</li><li>to emit, to give forth</li></ol><p><i>сочи́ть</i> (sočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>articulation (joint or collection of joints)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>juicy (containing juice)</li><li>luscious</li><li>saturated</li></ol><p><i>сок</i> (sok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sympathetically</li></ol><p><i>сочу́вственный</i> (sočúvstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sympathetic</li></ol><p><i>сочу́вствие</i> (sočúvstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) compassion, sympathy</li><li>condolence</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo, “feeling”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije, abstract noun suffix), a calque of Ancient Greek <i>συμπάθεια</i> (sumpátheia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sympathetic towards someone</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;rhetorical) descent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съшьствиѥ</i> (sŭšĭstvije), <i>съшествиѥ</i> (sŭšestvije). By surface analysis, <i>соше́д</i> (sošéd) +&lrm; <i>-ствие</i> (-stvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>соха́</i> (soxá, “sokha, Russian ard”)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) steering joint</li><li>(<i>military</i>) bipod, shooting stick (a portable mount for a weapon)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) bipod leg</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) monopod</li><li>(military,&#32;obsolete) musket rack</li><li>(military,&#32;historical) fork rest (a monopod mount for a musket)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) unimportant person, nonentity, sideman</li></ol><p><i>соха́</i> (soxá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;soxa</i> (“forked stick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to squint (one's eye(s))</li></ol><p><i>со-</i> (so-) +&lrm; <i>щу́рить</i> (ščúritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Soviet) Union</li></ol><li>alliance</li><li>confederation, confederacy</li><li>league</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conjunction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)ǫzъ</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Союз)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soviet Union (a former country in Europe and Asia, USSR)</li><li>short for the Soyuz spacecraft</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>Сове́тский Сою́з</i> (Sovétskij Sojúz) or <i>Сою́з Сове́тских Социалисти́ческих Респу́блик</i> (Sojúz Sovétskix Socialistíčeskix Respúblik). Literally, “the Union”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ally (one united to another by treaty or league)</li></ol><p><i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allied</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) union</li></ol><p><i>сою́з</i> (sojúz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сою́з</i> (sojúz).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soy, soya</li><li>soybean</li></ol><p>From Satsuma Japanese <i>醤油</i> (そい, soi) [soj] (a variant of standard Japanese <i>醤油</i> (しょうゆ, shōyu)), from Old to Middle Japanese <i>醬油</i> (しやうゆ, shauyu), from Middle Chinese <i>醬油</i> (tsjàng-yuw), from <i>醬</i> (tsjang<sup>H</sup>, “bean paste”) + <i>油</i> (“oil”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Соя)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sōya (a subprefecture of Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>宗谷</i> (そうや, Sōya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spa centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spaghetti (pasta)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>spaghetti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decline, slump, abatement</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpadъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abate, to subside (to decrease or become less in strength)</li><li>to fall, to come down</li><li>to drop</li><li>to decline</li><li>to fall away</li><li>to sag</li></ol><p><i>спа́д-</i> (spád-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink (<i>of an elastic object or bodily organ</i>)</li></ol><p><i>спа́сться</i> (spástʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spasm (contraction of a muscle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spasmodic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spasmology, an area of neurology that deals with spasticity and its management. Spasmologists employ a spasmometer (an instrument for measuring the force and frequency of spasms) and a spasm system that reconciles the spasmometer readings. (This word may be a protologism.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adhesion (abnormal union of surface)</li><li>joint, seal</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) commissure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bedroom</li></ol><p><i>спа́льня</i> (spálʹnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn, to scorch; to singe; to destroy with fire</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to catch someone doing something illegal or disallowed, to catch red-handed</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пали́ть</i> (palítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ellipsis of <i>спа́льная ко́мната</i> (spálʹnaja kómnata): bedroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeping (<i>car, bag, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeping bag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedroom</li><li>suite of furniture for a bedroom</li></ol><p><i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) spam (a collection of unsolicited bulk electronic messages)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spam</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spammer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spammer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spaniel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) asparagus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>sparagio</i>, from Latin <i>asparagus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἀσπάραγος</i> (aspáragos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) asparagus</li></ol><p><i>спа́рж(а)</i> (spárž(a)) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparring (practice match)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sparring</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Spartakiad (name of various international and national sporting games played in the Eastern Bloc, sponsored by the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p>From <i>Спарта́к</i> (Sparták, “Spartacus”) + <i>-иа́да</i> (-iáda), by analogy with <i>олимпиа́да</i> (olimpiáda, “Olympiad”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) member of the Spartacus League (Marxist revolutionary movement in Germany)</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) member of the Spartak sports club</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove (something sewn) by tearing out the stitches</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blurt out</li></ol><p><i>споро́ть</i> (sporótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) rescue, salvation</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>спасти́</i> (spastí).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Спас)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Savior; Jesus Christ</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;inanimate) icon with an image of Jesus Christ the Savior</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;inanimate) church in honor of Jesus Christ the Savior</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съпасъ</i> (sŭpasŭ, “savior”), deverbal from Old East Slavic <i>съпасти</i> (sŭpasti) (compare Russian <i>спасти́</i> (spastí)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rescue worker, rescuer</li></ol><p><i>спаса́ть</i> (spasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rescue, life-saving</li></ol><p><i>спаса́ть</i> (spasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to rescue</li><li>to salvage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save oneself, to escape</li><li>to use as the thing that helps</li></ol><p><i>спаса́ть</i> (spasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rescue, salvation (act of rescuing, saving)</li></ol><p><i>спас-</i> (spas-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) gratitude, expression of gratitude</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the plural) Used to express gratitude.</li></ol><p><i>спаси́бо</i> (spasíbo) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>thank you, thanks [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘for’</small>]</li></ol><p>From earlier Old East Slavic <i>&#42;съпаси богъ</i> (&#42;sŭpasi bogŭ, “God save (you)”) (modern Russian <i>спаси́ Бог</i> (spasí Bog)). Compare Ukrainian <i>спаси́бі</i> (spasýbi) and Belarusian <i>спасі́ба</i> (spasíba). Also compare the etymological development of English <i>goodbye</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>help!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savior (a person who rescues another from harm)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>съпаситель</i> (sŭpasitelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>спас-</i> (spas-) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>saving, salutary</li><li>bringing salvation</li></ol><p><i>спаси́тель</i> (spasítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Spassky (surname of several notable Russians and Soviets&#59; especially Boris Spassky, Soviet chess player and Igor Spassky, Russian scientist, engineer and entrepreneur)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Spas, Spassk, Spassky (any of various Russian settlements with these names)</li></ol><p><i>Спас</i> (Spas) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to rescue</li><li>to salvage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съпасти</i> (sŭpasti), which is a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>съпасти</i> (sŭpasti). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> + <i>&#42;pasti</i> (“to pasture, herd”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save oneself, to escape</li><li>to use as the thing that helps</li></ol><p><i>спасти́</i> (spastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abate, to fall (to decrease or become less in strength)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink (<i>of an elastic object or bodily organ</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>infinitive only</i>) diminutive of <i>спать</i> (spatʹ); to sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>спа́ть</i> (spátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ки</i> (-ki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to have sex, to sleep with</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpati</i>, distantly related to English <i>soporific</i>, <i>hypnosis</i> and <i>somnolent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) (for sleep) to be like</li></ol><p><i>спать</i> (spatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choir practice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caking, agglomeration (act of collecting in a mass)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of blood</i>) to coagulate, to curdle</li><li>to cake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>performance, play, matinee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>spectacle</i>, from Latin <i>spectaculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;mathematics,&#32;chemistry) spectrum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>spectre</i>, from Latin <i>spectrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spectral</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>spectral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectroscopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>financial</i>) to speculate, to profiteer, to gamble (<i>in</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to exploit, to take advantage of</li><li>to speculate (<i>on</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>spekulieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>speciō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speculator, profiteer, reseller (one who purchases goods or securities with the intention of reselling them)</li><li>scalper (one who sells things unofficially)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speculative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speculation</li><li>scalping, profiteering, price gouging</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>speculātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speleologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speleological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speleology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Speläologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spelling (usually about English spelling)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spelling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(cooking,&#32;dated) until ready</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ripe</li></ol><p><i>спеть</i> (spetʹ, “to ripen”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first, firstly</li><li>at first, originally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the front, at the front</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съ переди</i> (sŭ peredi), the latter the locative case of <i>передь</i> (peredĭ), which was displaced by modern перёд (perjód), from <i>передъ</i> (peredŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to filch, to pilfer, to swipe, to pinch, to lift</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) sperm, semen</li><li>spermatozoa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spermatozoon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spermaceti</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spermazet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uppity, haughty, arrogant, proud as a peacock (having excessive pride)</li></ol><p><i>спесь</i> (spesʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride, hubris, arrogance (act or habit of arrogating, or making undue claims in an overbearing manner)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ), <i>спех</i> (spex).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sung</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen</li><li>to sing</li><li>to crow</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>спѣти</i> (spěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;spěti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;spḗˀtei</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speh₁-</i>, whence English <i>spare</i>. Akin to Slovene <i>speti</i>, Slovak <i>dospieť</i>, Czech spět. Non-Slavic cognates include Ossetian <i>ӕфсад</i> (æfsad) and Latin <i>spes</i>.</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>петь</i> (petʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only)</li><li>to reach a harmony in singing (as in a choir singing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to form a partnership, to buddy up</li></ol><p><i>спе́ть</i> (spétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>in expressions only</i></li></ol><p>Verbal noun of <i>спешить</i> (spešitʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;spěxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) specialist, expert, pundit</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>специали́ст</i> (specialíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specialization (the process of specializing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special-purpose, specialized, special, dedicated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to specialize [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <i>or</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘in a subject’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>специализи́ровать</i> (specializírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specialist, expert</li><li>specialist degree (a five-year university or college degree similar to BSc or MS)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, Latin <i>speciālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist). Further from Latin <i>speciēs</i>, from <i>speciō</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>веду́н</i> (vedún), <i>све́дущий</i> (svéduščij), <i>ве́дьма</i> (védʹma) (akin to <i>ве́дать</i> (védatʹ), <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special education</li></ol><p>Literally, “special pedagogy”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>specially, expressly</li><li>intentionally, on purpose</li></ol><p><i>специа́льный</i> (speciálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specialty, trade, profession (that in which one specializes)</li></ol><p><i>специа́льный</i> (speciálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special, custom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specific character, peculiarity, specificity, specifics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specification (explicit set of requirements)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>specifically</li></ol><p><i>специфи́ческий</i> (specifíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>specific, particular</li></ol><p>From <i>специ́фика</i> (specífika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij) or <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specificity, peculiarity</li></ol><p><i>специфи́чный</i> (specifíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) specific, peculiar</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>spezif(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj) or from <i>специ́фика</i> (specífika) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spice, seasoning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) Spetsnaz</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>войска́ специа́льного назначе́ния</i> (vojská speciálʹnovo naznačénija, “special purpose forces/troops”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;law enforcement) Spetsnaz soldier, special forces soldier</li></ol><p><i>спецна́з</i> (specnáz) +&lrm; <i>-овец</i> (-ovec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>production-work clothing (jacket, overalls, smock)</li></ol><p><i>спец(иа́льный)</i> (spec(iálʹnyj)) +&lrm; <i>-о́вка</i> (-óvka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overalls</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льная оде́жда</i> (speciálʹnaja odéžda, “special clothing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) special military operation</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) the Russian invasion of Ukraine</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льная опера́ция</i> (speciálʹnaja operácija, “special operation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) special forces unit</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льное подразделе́ние</i> (speciálʹnoje podrazdelénije, “special unit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special representative</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льный представи́тель</i> (speciálʹnyj predstavítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special service, secret service</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льная слу́жба</i> (speciálʹnaja slúžba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spetskhran (the limited-access section of a Soviet-era library)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>отдел</i> (otdel, “department”) +&lrm; <i>специального</i> (specialʹnovo, “of special”) +&lrm; <i>хранения</i> (xranenija, “storage”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specialized school</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специализи́рованная шко́ла</i> (specializírovannaja škóla, “specialized school”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>special effect</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>специа́льный эффе́кт</i> (speciálʹnyj effékt, “special effect”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bake</li><li>to scorch <i>(said of the sun)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(blood)</i> to coagulate, curdle</li><li>to cake, sinter</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be exhausted, be burned out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, come a cropper</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>печь</i> (pečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismount</li></ol><p><i>спе́шиться</i> (spéšitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to hasten, to make haste, to be in a hurry</li><li>to be fast (<i>said of clocks, watches</i>)</li><li>to cause to dismount (<i>from a horse or bicycle, get out of a vehicle</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>спѣшити</i> (spěšiti), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;spěšiti</i> (“to hurry”), from <i>&#42;spěxъ</i> (“haste”), from <i>&#42;spěti</i> (“to advance, ripen, hurry”) + <i>&#42;-xъ</i>. Equivalently from <i>спех</i> (spex), from <i>спеть</i> (spetʹ).</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пе́ший</i> (péšij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismount</li></ol><p><i>спе́шить</i> (spéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hurry, haste</li></ol><p><i>спеши́ть</i> (spešítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hastily, apace</li></ol><p>From <i>спе́шный</i> (spéšnyj), from <i>спех</i> (spex), q.v.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hasty, hurried</li><li>urgent, pressing</li></ol><p><i>спех</i> (spex, “hurry”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any skipper butterfly of the genus <i>Spialia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Spialia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a drunkard, to ruin oneself by drinking [with drink]</li></ol><p><i>спи́ться</i> (spítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) speedometer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>speedometer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) speedrun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>speedrun</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gaming</i>) to speedrun</li></ol><p>From <i>спидра́н</i> (spidrán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lie, to bullshit</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пизде́ть</i> (pizdétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to steal</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пи́здить</i> (pízditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) speaker (presiding officer in a legislative assembly)</li><li>loudspeaker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>speaker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пили́ть</i> (pilítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fiction</i>) spin-off (fictional work where the protagonist was introduced in a preceding work or at least shares the same setting, often in a different aspect)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spin-off</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back, the rear of the torso</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the back of a piece of furniture etc.</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) spine, backbone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>spina</i>, from Latin <i>spīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>спина́</i> (spiná)</li><li>back (of a chair, couch or other furniture, of a dress or a shirt, of a tool, of an animal, or as a cut of meat); headboard; backrest</li></ol><p>From <i>спина́</i> (spiná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spinal, dorsal</li></ol><p><i>спина́</i> (spiná) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spinal cord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiral, spire helix</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) loop, Lippes loop</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>spīrālis</i> from Latin <i>spīra</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “wreath, coil, twist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) spiral galaxy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiral</li><li>helical</li></ol><p><i>спира́ль</i> (spirálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirea (<i>Spiraea</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Spiraea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiritualism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spiritualist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alcohol, spirits</li></ol><p>From English <i>spirit</i>, from Latin <i>spīritus</i> (&lt; <i>spīrō</i>). Can be calqued as **духло́. Doublet of <i>спрайт</i> (sprajt).</p><p>Compare natively formed <i>гори́лка</i> (gorílka) (akin to <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nominalized) alcoholic drinks, spirits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alcoholic, spirituous</li></ol><p><i>спирт</i> (spirt) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit lamp, alcohol burner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alcohol, spirit</li></ol><p><i>спирт</i> (spirt, “alcohol, spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alcohol, spirits</li></ol><p><i>спирт</i> (spirt) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>write-off</li></ol><p><i>списа́ть</i> (spisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to copy (writings)</li><li>to plagiarize (writings)</li><li>to deduct, to charge, to debit (a monetary sum from an account)</li><li>to write off, to decommission</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish communication or agree on something by exchanging writings</li></ol><p><i>списа́ть</i> (spisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>list, register, roll (register or roll of paper consisting of an enumeration or compilation of a set of possible items)</li><li>handwritten or handpainted copy (<i>especially of a manuscript or a religious icon</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plagiarising, cheating (in a test or exam)</li><li>writing off, write-off</li><li>decommissioning</li></ol><p><i>спи́сывать</i> (spísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije, verbal abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to copy (writings)</li><li>to plagiarize (writings)</li><li>to deduct, to charge, to debit (a monetary sum from an account)</li><li>to write off</li></ol><p><i>списа́ть</i> (spisátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave or resign (usually from a ship's crew)</li><li>to establish communication or agree on something by exchanging writings</li></ol><p><i>спи́сывать</i> (spísyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a drunkard, to ruin oneself by drinking [with drink]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove off (e.g. a boat from the shore)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to shove together, to cause to collide or clash</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to kick out; to cause to resign</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of (something bad)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get (something) over and done with</li></ol><p><i>спих(ну́ть)</i> (spix(nútʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to shove off (e.g. a boat from the shore)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;figurative) to shove together, to cause to collide or clash</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative) to kick out; to cause to resign</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to get rid of (something bad)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get (something) over and done with</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пихну́ть</i> (pixnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoke</li><li>knitting needle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stъpica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech (an oration, session of speaking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>speech</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) match (stick for producing a flame)</li></ol><p><i>спи́чка</i> (spíčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>match (a device to make fire)</li></ol><p><i>спи́ца</i> (spíca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speechwriter (someone who writes speeches for others, especially as a profession)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>speechwriter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alloy (metal composed of two or more elements, at least one of which is a metal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) alloy, fusion, synthesis</li><li>float, floating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raft, to go rafting, to paddle down (a river)</li></ol><p><i>спла́вить</i> (splávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raft, to go rafting, to paddle down (a river)</li></ol><p><i>сплавля́ть</i> (splavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plan, to project, to schedule</li><li>to glide</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>плани́ровать</i> (planírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raft, to join</li><li>to unite, to rally</li></ol><p><i>сплоти́ть</i> (splotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to close ranks</li></ol><p><i>спла́чивать</i> (spláčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave (to create by weaving)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to create (something complicated or confusing)</li><li>to weave together, to interweave, to plait, to braid, to interlace</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to connect, to interlock</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to invent, to compose</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to intertwine, to interconnect</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to connect, to merge, to blend</li></ol><p><i>сплести́</i> (splestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave (to create by weaving)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to create (something complicated or confusing)</li><li>to weave together, to interweave, to plait, to braid, to interlace</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to connect, to interlock</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to invent, to compose</li></ol><p><i>сплет-</i> (splet-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to intertwine, to interconnect</li><li>(figurative,&#32;intransitive) to connect, to merge, to blend</li></ol><p><i>сплета́ть</i> (spletátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interlacement, intertwinement</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) plexus</li></ol><p><i>сплет-</i> (splet-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gossip (person)</li></ol><p><i>спле́т(ня)</i> (splét(nja)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gossip, to tittle-tattle</li><li>to talk scandal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gossip, scuttlebutt (idle talk)</li><li>tale, whispering</li><li>guff, furphy</li></ol><p><i>сплет-</i> (splet-, “to invent, to compose”) (<i>colloquial</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Split</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raft, to join</li><li>to unite, to rally</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>плоти́ть</i> (plotítʹ), from <i>плот</i> (plot, “raft”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to close ranks</li></ol><p><i>сплоти́ть</i> (splotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniting, unity, rallying</li></ol><p><i>сплоти́ть</i> (splotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cohesion</li><li>unity, solidarity</li></ol><p><i>сплочённый</i> (spločónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>connected in bundles or rafts (<i>of logs or boards</i>)</li><li>joined closely together, cohesive</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) united, unanimous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid, compact</li><li>sheer, complete, nothing but</li><li>continuous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, entirely</li><li>throughout, all over</li><li>nothing but</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) all the time, very often, almost always</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out</li><li>to spit</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>плю́нуть</i> (pljúnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press flat, to flatten by means of pressure</li></ol><p><i>сплю́щить</i> (spljúščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press flat, to flatten by means of pressure</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>плю́щить</i> (pljúščitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance (a dance)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пляса́ть</i> (pljasátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out</li><li>to spit</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-плёвывать</i> (-pljóvyvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>associate, fellow fighter/champion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to vouchsafe, to grant or bestow [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘grace, mercy, an honor, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘the honor of doing something’</small>] (<i>of God</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic,&#32;usually&#32;impersonal) to be managed [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘by someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>manage</i> with the accusative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бие</i> (podóbije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to be worthy of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘an honor, recognition, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) to be able, to deign [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>сподо́бить</i> (spodóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to vouchsafe, to grant or bestow [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘grace, mercy, an honor, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘the honor of doing something’</small>] (<i>of God</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic,&#32;usually&#32;impersonal) to be managed [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘by someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>manage</i> with the accusative object as the subject)</li></ol><p><i>сподо́бить</i> (spodóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to be worthy of [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘an honor, recognition, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>ironic</i>) to be able, to deign [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>сподобля́ть</i> (spodobljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very early in the morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make drunk</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пои́ть</i> (poítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aeronautics</i>) spoiler (a device to reduce lift and increase drag)</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) spoiler (a device to reduce lift and increase downforce)</li><li>spoiler (a document, review or comment that discloses the ending or some key surprise or twist in a story)</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) A collapsed display element.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spoiler</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>spoliō</i>, from Latin <i>spolium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;childish) nighty night, night night</li></ol><p>From <i>споко́йной но́чи</i> (spokójnoj nóči, “goodnight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>споко́йный</i> (spokójnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>споко́йно</i> (spokójno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>calmly, quietly</li><li>easily; casually</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>quiet!</li></ol><p><i>споко́йный</i> (spokójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good night</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calm (unworried, free from anger)</li><li>placid, quiet, serene, tranquil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpokojьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>поко́й</i> (pokój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calmness (the state of being calm&#59; tranquillity&#59; silence)</li><li>composure (calmness of mind or matter, self-possession)</li></ol><p><i>споко́й(ный)</i> (spokój(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) long since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rinse <i>(out)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip down, to climb down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scramble down</li><li><i>(clothes, headgear, etc.)</i> to slip down</li><li>to slip <i>(into)</i>, to fall away <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>сползти́</i> (spolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>по́лзать</i> (pólzatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip down, to work down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scramble down</li><li><i>(clothes, hedgear etc)</i> to slip down</li><li>to slip <i>(into)</i>, to fall away <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) to act in a cunning way hiding one's true intentions</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>entirely, completely, in full (usually about cash settlements or in similar figurative senses)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>полна́</i> (polná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rinse <i>(out)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alarm</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;regional) turmoil, anxiety, commotion</li><li>aurora borealis</li><li>lightning, flash of lightning</li><li>flash of light or fire</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>поло́х</i> (polóx, “fear, fright”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spondee</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>σπονδεῖος</i> (spondeîos, “associated with a libation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) sponge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sponge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) diminutive of <i>спонж</i> (sponž); makeup sponge</li></ol><p><i>спонж</i> (sponž) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sponsor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sponsor (one that pays all or part of the cost of an event)</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) sugar daddy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sponsor, sponsorship; sponsor's</li></ol><p><i>спо́нсор</i> (spónsor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>spontaneously, ad hoc (on the spur of the moment)</li></ol><p><i>спонта́нный</i> (spontánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spontaneous</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>spontan</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Latin <i>spontāneus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispute, controversy, argument, quarrel</li><li>bet, wager</li><li>(<i>law</i>) argument</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъporъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) spore (reproductive particle)</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>spora</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σπορά</i> (sporá, “sowing, seed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute, to argue [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to discuss, to debate</li><li>to bet, to wager</li></ol><p><i>спор</i> (spor) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disputable, controversial, arguable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъporьnъ</i>, formed as <i>спор</i> (spor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove (something sewn) by tearing out the stitches</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blurt out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>поро́ть</i> (porótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sport (any athletic activity that uses physical skills)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sport</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) gymnasium, gym</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>спорти́вный зал</i> (sportívnyj zal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pertaining to sports and tourism</li></ol><p><i>спорти́вно-</i> (sportívno-) +&lrm; <i>тури́стский</i> (turístskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sporting, sports, athletic</li><li>sporty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tracksuit, sportswear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sports complex</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>спорти́вный ко́мплекс</i> (sportívnyj kómpleks, “sports complex”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sports ground</li></ol><p><i>спорт</i> (sport) +&lrm; <i>площа́дка</i> (ploščádka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athlete, sportsman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sportsman</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>спортист</i> (sportist), Polish <i>sportowiec</i>, Slovene <i>športnik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) debater, disputant</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) argumentative person</li></ol><p><i>спор</i> (spor) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>method, means, manner, mode, way</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from Old Czech <i>zpósob</i> (whence Czech <i>způsob</i>), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъposobъ</i> (itself from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ</i> + Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;posobiti</i>.) Cognate with Ukrainian <i>спо́сіб</i> (spósib), Belarusian <i>спо́саб</i> (spósab), Slovak <i>spôsob</i>, Polish <i>sposób</i>, Bulgarian <i>спо́соб</i> (spósob).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability, capability (the quality or state of being able)</li><li>faculty</li><li>capacity</li><li>talent (often plural)</li><li>power</li><li>quality</li></ol><p>From <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable (at), able, apt (at), gifted (at/in), talented (at) (having ability (in)) [<i>with</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘at a subject or skill’</small>]</li><li>capable (of), able to (do) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘do(ing) something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъposobьnъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>спосі́бний</i> (sposíbnyj), Serbo-Croatian <i>sposoban</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promote, to further, to favour/favor, to be conducive to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to assist [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble, to trip [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘over something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stumble <i>(over)</i>, to get stuck <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip, to make a mistake</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stumble, to trip [<i>with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + accusative) <small>‘over something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to stumble <i>(over)</i>, to get stuck <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip, to make a mistake</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>-тыка́ть</i> (-tykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), with meaning following the corresponding perfective <i>споткну́ться</i> (spotknútʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bethink, to think / remember / recollect suddenly</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>по-</i> (po-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bethink, to think / remember / recollect suddenly</li></ol><p><i>спохвати́ться</i> (spoxvatítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(nase,&#32;meteorology) An unexpected accumulation of snow, especially in narrow valleys.</li><li>(<i>spaced weeds</i>) An uncontrollable invasion of plants, notably those toxic for rodents.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the right, to the right</li><li>from the right</li></ol><p>Cognates</p><p>East Slavic cognates include Belarusian <i>спра́ва</i> (správa) and Ukrainian <i>спра́ва</i> (správa).</p><p>South Slavic cognates include Serbo-Croatian <i>sprȁva</i>/<i>спра̏ва</i> and Macedonian <i>справа</i> (sprava).</p><p>Western Slavic cognates include Polish <i>sprawa</i>, Kashubian <i>sprawa</i>, Silesian <i>sprawa</i>, Czech <i>správa</i>, and Slovak <i>správa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>справедли́вый</i> (spravedlívyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>справедли́во</i> (spravedlívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>justly, fairly (in a just or fair manner)</li></ol><p><i>справедли́вый</i> (spravedlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>justice, fairness</li><li>truth</li></ol><p><i>справедли́вый</i> (spravedlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>just, fair</li><li>true, correct</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вда</i> (právda) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to celebrate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy <i>(for)</i>, to acquire <i>(for)</i></li><li><i>in expression only</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пра́вить</i> (právitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cope (with), to deal (with), to handle, to manage, to overcome</li><li>to enquire/inquire (after, about), to ask (about), to look up, to consult</li><li>to reference, to compare, to check</li></ol><p><i>спра́вить</i> (správitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>certificate (confirming something), note, statement, reference</li><li>enquiry/inquiry, consulting, looking up (a seeking of information)</li><li>help, information, proof (information resulting from such an action)</li></ol><p><i>спра́вить(ся)</i> (správitʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to celebrate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to buy <i>(for)</i>, to acquire <i>(for)</i></li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>)</li></ol><p><i>спра́вить</i> (správitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cope (with), to deal (with), to handle, to manage, to overcome</li><li>to enquire/inquire (after, about), to ask (about), to look up, to consult</li><li>to reference, to compare, to check</li></ol><p><i>справля́ть</i> (spravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reference book (book providing factual information)</li><li>manual, handbook</li><li>telephone directory</li></ol><p><i>спра́вка</i> (správka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>information desk, inquiry office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) inquiry</li><li>(relational,&#32;engineering,&#32;computing) reference</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) benchmark</li></ol><p><i>спра́вка</i> (správka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computer graphics</i>) sprite (two-dimensional image or animation that is integrated into a larger scene)</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) sprite (large electrical discharge that occurs high above an active thunderstorm)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sprite</i>. Further via French from Latin <i>spīritus</i> (&lt; <i>spīrō</i>). Doublet of <i>спирт</i> (spirt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sprite</li></ol><p>From <i>спрайт</i> (sprajt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask (to ask a question of) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about something’</small> <i>or</i> indirect question]</li><li>to ask for, to request, to demand [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li><li>to call to account, to hold accountable, to make answer (for), to confront, to challenge, to question [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘about/for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>спроси́ть</i> (sprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spray, sprayer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>spray</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress into a dense mass</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>прессова́ть</i> (pressovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress into a dense mass</li></ol><p><i>спрессова́ть</i> (spressovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>English springer spaniel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>springer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprint (short race at top speed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sprint</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprinter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sprinter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>douche; syringe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, to pack off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away, to pack off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provoke</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>провоци́ровать</i> (provocírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to predict, to foretell, to prognosticate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>прогнози́ровать</i> (prognozírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to design, to project, to plan</li><li>to plan</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>проекти́ровать</i> (proektírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to project (onto)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>проеци́ровать</i> (proecírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) asking (the action of the verb to спрашивать (sprašivatʹ), спросить (sprositʹ))</li><li>demand (for something)</li><li>demand (for responsibility)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>спроси́ть</i> (sprosítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask (to ask a question of) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>о</b> (o, + prepositional) <small>‘about something’</small> <i>or</i> indirect question]</li><li>to ask for, to request, to demand [<i>with</i> <b>у</b> (u, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li><li>to call to account, to hold accountable, to make answer (for), to confront, to challenge, to question [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘about/for something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) semi-asleep, half-asleep, half-awake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) semi-asleep, half-asleep, half-awake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for no reason</li><li>innocently, without malice</li><li>straight to the point</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>проста́</i> (prostá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a large) octopus</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a network of something dangerous</li></ol><p>From Norwegian <i>(blekk)sprut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump off, to leap off</li></ol><p><i>спры́гнуть</i> (sprýgnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump off, to leap off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пры́гнуть</i> (prýgnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conjugate (to inflect (a verb) for each person)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpręgti</i>?</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) conjugation (act of conjugating a verb)</li></ol><p><i>спряга́ть</i> (sprjagátʹ, “to conjugate”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije, verbal abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin (to make yarn)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>прясть</i> (prjastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceal, to hide</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, to conceal oneself (intransitive)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) + <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ, “to hide”) + <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harness together (<i>a beast of burden</i>), to yoke together (<i>an ox</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) thx (abbreviation of <i>спаси́бо</i> (spasíbo))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten off/away, to scare off/away</li></ol><p><i>спугну́ть</i> (spugnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten off/away, to scare off/away</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пугну́ть</i> (pugnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) bushel, vessel (<i>for grain</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cache, vault</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) heaviness, weight</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) burden, oppression</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>спѫдъ</i> (spǫdŭ, “modius, bushel; vessel of this size”), Russian Church Slavonic <i>спꙋдъ</i> (spudŭ); compare Slovene <i>spọ̑d</i> (“bucket”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Old Polish <i>spąd</i> (“bucket”), from Proto-Slavic. More distantly cognate with (and per Trubachev, borrowed from) Middle Low German <i>span</i> (“wooden bucket, dry measure”) (genitive <i>spannes</i>); also cognate with Danish <i>spand</i> (“bucket”), Old Armenian <i>փունդ</i> (pʻund, “vessel, case”), Lithuanian <i>spañgis</i> (“bucket”) (genitive <i>spangė̃</i>; possibly a borrowing from Low German). Eventually from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spondʰos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lowering, hauling down, launch <i>(the ship)</i>, lowering, descent</li><li>slope, incline</li><li>sear, detent</li><li>(<i>polygraphy</i>) imposition</li><li>drainage, discharge</li><li>release</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let down, to lower, to sink (to allow to descend)</li><li>to launch, to lower (a boat)</li><li>to unchain, to unleash, to let loose</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ejaculate (sperm)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend, to go down</li><li>to go with the stream, to go down stream</li></ol><p><i>спуска́ть</i> (spuskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to come down to earth (to be brought back to reality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) release</li></ol><p><i>спуск</i> (spusk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let down, to lower, to sink (to allow to descend)</li><li>to launch, to lower (a boat)</li><li>to unchain, to unleash, to let loose</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ejaculate (sperm)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to descend, to go down</li><li>to go with the stream, to go down stream</li></ol><p><i>спусти́ть</i> (spustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to come down to earth (to be brought back to reality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after, later</li></ol><p>Alternative form of past adverbial perfective participle of <i>спустить</i> (spustitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carelessly, anyhow</li></ol><p>Literally, “with sleeves let down”. The formal clothes in Old Rus/Russia had very long sleeves. To do any work, one needed to tuck in long sleeves. Compare Russian <i>хала́тно</i> (xalátno), <i>хала́тный</i> (xalátnyj) related to <i>хала́т</i> (xalát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix up</li><li>to confuse</li><li>to tangle</li><li>to consider mistakenly, to take for someone else</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пу́тать</i> (pútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tangled</li><li>to be misled, to get confused, to get mixed up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to keep company <i>(with)</i>, to get mixed up <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>спу́тать</i> (spútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) a fellow traveller; companion</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) satellite, moon (a natural satellite of a planet)</li><li>an artificial satellite of a planet or moon, sputnik</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>пу́тник</i> (pútnik, “traveller”), from <i>путь</i> (putʹ, “way, journey”) + agent suffix <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) satellite</li></ol><p>From <i>спу́тник</i> (spútnik, “satellite”, stem) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, relational adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a drunk state</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) in a drunk state</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go balmy, to go off one's rocker</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>пя́тить</i> (pjátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormancy (state of being dormant)</li><li>sleepiness, lethargy</li><li>hibernation, aestivation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asleep, dormant</li><li>latent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeper (someone who sleeps)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stuffy, close (poorly-ventilated; close)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>compare, cf.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>term, date</li><li>Wednesday</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) neuter, neut., n.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>urgent, pressing</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>сравни́</i> (sravní).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>сро́ком</i> (srókom).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>сро́чный</i> (sróčnyj).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>среда́</i> (sredá).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>сре́дний род</i> (srédnij rod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning of operation, starting to function, going off, firing</li></ol><p><i>сраба́тывать</i> (srabátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, to function (<i>of a machine</i>)</li><li>to fire, to go off (<i>of a weapon, alarm, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed, to work (of an attempt)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to make, to produce</li></ol><p><i>срабо́тать</i> (srabótatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coordination (the state of working together)</li><li>wear (damage caused by use over time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, to function (<i>of a machine</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to succeed, to work (of an attempt)</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high style</i>) to make, to produce</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́тать</i> (rabótatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out</li><li>to reach harmony in working with someone, to build a good working relationship</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́таться</i> (rabótatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comparison</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) simile</li></ol><p><i>сравни́ть</i> (sravnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comparing, comparison (act of comparing or the state of being compared)</li></ol><p><i>сра́внивать</i> (srávnivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare</li><li>to level</li><li>to equal, to equalize</li></ol><p><i>сравни́ть</i> (sravnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>сровня́ть</i> (srovnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>сравня́ть</i> (sravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparable (able to be compared)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) comparative linguistics (a branch of historical linguistics that is concerned with comparing languages in order to establish their historical relatedness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) comparative degree, comparative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparatively (in a comparative manner)</li><li>relatively</li></ol><p><i>сравни́тельный</i> (sravnítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) comparative</li></ol><p><i>сравни́ть</i> (sravnítʹ, “to compare”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective suffix), a calque of Latin <i>comparātīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compare</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ра́вный</i> (rávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equal <i>(in)</i>, to match <i>(in)</i></li><li>to compare <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сравни́ть</i> (sravnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equal, to equalize</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>равня́ть</i> (ravnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to hit, to smite</li><li>to strike down, to defeat, to crush</li></ol><p><i>срази́ть</i> (srazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to battle</li><li>to contend, to play</li></ol><p><i>сража́ть</i> (sražátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to tilt at windmills</li></ol><p>From Cervantes's <i>Don Quixote</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) battle, engagement (general action, fight, or encounter; a combat)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъraženьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>срази́ть</i> (srazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сраже́ние</i> (sražénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to hit, to smite</li><li>to strike down, to defeat, to crush</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъraziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>рази́ть</i> (razítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, to battle</li><li>to contend, to play</li></ol><p><i>срази́ть</i> (srazítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once, simultaneously</li><li>at one stroke</li><li>at once, right away, straight away</li><li>straight off, out of hand</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>ра́зу</i> (rázu), in the partitive case rather than the genitive.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>на раз-два</i> (na raz-dva), <i>в два счёта</i> (v dva sčóta), English <i>at once</i>. Also compare <i>одни́м ма́хом</i> (odním máxom), English <i>at a stroke</i>; <i>с полпинка́</i> (s polpinká), <i>с полтычка́</i> (s poltyčká), <i>с полоборо́та</i> (s poloboróta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) a toilet</li></ol><p><i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to shit”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, “a doer”). Compare English <i>shitter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) restroom, shithouse</li></ol><p><i>срать</i> (sratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, disgrace</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>срамъ</i> (sramŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sormъ</i>. Doublet of <i>соро́м</i> (soróm), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shameless person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) to shame, to disgrace, to dishonor</li><li>(folk poetic,&#32;imperfective only) to scold</li></ol><p><i>срам</i> (sram) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shame</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sranan Tongo (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shitty (cheap, of poor quality)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking, goddamn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something bad, shit, crap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrete, to grow together, to knit (<i>of bones</i>), to inosculate (<i>of fibers, blood vessels</i>)</li><li>to merge, to fuse, to coalesce, to interpenetrate</li></ol><p><i>срасти́сь</i> (srastísʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrete, to grow together, to knit (<i>of bones</i>), to inosculate (<i>of fibers, blood vessels</i>)</li><li>to merge, to fuse, to coalesce, to interpenetrate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>расти́</i> (rastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit (to defecate)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;impersonal,&#32;usually in the infinitive even when translated with a finite verb) for (someone) to not give a shit [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘about someone/something’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>not give a shit</i> with the dative object as the subject)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrati</i>. Related to Russian <i>сор</i> (sor, “trash, rubbish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit oneself</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to debate or argue, usually about politics and usually in online forums</li></ol><p><i>срать</i> (sratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) shit, shitting (that which is shat)</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;colloquial) dirt, litter, jumble, clutter, mess</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;colloquial) controversy with strong language, quarrel, squabble, wrangle</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;Internet slang) flamewar (internet)</li></ol><p><i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to shit”) +&lrm; <i>-а́ч</i> (-áč)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catasarka (altar cloth)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) shirt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>срачица</i> (sračica). Related to <i>соро́чка</i> (soróčka). Doublet of <i>сорочи́ца</i> (soročíca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) diarrhea, shits</li></ol><p>From <i>срать</i> (sratʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to react, to respond [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘to something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>реаги́ровать</i> (reagírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) silvery, silver (color)</li></ol><p><i>сребро́</i> (srebró) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) to coat with silver, to silver-plate</li></ol><p><i>сребро́</i> (srebró) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) silver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сьребро</i> (sĭrebro), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrebro</i>. Doublet of <i>серебро́</i> (serebró, “silver”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) (<i>relational</i>) silver</li></ol><p><i>сребро́</i> (srebró) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wednesday</li><li>environment, surroundings, sphere</li><li>midst</li><li>medium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>срѣда</i> (srěda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serda</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>середа</i> (sereda, “middle; Wednesday”) (cf. <i>середа́</i> (seredá)), <i>третииникъ</i> (tretiinikŭ, “Wednesday”, literally “third (day)”).</p><p>Same as Etymology 1</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among, in the midst of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>inter- (among, between)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mediterranean</li></ol><p>From <i>Средизе́мное мо́ре</i> (Sredizémnoje móre, “Mediterranean Sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mediterranean</li><li>inland</li></ol><p><i>среди-</i> (sredi-) +&lrm; <i>зем(ля́)</i> (zem(ljá)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) middle</li></ol><p>Apparently, a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serdina</i>; a doublet of inherited <i>середи́на</i> (seredína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) middle, located in the middle</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) average, ordinary, commonplace</li></ol><p><i>среди́на</i> (sredína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>moderately, averagely</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Central Asian</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>азиа́тский</i> (aziátskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medieval</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the Middle Ages (such as implying outdated customs)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Средневековье)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Middle Ages (time period)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>average annual</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>годово́й</i> (godovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>average, mean, average value, mean value</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>average monthly</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сячный</i> (mésjačnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij); average, mediocre, middling, mid-size</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) averagely, mediocrely, middlingly</li></ol><p><i>сре́дненький</i> (srédnenʹkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medium-term</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>average, typical</li></ol><p><i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>стати́ст(ика)</i> (statíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle</li><li>medium</li><li>central</li><li>average</li><li>mean</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) neuter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>срѣдьнъ</i> (srědĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;serdьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>сере́дний</i> (serédnij), the inherited East Slavic form. By surface analysis, <i>среда́</i> (sredá) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle class (a social and economic class lying above the working class and below the upper class)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midbrain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle finger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) neuter gender</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) secondary school, high school</li></ol><p>Literally, “middle school”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mediastinum; a membranous cavity between two other organs; the space between two lungs</li><li>(figurative) The center or heart of something; lying between two things.</li></ol><p>From Latin mediastinum</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>focus, centre (point)</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Mittelpunkt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>livelihood, sustenance, support, bread and butter, living</li><li>subsistence, means of subsistence</li><li>maintenance, alimony (the means to support life)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass media (public communication that reaches a large audience)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means, facility</li><li>tool, instrument, equipment</li><li>remedy, drug</li><li>(business,&#32;finance,&#32;in the plural) assets; means; funding</li><li>product</li></ol><p><i>среда́</i> (sredá) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of production</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Produktionsmittel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>among, in the midst of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in broad daylight</li></ol><p>Literally, “amidst a white day”. <i>бе́ла</i> (béla) here is an old short masculine genitive singular.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cut <i>(in/off)</i></li><li>microscopic section</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) shear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to shear off, to slice off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>сре́зать</i> (srézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to shear off, to slice off</li></ol><p><i>сре́зать</i> (srézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to level, to even</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ровня́ть</i> (rovnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>akin (of the same kin&#59; related by blood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring closer together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>родни́ть</i> (rodnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become intimate <i>(with)</i>, feel at home <i>(among)</i></li><li>to get to like, become (emotionally) involved <i>(with)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>affinity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) never ever (followed by <i>не</i> (ne))</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) always</li></ol><p><i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>ро́ду</i> (ródu)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period, span</li><li>date, term, time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сърокъ</i> (sŭrokŭ), original meaning "agreement", from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъrokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;military) drafted conscript, soldier serving a fixed period</li></ol><p>From <i>срочная служба</i> (sročnaja služba) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>urgently (with a sense of urgency)</li></ol><p><i>сро́чный</i> (sróčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urgency</li><li>rush</li><li>limited time</li><li>time limit</li></ol><p><i>сро́чный</i> (sróčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>urgent, pressing</li><li>timed, at a fixed date, for a fixed period</li><li>periodic, routine</li></ol><p><i>срок</i> (srok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felling (<i>trees</i>)</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) the log or timber walls framing and enclosing a room or structure; a log shell; cribbing</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) a log cabin or other log structure</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>сруби́ть</i> (srubítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fell, to cut down</li><li>to build <i>(of logs)</i></li></ol><p><i>сруби́ть</i> (srubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fell, to cut down</li><li>to build <i>(of logs)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>руби́ть</i> (rubítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>derangement, frustration</li><li>failure</li><li>breakdown</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>срыва́ть</i> (sryvátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick, to pluck (<i>flowers or fruits</i>)</li><li>to tear off</li><li>to ruin, to destroy, to frustrate, to derange (e.g. a plan, work)</li><li>to wrench off (thread)</li><li>to vent (upon)</li><li>to level, to raze (to the ground), to demolish (a building)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ)</p><p><i>срыть</i> (srytʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break loose, to come unhinged, to come off</li><li>to fall down (from a cliff)</li><li>(of screw threads) to lose integrity, to become broken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fail, to end with a failure</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart off, to dash off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose one's temper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burp, to throw up, to regurgitate (often about babies or young animals)</li></ol><p><i>срыгну́ть</i> (srygnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>ивать</i> (ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burp, to throw up, to regurgitate (often about babies or young animals)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>рыгну́ть</i> (rygnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to level, to raze (to the ground), to demolish (a building)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) steadily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abrasion (medicine&#58; superficial wound)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) piss, urine</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) dick, penis</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>сьсака</i> (sĭsaka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьcaka</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>сця́ки</i> (scjáky).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pissed (covered in urine)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucking, goddamn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to piss, to urinate</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be afraid, to fear, to chicken out</li></ol><p>An assimilation of <i>сцать</i> (scatʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьcati</i> (“to piss”) &lt; <i>&#42;sьkati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sikʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;idiomatic) to piss in the wind (to engage in a fruitless task&#59; to oppose unassailable authority figures)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to piss against the wind”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarrel</li><li>disagreement</li><li>brawl, battle</li><li>controversy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъsora</i>; compare Old Church Slavonic <i>сваръ</i> (svarŭ, “quarrel”). Ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swer-</i>; further related to English <i>swear</i> via Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swarō</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embroil <i>(with)</i>, to cause to quarrel <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ссора</i> (ssora) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quarrel, to fall out <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ссо́рить</i> (ssóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend, to loan</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lend, to loan</li></ol><p><i>ссуди́ть</i> (ssudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunch (one's back)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>суту́лить</i> (sutúlitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunch over, to slouch, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>ссуту́лить</i> (ssutúlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) cowardly</li></ol><p><i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) pussy; chicken; chickenshit; coward</li></ol><p>From <i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) pisser; bedwetter</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) pussy; chicken; chickenshit; coward</li></ol><p>From <i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to banish, to exile</li><li>to make someone refer to</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-сыла́ть</i> (-sylátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to refer to, to allude to, to cite, to quote [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ссыла́ть</i> (ssylátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reference</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) link</li><li>(<i>history</i>) exile, banishment (the state of being banished from one's home or country)</li></ol><p>In the meaning "exile", from <i>ссыла́ть</i> (ssylátʹ, “to exile”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); in the meaning "reference", from <i>ссыла́ть(ся)</i> (ssylátʹ(sja), “to refer to”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in exile</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exile</li></ol><p><i>ссыла́ть</i> (ssylátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>shhh!, silence!</li><li>be still!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija, “station”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>статья́</i> (statʹjá, “article”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ста́рший</i> (stáršij, “senior, (<i>military</i>) first”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>старославя́нский</i> (staroslavjánskij): OCS, Old Church Slavonic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;WikiProjects) stub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stub</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stabilizer</li><li>fin (aircraft component)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stabilization</li></ol><p><i>стабилиза́ция</i> (stabilizácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stabilization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stabilize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>stabilisieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stabilize, to become stable</li></ol><p><i>стабилизи́ровать</i> (stabilizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stably, steadily, constantly</li></ol><p><i>стаби́льный</i> (stabílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stability</li></ol><p><i>стаби́льный</i> (stabílʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stable, steady, constant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shutter (protective panels over windows)</li></ol><p>From <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ) or <i>став</i> (stav) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, to place, to set, to stand, to station</li><li><i>(a compress)</i> to apply, to put on</li><li><i>(a play)</i> to put on stage, to stage, to produce, to present</li><li>(<i>gambling</i>) to wager, to place a bet, to stake</li><li><i>(a question)</i> to raise, to put</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ставити</i> (staviti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;staviti</i> (“to place, put”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to put the cart before the horse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bet, wager</li><li>rate, wage, salary</li><li>stake</li><li>(<i>military</i>) headquarters</li></ol><p><i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henchman</li><li>protégé</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) shutter (protective panels, usually wooden, placed over windows to block out the light)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stavropol (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Ста́врополь</i> (Stávropolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stagnation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stagflation</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>стагна́ция</i> (stagnácija) +&lrm; <i>инфля́ция</i> (infljácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) stadium (venue where sporting events are held)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>στᾰ́δῐον</i> (stắdĭon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage, phase, step</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd, flock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стадо</i> (stado), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stado</i> (“herd, flock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experience, seniority, service, length of service, longevity, standing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>stage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>training (especially on a job), (period or status of) probation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trainee</li><li>intern</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>stagiaire</i>, altered under the influence of <i>-ёр</i> (-jór) (itself borrowed from French).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ста́я</i> (stája): (small) flock, herd, pack, troop</li></ol><p><i>ста́я</i> (stája) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glass, cup, beaker, tumbler (a cylindrical drinking vessel without a stem or handle)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) beaker</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) cylindrical case, (hollow) cylinder</li><li>cup (a customary unit of measure used in cooking, equal to 200 ml)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>стака́нъ</i> (stakán), from <i>достака́нъ</i> (dostakán), from Old East Slavic <i>достоканъ</i> (dostokanŭ), borrowed from Turkic dialectal <i>dostaqan</i> (compare Chagatai (tostakan, “wooden bowl”), Kazakh <i>тостаған</i> (tostağan, “wooden cup”), Tatar <i>тустыган</i> (tustığan, “cup”), Bashkir <i>туҫтаҡ</i> (tuśtaq, “cup for drinking koumiss”)), borrowed from Persian دوستگان (dustgân), دوستکان (dustkân, “beloved; wine that is drunk with one's beloved; big drinking cup”), from دوستکام (dustkâm, “beloved”), from دوست (dôst) + کام (kâm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glass (drinking vessel)</li></ol><p><i>стака́н</i> (stakán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стака́н</i> (stakán): (small) drinking glass</li></ol><p><i>стака́н</i> (stakán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) staysail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stagsegel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stalagmite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stalactite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stalactite</li></ol><p><i>сталакти́т</i> (stalaktít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steelmaker</li></ol><p>From <i>сталь</i> (stalʹ, “steel”) + <i>-е-</i> (-e-) + a deverbal form of <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to weld”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stalingrad</li></ol><p><i>Сталингра́д</i> (Stalingrád) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stalinism</li></ol><p><i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stalinist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a Stalin-era building (a residential building built in the USSR during the rule of Joseph Stalin)</li></ol><p><i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stalin, Stalin's (of, relating to, or honoring Joseph Stalin)</li><li>Stalin-era, Stalinist (relating to or resembling the Soviet Union in 1929–1953)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stalino, Stalinsk</li></ol><p><i>Ста́лин</i> (Stálin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stalker (person who secretly follows someone else)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) stalker (a person who stalks game) (<i>see usage notes</i>)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) any person, whose occupation or activity is dangerously similar to those of characters from "Roadside Picnic".</li></ol><p>The word was coined by the Strugatsky brothers for their novel "Roadside Picnic" (1972), as an allusion to Rudyard Kipling's character <i>Stalky</i> from the "Stalky & Co." stories and the English <i>stalker</i>. Ста́лки (Stálki) was well remembered by the Strugatskys from their childhood, when they read the stories in Russian translation. In the "Roadside Picnic", <b>сталкер</b> was a common nickname for men engaged in the illegal trade of prospecting for and smuggling of alien artifacts from the mysterious and dangerous "Zone".</p><p>The modern meaning is a late semantic loan from English <i>stalker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) form of urban exploration of abandoned areas</li></ol><p><i>ста́лкер</i> (stálker) +&lrm; <i>-инг</i> (-ing)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) form of urban exploration of abandoned areas</li><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) stalking (form of harassment)</li></ol><p><i>ста́лкер</i> (stálker) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push (off or away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring together</li><li>to cause to collide</li></ol><p><i>столкну́ть</i> (stolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collide (with), to run (into), to bump (into), to come across (with) (с - with)</li><li>to face, to be confronted with</li></ol><p><i>ста́лкивать</i> (stálkivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) form of urban exploration of abandoned areas</li><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) stalking (form of harassment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stalking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, consequently, thus, hence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stahl</i> and/or Dutch <i>staal</i>, both “steel”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steel</li><li>(relational,&#32;figuratively) steel, iron</li></ol><p><i>сталь</i> (stalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(wood) chisel (tool consisting of a slim oblong block of metal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stemmeisen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure, stature, torso</li><li>camp, campground</li><li>side, camp</li><li>mill, machine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>станъ</i> (stanŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stanъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard (level of quality)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>standard</i>. Doublet of <i>штандарт</i> (štandart).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standardization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to standardise (to establish a standard)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>statistics</i>) standard deviation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standard</li></ol><p><i>станда́рт</i> (standárt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cossack village, stanitsa</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Old East Slavic <i>станъ</i> (stanŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cossack stanitsa dweller</li></ol><p>From <i>стани́ца</i> (staníca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Cossack village, stanitsa</li></ol><p><i>стани́ца</i> (staníca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) machine tool; machine, lathe, press</li><li>(art,&#32;relational) easel; painted at an easel (<i>as contrasted with e.g. mural works</i>)</li><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) heavy; requiring or equipped with a tripod, wheeled, or swivel mount (<i>of a machine gun or a portable artillery piece</i>)</li></ol><p><i>стано́к</i> (stanók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavy machine gun</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin (element)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand, to take a stand</li><li>to get, to become, to grow</li></ol><p>Nasal present of <i>стать</i> (statʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). See also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formation, establishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine, lathe, press</li><li>mount, mounting, bench</li><li>support, prop, easel</li><li>safety razor</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) ass, booty (buttocks, typically female)</li></ol><p><i>стан</i> (stan) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumpjack</li></ol><p><i>стано́к</i> (stanók) +&lrm; <i>кача́лка</i> (kačálka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance</li><li>to perform (a dance)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>танцева́ть</i> (tancevátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) station (<i>railway, broadcasting, space, etc.</i>)</li><li>(relational,&#32;telephony) office, exchange</li><li>(relational,&#32;rail transport) yard</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) power plant</li></ol><p><i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>station</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) office, exchange</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) yard</li><li>power plant</li></ol><p>A conflation of two sources — Russian archaic <i>стан</i> (stan, “postal station, stage”), <i>стани́ца</i> (staníca, “stanitsa”) and the European words for “station” descended from Latin <i>statio</i>, such as Polish <i>stacja</i>, French <i>station</i>, Italian <i>stazione</i>, etc. Both sources ultimately derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) building slip(s), stocks, launching slipway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stapel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fuse, to melt</li></ol><p><i>стопи́ть</i> (stopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear down (shoes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trample</li></ol><p><i>стопта́ть</i> (stoptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effort, endeavour (the amount of work involved in achieving something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently, industriously (in a diligent manner)</li></ol><p><i>стара́тельный</i> (starátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diligence, assiduity</li></ol><p><i>стара́тельный</i> (starátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assiduous, diligent, hard-working</li></ol><p><i>стара́ть(ся)</i> (starátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try, to attempt</li><li>to endeavour/endeavor, to make an effort</li><li>to pursue</li><li>to strive</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starati</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>стара́тися</i> (starátysja), Belarusian <i>стара́цца</i> (starácca), Bulgarian <i>стара́я се</i> (starája se), Serbo-Croatian <i>ста̏рати се</i> (“to try to achieve; to try”) (1sg. <i>ста̏ра̄м се</i>), Slovene <i>stȃrati se</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>starat se</i>, Slovak <i>starať sa</i>, Polish <i>starać się</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>starać so</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>staraś se</i>. Related to Russian <i>страда́</i> (stradá, “harvest”) (originally "hard work"), <i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ, “to suffer”). More distantly cognate with Lithuanian <i>stari̇̀nti</i> (“to pull with difficulty”) (1sg. <i>starinù</i>), Latvian <i>starîgs</i> (“zealous”), Old Prussian <i>stūrnawiskan</i>&nbsp;f (“seriousness, importance”, ins. sg.), Old English <i>stierne</i> (“serious, strict”), Latin <i>sternāx</i> (“absurd”), <i>strēnuus</i> (“active”) (also per Vasmer <i>cōnsternō</i> (“to shake”), inf. <i>cōnsternāre</i>, not <i>cōnsternere</i>, which is a different verb), Ancient Greek <i>στρηνής</i> (strēnḗs, “hard, sharp”), <i>στρῆνος</i> (strênos, “force”), Welsh <i>trîn</i> (“fight, work”) (from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;strēnā</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinster, old maid (unmarried woman)</li></ol><p>Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>бабадевојка</i>, <i>усиделица</i> (see <i>засиде́ться</i> (zasidétʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) old bag, crone (old woman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>старо́</i> (staró)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starějь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>older member of a community, community leader, elder</li><li>(<i>Mormonism</i>) elder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ageing/aging (process of becoming older)</li></ol><p><i>старе́ть</i> (starétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old, not young, elderly”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old, not new”)</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, to age, to advance in age</li><li>to become out of date</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) old man, whitebeard</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) monastic elder, starets</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>старьць</i> (starĭcĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ageing/aging, senescent (becoming elderly)</li><li>becoming old (<i>people or things</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old man</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) old-timer, that man who has worked or served somewhere more than his younger colleagues</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) father, foozle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) elderly husband for a wife</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;address) chap</li></ol><p>From <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Cognates include Czech <i>stařík</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;familiar) old man</li></ol><p><i>стари́к</i> (starík) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pejorative of <i>стари́к</i> (starík): geezer</li></ol><p><i>стари́к</i> (starík) +&lrm; <i>-а́шка</i> (-áška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old man's</li></ol><p><i>стари́к</i> (starík) +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стари́к</i> (starík).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olden time, olden days, antiquity</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) antiquity/ies, antique(s), relic(s) of the past</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;term of address) old man, old boy, old chap, old fellow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>старина́</i> (stariná)</li><li>(<i>used in an expression</i>)</li></ol><p><i>старина́</i> (stariná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antique, ancient, olden, age-old</li><li>old e.g. of relationships</li></ol><p><i>старина́</i> (stariná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make old, to make look old</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old, to age</li></ol><p><i>ста́рить</i> (stáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>former river-bed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стари́к</i> (starík) little old man</li></ol><p><i>стари́к</i> (starík) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Starka (a type of distilled alcoholic beverage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>old, in an old manner</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ancient, old</li></ol><p>From <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Old Believer</li><li>(figurative,&#32;ironic,&#32;sarcastic) traditionalist</li><li>(paganism,&#32;neologism) a follower of Russian neopaganism</li></ol><p>From <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вер</i> (-ver), combining form of <i>ве́ра</i> (véra, “faith”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old Believer</li></ol><p>From <i>старове́р</i> (starovér) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the past, what's old</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Старое)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of numerous settlements in Russia, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Staroye (a hamlet in Akatovskoye Rural Settlement, Gagarinsky Raion, Smolensk Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Staroye (a hamlet in Baskakovskoye Rural Settlement, Gagarinsky Raion, Smolensk Oblast, Central Federal District, Russia)</li><li>Staroye (a rural locality, the administrative centre of Staroselskoye Rural Settlement, Mezhdurechensky Raion, Vologda Oblast, Northwestern Federal District, Russia)</li></ol><li>Stare (a village in Voronkiv rural hromada, Boryspil Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1455)</li><li>Stare (a village in Zghurivka settlement hromada, Brovary Raion, Kyiv Oblast, Ukraine, founded in 1910)</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”). Compare Polish <i>Stare</i> and Ukrainian <i>Старе́</i> (Staré).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old-timer; old resident</li></ol><p>From <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-жил</i> (-žil), a combining form of <i>жить</i> (žitʹ, “to live”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outmoded, antique</li><li>old-fashioned, antiquated</li><li>frumpy, frumpish</li><li>fusty</li><li>stuffy</li><li>moldy</li><li>primitive</li><li>Victorian</li><li>fogyish</li></ol><p>From <i>ста̀ро-</i> (stàro-) +&lrm; <i>мо́дный</i> (módnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ancient Moscow</li></ol><p>From <i>ста̀ро-</i> (stàro-) +&lrm; <i>моско́вский</i> (moskóvskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Believer</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>обря́д</i> (obrjád, “ceremony”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old Believer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Staraya Russa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to the Russian Middle Ages</li><li>belonging to Russia or Russians for a long time, old Russian</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Middle Russian (related to early modern Russian)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Staraya Russa</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Church Slavonic, Old Slavonic</li></ol><p>From <i>ста̀ро-</i> (stàro-) +&lrm; <i>славя́нский</i> (slavjánskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bailiff</li><li>village elder</li><li>churchwarden</li><li>class monitor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starosta</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ста́рость</i> (stárostʹ, “old age”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a, suffix forming agent nouns).</p><p>Cognate with Czech and Polish <i>starosta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old age</li><li>age, years (<i>as the cause of dilapidation or decrepitude</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively) the old, the elderly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old France</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Old French (the Gallo-Romance dialect continuum spoken from the 9th century to the 14th century)</li></ol><p><i>ста̀ро-</i> (stàro-) +&lrm; <i>францу́зский</i> (francúzskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief mate</li></ol><p>Short for старший помощник</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>старый пердун</i> (staryj perdun, “old fart”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>start (beginning point of a distance race or a process)</li><li>launchpad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>start</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) startup (venture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>startup</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starter (electric motor that starts an internal-combustion engine)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) starter (person who gives the signal to start)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>starter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>space flight</i>) to take off, to launch</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to start</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) starting</li><li>launching</li><li>initial</li></ol><p><i>старт</i> (start) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starter (electric motor that starts an internal-combustion engine)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) starter (person who gives the signal to start)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>starter</i> via French <i>starter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;humorous) old lady (a close female friend or one's wife&#59; also used as a term of address)</li></ol><p><i>ста́рая</i> (stáraja) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anile (characteristic of a crone or a feeble old woman)</li></ol><p><i>стару́шка</i> (starúška) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) old age, senile</li><li>of monastic elders</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) high schooler</li></ol><p><i>ста́рший</i> (stáršij) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) high card</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ста́рший</i> (stáršij)</li></ol><p>Originally comparative degree of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>senior pupil, senior high school student</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elder, older, senior</li><li>eldest, oldest</li><li>higher, highest</li><li>(<i>military</i>) first (lieutenant)</li><li>senior (suffix used for names of elders)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreman</li><li>chief, man in charge</li></ol><p>Originally comparative degree of <i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj). Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starějь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) sergeant major</li><li>(dated,&#32;prerevolutionary&#32;Russia) foreman</li></ol><p><i>ста́рший</i> (stáršij) +&lrm; <i>-ина́</i> (-iná)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seniority</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) precedence (<i>of operators</i>)</li><li>(literary,&#32;military) position or rank of a sergeant major</li><li>(literary,&#32;prerevolutionary&#32;Russia) position or rank of a foreman</li></ol><p><i>ста́рший</i> (stáršij) +&lrm; <i>-инство́</i> (-instvó)</p><p><i>старшина́</i> (staršiná) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li><li>ancient, antique</li><li>olden</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>старъ</i> (starŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;starъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) old fart</li></ol><p>Literally, “old farting man”. May be a calque from English <i>old fart</i>. Often shortened as <i>старпёр</i> (starpjór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>старьёвщик</i> (starʹjóvščik)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruft, old junk (anything old or of inferior quality)</li></ol><p><i>ста́рый</i> (stáryj, “old”) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>junkman, ragpicker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag off, to drag down, to pull off, to pull down</li><li>to drag into, to pull into</li></ol><p><i>стаск-</i> (stask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gamer slang</i>) characteristics of player, mob or item (damage, speed etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>статья́</i> (statʹjá): small article (<i>e.g. in a newspaper or journal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>статья́</i> (statʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) statics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(theater,&#32;cinematography) extra</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) extra, do-nothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) statistics (mathematical science)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Statistik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) statistically (in a statistical manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>statistical</li></ol><p><i>стати́ст(ика)</i> (statíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>статисти́ческий</i> (statistíčeskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;computing) static</li></ol><p><i>ста́т(ика)</i> (stát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stately</li></ol><p><i>стать</i> (statʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) state councillor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>status</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>status</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>status quo (the state of things)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>status quo</i>. See English <i>status quo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statute, bylaw, charter, a body of legal provisions defined by a corporation for itself</li><li>the law applicable to a legal relationship in civil and commercial matters containing foreign elements, as determined by private international law</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>statūtum</i>, influenced by German <i>Statut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statuette, figurine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>statue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Statue of Zeus at Olympia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand (<i>in a certain location or near someone or something</i>)</li><li>to begin [<i>with</i> imperfective infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) to be willing, to be going [<i>with</i> imperfective infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (<i>used to express a determined negative attitude</i>)</li><li>to cost [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘some amount’</small>]</li><li>to become, to get, to grow [<i>with</i> instrumental noun <i>or</i> instrumental adjective <i>or</i> nominative adjective]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) for one to become [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> predicative <small>‘in a state’</small>]</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;in the negative) for one to continue living, for one to continue to be [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>build</li><li>trait, character, type</li><li>need</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стати</i> (stati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stati</i> (“to stand, become”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;statь</i>, related to Etymology 1 above. Cognate to Ukrainian <i>стать</i> (statʹ), English <i>stead</i>, Ancient Greek <i>στάσις</i> (stásis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become, to happen</li></ol><p><i>ста́ть</i> (státʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>article</li><li>item, entry</li><li>matter, business</li></ol><p><i>стать</i> (statʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) staphylinid, rove beetle</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Staphylinidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staphylococcus (bacterium of the genus <i>Staphylococcus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staffage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Staffage</i>. Analyzable as <i>стафф</i> (staff) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž), with a residual base component.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospital</li><li>permanent establishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stationary, fixed, permanent</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) hospital</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>statiōnārius</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). In the meaning "hospital (relational)", from <i>стациона́р</i> (stacionár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) strike, walkout</li></ol><p><i>ста́чка</i> (stáčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag off, to drag down, to pull off, to pull down</li><li>to drag into, to pull into</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal, to filch, to pinch, to swipe</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock, herd, pack, troop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стаꙗ</i> (staja, “cattleshed, shelter, tent”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;staja</i>. Cognate with <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ), <i>стать</i> (statʹ), <i>ста́до</i> (stádo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk, tree trunk</li><li>barrel (of a gun)</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) gun</li><li>a vertical part of a (coal) mine</li><li>shaft (of penis)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьvolъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;firearms) barrel</li><li>(relational,&#32;botany) trunk</li></ol><p><i>ствол</i> (stvol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) transit</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) range, alignment</li><li>створ плотины — dam location, dam site</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange (two objects) to be in a straight line (<i>as seen by the observer</i>)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to close the flap of (something)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-твори́ть</i> (-tvorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) leaf, fold, panel (One of the movably attached, opening and closing parts or halves of a structure, gate, window, etc. Each of the components of a portable folding mirror, screen, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>створи́ть</i> (stvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange (two objects) to be in a straight line (<i>as seen by the observer</i>)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to close the flap of (something)</li></ol><p><i>створи́ть</i> (stvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) stearin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stearin</i> or directly from French <i>stéarine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;intransitive) to make fun of someone (esp. using irony), to ridicule, to mock, to tease [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(slang,&#32;intransitive) to joke, to kid, to josh, to banter</li></ol><p>Uncertain; likely a cross between dialectal <i>стебну́ть</i> (stebnútʹ, perfective), <i>стёбывать</i> (stjóbyvatʹ, “to stitch; whip; eat up”, imperfective) and <i>издева́ться</i> (izdevátʹsja, “to jeer, bully”). The former may be an alteration of <i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ, “to whip; to stitch, to quilt”), or could be denominal from <i>сте́бель</i> (stébelʹ, “a stalk”), i.e. used as a whip.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stem, stalk (botany&#58; above-ground stalk of a vascular plant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьblь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steib</i>/<i>&#42;stib</i> (“stem, cane”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) stylommatophorous; relating to the Stylommatophora order of gastropods.</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Stylommatophora</i>, as <i>стебелёк</i> (stebeljók, “little stem, little stalk”) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix), literally “stalklet-eyed”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сте́бель</i> (stébelʹ): small stem, small stalk</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) peduncle (stem-like organ of an animal that connects tissue, organs, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>сте́бель</i> (stébelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) handle, grip</li><li>rod, pivot</li><li>stalk, trunk (part of the stalk left in the ground after cutting)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стьбло</i> (stĭblo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьblò</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stevia (a sweet herb and a sweetener)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from translingual <i>Stevia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) path</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьga</i> (“path”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steganography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whip, to lash, to flog</li><li>to quilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stegati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i>. Cognate with English stick and Greek στίγμα (stígma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) thigh</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stegno</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>стегно</i>/<i>stegno</i>, Slovak and Czech <i>stehno</i>, Slovene <i>stegno</i>. Machek compares Sanskrit <i>सक्थि</i> (sákthi, “thigh, thigh-bone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whip, to lash, to flog (<i>once</i>)</li></ol><p><i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stegosaurus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stitch</li><li>a stitching (an instance of stitching)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a short movement, a step</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a long, narrow trail left by something</li></ol><p><i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) path, way</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стьзꙗ</i> (stĭzja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьdza</i> (“path”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;stigaˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stigʰ-eh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>steak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steak</li></ol><p><i>стейк</i> (stejk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riding crop</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) stack (data structure)</li><li>(<i>scultping</i>) alternative form of <i>сте́ка</i> (stɛ́ka)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stick</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>stack</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>stecca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sculpting</i>) modeling tool (wooden, bone or metal tool for sculpting clay or similar soft material, with a wide, blade-like end)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>stecca</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runoff, draining</li></ol><p><i>стека́ть</i> (stekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow down, to streak down, to trickle down, to drain</li></ol><p><i>сте́к-</i> (sték-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow together</li><li>to gather, to throng</li></ol><p><i>сте́к-(ся)</i> (sték-(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glass</li><li>chimney (of kerosene lamp)</li><li>pane, window, lens</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) an extremely sad, heartbreaking story</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьklo</i>, from Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌹𐌺𐌻𐍃</i> (stikls), whence also <i>скля́нка</i> (skljánka, “phial; bottle”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i>, whence English <i>stick</i>. Cognates include Czech <i>sklo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glass wool</li></ol><p><i>стекло́</i> (stekló, “glass”) +&lrm; <i>ва́та</i> (váta, “cotton wool, wadding”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windshield wiper, windscreen wiper</li></ol><p>Compound: стекло (steklo, “window, also windshield”) + очистить (očistitʹ, “to clean”) + -тель (-telʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window crank, window lifter (a device, such as a handle or a button, used to raise or lower a car window)</li></ol><p>From <i>стекло́</i> (stekló, “pane, window”) +&lrm; <i>подъёмник</i> (podʺjómnik, “elevator”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glass harmonica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glass, glassy, vitreous (made of or resembling glass)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стьклѧнꙑи</i> (stĭklęnyi), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьklěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) glass</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) piece of glass, object made of glass</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) building made of glass, concrete, and steel</li></ol><p><i>стекло́</i> (stekló) +&lrm; <i>-я́шка</i> (-jáška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) glass (related to or for glass)</li></ol><p><i>стекло́</i> (stekló) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glazier</li></ol><p><i>стекло́</i> (stekló) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stele</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to lay, to make (<i>bed</i>)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) smooth talk</li></ol><p>See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьlati</i> (“to spread”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i> / <i>&#42;stl-</i>. Compare Polish <i>ścielić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spread, to spread out, to sprawl</li><li>to lie on the ground</li><li>to creep (<i>of a plant</i>)</li><li>to speed over a surface (<i>of animals and birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare for bed</li></ol><p><i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelves, rack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stellage</i>, from Dutch <i>stellage</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>стела́ж</i> (steláž), Belarusian <i>стэла́ж</i> (steláž), Polish <i>stelaż</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) stealth (technology for the production of military equipment to reduce its visibility in the radar)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) stealth aircraft, stealth fighter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stealth</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insole</li></ol><p><i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to get dark</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>темне́ть</i> (temnétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sthène (unit of measure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sthène</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stěna</i>, see also English <i>stone</i> and <i>stain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groaning, moaning</li><li>(figurative,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) complaint, moaning</li></ol><p><i>стена́ть</i> (stenátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to groan, to moan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stenàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall newspaper, handwritten or printed, published by a public institution or employees of an organization</li></ol><p>From <i>стена́</i> (stená) +&lrm; <i>газе́та</i> (gazéta), acronym of <i>стенна́я газе́та</i> (stennája gazéta, “wall newspaper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand, information shield</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) test bench</li><li>(<i>sports</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stand</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand-up (a performance of stand-up comedy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stand-up</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стена́</i> (stená, “wall”)</li><li>side (<i>of a container</i>)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) wall</li><li>(<i>furniture</i>) wall unit</li><li>(<i>soccer</i>) defending players who form a line to block the attacking team's free kick</li><li>(<i>soccer</i>) a one-two</li></ol><p><i>стена́</i> (stená) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wall</li></ol><p><i>стена́</i> (stená) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shorthand record/report, verbatim report, transcript</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stenographer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stenographic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>стеногра́фия</i> (stenográfija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stenography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angina pectoris</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>сте́нка</i> (sténka, “side (<i>of a container</i>), wall (<i>anatomy</i>), wall (<i>of defenders, soldiers, etc.</i>)”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>сте́нка</i> (sténka, “small wall”)</li></ol><p><i>сте́нка</i> (sténka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tap dance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reasonably, seriously, solidly</li><li>in a staid, sedate manner</li></ol><p><i>степе́нный</i> (stepénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staidness, sedateness</li></ol><p><i>степе́нный</i> (stepénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reasonable, solid, serious</li><li>staid, sedate, grave</li></ol><p>From <i>сте́пень</i> (stépenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>degree, extent</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) power</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) degree of comparison</li><li>rank, title, degree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stepy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stapler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stapler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caracal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) steppe</li></ol><p><i>степь</i> (stepʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steppe, veldt (the grasslands of Eastern Europe and Asia)</li></ol><p>Unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>offensive</i>) bitch, vixen, old witch (a malicious, quarrelsome or temperamental woman)</li><li>(dated,&#32;offensive) bastard, son of a bitch, asshole (a malicious, quarrelsome or temperamental person)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Old Russian, now obsolete <i>стерво</i> (stervo, “carrion”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьrvь</i>.</p><p>A similar word is Modern Russian <i>стервя́тник</i> (stervjátnik, “vulture, carrion-eating bird”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) scoundrel, villain</li></ol><p><i>сте́рва</i> (stérva, “a contemptible woman, a scoundrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>стерво</i> (stervo, “carrion”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьrvь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stereo recording, stereo technology</li><li>stereo music</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stereo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stereotype (a conventional, formulaic, and oversimplified conception, opinion, or image)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe, to erase, to efface</li><li>to rub raw</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be obliterated/effaced, to rub away, to become obliterated</li></ol><p><i>стере́ть</i> (sterétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to watch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sterťi</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;stergti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shank, rod, pivot, spindle (straight round stick, shaft, or bar)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьžьnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shank, rod, pivot</li></ol><p><i>сте́ржень</i> (stérženʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sterilization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sterilize</li><li>to spay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sterile</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>stérile</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) sterling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sterlet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Störling</i>, a diminutive form of <i>Stör</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sterlet</li></ol><p><i>сте́рлядь</i> (stérljadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stubble (short stalks left in a field after harvest)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьrnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stubble (short stalks left in a field after harvest)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьrnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, to endure</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siberian crane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стьркъ</i> (stĭrkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьrkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>constraint, restraint</li><li>uneasiness, shyness</li><li>(feeling of) shame or shyness, standing on ceremony</li><li>difficulty in breathing</li></ol><p><i>стесни́ть</i> (stesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shy, coy (reserved)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) constrained, pinched, cramped</li></ol><p><i>стесни́ть</i> (stesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/lay restraint <i>(on)</i>, to hinder, to hamper</li><li>to embarrass</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тесни́ть</i> (tesnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd together</li><li>to be sparing [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to clench (<i>of the heart or soul; from emotion</i>)</li></ol><p><i>стесни́ть</i> (stesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/lay restraint <i>(on)</i>, to hinder, to hamper</li><li>to embarrass</li></ol><p><i>стесни́ть</i> (stesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be uncomfortable</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to feel embarrassed</li><li>to be sparing [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>to crowd together</li><li>to be afraid, to be ashamed to [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to be afraid of, to be ashamed about [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>стесня́ть</i> (stesnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cramped, close-together</li><li>constrained, straitened, pinched, difficult (<i>of financial circumstances</i>)</li><li>difficult, labored (<i>of breathing, movements, etc.</i>)</li><li>heavy, oppressed, burdened (<i>due to emotions</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) stethoscope (medical instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) stoichiometry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confluence (convergence or combination)</li></ol><p><i>сте́к-</i> (sték-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow down, to streak down, to trickle down, to drain</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow together</li><li>to gather, to throng</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antimony (chemical element)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>stibium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to steal</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ти́брить</i> (tíbritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) STEVE, an atmospheric phenomenon similar to an aurora</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Steve</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vape cartridge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stick</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) style; stylistic</li></ol><p><i>стиль</i> (stilʹ, “style”) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stylized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to style (to give (something) the characteristics of a particular style)</li><li>to stylize</li></ol><p>French <i>styliser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylistics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stylistic</li></ol><p><i>стили́ст(ика)</i> (stilíst(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylus (tool for writing on clay tablets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylus (tool for writing on clay tablets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stylus (tool for writing on clay tablets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>style</li><li>fashion</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) build, order</li><li>calendar, style</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stylish</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) period</li></ol><p><i>стиль</i> (stilʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) dandy, fop, fashionista; member of the stilyagi youth subculture of the 1940s–1960s</li></ol><p><i>стиль</i> (stilʹ, “style, fashion”) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stimulus, incentive</li><li>drive; impetus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>stimulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incitement, inspiration, motivation, provision of incentives, stimulation</li></ol><p><i>стимули́ровать</i> (stimulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stimulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stimulant</li><li>pacemaker</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>stimulātor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stimulation (various senses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bursary, scholarship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>stīpendium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) washing machine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eraser, rubber</li></ol><p><i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ, “to erase, to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing machine (machine that washes clothes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) washing</li></ol><p><i>стир-</i> (stir-) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing powder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attrition, abrasion (wearing by friction)</li><li>deletion (e.g. of a recording), erasure</li></ol><p><i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) erasing</li></ol><p><i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to erase something from a surface</li><li>to clean, to wash cloth or clothes</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ)</p><p>Probably ultimately the same as etymology 1, but reinterpreted as an unprefixed verb.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be obliterated/effaced, to rub away, to become obliterated</li><li>to wash</li></ol><p><i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laundry (laundering&#59; washing)</li></ol><p><i>стира́ть</i> (stirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>стиск-</i> (stisk-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ти́снуть</i> (tísnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line of poetry</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) verse</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) poem, poems</li><li>work of ancient folk poetry on a religious theme (usually with the adjective Духо́вный (Duxóvnyj, “Spiritual”)).</li><li>mood</li></ol><p>From the Old East Slavic <i>стихъ</i> (stixŭ), from the Old Church Slavonic <i>стихъ</i> (stixŭ), from the Ancient Greek <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos, “row, line, verse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) sticharion (liturgical vestment of Eastern Christianity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>στιχάριον</i> (stikhárion), from <i>στῐ́χος</i> (stĭ́khos). Compare Bulgarian <i>стиха́р</i> (stihár), Macedonian <i>стихар</i> (stihar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet down, to abate, to die down (<i>of a storm, war, fighting, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сти́хнуть</i> (stíxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>spontaneously</li></ol><p><i>стихи́йный</i> (stixíjnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural disaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spontaneous</li><li>elemental</li><li>natural</li></ol><p><i>стихи́я</i> (stixíja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>element (one of the four classical elements of Ancient Greek philosophy)</li><li>elemental force of nature, and their spheres of influence, elements</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) force of societal life that does not succumb to control by people or society</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) element (a place or state of being that an individual or object is best suited to&#59; one's familiar, comfortable, or usual surroundings)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стихия</i> (stixija), from Old Church Slavonic <i>стѵхии</i> (stüxii) (feminine form <i>стѵхиꙗ</i> (stüxija)), from Ancient Greek <i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon). Compare Ukrainian <i>стихі́я</i> (styxíja), Belarusian <i>стыхі́я</i> (styxíja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet down, to abate, to die down (<i>of a storm, war, fighting, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ти́хнуть</i> (tíxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetaster</li></ol><p><i>стих</i> (stix) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetastress (female poetaster)</li></ol><p><i>стихоплёт</i> (stixopljót) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem (literary piece written in verse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>high style</i>) poet</li><li>versemaker</li></ol><p><i>стихотво́р(ный)</i> (stixotvór(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) verse</li><li>written/expressed in verse</li><li>poetic</li></ol><p><i>стих</i> (stix) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твор(и́ть)</i> (tvor(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стих</i> (stix).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стих</i> (stix): (short) rhyme, poem</li></ol><p><i>стих</i> (stix) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread, to lay, to make (<i>bed</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьlati</i> (“to spread”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i> / <i>&#42;stl-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to spread, to spread out, to sprawl</li><li>to lie on the ground</li><li>to creep (<i>of a plant</i>)</li><li>to speed over a surface (<i>of animals and birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to prepare for bed</li></ol><p><i>стла́ть</i> (stlátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred (100)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred thousand (100,000)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haystack, stack, haycock, hayrick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stogъ</i>, related to English <i>thatch</i> and <i>deck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) hundred-headed</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative,&#32;of a text) many chaptered</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative,&#32;of architecture) many domed</li></ol><p>From <i>сто</i> (sto, “hundred”) +&lrm; Old Church Slavonic <i>глава́</i> (glavá, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, “-ed”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoic (all senses)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Στωϊκός</i> (Stōïkós), from <i>Ποικίλη Στοά</i> (Poikílē Stoá, “painted portico”), the portico in Athens where Zeno was teaching.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cost</li></ol><p><i>сто́имость</i> (stóimostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cost, value, worth</li></ol><p><i>сто́ить</i> (stóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cost</li><li>to be worth</li><li>to be worthy of, to deserve</li><li>(idiomatic,&#32;impersonal) <i>сто́ит то́лько</i> (stóit tólʹko) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>it is sufficient (for some purpose), all you have to do is, one need only</li><li>once (one thing happens another thing certainly happens)</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stojiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoicism (all senses)</li></ol><p><i>стоиц-</i> (stoic-) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>stoically (all senses)</li></ol><p><i>сто́ик</i> (stóik) +&lrm; <i>-ески</i> (-eski); see also <i>-и́чески</i> (-íčeski).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stoic (all senses)</li></ol><p><i>сто́ик</i> (stóik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij); see also <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand (a device to hold something upright or aloft)</li><li>support</li><li>counter</li><li>stance</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stojati</i> (“to stand”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>robust, sturdy, hardy, durable</li><li>stubborn, obstinate, resistant</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) stable</li></ol><p><i>сто́йка</i> (stójka) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steadfastly, stubbornly, persistently</li><li>stoically</li></ol><p><i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmness, steadfastness, staunchness, stableness</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) stability</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) resistance, durability</li></ol><p><i>сто́йкий</i> (stójkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A horse stall or stable (box)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flow, flowing, drainage</li><li>drain, gutter, sewer</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>стечь</i> (stečʹ, “to flow down, to drain”) (compare stem <i>стек-</i> (stek-)), with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) many times, oftentimes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) board, fare, cuisine</li><li>department, section, office, bureau</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) throne (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>aerospace</i>) pad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>столъ</i> (stolŭ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stolъ</i>. Cognate with English <i>stool</i>. Doublet of <i>стул</i> (stul).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>post, pole, pillar, column</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stъlbъ</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>стлъба</i> (stlŭba, “summit”), Lithuanian <i>stul̃bas</i>, Latvian <i>stulbs</i>, Old Norse <i>stolpi</i> (“pillar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be dumbfounded, to be petrified, to freeze (from shock)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>column</li></ol><p><i>столб</i> (stolb) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>столб</i> (stolb): (small) column</li></ol><p><i>столб</i> (stolb) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tetanus</li></ol><p>Fom <i>столбенеть</i> (stolbenetʹ, “to freeze, to lose control of movement”) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;also&#32;idiomatic) high-road</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) post, pillar, column</li></ol><p><i>столб</i> (stolb) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>century, hundred-year period</li><li>centenary, hundredth anniversary, hundredth birthday</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>stoljeće</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hundred-year</li><li>hundred-year-old</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сто́лько</i> (stólʹko)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сто́лько</i> (stólʹko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>furniture</i>) tabletop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стол</i> (stol): little table</li><li>ellipsis of <i>журна́льный сто́лик</i> (žurnálʹnyj stólik); coffee table</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a small table for visitors to a public catering establishment or for service purposes</li></ol><p><i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capital (city designated as seat of government)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stolica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) capital (<i>city</i>)</li><li>metropolitan, city (characteristic of urban life in the capital, especially when contrasted with small towns and rural areas)</li></ol><p><i>столи́ца</i> (stolíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collision</li><li>clash</li></ol><p>From <i>столкну́ться</i> (stolknútʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push (off or away)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bring together</li><li>to cause to collide</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collide (with), to run (into), to bump (into), to come across (with) (с - with)</li><li>to face, to be confronted with</li></ol><p><i>столкну́ть</i> (stolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dining room</li><li>canteen, cafeteria, restaurant</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>столо́вая ко́мната</i> (stolóvaja kómnata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) eatery, dining hall</li></ol><p><i>столо́вый</i> (stolóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) table</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) dinner</li></ol><p><i>стол</i> (stol) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) section chief, department head (a mid-level bureaucrat position in Tsarist Russia, usually held by an official of the 7th rank, i.e. court councillor)</li></ol><p><i>стол</i> (stol, “department, section, office, bureau”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нача́льник</i> (načálʹnik, “chief, head”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, to crush, to grind (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) archaic form of <i>столб</i> (stolb)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) column</li><li>(literary,&#32;figuratively) pillar, prominent supporter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stъlpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, to throng, to cluster</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>толпи́ться</i> (tolpítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pandemonium, chaos</li><li>babel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стлъпотвореніє</i> (stlŭpotvorenije, “tower building”) (in the context of Tower of Babel), from Old Church Slavonic <i>стлъпотворѥниѥ</i> (stlŭpotvorjenije), calque of Ancient Greek <i>πυργοποιΐα</i> (purgopoiḯa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стол</i> (stol).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so, such</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>equally</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>this much, this long</li><li>so much</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this/that much/many</li><li>so much, so many</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>that/this much, that/this many, so and so much/many</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Stolnik (a Russian court rank from the 13th to the 17th century)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a hundred roubles (or any currency); a hundred</li></ol><p><i>сто́ль</i> (stólʹ, “table”) (<i>Pre-1918 reform orthography</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, “masculine noun suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) sovereign, regnant (<i>holding supreme power in some land; not subservient to other monarchs</i>)</li><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic,&#32;attributive) capital (<i>city</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>стол</i> (stol, “throne”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) capital city</li></ol><p><i>сто́льный</i> (stólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>град</i> (grad)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabinetmaker, carpenter, joiner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>stolarz</i>, itself a calque of German <i>Tischler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joinery, carpenter's work</li></ol><p><i>столя́р</i> (stoljár) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joiner's</li></ol><p><i>столя́р</i> (stoljár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dentist, stomatologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moan, groan (a low cry of pain)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стонати</i> (stonati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stonъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to groan, to moan</li><li>to suffer, to languish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стонати</i> (stonati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stonati</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>стон</i> (ston), Czech <i>stonat</i>. Non-Slavic cognates include Ancient Greek <i>στόνος</i> (stónos, “a sighing or groaning”), Latin <i>tonō</i> (“to thunder, to resound”) and Sanskrit <i>स्तनति</i> (stanati, “to roar, to cry”). Related to <i>стена́ть</i> (stenátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>groaning, moaning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>stop!</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) that'll do, enough</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency brake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) foot</li><li>footstep</li><li>an old unit of length equal to 28.8 cm (whereas an English foot is 30.48 cm)</li><li>foot, metrical foot, tonic foot (of a verse)</li><li>ream (of paper)</li><li>pile, heap, stack (of flat objects)</li><li>goblet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stopa</i>. Related to <i>ступе́нь</i> (stupénʹ, “step, degree”) and <i>сте́пень</i> (stépenʹ, “degree”).</p><p>See Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) diminutive of <i>сто́пка</i> (stópka); shot glass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fuse, to melt</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pile, heap</li><li>ream, rouleau</li><li>cup, small drinking glass</li><li>shot glass</li></ol><p><i>стопа́</i> (stopá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) stopper (device for holding parts in place)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) stopper (metal clamp for stopping running rigging)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stopper</i>. First attested in Russian in the form "стоперс" in 1720.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to stop, to bring to a halt/standstill (to make something stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to stop the reverse movement of wheels or propellers</li></ol><p><i>сто́пор</i> (stópor) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stall, to falter, to come to a halt, to face a standstill (to stop moving, working, progressing or increasing)</li></ol><p><i>сто́порить</i> (stóporitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>сто́пка</i> (stópka, “pile, small drinking glass”)</li></ol><p><i>сто́пка</i> (stópka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundred-per-cent</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>проце́нт</i> (procént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear down (shoes)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trample</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) curtain, shade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) stories (format of multimedia content in the form of short videos or photos with various effects, shown like a slideshow)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) story (separate element of such content)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) stories (multiple elements of such content)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stories</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>(antiquated)</i> hundredfold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hundredfold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watch, watchman, guard, custodian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сторожь</i> (storožĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storžь</i> (“guard”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;storʔg-</i>. Doublet of <i>страж</i> (straž), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) patrol, watch (<i>often involving the idea of sentry</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сто́рож</i> (stórož) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard, to watch, to watch over</li></ol><p><i>сто́рож</i> (stórož) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gatehouse</li></ol><p><i>сторожи́ть</i> (storožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side, hand, party</li><li>direction, quarter</li><li>place, region, country</li><li>distance</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storna</i>. Doublet of <i>страна́</i> (straná), a borrowing from old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point of the compass</li></ol><p><i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>све́та</i> (svéta)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to avoid, to shun, to eschew</li><li>to step aside, to make way</li></ol><p><i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сторона́</i> (storoná)</li></ol><p><i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) foreign, alien, outside</li><li>(<i>law</i>) third-party</li></ol><p><i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supporter, champion, advocate, proponent, adherent</li></ol><p>From <i>сторона́</i> (storoná, “faction, side”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, relational noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hundred-rouble note</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundred-handed</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) many-handed</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geometry,&#32;rare) decakismyriagon (100,000-sided polygon)</li></ol><p><i>сто ты́сяч</i> (sto týsjač) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hectogon</li></ol><p><i>сто</i> (sto) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stochastic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drainage</li><li>having drainage</li></ol><p><i>сток</i> (stok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>standing, upright (in or into an upright position)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertical support, post, stanchion</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) boner, hard-on or hardon, stiffy, woody <i>or</i> woodie (<i>erect penis</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing</li></ol><p><i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ние</i> (-jánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stop, stand, station</li><li>(fixed) quarters</li><li>anchorage, moorage</li><li>car park, parking, parking lot (for cars)</li><li>(<i>archaeology</i>) site, camp (of primitive people), stopover (of nomads)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) parking</li></ol><p><i>стоя́нка</i> (stojánka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parking brake</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li><li>to be situated (usually about a towering or potentially towering object)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be in a certain place within a formal structure</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to firmly follow or adhere [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + prepositional) <small>‘to something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be idle, to be in a state of malfunction or downtime</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to withstand, to survive, to not succumb</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (<i>figuratively</i>) to be in line, to be in a queue</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be frozen</li><li>(<i>imperative</i>) expresses a demand to stop or stop moving</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be erect</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стоꙗти</i> (stojati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stojati</i> (“to stand”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to have one foot in the grave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing, stand-up</li><li>stagnant</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) erect, up, with a hard-on</li></ol><p><i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing</li><li>worth doing, worthwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>строе́ние</i> (strojénije, “building”)</li><li>abbreviation of <i>строка́</i> (stroká, “line, string, row”)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) abbreviation of <i>страни́ца</i> (straníca, “page”), see <i>с.</i> (s.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to egg on, to pit against, to make the feathers fly</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) to remove by chemical means</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to pay out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move out, to shift</li></ol><p><i>строг-</i> (strog-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(out)</i></li></ol><p><i>стра́гивать</i> (strágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(lexicography,&#32;grammar) clipping of <i>страдательный</i> (stradatelʹnyj, “passive, passive voice”)</li></ol><p>From <i>страдательный</i> (stradatelʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest (process of harvesting, gathering the ripened crop)</li><li>harvest time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strada</i>. Compare <i>&#42;stradati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufferer</li></ol><p><i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of suffering</li></ol><p><i>страда́лец</i> (stradálec) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) suffering</li></ol><p><i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) passive present participle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) passive past participle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) passive</li></ol><p><i>страда́ть</i> (stradátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passive voice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer</li><li>to be subject to</li><li>to be poor <i>of quality</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stradati</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>страдати</i> (stradati), Serbo-Croatian <i>страдати</i>, Bulgarian <i>страдам</i> (stradam), Old Polish <i>stradać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering</li><li>afflicted</li><li>anguished (experiencing extreme discomfort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian</li><li>sentinel (a sentry or guard)</li><li>custodian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>стражь</i> (stražĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storžь</i> (“guard”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;storʔg-</i>. Doublet of <i>сто́рож</i> (stórož).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard, watch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>стража</i> (straža), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storža</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storgja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) police constable</li></ol><p><i>стра́жа</i> (stráža) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhinestone, paste, diamante</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strass</i>. Pronunciation change due to homophony with <i>страсть</i> (strastʹ), or in analogy to <i>алма́з</i> (almáz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rhinestone, paste, diamante</li></ol><p><i>стра́з</i> (stráz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) strike</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>strike</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airsoft</li></ol><p>Pseudo-anglicism, derived from <i>strike</i> +&lrm; <i>ball</i>, likely modeled on <i>пейнтбо́л</i> (pejntból, “paintball”); coined by early Russian airsoft players circa 1997 and quickly adopted because Cyrillic transliterations of English <i>airsoft</i> (<i>эйрсо́фт</i> (ejrsóft) and <i>аирсо́фт</i> (airsóft)) are awkward for Russian speakers to pronounce.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an airsoft player</li></ol><p>From <i>страйкбо́л</i> (strajkból) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) airsoft</li></ol><p>From <i>страйкбо́л</i> (strajkból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>country</li><li>region</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>страна</i> (strana), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>страна</i> (strana), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storna</i>. Compare the regular East-Slavic reflex with pleophony <i>сторона́</i> (storoná).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>country</i> (<&lt; Latin <i>contrā</i>). Also compare <i>окра́ина</i> (okráina), <i>край</i> (kraj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Russia</i>) Ukraine</li><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) Russia</li></ol><p>From Russian страна́ (straná, “country”) and оши́бка 404 (ošíbka 404, “error 404, an online error”) (страна́ четы́реста четы́ре), suggesting claims about Ukraine as an illegitimate state. Later repurposed by Ukrainian netizens.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compass point, point of the compass, cardinal point</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participating country (in an international level conference or agreement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>page</li><li>column</li><li>webpage</li></ol><p><i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>страни́ца</i> (straníca): page</li></ol><p><i>страни́ца</i> (straníca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer, traveller; vagrant, vagabond, rover, roamer, pilgrim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>страньникъ</i> (stranĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stornьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Compare Old East Slavic <i>стороньникъ</i> (storonĭnikŭ). Formally, a doublet of <i>сторо́нник</i> (storónnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strangely, strange, funnily, curiously, oddly, peculiarly</li></ol><p><i>стра́нный</i> (stránnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strangeness</li><li>singularity, oddity, eccentricity, crank</li></ol><p><i>стра́нный</i> (stránnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strange, odd, weird</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>страньнъ</i> (stranĭnŭ, “fellow countryman, compatriot, strange”). Derived from Old Church Slavonic <i>страна</i> (strana, “country”) (whence Russian <i>страна́</i> (straná)). Sense development "country" → "native land, homeland" → "fellow countryman" → "strange, odd". By surface analysis, <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strange quark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>country-specific studies, regional geography</li></ol><p><i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering, roaming, travel, travelling</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>страньство</i> (stranĭstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, to travel</li><li>to lead the life of a wanderer</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>страньство</i> (stranĭstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering, migratory (<i>of an animal</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering albatross</li></ol><p><i>стра́нствующий</i> (stránstvujuščij) +&lrm; <i>альбатро́с</i> (alʹbatrós)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robin (<i>Turdus migratorius</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap-on (dildo)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>strap-on</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>passionately (in a passionate manner)</li></ol><p><i>стра́стный</i> (strástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;religion) the week just before Easter (<i>used only in the terms below</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>страстьнъ</i> (strastĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion</li></ol><p><i>стра́стный</i> (strástnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>passionate</li><li>impassioned, ardent, torrid, fervid</li><li>hot, sultry</li><li>warm</li><li>violent, vehement</li><li>tropical, tropic</li><li>hot-blooded</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strastьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>страсть</i> (strastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion-bearer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passion; obsession</li><li>strong love (between people)</li><li>strong obsession with, attraction to some activity</li><li>the subject of someone's passion</li><li>(religion,&#32;in the plural) horror; suffering</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) horror (story)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very, awfully</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strastь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stratum, tier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strategist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strategically</li></ol><p><i>стратеги́ческий</i> (strategíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strategic</li></ol><p><i>страте́гия</i> (stratégija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strategy</li><li>policy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stratigraphy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stratopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stratosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ostrich</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strauß</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ostrich</li></ol><p><i>стра́ус</i> (stráus) +&lrm; <i>-иный</i> (-inyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear</li><li>(dated,&#32;in set expressions) risk, peril</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) awfully</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;straxъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insurance (business)</li></ol><p><i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policyholder</li></ol><p><i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure (to provide for compensation if some risk occurs)</li><li>to guard against</li><li>to stand by (in sports)</li><li>(<i>climbing</i>) to belay</li></ol><p><i>страх</i> (strax) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insure oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insurance (indemnity)</li><li>safety (rope)</li><li>(<i>climbing</i>) protection</li><li>(<i>climbing</i>) belaying</li></ol><p><i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) insurance</li></ol><p><i>страх</i> (strax) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insurer</li><li>insurance company</li></ol><p><i>страхова́ть</i> (straxovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) fright, scarecrow, monster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short children's horror story</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) information meant to scare or disturb</li></ol><p><i>страши́ть</i> (strašítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to scare, to awe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strašiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be afraid of, to dread, to fear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>страши́ть</i> (strašítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>стра́шный</i> (strášnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>стра́шно</i> (strášno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>terrifyingly</li><li>very, badly</li></ol><p><i>стра́шный</i> (strášnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat scarily</li></ol><p><i>страшнова́тый</i> (strašnovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat scary</li></ol><p><i>стра́шный</i> (strášnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, scary [<i>with</i> instrumental <i>or</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in (e.g. one's rage)’</small>] (<i>literally or as an intensifier</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ugly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strašьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;straxъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;strākš-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)treg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to frighten, to scare, to intimidate</li><li>to browbeat</li></ol><p>From <i>страсть</i> (strastʹ, “horror”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deep stream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poker,&#32;inanimate) straight</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) straight person (non-gay person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>straight</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) straight flush</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>straight flush</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragonfly (an insect of the suborder Anisoptera)</li></ol><p><i>стрекота́ть</i> (strekotátʹ) or <i>стрека́ть</i> (strekátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Orthetrum cancellatum</i>, the black-tailed skimmer, a species of dragonfly.</li></ol><p><i>стрекоза́</i> (strekozá, “dragonfly”) +&lrm; <i>решётчатый</i> (rešótčatyj, “latticed, slatted”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chirr, chirrup</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) chatter</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>стрекота́ть</i> (strekotátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chirr (<i>of insects</i>)</li><li>to rattle, to chatter (<i>of machine guns, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, onomatopoeic. Compare Latin <i>strīdeō</i> (“to squeak, to crack, to buzz, to whistle”), also <i>strīdō</i> (infinitive <i>strīdere</i>), Ancient Greek <i>τρίζω</i> (trízō, “to twitter, to squeak, to chirp”) (perfect <i>τέτρῑγα</i> (tétrīga)), <i>στρίξ</i> (stríx, “(type of night bird)”) (gen. sg. <i>στρίγος</i> (strígos)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li><li>pointer, indicator</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) shaft</li><li><i>(technical):</i> crane arm, jib, overhang beam, boom <i>(of a derrick)</i></li><li>derrick</li><li>cantilever</li><li>rise <i>(of an arch)</i>, camber</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strěla</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strel-</i>, <i>&#42;strēl-</i>, from <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“line, streak, stripe; jet, spurt; beam”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) strelets <i>(member of Russian military corps in 16th–17th c.)</i></li><li>(astrology,&#32;astronomy) Sagittarius</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strělьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Стрелец)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Sagittarius (constellation of the zodiac in the shape of an archer)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Sagittarius (zodiac sign for the archer, covering November 22 — December 21)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand, pointer, indicator</li><li>needle</li><li>railroad switch</li><li>arrow <i>(in a drawing)</i></li><li>promontory at a confluence of two rivers</li><li>crease <i>(of trousers)</i></li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) frog</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) business meeting for sorting things out</li><li>(<i>textile</i>) ladder</li><li>Eurasian bluet (<i>Coenagrion</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>From <i>стрела́</i> (strelá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rifle, infantry, shooting</li></ol><p><i>стрело́к</i> (strelók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the quality of being like an arrow</li></ol><p><i>стрелови́дный</i> (strelovídnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrow-like</li></ol><p><i>стрела́</i> (strelá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooter, shot</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rifleman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) arrowhead (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стре́лка</i> (strélka), diminutive of <i>стрела́</i> (strelá): little arrow, little shaft, etc.</li></ol><p><i>стре́лка</i> (strélka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) switchman, pointsman</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) scapegoat, whipping boy</li></ol><p><i>стре́лка</i> (strélka) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooting</li></ol><p><i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archery (the practice)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>tir à l'arc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) shooting ground</li></ol><p><i>стрельба́</i> (strelʹbá) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot <i>(at)</i>, to fire <i>(at)</i>, to fire a shot <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cadge, to scrounge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become popular</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot, to fire</li><li>to shoot <i>feel acute pains</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cadge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стрѣляти</i> (strěljati, “throw arrows”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strěľati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight a duel <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to commit suicide</li></ol><p><i>стреля́ть</i> (streljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>precipitately, very fast, headlong</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) with the head down, headlong</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>стрьмоглавъ</i> (strĭmoglavŭ, “with the head down”) via Russian Church Slavonic forms стрьмъглавъ, стремоглавъ, стремглавъ, which acquired modern meaning in 15th century, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьmъ</i> + <i>&#42;golva</i> + &#42;-ь. Related to Serbo-Croatian <i>strmoglavce</i>.</p><p>For sematic parallels compare English <i>headfirst</i>, <i>headforemost</i>, <i>headlong</i>, Latin <i>praeceps</i> (whence English <i>precipice</i>, <i>precipitate</i>, <i>precipitation</i>). Also compare Russian <i>сломя́ го́лову</i> (slomjá gólovu), <i>очертя́ го́лову</i> (očertjá gólovu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>headlong (with an unrestrained forward motion)</li><li>by leaps and bounds</li></ol><p><i>стреми́тельный</i> (stremítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rashness, precipitancy, impetuosity</li></ol><p><i>стреми́тельный</i> (stremítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impetuous, violent, rash, headlong</li><li>rapid, swift</li></ol><p><i>стреми́ть(ся)</i> (stremítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) to direct (something towards something), to fix (something onto something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьměti</i>. Cognate with Old East Slavic <i>стрьмление</i> (strĭmlenije), Slovene <i>strmẹ́ti</i> (“to stare”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>strmět</i> (“to rise”), Slovak <i>strmieť</i> (“to rise”), Polish <i>trzmić</i> (“to stick out”). Related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьmъ</i> (“steep”). Per Vasmer, possibly derived from the vowel assimilation found in Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьmь</i>, <i>&#42;strьmьnъ</i> (compare <i>стрём</i> (strjom, “fear”), <i>стрёмный</i> (strjómnyj, “scary”)), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strъm-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aspire, to strive, to aim, to long, to crave</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to tend (to/toward)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to speed, to rush</li></ol><p><i>стреми́ть</i> (stremítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspiration, yearning (wistful or melancholy longing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep, precipitous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stirrup</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) stapes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stremę</i>, <i>&#42;strьmę</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>strzemię</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepladder</li></ol><p>From <i>стремя́н</i> (stremján) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); based on the genitive plural of <i>стре́мя</i> (strémja, “stirrup”), as commonly with the <i>-ка</i> (-ka) suffix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hobble <i>(a horse)</i></li></ol><p><i>стрено́жить</i> (strenóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hobble</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>трено́жить</i> (trenóžitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little bustard (Tetrax tetrax)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stress, strain (emotional pressure)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stress</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>distringō</i> (p.p.p. <i>districtus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stress</li></ol><p><i>стресс</i> (strɛss, stress) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stress tolerance, stress immunity, resistance to stress</li></ol><p><i>стресс</i> (stress) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>усто́йчивость</i> (ustójčivostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) eaves</li><li>thatched roof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strěxa</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>стрѣха</i> (strěxa), Serbo-Croatian <i>strèha</i>, Polish <i>strzecha</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;religion) Strigolnik</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стригольникъ</i> (strigolĭnikŭ), relating to <i>стричь</i> (stričʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swift (bird)</li><li>Strizh (Soviet space suit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strižь</i>, <i>&#42;strěžь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-haired, <i>(sheep)</i> shorn, <i>(hair)</i> short</li><li><i>(trees)</i> clipped, <i>(grass)</i> mown</li></ol><p><i>стричь</i> (stričʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haircut, clipping (act of cutting of hair)</li><li>shearing (of sheep)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strižьka</i>. Analyzable as <i>стриг-</i> (strig-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) live stream (a real-time broadcast of audio or video over the Internet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stream</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;Internet) streamer (one who streams to a live audience online)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;computing) streamer (data storage system)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>streamer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) streamer</li></ol><p>From <i>стри́мер</i> (strímer) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(technical,&#32;nautical,&#32;aeronautics) stringer (a longitudinal structural element of the body (frame) of a ship or aircraft)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>stringer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striptease</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>striptease</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) striptease</li></ol><p><i>стрипти́з</i> (striptíz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stripper (male)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>stripteaseur</i> or formed in Russian as <i>стрипти́з</i> (striptíz) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poker,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>стрейт</i> (strɛjt)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>straight</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poker,&#32;proscribed) alternative form of <i>стре́йт-фле́ш</i> (strɛ́jt-flɛ́š, “straight flush”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strychnine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Strychnos</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Strychnos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut (hair, fur, grass, etc.), to shear, to clip, to trim</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стричи</i> (striči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;striťi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;streyg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut one's hair, to get a haircut</li><li>to wear one's hair trimmed (<i>in a specified way</i>)</li></ol><p><i>стри́чь</i> (stríčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sliced frozen meat or fish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sliced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plane, to shave (in terms of wood)</li><li>to slice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стръгати</i> (strŭgati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strъgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, strict, austere, stern [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <i>or</i> <b>к</b> (k, + dative) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rigorously, strictly, strongly, pronouncedly, religiously, definitely</li></ol><p><i>стро́гий</i> (strógij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severity, austerity</li><li>strictness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strogostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>стро́гий</i> (strógij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) drill</li><li>(<i>military</i>) frontline</li><li>(relational,&#32;construction) building</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>стро́(ить)</i> (stró(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building</li><li>structure</li></ol><p><i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>стро́гий</i> (strógij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>стро́гий</i> (strógij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>стро́го</i> (strógo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder, constructor</li><li>construction worker</li></ol><p>From <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ, “build”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>building, construction</li></ol><p><i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) builder, construction worker</li></ol><p><i>строи́тель</i> (stroítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction, building</li><li>construction area</li></ol><p><i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельство</i> (-telʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, to construct</li><li>to make, to construct</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to form, to draw up, to dress up</li><li>to connect (string, yarn, etc.) in three strands</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;colloquial) to strike three items</li><li>to plough (a field) for the third time</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>строити</i> (stroiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strojiti</i>, from <i>&#42;strojь</i>.</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>трои́ть</i> (troítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be built, to be under construction</li><li>to build a house for oneself</li><li>to draw up, to form, to assume formation</li></ol><p><i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>system, structure</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tuning</li><li>row, line (<i>of books, bottles, etc. arranged in sequence</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rank, row (<i>of soldiers, tanks, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) formation (<i>of soldiers, tanks, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>строи</i> (stroi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strojь</i>. Cognate to Russian <i>строить</i> (stroitʹ, “to build, construct”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building, construction</li><li>project, construction project</li><li>construction site, project site</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стройи</i> (stroji), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strew-</i> (“to strew, to spread out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building material</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>строи́тельный материа́л</i> (stroítelʹnyj materiál).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause one to look more slender or slim than usual</li><li>to cause one to lose weight</li></ol><p>Deadjectival, from <i>стройный</i> (strojnyj, “slender”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). An early 20th-century formation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>harmoniously</li></ol><p><i>стро́йный</i> (strójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slimness</li></ol><p><i>стро́йный</i> (strójnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slender, slim, svelte</li><li>well-composed, harmonious, orderly</li><li>harmonious (<i>of sounds</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strojьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>стро́ить</i> (stróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building site</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>строи́тельная площа́дка</i> (stroítelʹnaja ploščádka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line, string, row (single horizontal row of text on a screen, printed paper, etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stroka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) strontium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strontium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) roof, attic, loft</li><li>(technical,&#32;nautical) sling, hitch</li><li>parbuckle</li><li>strap, strop</li><li>shroud (of a parachute)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стропъ</i> (stropŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stropъ</i>. Cognate with English <i>roof</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rafter (series of sloped beams)</li><li>truss</li></ol><p>From <i>стропить</i> (stropitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo), from <i>строп</i> (strop) (related to English <i>roof</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obstinate, obdurate, refractory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>стръпътивъ</i> (strŭpŭtivŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strъpъtivъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stanza (a unit of a poem)</li><li>strophe</li><li>verse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>stropha</i>, itself a borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>στροφή</i> (strophḗ, “a turn, bend, twist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scribble, to dash off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bang away</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>строка́</i> (stroká) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seam, stitch</li><li>diminutive of <i>строка́</i> (stroká)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(orthography,&#32;typography) minuscule (lower-case letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lower-case</li><li>sewn in stitches</li></ol><p><i>строка́</i> (stroká) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) line</li></ol><p><i>строка́</i> (stroká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>C-clamp, G-clamp (tool, a type of clamp device containing a screw)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schraubzwinge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter's plane, adze, drawknife (a tool)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) boat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стругъ</i> (strugŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strugъ</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>струг</i> (strug), Old Church Slavonic <i>строугъ</i> (strugŭ), Slovene <i>strȗg</i>, Czech <i>struh</i>, Polish <i>strug</i>. See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strugati</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(wood)</i> to plane, to shave</li><li>to slice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стругати</i> (strugati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strugati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay-slicing machine, planer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swarf</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaving, shavings, chips</li></ol><p><i>струга́ть</i> (strugátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) swarf</li></ol><p><i>стру́жк(а)</i> (strúžk(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spout, to stream, to flow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strujiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>струя́</i> (strujá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>струя́</i> (strujá)</li></ol><p><i>струя́</i> (strujá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>structure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>structuralist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>structuring</li></ol><p><i>структури́ровать</i> (strukturírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to structure</li></ol><p><i>структу́ра</i> (struktúra) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>structural</li></ol><p><i>структу́ра</i> (struktúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) brook, stream</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strumy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a string (such as that of a musical instrument or a tennis racket)</li><li>(<i>transferred sense</i>) a major and relatively straight intra-city highway</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a string of the human soul which is thought of as causing some emotion upon being touched</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) string (the main object of study in string theory, a branch of theoretical physics)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;struna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) chord (<i>e.g. of emotions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>струна́</i> (struná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) string (<i>of a musical instrument</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) string instrument</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) sonorous, musical</li></ol><p><i>струна́</i> (struná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scab</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>струпъ</i> (strupŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strupъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) to chicken out, to lose one's nerve, to be scared, to be cowardly</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to shake off, to shake out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тру́сить</i> (trúsitʹ)</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>труси́ть</i> (trusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pod (seed case)</li></ol><p><i>стручо́к</i> (stručók) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pod, husk (seed case)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strǫkъ</i> + <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) pods, husks</li></ol><p><i>струч-</i> (struč-, “pod”) +&lrm; <i>-ья</i> (-ʹja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jet, air blast (stream of fluid)</li><li>spurt, squirt, stream</li><li>current, efflux, flow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>струя</i> (struja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;struja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) paternal uncle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strъjь</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Стрый)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stryi (a city, the administrative center of Stryi Raion, Lviv Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Stryi (a river in Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cook, to cook food</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to concoct, to cook up</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стрѧпати</i> (strępati, “to work (slowly, meticulously), to array”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strępati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cooking; dish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) concoction, fudge</li></ol><p>From <i>стря́пать</i> (strjápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) female cook</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;dated,&#32;law) solicitor; lawyer</li></ol><p>From <i>стря́пать</i> (strjápatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, to shake down</li></ol><p><i>стрясти́</i> (strjastí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to befall, to happen <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>стряса́ть</i> (strjasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off, to shake down</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to befall, to happen <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>стрясти́</i> (strjastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off</li></ol><p><i>стряхну́ть</i> (strjaxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake off</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тряхну́ть</i> (trjaxnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) fear, anxiety, dread (as a result of withdrawal syndrome)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) guard, alert, watch, lookout</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) danger, threat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>стрёмный</i> (strjómnyj)</li></ol><p>From <i>стрёмный</i> (strjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) scary, scary-hairy <i>(dangerous)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) shameful</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) ugly, unattractive; bad, uncool; unpleasant, etc.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strьmьnъ</i> (“steep”). By surface analysis, <i>стрём</i> (strjom) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стрела́</i> (strelá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring (water source)</li><li>well (hole sunk into the ground)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>студеньць</i> (studenĭcĭ, “spring; spring, source”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studenьcь</i> (“cold source”), from <i>&#42;studenъ</i> (“cold”) + <i>&#42;-ьcь</i>, from <i>&#42;studъ</i> (“cold, shame”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>student (<i>in a university or college</i>), undergraduate</li></ol><p>Probably from Polish <i>student</i> or German <i>Student</i>, from Latin <i>studēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) student</li></ol><p><i>студе́нт</i> (studént) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) student</li></ol><p><i>студе́н(т)</i> (studén(t)) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time when one is a student</li><li>(uncountable,&#32;collective) students</li></ol><p>From <i>студе́нт</i> (studént) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo), with unexpected iotation; modeled after <i>студе́нческий</i> (studénčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspic (dish)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) December</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) student</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studènъ</i> (“cold”). Related to студёный (studjónyj) and сту́жа (stúža).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>студьнъ</i> (studĭnŭ, “December”). Cognates include Belarusian <i>студзень</i> (studzjenʹ, “January”) and Serbo-Croatian <i>stùdenī</i> (Croatian). Related to <i>студёный</i> (studjónyj). See etymology 1.</p><p>Contraction and pun of <i>студе́нт</i> (studént) (more contracted in plural: <i>сту́дни</i> (stúdni) instead of <i>студе́нты</i> (studénty)). See etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) studio</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) art school</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) theater troupe (<i>of young actors</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>сту́дия</i> (stúdija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>студі́йний</i> (studíjnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cool</li></ol><p>Denominal of Old Church Slavonic <i>студъ</i> (studŭ, “cool, chill”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studiti</i>. Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studenъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>studio (artist's or photographer's workshop)</li><li>studio (television or radio studio)</li><li>studio apartment</li><li>art school</li><li>theater troupe (<i>of young actors</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>studio</i>. Compare Belarusian <i>сту́дыя</i> (stúdyja) and Ukrainian <i>сту́дія</i> (stúdija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studenъ</i> (“cold”), from <i>&#42;studъ</i> (“cold, shame”). See <i>сту́жа</i> (stúža) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severe cold, hard frost</li></ol><p>From Old Church Slavonic <i>студъ</i> (studŭ, “cold”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studenъ</i> (“cold”), from <i>&#42;studъ</i> (“cold, shame”) and thus cognate with <i>стыд</i> (styd, “shame”). Compare Polish <i>ostuda</i>, Bulgarian <i>студ</i> (stud).</p><p>Vasmer links the PIE root with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewd-</i> which, that being true, would expand the list of cognates to the likes of Sanskrit <i>तुदति</i> (tudati, “to vex, to bother”), Sanskrit <i>तोद</i> (toda, “instigator, propeller”), Latin <i>tundō</i> (“to beat, to crush”), Ancient Greek <i>Τῡδεύς</i> (Tūdeús, “Tydeus”), German <i>stoßen</i> and English <i>stot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knock</li><li>rattle, clatter, noise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стукъ</i> (stukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stukъ</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>стук</i> (stuk), Polish <i>stuk</i>, Serbian Church Slavonic <i>стукъ</i> (stukŭ, “sound”). Onomatopoeic in origin; see <i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ) for further info.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li><li>to rap, to tap</li><li>to strike, to bang, to hit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stukati</i>. Of onomatopoeic origin; see <i>стук</i> (stuk), <i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ).</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>сту́кати</i> (stúkaty), Belarusian <i>сту́каць</i> (stúkacʹ), Polish <i>stukać</i>. Compare also Latvian <i>stukât</i> (“to walk with small steps (<i>of children learning to walk</i>)”), <i>stuknît</i> (“to push forward with blows”), <i>stucinât</i> (“to knock, to rumble”), <i>stukme</i> (“trampled place in a meadow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock <i>(against)</i>, to bump <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) informer, snitch, stooge, dobber, grass</li></ol><p>Morphologically can be from <i>сту́кать</i> (stúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač), semantically can be from <i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стука́ч</i> (stukáč): informer, snitch, stooge, dobber, grass</li></ol><p><i>стука́ч</i> (stukáč) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li><li>to rap, to tap</li><li>to strike, to bang, to hit</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to squeal on</li></ol><p><i>стук</i> (stuk) +&lrm; <i>-ну</i> (-nu) +&lrm; <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock <i>(against)</i>, to bump <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>сту́кнуть</i> (stúknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chair, seat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) knock at the door, four in the lotto game</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) stool, feces</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стулъ</i> (stulŭ), borrowed from a Germanic language, probably Old Norse or Middle Low German, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stōlaz</i>. Cognate with English <i>stool</i>. Doublet of <i>стол</i> (stol).</p><p>Loan sense from English <i>stool</i> and/or German <i>Stuhl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стул</i> (stul).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стул</i> (stul): (small) chair</li></ol><p><i>стул</i> (stul) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar (a hollow vessel used to grind ingredients with a pestle)</li><li>stupa (a dome-shaped Buddhist monument, used to house relics of the Lord Buddha)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stǫpa</i>.</p><p>From Sanskrit <i>स्तूप</i> (stūpa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to step on</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to leave</li></ol><p><i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in steps</li></ol><p><i>ступе́нчатый</i> (stupénčatyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gradation, gradualness</li></ol><p><i>ступе́нчатый</i> (stupénčatyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) step</li><li>step-like</li><li>graduated</li><li>staggered</li></ol><p><i>ступе́нь</i> (stupénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) step (interval between two contiguous degrees of the scale)</li><li>step (in a staircase), footstep, rung</li><li>degree, grade, level, phase, stage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stǫpenь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ступе́нь</i> (stupénʹ) in the meaning "step, rung"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread, to step on</li><li>to blunt, to make dull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ступити</i> (stupiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stǫpiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stab-</i> (“to support”).</p><p>Compare Upper Sorbian <i>stupić</i>, Polish <i>stąpić</i>, Czech <i>stoupit</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>stupiti</i>.</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hub</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) foot</li><li>sole</li></ol><p>Related to ступи́ть (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) stupor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>stupor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li><li>to rap, to tap</li><li>to throb <i>(especially the heart)</i></li><li>to chatter, to clatter</li><li>to bang</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) to snitch on</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>стук</i> (stuk) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock</li></ol><p><i>стуча́ть</i> (stučátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become unnoticeable</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to efface oneself, to retire to the background</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become embarrassed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to go away, to retire</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тушева́ться</i> (tuševátʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become unnoticeable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) alternative form of <i>сти́брить</i> (stíbritʹ); to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame</li><li>embarrassment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stydъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, to shame</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to defame, to dishonor</li></ol><p><i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>стыди́ть</i> (stydítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shyly, bashfully</li></ol><p><i>стыдли́вый</i> (stydlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bashfulness, shyness</li></ol><p><i>стыдли́вый</i> (stydlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bashful, shy</li></ol><p><i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameful</li></ol><p><i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame</li></ol><p><i>стыд</i> (styd) +&lrm; <i>-оба</i> (-oba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint, juncture (point of a rigid joint, means of joining in carpentry. Also metaphorical, e.g., between two places.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to attach, to join</li><li>to dock, to couple tightly (<i>of spaceships, boats, etc.</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to communicate or meet (with someone)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;intransitive) to agree, to match, to not be in contradiction</li></ol><p><i>стыкова́ть</i> (stykovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>space flight</i>) docking (the process of connecting one spacecraft to another)</li></ol><p><i>стыкова́ться</i> (stykovátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) junction (the process of connecting one object to another)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) docking (the process of connecting one spacecraft to another)</li></ol><p><i>стыко́вка</i> (stykóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) from behind, from the back</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, to shrivel, to fade, to dry up (<i>e.g. of flowers</i>)</li><li>to get cool / cold, to cool down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to freeze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stydnǫti</i> (“to cool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cold, frost</li></ol><p><i>сты́нуть</i> (stýnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pinch, to nick, to steal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take for one's own, to misappropriate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ты́рить</i> (týritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be getting cool / cold; to be cooling down</li><li>to freeze, to be frozen, to be cold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirmish (brief battle between small groups)</li></ol><p>Per Dal, from <i>стыка́ть</i> (stykátʹ, “to connect, to join”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka); presumably the sense development was "joining" → "meeting" → "skirmishing".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward</li><li>flight attendant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>steward</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) banner, flag</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>стѧгъ</i> (stęgŭ), which is most probably from Old Norse <i>stǫng</i> (whence English <i>stang</i> and Danish <i>stang</i>). Compare <i>шта́нга</i> (štánga), from German <i>Stange</i>. Alternative reconstructions point towards Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stěgъ</i> which was limited to South Slavic languages. Cognate with Belarusian <i>сцяг</i> (scjah) and Ukrainian <i>стяг</i> (stjah).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten, to tie up, to strap down/up</li><li>to gather, to draw up</li><li>to pull off/away</li></ol><p><i>стяг-</i> (stjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tightened</li><li>to be concentrated, to draw, to gather</li></ol><p><i>стя́гивать</i> (stjágivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, gaining, acquisition</li></ol><p><i>стяжа́ть</i> (stjažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>money-grubber</li></ol><p><i>стяжа́ть</i> (stjažátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tighten, to tie up, to strap down/up</li><li>to gather, to draw up</li><li>to pull off/away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to filch, to swipe, to pinch</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tightened</li><li>to be concentrated, to draw, to gather</li></ol><p><i>стяну́ть</i> (stjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) <i>(usually uncountable)</i> banter, teasing, fun, sneer, ridicule</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quilted (made of two layers of fabric stitched together, with insulation in between)</li></ol><p><i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>action of</i> <i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ, “to quilt”)</li><li>a quilting seam</li><li>diminutive of <i>стежь</i> (stežʹ): small path, trail</li></ol><p><i>стега́ть</i> (stegátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьžьka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>стежь</i> (stežʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), related to <i>стезя́</i> (stezjá). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>сте́жка</i> (stéžka), Belarusian <i>сце́жка</i> (scjéžka), Polish <i>ścieżka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>стекло́</i> (stekló): a (small) piece of glass</li></ol><p><i>стекло́</i> (stekló) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Donbass) eraser, rubber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worn thin</li><li>hackneyed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>стена́</i> (stená).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>com-, con- (forms nouns denoting a connection or link)</li><li>(forms nouns denoting incompleteness)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫ-</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫ-</i> (sǫ-). From either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-</i> (“one, same”) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱom</i> (“with”). Further cognate with Old Prussian <i>san-</i>, <i>sen</i>, Lithuanian <i>sán-</i>, <i>są́-</i>, Latvian <i>suo-</i> (the latter three being nominal prefixes), along with Old Lithuanian <i>sa-</i>, <i>sù-</i>, Sanskrit <i>सम्</i> (sam), <i>स</i> (sa), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬨-</i> (ham-), <i>𐬵𐬀-</i> (ha-), Ancient Greek <i>ὁμοῦ</i> (homoû, “together with”), <i>ἅμα</i> (háma, “together with”), <i>ἁ-</i> (ha-, “with”), Latin <i>semel</i> (“once”).</p><p>Etymologically identical to the verbal prefix <i>с-</i> (s-), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)-</i>. The difference in development is because of the looser juncture between early Proto-Slavic verbs and preverbs: thus, preverbal *sun was subject to Slavic <i>Auslautgesetze</i>. A similar situation is found in modern German. Not to be confused with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ-</i> (“good”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-</i>.</p><p>A similar or equivalent prefix appears in a few words in Old Church Slavonic as <i>соу-</i> (sou-), with no trace of a nasal vowel, e.g. <i>соугоубь</i> (sugubĭ, “double”), with the root of <i>-гиба́ть</i> (-gibátʹ, “to bend”); compare Russian <i>сугу́бый</i> (sugúbyj, “strict, pure, (originally) double”). Some linguists believe this to be a distinct suffix, while Vasmer sees (in this case at least) assimilation from earlier <i>&#42;sǫ-</i>, as above.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soirée (formal evening party)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>soirée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swahili (language)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swahili <i>swahili</i>, from plural of Arabic سَاحِل (sāḥil, “coast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>sub- (under, beneath)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) subatomic</li></ol><p><i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>а́томный</i> (átomnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturday</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>субота</i> (subota), from Byzantine Greek <i>&#42;σάμβατον</i> (&#42;sámbaton), from Ancient Greek <i>σάββατα</i> (sábbata), from Aramaic <i>שַׁבְּתָא</i> (šabbǝtā) or Hebrew <i>שַׁבָּת</i> (šabbāt). The <b>-бб-</b> arose from the influence of <b>-bb-</b> in the Western languages.</p><p>Near cognates: Ukrainian <i>субо́та</i> (subóta); Belarusian <i>субо́та</i> (subóta); Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫбота</i> (sǫbota); Bulgarian <i>съ́бота</i> (sǎ́bota); Serbo-Croatian <i>су́бота</i>.</p><p>But also there is Old Church Slavonic <i>собота</i> (sobota) (Czech, Slovak, Polish, Polabian, Lower Sorbian <i>sobota</i>) that is from Medieval Latin <i>sabbatum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σάββατα</i> (sábbata).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Saturday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subbotnik (voluntary unpaid work)</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) a member of Russian sect of Sabbath keepers (or any similar sect).</li></ol><p><i>суббо́та</i> (subbóta, “Saturday”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), since 1919 when the first subbotnik was held.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subdominant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subcontinent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subculture</li></ol><p><i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>культу́ра</i> (kulʹtúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sublimation (phase transition)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evanesce (to transition from solid to gas state)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) subnitride (any nitride containing a smaller proportion of nitrogen than a related compound)</li></ol><p><i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>нитри́д</i> (nitríd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(system of) seniority, subordination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subcontract</li></ol><p>From <i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>подря́д</i> (podrjád).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>byproduct, subproduct (secondary or additional product)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsidization</li></ol><p><i>субсиди́ровать</i> (subsidírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subsidize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsidy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) substantivized, nominalized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) substantival, substantive (used like a noun)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) substantive, substantial (having substance or being essential)</li></ol><p><i>субстанти́в</i> (substantív) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), borrowed from Latin <i>substantīvus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) substantial</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) essential, fundamental</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substance (matter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substratum</li><li>substrate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subtype</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from English <i>subtype</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cinematography,&#32;television) subtitle</li></ol><p><i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>титр</i> (titr). From French <i>sous-titre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) subfossil (a partially fossilized organism)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>subfossil</i>. By surface analysis, <i>суб-</i> (sub-) +&lrm; <i>фосси́лия</i> (fossílija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subunit (<i>part of a larger unit</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>суб-</i> (sub-, “sub-”) +&lrm; <i>едини́ца</i> (jediníca, “unit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) subject</li><li>character</li><li>individual</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>subiectum</i>. Doublet of <i>сюже́т</i> (sjužét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subjectivity</li></ol><p><i>субъекти́вный</i> (subʺjektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subjective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>souvenir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) souvenir</li></ol><p><i>сувени́р</i> (suvenír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereign, ruler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>souverain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereignty</li></ol><p>Ultimately from French <i>souveraineté</i>, likely through German <i>Souveränität</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sovereign</li></ol><p><i>сувере́н</i> (suverén) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) vorticella</li></ol><p><i>суво́й</i> (suvój) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Suvorov (referring to anyone named Suvorov, especially Alexander Suvorov, Russian military hero)</li></ol><p><i>Суво́ров</i> (Suvórov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) loam</li></ol><p>From <i>су-</i> (su-) +&lrm; <i>гли́н(а)</i> (glín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snowbank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strictly, purely</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) special, exclusive</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) twofold; double</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫgubъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) court</li><li>judgment</li><li>tribunal</li><li>trial</li><li>justice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѫдъ</i> (sǫdŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫdъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zander, pike-perch (the fish or its meat, which is highly regarded)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫdakъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zander, pike-perch</li></ol><p><i>суда́к</i> (sudák) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sudanese</li></ol><p><i>Суда́н</i> (Sudán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;vernacular) sweetheart, mistress</li></ol><p><i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;vernacular) diminutive of <i>суда́рка</i> (sudárka); sweetheart, mistress</li><li>(archaic,&#32;vernacular) Used as an affectionate address to a woman or girl.</li></ol><p><i>суда́рка</i> (sudárka) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sir</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>госуда́рь</i> (gosudárʹ, “sire”). Rather archaic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zander, pike-perch</li></ol><p><i>суда́к</i> (sudák) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to engage in gossip</li></ol><p>Compare <i>порта́чить</i> (portáčitʹ), <i>напорта́чить</i> (naportáčitʹ) (from <i>по́ртить</i> (pórtitʹ), <i>напо́ртить</i> (napórtitʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injunction (an official writ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>judicial, judiciary (relating to a court of law)</li><li>forensic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>судьбьныи</i> (sudĭbĭnyi). By surface analysis, <i>суд</i> (sud) +&lrm; <i>-е́бный</i> (-ébnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>judicial</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) referee's, umpire's</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) officer of the court</li></ol><p><i>судья́</i> (sudʹjá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>refereeing</li><li>judging</li><li>umpiring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;dated) court of law, trial</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) unfair court of law, unfair trial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫдилище</i> (sǫdilište). By surface analysis, <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ, “to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>previous convictions</li></ol><p><i>суди́мый</i> (sudímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to judge, to try, to adjudicate</li><li>to judge (to form an opinion on)</li><li>(<i>poetic&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to destine, to predetermine</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>судити</i> (suditi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǭdìti</i>. See also <i>суд</i> (sud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to law, to be at law <i>(with)</i>, to have legal proceedings <i>(with)</i></li><li>to have a criminal record, to have been convicted in court</li></ol><p><i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>судья́</i> (sudʹjá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forensic expert, medical examiner</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>суде́бно медици́нский экспе́рт</i> (sudébno medicínskij ekspért).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship, vessel</li><li>bedpan</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>судьно</i> (sudĭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫdьno</i>. Cognates include сосуд, посуда, судок. Ultimately from <i>&#42;som-</i> + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> + <i>&#42;-os</i>.</p><p>The nautical meaning is a late calque from French <i>vaisseau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hovercraft</li></ol><p>From <i>су́дно</i> (súdno, “boat”) +&lrm; <i>суд</i> (sud, “on”) +&lrm; <i>воздушный</i> (vozdušnyj, “air; aerial”) +&lrm; <i>подушка</i> (poduška, “cushion, pillow”), literally “boat on an air-cushion”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydrofoil (vessel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;dated) law court, lawsuit</li></ol><p><i>суд</i> (sud) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgement day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ship; ship's</li></ol><p><i>су́д(но)</i> (súd(no)) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cruet, cruet-stand</li><li>a set of containers for the transportation of food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sudoku (a Japanese number puzzle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>数独</i> (すうどく, sūdoku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) scullery maid (a woman who washes dishes)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) scullery (room for washing dishes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishwashing room; scullery</li></ol><p>From <i>суд</i> (sud, “vessel”) (<i>obsolete</i>) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-мой</i> (-moj, “to wash”, combining form) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja). Here, <i>суд</i> (sud) is an obsolete word for "vessel (container)" (nowadays <i>сосу́д</i> (sosúd)), inherited from Old East Slavic <i>судъ</i> (sudŭ) and cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫдъ</i> (sǫdŭ), Ukrainian <i>суд</i> (sud), Belarusian <i>су́ды</i> (súdy, plural), Bulgarian <i>съд</i> (sǎd), Serbo-Croatian <i>су̑д</i> (plural <i>су̑ди</i> or <i>су̏дови</i>), Slovene <i>sо̣̑d</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>sud</i> (“barrel”), Slovak <i>sud</i> (“barrel”), Polish <i>sąd</i> (genitive <i>sędu</i>), Upper Sorbian <i>sud</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>sud</i> (“barrel, tub”). Here also, <i>-мой</i> (-moj) is a combining form of <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ, “to wash”); compare 1sg. <i>мо́ю</i> (móju).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) legal proceedings(s)</li></ol><p>From <i>суд</i> (sud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>произво́дство</i> (proizvódstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cramp, spasm (painful contraction of a muscle)</li></ol><p>From <i>су-</i> (su-) +&lrm; <i>доро́г</i> (doróg) (associated with <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ, “to pull”)). See Lithuanian <i>sudi̇̀rgti</i> (“to get angry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>convulsively, spasmodically, fitfully</li></ol><p><i>су́дорожный</i> (súdorožnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convulsive, spasmodic, fitful</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cramp, spasm</li></ol><p><i>су́дорога</i> (súdoroga) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipbuilding</li></ol><p><i>су́дно</i> (súdno) +&lrm; <i>строе́ние</i> (strojénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>navigable</li></ol><p>From <i>су́дно</i> (súdno) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ход</i> (xod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Combining form <i>&#42;судо-</i> (&#42;sudo-) is based on the plural <i>суда́</i> (sudá) of <i>су́дно</i> (súdno, “ship”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ship navigation</li></ol><p><i>судохо́д(ный)</i> (sudoxód(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>суд</i> (sud).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destiny, fate, doom, fortune, predestination</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>судьба</i> (sudĭba), from <i>судити</i> (suditi, “to judge”) + <i>-ьба</i> (-ĭba) (synchronically, <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba)). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫдьба</i> (sǫdĭba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destiny, fate, doom, fortune, predestination</li></ol><p>From <i>судьба</i> (sudʹba) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) fateful, crucial, make-or-break</li></ol><p><i>судьба́</i> (sudʹbá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge</li><li>arbitrator, referee, umpire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫdì</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;som-dʰh₁-ih₂</i>, from <i>&#42;som-</i> + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> + <i>&#42;-ih₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by/from the look(s) of it, by all accounts, it appears, from what I can see</li></ol><p>Literally, “judging by/from everything”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>су́дно</i> (súdno): small boat</li><li>pejorative of <i>су́дно</i> (súdno): bad boat</li></ol><p><i>су́дно</i> (súdno) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>futile, useless</li><li>superficial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>соуѥ-</i> (suje-), calque of Ancient Greek <i>ματαιο-</i> (mataio-). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sujь</i> (“futile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) superstitious person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superstition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>соуѥвѣрьѥ</i> (sujevěrĭje), equivalent to <i>суе-</i> (suje-, “superficial”) +&lrm; + <i>ве́ра</i> (véra) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superstitious (susceptible to superstitions)</li></ol><p><i>суеве́рие</i> (sujevérije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) idle claim/talk/promise (claim, talk, promise without content), verbiage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>соуѥсловьѥ</i> (sujeslovĭje), calque of Ancient Greek <i>ματαιολογία</i> (mataiología). Equivalent to <i>суе-</i> (suje-, “futile”) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo, “word, speech”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustle, fuss, razzle-dazzle</li><li>vanity</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>суи</i> (sui, “empty, vain”) or Old Church Slavonic <i>соуи</i> (sui). Compare with <i>всу́е</i> (vsúje, “in vain”) and terms with prefix <i>суе-</i> (suje-), e.g. <i>суеве́рие</i> (sujevérije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fuss, to bustle</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>суета́</i> (sujetá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fussily</li><li>restlessly, in a bustling manner</li></ol><p><i>суетли́вый</i> (sujetlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fidgety, fussy; bustling</li></ol><p><i>суета́</i> (sujetá) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bustling</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) vain, empty</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) vain, worldly, carnal</li></ol><p><i>суета́</i> (sujetá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>су́живать</i> (súživatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>су́зить</i> (súzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>су́живаться</i> (súživatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>сужа́ть</i> (sužátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judgment (conclusion or result of judging)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowing, contraction</li></ol><p><i>су́зить</i> (súzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrow, to taper <i>(off)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to judge (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>су́зить</i> (súzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>суди́ть</i> (sudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrow, to get/grow narrow, to taper <i>(off)</i></li></ol><p><i>су́живать</i> (súživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrow, to narrow down</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫziti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>у́з(кий)</i> (úz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to narrow, to get/grow narrow, to taper <i>(off)</i></li></ol><p><i>су́зить</i> (súzitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) suicide (the act of killing oneself)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from New Latin <i>suīcīdium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bough, limb, branch</li><li>(Arab world,&#32;rare) souq</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѫкъ</i> (sǫkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫkъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Arabic سُوق (sūq, “market”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitch (female dog)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) bitch (contemptible person, usually but not necessarily female)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) frivolous, promiscuous female</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) sometimes used to denote any female</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) snitch, inmate who collaborates with prison administration</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) shit!, fuck!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сука</i> (suka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sùka</i>, further etymology unclear. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;swear word,&#32;vulgar) son of a bitch!; fucking hell!; what the fuck (expression of anger or disappointment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) misspelling of <i>су́ка блядь</i> (súka bljadʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(possessive,&#32;vulgar,&#32;offensive) bitch’s</li></ol><p><i>су́ка</i> (súka) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;invective,&#32;highly offensive) son of a bitch, dirty bastard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sukiyaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>鋤焼き</i> (すきやき, sukiyaki) or <i>好き焼き</i> (すきやき, sukiyaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) sukkah, temporary dwelling used on Sukkot.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>סוכה \ סֻכָּה</i> (suká).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) succubus (female demon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloth, broadcloth (a dense, smooth woolen or (rarely) cotton fabric)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) desk, table (<i>with a smooth cloth-covered tabletop; e.g. a card table or a billiards table</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;suk(ъ)no</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fullery, fulling mill</li></ol><p><i>сукно́</i> (suknó) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>валя́ть</i> (valjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cloth</li><li>uninspired, dull (<i>speech, words, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>сукно́</i> (suknó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) vinegar</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of the phrase <i>с у́ксусом</i> (s úksusom).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulema, mercuric chloride</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>сублимат</i> (sublimat). Compare English <i>sulema</i> and Swedish <i>sublimat</i> (“calomel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) to promise</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to foreshadow, to portend, to bode</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to predict, to prophesy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) metal-tipped javelin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сулица</i> (sulica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sudlica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sultan</li><li>plume <i>on the headgear</i></li><li>plume (<i>of smoke</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic سُلْطَان (sulṭān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sultanate</li></ol><p><i>султан</i> (sultan) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) gallinule <i>(Porphyrio)</i></li><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) purple gallinule</li><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) goatfish <i>(Mullus)</i></li><li>purple swamp hen</li><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) mullet <i>(Mullus)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sultanic</li></ol><p><i>султан</i> (sultan) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sulguni (brined Georgian cheese)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>სულგუნი</i> (sulguni).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) sulfate</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>sulphatum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) sulfide, sulphide (compound of sulfur and a metal or other electropositive element or group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) sulfo-group/sulpho-group</li></ol><p>Possibly an internationalism from English <i>sulfo-group</i> or English <i>sulpho-group</i>. Constructed via <i>сульфо-</i> (sulʹfo-, “sulfo-, sulfur”) +&lrm; <i>группа</i> (gruppa, “group”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, pouch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>suma</i>, from German <i>Saum</i> (“burden of a pack animal”), from Middle High German <i>soum</i> (“weight, pack animal”), from Old High German <i>soum</i> (“weight, pack animal”), from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;sauma</i> (“packsaddle”), from Latin <i>sagma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σάγμα</i> (ságma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) madcap, screwball, nut, madman</li></ol><p>Compare <i>сумасшедший</i> (sumasšedšij) and <i>сброд</i> (sbrod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act in a crazy or mad manner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) madcap, screwball, nut, madman</li></ol><p><i>сумасбро́д</i> (sumasbród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) madly (to a very high degree)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) recklessly, crazily, abnormally</li></ol><p><i>сумасше́дший</i> (sumasšédšij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mad, crazy</li></ol><p><i>с</i> (s) +&lrm; <i>ума́</i> (umá, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>соше́дший</i> (sošédšij, “went out of”) (the adjectival rendition of the idiom, <i>сойти́ с ума́</i> (sojtí s umá, “to go out of one's mind, to go insane”))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) insane asylum (mental hospital), madhouse (also figurative)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mental illness, madness, insanity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act like a madman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustle, turmoil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сумато́шный</i> (sumatóšnyj)</li></ol><p><i>сумато́ха</i> (sumatóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fidgety, fussy; bustling</li></ol><p>From <i>сумато́ха</i> (sumatóxa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sumatran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sumac</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confusion</li></ol><p>The word has been known since the mid-18th and was noted by Lomonosov in the materials for his work "Russian Grammar".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confusing, unclear, tangled, difficult to understand</li></ol><p><i>сумбу́р</i> (sumbúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) twilight, dusk</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) crepuscular</li><li>dim, crepuscular</li></ol><p><i>су́мерки</i> (súmerki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dusk, twilight</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) twilight, fading</li></ol><p>Related to Old East Slavic <i>суморокъ</i> (sumorokŭ), with vocalism change; ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫmorkъ</i>. See also <i>су́мрак</i> (súmrak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be able, to manage, to succeed</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>уме́ть</i> (umétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, handbag (US: purse)</li><li>pouch</li><li>satchel</li><li>wallet</li></ol><p><i>сума́</i> (sumá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bursiform (shaped like a purse or bag)</li></ol><p><i>су́мка</i> (súmka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;finance) sum (quantity obtained by addition or aggregation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>summa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>total, aggregate</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) summary</li><li>general</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>summārium</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to summarize, to sum up, to abridge (to shorten or contract by using fewer words)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sumo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>相撲<rp>(</rp><rt>すもう</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>sumō</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>су́мка</i> (súmka): (small) bag, handbag, purse</li></ol><p><i>су́мка</i> (súmka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twilight, dusk, duskiness</li><li>gloom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫмракъ</i> (sǫmrakŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫmorkъ</i>. Displaced native Old East Slavic <i>суморокъ</i> (sumorokŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gloomily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloomy</li></ol><p><i>су́мрак</i> (súmrak) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsupial (mammal of which the female typically has a pouch)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marsupial (of or pertaining to a marsupial)</li></ol><p><i>су́мка</i> (súmka) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustle, commotion, turmoil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk, chest, coffer, strongbox</li></ol><p>Etymology treeOttoman Turkish صندوق (sandık)bor.Russian <i>сундук</i></p><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish صندوق (sandık).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сунду́к</i> (sundúk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trunk, chest, coffer, strongbox</li></ol><p><i>сунду́к</i> (sundúk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сунду́к</i> (sundúk)</li></ol><p><i>сунду́к</i> (sundúk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunnah</li></ol><p>From Arabic سُنَّة (sunna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunnism</li></ol><p><i>су́нна</i> (súnna) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sunnite</li></ol><p><i>су́нна</i> (súnna) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sunnite</li></ol><p><i>сунни́т</i> (sunnít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Song</li></ol><p>From <i>Сун</i> (Sun) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (in)</li><li>to slip, to give</li><li>to poke, to stick in</li><li>to butt in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bribe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sunǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory,&#32;figuratively) to stick one's nose in (to be nosy&#59; to meddle or interfere in the affairs of another)</li></ol><p>Compare French <i>fourrer son nez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to try to get <i>(somewhere)</i>, to try to enter <i>(a place)</i>, to poke one's head in</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to turn <i>(to)</i>, to go <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to butt <i>(in)</i>, to poke one's nose <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>су́нуть</i> (súnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soup (dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>soupe</i>. Further from Latin <i>suppa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) great! super! cool!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rarely) clipping of <i>суперобложка</i> (superobložka): wraparound, dust jacket (of a book)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>суперма́ркет</i> (supermárket): supermarket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>super</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>super-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;ironic) super-duper</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;ironic) super-duper</li></ol><p>Rhyming reduplication of <i>су́пер</i> (súper). Compare borrowed <i>су́пер-ду́пер</i> (súper-dúper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superheroic</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>геро́й</i> (gerój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superhero</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>геро́й</i> (gerój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supergirl</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>де́вочка</i> (dévočka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superpower</li></ol><p><i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ва</i> (deržáva)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superstar (a high-level celebrity)</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá); calque of English <i>superstar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervillain</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>злоде́й</i> (zlodéj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>virology</i>) viral envelope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supercargo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) superlative degree, superlative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supermarket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>supermarket</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;ironic</i>) superman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>superman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supermodel, top model</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superreliable</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>надёжный</i> (nadjóžnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) dust jacket</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>обло́жка</i> (oblóžka). Compare Ukrainian <i>суперобкла́динка</i> (superobkládynka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superpatriotism</li></ol><p><i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>патриоти́зм</i> (patriotízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superpopular</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>популя́рный</i> (populjárnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) super, great, awesome</li></ol><p><i>су́пер</i> (súper) +&lrm; <i>-ски</i> (-ski)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superstylish</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>сти́льный</i> (stílʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supertoxic</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>токси́чный</i> (toksíčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superthin</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supersuccessful</li></ol><p>From <i>супер-</i> (super-) +&lrm; <i>успе́шный</i> (uspéšnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>суп</i> (sup)</li></ol><p><i>суп</i> (sup) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrinkle, to pull in (one's eyebrows)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫpiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tureen</li></ol><p><i>суп</i> (sup) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tureen</li></ol><p><i>суп</i> (sup) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;high-style,&#32;derogatory) adversary, enemy, foe</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) villain, scoundrel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>супостатъ</i> (supostatŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫpostatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against</li><li>in spite of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband, male spouse</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) husband and wife, couple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫprǫgъ</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>coniūnx</i>, Ancient Greek <i>σύζυγος</i> (súzugos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery (sexual intercourse by a married person with someone other than their spouse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adultery (sexual intercourse by a married person with someone other than their spouse)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>matrimonial</li></ol><p><i>супру́г</i> (suprúg) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matrimony, marriage</li></ol><p><i>супру́г</i> (suprúg) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>суп</i> (sup, “soup”)</li></ol><p><i>суп</i> (sup) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) sura (any of the 114 chapters of the Quran)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic سُورَة (sūra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sealing wax</li></ol><p>Mid-17th century from a Turkic language which compounded the elements represented in Modern and Ottoman Turkish as صیر (sır, “varnish, glaze, enamel”) + ـغج (-gaç, -gıç, -geç, -giç).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sealing wax</li></ol><p><i>сургу́ч</i> (surgúč) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sealing wax</li></ol><p><i>сургу́ч</i> (surgúč) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow rocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow rocket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yellow rocket</li></ol><p><i>суре́п(ица)</i> (surép(ica)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>а mixture of wheat and rye, barley and rye, barley and oats, and similar; maslin</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Surzhyk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>су́ржик</i> (súržyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meerkat, suricate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Surinamese</li></ol><p><i>Сурина́м</i> (Surinám) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kohl</li></ol><p><i>сурм(а́)</i> (surm(á)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hard, harshly</li></ol><p><i>суро́вый</i> (suróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severity (degree of something undesirable&#59; badness)</li><li>austerity</li><li>sternness</li></ol><p><i>суро́вый</i> (suróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>severe, strict, harsh, stern, austere, rigorous</li><li>severe, inclement</li><li>stern, inexorable, severe</li><li>unbleached, brown (cloth)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>суровъ</i> (surovŭ), itself inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;surovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Surozh/Sourozh (former name of Sudak, town in Crimea)</li></ol><p><i>Су́рож</i> (Súrož) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marmot</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, there are two possible origins:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surrogate, substitute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antimony</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Compare Turkish <i>sürme</i>, Azerbaijani <i>sürmə</i>, Crimean Tatar <i>sürme</i>, Tatar <i>сөрмә</i> (sörmä), Persian سرمه (sorme), from سرب (sorb, “lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) mundane, ordinary, common</li></ol><p>From <i>су-</i> (su-) +&lrm; <i>свет</i> (svet, “world”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>mundane</i> (<&lt; Latin <i>mundus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain bin, corn bin, hopper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сусѣкъ</i> (susěkŭ) or borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫсѣкъ</i> (sǫsěkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫsěkъ</i>, related to <i>&#42;sěťi</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain bin, corn bin, hopper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сусѣкъ</i> (susěkŭ) or borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫсѣкъ</i> (sǫsěkŭ), related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sushi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>寿司</i> (すし, sushi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suslik, ground squirrel, spermophile, gopher</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сусолу</i> (susolu), that from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sysьlъ</i>, probably onomatopoeic of its calls; related to Bulgarian <i>съскам</i> (sǎskam, “I hiss”) and Old Church Slavonic <i>свистати</i> (svistati, “to whistle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>brewing</i>) wort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) suspension</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>suspēnsiō</i>. Further from Latin <i>pendō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jockstrap; suspensory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) joint, articulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>articular</li></ol><p><i>суста́в</i> (sustáv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Anglicanism,&#32;Lutheranism,&#32;Methodism,&#32;Presbyterianism,&#32;Roman Catholicism) cassock, soutane (item of clerical clothing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>soutane</i>, from Italian <i>sottana</i>, from Latin <i>subtana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimp, ponce, procurer, pander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>souteneur</i> (“pimp, supporter”) (whence also Bulgarian <i>сутеньор</i> (sutenjor), literally “поддерживатель”, also compare German <i>Zuhälter</i>), from <i>soutenir</i> (whence also Bulgarian <i>сутиен</i> (sutien)). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>sustineō</i> (whence also English <i>sustain</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>24 hours (of the clock), day, day and night, nychthemeron</li></ol><p>су- (su-) + *тъка (related to ты́кать (týkatʹ)), resulting in <i>стык дня и но́чи</i> (styk dnja i nóči, “union of day and night”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hustle and bustle, commotion</li></ol><p>Related to <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ, “to crush”), <i>толо́чься</i> (tolóčʹsja, “to crowd, to mill about”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bustly, fidgety</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hustle and bustle, commotion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daily (24 hours of the clock)</li></ol><p><i>су́точный</i> (sútočnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daily, diurnal, round-the-clock</li></ol><p><i>су́тки</i> (sútki) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sutra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>सूत्र</i> (sū́tra, “thread, yarn, string; rule”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunch (one's back)</li></ol><p><i>суту́лый</i> (sutúlyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunch over, to slouch, to stoop</li></ol><p><i>суту́лить</i> (sutúlitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hunched, stooped</li><li>(compound expressivizer of <i>соба́ка</i> (sobáka), <i>пси́на</i> (psína), etc.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫtulъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>essence, core, gist, main point</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѫть</i> (sǫtĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫtь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;derogatory) litigious person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soufflé</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>soufflé</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) to prompt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prompter (person in a theater)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>souffleur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) derivational suffix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>суха́рь</i> (suxárʹ): a small rusk, a small, dry biscuit, a crouton</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) dentil</li></ol><p><i>суха́рь</i> (suxárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake-basket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rusk</li></ol><p><i>суха́рь</i> (suxárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;countable) rusk (a piece or slice of bread, dried or toasted until hard and crisp)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;countable,&#32;by extension) biscuit, hardtack, cracker, crouton, crispbread (a piece of any dry bread-like food)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;figuratively) a dried-up man, a dry old stick (a very thin person)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;figuratively) a heartless, uncaring, emotionless person</li><li>(<i>mechanical engineering</i>) cotter (a metal wedge or pin used to hold parts of a machine together)</li></ol><p><i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dryly (in a dry manner)</li></ol><p><i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dryish</li></ol><p><i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) dry cargo ship, freighter (any type of cargo ship designed to carry dry goods&#59; for example, a general cargo ship, a bulk carrier, or a container ship)</li></ol><p>From <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>груз</i> (gruz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tendon (tough band of inelastic fibrous tissue that connects a muscle with its bony attachment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry</li><li>lean</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) land</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cool, cold</li><li>boring, dull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;suxъ</i>. Cognates include German <i>sohr</i>, Ancient Greek <i>αὖος</i> (aûos), Latvian <i>sauss</i>, Lithuanian <i>saũsas</i>, Persian خشک (xošk), Sanskrit <i>शुष्क</i> (śuṣka).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Сухой)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname, Sukhoy or Sukhoi</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) A Russian aircraft manufacturer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry law, Prohibition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry ice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój, “dry”): a little dry</li></ol><p><i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-онький</i> (-onʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lean, wiry, sinewy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) land forces, army</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>land, by land (as opposed to sea or air)</li></ol><p>From <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>путь</i> (putʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>сухопу́тний</i> (suxopútnyj), Belarusian <i>сухапу́тны</i> (suxapútny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) without the use of one arm, having a withered arm</li></ol><p><i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who can't control his/her arm(s) or hand(s) (<i>due to an injury or something else</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) snags; standing dead trees or bushes</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) dry period (time when a cow stops producing milk before calving)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anejaculation (the inability to ejaculate)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dryness (various senses)</li></ol><p>From <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dried fruit (small fruit that has been preserved by drying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lean, meagre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin, to twist, to throw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to jerk, to work</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sučiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>су́ка</i> (súka): bitch (<i>literal and abusive senses</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loppers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small bough, small branch, diminutive of <i>сук</i> (suk)</li><li>knot (whorl in wood left by a branch)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) trashy person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land, dry land</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;suša</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Russian <i>сухо́й</i> (suxój, “dry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sushi</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) kimbap</li></ol><p>Borrowed through English <i>sushi</i> from Japanese <i>寿司</i> (sushi, “sushi (sour rice)”), ultimately from archaic conjugation <i>酸し</i> (sushi, “sour, vinegared”) of modern adjective <i>酸い</i> (sui, “sour, vinegared”).</p><p>The sense of "kimbap" was due to a mistake that wrongly assumes that Korean kimbap looks like sushi.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sushi bar, sushi restaurant</li></ol><p>First attested in 1995. Borrowed from English <i>sushi bar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dryer (household appliance for drying clothing)</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ, “to dry”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumble dryer (electrical device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drying</li></ol><p><i>суш(и́ть)</i> (suš(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying house, hothouse</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dryer (someone who dries something - a person)</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry tree</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry</li><li>to roast, to toast, to parch</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to wear out, to exhaust, to torment</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to harden (<i>one's heart</i>), to make heartless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sušìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) to get ready to be arrested or imprisoned</li></ol><p>Referring to the idea of preparing some nonperishable food to eat on the march to katorga.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding</i>) to cut, to lose body mass, aiming to keep muscle but lose body fat</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying (process); desiccation</li><li>sooshka / sushka, ring-shaped cracknel, bagel</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding</i>) cutting, to lose body mass, aiming to keep muscle but lose body fat</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) dry leaves, dry branches</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong thirst, such as the next day after a drinking party</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Kazakhstan), any non-alcoholic drink</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) dry wine</li></ol><p>From <i>су́ша</i> (súša) +&lrm; <i>-няк</i> (-njak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry spell, drought; dryness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, dried (without water or moisture)</li></ol><p><i>суши́ть</i> (sušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) entity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>considerably, substantially</li></ol><p><i>суще́ственный</i> (suščéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>essential (of high importance)</li><li>substantial (large in size, quantity, or value)</li></ol><p><i>существо́</i> (suščestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noun, substantive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>substantive</li><li>(rare,&#32;proscribed) substantival</li></ol><p>From <i>существова́ть</i> (suščestvovátʹ, “to exist, to be”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creature, being</li><li>essence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сѫщьство</i> (sǫštĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>су́щий</i> (súščij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>existence, being</li><li>subsistence</li></ol><p><i>существова́ть</i> (suščestvovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exist, to be, to live</li></ol><p><i>существо́</i> (suščestvó, “essence”) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>existing (that exists)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mere</li><li>sheer, rank</li><li>arrant (utter)</li><li>very</li><li>regular</li><li>great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>сꙑ</i> (sy), present active participle of <i>бꙑти</i> (byti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sy</i>, present active participle of <i>&#42;byti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>essential</li></ol><p><i>су́щность</i> (súščnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entity</li><li>essence</li><li>substance</li></ol><p>From (<i>archaic</i>) <i>су́щный</i> (súščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Suez</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) the Suez Canal</li></ol><p><i>Суэ́ц</i> (Suéc) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fabricated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manufacture, to produce</li><li>to fabricate, to forge</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>фабрикова́ть</i> (fabrikovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astrophysics</i>) event horizon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;mathematics</i>) spherical</li></ol><p><i>сфе́ра</i> (sféra) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spheroid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) sphericon</li><li>(<i>paleontology</i>) spherocone</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>sphericon</i> or <i>spherocone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sphinx</li><li>Sphynx cat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) sphincter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, to shape, to mould, to make up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>формирова́ть</i> (formirovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to formulate, to phrase, to word, to lay down (rules, demands, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>формули́ровать</i> (formulírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to photograph</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>фотографи́ровать</i> (fotografírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be photographed, to have one's photo/picture taken</li></ol><p><i>сфотографи́ровать</i> (sfotografírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigillography</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sphragistik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat</li></ol><p>From <i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ха́вать</i> (xávatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, to grab, to seize, to snap, to snatch, to catch, to catch hold of</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, to hold</li><li>to grapple with, to come to blows with, to skirmish with</li><li><i>(paint or glue)</i> to grab, to grip, <i>(cement)</i> to set, to stiffen</li></ol><p><i>схвати́ть</i> (sxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirmish, fight, combat, encounter</li><li>scuffle, squabble</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) contraction (a strong and often painful shortening of the uterine muscles prior to or during childbirth)</li></ol><p><i>схвати́ть</i> (sxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, to grab, to seize, to snap, to snatch, to catch, to catch hold of</li></ol><p><i>схвати́ть</i> (sxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scheme, schema, arrangement, layout, outline, framework (combination of components and the relationships between them, or a visual or verbal explanation of this)</li><li>diagram, chart, map, schematic, blueprint (generalised image of a structure or process)</li><li>(electronic) circuit, circuitry</li><li>scheme, plan, pattern, framework, model, regimen (order or method of an action, flow of a process)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) schism</li><li>(<i>music</i>) schisma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>σχίσμα</i> (schísma), from Ancient Greek <i>σχίσμα</i> (skhísma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sly/cunning/crafty, to try to fool</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>хитри́ть</i> (xitrítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to procure</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to get (something bad)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>хлопота́ть</i> (xlopotátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to subside, to ebb (<i>of a mass of water, a crowd, fear, tension, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>хлы́нуть</i> (xlýnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering</li><li>descent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to come down, to descend, to get off</li><li>to come off, to disappear</li><li>to pass by</li><li>to pass for, to be taken for</li><li>to drift</li><li>to go and get, to fetch [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to go and come back</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go crazy, to lose one's mind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet, to come together</li><li>to converge, to coalesce</li><li>to become friends; to become close or sexually intimate</li><li>to match, to tally, to harmonize, to agree</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) public meeting, public assembly (<i>of villagers, workers, etc.</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) gathering, meeting, get-together</li></ol><p>From <i>сходи́ться</i> (sxodítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>similar</li><li>analogous</li><li>kindred</li><li>cognate</li><li>saleable</li><li>even</li><li>near</li></ol><p><i>сход(и́ться)</i> (sxod(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangplank, gangboard</li></ol><p><i>сходи́ть</i> (sxodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity</li><li>resemblance</li><li>likeness, semblance</li><li>affinity, kinship</li><li>analogy</li><li>sameness</li><li>similitude</li><li>conformity</li><li>congeniality</li></ol><p><i>сходи́ть(ся)</i> (sxodítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>с хо́ду</i> (s xódu)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>similarity, congeniality</li></ol><p><i>схо́жий</i> (sxóžij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>similar</li></ol><p><i>сход</i> (sxod) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scholastic (a member of the medieval philosophical school of scholasticism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury, to inter</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;colloquial) to hide, to conceal, to lay away</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>хорони́ть</i> (xoronítʹ, “to buy, to hide”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to piss, to urinate</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be afraid, to fear, to chicken out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьcati</i> (“to piss”) &lt; <i>&#42;sьkati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sikʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stage, scene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Szene</i>, from Latin <i>scaena</i>. Further from Ancient Greek <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scenario (outline or model of an expected or supposed sequence of events)</li><li>script (text of the dialogue and action for a drama)</li><li>(video games,&#32;flight simulators) scenery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screenwriter, scenario / script writer</li></ol><p>Likely from German <i>Szenarist</i>. Analyzable as <i>сцена́рий</i> (scenárij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) script (<i>of a movie</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) scenario, plan</li></ol><p><i>сцена́рий</i> (scenárij) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stage, scenic, theatrical</li></ol><p><i>сце́на</i> (scéna) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сце́на</i> (scéna)</li><li>sketch</li></ol><p><i>сце́на</i> (scéna) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) scenographic (related to scenography)</li></ol><p>From <i>сценогра́фия</i> (scenográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scenography (art of designing and&#47;or executing the sets for a show, especially theatrical)</li><li>scenography (set of material elements that make up a setting or that are displayed on a stage)</li><li>scenography (art of painting or drawing according to the rules of perspective)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Szenographie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to couple</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) to be coupled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grapple <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сцепи́ть</i> (scepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clutch (a device to interrupt power transmission)</li><li>adherence (union of two objects)</li></ol><p><i>сцепи́ть</i> (scepítʹ, “to couple”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to couple</li></ol><p><i>сцепи́ть</i> (scepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) to be coupled</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grapple <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>сцепля́ть</i> (scepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any beetle in the genus <i>Scymnus</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Scymnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skink</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>scincus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>сейча́с</i> (sejčás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lucky dog, lucky man</li></ol><p>From <i>счастли́вый</i> (sčastlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>An informal farewell.</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>happily (in a happy manner)</li></ol><p><i>счастли́вый</i> (sčastlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bon voyage!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>счастли́вец</i> (sčastlívec) lucky man</li></ol><p><i>счастли́вец</i> (sčastlívec) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy</li><li>lucky</li></ol><p>From <i>сча́стье</i> (sčástʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>щасли́вий</i> (ščaslývyj), Polish <i>szczęśliwy</i> and Czech <i>šťastlivý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lucky ticket (numerological superstition related to the numbers of passenger tickets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, good luck</li><li>luck, fortune</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>съчастиѥ</i> (sŭčastije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъčęstьje</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Счастье)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shchastia (a city, the administrative center of Shchastia Raion, Luhansk Oblast, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compute, to count, to reckon</li><li>to consider, to think <i>(to be)</i>, to believe</li><li>to think <i>(that)</i>, to believe <i>(that)</i>, to consider (that)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accountant, accounts clerk, ledger clerk</li></ol><p>From <i>счёт</i> (sčot) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) accounting, book-keeping, countinghouse</li></ol><p><i>счетово́д</i> (sčetovód) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) basically, practically, essentially (used to suggest that someone assume something is true or as good as true)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counting rhyme (short rhyme used among children to count through a group and pick someone)</li></ol><p><i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>счита́лка</i> (sčitálka); counting rhyme (short rhyme used among children to count through a group and pick someone)</li></ol><p><i>счита́лка</i> (sčitálka) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very small in number, very few</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calculate</li><li>to count</li><li>to think, to opine, to consider</li><li>to read off, to sense, to compare, to check</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-чита́ть</i> (-čitátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>count</i>, <i>reckon</i>; note the semantic evolution to Mongolian <i>санах</i> (sanax).</p><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to footle around, dawdle, lallygag</li></ol><p>Literally, “to count the jackdaws”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle accounts (with), to consider, to take into consideration, to reckon (with)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to be considered / reputed (someone), to be, to pass (for)</li></ol><p><i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read off, to sense, to compare, to check</li></ol><p><i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>count (the result of a tally that reveals the number of items in a set), counting, reckoning, calculation</li><li>invoice, bill</li><li>account, subject</li><li>score <i>(in a game)</i></li><li>account (an authorization to use a service)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>счита́ть</i> (sčitátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invoice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account book (a book in which accounts are kept)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) count form (in some Slavic languages, such as Bulgarian and Russian, a distinct plural form taken by certain nouns when explicitly modified by a quantity)</li></ol><p>From <i>счётная</i> (sčótnaja, “counting”) +&lrm; <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma, “form”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) counting, account, bookkeeping</li><li>calculating, counting, computing</li><li>countable</li></ol><p><i>счёт</i> (sčot) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter, meter</li><li>calculator, computer (person)</li></ol><p>From <i>счёт</i> (sčot) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abacus (calculating frame)</li><li>calculations, reckoning</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) score</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock down</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to get, to bag, to scrounge, to cadge</li></ol><p><i>сшиби́ть</i> (sšibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock down</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to get, to bag, to scrounge, to cadge</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stapler</li></ol><p>From <i>сшива́ть</i> (sšivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew <i>(transitive)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to order (e.g., a dress)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>шить</i> (šitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) clipping of <i>сейча́с</i> (sejčás)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>с</i> (s).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to run away, to make off, to flee</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to escape</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to leave, to depart, to go (away)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ебать</i> (jebatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to eat up</li></ol><p><i>съе́д-</i> (sʺjéd-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edibility, edibleness, eatability</li></ol><p>From <i>съедо́бный</i> (sʺjedóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>edible, comestible (that may be eaten without harm; suitable for consumption)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>descent</li><li>exit, ramp</li><li>congress, convention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go somewhere and come back, to fetch something/to pick up someone (by a vehicle) [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘somewhere’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit <i>(on)</i>, to sock <i>(in)</i>, to give a smack <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to slide down</li><li>to move out</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet <i>(on one's way)</i></li><li>to assemble, to come together, to gather</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja, “(<i>reflexive</i>)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) police board</li><li>detention cell, police brig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, to eat up</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, to slide down</li><li>to move (from a place of residence)</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet <i>(on one's way)</i></li><li>to assemble, to come together, to gather</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сыгра́ть</i> (sygrátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say (something) sarcastically</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>язви́ть</i> (jazvítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off, alternative form of <i>съёбываться</i> (sʺjóbyvatʹsja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrivel, to shrink</li><li>to curl up, to draw oneself together</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cringe</li></ol><p><i>съёжиться</i> (sʺjóžitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrivel, to shrink</li><li>to curl up, to draw oneself together</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to cringe</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ёжиться</i> (jóžitʹsja), <i>съёжить</i> (sʺjóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal, extraction</li><li>rental</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pickup (flirtation, courting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal</li><li>photo or video shoot, instance of filming</li><li>rental</li><li>(geography,&#32;geodesy) survey</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>снять</i> (snjatʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>съём-</i> (sʺjom-), derived from the obsolete present tense stem <i>съем-</i> (sʺjem-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>removable, detachable</li></ol><p><i>съём(ка)</i> (sʺjóm(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) shooting, filming</li><li>(<i>geodesy</i>) survey</li></ol><p><i>съёмка</i> (sʺjómka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tenant</li><li>photographer</li><li>(geography,&#32;geodesy) surveyor</li></ol><p><i>съём(ка)</i> (sʺjóm(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>съезд</i> (sʺjezd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whey</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) serum (blood serum)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;syrovatъka</i>, reinterpreted by <i>вороти́ть</i> (vorotítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play</li><li>to act, to perform</li><li>to gamble</li><li>(<i>of a storm</i>) to rage</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) to sparkle</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) to kick the bucket (to die)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form a good team, to play well together</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>игра́ть</i> (igrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>since</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>again</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic, to imitate</li><li>to simulate</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>имити́ровать</i> (imitírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son</li><li>child</li><li><i>(poetic plural):</i> sons</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сꙑнъ</i> (synŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;synъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sū́ˀnus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suHnús</i>.</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;suHnús</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an orphan boy adopted by a military unit; an orphan child camp follower; literally, “son of a regiment”</li></ol><p>Perhaps ultimately from Donizetti's <i>La fille du régiment</i> (1840). Popularized by Valentin Kataev's eponymous novella (1945).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;only in the negative) a see-through person</li></ol><p>Literally, “a glazier's son”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сын</i> (syn): son, sonny</li></ol><p><i>сын</i> (syn) +&lrm; <i>-и́шка</i> (-íška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) son’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) son; filial, son's</li></ol><p><i>сын</i> (syn) +&lrm; <i>-о́вний</i> (-óvnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filial piety</li></ol><p>From <i>сыновний</i> (synovnij, “filial”) +&lrm; <i>благочестие</i> (blagočestije, “piety”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>сын</i> (syn): son, sonny, junior</li></ol><p><i>сын</i> (syn) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сын</i> (syn): son, sonny</li><li>diminutive of <i>сыно́к</i> (synók): son, sonny</li></ol><p><i>сыно́к</i> (synók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сын</i> (syn, “son”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сын</i> (syn).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to pour (<i>of friable/solid objects, such as sand; not liquids</i>)</li><li>to squander</li><li>(regional,&#32;nonstandard) to pour (<i>either liquids or solids</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сꙑпати</i> (sypati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sỳpati</i> (“to pour, strew”). Cognate to Latin <i>supō</i>, Latin <i>dissipō</i> (whence Russian <i>диссипа́ция</i> (dissipácija), English <i>dissipate</i>, English <i>dissipation</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) rub salt in the wound, to add insult to injury, to rub it in (to further a loss with mockery or indignity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to pour, to run out</li><li>to rain down, to pour forth</li><li>to fall thick and fast</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(of cloth)</i> to fray out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flunk, to fail</li></ol><p><i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loose, friable, free-flowing</li></ol><p><i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ, “to pour (of friable/solid objects)”) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rash, eruption</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;syrъ</i> (“cheese”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sū́ˀras</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;súHros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;ironic) hubbub, fuss, commotion</li></ol><p>Literally, “a damp pine forest”. From <i>сыр</i> (syr, “damp”) +&lrm; <i>бор</i> (bor, “pine forest”); see usage note below.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become damp, to become wet</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to get unhealthily fat</li></ol><p><i>сыро́й</i> (syrój) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semi-processed food or material</li></ol><p><i>сыро́й</i> (syrój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syrnik, a fried quark pancake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>си́рник</i> (sýrnyk). By surface analysis, <i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), though <i>сыр</i> (syr) is not usually used to refer to quark in Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for cheese</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheese</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesemaker</li></ol><p>Agent noun of <i>вари́ть сыр</i> (varítʹ syr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesemaking</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>варе́ние</i> (varénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheesemaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese dairy</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вари́ть</i> (varítʹ, “to cook, to boil”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheesemaker</li></ol><p><i>сырова́р</i> (syrovár) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesemaker</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́л(ать)</i> (dél(atʹ))</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesemaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheesemaking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheesery</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheesemaker</li></ol><p><i>сыроде́л</i> (syrodél) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw food eater</li></ol><p><i>сырой</i> (syroj, “raw”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw food diet</li></ol><p><i>сырой</i> (syroj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ние</i> (-nije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any mushroom of the genus <i>Russula</i>.</li></ol><p><i>сыро́й</i> (syrój, “raw”) +&lrm; <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>damp, moist</li><li>(<i>weather</i>) rainy, drizzly, sleety</li><li>raw, uncooked, unbaked</li><li>crude, raw (unprocessed)</li><li>green, unripe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;syrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>сыр</i> (syr, “cheese”)</li><li>cheese curd</li><li>ellipsis of <i>глазированный сырок</i> (glazirovannyj syrok): curd snack</li><li>peled, northern whitefish</li></ol><p><i>сыр</i> (syr) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p><p>Borrowed from Khanty (compare Northern Khanty <i>сурәх</i> (surəh)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dampness; moisture</li></ol><p><i>сыро́й</i> (syrój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syrt (kind of elevated flatland in Russia and Central Asia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kyrgyz <i>сырт</i> (sırt), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;sïrt</i> (“highland; ridge; backbone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semi-processed</li></ol><p><i>сыре́ц</i> (syréc) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) raw material</li></ol><p><i>сырьё</i> (syrʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw material, staple, stock (material in its unprocessed, natural state)</li></ol><p><i>сыро́й</i> (syrój) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminal search and investigation; espial; tracing</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>иск</i> (isk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to find</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>иска́ть</i> (iskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to find</li></ol><p><i>сыска́ть</i> (syskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) detective</li></ol><p><i>сыск</i> (sysk) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hearty, filling (<i>meal, food</i>)</li><li>having plenty of food</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) prosperous</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) lucrative</li></ol><p><i>сы́т(ый)</i> (sýt(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satiety, fullness</li></ol><p><i>сы́тый</i> (sýtyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>satiated</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) satisfied, full, sated</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sytъ</i> (“satiated, full”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;s(e)h₂tos</i>, from <i>&#42;seh₂-</i> (“to satiate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an owl of genus <i>Athene</i> or <i>Aegolius</i></li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) owl (any bird of the order Strigiformes)</li><li>recluse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;syťь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be afraid (to do something), to be too scared (to do something)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be a recluse</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to spend all one's time on the internet, not talking to people in real life</li></ol><p><i>сыч</i> (syč, “owl, recluse”) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) Sichuanese person</li></ol><p>From <i>Сычуа́нь</i> (Syčuánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Sichuan; Sichuanese</li></ol><p>From <i>Сычуа́нь</i> (Syčuánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;food) abomasum, maw, rennet-bag, the fourth compartment of the stomach of a ruminant</li></ol><p>Related to synonymous Ukrainian <i>сичу́г</i> (syčúh), Belarusian <i>сычу́г</i> (syčúh).</p><p>Usually considered a Turkic borrowing and compared to Kipchak <i>suzug</i> (“intestines”) (Codex Cumanicus), Chagatai (sučuk, “stuffed intestines”), Ottoman Turkish صوجوق (sucuk, “sausage”). On the other hand, Trubačev considers <i>сычуг</i> (syčug) a native formation from <i>сыти́ть</i> (sytítʹ, “to sweeten”), typologically comparing German <i>Lab</i> (“rennet”), <i>Labmagen</i> (“rennet-bag”) : <i>laben</i> (“to refresh with, to enliven by exposing to, to let feast”), Ossetian <i>а́хсӕн</i> (áxsæn, “rennet-bag; ferment (starter) in making cheese”) : <i>а́хсын</i> (áxsyn, “to curdle”), and noting the ability of the abomasum to secrete an enzyme used in cheese-making.</p><p>Compare also Old Armenian <i>շիճուկ</i> (šičuk, “whey”) and dialectal Turkish <i>cücük, cıcuk, cucuk, çuçuk</i> (“ferment (starter) in making curds and cheese”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) abomasum, maw, rennet-bag, the fourth compartment of the stomach of a ruminant</li></ol><p><i>сычу́г</i> (syčúg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detective, investigator</li><li>sleuth, bloodhound, snoop; plain-clothes policeman</li></ol><p><i>сыска́ть</i> (syskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sierra Leonean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to spare</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>эконо́мить</i> (ekonómitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandwich</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sandwich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сенсе́й</i> (sɛnsɛ́j)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seppuku, harakiri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>切腹</i> (せっぷく, seppuku).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sir (<i>address to a man in an English context, e.g. in translations from English</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(to) here, hither</li><li>this way</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѭда</i> (sjǫda), <i>сѫда</i> (sǫda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dialectal) alternative form of <i>сюда́</i> (sjudá); (to) here, hither</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>сюдо́й</i> (sjudój)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subject</li><li>(<i>narratology</i>) plot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>sujet</i>, from Latin <i>subiectum</i>/<i>subjectum</i>. Doublet of <i>субъе́кт</i> (subʺjékt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) story</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) scene</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) plot</li><li>having a strong plot</li></ol><p><i>сюже́т</i> (sjužét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) suite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>suite</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>sequor</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to follow”). Doublet of <i>сви́та</i> (svíta). Via Latin also cognate with <i>секу́нда</i> (sekúnda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ху́нну</i> (xúnnu) or <i>хунну́</i> (xunnú)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>匈奴</i> (Xiōngnú).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a surreal situation; absurdity</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>сюрреали́зм</i> (sjurrealízm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shuriken</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>手裏剣</i> (しゅりけん, shuriken).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surprise (something unexpected)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>surprise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surrealism</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a surreal situation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>surréalisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frock coat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>surtout</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>сюрту́к</i> (sjurtúk): (small) frock coat</li></ol><p>From <i>сюрту́к</i> (sjurtúk) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) surjective</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) surjection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>себя́</i> (sebjá)</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>себя́</i> (sebjá) and probably not a direct descendant of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sę</i> (whence <i>-ся</i> (-sja)) or Old Church Slavonic <i>сѧ</i> (sę).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a contemptible person (<i>as a petty swindler, teenage thief, young prostitute, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to reach</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sęgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antenna (feeler organ on the head of an insect)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry out (of a river or water basin)</li><li>to run out, to be depleted</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sęknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сѣмо</i> (sěmo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sěmo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shamisen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>三味線</i> (shamisen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xiangqi, Chinese chess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 象棋 (<i>xiàngqí</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shogi, Japanese chess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>将棋</i> (しょうぎ, shōgi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shogun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>将軍</i> (shōgun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) shogunate</li></ol><p><i>сёгун</i> (sjógun) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salmon (the fish and the seafood)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; compare Bulgarian <i>сьо́мга</i> (sjómga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slurp (drink or eat (liquid food) noisily)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrbati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srebʰ-</i>. Cognate with English <i>slurp</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slurp (drink or eat (liquid food) noisily)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>water sports</i>) surfing</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) surfing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>surfing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́верный</i> (sévernyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́вер</i> (séver).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>седло́</i> (sedló).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́мя</i> (sémja).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́ни</i> (séni).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́но</i> (séno).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сень</i> (senʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́ра</i> (séra).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ).</li></ol><p><i>с-</i> (s-) +&lrm; <i>ѣсть</i> (jěstʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>се́ть</i> (sétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сигкли́т</i> (sigklít).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>си́мвол</i> (símvol).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сино́д</i> (sinód).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>сирийский</i> (sirijskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ton</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>то́нна</i> (tónna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč, “comrade”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего го́да</i> (tekúščevo góda): of the current year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>то́ есть</i> (tó jestʹ): i.e., that is</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>та́к как</i> (ták kak): since, inasmuch as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего ме́сяца</i> (tekúščevo mésjaca): of the current month, inst.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>так называ́емый</i> (tak nazyvájemyj, “so-called”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>таки́м о́бразом</i> (takím óbrazom): thus, in this way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>таки́м о́бразом</i> (takím óbrazom): thus, in this way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего го́да</i> (tekúščevo góda): of the current year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>то́ есть</i> (tó jestʹ): i.e., that is</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>та́к как</i> (ták kak): since, inasmuch as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего ме́сяца</i> (tekúščevo mésjaca): of the current month, inst.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>так называ́емый</i> (tak nazyvájemyj, “so-called”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shopping centre</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего го́да</i> (tekúščevo góda): of the current year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>initialism of <i>теку́щего ме́сяца</i> (tekúščevo mésjaca): of the current month, inst.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;often associated with Ukrainians) alternative form of <i>да</i> (da)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tobacco</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tabac</i>, from Spanish <i>tabaco</i>, from Arabic طَبَاق (ṭabāq) or Taíno. See <i>tobacco</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snuffbox</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Italian <i>tabacchiera</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>табак</i> (tabak).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>таба́к</i> (tabák, “tobacco”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tabac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tobacco</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rotten</li></ol><p><i>таба́к</i> (tabák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>таба́к</i> (tabák, “tobacco”)</li></ol><p><i>таба́к</i> (tabák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>timeboard</li><li>time sheet, time card</li><li>school progress record</li><li>table (in the expression <i>та́бель о ра́нгах</i> (tábelʹ o rángax, “table of ranks”))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tabella</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timekeeper</li></ol><p><i>та́бель</i> (tábelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pill, tablet (small object for swallowing)</li><li>coin cell</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table (grid of data)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>tablica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) periodic table of the elements</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plate, sign</li></ol><p><i>табли́ца</i> (tablíca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indicator board, panel, tableau</li><li>scoreboard (on a stadium)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) face, mug (<i>especially as a target for a punch or hit</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tableau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tabloid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gypsy band</li><li>Gypsy encampment/campsite/camp</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) camp</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) crowd, throng, gang</li></ol><p>Borrowing from a Turkic language; compare Ottoman Turkish طابور (tabur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taboo</li></ol><p>From Tongan <i>tapu</i> (“prohibited, sacred”), from Proto-Polynesian <i>&#42;tapu</i>, from Proto-Oceanic <i>&#42;tabu</i>, from Proto-Eastern Malayo-Polynesian <i>&#42;tambu</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tabulation (the act or process of tabulating)</li><li>tab (a space character that extends to the next column)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd (of horses)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) crowd, herd (of people)</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing: compare Tatar <i>табын</i> (tabın), Kazakh <i>табын</i> (tabyn), Crimean Tatar <i>tabın</i>, dialectal Azerbaijani <i>tabın</i>. Akin to Georgian <i>თაბუნი</i> (tabuni), Armenian <i>թաբուն</i> (tʻabun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stool (a seat)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from French <i>tabouret</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stool (a seat)</li></ol><p><i>табуре́т</i> (taburét) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taw, the last letter of the Hebrew alphabet.</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>תָּו</i> (tav).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;foreign context</i>) tavern, bar, pub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>taverne</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>trabs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(construction,&#32;mechanical engineering) T-beam</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a person of the Tauri people of ancient Crimea</li><li>(furry fandom,&#32;neologism) taur (an anthropomorphic character, often a furry, with a human-like upper body and four-legged lower body, similar to a centaur)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Ancient Greek <i>ταῦ</i> (taû, “the letter tau”), referring to the letter's shape.</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ταῦρος</i> (Taûros).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>taur</i>, with transcription matching that of <i>кента́вр</i> (kentávr).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тавр)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taurus (mountain range in Anatolia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ταῦρος</i> (Taûros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brand (burned mark)</li><li>branding iron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tautology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tagalog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trivet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>таган</i> (tağan), cognate with Crimean Tatar <i>tуgаn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tajik (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tajik</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tajik (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thai (person from Thailand or of Thai origin)</li></ol><p>Thai <i>ไทย</i> (tai) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basin (a wide, relatively shallow vessel, e.g. for washing or cooking)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pelvis</li><li>member of the Taz ethnicity</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a Lada automobile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тазъ</i> (tazŭ), a Turkic borrowing. Compare Crimean Tatar <i>tas</i>, Turkish <i>tas</i>, Tatar <i>тас</i> (tas), etc. Ultimately from Persian تاس (tâs).</p><p>From Chinese <i>韃子</i>&nbsp;/ <i>鞑子</i> (dázi), originally meaning any foreign ethnic group in Northern China, from Chinese <i>韃靼</i>&nbsp;/ <i>鞑靼</i> (dádá), from Old Turkic <i>𐱃𐱃𐰺</i> (t¹t¹r¹).</p><p>Humorous alteration of <i>ВАЗ</i> (VAZ), the main factory manufacturing Lada-brand automobiles, influenced by the noun <i>таз</i> (taz, “basin”) with the implied sense of “low-tech metal item” or “rustbucket”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>таз</i> (taz): (small) basin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Thailand, Thai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mysteriously, enigmatically</li><li>secretively</li></ol><p><i>таи́нственный</i> (taínstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mystery, mysteriousness</li></ol><p><i>таи́нственный</i> (taínstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mysterious (of unknown origin)</li><li>enigmatic</li><li>(<i>of a person's expression or manner</i>) secretive (having an enigmatic or mysterious quality)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajьnьstvenъ</i>. Morphologically, from <i>та́инство</i> (táinstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) sacrament; ordinance</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;literary</i>) mystery; secret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>таиньство</i> (tainĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajьnьstvo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, to conceal; to harbour (a feeling)</li><li>to hold back; to keep secret</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajìti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teh₂y-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide</li><li>to keep a low profile</li><li>to conceal/hide one's feelings <i>(from)</i></li><li>to be hidden/concealed</li></ol><p><i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tahitian</li></ol><p><i>Таи́ти</i> (Taíti) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Taiwanese person (male)</li></ol><p><i>Тайва́нь</i> (Tajvánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Taiwanese</li></ol><p>From <i>Тайва́нь</i> (Tajvánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taiga</li><li>(<i>Siberia</i>) an impassable place in a forest</li><li>(<i>Altay</i>) mountain, mountains</li></ol><p>Attested since 1790s, probably from Khakas <i>тайға</i> (tayğa, “taiga; mountain”) or a related (extinct?) Turkic language, further origin unknown. Meaning 'forest', likely developed in Turkic from the original 'mountain' (cf. Tuvan <i>тайга</i> (tayga, “mountain”)), first attested in 1735 in some "Chulym Tatar" language</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тайга)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tayga (a town in Kemerovo Oblast, Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>тайга́</i> (tajgá, “taiga”), due to the surrounding taiga landscape.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the sly, secretly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taikonaut (Chinese astronaut)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) half time, half, round</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>time</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) time-out</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) time-out, a temporary suspension of some activity, break</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>time-out</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timeslot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>time slot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taimen (any fish of the genus <i>Hucho</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>таймень</i> (tajmenʹ), from Old Novgorodian <i>таимень</i> (taimenĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>timer</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>minutnik</i> *минутник, Czech <i>časovač</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) timing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>timing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) timing</li></ol><p><i>та́йминг</i> (tájming) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mystery (something secret or unexplainable)</li><li>secret</li><li>secrecy</li><li>obscurity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>таина</i> (taina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajьna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiding place, cache, secret compartment</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly (in secret)</li></ol><p><i>та́йный</i> (tájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret ballot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cryptography, steganography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret</li><li>stealthy; surreptitious</li><li>vague</li><li>privy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>таинꙑи</i> (tainyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Thai</li></ol><p>Thai <i>ไทย</i> (tai) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muay Thai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typhoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>typhoon</i>, probably ultimately of Sinitic origin, Mandarin <i>大风</i> (dàfēng, “big wind”), Cantonese <i>大風</i>&nbsp;/ <i>大风</i> (daai<sup>6</sup> fung<sup>1</sup>, “big wind”), via Arabic طوفان (ṭūfān), Hindi <i>तूफ़ान</i> (tūfān), and Persian توفان (tufân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taiji, tai chi</li></ol><p>From Mandarin 太極／太极 (<i>tàijí</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taijiquan, tai chi chuan</li></ol><p>From Mandarin 太極拳／太极拳 (<i>tàijíquán</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a verb or short-form adjective</i>) like that, like this (in that&#47;this way, in that&#47;this manner) (<i>translated as "that" or "this" with some verbs</i>)</li><li>Used to indicate a high degree of something.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>before a short-form adjective or adverb</i>) so; <i>in the negative:</i> that (to a high degree)</li><li>(<i>before a verb</i>) so much; <i>in the negative:</i> that much (to a high degree) (<i>translated as "so" or (in the negative) "that" with some verbs that are translated with adjectives in English</i>)</li></ol><li>(before a content clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što)) Used to indicate a sufficient degree of something (such that the following clause is true).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>before a short-form adjective or adverb</i>) so (to a sufficient degree) (<i>including in the negative</i>)</li><li>(<i>before a verb</i>) so much (to a sufficient degree) (<i>including in the negative</i>) (<i>translated as "so" with some verbs that are translated with adjectives in English</i>)</li></ol><li>Used to refer to a previously mentioned or demonstrated degree of something.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>before a short-form adjective or adverb</i>) that (to that degree, to the previously mentioned or demonstrated degree) (<i>including in the negative; often followed by <i>уж и</i> (už i) in the negative</i>)</li><li>(<i>before a verb</i>) that much (to that degree, to the aforementioned degree) (<i>including in the negative; often followed by <i>уж и</i> (už i) in the negative</i>) (<i>translated as "that" with some verbs that are translated with adjectives in English</i>)</li></ol><li>(before a short-form adjective or adverb and optionally a clause beginning with <i>как</i> (kak); often with <i>же</i> (že) to emphasise equality) as (to the same or a similar degree)</li><li>(with a verb or short-form adjective and immediately before a clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što) or <i>что́бы</i> (štóby)) such, in such a way, so</li><li>(with a verb or short-form adjective and immediately before a clause beginning with <i>как</i> (kak); often with <i>же</i> (že) to emphasise equality) like, the same, (in) the same way, similar to, as</li><li>(in the following construction with <i>как</i> (kak) and <i>и</i> (i)) and, as well as</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;with a verb) (just) like that (suddenly&#47;unexpectedly, without action, without consideration or without consequences)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;often followed by <i>себе́</i> (sebé)) not great, not very well, meh, mediocre(ly), middling(ly), so-so</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) right, okay, yeah (while listening, indicates that one is awaiting the continuation of what is being said)</li><li>(<i>introductory</i>) so (indicates a following explanation or example&#59; can also replace "for example")</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nothing (special); no one (special); nowhere (special); no reason; etc. (used as a dismissive or unwilling response)</li><li>(<i>in the following construction</i>) Used to express that something is what should be or happen.</li><li>yes, indeed</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;when repeated) well, well(, well)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;when repeated) now, now; okay, okay; alright, alright</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;with numbers) around, about (used to express approximation)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so (therefore, thus)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) but (often with a sense of obviousness)</li><li>Used similarly to <i>то</i> (to) in the following constructions (see <i>то</i> (to))&#59; no translation.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тако</i> (tako), <i>такъ</i> (takŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tako</i> (adverb), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;takъ</i> (adjective). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>так</i> (tak), <i>тако</i> (tako); Belarusian <i>так</i> (tak); Bulgarian <i>така</i> (taka); Serbo-Croatian <i>тако</i>, <i>так</i>; Slovene <i>tako</i>, <i>tàk</i>; Czech and Polish <i>tak</i>; Yiddish <i>טאַקע</i> (take); Upper and Lower Sorbian <i>tak</i>; Lithuanian <i>tóks</i>, <i>tokià</i>; and Latin <i>tālis</i>. Akin to Old Church Slavonic pronoun <i>тъ</i> (tŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>right, isn't it (used at the end of an affirmative sentence, turning what was said into a question, asking for confirmation)</li><li>Used other than idiomatically&#58; see <i>так</i> (tak) and <i>ведь</i> (vedʹ)&#59; usually means colloquial "but" (with a sense of obviousness).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) steady as you go, keep her so (instruction to a helmsman to maintain the current course of a vessel)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) keep it up, way to go (expression of approval of the results of an action)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise, just as; the same way as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never (did)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one way or another, somehow or other, somehow, anyhow (in any way, in any manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as, since, because, inasmuch as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>so-called (<i>with or without any connotation</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;regional,&#32;Ukraine) also</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>так са́мо</i> (tak sámo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not great, not very well, meh, mediocre(ly), middling(ly), so-so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so to speak (in a manner of speaking)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal&#32;or&#32;military</i>) yessir; affirmative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so</li><li>so that</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) yes, sir</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) yes, ma'am</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>так</i> (tak) +&lrm; <i>-с</i> (-s), which is an abbreviated form of <i>су́дарь</i> (súdarʹ)/<i>суда́рыня</i> (sudárynja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) taka (currency of Bangladesh)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bengali <i>টাকা</i> (ṭaka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) takbir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic تَكْبِير (takbīr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) rigging</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>takelage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taqueria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>taquería</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, too, as well, likewise</li><li>(<i>in negative constructions</i>) either, neither, nor</li></ol><p>From <i>так</i> (tak) +&lrm; <i>же</i> (že).</p><p>Cognates include Czech <i>takže</i>, Kashubian <i>także</i>, Polish <i>także</i>, Silesian <i>także</i>, Slovak <i>takže</i> and Serbo-Croatian <i>такође</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) still, after all, in spite of (everything)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thus, therefore, in this way</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taco</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>taco</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>like that, like this (<i>sometimes translated as simply "that"/"those"" or "this"/"these"</i>)</li><li>(immediately before a clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što)) such (in such a way that the following clause is true)</li><li>(immediately before a clause beginning with <i>како́в</i> (kakóv), <i>како́й</i> (kakój) or <i>как</i> (kak)) as, the way (in the way described in the following clause)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li><i>More formal </i>synonym of <i>тако́й</i> (takój)<i> in the main determiner and pronoun senses. Mainly used in certain collocations.</i></li></ol><p><i>так</i> (tak) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>something like that, something like this, such a thing, that kind/sort of thing, stuff like that (<i>often translated as simply "that" or "this", meaning not only the specific thing in question but also any instance of that thing resembling it</i>)</li><li>(after <i>что</i> (što)) used with a pronoun, name or noun to specify the meaning of <i>что</i> (što) as "what's the nature&#47;essence of" and not just a request to identify something&#59; usually not translated</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;in the genitive case) used to indicate something perceived as deserving attention or special&#47;unusual (even if not perceived as such by the subject)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>such (a), such, that/this kind/sort/type of, those/these kinds/sorts/types of, like that/this/those/these; <i>in the negative also:</i> no such (<i>often translated as simply "that" or "this", meaning not only the specific thing in question but also any instance of that thing resembling it</i>)</li><li>(before a content clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što) or <i>что́бы</i> (štóby)) such (a), such (used to indicate a sufficient degree of something, such that the following clause is true)</li><li>(after <i>кто</i> (kto)) used with a pronoun, name or noun to specify the meaning of <i>кто</i> (kto) as "what's the nature&#47;essence (of)" and not just a request to identify someone. Usually not translated.</li><li>used with (usually after) a noun when speaking of the existence of something resembling what has just been observed or mentioned&#59; usually not translated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) used after an adjective for intensification</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;often followed by <i>себе́</i> (sebé)) a kind/sort/type of, some kind/sort/type of</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;often followed by <i>себе́</i> (sebé)) not great, meh, mediocre, middling, so-so</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>like that, like this (<i>sometimes translated as simply "that"/"those"" or "this"/"these"</i>)</li><li>(immediately before a content clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što)) such (in such a way that the following clause is true)</li><li>(immediately before a clause beginning with <i>како́в</i> (kakóv), <i>како́й</i> (kakój) or <i>как</i> (kak)) as, the way, like (in the way described in the following clause)</li><li>one like that/this, ones like that/this</li><li>(mostly indefinite,&#32;before a relative clause) used to refer to any instance &#47; instances of the noun in question or just spoken about, or, with <i>кто</i> (kto), any person&#47;people, that fall(s) into a particular category</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>one; <i>in the plural:</i> ones, those</li><li>(before <i>кто</i> (kto)) someone, a person, anyone, whoever; <i>in the plural:</i> those, (the/those) people, anyone, whoever, some (people)</li></ol><li>(indefinite,&#32;before a relative clause) used to refer to the kind&#47;sort&#47;type of the noun in question or just spoken about, or, with <i>кто</i> (kto), person&#47;people, described in the following clause</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the kind/sort/type (of [noun]), the kinds/sorts/types (of [noun in plural])</li><li>(before <i>кто</i> (kto),&#32;can also be pluralised and placed after <i>из</i> (iz) in the genitive case) the kind/sort/type of person/people, the type (to), one(s) (to)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a long-form adjective</i>) so; <i>in the negative:</i> that (to a high degree)</li><li>(before a long-form adjective and a content clause beginning with <i>что</i> (što)) so (to a sufficient degree, such that the following clause is true) (<i>including in the negative</i>)</li><li>(<i>before a long-form adjective</i>) that (to that degree, to the previously mentioned or demonstrated degree) (<i>including in the negative; often followed by <i>уж и</i> (už i) in the negative</i>)</li><li>(before a long-form adjective and optionally a clause beginning with <i>как</i> (kak); often with <i>же</i> (že) to emphasise equality) as (to the same or a similar degree)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) used to report speech, emotion or action&#59; am/are/is like, was/were like</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>такъ</i> (takŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;takъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>such-and-such, so-and-so (unstated, given)</li><li>such person (<i>in a document</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fixed rate, tariff</li><li>dachshund</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Taxe</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>taxa</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Dachshund</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxi; cab</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>taxi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxidermist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxidermy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxi driver</li></ol><p><i>такси́</i> (taksí) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taximeter (device in a taxicab that calculates the fare)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) taxicab</li></ol><p>From <i>такси́</i> (taksí) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>мото́р</i> (motór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cabstand, taxi rank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) taxicab</li></ol><p>From <i>таксомото́р</i> (taksomotór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taxonomy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payphone</li></ol><p>From <i>такс</i> (taks) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>фон</i> (fon); final element an abbreviation of <i>телефон</i> (telefon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) taxi driver</li></ol><p><i>такси́</i> (taksí) +&lrm; <i>-уг</i> (-ug) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>таксюга́н</i> (taksjugán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time</li><li>(<i>music</i>) measure, bar</li><li>tact</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tactician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tactics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tactical</li></ol><p><i>та́кт(ика)</i> (tákt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tactfully</li></ol><p><i>такти́чный</i> (taktíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tactful</li></ol><p><i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) alternative form of <i>та́ктовый</i> (táktovyj)</li></ol><p><i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;music) measure, bar</li></ol><p><i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>takyr</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>тақыр</i> (taqyr) and other Turkic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow, osier, <i>Salix</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tal</i>. Compare Kazakh <i>тал</i> (tal).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) talent, gift</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial) man or woman of talent, gifted person, talent</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;historical) talent (measure of weight and monetary unit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>тала́нт</i> (talánt, “talent”)</li></ol><p><i>тала́нт</i> (talánt, “talent”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a talented manner, with talent</li></ol><p><i>тала́нтливый</i> (talántlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gifted, talented</li><li>performed with talent</li></ol><p><i>тала́нт</i> (talánt) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang) an Estonian person.</li></ol><p>From Estonian <i>talupoeg</i> (“a peasant, a farmer”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) a thalassocracy</li></ol><p>From English <i>thalassocracy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go on and on (<i>about something</i>), to harp on [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>о</b> (o) <i>or</i> <b>про</b> (pro) <i>or</i> <b>насчёт</b> (nasčót) <i>or</i> without object]</li></ol><p>From dialectal <i>толды́</i> (toldý, “then”), as also <i>товды́</i> (tovdý), <i>тогды́</i> (togdý), <i>товда́</i> (tovdá), <i>толда́</i> (toldá) for now standard <i>тогда́</i> (togdá), Carpathian Rusyn <i>тогди́</i> (tohdý), <i>товди́</i> (tovdí), <i>тувди́</i> (tuvdí). In dialects also <i>талдо́нить</i> (taldónitʹ), <i>толдо́нить</i> (toldónitʹ), <i>талды́кать</i> (taldýkatʹ), <i>толды́кать</i> (toldýkatʹ). And he who holds the sermons is called <i>талды́ка</i> (taldýka), <i>толды́ка</i> (toldýka), which can be deemed the origin of the verb, derived from <i>-ка</i> (-ka) or <i>-ыка</i> (-yka) (an uncommon variant of the former seen in <i>влады́ка</i> (vladýka) reanalyzed on pre-Slavic <i>ū</i>-stems), then for the verb <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talib (member of the Taliban)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic طَالِب (ṭālib).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talisman, amulet, mascot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>taille</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thallium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θαλλός</i> (thallós, “a sprouting shoot or twig”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tallinn (the capital of Estonia)</li></ol><p><i>Та́ллин</i> (Tállin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talmud</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a bulky and thick book</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>תלמוד</i> (talmud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coupon, ticket, card, voucher</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>talon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thawed, melted</li><li>thawing, melting, slushy (<i>e.g. of snow</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;talъ</i>. Cognate with Russian <i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ, “to melt, thaw”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talysh</li></ol><p>From Talysh <i>Tolışon</i>, <i>толыши</i>, تالشان, possibly ultimately related to the name of the <i>Cadusii</i>, a Median tribe.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain hoist, chain block, pulley block, hoisting tackle, block and tackle</li><li>hostage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>talie</i>, from Italian <i>taglia</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>таль</i> (talĭ, “hostage”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;talь</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>таль</i> (talʹ), Serbo-Croatian <i>талац</i> (“hostage”), Slovene <i>talec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) talc</li></ol><p><i>тальк</i> (talʹk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow bush</li></ol><p>From <i>тал</i> (tal) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talyanka (a variety of garmon, a Russian musical instrument, a small accordion)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>италья́нка</i> (italʹjánka, “Italian woman/girl or (jocularly), something made in Italy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тальянка</i> (talʹjanka): (little) talyanka (a variety of garmon, a Russian musical instrument, a small accordion)</li><li>popular Russian song</li></ol><p><i>талья́нка</i> (talʹjánka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) used to reference another topic in a list; next, then, else</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Filler word, generally before or after stating some example or quality that required thought to find the best word(s)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тамо</i> (tamo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tamo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same place</li><li>ibid.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here and there</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here and there</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere (in a specific but unnamed place)</li><li>that was where; in that place</li></ol><p>From <i>там</i> (tam) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>там</i> (tam, “there”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tamagotchi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese blend of <i>卵</i> (たまご, tamago, “egg”) +&lrm; <i>ウオッチ</i> (uotchi, from English <i>watch</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamada (toastmaster)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>თამადა</i> (tamada).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamarin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tamarin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamarind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>tamarindus</i>, from Arabic تَمْر هِنْدِيّ (tamr hindiyy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tambov (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Тамбо́в</i> (Tambóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) tambour</li><li><i>(at the entrance)</i> lobby</li><li>vestibule <i>(of a train carriage)</i></li><li>chain stitch</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tambour</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tamboura</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tamil</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tamil (the language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>customs officer</li></ol><p><i>тамо́жня</i> (tamóžnja) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) customs (import duties)</li></ol><p><i>тамо́жня</i> (tamóžnja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>customs, custom house</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>тамъга</i> (tamŭga), from a Turkic source (compare Bashkir <i>тамға</i> (tamğa) "brand, mark"). The suffix <i>-ня</i> (-nja) is intended to create nouns of place.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) of that place, of those places, local</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>тамо</i> (tamo, “there”) + <i>-шний</i> (-šnij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Templar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>templier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tampon, wad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>там</i> (tam).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) thane (Scottish title)</li><li>tan (savory yogurt-based beverage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>thane</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>թան</i> (tʻan).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tang; the Tang dynasty (Chinese dynasty that lasted from 618–907 <small>C.E.</small>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>唐</i> (Táng)</li><li>a surname, Tang, from Chinese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>唐</i> (Táng).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tanager (type of bird)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thanatophobia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from New Latin <i>thanatophobia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) tangent (in trigonometry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tangens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tango (ballroom dance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>tango</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tangut (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tangutology (study of the Tangut people, culture and language)</li></ol><p>From <i>тангу́т</i> (tangút) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tangut</li></ol><p><i>тангу́т</i> (tangút) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tandem (<i>bicycle, machine, engine, activity, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tandem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance (movements to music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>taniec</i>, from Middle High German <i>tanz</i>, from Old French <i>dance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly dance (form of dance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tanzanian</li></ol><p><i>Танза́ния</i> (Tanzánija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) tannin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>Та́нин</i> (Tánin)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>tannin</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Танин)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Tanya's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tank (<i>vehicle</i>)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) tank (<i>container</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tank</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tanker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tanker</i>, from English <i>tank</i> (whence also Russian <i>танк</i> (tank)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tankman, tanker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) armored troops, armoured troops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tank</li><li>armored</li></ol><p><i>танк</i> (tank) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tank range, tank park</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tang</li></ol><p>From <i>Тан</i> (Tan) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tantalum</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тантал)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Tantalus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Τάνταλος</i> (Tántalos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tantalean, relating or belonging to Tantalus</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Танталов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tantalus's</li></ol><p><i>Та́нта́л</i> (Tántál, “Tantalus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tantalean torment, Tantalean punishment, tantalization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tantalum</li></ol><p><i>танта́л</i> (tantál) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tantrism, tantric doctrines</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dancing</li></ol><p>From <i>танцева́ть</i> (tancevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-альный</i> (-alʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance (move rhythmically to music)</li></ol><p><i>танц-</i> (tanc-) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to dance (move rhythmically to music)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dancer (person who dances)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance floor</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>танцева́льная площа́дка</i> (tanceválʹnaja ploščádka, “dancing area”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bouncing, springing, rocking (<i>of a step or gait</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) with a bouncing or springing step (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) carelessly sweeping, uneven (<i>of handwriting</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tapir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) slipper</li></ol><p>From <i>то́пать</i> (tópatʹ), <i>ота́птывать</i> (otáptyvatʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>та́почки</i> (tápočky), Czech <i>ťapky</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>шлёпанцы</i> (šljópancy), <i>шлёпки</i> (šljópki) (&lt; <i>шлёпать</i> (šljópatʹ)), Polish <i>klapek</i> (&lt; <i>klapać</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) slipper</li><li>(<i>colloquial, humorous</i>) an outdated, low-powered smartphone</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>та́пки</i> (tápki) as the nominative plural of a masculine singular noun formed with <i>-ок</i> (-ok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often in plural</i>) slipper</li></ol><p><i>та́пка</i> (tápka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packaging</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) container(s)</li><li>tare (<i>weight</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tara</i>, from Italian <i>tara</i>, from Arabic طَرْح (ṭarḥ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bafflegab, baragouin</li></ol><p><i>тараба́ра</i> (tarabára) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gibberish (unintelligible speech or writing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockroach</li></ol><p>Unclear. Attested since 15th century as тороканъ (torokan). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тарган</i> (tarhan), торган (torhan), Belarusian <i>таркан</i> (tarkan). Usually claimed to be of Turkic origin:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cockroach</li></ol><p><i>таракан</i> (tarakan) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taramosalata</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>ταραμοσαλάτα</i> (taramosaláta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) battering ram</li><li>(naval,&#32;technical) ram</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) battering ram attack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>taran</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ram</li></ol><p>From <i>тара́н</i> (tarán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Compare Ukrainian <i>тара́нити</i> (tarányty), Belarusian <i>тара́ніць</i> (taránicʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) anklebone (the talus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarantass (a low horse-drawn carriage without springs used in Russia)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clunker, rustbucket (an old, broken car or other vehicle)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Vasmer discounts various proposed Mari, Tatar, and Sanskrit etymons as reversing the direction of borrowing or unbelievable. Perhaps cognate with <i>тарата́йка</i> (taratájka, “gig”). Not cognate with <i>таранти́ть</i> (tarantítʹ, “to chatter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarantula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Italian <i>tarantola</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sea roach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;offensive,&#32;slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;uncommon) Trolling towards Ukrainian Internet users, especially to support the 2022 Russian invasion of Ukraine.</li></ol><p><i>Тара́с</i> (Tarás, “"Taras", a Ukrainian person”) +&lrm; <i>хара́ссмент</i> (xarássment, “harassment”), second part borrowed from English <i>harassment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gig (horse-drawn carriage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak very fast, to jabber, to babble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tortoriti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to rattle, to clatter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to chatter, to talk idly</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic; compare <i>тарато́рить</i> (taratóritʹ, “to chatter”), <i>тарара́м</i> (tararám, “uproar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare, to goggle</li></ol><p><i>тара́щить</i> (taráščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fez, tarboosh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic طَرْبُوش (ṭarbūš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plate (a serving dish)</li><li><i>(plural)</i> cymbals</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) plate, disc</li></ol><p>From Middle High German <i>talier</i> through Polish <i>talerz</i> (“plate”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), with metathesis (transposition) of <i>l</i> and <i>r</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>таре́лка</i> (tarélka): little plate</li></ol><p><i>таре́лка</i> (tarélka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plate</li></ol><p><i>таре́лка</i> (tarélka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>platelike</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calibration, graduating, graduation, taring</li></ol><p><i>тари́ровать</i> (tarírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calibrate, to gage, to tare</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tariff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>tariffa</i>, ultimately from Arabic تَعْرِيف (taʕrīf).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tariff</li></ol><p><i>тари́ф</i> (taríf) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) packaging, container</li></ol><p><i>та́ра</i> (tára) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarot (card game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tarot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tart (pie, pastry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tarte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tartlet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) hell, nether world (usually in expressions with <i>в</i> (v) - <i>в тартарары́</i> (v tartararý))</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Τάρταρος</i> (Tártaros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tartrate of lysergic acid diethylamide, acid, LSD</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarkhun (a Georgian carbonated soft drink flavoured/flavored with tarragon or woodruff)</li><li>estragon, tarragon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ტარხუნა</i> (ṭarxuna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) tasbih</li></ol><p>From Arabic تَسْبِيح (tasbīḥ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) hauling, dragging, carrying</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;figuratively) scolding, reprimand, telling-off</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry, to lug, to drag</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to pull along, to drag along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear (<i>clothes or shoes, long or carelessly</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull (<i>causing pain</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> <b>за</b> (za, + accusative) <small>‘by some body part’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pinch, to swipe, to pilfer, to filch</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>таска́ти, тащи́ти</i> (taskáty, taščýty), Czech <i>tasiti</i>, Polish <i>taskać, taszczyć</i>. Further etymology is unclear. Schmidt and Uhlenbeck suggested that the term is cognate with Sanskrit <i>तस्कर</i> (taskara, “thief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag oneself <i>(to a place)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry about, to carry along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gad about, to hang about</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to follow, to trail along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to run <i>(after)</i>, to dangle <i>(after)</i>, to chase <i>(after)</i></li></ol><p><i>таска́ть</i> (taskátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shuffle <i>(cards)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tasser</i> (“to pile together”) + <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) funny, amusing, humorous</li></ol><p><i>та́ска</i> (táska) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) dad, daddy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tatami (Japanese straw matting)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) sports mats used in training Japanese martial arts.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>畳</i> (たたみ, tatami).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Tatars</li></ol><p>From <i>тата́рин</i> (tatárin) +&lrm; <i>-ва</i> (-va).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tatar person (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring onion (<i>Allium fistulosum</i>)</li><li>obsolete form of <i>тата́рник</i> (tatárnik, “<i>Onopordum</i>, <i>Cirsium eriophorum</i>, etc.”)</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;Ryazan) a short plait whip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tatar</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tatar (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tattoo (an image made in the skin with ink and a needle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tattoo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tattoo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>tätowieren</i>. By surface analysis, <i>тату́</i> (tatú) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tattoo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tattoo artist, tattooist, tattooer</li></ol><p><i>татуи́ровать</i> (tatuírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) tattoo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) diminutive of <i>тату́</i> (tatú): tattoo</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) one of the members of the t.A.T.u. (Julia Volkova or Lena Katina)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) thief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тать</i> (tatĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tatь</i> (“thief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tau (the Greek letter Τ&#47;τ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ταῦ</i> (taû).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taffeta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tachyon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) tachometer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ottoman, couch <i>(furniture)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian تخته (taxte).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of spring cart (some military types were fitted with machine guns facing backwards during the Civil War in Russia 1917-1922)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tačati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheelbarrow</li><li>shopping cart</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) taxi</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) car</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>тача́ть</i> (tačátʹ, “to stitch”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). But compare Serbo-Croatian <i>tačke</i>, Polish <i>taczka</i>, etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) touchpad (flat surface which is sensitive to touch)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>touchpad</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) sabretache</li></ol><p>From German <i>Tasche</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tashkent (the capital of Uzbekistan)</li></ol><p><i>Ташке́нт</i> (Tašként) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, to drag, to haul</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry (unwillingly or something heavy)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to carry off, to steal, to pinch</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to please, to cause to revel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;taščiti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тащи́ти, таска́ти</i> (taščýty, taskáty), Czech <i>tasit</i>, Polish <i>taskać, taszczyć</i>. Further etymology is unclear. Schmidt and Uhlenbeck suggested that the term is cognate with Sanskrit <i>तस्कर</i> (taskara, “thief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trail along</li><li>to drag oneself along</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trail along <i>(behind)</i>, to follow</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go, to drag oneself <i>(to a place)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to get high <i>(on drugs)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be in ecstasies <i>(about)</i>, to be sent into ecstasies, to be wild <i>(about)</i></li></ol><p><i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atayal (person or people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thawing, melting</li><li>melting away, waning, dwindling</li><li>languishing</li><li>dissipation</li></ol><p><i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt, to thaw</li><li>to melt away, to wane, to dwindle</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to waste away, to pine <i>(with)</i>, to languish (with)</li><li>to melt <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tàjati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) taiga</li></ol><p><i>тайга́</i> (tajgá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tbilisi (the capital of Georgia)</li></ol><p><i>Тбили́си</i> (Tbilísi) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creature, being, animal, beast, monster</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) someone mean, vile, worthless</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тварь</i> (tvarĭ, “item, object, creation”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvarь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to harden</li></ol><p><i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep repeating, to harp on; to talk on and on (<i>о</i> (o) about)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to memorize through repetition</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvьrditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-headed</li></ol><p><i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj, “firm”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лоб</i> (lob, “forehead”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) stronghold (also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ы́ня</i> (-ýnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;stilted) dry land, earth</li><li>(poetic,&#32;stilted) firmament</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>твьрдь</i> (tvĭrdĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvьrdь</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>тврьдь</i> (tvrĭdĭ, “citadel, base, firmament”) and archaic Bulgarian <i>твърд</i> (tvǎrd, “earth, sky, citadel, firmament”). Compare also <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj, “hard, firm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) citizen of Tver</li></ol><p><i>Тверь</i> (Tverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-итянин</i> (-itjanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to twerk (to dance in a sexually-provocative way using hip thrusts in a low squatting stance while shaking the buttocks; to take part in twerking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tver</li></ol><p><i>Тверь</i> (Tverʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) sober</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тверезъ</i> (tverezŭ), a metathesised form of <i>терезвъ</i> (terezvŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terzvъ</i>.</p><p>Doublet of <i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tweed</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tweed</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gay slang</i>) twink (a young, attractive, slim man, usually having little body hair)</li><li>(online gaming,&#32;mildly&#32;derogatory) twink (a lower-level character in a roleplaying game (MMO) which is artificially overgeared or overpowered, due to being given advanced equipment or resources via a higher-level character controlled by the same player)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twink</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;online gaming) to create or train up a twink character (one which is artificially overgeared or overpowered for its level)</li></ol><p>From <i>твинк</i> (tvink) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), from English <i>twink</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;online gaming) relating to a twink character</li></ol><p>From <i>твинк</i> (tvink) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij), from English <i>twink</i> (“low-level character which is artificially overgeared”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;online gaming) relating to a twink character</li></ol><p>From <i>твинк</i> (tvink) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj), from English <i>twink</i> (“low-level character which is artificially overgeared”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twist (type of dance)</li><li>An unexpected turn in the plot of a movie or book.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) to tweet</li></ol><p>English <i>tweet</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) your, yours, thy, thine <i>(2nd-person familiar, singular only)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>твои</i> (tvoi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation, act of creating</li><li>creation, work</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) creature, being</li></ol><p>From <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ, “to create”) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creator, maker</li><li>author</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvorьcь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) instrumental</li></ol><p>From <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrumental case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create</li><li>to do, to make</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to knead (dough or clay)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvorìti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;impersonal) to go on, to happen</li></ol><p><i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quark, cottage cheese, tvorog</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;uncommon) smegma, dickcheese</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тваро́гъ</i> (tvarógŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvarògъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fried quark pancakes, syrniki</li></ol><p><i>творог</i> (tvorog) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tvorog</li></ol><p><i>тво́рог</i> (tvórog) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>creatively</li><li>constructively</li></ol><p><i>тво́рческий</i> (tvórčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>creative</li></ol><p><i>творе́ц</i> (tvoréc) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation (act of creation)</li><li>creative work</li><li>oeuvre</li></ol><p><i>творе́ц</i> (tvoréc) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear energy</i>) fuel rod</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>тепловыделя́ющий элеме́нт</i> (teplovydeljájuščij elemént).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, firm</li><li>firmly, decidedly, definitely</li><li>thoroughly</li></ol><p><i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solid</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj, “solid”) +&lrm; <i>тело</i> (telo, “body”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solidity</li><li>hardness</li><li>firmness, steadfastness</li></ol><p>From <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, solid</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) solid</li><li>firm</li><li>steadfast, steady</li><li>fixed <i>(prices)</i></li><li>sound, good</li><li>sure</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) unpalatalized</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>твьрдъ</i> (tvĭrdŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvьrdъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;twírˀtas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twerH-</i>. Doublet of <i>кварц</i> (kvarc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of the letter Ъ, ъ, the hard sign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>твёрдый</i> (tvjórdyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>твёрдо</i> (tvjórdo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to twerk (to dance in a sexually-provocative way using hip thrusts in a low squatting stance while shaking the buttocks; to take part in twerking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twerking (a sexually-provocative dance, involving the performer thrusting their hips back from a low squatting stance and shaking their buttocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>twerking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>тебе́</i> (tebé)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatre/theater</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>théâtre</i>, from Latin <i>theātrum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>θέατρον</i> (théatron) (a verbal noun of <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai)). Displaced <i>феа́тр</i> (feátr), which had been borrowed from the Byzantine Greek.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>divadlo</i> (i.e **дива́ло, akin to <i>диви́ться</i> (divítʹsja)); Serbo-Croatian <i>позориште</i> (akin to <i>позо́рище</i> (pozórišče) (the meaning <i>theater</i> in Russian is obsolete)), Serbo-Croatian <i>казалиште</i> (i.e. **каза́лище, akin to <i>показа́ть</i> (pokazátʹ)), Slovene <i>gledališče</i> (i.e. **гляда́лище, akin to <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) theater of war (the area in which armed conflict takes place)</li></ol><p>Literally, “theater of military operations/actions”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatergoer, theater buff</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>театра́льный</i> (teatrálʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) dramatized (adapted for theater); theatrical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatre mask, theatrical mask</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatricality (the totality of means and techniques specific to theater)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) theatrics, histrionics, melodrama</li></ol><p><i>театра́льный</i> (teatrálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) theater; theatrical</li><li>theatrical (ostentatious, exaggerated)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>théâtral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теа́тр</i> (teátr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Teuton</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) German</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Teutonī</i> (“the Teutons”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Teuton</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) German</li></ol><p><i>тевто́н</i> (tevtón) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Teutonic</li></ol><p><i>тевто́н</i> (tevtón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tehrani</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tehrani</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thesaurus (book of synonyms)</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from German <i>Thesaurus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thesis (statement supported by arguments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in brief, in summary, abstractly, broadly, concisely, succinctly</li></ol><p><i>те́зисный</i> (tɛ́zisnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thesis</li><li>brief, summary, abstract, broad, concise, succinct</li></ol><p><i>те́зис</i> (tɛ́zis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) theism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) theist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theistic</li></ol><p><i>теи́ст</i> (tɛíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tequila</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>tequila</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tequila</li></ol><p><i>теки́ла</i> (tekíla) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;botany) trumpetbush, tecoma (<i>Tecoma</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Tecoma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>text, wording</li><li>lyrics</li><li>transcript</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>textus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) textiles</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) textile worker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Textil</i>, or directly from French <i>textile</i>; ultimately from Latin <i>textilis</i> (“woven”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) textile</li></ol><p><i>тексти́ль</i> (tekstílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abaca, abaka (plant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) text; textual</li></ol><p><i>текст</i> (tekst) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) text editor (something used to edit text in computers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>textologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>textological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>textology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>texture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tektite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tectonics (study of crustal plates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tectonic</li></ol><p><i>текто́н(ика)</i> (tektón(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluidity (the state of being fluid rather than viscous)</li></ol><p><i>теку́чий</i> (tekúčij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) fluid</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) flowing, smooth</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) changeable, unstable</li></ol><p><i>тек-</i> (tek-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>of the current year</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowing</li><li>current, present-day (existing or occurring at the moment)</li><li>everyday, routine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tele- (over a distance&#59; relating to television)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “at a distance, far off, far away, far from”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teletheology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchorman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television (medium)</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>ви́дение</i> (vídenije), calque of French <i>télévision</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) TV, television; tele-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television channel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) TV worker, member of a TV crew, broadcaster</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television set</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) television set</li></ol><p><i>телеви́зор</i> (televízor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>телеви́зор</i> (televízor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telephoto lens</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cart, telega (a simple, open four-wheeled horse-drawn vehicle for transporting cargo and&#47;or people)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a cartful, a load (a large quantity of something)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) shopping cart</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) complaint</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) Telegram (messenger app)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;telěga</i>, or perhaps, ultimately, from Khazar. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>tàljige</i> / Serbo-Croatian <i>та̀љиге</i>, Czech <i>taliha</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>телѣга</i> (telěga).</p><p>Alteration of the trademark Telegram, influenced by the noun <i>теле́га</i> (teléga, “cart”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>Телеграм</i> (Telegram)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telegram, wire, cable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telegraph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telegraphist, telegrapher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) telegraph</li><li>telegraphic</li></ol><p><i>телегра́ф</i> (telegráf) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>теле́жка</i> (teléžka); small telega, (<i>Internet slang</i>) Telegram (messenger app) etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>теле́жка</i> (teléžka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>теле́га</i> (teléga); small telega</li><li>hand truck, dolly</li><li>wheelbarrow</li><li>(<i>transport</i>) bogey (an assembly of four or six wheels forming a pivoted support at either end of, for example, a railway coach)</li></ol><p><i>теле́га</i> (teléga, “cart”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka, “diminutive suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV viewer</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>зри́тель</i> (zrítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(proscribed,&#32;colloquial) misspelling of <i>те́лик</i> (télik); television</li></ol><p>From the first syllable of <i>телеви́зор</i> (televízor, “television”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, diminutive suffix), reinterpreted (due to identical pronunciation and concordance with the prefix <i>теле-</i> (tele-)) as the suffix <i>-ек</i> (-ek).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV camera, telecamera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television channel</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>кана́л</i> (kanál)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telekinesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) telecommunications</li></ol><p><i>телекоммуника́ции</i> (telekommunikácii) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telecommunication</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV company, broadcasting company</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telemarketing (selling products or services by making calls to potential customers)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) telemedicine (transfer of medical information via telecommunication technologies)</li></ol><p>From <i>тѐле-</i> (tèle-) +&lrm; <i>медици́на</i> (medicína).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>television</i>) videoconference, remote telecast</li><li>(television,&#32;historical) space bridge (one of a series of Cold War-era remote telecasts connecting Soviet Bloc and Western nations)</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>мост</i> (most), literally “telebridge”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephoto lens</li></ol><p>From <i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>объектив</i> (obʺjektiv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teleosaurus, <i>Teleosaurus</i></li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from translingual <i>Teleosaurus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>parapsychology</i>) telepathist, telepath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telepathy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) long, two-handed bludgeon</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) tongue of a bell</li><li>(regional,&#32;figurative,&#32;animate) clumsy, ungainly person</li><li>(regional,&#32;figurative,&#32;animate) stupid person, idiot</li></ol><p>From <i>телепа́ть</i> (telepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ень</i> (-enʹ). Cf. Ukrainian <i>телепень</i> (telepenʹ) and Belarusian <i>целяпень</i> (cjeljapjenʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television program, television show</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV guide (something that tells what's on TV)</li><li>television program, TV program</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) (TV and radio) broadcasting</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>радио-</i> (radio-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;ironic) the body of a fat person</li></ol><p>From the Old Church Slavonic plural of <i>тѣло</i> (tělo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>series, TV series (television program)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telescope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corporeality, physicality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) body; bodily, corporeal, corporal</li><li>pale pink</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тѣлесьнъ</i> (tělesĭnŭ), from тѣло (tělo), an s-stem.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teleprompter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>télésouffleur</i>. By surface analysis, <i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>суфлёр</i> (sufljór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teletype, teleprinter</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>teletype</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teletext</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television movie, made-for-TV film, telefilm</li></ol><p>From <i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>фильм</i> (filʹm), or borrowed from a European language (compare English <i>telefilm</i>, Spanish <i>telefilm</i>, Italian <i>telefilm</i>, French <i>téléfilm</i>, etc.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) telephone number</li><li>small speaker near ears, earphone, headphone.</li></ol><p>Internationalism; ultimately from French <i>téléphone</i>, composed of Ancient Greek <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “afar”) + <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice, sound”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pay telephone, payphone</li></ol><p><i>телефо́н</i> (telefón) +&lrm; <i>автома́т</i> (avtomát)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to telephone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephone operator, telephonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) telephone, call; telephonic</li></ol><p><i>телефо́н</i> (telefón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephone number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>telephone message</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>телефо́н</i> (telefón)</li></ol><p><i>телефо́н</i> (telefón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) calf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>теля</i> (telja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;telę</i>. See also the more common <i>телёнок</i> (teljónok) and the (<i>regional</i>) <i>теля́</i> (teljá).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Телец)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Taurus (constellation of the zodiac containing the star Aldebaran, in the shape of a bull calf)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Taurus (zodiac sign for the bull, covering April 20 — May 20)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teletsunami</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television show</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>teleshow</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TV screen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>television broadcast</li></ol><p><i>теле-</i> (tele-) +&lrm; <i>эфи́р</i> (efír).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) television (device for receiving television signals)</li></ol><p>From the first syllable of <i>телеви́зор</i> (televízor, “television”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calve, <i>(deer)</i> to fawn</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) to dillydally, to dally about</li></ol><p><i>теля</i> (telja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tellurium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tellurian, telluric, terrestrial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body (physical structure of a human or animal)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) body</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѣло</i> (tělo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tělo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) traditional Russian women's sweater that is usually sleeveless</li><li>padded jacket, quilted jacket</li></ol><p>From <i>те́ло</i> (télo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-грей</i> (-grej) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on a combining form of <i>греть</i> (gretʹ, “to heat”) (compare 1sg. present <i>гре́ю</i> (gréju)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gesture, body movement</li></ol><p><i>те́ло</i> (télo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>движе́ние</i> (dvižénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) calf (young cow)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) inexperienced, awkward youth</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) wimp, weakling, coward, pansy</li></ol><p><i>тел(ёнок)</i> (tel(jónok)) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>build, frame, figure, constitution (physique)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lichenology</i>) teloschistaceous</li></ol><p>translingual <i>Teloschistes</i> +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodyguard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Telugu (Dravidian people of India)</li><li>Telugu (Dravidian language of India)</li><li>Telugu (Dravidian script of India)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an old Russian dish of fish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flesh (color)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sailor's shirt (usually striped); telnyashka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>те́ло</i> (télo): (small) body</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veal</li></ol><p>From the plural reducible stem of телёнок (teljónok), <i>теля́т</i> (telját) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina). Compare Polish <i>cielęcina</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>tèletina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>теля́тина</i> (teljátina, “veal”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) calf</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) veal</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) simple-minded</li></ol><p>From <i>теля́та</i> (teljáta) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), with irregular iotation (compare <i>ребя́чий</i> (rebjáčij) and <i>щеня́чий</i> (ščenjáčij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf, bull calf</li></ol><p>From a diminutive of Old East Slavic <i>телѧ</i> (telę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;telę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(in the following construction with <i>чем</i> (čem)) the (to that degree, to the same degree)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>especially (particularly)</li><li>all the more so</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the meantime, meanwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nevertheless</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(stilted,&#32;slightly&#32;dated) all the more (even more)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereby, thus, in doing so, consequently</li><li>Used other than figuratively or idiomatically&#x3a; <i>see</i> тем (tem),&lrm; са́мым (sámym).</li></ol><p>Literally, “by that very thing”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theme, subject, topic</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Thema</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subject matter, subject area</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to subject</li><li>theme</li><li>(<i>music</i>) thematic</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) thematic, topical</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>thematisch</i> + <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij) or <i>тема́тика</i> (temátika) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timbre (quality of a sound independent of its pitch and volume)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>timbre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) darkness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>те́мя</i> (témja): crown of the head</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fontanelle</li></ol><p><i>те́мя</i> (témja, “crown of the head”) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko, “(usually endearing diminutive suffix)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, to become dark</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to get dark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken, to make darker</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pull the wool over someone's eyes</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) dungeon, gaol, prison</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьmьnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>darkly</li><li>obscurely</li><li>gloomily</li><li>shadily, dubiously</li><li>evilly, maliciously</li><li>ignorantly, slowly, backwardly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>darkish, rather dark</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark-haired</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>во́лос</i> (vólos) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) dark-skinned</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj, “dark”) +&lrm; <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grizzled skipper (<i>Pyrgus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj, “dark”) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dark, darkness (a complete or (more often) partial absence of light)</li><li>ignorance, backwardness</li><li>obscurity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rate, speed, pace</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tempo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) temperament, temper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vibrant, spirited</li><li>temperamental</li></ol><p><i>темпера́мент</i> (temperáment) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temperature</li><li>fever (about disease)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) temperature</li></ol><p><i>температу́ра</i> (temperatúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempura</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>天麩羅</i> (tenpura).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown of the head</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těmę</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>це́мя</i> (cjémja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>те́мя</i> (témja)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) misspelling of <i>те́мечко</i> (témečko, “crown of the head”)</li></ol></ol><p><i>те́мя</i> (témja, “crown of the head”) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko, “(usually endearing diminutive suffix)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copyholder (a device that holds copy in place for typesetting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tenakel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) tenge (currency of Kazakhstan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>теңге</i> (teñge).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Tengrism, the native religion of the Turkic and Mongol peoples.</li></ol><p><i>Те́нгри</i> (Téngri, “Tengri, Turkic sky god”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Tengrism, the native religion of the Turkic and Mongol peoples.</li></ol><p><i>Те́нгри</i> (Téngri, “Tengri, Turkic sky god”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tendentious; biased, prejudiced</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>tendenziös</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tendency (likelihood of behaving in a particular way)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;business) tender (offer or proposal made for acceptance)</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) tender (a railroad car hauled by a steam locomotive to carry fuel and water)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tender</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shadow</li><li>shady</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) shady (seamy, questionable)</li></ol><p><i>тень</i> (tenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>тенёто</i> (tenjóto)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shady (abounding in shades)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>painting</i>) to shade</li></ol><p><i>тень</i> (tenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tennessine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) tennis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tennis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tennis player</li></ol><p>From <i>те́ннис</i> (tɛ́nnis, “tennis”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>теніси́ст</i> (tenisýst) and Belarusian <i>тэнісі́ст</i> (tenisíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>T-shirt (type of shirt)</li></ol><p><i>те́ннис</i> (tɛ́nnis, “tennis”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tennis</li></ol><p><i>те́ннис</i> (tɛ́nnis) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tennis court</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) tenor (voice)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) tenor (singer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awning, canopy, tent, baldachin (a rooflike cover)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shade</li><li>shadow</li><li>vestige, particle, atom, hint</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) eye shadow</li><li>apparition, phantom, ghost</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>тѣнь</i> (těnĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těnь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) net used for trapping rabbits or other animals</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тенето</i> (teneto), <i>тонето</i> (toneto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;teneto</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i>. Cognates include Lithuanian <i>tiñklas</i>, German <i>Dohne</i>, Latin <i>tenus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theogony</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теого́ния</i> (teogónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theodolite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theocracy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теокра́тия</i> (teokrátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theologian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theological</li></ol><p><i>теоло́гия</i> (teológija, tɛológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) theorem</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theorist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>theoretically (in theory)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theoretical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) grand unification theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) probability theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conspiracy theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) game theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) set theory (mathematical theory of sets)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) theory of relativity (generic term of special and general relativity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) chaos theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) number theory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) theosophy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θεοσοφία</i> (theosophía, “knowledge of things divine”), from <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) + <i>σοφία</i> (sophía, “wisdom”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теосо́фия</i> (teosófija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>theocentric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>теоцентри́ческий</i> (teocentríčeskij, “theocentric”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>present, contemporary, of now</li></ol><p><i>тепе́рь</i> (tepérʹ) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, at present</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>топере</i> (topere), itself inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topьrvo</i>. First recorded in Russian as тепе́речи (tepéreči) in 1562.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get warm, to warm up, to grow warm</li></ol><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to soften, to warm, to make kinder (<i>e.g. one's eyes or soul</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to burn (a candle or lamp)</li></ol><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glimmer, to gleam</li></ol><p><i>те́плить</i> (téplitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hothouse, greenhouse (building in which plants are grown faster than outside)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attributive</i>) hothouse, greenhouse</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) favorable, coddling, sheltered (<i>of artificially or overly protective conditions</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;idiomatic) sheltered, coddled (grown or developed under overly protective conditions)</li></ol><p><i>тепли́ца</i> (teplíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat (physics: thermal energy)</li><li>warmth</li><li>(<i>weather</i>) warm weather</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>warm, warmly (adverb form of <b>тёплый</b>)</li><li>cordially</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>цяпло́</i> (cjapló).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tepid, lukewarm</li></ol><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermal camera, thermocamera</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>internal combustion locomotive; (<i>more narrowly</i>) diesel locomotive, diesel engine (self-propelled diesel-powered tractor that runs on rails)</li></ol><p>From <i>тепло-</i> (teplo-) +&lrm; <i>-воз</i> (-voz), deverbal from <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heat; thermal</li></ol><p><i>тепло́</i> (tepló) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear energy</i>) producing decay heat</li></ol><p><i>тепло́</i> (tepló, “heat”) +&lrm; <i>выделя́ющий</i> (vydeljájuščij, “releasing, producing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nuclear energy</i>) fuel rod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) warm-blooded, endothermic</li></ol><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat exchanger (device for transferring heat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat supply, heating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmth</li><li>warmth, cordiality</li></ol><p><i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heating pipeline, heating main</li></ol><p><i>тепло́</i> (tepló) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>тра́сса</i> (trássa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor ship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) heated boxcar (<i>used to carry people</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) warm room functioning as a temporary shelter</li></ol><p><i>тепло́</i> (tepló) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) terabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>тера-</i> (tɛra-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) terabit</li></ol><p>From <i>тера-</i> (tɛra-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of terrorism, act of terror, terrorist act</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>террористи́ческий акт</i> (terroristíčeskij akt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>therapeutist, physician, general practitioner, internist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θεραπευτής</i> (therapeutḗs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>therapeutic</li></ol><p><i>терапе́вт</i> (terapévt, tɛrapévt) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>therapy (treatment of disease)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>thérapie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terbium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) syllabic abbreviation of <i>тео́рия вероя́тностей</i> (teórija verojátnostej, “probability theory”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull (at something), to finger, to play with, to pinch, to pluck (at)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bother, to pester</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>теребити</i> (terebiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower, tower-room; a living area in a building or upper part of a building in the shape of a tower, especially in ancient Rus.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;termъ</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>trijem</i>/<i>тријем</i>, Old Polish <i>trzem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>те́рем</i> (térem)</li></ol><p><i>те́рем</i> (térem) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub</li><li>to polish</li><li>to rub sore, to chafe, to abrade</li><li>to grate, to grind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>terō</i> (“to rub”), Ancient Greek <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to rub”), Lithuanian <i>trinti</i> (“to rub”), Latvian <i>trīt</i> (“to rub”), Armenian <i>թրել</i> (tʻrel, “to knead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub oneself</li><li>to rub against something</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to hang around <i>(somewhere)</i></li></ol><p><i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torment, agony, anguish (extreme pain)</li><li>laceration, excruciation, stabbing</li></ol><p><i>терза́ть</i> (terzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to torment, to rack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrzati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tormented, to suffer (<i>morally or physically</i>)</li></ol><p><i>терза́ть</i> (terzátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teriyaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>照り焼き</i> (てりやき, teriyaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) thermal (pertaining to heat and temperature)</li></ol><p>From French <i>thermal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) theremin (electronic musical instrument)</li></ol><p>From <i>Термен</i> (Termen), the Russian version of its inventor's name, Léon Theremin, + Latin <i>vox</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>term (word or phrase, especially one from a specialised area of knowledge)</li><li>(Germany,&#32;Austria) appointment, fixed date</li></ol><p>Via Polish <i>termin</i> from Medieval Latin <i>terminus</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Termin</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>terminus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(economy,&#32;aviation,&#32;informatics) terminal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>terminal</i>, English <i>terminal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) terminalia (<i>Terminalia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Terminalia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;inanimate) terminator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terminology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>termite, white ant</li><li>thermite, thermit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;engineering) thermal, thermic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>therm(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermodynamics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thermodynamic</li></ol><p><i>термодина́м(ика)</i> (termodinám(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermometer (apparatus used to measure temperature)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) thermoplastic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thermoplastic</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>термопла́стик</i> (termoplástik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermoplastic</li></ol><p><i>термо-</i> (termo-) +&lrm; <i>пластма́сса</i> (plastmássa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermos (vacuum flask)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermostat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thermosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thermonuclear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sensu stricto</i>) black tetra (<i>Gymnocorymbus ternetzi</i>)</li><li>(<i>sensu lato</i>) Any fish of the genus <i>Gymnocorymbus</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>ternetzi</i> (as the specific epithet of <i>Gymnocorymbus ternetzi</i>). The species was named after ichthyologist Carl Ternetz (whence also <i>Anostomus ternetzi</i> and <i>Liotyphlops ternetzii</i>). The surname originates from the Slavic languages. Compare <i>Терниц</i> (Ternic), likely related to Russian <i>те́рница</i> (térnica), Ukrainian <i>те́рниця</i> (térnycja), possibly derived from an agricultural occupational surname.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn, prickly plant</li><li>thorn, prickle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hardship, trouble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrnьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackthorn (<i>fruit and tree</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ternopil (<i>city in Ukraine</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Терно́поль</i> (Ternópolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) territorial defense</li></ol><p>Clipped compound of <i>территориа́льная оборо́на</i> (territoriálʹnaja oboróna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>терпели́вый</i> (terpelívyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>терпели́во</i> (terpelívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>patiently (in a patient manner)</li></ol><p><i>терпели́вый</i> (terpelívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, endurance (quality of being patient)</li></ol><p><i>терпели́вый</i> (terpelívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient (not losing one’s temper while waiting), enduring</li></ol><p>From <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>терпели́вий</i> (terpelývyj), Belarusian <i>цярплі́вы</i> (cjarplívy), Polish <i>cierpliwy</i>, Slovak <i>trpezlivý</i>, Czech <i>trpělivý</i>, Macedonian <i>трпелив</i> (trpeliv), Bulgarian <i>търпели́в</i> (tǎrpelív), Slovene <i>potrpežljīv</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>стр̀пљив</i> / Serbo-Croatian <i>str̀pljiv</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience, endurance (quality of being patient)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrpěnьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to endure, to suffer, to tolerate, to stand</li><li>to undergo (a change, <i>especially</i> a negative one)</li><li>to hold it, to wait for opportunity to urinate or defecate despite physical urge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тьрпѣти</i> (tĭrpěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrpěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a person unable to stand up for themselves</li></ol><p><i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ, “to suffer, to endure”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ла</i> (-íla).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>patiēns</i> (p.a.p. of <i>patior</i>), whence <i>пацие́нт</i> (pacijént).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tolerance, indulgence</li><li>tolerability, tolerableness</li></ol><p><i>терпи́мый</i> (terpímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tolerant, indulgent, forbearing</li><li>tolerable, bearable, endurable</li></ol><p>From <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astringent, tart, acerbic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrpъkъ</i>. Related to <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;science fiction) a Terran (an Earthling, someone from Earth)</li></ol><p><i>Те́рра</i> (Térra, “Terra, Earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec, “-ian”), from Latin <i>Terra</i>; calque of English <i>Terran</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;science fiction) a Terran woman or girl (a female Earthling)</li></ol><p><i>терра́н(ец)</i> (terrán(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terrace</li></ol><p>Derived from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;tеrrасеа</i>, from Latin <i>terra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terraforming</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoil tip (heap formed of material discarded during mining or excavation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>territorial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>territory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>territorium</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terror (terrorist activities)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>terror</i>&nbsp;m.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrorize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) terrorism</li></ol><p><i>терро́р</i> (terrór) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terrorist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>terroriste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrorist</li></ol><p><i>террори́ст</i> (terroríst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) terzetto, trio</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) tercet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>терце́т</i> (tercét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>тепе́рь</i> (tepérʹ); now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terrier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>terrier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose, to waste</li><li>to shed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>теряти</i> (terjati, “wreck, ruin”), iterative of <i>терети</i> (tereti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těřati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass out, lose consciousness</li></ol><p><i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ, “lose”) +&lrm; <i>созна́ние</i> (soznánije, “consciousness”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get lost, to disappear</li><li>to lose one's way</li><li>to lose one's presence of mind, to get flustered</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to fail, to weaken (<i>eyesight, hearing, memory, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional,&#32;rare) sober</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>терезвъ</i> (terezvŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terzvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) a fascine knife, a cutlass</li><li>a hatchet; an axe for shaving wood</li></ol><p><i>теса́ть</i> (tesátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew (<i>with an axe</i>), to make (something) by cutting with an axe</li><li>to shave the surface layer of (<i>with an axe</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тесати</i> (tesati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tesàti</i> (“to hew”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothing and shoes</i>) too tight, too narrow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>unit</i>) tesla</li></ol><p>Named after Serbian-American&#32;inventor&#32;Nikola Tesla.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tesla</i> / <i>&#42;teslo</i> (“adze”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tetḱ-dʰlo-</i>, from <i>&#42;tetḱ-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>те̏сла</i>, Polish <i>ciosła</i>, Old Norse <i>þexla</i> (“axe”), Old High German <i>dehsala</i> (“axe, adze”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorge, pass, ravine, defile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press, to crowd</li><li>to oppress</li><li>to squeeze, to be too tight</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těsniti</i> (“to narrow, to squeeze”).</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>те́сный</i> (tésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, to be herded, to cluster, to jostle one another</li><li>to be squeezed, to sit close</li><li><i>(thoughts, emotions etc)</i> to crowd</li></ol><p><i>тесни́ть</i> (tesnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>narrowly, tightly, closely</li></ol><p><i>те́сный</i> (tésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rather small (e.g. of a room); rather narrow; rather tight (e.g. of clothes or shoes)</li></ol><p><i>те́сный</i> (tésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowness; tightness; closeness</li><li>cram, crush</li></ol><p>From <i>тесный</i> (tesnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tight, close, narrow</li><li>small</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѣснъ</i> (těsnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těsnъ</i> (“narrow”). According to Václav Machek, it is related to Ancient Greek <i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow, tight”), thus making both derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ténh₂us</i> (“thin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) boards, planks</li></ol><p><i>тёс</i> (tjos) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test (challenge, trial, exam, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>test</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test system</li></ol><p><i>тест</i> (tɛst) +&lrm; <i>систе́ма</i> (sistéma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tester, multimeter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test</li></ol><p>From <i>тест</i> (test) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>тэсці́раваць</i> (tescíravacʹ), Ukrainian <i>тестува́ти</i> (testuváty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tester (a person administering a test)</li></ol><p><i>тести́ровать</i> (tɛstírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dough, paste, batter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѣсто</i> (těsto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těsto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) test</li></ol><p><i>тест</i> (tɛst) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testosterone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the father of one's wife; father-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьstь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dough</li></ol><p><i>те́ст(о)</i> (tést(o)) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, lace, tape, ribbon</li><li>webbing</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, from Proto-Common Turkic <i>&#42;tasma</i> (“leash, strap”). Compare Uzbek <i>tasma</i>, Kyrgyz <i>тасма</i> (tasma), Bashkir <i>таҫма</i> (taśma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>тесьма́</i> (tesʹmá): (small) ribbon, band, tape</li></ol><p><i>тесьма́</i> (tesʹmá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tête-à-tête, face to face (in person)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tête-à-tête (a face-to-face meeting, or private conversation between two people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tête-à-tête</i> (“head-to-head”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) bridgehead</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tête de pont</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theta (the Greek letter Θ (Th)&#47;θ (th))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θῆτα</i> (thêta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black grouse (game bird of genus <i>Tetrao</i>, of the family Tetraonidae)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) fool, bonehead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tetervь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>northern goshawk</li></ol><p>From <i>те́терев</i> (téterev) +&lrm; <i>ять</i> (jatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowstring</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѧтива</i> (tętiva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tętiva</i>. A rare example when <i>ѧ</i> in the root becomes unstressed in the inherited term (and thus becomes <i>е</i>, not <i>я</i>, compare <i>лека́ло</i> (lekálo)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tetra-</li></ol><p>From combining form of Ancient Greek <i>τέτταρες</i> (téttares), <i>τέσσαρες</i> (téssares, “four”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tetragrammaton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тетра́дь</i> (tetrádʹ)</li></ol><p><i>тетра́дь</i> (tetrádʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exercise book</li></ol><p><i>тетра́дь</i> (tetrádʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise book, notebook</li><li>(<i>printing</i>) signature</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τετράδιον</i> (tetrádion, “quaternion of parchment”), from <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrarch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) carbon tetrachloride, tetrachloromethane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) tetrahedron (polyhedron)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>τετράεδρον</i> (tetráedron).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tetum (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tetum (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theurgist, theurge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θεουργός</i> (theourgós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theurgy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θεουργία</i> (theourgía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teflon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Tephrocactus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Tephrocactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meatballs, koftas</li></ol><p>Via Yiddish from Romanian <i>chifteluțe</i>, plural of <i>chifteluță</i>, derivative of <i>chiftea</i>, from Ottoman Turkish كوفته (köfte), from Persian کوفته (kufte).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Texan</li></ol><p><i>Теха́с</i> (Texás) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Texas; Texan</li></ol><p><i>Теха́с</i> (Texás) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(electronics,&#32;business,&#32;often&#32;in the plural) tech giant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tech giant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) technician (someone engaged in technical sciences)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) student in a technical institute</li><li>athlete with technical virtuosity</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;slang) technical defeat</li></ol><p><i>те́хн(ика)</i> (téxn(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technetium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technician, technologist (occupation)</li><li>artificer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engineering, technics</li><li>technology, tech</li><li>machinery, equipment</li><li>material</li><li>technique</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Technik</i>, from Latin <i>technica</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τεχνική</i> (tekhnikḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>technical and economic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) feasibility</li></ol><p><i>те́хника</i> (téxnika) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>экономи́ческий</i> (ekonomíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technical college, technical school, vocational school, technicum (institution of &#91;further education&#93; at an intermediate level)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Technikum</i>, cognate with Polish <i>technikum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) technically</li></ol><p><i>техни́ческий</i> (texníčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>technological</li><li>technical</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) engineering; mechanical</li><li>industrial</li></ol><p><i>те́хн(ика)</i> (téxn(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) techno</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>techno- (used to form words relating to technology)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “skill”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>technogenic (caused by technology)</li><li>man-made, anthropogenic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from English <i>technogen(ic)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technocrat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technocracy</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) + <i>-κρατία</i> (-kratía). With <i>техно-</i> (texno-) prefix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technologist, product engineer, process engineer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>technological</li><li>technical or service</li></ol><p><i>техноло́гия</i> (texnológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>science park, technology park</li></ol><p>From <i>те́хно-</i> (téxno-) +&lrm; <i>парк</i> (park).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>technical leader</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>техни́ческий руководи́тель</i> (texníčeskij rukovodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maintenance (<i>of machinery</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>техни́ческое обслу́живание</i> (texníčeskoje obslúživanije, “technical maintenance”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(technical) inspection (<i>e.g. of a car</i>)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>техни́ческий осмо́тр</i> (texníčeskij osmótr, “technical inspection”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor vehicle registration</li></ol><p>Derivative of паспорт (pasport).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>current, flow, stream</li><li>current, tendency, trend</li></ol><p><i>те́к-</i> (ték-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estrus; rut</li></ol><p><i>течь</i> (tečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow, to run, to stream, to move</li><li>to leak</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>течи</i> (teči), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;teťi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse, to entertain</li><li>to comfort, to console</li><li>to please</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѣшити</i> (těšiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těšiti</i>. Cognate to <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij, “quiet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to amuse oneself, to have fun</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to jeer, to mock</li></ol><p><i>те́шить</i> (téšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, too, as well, likewise</li><li>it is the very same of....</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>та́кже</i> (tákže).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>то же</i> (to že).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>T, t (Latin letter)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tibetan man</li></ol><p><i>Тибе́т</i> (Tibét) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tibetan</li></ol><p><i>Тибе́т</i> (Tibét) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) Tibetan raven (<i>Corvus corax tibetanus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>тибе́тский</i> (tibétskij, “Tibetan”) +&lrm; <i>во́рон</i> (vóron, “raven”), a calque of translingual <i>Corvus corax tibetanus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Thibaudia</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Thibaudia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to steal</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Not related to Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐍆𐍃</i> (þiufs, “thief”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crucible (heat-resistant container in which metals are melted)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tiegel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crucible (heat-resistant container in which metals are melted)</li></ol><p><i>ти́гель</i> (tígelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тигръ</i> (tigrŭ), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>τίγρις</i> (tígris).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тигр)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tigris (a river in Iraq, Syria and Turkey in Mesopotamia)</li><li>the Tiger <i>(one of the twelve years of the Chinese calendar)</i></li><li>Tiger <i>(person born in the year of the Tiger)</i></li><li>Tiger tank <i>(heavy German tank of World War II)</i></li></ol><p>Possibly from English <i>tiger</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tigrinya (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tiger</li></ol><p><i>тигр</i> (tigr, “tiger”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiger cub</li></ol><p><i>тигр</i> (tigr) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaser (a short film or quote meant to draw an audience to a film or show)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>teaser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tic (local and habitual convulsive motion)</li><li>tick, ticking (fabric)</li><li>teak (tree&#59; timber)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tic</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>tijk</i> or English <i>tick</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>teak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>tick-tock</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tick (of a clock)</li><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine) to run away</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. From the tick-tack sound of a clock.</p><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>тіка́ти</i> (tikáty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ticking (fabric)</li></ol><p><i>тик</i> (tik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thixotropic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tilde (diacritical mark or separate character)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>tilde</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poker,&#32;video games,&#32;chess,&#32;slang) tilt (a state of frustration and worsened performance resulting from a series of losses)</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) a state of frustration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tilt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be on tilt</li></ol><p><i>тильт</i> (tilʹt) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(oncology,&#32;pathology) thymoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) timbrel</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) tympanum</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tympanum, middle ear</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tympanic membrane</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thyme</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>темьанъ</i> (temĭanŭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>темьꙗнъ</i> (temĭjanŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>θῡμίαμα</i> (thūmíama, “incense for smoking”), from <i>θῡμιάω</i> (thūmiáō, “to burn incense, to burn, to smoke (something)”), ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰuh₂mós</i> (“smoke”) (cognate with Russian <i>дым</i> (dym, “smoke”), Lithuanian <i>dū́mas</i> (“smoke”), Latin <i>fūmus</i> (“smoke”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke”). Doublet of <i>фимиа́м</i> (fimiám)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scum, slime (<i>greenish water vegetation</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тина</i> (tina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tīmnā</i> or <i>&#42;tiňa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thing (a public or military assembly)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>þing</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teenager (person aged from thirteen to nineteen, refers to both boys and girls)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>teenager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tincture (alcoholic extract used as medicine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type, model</li><li>character</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) man, guy</li><li>(zoology,&#32;animate) phylum</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>τύπος</i> (túpos, “mark, impression, type”), compare also the colloquial meaning of Greek <i>τύπος</i> (týpos, “man, guy”) and German <i>Typ</i> (“guy, bloke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) like, seemingly, sort of (often used as filler, or can express hesitation, uncertainty, or bewilderment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type, character (a set of characteristic traits&#59; a person having such traits)</li><li>(<i>programming</i>) trait (an abstract data type consisting of a collection of methods)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>typage</i>. By surface analysis, <i>тип</i> (tip) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>typically</li></ol><p><i>типи́чный</i> (tipíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typical</li><li>normal, average</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) type</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) standard, model</li></ol><p><i>тип</i> (tip) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>printer, graphic designer</li><li>typograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>printing house, printshop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typographical, (<i>relational</i>) printing</li></ol><p><i>типогра́ф(ия)</i> (tipográf(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>printer (a person)</li></ol><p><i>типограф</i> (tipograf) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>типогра́фия</i> (tipográfija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) man, guy</li></ol><p>From тип, in the colloquial meaning of Greek <i>τύπος</i> (týpos, “man, guy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тип</i> (tip).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shooting range, shooting gallery, rifle range</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tir</i> ("shooting").</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тир)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyre (a port city, the capital of the Tyre District, South Governorate, Lebanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tirade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number of printed copies</li><li>circulation (of a periodical)</li><li>drawing (of a lottery)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tirage</i>, further from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;tīrāre</i> + <i>-āticum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duplication, replication (e.g. of newspapers, books)</li></ol><p><i>тиражи́ровать</i> (tiražírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiramisu (dessert)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>tiramisù</i> (literally “pick-me-up”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tyrant (also figurative)</li><li>bully</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tyrann</i>, from Latin <i>tyrannus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τύραννος</i> (túrannos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tyrannize</li><li>to torment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tyranny</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>τυραννίς</i> (turannís) +&lrm; <i>-ия</i> (-ija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female tyrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>тира́н</i> (tirán)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tyrannosaurus (dinosaur)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>typography</i>) dash</li><li>dash, dah (<i>in Morse code</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tiret</i> (“dash”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thyristor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tyrian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yew <i>(tree or wood)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tisъ</i>, of disputed origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squeeze, to press</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snuggle, to hug (pets, little kids)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to print, to publish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тискати</i> (tiskati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tìskati</i> (“to press, squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vise (two-jawed instrument for holding work)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embossing</li><li>embossed relief image</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) printing, publishing</li></ol><p><i>ти́снуть</i> (tísnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze once</li><li>to squeeze</li><li>to push, to shove once</li><li>to push, to shove</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to publish once, to print once</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to publish, to print</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to filch, to pilfer, to swipe, to walk off, to walk away <i>(with)</i>, to steal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ти́скать</i> (tískatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yew</li></ol><p><i>тис</i> (tis) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>titanium</li><li>Titan</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Soviet Union) boiler, wood-fired water heater</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Титан)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titan, a member of an earlier race of gods in Greek mythology.</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Titan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) titanium</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Titans; Titanic, gigantic, colossal, monstrous</li><li>huge, grandiose, titanic</li></ol><p><i>тита́н</i> (titán) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) titanium</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) boiler, wood-fired water heater</li></ol><p><i>тита́н</i> (titán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titoism</li></ol><p><i>Ти́то</i> (Títo, “Tito”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm, “-ism”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;biology) titre</li><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) caption, title, subtitle, <i>(plural)</i> credits</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>tytuł</i>. Further from Latin <i>titulus</i>. Doublet of <i>тильда</i> (tilʹda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>titled (having a title of nobility)</li><li>titled (having a title of recognition or honor, such as granted based on achievement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;printing) title</li></ol><p><i>ти́тул</i> (títul) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) titular councillor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;derogatory) titushky, a violent pro-government vigilante involved in cracking down on popular protest; attacking picketers and demonstrators in anti-government rallies, a member of a pogrom mob</li></ol><p>First used during the Maidan movement in Kiev in 2013. Named after an active mob member called <b>Titushko</b>, Russian: <i>Титу́шко</i> (Titúško), Ukrainian: <i>Тіту́шко</i> (Titúško).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar) teat, tit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>typhus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tiflis</li></ol><p><i>Тифли́с</i> (Tiflís) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person who does something secretly from others (having an unpleasant undertone and&#47;or egoistically)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) person who cannot rebuff</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Belarus) plain-clothed silovik taking part in arresting protesters</li></ol><p><i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tikhvin (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Ти́хвин</i> (Tíxvin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quiet, still</li><li>calm, pacific</li><li>soft, gentle</li><li>slow</li><li>dull, flat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тихъ</i> (tixŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tixъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet down, to become quieter</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to calm down</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tixnǫti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quietly, softly, gently, faintly, silently</li><li>quietly, calmly, peacefully</li><li>slowly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>be quiet!</li></ol><p><i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o). Compare Polish <i>cicho</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>тихо́нько</i> (tixónʹko): very quietly</li></ol><p><i>тихо́нько</i> (tixónʹko) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly, quietly</li><li>slowly</li><li>gently, carefully</li><li>on the sly</li></ol><p>From <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-о́нько</i> (-ónʹko, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiet, gentle or shy person, wallflower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Oncorhynchus</i> (a genus under the Salmoninae subfamily&#59; native to the Pacific Ocean region)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Pacific (referring to the Pacific Ocean)</li></ol><p><i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>океа́н</i> (okeán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any kingfisher in the genus <i>Todiramphus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>тихоокеа́нский</i> (tixookeánskij, “Pacific”) +&lrm; <i>зиморо́док</i> (zimoródok, “kingfisher”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) water bear, moss piglet (a member of the phylum Tardigrada)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ти́хо</i> (tíxo)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>silence!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence, quietness, hush (the lack of any sound)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>тишина</i> (tišina), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tišina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ти́хий</i> (tíxij) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) covertly, secretly, furtively</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) imperceptibly, quietly</li></ol><p>Instrumental singular of unpreserved noun <i>тишо́к</i> (tišók), related to <i>тишина́</i> (tišiná, “silence”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiet, silence</li><li>calm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fabric</li><li>(relational,&#32;anatomy) tissue</li></ol><p><i>ткань</i> (tkanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabric, cloth</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) tissue</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъkanь</i>, derived from <i>ткать</i> (tkatʹ, “to weave”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъkati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trūk-</i>, <i>&#42;trūg-</i> (“to press, beat”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъkati</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>тъкати</i> (tŭkati) and possibly Ancient Greek <i>τύκος</i> (túkos, “hammer, chisel”), <i>τυκίζω</i> (tukízō, “I polish (stone)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) weaving, weaver</li></ol><p>From <i>ткач</i> (tkač) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij). Compare Kashubian <i>tkacczi</i>, Silesian <i>tkacki</i>, and Polish <i>tkacki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loom (weaving frame)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaver</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъkačь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ткать</i> (tkatʹ, “to weave”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaving</li></ol><p><i>ткач</i> (tkač) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poke, to jab, to stick, to prod</li><li>to hit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock <i>(against, on)</i> <i>(the door)</i></li><li>to knock on someone's door</li><li>to nuzzle up</li></ol><p><i>ткну́ть</i> (tknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) decay, rot</li><li>dust</li><li>dust, vanity, nothingness, sham</li><li>(<i>neologism&#32;or&#32;slang</i>) despair</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlěnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smouldering, smoldering</li><li>decay, putrefaction, decomposition</li></ol><p><i>тлеть</i> (tletʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) perishability, transience</li></ol><p><i>тле́нный</i> (tlénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) perishable, prone to decay</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) transient, not eternal</li></ol><p><i>тлеть</i> (tletʹ) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pernicious, corruptive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rot, to decay, to putrefy, to moulder</li><li>to smoulder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьlěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;dated) to rot, to spoil</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тьлити</i> (tĭliti, “to wreck, to ruin”), transitive equivalent of <i>тлеть</i> (tletʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) floor, ground</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) base, bottom</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тьло</i> (tĭlo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьlo</i>, whence also Macedonian <i>тло</i> (tlo), Serbo-Croatian <i>тло</i>, Slovene <i>tla</i> (“floor”). Non-Slavic cognates include Latin <i>tellūs</i> (“the Earth”), Sanskrit <i>तल</i> (tala, “surface”), Old Irish <i>talam</i> (“earth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aphid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тлеть</i> (tletʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tmesis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caraway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>тмин</i> (tmyn), кмин (kmyn), тмін (tmin), кмін (kmin), from Polish <i>kmin</i>, via Middle High German from Latin <i>cumīnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) caraway, cumin</li></ol><p><i>тмин</i> (tmin) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to darken, to make dark</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьmiti</i>. Compare <i>тьма</i> (tʹma), <i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>тьмутарака́нь</i> (tʹmutarakánʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Used to link an interrogative content clause in the following constructions. Usually omitted in English translations. In some cases can also be, and sometimes usually is, omitted in Russian, along with any linked prepositions.</li><li>(usually before <i>что</i> (što)) Used to link a declarative content clause in the following constructions. Often omitted in English translations, but can sometimes be translated as "the fact (that)". In some cases can also be, and sometimes usually is, omitted in Russian, along with any linked prepositions.</li><li>Optionally used in the following construction to refer to the thing denoted by <i>что</i> (što) earlier in the sentence. Can be omitted, and in English usually the sentence is translated without it too. Has the meaning "that thing".</li><li>Used in various collocations to refer to the content of a sentence or clause just mentioned by the speaker.</li><li>Used in certain collocations to emphasise contrast with something else.</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Optional part of "<i>е́сли</i> (jésli, “if”) …, <i>то</i> (to, “then”) …"–type constructions, sometimes translated to "then" in English (but used more often than the English equivalent).</li><li>Used to denote repeated alternation of enumerated actions, events or objects.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>that is, in other words, i.e.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) what do you mean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(often followed by <i>и</i> (i)) the same as, the same thing as</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now and then, every now and then</li><li>continually, incessantly, constantly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>something, a certain thing, whatever, this or that</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>maybe…or maybe; either…or (<i>of an uncertain or unclear choice between possibilities</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Aha!, That's it!, There we go! What did I tell you?</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) how (emphatic, limited usage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comrade</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commodity, product, article</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) goods, wares</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tovarъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comrade, friend (male or female)</li><li>mate, companion, pal (male or female)</li><li>colleague, assistant (male or female)</li><li>classmate, fellow student (male or female)</li><li>a word used to address compatriots, fellow citizens (male or female) in the Soviet era</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>товарищь</i> (tovariščĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tovariščь</i>, from <i>&#42;tovarъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-iščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) unofficial</li></ol><p><i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comradeship, fellowship</li><li>association, company</li></ol><p><i>това́рищ</i> (továrišč) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) commodity</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) goods, freight</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) trade</li></ol><p><i>това́р</i> (továr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) freight train</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barter, exchange of commodities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goods turnover</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commodity producer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>товар</i> (tovar).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toga</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then (at that time)</li><li>then (in that case, only then)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тогда</i> (togda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;togъda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>whereas (but in contrast; whilst on the contrary…)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just then</li><li>that was the time when; that was just when</li></ol><p>From <i>тогда́</i> (togdá) +&lrm; <i>-то</i> (-to).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) of that time, of those times, then</li></ol><p><i>тогда́</i> (togdá) +&lrm; <i>-шний</i> (-šnij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) you know, um (<i>a hesitation marker</i>)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Того)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Togo (a country in West Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Togolese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) A toddler.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>toddler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>то́же</i> (tóže)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>identical</li></ol><p><i>то́ждество</i> (tóždestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identity, equivalence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тождьство</i> (toždĭstvo). Calque of Latin <i>identitās</i> (literally “sameness”), from Church Slavonic <i>тожде</i> (tožde), from Old Church Slavonic <i>тъжде</i> (tŭžde, “the same”). Compare Russian <i>то́же</i> (tóže), <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>also, too, as well, likewise</li><li>(<i>in negative constructions</i>) either, neither, nor</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>expresses disbelief, sarcasm, irony, often combined with <i>мне</i> (mne)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;to že</i>. Can be analyzed as <i>тот</i> (tot, “that”) +&lrm; <i>же</i> (že).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) A car of the brand Toyota</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>トヨタ</i> (Toyota).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toy terrier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>toy terrier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(water,&#32;electricity) current</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) threshing floor</li><li>mating place (of birds); lek</li><li>toque</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) pronunciation spelling of <i>то́лько</i> (tólʹko); only</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tokъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>toque</i>.</p><p>Pronunciation spelling representing rapid speech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk show</li></ol><p>First attested in 1993. Borrowed from English <i>talk show</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tokamak (a toroidal chamber in which a plasma is magnetically confined)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>тороида́льная ка́мера с магни́тными кату́шками</i> (toroidálʹnaja kámera s magnítnymi katúškami, “toroidal chamber with magnetic coils”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lathe (machine tool used to shape a piece of material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) turnery</li></ol><p><i>то́карь</i> (tókarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turner, lathe operator (person working a lathe)</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tokyoite</li></ol><p><i>То́кио</i> (Tókio) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tokyo</li></ol><p><i>То́кио</i> (Tókio) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) only</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъkъmo</i>. Cognate with Bulgarian <i>тъкмо</i> (tʺkmo, “just, exactly”), Macedonian <i>токму</i> (tokmu, “just, exactly”) and Serbo-Croatian <i>такмен</i>, <i>takmen</i> (“equal”). Related to <i>точка</i> (točka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) to display/perform courtship rituals, to lek</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) pantograph</li></ol><p><i>ток</i> (tok, “current”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>приёмник</i> (prijómnik, “receiver”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;youth slang) a toxic person (a mean or unpleasant person)</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) clipping of <i>токсикома́н</i> (toksikomán); a substance abuser</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>toxic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toxicologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toxicology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toxin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;youth slang) to be toxic (to behave in an unpleasant or mean manner)</li></ol><p><i>то́ксик</i> (tóksik) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toxicity</li></ol><p><i>токси́чный</i> (toksíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toxic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>tox(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TNT, trinitrotoluene</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>тринитротолуо́л</i> (trinitrotoluól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tolerance</li></ol><p><i>толера́нтный</i> (tolerántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tolerant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) One who practices or tolerates those cultural values most strongly opposed by Russian nationalists.</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) A leftist or a liberal.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>толера́нтный</i> (tolerántnyj, “tolerant”) +&lrm; <i>педераст</i> (pederast, “pederast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;politics) Tolerance of those cultural values most strongly opposed by Russian nationalists.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>толерантность</i> (tolerantnostʹ, “tolerance”) +&lrm; <i>педерастия</i> (pederastija, “pederasty”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) a small amount, relish, shred</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toliko</i>. Related to <i>то́лько</i> (tólʹko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense, use, judgment</li><li>talk, rumor</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) doctrine, trend, sort</li><li>push, shove, thrust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тълкъ</i> (tŭlkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push, to shove, to thrust</li><li>to incite, to instigate</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to sell</li></ol><p>See <i>толкнуть</i> (tolknutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push one another, to jostle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock at/on someone's door, to attempt to see someone</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to loaf about/lounge about</li></ol><p><i>толка́ть</i> (tolkátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) helper engine, pusher engine, bank engine (a railway locomotive that temporarily assists a train that requires additional power or traction to climb a gradient)</li><li>pusher tugboat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one who pushes</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) pestle</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) a drug dealer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push, to shove, to thrust</li><li>to incite, to instigate</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to sell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тълкнути</i> (tŭlknuti, “to push; to knock; to attack”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьlknǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push one another, to jostle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to knock at/on someone's door, to attempt to see someone</li></ol><p><i>толкну́ть</i> (tolknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpretation, explanation</li></ol><p><i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An interpreter, who uses his talents of interpretation to explain something.</li></ol><p><i>толкова́ть</i> (tolkovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain, to interpret</li><li>to talk (about something), to discuss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlkovati</i> (“to interpret”). By surface analysis, <i>толк</i> (tolk) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sensibly, intelligently</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intelligibly, clearly</li></ol><p><i>толко́вый</i> (tolkóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensible, intelligent (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intelligible, clear</li><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) explanatory, explicative, interpretative</li></ol><p><i>толк</i> (tolk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>plainly, clearly</li><li>seriously, earnestly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crush (<i>of people</i>)</li></ol><p><i>толк-</i> (tolk-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crush, squash</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flea market, second-hand market</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) interpreter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlmačь</i>, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tilmač</i> (compare Turkish <i>dilmaç</i> (“interpreter”)), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;til</i> (“tongue”) (Turkish <i>dil</i>) + Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;-mač</i> (“noun suffix”). Cognate with Macedonian <i>толмач</i> (tolmač), Slovene <i>tolmač</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>ту̀ма̄ч</i>/<i>tùmāč</i>, Polish <i>tłumacz</i> (“translator”). Non-Slavic cognates include German <i>Dolmetscher</i> and Hungarian <i>tolmács</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry-roasted oatmeal or barleymeal</li><li>a porridge made from this meal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>толокъно</i> (tolokŭno), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tolkъno</i>, related to <i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ, “to pound”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, to crush, to grind (to make smaller by breaking with a device)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;telťi</i> (“to pound”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beat the air, to mill the wind</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang about, to hang around</li></ol><p><i>толо́чь</i> (tolóčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowd, throng</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тълпа</i> (tŭlpa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlpa</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>тлъпа</i> (tlŭpa), Bulgarian <i>тълпа</i> (tǎlpa), Czech <i>tlupa</i> (“band”) and further related to Polish <i>tłum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crowd, to throng, to cluster</li></ol><p><i>толпа́</i> (tolpá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) large intestine, colon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very fat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very thick</li></ol><p><i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj, “thick, fat”)</li></ol><p><i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on weight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thickly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) tolstovka (man's long belted blouse)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sweatshirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tolstoy; Tolstoy's, Tolstoyan (relating to Tolstoy)</li></ol><p><i>Толсто́й</i> (Tolstój) +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) skipper (Hesperiidae)</li></ol><p><i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj, “thick, large, fat”) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, nominative suffix), literally “fat-header”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-lipped</li></ol><p>From <i>то́лст(ый)</i> (tólst(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>губ(а́)</i> (gub(á)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-skinned</li></ol><p>From <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj) +&lrm; <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moneybags (wealthy person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick</li><li>large, big</li><li>stout, fat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlstъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stout/fat/corpulent man, fat boy, fatty, fatso</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>толстя́к</i> (tolstják); a (little) fat man</li></ol><p><i>толстя́к</i> (tolstják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowd, crush, confusion, mess</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) pounder, pestle, gravity stamp</li><li>broken water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>push, impulse</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) toilet (fixture), toilet bowl</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flea market</li><li>(<i>phonology</i>) stød</li></ol><p>From <i>толк</i> (tolk) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). The sense “stød” is a semantic loan from Danish <i>stød</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mashed potatoes</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickness</li><li>stratum, thick layer, depth</li><li>mass</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъlšča</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thickness</li><li>fatness</li></ol><p>From <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj) +&lrm; <i>-щина́</i> (-ščiná). Compare Ukrainian <i>товщина́</i> (tovščyná), Belarusian <i>таўшчыня́</i> (tawščynjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tarpaper (roofing and waterproofing material made by impregnating roofing paper with coal tar or shale oil products)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) only, whether, so, such</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tôle</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toli</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>tyle</i>. See <i>то́лько</i> (tólʹko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only, but</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>толико</i> (toliko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toliko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) easily, beyond a shadow of a doubt, without hesitation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just, just now</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only just (barely)</li><li>only just (just recently)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tolyatti, Togliatti (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Толья́тти</i> (Tolʹjátti) +&lrm; <i>-инский</i> (-inskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volume <i>(of a book, magazine or disk)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tome</i>, from Latin <i>tomus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τόμος</i> (tómos), from Ancient Greek <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;temh₁-</i> (whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęti</i>, Latin <i>temnō</i>, <i>tondeō</i>, and possibly <i>tempus</i>, <i>templum</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomahawk (American Indian axe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tomahawk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomato</li><li>tomato sauce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>tomate</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tomato</li></ol><p><i>тома́т</i> (tomát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tomboy (girl who behaves in a typically boyish manner)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tomboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tomboy</li></ol><p>From <i>томбо́й</i> (tombój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>tomboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tomboyish</li></ol><p>From <i>томбо́й</i> (tombój) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), from English <i>tomboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>томбо́й</i> (tombój, “tomboy”)</li></ol><p>From <i>томбо́й</i> (tombój) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>том</i> (tom): volume (<i>of a book, magazine or disk</i>)</li></ol><p><i>том</i> (tom) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>longingly, restlessly, listlessly</li></ol><p><i>томи́тельный</i> (tomítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agonizing, painful, oppressive</li><li>tiresome, tedious, trying</li><li>stirring, titillating, tantalizing</li></ol><p><i>томи́ть</i> (tomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weary (<i>with a rather negative intention</i>), to torment, to torture, to languish</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to stew</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>томити</i> (tomiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tomiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;temH-</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>томити</i> (tomiti).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pine <i>(for)</i>, to languish</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to stew</li></ol><p><i>томи́ть</i> (tomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic tomcod (<i>Microgadus tomcod</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>languor</li></ol><p><i>томи́ть</i> (tomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>languishing</li></ol><p><i>том(и́ть)</i> (tom(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>CAT scanner, CT scanner, tomograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>CAT scanning, CT scanning, tomography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tombac, pinchbeck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tomsk (city in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tom River (<i>river in Russia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Томь</i> (Tomʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ago</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tone, plural: <i>то́ны</i> (tóny)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) tone, plural: <i>то́ны</i> (tóny)</li><li>(<i>of a color</i>) tone, shade, plural: <i>тона́</i> (toná)</li><li>intonation</li><li>(<i>physiology</i>) muscle tone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tonus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τόνος</i> (tónos); cf. French <i>ton</i>. See also <i>то́нус</i> (tónus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tonality, key (scale of musical notes)</li></ol><p><i>тона́льный</i> (tonálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tone (<i>musical, linguistic or color</i>)</li><li>tonal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>tonal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij, “thin”): very thin</li></ol><p><i>то́н(кий)</i> (tón(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tonsure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tonic water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tonic</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>tonica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproduce the full sound of (a musical or spoken text)</li><li>to decorate, to tint, to tone (to touch up, giving a desired color or tint)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to set the tone, to prescribe the manner of behavior</li></ol><p><i>тон</i> (ton) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tonkatsu (a Japanese dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>豚カツ</i>, <i>とんかつ</i>, or <i>トンカツ</i> (tonkatsu, “pork cutlet”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) small intestine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thin</li><li>slim, slender</li><li>small, fine, delicate</li><li>fine, high-quality (<i>of food, wine, etc.</i>)</li><li>subtle</li><li>keen</li><li>high <i>(voice)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тънъкъ</i> (tŭnŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьnъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thinly (not thickly)</li><li>subtly</li><li>delicately, finely (e.g. of produced workmanship)</li></ol><p><i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thinness, fineness</li><li>slenderness, slimness</li><li>delicacy, finesse</li><li>subtlety, finesse, fineness</li><li>fine point, nicety, subtlety</li></ol><p><i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metric ton, tonne (1000 kilograms)</li><li>large number, large amount, large weight</li><li>ton</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a thousand units</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tonne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tunnel</li><li>stretched ear piercing, flesh tunnel, gauge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physiology,&#32;medicine) tone</li><li>energy, vigour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tonus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τόνος</i> (tónos). See also <i>тон</i> (ton).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to go down</li><li>to drown</li><li>to be lost</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topnǫti</i>, from <i>&#42;topiti</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-nǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>то́нко</i> (tónko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishery, fishing ground</li><li>haul of fish</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тонꙗ</i> (tonja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topnь</i> or <i>&#42;tорni̯а</i>. Related to топи́ть (topítʹ).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тоня)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diminutive, Tonya, of the female given name Антони́на (Antonína)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top (<i>of the mast</i>)</li><li>cheese, note (pressed mass of tobacco in tobacco cutting machine)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>stamp (sound of stamping when walking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>top</i>, English <i>top</i>, or Low German <i>Topp</i>.</p><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>CEO, chief executive officer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>top manager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supermodel, top model</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topaz (gemstone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stomp, to stamp [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘one's feet’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъpati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewp-</i>. Related to Old Church Slavonic <i>тъпати</i> (tŭpati), Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;túpťō</i>, Latin <i>stupeo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) topic</li><li>diminutive of <i>топ</i> (top) <i>(female garment for the upper body)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>topic</i>.</p><p><i>топ</i> (top) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;neologism) OP, original poster (one who started a thread on a forum, blog, etc.)</li></ol><p>Pseudo-anglicism, derived from <i>topic</i> +&lrm; <i>starter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stoke (an oven)</li><li>to heat</li><li>to melt, to render (fat)</li><li>to sink</li><li>to drown (transitive)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to support (a club or political party)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to topple (<i>a political figure</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flunk, to fail (to give a student a failing grade)</li><li>(<i>automotive&#32;slang</i>) to put the pedal to the metal, to floor it</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topìti</i> (“to heat”). Related to Russian <i>тёплый</i> (tjóplyj, “warm”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topìti</i> (“to heat”) (same as Etymology 1).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topìti</i> (“to drown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drown oneself</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to melt</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be lit (<i>of a stove</i>)</li></ol><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heating, stoking</li><li>fire chamber, furnace, firebox</li><li>melting (down)</li></ol><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oozy, mucky, boggy, marshy, miry, quaggy (describing wet ground or muck into which one's feet may sink)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) easily meltable, having a low melting point</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) hot-burning, highly suitable as fuel</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rare) easily stoked, providing plenty of heat</li></ol><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to sink, to drown”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to melt”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to stoke”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fuel and energy</li></ol><p><i>то́пливный</i> (tóplivnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>энергети́ческий</i> (ɛnɛrgetíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fuel, petrol</li></ol><p><i>то́пливо</i> (tóplivo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuel tank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fuel-efficient</li></ol><p><i>то́пливо</i> (tóplivo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baked milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a food</i>) melted, molten</li></ol><p><i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skip bomber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stomp, to stamp one's foot</li></ol><p><i>то́пать</i> (tópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>topographic</li></ol><p><i>топогра́ф(ия)</i> (topográf(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topolect (set of similar dialects that pertain to a larger distinct dialect)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poplar, cottonwood (any of various deciduous trees of the genus <i>Populus</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topolь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) Frémont's cottonwood (<i>Populus fremontii</i>)</li></ol><p><i>то́поль</i> (tópolʹ, “poplar”) +&lrm; <i>Фре́монт</i> (Frémont, “Frémont”), a calque of translingual <i>Populus fremontii</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toponym, place name</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toponymy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe, ax, hatchet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>топоръ</i> (toporŭ, “poleaxe”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toporъ</i>. Further etymology is disputed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>топо́р</i> (topór): small ax, hatchet</li><li>tufted puffin</li><li>(slang,&#32;in the plural) double hockey stick, seventy-seven in the lotto game</li></ol><p><i>топо́р</i> (topór) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe handle, helve</li><li>augmentative of <i>топо́р</i> (topór, “axe”)</li></ol><p><i>топо́р</i> (topór) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) axe</li><li>clumsy, coarse, crude</li><li>crude, vulgar, graceless</li></ol><p><i>топо́р</i> (topór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause (hair, fur) to bristle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand on end (<i>of hair or fur</i>)</li><li>to wrinkle, to pucker (<i>of material</i>)</li><li>to stick out</li></ol><p><i>топо́рщить</i> (topórščitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>топо́р</i> (topór).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footfall, tread, tramp, trample, stamp, stomp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъpъtъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewp-</i> or ultimately, onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) <i>(fore-and-aft)</i> topsail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>top-zeil</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trampling</li></ol><p><i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trample down</li><li>to tread dirt <i>(into)</i>, to leave dirty footmarks <i>(on)</i>, to track dirt <i>(on)</i></li><li>to knead</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear <i>(shoes)</i> unsparingly</li><li>to trample <i>(on)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) <i>(of fowl)</i> to cover, to mount</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъpъtati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>то́пот</i> (tópot) +&lrm; <i>&#43;ать</i> (&#43;atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shift one's feet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang about, to hang around, to loiter, to wait</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dawdle, to dillydally</li></ol><p><i>топта́ть</i> (toptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark time, to make no headway</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trestle bed, wooden bed</li><li>a kind of outdoor furniture used in Central Asia</li><li>a kind of backless couch</li></ol><p>From Turkic, from Middle Chinese <i>道場</i> probably via Mongolian, a calque of Sanskrit <i>बोधिमण्ड</i> (bodhimaṇḍa). Compare Ukrainian <i>тапчан</i> (tapčan) and Polish <i>tapczan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) topshur (a plucked two-string lute traditionally used by the Altai people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Southern Altai <i>топшуур</i> (topšuur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to spread wide apart, to splay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swamp, bog, marsh</li></ol><p>From <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ, “to sink”) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;topiti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tore, torus</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тор)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) Thor, the Norse god of thunder.</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>Þórr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorax (region of the mammalian body)</li><li>thorax (middle of three distinct divisions in an insect, crustacean or arachnid body)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack, bag, bindle (<i>carried over one's shoulder, especially by a hobo or an itinerant worker</i>)</li><li>(by extension,&#32;colloquial,&#32;humorous) any large bag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish طوربه (torba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haggling, bargaining</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) auction</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) market, mart, bazaar</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) trade, transaction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>търгъ</i> (tŭrgŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъrgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petty trader</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) haggler</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) moneygrubber</li></ol><p><i>торг</i> (torg) +&lrm; <i>-а́ш</i> (-áš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trade, to deal in [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a commodity’</small> <i>and</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъrgovati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>торг</i> (torg) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to haggle, to bargain</li><li>to argue, to wrangle</li></ol><p><i>торгова́ть</i> (torgovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchant, salesman, retailer, trader, dealer (person who traffics in commodities)</li></ol><p><i>торгова́ть</i> (torgovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade, commerce, sale, business</li></ol><p>From <i>торгова́ть</i> (torgovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ля</i> (-lja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) commerce and industry; commercial and industrial, commercial-industrial</li></ol><p><i>торго́вый</i> (torgóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trade and economic</li></ol><p><i>торго́вый</i> (torgóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>экономи́ческий</i> (ekonomíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commercial; (<i>relational</i>) trade</li><li>(nautical,&#32;relational) merchant</li></ol><p><i>торг</i> (torg) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vending machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shopping centre, mall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butt end</li><li>wooden paving block</li></ol><p>First attested in the 19th century. From Italian <i>tor(so)</i> +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>solemnly</li></ol><p><i>торже́ственный</i> (toržéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandeur, stateliness, solemnity</li></ol><p><i>торже́ственный</i> (toržéstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grand, stately</li><li>ceremonial, solemn</li><li>festive</li></ol><p>From <i>торжество́</i> (toržestvó) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival, celebration, festivity</li><li>triumph, victory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тръжьство</i> (trŭžĭstvo), from <i>тръгъ</i> (trŭgŭ) + <i>-ьство</i> (-ĭstvo). By surface analysis, <i>торг</i> (torg) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;imperfective only) to celebrate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to triumph over</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to exult, to rejoice, to glory (in something)</li></ol><p><i>торжество́</i> (toržestvó) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>triumphantly</li></ol><p><i>торжеству́ющий</i> (toržestvújuščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triumphant, exultant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Shinto</i>) torii</li></ol><p>From Japanese <i>鳥居</i> (torii, literally “bird abode”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to tread, to clear (a path or road) (<i>by frequent walking or traveling</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toriti</i> (“to tread, to make way”). By surface analysis, <i>тор</i> (tor, “beaten path”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тори́ти</i> (torýty); further, inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;torъ</i> and cognate with Ukrainian <i>тор</i> (tor, “rut”) (gen. sg. <i>то́ру</i> (tóru)), Serbo-Croatian <i>то̑р</i> (“wattle fence, paddock”) (gen. sg. <i>то̏ра</i>), Sakam <i>то̏р</i> (“track, fence”) (gen. sg. <i>то́ра</i>), Slovene <i>tòr</i> (“friction, rubbing”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>) (gen. sg. <i>tọ́ra</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>)), Moravian dialect <i>tor</i> (“road”), Polish <i>tor</i> (“beaten track”) (gen. sg. <i>toru</i>). Derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terti</i> (“to rub”), whence <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to push, to shove</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to inject (illegal drugs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) to push, to shove</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to inject (illegal drugs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braking</li></ol><p><i>тормози́ть</i> (tormozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brake, brakes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) drag</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) slow-witted person, retard</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) slowpoke</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>τόρμος</i> (tórmos, “socket into which a pin or peg is inserted”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tórmos</i> (“hole”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i> (“to rub, bore, twist”) (whence also Russian <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)</p><p>See <i>Russian etymological dictionary</i>—Terence R. Wade, Terence Leslie Brian Wade.</p><p>Oleg Trubachev thinks Greek origin is unlikely. Nikolai Dmitriev suggests adoption from Turkic turmaz ("not stoppable" or "pad for the wheel of waggon"). See «Лексикографический сборник», 3, 1958, p. 45</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brake</li><li>to hinder, to impede, to slow down</li></ol><p>From <i>то́рмоз</i> (tórmoz, “brake, brakes”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) brake, braking</li></ol><p><i>то́рмоз</i> (tórmoz) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to brake once</li></ol><p><i>тормози́ть</i> (tormozítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>то́рмоз</i> (tórmoz): brake, a braking device</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) a packed (simple) lunch (e.g. taken by workers to the shift work)</li></ol><p><i>то́рмоз</i> (tórmoz) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull <i>(at, about)</i></li><li>to tug/shake in order to awake, to wake up by tugging/shaking</li><li>to bother, to pester, to worry, to give no peace</li><li>to stir up, to spur/awake to activity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tornado (column of air)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tornado</i>, from Spanish <i>tronada</i> (“thunderstorm”), from <i>tronar</i> (“to thunder”), from Latin <i>tonāre</i> (“to thunder”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tene-</i> (“to thunder”). The 'o' and 'r' were reversed in English (metathesis) under influence of Spanish <i>tornar</i> (“to twist, to turn”), from Latin <i>tornāre</i> (“to turn”). Spanish later borrowed the word <i>tornado</i> with the same spelling and meaning as in English.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toroidal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game straps (at the back of a seat for tying game)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;torkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to hasten, to precipitate, to rush</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;torpiti</i>. Vasmer adduces Ancient Greek <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “to turn”) (from the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terkʷ-</i>), and possibly Ancient Greek <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō) (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terp-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, to be in a hurry, to hasten</li></ol><p><i>торопи́ть</i> (toropítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hastily (in a hasty manner)</li></ol><p><i>торопли́вый</i> (toroplívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haste, hurry</li></ol><p><i>торопли́вый</i> (toroplívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hasty, hurried</li></ol><p><i>торопи́ть</i> (toropítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Toropets</li></ol><p><i>Торо́пец</i> (Torópec) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a person who is always in a hurry</li></ol><p>From <i>торопи́ться</i> (toropítʹsja). Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;torpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torpedo</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) dashboard of an automobile</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>torpedo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torpedoing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(weaponry,&#32;relational) torpedo</li></ol><p><i>торпе́да</i> (torpéda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) motor torpedo boat, PT boat, MTB</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;automotive) dashboard, dash</li><li>(animate,&#32;zoology) torpedo, electric ray, coffin ray, crampfish, numbfish</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;archaic,&#32;automotive) torpedo (early 20th century automobile body style)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;archaic,&#32;military) archaic spelling of <i>торпе́да</i> (torpéda)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;file sharing) torrent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>torrent</i>, originally derived from the protocol and program BitTorrent.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(human's)</i> trunk</li><li>(<i>art</i>) torso</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) torso</li></ol><p><i>торс</i> (tors) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake, torte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>torta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortilla (a flat round bread)</li><li>Spanish omelette, Spanish tortilla</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>tortilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cake, torte</li></ol><p><i>то́рт</i> (tórt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Torf</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Macedonian <i>тресет</i> (treset), Serbo-Croatian <i>тресет</i> (akin to <i>тряси́на</i> (trjasína)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peat bog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) peat</li></ol><p><i>торф</i> (torf, “peat”) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick out, to protrude, to jut out</li><li>to stand on end, to bristle (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li>to loiter, to stick around, to hang around</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to feel a euphoria, to get a kick, to get high</li><li>(slang,&#32;ditransitive) to owe money to someone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(s)tъrčati</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>sterczeć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standard lamp, floor lamp, torchiere</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>torchère</i>, from <i>torche</i> (“torch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) standard lamp, floor lamp, torchiere</li></ol><p><i>торше́р</i> (toršér) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melancholy, depression</li><li>boredom, ennui, weariness</li><li>longing, yearning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъska</i>, which is perhaps related to <i>то́щий</i> (tóščij) or <i>ти́скать</i> (tískatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Kazakhstan</i>) to pot, put a child on the potty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>тосу</i> (tosu, “to pot a child”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>drearily</li><li>sadly, in a sad voice</li><li>dully</li></ol><p><i>тоскли́вый</i> (tosklívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dreary</li><li>sad, melancholy</li><li>dull, boring</li></ol><p><i>тоска́</i> (toská) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sad/melancholic</li><li>to be bored</li><li>to long, to pine, to miss, to have a nostalgia, to grieve</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yearning, wistful</li><li>grieving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toast (salutation while drinking alcohol)</li><li>toast (toasted bread)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>toast</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toaster (electrical device for toasting bread, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>toaster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>that, those (distal to an observer)</li><li>that, those (already being talked about)</li><li>used to link a noun with a relative clause, as in the following constructions&#59; usually translated as "the" or, additionally in the plural, "those"</li><li>(<i>before a relative clause</i>) the kind/sort/type of</li><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) the wrong, not the right</li><li>the other, the opposite, the distant, the far (side etc.)</li><li>Used in certain collocations to emphasise contrast with something else.</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(uncommon,&#32;usually with <i>вон</i> (von)) that one, those ones (something distal to an observer)</li><li>(definite,&#32;before a relative clause) used to refer to the instance &#47; instances of the noun in question or just spoken about, or, with <i>кто</i> (kto), the person&#47;people, that fall(s) into a particular category</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that, the one; <i>in the plural:</i> those, the ones</li><li>(before <i>кто</i> (kto)) the person, the guy/girl/etc.; <i>in the plural:</i> the people etc.</li></ol><li>(mostly indefinite,&#32;before a relative clause) used to refer to any instance &#47; instances of the noun in question or just spoken about, or, with <i>кто</i> (kto), any person&#47;people, that fall(s) into a particular category</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>one; <i>in the plural:</i> those, ones</li><li>(before <i>кто</i> (kto)) someone, a person, anyone, whoever; <i>in the plural:</i> those, (the/those) people, anyone, whoever, some (people)</li></ol><li>(indefinite,&#32;before a relative clause) used to refer to the kind&#47;sort&#47;type of the noun in question or just spoken about, or, with <i>кто</i> (kto), person&#47;people, described in the following clause</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the kind/sort/type (of [noun]), the kinds/sorts/types (of [noun in plural])</li><li>(before <i>кто</i> (kto),&#32;can also be pluralised and placed after <i>из</i> (iz) in the genitive case) the kind/sort/type of person/people, the type (to), one(s) (to)</li></ol><li>(<i>in the masculine singular</i>) Optionally used in the following construction to refer to the person denoted by <i>кто</i> (kto) earlier in the sentence. Can be omitted, and in English usually the sentence is translated without it too. Has the meaning "that person".</li><li>(<i>masculine only</i>) Used to refer to the last-mentioned person in a sentence&#59; usually simply translated with the relevant pronoun or the person's name&#47;role. Has the meaning "the latter".</li><li>Used in certain collocations to emphasise contrast with something else.</li><li>(<i>in the negative</i>) the wrong one, not the right one, wrong</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тътъ</i> (tŭtŭ), reduplication of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i>, <i>&#42;séh₂</i>, <i>&#42;tód</i> (“this, that”). Cognate with Old Polish <i>tet</i>, Latvian <i>tas</i>, Lithuanian <i>tas</i>, <i>ta</i>, Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), Sanskrit <i>तत्</i> (tat), Old English <i>se</i> (whence English <i>the</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>the same</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the same (one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>the same</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the same one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, instantly; without delay</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>some, any, (a) particular, (a) certain, (a) given, one [noun] or another, this or that</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(a) particular one(s), (a) certain one(s), (a) given one(s), one or another, this or that one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>the very (selfsame)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the very (selfsame) one</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the afterlife, the next world, kingdom come, the hereafter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(emphasised) that (one)</li><li>so and so (often duplicated after <i>и</i> (i))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>totalitarianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>totalitarian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>total, all-out, comprehensive, complete.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>totem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>totem</i>, from Ojibwe <i>(o)doodem(an)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>totem animal, totemic animal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, instantly</li></ol><p>From <i>тот</i> (tot) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тот</i> (tot).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tofu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>豆腐</i> (とうふ, tōfu), from Middle Chinese 豆 (<i>MC duwH</i>, “bean”) + <i>腐</i> (bjú, “rotten, fermented”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) point</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) dot, dotty</li></ol><p><i>то́чка</i> (tóčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sharpener <i>(for knives or pencils)</i></li></ol><p><i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “to sharpen”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whetstone, grindstone</li></ol><p><i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grindery (a mill where something is ground)</li></ol><p><i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen, to hone, to whet</li><li>to produce, to make (using a lathe)</li><li>to wear away, to wear down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to nag</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to emit, to exude (smell, light, etc.)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to shed (tears)</li></ol><p>Follows from the diachronic convergence of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъčiti</i> (“to point, to sharpen”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewk-</i> (“to hew”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;točiti</i> (“to stiffen”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tek-</i> (“to rig, to fixate”). Morphologically, an iterative of Russian <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ, “to poke”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;točiti</i> (“to drain”), causative of <i>ток</i> (tok, “current”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period, dot, full stop</li><li>point</li><li>dot, (for Morse code&#59; colloquially dit)</li><li>pixel</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>enough!</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъčьka</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>taškas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point of view (a position from which something is seen&#59; outlook&#59; standpoint)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semicolon (punctuation mark indicating a pause longer than the comma)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>то́чно</i> (tóčno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactly, precisely, accurately</li><li>definitely</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exactly!, yes!, bingo!</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as though, as if</li></ol><p>From <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>то́чно</i> (tóčno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exactness, precision, accuracy, punctuality (the state of being exact)</li></ol><p><i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exact, precise, accurate</li><li>correct</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тъчьнъ, тъчьнꙑи</i> (tŭčĭnŭ, tŭčĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ты́кать, то́чка</i> (týkatʹ, tóčka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>pünktlich</i> &lt; German <i>Punkt</i> (<&lt; Latin <i>punctus</i> (p.p.p. of <i>pungō</i>), whence also English <i>point</i>, <i>punctual</i>, <i>пунктуа́льный</i> (punktuálʹnyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactly, spot on, right to a T</li><li>word for word</li></ol><p>From <i>то́чный</i> (tóčnyj); the form "точь" is possibly an abbreviation of <i>то́чка</i> (tóčka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharpened</li><li>lathed (formed with a lathe)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) chiseled (<i>features</i>)</li></ol><p><i>точ(и́ть)</i> (toč(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to cause nausea</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>то́шный</i> (tóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) disgustingly, sickeningly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) miserably</li></ol><p><i>то́шный</i> (tóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) sickness, nausea (qualmishness; as, sickness of stomach)</li><li>loathing</li></ol><p><i>то́шный</i> (tóšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nauseating; nauseous; sickening</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) nauseating; sickening; disgusting</li></ol><p><i>то́шный</i> (tóšnyj, “nauseating”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-, “to create”) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) nauseating, disgusting, sickening</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tedious</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъščьnъ</i>. Related to <i>&#42;tъska</i> (“grief”). Compare Old Polish <i>tesny</i> (“upsetting, wistful, boring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gaunt, scrawny, thin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъščь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuskjas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tus-sḱ-yós</i>, from <i>&#42;tews-</i> (“to empty”), from <i>&#42;tew-</i> (“to swell”).</p><p>Cognate with Polish <i>czczy</i>, Lithuanian <i>tùščias</i>, Latvian <i>tukšs</i>, Vedic Sanskrit <i>तुच्छ्य</i> (tucchyá), Persian تهی (tohi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>whoa (when commanding a horse to stop or imitative thereof)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yada yada yada, and so on; and so forth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;youth slang,&#32;often in the plural) trouble (unpleasant situation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trouble</i>. Entered the Russian language as slang during the heyday of rap and hip-hop culture in the late 1990s and early 2000s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass</li><li>herb</li><li>weed</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) marijuana, weed; any other plant drug</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trava</i> (“grass”).</p><p>Related to <i>трави́ть</i> (travítʹ, “to spoil or damage the grass”) and <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ, “to rub”). Possibly a cognate of Ancient Greek <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “I eat, devour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) May</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>травьнъ</i> (travĭnŭ, “May”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;travьnъ</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>тра́вень</i> (trávenʹ, “May”), Belarusian <i>тра́вень</i> (trávjenʹ), Serbo-Croatian <i>travanj</i> (“April”). Related to Russian трава́ (travá, “grass, herb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam, traverse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>traverse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(theater,&#32;inanimate) travesti role (theatrical role of a boy or young man, played by an actress dressed as a man)</li><li>(theater,&#32;inanimate) job of an actress (or actor in rare cases) playing a travesti role</li><li>(theater,&#32;animate) travesti actress (an actress playing a travesti role)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) a type of humorous poetry, characterized by grotesque parody or satire</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>travesti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>трава́</i> (travá): a blade of grass</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poison</li><li>to etch</li><li><i>(grass)</i> to trample down, to spoil, to damage</li><li>to hunt</li><li>to persecute, to badger, to torment</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to pay out, to slacken out, to slack away, to ease out, to veer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tell</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;traviti</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>травити</i> (traviti), Ukrainian <i>трави́ти</i> (travýty), Serbo-Croatian <i>trovati</i>, Czech <i>trávit</i>, Slovak <i>trávit᾽</i>, Polish <i>trawić</i> (first singular <i>trawię</i>).</p><p>Further cognate with Ancient Greek <i>τρώω</i> (trṓō, “to hurt”), <i>τιτρώσκω</i> (titrṓskō), <i>τρύ̄ω</i> (trú̄ō), Old High German <i>drawa</i> (“threat”), <i>drōа</i> (“threat”), Old English <i>þrēa</i> (“threat, punishment”). More distantly related to <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ, “to rub”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>трава́</i> (travá): grass</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) marijuana; any vegetative drug</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) etching (the process)</li></ol><p><i>трава́</i> (travá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting; baiting</li><li>persecution, bullying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trauma, injury (any serious injury to the body, often resulting from violence or an accident)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the aggregate of injuries occurring in a population group over a set period</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>traumatisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to injure</li><li>to traumatize (physically or psychologically)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>тра́вма</i> (trávma) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herbalist</li><li>(<i>book</i>) herbal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;travьnikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grass, herb</li></ol><p><i>трава́</i> (travá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>herbivorous</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) peaceful, harmless (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>трава́</i> (travá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-яд</i> (-jad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); calque of Latin <i>herbivorus</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grassy</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) herbaceous</li></ol><p>From <i>травян(о́й)</i> (travjan(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>трав'яни́стий</i> (travʺjanýstyj), Belarusian <i>травяні́сты</i> (travjanísty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) grass; grassy</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) herbaceous</li></ol><p><i>трава́</i> (travá) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green snake (<i>Opheodrys</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>From <i>травяно́й</i> (travjanój, “grass”) +&lrm; <i>уж</i> (už, “snake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tragedy (drama or similar work)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tragedy (of a situation); tragic element</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tragicomic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tragicomic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tragically</li></ol><p><i>траги́ческий</i> (tragíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tragic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tragically</li></ol><p><i>траги́чный</i> (tragíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tragic</li></ol><p><i>траг(е́дия)</i> (trag(édija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traditionalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>traditionally (in a traditional manner)</li></ol><p><i>традицио́нный</i> (tradiciónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>traditional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tradition, heritage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>traditio</i>. Compare cognate Russian <i>преда́ние</i> (predánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) traditional art (art made with physical media)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trajectory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trajectory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) high road, highway</li><li>route</li><li>section <i>(of an radioelectronical scheme)</i></li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tract</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tract, treatise</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) <i>(international)</i> treaty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Traktat</i>, from Latin <i>tractātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) inn (an establishment providing lodging, food, and drink)</li><li>tavern; pub; dive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>traktjer</i>, from Latin <i>tractōria</i>. Compare Italian <i>trattoria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tavern</li></ol><p><i>тракти́р</i> (traktír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innkeeper</li></ol><p><i>тракти́р</i> (traktír) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>traktować</i> (“to discuss”), from Latin <i>tractāre</i>; compare <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ). Via Latin doublet of <i>трети́ровать</i> (tretírovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpretation, treatment, version</li></ol><p><i>трактова́ть</i> (traktovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) tractor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tractor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tractor driver</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tractor</li></ol><p><i>тра́ктор</i> (tráktor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trawl</li><li>(<i>military</i>) sweep, minesweep</li><li>a large flatbed trailer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trawl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trawler</li><li>(<i>military</i>) minesweeper</li></ol><p><i>трал</i> (tral) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ram, to tamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tram (passenger vehicle), streetcar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tramway</i>, from English <i>tramway</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tram, streetcar</li></ol><p><i>трамва́й</i> (tramváj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>трамва́й</i> (tramváj, “tram”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>US politics</i>) Trumpism (the philosophy and politics espoused by Donald Trump; a remark made by Donald Trump)</li></ol><p>From English <i>Trumpism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(swimming,&#32;gymnastics&#32;or&#32;figuratively) springboard</li><li>(<i>skiing</i>) ski jumping hill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tremplin</i>, from Italian <i>trempellino</i>, from <i>trempellare</i>, from an unidentified Germanic form. Compare Middle Low German, Middle High German <i>trampeln</i>, Middle English <i>trample</i>.</p><p>Attested since 1830.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squanderer (one who wastes e.g. money)</li></ol><p>From <i>транжи́рить</i> (tranžíritʹ, “to squander”) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander, to waste, to blow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>tranchieren</i>, from French <i>trancher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(banking,&#32;finance,&#32;computing) transaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transistor</li><li>transistor radio</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) transit</li></ol><p><i>транзи́т</i> (tranzít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) transient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trance</li><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) a transgender person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trance</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>trans-</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>trāns</i> (“across, on the far side, beyond”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transatlantic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transvestite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transgender (a transgender person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>transgender</i>, from Latin <i>trāns</i> (“across”) + <i>genus</i> (“sort”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transgender</li></ol><p><i>трансге́ндер</i> (transgéndɛr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trans fat</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>транс-</i> (trans-) +&lrm; <i>жир</i> (žir).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Transylvanian (native or inhabitant of Transylvania) (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Transylvanian (related to Transylvania)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transcribe (to convert a representation of language into another)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;linguistics) transcription</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transmit, to broadcast, to relay</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to translate, to compile</li></ol><p>Evidently backformed from <i>трансля́ция</i> (transljácija) (ultimately from Latin <i>trānslatiō</i>), with the suffix <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>транслитера́ция</i> (transliterácija); transliteration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) transliteration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transliterate (to represent letters or words in the characters of another alphabet or script)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broadcast, transmission, translation (a radio or TV transmission on air)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>trānslātiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmission (assembly of gears)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>transmissio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transnational</li></ol><p><i>транс-</i> (trans-) +&lrm; <i>национа́льный</i> (nacionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trans-Neptunian</li></ol><p><i>транс-</i> (trans-) +&lrm; <i>Непту́н</i> (Neptún) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banner, billboard, placard, sign</li><li>lined guide sheet to be put underneath unlined writing paper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to transplant (medicine: transfer (tissue/organ))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transport, transportation</li><li>consignment</li><li><i>military</i> transport, train</li><li><i>navy</i> transport, supply ship, troop transport, troopship, troop-carrier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>transport</i>, Dutch <i>transport</i> or German <i>Transport</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>trānsportāre</i>. First documented in 1720.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) protractor</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>trānsportō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transport</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>transporter</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transport, transportation, carriage, conveyance, haulage, hauling</li></ol><p><i>транспорти́ровать</i> (transportírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) transportation (the act of transporting)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipper (person or organization that ships (sends) something)</li><li>transporter (person or organization that transports something)</li></ol><p><i>транспорти́ровать</i> (transportírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) means of transportation (general term for the different kinds of transport facilities that are often used to transport people or cargo)</li><li>vehicle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) transportation, transport, shipping</li></ol><p><i>тра́нспорт</i> (tránsport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) conveyor, transport</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transsexual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transsexuality (the state, condition, or properties of being transsexual)</li></ol><p><i>транссексуа́льный</i> (transsɛksuálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transsexual (being a transsexual)</li></ol><p><i>транссексуа́л</i> (transsɛksuál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) trans woman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>molecular biology</i>) transfection</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transfer</li><li>shuttling, transport (at an airport, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(finance,&#32;law,&#32;politics,&#32;etc.) transfer</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>transfert</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transphobia (fear or hatred of transsexuality)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transformer (electrical device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transformation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform, to convert</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>transformieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be/become transformed/altered</li></ol><p><i>трансформи́ровать</i> (transformírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) transcendental</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>transzendental</i>; ultimately from Latin <i>trānscendēns</i> (“transcending”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) transcendental number</li></ol><p>From <i>трансценде́нтный</i> (transcendéntnyj, “transcendental”) +&lrm; <i>число́</i> (čisló, “number”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>transcendent (lying beyond the limits of experience)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) transcendental</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>trānscendens</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Latin <i>trānsscendō</i>, from Latin <i>trāns-</i> + Latin <i>scandō</i> (whence also <i>scāla</i>, whence Russian <i>шкала́</i> (škalá), <i>эскала́тор</i> (eskalátor)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) tranche</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tranche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trench, ditch, dyke, fosse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tranchée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gangway, ramp</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Someone who is anatomically male but who passes as female.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>trap</i> (“stairs; ladder”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>trap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) now obsolete type of cheap cloth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) meal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>стол</i> (stol), <i>засто́лье</i> (zastólʹje). Also compare Latin <i>mēnsa</i> (whence <i>ми́ска</i> (míska) and many other descendants).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) trapezium (polygon with two parallel sides)</li><li>trapeze (a swinging horizontal bar, suspended at each end by a rope)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>route, line, track</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Trasse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low-quality mixture of hay with straw used as fodder</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Trasyanka (an interlanguage with elements of Russian and Belarusian)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>трася́нка</i> (trasjánka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expenditure</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend, to expend</li><li>to waste</li><li>to fritter away (time)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tratiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend money, to spend up [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + dative) <small>‘on someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning</li><li>mourning clothing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Trauer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mourning, funeral</li><li>mournful, sorrowful</li></ol><p><i>тра́ур</i> (tráur) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stencil (utensil consisting of a perforated sheet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>traforetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stencil</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stencilled</li></ol><p><i>трафаре́т</i> (trafarét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet,&#32;telephony,&#32;automotive) traffic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>traffic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bang (verbal percussive sound)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) an act of sexual intercourse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucker, someone who has sexual intercourse</li></ol><p><i>тра́хать</i> (tráxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike, to hit</li><li>(transitive,&#32;vulgar) to fuck, to bonk, to screw</li></ol><p><i>трах</i> (trax) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have sex <i>(with)</i>, to bang, to screw</li><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to be unpleasantly busy with something for a long time</li></ol><p><i>тра́хать</i> (tráxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trachea, windpipe (thin-walled, cartilaginous tube connecting the larynx to the bronchi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trachymedusa (Trachymedusae sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Trachymedusae</i> and its singular form in New Latin <i>trachymedusa</i>. From New Latin <i>trachy-</i> (“spiky, jagged”, from Ancient Greek <i>τρᾱχῠ́ς</i>&nbsp;m (trākhŭ́s)) +&lrm; New Latin <i>medūsa</i>&nbsp;f.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to bang, to boom</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to crash, to bang</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to lay, to bang, to screw</li></ol><p><i>трах</i> (trax) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have sex <i>(with)</i>, to bang, to screw</li></ol><p><i>тра́хнуть</i> (tráxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trachoma</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tri-, tre- (three)</li><li>Found in a small number of words describing a state or action&#59; has an intensifying effect.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rite, ceremony (an irregular church ceremony performed at the request of a parishioner)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>трѣба</i> (trěba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terba</i>. Displaced the Old East Slavic inherited form <i>тереба</i> (tereba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Book of Needs, breviary, ordinary</li></ol><p><i>тре́ба</i> (tréba) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demand, request, claim (act of requesting)</li></ol><p><i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactingly, particularly, fastidiously</li><li>commandingly</li></ol><p><i>требова́тельный</i> (trebovátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exactingness, demandingness</li></ol><p><i>тре́бовательный</i> (trébovatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demanding, exacting, particular, fastidious</li><li>commanding</li></ol><p>From <i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demand, to request</li><li>to need, to call for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трѣбовати</i> (trěbovati), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>трѣбовати</i> (trěbovati), from Proto-slavic root <i>&#42;terb-</i> (see <i>&#42;terba</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to need, to require</li><li>to take (time, effort, money, etc. for something)</li></ol><p><i>тре́бовать</i> (trébovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>required, requisite, necessary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chitterlings</li><li>garbage</li><li>offal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьbuхъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entrails</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>travel blogger (one who creates a travel blog)</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from English <i>travel blogger</i>, or alternatively a blend of <i>трѐвел-бло́г</i> (trèvel-blóg, “travel blog”) +&lrm; <i>бло́гер</i> (blóger, “blogger”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarm, alert</li><li>trouble, anxiety, uneasiness, fluster, discomposure, disquiet, disquietude</li></ol><p>First attested in the first third of XVIII, most likely borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>трево́га, триво́га</i> (trevóha, trivóha), from Old Polish <i>trwoga</i>, ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьvoga</i>, from <i>&#42;trъvati</i>. Alternatively, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьvoga</i> with uncertain etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disturb</li><li>to worry, to trouble, to harass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>трво́жити</i> (trvóžiti) or directly from Middle Polish <i>trwożyć</i> in XVIII, ultimately from Old Polish <i>trwożyć</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьvožiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>трево́га</i> (trevóga) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be anxious/uneasy/worried <i>(about)</i>, to become anxious <i>(about)</i></li><li>to trouble oneself, to bother oneself</li></ol><p><i>трево́жить</i> (trevóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>трево́жный</i> (trevóžnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>трево́жно</i> (trevóžno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>uneasily, alarmedly, with agitation, discomposedly</li></ol><p><i>трево́жный</i> (trevóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anxiety, uneasiness</li></ol><p><i>трево́жный</i> (trevóžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alarming, disturbing</li><li>anxious</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) alert, alarm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьvožьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>трево́га</i> (trevóga, “alarm, alert, anxiety”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) go bag, bug-out bag (<i>in the form of a suitcase</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally, “alert suitcase”, i.e. a suitcase prepared for use in case of a military or emergency alert.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>трево́жный чемода́н</i> (trevóžnyj čemodán); go bag, bug-out bag (<i>in the form of a small suitcase</i>)</li></ol><p><i>трево́жный чемода́н</i> (trevóžnyj čemodán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme anxiety, disquiet, agitation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>трьволнъниѥ</i> (trĭvolnŭnije), composed of <i>трь-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) + <i>влъна</i> (vlŭna, “wave”). A calque of Ancient Greek <i>τρι-κυμία</i> (tri-kumía, literally “third wave”), from the belief that the third wave is the most dangerous one (compare <i>девя́тый вал</i> (devjátyj val)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;computing) thread (a unit of execution)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) thread (a series of messages)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>thread</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>tredecillion (10<sup>42</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) teetotaler, non-drinker, abstainer</li></ol><p><i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-енник</i> (-ennik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstinence (specifically, abstinence from alcohol)</li></ol><p><i>тре́звенный</i> (trézvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sober (<i>of mind</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) overly practical and moderate</li></ol><p><i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sober <i>(up)</i>, to become/grow sober</li></ol><p><i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>soberly</li></ol><p><i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ringing of all the bells (as in a church/belfry)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) continuous or frequent ringing</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) ringing of an alarm bell on a ship</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) spreading of the word, lively gossip, divulging of information</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) noise, commotion</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>трезво́нить</i> (trezvónitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring all the bells (as in a church/belfry)</li><li>to ring continuously or often</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to ring an alarm bell on a ship</li><li>to make a sound similar to church bells ringing</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to spread the word, to gossip, to divulge information</li></ol><p><i>тре-</i> (tre-) +&lrm; <i>звони́ть</i> (zvonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soberness</li><li>temperance</li><li>sobriety</li></ol><p><i>тре́звый</i> (trézvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sober</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>трѣзвъ</i> (trězvŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terzvъ</i>. Doublet of <i>тверёзый</i> (tverjózyj), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trident</li></ol><p>From <i>тре-</i> (tre-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub, “tooth”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) stock trader</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trader</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trailer (vehicle towed behind another, used for carrying equipment)</li><li>trailer (preview of a film)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trailer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parkourist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;sports) track</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>track</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(software,&#32;computing) tracker (computer program that monitors something)</li><li>(music,&#32;computing) tracker (computer software for composing music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tracker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tracklist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>tracklist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;as an expletive) cursed, damn, damned</li></ol><p><i>тре-</i> (tre-) +&lrm; <i>кля́тый</i> (kljátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;phonetics) trill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trellis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trellis</li></ol><p><i>трелья́ж</i> (trelʹjáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) trembita (an alpine horn made of wood)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>трембіта</i> (trembita), from Hungarian <i>trombita</i>, from Italian <i>trombetta</i>, diminutive of <i>tromba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Donbass) coat hanger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Drempel</i>, <i>Trempel</i> (“knee wall”); see details under Ukrainian <i>тре́мпель</i> (trémpelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The elongated fabric that is drawn behind a bride’s gown, train</li><li>(bodybuilding,&#32;slang) trenbolone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>train</i>.</p><p>Clipping of <i>тренболо́н</i> (trenbolón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise machine, trainer (fitness device)</li><li>simulator</li></ol><p><i>трена́ж</i> (trenáž) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exercise machine, trainer (fitness device)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) simulator</li></ol><p><i>тренажёр</i> (trenažór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trenbolone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown <i>of an anchor</i></li><li>trend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) trendy, popular</li></ol><p>From <i>тренд</i> (trend) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coach, trainer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trainer, coach, coaching</li></ol><p><i>тре́нер</i> (tréner) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snaffle, bridoon (type of horse's bit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Trense</i> of the same meaning which has been borrowed from the Dutch <i>trense</i> (“rope, bridle”) in the sixteenth century when they were connected to the Spanish <i>trenza</i> (“plait”), which is perhaps a blend of Old Spanish <i>treça</i> (“plait”), a borrowing from Old French <i>treça</i> (“plait”) – which is possibly with Italian <i>treccia</i> (“plait”) via Vulgar Latin from Ancient Greek <i>τριχία</i> (trikhía, “rope”) –, and <i>trena</i> (“plait”) from Latin <i>trīna</i>, the feminine of <i>trīnus</i> (“tripartite”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friction, rubbing</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) friction, clashes, conflict</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;plural only) sports trousers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>training</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>training</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to train, to coach</li></ol><p>Ultimately from English <i>train</i> + <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to train</li></ol><p><i>тренирова́ть</i> (trenirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>training, coaching</li></ol><p><i>тренирова́ть</i> (trenirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) training, practice</li></ol><p><i>трениро́вка</i> (treniróvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tripod (a three-legged stand or mount)</li><li>hobble (a binding to keep beasts from running)</li></ol><p><i>тре-</i> (tre-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “leg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hobble</li></ol><p><i>трено́га</i> (trenóga) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tripod (a three-legged stand or mount)</li></ol><p><i>трено́га</i> (trenóga) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A trench coat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trenchcoat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tinkle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trepak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(flax etc)</i> to scutch, to swingle</li><li>to pull about, to blow about, to flutter, to tousle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out, to fray, to tear</li><li>to pat</li><li>to beat up, to maul, to pummel</li><li>to give a hard life/time</li><li>to mention too often, to make too many references <i>(to)</i>, to hackney</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трепати</i> (trepati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trepati</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>трепа́ти</i> (trepáty), Belarusian <i>трепа́ць</i> (trjepácʹ), Slovene <i>trépati</i> (first singular <i>trẹ́pljem</i>), Czech <i>třepat</i>, Slovak <i>trepať</i>, Polish <i>trzepać</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>třepać</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>tśepaś</i>, <i>tśapaś</i>. Further cognate with Lithuanian <i>trepsė́ti</i> (“to stomp”), <i>trapinė́ti</i> (“to kick”), Old Prussian <i>ertreppa</i> (“to cross”), Ancient Greek <i>τραπέω</i> (trapéō, “to squeeze”), Latin <i>trepidus</i> (“agitated”), Sanskrit <i>तृप्र</i> (tṛprá, “unstable”), <i>तृपल</i> (tṛpala, “unstable”). Compare with English <i>throb</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flutter</li><li>to get worn out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to twaddle, to prattle, to chew the fat, to chew the rag</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to blab, to blabber</li></ol><p><i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) twaddler, prattler, blabbermouth, flapjaw, windbag, gasbag</li></ol><p><i>трепа́ться</i> (trepátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrill</li><li>awe, trepidation (feeling of fear and reverence)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trepetъ</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>тре́пет</i> (trépet), Slovene <i>trepèt</i>, Polish <i>trzpiot</i> and Ukrainian <i>тре́пет</i> (trépet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, to quiver, to flicker</li><li>to thrill, to tremble</li><li>to fear, to tremble <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>трепетати</i> (trepetati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trepetati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trembling, flickering, quivering</li><li>anxious</li><li>reverent</li><li>timid</li><li>tender, gentle</li></ol><p><i>тре́пет</i> (trépet) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) garrulous, mendacious, boastful person, liar</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a tool for beating fibers in the form of three oak planks (the middle of which is an isosceles triangle, and the other two oblique parallelograms), glued together and attached to a lime wedge or handle</li></ol><p><i>треп(а́ться)</i> (trep(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p><p><i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to thrash around</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to quiver, to flutter</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to beat, to flap [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘e.g. one's wings’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to thrash around</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to quiver, to flutter, to flap</li></ol><p><i>трепыха́ть</i> (trepyxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crack, crash, snap (sharp sound made when solid material breaks)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trěskъ</i> (“crack, bang”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cod (marine fish of the family Gadidae)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трѣска</i> (trěska), <i>треска</i> (treska), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trěska</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arctic cod (<i>Arctogadus glacialis</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to chap</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>тре́скать</i> (tréskatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trěskati</i>, or from <i>треск</i> (tresk) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). The same root appears in <i>тре́снуть</i> (trésnutʹ) and <i>треща́ть</i> (treščátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gadidae (a biological family that includes cod, haddock, whiting, and pollock)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cod (marine fish of the family Gadidae)</li></ol><p><i>треска́</i> (treská) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gadiformes (a biological order that includes cods)</li></ol><p><i>треска</i> (treska) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crackle, crackling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chatter, prattle</li></ol><p><i>треск-</i> (tresk-) +&lrm; <i>-отня́</i> (-otnjá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, to burst, to pop</li><li>to (begin to) collapse, to break down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>треск</i> (tresk) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic tomcod (<i>Microgadus tomcod</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trust (a group of businessmen or traders)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trust</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolidation of several enterprises into trusts</li></ol><p>From <i>трести́ровать</i> (trestírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije), from трест (trest).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arbitral</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) arbitration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>треть</i> (tretĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tretь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tretias</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous,&#32;ironic) third wheel, gooseberry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a third (<i>of some unit of measure</i>)</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) an alloy containing 1/3 of one metal and 2/3 of another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) quick solder, tinman's solder (an alloy of tin and lead containing approximately 1&#47;3 lead by mass&#59; Sn<sub>62</sub>Pb<sub>38</sub> or Sn<sub>60</sub>Pb<sub>40</sub>)</li></ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a holder of a one-third share</li></ol><p><i>треть</i> (tretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>third (one of three equal parts of a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>third course (meal), dessert</li><li>thirdly, used as <i>во-вторы́х</i> (vo-vtorýx)</li><li>the third (in dates)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) third person</li><li>(<i>law</i>) third party</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>day before yesterday, two days previously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insignificant, of little importance, nonmain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>third (fraction)</li></ol><p>Substantivized feminine of the adjective <i>тре́тий</i> (trétij, “third”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tretyakov (referring to anyone with this last name, or to anything named after such a person, such as Tretyakovsky Proyezd, a street in Moscow)</li></ol><p><i>Третьяко́в</i> (Tretʹjakóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tricorn (hat)</li></ol><p>From <i>треуго́л(ьный)</i> (treugól(ʹnyj), “triangular”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, colloquial noun-forming suffix), likely as a calque of German <i>Dreispitz</i> or French <i>tricorne</i> (“tricorn”), from Latin <i>tricornis</i> (“three-horned”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) triangle</li><li>set square, triangle</li><li>(<i>music</i>) triangle</li></ol><p>From <i>тре-</i> (tre-) +&lrm; <i>-уго́льник</i> (-ugólʹnik), literally “three-angle-er”. Compare Ukrainian <i>трику́тник</i> (trykútnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Lenné Triangle (the gore delineated by the <i>Bellevue-</i>, <i>Ebert-</i>, and <i>Lennéstraßen</i> which, between 1961 and 1988, was part of the German Democratic Republic <i>de jure</i>, but was separated from it by the Berlin Wall, which ran along the <i>Ebertstraße</i>)</li></ol><p>Partial calque of German <i>Lenné-Dreieck</i>: <i>Dreieck</i> → <i>треуго́льник</i> (treugólʹnik); transliteration of German <i>Lenné</i> (surname) → <i>Ленне́</i> (Lenné).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triangular (shaped like a triangle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) treyf</li></ol><p>From Yiddish <i>טרייף</i> (treyf), from Hebrew <i>טְרֵפָה</i> (trefá). Doublet of <i>трефа</i> (trefa) of newer origin, with same sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) clubs (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♣)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Treff</i>, from French <i>trèfle</i> (“clover”), from Ancient Greek <i>τρίφυλλιον</i> (tríphullion, “clover”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>трефный</i> (trefnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) treyf, unkosher</li></ol><p>From Yiddish <i>טרייף</i> (treyf) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj) or from <i>треф</i> (tref).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) clubs (suit in card games)</li></ol><p><i>тре́фы</i> (tréfy) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of clubs (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) clubs (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♣)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) trash <i>(worthless things or stuff)</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) <i>used to express strong or powerful emotions</i></li><li>(<i>slang</i>) <i>used to express a negative attitude to a person or a current situation</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;music) thrash metal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trash</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) trash stream; a type of live broadcast containing immoral, degenerate, dangerous content, such as drinking lots of alcohol, physical and verbal violence, humiliation, sexual harassment usually in order to receive money from the viewers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) someone who makes trash stream</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criocerine (Criocerinae)</li></ol><p>From <i>треща́л</i> (treščál, “rattled”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crackle (make a fizzing sound)</li><li>to chirr (make a trilled sound)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chatter, to jabber</li><li>to crack, to be on the point of collapse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trěščati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>треск</i> (tresk) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ), with iotation. The same root appears in the related verbs <i>тре́скаться</i> (tréskatʹsja), <i>тре́снуть</i> (trésnutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crevice, crack (thin space opened in a previously solid material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тре́щина</i> (tréščina, “crack”)</li></ol><p><i>тре́щина</i> (tréščina) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) rattle</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;sometimes&#32;plural only) treshchotka (musical instrument)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) chatterbox, rattle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three (3)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трие</i> (trije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trьje</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tríjes</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tréyes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) 777 (a brand of cheap port wine)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) M777 howitzer</li></ol><p>Literally, “three axes”, from the similarity of the digit 7 to the shape of a carpenter's axe.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tri-, tre- (three)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triathlon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribology</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to rub”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ло́гия</i> (-lógija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) tribune (Ancient Roman official)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>podium, platform, rostrum, tribune</li><li>stand, grandstand (raised structure for spectators)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tribūnus</i>, from Latin <i>tribus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribunal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trivial (common, ordinary)</li><li>obvious</li><li>hackneyed</li><li>truistic</li><li>cut and dried</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trigonometric function</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trigonometric</li></ol><p><i>тригономе́трия</i> (trigonométrija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) trigonometry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>trigōnometria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>τρίγωνον</i> (trígōnon, “triangle”) + <i>-metria</i> (“-metry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) thrice ninth, twenty-seventh</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;folk poetic) far away, distant</li></ol><p>From <i>три́девять</i> (trídevjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;folk poetic) thrice nine; twenty-seven</li></ol><p>From <i>три-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>де́вять</i> (dévjatʹ), literally, “thrice nine”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirty-year period</li><li>thirtieth anniversary, thirtieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>три́дцать</i> (trídcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thirty-year</li><li>thirty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>три́дцать</i> (trídcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triacontagon</li></ol><p><i>три́дцать</i> (trídcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sum of 30 rubles</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anything with the number 30, e.g. a bus route, school, etc.</li></ol><p><i>три́дцать</i> (trídcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirtieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty (30)</li></ol><p>From <i>три</i> (tri) +&lrm; <i>-дцать</i> (-dcatʹ), or "thrice ten".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-eight (38)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-two (32)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-nine (39)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-one (31)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-five (35)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-seven (37)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-three (33)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-four (34)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty-six (36)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a Soviet tank T-34</li></ol><p><i>три́дцать</i> (trídcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>четвёрка</i> (četvjórka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>30×, 30 times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) triune (three-in-one&#59; both three and one at the same time)</li></ol><p>From <i>три-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj, “unified”). Compare Ukrainian <i>триєди́ний</i> (tryjedýnyj), Belarusian <i>трыадзі́ны</i> (tryadzíny), Polish <i>trójjedyny</i> and Bulgarian <i>триеди́нен</i> (triedínen).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) grain sorting machine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>trieur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>three times, thrice, on three occasions</li><li>3×, three times as much</li></ol><p><i>три</i> (tri) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) funeral feast; wake</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, possibly inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tryzna</i>. Compare Czech <i>trýzeň</i> (“torture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tricot</li><li>tights, leotard</li><li>jogging bottoms, sweatpants</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tricot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tricolour/tricolor</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) the flag of Russia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tricolore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tricot, knitted fabric or garment thereof</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tricotage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) knit, of tricot</li></ol><p><i>трикота́ж</i> (trikotáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trictrac, tricktrack (<i>a variety of backgammon, short backgammon</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>trictrac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trefoil, shamrock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thriller (a suspenseful, sensational genre of fiction)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>thriller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>trillion (1,000,000,000,000, 10<sup>12</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trilobite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trilogy</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τριλογία</i> (trilogía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trimaran</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) trimethylamine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) tridecahedron</li></ol><p><i>тринадцать</i> (trinadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirteen-year period</li><li>thirteenth anniversary, thirteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>трина́дцать</i> (trinádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thirteen-year</li><li>thirteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>трина́дцать</i> (trinádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tridecagon</li></ol><p><i>трина́дцать</i> (trinádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteen (13)</li></ol><p>From <i>три</i> (tri, “three”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>13×, thirteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) trinitrotoluene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Trinitrotoluol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>trio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) triode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>triode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetry</i>) triolet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threesome</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>triolisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triplet (group of three)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tripolitan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) three of a kind</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) thrips</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θρίψ</i> (thríps, “wood-worm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triptych</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τρίπτυχος</i> (tríptukhos, “consisting of three layers, threefold”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>три́птих</i> (tríptix).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) trysail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trysail</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three hundred (300)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tri</i> <i>&#42;sъta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newt (type of salamander)</li><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) triton (nucleus of tritium)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) tritone (musical interval of three tones)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>triton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triumvirate (a group of three)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>triumviratus</i>, from <i>triumviri</i> + <i>-atus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triumph (conclusive success&#59; victory&#59; conquest)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The state of being triumphant; triumph, triumphantness.</li></ol><p><i>триумфа́льный</i> (triumfálʹnyj, “triumphant”, adjective) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness”, abstract-noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triumphal</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>triumphālis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Ancient Rome) triumpher (military commander who has been honored for victory by a triumph)</li><li>(literary,&#32;figurative) victor</li></ol><p><i>триу́мф</i> (triúmf) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triphthong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Trichosanthes</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Trichosanthes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tricholoma, <i>Tricholoma</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Tricholoma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triceps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>триллио́н</i> (trillión, “trillion)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>touchingly, in a moving manner</li></ol><p><i>тро́гательный</i> (trógatelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>touching, moving (emotionally)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>touchant</i> (“touching, moving”). By surface analysis, <i>тро́гать</i> (trógatʹ, “to touch, to arouse emotion”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to move (<i>emotionally</i>)</li><li>to bother, to annoy, to disturb</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to start moving</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic.</p><p>From the same root as Latin <i>trahō</i> according to Goryaev, which would be Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tregʰ-</i>, whence English <i>drag</i>, <i>treat</i>, <i>trace</i>, and <i>traction</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start (for), to get moving, to be off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go nuts</li><li>to be touched, to be moved (emotionally)</li></ol><p><i>тро́гать</i> (trógatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anthropology</i>) troglodyte (<i>1</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) ignoramus, troglodyte (<i>3</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) glutton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>τρωγλοδύτης</i> (trōglodútēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three (in a group together), three of them</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trojь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;humorous) a short-living socialist city government in Irkutsk from end of 1919 to January 1920 during the Russian Civil War.</li></ol><p>A mix between <i>директория</i> (direktorija) and <i>трое</i> (troje), homonymous to <i>траектория</i> (trajektorija, “trajectory”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a sign of the cross made with three fingers</li></ol><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje, “three”) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>перст</i> (perst, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) trio, the three of them</li></ol><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Троица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Trinity</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;colloquial) Whitsunday</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Trinity</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Troitsky (referring to anyone with this last name, or to anything named after such a person)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Troitsky (referring to various locales)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Troitsk (referring to various locales)</li></ol><p>All meanings ultimately derived from <i>Тро́ица</i> (Tróica) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Троицкий)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Russian surname, Troitsky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Trinity Sunday, Whit Sunday</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) triune</li><li>ternary</li><li>threefold</li></ol><p><i>Тро́ица</i> (Tróica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) the number three or anything identifiable by number three</li><li><i>(mark)</i> three <i>(out of five)</i>, C mark, C grade</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) three, trey</li><li>troika (a party or group of three, especially a ruling council of three people in Soviet or Russian contexts)</li><li>troika (<i>Russian carriage drawn by a team of three horses abreast</i>)</li><li>a three-piece suit (trowsers/vest/jacket)</li></ol><p>From <i>трой-</i> (troj-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) tee, T-joint, T-bend</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) junction box</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) three-way power strip</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) three-barreled hunting rifle</li><li>(<i>fishing</i>) fishhook with three points</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) Troynoy cologne (a Soviet brand of cologne often drunk illegally)</li></ol><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triple, threefold, treble</li></ol><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triplets (a group of three siblings born at the same time to the same mother)</li></ol><p><i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one of three twins</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trial; triple; threefold (consisting of three parts)</li><li>triple; trial; triune (exhibiting triality&#59; manifesting in three forms or ways)</li><li>tripartite (having three parties)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) trial (pertaining to a grammatical number in certain languages that refers to three of something)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any hide beetle of the genus <i>Trox</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Trox</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trolleybus, trolley bus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trolley bus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trolley bus</li></ol><p><i>тролле́йбус</i> (trolléjbus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;fishing) trolling (luring fish with bait)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet) trolling (posting inflammatory material in an attempt to lure someone into a dispute)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trolling</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;chiefly&#32;Internet) to troll (to post inflammatory material in an attempt to lure someone into a dispute)</li></ol><p><i>тролль</i> (trollʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), from English <i>troll</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) trollface</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>trollface</i> (“лицо тролля”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) troll (supernatural being in Scandinavian mythology)</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) troll (provocative, disruptive person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>troll</i> or any other Scandinavian language.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>troll</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrombus, blood clot (blood clot formed in blood vessels that leads to thrombosis)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrombosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) trombone</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>trombone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trombonist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hematology,&#32;cytology) thrombocyte, platelet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θρόμβος</i> (thrómbos) + <i>κύτος</i> (kútos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “chair, throne”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>touched, moved (emotionally)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) touched, batty, cracked</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch</li><li>to move (<i>emotionally</i>)</li><li>to bother, to annoy, to disturb</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to start moving</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to damage (<i>e.g. of frost</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start (for), to get moving, to be off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go nuts</li><li>to be touched, to be moved (emotionally)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path, trail (a trail for the use of, or worn by, pedestrians)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tropa</i>. Akin to <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically Ancient Greek <i>τρίβος</i> (tríbos) (&lt; <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō) <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i>, whence also <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ)). Also compare <i>доро́га</i> (doróga) (akin to <i>дёргать</i> (djórgatʹ), <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>church</i>) troparion, hymn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тропарь</i> (troparĭ) (also <i>трепарь</i> (treparĭ)), from Ancient Greek <i>τροπάριον</i> (tropárion). Suffix influenced by <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tropic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tropic of Capricorn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tropic of Cancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>path, small path (a trail for the use of, or worn in by, pedestrians)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tropical</li></ol><p><i>тро́п(ик)</i> (tróp(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тропа́</i> (tropá)</li></ol><p><i>тропа́</i> (tropá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tropopause</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troposphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawser, line, rope (<i>thick</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>tros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>трость</i> (trostʹ, “cane, walking stick”)</li></ol><p><i>трость</i> (trostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reed, rush (grass-like plant)</li></ol><p><i>трость</i> (trostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reed, rush (grass-like plant)</li></ol><p>From <i>тростни́к</i> (trostník) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>трость</i> (trostʹ, “cane, walking stick”)</li></ol><p><i>тро́стка</i> (tróstka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cane, stick, walking stick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trъstь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>TNT, trinitrotoluene</li></ol><p>Likely from German <i>Trotyl</i>; cf. Polish <i>trotyl</i>, Romanian <i>trotil</i>. Ultimately, from an abbreviation of German <i>Trinitrotoluol</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>UK</i>) pavement, (<i>US</i>) sidewalk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>trottoir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pavement, sidewalk</li></ol><p><i>тротуа́р</i> (trotuár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trophy, prize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trophy, captured</li></ol><p><i>трофе́й</i> (troféj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trotskyism</li></ol><p>From <i>Тро́цкий</i> (Tróckij) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trotskyite, Trotskyist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) a bit, a little, slightly</li></ol><p><i>тро́хи</i> (tróxi) +&lrm; <i>ки</i> (ki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>denotes a relative in the same generation as the head noun but two degrees greater in separation</li></ol><p>From <i>тро́е</i> (tróje) +&lrm; <i>-ю-</i> (-ju-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with <i>-ю-</i> (-ju-) by analogy with <i>двою́родный</i> (dvojúrodnyj) (see there for further details).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second cousin <i>(great uncle's grandson)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first cousin twice removed <i>(first cousin's grandson)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second cousin once removed <i>(second cousin of one’s father or mother)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second cousin once removed <i>(son of a second cousin)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>first cousin three times removed downwards (first cousin's great-grandson)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;historical) three rubles</li><li><i>(mark)</i> three <i>(out of five)</i>, C mark, C grade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trojan</li></ol><p><i>Тро́я</i> (Trója) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) true, real</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;terti</i>.</p><p>From English <i>true</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipe</li><li>chimney, flue</li><li>(<i>music</i>) trumpet</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) undesirable situation, seemingly hopeless (де́ло труба́)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mobile phone</li><li>(<i>colloquial, figurative</i>) telephone</li><li>(hunting,&#32;dated,&#32;slang) fox tail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>труба</i> (truba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫba</i>.</p><p>Noncognate with Russian <i>трубаду́р</i> (trubadúr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troubadour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>troubadour</i>. Several steps omitting from Ancient Greek <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos) (whence English <i>trope</i>, Russian <i>троп</i> (trop)).</p><p>Noncognate with Russian <i>труба́</i> (trubá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trumpet player, trumpeter</li></ol><p>Formally can be from <i>труба́</i> (trubá, “trumpet”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač), semantically from <i>труби́ть</i> (trubítʹ, “to trumpet”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow <i>(the trumpet)</i></li><li>to sound, to blare</li><li>to sound <i>(a signal)</i></li><li>to trumpet, to proclaim from the housetops</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tube, small pipe</li><li>roll, scroll</li><li>telephone receiver</li><li>tobacco pipe</li><li>snorkel</li></ol><p><i>труба́</i> (trubá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aardvark</li></ol><p><i>тру́бка</i> (trúbka, “pipe, tube”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зуб</i> (zub, “tooth”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) trumpet</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) pipe</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) loud, booming</li></ol><p><i>труба́</i> (trubá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipeline</li></ol><p><i>труба́</i> (trubá) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>прово́д</i> (provód)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pipeline</li></ol><p><i>трубопрово́д</i> (truboprovód) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chimney sweep</li></ol><p>From <i>труба́</i> (trubá) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) +&lrm; <i>-чист</i> (-čist), combining form of <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ, “to clean, to scour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тру́бка</i> (trúbka)</li><li>drinking straw</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) puff</li></ol><p><i>тру́бка</i> (trúbka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pipe-shaped</li></ol><p><i>труба́</i> (trubá) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) work, labor/labour</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) effort, pains</li><li>(countable,&#32;literary) work (<i>of literature or scholarly publication</i>)</li><li>(<i>singular only</i>) shop, handicrafts (a school class teaching basic vocational skills and working with one's hands)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трудъ</i> (trudŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trudъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trewd-</i>, whence also English <i>threat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burden (with work)</li><li>to make tired</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to hamper, to hinder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;truditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, to labor, to toil</li></ol><p><i>труди́ть</i> (trudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>тру́дно</i> (trúdno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj): hardest, most difficult</li></ol><p><i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty</li></ol><p><i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o). Compare Polish <i>trudno</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) with some difficulty</li></ol><p><i>труднова́тый</i> (trudnovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rather difficult, rather hard</li></ol><p><i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arduous (hard to climb)</li><li>difficult of approach</li><li>hard to reach, remote</li><li>hard to get</li></ol><p><i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>до́ступ</i> (dóstup) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparingly soluble, poorly soluble</li></ol><p>From <i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>раствори́ть</i> (rastvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>важкорозчи́нний</i> (važkorozčýnnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difficulty (state of being difficult)</li><li>obstacle, problem, trouble</li></ol><p><i>тру́дный</i> (trúdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, difficult [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>для</b> (dlja, + genitive) <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trudьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shop teacher, handicrafts teacher</li><li>(politics,&#32;historical) a Trudovik (a member of the Labour Group party)</li></ol><p>From <i>трудово́й</i> (trudovój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) labor, work</li><li>working, worker's</li><li>earned</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) service</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workaholic (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work-day, man-day (unit of accounting of Soviet collective farms in the period from 1930 to 1966)</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>трудолюби́вый</i> (trudoljubívyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>трудолюби́во</i> (trudoljubívo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>industriously</li></ol><p><i>трудолюби́вый</i> (trudoljubívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard-working, industrious</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diligence, industriousness</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люб(и́ть)</i> (ljub(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability to work (a certain amount and quality)</li></ol><p><i>трудоспосо́бный</i> (trudosposóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able-bodied (able to work)</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>спосо́бный</i> (sposóbnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange work (for someone), to provide work (for someone)</li></ol><p><i>трудоустро́ить</i> (trudoustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange work (for someone), to provide work (for someone)</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(job) placement, employment, employment assistance</li></ol><p><i>трудоустро́ить</i> (trudoustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>labor-intensive, laborious</li></ol><p><i>труд</i> (trud) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ёмкий</i> (jómkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labour-intensiveness, laboriousness</li></ol><p><i>трудоёмкий</i> (trudojómkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) workaholic, hard worker</li></ol><p><i>труд(и́ться)</i> (trud(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker, working man, laborer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toiler, worker</li></ol><p>From труд (trud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a joke, to laugh (<i>in good fun</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>над</b> (nad, + instrumental) <small>‘about something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, possibly derived from <i>труд</i> (trud, “work, effort”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>the sound of a string instrument being strummed, such as a guitar</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse, dead body</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trupъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>troupe (company of actors, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coward</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) earthquake</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫsъ</i> (“earthquake, shaking”), from <i>&#42;tręsǫ</i> (“I shake”). Akin to <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí), <i>тру́сить</i> (trúsitʹ) and <i>труси́ть</i> (trusítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>трусы́</i> (trusý): panties, knickers</li></ol><p><i>трусы́</i> (trusý) +&lrm; <i>-ики</i> (-iki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) to chicken out, to lose one's nerve, to be scared, to be cowardly</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to shake off, to shake out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to trot</li></ol><p><i>трус</i> (trus) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫsiti</i>. Cognate to <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí), <i>трус</i> (trus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine) crunch, crackle</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;regional,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine) brushwood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трускъ</i> (truskŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cowardly</li><li>faint-hearted, timid</li></ol><p><i>трус</i> (trus) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowardice</li></ol><p><i>трус</i> (trus) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jog (exercise)</li><li>trot, trotting</li></ol><p>Related to <i>трясти́</i> (trjastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underwear, underpants, shorts</li><li>swimming trunks</li></ol><p>Related to French <i>trousse</i>, English <i>truss</i>, or perhaps to English <i>trouse</i> (“trousers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinder, amadou</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>трудъ</i> (trudŭ, “tinder fungus”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫdъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drone, male bee</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) drone, idler, sluggard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫtъ</i>. Cognate to German <i>Drohne</i>, Lithuanian <i>trãnas</i>, Latvian <i>trans</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) drone, male bee</li></ol><p><i>тру́тень</i> (trútenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rot; something dry, old and crumbling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rotten, old and broken</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;truxlъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>труха́</i> (truxá) +&lrm; <i>-ля́вый</i> (-ljávyj).</p><p>Related to Macedonian <i>трул</i> (trul), Serbo-Croatian <i>труо</i>, Slovene <i>truhel</i> and Slovak <i>trúchlý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be frightened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) true, real</li></ol><p>From English <i>true</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) dense forest, thicket, jungle</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) backwoods, the sticks (remote, rural area)</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) slum (dilapidated neighborhood)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hovel, shack (dilapidated house)</li></ol><p><i>труск</i> (trusk) +&lrm; <i>-оба</i> (-oba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>треш</i> (trɛš)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, stunt, feat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>truc</i>. Several steps omitting from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;trūdicāre</i> (&lt; Latin <i>trūdō</i> + <i>-icō</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trewd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) hold (cargo area)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>'t ruim</i> (from <i>het ruim</i>). Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rūmą</i> (whence also English <i>room</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trumeau (the pillar or center post supporting the lintel in the middle of a doorway)</li><li>pier glass, trumeau mirror</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>trumeau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) skysail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truffle (mushroom)</li><li>chocolate truffle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Trüffel</i>, from Italian <i>tartufolo</i>, from Latin <i>tuber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) truffle</li></ol><p><i>трю́фель</i> (trjúfelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (small) rag</li></ol><p><i>тряпьё</i> (trjapʹjó) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an anarchist guerrilla movement on the lowest parts of Amur river during the Russian Civil War</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rag</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) cloth</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) weak-willed, lily-livered</li></ol><p><i>тряпи́ца</i> (trjapíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag (piece of cloth)</li><li>(animate,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine) milksop, softy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тряпъка</i> (trjapŭka). Connected with Russian <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ) with expressive nasalization.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тря́пка</i> (trjápka): (small) rag (piece of cloth), duster</li></ol><p><i>тря́пка</i> (trjápka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) rags</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quag, quagmire</li></ol><p><i>тряс(ти́)</i> (trjas(tí)) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína). Cognate with Bulgarian <i>тресавище</i> (tresavište), Czech <i>třasovisko</i>, Polish <i>trzęsawisko</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>тресава</i>, Ukrainian <i>трясовина</i> (trjasovyna).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>зыбь</i> (zybʹ), <i>зыбу́н</i> (zybún) (akin to <i>зы́бить</i> (zýbitʹ), <i>зы́бкий</i> (zýbkij)); Belarusian <i>дры́гва</i> (drýhva) (akin to <i>дры́гать</i> (drýgatʹ)), <i>дрегва</i> (dregva), <i>дрягва</i> (drjagva) (akin to <i>дрягать</i> (drjagatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaking, jolting</li></ol><p><i>тряс(ти́)</i> (trjas(tí)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jolty (<i>of a vehicle</i>)</li><li>bumpy (<i>of a road</i>)</li></ol><p><i>тряс(ти́)</i> (trjas(tí)) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wagtail</li></ol><p>The first part is from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tręsti</i> (“shake”), while the second part is from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫzъ</i> (“butt, rump”). Literally, “rump shaker” (because of the movements this bird does with its tail)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shake</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to jolt, to shake (of a vehicle, etc.)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfective only) to be shaking</li><li>to shake out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tręstì</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake</li><li>to tremble, to shiver</li><li>to be jolted, to ride something jolty</li><li>to tremble <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>трясти́</i> (trjastí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to shake (once)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) babble, chatter (idle talk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruffling, disheveling</li><li>blowing around (<i>intransitive, of the wind</i>)</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) beating, flaying, thrashing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fraying</li><li>pulling (<i>of hair, ears, etc.</i>)</li><li>racking (<i>of fever</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) patting, stroking</li><li>scutching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scolding, vilifying</li></ol><p><i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tri-, triple (three)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three years</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>год</i> (god) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) trihedron</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “three-side-er”. Compare Ukrainian <i>тригра́нник</i> (tryhránnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-day</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-wheeled</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-room</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ко́мната</i> (kómnata) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>triple</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>крат</i> (krat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>three-year period</li><li>third anniversary, third birthday</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-year</li><li>three-year-old</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mosin-Nagant rifle</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ли́ния</i> (línija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from три ли́нии (tri línii, “3 lines (in the historical sense of tenths of an inch)”), the rifle's caliber (7.62 mm in metric units).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-liter</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>литр</i> (litr) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-dimensional</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-month (lasting three months)</li><li>three-month-old</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>ме́сяц</i> (mésjac) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-week (lasting three weeks)</li><li>three-week-old</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>неде́ля</i> (nedélja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-legged</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having three windows</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>окно́</i> (oknó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-hundredth, 300th</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a wounded person; WIA</li></ol><p><i>трёхсо́т</i> (trjoxsót) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p><p>From <i>груз 300</i> (gruz 300).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-sided</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) three-party; tripartite</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) three-stringed</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>струна́</i> (struná, “string”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) nonstandard form of <i>треуго́лка</i> (treugólka, “tricorn hat”)</li></ol><p>From <i>трё̀хуго́л(ьный)</i> (trjòxugól(ʹnyj), “triangular”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, colloquial noun-forming suffix), likely as a calque of German <i>Dreispitz</i> or French <i>tricorne</i> (“tricorn”), from Latin <i>tricornis</i> (“three-horned”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal&#32;or&#32;archaic</i>) nonstandard form of <i>треуго́льник</i> (treugólʹnik, “triangle”)</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>-уго́льник</i> (-ugólʹnik), literally “three-angle-er”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-levelled; three-tiered</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>у́ровень</i> (úrovenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tricolor, three-coloured/colored</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) three hours</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-tiered</li></ol><p>From <i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>я́рус</i> (járus) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) three-storey building</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) four-letter word (swearword)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-storeyed, having three storeys</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;euphemistic) with swearwords</li></ol><p><i>трёх-</i> (trjox-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) three roubles, three-rouble note</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) a coin worth three kopeks</li><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) a banknote worth three rubles</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) a silver coin worth one kopek (which amounted to three and a half kopeks in terms of Assignation ruble)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тре́ба</i> (tréba).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>psst! (listen to this!)</li><li>shhh!, shh! (quiet!)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>psst! (listen to this!)</li><li>shhh!, shh! (quiet!) hush</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) the sound of a train horn</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) toilet, WC, restroom, lavatory</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) dress, attire</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) toilet, grooming (<i>care for one's personal appearance</i>)</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) dressing table</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>toilettes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet tissue, toilet paper, toilet roll (paper on a roll)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eau de toilette, toilet water (lightly scented perfume)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) toilet</li></ol><p><i>туале́т</i> (tualét) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>туале́тный стол</i> (tualétnyj stol)</li><li>dressing table (the diminutive form is more common)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tuba</li><li>tube</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tuba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) tuberculosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tuberculosis</li><li>tubercular (having tuberculosis)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuberculosis patient</li></ol><p><i>туберкулёз</i> (tuberkuljóz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuberose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>juvenile&#32;slang</i>) synonym of <i>туале́т</i> (tualét)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tube, barrel</li><li>drawtube microscope</li><li>snoot (theater&#58; cylindrical or conical attachment on a spotlight)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tuvan</li></ol><p>From <i>Тува́</i> (Tuvá, “Tuva”) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij, relational adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tightly</li><li>slowly</li></ol><p><i>туго́й</i> (tugój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tight, taut (under high tension)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тугъ</i> (tugŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫgъ</i> (“hard, strong”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tengʰ-</i> (extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard of hearing (having difficulty hearing)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stiffness, tightness</li></ol><p><i>туго́й</i> (tugój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) tugrik</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any currency (<i>jokingly</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolian <i>төгрөг</i> (tögrög).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(to) there, that way, thither</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѫда</i> (tǫda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back and forth (from one place to another and back again)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back and forth (from one place to another and back again)</li><li>around, about (from one place to another, to many different places)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>тудо́й</i> (tudój)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;humorous) there, thither, that way, alternative form of <i>туда́</i> (tudá)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>туди́</i> (tudý), Belarusian <i>туды́</i> (tudý), Polish <i>tędy</i>, Czech <i>tudy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) helluva lot (<i>Russian spoonerism for ху́ева ту́ча (xújeva túča)</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) chain-tug, tug</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>toueur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small box made of birchbark</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Komi-Zyrian. Compare to Finnish <i>tuohinen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ту́ес</i> (tújes): a small box made of birchbark</li></ol><p><i>ту́ес</i> (tújes) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>туго́й</i> (tugój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ту́го</i> (túgo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push; to strain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to exert oneself; to make an effort</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫžiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pea jacket, mess jacket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>toujours</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) ace (card with a single spot)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bigwig</li><li>two-oar dinghy</li><li>ass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>tuz</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>two</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of diamonds (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of spades (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of clubs (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of hearts (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) native, aborigine, indigene</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aboriginal (original&#59; indigenous)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) fat, lard</li><li>fertilizer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тукъ</i> (tukŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tukъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>knock-knock (verbal knocking sound)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(India, Thailand) a tuk-tuk (motorized rickshaw)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic</p><p>Borrowed from Thai <i>ตุ๊ก ๆ</i> (dtúk-dtúk) or <i>ตุ๊กตุ๊ก</i> (dtúk-dtúk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toucan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>toucan</i> or Spanish <i>tucán</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tukulan (a kind of sand dune, a relief form shaped by aeolian processes of the Central Yakutian Lowland, Yakutia, Russian Federation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yakut <i>тукулаан</i> (tukulaan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thulium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) torso, trunk, body</li></ol><p><i>ту́лово</i> (túlovo) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin coat, overcoat, tulup (Russian-style long warm sheepskin&#47;hareskin coat with a collar)</li><li>(sports,&#32;figure skating) toe loop</li></ol><p>Unclear. There are two theories:</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>toe loop</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tula</li><li>from Tula</li></ol><p><i>Ту́ла</i> (Túla) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The top section of a hat, above the brim.</li></ol><p>From туловище.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resident of Tula</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>punch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) fog</li></ol><p>Attested since the 17th century.</p><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source. Ultimately, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tuman</i> (“mist, fog”), or from Avestan <i>𐬛𐬎𐬥𐬨𐬀𐬥</i> (dunman, “fog, mist”), <i>𐬛𐬎𐬎𐬄𐬥𐬨𐬀𐬥</i> (duuąnman, “cloud”), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;dʰuHmás</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;dʰuHmás</i>. Doublet of <i>дым</i> (dym), which was inherited.</p><p>Compare Turkish <i>duman</i>, Kazakh <i>тұман</i> (tūman), Tatar <i>томан</i> (toman), Persian دود (dud): دودمان (dudmân, “smoke, soot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to obscure, to dim, to cloud, to fog</li></ol><p><i>тума́н</i> (tumán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obscurely, nebulously, vaguely, unclearly</li></ol><p><i>тума́нный</i> (tumánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>тума́нный</i> (tumánnyj): fogginess, dimness</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) nebula</li></ol><p><i>тума́нный</i> (tumánnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fog</li><li>foggy, misty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) obscure, nebulous, vague, unclear</li></ol><p><i>тума́н</i> (tumán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupboard, cabinet, pedestal</li><li>advertising pillar</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) stone (<i>to bind horses</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fat/rotund person, tub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tumbe</i> (“grave, coffin”), from Vulgar Latin <i>tumba</i> (“grave”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>бюст</i> (bjust) <&lt; Latin <i>bustum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightstand, bedside table, bedside cabinet</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ту́мба</i> (túmba): (low) cabinet</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) short, fat woman</li></ol><p><i>ту́мба</i> (túmba) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tun (large cask especially for wine, commonly containing 252 gallons)</li></ol><p>Probably from English <i>tun</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tungusic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tundra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kildin Sami <i>тӯндрэ</i> (tūndre), the accusative/genitive form of <i>тӯндар</i> (tūndar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tundra</li></ol><p><i>ту́ндра</i> (túndra) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cygnus columbianus</i>, tundra swan</li></ol><p>From тундровый and лебедь.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuna</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idler, parasite, freeloader</li></ol><p>Compound formed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тоуне</i> (tune) 'given freely; for no purpose' (also found in the adverb <i>вту́не</i> (vtúne)), and <i>&#42;я́дец</i> (&#42;jádec), 'eater' (related to <i>еда́</i> (jedá, “food”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitism (about people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tunic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tunica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Tunisian</li></ol><p><i>Туни́с</i> (Tunís) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tunnel</li><li>stretched ear piercing, flesh tunnel, gauge</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting, from Latin <i>tunna</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Slovene <i>predor</i> (i.e. **предо́р**, akin to <i>драть</i> (dratʹ), <i>(раз)до́р</i> ((raz)dór)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tuna</li></ol><p><i>тун(е́)ц</i> (tun(é)c) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to make a stupid mistake, to screw up, to mess up, to have a brain fart</li></ol><p><i>тупи́ть</i> (tupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a stupid, dumb man</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>тупо́й</i> (tupój)</li></ol><p>From <i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become blunt or more blunt, to become dull or more dull</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figuratively) to become dumb or dumber</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) (extreme) stupidity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puffin</li><li>dead end, impasse (a path that goes nowhere)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) impasse</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Compare Bulgarian <i>тъпоклюна кайра</i> (tǎpokljuna kajra).</p><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) puffin</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) dead end; dead-end</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) hopeless, prospectless (<i>of a position or situation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ту́пик</i> (túpik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p><p><i>тупи́к</i> (tupík) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dull, to blunt</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to lower (one's eyes, gaze, etc.) (so as not to look at someone)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to make a stupid mistake, to screw up, to mess up, to have a brain fart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫpiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dummy, blockhead, dimwit, dunce (stupid person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dully, stupidly, with a stupid air, unthinkingly, thoughtlessly, blindly, stubbornly, obstinately</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) literally, just</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blockheaded, pigheaded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, blunt</li><li>obtuse</li><li>dull, stupid</li><li>apathetic</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тупъ</i> (tupŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) dumb as a doornail</li></ol><p>Literally, “dumb (dull) as a felt boot (valenok)”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blunt-nosed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoologic</i>) blunt snout, blunt-nosed</li><li>stupid, dumb</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ры́ло</i> (rýlo) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bluntness</li><li>dullness, stupidity</li><li>senselessness, vacancy</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) dullness (<i>of sound when auscultating</i>)</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obtuse-angled (of a triangle)</li></ol><p><i>тупо́й</i> (tupój) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obtuse triangle (triangle with an obtuse angle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Estonia,&#32;slang,&#32;colloquial) snus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Estonian <i>tups</i>, with added <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round</li><li>tour</li><li>turn (dance)</li><li>cairn</li><li>aurochs</li><li>tur (a couple of species&#58; West Caucasian goat (<i>Capra caucasica</i>) and East Caucasian goat (<i>Capra cylindricornis</i>))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tour</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tȗrъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;táwros</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chess,&#32;informal) rook, castle</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tower</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tour</i>, from Latin <i>turris</i> (“tower; rook”) (whence also English <i>tower</i>, English <i>turret</i>). Doublet of <i>туре́ль</i> (turélʹ).</p><p>Compare also Ukrainian <i>тура</i> (tura).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Тура)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tura (an urban village in Evenk Autonomous Okrug, Russia)</li><li>Tura (an urban village in Krasnoyarsk Krai, Russia)</li><li>Tura (a river in Sverdlovsk Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Tura (a river in Tyumen Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Tura (a river in Chita Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Tura (a river in Tomsk Oblast, Russia)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tourist camp, tourist base, tourist center (type of campsite with accommodation, food, cultural and recreational facilities)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>тури́стская ба́за</i> (turístskaja báza, “tourist base”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbine (rotary machine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>turbine</i>, from Latin <i>turbo, turbinem</i> (“circular movement, top, reel, spindle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) turbine</li></ol><p>From <i>турби́на</i> (turbína) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>turbo-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbocharging</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>turbine</i>, from Latin <i>turbō</i> (“circular movement”) + <i>наддува́ть</i> (nadduvátʹ, “to pressurize by blowing”), from <i>над-</i> (nad-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>дуть</i> (dutʹ, “to blow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) turbojet</li></ol><p>From <i>ту̀рбо-</i> (tùrbo-, “turbo”) +&lrm; <i>реакти́вный</i> (reaktívnyj, “jet-propelled”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbojet engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turbojet</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>ту̀рбореакти́вный</i> (tùrboreaktívnyj), from <i>ту̀рбо-</i> (tùrbo-, “turbo”) + <i>реакти́вный</i> (reaktívnyj, “jet-propelled”). By surface analysis, <i>ту̀рбо-</i> (tùrbo-) +&lrm; <i>реа́ктор</i> (reáktor).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Turgenev (surname of several notable Russians and Soviets&#59; especially Ivan Turgenev, Russian writer and poet)</li></ol><p><i>Турге́нев</i> (Turgénev) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Turkish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkish (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ту́рция</i> (Túrcija) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij), with unexpected epenthesis of stressed -е́-.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tourism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tourisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tourist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tourist, touristic</li></ol><p><i>тури́ст</i> (turíst) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tourists, traveller's; touristic, travelling</li></ol><p><i>тури́ст</i> (turíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jezve, Turkish coffee pot (<i>with a long handle and without lid</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Turkestan</li></ol><p><i>Туркеста́н</i> (Turkestán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkmen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Turkmen</li></ol><p><i>Туркме́ния</i> (Turkménija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tour (of politician, businessman, artist, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tournée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>turnips</i>, reanalyzed as a singular.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sports,&#32;gymnastics) horizontal bar</li></ol><p>French <i>tourner</i> (“to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnstile</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) tourniquet, garrot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tourniquet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tournament, tourney (series of games)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tournament</li></ol><p><i>турни́р</i> (turnír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turk (a person from Turkey or of Turkish origin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>travel agency</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>туристи́ческая фи́рма</i> (turistíčeskaja fírma, “tourist firm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkish boy, young Turk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of bean pickle, known in Russia to be part of the Armenian cuisine.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>թուռշի</i> (tʻuṙši) or <i>թուրշի</i> (tʻurši), from Persian ترشی (torši).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth&#32;slang</i>) abbreviation of <i>тусо́вка</i> (tusóvka, “party, gettogether”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to have fun together, to hang out</li></ol><p>See <i>тусо́вка</i> (tusóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;rare) darkness, fog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuskъ</i> (“fog, darkness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dimly</li></ol><p><i>ту́склый</i> (túsklyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dim, dull, dingy</li><li>lackluster, lusterless (<i>of eyes</i>)</li><li>dark, poorly lit</li></ol><p>From <i>туск</i> (tusk, “darkness, fog”) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun beetle (<i>Amara</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ту́склый</i> (túsklyj, “dim, dull, dark”) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow dim, to dull, to lose its lustre</li><li>to pale</li><li>to wane</li><li>to pale <i>(before)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tusculan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;intransitive) synonym of <i>тусова́ться</i> (tusovátʹsja); to have fun together, to hang out</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to maintain informal communication with a business partner at the company's expense</li></ol><p>See <i>тусо́вка</i> (tusóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hang out, to gather in a group for passing time or for a conversation</li></ol><p>See <i>тусо́вка</i> (tusóvka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informal party for young people, get-together, "tusovka"</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a group of people with a common interest, such as musicians, artists</li></ol><p>Unclear. Thought to be related to <i>тасова́ть</i> (tasovátʹ, “to shuffle (cards)”), which is from French <i>tasser</i> (“to pile together”), from <i>tas</i> (“pile, heap”). Alternatively, thought to derive from French <i>tous</i> (“everyone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a club kid, a person who often attends hangouts, especially teenager’s hangouts</li><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) a person who is a member of a coterie</li></ol><p>тусоваться + -щик</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;figuratively) female who is a member of a coterie</li></ol><p><i>тусо́в(щик)</i> (tusóv(ščik)) +&lrm; <i>-щица</i> (-ščica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>location</i>) here</li><li>(<i>time</i>) then, at this point</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulberry (fruit or tree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>туто</i> (tuto), <i>тутъ</i> (tutŭ, “here; thereto; herewith”), <i>ту</i> (tu, “there; thereto; then; here, herewith”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuto</i>, <i>&#42;tu</i>.</p><p>Via Turkic from Persian توت (tut).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>right, ready at hand, at once, immediately, instantly, right then, just then, in no time at all, then and there.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) already there; there he is, they they are, etc. (said about someone or something that appears very quickly, on time or too early)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (exactly) here, right here</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>тут</i> (tut, “mulberry”)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) alternative form of <i>тут</i> (tut, “here”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тут</i> (tut).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tufa, tuff</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>tōfus</i> (via Italian <i>tufo</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ту́фля</i> (túflja): (small) shoe, especially for women or children</li></ol><p><i>ту́фля</i> (túflja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low shoe (not covering above the ankle)</li></ol><p>From the Middle Low German <i>tuffele</i> (compare Low German <i>Tuffel</i> (“shoe”)), from older <i>pantuffel</i>, from French <i>pantoufle</i> (“slipper”). Compare Norwegian and Danish <i>tøffel</i> (“slipper”), Swedish <i>toffel</i> (“slipper”), Latvian <i>tupele</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) bullshit, nonsense, poppycock, rubbish</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) fake, fraud</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>slang</i>) Cheating on quota or bureaucratic requirements, as for example in labour camps, in state enterprises of planned economies, or in municipal affairs.</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>taffetas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rotten and foul-smelling</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) musty, stale, stagnant (<i>of water and air</i>)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bad, hopeless, sad, dead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫxlъ</i>, also found as Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъxlъ</i> (as in dialectal <i>то́хлый</i> (tóxlyj, “rotten and foul-smelling”)), which may represent an earlier variant. Compare <i>ту́хнуть</i> (túxnutʹ, “to become rotten and foul-smelling”), Belarusian <i>тхліць</i> (txlicʹ, “to cause to spoil”), <i>ту́хнуць</i> (túxnucʹ, “to become rotten and foul-smelling”), Slovene <i>zatȯ́hǝl</i> (“giving off dampness”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), <i>otȯ́hlica</i> (“stuffiness”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), <i>otȯ́hniti se</i> (“to spoil”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>stuchlý</i> (“stale, rotten”), <i>tuchnout</i> (“to fade”), Slovak <i>stuchlý</i> (“stale, rotten”) and <i>stuchnúť</i> (“to fade”), Polish <i>stęchły</i> (“musty”), <i>tęchnąć</i> (“to become rotten and foul-smelling”), Upper Sorbian <i>tuchły</i> (“rotten and foul-smelling”), <i>tuchnyć</i> (“to become rotten and foul-smelling”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to die out, to wink out (of light, fire)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to grow empty, to grow lifeless (of look, eyes)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to die down, to grow weak, to diminish</li><li>to go bad, to spoil (of food)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to stagnate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tuxnǫti</i>. Related to <i>туши́ть</i> (tušítʹ).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫxnǫti</i> or <i>&#42;tuxnǫti</i>, related to <i>ту́хлый</i> (túxlyj). Possibly related to Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>uhn tiss uhn tiss, boots (and) cats (and) boots (and) cats (the sound of a typical four-to-the-floor dance music beat)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) rain cloud; dark, heavy cloud</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) moving multitude; cloud, host</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>туча</i> (tuča), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tǫča</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tenk-</i> (“to contract”). Compare the Slavic Old Church Slavonic <i>тѫча</i> (tǫča), Ukrainian <i>ту́ча</i> (túča), Polish <i>tęcza</i> and the Non-Slavic Gothic <i>𐌸𐌴𐌹𐍈𐍉𐌽</i> (þeiƕōn) and Old Norse <i>þéttr</i>.</p><p>Unrelated to Russian <i>ту́чный</i> (túčnyj) (which is related to <i>тук</i> (tuk) and historically had nonnasal vowel <i>у</i> (u)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) (rain) cloud</li></ol><p><i>ту́ча</i> (túča) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ту́ча</i> (túča, “cloud”): cloudlet, small (rain) cloud</li></ol><p><i>ту́ча</i> (túča) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow stout/fat, to put on flesh</li><li><i>(earth)</i> to become fertile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adiposity</li><li>avoirdupois</li><li>corpulence, fat, obesity, portliness, stoutness</li></ol><p><i>ту́чный</i> (túčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat, obese, corpulent (extremely overweight)</li><li>large (of grains)</li><li>dense, thick, plentiful (of grass)</li><li>fertile (of land)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tučьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>тук</i> (tuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Unrelated to Russian <i>ту́ча</i> (túča) (which historically had nasal vowel <i>ѫ</i> (ǫ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) flourish</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tusch</i>, probably from French <i>touche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carcass</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) fat person</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) meathead, stupid muscular person</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>тук</i> (tuk, “fat, lard”), <i>тыть</i> (tytʹ, “тучнеть, to grow stout/fat, to put on flesh”) as Russian <i>кры́ша</i> (krýša, “roof”) related to Russian <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ, “to cover, to roof”) (Vasmer, Shansky, Tsyganenko). Alternatively related to <i>ту́хнуть</i> (túxnutʹ) (mentioned by Vasmer); or to <i>то́щий</i> (tóščij), or from Turkic (Chernych).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shade, to make shadows on (a painting)</li><li>to blur, to make unclear or dim (the outlines of an object)</li><li>to muffle, to make less distinct or noticeable (a sound or one's character)</li></ol><p><i>тушь</i> (tušʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be shy, to be embarrassed</li></ol><p><i>тушева́ть</i> (tuševátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extinguishing, putting out (of fire)</li><li>stewing, braising</li></ol><p><i>туши́ть</i> (tušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish, to put out</li><li>to turn off (light)</li><li>to quench</li><li>to suppress</li><li>to stew, to braise (in cooking)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tušiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ту́ша</i> (túša): trunk, body, carcass (the body of a recently killed animal, typically a small one)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bod (a person's body, especially if fat or clumsy)</li><li>(<i>photography&#32;slang</i>) camera body without a lens</li><li>(aviation&#32;slang,&#32;diminutive) Tupolev aircraft</li></ol><p><i>ту́ша</i> (túša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p><p>From <i>Ту</i> (Tu) +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška), based on the Tupolev design office prefix.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jerboa (small rodent)</li></ol><p>Attested at least since 1780s, akin to later attested Russian <i>тушкан</i> (tuškan), from Kazakh <i>тышқан</i> (tyşqan, “mouse”), from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;sïčgan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>India ink</li><li>mascara</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Tusche</i>, from French <i>touche</i>, from <i>toucher</i> (“to touch”). The sense “mascara” also perhaps follows German <i>Wimperntusche</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinned stewed meat (usually of pork and corned beef)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stewed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>tree</i>) thuja, thuya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taekwondo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>태권도</i> (taegwondo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>telegram</i>) dot (punctuation)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>то́чка</i> (tóčka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) teshuva</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>תְּשׁוּבָה</i> (t'shuvá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;literary) diligence, assiduity, zeal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>carefully, thoroughly</li></ol><p><i>тща́тельный</i> (tščátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carefulness, thoroughness, care</li></ol><p><i>тща́тельный</i> (tščátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful, thorough (meticulous)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>тъщати</i> (tŭščati, “to hurry, to rush”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj). Probably related to <i>тоска́</i> (toská). Compare <i>дото́шный</i> (dotóšnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble, frail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тщедушнъ</i> (tštedušnŭ), a compound of <i>тъщь</i> (tŭštĭ) (compare <i>то́щий</i> (tóščij)) and <i>душа</i> (duša) (compare <i>душа́</i> (dušá)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vanity, vainglory (excessive vanity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тъщеславьѥ</i> (tŭšteslavĭje, “vainglory”), itself a calque of Ancient Greek <i>κενοδοξία</i> (kenodoxía, “vainglory”). By surface analysis, <i>тще-</i> (tšče-) +&lrm; <i>слава</i> (slava) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vain, vainglorious, conceited, arrogant</li></ol><p><i>тщесла́вие</i> (tščeslávije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) vanity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тъщета</i> (tŭšteta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъščeta</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;tъščь</i> (whence <i>то́щий</i> (tóščij)) + <i>&#42;-ota</i>.</p><p>Further back, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tweskʷ-</i> (“desert, barren”). Compare Sanskrit <i>तुच्छ</i> (tuccha, “empty, vain”), Latin <i>tesqua</i> (“desert”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain (without success)</li></ol><p><i>тще́тный</i> (tščétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futility, vainness, uselessness</li></ol><p><i>тще́тный</i> (tščétnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>futile, fruitless, vain, unavailing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тъщетьнъ</i> (tŭštetĭnŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to endeavor, to strive, to try (<i>usually in vain</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тъщатисѧ</i> (tŭščatisę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tъščati</i>.</p></html>:/tɨ/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>familiar</i>) second-person singular, nominative case: you, thou</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тꙑ</i> (ty), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ty</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;túh₂</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>тꙑ</i> (ty).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) alternative form of <i>ти́брить</i> (tíbritʹ); to steal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ты́кание</i> (týkanije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poke, to prod, to jab, to thrust</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to thou, to thee and thou, to treat with familiarity [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tykati (sę)</i>.</p><p><i>ты</i> (ty) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock <i>(against, on)</i> <i>(the door)</i></li><li>to knock on someone's door</li><li>to nuzzle up</li><li>to bustle/fuss about</li></ol><p><i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumpkin, cucurbit</li><li>gourd</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) head</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тꙑкꙑ</i> (tyky), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tyky</i>.</p><p>(<i>head</i>): Compare typologically <i>ре́па</i> (répa), <i>коча́н</i> (kočán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pumpkin</li></ol><p><i>ты́ква</i> (týkva) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back, rear</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rear (the part of an army or fleet which comes last)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) home front</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тꙑлъ</i> (tylŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tylъ</i> (“back of the head, back”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) man serving in rear</li><li>(<i>military</i>) logistics officer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) rear; in the rear</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) logistics</li></ol><p><i>тыл</i> (tyl) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>back, rear</li></ol><p><i>тыл</i> (tyl) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence (especially one made of twigs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тꙑнъ</i> (tynŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tynъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pinch, to nick, to steal</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take for one's own, to misappropriate</li></ol><p>Assumed to be related to Romani <i>те терес</i> or <i>тэ тырдэс</i> ("to hold, to have, to take, to wait"). Established in dictionaries by the 1850s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dialectal,&#32;Crimea) vine bud moth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>tırtır</i>, from Armenian <i>թրթուր</i> (tʻrtʻur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thousand (1000)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>set of thousand</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) one thousand ruble banknote</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tỳsęťa</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tū́ˀsantis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tuHsont-</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тисяча</i> (tysjača), Serbo-Croatian <i>тисућа</i>, <i>tisuća</i>, Slovene <i>tisoč</i>, Czech and Slovak <i>tisíc</i>, Polish <i>tysiąc</i>. Akin to Lithuanian <i>tūkstantis</i>, Latvian <i>tūkstotis</i>, Gothic <i>𐌸𐌿𐍃𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌹</i> (þūsundi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millennium</li></ol><p>From <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Compare Ukrainian <i>тисячолі́ття</i> (tysjačolíttja), Polish <i>tysiąclecie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thousand-year</li><li>thousand-year-old</li></ol><p><i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) milfoil, yarrow (any of several pungent Eurasian herbs of the genus <i>Achillea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiliagon</li></ol><p><i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thousandth part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thousandth</li></ol><p><i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stamen (a flower part that produces pollen)</li></ol><p>Parallel with Bulgarian <i>тичинка</i> (tičinka), Czech <i>tyčinka</i>. Further cognate with Czech <i>tyč</i>, Polish <i>tyka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) poke, jab</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) thing sticking up in the air</li><li>(<i>specialist</i>) short, vertical brick face in masonry</li></ol><p><i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>ты́сяча</i> (týsjača)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dark, darkness</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thousands, a multitude, a host, lots of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тьма</i> (tĭma), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьma</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;timāˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;temH-</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тьма</i> (tĭma), borrowed from a Mongolic language, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;tümen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an obscure and remote place far away from civilization; similar to the idiomatic use of <i>Timbuktu</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>Тмутарака́нь</i> (Tmutarakánʹ), a medieval Russian town in what is now Krasnodar Krai. In modern Russian, the name sounds like the words for "dark cockroach city" (тьма таракан).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Mimics the sound of spitting.</li><li>fie!; for shame!; nuts!; damn it!, dammit! (<i>expresses strong disgust or disappointment</i>)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, mimics the sound of spitting.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>knock on wood (said to void a jinx)</li><li>(<i>elliptical</i>) Implies something positive, or a hope for something positive.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Т, т.</li><li>The Roman letter T, t.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temakizushi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>手巻き寿司</i> (temakizushi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tempura</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>天麩羅</i> (tenpura).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tengu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>天狗</i> (tengu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teriyaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>照り焼き</i> (てりやき, teriyaki).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(UK politics,&#32;politics) Thatcherism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Thatcherism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) exclamation expressing surprise, bewilderment or disappointment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tyubeteika</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>түбәтәй</i> (tübätäy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collapsible) tube (container)</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) A man with neglectful or manipulative qualities</li></ol><p>From French <i>tube</i> +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) collapsible tube</li></ol><p><i>тю́бик</i> (tjúbik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>package, bale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rap, to peck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peck</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tulle (a type of netting)</li></ol><p><i>тюль</i> (tjulʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal (pinniped)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) A clumsy person, an oaf</li></ol><p>From a Sami language. Compare Northern Sami <i>dealljá</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal calf, baby seal</li></ol><p><i>тюле́нь</i> (tjulénʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tulle (kind of silk lace)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>tulle</i>, from French <i>Tulle</i>, a town named after Latin <i>Tūtēla</i> (a Roman deity), from Latin <i>tūtēla</i>, from Latin <i>tueor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tulip</li></ol><p>From obsolete French <i>tulipan</i>, from Italian <i>tulipano</i>, from Ottoman Turkish دلبند (dülbend, “turban”) (Turkish <i>tülbent</i>), from Persian دلبند (dolband, “turban”).</p><p>Doublet of <i>тюрба́н</i> (tjurbán, “turban”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Tulipa lehmanniana</i>, a species of tulip.</li></ol><p><i>тюльпа́н</i> (tjulʹpán, “tulip”) +&lrm; <i>Ле́ман</i> (Léman, “Lehmann”), a calque of translingual <i>Tulipa lehmanniana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tulip</li></ol><p><i>тюльпа́н</i> (tjulʹpán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тюльпа́н</i> (tjulʹpán, “tulip”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tyumen (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Тюме́нь</i> (Tjuménʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turban</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>turban</i>, from Turkish <i>tülbent</i> (“muslin; cheesecloth”), from Persian دولبند (dolband). Doublet of <i>тюльпа́н</i> (tjulʹpán). The second part of the word ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) prison</li></ol><p><i>тюрьма́</i> (tjurʹmá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jailer (one who guards inmates)</li></ol><p><i>тюрьма́</i> (tjurʹmá) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thuringian (member of an ancient Germanic tribe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turk (a speaker of the various Turkic languages)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Turkic</li></ol><p><i>тюрк</i> (tjurk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turk (member of any Turkic ethnic group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison, jail</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тюрма</i> (tjurma), traditionally believed to be derived from Polish <i>turma</i>, from Middle High German <i>turm</i>, <i>turn</i>, from Old French <i>&#42;torn</i>, from Latin <i>turrem</i>/<i>turrim</i>, accusative singular of <i>turris</i>, whence English <i>tower</i>. But more likely from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;türmä</i> (“dungeon”); compare Tatar <i>төрмә</i> (törmä, “prison”), Kyrgyz <i>түрмө</i> (türmö, “prison”).</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>тюрма</i> (tjurma).</p><p>For the fortress and jail meanings relation, compare typologically <i>остро́г</i> (ostróg); <i>бастио́н</i> (bastión), <i>басти́лия</i> (bastílija) (English <i>bastion</i>, <i>bastille</i>); <i>донжо́н</i> (donžón) (English <i>dungeon</i>, <i>donjon</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joint, jug (prison, jail)</li></ol><p><i>тюрь(ма́)</i> (tjurʹ(má)) +&lrm; <i>-я́га</i> (-jága).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>тю́тя</i> (tjútja): small detail, point</li></ol><p><i>тю́тя</i> (tjútja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the dot, on the button</li><li>spot on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) zero, zip, nada, zilch (a gesture by showing a naked fist and forearm raised up with the other arm resting on the epicondyle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) mattress; a sack filled with something soft (e.g. hay) to be used as a mattress</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) wimp, weakling, wuss, coward</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) mattress (retaining wall of brushwood, concrete, etc. to protect the river bed and banks of bridges and dams from erosion)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;Siberia,&#32;animate) sandpiper</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) early type of firearm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>tüşäk</i> (“mattress”); dialectally <i>тюша́к</i> (tjušák). Modern form probably influenced by <i>тюфя́к</i> (tjufják, “early type of firearm”) (see below).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тюфякъ</i> (tjufjakŭ), from Old Turkic <i>tüfäk</i> (“tube, gun, crossbow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>тебя́</i> (tebjá)</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>тебя́</i> (tebjá) and probably not a direct descendant of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tę</i> or Old Church Slavonic <i>тѧ</i> (tę).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) bow-wow, yap, yip (sound a pup or a small dog makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yap, to yelp (of a small dog, to bark in a non-growly manner)</li><li>(<i>disparagingly</i>) to object, to protest aloud, to talk back, to complain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yap, to yelp (of a small dog, to bark in a non-growly manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draft/draught, pull, draw (in a pipe, tunnel, chimney)</li><li>traction, pulling, hauling</li><li>thrust</li><li>pull rod, connecting rod</li><li>stay, brace</li><li>laboratory hood</li><li>bent, desire</li><li>(<i>hunting</i>) roding, flight of woodcock in mating season</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тяга</i> (tjaga), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęgъ</i> or <i>&#42;tęga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>control rod</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to pull, to drag</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to carry around (especially something heavy)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;dated) to take to task, to bring to justice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tędzati</i>, <i>&#42;tęgati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly in the negative) to compete (to be better at or outdo someone)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) to litigate, to have legal proceedings</li></ol><p><i>тяга́ть</i> (tjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prime mover, tractor (tractor-trailer), semi</li></ol><p>From <i>тяга́ть</i> (tjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) system by which peasants paid taxes through work, in the Russian state between the 15th and early 18th centuries</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;dated) burden</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;relational) serfish</li></ol><p><i>тя́гл-</i> (tjágl-) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) traction; tractive</li></ol><p><i>тя́га</i> (tjága) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) botheration, protracted annoyance</li></ol><p><i>тя́га</i> (tjága) +&lrm; <i>мота́ть</i> (motátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́та</i> (-óta) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), probably influenced by <i>бегемот</i> (begemot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burdensome, onerous</li><li>painful, distressing</li></ol><p><i>тя́гость</i> (tjágostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden</li></ol><p><i>тяг-</i> (tjag-) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) burden, weight, tie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>тѧгота</i> (tęgota). Related to <i>тя́жкий</i> (tjážkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gravity, gravitation</li><li>pull, attraction</li></ol><p><i>тяготе́ть</i> (tjagotétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be strongly attracted, to have a propensity/bent, to be drawn, to be strongly attached</li><li>to hang <i>(over)</i>, to weigh <i>(upon)</i>, to threaten</li></ol><p><i>тя́гота</i> (tjágota) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a burden <i>(on)</i></li><li>to oppress</li></ol><p><i>тя́гота</i> (tjágota) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel burdened [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘by something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>тяготи́ть</i> (tjagotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viscosity (the state of being viscous)</li></ol><p><i>тягу́ч(ий)</i> (tjagúč(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viscous, viscid, syrupy, malleable, ductile</li><li><i>(song etc)</i> slow, leisured, leisurely</li></ol><p><i>тяг-</i> (tjag-) +&lrm; <i>-у́чий</i> (-účij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;dated) suit, lawsuit</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) argument, dispute</li></ol><p><i>тяга́ть</i> (tjagátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба</i> (-ba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>тяжело́</i> (tjaželó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow heavy, to increase in weight</li><li>to put on weight, to grow sluggish</li><li><i>(eyes, eyelids)</i> to become heavy with sleep</li></ol><p><i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>heavily, heftily</li><li>difficultly, hard</li><li>grievously, laboriously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy bomber</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) heavyweight (boxer)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) draft horse</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) heavy-duty vehicle</li></ol><p><i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy, weighty</li><li>massive, cumbersome</li><li>graceless, unsubtle</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) heavyweight</li></ol><p><i>тяжёлый</i> (tjažólyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вес</i> (ves) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draft horse, carthorse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) heavily armed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaviness</li><li>weight; load; burden</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) gravity</li><li>seriousness</li><li>painfulness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęžestь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>тя́жкий</i> (tjážkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, grave</li><li>terrible, painful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тѧжькъ</i> (tęžĭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęžьkъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;tingús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tengʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sorely, gravely, heavily</li><li>with difficulty</li></ol><p>From <i>тя́жкий</i> (tjážkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) heavy artillery (large and powerful artillery, often large-caliber and large-distance)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heavy artillery, big guns</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavy industry (production of products which are either heavy in weight or in the processes leading to their production)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy</li><li>difficult, hard, tough</li><li>laborious</li><li>serious</li><li>severe, grave</li><li>grievous, oppressive, painful, sad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęželъ</i>. Related to <i>тя́жкий</i> (tjážkij); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow to act, sluggish, lazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anime and manga fandom</i>) -chan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) girl, young woman</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) one's female romantic partner, generally young and attractive; one's girlfriend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ちゃん</i> (-chan, diminutive suffix), originally through anime fandom.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull, to draw, to haul, to drag</li><li>to lay (a cable)</li><li>to draw (a wire)</li><li>to drawl, to drag out (speak slowly)</li><li>to drag out, to delay, to protract, to procrastinate</li><li>to make someone go, to force to go</li><li>to weigh</li><li>to draw up</li><li>to squeeze, to extort</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be drawn to, to long for, to miss</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to feel an urge to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tęgnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drag out (to do something slowly and inconclusively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch</li><li>to stretch out, to stretch oneself</li><li>to stretch, to extend</li><li>to drag on; to crawl, to hang heavy</li><li>to last out, to hold out</li><li>to reach (for), to reach out (for); to strive (after)</li><li>to drift</li></ol><p><i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sticky soft candy/sweet (originally made from milk and sugar)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something (especially boring) that drags for a long time, red tape</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who lingers, procrastinates</li></ol><p><i>тяну́ть</i> (tjanútʹ, “drag, draw”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Tianjin</li></ol><p>From <i>Тяньцзи́нь</i> (Tjanʹczínʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) willy-nilly, slapdash (hastily or haphazardly)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) incompetently, half-assed</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>ляп</i> (ljap), of onomatopoeic origin. Compare <i>тяпать</i> (tjapatʹ), <i>ляпать</i> (ljapatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoe (agricultural tool)</li></ol><p>Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tępati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(familiar,&#32;childish) daddy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tetę</i>, cf. Ukrainian <i>та́то</i> (táto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>namesake (person, place or thing having the same name as another)</li></ol><p>From <i>тёза</i> (tjóza) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьzъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heifer (cow)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) chick</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>телёнок</i> (teljónok, “calf”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heifer (young cow)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) chick (young woman)</li></ol><p><i>тёлка</i> (tjólka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(astronomy,&#32;astrophysics,&#32;particle physics,&#32;cosmology) dark matter (<i>all senses</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately a calque of German <i>Dunkle Materie</i>, from <i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj, “dark”) +&lrm; <i>мате́рия</i> (matérija, “matter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(herpetology,&#32;sensu stricto) northern dusky salamander (<i>Desmognathus fuscus</i>).</li><li>(herpetology,&#32;sensu lato) dusky salamander (<i>Desmognathus</i>).</li></ol><p>From <i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj, “dark”) +&lrm; <i>салама́ндра</i> (salamándra, “salamander”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(astronomy,&#32;astrophysics) dark energy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark cerise</li></ol><p><i>тёмн(ый)</i> (tjómn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вишнёвый</i> (višnjóvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark-green</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark brown</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кори́чневый</i> (koríčnevyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark red</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark blond</li></ol><p><i>тёмн(ый)</i> (tjómn(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ру́сый</i> (rúsyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>auburn (reddish-brown)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark gray</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>се́рый</i> (séryj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark blue, navy blue</li></ol><p><i>тёмный</i> (tjómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>си́ний</i> (sínij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aubergine, dark purple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li><li>ignorant, uneducated <i>(person)</i></li><li>obscure, vague</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьmьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;teplъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warm day</li><li>warm weather</li></ol><p>(тёплый) ‘warm’ + (день) ‘day’</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grater</li><li>float (trowel for smoothing plaster)</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) huller (machine for extracting seeds from the heads of clover, flax, etc.)</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) radula</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) squabble</li></ol><p><i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sloe, blackthorn (<i>fruit and tree</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьrnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>тёрн</i> (tjorn, “sloe, blackthorn”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grated</li><li>pounded, kneaded, ground</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) experienced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boards, planks</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tesъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing form of <i>тётя</i> (tjótja): auntie</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) granny, auntie (<i>referring to an older or elderly woman</i>); an adult woman (<i>from a child's perspective or as a form of address like "madam" but much less formal</i>)</li></ol><p><i>тётя</i> (tjótja) +&lrm; <i>-енька</i> (-enʹka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aunt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) woman</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chick, babe, doll, wench</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) monthly visitor, period, the curse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tetъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>тётя</i> (tjótja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auntie (diminutive of aunt)</li></ol><p><i>тётя</i> (tjótja) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aunt</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) woman, lady; auntie</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тета</i> (teta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;teta</i>.</p><p>Compare Polish <i>ciocia</i>, Ukrainian <i>цьо́ця</i> (cʹócja), Belarusian <i>цёця</i> (cjócja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mother of one's wife; mother-in-law</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tьšča</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>те́ло</i> (télo).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>те́м вре́менем</i> (tém vrémenem).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>те́сен</i> (tésen).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>те́сный</i> (tésnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>те́сто</i> (tésto).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>У</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>U</i>/<i>u</i>.</li><li>Wu (<i>language</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>at, by, near</li><li>in the possession of (<i>used to express "to have"</i>)</li><li>belonging to (<i>used to denote ownership of body parts or appendages of objects</i>)</li><li>from, of (in some constructions)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic&#32;<i>у</i>&#32;(<i>u</i>), from Proto-Slavic&#32;<i>&#42;u</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hello (said upon picking up the telephone)</li></ol><p>Literally, “at the (telephone) device”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) on everyone's lips</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ў</i>/<i>ў</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ў</i>/<i>ў</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ў</i>/<i>ў</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ў</i>/<i>ў</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the middle of nowhere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>away</li><li>with a more concrete purpose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;u-</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) abbreviation of <i>уе́здный го́род</i> (ujézdnyj górod)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) initialism of <i>усло́вная едини́ца</i> (uslóvnaja jediníca): conventional unit (<i>Soviet Union: prices displayed in these were sold in foreign currency only</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) UAZ-brand car</li></ol><p><i>УА́З</i> (UÁZ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> Y, y</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to lessen, to reduce</li><li>to shorten</li><li>to narrow</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-ба́вить</i> (-bávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, decrease</li></ol><p><i>уба́вить</i> (ubávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to lessen, to reduce</li><li>to shorten</li><li>to narrow</li></ol><p><i>уба́вить</i> (ubávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lull, to rock to sleep</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>баю́кать</i> (bajúkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lull</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away, to make off, to flee</li><li>to escape</li><li>to boil over (of liquid)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бе́гать</i> (bégatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleeing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>earnestly</li><li>convincingly</li></ol><p><i>убеди́тельный</i> (ubedítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasiveness (capability to persuade)</li></ol><p><i>убеди́тельный</i> (ubedítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convincing, persuasive</li><li>earnest</li></ol><p><i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince</li><li>to persuade, to prevail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оубѣдити</i> (uběditi). Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uběditi</i>, from <i>&#42;běditi</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>convincō</i> (whence English <i>convince</i>) &lt; <i>vincō</i> (akin to <i>victor</i>) (whence English <i>vanquish</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be convinced, to receive evidence</li><li>to make sure, to make certain</li></ol><p><i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away, to make off, to flee</li><li>to escape</li><li>to boil over (of liquid)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бежа́ть</i> (bežátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince</li><li>to persuade</li></ol><p><i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be convinced, to receive evidence</li><li>to make sure, to make certain</li></ol><p><i>убежда́ть</i> (ubeždátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belief, conviction</li><li>persuasion (the act of persuading)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with conviction</li></ol><p><i>убеждённый</i> (ubeždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conviction (firmly held belief)</li></ol><p><i>убеждённый</i> (ubeždjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convinced (<i>of something</i>)</li><li>staunch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asylum, refuge, sanctuary (a state of safety, protection or shelter)</li></ol><p><i>убежа́ть</i> (ubežátʹ, “to flee”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to safeguard <i>(against)</i>, to guard <i>(against)</i>, to keep <i>(from)</i>, to preserve <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>уберег-</i> (ubereg-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to safeguard <i>(against)</i>, to guard <i>(against)</i>, to keep <i>(from)</i>, to preserve <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бере́чь</i> (beréčʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) alternative form of <i>убежа́ть</i> (ubežátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бечь</i> (bečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li><li>to murder</li><li>to beat (in cards)</li><li>to drive to despair</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p><i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hurt/bruise oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grieve <i>(over)</i></li><li>to knock oneself out</li></ol><p><i>убива́ть</i> (ubivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>liturgical, literary</i>) Murder, killing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) murdered</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) ruined, crushed</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) despairing, depressed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) murder victim, murderee</li></ol><p><i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нный</i> (-énnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>killing</li><li>murderous, deadly</li></ol><p><i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder, homicide (the action of killing someone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оубииство</i> (ubiistvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ubьjьstvo</i>. In case of native origin <i>&#42;убе́йство</i> (&#42;ubéjstvo) would be expected, compare <i>семе́йство</i> (seméjstvo) and <i>суде́йство</i> (sudéjstvo). By surface analysis, <i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Also compare Russian <i>уби́вство</i> (ubívstvo) (obsolete) and Ukrainian <i>уби́вство</i> (ubývstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murderer</li><li>assassin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оубиица</i> (ubiica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ubьjьca</i>. In case of native origin <i>&#42;убе́йца</i> (&#42;ubéjca) would be expected. By surface analysis, <i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ца</i> (-ca). Compare Russian <i>уби́вец</i> (ubívec), <i>уби́вца</i> (ubívca) (dialectal) and Ukrainian <i>уби́вця</i> (ubývcja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, to take away</li><li>to strike (nautical)</li><li>to clean up, to tidy, to tidy up</li><li>to put away</li><li>to clear, to clear up, to clear out</li><li>to deck out</li><li>to decorate</li><li>to take in</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-бира́ть</i> (-birátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean up, to tidy up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat it, to clear off</li></ol><p><i>убира́ть</i> (ubirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressed, crushed, broken-hearted</li><li>padded</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead person; killed person, murder victim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li><li>to murder</li><li>to beat (in cards)</li><li>to drive to despair</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оубити</i> (ubiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ubiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ).</p><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>уби́ти</i> (ubýty), Serbo-Croatian <i>ùbiti</i>, Slovene <i>ubíti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to kill two birds with one stone</li></ol><p>Literally, “to kill two hares with one shot”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hurt/bruise oneself</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grieve <i>(over)</i></li><li>to knock oneself out</li></ol><p><i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indulge, to humour, to please, to gratify, to pamper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оублажати</i> (ublažati). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indulge, to humour, to please, to gratify, to pamper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оублажити</i> (ublažiti). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бла́го</i> (blágo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bastard (person born to unmarried parents)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) bastard</li></ol><p>From ублу́док (ublúdok), broken down to <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>блуди́ть</i> (bludítʹ, “to fornicate”) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Its current form was influenced by the word блядь (bljadʹ, “whore”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;relational) bastard</li></ol><p><i>ублю́док</i> (ubljúdok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wretched, poor, poverty-stricken, squalid, miserable, sordid, beggarly</li><li>disabled, crippled</li><li>small-minded, petty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pauper</li><li>disabled person, cripple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ubogъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disability, handicap</li><li>wretchedness, poverty, squalor</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mediocrity, insignificance, poverty (<i>e.g. of thought or ideas</i>)</li></ol><p><i>убо́гий</i> (ubógij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squalor, poverty, wretchedness, beggarliness</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) mutilation, disability</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;figurative) mediocrity, insignificance, poverty (<i>e.g. of thought or ideas</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>убо́гий</i> (ubógij) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) murder</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>уби́ть</i> (ubítʹ), with the same vowel change as in deverbal <i>бой</i> (boj) from <i>бить</i> (bitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) for slaughter, (<i>relational</i>) slaughter</li><li>(<i>military</i>) destructive</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smashing, crushing</li></ol><p><i>убо́й</i> (ubój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) attire, dress</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>убра́ть</i> (ubrátʹ, “to remove, clean, arrange”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) harvest, gathering in</li><li>removal, disposal</li><li>cleaning, tidying up, doing up</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>убра́ть</i> (ubrátʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>убор-</i> (ubor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>убер-</i> (uber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) dressing room</li><li>restroom, lavatory</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;agriculture) harvest</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>убо́рка</i> (ubórka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duster, cleaner (person who dusts or cleans)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>убра́ть</i> (ubrátʹ) + <i>-щик</i> (-ščik), based on the nominal combining stem <i>убор-</i> (ubor-), derived from the present tense stem <i>убер-</i> (uber-) with Indo-European <i>o</i>-vocalization characteristic of deverbal nouns.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decoration, ornamentation</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) attire, dress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove, to take away</li><li>to strike (nautical)</li><li>to clean up, to tidy, to tidy up</li><li>to put away</li><li>to clear, to clear up, to clear out</li><li>to deck out</li><li>to decorate</li><li>to take in</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>брать</i> (bratʹ, “take, get, accept”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean up, to tidy up</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beat it, to clear off</li></ol><p><i>убра́ть</i> (ubrátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decrease, reduction, waning (<i>action or result</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) departure, leaving, going away, disappearance (<i>action or result</i>)</li></ol><p><i>убыва́ть</i> (ubyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to decrease; <i>(water)</i> to become lower, to subside, to go down; <i>(moon)</i> to wane, to be on the wane</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave</li></ol><p><i>убы́ть</i> (ubýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminution, decrease</li><li>subsidence</li><li>(<i>military</i>) losses, casualties</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speed up, to hasten <i>transitive</i></li></ol><p><i>убыстри́ть</i> (ubystrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) loss, damage (negative result on balance)</li></ol><p><i>убы́ть</i> (ubýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprofitable, disadvantageous</li></ol><p><i>убы́ток</i> (ubýtok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to decrease; <i>(water)</i> to become lower, to subside, to go down; <i>(moon)</i> to wane, to be on the wane</li><li>to leave</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>быть</i> (bytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ubykh</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ubykh (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>убега́ть</i> (ubegátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бѣ́гать</i> (bě́gatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>убеди́ть</i> (ubedítʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>убежа́ть</i> (ubežátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>бѣжа́ть</i> (běžátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>убежда́ть</i> (ubeždátʹ).</li></ol><p><i>убѣди́ть</i> (ubědítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) dear, esteemed (a formal way of addressing someone at the beginning of a letter)</li><li>respectable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admire, to esteem, to have a good opinion of someone</li><li>to honour/honor, to respect, to treat politely</li><li>to abide by, to comply, to observe, to not violate</li><li>to like, to be fond of</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>uważać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>respect, esteem, deference (admiration for a person or entity because of perceived merit)</li></ol><p><i>ува́жить</i> (uvážitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>respectfully</li><li>validly</li></ol><p><i>уважи́тельный</i> (uvažítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valid, good</li><li>respectful, deferential</li></ol><p><i>ува́жить</i> (uvážitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to consider</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to humor (someone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to respect</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grant, to comply with (a request)</li></ol><p>Connected with <i>уважа́ть</i> (uvažátʹ, “to respect”), which is (per Vasmer) borrowed from Polish <i>uważać</i> (“to consider”), in turn connected with German <i>Waage</i> (“scales”). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-важ-</i> (-važ-) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) respect</li></ol><p><i>уваж(а́ть)</i> (uvaž(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ridge <i>(elongated elevation with flat top and gente slopes)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galoot, lubber, bumpkin, lazybones (clumsy or lazy person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify (to give (someone) notice of (something))</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ве́домо</i> (védomo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) notice, notification</li><li>return receipt for a certified correspondence</li></ol><p><i>уве́домить</i> (uvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inform, to notify (to give (someone) notice of (something))</li></ol><p><i>уве́домить</i> (uvédomitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to carry away (by vehicle)</li><li>to abduct, to kidnap</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>везти́</i> (veztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to immortalize (to perpetuate forever in the memory of someone's offspring, especially by praising or glorifying)</li><li>to perpetuate, to make eternal and unchangeable (<i>e.g. a custom</i>)</li></ol><p><i>увекове́чить</i> (uvekovéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to immortalize (to perpetuate forever in the memory of someone's offspring, especially by praising or glorifying)</li><li>to perpetuate, to make eternal and unchangeable (<i>e.g. a custom</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>векове́ч(ный)</i> (vekovéč(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase (act of becoming or making larger)</li><li>enlargement</li></ol><p><i>увели́чить</i> (uvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enlarge, to increase</li><li>to magnify</li></ol><p><i>увели́чить</i> (uvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase, to rise, to grow</li></ol><p><i>увели́чивать</i> (uvelíčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnifying glass (instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnifying</li></ol><p><i>увели́чить</i> (uvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, to enlarge, to enhance</li><li>to magnify</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) to bump (software version, for example)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase, to rise, to grow</li></ol><p><i>увели́чить</i> (uvelíčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>венча́ть</i> (venčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be crowned</li></ol><p><i>увенча́ть</i> (uvenčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>увенча́ть</i> (uvenčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be crowned</li></ol><p><i>уве́нчивать</i> (uvénčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assurance</li></ol><p><i>уве́рить</i> (uvéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>confidently (in a confident manner)</li><li>to down, to slam (an alcoholic drink)</li></ol><p><i>уве́ренный</i> (uvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firmness, assurance</li><li>certainty</li><li>confidence</li></ol><p><i>уве́ренный</i> (uvérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confident, sure, certain [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in/of someone/something’</small>] (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>confident, firm, resolute (<i>in general; of a person, step, tone, action, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure</li><li>to make believe</li><li>to convince, to persuade</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ве́рить</i> (véritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make sure of, to verify</li><li>to be convinced of, to be confident of</li></ol><p><i>уве́рить</i> (uvéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dodge, to evade [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>верну́ть</i> (vernútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to come to believe</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ве́ровать</i> (vérovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) overture</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ouverture</i>. Doublet of <i>аперту́ра</i> (apertúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assure</li><li>to make believe</li><li>to convince, to persuade</li></ol><p><i>уве́рить</i> (uvéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make sure of, to verify</li><li>to be convinced of, to be confident of</li></ol><p><i>уверя́ть</i> (uverjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusement, entertainment, fun (<i>action or result</i>)</li><li>theatrical performance, spectacle</li></ol><p><i>увесели́ть</i> (uveselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) amusement, entertaining</li></ol><p><i>увесели́ть</i> (uveselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain, to amuse</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>весели́ть</i> (veselítʹ), or from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>весёлый</i> (vesjólyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entertain, to amuse</li></ol><p><i>увесели́ть</i> (uveselítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) heavy, bulky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to lead away, to withdraw <i>(troops)</i></li><li>to carry off, to lift</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вести́</i> (vestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maim, to mutilate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>век</i> (vek, “(originally) strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crippled, lame, maimed, mutilated</li></ol><p><i>уве́чить</i> (uvéčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mutilation (act of mutilating or the state of being mutilated)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>уве́чить</i> (uvéčitʹ, “to maim, mutilate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ве́шать</i> (véšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>уве́шать</i> (uvéšatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge, to exhort, to admonish</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-вести́ть</i> (-vestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) exhorting, exhortation (action)</li><li>admonition, exhortation (result)</li></ol><p><i>увещева́ть</i> (uveščevátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to urge, to exhort, to admonish</li></ol><p><i>увеще-</i> (uvešče-) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie</li><li>to entwine</li></ol><p><i>уви́ть</i> (uvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself up</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to hang around (someone)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to follow (someone) around (due to sexual interest), to chase after (someone)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;imperfective only) to court, to woo</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to wheedle, to fawn upon</li></ol><p><i>увива́ть</i> (uvivátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to see</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вида́ть</i> (vidátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, to catch sight of, to notice</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ви́деть</i> (vídetʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see each other, to meet</li></ol><p><i>уви́деть</i> (uvídetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>see you later</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evade</li><li>to shirk, to elude, to weasel out</li></ol><p><i>увильну́ть</i> (uvilʹnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evade</li><li>to shirk, to elude, to weasel out</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вильну́ть</i> (vilʹnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie</li><li>to entwine</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вить</i> (vitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself up</li></ol><p><i>уви́ть</i> (uvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisturization, humidification, hydration</li><li>moisture</li></ol><p><i>увлажни́ть</i> (uvlažnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humidifier, moisturizer, humectant</li></ol><p><i>увлажни́ть</i> (uvlažnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten, to damp, to wet</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вла́жный</i> (vlážnyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moisten, to damp, to wet</li></ol><p><i>увлажни́ть</i> (uvlažnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fascinating, engrossing, enthralling, exciting, intriguing, absorbing</li></ol><p><i>увлека́ть</i> (uvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fascinate, to infatuate</li><li>to carry away</li></ol><p><i>увле́к-</i> (uvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) to take an interest in</li><li>to get carried away (by/with)</li></ol><p><i>увлека́ть</i> (uvlekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enthusiasm, animation</li><li>hobby, interest</li><li>infatuation, flame, crush (the act of infatuating&#59; the state of being infatuated&#59; folly&#59; that which infatuates)</li></ol><p><i>увле́к-</i> (uvlék-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fascinate, to infatuate</li><li>to carry away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>влечь</i> (vlečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take a great interest in</li><li>to get carried away (by/with)</li><li>to take a fancy to, to be infatuated with, to fall for</li></ol><p><i>увле́чь</i> (uvléčʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enthusiastically</li></ol><p><i>увлечённый</i> (uvlečónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enthusiasm</li></ol><p><i>увлечённый</i> (uvlečónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enthusiastic, rapt</li><li>engrossed, absorbed (fully occupied with one's thoughts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withdrawal</li><li>carrying off, theft, rustling</li><li>(<i>business</i>) forward inclined part of chimney</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to lead away, to withdraw <i>(troops)</i></li><li>to carry off, to lift</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying off, theft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to carry away (by vehicle)</li><li>to abduct, to kidnap</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discharge, to dismiss, to fire, to sack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resign, to retire, to quit (work or service)</li></ol><p><i>уво́лить</i> (uvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discharge, dismissal (act of expelling or letting go)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) leave (permission for departure)</li></ol><p>From <i>увольня́ть</i> (uvolʹnjátʹ, “to discharge, dismiss”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discharge, to dismiss, to fire, to sack</li></ol><p>From <i>уво́лить</i> (uvólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ), with unexpected interpolation of -н- influenced by <i>во́льный</i> (vólʹnyj, “free”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resign, to retire, to quit (work or service)</li></ol><p><i>увольня́ть</i> (uvolʹnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alas!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uvy</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wai</i>. Cognate with English <i>woe</i>, Latin <i>vae</i>, Lithuanian <i>vai̇̃</i>, Sanskrit <i>उवे</i> (uvé), Hittite (uwai), Persian وای (vây).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>withering</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) decline, decay</li></ol><p><i>увяда́ть</i> (uvjadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, to fade, to waste away (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>увя́д-</i> (uvjád-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>withered, faded, sere, sapless, flaccid, dead (<i>about flowers, trees, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) lost youth, freshness, beauty; passé</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) slacken, drooping</li></ol><p><i>увядать</i> (uvjadatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ш</i> (-š) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij) (formed as a past-active participle)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to pack up, to strap</li><li>to coordinate <i>(with)</i>, to harmonize <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get the agreement <i>(of)</i></li><li>to link <i>(to)</i>, to make conditional <i>(on)</i></li><li>to stick <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get bogged down <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)</p><p><i>увя́знуть</i> (uvjáznutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie in, to coordinate [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tag along [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to follow, to trail [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>увяза́ть</i> (uvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tying up, tying together, linking</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) coordination</li></ol><p><i>увяза́ть</i> (uvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stick <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get bogged down <i>(in)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вя́знуть</i> (vjáznutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up, to pack up, to strap</li><li>to coordinate <i>(with)</i>, to harmonize <i>(with)</i></li><li>to get the agreement <i>(of)</i></li><li>to link <i>(to)</i>, to make conditional <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>увяза́ть</i> (uvjazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie in, to coordinate [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tag along [<i>with</i> <b>с</b> (s, + instrumental) <small>‘with someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to follow, to trail [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>увя́зывать</i> (uvjázyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wither, to shrivel, to fade, to dry up</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>вя́нуть</i> (vjánutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subterfuge, dodge, evasion, loophole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dodge, to evade [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уверт-</i> (uvert-) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>уве́чный</i> (uvéčnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess (right)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гада́ть</i> (gadátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guess (right)</li></ol><p><i>угада́ть</i> (ugadátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be discernible</li></ol><p><i>уга́дывать</i> (ugádyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ugandan (citizen, resident)</li></ol><p><i>уганди́йский</i> (ugandíjskij) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ugandan</li></ol><p><i>Уга́нда</i> (Ugánda) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Uganda; Ugandan</li></ol><p><i>Уга́нда</i> (Ugánda) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbon monoxide</li><li>metal loss</li><li>fumes (from the exhaust pipe)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) frenzy, craze</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something very funny</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) + Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;garъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) pronunciation spelling of <i>угора́ть</i> (ugorátʹ); to die (to laugh intensely) (<i>with various prepositions</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) funnily, in a silly way</li></ol><p><i>уга́рный</i> (ugárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to die (to laugh intensely) (<i>with various prepositions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>угара́ть</i> (ugarátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to carbon monoxide or CO<sub>2</sub>-containing fumes</li><li>containing, emitting or filled with carbon monoxide or CO<sub>2</sub>-containing fumes</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) in a state of extreme intoxication</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) in a state of frenzy, extreme arousal, ecstasy</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) laughable, extremely funny; cute</li></ol><p><i>уга́р</i> (ugár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) carbon monoxide, CO</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extinction (<i>of a fire</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) extinction, slow death, fading out</li></ol><p><i>угаса́ть</i> (ugasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to become extinct</li><li>to die away</li></ol><p><i>уга́снуть</i> (ugásnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, to become extinct</li><li>to die away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>га́снуть</i> (gásnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dirty, to soil</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гва́здать</i> (gvázdatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbohydrate (organic compounds; sugar, starch or cellulose)</li></ol><p>From <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ, “coal”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вода́</i> (vodá, “water”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) hydrocarbon (compound of carbon and hydrogen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hydrocarbon</li></ol><p><i>углеводоро́д</i> (uglevodoród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;chemistry) carbonic acid</li></ol><p><i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>ки́слый</i> (kíslyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbon</li></ol><p>From <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ, “coal”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-род</i> (-rod), combining form of <i>роди́ть</i> (rodítʹ, “to give birth”), because coal consists primarily of carbon. Modeled on <i>кислоро́д</i> (kisloród), <i>водоро́д</i> (vodoród).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) carbon</li></ol><p><i>углеро́д</i> (ugleród) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Uglich</li></ol><p><i>У́глич</i> (Úglič) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angular</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) awkward</li></ol><p><i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle bracket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle grinder (power tool)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angular acceleration (rate of change of angular velocity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>angular (relating to or forming an angle&#59; sharp-cornered)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) corner</li></ol><p><i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deepen</li><li>to extend, to intensify</li><li>to aggravate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>глубь</i> (glubʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deepen, to become deeper</li><li>to go deep <i>(into)</i>, to get deeper <i>(into)</i>, to cut <i>(into)</i></li><li>to delve deeply <i>(into)</i>, to become absorbed <i>(in)</i>, to study in depth</li><li>to become more profound/acute, to be intensified, to aggravate</li></ol><p><i>углуби́ть</i> (uglubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deepening</li><li>hollow, depression</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) draught</li><li>intensification</li></ol><p><i>углуби́ть</i> (uglubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deepen</li><li>to extend, to intensify</li><li>to aggravate</li></ol><p><i>углуби́ть</i> (uglubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deepen, to become deeper</li><li>to go deep <i>(into)</i>, to get deeper <i>(into)</i>, to cut <i>(into)</i></li><li>to delve deeply <i>(into)</i>, to become absorbed <i>(in)</i>, to study in depth</li><li>to become more profound/acute, to be intensified, to aggravate</li></ol><p><i>углубля́ть</i> (uglubljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep (<i>in</i>), absorbed (<i>in; of a person</i>)</li><li>deep, in-depth, profound, advanced (<i>of a study, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find while looking intensely or attentively</li><li>to save from the danger by watching</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal anything that can move by itself (vehicle, vessel, cattle, etc)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гнать</i> (gnatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppressor</li></ol><p><i>угнета́ть</i> (ugnetátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress</li><li>to depress, to dispirit</li></ol><p>Related to perfective <i>угнести́</i> (ugnestí), from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гнести́</i> (gnestí, “to oppress”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oppression (act of oppressing, or the state of being oppressed)</li><li>depression, dejection</li></ol><p><i>угнет-</i> (ugnet-, “to depress, to oppress”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oppressed</li><li>depressed (experiencing depression) (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>depressed (expressing depression) (<i>mood, etc.; usually inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persuade, to talk (into), to urge</li></ol><p><i>уговори́ть</i> (ugovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree, to arrange</li></ol><p><i>угова́ривать</i> (ugovárivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasion, <i>plural</i> remonstrance</li><li>agreement, compact, understanding</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persuade, to talk (into), to urge</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>говори́ть</i> (govorítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree, to arrange</li></ol><p><i>уговори́ть</i> (ugovorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ugoda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, to oblige</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get to (some place or situation, unexpectedly) (<i>no imperfective</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit (a target)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bump into/against</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ugodìti</i>; compare <i>&#42;godìti</i> (“to please”). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>годи́ть</i> (godítʹ), but <i>годи́ть</i> (godítʹ) no longer has its original meaning.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pleaser, someone who is always trying to please someone</li></ol><p><i>уго́да</i> (ugóda) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be obsequious; to fawn (upon), to cringe (to)</li></ol><p><i>уго́дник</i> (ugódnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>with pronoun or adverb, meaning "any"</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welcome (to), pleasing (to)</li></ol><p><i>уго́да</i> (ugóda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, land, estate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, to oblige, to play up <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>угоди́ть</i> (ugodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Action by verb “to please”, pleasing.</li><li>That which is used to please, action aimed at winning someone’s favor.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner</li><li>angle</li><li>dwelling, quarters</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgъlъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂engulos</i> (“joint?”), from <i>&#42;h₂eng-</i> (“corner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angle of attack (angle between the chord line of an airfoil and the airflow over it)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) criminal investigation</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) criminal case, criminal charge</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crime</li></ol><p>From <i>уголо́вный</i> (ugolóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminal, especially one who has been sentenced/imprisoned.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>criminally</li></ol><p><i>уголо́вный</i> (ugolóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) criminal procedure</li></ol><p><i>уголо́вный</i> (ugolóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>процессуа́льный</i> (processuálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>criminal case</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crime; criminal, penal (being against the law)</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>голова́</i> (golová) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). In Old East Slavic, <i>голова́</i> (golová) also meant "dead man"; compare Latin <i>capitālis</i> (“of the head; deadly”), as in "capital punishment".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) criminal code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) criminals (collectively), crime, criminality</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́гол</i> (úgol): small corner (part or region)</li><li>nook</li></ol><p><i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coal (a black rock formed from prehistoric plant remains, composed largely of carbon and burned as a fuel)</li><li>coal (a piece of coal used for burning)</li><li>charcoal (a stick of black carbon material used for drawing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>угъль</i> (ugŭlĭ) or <i>угль</i> (uglĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫglь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ánˀglis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁óngʷl̥</i> (“charcoal”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>ѫгль</i> (ǫglĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) coals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) carbonic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>square (L- or T-shaped tool used to place objects or draw lines at right angles)</li></ol><p>From <i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “angle”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, instrument suffix). Doublet of <i>-уго́льник</i> (-ugólʹnik, “-gon; -angle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firepan</li></ol><p><i>у́гольный</i> (úgolʹnyj, “coal-related”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) coal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) carbon; carbonic</li><li>coal-black, jet-black</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) corner</li></ol><p><i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ, “coal”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p><p><i>у́гол</i> (úgol, “corner”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collier (person or vessel)</li></ol><p><i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ): coal, ember (glowing piece of coal or wood)</li></ol><p><i>у́голь</i> (úgolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to calm</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гомони́ть</i> (gomonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to calm down, to become/get quiet</li></ol><p><i>угомони́ть</i> (ugomonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to calm</li></ol><p><i>угомони́ть</i> (ugomonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driving away</li><li>capture, captivity</li><li>stealing, car theft, hijacking, skyjacking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hijacker, cattle rustler (thief who steals things capable of independent movement)</li></ol><p><i>угон-</i> (ugon-) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal anything that can move by itself (vehicle, vessel, cattle, etc)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гоня́ть</i> (gonjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to prompt (someone to do something)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;sarcastic,&#32;impersonal) to manage to</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гора́зд</i> (gorázd) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be poisoned by fumes (such as monoxide or charcoal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lose the ability to think</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to laugh loudly, to LOL, to be very amused (<i>with various prepositions</i>)</li></ol><p><i>угоре́ть</i> (ugorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) suffered from exposure to fumes</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) mad/crazy</li></ol><p><i>угоре́ть</i> (ugorétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be poisoned by fumes (such as monoxide or charcoal)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lose the ability to think</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>горе́ть</i> (gorétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated&#32;or&#32;dialectal,&#32;Ukraine) a Hungarian person</li></ol><p>Unknown, related to <i>&#42;ǫgъrinъ</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>уго́рець</i> (uhórecʹ), Belarusian <i>вуго́рац</i> (vuhórac).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eel</li><li>blackhead, comedo, acne, pimple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgořь</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgrъ</i>. Outside Slavic, cognate with Lithuanian <i>ankštiras</i>. It is not known whether the two senses of the word are cognate. Folk etymology suggests that a blackhead, when squeezed out, resembles an eel in its elongated form, hence the origin of the second sense.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat (someone to something)</li><li>to entertain (someone)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гости́ть</i> (gostítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to partake in, to help oneself to [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>угости́ть</i> (ugostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>угото́вить</i> (ugotóvitʹ)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вый</i> (gotóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>гото́вить</i> (gotóvitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat <i>(someone, to something)</i></li><li>to entertain</li></ol><p>From <i>угости́ть</i> (ugostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ), from <i>гости́ть</i> (gostítʹ, “to be on visit, to stay”). See there for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to partake in, to help oneself to [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>угоща́ть</i> (ugoščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treat, food, fare, entertainment (with something)</li></ol><p><i>угоща́ть</i> (ugoščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) acne</li></ol><p><i>у́горь</i> (úgorʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) a Hungarian person</li><li>(usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;rare&#32;in the singular) Ugric person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫgъrinъ</i>, Old East Slavic <i>угринъ</i> (ugrinŭ), <i>plural</i> <i>угре</i> (ugre), doublet of <i>венгр</i> (vengr) and <i>уго́рец</i> (ugórec) (<i>dated or dialectal</i>). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>ѫгринъ</i> (ǫgrinŭ), <i>plural</i> <i>ѫгре</i> (ǫgre), Polish <i>Węgier</i> or <i>Węgrzyn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kill, to do to death, to give the business</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to throw away, to burn up, to send down the drain</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li></ol><p>Verbal form of <i>угро́за</i> (ugróza, “threat, danger”), from <i>гроза́</i> (grozá, “terror”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groza</i> (“terror”) (vd. there for cognates)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>threateningly, menacingly</li></ol><p><i>угрожа́ющий</i> (ugrožájuščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threatening, menacing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threat (expression of intent to injure or punish another)</li><li>danger</li></ol><p>From <i>гроза́</i> (grozá, “terror”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groza</i> (“terror”). See there for cognates.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in expression</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>morosely, sullenly</li><li>bleakly, forbiddingly</li></ol><p><i>угрю́мый</i> (ugrjúmyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sullenness</li></ol><p>From <i>угрюмый</i> (ugrjumyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sullen, sulky, gloomy, morose (showing a brooding ill humour)</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, possibly related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grustь</i>, <i>&#42;grustiti</i></p></html>:/m̩ˈhm̩/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mhm (yes)</li></ol></html>:/m̩ˈhm̩/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>угу́</i> (uhú, ugú); mhm (yes)</li></ol></html>:/m̩ˈhm̩s/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) alternative form of <i>угу́м</i> (uhúm, ugúm); mhm (yes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) limb</li><li>(dated,&#32;rare) penis</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) fishing rod</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) vessel</li><li>oud (Arab string instrument)</li><li>(school,&#32;slang) satisfactory grade, C</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оудъ</i> (udŭ, “limb, piece”), also found as <i>оудо</i> (udo), plural <i>оудеса</i> (udesa); from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;udъ</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>оудъ</i> (udŭ, “limb”), Bulgarian <i>уд</i> (ud, “limb”), Serbo-Croatian <i>у̑д</i> (“limb”), <i>у́до</i> (“piece of meat”), Slovene <i>úd</i> (“limb”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>úd</i> (“limb”), Slovak <i>úd</i> (“limb”), Polish <i>ud</i> (“thigh”), also <i>udo</i>. Further connections unclear.</p><p>Borrowed from Arabic عُود (ʕūd).</p><p>Clipping of <i>удовлетвори́тельно</i> (udovletvorítelʹno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) fishing rod</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>уда</i> (uda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫda</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;and-</i>, <i>&#42;end-</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>ву́дка</i> (vúdka), Belarusian <i>вуда́</i> (vudá), Church Slavonic <i>ѫда</i> (ǫda), <i>ѫдица</i> (ǫdica), Bulgarian <i>въ́дица</i> (vǎ́dica), Serbo-Croatian <i>у̏дица</i>, Slovene <i>ȯ́dica</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Czech <i>udice</i>, Polish <i>węda</i>, <i>wędka</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>wuda</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>huda</i>.</p><p>Normally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en-</i> (“on”) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to place”). Compare Latvian <i>uodne</i> (“bar on which the sleigh body stands”), <i>eñdas</i> (“part of a peasant's sleigh”), Lithuanian <i>iñdas</i> (“vessel”), Slovene <i>nȃda</i> (“extension”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>). Sometimes also compared with Sanskrit <i>अन्दु</i>&nbsp;f (andu, “foot chain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boa constrictor</li></ol><p>Verbal noun of <i>удави́ть</i> (udavítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out well, to be a success</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to succeed</li></ol><p><i>уда́ться</i> (udátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to strangle, to suffocate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дави́ть</i> (davítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strangle oneself, to hang oneself</li></ol><p><i>удави́ть</i> (udavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running knot, slip-knot, timber-hitch</li><li>garrote, any kind of choking weapon</li><li>choke collar</li></ol><p><i>удави́ть</i> (udavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;biblical) flesh of a strangled animal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оудавлѥнина</i>&nbsp;/ <i>ⱆⰴⰰⰲⰾⰵⱀⰻⱀⰰ</i> (udavljenina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;transitive) to strangle, to suffocate</li></ol><p><i>удави́ть</i> (udavítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to strangle oneself, to hang oneself</li></ol><p><i>уда́вливать</i> (udávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moving off, moving away</li><li>sending away, sending off</li><li>deletion, removal (the act of deleting)</li><li>ablation, surgical removal</li><li>distance</li></ol><p><i>удали́ть</i> (udalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) daredevil; a slashing fellow</li></ol><p><i>удало́й</i> (udalój) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove</li><li>to send away, to dismiss</li><li>to delete</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to put off, to postpone</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-дали́ть</i> (-dalítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away, to retreat, to withdraw, to retire</li></ol><p><i>удали́ть</i> (udalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bold, prow</li></ol><p><i>у́даль</i> (údalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ой</i> (-oj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prowess, boldness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>audacity</li></ol><p><i>у́даль</i> (údalʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove</li><li>to send away, to dismiss</li><li>to delete</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to put off, to postpone</li></ol><p><i>удали́ть</i> (udalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ), see also Serbo-Croatian <i>udaljiti</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move away, to retreat, to withdraw, to retire</li></ol><p><i>удаля́ть</i> (udaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) remote access</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) remote work</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) remote studies</li></ol><p>From <i>удалённый</i> (udaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>remotely</li></ol><p><i>удалённый</i> (udaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remoteness (quality of being remote)</li></ol><p><i>удалённый</i> (udaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distant, remote (not nearby)</li><li>far, distant [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from something’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remote worker, telecommuter</li></ol><p>From <i>удалённый</i> (udaljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit, blow, strike, kick, punch, cuff</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the sound of such an impact</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;military) a quick, sudden offensive</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;military) bombardment, shelling</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) blow (heavy shock, unexpected trouble)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;medicine) apoplexy, sudden severe disease</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>уда́рить</i> (udáritʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) accent, stress, emphasis (on a syllable)</li></ol><p><i>уда́рить</i> (udáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;linguistics) stress, accent</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) stressed, accented (<i>of a vowel, syllable, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to knock, to hit, to punch</li><li>to butt, to bump</li><li>to kick</li><li>to set about, to start, to set in</li><li>to attack</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;udariti</i>. See also <i>удар</i> (udar), <i>драть</i> (dratʹ) and <i>раздо́р</i> (razdór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take <i>(to)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>уда́рить</i> (udáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) shock wave (powerful compression wave)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;animate) drummer, percussionist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) striker, firing pin (<i>inanimate</i>)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) plunger (<i>inanimate</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;animate) udarnik, shock worker</li></ol><p>From <i>уда́р</i> (udár) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hit, strike, percussion, shock, impact; percussive</li><li>accelerated, shock (<i>of work</i>)</li><li>(relational,&#32;military) shock</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) stressed</li><li>effective, giving a quick result</li><li>particularly important, necessary (<i>of business, work, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>уда́р</i> (udár) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) percussion instrument (type of instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to strike</li></ol><p><i>уда́рить</i> (udáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit, to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take <i>(to)</i>, to plunge <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>ударя́ть</i> (udarjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out well, to be a success</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to succeed</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Compare Czech <i>udat</i>, Polish <i>udać</i>, and Ukrainian <i>уда́тися</i> (udátysja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good luck, success (achievement of one's aim or goal)</li></ol><p>Derived from уда́ться (udátʹsja), and then <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дать</i> (datʹ). Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dati</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;dṓˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dédeh₃ti</i> (“to give”), whence English <i>donate</i>, <i>date</i> and <i>data</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good luck!</li></ol><p>Genitive case; ellipsis of <i>жела́ю уда́чи</i> (želáju udáči). Compare with Ukrainian <i>уда́чі</i> (udáči).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky, fortunate (of people, having good fortune)</li><li>prosperous</li><li>successful</li></ol><p><i>уда́ча</i> (udáča) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lucky dog, lucky devil (a lucky person)</li></ol><p>From <i>удача</i> (udača, “good luck, success”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fortunately, luckily</li></ol><p><i>уда́чный</i> (udáčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successful</li><li>fortunate</li><li>lucky, prosperous</li><li>felicitous, happy</li><li>fortuitous</li><li>chancy</li><li>apposite (appropriate, relevant, well-suited)</li><li>well-turned</li></ol><p><i>уда́ча</i> (udáča) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to double, to redouble</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to reduplicate</li></ol><p><i>удво́ить</i> (udvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubling, redoubling; duplication; gemination</li></ol><p><i>удво́ить</i> (udvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) redoubled, strengthened, intensified, greatly increased (<i>of effort, energy, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) doubled, double, geminate (<i>of a letter written twice or a sound pronounced geminate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to double, to redouble</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to reduplicate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дво́е</i> (dvóje) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) lot, destiny</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) appanage, independent principality <i>(in mediaeval Russia)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allot, to allocate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дели́ть</i> (delítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) specific</li><li>per capita, per unit</li><li>(relational,&#32;historical) appanage (related to an <i>уде́л</i> (udél))</li></ol><p><i>уде́л</i> (udél) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>percentage, proportion</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) specific gravity</li></ol><p>From <i>уде́льный</i> (udélʹnyj, “specific, per capita”) +&lrm; <i>ве́с</i> (vés, “weight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to allot, to allocate</li></ol><p><i>удели́ть</i> (udelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) restraint, control</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeping, retention</li><li>(<i>accounting</i>) charge, withholding</li></ol><p><i>удержа́ть</i> (uderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retain, to hold, to withhold</li><li>to balance, to place or set an object so that it does not fall</li><li>to suppress</li><li>to deduct, to keep back</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>держа́ть</i> (deržátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, to remain</li><li>to hold one's ground, to hold out</li><li>to keep <i>(from)</i>, to refrain <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>удержа́ть</i> (uderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holding, retention</li><li>ahold, hold</li><li>refraining</li></ol><p><i>уде́рживать</i> (udérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retain, to hold, to withhold</li><li>to balance, to place or set an object so that it does not fall</li><li>to suppress</li><li>to deduct, to keep back</li></ol><p><i>удержа́ть</i> (uderžátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, to remain</li><li>to hold one's ground, to hold out</li><li>to keep <i>(from)</i>, to refrain <i>(from)</i></li></ol><p><i>уде́рживать</i> (udérživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make cheaper, to reduce the price of, to cheapen</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дешёвый</i> (dešóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reduction in price, cheapening</li></ol><p><i>удешеви́ть</i> (udeševítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make cheaper, to reduce the price of, to cheapen</li></ol><p><i>удешеви́ть</i> (udeševítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become cheaper, cheapen</li></ol><p><i>удешевля́ть</i> (udeševljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extremely</li><li>astonishingly, surprisingly</li></ol><p><i>удиви́тельный</i> (udivítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful, marvelous</li></ol><p><i>удиви́ть</i> (udivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to astonish, to surprise, to amaze</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>диви́ть</i> (divítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wonder (at), to be surprised, to be amazed</li></ol><p><i>удиви́ть</i> (udivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astonishment, amazement, surprise, wonder (feeling that something unexpected has happened)</li></ol><p><i>удиви́ть</i> (udivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to astonish, to surprise, to amaze</li></ol><p><i>удиви́ть</i> (udivítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wonder (at), to be surprised, to be amazed</li></ol><p><i>удивля́ть</i> (udivljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>amazedly, surprisedly</li></ol><p><i>удивлённый</i> (udivljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surprised, amazed (<i>experiencing surprise or amazement; of a person</i>)</li><li>surprised, amazed (<i>expressing surprise or amazement; of a look, face, tone, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bit (metal in horse's mouth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing rod, fishing pole</li></ol><p><i>удила́</i> (udilá) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angler (person who fishes with a hook and line)</li><li>angler (angler fish)</li></ol><p><i>уди́ть</i> (udítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee, to run for one's life</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-дира́ть</i> (-dirátʹ). Compare <i>дать дёру</i> (datʹ djóru).</p><p>Compare typologically German <i>ausreißen</i> (<~ <i>aus-</i> + <i>reißen</i>). Also compare <i>рвану́ть</i> (rvanútʹ), <i>рва́ться</i> (rvátʹsja), <i>сорва́ться</i> (sorvátʹsja), <i>ломану́ть</i> (lomanútʹ), <i>ломану́ться</i> (lomanútʹsja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to angle, to fish</li></ol><p><i>уда́</i> (udá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extension, elongation, prolongation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extension cord (an electrical cord)</li></ol><p><i>удлини́ть</i> (udlinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lengthen, to make longer, to elongate</li><li><i>(term)</i> to prolong, to extend</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>длина́</i> (dliná) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lengthen, to make longer, to elongate</li><li><i>(term)</i> to prolong, to extend</li></ol><p><i>удлини́ть</i> (udlinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lengthen, to become longer, to become elongated</li><li>to be prolonged/extended, to draw out</li></ol><p><i>удлиня́ть</i> (udlinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elongated, lengthened</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Udmurt (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Udmurt <i>удмуртъёс</i> (udmurtjos), said to mean "meadow people," from the Proto-Permic <i>&#42;od(o)-</i> (“glade, meadow”) + <i>&#42;mari</i> (“person”), itself borrowed from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;mártyas</i> (“human, mortal, one who has to die”). Compare <i>Mari</i>. More at Udmurt.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Udmurt</li></ol><p><i>Удму́ртия</i> (Udmúrtija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>удо́бно</i> (udóbno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>удо́бно</i> (udóbno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comfortably, snugly</li><li>conveniently</li></ol><p><i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comfortable, convenient</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;udobьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>digestible (of food and information)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legibility, readability</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>legible, readable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fertilization (the process of fertilizing)</li><li>fertilizer, manure</li></ol><p><i>удо́брить</i> (udóbritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fertilize (plants, soil)</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to bring into a good mood</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>до́брый</i> (dóbryj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fertilize (plants, soil)</li><li>(dated,&#32;figurative) to bring into a good mood</li></ol><p><i>удо́брить</i> (udóbritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convenience, amenity (something that makes life easier or more pleasant)</li><li>accommodation</li><li>commodity</li><li>easement</li><li>boon</li></ol><p>From the radical of <i>удо́бный</i> (udóbnyj) + <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satisfaction</li></ol><p><i>удовлетвори́ть</i> (udovletvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>satisfactory</li></ol><p><i>удовлетвори́ть</i> (udovletvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy</li><li>to fulfill</li><li>to gratify</li><li>to suffice</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>satisfaire</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у</i> (u) +&lrm; <i>довле</i> (dovle) +&lrm; <i>твори́ть</i> (tvorítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to content oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>удовлетвори́ть</i> (udovletvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy</li><li>to fulfill</li><li>to gratify</li><li>to suffice</li></ol><p><i>удовлетвори́ть</i> (udovletvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to content oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>удовлетворя́ть</i> (udovletvorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with satisfaction</li></ol><p><i>удовлетворённый</i> (udovletvorjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contentment, satisfaction</li></ol><p><i>удовлетворённый</i> (udovletvorjónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy, satisfied (experiencing happiness or satisfaction) (<i>of a person</i>) [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><li>happy, satisfied (expressing happiness or satisfaction) (<i>look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) satisfy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>удоволити</i> (udovoliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;udovoliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) satisfaction</li><li>pleasure</li></ol><p><i>удово́льство</i> (udovólʹstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) satisfaction</li></ol><p>15th century Church Slavonic, <i>удово́лить</i> (udovólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to satisfy</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to fulfill</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to gratify</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to suffice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be content/satisfied <i>(with)</i>, to content oneself <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дово́льствоваться</i> (dovólʹstvovatʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoopoe</li></ol><p>Of onomatopoeic origin. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>о́дуд</i> (ódud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>udon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>饂飩</i> (うどん, udon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price increase, appreciation</li></ol><p><i>удорожа́ть</i> (udorožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise the price of</li></ol><p><i>удорожи́ть</i> (udorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase in price</li></ol><p><i>удорожа́ть</i> (udorožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise the price of</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>дорожи́ть</i> (dorožítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase in price</li></ol><p><i>удорожи́ть</i> (udorožítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confer, to award</li><li>to favour/favor, to honour/honor, to vouchsafe, to deign</li></ol><p><i>удосто́ить</i> (udostóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be honoured/honored <i>(with)</i>, to be awarded</li></ol><p><i>удоста́ивать</i> (udostáivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identificatory certificate</li></ol><p><i>удостове́рить</i> (udostovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ID card, identity card (card or badge showing the official identity of the wearer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to certify, to attest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascertain, to make sure, to make certain</li></ol><p><i>удостове́рить</i> (udostovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to certify, to attest</li></ol><p><i>удостове́рить</i> (udostovéritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascertain, to make sure, to make certain</li></ol><p><i>удостоверя́ть</i> (udostoverjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confer, to award</li><li>to favour/favor, to honour/honor, to vouchsafe, to deign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be honoured/honored <i>(with)</i>, to be awarded</li></ol><p><i>удосто́ить</i> (udostóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find time <i>(for)</i>, to get around <i>(to)</i>, to manage <i>+to)</i></li></ol><p><i>удосу́житься</i> (udosúžitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to find time <i>(for)</i>, to get around <i>(to)</i>, to manage <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>досу́г</i> (dosúg) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption (of girls only)</li></ol><p><i>удочери́ть</i> (udočerítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt <i>(as a daughter)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt <i>(as a daughter)</i></li></ol><p><i>удочери́ть</i> (udočerítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing rod (rod used for angling)</li></ol><p><i>уда́</i> (udá) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee, to run for one's life</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>драть</i> (dratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distress, to deject, to cause to be depressed, to sadden, to aggrieve</li><li>to wear down, to beat down, to aggravate, to complicate the life of</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to burden</li></ol><p><i>удручи́ть</i> (udručítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burdensome, oppressive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distress, to deject, to cause to be depressed, to sadden, to aggrieve</li><li>to wear down, to beat down, to aggravate, to complicate the life of</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to burden</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;udrǫčiti</i>, from <i>&#42;drǫčiti</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>оудрѫчити</i> (udrǫčiti, “to distress”), Polish <i>dręczyć</i> (“to torment”). Ultimately related to Russian <i>друк</i> (druk, “lever, pole”), Old Church Slavonic <i>дрѫгъ</i> (drǫgŭ, “beam, log”), Bulgarian <i>дръг</i> (drǎg, “pole”), archaic Serbo-Croatian <i>друг</i> (“pole”), Slovene <i>drȏg</i> (“pole, ladder”), Polish <i>drąg</i> (“pole, lever”), <i>drączek</i> (“lever”), Slovak <i>druk</i> (“lever”). Per Vasmer, forms with /k/ and /g/ are both old, but only the forms with /g/ have cognates in other languages, e.g. Lithuanian <i>dránga</i> (“thick pole”) (Zhemaitian/Western), <i>drángos</i>&nbsp;pl (“carts without wheels”) (Eastern), Old Norse <i>drangr</i> (“stone sticking out of the ground”), <i>drengr</i> (“thick trunk, stick”), Norwegian <i>dreng</i> (“thick stick, column”), Old Irish <i>dringim</i> (“to get up, to climb”), Welsh <i>dringo</i> (“to get up, to climb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressed, despondent (<i>experiencing depression or despondency; of a person</i>)</li><li>depressed, despondent, downcast (<i>expressing depression or despondency; of a face, look, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strangulation, asphyxiation, suffocation, choking</li></ol><p><i>удуши́ть</i> (udušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stifling, stale, stagnant (<i>of air</i>)</li><li>suffocating, asphyxiating, choking</li><li>accompanied by suffocation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) depressing, stagnant (<i>of an environment or situation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>удуши́ть</i> (udušítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphyxia (condition with extreme decrease of oxygen and increase of carbon dioxide)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnography</i>) Udihe (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) male Udihe person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>уде́л</i> (udél).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>уде́льный</i> (udélʹnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) retard, dumbass, fuckwit (stupid person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bad, low-quality</li></ol><p><i>уеба́н</i> (ujebán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;expressive,&#32;transitive,&#32;with a person) to fuck, to have sexual intercourse with</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to abruptly hit with force [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;transitive) to break, to ruin (something)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;transitive) to exhaust (someone), to leave (someone) without strength</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;transitive) to kill (someone)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to leave (including quickly), to retreat; to fuck off</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively) to run away, to get away; to get the fuck out</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to hit, to knock</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fuck off (to leave, to go somewhere)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be fucking exhausted, to be worn the fuck out</li></ol><p><i>уеба́ть</i> (ujebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;figuratively,&#32;transitive) to exhaust (someone), to leave (someone) without strength</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ебсти́</i> (jebstí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solitude, seclusion, retirement; privacy</li></ol><p><i>уедини́ть</i> (ujedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative, old-fashion spelling of уединение</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seclude, to isolate, to disconnect (to remove from communication with others)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>еди́ный</i> (jedínyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seclude oneself, to go be alone</li><li>to retreat to a secluded place, to become a hermit</li></ol><p><i>уедини́ть</i> (ujedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seclude, to isolate, to disconnect (to cut off from communication with others)</li></ol><p><i>уедини́ть</i> (ujedinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seclude oneself, to go be alone</li><li>to retreat to a secluded place, to become a hermit</li></ol><p><i>уединя́ть</i> (ujedinjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secluded (<i>of a house, street, tree, etc.</i>)</li><li>solitary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) uyezd; district (administrative unit in the Russian Empire before 1917)</li><li>administrative region of various countries, including Estonia, Lithuania, People's Republic of China, North and South Korea (county) and Japan (district, <i>郡</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>уѣздъ</i> (ujězdŭ), from <i>&#42;u-</i> (compare Russian <i>у-</i> (u-)) + <i>&#42;jězdъ</i>, deverbal of <i>&#42;ězditi</i> (compare Russian <i>е́здить</i> (jézditʹ)). Compare Old Polish <i>ujazd</i> (“lawful circuit around one's land for the purposes of determining the borders”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) uyezd, district</li></ol><p><i>уе́зд</i> (ujézd) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) uyezd town <i>(an administrative center of the pre-1929 subdivision, uyezd)</i>, district town, chief town, county town.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to drive off, to leave, to depart (by vehicle)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-езжа́ть</i> (-jezžátʹ, “to go (by vehicle)”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>уебсти́</i> (ujebstí)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, to drive off, to leave, to depart (by conveyance)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) already</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Used as a particle to express intensification of emotion, often when admonishing or requesting of someone, including in a few set phrases.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass snake (<i>Natrix natrix</i>)</li></ol><p>Shortened form of <i>уже́</i> (užé).</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѫжь</i> (ǫžĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫžь</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>anguis</i>, Old Prussian <i>angis</i>, Lithuanian <i>angis</i>, Latvian <i>odze</i>, Old High German <i>unc</i> and Old Armenian <i>աւձ</i> (awj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of insects</i>) to sting</li><li>(<i>of snakes</i>) to bite</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>жа́лить</i> (žálitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horror, terror</li><li>disaster, mess</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>it's terrible!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ужасъ</i> (užasŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;užasъ</i> (“amazement, horror”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify, to horrify, to awe, to appall</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be terrified/horrified</li></ol><p><i>ужаса́ть</i> (užasátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrifying, terrific</li><li>horrifying</li><li>horrific, horrid</li><li>appalling</li><li>shocking</li><li>bloodcurdling</li><li>flagrant</li><li>spine-chilling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>terribly, horribly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;as an intensifier) very, awfully, frightfully</li></ol><p><i>ужа́сный</i> (užásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify, to horrify, to awe, to appall</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žasnǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be terrified/horrified</li></ol><p><i>ужасну́ть</i> (užasnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, horrible, dreadful, awful (<i>in senses of either inducing fear or very unpleasant</i>)</li><li>(<i>as an intensifier</i>) terrific, terrible, frightful</li></ol><p><i>у́жас</i> (úžas) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) horror movie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress, to squeeze smaller</li><li>to reduce the size of (e.g. a dress)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reduce (a budget, a salary, etc.)</li><li>to cut (grain, etc.) at the root using a scythe or reaper</li><li>to harvest (all of a field)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to press, to squeeze”)</p><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>жать</i> (žatʹ, “to reap, to harvest”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>already; carries the sense of the perfect continuous tenses in English.</li><li>by now, (as of) now</li><li>by then, by that point</li><li>(<i>in a negative sentence</i>) anymore, any longer</li><li>comparative degree of <i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>у́зко</i> (úzko)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Used to emphasise an amount or quantity of something; now, already, as early as, as late as, as recently as</li><li>Used to emphasise a certain word or collocation.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(j)uže</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫže</i>.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫži</i> (ending taken from the hard paradigm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, to harshen (conditions, rules, regime, penalties)</li></ol><p><i>ужесточи́ть</i> (užestočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toughening</li></ol><p>First attested in the 1950s. From <i>ужесточи́ть</i> (užestočítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harden, to harshen (conditions, rules, regime, penalties)</li></ol><p>First attested in the 1950s. From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>жесто́кий</i> (žestókij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get on <i>(with)</i>, to get along together <i>(with)</i></li><li>to go together <i>(with)</i>, to be combined <i>(with)</i>, to go hand in hand <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>ужи́ться</i> (užítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress, to squeeze smaller</li><li>to reduce the size of (e.g. a dress)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reduce (a budget, a salary, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-жима́ть</i> (-žimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grimace (a distortion of the countenance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supper, late dinner (the evening meal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ужина</i> (užina, “afternoon snack; evening meal”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;južina</i> (“meal at noon”), from <i>&#42;jugъ</i> (“South, south wind”). Compare Old East Slavic <i>угъ</i> (ugŭ, “South, noon”).</p><p>The meaning evolved like this: "south" ⇒ "the time of the day when the sun is in the south" ⇒ "supper".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dine; to have dinner; to have supper</li><li>to cut (grain, etc.) at the root using a scythe or reaper</li><li>to harvest (all of a field)</li></ol><p><i>у́жин</i> (úžin) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-жина́ть</i> (-žinátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get on <i>(with)</i>, to get along together <i>(with)</i></li><li>to go together <i>(with)</i>, to be combined <i>(with)</i>, to go hand in hand <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>жить</i> (žitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards, later in the day, later when you have the time</li><li>(<i>predicative</i>) just you wait, soon enough when the time comes</li><li>Used exclamatively as a promise of imminent punishment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) adder's tongue (<i>Ophioglossum</i> sp.)</li></ol><p><i>ужо́вый</i> (užóvyj, “of <i>Natrix natrix</i>”, relational adjective) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, “diminutive”), literally “grass-snakeling”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;archaic,&#32;Kaluga) to understand, to comprehend</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) (<i>Perm</i>) to take a turn for the worse; to grow lazy, to slack off</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legalization, legitimization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to legalize, to legitimize</li></ol><p><i>узако́нить</i> (uzakónitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to legalize, to legitimate, to legitimatize</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>зако́н</i> (zakón) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>узако́нивать</i> (uzakónivatʹ)</li></ol><p><i>узако́нить</i> (uzakónitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uzbek (a person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Uzbek</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;archaic) ("Arkhangelsk") The shards of a broken mirror</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridle (headgear for horse)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>узда</i> (uzda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uzda</i>. Compare Bulgarian <i>юзда́</i> (juzdá), Czech <i>uzda</i>, Macedonian <i>узда</i> (uzda), Polish <i>uzda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridle (headgear for horse)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) frenulum, frenum</li></ol><p><i>узде́ц-</i> (uzdéc-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knot, bundle</li><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) junction</li><li>node</li><li>center, centre, hub</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ganglion</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) knot (unit of speed)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫzlъ</i> (“knot”). Doublet of <i>ве́нзель</i> (vénzelʹ), a borrowing from Polish <i>węzeł</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>ѫзлъ</i> (ǫzlŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́зел</i> (úzel): (small) knot, nodule</li><li>a small parcel, bundle</li></ol><p><i>у́зел</i> (úzel) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij, “narrow”)</li></ol><p><i>у́з(кий)</i> (úz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow</li><li>tight</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism,&#32;offensive) deliberate misspelling of <i>русский</i> (russkij, “Russian, related to Russia”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫzъkъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énǵʰus</i>. Cognates include Armenian <i>անձուկ</i> (anjuk, “narrow”), German <i>eng</i> (“narrow”), Irish <i>cúng</i> (“narrow”), Latin <i>angustus</i> (“narrow”), Sanskrit <i>अंहु</i> (aṃhu, “narrow”).</p><p>Sense 3 was popularised by a parody of Shaman's music video "Ya Russkiy" ("I am a Russian") as a pun on "ру́сский" ("a Russian").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>narrowly (by a narrow margin)</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a gook (Asian person)</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij, “narrow, thin”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any jewel beetle in the genus <i>Agrilus</i>.</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>узкоте́лая зла́тка</i> (uzkotélaja zlátka). By surface analysis, <i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>зла́тка</i> (zlátka, “jewel beetle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;rail transport) narrow gauge</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>колея́</i> (kolejá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) angustifolious, narrow-leaved</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrow-mindedness</li></ol><p><i>узколо́бый</i> (uzkolóbyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow-browed, having a narrow forehead</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) narrow-minded</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лоб</i> (lob) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrowly professional (relating only to a specific profession or occupation)</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>профессиона́льный</i> (professionálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any jewel beetle in the genus <i>Agrilus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>узкоте́лый</i> (uzkotélyj, “narrow-bodied”) +&lrm; <i>зла́тка</i> (zlátka, “jewel beetle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow-bodied; having a body which is narrow or thin</li></ol><p><i>у́зкий</i> (úzkij, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) junction, transfer</li><li>nodal, nodular</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) pivotal, key, main, central</li></ol><p><i>у́зел</i> (úzel) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>recognizable, familiar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cognizance, recognition</li></ol><p><i>узнава́ть</i> (uznavátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, to recognize</li><li>to learn, to get to know</li><li>to find out, to inquire</li><li>to experience</li></ol><p><i>узна́ть</i> (uznátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recognize</li><li>to get to know</li><li>to learn, to find out, to hear</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>знать</i> (znatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner (also figuratively)</li></ol><p><i>у́зы</i> (úzy) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik). Cognate with Czech <i>vězeň</i>, Polish <i>więzień</i> (each from a verb with prothetic <i>в</i>, see Russian <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ)); Serbo-Croatian <i>сужањ</i>, Slovene <i>suženj</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothing and shoes</i>) too narrow, too tight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pattern, design, tracery</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>узоръ</i> (uzorŭ). Cognate with <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ, “to look, see”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patterned</li></ol><p><i>узо́р</i> (uzór) +&lrm; <i>-чатый</i> (-čatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowness</li></ol><p><i>у́з(кий)</i> (úz(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to see, to behold</li><li>(dated,&#32;literary) to gaze <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usurper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usurpation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usurpation</li></ol><p><i>узурпи́ровать</i> (uzurpírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to usurp (to seize power)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) usage, standard, customary practice.</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) usage (generally accepted use of language units)</li></ol><p>From French <i>usus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>у́зус</i> (úzus).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonds, ties</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫza</i>, the form derives from the plural (*ǫzy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weekend</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>weekend</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) maternal uncle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ujь</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Polish <i>wuj</i>, Slovak <i>ujo</i>, Slovene <i>ujec</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>ујак</i>, <i>ujak</i>, also <i>ујко</i>, <i>ujko</i>, Bulgarian <i>вуйчо</i> (vujčo), Macedonian <i>вујко</i> (vujko). Non-Slavic cognates include Old Prussian <i>avis</i> (“maternal uncle”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍅𐍉</i> (awō, “grandmother”), Latin <i>avus</i>, Old Armenian <i>հաւ</i> (haw).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uyghur (a member of a Turkic ethnic group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Uyghur</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lots, heaps, a tremendous lot, masses</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ujьma</i>, related to <i>&#42;ęti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to depart, to go (away) (by foot)</li><li>to escape, to evade</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to resign, to retire</li><li>to be lost</li><li>to fail</li><li>to sink, to plunge</li><li>to be spent</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) to die</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-йти́</i> (-jtí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take French leave; to take a sudden or unannounced departure, or one taken without permission; to go AWOL</li></ol><p>Literally, “to leave in an English way”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enactment, ordinance, decree, edict, ukase</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction, direction</li><li>indication</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stated, mentioned, indicated, specified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>index, indicator, guide, pointer</li><li>cursor</li><li>traffic sign</li><li>place-name sign (typically <i>указа́тель населённого пу́нкта</i>)</li></ol><p><i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>demonstrative pronoun (pronoun which replaces a noun)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicating, indicatory</li></ol><p><i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefinger, index finger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signpost (post bearing a sign that gives information on directions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to indicate</li><li>to point</li><li>to give instructions, to explain</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>каза́ть</i> (kazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) orders, instructions</li></ol><p><i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to indicate</li><li>to point</li><li>to give instructions, to explain</li></ol><p><i>указа́ть</i> (ukazátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick, to sting, to puncture</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to rebuke, to ridicule</li></ol><p><i>уколо́ть</i> (ukolótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out, to tire out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to send away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ката́ть</i> (katátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave, to drive off</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>кати́ть</i> (katítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wear out, to tire out</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to send away</li><li>to roll away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to leave, to drive off</li></ol><p><i>укати́ть</i> (ukatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p><p><i>уката́ть</i> (ukatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion sickness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>structure, setup</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piling, stacking, packing, stowage</li><li>hair setting, hair styling</li><li>laying</li><li>little chest, little box</li></ol><p><i>укла́д(ывать)</i> (uklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packer, layer</li><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) lip-synched script writer</li></ol><p><i>укла́д(ывать)</i> (uklád(yvatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay</li><li>to pack up</li><li>to stow, to pile, to stack</li><li>to arrange, to style (in terms of hair)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pack (<i>up</i>), to be packing (<i>up</i>), to be packing one's things</li><li>to go (<i>in, into</i>)</li><li>to keep (<i>within</i>), to confine oneself (<i>to</i>)</li><li>to lie down</li><li>to penetrate into consciousness, to become clear; to be assimilated</li></ol><p><i>укла́дывать</i> (ukládyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleak (small European river fish)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uklěja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination, rake</li><li>slope, declivity, gradient</li><li>bias</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) deviation (heretical belief, especially relative to a prescribed form of Communism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evasion, avoidance; dodge</li><li>swerve</li><li>digression</li><li>deviation</li><li>deflexion</li></ol><p><i>уклони́ть</i> (uklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) deviationism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) deviationist</li><li>(<i>military</i>) (conscription, draft) evader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;transitive) to move aside; to cause to slant</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>клони́ть</i> (klonítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to dodge</li><li>to deviate <i>(from a direction)</i></li><li>to avoid, to dodge, to evade, to elude</li><li>to digress, to wander away</li></ol><p><i>уклони́ть</i> (uklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>evasively</li></ol><p><i>укло́нчивый</i> (uklónčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evasive</li></ol><p><i>уклон(и́ться)</i> (uklon(ítʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;transitive) to move aside; to cause to slant</li></ol><p><i>уклони́ть</i> (uklonítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, to dodge</li><li>to deviate <i>(from a direction)</i></li><li>to avoid, to dodge, to evade, to elude</li><li>to digress, to wander away</li></ol><p><i>уклоня́ть</i> (uklonjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rowlock, oarlock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prick, pricking</li><li>(<i>fencing</i>) hit, touch</li><li>injection, shot</li><li>piquing remark, provoking remark</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick, to sting, to puncture</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to rebuke, to ridicule</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>уко́л</i> (ukól)</li></ol><p><i>уко́л</i> (ukól) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete, to replenish</li><li>to staff, to man, to bring up to strength</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>комплектова́ть</i> (komplektovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten</li></ol><p><i>укороти́ть</i> (ukorotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten, to become shorter, <i>(day length)</i> to draw in</li></ol><p><i>укора́чивать</i> (ukoráčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-корени́ть</i> (-korenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to root itself, to take/strike root</li><li>to get implanted, to take/strike root</li></ol><p><i>укорени́ть</i> (ukorenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant</li></ol><p><i>укорени́ть</i> (ukorenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to root itself, to take/strike root</li><li>to get implanted, to take/strike root</li></ol><p><i>укореня́ть</i> (ukorenjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproachment</li></ol><p>From <i>уко́р</i> (ukór) +&lrm; <i>-изна</i> (-izna).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>reproachfully, disapprovingly</li></ol><p><i>укори́зненный</i> (ukoríznennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reproachful, disapproving</li></ol><p><i>укори́зна</i> (ukorízna) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shorten</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stub</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach</li><li>to admonish</li></ol><p><i>укори́ть</i> (ukorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a Ukrainian person</li></ol><p>From a nationalistic fringe theory deriving the name of Ukraine from an ancient people of the same name.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>furtively</li><li>stealthily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainian (ethnic/citizen)</li></ol><p><i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainization; becoming more Ukrainian.</li></ol><p><i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína) +&lrm; <i>-иза́ция</i> (-izácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainophile, one who loves Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>Украина</i> (Ukraina) +&lrm; <i>-фил</i> (-fil). Compare Ukrainian <i>українофі́л</i> (ukrajinofíl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainophilia, love of Ukraine</li></ol><p>From <i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína) +&lrm; <i>-филия</i> (-filija). Compare Ukrainian <i>українофі́лія</i> (ukrajinofílija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainophilia, love of Ukraine</li></ol><p><i>украинофи́л</i> (ukrainofíl) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Compare Ukrainian <i>українофі́льство</i> (ukrajinofílʹstvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainophobe, one who hates or fears Ukraine or Ukrainians</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainophobia; the fear, hostility or prejudice towards Ukraine or Ukrainians</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainian (language), ellipsis of <i>украи́нский язы́к</i> (ukraínskij jazýk)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ukrainian</li></ol><p><i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, to embellish, to beautify, to decorate, to ornament</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сить</i> (krásitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ukrasti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn, to embellish, to beautify, to decorate, to ornament</li></ol><p><i>укра́сить</i> (ukrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decoration, ornament, ornamentation</li></ol><p><i>укра́сить</i> (ukrásitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>to consolidate</li><li>to fasten</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>кре́п(кий)</i> (krép(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stronger, to consolidate</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to fortify one's position</li></ol><p><i>укрепи́ть</i> (ukrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strengthening, consolidation, reinforcement</li><li>fortification, stockade</li></ol><p><i>укрепи́ть</i> (ukrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>to consolidate</li><li>to fasten</li></ol><p><i>укрепи́ть</i> (ukrepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stronger, to consolidate</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to fortify one's position</li></ol><p><i>укрепля́ть</i> (ukrepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secluded</li><li>comfortable, cozy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>кро́ме</i> (króme) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;politics) Ukronazi; any Ukrainian national who supports the Euromaidan movement and the subsequent Revolution of Dignity</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) any Ukrainian whatsoever</li></ol><p><i>укр</i> (ukr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>наци</i> (naci), contraction from украинец (a Ukrainian) and нацист (a Nazi).</p><p>Derived from accusations against the Ukrainian government of being a "Nazi government" due to the usage of the Azov Battalion into its armed forces, which used fascistic symbols; or the popularity of Stephan Bandera as a symbol used by some Ukrainian nationalist groups.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;politics) Ukronazi; any Ukrainian national who supports the Euromaidan movement and the subsequent Revolution of Dignity</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) any Ukrainian whatsoever</li></ol><p><i>укр</i> (ukr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>наци́ст</i> (nacíst).</p><p>Derived from accusations against the Ukrainian government of being a "Nazi government" due to the usage of the Azov Battalion into its armed forces, which used fascistic symbols; or the popularity of Stephan Bandera as a symbol used by some Ukrainian nationalist groups.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) dill (<i>Anethum graveolens</i>)</li><li>fennel (<i>Foeniculum vulgare</i>)</li><li>dropwort (<i>Oenanthe aquatica</i>, syn <i>Oenanthe phellandrium</i>)</li><li>rock samphire (<i>Crithmum maritimum</i>)</li><li>mayweed (<i>Anthemis cotula</i>)</li><li>sulphurweed</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Ukrainian (member of a side of the war in Donbass since 2014)</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;by extension) any Ukrainian in general</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ukropъ</i>.</p><p>Sense 7 mocks the similarity of the word with the name <i>Украи́на</i> (Ukraína), and the use of the dill plant (Anethum graveolens) in traditional Ukrainian cuisine.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang) a Ukrainian ultranationalist</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;slang,&#32;by extension) any Ukrainian person</li></ol><p><i>Украина</i> (Ukraina, “Ukraine”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>питек</i> (pitek, “austropithecus, brute”). Originally coined in mockery towards Ukrainian nationalist beliefs about their country as "the oldest nation in the world", later used to dehumanize Ukrainian nationalists and people in general as fanatics, or Ukraine itself as a backwards country.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dill</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) fennel</li></ol><p><i>укро́п</i> (ukróp) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tameable</li><li>controllable</li></ol><p><i>укроти́ть</i> (ukrotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamer</li></ol><p><i>укроти́ть</i> (ukrotítʹ, “to tame”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame</li><li>to subdue, to control, to curb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оукротити</i> (ukrotiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krotiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>кро́т(кий)</i> (krót(kij)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame</li><li>to subdue, to control, to curb</li></ol><p><i>укроти́ть</i> (ukrotítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;uncommon,&#32;neologism) the Ukrainian-speaking community on Twitter.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>украи́нский</i> (ukraínskij, “Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>Тви́ттер</i> (Tvítter, “Twitter”). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>укртві́</i> (ukrtví)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enlargement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbourer</li></ol><p><i>укрыва́ться</i> (ukryvátʹsja, “to conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment; harboring; cover-up</li></ol><p><i>укрыва́тель</i> (ukryvátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shelter</li><li>to conceal</li></ol><p><i>укры́ть</i> (ukrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover/wrap oneself</li><li>to seek shelter, to find/take shelter/cover, to take cover</li><li>to escape</li></ol><p><i>укрыва́ть</i> (ukryvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, wrapping</li><li>concealment</li><li>cover, shelter</li></ol><p><i>укры́ть</i> (ukrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тие</i> (-tije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><li>to shelter</li><li>to conceal</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover/wrap oneself</li><li>to seek shelter, to find/take shelter/cover, to take cover</li><li>to escape</li></ol><p><i>укры́ть</i> (ukrýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>уксусъ</i> (uksusŭ), from Ancient Greek <i>ὄξος</i> (óxos, “vinegar”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acetic acid, ethanoic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar cruet (vessel for serving vinegar)</li></ol><p><i>у́ксус</i> (úksus) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar cruet (vessel for serving vinegar)</li></ol><p><i>у́ксус</i> (úksus) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) ukulele</li></ol><p>From English <i>ukulele</i>, from Hawaiian <i>ʻukulele</i>, from <i>ʻuku</i> (“flea”) + <i>lele</i> (“jump, jumping”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li><li>to sting</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>куси́ть</i> (kusítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap up, to muffle <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ку́тать</i> (kútatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap oneself up (<i>in warm clothing or a blanket</i>); to bundle up</li></ol><p><i>уку́тать</i> (ukútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap up, to muffle <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>уку́тать</i> (ukútatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>St., street</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>у́лица</i> (úlica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detect, to catch, to perceive</li><li>to locate, to pick up, to receive</li><li>to catch, to understand</li></ol><p><i>улови́ть</i> (ulovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to arrange, to fix up, to reconcile, to make up, to patch up</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ла́дить</i> (láditʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to arrange, to fix up, to reconcile, to make up, to patch up</li></ol><p><i>ула́дить</i> (uláditʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uhlan, lancer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ulaanbaatar (the capital of Mongolia)</li></ol><p><i>Улан-Ба́тор</i> (Ulan-Bátor) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hive, beehive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ulьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) beehive</li></ol><p>From <i>у́лей</i> (úlej) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with stress shift (as is common with <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scarper, to skedaddle (to quickly run away)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ле́пет</i> (lépet, “babble”) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scarper, to skedaddle (to quickly run away)</li></ol><p><i>улепетну́ть</i> (ulepetnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to depart (by flying)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be carried away or transported in thoughts or feelings</li></ol><p><i>улете́ть</i> (uletétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly away, to depart (by flying)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be carried away or transported in thoughts or feelings</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лете́ть</i> (letétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to evaporate, to vaporize, to volatilize</li></ol><p><i>улету́чить</i> (uletúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evaporate, to vaporize</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to vanish</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to scatter in space (<i>of light or sound</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to be quickly spent or used up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to leave quickly, to scram</li></ol><p><i>улету́чивать</i> (uletúčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to evaporate, to vaporize, to volatilize</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лету́чий</i> (letúčij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evaporate, to vaporize</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to vanish</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to scatter in space (<i>of light or sound</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to be quickly spent or used up</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to leave quickly, to scram</li></ol><p><i>улету́чить</i> (uletúčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down</li><li>to settle</li><li>to calm down, to subside</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лечь</i> (lečʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip away, to sneak away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to evade [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘something unpleasant’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лизну́ть</i> (liznútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) evidence, corpus delicti, proof</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>уличи́ть</i> (uličítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snail</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cochlea</li></ol><p><i>ули́та</i> (ulíta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), apparently from <i>у́лей</i> (úlej), compare for the suffix <i>волоки́та</i> (volokíta), <i>раки́та</i> (rakíta). In Belarusian <i>улі́тка</i> (ulítka); not used in Ukrainian (borrowed however in technical senses as <i>улі́тка</i> (ulítka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snail</li></ol><p><i>ули́тка</i> (ulítka) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) snail</li></ol><p><i>ули́тка</i> (ulítka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>улица</i> (ulica), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ulica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;ironic) to behold (to see in close proximity)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лицезре́ть</i> (licezrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish the guilt <i>(of)</i></li><li>to expose</li></ol><p><i>уличи́ть</i> (uličítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish the guilt <i>(of)</i></li><li>to expose</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-личи́ть</i> (-ličítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) street</li></ol><p><i>у́лица</i> (úlica, “street”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catch, take (fish)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>улови́ть</i> (ulovítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tangible</li><li>understandable</li><li>perceptible, noticeable</li></ol><p><i>улови́ть</i> (ulovítʹ, “to perceive, to understand”) +&lrm; <i>-и́мый</i> (-ímyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to detect, to catch, to perceive</li><li>to locate, to pick up, to receive</li><li>to catch, to understand</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лови́ть</i> (lovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruse, dodge, trick, subterfuge</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ulovъka</i>. Equivalent to <i>улови́ть</i> (ulovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;law) code</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay</li><li>to pack up</li><li>to stow, to pile, to stack</li><li><i>(hair)</i> to arrange, to style</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ложи́ть</i> (ložítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pack <i>(up)</i>, to be packing <i>(up)</i>, to be packing one's things</li><li>to go <i>(in, into)</i></li><li>to keep <i>(within)</i>, to confine oneself <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>уложи́ть</i> (uložítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́лица</i> (úlica)</li></ol><p><i>ул(ица)</i> (ul(ica)) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oolong</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ulus, uls (various historical or modern Turkic or Mongolian regions)</li></ol><p>From Turkic or Mongolic. Compare Mongolian <i>улс</i> (uls) / <i>ᠤᠯᠤᠰ</i> (ulus), Kazakh <i>ұлыс</i> (ūlys), Tatar <i>олыс</i> (olıs), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find (time)</li><li>to seize (a moment)</li></ol><p><i>улучи́ть</i> (ulučítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find (time)</li><li>to seize (a moment)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лучи́ть</i> (lučítʹ, “to allow, originally to accomplish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve, to make better, to ameliorate</li></ol><p><i>улу́чшить</i> (ulúčšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to improve, to ameliorate, to take a turn for the better</li></ol><p><i>улучша́ть</i> (ulučšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improvement</li></ol><p><i>улу́чшить</i> (ulúčšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve, to make better, to ameliorate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лу́чший</i> (lúčšij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to improve, to ameliorate, to take a turn for the better</li></ol><p><i>улу́чшить</i> (ulúčšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to like, to appreciate, to relish</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>лы́бить</i> (lýbitʹ, “to smile”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smile</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ulybъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>улыба́ть(ся)</i> (ulybátʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) to cheer up</li><li>(dated,&#32;dialectal) to deceive, to cheat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile</li></ol><p><i>улыбну́ть</i> (ulybnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>улы́бка</i> (ulýbka)</li></ol><p><i>улы́бка</i> (ulýbka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smiling</li></ol><p><i>улыб(а́ться)</i> (ulyb(átʹsja)) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Ulvophyceae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>ульвофици́евый</i> (ulʹvoficíjevyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ultimatum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ultra- (excessively, to an extreme)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) ultrahyperbolic</li></ol><p><i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>гиперболи́ческий</i> (giperbolíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;rare) westoid, doggedly pro-Western</li></ol><p><i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>за́падный</i> (západnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ultrasound (sound with a frequency greater than the upper limit of human hearing)</li></ol><p><i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ultrasonic</li></ol><p><i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>звук</i> (zvuk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>VHF radio</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ultramarine (pigment, color)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ultranationalism (extreme nationalism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ultrapatriotism</li></ol><p><i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>патриоти́зм</i> (patriotízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) ultraright</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ultraviolet</li></ol><p>From <i>ультра-</i> (ulʹtra-) +&lrm; <i>фиоле́товый</i> (fiolétovyj) or <i>ультрафиоле́т</i> (ulʹtrafiolét) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>ультрафіоле́товий</i> (ulʹtrafiolétovyj), Belarusian <i>ультрафіяле́тавы</i> (ulʹtrafijaljétavy), Polish <i>ultrafioletowy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ulyanovsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Улья́нов(ск)</i> (Ulʹjánov(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) booing, hooting, mocking</li></ol><p><i>улюлю́кать</i> (uljuljúkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><li>intellect</li><li>cleverness, brains, wit</li><li>reason</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>умъ</i> (umŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;umъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good sense</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>ра́зум</i> (rázum)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>уменьши́тельный</i> (umenʹšítelʹnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to reduce, to lessen, to diminish</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to reduce the role, to value or significance of</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to belittle, to humiliate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ма́лый</i> (mályj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mad, crazy, out of one's mind</li></ol><p><i>ума́</i> (umá) +&lrm; <i>лишённый</i> (lišónnyj). Literally, “deprived of mind”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass over in silence, to hold back, to hush up</li></ol><p><i>умолча́ть</i> (umolčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to reduce, to lessen, to diminish</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) to reduce the role, the value or significance of</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) to belittle, to humiliate</li></ol><p><i>умали́ть</i> (umalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to belittle</li></ol><p><i>умаля́ть</i> (umaljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skilled craftsman/artisan</li><li>jack of all trades</li><li>do-it-himself man, do-it-himselfer</li></ol><p><i>уме́ть</i> (umétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лец</i> (-lec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ably, skillfully (with great ability)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able, skillful, skilled, competent, expert</li></ol><p><i>уметь</i> (umetʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ability (a skill or competence)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>уме́ть</i> (umétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>уменьши́тельный</i> (umenʹšítelʹnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>уме́ние</i> (uménije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) minuend (number from which another is subtracted)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to decrease, to lessen, to reduce, to abate</li></ol><p><i>уме́ньшить</i> (uménʹšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to decrease</li></ol><p><i>уменьша́ть</i> (umenʹšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reduction (act, process, or result of reducing)</li><li>diminution</li><li>lessening</li><li>mitigation</li></ol><p><i>уме́ньшить</i> (uménʹšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocorism (a term of endearment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive (word form expressing smallness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) diminutive</li></ol><p><i>уменьши́тельный</i> (umenʹšítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ласка́тельный</i> (laskátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocorism (a term of endearment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive (word form expressing smallness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive</li></ol><p><i>уме́ньшить</i> (uménʹšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to decrease, to lessen, to reduce, to abate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to diminish, to decrease</li></ol><p>From <i>уме́ньшить</i> (uménʹšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (stem-stressed) or from <i>уменьши́ть</i> (umenʹšítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja) (ending-stressed).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>moderately</li></ol><p><i>уме́ренный</i> (umérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moderation</li></ol><p><i>уме́ренный</i> (umérennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate (<i>not excessive</i>), temperate, mild</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die of natural causes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to disappear, to cease</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to become inoperable, to stop working</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to feel an emotion extremely strongly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;umerti</i>, (<i>*u-</i> + <i>*merti</i>) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to lessen the force or magnitude of</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ме́рить</i> (méritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to kill, to destroy, to make unfit for habitation or growth</li><li>to suppress (thoughts, feelings, etc.)</li><li>to deprive (<i>of hope, joy, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оумрьтвити</i> (umrĭtviti). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мёртвый</i> (mjórtvyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceased person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to kill, to destroy, to make unfit for habitation or growth</li><li>to suppress (thoughts, feelings, etc.)</li><li>to deprive (<i>of hope, joy, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оумрьщвлѣти</i> (umrĭštvlěti). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мёртвый</i> (mjórtvyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to diminish, to lessen the force or magnitude of</li></ol><p><i>уме́рить</i> (uméritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find room <i>(for)</i>, to make go in, to put in</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-мести́ть</i> (-mestítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find/have enough room for oneself</li><li>to go in, to fit in, to go <i>(into)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>умести́ть</i> (umestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pertinently</li><li>to the point</li><li>apropos (timely, at a good time)</li></ol><p><i>уме́стный</i> (uméstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relevance, relevancy</li><li>appropriateness</li><li>appositeness (the state of being apposite)</li><li>propriety</li><li>pertinence, pertinency</li><li>aptitude</li></ol><p><i>уме́стный</i> (uméstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relevant</li><li>pertinent, apt, apposite (appropriate, well-suited)</li><li>opportune</li><li>germane</li><li>in place</li><li>apropos</li><li>right</li><li>pat</li><li>felicitous</li><li>pointful</li><li>seasonable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be able to; can</li><li>to know how to</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оумѣти</i> (uměti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uměti</i>. Cognate to <i>ум</i> (um).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find room <i>(for)</i>, to make go in, to put in</li></ol><p><i>умести́ть</i> (umestítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find/have enough room for oneself</li><li>to go in, to fit in, to go <i>(into)</i>, to fit <i>(into)</i></li></ol><p><i>умеща́ть</i> (umeščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touching, moving (<i>emotionally</i>)</li><li>tender emotion</li></ol><p><i>умили́ть</i> (umilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Умиление)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eleousa, Umileniye, Glykophilousa, Mater Amabilis; an iconographic type of sacred image of Mother of God</li></ol><p>Literally, “tenderness”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;obsolete) alternative form of <i>умиле́ние</i> (umilénije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to move (<i>emotionally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ми́лый</i> (mílyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be touched (<i>emotionally</i>), to experience tenderness</li><li>to convey tenderness</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to become kindhearted</li></ol><p><i>умили́ть</i> (umilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propitiate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ми́лостивый</i> (mílostivyj, “gracious”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propitiation, obsecration</li></ol><p><i>умилостивля́ть</i> (umilostivljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to propitiate</li></ol><p><i>уми́лостивить</i> (umílostivitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, to move (<i>emotionally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>умили́ть</i> (umilítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be touched (<i>emotionally</i>), to experience tenderness</li><li>to convey tenderness</li><li>(figurative,&#32;dated) to become kindhearted</li></ol><p><i>умиля́ть</i> (umiljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>emotionally moved, touched, (highly) emotional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dying (process of approaching death)</li></ol><p><i>умира́ть</i> (umirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die of natural causes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to disappear, to cease</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) to become inoperable, to stop working</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to feel an emotion extremely strongly</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-мира́ть</i> (-mirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dying, moribund</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dying man</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pacify, to reconcile</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мири́ть</i> (mirítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appeasement, pacification</li></ol><p><i>умиротвори́ть</i> (umirotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pacify</li><li>to appease</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to make peace, to end hostilities</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мир</i> (mir, “peace”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>твори́ться</i> (tvorítʹsja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pacify</li><li>to appease</li></ol><p><i>умиротвори́ть</i> (umirotvorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to make peace, to end hostilities</li></ol><p><i>умиротвори́ться</i> (umirotvorítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>ум</i> (um)</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>ум</i> (um, “intellect”): (<i>colloquial</i>) big, intellect, big mind</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(orthography,&#32;phonology) umlaut (diacritical mark or partial assimilation of a vowel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Umlaut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(orthography,&#32;phonology) umlaut (diacritical mark or partial assimilation of a vowel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Umlaut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) ummah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic أُمَّة (ʔumma),</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>умно́</i> (umnó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>умно́</i> (umnó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become wiser</li></ol><p><i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>know-all</li><li>smart aleck, smartass</li></ol><p><i>уме́ть</i> (umétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clear head</li><li>clever boy, good boy, clever girl, good girl</li><li>clever person, wise person, good person</li></ol><p><i>у́м(ный)</i> (úm(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to act smart, to be a smartarse/smartass</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapproving) to overanalyse things, to overcomplicate things</li></ol><p><i>у́мник</i> (úmnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clever boy, clever girl</li></ol><p><i>у́мник</i> (úmnik) +&lrm; <i>-чка</i> (-čka) or <i>у́мница</i> (úmnica) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cleverly, wisely, sensibly</li></ol><p><i>у́мный</i> (úmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to multiply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>умъножати</i> (umŭnožati). By surface analysis, <i>умно́жить</i> (umnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase (<i>in number or degree</i>)</li></ol><p><i>умножа́ть</i> (umnožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, augmentation</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) multiplication (process)</li></ol><p><i>умно́жить</i> (umnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) to multiply [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘by something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>Either from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мно́жить</i> (mnóžitʹ), or from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мно́го</i> (mnógo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase (<i>in number or degree</i>)</li></ol><p><i>умно́жить</i> (umnóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clever, smart, intelligent</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conclusion, deduction</li><li>inference; reasoning</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>заключе́ние</i> (zaključénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speculation</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зре́ние</i> (zrénije, “sight, seeing”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speculative</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зреть</i> (zretʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) delirium</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>исступле́ние</i> (isstuplénije, “ecstasy, frenzy; rage”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move <i>(+to)</i>, to move by entreaties</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>моли́ть</i> (molítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(in expressions only)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(person)</i> to fall/become silent, to lapse into silence</li><li><i>(sound, noise)</i> to stop</li></ol><p><i>умо́лкнуть</i> (umólknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(person)</i> to fall/become silent, to lapse into silence</li><li><i>(sound, noise)</i> to stop</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мо́лкнуть</i> (mólknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passing over in silence; failure to mention; reticence; non-disclosure</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) preterition</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) default</li></ol><p><i>умолча́ть</i> (umolčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass over in silence, to hold back, to hush up</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>молча́ть</i> (molčátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entreat, to beg, to implore</li></ol><p><i>умоли́ть</i> (umolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pleadingly, imploringly, entreatingly, beseechingly</li></ol><p><i>умоля́ющий</i> (umoljájuščij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleading, imploring, suppliant, entreating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) mentality, mindset, frame of mind, state of mind</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>настрое́ние</i> (nastrojénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness, insanity, lunacy</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>помеша́тельство</i> (pomešátelʹstvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(temporary) insanity, aberration (minor or temporary mental disorder)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) prodigious, breathtaking, astounding, terrific</li></ol><p><i>ум</i> (um, “mind, intellect”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>помрачи́ть</i> (pomračítʹ, “to darken, to obscure”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme exhaustion, extreme fatigue</li><li>(predicative,&#32;colloquial) side-splitting, awfully funny, a scream</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>умори́ть</i> (umorítʹ), <i>умори́ть</i> (umorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hilarious, very funny</li></ol><p><i>умори́ть</i> (umorítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) umrah (a minor hajj, a lesser pilgrimage to Mecca, other than at the time of the hajj)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عُمْرَة (ʕumra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mentally (in a mental manner)</li></ol><p><i>у́мственный</i> (úmstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intellectual, mental (belonging to, or performed by, the intellect&#59; mental or cognitive)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to ponder</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;disapprovingly) to philosophise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make wise, to make wiser</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мудр-</i> (mudr-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become wiser</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage to, to contrive to (to succeed in doing something difficult) [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) to manage to (to do something that could easily have been avoided) [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>умудри́ть</i> (umudrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make wise, to make wiser</li></ol><p><i>умудри́ть</i> (umudrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become wiser</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage to, to contrive to (to succeed in doing something difficult) [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) to manage to (to do something that could easily have been avoided) [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>умудря́ть</i> (umudrjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whirl away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мчать</i> (mčatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dash/whirl away, <i>(time)</i> to fly past</li></ol><p><i>умча́ть</i> (umčátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sink</li><li>washstand</li><li>wash basin</li><li>lavabo water tank</li></ol><p>From <i>умыва́ть</i> (umyvátʹ, “to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-льник</i> (-lʹnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washroom</li></ol><p><i>умыва́ть</i> (umyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash up (wash one's hands and face)</li></ol><p><i>умыва́ть</i> (umyvátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>design, intention</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to plan <i>(+to)</i>, to intend <i>(+to)</i>, to plot <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мы́слить</i> (mýslitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>мыть</i> (mytʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash up (wash one's hands and face)</li></ol><p><i>умы́ть</i> (umýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>deliberately, intentionally</li></ol><p><i>умы́шленный</i> (umýšlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deliberate, intentional, designed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to plan <i>(+to)</i>, to intend <i>(+to)</i>, to plot <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>умы́слить</i> (umýslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jujube (fruit and tree)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>უნაბი</i> (unabi), from Arabic عُنَّاب (ʕunnāb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to inherit (property, traditions, vices, etc.)</li><li>to receive an inheritance [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘from someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>насле́довать</i> (naslédovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbibium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbiquadium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbinilium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbipentium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbitrium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbiunium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>undecillion (10<sup>36</sup>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to take off, to carry (away)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take (a life), to claim (a life)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>нести́</i> (nestí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave quickly, to speed off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to fly by (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be carried back (<i>of thoughts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>унести́</i> (unestí) +&lrm; <i>-сь</i> (-sʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>uni- (one)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) uni (university)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>университе́т</i> (universitét).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>department store</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>универса́льный магази́н</i> (universálʹnyj magazín, “universal store”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) jack of all trades, all-round craftsman, factotum</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) universalist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) estate car, station wagon</li><li>(historical,&#32;law,&#32;inanimate) type of legal decree or manifest issued in Poland and Ukraine</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>ūniversālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>universalization</li></ol><p>From <i>универсализи́ровать</i> (universalizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ация</i> (-acija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>universality</li></ol><p><i>универса́льный</i> (universálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>universal (useful for many purposes)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>ūniversālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>универса́льный магази́н самообслу́живания</i> (universálʹnyj magazín samoobslúživanija, “universal self-service store”): supermarket</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>university</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Universität</i>, from Latin <i>ūniversitās</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) university; academic (relating to an academy or other higher institution of learning)</li></ol><p><i>университе́т</i> (universitét) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>университе́т</i> (universitét).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate, to abase</li></ol><p>From <i>уни́зить</i> (unízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ). Compare Kashubian <i>ùniżac</i>, Silesian <i>uniżać</i>, and Polish <i>uniżać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble oneself, to grovel</li><li>to stoop to the point of [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘taking some action, e.g. a lie, a question’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>унижа́ть</i> (unižátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humiliation, abasement</li></ol><p><i>уни́зить</i> (unízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humiliated (<i>experiencing or expressing humiliation</i>)</li><li>abject (cast down&#59; rejected&#59; low-lying)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humiliating, degrading</li></ol><p><i>уни́зить</i> (unízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humiliate, to abase</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-ни́зить</i> (-nízitʹ). Compare Silesian <i>uniżyć</i> and Polish <i>uniżyć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to humble oneself, to grovel</li><li>to stoop to the point of [<i>with</i> <b>до</b> (do, + genitive) <small>‘taking some action, e.g. a lie, a question’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уни́зить</i> (unízitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniqueness (state or quality of being unique or one-of-a-kind)</li></ol><p><i>уника́льный</i> (unikálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unique</li></ol><p>From German <i>unikal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet, to calm (baby), to soothe, to make someone stop shouting, crying or behave violently, to stop (bleeding)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-нима́ть</i> (-nimátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow quiet, to quiet down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to abate, to stop</li></ol><p><i>унима́ть</i> (unimátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) unison (simultaneous playing of an identical note more than once)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet (ceramic bowl), flush toilet, water closet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ūnitās</i> (“unity”), with the final consonant perhaps influenced by <i>таз</i> (taz, “basin”). <i>Unitas</i> was originally introduced as the name of an early water closet invented by Thomas Twyford in 1884.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) unitary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>unitaire</i>, from <i>unité</i> +&lrm; <i>-aire</i>, from Latin <i>ūnitās</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unification</li><li>harmonization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to unify</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>unifizieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniform</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) circus staff</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pejorative (disparaging, belittling or derogatory)</li></ol><p><i>уничижи́ть</i> (uničižítʹ, “to humiliate”) (<i>dated</i>) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to annihilate, to obliterate</li><li>to abolish, to do away with</li><li>to crush</li></ol><p><i>уничто́жить</i> (uničtóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to go missing</li><li>to cease to exist</li></ol><p><i>уничтожа́ть</i> (uničtožátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, annihilation</li><li>extermination, elimination, eradication</li></ol><p><i>уничто́жить</i> (uničtóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destroyer (someone who destroys)</li></ol><p><i>уничто́жить</i> (uničtóžitʹ, “to destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, to annihilate, to obliterate, to wipe out, to blot out, to crush</li><li>to abolish, to do away with</li><li>to crush (<i>morally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ничто́ж(ный)</i> (ničtóž(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to go missing</li><li>to cease to exist</li></ol><p><i>уничто́жить</i> (uničtóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union (<i>of states under one monarch or of churches in full communion</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ūnio</i> (“unity, union”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away, to take off, to carry (away)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take (a life), to claim (a life)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave quickly, to speed off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to fly by (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be carried back (<i>of thoughts</i>)</li></ol><p><i>уноси́ть</i> (unosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;colloquial) NCO, noncommissioned officer</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>у́нтер-офице́р</i> (únter-oficér).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) non-commissioned officer; corporal; petty officer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Unteroffizier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununbium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununhexium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununquadium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununoctium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununpentium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununseptium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununtrium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ununennium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ounce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ūncia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose heart, to be cast down, to be dejected or depressed</li></ol><p><i>уны́ть</i> (unýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dejectedly, despondently</li><li>dismally, cheerlessly</li></ol><p><i>уны́лый</i> (unýlyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloomy, depressing, bleak</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>уны́ть</i> (unýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to lose heart, to be cast down, to be dejected or depressed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>оуныти</i> (unyti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyti</i> (“to yearn”). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ныть</i> (nytʹ, “to whimper, to whine, to ache”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet, to calm (baby), to soothe, to make someone stop shouting, crying or behave violently, to stop (bleeding)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-ня́ть</i> (-njátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow quiet, to quiet down</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to abate, to stop</li></ol><p><i>уня́ть</i> (unjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walkie-talkie (portable radio)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>walkie-talkie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fallen, dropped</li><li>sank, declined</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>in expressions only</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decadent sentiments</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decline, decay, decadence</li><li>collapse, breakdown, decline</li></ol><p><i>упад-</i> (upad-) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Probably adapted as an enlightenment term mid-18th century after Polish <i>upadek</i>, also <i>upad</i>, both also in the most literal senses of falling (on the head), the setting of the Sun etc., for the Latinate terms of <i>decadence</i>, a recurring idea in such works as Montesquieu’s Considerations on the Causes of the Greatness of the Romans and their Decline and Edward Gibbon’s History of the Decline and Fall of the Roman Empire, and their Russian titles.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>depressive, declining</li></ol><p><i>упа́док</i> (upádok) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pack <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пакова́ть</i> (pakovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(action)</i> packing, wrapping</li><li><i>(material)</i> wrapping, wrapper, packing, packaging</li></ol><p><i>упакова́ть</i> (upakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) packaging, wrapping</li></ol><p>From <i>упако́вка</i> (upakóvka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pack <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p><i>упакова́ть</i> (upakovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften away by steaming</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) to murk, to snuff</li></ol><p><i>упа́рить</i> (upáritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften away by steaming</li><li>(slang,&#32;euphemistic) to murk, to snuff</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>па́рить</i> (páritʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to save, to preserve</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пасти́</i> (pastí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, to drop</li><li>to sink, to decline, to worsen</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пасть</i> (pastʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to faint, to swoon (to lose consciousness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against)</i>, to lean <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal, to pilfer, to filch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lay stress <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пере́ть</i> (perétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against)</i></li><li>to jib, to balk, to refuse</li><li>to be hampered, to be held back, to hinge <i>(on)</i></li><li>to meet, to run into <i>(an obstacle)</i></li></ol><p><i>упере́ть</i> (uperétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to variegate, to diversify, to vary</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пёстр(ый)</i> (pjóstr(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to variegate, to diversify, to vary</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пёстр(ый)</i> (pjóstr(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get drunk</li><li>to revel <i>(in)</i>, to be intoxicated <i>(by)</i>, to feast one's eyes <i>(upon)</i>, to gloat <i>(over)</i></li></ol><p><i>упи́ться</i> (upítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against)</i>, to lean <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to steal, to pilfer, to filch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lay stress <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-пира́ть</i> (-pirátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rest <i>(against)</i>, to set <i>(against)</i></li><li>to jib, to balk, to refuse</li><li>to be hampered, to be held back, to hinge (<i>on</i>)</li></ol><p><i>упира́ть</i> (upirátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill in, to fill a space with writing</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>писа́ть</i> (pisátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fill in, to fill a space with writing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat with gusto, eat heartily</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>писыва́ть</i> (pisyvátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump, well-fed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten up</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пита́ть</i> (pitátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fatten up</li></ol><p><i>упита́ть</i> (upitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get drunk</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пить</i> (pitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>плати́ть</i> (platítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay</li></ol><p><i>уплати́ть</i> (uplatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compaction, consolidation</li><li>packing, sealing, gasketing</li><li>gasket, packing, seal</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) infiltration, hardening</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal</li><li>thickener</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compact, consolidate</li><li>to gasket, pack, seal</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) <i>(dwellers)</i> to make give up a part of their accommodation, to make share their dwelling with another family</li></ol><p><i>уплотни́ть</i> (uplotnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compressed, condensed, packed, tight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim away</li><li>to sail away</li></ol><p><i>уплы́ть</i> (uplýtʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim away</li><li>to sail away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>плы́ть</i> (plýtʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;high-style</i>) hope</li></ol><p><i>упова́ть</i> (upovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) to hope (for), to set one's hopes (upon) [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘something/someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оупъвати</i> (upŭvati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;upъvati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make similar [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>to liken, to compare [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>подо́бие</i> (podóbije) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become like, to resemble or start to resemble [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уподо́бить</i> (upodóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make similar [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>to liken, to compare [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone/something else’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уподо́бить</i> (upodóbitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become like, to resemble or start to resemble [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уподобля́ть</i> (upodobljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapture, ecstasy, thrill</li></ol><p><i>упои́ть</i> (upoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ravishing, entrancing</li></ol><p><i>упои́ть</i> (upoítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;religion) to bring peace to (someone), to free (someone) from mental anguish, to cause (someone) to rest in peace</li></ol><p><i>упоко́ить</i> (upokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;religion) causing to rest in peace, freeing from mental anguish</li><li>(dated,&#32;religion) burial place</li><li>(dated,&#32;religion) repose (stay of someone's soul in a state of complete rest in the afterlife)</li></ol><p><i>упоко́ить</i> (upokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;religion) to bring peace to (someone), to free (someone) from mental anguish, to cause (someone) to rest in peace</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>поко́й</i> (pokój) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repose, eternal rest (<i>of a soul</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>поко́й</i> (pokój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li></ol><p><i>уползти́</i> (upolztí) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crawl away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ползти́</i> (polztí)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorized representative, agent</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authorization (act of authorizing)</li></ol><p><i>уполномо́чивать</i> (upolnomóčivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to authorize, to depute</li></ol><p><i>уполномо́чить</i> (upolnomóčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to authorize, to depute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mention, mentioning; allusion; reference</li></ol><p><i>упомина́ть</i> (upominátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mention, to refer</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>помина́ть</i> (pominátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to remember, to commit to memory</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to recall</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>по́мнить</i> (pómnitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mention, to refer</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>помяну́ть</i> (pomjanútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, rest (object designed to be used to support something else)</li><li>stop, prop (e.g. a door stop)</li><li>stress, emphasis</li><li>(<i>music</i>) spike (of stringed instruments)</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>упира́ть</i> (upirátʹ, “to rest upon or lean on”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hard</li><li>stubbornly</li></ol><p><i>упо́рный</i> (upórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>persistent, insistent</li><li>stubborn (refusing to move or change one's opinion), resistant</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) thrust (<i>of a bearing or block</i>)</li></ol><p><i>упо́р</i> (upór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) obsessive, obsessed with something, crazy about something</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) high (under the influence of a drug)</li></ol><p><i>упоро́ться, упоро́ть</i> (uporótʹsja, uporótʹ); <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persistence, unyieldingness</li><li>obstinacy, stubbornness, pertinacity, doggedness</li></ol><p>From <i>упо́р</i> (upór) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persist <i>(in)</i>, to be stubborn/obstinate/unyielding</li></ol><p><i>упо́рство</i> (upórstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>streamlining, orderliness</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;informatoics) ordering, sorting</li><li>adjustment</li></ol><p><i>упоря́дочить</i> (uporjádočitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order, to normalize, to regulate</li></ol><p><i>упоря́дочить</i> (uporjádočitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in order, to normalize, to regulate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to apply</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>потреби́ть</i> (potrebítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, usage, application</li></ol><p><i>употреби́ть</i> (upotrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>употребле́ние</i> (upotreblénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use, to apply, to consume</li></ol><p><i>употреби́ть</i> (upotrebítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in use</li></ol><p>From <i>употребля́ть</i> (upotrebljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) check (<i>on someone</i>), control, justice (a way to control someone's wilful or lawless acts)</li><li>board, council</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) rule, administration, management</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) restitution, satisfaction (<i>for an injury or loss</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uprava</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resident manager, janitor, building superintendent, building supervisor</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>управля́ющий до́мом</i> (upravljájuščij dómom, “administrator of the house”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage, to cope <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>упра́вить</i> (uprávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manager, member of administrative staff</li></ol><p>From <i>управля́ть</i> (upravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нец</i> (-énec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administration, direction</li><li>management</li><li>board</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) control</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) government</li></ol><p><i>управля́ть</i> (upravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) management; administrative, managerial</li></ol><p><i>управле́ние</i> (upravlénije) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>управле́ние</i> (upravlénije).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manageability, controllability, manoeuvrability, dirigibility</li></ol><p><i>управля́емый</i> (upravljájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>controllable, manageable</li><li>guided</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guided missile</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roadside bomb (explosive device)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to govern, to rule [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to control, to manage [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to operate, to run [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a machine’</small>]</li><li>to drive, to steer [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a vehicle’</small>]</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to govern [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a specified case, etc.’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage, to cope <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>управля́ть</i> (upravljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manager, administrator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise</li></ol><p><i>упражня́ть</i> (upražnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exercise, to train</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice, to exercise</li></ol><p><i>упражня́ть</i> (upražnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abolition</li></ol><p><i>упраздни́ть</i> (uprazdnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abolish, to abrogate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оупраздьнити</i> (uprazdĭniti). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пра́здный</i> (prázdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abolish, to abrogate</li></ol><p><i>упраздни́ть</i> (uprazdnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entreat, to beg, to beseech</li></ol><p><i>упроси́ть</i> (uprosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preemptive strike (an attack launched as a defensive measure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, to upbraid, to blame</li></ol><p><i>упрёк</i> (uprjók) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reproach, to upbraid, to blame</li></ol><p><i>упрека́ть</i> (uprekátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entreat, to beg, to beseech, to prevail <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>проси́ть</i> (prosítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to simplify</li><li>to oversimplify</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>просто́й</i> (prostój) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ); less likely from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>прости́ть</i> (prostítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strengthening</li><li>consolidation</li></ol><p><i>упро́чить</i> (upróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ение</i> (-enije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to simplify</li><li>to oversimplify</li></ol><p><i>упрости́ть</i> (uprostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simplification</li></ol><p><i>упрости́ть</i> (uprostítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oversimplifying (tending to oversimplify)</li></ol><p><i>упроще́нец</i> (uproščénec) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simplified</li><li>simplistic, oversimplified</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resilient, elastic, bouncy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elasticity, resiliency, resilience</li></ol><p><i>упру́гий</i> (uprúgij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>team, relay <i>(of draught animals)</i></li><li>harness, gear</li></ol><p><i>у́пряжь</i> (úprjažʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harness (<i>for horses or other animals</i>)</li><li>(rail transport,&#32;obsolete) coupling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>а pigheaded, stubborn person</li></ol><p>From <i>упря́мый</i> (uprjámyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be obstinate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obstinately (in an obstinate manner)</li></ol><p><i>упря́мый</i> (uprjámyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstinacy, stubbornness</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stubborn (refusing to move or change one's opinion)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>прямо́й</i> (prjamój) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>несгиба́емый</i> (nesgibájemyj), <i>непрекло́нный</i> (nepreklónnyj), <i>неукло́нный</i> (neuklónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to take/put away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put away, to condemn <i>(to)</i>, to confine <i>(in)</i>, to incarcerate <i>(in)</i>, to coop up</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пря́тать</i> (prjátatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hide, to take/put away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to put away, to condemn <i>(to)</i>, to confine <i>(in)</i>, to incarcerate <i>(in)</i>, to coop up</li></ol><p><i>упря́тать</i> (uprjátatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reproach, reproof, rebuke (mild rebuke, or an implied criticism)</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;прёк</i> (&#42;prjok, “crosswise”), from <i>&#42;перёк</i> (&#42;perjók); see <i>поперёк</i> (poperjók). Original meaning "objection".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>oops (acknowledging a minor mistake)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>oops</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, to let slip</li><li>to miss, to lose</li><li>to overlook</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пуска́ть</i> (puskátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, to let slip</li><li>to miss, to lose</li><li>to overlook</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>пусти́ть</i> (pustítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omission, lapse, overlook</li></ol><p><i>упусти́ть</i> (upustítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Slavic mythology</i>) vampire</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively,&#32;derogatory) bloodsucker, ghoul, sadist (a cruel person)</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) asshole, leech (a mean and contemptible person, especially one who takes advantage of others)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫpyrь</i>. Doublet of <i>вампи́р</i> (vampír).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be breathless; to be out of breath</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>уро́чище</i> (uróčišče)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hoorah, hooray (elated expression of approval, especially as a battle cry)</li><li>yay (expression of happiness)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) jingoist; aggressive patriot; hypernationalist</li></ol><p>From <i>ура́</i> (urá, a warcry, literally “hooray”) +&lrm; <i>патрио́т</i> (patriót, “patriot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;derogatory) jingoism, aggressive patriotism</li></ol><p>From <i>ура́</i> (urá, a warcry, literally “hooray”) +&lrm; <i>патриоти́зм</i> (patriotízm, “patriotism”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) equation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to equalize (weight, size, rights, etc.)</li><li>to smooth, to make smooth</li><li>to level, to make even or level</li></ol><p><i>уравня́ть</i> (uravnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p><p><i>уровня́ть</i> (urovnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) top-down enforcement of uniformity</li></ol><p><i>ура́внивать</i> (urávnivatʹ, “to equalize, to save up”) +&lrm; <i>-ловка</i> (-lovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) equalizer, leveller (one who equalizes or levels)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>leveller (supporter of social equality)</li><li>(<i>history</i>) Leveller (member of the populist political movement during the Wars of the Three Kingdoms)</li></ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) equalizer, leveller (that which equalizes or levels)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>equalizer (device)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) equalizer</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to balance, to counterpoise</li><li>to counterbalance, to countervail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steady, balanced, even-tempered (<i>of one's temper, of a person</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to balance, to counterpoise</li><li>to counterbalance, to countervail</li></ol><p><i>уравнове́сить</i> (uravnovésitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figurative</i>) to equalize (weight, size, rights, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>равня́ть</i> (ravnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurricane</li><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) hurricane-force wind; a windstorm with wind speeds over 32 m/s</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ouragan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hurricane</li></ol><p><i>урага́н</i> (uragán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, to understand, to get the hang of</li></ol><p><i>уразуме́ть</i> (urazumétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comprehend, to understand, to get the hang of</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>разуме́ть</i> (razumétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Urals</li></ol><p><i>Ура́л</i> (Urál) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uranium (U)</li></ol><p>Named after the planet Uranus (<i>Ура́н</i> (Urán)).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Уран)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Uranus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Uranus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uranist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) uranium</li></ol><p><i>ура́н</i> (urán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) uracil</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urbanization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urbanist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urbanonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>food</i>) urbech</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kumyk <i>гьюрбеч</i> (hürbeç).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grab, to snatch</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to procure, to get</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to find (time)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>рвать</i> (rvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Urdu (language)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Urdu اردو (urdū).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement (the state of being settled)</li></ol><p><i>урегули́ровать</i> (uregulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, to adjust</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>регули́ровать</i> (regulírovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting (e.g. of funds)</li><li>retrenchment</li><li>abbreviation (act or result of shortening or reducing)</li></ol><p><i>уреза́ть</i> (urezátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li><li>to cut down, to reduce, to curtail, to skimp</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ре́зать</i> (rézatʹ)</p><p><i>уре́зать</i> (urézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince, to persuade or to curb (to restrain/control), to make calm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to convince, to persuade or to curb (to restrain/control), to make calm</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>резо́н</i> (rezón) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off</li><li>to cut down, to reduce, to curtail, to skimp</li></ol><p><i>уре́зать</i> (urézatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ureilite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) small woods and shrubs growing in the low-lying river valleys</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) urethra</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>οὐρήθρα</i> (ourḗthra, “the passage for urine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) professional criminal, career criminal, gangster, thug, thief, crook</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) career convict, inmate</li></ol><p><i>ур(ва́ть)</i> (ur(vátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urn</li><li>ballot box</li><li>refuse bin, litter receptacle, litter bin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>urna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>level, standard, amount</li><li>level, gauge</li><li>level, rate, degree</li><li>(<i>tool</i>) level, level gauge, spirit level, bubble level</li><li>(<i>video games</i>) a level</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dignity</li></ol><p>A deverbal of <i>уровня́ть</i> (urovnjátʹ, “straighten out”), a verb based on <i>ро́вный</i> (róvnyj, “flat, level”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth, to make smooth</li><li>to level, to make even or level</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ровня́ть</i> (rovnjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster</li><li>ugly creature</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>уродъ</i> (urodŭ, “feebleminded, witless, foolish”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫrodъ</i>, ancient deverbative of <i>&#42;roditi</i> with nominal negative prefix <i>&#42;ǫ-</i>, zero grade of <i>&#42;ne</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>уро́д</i> (uród, “freak, ugly person”)</li></ol><p><i>уро́д</i> (uród) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bag, fright, freak (ugly woman)</li></ol><p><i>урод</i> (urod) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deformed, misshapen</li><li>ugly, hideous (displeasing to the eye; not aesthetically pleasing)</li></ol><p><i>уро́д</i> (uród) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disfigure, to deform, to mar</li><li>to mutilate, to maim, to cripple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deformity, abnormality</li><li>handicap</li><li>ugliness</li></ol><p><i>уро́д</i> (uród) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crop, harvest, yield (yield of harvesting)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uroďajь</i>, whose middle part (<i>&#42;rodъ</i>) yielded <i>род</i> (rod). Cognates include Polish <i>urodzaj</i>, Ukrainian <i>врожа́й</i> (vrožáj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>productivity, crop capacity</li></ol><p><i>урожа́йный</i> (urožájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) harvest</li><li>rich, bountiful (<i>in terms of harvest; of land or time</i>)</li><li>high-yield (<i>of plants</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chock-full, plentiful, abundant [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘of/with’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>урожа́й</i> (urožáj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>née (<i>in Russia before 1917</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) born (<i>leader, poet, etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native</li></ol><p><i>уроди́ться</i> (urodítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-е́нец</i> (-énec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesson</li><li>task</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>урокъ</i> (urokŭ, “agreement, condition”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;urokъ</i>, Proto-Slavic root <i>&#42;rok-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>losses, damage</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>урони́ть</i> (uronítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop (something), usually unintentionally</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-рони́ть</i> (-ronítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to establish the boundaries of</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to hex, to bewitch, to cast the evil eye on</li></ol><p>From <i>уро́к</i> (urók) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Compare Old East Slavic <i>уро́къ</i> (urókŭ, “condition, completion, transaction, rule, payment”), <i>уро́чный</i> (uróčnyj, “established, agreed”), <i>уро́к</i> (urók, “sickness caused by the evil eye, hex (<i>archaic or dialectal</i>)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) natural boundary, natural border (<i>e.g. a mountain chain or river</i>)</li><li>(<i>geography</i>) natural landmark (<i>e.g. a copse of trees in an open plain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>уро́чить</i> (uróčitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uruguayan (person from Uruguay)</li></ol><p>From <i>Уругва́й</i> (Urugváj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Compare Ukrainian <i>уругва́єць</i> (uruhvájecʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Uruguayan</li></ol><p><i>Уругва́й</i> (Urugváj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rumbling</li><li>grumble (the sound made by a hungry stomach)</li></ol><p><i>урча́ть</i> (určátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grumble (to make a low growling or rumbling stomach noise)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grab, to snatch</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to procure, to get</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to find (time)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-рыва́ть</i> (-ryvátʹ, “to tear”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) brokenly, in snatches, intermittently, in fits and starts</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whole dried apricots (with the pit)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a Central Asian person</li></ol><p>From a Central Asian Turkic source. Compare Kazakh <i>өрік</i> (örık), Kyrgyz <i>өрүк</i> (örük), Uzbek <i>oʻrik</i> (“apricot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) common practices, custom way of something</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) process of serving temporary military or official assignment; brevet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;urędъ</i>. Compare with Czech <i>úřad</i>, Slovak <i>úrad</i>, Polish <i>urząd</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>úred</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) village constable</li></ol><p>From <i>уря́д</i> (urjád) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) small woods and shrubs growing in the low-lying river valleys</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) moustache</li><li>whisker</li><li>feeler, antenna</li><li>tendril, awn</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>усъ</i> (usŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫsъ</i> &lt; <i>&#42;vǫsъ</i> &lt; <i>&#42;vǫdsъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wónsa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wondʰsom</i>, s-expansion of root <i>&#42;wendʰ-</i> (“hair, wool, beard”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or let someone sit down, to seat</li><li>to ask someone to sit down</li><li>to set (someone to do something)</li><li>to plant (e.g. trees or flowers)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сади́ть</i> (sadítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmstead</li><li>homestead</li><li>place</li><li>barton</li><li>steading</li><li>hall (a manor house)</li><li>messuage</li><li>toft</li></ol><p><i>усади́ть</i> (usadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ба</i> (-ba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or let someone sit down, to seat</li><li>to ask someone to sit down</li><li>to set (someone to do something)</li><li>to plant (e.g. trees or flowers)</li></ol><p><i>усади́ть</i> (usadítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down (comfortably), to take a seat</li></ol><p><i>уса́живать</i> (usáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja); pairing with <i>усе́сться</i>&nbsp;pf (uséstʹsja) corresponds to pairing between <i>сади́ться</i>&nbsp;impf (sadítʹsja) and <i>сесть</i>&nbsp;pf (sestʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moustached (having moustache)</li><li>having whiskers</li><li>having feelers (antennae)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫsatъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ус</i> (us) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A man with a mustache, especially a large one.</li><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) barb (fish)</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) longhorn beetle</li></ol><p>From <i>ус</i> (us, “moustache”) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to master, to learn</li><li>to adopt (a habit, a custom)</li><li>to digest, to assimilate</li></ol><p><i>усво́ить</i> (usvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assimilation (the metabolic conversion of nutriments)</li><li>digestion</li><li>adoption</li></ol><p><i>усво́ить</i> (usvóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to master, to learn</li><li>to adopt (a habit or custom)</li><li>to digest, to assimilate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>свой</i> (svoj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assimilability</li></ol><p><i>усвоя́емый</i> (usvojájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dot <i>(with)</i>, to strew <i>(with)</i>, to litter <i>(with)</i>, to pepper <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>усе́ять</i> (uséjatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to truncate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to understand</li></ol><p><i>усе́к-</i> (usék-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagerness, zeal</li><li>diligence, assiduity</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оусрьдьѥ</i> (usrĭdĭje). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>diligently, industriously (in a diligent manner)</li></ol><p><i>усе́рдный</i> (usérdnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager, zealous</li><li>assiduous</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оусрьдьнъ</i> (usrĭdĭnŭ). By surface analysis, <i>усе́рдие</i> (usérdije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) zeal, diligence, effort, fervour</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>се́рдце</i> (sérdce) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be diligent [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘in something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be excessively zealous or eager</li></ol><p><i>усе́рдство</i> (usérdstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down (comfortably), to take a seat</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сесть</i> (sestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, to truncate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get, to understand</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сечь</i> (sečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) truncated</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) incomplete, with missing parts (<i>of any piece of information</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dot <i>(with)</i>, to strew <i>(with)</i>, to litter <i>(with)</i>, to pepper <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>се́ять</i> (séjatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to remain sitting (<i>after being pushed</i>)</li><li>(<i>perfective only</i>) to sit still (<i>despite one's desires or intentions</i>)</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;colloquial) to hold on (<i>to a position or job</i>), to remain (<i>in a position or job</i>)</li><li>(perfective only,&#32;chiefly in the negative) to stay in place, to refrain from leaving</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to sit at the table and eat or drink (something) (<i>fully or in large quantities</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сиде́ть</i> (sidétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) able to sit (in one place) for a very long time</li><li>hard-working, diligent</li></ol><p><i>усиде́ть</i> (usidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) to sit at the table and eat or drink (something) (<i>fully or in large quantities</i>)</li></ol><p><i>усиде́ть</i> (usidétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>ус</i> (us): little/short moustache or whisker</li><li>barbel, cirrus (<i>of fish</i>)</li><li>feeler, antenna (feeler organ on the head of an insect)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) tendril, awn, runner</li></ol><p><i>ус</i> (us) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strengthening; intensification</li><li>amplification</li></ol><p><i>уси́лить</i> (usílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with increased strength or force</li></ol><p><i>уси́ленный</i> (usílennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intensified, stronger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, to reinforce, to amplify</li></ol><p><i>уси́лить</i> (usílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stronger, to intensify, to gain strength</li><li>to swell, to grow louder</li><li>to gather momentum</li><li>to deepen</li></ol><p><i>уси́ливать</i> (usílivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effort, exertion, endeavour</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>си́ла</i> (síla) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Akin to Bulgarian <i>усилие</i> (usilie), Czech <i>úsilí</i>, Polish <i>wysiłek</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>effort</i> (&lt; Latin <i>ex-</i> + <i>fortis</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amplifier</li><li>booster</li><li>enhancer</li><li>magnifier</li></ol><p><i>уси́лить</i> (usílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen, to reinforce, to amplify</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-си́лить</i> (-sílitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become stronger, to intensify, to gain strength</li><li>to swell, to grow louder</li><li>to gather momentum</li><li>to deepen</li></ol><p><i>уси́лить</i> (usílitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip off, to slip away</li><li>to steal <i>(away)</i>, to sneak off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to escape</li></ol><p><i>ускользну́ть</i> (uskolʹznútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip off, to slip away</li><li>to steal <i>(away)</i>, to sneak off</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to escape</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>скользну́ть</i> (skolʹznútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acceleration (act, state)</li></ol><p><i>уско́рить</i> (uskóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speeded up, faster, (more) rapid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accelerator (a device for causing acceleration, anything or anyone that accelerates but not the accelerator pedal)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) accelerant</li></ol><p><i>уско́рить</i> (uskóritʹ, “to accelerate”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hasten, to quicken, to accelerate</li><li>to expedite, to speed up</li><li>to precipitate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken, to accelerate</li><li>to move faster, to go faster</li></ol><p><i>уско́рить</i> (uskóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hasten, to quicken, to accelerate</li><li>to expedite, to speed up</li><li>to precipitate</li></ol><p><i>уско́рить</i> (uskóritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quicken, to accelerate</li><li>to move faster, to go faster</li></ol><p><i>ускоря́ть</i> (uskorjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange <i>(with, with + to)</i>, to agree <i>(with on, with + to )</i>, to settle <i>(with, with + to)</i></li></ol><p><i>усло́виться</i> (uslóvitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) enjoyment, pleasure, joy, delight</li><li>a delight, a joy, a pleasure (something that provides these feelings)</li></ol><p><i>услади́ть</i> (usladítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а</i> (-a)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight, to please, to satisfy (especially the senses)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>слади́ть</i> (sladítʹ, “to sweeten”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight, to please, to satisfy (especially the senses)</li></ol><p><i>услади́ть</i> (usladítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep sight of, to watch</li><li>to keep one's focus on, to keep track of, to follow [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to look out for, to keep an eye on, to keep from harm [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>следи́ть</i> (sledítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep sight of, to watch</li><li>to keep one's focus on, to keep track of, to follow [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to look out for, to keep an eye on, to keep from harm [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уследи́ть</i> (usledítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condition</li><li>term, stipulation, proviso</li><li>agreement, contract</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uslovьje</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange <i>(with, with + to)</i>, to agree <i>(with on, with + to )</i>, to settle <i>(with, with + to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prearranged (<i>signal, meeting place, etc.</i>)</li><li>agreed-upon (<i>amount, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>усло́вие</i> (uslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>усла́вливаться</i> (uslávlivatʹsja)</li></ol><p><i>усло́виться</i> (uslóvitʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tentatively, conditionally</li></ol><p><i>усло́вный</i> (uslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conditional character, conditionality</li><li>convention, conventionality</li></ol><p><i>усло́вный</i> (uslóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conditional (<i>depending upon a condition</i>)</li><li>conventional (<i>by convention</i>)</li><li>prearranged (<i>secretly agreed upon beforehand</i>)</li><li>make-believe (<i>pretend, imaginary, symbolic, notional, nominal</i>)</li><li>reference (<i>of a standard unit used as a basis for calculation</i>)</li></ol><p><i>усло́вие</i> (uslóvije) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>complication, complicating</li></ol><p><i>усложни́ть</i> (usložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complicate</li><li>to perplex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more complex, to become more complicated</li><li>to become more difficult (<i>e.g. to implement or maintain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>усложни́ть</i> (usložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complicate</li><li>to perplex</li></ol><p><i>усложни́ть</i> (usložnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more complex, to become more complicated</li><li>to become more difficult (<i>e.g. to implement or maintain</i>)</li></ol><p><i>усложня́ть</i> (usložnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, favour/favor, good turn</li><li><i>plural</i> services, attendance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;low colloquial</i>) service (<i>as a servant or maid</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) servility, ministering (<i>to someone</i>), pleasing (<i>someone</i>)</li></ol><p><i>услужи́ть</i> (uslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do/render a service, to do a good turn</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to oblige</li></ol><p><i>услужи́ть</i> (uslužítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do/render a service, to do a good turn</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) to oblige</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>служи́ть</i> (služítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>helpfully, obligingly</li></ol><p><i>услу́жливый</i> (uslúžlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obligingness</li></ol><p><i>услу́жливый</i> (uslúžlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>helpful, obliging</li></ol><p><i>услуж(и́ть)</i> (usluž(ítʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to manage to hear</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>слыха́ть</i> (slyxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) to smell, to feel</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>слы́шать</i> (slýšatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see <i>(in)</i>, to perceive <i>(in)</i>, to discover <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look <i>(after)</i>, to attend <i>(to)</i>, to keep an eye <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>усмотре́ть</i> (usmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grin, to smile, to smirk</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>смех</i> (smex) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grin, to smile, to smirk</li></ol><p><i>усмеха́ться</i> (usmexátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironic smile</li><li>wry grin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pacify</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>смири́ть</i> (smirítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discretion, judgement</li></ol><p><i>усмотре́ть</i> (usmotrétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see <i>(in)</i>, to perceive <i>(in)</i>, to discover <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to look <i>(after)</i>, to attend <i>(to)</i>, to keep an eye <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dormant volcano</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall asleep, to go to sleep</li><li>(<i>of fish</i>) to die</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;usъnǫti</i> (“to fall asleep”), from <i>&#42;u-</i> (“away”) + <i>&#42;sъpati</i> (“to sleep”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) intestine war; feud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>improvement, betterment; perfection (action)</li></ol><p><i>усоверше́нствовать</i> (usoveršénstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>improved, more advanced</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enhance, to upgrade, to improve, to refine</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>соверше́нствовать</i> (soveršénstvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enhance oneself, to upgrade oneself, to improve oneself, to refine oneself</li></ol><p><i>усоверше́нствовать</i> (usoveršénstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to doubt, to feel doubt about [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сомн-</i> (somn-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to die</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) to fall asleep</li></ol><p>Formed after the past stem of Old East Slavic <i>усънути</i> (usŭnuti) and Old Church Slavonic <i>усънѫти</i> (usŭnǫti), <i>усъп</i>-, further derived from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;usъnǫti</i> (“to fall asleep”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) deceased</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceased person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrivel, to wither, becoming hard</li><li>to dry up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrink (<i>as a result of drying</i>)</li><li>to shrivel up (<i>from old age</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>со́хнуть</i> (sóxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progress (<i>in studies</i>)</li></ol><p>Constructed as if from <i>&#42;успева́емый</i> (&#42;uspevájemyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ), from <i>успева́ть</i> (uspevátʹ, “to advance in one's studies”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have time, to find time, to manage, to succeed</li><li>to arrive in time (for), to be in time (for)</li><li>to catch (a train, bus)</li><li>to get on well, to make progress, to advance (<i>in one's studies</i>)</li><li>to keep pace <i>with someone</i></li></ol><p><i>успе́ть</i> (uspétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(in Eastern Orthodoxy)</i> Dormition, Assumption</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>усъпение</i> (usŭpenije, “falling asleep; a sleep; death; religious feast day”). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>κοίμησις (τῆς Θεοτόκου)</i> (koímēsis (tês Theotókou), “Dormition (of the Mother of God)”). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>спать</i> (spatʹ, “sleep”) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije, “-tion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Assumption, Dormition (Christian festival in honor of the ascent of the Virgin Mary to heaven)</li></ol><p><i>успе́ние</i> (uspénije) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have time, to find time</li><li>to manage (to do something), to succeed</li><li>to make it, to arrive in time (for), to be in time (for)</li><li>to catch (a train, bus)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>спеть</i> (spetʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;spěti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spewd-</i> (“press, hurry”), whence English <i>speed</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success, progress</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uspěxъ</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>успіх</i> (uspix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>successfully</li></ol><p><i>успе́шный</i> (uspéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>success, successfulness</li></ol><p><i>успе́шный</i> (uspéšnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>successful (resulting in success)</li></ol><p><i>успе́х</i> (uspéx) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calm, to quiet, to soothe</li><li>to reassure</li><li>to assuage, to appease</li></ol><p><i>успоко́ить</i> (uspokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calm down, to quiet down</li><li>to abate</li></ol><p><i>успока́ивать</i> (uspokáivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calming</li><li>soothing, assuasive</li><li>sedative, tranquilizing, tranquillizing</li><li>restful</li><li>anodyne (soothing or relaxing)</li><li>lulling</li><li>bland</li><li>composing</li><li>lenitive</li><li>balmy</li><li>depressant</li><li>demulcent</li><li>mollifying</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfort, solace</li><li>pacification, mollification</li><li>appeasement, assuagement, propitiation</li><li>sedation, tranquillization, tranquilization</li><li>subsidence</li><li>anodyne (a source of relaxation or comfort)</li></ol><p><i>успоко́ить</i> (uspokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soothing, calming, sedative</li></ol><p><i>успоко́ить</i> (uspokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calm, to quiet, to soothe</li><li>to reassure</li><li>to assuage, to appease</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>споко́й(ный)</i> (spokój(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to calm down, to quiet down</li><li>to abate</li></ol><p><i>успоко́ить</i> (uspokóitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>успе́шность</i> (uspéšnostʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to cover in shit</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to spend, to waste</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to shit one's pants, to shit oneself</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to get frightened, to chicken out</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to make fun out of something, consider something extremely funny, profusely laugh about it, laugh one's ass off over something</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to show extreme effort in doing or trying to do something</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>срать</i> (sratʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to average</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make average, to bring to an average level (<i>e.g. one's abilities</i>), to bring to common standards or norms</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сре́дний</i> (srédnij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to average</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make average, to bring to an average level (<i>e.g. one's abilities</i>), to bring to common standards or norms</li></ol><p><i>усредни́ть</i> (usrednítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to piss one's pants, to piss oneself</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to become afraid, to chicken out</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ссать</i> (ssatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) abbreviation of <i>устаре́вший</i> (ustarévšij, “obsolete, antiquated”)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>устаре́вший</i> (ustarévšij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) mouth, lips</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;usta</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃éh₁os</i> (“mouth”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>आस्</i> (ās, “mouth”) and Latin <i>ōs</i> (“mouth”). Compare Polish <i>usta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regulations, statute, charter, manual</li><li>ustav</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired</li></ol><p><i>уста́ть</i> (ustátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li><li>to place, to put</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ста́вить</i> (stávitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to have room, to find / to have a place</li><li>to stare, to fix one's eyes on, to gaze</li></ol><p><i>уста́вить</i> (ustávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set <i>(with)</i>, to cover <i>(with)</i></li><li>to place, to put</li></ol><p><i>уста́вить</i> (ustávitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, to have room, to find / to have a place</li><li>(+ на + acc) to stare at, to fix one's eyes on, to gaze</li></ol><p><i>уставля́ть</i> (ustavljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regulatory, statutory</li><li>authorized</li></ol><p>From <i>уста́в</i> (ustáv) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>уставно́й</i> (ustavnój)</li></ol><p>From <i>уста́в</i> (ustáv) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to resist, to withstand, to stand up <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>устоя́ть</i> (ustojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wearily, tiredly (in a weary manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weariness, tiredness, fatigue, languor, lassitude</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) fatigue (<i>of repeatedly stressed material</i>)</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) exhaustion (<i>of soil</i>)</li></ol><p><i>уста́лый</i> (ustályj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weary, tired, fatigued</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;archaic) fatigue</li></ol><p><i>уста́ть</i> (ustátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ль</i> (-lʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to install, to place, to mount</li><li>to establish</li><li>to determine, to fix, to ascertain</li></ol><p><i>установи́ть</i> (ustanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be settled, to be formed, to set in</li></ol><p><i>устана́вливать</i> (ustanávlivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to install, to place, to mount</li><li>to establish, to set</li><li>to determine, to fix, to ascertain</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-станови́ть</i> (-stanovítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be settled, to be formed, to set in</li></ol><p><i>установи́ть</i> (ustanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement</li><li>mounting, installation, mount</li><li>setting</li><li>plant, installation, device or apparatus</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) orientation, aim, purpose</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) directions</li></ol><p><i>установи́ть</i> (ustanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>establishment</li><li>ascertainment</li></ol><p><i>установи́ть</i> (ustanovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>statutory</li><li>fixed, set</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>устаре́вшее</i> (ustarévšeje, “obsolete, antiquated”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become obsolete, to become dated, to become out-of-date</li></ol><p><i>устаре́ть</i> (ustarétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ва́ть</i> (-vátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obsolete, antiquated, outdated</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antiquated, archaic</li><li>obsolete</li><li>outdated, superannuated</li><li>out-of-date</li><li>musty</li><li>passe</li><li>old-fashioned</li><li>old-hat</li><li>outworn</li><li>behind the times</li><li>geriatric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become obsolete, to become dated, to become out-of-date</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>старе́ть</i> (starétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стать</i> (statʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>устла́ть</i> (ustlátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стели́ть</i> (stelítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover the whole surface of; to carpet, to pave, etc.</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-стила́ть</i> (-stilátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor cover made of straw in a cowshed, etc.</li></ol><p><i>устила́ть</i> (ustilátʹ, “to cover the surface of, to carpet”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover the whole surface of; to carpet, to pave, etc.</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стлать</i> (stlatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>orally (spoken as opposed to written)</li></ol><p><i>у́стный</i> (ústnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oral, verbal</li><li>spoken (<i>as opposite to written</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ustьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>уста́</i> (ustá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abutment, pier</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cream</li><li>principle, foundation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steadily, stably</li><li>steadfastly</li></ol><p><i>усто́йчивый</i> (ustójčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steadiness, firmness, stability</li></ol><p><i>усто́йчивый</i> (ustójčivyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steady, stable</li><li>steadfast, resistant to external influence</li><li>hardy, resistant (<i>of plants or animals</i>)</li><li>sustainable</li></ol><p><i>устоя́ть</i> (ustojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to mature (of a man)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>усторобити ся</i> (ustorobiti sja), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;storbiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep one's balance, to remain on one's feet, to stand one's ground, to stand fast</li><li>to resist, to withstand, to stand up <i>(against)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стоя́ть</i> (stojátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, <i>(milk)</i> to cream</li><li>to become well-established, to take <i>(final)</i> shape</li></ol><p><i>устоя́ть</i> (ustojátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange, to organize, to establish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make</li><li>to settle, to put in order</li><li>to place, to fix up, to set up</li><li>to suit, to satisfy, to be convenient (<i>for</i>)</li></ol><p><i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle down, to get settled</li><li>to turn out right, to get fine, to be OK</li><li>to get a job somewhere</li></ol><p><i>устра́ивать</i> (ustráivatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elimination, removal, disposal</li><li>removal (from power or a position)</li></ol><p><i>устрани́ть</i> (ustranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eliminate</li><li>to remove</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оустранити</i> (ustraniti). By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to be eliminated</li><li>to stop participating, to pull away [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>устрани́ть</i> (ustranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eliminate</li><li>to remove</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оустранꙗти</i> (ustranjati). By surface analysis, <i>устрани́ть</i> (ustranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disappear, to be eliminated</li><li>to stop participating, to pull away [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘from something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>устраня́ть</i> (ustranjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to scare, to intimidate</li></ol><p><i>устраши́ть</i> (ustrašítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awesome (causing awe or terror)</li><li>deterrent</li><li>horrendous</li><li>redoubtable, redoubted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intimidation</li><li>frightening</li><li>deterrence</li></ol><p><i>устраши́ть</i> (ustrašítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten, to scare, to intimidate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>страши́ть</i> (strašítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush</li><li>to turn <i>(to)</i>, to direct <i>(at, towards)</i>, to fix <i>(on, upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стреми́ть</i> (stremítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush</li><li>to be turned <i>(to)</i>, to be directed <i>(at, towards)</i>, to be fixed <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>устреми́ть</i> (ustremítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspiration; rush</li></ol><p><i>устреми́ть</i> (ustremítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush</li><li>to turn <i>(to)</i>, to direct <i>(at, towards)</i>, to fix <i>(on, upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>устреми́ть</i> (ustremítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush</li><li>to be turned <i>(to)</i>, to be directed <i>(at, towards)</i>, to be fixed <i>(upon)</i></li></ol><p><i>устремля́ть</i> (ustremljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspiration</li></ol><p><i>устремлённый</i> (ustremljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oyster (mollusk)</li></ol><p>Derived from Dutch <i>oester</i> + <i>-ица</i> (-ica). First recorded in 1724. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>у́стриця</i> (ústrycja) and Belarusian <i>ву́стрыца</i> (vústryca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) oyster</li></ol><p><i>у́стрица</i> (ústrica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) arranging, putting in order</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) arrangement, order</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) creation, organization</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) placing (a person somewhere)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) structure, device</li></ol><p><i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>organized, well-adjusted, orderly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>organizer</li></ol><p><i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange, to organize, to establish</li><li>to settle, to set up, to put in order</li><li>to suit, to satisfy</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>строить</i> (stroitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle down, to get settled</li><li>to turn out right, to get fine, to be OK</li><li>to get a job somewhere</li></ol><p><i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement</li><li>establishment</li><li>equipment</li><li>installation</li><li>organization</li><li>system</li><li>mechanism, device</li></ol><p>From <i>устро́ить</i> (ustróitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(in a wall or a rock)</i> ledge, projection, bench, terrace</li><li>(<i>military</i>) echelon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cede, to let have [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to yield [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to be inferior to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to reduce something as part of agreement</li><li>to discount, to abate, to reduce price</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ступа́ть</i> (stupátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concessive</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) expressing contrasting clauses in a sentence</li></ol><p><i>уступи́ть</i> (ustupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cede, to let have [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to yield [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to be inferior to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li><li>to sell</li><li>to discount, to abate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ступи́ть</i> (stupítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concession</li><li>discount</li></ol><p><i>уступи́ть</i> (ustupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amenable, conciliatory, compromising</li></ol><p><i>уступи́ть</i> (ustupítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-чивый</i> (-čivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>стыди́ть</i> (stydítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of someone/something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>устыди́ть</i> (ustydítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shame</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth, outflow, discharge, issue</li><li>mouth, estuary (of a river)</li><li>orifice, opening, aperture</li><li>mouthpiece</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ustьje</i>, from <i>&#42;usta</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьje</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stoma</li></ol><p><i>у́стье</i> (ústʹje) +&lrm; <i>-ице</i> (-ice)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stomatal</li></ol><p><i>у́стьиц</i> (ústʹic) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Veliky Ustyug</li></ol><p><i>У́стю́г</i> (Ústjúg) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redouble, to intensify, to increase, to aggravate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сугу́бый</i> (sugúbyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to redouble, to intensify, to increase, to aggravate</li></ol><p><i>усугуби́ть</i> (usugubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worsen (of a problem, unfavorable state of anything, crisis)</li></ol><p><i>усугубля́ть</i> (usugubljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to doubt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moustache</li><li>whiskers (<i>of animals</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to affiliate (to take by choice into relationship as child)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adoption (act of adopting, or state of being adopted)</li></ol><p><i>усынови́ть</i> (usynovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adopt, to affiliate (to take by choice into relationship as child)</li></ol><p><i>усынови́ть</i> (usynovítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burial vault or crypt, typically for members of a single family</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew <i>(with)</i>, to bestrew <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>сы́пать</i> (sýpatʹ)</p><p><i>усы́пать</i> (usýpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/lull to sleep</li><li>to allay (suspicions)</li><li>to euthanize (an animal)</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-сыпи́ть</i> (-sypítʹ), causative of <i>спать</i> (spatʹ), from Old East Slavic <i>съпати</i> (sŭpati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъpati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put/lull to sleep</li><li>to allay (suspicions)</li><li>to euthanize (an animal)</li></ol><p><i>усыпи́ть</i> (usypítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrivel, to wither, becoming hard</li><li>to dry up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shrink (<i>as a result of drying</i>)</li><li>to shrivel up (<i>from old age</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-сыха́ть</i> (-syxátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal, to keep to oneself, to keep a secret</li><li>to steal, to appropriate</li></ol><p><i>утаи́ть</i> (utaítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal, to keep to oneself, to keep a secret</li><li>to steal, to appropriate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>таи́ть</i> (taítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment</li></ol><p><i>утаи́ть</i> (utaítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to melt (milk) in light heat</li></ol><p><i>утопи́ть</i> (utopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag away, to carry off, to make away <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lead away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make off <i>(with)</i>, to steal</li></ol><p><i>утаск-</i> (utask-) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag away, to carry off, to make away <i>(with)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lead away</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make off <i>(with)</i>, to steal</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тащи́ть</i> (taščítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) utensils</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>affirmatively, positively, in the affirmative</li></ol><p><i>утверди́тельный</i> (utverdítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affirmative (relating to truth)</li></ol><p><i>утверди́ть</i> (utverdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assert, to argue, to allege, to state</li><li>to claim</li><li>to approve</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тверди́ть</i> (tverdítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alleged, asserted</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assert, to argue, to allege, to state</li><li>to claim</li><li>to approve</li></ol><p><i>утверди́ть</i> (utverdítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strengthening, consolidation</li><li>approval, confirmation, ratification</li><li>assertion, affirmation, statement (declaration or remark), claim</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leak, to flow away, to escape (<i>of liquid or gas</i>)</li><li>to fly by, to pass quickly (<i>of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>утек-</i> (utek-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insulate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wrap in warm clothes</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тепли́ть</i> (teplítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insulate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wrap in warm clothes</li></ol><p><i>утепли́ть</i> (uteplítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe, to dry</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тере́ть</i> (terétʹ). Compare Czech <i>utřít</i>, Kashubian <i>ùtrzéc</i>, Silesian <i>utrzić</i>, and Polish <i>utrzeć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold oneself back, to restrain oneself</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>терпе́ть</i> (terpétʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose, to forfeit</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>теря́ть</i> (terjátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fun, amusement</li><li>joy, delight</li><li>comfort, consolation</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;utěxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leakage</li></ol><p><i>утек-</i> (utek-) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain drain</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>brain drain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leak, to flow away, to escape (<i>of liquid or gas</i>)</li><li>to fly by, to pass quickly (<i>of time</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>течь</i> (tečʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to console, to comfort</li></ol><p><i>уте́шить</i> (utéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop crying, to calm down</li><li>to comfort oneself, to take comfort [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with/in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>утеша́ть</i> (utešátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consolation, comfort, solace</li></ol><p><i>уте́шить</i> (utéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>higher register</i>) consoler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consolatory, consoling, comforting</li></ol><p><i>уте́шить</i> (utéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to console, to comfort</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>те́шить</i> (téšitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stop crying, to calm down</li><li>to comfort oneself, to take comfort [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘with/in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>уте́шить</i> (utéšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>…</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utilization</li><li>recycling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) utilitarian</li><li>useful, utilitarian</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>utilitaire</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) scrap, recycling (discarded objects which can be recycled)</li><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial,&#32;by extension) scrap, junk (old, unwanted things in general)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scrap processor, recycling plant, recycling facility</li></ol><p>Attested 1920s; probably a back-formation from <i>утѝльсырьё</i> (utìlʹsyrʹjó), abbreviated from <i>утилизи́рованное сырьё</i> (utilizírovannoje syrʹjó) and <i>утилиза́ция сырья́</i> (utilizácija syrʹjá), with the form influenced by French <i>utile</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) scrap materials, recyclable materials</li></ol><p>Attested 1920s, abbreviation of <i>утилизи́рованное сырьё</i> (utilizírovannoje syrʹjó, “recycled materials”) and <i>утилиза́ция сырья́</i> (utilizácija syrʹjá, “material recycling”).</p><p>Per Chernykh, <i>ути́ль</i> (utílʹ) is probably a back-formation from <i>утильсырьё</i> (utilʹsyrʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) duck</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) duck-like</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>у́т(ка)</i> (út(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj); compare Old East Slavic <i>уты</i> (uty), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtinъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe, to dry</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-тира́ть</i> (-tirátʹ). Compare Czech <i>utírat</i>, Kashubian <i>ùcerac</i>, Silesian <i>uciyrać</i>, and Polish <i>ucierać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet down, to calm down</li></ol><p>From <i>ути́хнуть</i> (utíxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quiet down, to calm down</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ти́хнуть</i> (tíxnutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make calm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make calm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duck (<i>also as a food</i>)</li><li>canard</li><li>bed urinal</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) cleat</li><li>(ironic,&#32;figuratively) clumsy woman</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;journalism) deliberately implausible news or sensations; tabloidism</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙋтъка</i> (utŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtъka</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;ǫty</i>. By surface analysis, <i>уть</i> (utʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate to Lithuanian <i>antis</i>, Latin <i>anas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bury, to hide</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ткнуть</i> (tknutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bury oneself <i>(in)</i>, to hide <i>(one's face)</i> <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to rest <i>(upon, against)</i>, to be stopped <i>(by)</i>, to hit</li></ol><p><i>уткну́ть</i> (utknútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>platypus (<i>Ornithorhynchus anatinus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у́тка</i> (útka) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нос</i> (nos)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragile</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>утьлъ</i> (utĭlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtьlъ</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weft, woof</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>утъкъ</i> (utŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;ǫ-</i> (Russian <i>у-</i> (u-)) + <i>&#42;tъkъ</i>, cognate with or deverbal from <i>&#42;tъkati</i> (Russian <i>ткать</i> (tkatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quench, to slake <i>(thirst)</i>; to satisfy, to appease <i>(hunger)</i>; to lull, to soothe, to alleviate <i>(pain)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-толи́ть</i> (-tolítʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;toliti</i> (“to calm, to soothe”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to make thicker</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>то́лстый</i> (tólstyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thicken, to become thicker</li></ol><p><i>утолсти́ть</i> (utolstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, to make thicker</li></ol><p><i>утолсти́ть</i> (utolstítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to thicken (intransitive), to get thicker, to swell up</li></ol><p><i>утолща́ть</i> (utolščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quench, to slake <i>(thirst)</i>; to satisfy, to appease <i>(hunger)</i>; to lull, to soothe, to alleviate <i>(pain)</i></li></ol><p><i>утоли́ть</i> (utolítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wearisome, tedious, tiresome</li></ol><p><i>утоми́ть</i> (utomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fatigue, to weary, to tire</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>томи́ть</i> (tomítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired</li></ol><p><i>утоми́ть</i> (utomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatigue</li></ol><p><i>утоми́ть</i> (utomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fatigue, to weary, to tire</li></ol><p><i>утоми́ть</i> (utomítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired</li></ol><p><i>утомля́ть</i> (utomljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tired (<i>experiencing fatigue; of a person, nerves, etc.</i>)</li><li>tired (<i>expressing fatigue; of a look, face, tone, etc.; inanimate</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sink, to go down, to drown</li><li>to be lost</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тону́ть</i> (tonútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (more) subtle</li><li>to refine, to make (more) refined</li><li>to make (more) sophisticated</li></ol><p><i>утончи́ть</i> (utončítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become/get thin</li><li>to become refined</li></ol><p><i>утонча́ть</i> (utončátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (more) subtle</li><li>to refine, to make (more) refined</li><li>to make (more) sophisticated</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>то́нкий</i> (tónkij) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>refined, subtle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;figuratively</i>) to drown (in); to wallow in, to roll in</li><li>to trample by stamping the foot</li></ol><p><i>утоп-</i> (utop-) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>то́пать</i> (tópatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person drowning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utopianist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sink [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘into something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drown</li><li>(figurative,&#32;transitive) to hide, to drown [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>в</b> (v, + accusative) <small>‘in something else’</small>]</li><li>(figurative,&#32;transitive) to drown (one's sorrows, etc.), to stifle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to defame, to destroy, to ruin (someone)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) to sink (a nail) so it is flush with the surface</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>топи́ть</i> (topítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drown oneself</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to be melted (<i>of milk, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>утопи́ть</i> (utopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>utopian</li></ol><p><i>уто́пия</i> (utópija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utopian socialism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>utopia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drowning (action, result, murder method, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>утопи́ть</i> (utopítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drowned man</li></ol><p>From <i>утопле́ние</i> (utoplénije) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flush intake (jet fighters, rockets, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́тка</i> (útka): duck, little duck</li></ol><p><i>у́тка</i> (útka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>more precise / accurate definition; clarification, specification, elaboration, qualification</li></ol><p><i>уточни́ть</i> (utočnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to specify, to make more exact/precise</li><li>to inquire, to clarify by inquiring, to straighten out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more accurate</li></ol><p><i>уточни́ть</i> (utočnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to specify, to make more exact/precise</li><li>to inquire, to clarify by inquiring</li></ol><p><i>уточни́ть</i> (utočnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more accurate</li></ol><p><i>уточня́ть</i> (utočnjátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a.m.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ram, to tamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ram, to tamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss (something lost)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>тра́тить</i> (trátitʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose, to forfeit</li></ol><p><i>утра́тить</i> (utrátitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>morning-, of the morning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;utrьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у́тро</i> (útro, “morning”) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matinee</li><li>predawn frost</li></ol><p><i>у́трен(ний)</i> (útren(nij)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) matins</li></ol><p>From <i>у́тро</i> (útro) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja), with irregular vowel epenthesis after the stem instead of between the last two consonants (compare <i>у́тренний</i> (útrennij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>у́тро</i> (útro, “morning”)</li></ol><p><i>у́тро</i> (útro) +&lrm; <i>-ечко</i> (-ečko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exaggeration, overstatement, hyperbole, distortion</li></ol><p>From <i>утри́ровать</i> (utrírovatʹ) (to exaggerate, to amplify), from French <i>outrer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exaggerate</li></ol><p>From French <i>outrer</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(j)utro</i> (“morning, dawn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb</li><li>belly</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>утроба</i> (utroba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫtroba</i> (“internal organs”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) womb</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the morning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, to bother</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>труди́ть</i> (trudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, to bother</li></ol><p><i>утруди́ть</i> (utrudítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bury, to hide</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to set <i>(with)</i> <i>(needles, sticks)</i></li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ты́кать</i> (týkatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bury oneself <i>(in)</i>, to hide <i>(one's face)</i> <i>(in)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to come to rest <i>(upon, against)</i>, to be stopped <i>(by)</i>, to hit</li></ol><p><i>утыка́ть</i> (utykátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) undesirable person, fucker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron (for pressing clothes)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, from Old Turkic <i>ütüg</i>, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;ütüg</i>; whence also Turkish <i>ütü</i> (“iron”), Bulgarian <i>ютия</i> (jutija).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>žehlička</i> (akin to <i>жечь</i> (žečʹ)), <i>глачало</i> (akin to <i>гла́дить</i> (gláditʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to iron (clothes)</li></ol><p>From <i>утюг</i> (utjug)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make heavier, increase the weight</li></ol><p><i>утяжели́ть</i> (utjaželítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ять</i> (-jatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duck (meat)</li></ol><p>From <i>утя́т-</i> (utját-) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina), the former being the plural stem of <i>утёнок</i> (utjónok, “duckling”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duckling</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;slang) two little ducks, twenty-two in the lotto game</li></ol><p><i>ут(ка)</i> (ut(ka)) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, cliff, crag</li></ol><p>From утеса́ть (utesátʹ), теса́ть (tesátʹ, “to hew, to cleave”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>скала́</i> (skalá) (akin to <i>щель</i> (ščelʹ), <i>расще́лина</i> (rasščélina)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>утёс</i> (utjós).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>phew! (I’m tired&#33;)</li><li>oof (sound of pain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ufologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ooh!, gosh!, oof!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wow! (<i>expresses strong surprise or admiration for something unusual</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ukha, a Russian fish soup</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>уха</i> (uxa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;juxa</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yew(ǝ)-</i> (“to blend, mix (food), knead”). Cognates include Latin <i>ius</i> (whence English <i>juice</i>), Ancient Greek <i>ζύμη</i> (zúmē) (whence Russian <i>энзи́м</i> (enzím, “enzyme, ferment”)), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;justaz</i>, Sanskrit <i>यूष</i> (yūṣa).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bump (on a road), (plural) pits and bumps</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>With frequent bumps; bumpy.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nursing, tending, caring, looking after (someone or something)</li><li>courtship, dating, wooing, attentions (act of wooing in love)</li></ol><p><i>уха́живать</i> (uxáživatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care, to tend, to nurse, to look after</li><li>to court, to date</li></ol><p><i>уходи́ть</i> (uxodítʹ, “to care for, to look after”, perfective) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admirer, boyfriend, lady's man</li></ol><p><i>уха́ж(ивать)</i> (uxáž(ivatʹ), “to court, to date”) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash, to bang</li><li>to hoot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven fork</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to lay hold of, to grasp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to understand, to catch, to grasp</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch/lay hold <i>(of)</i>, to grasp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to grasp <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>ухвати́ть</i> (uxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trait, habit, manner</li><li>skill, proficiency, dexterity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, to lay hold of, to grasp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to understand, to catch, to grasp</li></ol><p><i>ухвати́ть</i> (uxvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch/lay hold <i>(of)</i>, to grasp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to grasp <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>ухва́тывать</i> (uxvátyvatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive <i>(to)</i>, to manage <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>хитри́ть</i> (xitrítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive <i>(to)</i>, to manage <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>ухитри́ться</i> (uxitrítʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick; gimmick, ruse, wile, stratagem</li><li>contrivance, device, shift</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>оухыщрениѥ</i> (uxyštrenije), from <i>оухытрити</i> (uxytriti, “to come up with, to figure out, to do”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xytrъ</i> (“sly, wise”). Compare <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grin, smirk, (sarcastic) smile</li></ol><p><i>ухмыля́ть(ся)</i> (uxmyljátʹ(sja), “to smirk, to grin”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smirk, to grin</li></ol><p>Probably <i>ухмыля́ться</i> (uxmyljátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smirk, to grin</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, cognate with Middle High German <i>smollen</i> (“to smile”), <i>smielen</i> (“to smile”), archaic Dutch <i>smuylen</i> (“to smile”), Bavarian <i>schmollen</i> (“to smile”), Norwegian <i>smolla</i> (“to smile”) (also <i>smulla</i>, <i>smusla</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash, to bang</li><li>to hoot</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squander, to lose at one stroke</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to slip, to fall</li><li>to bang, to slap</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall heavily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uxo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ṓws</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ունկն</i> (unkn), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐍃𐍉</i> (ausō), Ancient Greek <i>οὖς</i> (oûs), Latin <i>auris</i>, Persian هوش (huš, hôš, “intellect”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) otorhinolaryngologist</li></ol><p>From <i>у́хо</i> (úxo, “ear”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>го́рло</i> (górlo, “throat”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>но́с</i> (nós, “nose”). Literally, “ear-throat-nose”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care (treatment of those in need)</li><li>escape</li><li>withdrawal, resignation</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) death</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>уходи́ть</i> (uxodítʹ, perfective).</p><p>Deverbal from <i>уходи́ть</i> (uxodítʹ, imperfective).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, to depart, to go (away) (by foot)</li><li>to escape, to evade</li><li>to resign, to retire</li><li>to be lost</li><li>to fail</li><li>to sink, to plunge</li><li>to be spent</li><li>(<i>folk&#32;colloquial</i>) to exhaust, to torment</li><li>to look after, to care for (someone) (<i>usually only used in the past passive participle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outgoing</li><li>parting, leaving</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-groomed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deteriorate, to worsen (to make worse)</li></ol><p><i>уху́дшить</i> (uxúdšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become worse, to deteriorate, to take a turn for the worse</li></ol><p><i>ухудша́ть</i> (uxudšátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worsening, deterioration</li></ol><p><i>уху́дшить</i> (uxúdšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deteriorate, to worsen (to make worse)</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ху́дший</i> (xúdšij) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become worse, to deteriorate, to take a turn for the worse</li></ol><p><i>уху́дшить</i> (uxúdšitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>survivor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay whole (intact, unhurt), to survive, to be spared</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark down, to reduce the price</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to grab</li></ol><p><i>у</i> (u) +&lrm; <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch/lay hold <i>(of)</i>, to seize <i>(on)</i>, to grasp <i>(at)</i></li></ol><p><i>уцепи́ть</i> (ucepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take part, to participate, to collaborate</li><li>to have a share</li></ol><p>From <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>часть</i> (častʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participation, collaboration, complicity</li><li>share, stake</li><li>sympathy, interest, concern</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>участие</i> (učastije), from Old Church Slavonic <i>оучѧстиѥ</i> (učęstije). By surface analysis, <i>участь</i> (učastʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). The modern meanings arose due to French influence, compare French <i>avoir part</i> and French <i>prendre part</i>, which were calqued as <i>принима́ть уча́стие</i> (prinimátʹ učástije, “to take part”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do more often</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>ча́стый</i> (částyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more frequent</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>участи́ть</i> (učastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) division, district</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nominalized) district police officer (in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>уча́сток</i> (učástok) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sympathetically, compassionately</li></ol><p><i>уча́стливый</i> (učástlivyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sympathetic, compassionate</li></ol><p><i>уча́стие</i> (učástije) +&lrm; <i>-ливый</i> (-livyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participant, participator</li><li>user</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) competitor, member</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učęstьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>уча́стие</i> (učástije) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to participants (<i>уча́стник</i> (učástnik))</li></ol><p><i>уча́стник</i> (učástnik, “participant”) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, section, portion</li><li>lot, plot, region, district, zone, sector, precinct</li><li>area</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>часть</i> (častʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, destiny, lot</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učęstь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>часть</i> (častʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do more often</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>участи́ть</i> (učastítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more frequent</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to accelerate</li></ol><p><i>учаща́ть</i> (učaščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schoolgirl, pupil, student</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schoolboy, pupil, student</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>textbook</li><li>manual</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>уче́бник</i> (učébnik, “textbook; manual”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relating to school or learning, academic, training</li></ol><p><i>учёба</i> (učóba) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning, instruction, training, drill, exercising</li><li>doctrine</li><li>apprenticeship</li></ol><p><i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school student, schoolboy, pupil</li><li>apprentice, learner</li><li>disciple, follower</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učenikъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) student; student's</li><li>crude, amateurish</li></ol><p><i>учен(и́к)</i> (učen(ík)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>period spent as a pupil or student</li><li>apprenticeship</li></ol><p><i>учени́к</i> (učeník) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>уче́ние</i> (učénije)</li></ol><p><i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́нье</i> (-énʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take into account, to take into consideration, to appreciate (to be aware of)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>честь</i> (čestʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specialized school, college</li></ol><p><i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) female schoolteacher</li></ol><p><i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ, “to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to commit</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>чини́ть</i> (činítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make, to commit</li></ol><p><i>учини́ть</i> (učinítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, instructor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učiteľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teachers' lounge, staffroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) teaching, teacher; teacher's, teachers'</li></ol><p><i>учи́тель</i> (učítelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take into account, to take into consideration, to appreciate (to be aware of)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all things considered (all in all)</li></ol><p>Literally, “taking into account all circumstances”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all things considered (all in all)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, to instruct (<i>often not in an academic setting</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> dative <small>‘a skill, principle, etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>; <i>or</i> intransitive (<i>to be a teacher</i>)]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to learn, to study [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> <b>по</b> (po, + dative) <small>‘using a book or method’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to memorize by rote</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to beat, to whip (<i>of corporal punishment, often towards a child</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘with or using something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>учити</i> (učiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;idiomatic,&#32;usually negative) to teach grandma how to suck eggs</li></ol><p>Literally, “to teach an eagle [how] to fly”. Compare Czech <i>neuč orla létat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn (to receive knowledge or skills or to gain useful experience)</li><li>to learn [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘a skill, principle, thing etc.’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), from Old East Slavic <i>учити</i> (učiti) and <i>сѧ</i> (sę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učiti</i> and <i>&#42;sę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;historical) a Constitutional Congress, especially about the Russian Constituent Assembly (January 1918) and the attempts of its revival during the Civil War</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>учредительное собрание</i> (učreditelʹnoje sobranije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder</li></ol><p><i>учреди́ть</i> (učredítʹ, “to found”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constituent, constitutive, founding</li></ol><p><i>учреди́ть</i> (učredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, to found, to institute</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to establish, to found, to institute</li></ol><p><i>учреди́ть</i> (učredítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>establishment, founding, setting up (the state of being established)</li><li>institution, establishment, office</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>politeness (act of being polite)</li></ol><p><i>учти́вый</i> (učtívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civil, courteous, polite, considerate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;učьstivъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>чтить</i> (čtitʹ, “to honour/honor, respect”) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj). Cognate with Polish <i>uczciwy</i> (“honest”), Czech <i>uctivý</i> (“respectful”), Serbo-Croatian <i>у̀чтив</i> (“polite”), Old Church Slavonic <i>оучьтивъ</i> (učĭtivŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obstruction build in a river where fish get caught, often of a large and continuous kind that sustains whole populations &lsqb;used until the beginning of the 19<sup>th</sup> century and then largely outlawed&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>учуг</i> (uçuğ, “string”) in the sixteenth century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act weirdly, to fool about</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>чуди́ть</i> (čudítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to nose out, to sense</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to sense</li></ol><p><i>учу́ять</i> (učújatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell, to nose out, to sense</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to sense</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>studies, learning</li><li>study, training, drill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>education</i>) degree (a stage of proficiency or qualification in a course of study, now especially an award bestowed by a university/college, as a certification of academic achievement)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning, erudition, scholarship</li></ol><p><i>учёный</i> (učónyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>learned, adept, erudite</li><li>scientific, academic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scholar, scientist</li></ol><p>From <i>учи́ть</i> (učítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation, stock-taking</li><li>registration</li><li>taking into account</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>уче́сть</i> (učéstʹ), based on the stem found in past tense masculine singular <i>учёл</i> (učól) (from <i>&#42;учётл</i> (&#42;učótl)) and past adverbial participle <i>учётши</i> (učótši).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account (an authorization to use a service)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) registration, record</li><li>(relational,&#32;finance) discount</li></ol><p><i>учёт</i> (učót) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ushanka (traditional Russian fur cap with ear flaps)</li></ol><p>From <i>уши</i> (uši, “ears”) +&lrm; <i>-анка</i> (-anka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) big-eared</li><li>with ears (<i>of a hat or other headdress</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у́хо</i> (úxo) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tub, bucket (broad, flat-bottomed vessel)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) headphones</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) contusion, bruise, injury</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise, to hurt</li></ol><p><i>ушиби́ть</i> (ušibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise, to hurt</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-шиби́ть</i> (-šibítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bruise oneself, to hurt oneself</li></ol><p><i>ушиби́ть</i> (ušibítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>уха</i> (uxa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) synonym of <i>за́яц</i> (zájac): hare</li></ol><p><i>у́шко́</i> (úškó, diminutive of у́хо (úxo, “ear”)) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>у́хо</i> (úxo); ear</li><li>eye (of a needle)</li></ol><p>From <i>у́хо</i> (úxo) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko) (stem-stressed variant) or from <i>у́хо</i> (úxo) +&lrm; <i>-ко́</i> (-kó) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shrewd, smart, cunning, quirky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) undesirable person, fucker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auricle, pinna (outer ear)</li></ol><p>Compare typologically Dutch <i>oorschelp</i> (&lt; <i>oor</i> + <i>schelp</i>), German <i>Ohrmuschel</i> (&lt; <i>Ohr</i> + <i>Muschel</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ear; aural, auricular</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ušьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>у́хо</i> (úxo) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wushu</li></ol><p>From Mandarin 武術／武术 (<i>wǔshù</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorge, ravine, canyon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pain (<i>morally</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to infringe, to restrict, to restrain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to offend</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>щеми́ть</i> (ščemítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinching, strangulation</li><li>infringement, encroachment (e.g. on rights)</li></ol><p><i>ущеми́ть</i> (uščemítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to pain (<i>morally</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to infringe, to restrict, to restrain</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to offend</li></ol><p><i>ущеми́ть</i> (uščemítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damage</li><li>wane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;uščьrbъ</i>. Compare Polish <i>uszczerbek</i>, Bulgarian <i>щъ̀рбел</i> (štǎ̀rbel) and Czech <i>štěrba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give damage</li><li>to limit</li></ol><p><i>ущерби́ть</i> (uščerbítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defectiveness, inferiority</li></ol><p><i>уще́рбный</i> (uščérbnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>(moon)</i> on the wane, waning</li><li>defective</li></ol><p><i>уще́рб</i> (uščérb) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pinch (<i>with fingers or a beak</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pinch off, to remove (a small amount) by pinching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hurt (<i>morally or mentally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>щипну́ть</i> (ščipnútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pinch (<i>with fingers or a beak</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pinch off, to remove (a small amount) by pinching</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hurt (<i>morally or mentally</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ущипну́ть</i> (uščipnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Welsh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cosiness, comfort</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ujǫtъ</i>, from <i>&#42;jǫtъ</i> (“roof”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comfortably, cozily</li></ol><p><i>ую́тный</i> (ujútnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comfortable, cosy</li></ol><p>From <i>ую́т</i> (ujút) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulnerability (state of being vulnerable)</li></ol><p><i>уязви́мый</i> (ujazvímyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vulnerable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sting, to wound</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>язви́ть</i> (jazvítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sting, to wound</li></ol><p><i>уязви́ть</i> (ujazvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, to grasp</li></ol><p><i>у-</i> (u-) +&lrm; <i>-ясни́ть</i> (-jasnítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to understand, to grasp</li></ol><p><i>уясни́ть</i> (ujasnítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;inanimate) something ugly or extremely unpleasant (usually of large size)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;animate) fucker, motherfucker, fuck</li></ol><p><i>уеба́ть</i> (ujebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) ugliness, foulness</li></ol><p>From <i>уёбищный</i> (ujóbiščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;relational) characteristic of a fucker, motherfucker (an annoying, bad or stupid person)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) bad, low-quality</li></ol><p><i>уёбище</i> (ujóbišče, “fucker”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucker, motherfucker, fuck, shithead, asshole (an annoying, bad or stupid person)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory,&#32;about someone else's child) fucker, shit</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;humorous,&#32;ironic,&#32;about oneself) fool, idiot, moron</li></ol><p><i>уеба́ть</i> (ujebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok). Compare Ukrainian <i>уйо́бок</i> (ujóbok).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get away, fuck off, get the fuck out, to go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>уе́зд</i> (ujézd).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>musical note</i>) F</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) fascist</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>фаши́ст</i> (fašíst, “fascist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>factory, plant, mill</li></ol><p>Attested 1705. Probably borrowed from Polish <i>fabryka</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>fabrica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>factory-kitchen</li></ol><p><i>фа́брика</i> (fábrika, “factory”) +&lrm; <i>ку́хня</i> (kúxnja, “kitchen”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manufacturer, factory owner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fabrikant</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>fabrica</i>. Cognate with <i>фа́брика</i> (fábrika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manufacture, to produce</li><li>to fabricate, to forge</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>fabric(āre)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) factory</li><li>industrial, manufacturing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) factory worker</li></ol><p><i>фа́бр(ика)</i> (fábr(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>narratology</i>) plot, story, tale</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fabula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favela</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Brazilian Portuguese <i>favela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favorite, minion</li><li><i>sport</i> favorite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>favouritism/favoritism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) favus</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>favus</i> (“honeycomb”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bassoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fagotto</i>, perhaps via German <i>Fagott</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фаго́т</i> (fagót).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) phagocyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Phase</i>, from Latin <i>phasis</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φάσις</i> (phásis). Doublet of <i>фа́зис</i> (fázis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pheasant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fasan</i>, from Latin <i>phasianus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φασιανός</i> (phasianós, “bird of the river <i>Φᾶσις</i> (Phâsis)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pheasant</li></ol><p><i>фаза́н</i> (fazán) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fazenda (a Brazilian plantation, often associated with slavery during the colonial period)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a large (and fancy) dacha, summer house</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Portuguese <i>fazenda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) phase (stage of some process)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>phasis</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φάσις</i> (phásis). Doublet of <i>фа́за</i> (fáza), which was borrowed via the intermediary of German.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;physics) phase space</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) phasic</li></ol><p><i>фа́за</i> (fáza) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) phase transition</li><li>(<i>Marxism</i>) phase transition of social formations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) synchrocyclotron (type of particle accelerator)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) file</li><li>(<i>office</i>) punched pocket</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>file</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>файл</i> (fajl): little file</li></ol><p><i>файл</i> (fajl) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;humorous) fileserver</li></ol><p><i>файл</i> (fajl) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>помойка</i> (pomojka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) firewall</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>firewall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) fighting game</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fighting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) college, school, department (a division of a university)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) the finger (gesture)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) fuck!</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>факульте́т</i> (fakulʹtét, “college”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>fuck</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch (a stick with a flame at one end)</li><li>jet, tongue, flare</li><li>flame body</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fackel</i>, from Middle High German <i>vackel</i>, from Old High German <i>faccala</i>, from Latin <i>facula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фа́кел</i> (fákel)</li></ol><p><i>фа́кел</i> (fákel) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) torchbearer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) torch, flare</li></ol><p><i>фа́кел</i> (fákel) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torchbearer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) incendiary, firebug</li></ol><p><i>фа́кель</i> (fákelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fakir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fax</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fax</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facsimile (copy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fact</li><li>fait accompli</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in fact (actually, in truth)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>factual, actual, real</li><li>de facto</li></ol><p><i>факт</i> (fakt) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>factor (significant influence)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) factor</li><li>factor, factotum, agent, broker (doer, maker)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>factor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>factorial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>invoice, bill; bill of parcels</li><li>texture; pattern</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>factūra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optional, elective</li><li>extracurricular</li><li>facultative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faculty (division of a university)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fakultät</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) phalanx</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) phalanx</li><li>sun spider, wind scorpion, camel spider, sun scorpion, Solifugae (Latin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φάλαγξ</i> (phálanx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falafel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic فَلَافِل (falāfil), the plural of فِلْفِل (filfil, “pepper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coattail</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phallic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dildo (artificial phallus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;slang,&#32;humorous) to be overwhelmed with excitement</li></ol><p>Jocular Latin translation of <i>хуе́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (xujétʹ), <i>охуе́ть</i>&nbsp;pf (oxujétʹ), <i>охуева́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (oxujevátʹ). Ultimately derived from Latin <i>phallus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsifier (one that falsifies)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsification</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to falsify, to forge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false start (starting before the signal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>false start</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsetto</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Falsett</i>, from Italian <i>falsetto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaponry</i>) falchion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) forgery, fabrication, counterfeit, fake</li></ol><p><i>фальш(ь)</i> (falʹš(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing or play (e.g. a musical instrument) out of tune</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to be hypocritical</li></ol><p>From <i>фальшь</i> (falʹšʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgery, fabrication, counterfeit, fake</li></ol><p><i>фальши́вый</i> (falʹšívyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false, fake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from</p><p>German <i>falsch</i>,</p><p>Polish <i>fałszywy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falsity, falsehood</li><li>insincerity</li><li>(<i>music</i>) false note(s); singing / playing out of tune</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>falsch</i> (“false”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surname, last name, family name</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) clan</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) family (<i>the original meaning</i>), see <i>фами́льный</i> (famílʹnyj)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Familie</i> or Polish <i>familia</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>familia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) family</li></ol><p><i>фами́лия</i> (famílija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>familiarity, unceremoniousness</li></ol><p><i>фамилья́рный</i> (familʹjárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>familiar, unceremonious (inappropriately intimate or friendly)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>familiarny</i>, from Latin <i>familiāris</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фами́лия</i> (famílija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fan (admirer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) fanart, artworks created by people who like an existing work.</li></ol><p>From English <i>fanart</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) fanbase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fan base</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan, devotee (admirer)</li><li>fanboy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;slang) to be a fan (of), to fan (over) [<i>with</i> <b>от</b> (ot, + genitive) <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>фана́т</i> (fanát) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fanaticism, bigotry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fanatic, sectarian, zealot</li><li>bigot, devotee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fanatic, fanatical</li></ol><p><i>фана́т(ик)</i> (fanát(ik)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reverse vending machine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pfandautomat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fundraising (the process of collecting money by requesting donations from individuals and businesses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fundraising</i> (<i>misread</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plywood (construction material)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) phonogram, sound track</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Furnier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) plywood</li></ol><p><i>фане́ра</i> (fanéra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Chinese or Korean house</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>房子</i> (fángzi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) piano</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>фортепиа́но</i> (fortepiáno).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;plural only) fant (game in which players perform comic tasks, assigned by lot to the owner of an object, the "fant")</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) object whose owner is required to perform a comic task and which must be given back, in the game of fant</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) task performed in this way</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>fant</i>, from German <i>Pfand</i> (“pledge”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasizing</li></ol><p><i>фантази́ровать</i> (fantazírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fantasize, to dream (to indulge in fantasy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasy (imagining)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasist, daydreamer, dreamer (one living in a fantasy world)</li></ol><p><i>фантазия</i> (fantazija) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantasmagoria (a dreamlike state)</li><li>nonsense, ridiculousness, absurdity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dreamer, fantasist</li><li>visionary</li><li>science fiction writer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fantast</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasy, fabulousness</li><li>(<i>literature</i>) fantasy</li><li>(<i>predicative</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fantastically, fabulously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fantastical</li></ol><p><i>фанта́ст(ика)</i> (fantást(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>фант</i> (fant): (small) fant (object whose owner must perform a comic task in the game of fant, or the task so performed)</li><li>candy wrapper</li></ol><p><i>фант</i> (fant) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik). Meaning <i>candy wrapper</i>, possibly influenced by <i>фу́нтик</i> (fúntik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fantôme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fanfare</li><li>trumpet, bugle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) fanfiction, stories written by other people who like an existing work.</li></ol><p>From English <i>fanfic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;online slang) to masturbate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fap</i> + <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headlight, headlamp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>phare</i>. Further from Latin <i>pharus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Φάρος</i> (Pháros).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>světlomet</i>, i.e. **светломёт, Slovene <i>žaromet</i>, i.e. **жаромёт.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharaoh</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) cop, policeman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Φαραώ</i> (Pharaṓ, “Pharaoh”), from Biblical Hebrew <i>פַּרְעֹה</i> (parʿōh), from Egyptian <i>pr-ꜥꜣ</i> (“palace, pharaoh”, literally “<i>pr</i> (“house”) + <i>ꜥꜣ</i> (“great, big”)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фарао́н</i> (faraón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterway, fairway (navigable body of water)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>vaarwater</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faroese, Faroe Islander</li></ol><p>From <i>Фаре́ры</i> (Farɛ́ry) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Faroese</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faroese (language)</li></ol><p><i>Фаре́ры</i> (Farɛ́ry) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharisee</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy</li></ol><p><i>фарисе́й</i> (fariséj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;automotive) tow bar, tow hitch, trailer hitch, tow ball, ball mount, hitch ball</li></ol><p>Unknown. A folk etymology derives the word from German <i>Fahrkopf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;low colloquial) freemason, mason</li><li>(dated,&#32;low colloquial) freethinker, nihilist</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) peddler of counterfeit jewelry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>franc-maçon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacology (science that studies the effects of chemical compounds on living animals)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmaceutics</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from Ancient Greek <i>φαρμακευτική</i> (pharmakeutikḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pharmaceutical</li></ol><p><i>фармаце́вт(ика)</i> (farmacévt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farce</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>farce</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) luck, fortune</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) destiny</li></ol><p>Originally a fenya cant term, possibly from German <i>Fahrt</i> (“ride”), from <i>fahren</i> (“to drive”), because the corresponding Russian verb <i>везти</i> (vezti) means both “to drive” and “to have luck”; alternatively, perhaps from <i>фортуна</i> (fortuna, “fortune, luck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lucky, jammy</li></ol><p><i>фарт</i> (fart) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apron (clothing)</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>fartuch</i>, from Middle High German <i>vortuoch</i> (whence German <i>Fürtuch</i>, regional German <i>Vortuch</i> (“apron”)). Compare dialectal <i>фа́ртух</i> (fártux), Ukrainian <i>фарту́х</i> (fartúx), Belarusian <i>хварту́к</i> (xvartúk). For the second part of the word doublet of <i>га́лстук</i> (gálstuk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porcelain, china</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فغفوری (fağfûrî, “Chinese [porcelain]”), from Persian فغفوری (faġfuri, “Chinese [porcelain]”), from فغفور (faġfur, “emperor of China”) + ـی (-i), from Sogdian <i>𐼱𐼲𐼾𐼰𐼴𐽀</i> (βɣpʾwr /⁠βaγpūr⁠/); see it for more. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂g-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) porcelain, china</li></ol><p><i>фарфо́р</i> (farfór) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) in the USSR, hucksterism, the activity of illegally selling things.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;strongly disapproving) huckster, spiv (a person engaged in <b>фарцо́вка (farcóvka)</b>. <b>Фарцовщик</b> was a Soviet era term for a person illegally selling something, a "private vendor", "private seller", as it was forbidden in the USSR to be self-employed)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minced meat or fish, mince</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>farce</i> (“stuffing”) and <i>farcir</i> (“to stuff”). Further from Latin <i>farciō</i>. First attested 18th century. Doublet of <i>фарс</i> (fars).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to stuff with mincemeat</li></ol><p><i>фарш</i> (farš) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>фаре́рский</i> (farérskij)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>фаре́рский</i> (farérskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front, face</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li><i>(a command to a dog)</i> attack!, get'em!, bite!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>face</i> (“face, front part”). Further from Latin <i>faciēs</i> (whence English <i>face</i>).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>fass</i> (“catch, snatch”, imperative form).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facade, frontage, front <i>(also figuratively)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>façade</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>faciēs</i> (whence English <i>face</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bezel (the oblique side or face of a cut gem)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>facette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facet (one member of a compound eye)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) facility management; facility maintenance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>facility management</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepack (retail goods)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>fasować</i>, from German <i>fassen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bean</li></ol><p><i>фасо́ль</i> (fasólʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(uncountable,&#32;collectively) beans</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>fasola</i>, from Latin <i>phaseolus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fashion, style; cut (of clothing)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) swank, showing off</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>façon</i> &lt; Old French <i>façon</i> (whence also English <i>fashion</i>). Further from Latin <i>factiōnem</i> &lt; Latin <i>faciō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fast food (type of meal that is often pre-prepared and served quickly)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fast food</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) fop; coxcomb; dandy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fat</i>, Latin <i>fatuus</i> (“stupid”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridal veil</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) any translucent light fabric of untwisted threads of silk or paper or both</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>фата</i> (fata), <i>фота</i> (fota), from Ottoman Turkish فوطه (<i>futa</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fate</li><li>inevitable</li><li>fatal</li></ol><p>French <i>fatal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Further from Latin <i>fātālis</i>, from Latin <i>fātum</i> + <i>-ālis</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>роково́й</i> (rokovój) (&lt; <i>рок</i> (rok), akin to <i>речь</i> (rečʹ), <i>-речь</i> (-rečʹ), <i>-река́ть</i> (-rekátʹ) (<i>наре́чь</i> (naréčʹ), <i>нарека́ть</i> (narekátʹ); <i>изре́чь</i> (izréčʹ), <i>изрека́ть</i> (izrekátʹ))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Fatimid</li></ol><p><i>Фатими́д</i> (Fatimíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(linguistics,&#32;psychology) phatic (<i>of language intended primarily as a social gesture</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>φατός</i> (phatós, “spoken”) + <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij), from <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “I say”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) fate</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>fatum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The German letter V, v.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Vau</i>, name for the letter v.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) fauna (animals considered as a group)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) phacelia (<i>Phacelia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Phacelia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fascistization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fascistize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) fascism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fascismo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;historical,&#32;specifically) an soldier or officer of Nazi Germany who fought against the Soviet Union in World War II; a Kraut</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any fascist</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any Nazi</li></ol><p><i>фаш&#91;и́ст&#93;</i> (faš&#91;íst&#93;) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fascine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Faschine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fascist (proponent of fascism)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Nazi (<i>during World War II</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fascista</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fascist, fascist-like</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fascist</li></ol><p><i>фаши́ст</i> (fašíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;collective,&#32;derogatory,&#32;politics) Fascists.</li></ol><p><i>фаши́ст</i> (fašíst) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phaeton (a type of horse carriage)</li><li>phaeton (a type of automobile body)</li><li>tropicbird</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>phaéton</i>, ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>Φαέθων</i> (Phaéthōn).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Фаэтон)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phaëthon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Φαέθων</i> (Phaéthōn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delft, faience (type of tin-glazed earthenware ceramic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>faïence</i>, from the Italian city of <i>Faenza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) faience</li></ol><p><i>фая́нс</i> (fajáns) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фая́нс</i> (fajáns).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>теа́тр</i> (teátr)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>θέατρον</i> (théatron).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>февра́ль</i> (fevrálʹ) or <i>февра́ля</i> (fevrálja, “February”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>February</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>феврарь</i> (fevrarĭ), <i>феуларь</i> (feularĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>февроуари</i> (fevruari), from Byzantine Greek <i>Φεβρουάριος</i> (Phebrouários), from Latin <i>Februārius</i>, from Latin <i>februa</i>, from Latin <i>febris</i> (whence also English <i>fever</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰegʷʰ-</i> (“to burn; warm, hot”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) February</li></ol><p><i>февра́ль</i> (fevrálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fed (a federal government employee, a federal agent)</li><li>a federal soldier (a soldier in Russia's federal forces deployed in the Chechen wars and various insurgencies)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>fédéral</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>federalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>federalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>federal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>federative, federal</li></ol><p>From <i>федера́ция</i> (federácija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>федерати́вний</i> (federatývnyj) and Belarusian <i>федэраты́ўны</i> (fjederatýwny).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>federation</li></ol><p>From French <i>fédération</i>, from Late Latin <i>foederatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enchantingly, magically, fabulously</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) unbelievably, astonishingly</li></ol><p><i>феери́ческий</i> (fejeríčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-ски</i> (-ski)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) féerie (related to or characteristic of a féerie)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) enchanting, magical, fairy-tale (making an extraordinary impression)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) extraordinary (showing a quality with great intensity)</li></ol><p><i>фее́рия</i> (fejérija, “féerie”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enchantingly, spectacularly, fabulously, fantastically</li></ol><p><i>феери́чный</i> (fejeríčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enchanting, extravagant, spectacular, magical, extraordinary</li></ol><p><i>фее́рия</i> (fejérija) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>féerie</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a magical, fabulous or marvelous spectacle, event or incident</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>féerie</i>, from French <i>fée</i>, from Late Latin <i>fātа</i> (“goddess of fate”), from Latin <i>fātum</i> (“fate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firework</li><li>firecracker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Feuerwerk</i>. Attested since the late 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) fake</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) fake news</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fake news</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism) fake, false</li></ol><p><i>фейк</i> (fejk, fɛjk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;jargonism) face</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>face</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;relational) Facebook</li></ol><p><i>Фейсбук</i> (Fejsbuk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) a night club employee who exercises face control</li></ol><p>Pseudo-anglicism, derived from <i><b>face</b> control</i> +&lrm; <i>-er</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face control (policy of upscale nightclubs to restrict entry based on a bouncer's snap judgment of the suitability of a person's looks, money, style or attitude)</li></ol><p>Pseudo-anglicism, derived from <i>face</i> +&lrm; <i>control</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feijoa (<i>Feijoa sellowiana</i>, an evergreen bush of the myrtle family).</li><li>The fruit of this bush with a smell of strawberries and pineapple.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feijoa (<i>Feijoa sellowiana</i>, an evergreen bush of the myrtle family).</li><li>The fruit of this bush with a smell of strawberries and pineapple.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) faeces (digested waste material discharged from the bowels)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellah</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellatio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fellation</i>, from Latin <i>fellatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) field marshal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Feldmarschall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;military) feldwebel (a non-commissioned officer (NCO))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Feldwebel</i>. Compare with Swedish <i>fältväbel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) doctor's / medical assistant, medical attendant, paramedic, male nurse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Feldscher</i> (“surgeon”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feuilleton, a satirical article</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>feuilleton</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>femboy (a young man who adopts a feminine aesthetic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>femboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) femboy</li></ol><p>From <i>фембо́й</i> (fembój) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>femboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) femboyish</li></ol><p>From <i>фембо́й</i> (fembój) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij), from English <i>femboy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фембо́й</i> (fembój, “femboy”)</li></ol><p>From <i>фембо́й</i> (fembój) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>BDSM</i>) femdom (sexual practice in which women dominate men or other women)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>femdom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>феминити́в</i> (feminitív)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feminization</li></ol><p>From <i>феминизи́ровать</i> (feminizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feminism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) female-specific name for a profession, e.g. actress vs actor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hairdryer (electrical appliance for drying hair)</li><li>(slang,&#32;drug addicts) amphetamine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Föhn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fennec fox</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phoenix</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>phoenīx</i> (likely via a Western European language; cf. French phénix), itself from Ancient Greek <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фе́никс</i> (féniks).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phenomenon (unusual occurrence)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) phenomenon (exceptional person)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) paradox</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;psychology) phenomenon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φαινόμενον</i> (phainómenon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phenomenal</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>phénoménal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phenomenologist</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>феноменоло́гия</i> (fenomenológija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phenomenological</li></ol><p><i>феноменоло́гия</i> (fenomenológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phenomenology</li></ol><p>From German <i>Phänomenologie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>φαινόμενον</i> (phainómenon, “thing appearing to view”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) fentanyl</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from English <i>fentanyl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fennel (plant)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fenchel</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>fēnuculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) Fenya, criminal argot, slang, cant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feudal lord</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>féodal</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feudalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) feudal division</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feudal</li></ol><p><i>феода́л</i> (feodál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of, from, or pertaining to Theodosia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) queen</li></ol><p>From Persian فرزین (farzin, “the queen in chess”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farm</li><li>girder, rib, truss</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>ferme</i> in the early 19th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) enzyme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermentation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>farmer</i> or German <i>Farmer</i> and changed to match <i>фе́рма</i> (férma) (<i>derived from French</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>farmer's, farmers'</li></ol><p><i>фе́рмер</i> (férmer) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farming, agriculture (the art or science of cultivating the ground)</li><li>farmers (<i>collectively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>фе́рмер</i> (férmer) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) fermion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) fermionic</li></ol><p><i>фермио́н</i> (fermión) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Ferocactus</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ferocactus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferroboron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fes (a Turkish hat in the shape of a truncated cone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فس (fes).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fez (a Turkish hat in the shape of a truncated cone)</li></ol><p><i>фес</i> (fes) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian</li></ol><p><i>Фесса́лия</i> (Fessálija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival (celebration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) festival</li></ol><p><i>фестива́ль</i> (festiválʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feta <i>(Greek cheese)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>φέτα</i> (féta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetish (all senses)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetishism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetishist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) fencing</li></ol><p><i>фехтова́ть</i> (fextovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) to fence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>fechten</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fehtaną</i> (whence also English <i>fight</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i>.</p><p>For the term being a descendant from PIE <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to pluck (wool, hair)”), compare typologically <i>драть</i> (dratʹ), <i>дра́ться</i> (drátʹsja). For another descendant from the PIE root, <i>пеньюа́р</i> (penʹjuár), note <i>дерю́га</i> (derjúga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fashionable</li></ol><p>From English <i>fashionable</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) fairy</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) enchantress (a good-hearted, charming woman)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;euphemistic) prostitute, sex worker</li></ol><p>Via German <i>Fee</i> or directly from French <i>fée</i>, from Vulgar Latin <i>Fāta</i> (“goddess of fate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phi (the Greek letter Φ&#47;φ)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used to show (usually arrogant) lack of admiration, interest, or inspiration: meh, whatever</li><li>Used to show (usually arrogant) disapproval, discontent: yuck, bah, pah, pfft (same as фу (fu))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φεῖ</i> (pheî).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) violet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>фїа́лка</i> (fiálka) (att. early 17th c.) at the turn of 18th c., itself with a gender change from Middle Polish <i>fiałek</i>, a variant of <i>fiołek</i>, from Old Polish <i>fiołek</i> (att. 1447), from Middle High German <i>vîol</i>, from Latin <i>viola</i> (whence also <i>фиоле́товый</i> (fiolétovyj)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ро́за</i> (róza) (whence <i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj)), <i>ли́лия</i> (lílija) (akin to <i>лило́вый</i> (lilóvyj)). Also note <i>цвет</i> (cvet), <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók).</p><p>Also note female personal names <i>Вио́ла</i> (Vióla) (<i>Виоле́тта</i> (Violétta)), <i>Ро́за</i> (Róza), <i>Ли́лия</i> (Lílija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) mountain pansy (<i>Viola lutea</i>)</li></ol><p><i>фиа́лка</i> (fiálka, “violet”) +&lrm; <i>жёлтый</i> (žóltyj, “yellow”), literally “yellow violet”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wild pansy (<i>Viola tricolor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>фиа́лка</i> (fiálka, “violet (plant)”) +&lrm; <i>трёхцве́тный</i> (trjoxcvétnyj, “tricolor, three-colored”), a calque of translingual <i>Viola tricolor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) viola, violet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiasco, flop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fiasco</i>. Doublet of <i>флако́н</i> (flakón) and <i>фля́жка</i> (fljážka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fibula <i>(fastener)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hand gesture made by making a fist and putting the thumb between the index finger and middle finger, usually carries a vulgar meaning (known as the "fig sign"); ASL letter "t"</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fīcus</i> (“fig”), likely via Polish <i>figa</i>, from Middle High German <i>vîge</i>, from Old High German <i>fîga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig (fruit, wood, or tree)</li><li>a rude gesture, in which the hand is in the form of a fist with the thumb placed between the index and middle fingers</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, probably borrowed from Polish <i>figa</i>, from Middle High German <i>vîge</i>, from Old High German <i>fîga</i>, from Latin <i>fīcus</i> (“fig”). The rude gesture has the same origin, and is known as the "fig sign" in English.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>short for <i>ни фига́ себе́</i> (ni figá sebé)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move impetuously, to dash</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beat heavily, to whale on</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to become impudent/brazen/insolent</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, (nearly) to die of hunger, to cold, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>фиг</i> (fig).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buffoon, clown, jester</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>figlarz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) stuff</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rubbish <i>(nonsense)</i></li><li>(colloquial,&#32;of a thing) rubbish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trifle <i>(something insignificant)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>фиг</i> (fig) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá); compare <i>херня́</i> (xernjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) thing, object, thingy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fig</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bad, useless, of poor quality; lousy, rubbish</li></ol><p><i>фи́га</i> (fíga, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p><p><i>фиг</i> (fig, “rude gesture”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) piece</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) face card</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) figure of speech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>figuratively (in a figurative manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figurant, actor, extra</li><li>person involved in</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to figure <i>(as)</i>, to appear</li></ol><p>Adapted borrowing of German <i>figurieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure skater (someone who figure skates)</li></ol><p><i>фигур(а)</i> (figur(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фигу́ра</i> (figúra): figure</li><li>figurine</li></ol><p><i>фигу́ра</i> (figúra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) figure skating (sport where people perform spins, jumps and other moves on ice)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>figure skating</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaped, made in certain shape</li></ol><p><i>фигу́ра</i> (figúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang) feedback (critical assessment of process or activity)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) feedback (as from an amplifier and an electric guitar)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>feedback</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) phygital</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>phygital</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;fashion) pannier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physalis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical exercise</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>физи́ческая заря́дка</i> (fizíčeskaja zarjádka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear physicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physics (branch of science)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>particle physics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) physics and mathematics</li></ol><p><i>фи́зика</i> (fízika) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>матема́т(ика)</i> (matemát(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>physical and chemical, physico-chemical</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) physical chemistry</li></ol><p><i>фи́зика</i> (fízika) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>хими́ческий</i> (ximíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physiologist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>physiological</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sensual, carnal (<i>as opposed to romantic</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>физиоло́гия</i> (fiziológija, “physiology”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physiology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grimace, facial expression, mien</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) face</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physiotherapy (therapy that uses physical techniques such as massage and exercise)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>physically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) physics</li><li>physical, bodily</li></ol><p><i>фи́зика</i> (fízika) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) natural person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(especially&#32;military,&#32;school&#32;or&#32;higher education) physical education, physical culture, gymnastics, sport</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>физи́ческая культу́ра</i> (fizíčeskaja kulʹtúra, “physical culture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athlete</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) physical education teacher</li></ol><p><i>физкульту́ра</i> (fizkulʹtúra) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) clipping of <i>физи́ческое лицо́</i> (fizíčeskoje licó, “natural person”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>school slang</i>) physical education, gymnastics</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>физкульту́ра</i> (fizkulʹtúra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) physical training/education teacher</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>физкульту́рный руководи́тель</i> (fizkulʹtúrnyj rukovodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military&#32;or&#32;school&#32;slang</i>) physical education, gymnastics</li><li>(slang,&#32;by extension) physical strength, physical power, physical training, fitness, physique</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>photography</i>) fixer</li></ol><p>From French <i>fixage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fixation, fixing</li><li>record, recording (<i>in a permanent medium</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) commit (<i>to a database</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fixed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fix, to set, to settle, to state</li><li>to record</li><li>to fix <i>(a photograph)</i></li></ol><p>From German <i>fixieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to register</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) to fixate <i>(on)</i></li></ol><p><i>фикси́ровать</i> (fiksírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fictional, fictitious</li><li>forged, counterfeit, fake, sham</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>fiktiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ficus, rubber plant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiction (invention)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) elephant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian فیل (fil, “elephant”), from Arabic فِيل (fīl). Replaced <i>слон</i> (slon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) phylactery (small leather case, containing biblical scrolls, worn by Jewish men at morning prayer)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) phylactery (case in which (Christian) relics were preserved)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) phylactery (small object worn for its magical or supernatural power)</li><li>(fantasy,&#32;roleplaying games) phylactery</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filament lamp</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philanthropist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>philanthropic</li></ol><p><i>филантро́п</i> (filantróp) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philharmonic society</li><li>philharmonic orchestra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philatelist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philately</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>philatélie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>food</i>) fillet, filet</li><li>(<i>handicraft</i>) filet lace, filet-work</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>filet</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fillet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch office, branch (office of an organization with several locations)</li><li>subsidiary</li><li>affiliate</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>fīliālis</i>, from Latin <i>fīlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle owl</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Has been compared to Ukrainian <i>квили́ти</i> (kvylýty, “to groan, to moan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Filipino (<i>language</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Filipino (relating to the Philippines)</li></ol><p><i>Филиппи́ны</i> (Filippíny) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philistine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phylogenesis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Phylogenese</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phylogenetics</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Phylogenetik</i>, influenced by <i>гене́тика</i> (genɛ́tika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phylogenetic</li></ol><p><i>филогене́т(ика)</i> (filogenɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philologist (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>philological</li></ol><p><i>филоло́гия</i> (filológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slack off, to avoid work or study</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosopher</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosophaster (a pretender to philosophy; a petty or charlatan philosopher)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosophy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>philosophically</li></ol><p><i>филосо́фский</i> (filosófskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) philosopher; philosopher's, philosophers'</li><li>philosophic, philosophical</li></ol><p><i>фило́соф</i> (filósof) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosopher's stone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosophizing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to reason or reflect on philosophical themes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to philosophize</li></ol><p><i>филосо́фия</i> (filosófija) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фило́соф</i> (filósof).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phillumenist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philology department (in a university)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>филологи́ческий факульте́т</i> (filologíčeskij fakulʹtét, “Faculty of Philology”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film, movie</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>film</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horror movie, horror film (motion picture which horrifies or frightens)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filter (device for separating impurities from a fluid or other substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>filtre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filtration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(also&#32;figurative,&#32;physics) to filter</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>filtrer</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense</li></ol><p>from Ancient Greek <i>θῡμίαμα</i> (thūmíama, “incense for smoking”), from <i>θῡμιάω</i> (thūmiáō, “to burn incense, to burn, to smoke (something)”), ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰuh₂mós</i> (“smoke”) (cognate with Russian <i>дым</i> (dym, “smoke”), Lithuanian <i>dū́mas</i> (“smoke”), Latin <i>fūmus</i> (“smoke”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finale, ending, denouement</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) final</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) final (final part of a syllable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>final</li></ol><p><i>фина́л</i> (finál) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funding, financing (money provided as funds or the act of funding)</li></ol><p><i>финанси́ровать</i> (finansírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finance, to fund</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>finanzieren</i>, from French <i>financer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>financier</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) finance and industry; financial-industrial</li></ol><p><i>фина́нсовый</i> (finánsovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>промы́шленный</i> (promýšlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) finance and economics, financial-economic</li></ol><p><i>фина́нсовый</i> (finánsovyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>экономи́ческий</i> (ekonomíčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>financial</li></ol><p><i>фина́нсы</i> (finánsy) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finance (management of money and other assets, the monetary resources)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) black eye, shiner</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>date (fruit of the date palm)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix, “date”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenician</li></ol><p><i>Финики́я</i> (Finikíja) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) date (fruit of the date palm)</li></ol><p><i>фи́ник</i> (fínik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li><i>finita la commedia</i>, “the comedy is over”, (said when something abruptly ends or is cancelled)</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>finita la commedia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) finitary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enamelling (decorative application of vitreous enamel to metal&#59; especially in reference to traditional Russian art)</li><li>an enamel (an artwork or decorative piece produced using enamelling)</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) color (azure, gules, sable, vert, or purpure)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>финиптъ</i> (finiptŭ), <i>химипетъ</i> (ximipetŭ), from Byzantine Greek <i>χυμευτόν</i> (khumeutón, “enamel”), ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>χυμεία</i> (khumeía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) vitreous enamel</li></ol><p><i>фини́фт(ь)</i> (finíft(ʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-яный</i> (-janyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finish (end of race, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>finish</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to come in</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>фи́ниш</i> (fíniš) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) finish (<i>especially of a race</i>), finish line</li></ol><p><i>фи́ниш</i> (fíniš) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Finnish knife, puukko</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Finland, Finnish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Finn (male person from Finland)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>finne</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Finno-Ugric (of or relating to Finno-Ugric languages)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Finnish language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Finnish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a knife with a straight, fixed, single-edged blade; a "Finnish knife"; a puukko</li></ol><p>Literally, “Finnish knife”, referring to the puukko, the traditional Finnish utility knife. Unlike the typical puukko, the Russian <i>фи́нский нож</i> (fínskij nož) often had a guard and a clip point.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bagatelle, foofaraw</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) light-minded, frivolous girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine champagne (cognac blend)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fine champagne</i>, a clipping of <i>eau-de-vie fine de la Champagne</i> (“fine brandy from the Champagne region”). Compare Belarusian, Ukrainian <i>фіньшампа́нь</i> (finʹšampánʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple (color)</li><li>violet (color)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>violett</i>. Further from Latin <i>viola</i> (whence also <i>фиа́лка</i> (fiálka)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ро́зовый</i> (rózovyj) (&lt; <i>ро́за</i> (róza)), <i>лило́вый</i> (lilóvyj) (akin to <i>ли́лия</i> (lílija)). Also note <i>цвет</i> (cvet), <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>фьорд</i> (fʹord, “fjord”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фио́рд</i> (fiórd): small fjord</li></ol><p>From <i>фио́рд</i> (fiórd) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) alternative form of <i>фьо́рдовый</i> (fʹórdovyj, “fjord”)</li></ol><p>From <i>фио́рд</i> (fiórd) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firm, company</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>firme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) firm, company, brand; proprietary</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very good, high-quality</li></ol><p><i>фи́рма</i> (fírma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiscal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pistachio (nut)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فستق (fıstıq) (modern Turkish <i>fıstık</i>), from Persian فستق (fostoq, fostaq), from Arabic فِسْتَق (fistaq), of Iranian origin (compare Middle Persian <i>pstk'</i> (pistag)). The ending is influenced by French <i>pistache</i> and the diminutive suffix <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with English <i>pistachio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pistachio</li><li>pistachio green</li></ol><p><i>фиста́шка</i> (fistáška) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Pfiesteria</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Pfiesteria</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) feature (collaboration on a work of music)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>feat.</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fita (obsolete Cyrillic letter Ѳ, ѳ)</li><li>theta (modern Greek letter Θ (Th)&#47;θ (th))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѳита</i> (θita), from Ancient Greek <i>θῆτᾰ</i> (thêtă).</p><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>θήτα</i> (thíta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wick</li><li>fuse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>фитиль, фетиль, ѳитиль, ѳетиль</i> (fitilʹ, fetilʹ, fitilʹ, fetilʹ) (att. since 1610s in R. James' dictionary, also likely related pl. ветили in 1597), borrowed – probably in the context of firearms and artillery technology, where it displaced indigenous <i>жагра</i> (žagra, “tinder, especially a fungal one; match in a matchlock”) – from Ottoman Turkish فتیل (“wick; fuse”) (whence Turkish <i>fitil</i>) from Arabic فَتِيل (fatīl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wick, fuse</li></ol><p><i>фити́ль</i> (fitílʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) phytotoxin</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>фито-</i> (fito-) +&lrm; <i>токси́н</i> (toksín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phytotoxic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phytotoxic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;botany) phytochemistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) herbal tea</li></ol><p><i>фито-</i> (fito-, “phyto-”) +&lrm; <i>чай</i> (čaj, “tea”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) frivolous young woman; bimbo</li></ol><p><i>фи</i> (fi) +&lrm; <i>фи</i> (fi)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually as a predicative) fifty-fifty (equally split or involving equal likelihoods)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) not great, not very well, mediocre(ly), middling(ly)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) kind of, sort of, to some extent, a little, not much, not particularly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>skateboarding</i>) 50-50 grind</li><li>(<i>snowboarding</i>) 50-50 slide</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fifty-fifty</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;slang) feature (function in a piece of software)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;slang) feature (function, amenity)</li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>feature</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>games</i>) piece, counter, dib, marker, peg, token</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) invention, contrivance, device, (funny or special) idea</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) feature, peculiarity</li></ol><p>From French <i>fiche</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flag, banner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>vlag</i>. Influenced by English <i>flag</i> (as by spelling with ⟨ф⟩ instead of ⟨в⟩) so as not to clash with <i>влах</i> (vlax, “Vlach”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flag officer</li><li>ellipsis of <i>фла́гманский кора́бль</i> (flágmanskij koráblʹ): flagship, admiral (the ship which carries the admiral)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>vlagman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flagpole</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>vlaggestok</i> (“flagpole”), from <i>vlag</i> (“flag”) +&lrm; <i>stok</i> (“pole”). Influenced by English <i>flag</i> (as by spelling with ф instead of в) so as not to clash with влах (vlax, “Vlach”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small flag</li><li>(<i>graphical user interface</i>) checkbox</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) human flag</li></ol><p><i>флаг</i> (flag) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(small) bottle, vial, flask (<i>usually of perfume, shampoo, cosmetics, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>flacon</i>. Doublet of <i>фиа́ско</i> (fiásko) and <i>фля́жка</i> (fljážka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>флако́н</i> (flakón, “small bottle, vial”)</li></ol><p><i>флако́н</i> (flakón) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Flemish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flemish (the Dutch language as it is spoken in Flanders)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Ancient Rome) flamen (priest)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>flamen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flamingo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flank, wing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>flanc</i>, possibly through Dutch <i>flank</i> or German <i>Flanke</i>.</p><p>Attested since the time of Peter the Great.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flannel</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flannel, fustian (soft cloth material)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flange</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Flansch</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>příruba</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flaneur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>flâneur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;obsolete) phlegm</li><li>phlegm, coolness, apathy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) phlegmatic person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlegmatization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phlegmatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) flute (woodwind instrument)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Flöte</i>, possibly via Yiddish <i>פֿלייט</i> (fleyt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) piccolo</li></ol><p>From <i>фле́йта</i> (fléjta) +&lrm; <i>пи́кколо</i> (píkkolo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flutist, flautist</li></ol><p><i>фле́йта</i> (fléjta) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) rail-babbler (<i>Eupetes macrocerus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>флейти́ст</i> (flejtíst, “flautist”) +&lrm; <i>пастушо́к</i> (pastušók, “railbird”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(female)</i> flutist, flautist</li></ol><p><i>флейтист</i> (flejtist) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flute</li></ol><p><i>фле́йт(а)</i> (fléjt(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat <i>(wall)</i> brush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flexagon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inflectional</li></ol><p><i>фле́ксия</i> (fléksija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flex one's muscles</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to flex (flaunt one's superiority or show off)</li></ol><p>From English <i>flex</i> +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflectional ending, inflectional affix, inflection</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) flexion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flerovium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) flush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flash memory</li></ol><p>Partial calque of English <i>flash memory</i>, English <i>flash</i> +&lrm; <i>память</i> (pamjatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) royal flush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;computer hardware) USB memory stick</li></ol><p>From English <i>flash</i> (<i>as in flash memory</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>флэшмо́б</i> (flɛšmób)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filibuster, buccaneer (pirate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>flibustier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) wing (extension of a building); by extension, a secondary (possibly detached) dwelling located on an estate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Flügel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flirt (act of flirting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>flirt</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flirt (to play at courtship)</li></ol><p><i>флирт</i> (flirt) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlogiston</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felt-tip pen</li></ol><p>From Flo-Master, a brand of felt-tip pens.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;computing) flop (floating-point operations per second)</li></ol><p>From <i>флоп</i> (flop, “flop”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>FLOP</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) flop (floating-point operations per second&#59; a unit of measure of processor speed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>FLOPS</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncommon form of <i>фло́пный</i> (flópnyj)</li></ol><p>From <i>флопс</i> (flops, “flop”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from English <i>FLOPS</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flora</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floorball (hockey-like ball game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>floorball</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleet, naval fleet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>vloot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flotilla, small fleet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) navy; naval</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sailor</li></ol><p><i>флот</i> (flot) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) a flood of superfluous text messages</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>flood</i>. Compare Portuguese <i>flood</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) to flood (post meaningless streams of information)</li></ol><p><i>флуд</i> (flud) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an overnight house party</li></ol><p>From Borrowed from English <i>flat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flash mob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>flash mob</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vane, weather vane, windvane, weathercock</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) weathercock, opportunist, timeserver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from either Dutch <i>vleugel</i> (“wing”) or Swedish <i>flöjel</i> (“weather vane”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) feathering (of a propeller)</li></ol><p><i>флюги́ровать</i> (fljugírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) to feather (a propeller)</li></ol><p><i>флю́гер</i> (fljúger, “weathervane”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluid (especially in biology)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canteen (water bottle)</li><li>can (large cylindrical aluminum container with lid or cover, intended to hold liquids)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>фля́жка</i> (fljážka) (as if it were a diminutive), older <i>фляшка</i> (fljaška), borrowed from a Germanic language, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaskǭ</i>. Compare Old High German <i>flasca</i> (Modern German <i>Flasche</i>), English <i>flask</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flask, hip flask (container for a small amount of beverage)</li></ol><p>From older <i>фляшка</i> (fljaška), borrowed from a Germanic language, from Old High German <i>flasca</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;flaskā</i>, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaskǭ</i>. Compare Old High German <i>flasca</i> (Modern German <i>Flasche</i>), English <i>flask</i>. Doublet of <i>флако́н</i> (flakón) and <i>фиа́ско</i> (fiásko).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a soft, sheer fabric</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) illusion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) fleur-de-lis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fleur de lis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orange-flower-water</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incorrect movement, step</li></ol><p>Unadapted borrowing from French <i>faux pas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) phobia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fauvism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobby, foyer (<i>in a theater, cinema, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>foyer</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>focus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>focalism (revolutionary strategy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>foquismo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox terrier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fox terrier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxtrot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>foxtrot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hocus-pocus, trick, sleight of hand</li><li>freak, whim</li><li>focus (in optics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hocus-pocus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juggler, conjurer; magician; prestidigitator</li></ol><p><i>фо́кус</i> (fókus) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) foul</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>foul</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folio</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) large book, tome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foil (thin sheet of metal)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>folia</i>, <i>folga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) foil (a thin sheet of metal)</li></ol><p><i>фольг(а́)</i> (folʹg(á)) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folklore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>folklore</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>folk, folkloric</li></ol><p><i>фолькло́р</i> (folʹklór) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volkswagen car</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Volkswagen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>background, backdrop</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) background radiation</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) phon (logarithmic unit of loudness level)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fon people of Benin and Nigeria</li><li>Fon language</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>von (particle used in certain German names indicating a noble, aristocratic origin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fond</i> via German <i>Fond</i>. Further from Latin <i>fundus</i> (whence also <i>фунда́мент</i> (fundáment)). Doublet of <i>фонд</i> (fond).</p><p>From Greek <i>φωνή</i> (foní, “voice, sound”).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>von</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to lose one's mind, to go nuts</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to get very surprised</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фона́рь</i> (fonárʹ): (small) lantern, (small) flashlight</li><li>torch, flashlight; headlamp, handlamp</li></ol><p>From <i>фона́рь</i> (fonárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>фона́рь</i> (fonárʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lantern, lamp</li></ol><p><i>фона́рь</i> (fonárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) lamplighter</li></ol><p><i>фона́рь</i> (fonárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lantern, streetlight, streetlamp</li><li>flashlight, electric torch, pocket lamp</li><li>taillight, taillamp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shiner, black eye</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) canopy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>φανάριον</i> (phanárion, “lamp, torch”). Suffix influenced by <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ). Compare Modern Greek <i>φανάρι</i> (fanári).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fund</li><li>stock</li><li>fund, collection</li><li>foundation (endowed institution or charity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fonds</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stock exchange</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fund</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) stock</li></ol><p><i>фонд</i> (fond) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fondue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fondue</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>fundō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰewd-</i>, extended from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phoneme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phonetics</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>φωνητικός</i> (phōnētikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phonetic</li></ol><p><i>фоне́т(ика)</i> (fonɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) phonk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>phonk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) background</li><li>native (<i>of species</i>)</li></ol><p><i>фон</i> (fon) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phonogram, sound recording, soundtrack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phonograph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>phonograph</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) phonologist</li></ol><p>From <i>фоно-</i> (fono-, “phono-”) +&lrm; <i>-лог</i> (-log, “-logist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phonological</li></ol><p>From <i>фоноло́гия</i> (fonológija, “phonology”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phonology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Phonologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) phonon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(typography,&#32;colloquial) font</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fountain</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>fontāna</i>, further from Latin <i>fons</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Bulgarian <i>водоскок</i> (vodoskok), Polish <i>wodotrysk</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фонта́н</i> (fontán)</li></ol><p><i>фонта́н</i> (fontán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head start, odds</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fora</i>, a variant of <i>fuori</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>microbiology</i>) foraminifer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Foraminifera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) forward (player nearest to the opposing soccer team)</li><li>(finance,&#32;economics) forward</li><li>(computing,&#32;email) forward</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>forward</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trout</li></ol><p>From German <i>Forelle</i>, possibly through Dutch <i>forel</i>. Ultimately from Old High German <i>forhana</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) forint</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hungarian <i>forint</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, shape</li><li>form (document to be filled)</li><li>form, structure, frame</li><li>model</li><li>mold</li><li>uniform (military, sports)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>forme</i>, from Latin <i>fōrma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formalization</li></ol><p>From <i>формализова́ть</i> (formalizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija) or from <i>формализи́ровать</i> (formalizírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formalism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to formalise (to make formal)</li></ol><p>German <i>formalisier(en)</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>форма́льный</i> (formálʹnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>форма́льно</i> (formálʹno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>formally (in a formal manner)</li><li>technically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formality</li></ol><p><i>форма́льный</i> (formálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistic morphology</i>) formative (language unit, typically a morph, that has a morphological function)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a formant (physics,&#32;phonetics), a band of frequencies, in a sound spectrum, that have a greater intensity; they determine the quality of a sound; especially the characteristic sounds of the consonants.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formant, related to formants</li></ol><p><i>форма́нта</i> (formánta, “formant”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>size, format</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formatting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to format</li></ol><p><i>форма́т</i> (formát) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;economics) formation, structure</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) formation</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>formatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) uniform</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) regular, downright</li></ol><p><i>фо́рма</i> (fórma) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formation, forming, generation, putting together</li><li>(<i>military</i>) unit, formation</li></ol><p><i>формирова́ть</i> (formirovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, to shape, to mould, to make up</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form, to mold</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фо́рма</i> (fórma)</li><li>cookie cutter (a kitchen utensil used to cut flattened dough into shapes before baking)</li></ol><p><i>фо́рмочка</i> (fórmočka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;chemistry,&#32;logic) formula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fōrmula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formulation</li></ol><p><i>формули́ровать</i> (formulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to formulate, to phrase, to word, to lay down (rules, demands, etc.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>formulating, formulation, verbalizing</li><li>formula, statement, wording (presentation of opinion or position)</li></ol><p><i>формули́ровать</i> (formulírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form (document to be filled)</li><li>logbook</li><li>record of service</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Formular</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) phorophyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;also&#32;figuratively) advanced post, outpost</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Vorposten</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of God, force majeure (an unforeseen and unavoidable circumstance preventing someone from fulfilling their obligations)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>force majeure</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) afterburner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>forçage</i> (“forcing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;aviation) afterburner</li></ol><p><i>форса́ж</i> (forsáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forcing</li><li>amplification, acceleration, boosting</li></ol><p><i>форси́ровать</i> (forsírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to force, to speed up</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to force a crossing <i>(over a river)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>forcieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ); see also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show off; to swank</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to boast; to brag; to put on airs</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to aggressively push or promote (something) in an artificial manner in order to generate forced hype, especially online</li></ol><p>From <i>форс</i> (fors, “chic; stylishness; showing off”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ), from French <i>force</i>.</p><p>From English <i>force</i> +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fort (fortified defensive structure stationed with troops)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fort</i>. Further from Latin <i>fortis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) forte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>forte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) piano</li></ol><p><i>фортепиа́но</i> (fortɛpiáno) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piano</li><li>fortepiano; pianoforte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fortepiano</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>фортепиа́но</i> (fortɛpiáno)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>fortepiano</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortochka (a small ventilation window spanning the frame of one window pane, opening independently of the whole window)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pförtchen</i> (“little door”), diminutive of <i>Pforte</i> (“gate”), an ancient borrowing from Latin <i>porta</i> (“gate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) a thief who burglarizes houses by breaking in through windows</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortune (good luck, fate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fortūna</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) forum</li><li>(<i>Internet</i>) forum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>forum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forechecking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>forechecking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vorschmack (a dish made of minced meat, onion, water, anchovies and&#47;or herring)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something bad, rotten, poorly made</li><li>(prison slang,&#32;offensive) punk, prison bitch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>פֿאָרשמאַק</i> (forshmak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>naval</i>) wooden or steel beam in the bow of the ship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>voorsteven</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phosphate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) phosphide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phosphorus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φωσφόρος</i> (phōsphóros, “the bearer of light”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phosphorylation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>phosphorescent</li></ol><p><i>фо́сфор</i> (fósfor) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) phosphorus; phosphoric</li></ol><p><i>фо́сфор</i> (fósfor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to photograph, to take a photo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) photo</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photo, photograph</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>фотогра́фия</i> (fotográfija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>photo- (light)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;chemistry) photoactivation</li></ol><p><i>фото-</i> (foto-) +&lrm; <i>актива́ция</i> (aktivácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photo album</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>photo album</i>, from <i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>альбо́м</i> (alʹbóm).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>still camera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fotoapparat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photopaper (paper for photographic prints)</li></ol><p><i>фо́то-</i> (fóto-) +&lrm; <i>бума́га</i> (bumága)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>photogenic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photographer (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photography, photographing</li></ol><p><i>фотографи́ровать</i> (fotografírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to photograph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be photographed, to have one's photo/picture taken</li></ol><p><i>фотографи́ровать</i> (fotografírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>photographic</li></ol><p><i>фотогра́ф(ия)</i> (fotográf(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photograph</li><li>photography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photodiode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;neologism,&#32;humorous) an image, a (photoshopped) caricature of a person or an event to make someone or something look comical</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto, “photo”) +&lrm; <i>жа́ба</i> (žába, “toad”). From a humorous modification of <i>фотошо́п</i> (fotošóp, “photoshop”) first as <i>фотожо́п(а)</i> (fotožóp(a), “photoass”), alluding to the expression <i>че́рез жо́пу</i> (čérez žópu, “in a botched manner”), and then euphemistically as the present form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;vulgar,&#32;humorous,&#32;neologism) an image, a (photoshopped) caricature of a person or an event to make someone or something look comical</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto, “photo”) +&lrm; <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “ass”). From a humorous modification of <i>фотошо́п</i> (fotošóp, “photoshop”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar) to photoshop</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>фотошо́пить</i> (fotošópitʹ, “to photoshop”) +&lrm; <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “arse”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camera</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ка́мера</i> (kámera)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photograph, photo, picture</li></ol><p><i>фо́то-</i> (fóto-) +&lrm; <i>ка́рточка</i> (kártočka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photocopy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>press photographer</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>корреспонде́нт</i> (korrespondént)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>model who poses for photographs; fashion model</li><li>model hired to show items or goods to the public</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) beautiful woman (<i>particularly one who corresponds to a particular standard of beauty</i>)</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>моде́ль</i> (modɛ́lʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) photon</li><li>(<i>astronautics</i>) Foton (Soviet and Russian science satellites)</li><li>(<i>video games</i>) Photon (Soviet arcade cabinet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(wallpaper) mural, photo wallpaper</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto, “photo”) +&lrm; <i>обо́и</i> (obói, “wallpaper”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) photooxidation (reaction with oxygen in the presence of light)</li></ol><p><i>фото-</i> (foto-) +&lrm; <i>окисле́ние</i> (okislénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wildlife photography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photographic film</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) camera roll</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плёнка</i> (pljónka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gun camera, guncam</li></ol><p><i>фо́то-</i> (fóto-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пулемёт</i> (pulemjót)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picture frame</li></ol><p><i>фо́то</i> (fóto) +&lrm; <i>ра́мка</i> (rámka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) photorealistic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>identikit (facial composite)</li></ol><p><i>фо́то-</i> (fóto-) +&lrm; <i>ро́бот</i> (róbot)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photo shoot, photo session</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) photosynthesis (biological process)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snapshot, photograph</li></ol><p><i>фото-</i> (foto-) +&lrm; <i>сни́мок</i> (snímok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially&#32;law</i>) photographic evidence</li></ol><p><i>фото-</i> (foto-, “photographic”) +&lrm; <i>ули́ка</i> (ulíka, “evidence, proof”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>photofluorography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial,&#32;Internet slang) to photoshop</li></ol><p>From English <i>photoshop</i> +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an oriental precious cloth worn by royalty in the Byzantine Empire and medieval East Slavs</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a name for warm outerwear, later known as фуфайка (fufajka)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;Ukraine) extreme Orthodox Christian views; especially supporting Russian ultranationalism</li></ol><p>Derived from Old East Slavic <i>фофудиꙗ</i> (fofudija), borrowed from Greek <i>φουφουδότης</i> (foufoudótis).</p><p>Sense 3 was first used by Ukrainian online users in 2006 to make fun of Russian ultranationalists, along with жидобанде́ровец (židobandérovec, “Jewish Banderite”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fr, franc</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>франк</i> (frank).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li><li>excerpt, snippet, clip, passage, section</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fragmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>marionology</i>) a successful drop into tares</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) not a criminal person, a potential victim of a crime, any person (male); a person criminals may consider as deserving of respect, even if he doesn't belong to their group.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) an unpleasant person, e.g. someone who behaves arrogantly, spoils a party, etc.</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) A naive or inexperienced person, a sucker, mug, patsy or sap; one who accedes to all requests, and is easily taken advantage of.</li></ol><p>There are a number of theories about the origin of this term:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sentence, phrase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φράσις</i> (phrásis), via German <i>Phrase</i> or French <i>phrase</i> in the 17th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) phraseological unit, phraseme, set expression, set phrase, idiom</li></ol><p>Compare English <i>phraseologism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phraseology (study of set or fixed expressions)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фраза</i> (fraza): sentence, phrase</li></ol><p><i>фра́за</i> (fráza) +&lrm; <i>-очка</i> (-očka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Freikorps</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Freikorps</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tailcoat (formal evening jacket)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Frack</i> or French <i>frac</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fractal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faction (<i>inside a political party</i>)</li><li>group (<i>in a parliament</i>)</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) fraction</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fractio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) franc</li><li>Frank (member of a Germanic tribe)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Franco-, French (of France)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Frankfurt am Main</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Frankfurt an der Oder</li></ol><p><i>Фра́нкфурт</i> (Fránkfurt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandy, fop, beau</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>frant</i> (“rogue, clown, comedian”), ultimately from a diminutive of Czech <i>František</i> (given name).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>francium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French, Frenchman</li><li>(dated,&#32;regional,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;colloquial) A Jew.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian, from Old Ruthenian, from Polish <i>Francuz</i>, from Middle High German <i>franzôs</i>, Middle Low German <i>franzôs</i> (“French; Frenchman”), from Old French <i>franc̨ois</i> with dialectal <i>o</i> &lt; <i>oi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave in the French manner; to speak French</li></ol><p><i>францу́з</i> (francúz) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>French</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French language</li></ol><p><i>францу́з</i> (francúz) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French kiss</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;rare,&#32;erroneous) oral sex; blowjob, cunnilingus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>францу́зский</i> (francúzskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French people, the French</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>franchising (<i>the establishment, granting, or use of a franchise</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>franchising</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) franchise (authorization granted by a company to sell or distribute its goods or services in a certain area)</li><li>(<i>insurance</i>) excess (UK), deductible (US) (amount a claimant must contribute in order to be compensated)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>franchise</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Germany,&#32;Austria,&#32;Switzerland,&#32;etc.) madam; Mrs.; frau</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Frau</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freight</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cargo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fracht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chartering, affreightment (hire a vessel for cargo)</li><li>freightage</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) frigate</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) frigatebird</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>frégate</i>, itself from Italian <i>fregata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>woodworking</i>) router</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) machine for soil cultivation</li><li>milling machine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fräser</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Freudianism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Freudian</li></ol><p><i>Фрейд</i> (Frejd) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fracking</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fracking</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service jacket (single-breasted jacket with a collar stand and four external pockets)</li></ol><p>Named after Field Marshal John Denton French, British officer during the Second Boer War and First World War.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fresco</li></ol><p>Several steps omitting from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;friscum</i>, further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;friskaz</i> (whence also English <i>fresh</i>, <i>frisk</i>). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;prěsnъ</i> (whence <i>пре́сный</i> (présnyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frivolity</li></ol><p><i>фриво́льный</i> (frivólʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frivolous</li><li>immodest</li></ol><p>French <i>frivole</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freeganism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>freeganism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frigid (sexually unresponsive, especially of a woman)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frisian</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) frieze</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) hang (in the sense that the device stops responding to manual input)</li></ol><p>From Old Frisian <i>Frēsa</i>, <i>Frīsa</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frisaz</i> (“curly, frizzy”).</p><p>Borrowed from French <i>frise</i> (“frieze”), from French <i>friser</i> (“to curl”), from Old Frisian <i>frisle</i> (“curly hair”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frisaz</i> (“curly, frizzy”).</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>freeze</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frieze (a kind of cloth)</li><li>(architecture,&#32;relational) frieze</li></ol><p>From <i>фриз</i> (friz) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freak (person who is extremely abnormal in appearance, social behavior, sexual orientation, gender identity, and&#47;or business practices)</li><li>freak (enthusiast, or person who has an obsession with, or extreme knowledge of, something)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) wingnut; extremist; oddball (person with fringe or marginal views)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>freak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meatball (especially in a soup)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Frikadelle</i> or French <i>fricadelle</i>, from Italian <i>frittadella</i>. Further from Latin <i>frīgō</i> (whence also English <i>fry</i>). Analyzable as German <i>Frikadelle</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) fricative</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) freestyle skiing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) A medium or substance used for frying (e.g. fat or oil)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>friture</i> (“frying, medium for frying, fritter”), ultimately from Late Latin <i>&#42;frīctūra</i> (“frying”), from Latin <i>frīgō</i> (“to fry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deep fryer (vessel for deep frying)</li></ol><p>French <i>friture</i> +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фритю́р</i> (fritjúr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Friulian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friulian (Romance language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a German person, Fritz, often in the World War 2 context</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fritz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;obsolete) breakfast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Frühstück</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>German-speaking countries</i>) fraulein, Miss</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fräulein</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a participant of the Fronde</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;literary) frondeur, malcontent, rebel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>frondeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) front (large operational military command that controls several armies&#59; equivalent to U.S. army group)</li><li>battle front, front line</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Front</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>frōns, frontis</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) front camera, front-facing camera (on a device with multiple-facing cameras, such as a smartphone)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) front-loading washing machine</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) folding knife, jack-knife (with the blade extending from the front of the handle)</li></ol><p><i>фронта́льный</i> (frontálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(general,&#32;military,&#32;meteorology) frontal</li><li>general</li></ol><p>French <i>frontal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) front man (the lead singer in a musical group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>front man</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>фронтме́н</i> (frontmén).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front-line soldier</li></ol><p><i>фронт</i> (front) +&lrm; <i>-ови́к</i> (-ovík).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military&#32;or&#32;meteorology) front</li><li>frontline</li></ol><p><i>фронт</i> (front) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) pediment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fronton</i>, from Italian <i>frontone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) fruit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>frukt</i>, from Latin <i>frūctus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fruit</li></ol><p><i>фрукт</i> (frukt) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fructose</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Frunze (referring to anyone with this name, especially Mikhail Frunze, Bolshevik military leader)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Frunze (former name of Bishkek, capital of Kyrgyzstan)</li></ol><p><i>Фру́нзе</i> (Frúnzɛ) +&lrm; <i>-енский</i> (-enskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) phthisiology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>φθίσις</i> (phthísis, “consumption”) + <i>ἰατρεία</i> (iatreía, “healing”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluorine</li></ol><p>From French <i>phthore</i> (an obsolete early synonym of <i>fluor</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>φθορά</i> (phthorá, “destruction, deterioration”), because of the corrosive action of hydrofluoric acid.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) fluoropolymer (any of many chemically and thermally stable polymers that are fluorine derivatives)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>pew!, ew!, yuck! <i>(expresses disgust)</i></li><li>phew! (<i>shows relief</i>)</li><li><i>(a command for a dog)</i> stop that!, don't do that!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>pew!, P U!, pee-you! (it stinks!)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foie gras (fattened liver of geese or ducks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>foie gras</i> (literally “fat liver”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) fugue</li><li>clearance necessary for unobstructed movement of furniture parts</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fuge</i> or directly from Italian <i>fuga</i>, from Latin <i>fuga</i> (“flight”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) fougasse</li><li>(<i>military</i>) field explosive, explosive charge (<i>planted in the ground or underwater</i>), mine</li><li>(military,&#32;informal) a high explosive munition (<i>of artillery shells, bombs, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fougasse</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>focus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highly explosive (of bombs, shells)</li></ol><p><i>фуга́с</i> (fugás) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fugu, blowfish (a delicacy popular in Japan served raw as sushi that may, if improperly prepared, contain deadly levels of neurotoxins)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>フグ</i> (fugu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food court</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>food court</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tall wine glass</li></ol><p>Late 19th century after French <i>verre de fougère</i>, a certain quality grade of wine glasses, made from <i>fougère</i> (“fern”) ash, manufactured particularly in the town <i>Fougères</i> having its name from abundance in fern and later a glassware industry by reason of this fern presence.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuchsin, rosein</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuchsia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) alternative form of <i>фулл-ха́ус</i> (full-xáus, “full house”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>full house</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) full house</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>full house</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation, base, basis (that upon which anything is founded)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>fundāmentum</i> (“foundation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(finance,&#32;politics,&#32;religion) fundamentalism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fundamentalism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fundamentalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) fundamental interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid, fundamental, thorough, substantial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelnut, hazel (nut)</li><li>filbert</li></ol><p>Initially (in the 19th century) denoting filbert (<i>Corylus maxima</i> syn. <i>C. tubulosa</i>) cultivated in subtropics of South Crimea, in the 20th century it began to be used interchangeably with Slavic <i>лещи́на</i> (leščína) (common hazel, <i>Corylus avellana</i>) and by the 21st century became more common.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funicular</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>funiculaire</i>. Further from Latin <i>fūniculus</i> + <i>-ārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) functional (a function that takes a function as its argument)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;computing,&#32;slang) higher-order function (function that takes one or more functions as an argument)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;computing,&#32;slang,&#32;collective) functionality (set of functions or capabilities of a piece of software)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;slang,&#32;collective) functions (list of duties performed by an employee)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) functionally</li></ol><p><i>функциона́льный</i> (funkcionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>functionality</li></ol><p><i>функциона́льный</i> (funkcionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;medicine,&#32;architecture) functional</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) functional (useful, serving a purpose)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) functional (based on function)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apparatchik (disapprovingly&#58; blindly loyal bureaucrat)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>functioning, operation, operating</li></ol><p><i>функциони́ровать</i> (funkcionírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to function (to carry on a function)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>function</li><li>duty</li><li>purpose, role</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pound (409.51241 grams)</li><li>English pound (453.59237 grams)</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) pound</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Pfund</i> (“pound”). Doublet of <i>пуд</i> (pud).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) pound sterling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellophane noodles, glass noodles</li><li>funchoza, cellophane noodle salad (a Koryo-saram variant of japchae which is served cold)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dungan <i>фынтёзы</i> (fɨnti͡ozɨ); compare Mandarin <i>粉條子</i>&nbsp;/ <i>粉条子</i> (fěntiáozi), Mongolian <i>пүнтүүз</i> (püntüüz).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) large, long cart</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) wagon</li><li>(<i>somewhat&#32;dated</i>) van</li><li>semi-trailer truck, heavy vehicle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Fuhre</i> (“cart”).</p><p>Noncognate with Russian <i>фурго́н</i> (furgón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fodder (food for animals)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fourrage</i>, from Old French <i>fourage</i>, from Old French <i>fuerre</i> + <i>-age</i>. Further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fōdrą</i> + Latin <i>-āticum</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>British</i>) peaked cap, combination cap, forage cap</li><li>(<i>US</i>) service cap, wheel cap, combination cover</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>forage cap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portable loading room, van, caravan, wagon, waggon, camion (a covered vehicle used for carrying goods)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fourgon</i>. Further from Latin <i>fūror</i>, from Latin <i>fūr</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (whence Russian <i>брать</i> (bratʹ)).</p><p>Noncognate with Russian <i>фу́ра</i> (fúra) (which is <i>паро́м</i> (paróm) related instead).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Erinys</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Fury</li><li>hag, virago, shrew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small britchka or a kind of tachanka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an auxiliary material used in a production; fitting</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fourniture</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensation, splash (<i>usually following a verb such as "to make", "to cause"</i>)</li><li>jubilation, uproar</li><li>furor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fureur</i> or Italian <i>furore</i>. Sense "(to make) a splash", "(to cause) a sensation" is a calque of French <i>faire fureur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furry (anthropomorphic animal; a member of the furry fandom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>furry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a fursuit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fursuit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boil (on skin)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>furunculus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) furfural</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reception with light refreshments eaten standing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fourchette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to work, to function</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>фурча́ть</i> (furčátʹ), further akin to <i>фы́ркать</i> (fýrkatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fusha, Modern Standard Arabic, Classical Arabic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic الْفُصْحَى (al-fuṣḥā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot (unit of length)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>foot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>football (the game, UK English), soccer (<i>US English</i>)</li><li>A football / soccer match</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>football</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footballer (<i>British</i>); football player (<i>Britain</i>), soccer player (<i>US, Canada, Australia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>футбо́л</i> (futból, “football”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”). Compare Ukrainian <i>футболі́ст</i> (futbolíst), Belarusian <i>футбалі́ст</i> (futbalíst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>T-shirt (type of shirt)</li></ol><p><i>футбо́л</i> (futból, “football, soccer”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) football, soccer</li></ol><p><i>футбо́л</i> (futból) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>case, sheath, étui (protective container)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Futteral</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futomaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>太巻き</i> (futomaki, literally “fat roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>布団</i> (ふとん, futon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futurism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>futurist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweatshirt, sweater, jersey</li><li>quilted jacket, fufayka, vatnik</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trash-talk, wass</li><li>bowsy, disorderly person</li><li>low-quality ware, lemon</li></ol><p>Thieves’ cant. Perhaps after Bulgarian <i>фъ́фля</i> (fǎ́flja, “to mumble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>phew (expression of relief)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) Fujianese person</li></ol><p>From <i>Фуцзя́нь</i> (Fuczjánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Fujian; Fujianese</li></ol><p>From <i>Фуцзя́нь</i> (Fuczjánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snort, to sniff</li><li>to sniff scornfully, to scoff, to grouse, to grumble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuckle, to chortle, to snicker</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snort, to sniff</li><li>to sniff scornfully, to scoff, to grouse, to grumble</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chuckle, to chortle, to snicker</li></ol><p><i>фы́ркать</i> (fýrkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) fjord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>фьорд</i> (fʹord): small fjord</li></ol><p>From <i>фьорд</i> (fʹord) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fjord</li></ol><p>From <i>фьорд</i> (fʹord) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>whew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) futures, futures contract</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>futures</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>фан</i> (fan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) fandom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fandom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fantasy (literary genre)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fantasy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fengshui</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 風水／风水 (<i>fēngshuǐ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatphobia (fear or dislike of obese people)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>fatphobia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fuselage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>fuselage</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>fūsus</i>. Compare Russian <i>веретенообра́зный</i> (veretenoobráznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Führer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Führer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>хуйло</i> (xujlo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>хуесос</i> (xujesos)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>хуй</i> (xuj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>хуйло</i> (xujlo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;euphemistic,&#32;slang) the word "хохо́л (xoxól)" (an offensive slur against Ukrainian people).</li><li>(dysphemistic,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) a Ukrainian person.</li></ol><p><i>х</i> (x) +&lrm; <i>сло́во</i> (slóvo), literally meaning "x-word". Compare n-word, which hides a racist slur.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Х, х.</li><li>The Roman letter H, h, more often called аш (aš)</li><li>The German letter H, h.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ha-ha</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ha ha (sound of laughter)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;transport) hub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hub</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khabarovsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Хаба́ров(ск)</i> (Xabárov(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to eat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to accept, to enjoy, to believe</li></ol><p>Derived from Romani <i>мэ хава</i> (“I eat”). Per another version, inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) food</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hajj</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic source (compare Bashkir <i>хаж</i> (xaj), Turkish <i>hac</i>), from Arabic حَجّ (ḥajj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) hadith</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حَدِيث (ḥadīṯ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) long hair on men <i>(especially rock music fans)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hair</i> (into 1960s hippie slang).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go somewhere, to visit (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); frequentative of <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ)</li></ol><p><i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивать</i> (-ivatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) hideout</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khazar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khazar (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high humanitarian technologies</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hi-hume</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haida (indigenous nation)</li><li>Haida (indigenous language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haiku</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>俳<rp>(</rp><rt>はい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby><ruby>句<rp>(</rp><rt>く</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>haiku</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang) hyped, hyped up</li></ol><p>From <i>хайп</i> (xajp, “hype”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang) clout chaser, media whore</li></ol><p>From <i>хайп</i> (xajp, “hype”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-жор</i> (-žor), which is related to <i>жрать</i> (žratʹ, “to eat”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khakas (by ethnicity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Khakas</li></ol><p><i>Хака́сия</i> (Xakásija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) hacker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hacker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khaki (color)</li><li>khaki (clothing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>khaki</i>, from Hindustani <i>ख़ाकी</i> (xākī) / خاکی (xākī, “earthy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hakka (people)</li><li>Hakka (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>challah, twist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>חלה</i> (khalá), probably by way of Yiddish <i>חלה</i> (khale).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jalapeño</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>jalapeño</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dressing gown, bathrobe (for men or women)</li><li>dress (any kind that is knee-length to full and that opens down the front)</li><li>smock</li><li>surgical gown</li><li>khalat</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. Compare Ottoman Turkish خلعت (hilat, “kaftan”), Azerbaijani <i>xələt</i> (“dressing gown”), Uzbek <i>xalat</i> (“robe”). Ultimately from Arabic خِلْعَة (ḵilʕa, “robe of honor”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хала́т</i> (xalát)</li></ol><p><i>хала́т</i> (xalát) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>negligently, carelessly</li></ol><p><i>хала́тный</i> (xalátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carelessness, unconcern, negligence</li></ol><p><i>хала́тный</i> (xalátnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bathrobe</li><li>negligent, careless</li></ol><p><i>хала́т</i> (xalát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>спустя́ рукава́</i> (spustjá rukavá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halva</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish حلوا (halva, helva), from Arabic حَلْوَى (ḥalwā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caliph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caliphate</li></ol><p>From <i>халиф</i> (xalif) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at), ultimately from Arabic خَلِيفَة (ḵalīfa, “successor”) + Latin <i>-ātus</i> (“-ate”). Compare Belarusian <i>халіфат</i> (xalifat), Bulgarian <i>халифат</i> (halifat), Ukrainian <i>халіфа́т</i> (xalifát).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caliphal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halloumi (a traditional cheese from Cyprus, made from goat's and/or sheep's milk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;now largely unknown) funeral banquet, extra-treats in connection to someone being interred</li><li>(informal,&#32;slang) yields acquired or work performed irregularly, job of imperfect compliance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>undeclared work, black market job, supplementary contract, moonlighting, participation in informal economy, nixer; khaltura</li><li>shoddy work (something intentionally done poorly), sloppy or half-assed job; hack; a botch; a pot boiler; khaltura</li></ol></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>chartulārius</i>, relating to a chartulary (<i>хартула́рий</i> (xartulárij) privy to the imparting of additional earnings. Cognate to Ukrainian <i>хавту́ра</i> (xavtúra), <i>хавту́р</i> (xavtúr, “gift to clerics in kind; gift; graft, palm-oil”), Belarusian <i>хаўту́ры</i> (xawtúry, “funeral”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intentionally do something badly or poorly, to half-ass</li><li>to moonlight</li></ol><p>From <i>халту́ра</i> (xaltúra) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who does shoddy work, bungler</li></ol><p><i>халту́ра</i> (xaltúra) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hovel, shack, hut, shanty</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xalupa</i>. Further etymology unknown. Compare Ukrainian <i>халу́па</i> (xalúpa), Belarusian <i>халу́па</i> (xalúpa), Polish <i>chałupa</i>, Czech <i>chalupa</i>, Slovak <i>chalupa</i>, Upper Sorbian <i>chałupa</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>chałupa</i>. The connection to Ancient Greek <i>κᾰλῠ́βη</i> (kălŭ́bē) and Ottoman Turkish قولبه (kulübe, kulıba) is not confirmed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>халу́па</i> (xalúpa, “shack”): (small) shack</li></ol><p><i>халу́па</i> (xalúpa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khalkha</li></ol><p>From <i>халха́</i> (xalxá) or <i>Халха́</i> (Xalxá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bootleg, top of the boot</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) freebie</li></ol><p>For "top of the boot" meaning: Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoľeva</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>халя́ва</i> (xaljáva), Polish <i>cholewa</i>. Note also Ossetian <i>хӕлаф</i> (xælaf, “trousers”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) free, freebie</li></ol><p><i>халя́ва</i> (xaljáva, “freebie, bootleg”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) any halal act; any act permissible in Islam</li><li>(Islam,&#32;food) Fit to eat according to Muslim religious customs.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حَلَال (ḥalāl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) halal</li></ol><p>From <i>халя́ль</i> (xaljálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Arabic حَلَال (ḥalāl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) boor, cad, asshole (a сrass, impudent, rude man)</li></ol><p>From Хам (Xam, “Ham”), referring to Ham's disrespect towards his father Noah described in Genesis 9:20–23.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Хам)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ham (a son of Noah and the brother of Japheth and Shem)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chameleon (reptile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become boorish, rude</li></ol><p><i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be rude <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p><i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) uncultured person, boor, cad</li></ol><p><i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-ло́</i> (-ló)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammam, Turkish bath (a public bath in various Islamic countries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حَمَّام (ḥammām).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jamon (thigh of a hog cured for food, Spanish style)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>jamón</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European anchovy, Azov anchovy (small saltwater fish)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably from the Ottoman Turkish term that later developed into Turkish <i>hamsi</i> (dialectally <i>hamsa</i>). Also related to Azerbaijani <i>xəmsi</i>, <i>xəmsə</i>.</p><p>Further related forms include Greek <i>χαμψί</i> (champsí), <i>χαψί</i> (chapsí) (plural <i>χαμψιά</i> (champsiá), <i>χαψιά</i> (chapsiá)), Abkhaz <i>ақамшьи́а</i> (akamšía), Laz <i>ქოფსია</i> (kopsia), Mingrelian <i>ხამსა</i> (xamsa) and Georgian <i>ქაფშია</i> (kapšia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khamsin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rude, impudent</li></ol><p><i>хам</i> (xam, “boor”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boorishness, rudeness, loutishness; chutzpah</li></ol><p>From <i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo). Compare Ukrainian <i>ха́мство</i> (xámstvo), Belarusian <i>ха́мства</i> (xámstva), Polish <i>chamstwo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(collective,&#32;colloquial) uncultured persons, boors, cads</li></ol><p>From <i>хам</i> (xam) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khan</li></ol><p>From a Mongolic source, compare Mongolian <i>хаан</i> (xaan), ultimately from the same Turkic source as <i>кага́н</i> (kagán).</p><p>A possible intermediary is Chagatai خان (xan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hangul (Korean phonetic spelling)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>한글</i> (han'geul), from Archaic Korean <i>ᄒᆞᆫ</i> (“great”) + <i>글</i> (geul, “script”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melancholy, blues</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>hypochondria</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ὑποχόνδρια</i> (hupokhóndria). Further from Ancient Greek <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) + Ancient Greek <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mope, to sulk</li></ol><p>From <i>хандр(а́)</i> (xandr(á)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(ethnography,&#32;sciences,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) a Han person (male)</li></ol><p>From <i>хань</i> (xanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), from Mandarin <i>漢</i>&nbsp;/ <i>汉</i> (hàn), originally in the plural only as <i>ха́ньцы</i> (xánʹcy); the singular form may have been back-formed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bigot, hypocrite, Pharisee</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) baijiu</li></ol><p>From Ottoman Turkish حاجی (<i>hacı</i>, “Kaaba pilgrim”).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>свято́ша</i> (svjatóša) (&lt; <i>свято́й</i> (svjatój)), English <i>bigot</i> (akin to <i>god</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypocritical</li></ol><p><i>ханжа́</i> (xanžá) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy, sanctimony, sanctimoniousness</li></ol><p><i>ханжа́</i> (xanžá) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be hypocritical</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to beg (to request the help of someone, often in the form of money)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) raw opium</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) vodka; homebrew; moonshine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khanate</li></ol><p>From <i>хан</i> (xan) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хантыйский</i> (xantyjskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khanty-Mansi (relating to the Khanty-Mansi Autonomous Okrug in Russia)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khany-Mansiysk (town in Russia)</li></ol><p>For the autonomous okrug, <i>Ха́нты-Ма́нсия</i> (Xánty-Mánsija) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij); for the town, <i>Ха́нты-Манси́й(ск)</i> (Xánty-Mansíj(sk)) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to Khanty ethnic group or language</li><li>Khanty (<i>language</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ханты́</i> (xantý) +&lrm; <i>-йский</i> (-jskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) milksop, someone of feeble and timid constitution</li></ol><p>Criminal slang after <i>хано́рик</i> (xanórik), <i>хоно́рик</i> (xonórik), the hybrid of a polecat (<i>хорёк</i> (xorjók)) and mink (<i>но́рка</i> (nórka)), a male of which is sterile.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanja (Han character script used to write Korean)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>한자(漢字)</i> (hanja), from Middle Chinese 漢字 (<i>MC xanH dziH</i>, “Chinese character”, literally “Han Chinese + character”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnography</i>) Han (autonym of the Chinese ethnicity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>漢</i>&nbsp;/ <i>汉</i> (hàn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnography</i>) a Han person (female)</li></ol><p>From <i>хань</i> (xanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnic</i>) Chinese, Han</li></ol><p>From <i>хань</i> (xanʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanzi, Chinese character</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 漢字／汉字 (<i>hànzì</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaos</li><li>mess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χάος</i> (kháos, “vast chasm, void”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>chaotically (in a chaotic manner)</li></ol><p><i>хаоти́ческий</i> (xaotíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chaotic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>chaot(isch)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chaotic</li></ol><p>Late Latin <i>chaoti(cus)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) alternative form of <i>ха́пнуть</i> (xápnutʹ)</li></ol><p><i>ха́пнуть</i> (xápnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude) to grab (quickly); to steal; to take (from someone unlawfully)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude) to take bribes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xāpàti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dose, pull, shot (of a psychoactive substance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) grabber, (a greedy) bribetaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hara-kiri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>腹切り</i> (はらきり, harakiri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>character, temper, disposition</li><li>type</li><li>nature</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) principles</li><li>strength of will</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>charakter</i>, from Latin <i>character</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χαρακτήρ</i> (kharaktḗr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to characterize</li><li>to describe</li><li>to be typical of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be characterized, to be defined [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘by something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>характеризова́ть</i> (xarakterizovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>characterization, description (act or process of characterizing)</li><li>testimonial, reference (information about a person)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) characteristic (the integer part of a logarithm)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessing pronounced, distinctive features</li><li>characteristic, particular, typical, specific</li><li>(relational,&#32;theater) character (expressing a certain psychological type)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) strong-willed, having a strong character</li></ol><p><i>хара́ктер</i> (xarákter) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хара́ктер</i> (xarákter).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Damascus steel, or a type of damask steel used in medieval Rus</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>харалугъ</i> (xaralugŭ), not doubted to be Turkic – being present before the Mongol raids –, but controverted if exactly from</p><p>Some have related it as a polnoglasie to the name of the Carolingians, <i>карля́ги</i> (karljági), <i>корля́зи</i> (korljázi), <i>короля́зи</i> (koroljázi), but this seems to be a historical phantasy.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) harassment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>harassment</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardbass</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hard bass</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) enough!, stop!</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>хоро́ш</i> (xoróš); originally from criminal argot.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хоре́й</i> (xoréj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow-throated marten (<i>Martes flavigula</i>)</li></ol><p>Of Tungusic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charisma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χάρισμα</i> (khárisma, “grace, favour/favor, gift”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fuck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grayling (<i>Thymallus thymallus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Finnic language; compare to Finnish <i>harjus</i>, Estonian <i>harjus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate (phlegm or spit), to spit (noisily)</li><li>alternative form of <i>ха́ркать</i> (xárkatʹ)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъrkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expectorate (phlegm or spit), to spit (noisily)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) harpaline</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) The Harpalinae.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Harpalinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charter (ancient manuscript as well as the material on which it was written)</li><li>charter, declaration (official document with the declaration of anything)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Greek <i>χαρτί</i> (chartí, “paper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) grub, food</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>харчь</i> (xarčĭ), from Arabic خَرْج (ḵarj, “expenditure, outlay, expense, costs”), via a Turkic language: compare Turkish <i>harç</i>, Azerbaijani <i>xərc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) eating house</li></ol><p><i>харч</i> (xarč) +&lrm; <i>-е́вня</i> (-évnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kharcho, a spicy Georgian soup made from meat, rice, and vegetables with grated walnuts and tklapi/tkemali.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ხარჩო</i> (xarčo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) spit, spittle, loogie</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;animate) ditherer, weakling</li></ol><p><i>ха́ркать</i> (xárkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Kharkiv, Kharkov</li></ol><p><i>Ха́рьков</i> (Xárʹkov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Kharkov</li></ol><p><i>Ха́рьков</i> (Xárʹkov) +&lrm; <i>-чанин</i> (-čanin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) (ugly) mug, muzzle, phiz (face)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hasidism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husky</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hassium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a peasant's) house, hut</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) house, joint, a place one can booze or have sex</li><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) home</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) prison cell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ха́та</i> (xáta, “house; home”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xata</i>, from Scythian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ха́та</i> (xáta, “(a peasant's) house, hut”): (small) peasant's house, hut</li><li>a dwelling of a beaver or some other animal, located partly under water and made of branches and stems held together with mud (beaver's shelter, beaver lodge)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ха́тка</i> (xátka). Equivalent to <i>ха́та</i> (xáta) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house music, house</li><li>jungle cat, chaus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>house</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hausa (<i>language</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hafezian</li></ol><p><i>Хафи́з</i> (Xafíz) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>хафизовский</i> (xafizovskij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) suitor, lover</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) fop, popinjay, coxcomb</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) sharper, chevalier d'industrie, conman</li></ol><p>Probably of expressive origin, also reminiscent of words like <i>&#42;koxati</i> (“to love”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ха́халь</i> (xáxalʹ, “suitor, lover”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ха-ха-ха</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) alternative form of <i>ха́ханьки</i> (xáxanʹki, “laughter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Armenian person</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;by extension) commonly of any person native to the Caucasus region (Armenian, Georgian, Azerbaijani, Chechen, etc.)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>ха́чик</i> (xáčik), by reanalysing it as a formation with the suffix <i>-ик</i> (-ik) (compare зонт (zont)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khachapuri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ხაჭაპური</i> (xač̣aṗuri).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Armenian person</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;by extension) commonly of any person native to the Caucasus region (Armenian, Georgian, Azerbaijani, Chechen, Abkhazian, etc.)</li></ol><p>From the common Armenian male given name <i>Խաչիկ</i> (Xačʻik), the diminutive of <i>Խաչատուր</i> (Xačʻatur, literally “given by cross”), from <i>խաչ</i> (xačʻ, “cross”) +&lrm; <i>-ա-</i> (-a-) +&lrm; <i>-տուր</i> (-tur, “given”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khachkar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>խաչքար</i> (xačʻkʻar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) an old, rusty, awful car, which was extremely manneredly tuned</li><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) older or poorly maintained car owned or driven by people from the Caucasus region of Russia</li></ol><p><i>хач</i> (xač) +&lrm; <i>(авто)моби́ль</i> ((avto)mobílʹ, “car”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hashemite</li></ol><p><i>Ха́шим</i> (Xášim) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slander, to backbite, to slag off, to find fault (with), to run down</li></ol><p>Unclear. Brückner finds a parallel in Sanskrit <i>क्षीयते</i> (kṣīyate, “diminish”). However, derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰey-</i> is dismissed by Vasmer as unreliable.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise, glory</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хвала</i> (xvala), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvala</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compliment, to praise</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хвалити</i> (xvaliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvaliti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хвала́</i> (xvalá, “praise”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>to take credit for</li></ol><p><i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ironically</i>) (much) vaunted; celebrated</li></ol><p><i>хвали́ть</i> (xvalítʹ, “praise”) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хва́статься</i> (xvástatʹsja)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvastati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boast, to brag [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘of something’</small> <i>or</i> <b>что</b> (što, + clause) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boastfully promise [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>хва́стать</i> (xvástatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>braggingly (in a bragging manner)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boastful</li></ol><p>Morphologically, from <i>хва́стать</i> (xvástatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boasting, bragging</li></ol><p><i>хва́стать</i> (xvástatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овство́</i> (-ovstvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boaster, braggart</li></ol><p>From <i>хва́стать</i> (xvástatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) grip, grasp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) grasping fellow</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) energetic, resourceful, and daring person</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ, “to catch, to grip, to grasp”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apprehension (seizure)</li></ol><p><i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp, to grab, to seize, to snap, to snatch, to catch, to catch hold of</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to suffice, to be enough, to be sufficient, to adequate</li><li>(<i>idiomatic expressions</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хватати</i> (xvatati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvatati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to grip, to pluck <i>(at)</i>, to catch <i>(at)</i></li><li>to take up</li></ol><p><i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch and devour (<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>to gulp down (liquid)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get drunk</li><li>to finagle</li><li>to endure (something unpleasant)</li><li>to go too far in, to get carried away in (statements or plans)</li><li>to hit hard, to punch</li><li>to break by striking, to shatter</li><li>to strike (someone) (<i>of a heart attack, paralysis, etc.</i>)</li><li>to strike, to manifest suddenly (<i>of rain, frost, wind, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to damage</li><li>to break into (a song or dance), to suddenly start singing (a song) or dancing (a dance)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to suffice, to be sufficient / enough; to last out</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvatiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch <i>(at)</i>, to grip, to pluck <i>(at)</i>, to catch <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to miss, to notice the absence <i>(of)</i>, to find that someone/something is missing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to remember</li></ol><p><i>хвати́ть</i> (xvatítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grip, grasp, clutch</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) grip, tenacity</li></ol><p><i>хвата́ть</i> (xvatátʹ, “to grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Pskov</i>) synonym of <i>свет</i> (svet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>process in which coniferous trees shed their leaves (needles)</li></ol><p><i>хво́я</i> (xvója, “tree leaves”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>па́дать</i> (pádatʹ, “fall”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ephedra</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coniferous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvojьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хво́я</i> (xvója) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to be sick, to be ill</li></ol><p><i>хво́рый</i> (xvóryj) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional,&#32;Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine) illness; disease</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvoroba</i> or <i>хворь</i> (xvorʹ) +&lrm; <i>-оба</i> (-oba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brushwood (branches and twigs)</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) hvorost (straws, twiglets (pastry))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хворостъ</i> (xvorostŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvorstъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sick, ill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvorъ</i>, <i>&#42;xorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) illness</li></ol><p>Хворать (xvoratʹ) ("to be sick, to be ill") + -ь (-ʹ). Cognate with German Geschwür ("ulcer").</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tail</li><li>ponytail</li><li>line, queue</li><li>the tail end, the back of the queue, the end, the back</li><li>something follows behind</li><li>(<i>university slang</i>) academic arrears, backlog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvostъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) hairstreak</li></ol><p>From <i>хвоста́тый</i> (xvostátyj, “tailed”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tailed, caudate (having a tail)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хвост</i> (xvost): (small) tail</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an additional amount</li><li>squiggle (in writing), tail, end, hook (e.g., of the comma)</li><li>pigtail, ponytail</li></ol><p>From <i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>university slang</i>) document for tracking exam retakes (arrears, backlogs)</li><li>long-tailed tit (<i>Aegithalos caudatus</i> a.k.a. <i>Parus caudatus</i>)</li><li><i>Urochloa</i></li></ol><p><i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tail (<i>of an animal, airplane, etc.</i>); caudal</li></ol><p><i>хвост</i> (xvost) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хвост</i> (xvost).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsetail, mare's-tail (<i>Equisetum</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvoščь</i>, from <i>&#42;xvostъ</i> (“tail”) + <i>&#42;-jь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horsetail (plant)</li></ol><p><i>хвощ</i> (xvošč) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) needles (<i>of conifers</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xvoja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hehe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedging</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) headhunter (recruiter)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>headhunter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headhunting (active recruitment of executive or talented personnel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>headhunting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to defecate, to evacuate</li><li>(slang,&#32;rare) to be chicken, to be timid</li></ol><p>Through some cant from Greek <i>χέζω</i> (chézo, “to defecate, to evacuate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(especially&#32;Internet,&#32;neologism,&#32;colloquial) hater (one who hates&#59; male or female)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hater</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hake (fish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hake</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Хеллоуи́н</i> (Xɛllouín)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Хеллоуин)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halloween (31st of October)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Halloween</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pornography</i>) hentai</li></ol><p>From English <i>hentai</i>, a semantic shift from Japanese <ruby>変態<rp>(</rp><rt>へんたい</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>hentai</i>, “pervert”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;historical) the name of the letter Х, х</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) dick, cock</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) dick, contemptible person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хѣръ</i> (xěrŭ), of uncertain origin: Possibilities include:</p><p>Euphemistic form of хуй (xuj), from the name of the first letter of <i>хуй</i> (see above). Compare eff.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go or move somewhere disagreeable, or objectionable in any way manner or time</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do something in a very intense fashion, often for a long time</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sherry (fortified wine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>jerez</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sherry</li></ol><p><i>хе́рес</i> (xéres) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хе́рес</i> (xéres).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to cross out, to X out</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to destroy, to kill off, to put an end to</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to neglect, to forget about</li></ol><p><i>хер</i> (xer) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ, “old name for the Cyrillic letter <i>Х</i> (X)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something loathsome or worthless; crud, pants, stuff</li></ol><p>From <i>хер</i> (xer) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá); compare <i>хуйня́</i> (xujnjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lousily, wretchedly, crappily, shittily</li></ol><p><i>херо́вый</i> (xeróvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lousy, wretched, worthless, crappy</li></ol><p><i>хер</i> (xer, “prick, penis”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherub (winged creature represented in the Bible as attending on God)</li></ol><p>Old East Slavic <i>хероувимъ</i> (xeruvimŭ) (cf. Old Church Slavonic <i>херовимъ</i> (xerovimŭ)), from Ancient Greek <i>χερουβίμ</i> (kheroubím), ultimately from Hebrew <i>כְּרוּבִים</i> (k'ruvím), plural of <i>כְּרוּב</i> (k'rúv).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) hashtag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hashtag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) hee (<i>an expression of laughter</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chi (the Greek letter Χ&#47;χ (ch))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χεῖ</i> (kheî).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiasmus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) hiatus (syllable break between two vowels)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>hiātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hibakusha (survivor of the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>被爆者</i> (ひばくしゃ, hibakusha, “bomb affected people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hovel, shack (a small, crudely built house)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory&#32;or&#32;ironic) flop, shanty (a small or mean apartment or apartment building)</li></ol><p>Cognate with dialectal <i>хиба́ть</i> (xibátʹ, “to sway”), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xybъ</i> (“wobble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хиба́ра</i> (xibára, “shack”): (small) shack</li></ol><p><i>хиба́ра</i> (xibára) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) The Hebrew language.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Hebrew</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) hijab (headscarf)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic حِجَاب (ḥijāb).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabin, hut, shack (small wooden shed)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xyžina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hikikomori</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>引きこもり</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) hikikomori</li></ol><p>Probably a back-formation from <i>хи́кки</i> (xíkki), reanalysed as a nominative plural.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) hikikomori</li></ol><p>From <i>хи́кки</i> (xíkki) +&lrm; <i>-ан</i> (-an).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) hikikomori</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>ヒッキー</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to be a hikikomori, to be a recluse, to live an isolated life indoors</li></ol><p><i>хи́кка</i> (xíkka) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal one's self</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ethnic slur</i>) hillbilly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hillbilly</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sickly, puny</li><li>stunted, undergrown (<i>of plants</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xylъ</i>. Related to Bulgarian <i>хилав</i> (hilav, “feeble”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chemical weapon attack</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>химическая атака</i> (ximičeskaja ataka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) chimera (mythical monster)</li><li>(<i>genetics</i>) chimera</li><li>chimaera (fish)</li><li>(rare,&#32;sometimes racist) a term from the controversial theory of ethnogenesis created by Lev Gumilev, describing a long time period of close contact between two "opposing" cultures</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>χίμαιρα</i> (khímaira), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéyōm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) Chimaeriformes (a biological order that includes ghost sharks)</li></ol><p><i>химе́ра</i> (ximéra) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ные</i> (-nyje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) chemist (person working in chemistry)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chemistry student</li><li>(prison slang,&#32;Soviet Union) a convict who has been released to work in construction (<i>хи́мия</i> (xímija))</li><li>(<i>bodybuilding&#32;slang</i>) an athlete on juice, a roid user</li></ol><p><i>хи́мия</i> (xímija) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) chemicals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemotherapy</li></ol><p><i>хими-</i> (ximi-) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>терапи́я</i> (terapíja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chemical</li></ol><p><i>хи́мия</i> (xímija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemical element</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chemistry</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) perm <i>(hairstyle, short for <i>хими́ческая зави́вка</i> (ximíčeskaja zavívka))</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chemicals</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) machinations, tricks</li><li>(prison slang,&#32;Soviet Union&#32;from the 1960s,&#32;still in&#32;Belarus) construction work or other penal labour to which a convict (<i>хи́мик</i> (xímik)) is bound in place of detention proper</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>химиотерапи́я</i> (ximioterapíja)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syllabic abbreviation of <i>хими́ческая чи́стка</i> (ximíčeskaja čístka): dry cleaning</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hindi; one of the official languages of India</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>हिन्दी</i> (hindī) / <i>हिंदी</i> (hindī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip-hop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippie (1960s nonconformist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hippie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hipster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hipster</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiragana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>平仮名</i> (hiragana).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weaken, to grow sickly</li><li>to fall into decay</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xyrěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the place where after a day of harvest the cotton is unloaded to be tallied</li><li>a combined mass of cotton harvest of a day</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>хирмон</i> (xirmon) (Kazakh <i>қырман</i> (qyrman)), from Persian خرمن (xarman, xerman).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiromancer, palmist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiromancy, palmistry</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;formal) surgeon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χειρουργός</i> (kheirourgós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surgical</li></ol><p><i>хирург-</i> (xirurg-) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surgery (field of study)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit <i>(popular song)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sly person; crafty fellow, dodger, slyboots</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xytrьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec). Cognate with Polish <i>chytrzec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sly/cunning/crafty, to try to fool</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xytriti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slyly, cunningly</li><li>intricately, with cunning</li><li>adroitly</li></ol><p>From <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) (stem-stressed variant) or from <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) intricacy</li><li>(usually&#32;in the plural,&#32;figurative) intrigue, scheme, plot</li></ol><p><i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сплете́ние</i> (spleténije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craftiness, craft (shrewdness)</li><li>ruse</li><li>stratagem</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>хытрость</i> (xytrostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xytrostь</i>, from <i>&#42;xytrъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunning, resourceful, crafty, artful</li><li>intricate, sophisticated</li><li>ingenious, smart</li></ol><p><i>хи́трый</i> (xítryj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ум</i> (um) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sly, cunning, artful</li><li>intricate, involved</li><li>skilful, resourceful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хытръ</i> (xytrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xytrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to giggle, to chuckle, to snigger, to titter (laugh)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to giggle, to chuckle, to snigger, to titter (laugh)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embezzlement, theft; misappropriation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predator (any animal or other organism)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хꙑщьникъ</i> (xyštĭnikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xyťьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хищ-</i> (xišč-) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate bug</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>хи́щник</i> (xíščnik, “predator”) +&lrm; <i>кро́шка</i> (króška, “little one”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>predation (the preying of one animal on others)</li></ol><p><i>хи́щник</i> (xíščnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predatory</li></ol><p><i>хи́щный</i> (xíščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>predatory</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хꙑщьнъ</i> (xyštĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xyťьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хитить</i> (xititʹ, “to plunder, to reave”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;folk poetic,&#32;archaic) cold</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хладъ</i> (xladŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoldъ</i>. Doublet of <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sang-froid, composure (coolness in trying circumstances)</li></ol><p>From <i>хла́дный</i> (xládnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), a calque of French <i>sang-froid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coolly, cold-bloodedly, in cold blood</li></ol><p><i>хладнокро́вный</i> (xladnokróvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calm, cool</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) cold-blooded (<i>act, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>хладнокро́в(ие)</i> (xladnokróv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) cold</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хладьнъ</i> (xladĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoldьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>холо́дный</i> (xolódnyj). By surface analysis, <i>хлад</i> (xlad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughterhouse with refrigerators</li></ol><p><i>хлад</i> (xlad) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бой</i> (boj) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trash, rubbish</li><li>cruft, junk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlamъ&#47;&#42;xlomъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) chlamydomonas, chlamydomonad</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Chlamydomonas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu lato) The Chlamydomonadales.</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;sensu stricto) The Chlamydomonadaceae.</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>хламидомона́довый</i> (xlamidomonádovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) jack, knave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread</li><li>loaf</li><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) black bread, rye bread</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) grain</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) food, subsistence, living (cf. breadwinner)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хлѣбъ</i> (xlěbŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlěbъ</i>, from Gothic <i>𐌷𐌻𐌰𐌹𐍆𐍃</i> (hlaifs), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaibaz</i>, of unknown origin. Cognate with English <i>loaf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic) friendly treat, hospitality, friendly attitude</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) mouth, piehole</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) face</li></ol><p>From <i>хлеба́ть</i> (xlebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to eat (liquid food with a spoon), to slurp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gulp down, to drink</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlebati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>хлеб</i> (xleb): small loaf of bread</li><li>type of pastry (<i>especially a buttery croissant</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlěbьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хлеб</i> (xleb) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread box, bread bin (a container for storing bread)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlěbьnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) vodka</li></ol><p>Literally, “grain wine”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to take/swallow a mouthful <i>(of)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>хлеба́ть</i> (xlebátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;agriculture) grain (<i>US</i>), corn (<i>UK</i>), cereal</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) bread</li><li>baker's</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) rich, prosperous (<i>area</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) lucrative</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlěbьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хлеб</i> (xleb) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouthful</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bakery (shop where bread is baked)</li></ol><p><i>хлеб</i> (xleb) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пека́рня</i> (pekárnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadmaker, baker (<i>of bread</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-пёк</i> (-pjók), combining form of <i>печь</i> (pečʹ, “to bake”) (compare 1sg. present <i>пеку́</i> (pekú)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain grower, farmer</li></ol><p><i>хлеб</i> (xleb) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; Ukrainian <i>-роб</i> (-rob). Compare with Ukrainian <i>хліборо́б</i> (xliborób). The unproductive agent suffix <i>-роб</i> (-rob), identical to Russian <i>-дел</i> (-del) is believed to be of Ukrainian origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hospitable</li></ol><p><i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>соль</i> (solʹ, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread”)</li></ol><p><i>хле́б</i> (xléb) +&lrm; <i>-ушек</i> (-ušek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stable, stall, cowshed</li><li>sheep cote</li><li>pigsty, pigpen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlěvъ</i> (“stall”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lash, to switch, to whip</li><li>to beat down <i>(on)</i>, to lash <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to gush</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to guzzle <i>(down)</i>, to glug</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlestati</i>. See also <i>хлыст</i> (xlyst, “whip”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lash, to switch, to whip</li></ol><p><i>хлеста́ть</i> (xlestátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>хлёсткий</i> (xljóstkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>хлёстко</i> (xljóstko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) weak, fragile, flimsy</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) frail, sickly, puny</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) shaky, unsteady</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) weak-willed, spineless</li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) watery, diluted</li></ol><p>Dialectally also <i>хли́бкий</i> (xlíbkij), <i>хлибко́й</i> (xlibkój), <i>хли́бый</i> (xlíbyj); inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlibъkъ</i>. Cognate with <i>хли́бать</i> (xlíbatʹ, “to fall ill, to shake from weakness”), also <i>хли́бить</i> (xlíbitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) censored spelling of <i>хуйло</i> (xujlo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clapping</li><li>flapping</li><li>slapping, smacking</li><li>cracking (<i>of a whip</i>)</li><li>tapping</li><li>slamming (<i>of a door</i>)</li><li>banging, popping</li></ol><p><i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clap</li><li>to flap</li><li>to slap, to smack; to crack (a whip)</li><li>to tap</li><li>to slam (a door)</li><li>to bang, to pop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlopati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tune out, to let something gloss over oneself, to get one's brain fried</li><li>to rubberneck, to gawk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) lad, boy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>хло́пець</i> (xlópecʹ, “boy, lad”), cognate to Polish <i>chłopiec</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cotton; made of cotton</li></ol><p><i>хло́пок</i> (xlópok) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flap, to clap</li><li>to slam (a door)</li><li>to down, to slam (an alcoholic drink)</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton (material)</li><li>cotton (plant)</li><li>cotton (cloth)</li><li>clap (of hands)</li><li>crack, snap, pop (dull sound of an impact, an explosion, a gunshot, or similar)</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) explosion</li></ol><p>Unclear. Cognate with <i>хло́пья</i> (xlópʹja). Compare with Czech <i>chlup</i>, Slovak <i>chlp</i>, Polish <i>chłupy</i>.</p><p>From <i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók, suffix indicating an occurrence of an action).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bustle about, to diligently do things</li><li>to hustle, to fuss</li><li>to try to achieve</li><li>to try to help, to intercede (for), to plead (for) (<i>usually with the authorities</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>хло́поты</i> (xlópoty) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). Originally from <i>клопота́ть</i> (klopotátʹ) with an expressive "х" instead of "к". Cognates include Ukrainian <i>клопота́ти</i> (klopotáty, “to bother, to force to work”), Bulgarian <i>клопотя́</i> (klopotjá, “to bring to tears”), Czech <i>klороtаti</i> (“to knock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with much ado</li><li>busily, eagerly, with alacrity</li></ol><p><i>хлопотли́вый</i> (xlopotlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>troublesome, bothersome</li><li>bustling, fussy</li></ol><p><i>хлопота́ть</i> (xlopotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>troublesome</li></ol><p><i>хло́поты</i> (xlópoty) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) busybody, bustler</li></ol><p>From <i>хлопота́ть</i> (xlopotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble, efforts</li><li>care (of someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plopper, pop gun, flapper</li><li>flyflap</li><li>clapperboard</li></ol><p><i>хло́пать</i> (xlópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) cotton (plant)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cotton (raw material); cotton wool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cotton</li></ol><p><i>хлопча́тая бума́га</i> (xlopčátaja bumága) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cotton</li><li>flaky</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ragged, wispy (<i>clouds, puffs of smoke</i>)</li></ol><p><i>хло́пок</i> (xlópok) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) flake (of a soft, loose material composed of small, flattened individual pieces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flakes (soft, loose material composed of small, flattened individual pieces)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) puffs, tufts, wisps (<i>of clouds, smoke, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Cognate with <i>хло́пок</i> (xlópok, “cotton”). Compare with Czech <i>chlup</i>, Slovak <i>chlp</i>, Polish <i>chłupy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chlorine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chlore</i> or German <i>Chlor</i>, both from Ancient Greek <i>χλωρός</i> (khlōrós, “pale green”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) barium chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) potassium chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) calcium chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inorganic chemistry</i>) sodium chloride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chlorite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chemistry) bleach</li></ol><p><i>хло́рный</i> (xlórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from <i>хло́рная и́звесть</i> (xlórnaja ízvestʹ, “bleach”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chloric</li></ol><p><i>хлор</i> (xlor) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chloroplast (photosynthetic organelle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) chlorophyll (green pigment)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) chlorophytum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Chlorophytum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) chloroform (an anesthetic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chloropicrin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gush out, to spout</li><li>to pour, to flood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlynǫti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whip, switch (thin rod used as a whip)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a Khlyst</li></ol><p>Onomatopoetic according to Vasmer. See also <i>хлеста́ть</i> (xlestátʹ); Polish <i>chłosta</i>.</p><p>Uncertain. May be related to <i>христос</i> (xristos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) fop; coxcomb; dandy; snob</li></ol><p>From <i>хлыст</i> (xlyst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to squelch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flounder, to squelch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sniffle, to snuffle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to snivel</li></ol><p><i>хлюп</i> (xljup) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) weakling, wimp, sissy, milksop, pansy</li></ol><p><i>хлю́пать</i> (xljúpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abyss</li><li>quagmire</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xlębь</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>хлѧбь</i> (xlębĭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap; long narrow strip of cloth or leather used to hold something like a piece of clothing or headwear, or a part of some equipment or furniture</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>biting (<i>wind</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trenchant, scathing</li></ol><p><i>хлест(а́ть)</i> (xlest(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>хлёсткий</i> (xljóstkij)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>хлёстко</i> (xljóstko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “bread, loaf”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хлеб</i> (xleb, “grain”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хле́в</i> (xlév).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hmm, hum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to become intoxicated, to drunk, to tipsy or high</li><li>(figurative,&#32;colloquial) to become exhilarated</li></ol><p><i>хмель</i> (xmelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khmeli suneli (a traditional Georgian spicy herbs mixture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ხმელი-სუნელი</i> (xmeli-suneli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hop <i>(plant)</i></li><li>hops <i>(seeds)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) drunkenness, intoxication</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъmelь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intoxicating, heady</li><li>intoxicated, light-headed, tipsy</li></ol><p><i>хмель</i> (xmelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hops, hop (<i>plant</i>)</li><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>хмельно́й</i> (xmelʹnój)</li></ol><p><i>хмель</i> (xmelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frown</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xmuriti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frown</li><li>to gloom, to lour (of rain clouds)</li></ol><p><i>хму́рить</i> (xmúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gloomily, somberly, dismally</li></ol><p><i>хму́рый</i> (xmúryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloomy, sullen (affected with, or expressing, gloom; melancholy)</li><li>cloudy, overcast, dull</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xmura</i> (“a cloud”), related to <i>&#42;xmuriti</i> (“to make cloudy”). Cognates include Polish <i>pochmurny</i> (“cloudy”), Ukrainian <i>похму́рий</i> (poxmúryj, “cloudy, gloomy”). Possibly related to <i>па́смурный</i> (pásmurnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hem, to say hmm (Russian <i>хм</i> (xm)) out of hesitation</li></ol><p>Partly from <i>хм</i> (xm) and suffix <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hem, to say hmm (Russian <i>хм</i> (xm)) out of hesitation</li></ol><p><i>хмы́кать</i> (xmýkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) creep, weirdo, scumbag (a strange, unpleasant, sly, or pitiful man)</li></ol><p>Compare <i>хмурый</i> (xmuryj, “sullen, miserable”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) henna (<i>Lawsonia inermis</i>)</li><li>henna coloring</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to whine, to whimper</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) to complain tearfully, to sob</li></ol><p>Onomatopoetic in origin. Compare dialectal <i>хмы́кать</i> (xmýkatʹ, “to whine, to whimper”), Ukrainian <i>хни́кати</i> (xnýkaty, “to whine, to whimper”), and similar verbs in other languages, such as Czech <i>kníkati</i> (“to whine, to squeal”), also <i>kničeti</i>, <i>kňučeti</i>; Polish <i>knychać</i> (“to whine, to squeal”); Latvian <i>šnukstêt</i> (“to sob, to whine”), dialectal German <i>schnucken</i> (“to sigh, to sob, to whimper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ha (expressing laughter)</li><li>expressing surprise, irony, regret, or admiration</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hobby (activity done for enjoyment in spare time)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hobby</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hobbit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hobbit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trunk (extended nasal organ of an elephant)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xobotъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>хо́бат</i> (xóbat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) proboscissed, having a proboscis.</li></ol><p><i>хобото́к</i> (xobotók, “proboscis”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj, “adjectival suffix”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хо́бот</i> (xóbot): (small) trunk (of an elephant)</li><li>proboscis (elongated tube from the head or connected to the mouth, of an animal)</li></ol><p><i>хо́бот</i> (xóbot) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) to hide, to conceal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xovati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) hovercraft (a vehicle supported on a cushion of air)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hovercraft</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion, movement, travel, progress, locomotion, headway</li><li>course, path, way, run</li><li>speed, rate, gait, pace</li><li>stroke <i>(of a piston)</i></li><li>functioning, working, process, action</li><li>gear, thread, pitch</li><li>shape, trend, dependence <i>(of a curve)</i></li><li>range <i>(of a magnet)</i></li><li>entrance, entry</li><li>passage</li><li>(<i>games</i>) move, turn, lead</li><li>axle with wheels</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ходъ</i> (xodŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sod-</i>, whence Ancient Greek <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “threshold; road, path, journey”) and thereby English suffix <i>-ode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An unexpected crafty action made by someone in a difficult situation.</li></ol><p>Literally, “move of the knight [chessman]”. Calque of French <i>jouer le cavalier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) solicitor (on certain matters), mediator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ходатаи</i> (xodatai). By surface analysis, <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́тай</i> (-átaj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercession</li><li>petition, application (a verbal or written request)</li><li>motion</li><li>solicitation, soliciting</li><li>pleading</li><li>suit</li></ol><p><i>хода́тай</i> (xodátaj) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solicit <i>(for)</i>, to apply <i>(for)</i>, to intercede <i>(for)</i></li></ol><p><i>хода́тайство</i> (xodátajstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) peddler / pedlar; hawker</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) human billboard</li></ol><p><i>ходьба́</i> (xodʹbá) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) hodja (a Muslim schoolmaster)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خواجه (hoca), from Classical Persian خواجه (xwāja), ultimately from Sanskrit <i>उपाध्याय</i> (upādhyāya).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simple pendulum clock</li></ol><p><i>ход</i> (xod) +&lrm; <i>-ики</i> (-iki)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, to walk</li><li>to wear something (<i>imperfective only</i>)</li><li>to go (to), to attend, to visit</li><li>to run (of trains, ships, etc.)</li><li>to work, to run (of clocks)</li><li>to lead, to play, to move (in games)</li><li>to tend, to take care (of), to nurse (<i>imperfective only</i>)</li><li>to be good (about money)</li><li>(medicine or colloquial) to urinate, to defecate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fast, quick, going or able to go at a good clip</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) saleable, in demand, flying off the shelves</li></ol><p>From <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodъ</i>. Compare Polish <i>chodliwy</i> for both etymological and semantic development.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) auto mechanic specialized in repairing the running gear</li></ol><p>From <i>ходовая́ часть</i> (xodovajá častʹ, “running gear”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) motion, run</li><li>salable, marketable, best-selling, highly demanded (<i>of goods</i>)</li><li>common, commonly used (<i>of a word</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ход</i> (xod) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walker, goer</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) (foot) messenger, petitioner</li></ol><p><i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stiltwalker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a stilted manner</li></ol><p><i>ходу́льный</i> (xodúlʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stilt (relating to stilts)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stilted</li></ol><p><i>ходу́ля</i> (xodúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stilt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoduľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) shaking heavily, being unstable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking, pacing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodьba</i>, from <i>&#42;xoditi</i> + <i>&#42;-ьba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьба</i> (-ʹba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>walking</li><li>current</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) walking, personified, incarnate</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodę̀ťь</i>. Equivalent to <i>ходи́ть</i> (xodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij). Doublet of <i>ходя́щий</i> (xodjáščij), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking, pacing</li><li>circulation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cost accounting, self-financing</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>хозя́йственный расчёт</i> (xozjájstvennyj rasčót).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner, proprietor</li><li>landlord, head of household</li><li>boss, master, chief, principal, manager, employer, hirer</li><li>man, husband</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) host, master</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) host</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хозя</i> (xozja), Chuvash <i>хуҫа</i> (huś̬a). Cognates include Persian خواجه (xâje), Turkish <i>hoca</i>, Slavic: Ukrainian <i>хазя́їн</i> (xazjájin), Belarusian <i>хадзя́ін</i> (xadzjáin). Doublet of <i>ходжа́</i> (xodžá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostess</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) wife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>female owner's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep house, to manage a household</li><li>to do household chores</li><li>to boss it, to play the master, to lord it</li></ol><p><i>хозя́ин</i> (xozjáin) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>по-хозя́йски</i> (po-xozjájski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>owner, landlord, master, boss</li><li>lordly</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) host</li></ol><p><i>хозя́й-</i> (xozjáj-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>business manager</li><li>an economic/thrifty man</li><li>handyman, someone who is good with his hands and managing a property</li></ol><p><i>хозя́йственный</i> (xozjájstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>economic (relating to the economy)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) household</li><li>thrifty, economical</li><li>practical (having skills or knowledge that are practical)</li></ol><p><i>хозя́йство</i> (xozjájstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>economy, sector, economics</li><li>things necessary for the running of something, usually home-related</li><li>small enterprise, usually private/household and agricultural; farm, estate, farmstead, homestead, stead</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;colloquial) manhood, package, junk (male genitalia)</li></ol><p><i>хозя́ин</i> (xozjáin) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>management, managing</li></ol><p><i>хозя́йствовать</i> (xozjájstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep house</li><li>to manage</li></ol><p><i>хозя́йство</i> (xozjájstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хозя́йка</i> (xozjájka): female owner, hostess, wife, mistress</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(fandom slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;colloquial,&#32;neologism) Hearts of Iron IV, a grand strategy game.</li></ol><p>From <i>хой</i> (xoj, “Hearts of Iron IV”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(fandom slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;neologism) A player of Hearts of Iron IV, a grand strategy game.</li><li>(fandom slang,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;neologism) A fan of the same game.</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>хо́йка</i> (xójka, “Hearts of Iron IV”) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hockey player</li></ol><p>From <i>хокке́й</i> (xokkéj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) hockey</li><li>(slang,&#32;humorous) okay (rhymes with окей)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hockey</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) field hockey</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>хокке́й</i> (xokkéj, “hockey”) +&lrm; <i>на</i> (na, “on”) +&lrm; <i>трава́</i> (travá, “grass”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hockey, ice-hockey</li></ol><p><i>хокке́й</i> (xokkéj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holding company</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>holding</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) holding</li></ol><p>From <i>хо́лдинг</i> (xólding) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-groomed</li></ol><p><i>хо́лить</i> (xólitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-еный</i> (-enyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cholera</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cholera</i> (“bilious disease”), from Ancient Greek <i>χολέρα</i> (kholéra, “cholera”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) cholesterol (an essential component of mammalian cell membranes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tend, to care for</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoliti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хо́ля</i> (xólja) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) withers (part of the back of a draft animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xolъka</i>. Per Vasmer, probably derived from <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “crest”) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lobby, foyer, hall</li><li>large vestibule</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hall</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъlmъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>холм</i> (xolm): (small) hill, hillock, knoll</li></ol><p><i>холм</i> (xolm) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hilly</li></ol><p><i>холм</i> (xolm) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold (low temperature)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoldъ</i> (“coolness, cold”). Doublet of <i>хлад</i> (xlad), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;weather) to grow cold</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to get cold, suffer from cold</li></ol><p>From <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow cold</li></ol><p><i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspic (dish)</li></ol><p><i>холоди́ть</i> (xolodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) refrigeration; refrigerating</li></ol><p><i>холоди́ть</i> (xolodítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;colloquial) to cool, to make cold</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to cause a feeling of coolness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xolditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detention cell, cooler, lockup, calaboose, jail, prison, brig</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold war (a period of hostile relations)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>cold war</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>coldly</li></ol><p><i>холо́дный</i> (xolódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) cold weapon (a weapon that does not use fire or explosives)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) cold-blooded, ectothermic</li></ol><p><i>холо́дный</i> (xolódnyj) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coldness, frigidity</li></ol><p><i>холо́дный</i> (xolódnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoldьnъ</i>. Doublet of <i>хла́дный</i> (xládnyj), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic. By surface analysis, <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) cold front</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold-adapted</li></ol><p><i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>адапти́рованный</i> (adaptírovannyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) coolness, chill</li></ol><p><i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) fridge, refrigerator</li></ol><p><i>холод</i> (xolod) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cold hardiness</li></ol><p><i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сто́йкость</i> (stójkostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme cold, extremely cold weather</li></ol><p>From <i>хо́лод</i> (xólod) + deverbal from <i>дры́гать</i> (drýgatʹ, “to tremble (predominantly with the foot)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) Chechen</li><li>(<i>traditional</i>) loyal farm servitude</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Holocaust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) serf, bondman, kholop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xolpъ</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>хлапъ</i> (xlapŭ) with the same meaning or Polish <i>chłop</i> (“peasant”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>servile</li></ol><p>From <i>холо́п</i> (xolóp) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single (not married)</li><li>idle (not working)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) idle, free-running</li><li>(<i>ballistics</i>) blank</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unmarried person, single person, bachelor</li><li>blank (bullet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xolstъ</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>хластъ</i> (xlastŭ), Slovene <i>hlȃst</i>, Ukrainian <i>хо́лост</i> (xólost).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bachelor (unmarried man)</li></ol><p>From <i>холосто́й</i> (xolostój) +&lrm; <i>-я́к</i> (-ják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bachelor</li></ol><p><i>холостя́к</i> (xolostják) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canvas</li><li>sackcloth, burlap</li></ol><p>Multiple proposals exist. It could be onomatopoeic equivalent to Polish <i>chełst</i>, <i>chełstać</i>, it could be borrowed from Middle High German <i>hulst</i>&nbsp;f (“covering, sheath”), another derivation from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;huljaną</i> (“to cover”) as <i>&#42;hulistrą</i>, or secondarily related to that if <i>hulft</i>, <i>hulfter</i>, from which modern German <i>Halfter</i>, is the original. The Finnish fabric name <i>hursti</i> may be borrowed from Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canvas, sacking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) canvas, sacking</li></ol><p><i>холсти́н(а)</i> (xolstín(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) lackey, groveller</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xolujь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>servile</li></ol><p>From <i>холу́й</i> (xolúj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) canvas</li></ol><p>From <i>холст</i> (xolst) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj). Formerly spelled <i>холщёвый</i> (xolščóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) canvas</li></ol><p>From <i>холст</i> (xolst) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;low colloquial) care</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>хо́лить</i> (xólitʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homsi (inhabitant of Homs)</li></ol><p><i>Хомс</i> (Xoms) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Homsi (related to the city of Homs, Syria)</li></ol><p><i>Хомс</i> (Xoms) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хо́мсский</i> (xómsskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse collar</li><li>(<i>technology</i>) yoke, clamp, cable tie</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хомоуть</i> (xomutĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xomǫtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hamster (small, short-tailed European rodent)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rat</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sluggish person</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) homepage</li><li>(computing&#32;slang,&#32;chiefly in Linux) home directory</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;derogatory) fanboy, lemming, narrow-minded follower (<i>usually of a blogger or a virtual community</i>)</li><li>(slang,&#32;baseball) homer (home run)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хомѧкъ</i> (xomękŭ), <i>хомѣкъ</i> (xoměkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xomě̀kъ</i>, from <i>&#42;xoměstorъ</i> (“hamster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Cricetidae</li></ol><p>From <i>хомя́к</i> (xomják, “hamster”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хомя́к</i> (xomják)</li></ol><p><i>хомя́к</i> (xomják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chorus, choir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χορός</i> (khorós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hora (type of dance)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) territory used by the citizens of the ancient polis for farming</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a kind of fox</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) a male deer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>הוֹרָה</i> (hóra).</p><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croat, Croatian</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъrvatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croatian language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Croatian</li></ol><p><i>Хорва́тия</i> (Xorvátija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) chord, span (of arc, a line segment joining two points on a curve)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) spinal column, notochord (primary skeletal axis of vertebrates and man)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) chordate</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) notochord</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trochee</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) sharp pole for driving on reindeer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χορεῖος</i> (khoreîos), related to <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “choir, chorus”).</p><p>A Samoyedic borrowing: compare Tundra Nenets <i>хар</i> (xar°, “knife”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choreographer (male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>choreographic</li></ol><p><i>хореогра́фия</i> (xoreográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choreography</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chorégraphie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χορεία</i> (khoreía, “dance”) + <i>-γραφίᾱ</i> (-graphíā, “written form (of a word, etc.), spelling”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chorepiscopus</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>χωρεπίσκοπος</i> (khōrepískopos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choirmaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Chormeister</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;dialectal</i>) brave, courageous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хоробръ</i> (xorobrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorbrъ</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>харо́бры</i> (xaróbry).</p><p>Doublet of <i>хра́брый</i> (xrábryj, “brave”), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khorovod (a Slavic dance)</li></ol><p><i>хор</i> (xor) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) choir; choral</li></ol><p><i>хор</i> (xor) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a large wooden house in traditional Russian architecture, especially if composed of separate heated sections connected by unheated walkways and halls</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) mansion, palace</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хоромъ</i> (xoromŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xormъ</i>. Doublet of <i>храм</i> (xram), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury, to inter</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;obsolete) to hide, to conceal, to lay away</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorniti</i>; compare its borrowed doublet <i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;slang,&#32;colloquial) well done; cool</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pretty, comely, pin-up</li><li>adorable (befitting of being adored)</li></ol><p><i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij, diminutive adjectival suffix)</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>bellus</i> (modified diminutive of <i>duonus</i>). Also compare Czech <i>hezký</i>, Russian <i>приго́жий</i> (prigóžij) (&lt; <i>&#42;goditi</i>, akin to <i>го́дный</i> (gódnyj), cognate with English <i>good</i>). Also compare Ancient Greek <i>καλός</i> (kalós) (means both <i>beautiful</i> and <i>good</i>), and Mongolian <i>сайхан</i> (sajxan) (akin to <i>сайн</i> (sajn)). Also note the first element of Greek <i>όμορφος</i> (ómorfos) (<&lt; Ancient Greek <i>εὖ</i> (eû) + <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ)) (for the second one see <i>ле́пый</i> (lépyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very well, thoroughly, properly</li></ol><p><i>хорошо́</i> (xorošó) +&lrm; <i>-е́нько</i> (-énʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) goodness, good qualities</li></ol><p><i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij) +&lrm; <i>-есть</i> (-estʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become more beautiful, to grow prettier</li></ol><p>From <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij, “pretty, good-looking, handsome”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, nice, fine</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) large, significant, decent</li><li>(in the short form,&#32;sometimes with <i>собо́й</i> (sobój)) good-looking, handsome, pretty</li><li>(<i>as an address</i>) dear, darling, love, sweetie</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) drunk, tipsy</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually in the short form) Used to express objection or denial of something or a generally ironic attitude towards something.</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хорошь</i> (xorošĭ) (13th century), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoroščьnъ</i>, from <i>&#42;xorostь</i> (“beauty, pleasantness; convenience”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ьnъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšyj), Belarusian <i>харо́шы</i> (xaróšy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well, right</li><li>all right, nice, okay, good</li><li>(<i>academics</i>) four, B (a mark given to student’s work, indicating four out of five and corresponding roughly to a B in the American grading system)</li></ol><p><i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хоро́ший</i> (xoróšij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) gonfalon</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) banner (flag)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) banner (company-size cavalry unit in the armies of medieval and early modern Russia, Poland, and Lithuania)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorǫgy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferret, polecat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъxorь</i> (literally “ferret, stinky”). Cognate to <i>дух</i> (dux), <i>дохну́ть</i> (doxnútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polecat, ferret (mammal)</li><li>(rare,&#32;historical) a deserter who esaped the army and joined any para-military group during the Russian Civil War (1917-24)</li></ol><p>Diminutive of хорь (xorʹ), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dъxorь</i> (literally “ferret, stinky”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hosomaki</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>細巻</i> (hosomaki, literally “slender roll”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) host (computer attached to a network)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>host</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) hosting</li></ol><p>Transliteration of English <i>hosting</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hot dog</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hot dog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) wish, desire</li></ol><p>From <i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ, “to want”) +&lrm; <i>-лка</i> (-lka, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>now&#32;colloquial</i>) wish, desire, want, lust</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хоте́ние</i> (xoténije)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to want, to desire [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <i>or</i> infinitive <i>or</i> <b>что́бы</b> (štóby, + past-tense clause)]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хотѣти</i> (xotěti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xotěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to want, to desire, to feel like (doing/having/etc.)</li></ol><p><i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>although</li><li>even if</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>at least, if only</li><li>for example, even, you may, might as well</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, shortened from <i>хотя́</i> (xotjá), probably from Old East Slavic <i>хотѧ</i> (xotę, present active participle of <i>хотѣти</i> (xotěti)). Compare Polish <i>choć</i> and its derivation from Old Polish <i>chocia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>although, though (<i>contrasting two clauses</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) at least (at a lower limit), even, if only</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, probably inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хотѧ</i> (xotę, present active participle of <i>хотѣти</i> (xotěti)). Cognate with Polish <i>chocia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>though, even if, even though</li><li>if only</li><li>at least (at a lower limit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хоте́ть</i> (xotétʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khokhloma, a traditional style of Russian art painted on wooden handicrafts.</li></ol><p>Named after the village of Khokhloma in Nizhny Novgorod Oblast where it first appeared in the latter 17th century.</p><p>Not to be confused with the slur хохо́л (xoxól).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) Discussion of a political situation in Ukraine, or of relationships between Russia and Ukraine.</li></ol><p>From <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “a Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>срач</i> (srač, “shit, a quarrel”), from <i>срать</i> (sratʹ, “to shit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;diminutive) Ukrainian female</li><li>crested hen or any other bird, tappit-hen</li><li>pochard (<i>Netta</i>)</li><li>great crested grebe (<i>Colymbus cristatus</i>)</li><li>little auk (<i>Alle alle</i>)</li><li>(Kostroma,&#32;dialectal) lid</li></ol><p><i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “khokhol”) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p><p><i>хохли́ть</i> (xoxlítʹ, literally “to ruffle up”) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška) or <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “crest”) +&lrm; <i>-ушка</i> (-uška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) khokhol, a Ukrainian person.</li></ol><p><i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól, “a khokhol, a Ukrainian”) +&lrm; <i>-яцкий</i> (-jackij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joke, anecdote (funny or amusing story)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) joke (something said or done for amusement and not in seriousness)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>חכמה</i> (khokhme, “wit”), from Hebrew <i>חוכמה</i> (khokhmá, “wisdom”). A well-known folk etymology relates the noun to <i>хохотать</i> (xoxotatʹ, “to laugh”).</p><p>Seems to originate from Odessan dialect, and from there spread around during the 20th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) <i>(birds)</i> crest, <i>(human hair)</i> topknot, tuft of hair</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a Ukrainian, khokhol</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive,&#32;specifically) a Ukrainian nationalist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;хохолъ</i> (&#42;xoxolŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoxolъ</i> &lt; <i>&#42;koxolъ</i>. Compare Polish <i>chochoł</i>, Polish <i>chachoł</i> and Czech <i>chochol</i>.</p><p>The usage in reference to Ukrainian people comes from the оселе́дець (oselédecʹ), a traditional top-knot used by Ukrainian men. It acquired a derogatory connotation and is considered an ethnic slur as well as all the terms derived from it.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хохолъкъ</i> (xoxolŭkŭ). By surface analysis, <i>хохо́л</i> (xoxól) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laughter, roar, guffaw</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoxotъ</i>, related to <i>&#42;xoxotati</i> (“to laugh loudly”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh loudly, to laugh heartily, to roar with laughter, to guffaw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xoxotati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh loudly, to laugh heartily, to roar with laughter, to guffaw <i>(semelfactive)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) at least, if only, at least</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(fast speech,&#32;colloquial,&#32;dialectal) pronunciation spelling of <i>хо́чешь</i> (xóčešʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brave man</li></ol><p>From <i>хра́брый</i> (xrábryj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gamely, bravely, courageously, boldly</li></ol><p><i>хра́брый</i> (xrábryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bravery; courage, valour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>храбрость</i> (xrabrostĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorbrostь</i>. Displaced the native East Slavic form <i>хоробрость</i> (xorobrostʹ). By surface analysis, <i>хра́брый</i> (xrábryj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave, courageous (strong in the face of fear)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>храбръ</i> (xrabrŭ, “brave”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorbrъ</i> (“brave”). Displaced the native East Slavic form <i>хоро́брый</i> (xoróbryj, “brave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple, shrine</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) church, cathedral (a place of Christian worship that has an altar)</li><li>(also&#32;figuratively,&#32;poetic) shrine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>храмъ</i> (xramŭ) via Old East Slavic <i>храмъ</i> (xramŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xormъ</i>. Doublet of <i>хоро́мы</i> (xorómy) from <i>хоромъ</i> (xoromŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) temple</li></ol><p><i>храм</i> (xram) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khramulya (<i>Capoeta</i> and related genera)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ხრამული</i> (xramuli), from <i>ხრამი</i> (xrami).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>храм</i> (xram).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custody, safekeeping (temporary possession)</li><li>storage, storing</li></ol><p><i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depository, repository, depot, storehouse, warehouse (a location for storage, often for safety or preservation)</li></ol><p><i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) data warehouse</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>data warehouse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper, guardian, custodian</li><li>curator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хранитель</i> (xranitelĭ). By surface analysis, <i>храни́ть</i> (xranítʹ, “to keep, to guard”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep (to maintain possession of), to preserve, to store</li><li>to guard, to save, to protect</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хранити</i> (xraniti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xorniti</i>. Doublet of <i>хорони́ть</i> (xoronítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snore (act and noise of snoring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snore (breathe during sleep with harsh noises)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xrapěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snorer</li></ol><p>From <i>храпе́ть</i> (xrapétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ун</i> (-un).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spine, spinal column, backbone (<i>also figuratively</i>)</li><li>crest (<i>of a wave or hill</i>)</li><li>ridge, range (a chain of mountains)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xrьbьtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horseradish (plant, condiment)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;minced oath,&#32;colloquial) prick (penis); used as a general curse and intensifier: crap, hell, nuts</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;colloquial) prick, asshole (a term of contempt for a man)</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;colloquial) old fart</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хрѣнъ</i> (xrěnŭ)?, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xrěnъ</i> (“horseradish”).</p><p>Euphemistic alteration of <i>хуй</i> (xuj, a vulgar curse) or <i>хер</i> (xer, a less vulgar version), influenced by the noun <i>хрен</i> (xren, “horseradish”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;colloquial) upstart, fainaiguer, one who comes from far-off with dubious credentials</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) augmentative of <i>хрен</i> (xren); a big horseradish root</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;Siberia,&#32;Germany) khrenovina (a condiment made from shredded tomatoes, horseradish, garlic and salt)</li><li>(minced oath,&#32;mildly vulgar) thing, object, thingamajig</li><li>(minced oath,&#32;mildly vulgar,&#32;derogatory) wass, rubbish, nonsense</li></ol><p><i>хрен</i> (xren) +&lrm; <i>-о́вина</i> (-óvina).</p><p>A euphemistic alteration of <i>хуёвина</i> (xujóvina, vulgar curse), analyzable as <i>хрен</i> (xren) +&lrm; <i>-о́вина</i> (-óvina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hrenovuha, khrenovukha, khrenovka (a horseradish vodka made in Russia and Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lousily, wretchedly, crappily</li></ol><p><i>хрено́вый</i> (xrenóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hrenovuha, khrenovukha, khrenovka (a horseradish vodka made in Russia and Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horseradish</li><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial) worthless, bad, rotten, lousy, crappy</li></ol><p><i>хрен</i> (xren) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p><p>A euphemistic alteration of <i>хуёвый</i> (xujóvyj, a vulgar curse) or <i>херо́вый</i> (xeróvyj, a less vulgar version). By surface analysis, <i>хрен</i> (xren) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khrenovina (a condiment made from shredded tomatoes, horseradish, garlic and salt)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thingy, dealy, trifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mildly vulgar</i>) thing, object, thingy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reader, reading book</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) proverbial, hackneyed</li></ol><p><i>хрестома́тия</i> (xrestomátija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrestomathy, anthology, reader, reading book</li></ol><p>Internationalism attested since c. 1900, via Latin <i>chrestomatia</i> from Ancient Greek <i>χρηστομάθεια</i> (khrēstomátheia).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrysanthemum</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) chrysomelid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Chrysomelidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) chrysomeline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Chrysomelinae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) chrysoprase (a variety of light-green translucent quartz)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>chrysoprassus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χρυσόπρασος</i> (khrusóprasos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheeze (piping or whistling sound caused by difficult respiration)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wheeze</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to speak hoarsely, to croak</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to crackle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xripěti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хрип</i> (xrip) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hoarsely</li></ol><p><i>хри́плый</i> (xríplyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat hoarse</li></ol><p><i>хри́плый</i> (xríplyj) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hoarse, husky</li></ol><p><i>хрип</i> (xrip) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become hoarse</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;low colloquial) to wheeze (<i>once</i>)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;low colloquial) to say hoarsely</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>хрип</i> (xrip) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarseness</li></ol><p><i>хрип</i> (xrip) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>хрипота́</i> (xripotá, “hoarseness”)</li></ol><p><i>хрипота́</i> (xripotá) +&lrm; <i>-ца́</i> (-cá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Christadelphian (<i>male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Christadelphian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christian</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Χρῑστιᾱνός</i> (Khrīstiānós) +&lrm; <i>-и́н</i> (-ín).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Christian (of, like or relating to Christianity)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christianity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Christ’s, alternative letter-case form of <i>Христо́в</i> (Xristóv)</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Христов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) Christ’s</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Messiah, the anointed one</li><li>(<i>among the Khlysts</i>) a righteous man as the embodiment of Christ</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>χριστός</i> (khristós).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Христос)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christ</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós). Doublet of <i>крест</i> (krest) and <i>крёст</i> (krjost).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;chiefly&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy) to exchange a triple kiss as an Easter salutation</li></ol><p><i>Христо́с</i> (Xristós, “Christ”) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chromium, Cr</li><li>chrome</li><li>box calf (a chrome-tanned calf skin)</li></ol><p><b>Chemical element: Cr</b> (atomic number 24)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limp, to be lame</li><li>to be poor, to leave much to be desired, to be far from perfect, to not be up to standard</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>хромо́й</i> (xromój) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to formalise (to make formal)</li></ol><p>German <i>chromier(en)</i> +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chrome, chromium; chromic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) chromic acid</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) box calf</li></ol><p><i>хром</i> (xrom) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, limping (unable to walk properly because of a problem with one's feet or legs)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) lame (bad)</li><li>wobbly (<i>of furniture</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lame person</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xromъ</i> with <i>-о́й</i> (-ój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) chromosome</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) chromosphere</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lameness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xromota</i>. By surface analysis, <i>хром(о́й)</i> (xrom(ój)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) chronicle</li><li>chronicle, news items, current events, newsreel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Chronik</i>, from Latin <i>chronica</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>χρονικός</i> (khronikós), from <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos), of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chronic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chronological</li></ol><p><i>хроноло́гия</i> (xronológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chronology (determining the order of events)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chronometer (device for measuring time)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timekeeping, time study, timing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chronométrage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fragile (easily broken or destroyed)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xrupъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragility, frailty</li><li>delicacy</li></ol><p><i>хру́пкий</i> (xrúpkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crunch, crackle</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хруста́ль</i> (xrustálʹ); a small piece of cut glass or rock crystal</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lens (<i>of an eye</i>)</li></ol><p><i>хруста́ль</i> (xrustálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crystal, cut glass, lead glass (glass with a high refractive index, used for making fine glassware&#59; <i>by extension</i>, fine glassware made of such glass)</li><li>rock crystal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Byzantine Greek <i>κρουστάλλιον</i> (kroustállion), a diminutive form of <i>κρούσταλλον</i> (kroústallon), a variant of Byzantine Greek <i>κρύσταλλον</i> (krústallon), both ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>κρύσταλλος</i> (krústallos). Compare Greek <i>κρούσταλλο</i> (kroústallo). The starting <i>х-</i> was possibly influenced by <i>хрусте́ть</i> (xrustétʹ). The gender was likely influenced by the doublet <i>кристалл</i> (kristall), which entered Russian via Latin <i>crystallum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) crystal, cut glass (relating to glass with a high refractive index or to fine glassware made of such glass)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) rock crystal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) crystalline, crystal-clear (resembling fine glassware&#59; clear and transparent)</li></ol><p><i>хруста́ль</i> (xrustálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crunch, to crackle</li></ol><p><i>хру́стнуть</i> (xrústnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crunch, to crackle</li></ol><p>Apparently <i>хруст</i> (xrust) + <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockchafer</li><li>May beetle (<i>Phyllophaga</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>хрущъ</i> (xrušč), from Old East Slavic, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xrǫščь</i>. Cognate to <i>хрусте́ть</i> (xrustétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) khrushchevka (an unofficial name of a type of low-cost, concrete-paneled or brick three- to five-storied apartment building which was developed in the USSR during the early 1960s, during the time of its namesake Nikita Khrushchev)</li><li>apartment in such a building</li></ol><p><i>Хрущёв</i> (Xruščóv) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Named after Soviet&#32;Communist party First Secretary&#32;Nikita Khrushchev.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Khrushchev (former leader of the Soviet Union)</li></ol><p><i>Хрущёв</i> (Xruščóv) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) geezer, old fart, buzzard (old man)</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>хрю́кать</i> (xrjúkatʹ, “to oink, to grunt”), ultimately onomatopoeic. According to alternative hypotheses, the term might be related to <i>хра́кать</i> (xrákatʹ, “to expectorate”) or to Old East Slavic <i>гричь</i> (gričĭ, “dog”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) oink (sound a pig makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) oinking, grunting (<i>of pigs</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an oink, a grunt</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>хрю́кать</i> (xrjúkatʹ, “to oink, to grunt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oink, to grunt</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oink, to grunt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;disapproving) grub, slop (tasteless food prepared from a disorderly mixture of various products)</li></ol><p>The first element is related to <i>хрю</i> (xrju, “oink”) and derives from an association with pigfeed.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male pig; boar, hog</li><li>old fart</li></ol><p>From <i>хрю́кать</i> (xrjúkatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>crack</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cartilage, gristle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xręščь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хре́н</i> (xrén).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Southern Russia,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Belarus,&#32;colloquial,&#32;proscribed) nonstandard form of <i>кто</i> (kto, “who”)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>хто</i> (xto).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chthonic</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “ground, soil”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;neologism) evil spirit(s); evil forces</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collectively,&#32;neologism) people, electorate</li></ol><p>Related to <i>хтони́ческий</i> (xtoníčeskij, “chthonic”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Huaqiao, overseas Chinese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 華僑／华侨 (<i>huáqiáo</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing form of <i>худо́й</i> (xudój, “slender, thin”)</li></ol><p><i>худо́й</i> (xudój) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose weight, to grow thin, to lose flesh</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) hoodie, hoody (a sweatshirt with an integral hood)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hoodie</i> or <i>hoody</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, harm</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ill, badly</li></ol><p><i>худо́й</i> (xudój) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leanness, scragginess</li><li>home inventory, appliances</li><li>livestock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>худоба</i> (xudoba), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xudoba</i>, from <i>&#42;xudъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-oba</i>. By surface analysis, <i>худо́й</i> (xudój) +&lrm; <i>-оба</i> (-oba).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fiction, belles-lettres</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>artistically</li></ol><p><i>худо́жественный</i> (xudóžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artistic skills, artistry</li></ol><p><i>худо́жественный</i> (xudóžestvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) art, arts; artistic</li></ol><p><i>худо́жество</i> (xudóžestvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) art</li><li>(<i>disapproving</i>) willful act (an action that had not been approved)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>хѫдожьство</i> (xǫdožĭstvo). Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artist, painter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>художьникъ</i> (xudožĭnikŭ, “artist, painter, master”), from <i>худогъ</i> (xudogŭ, “skillful, knacky; experienced; versed, adept, conversant; lucky”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xǫdogъ</i>, from a reflex of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handugaz</i> (whence also English <i>handy</i>), further from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;handuz</i> (whence English <i>hand</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animator, cartoonist (one who creates an animation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thin, lean, skinny</li><li>bad</li><li>worn out, torn, holey</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xudъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lean, thin</li></ol><p><i>худо-</i> (xudo-) +&lrm; <i>-ща́вый</i> (-ščávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art director (e.g. in a theater)</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>худо́жественный руководи́тель</i> (xudóžestvennyj rukovodítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) hoodoo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hoodoo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój): worse (more inferior)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>худо́й</i> (xudój): worse (more inferior)</li><li>superlative degree of <i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój): worst (most inferior)</li><li>superlative degree of <i>худо́й</i> (xudój): worst (most inferior)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>худо́й</i> (xudój, “lean, slim”): (<i>colloquial</i>) lean, slim, scraggy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;derogatory,&#32;vulgar) fucking, damn, so-called <i>(used to describe a person's position that the said person isn't fit to or only pretends to hold)</i></li></ol><p><i>хуй</i> (xuj, “prick, penis”) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) helluva lot; a great many, shitload, fuckload, fuckton</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) liar, fibber, bullshitter</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “penis (vulgar)”) +&lrm; <i>плести́</i> (plestí, “to braid; to tell nonsense”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) cocksucker (<i>a man who performs fellatio</i>)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar) cocksucker; asshole, dickhead, bell-end (<i>UK</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “dick”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-сос</i> (-sos), combining form of <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ, “to suck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>хуесо́с</i> (xujesós); little cocksucker</li></ol><p><i>хуесо́с</i> (xujesós) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to have oral sex</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to attack, to scorn using vulgar language</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to harshly criticize, to talk shit, to shit on</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to punish</li></ol><p><i>хуесо́с</i> (xujesós) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) crap, shit (something worthless or unpleasant)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) crap, shit, nonsense (something hard to understand or pointless)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuss</li></ol><p><i>хуй</i> (xuj) +&lrm; <i>-ета́</i> (-etá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something worthless, low-quality</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) nonsense</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) some object</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to become impudent, to become brazen, to become insolent</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to be shocked, to be very surprised</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to feel very miserable for some reason, to freeze, (nearly) to die of hunger, to cold, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “prick”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>хуй</i> (xuj): (little) dick, cock, prick</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pejorative of <i>хуй</i> (xuj): (nasty) dick, cock, prick</li></ol><p><i>хуй</i> (xuj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>пло́хо</i> (plóxo)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ху́до</i> (xúdo)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>плохо́й</i> (ploxój)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>худо́й</i> (xudój)</li></ol><p>Comparative from Old East Slavic <i>худъ</i> (xudŭ, “bad”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xudъ</i> (“thin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) augmentative of <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock, dick”)</li></ol><p><i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock, dick”) +&lrm; <i>-ище</i> (-išče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) cock, dick, prick (meaning a penis or a highly contemptible male person)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) fuck all, nothing</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) nobody</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo&#32;slang) someone (male), a nobody</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>хуи</i> (xui), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xȗjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) the fuck you will! (<i>expl.</i> the hell you say!)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fuck you!, go fuck yourself! (<i>expl.</i> go fly a kite!)</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй &#43; тебе́</i> (xuj &#43; tebé, literally “dick to you”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;offensive) dickhead, dipshit, asshole</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit, crap (A problem or difficult situation)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit (nonsense, bullshit)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit (rubbish&#59; worthless matter)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit (stuff, things)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) nothing (not a real problem)</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) whatchamacallit (unrecognized object)</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “dick”) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja); compare <i>фигня́</i> (fignjá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>boxing</i>) hook</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hook</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;literary) animadversion, reviling, abuse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hulaguid</li></ol><p><i>хулагуи́д</i> (xulaguíd) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo slang) <i>(emphatic interrogative particle)</i> why the fuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo slang) <i>(emphatic interrogative particle)</i> what the fuck</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo slang) so fucking what, of fucking course! (in reply to a stupid question)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “penis”) + <i>ли</i> (li, “whether”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hooligan</li><li>halligan, Halligan bar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hooligan</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>halligan</i>, influenced by <i>хулига́н</i> (xuligán, “hooligan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave like a hooligan; to engage in hooliganism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rowdy, rampageous, ruffian-like, ruffianly</li></ol><p><i>хулига́н</i> (xuligán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hooliganism, disorderly conduct</li></ol><p><i>хулига́н</i> (xuligán) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to revile, to abuse, to defame, to slander</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xuliti</i>. Compare <i>хула́</i> (xulá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ху́ли</i> (xúli)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ху́ли</i> (xúli)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) hummus</li><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;Israel) chickpeas</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Arabic حُمُّص (ḥummuṣ), possibly via or reinforced by Hebrew <i>חוּמוּס</i> (khumus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hongweibing (<i>Red Guards in China, 1966–1967</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) vigilante; titushky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 紅衛兵／红卫兵 (<i>hóngwèibīng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) hongweibing (<i>Red Guards in China, 1966–1967</i>)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;derogatory) vigilante; titushky</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 紅衛兵／红卫兵 (<i>hóngwèibīng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Xiongnu (person or language)</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;historical) Xiongnu (ancient nomadic people)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>junta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>junta</i>, from Latin <i>iunctus</i>, perfect passive participle of <i>iungō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khural or hural, national congress (in Mongolia); also in Buryatia, Kalmykia or Tuva (in Russia)</li></ol><p>Mongolian <i>хурал</i> (xural) or <i>ᠬᠤᠷᠠᠯ</i> (qural).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persimmon (fruit)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خرما (hurmâ), from Classical Persian خُرْمَا (xurmā, “date (fruit)”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) shawl, neckpiece, kerchief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ху́стка</i> (xústka) or Belarusian <i>ху́стка</i> (xústka), cf. Polish <i>chustka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khutor, farm, farmstead</li><li>(Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) hamlet, Cossack settlement</li></ol><p>Ultimately, from Old High German <i>huntari</i> (“hundred”) (in the sense of an ancient administrative division); cognate with Ukrainian <i>ху́тір</i> (xútir), Polish <i>chutor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ху́тор</i> (xútor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer</li></ol><p><i>ху́тор</i> (xútor) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>хушу́р</i> (xušúr)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Mongolian and Buryat dish of khuushuur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mongolian <i>хуушуур</i> (xuušuur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonstandard</i>) alternative spelling of <i>хушу́р</i> (xušúr)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Hui person; the Hui people</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 回 (<i>huí</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) ellipsis of <i>ни хуя́ (себе́)</i> (ni xujá (sebé)): holy shit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do something intensively (replaces a number of verbs)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hit, to beat, to strike</li><li>to knock</li><li>to work (hard), to work one's ass off</li><li>to move, to walk (a long distance)</li><li>to play an instrument</li><li>to shake</li><li>to fall (of rain or snow)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) what the fuck, holy shit</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) having a large cock</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;dated) male</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “cock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do something intensively - to hit, to beat, to strike; to knock; to work (hard), to work one's ass off; to move, to walk (a long distance); to play an instrument; to shake; to fall (of rain or snow) (replaces a number of verbs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) thing, object, thingy</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) something incomprehensible</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lousily, wretchedly, crappily, shittily</li></ol><p><i>хуёвый</i> (xujóvyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) lousy, wretched, worthless, crappy, shitty</li></ol><p>From <i>хуй</i> (xuj, “prick, penis”) +&lrm; <i>-ёвый</i> (-jóvyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Heilongjiang</li></ol><p>From <i>Хэйлунцзя́н</i> (Xɛjlunczján) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Halloween</li></ol><p><i>Хэллоуи́н</i> (Xɛllouín) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happening (spontaneous or improvised event, especially one that involves audience participation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>happening</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hash (the &#35; symbol&#59; key generated by a hash function)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) hashtag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>hashtag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly in Korean media,&#32;men's speech) hyung (elder brother, or an affectionate term of address for a somewhat older man)</li><li>(<i>South Korean idol fandom</i>) hyung (older male member of an idol group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>형(兄)</i> (hyeong, “older brother of a man”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>хер</i> (xer, “letter Х, х”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (UK) tut-tut-tut, (US) tsk-tsk-tsk (sound of regret or disapproval)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass script, caoshu, tsao shu (a style of cursive script used in Chinese calligraphy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 草書／草书 (<i>cǎoshū</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) Used to denote a quick grasping action or movement, especially a cat scratch&#47;pounce.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;capъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch, snap, grab, seize</li><li>to scratch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;capati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heron, egret (bird)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) beanpole, a tall, thin person (m or f)</li><li><i>С</i>, <i>ts</i> (in the Russian spelling alphabet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čapľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>journal, pin, pivot (part of a shaft or axle that rests on bearings)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zapfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch, to scrape</li><li>to scrawl, to scribble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to scratch</li><li>to quarrel</li></ol><p><i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scratch, abrasion</li></ol><p>Deverbal of <i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ, “to scratch, scrawl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch</li></ol><p><i>цара́пать</i> (carápatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to reign, to rule, to dominate</li></ol><p><i>царь</i> (carʹ, “tsar”) +&lrm; <i>-ева́ть</i> (-evátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsarevich (son of a tsar, a prince in Russia or Bulgaria)</li></ol><p>From <i>царь</i> (carʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евич</i> (-evič). Piecewise doublet of <i>цесаре́вич</i> (cesarévič).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>царе́вич</i> (carévič).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basileiolatry</li></ol><p><i>царь</i> (carʹ, “tsar, king”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>поклон(я́ться)</i> (poklon(játʹsja), “to worship”) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>regicide (action)</li></ol><p>From <i>царь</i> (carʹ, “tsar”) +&lrm; <i>уби́йство</i> (ubíjstvo, “murder”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingslayer, king killer</li></ol><p>From <i>царь</i> (carʹ, “czar”) + <i>уби́йца</i> (ubíjca, “murderer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsarism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to be a tsar (monarch), to reign</li><li>to reign, to dominate, to prevail</li></ol><p>From <i>царь</i> (carʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empress, queen (in ancient or non-European monarchies)</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) queen</li></ol><p>From <i>царь</i> (carʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aqua regia (A.R.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) Great Hours</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>czarist, imperial, of the czar</li><li>royal, regal, kingly</li></ol><p><i>царь</i> (carʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ца́рские часы́</i> (cárskije časý).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ца́рский</i> (cárskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royalty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>royal, regal</li><li>kingly</li><li>queenly, queenlike</li><li>princely</li><li>august (noble, majestic, awe-inspiring)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsardom, kingdom, reign, ruling, governing (of an emperor)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;figuratively</i>) empire, kingdom; tsardom</li><li>rule</li><li>reign</li></ol><p><i>царь</i> (carʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsardom, kingdom, reign, ruling, governing (of an emperor)</li></ol><p><i>ца́рствовать</i> (cárstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reign</li></ol><p><i>ца́рство</i> (cárstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsar, Russian emperor</li><li>king (figuratively, or referring to ancient or non-European monarchs)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цьсарь</i> (cĭsarĭ), from <i>цѣсарь</i> (cěsarĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěsařь</i>, from a Germanic language, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kaisaraz</i>, from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́йзер</i> (kájzer), <i>ке́сарь</i> (késarʹ), <i>це́зарь</i> (cézarʹ), and <i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) toy</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) knickknack, trinket, tchotchke, tsatske</li></ol><p>From <i>ца́ца</i> (cáca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from a Proto-Slavic source also giving Polish <i>cacko</i>, Slovak <i>čačka</i>, Ukrainian <i>цяцька</i> (cjacʹka), of uncertain origin, but assumed to be an imitative/children's nursery word.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bloom, to blossom, to flower</li><li>to flourish, to prosper</li><li><i>water</i> to become overgrown, to be covered with duckweed/mold</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kvisti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>color</li><li>blossom, bloom, flower</li><li>(<i>figuratively&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) flower, cream, pick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цвѣтъ</i> (cvětŭ). Cognate with English <i>white</i>.</p><p>From <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colorful, gaudy, garish</li></ol><p><i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blossom, blossoming, blooming, flowering</li></ol><p><i>цвет-</i> (cvet-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) April</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;archaic) pollen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;květьňь</i> from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;květъ</i>. Related to цвето́к (cvetók, “flower”), referring to flowers which bloom at this time of year. Cognates include Polish <i>kwiecień</i> (“April”), Ukrainian <i>кві́тень</i> (kvítenʹ), Czech <i>květen</i> (“May”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cauliflower (vegetable)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower garden, parterre, flowerbed</li><li>a bevy of beautiful women</li></ol><p><i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>color/colour, colored/coloured (<i>as contrasted with black-and-white</i>)</li><li>colorful/colourful</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) non-ferrous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a non-European, a colored/coloured person</li><li>a mixed-race person</li></ol><p><i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) color</li></ol><p><i>цвет</i> (cvet) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) flowerpecker</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) flower chafer</li></ol><p><i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ед</i> (-ed, “-eater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower (plant that bears flowers)</li><li>flower (reproductive organ of plants)</li></ol><p>From <i>цвет</i> (cvet, “colour”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók): (small) flower, blossom, floweret, floret</li></ol><p><i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) flower</li><li>floral</li></ol><p><i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blooming, blossoming, flowering</li><li><i>(person)</i> blooming, in one's prime</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) blossom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цвести́</i> (cvestí).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цве́тень</i> (cvétenʹ, “April”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цве́тень</i> (cvétenʹ, “pollen”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók, “flower (flowering part of a plant)”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цвето́к</i> (cvetók, “flower (with stem), flowering plant; potted home plant”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цвет</i> (cvet, “color”).</li><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цвет</i> (cvet, “blossom, flower”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bobbin</li><li>bone (especially a hollow one)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) shinbone</li><li>tarsometatarsus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цѣвъка</i> (cěvŭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěvъka</i>, from <i>&#42;cěva</i> (“shine-bone, tube, bobbin, spool”) + <i>&#42;-ъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mechanics</i>) swivel, spindle</li><li>(<i>firearms</i>) handguard, forend</li></ol><p>From <i>цепь</i> (cepʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;chiefly&#32;Germany) compact disc</li></ol><p>From German <i>CD</i> +&lrm; <i>-шка</i> (-ška), the heterogram inside Russian sentences being pronounced <i>цэ-дэ́</i> (cɛ-dɛ́) as opposed to <i>си-ди́</i> (si-dí) for those whose archetypical Latin-script language is English.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strain, to filter; to decant</li><li>to speak through clenched teeth, to mutter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zest (the outer skin of a citrus fruit)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Caesar (title of a Roman emperor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caesar</i>, probably through German <i>Cäsar</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́йзер</i> (kájzer), <i>ке́сарь</i> (késarʹ), <i>царь</i> (carʹ), and <i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Цезарь)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesar (ancient Roman name)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caesar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caesium (<i>UK</i>), cesium (<i>US</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caesura (a pause or interruption)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Ceylon</li></ol><p><i>Цейло́н</i> (Cejlón) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeitnot (time trouble, time pressure (chess))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zeitnot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;obsolete) armoury, arsenal (place where arms are kept)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zeughaus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coelacanth (either of two species of deep-water fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>medicinal, salubrious, healthy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>цѣльбьнъ</i> (cělĭbĭnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cělьbьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, (<i>archaic</i>) <i>цельба́</i> (celʹbá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>marketing</i>) target audience (the primary group of people that something, usually an advertising campaign, is aimed at appealing to)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>marketing</i>) synonym of <i>целева́я аудито́рия</i> (celevája auditórija, “target audience”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a special purpose, purposeful, earmarked for a special purpose</li></ol><p><i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>purposefully, in a dedicated or committed manner</li></ol><p><i>целенапра́вленный</i> (celenaprávlennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purposeful, committed, dedicated</li></ol><p><i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>напра́вленный</i> (naprávlennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>expediently</li><li>advisably</li></ol><p><i>целесообра́зный</i> (celesoobráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasonability, suitability</li></ol><p><i>целесообра́зный</i> (celesoobráznyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expedient, expeditious; worthwhile, advisable</li></ol><p>From <i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>сообра́зный</i> (soobráznyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) targeting, target designation (precise indication of a goal or target, so it can be located by landmarks or through a topographic map)</li></ol><p><i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>указа́ние</i> (ukazánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purposefulness, resoluteness, (sense of) purpose</li></ol><p><i>целеустремлённый</i> (celeustremljónnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goal-driven, goal-oriented, focused (on a goal)</li></ol><p><i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>устремлённый</i> (ustremljónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coeliac disease (auto-immune disease characterised by sensitivity of the lining of the small intestine to gluten)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>coeliacus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κοιλῐᾰκός</i> (koilĭăkós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celibacy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholly, all of it</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tselina; virgin lands, virgin soil</li><li>unbroken expanse</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cělina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) virgin (of land, soil)</li></ol><p><i>целина́</i> (celiná) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healer</li></ol><p><i>цели́ть</i> (celítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healing, curing, salubrious</li></ol><p><i>цели́ть</i> (celítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim <i>(at)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to aim <i>(for; to become)</i></li><li>(<i>lofty</i>) to heal</li></ol><p><i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цѣлити</i> (cěliti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěliti</i>, from <i>&#42;cělъ</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-iti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>це́лый</i> (célyj) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ). Cognate with English <i>heal</i> (related to English <i>whole</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim</li></ol><p><i>це́лить</i> (célitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>virgin</li><li>heifer</li><li>cherry (virginity or hymen)</li></ol><p><i>це́лый</i> (célyj) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a silver coin worth one ruble</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;somewhat&#32;dated) ruble</li></ol><p>Ultimately from це́лый (célyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellophane (the transparent plastic film)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cellophane</li></ol><p><i>целлофа́н</i> (cellofán) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obsolete form of <i>целлюло́за</i> (celljulóza)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celluloid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>celluloid</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) celluloid</li></ol><p><i>целлуло́ид</i> (cellulóid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) celluloid</li></ol><p><i>целлуло́ид</i> (cellulóid) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellulite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cellulose</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>cēllula</i> via French <i>cellulose</i> or German <i>Zellulose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cellulosic</li></ol><p><i>целлюло́за</i> (celljulóza) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss, to give a kiss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цѣловати</i> (cělovati, “to greet; to kiss”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cělovati</i> (“to greet; to kiss”), from <i>&#42;cělъ</i> (“whole”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol><p><i>целова́ть</i> (celovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the whole</li><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) integer (whole number)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>integer, whole number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celosia (<i>Celosia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Celosia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chaste (аbstaining from sexual intercourse)</li></ol><p><i>целому́дрие</i> (celomúdrije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chastity (abstaining from sexual intercourse outside of marriage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>цѣломѫдриѥ</i> (cělomǫdrije). By surface analysis, <i>це́лый</i> (célyj, “whole”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>му́дрый</i> (múdryj, “wise”) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>integrity</li></ol><p><i>це́лостный</i> (célostnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholistic, integrated, unified</li></ol><p><i>це́лость</i> (célostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unity, wholeness</li><li>safety [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>це́лый</i> (célyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li><li>intact, safe</li><li>integer</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>цѣлъ</i> (cělŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cělъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;káilas</i>. Compare Czech <i>celý</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all day</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goal, target, aim, end, object</li><li>purpose</li><li>destination (<i>of traveller</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>cel</i> (“aim, goal”), in turn from Middle High German <i>zil</i> (whence German <i>Ziel</i>); compare Bulgarian <i>цел</i> (cel).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whole milk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wholeness, entirety</li><li>unbroken or uninjured state (persons or things)</li></ol><p><i>це́льный</i> (célʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, unbroken, of one piece</li><li>whole, undiluted</li><li>integral, unified</li><li>whole-hearted</li></ol><p>Possibly <i>цель</i> (celʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cement (a powdered substance)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zement</i>, from Latin <i>caementum</i> (“quarry stone; stone chips for making mortar”), from <i>caedo</i> (“to cut, hew”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cement</li></ol><p><i>цеме́нт</i> (cemént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price, cost</li><li>worth</li><li>value</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>цѣна</i> (cěna), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěna</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kainā́ˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷoynéh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to pay”), whence English <i>pain</i> and <i>penalty</i>. Probably, a doublet of <i>пе́ня</i> (pénja, “fine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) census (<i>in Ancient Rome</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) quit-rent</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) qualification, restriction, eligibility criterion (e.g. restriction on the right to vote, based on age, gender, education, possession of property, etc.)</li><li>conditions needed to get a business in a given list or registry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cēnsus</i>. Doublet of <i>чинш</i> (činš).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to censor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>censorship</li><li>censorship office</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>censura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to censor</li></ol><p><i>цензура</i> (cenzura) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ), or borrowed from German <i>zensurieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) censorship</li><li>printable, stageable, etc. (<i>under censorship rules</i>)</li><li>decent, proper, not obscene</li></ol><p><i>цензу́ра</i> (cenzúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to censor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appreciator, judge, connoisseur</li></ol><p><i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appreciate (to value highly)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěniti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be valued/estimated <i>(at)</i></li><li>to be valued highly, to have a high value</li></ol><p><i>цени́ть</i> (cenítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>це́нный</i> (cénnyj): most valuable, most precious, costliest</li></ol><p><i>це́нный</i> (cénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́йший</i> (-éjšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price tag</li><li>price list</li></ol><p><i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) value</li></ol><p><i>це́нность</i> (cénnostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>value, importance, worth</li><li>valuable (<i>often in plural</i>)</li></ol><p><i>це́нный</i> (cénnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>valuable, precious, costly, dear</li></ol><p><i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) price</li></ol><p><i>цена́</i> (cená) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price formation, pricing</li></ol><p>From <i>цена́</i> (cená, “price”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>образова́ние</i> (obrazovánije, “formation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cent (hundredth of a dollar, euro, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>centum</i> (“hundred”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;Greek mythology) centaur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>centillion (10<sup>303</sup>, or 10<sup>600</sup> long scale)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centner, quintal (<i>100 kilograms</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zentner</i>. Further from Latin <i>centēnārius</i>, from <i>centēnus</i>, from Latin <i>centum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>center, centre</li><li>(<i>telephony</i>) Charlie (<i>C</i> in the ICAO spelling alphabet)</li><li>downtown, city centre</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>centrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centralization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centralism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>centralized</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Central African</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>central</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>central bank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centrist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>centrist</li></ol><p><i>центри́ст</i> (centríst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centrifuge (device for separation of substances)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>centrifugal</li></ol><p><i>центр</i> (centr) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бег</i> (beg) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centurion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>centuriō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flail (tool)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěpъ</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>ције̑п</i>, Polish <i>cepy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow torpid, to become rigid, to freeze</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěpeněti</i>. Akin to <i>цеп</i> (cep).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>коле́ть</i>&nbsp;impf (kolétʹ), <i>околе́ть</i>&nbsp;pf (okolétʹ) (&lt; <i>кол</i> (kol)), <i>кочене́ть</i> (kočenétʹ) (akin to <i>коча́н</i> (kočán)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(parasitic) tapeworm (<i>referring to various species</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenacious, prehensile</li><li>retentive, tenacious</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) persistent, tenacious</li></ol><p><i>цеп-</i> (cep-) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tenaciously, firmly</li></ol><p><i>це́пкий</i> (cépkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persistence, tenaciousness</li></ol><p>From <i>це́пк(ий)</i> (cépk(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to hook <i>(up)</i></li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) to pick <i>(up)</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>-цепи́ть</i> (-cepítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch onto, to clutch at, to become attached to</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pick on, to find fault</li></ol><p><i>цепля́ть</i> (cepljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chain</li></ol><p><i>цепь</i> (cepʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>цепь</i> (cepʹ) chain, chainlet (series of interconnected rings or links)</li><li>choker (jewellery)</li></ol><p>From <i>це́пка</i> (cépka, “small chain, chainlet”) (<i>folk poetic</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), or directly from <i>цепь</i> (cepʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о́чка</i> (-óčka). In either case, the stress on the suffix is unexpected.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeppelin/Zeppelin (a type of large German dirigible airship of the early 20th century)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zeppelin</i>. Named after Ferdinand von <i>Zeppelin</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain</li><li>row, series</li><li>electric circuit</li><li>(<i>military</i>) line, file</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чепь</i> (čepĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cerebral (relating to the brain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cerebral palsy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ceremonial</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand upon ceremony</li><li>to show excessive scrupulousness, to take too much account of anyone, anything</li></ol><p><i>церемо́ния</i> (ceremónija) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceremony</li><li>formalities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>церемо́ния</i> (ceremónija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cereus (<i>Cereus</i>-genus cactus)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cereus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cerium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cercus (one of a pair of appendages attached to the last abdominal segment of certain insects)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>cercus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) church (building)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>це́рковь</i> (cérkovʹ, “church”): (small) church</li></ol><p><i>це́рковь</i> (cérkovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) cleric</li></ol><p><i>це́рковь</i> (cérkovʹ, “church”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) church rite; church ritual</li></ol><p>From <i>церко̀вно-</i> (cerkòvno-) +&lrm; <i>обря́довый</i> (obrjádovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Church Slavonic</li></ol><p>From <i>церко́вный</i> (cerkóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>славя́нский</i> (slavjánskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleric, churchman, clergyman, ecclesiastic, minister (of church)</li></ol><p>From <i>церко́вный</i> (cerkóvnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>служи́тель</i> (služítelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) church</li><li>ecclesiastical</li></ol><p>From <i>це́рковь</i> (cérkovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>church (religious group)</li><li>church (house of worship)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цьркꙑ</i> (cĭrky), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cьrky</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;kirikā</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κυριακόν</i> (kuriakón). Doublet of <i>ки́рха</i> (kírxa) and <i>ки́рка</i> (kírka), see also English <i>church</i> and other Germanic cognates.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) cercopid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cercopidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lantern flower (<i>Ceropegia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ceropegia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) tsesarevich (heir apparent or presumptive in the Russian Empire, from 1797 till 1917)</li></ol><p>From <i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евич</i> (-evič). Piecewise doublet of <i>царе́вич</i> (carévič).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guinea fowl</li></ol><p><i>це́сарь</i> (césarʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) emperor, king</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>цѣсарь</i> (cěsarĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cěsařь</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>Caesar</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́йзер</i> (kájzer), <i>ке́сарь</i> (késarʹ), <i>царь</i> (carʹ), and <i>це́зарь</i> (cézarʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shop, section (of a factory)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) guild (artisans’ group)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>cech</i>, from Middle High German <i>zech</i>, <i>zeche</i> (German <i>Zeche</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Jilin</li></ol><p>From <i>Цзили́нь</i> (Czilínʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jianzhi (Chinese papercutting)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 剪紙／剪纸 (<i>jiǎnzhǐ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jiaozi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 餃子／饺子 (<i>jiǎozi</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qi, the fundamental life-force or energy.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 氣／气 (<i>qì</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cyanidium</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cyanidium</i>&nbsp;n.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyanosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;literary) civilization; civilizational</li></ol><p><i>цивилиза́ция</i> (civilizácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civilization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civilised</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to civilize</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>civiliser</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;regional) cigarette (especially hand-rolled)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Spanish <i>cigarro</i>. Doublet of <i>сигарета</i> (sigareta) and <i>сигара</i> (sigara).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qigong, qi gong, chi kung</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese 氣功／气功 (<i>qìgōng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) note, memo</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>): alternative form of <i>циду́ла</i> (cidúla)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;Ukraine,&#32;especially&#32;Odessa) interestingly, curiously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;Ukraine,&#32;especially&#32;Odessa) interesting</li><li>(archaic&#32;or&#32;Ukraine,&#32;especially&#32;Odessa) curious, inquisitive</li><li>(regional,&#32;Novorossiysk) grumpy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>ціка́вий</i> (cikávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cicada</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) leafhopper</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Cicadellidae</li></ol><p><i>цика́да</i> (cikáda, “cicada”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix), a calque of translingual <i>Cicadella</i>, the type genus of the Cicadellidae.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cycle (process)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyclamen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(general,&#32;chemistry,&#32;art) cyclic</li></ol><p><i>цикл</i> (cikl) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meteorology</i>) cyclone</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>cyclone</i>, on the model of <i>цикл</i> (cikl), <i>циклоп</i> (ciklop) and other words ultimately derived from Ancient Greek <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos). First attested in 1863.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyclops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) cyclotron</li></ol><p>Internationalism.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) cyclotronic</li></ol><p>From <i>циклотро́н</i> (ciklotrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand tool consisting of a steel plate with a sharpened edge, for cleaning surfaces, scraper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Ziehklinge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicory</li></ol><p>Derived from Ancient Greek <i>κιχόρεια, κιχώρη</i> (kikhóreia, kikhṓrē)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chicory</li></ol><p><i>цико́рий</i> (cikórij) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) cixiid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cixiidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cylinder (geometry&#58; surface)</li><li>cylinder (object in the form of a circular cylinder)</li><li>(<i>of a printing machine</i>) roller, barrel, drum</li><li>top hat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zylinder</i>, from Latin <i>cylindrus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κύλινδρος</i> (kúlindros).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cylindrical</li></ol><p><i>цили́ндр</i> (cilíndr) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cholla (<i>Cylindropuntia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cylindropuntia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) cymbal</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) cymbals, dulcimer</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) scurvy (deficiency of vitamin C)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>цынга</i> (cynga), <i>цѣнга</i> (cěnga), both att. 1608, further origin uncertain. Perhaps from rare Old Polish <i>dzięgna</i> (“stomatitis”) (att. in 15th-16th cc.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cynicism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cynisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cynic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zyniker</i>, from Latin <i>cynicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cynically</li></ol><p><i>цини́чный</i> (ciníčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cynical</li></ol><p><i>ци́ник</i> (cínik) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zinc</li><li>(<i>military</i>) ammunition box (made of galvanized steel)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Zink</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to zinc, to galvanize with zinc, to electroplate with zinc</li></ol><p><i>цинк</i> (cink) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) zinc</li></ol><p><i>цинк</i> (cink) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynoglossidae (a biological family that includes tonguefishes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Qing</li></ol><p>From <i>Цин</i> (Cin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Qinghai</li></ol><p>From <i>Цинха́й</i> (Cinxáj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>qipao</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>旗袍</i> (qípáo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) cypridid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cyprididae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circus</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) cirque (curved depression in a mountainside)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>circus</i> (probably via German <i>Zirkus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circus performer</li></ol><p><i>цирк</i> (cirk) +&lrm; <i>-ач</i> (-ač).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) circus</li></ol><p><i>цирк</i> (cirk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zircon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zirconium</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zirkonium</i> (“zirconium”), from German <i>Zirkon</i> (“zircon”), both formed in the 1780s from Arabic زَرْقُون (zarqūn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цирко́н</i> (cirkón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to circulate</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>zirkulieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ). See also <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair of compasses</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zirkel</i>, from Latin <i>circulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circulaire (written directive to subordinates)</li><li>circular barbell (piercing jewelry in the form of an incomplete circle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zirkular</i> (“circular (notice for mass distribution)”), from Latin <i>circulāris</i>.</p><p>Probably borrowed from English <i>circular (barbell)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circulation</li><li>(<i>naval</i>) gyration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) circumfix</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumflex ( ˆ )</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>circumflexus</i> (“bent about”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цирк</i> (cirk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) cirrhosis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zirrhose</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyrtandra, <i>Cyrtandra</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Cyrtandra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barber</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>cyrulik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) barbershop</li></ol><p><i>цирю́льник</i> (cirjúlʹnik) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) cis-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>networking&#32;slang</i>) a Cisco networking device (<i>usually a router or a switch</i>)</li><li>(neologism,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;often&#32;derogatory) cissy (a cisgender person)</li></ol><p>From <i>Циско</i> (Cisko, mispronunciation of Cisco) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>From <i>цис</i> (cis, “cis”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;offensive) cissies (cisgender people)</li></ol><p>From <i>цис</i> (cis, “cis”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) bladder</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) cyst</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>κύστις</i> (kústis).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) storage tank, cistern</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cystitis</li></ol><p><i>ци́ста</i> (císta) +&lrm; <i>-и́т</i> (-ít).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citadel (a strong fortress that sits high above a city)</li><li>citadel (a stronghold or fortified place, also figuratively)</li></ol><p>From Italian <i>cittadella</i>, via Polish <i>cytadela</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quote, quotation, citation (a statement attributed to someone)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zitat</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>citō</i> (whence ultimately also English <i>citation</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citing, citation</li></ol><p><i>цити́ровать</i> (citírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quote, to cite</li></ol><p>From German <i>zitieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ), from Latin <i>citāre</i>, from <i>ciēre</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biology,&#32;medicine) cytodiagnosis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biochemistry,&#32;medicine) cytokine</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from English <i>cytokine</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cytology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zytologie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cytoplasm (contents of a cell excluding the nucleus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(cytology,&#32;toxicology) cytotoxin</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>цито-</i> (cito-, “cyto-”) +&lrm; <i>токси́н</i> (toksín, “toxin”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) zither</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citrus</li><li>citrus fruit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clock face, clock dial</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zifferblatt</i>, attested since 1815.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;humorous</i>) number, same as modern ци́фра (cífra)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) count (e.g. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5); calculation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure, digit, cipher, numeral (a symbol representing a number)</li><li>figures, numbers</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) digital format</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cifra</i>, ultimately from Arabic صِفْر (ṣifr, “zero”). Doublet of <i>шифр</i> (šifr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digitization</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) digital device (usually camera)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1996. From <i>цифрово́й</i> (cifrovój) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>digital (relating to numbers or computers)</li></ol><p><i>ци́фра</i> (cífra) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p><p>Originally referred to statistics, such as for example in "Цифровой материал для изучения переселений в Сибирь" <i>(Digital material for studying resettlement in Siberia)</i>, a publication from 1896.</p><p>More recent senses associated with electronics and especially computers are assumed to be a semantic loan from English <i>digital</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digital nomad (someone who earns a living from telecommunication technology while living in a nomadic manner, typically working remotely from various locations such as coffee shops and public libraries)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>digital nomad</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to click, to clatter, to clank</li><li>to utter the sound [t͡s], to click with the tongue to make a similar sound</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce the sound [t͡ɕ] as [t͡s] in some dialects (of any language), e.g. pronounce the Russian letter <i>ч</i> (č) the same way as <i>ц</i> (c).</li></ol><p><i>цок</i> (cok) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ). Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) socle (plinth, i.e. thickened bottom part of a wall or pedestal, often used to display art work)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) base (<i>e.g. the metal base of a light bulb, used to screw it into the socket</i>)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) basement, foundation</li></ol><p>Formerly <i>цо́коло</i> (cókolo) (in the time of Peter the Great); borrowed from Italian <i>zoccolo</i>, from Latin <i>socculus</i>, diminutive of <i>soccus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zokor (any of several Asiatic burrowing rodents native to Central Asia)</li></ol><p>A Mongolic borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;sokar</i> (“blind”) (whence Mongolian <i>сохор</i> (soxor)), which is often used with Proto-Mongolic <i>&#42;noman</i> (“mole; vole; zokor”).</p><p>The forms recorded in the dialects are <i>зо́кор</i> (zókor) and <i>со́кор</i> (sókor). The literary form <i>цо́кор</i> (cókor) was probably transcribed according to German orthography from <i>zokor</i> found in Pallas's works.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>церковнославя́нский</i> (cerkovnoslavjánskij): CS, Church Slavonic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) zugzwang</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Zugzwang</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) succade</li></ol><p><i>цука́т</i> (cukát) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to scold</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to pull/jerk a horse sharply</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zucchini</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>zucchini</i>, plural of <i>zucchino</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tsunami</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>津波</i> (つなみ, tsunamí), from <i>津</i> (tsu, “harbour”) + <i>波</i> (nami, “wave”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) puppy (of a small dog)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) a young and unexperienced person</li></ol><p>From Ukrainian <i>цу́цик</i> (cúcyk), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cucьkъ</i> (“dog, puppy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) Name of the Cyrillic letter Ц, ц, called <i>цэ</i> (cɛ) in modern Russian.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ци</i> (ci).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gypsy, Rom</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ἀτζίγγανος</i> (Atzínganos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Romani, Gypsy</li></ol><p><i>цыга́н</i> (cygán) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gypsy boy, Roma boy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chicken</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) chick, girl</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) chap, minor crack in the skin</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) small wart</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hen, chicken</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) chick (girl or young woman)</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>цы́пать</i> (cýpatʹ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka), based on the nominal combining stem <i>цып-</i> (cyp-), derived from the stem <i>цап-</i> (cap-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chick, young chicken</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>цыплёнок</i> (cypljónok): (cute / small) baby chick</li></ol><p><i>цыплёнок</i> (cypljónok, “(baby) chick”) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken, hen</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) chicklet (<i>woman</i>)</li></ol><p><i>цы́пка</i> (cýpka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiptoe</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often implies impolite tone</i>) hush! shush! shh!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ц</i>/<i>ц</i>.</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>C</i>/<i>c</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an employee of the CIA</li></ol><p>From <i>ЦРУ</i> (CRU) (phonetically: <i>цэ-эр-у́</i> (cɛ-er-ú)) + <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zurich</li></ol><p><i>Цю́рих</i> (Cjúrix) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>це́лый</i> (célyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цель</i> (celʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цена́</i> (cená).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цеп</i> (cep).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>цепь</i> (cepʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>це́рковь</i> (cérkovʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>h, hour</li></ol><p>From Glagolitic <i>Ⱍ</i>, from Hebrew <i>ץ</i>.</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>час</i> (čas).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency, accident</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>чрезвыча́йное происше́ствие</i> (črezvyčájnoje proisšéstvije) or <i>чрезвыча́йное положе́ние</i> (črezvyčájnoje položénije).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ч. П.)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emergency, accident</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>чрезвыча́йное происше́ствие</i> (črezvyčájnoje proisšéstvije) or <i>чрезвыча́йное положе́ние</i> (črezvyčájnoje položénije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>QED</li></ol><p>Initialism of <i>что и тре́бовалось доказа́ть</i> (što i trébovalosʹ dokazátʹ, “as it was required to demonstrate”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cha-cha, cha-cha-cha (<i>dance</i>)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) cha-cha, cha-cha-cha</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Southern Russia,&#32;Central Asia,&#32;Caucasus) shepherd</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, from Persian چوبان (čubân).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savory (<i>Satureja</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чабьръ</i> (čabĭrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čębьrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thyme</li></ol><p><i>ча́бер</i> (čáber) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to champ, to slurp, to eat loudly, to smack one's lips (while eating)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chinook salmon, king salmon (<i>Oncorhynchus tshawytscha</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Itelmen <i>човуича</i> (see <i>чʼучʼу</i>). Ultimately from Proto-Chukotko-Kamchatkan <i>&#42;æwæc(u)</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birch canker, chaga, clinker polypore, <i>Inonotus obliquus</i></li><li>chaga tea (<i>a tea- or coffee-like beverage made by brewing chaga</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Komi-Zyrian <i>тшак</i> (čak, “mushroom, growth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) dark ashy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke, fumes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) daze, frenzy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čadъ</i> (“smoke, fumes”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷēd-o-</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Чад)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chad (a country in Central Africa)# Lake Chad (a freshwater lake at the junction of Nigeria, Niger, Chad and Cameroon in Central Africa)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, to emit fumes</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čaditi</i>. By surface analysis, <i>чад</i> (čad) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoky</li></ol><p><i>чад</i> (čad) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;humorous) child, offspring, progeny</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čędo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) loving one's children</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chador, veil, niqab (a loose robe worn by Muslim women)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chadian</li></ol><p><i>Чад</i> (Čad) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit long over one's tea, to have a cosy tea</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tea grower</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tip, gratuity</li><li>(relational,&#32;rare) tea</li></ol><p><i>чай</i> (čaj) +&lrm; <i>-ево́й</i> (-evój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tip, gratuity (small amount of money left for a servant as a token of appreciation)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>чаевы́е де́ньги</i> (čajevýje dénʹgi, “tea money”). Compare Chinese <i>茶錢</i>&nbsp;/ <i>茶钱</i> (cháqián, “tip, gratuity; bribe”) and Burmese <i>လက်ဖက်ရည်ဖိုး</i> (lakhpak-ranyhpui&#58;, “bribe”), as well as French <i>pourboire</i> (“tip, gratuity”) and German <i>Trinkgeld</i> (“tip, gratuity”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tea-drinking</li></ol><p><i>чай</i> (čaj, “tea”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>питие</i> (pitije, “a drinking”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of a tea leaf</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tea (in all senses&#58; the drink, the plant, the dried leaves, or tea-drinking)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) tip (small monetary gratuity for a service worker)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) methinks, must be, may be, hopefully, probably</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چای (çay), from Chinese 茶 (<i>chá</i>).</p><p>The “tip” sense derives from the expression <i>дава́ть на чай</i> (davátʹ na čaj) / <i>дать на чай</i> (datʹ na čaj) (literally, “to give [some money] for tea”).</p><p>Clipping of <i>ча́ю</i> (čáju), first-person singular present of <i>ча́ять</i> (čájatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seagull</li><li>Chaika (Soviet watch brand)</li><li>Chaika (series of Soviet 35 mm half-frame cameras)</li><li>Chaika (Soviet luxury automobile)</li><li>Chaika (Soviet sesquiplane fighter)</li><li>Chayka (Soviet turboprop-powered amphibious aircraft)</li><li>Chaika (Russian amphibious aircraft)</li><li>Chayka (Soviet and Russian terrestrial radio navigation system)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čajьka</i>, from onomatopoeia of cry *kē.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chinatown</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Chinatown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teahouse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle, teakettle (a vessel for boiling water)</li><li>teapot (a vessel for brewing tea)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) dummy, novice, no-good, good-for-nothing</li></ol><p>From <i>чай</i> (čaj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tea caddie (a small box used for tea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tea</li><li>tea-scented, tea-colored</li></ol><p><i>чай</i> (čaj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teabag (packet with tea)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Central Asia,&#32;Iran,&#32;Azerbaijan) teahouse, teashop (a cafe that sells tea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian چای‌خانه (čây-xâne).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chak-chak (fried sweet)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>чәкчәк</i> (çäkçäk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chakra (spiritual energy nexuses)</li><li>chakram (Indian throwing weapon)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>चक्र</i> (cakra, “wheel, circle, point”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ethnic Russian lower class person, native to rural Siberia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) to bind, to moor</li></ol><p>Uncertain, first attested from 1771, likely from Ottoman Turkish چالمق (<i>çalmak</i>, “to warp”) or a Turkic cognate of it. The noun <i>чал</i> (čal, “rope”) is rare and possibly deverbal. There is also <i>ча́лка</i> (čálka, “rope”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turban (man’s headdress)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Turkic source.</p><p>Compare Tatar <i>чалма</i> (çalma), Karachay-Balkar <i>чалма</i> (çalma), Turkish <i>çalma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) rice, paddy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>çəltik</i>, from Persian شلتوک (šaltuk, “rice still in the husk; rice-hull”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) fenugreek (plant, seed and paste)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>չաման</i> (čʻaman).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jamo (jamo - Korean letters, building blocks of hangeul)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>자모</i> (jamo) (<i>字母</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tub, vat, tank; tun</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>тшанъ</i> (tšanŭ) or <i>тчанъ</i> (tčanŭ), from <i>дъщанъ</i> (dŭščanŭ, “made of boards”), akin to <i>доска́</i> (doská), <i>доща́тый</i> (doščátyj).</p><p>Several steps omitting from Latin <i>discus</i>, whence also English <i>dish</i>, <i>desk</i>. Compare typologically <i>ми́ска</i> (míska) <&lt; Latin <i>mēnsa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;Siberia) highwayman (<i>especially in Taimyr area</i>)</li></ol><p>The word is attested in Evenki (meaning a highwayman) and Even (meaning a legendary people, who lived at the Sea of Okhotsk before Evens).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) a humanoid creature common in northern Asian mythology; during its winter hibernation, the mucus, which flows out of its nose all the way down to the ground, freezes into an icicle; if this icicle breaks, the creature dies.</li><li>a kind of traditional Yukaghir house made of earth and wood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>goodbye (farewell)</li><li>ciao</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>ciao</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to walk, move slowly, with effort</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čapati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panhandle (handle of a pan)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) heron</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čapľa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;programming) A char.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) cup, glass, bowl, goblet (for drinking alcohol)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čara</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>चरस</i> (caras).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>csardas</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, glass, goblet (for drinking alcohol)</li><li>cup (volume of liquid that can fit in such a vessel)</li><li>charka, a unit of liquid measure in Russia till 1925 containing 123 ml</li></ol><p><i>ча́ра</i> (čára) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) cocaine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Charlie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wizard, sorcerer, magician</li><li>attractive male person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attractive female person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcerer, magician, wizard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чародѣи</i> (čaroděi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čarodějь</i>, from <i>&#42;čarъ</i> (“sorcery”) + <i>&#42;dějati</i> (“to do”). Can be analysed as made of <i>ча́ры</i> (čáry, “magic”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-дей</i> (-dej, “doer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ча́рка</i> (čárka): a (small) vessel for drinking alcohol</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charter (a contract for the commercial leasing of a vessel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>charter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) bewitchment, witchcraft, magic, incantation, sorcery, spell</li><li>(poetic,&#32;literary) charm</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čarъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ker-</i>, <i>&#42;kēr-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) hour, o'clock, time, time of day</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) hours, o'clock</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) clock, watch</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) time, day</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>часъ</i> (časŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;časъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush hour, peak hour (times of day when traffic jams are commonplace, principally due to commuting)</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>peak hour</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for hours</li></ol><p>From <i>часа́ми</i> (časámi), instrumental plural of <i>час</i> (čas, “hour”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>час</i> (čas): (short) hour</li></ol><p><i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chapel, oratory (place of worship)</li></ol><p><i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-о́вня</i> (-óvnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hour</li><li>one-hour, hour-long</li><li>by the hour</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) watch, clock, horological</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) sentry, sentinel</li></ol><p>From <i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój) (meaning "hour" (<i>relational</i>)) or from <i>часы́</i> (časý) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój) (meaning "clock, watch" (<i>relational</i>)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time zone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchmaker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>час</i> (čas)</li></ol><p><i>час</i> (čas) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sometimes, at times, now and then</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) by any chance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) book of hours</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) often</li></ol><p><i>ча́сто</i> (částo) +&lrm; <i>-е́нько</i> (-énʹko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to do something rapidly/hurriedly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to visit someone frequently, to see much of someone</li></ol><p><i>ча́стый</i> (částyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fraction <i>(of)</i>, (little) part <i>(of)</i></li><li>(<i>physics</i>) particle</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) particle</li></ol><p>From <i>часть</i> (častʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>часть</i> (častʹ): (small) part</li></ol><p><i>части́ца</i> (častíca) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>partly, partially (in part)</li></ol><p><i>части́чный</i> (častíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>partial</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) partitive</li></ol><p><i>части́ца</i> (častíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) partitive (partitive case)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private property</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) private owner, private entrepreneur, private practitioner, private motorist</li></ol><p><i>часть</i> (častʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) quotient (number resulting from division)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>particular</li></ol><p><i>ча́стный</i> (částnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>private</li><li>particular</li><li>individual</li></ol><p><i>часть</i> (častʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frequently, often</li><li>close, thickly</li></ol><p><i>ча́стый</i> (částyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palisade, paling, fence</li></ol><p>From <i>ча́стый</i> (částyj, “dense”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кол</i> (kol, “pale”). Other sources suggest it's from Polish <i>częstokół</i> (“palisade”), with the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frequency</li></ol><p><i>ча́стый</i> (částyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;dated) frequency (of oscillations, etc.)</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) frequency (of occurrence)</li></ol><p><i>часто́тный</i> (častótnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) frequency</li><li>frequent</li></ol><p><i>частота́</i> (častotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chastushka (a type of traditional Russian folk music originating in the 1870s)</li><li>a fine-meshed net used to catch small fish</li></ol><p><i>ча́стый</i> (částyj, “quick”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p><p><i>ча́стый</i> (částyj, “thick, close”) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dense (consisting of closely spaced parts)</li><li>frequent (spaced a short distance apart)</li><li>fast, frequent (consisting of rapidly successive movements)</li><li>repetitive (appearing at short intervals)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čę̑stъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part</li><li>share</li><li>piece</li><li>department, section</li><li>(<i>military</i>) unit <i>(regimental or smaller sized and administratively self-contained)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) line, branch</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) police station</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чѧсть</i> (čęstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čęstь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) part of speech</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>partly, in part, partially</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>час</i> (čas).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clock, watch</li><li>watch; guard (a particular time period when guarding is kept)</li></ol><p>From <i>час</i> (čas) (hour), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;časъ</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Czech <i>hodiny</i> (&lt; <i>hodina</i>); Mongolian <i>цаг</i> (cag).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) chat (exchange of text or voice messages in real time)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chatbot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chatbot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet</i>) diminutive of <i>чат</i> (čat); chat</li></ol><p><i>чат</i> (čat) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chutney</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hindi <i>चटनी</i> (caṭnī).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>withered</li><li>sickly</li></ol><p><i>ча́х(нуть)</i> (čáx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-лый</i> (-lyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wilt, to wither</li><li>to become emaciated, to waste away</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consumption, phthisis (atrophy of the body as a result of tuberculosis)</li></ol><p>From <i>ча́х(нуть)</i> (čáx(nutʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). For the formation, compare <i>чесо́тка</i> (česótka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) phthisis</li><li>phthisic</li></ol><p><i>чахо́тка</i> (čaxótka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chakhokhbili, a meat ragout, part of Georgian cuisine.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ჩახოხბილი</i> (čaxoxbili).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chacha - traditionally a clear strong liquor, which is sometimes called "vine vodka," "grape vodka," or "Georgian vodka."</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ჭაჭა</i> (č̣ač̣a).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl</li><li>cup, chalice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чаша</i> (čaša), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čaša</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ча́шка</i> (čáška): small cup</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) calyx</li><li>cup, cup-like object</li><li>patella, knee-cap</li></ol><p><i>ча́шка</i> (čáška) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cup</li><li>cup-shaped</li></ol><p><i>ча́шка</i> (čáška) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ча́ша</i> (čáša)</li><li>cup</li><li>pan</li><li>cap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čašьka</i>. Equivalent to <i>ча́ша</i> (čáša) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Cognate with Polish <i>czasza</i>, Sanskrit <i>चषक</i> (caṣaka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čęšča</i>, from <i>&#42;čęstъ</i> (“frequent, dense”) + <i>&#42;-ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ча́стый</i> (částyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ча́сто</i> (částo)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čęsťьjь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most of the time, usually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) thicket</li></ol><p><i>ча́ща</i> (čášča) +&lrm; <i>-о́ба</i> (-óba)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation, hope, dream, aspiration</li></ol><p><i>ча́ять</i> (čájatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яние</i> (-janije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to expect, to hope</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čàjati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) seagull</li></ol><p>From <i>ча́йка</i> (čájka) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), with irregular -я.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sit long over one's tea, to have a cosy tea, to indulge in tea-drinking</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чай</i> (čaj); tea</li></ol><p><i>чай</i> (čaj) +&lrm; <i>-ёк</i> (-jók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чаёк</i> (čajók); tea</li></ol><p><i>чаёк</i> (čajók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snobby, arrogant (having excessive pride)</li><li>stuffy</li><li>peacocky</li><li>snot-nosed</li></ol><p><i>чва́нить(ся)</i> (čvánitʹ(sja), “to swagger, to be conceited”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj). Possibly related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьvati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conceit, peacockery, (false) pride</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Ч, ч.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business</i>) chaebol (a large, family-controlled, South Korean business conglomerate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>재벌(財閥)</i> (jaebeol). Doublet of <i>дзайба́цу</i> (dzajbácu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to fall, to fall over</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheburek/chebureki, chiburekki (fried turnover with a filling of ground or minced meat and onions)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>çiberek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheburek restaurant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheburek</li></ol><p><i>чебуре́к</i> (čeburék) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) what for? why?</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;rude) what? what do you want?!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somehow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheddar cheese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Cheddar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative pronoun</i>) whose</li><li>(<i>relative pronoun</i>) whose</li><li>(<i>indefinite pronoun</i>) anyone's</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьи</i> (čĭi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьjь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kijas</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> somebody's, someone's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>anybody's (belonging to anybody&#59; possessive form of anybody)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>someone's (belonging to someone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheque, check</li><li>receipt, bill</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>check</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linchpin</li><li>safety pin</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Чека)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheka (Soviet security organization)</li></ol><p>From the pronunciation of <i>ЧК</i> (ČK), abbreviation of <i>Чрезвыча́йная Коми́ссия</i> (Črezvyčájnaja Komíssija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chisel, caulking hammer</li><li>stamp, die</li><li>(<i>bird</i>) chat</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чеканъ</i> (čekanŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čekanъ</i>, a Turkic borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;čakan</i> (“battle axe”), a derivation from <i>&#42;čak-</i> (“to hit, beat”). Compare modern Kazakh <i>шағу</i> (şağu), Turkish <i>çakmak</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Macedonian, Ukrainian <i>чекан</i> (čekan), Czech, Slovak <i>čakan</i>, Polish <i>czekan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mint, to coin</li><li>to stamp (an image on metal)</li><li>to perform repetitive actions forcefully and with separation</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) to chase, to caulk</li></ol><p><i>чека́н</i> (čekán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minting, coining</li><li>stamping (<i>of an image on metal</i>)</li><li>performing actions forcefully and with separation</li><li>enunciating with force</li><li>beating a tread</li><li>relief image in metal</li></ol><p><i>чека́нить</i> (čekánitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чека́н</i> (čekán).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) to check, to skip a move</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to check (general sense)</li><li>(regional,&#32;dated,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) to wait</li></ol><p>From English <i>check</i> +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čekati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) <i>Chekist</i>; an agent of the Cheka, the Soviet Russian secret police from 1917 to 1922, replacing the Okhrana secret police of Tsarist Russia; succeeded by the OGPU and NKVD</li><li>(by extension,&#32;metonymic) KGB man, FSB agent; a national security official employed by any of the state security intelligence agencies of the Soviet Union or Russian Federation</li></ol><p>From <i>Чека́</i> (Čeká) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) chekmen (cossack kaftan)</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. See Ottoman Turkish كپنك (kepenek) for potentially pertinent forms.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poker</i>) to check, to skip a move</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to check (general sense)</li></ol><p>From English <i>check</i> +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chequebook, checkbook (folder containing cheques)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cheque, check</li></ol><p><i>чек</i> (ček) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) man, guy</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;slang&#32;or&#32;colloquial) challenge (difficult task)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>challenge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shuttle</li><li>a small retail trader travelling from one place or country to another</li></ol><p><i>чёлн</i> (čoln) +&lrm; <i>-ок</i> (-ok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shuttle; related to frequent travels between two points</li></ol><p><i>челно́к</i> (čelnók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;anatomy) forehead, brow</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čelo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₃os</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>челоби́тье</i> (čelobítʹje)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) bowing down (till the forehead reaches the earth)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;now jocular) entreaty, petition</li></ol><p><i>чело́</i> (čeló) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бить</i> (bitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тье</i> (-tʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) human anthill, a densely populated apartment building or a high-rise, a district with such buildings</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “human being”) +&lrm; <i>мураве́йник</i> (muravéjnik, “anthill”). Possibly popularised with Alexander Zinoviev's 1997 novel <i>Global Humant Hill</i>, aka <i>Global Cheloveynik</i> - <i>«Глоба́льный челове́йник»</i> («Globálʹnyj čelovéjnik»).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person, human being, man</li><li>(collective,&#32;singular only) mankind, man, the human race</li><li><i>also plural when used with cardinal words</i>:</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>человѣкъ</i> (čelověkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьlověkъ</i>, <i>&#42;čelověkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Homo sapiens</i></li></ol><p>Calque of New Latin <i>Homo sapiens</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philanthropy</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije), calque of Ancient Greek <i>φιλανθρωπία</i> (philanthrōpía, “philanthropy”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misanthrope</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “human”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ненави́стник</i> (nenavístnik, “hater”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misanthropic</li></ol><p><i>человеконенави́стник</i> (čelovekonenavístnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misanthropy</li></ol><p>From <i>человеконенави́стник</i> (čelovekonenavístnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ape (animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humaniform (like a human or that of a human in form, seeming, or appearance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human-like, man-like</li><li>humanoid, android</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) (little) man/person, little man</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only used in <i>по-челове́чески</i> (po-čelovéčeski)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li><li>humane, sympathetic</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figurative) decent, proper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čelověčьskъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, humankind, mankind (human beings as a group)</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) human</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man-meat, long pig, humanflesh</li><li>(<i>expressive</i>) human relations, human presence or reminiscence, humanflesh</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “human”): little person</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ишко</i> (-iško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augmentative of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “human”)</li></ol><p><i>челове́к</i> (čelovék, “human”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ще</i> (-íšče).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humaneness, humanity (quality of being benevolent)</li></ol><p><i>челове́чный</i> (čelovéčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humane</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čelověčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>челове́к</i> (čelovék).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jaw, maxillary</li></ol><p><i>че́люсть</i> (čéljustʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaw (bone of the jaw)</li><li>jowl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čeľustь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chelyabinsk (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Челя́бинск</i> (Čeljábinsk) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) domestic, servant</li></ol><p>From <i>че́лядь</i> (čéljadʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ин</i> (-in).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;archaic) servants, menials</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čeľadь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>than</li><li>instead of, rather</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hellebore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suitcase, trunk</li><li>a heavy projectile, especially that of a naval gun (military slang since the first half of the 20th century, especially WWI; often in quotes)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) land yacht (a long, heavy sedan)</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic medium, from Persian جامه‌دان (jâme-dân, “suitcase”), from Persian جامه (whence also English <i>pajamas</i>, Russian <i>пижа́ма</i> (pižáma)).</p><p>Note that this Russian term has later become the source of re-borrowing into modern Persian</p><p>چمدان (čamedân) and many Turkic languages.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чемода́н</i> (čemodán): (small) suitcase, case</li></ol><p><i>чемода́н</i> (čemodán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>champion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>champion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>championship</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) champion</li></ol><p><i>чемпио́н</i> (čempión) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>чэнду́ский</i> (čɛndúskij, “relating to Chengdu”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Russian or Soviet military,&#32;military slang) a cafeteria located inside of the military unit where soldiers can buy food, snacks or coffee.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;sometimes&#32;derogatory) nonsense, rubbish</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) trifle</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, the exact etymology of this word is unknown. But it is said to be derived from a variant pronunciation of щепа́ (ščepá, “wood chips”), "чепа".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чепе́ц</i> (čepéc): bonnet (<i>of a woman or child</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чепе́ц</i> (čepéc) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) hearts (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♥)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) June</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>червьнъ</i> (červĭnŭ, “June”). Cognates include Belarusian <i>чэрвень</i> (červjenʹ, “June”), Czech <i>červen</i>, Polish <i>czerwiec</i>, and Bulgarian <i>червен</i> (červen, “July”). Ultimately, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvenъ</i>, which is a passive participle of <i>&#42;čьrviti</i> (“to dye red”), from <i>&#42;čьrvь</i> (“worm, larva”), referring to certain insect larvae being used to manufacture red dye at this time of year. Related to черво́нный (červónnyj) and червь (červʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appendix (vermiform appendix)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>че́рвы</i> (čérvy, “hearts”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wormy; grubby</li><li>(about mushrooms of fruits) infested with maggots</li></ol><p><i>червь</i> (červʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ивый</i> (-ivyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) dark-red, crimson (including the pigment derived from mealybugs or minerals from which the colour originates)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) the pigment derived from mealybugs or minerals from which the colour originates</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) gules</li></ol><p>See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvenъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) dark red, crimson</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) gules</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрвлѥнъ</i> (čĭrvljenŭ), <i>чьрвенъ</i> (čĭrvenŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvenъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hearts (card game)</li></ol><p><i>че́рва</i> (čérva) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace of hearts (playing card)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) chervonets, a ten rouble banknote or gold piece</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ten of any currency</li></ol><p>From <i>черво́нный</i> (červónnyj, “red (old)”). Old 10 rouble banknotes were red.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) red, scarlet</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) of hearts</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Ruthenian <i>черво́ный</i> (červónyj) or directly from Middle Polish <i>czerwony</i>, from Old Polish <i>czerwiony</i> with the depalatalization of '<b>w</b>' under the Czech influence, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvenъ or &#42;čьrvľenъ</i>, past passive participle of <i>&#42;čьrviti</i> (“to dye red”). Doublet of native <i>червлёный</i> (červljónyj). Also compare Old East Slavic <i>чьрвлѥнъ, чьрвенъ</i> (čĭrvljenŭ, čĭrvenŭ).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>vermiculus</i> (whence English <i>vermeil</i>, <i>vermilion</i>) (&lt; <i>vermis</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormhole</li></ol><p><i>червь</i> (červʹ, “worm”) +&lrm; <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ, “to wear away”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>card games</i>) hearts (one of the four suits of playing cards, marked with the symbol ♥)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm (invertebrate)</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) caterpillar, maggot, helminth, grub, larva</li><li>(by extension,&#32;figuratively) a gnawing feeling, often with the verb <i>точи́ть</i> (točítʹ)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) mere worm, an insignificant person</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) worm (<i>a computer program</i>)</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;archaic) name of the letter Ч.</li><li>(card cames,&#32;inanimate) same as <i>че́рвы</i> (čérvy)</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;inanimate) part of cerebellum (vermis cerebelli)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvь</i>, distantly related to English <i>kermes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) worm</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) maggot</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively) miserable, insignificant person, worm</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) worm screw</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvь</i> + <i>-я́к</i> (-ják).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>червя́к</i> (červják): small worm</li></ol><p><i>червя́к</i> (červják) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attic, garret, loft (an attic or similar space)</li></ol><p>Attested since 16th century. From Persian چارتاق (čârtâq, “four-arch”) via a Turkic language. Possibly a doublet of <i>чертог</i> (čertog).</p><p>Compare Turkish <i>çardak</i>, Azerbaijani <i>чардаг</i>/<i>çardaq</i>, Karakalpak <i>шердақ</i>/<i>sherdaq</i>, <i>шертек</i> (shertek), Crimean Tatar <i>çardak</i>, Karaim <i>čardak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) attic, loft</li></ol><p><i>черда́к</i> (čerdák) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) shoe; slipper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>череви́к</i> (čerevýk), from Old East Slavic <i>черевикъ</i> (čerevikŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;červikъ</i> (“shoe”), from <i>&#42;červo</i> (whence Russian <i>чре́во</i> (črévo, “belly, womb, inside, interior”)). Doublet of <i>череви́цы</i> (čerevícy), inherited directly from East Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ukraine</i>) diminutive of <i>череви́к</i> (čerevík): (small) shoe; (small) slipper</li></ol><p><i>череви́к</i> (čerevík) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>file, chain, train, procession (<i>of objects</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) succession, sequence (<i>of periodic events</i>)</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial,&#32;poetic) time, turn (<i>allotted for something</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) bur marigold, beggar's ticks, stickseed, tickseed (a plant from the <i>Bidens</i> genus)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerda</i> (“file, herd”). Doublet of <i>чреда́</i> (čredá), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternation, interchange</li><li>rotation, interchanging, alternating</li><li>interlacing</li></ol><p><i>чередова́ть</i> (čeredovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alternate <i>(with)</i></li></ol><p><i>черёд</i> (čerjód) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to alternate, to take turns</li></ol><p><i>чередова́ть</i> (čeredovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternating, rolling, alternant</li><li>interchangeable</li><li>rotative, rotational</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>through</li><li>across, over</li><li><i>(time)</i> in, after</li><li>via</li><li>with, with the help of</li><li>every other</li><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) because of</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) leather belt with purse for money, bysack-girdle, money belt</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чересъ</i> (čeresŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čersъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kert-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>. Doublet of <i>чрез</i> (črez), a borrowing from Old Church Slavonic.</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čersъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival and nominal prefix, used in specialized terminology, slightly productive: trans-, cross-</li><li>Adjectival and nominal prefix, used in specialized terminology, slightly productive: alternating</li></ol><p>From <i>через</i> (čerez).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>че́рез</i> (čérez).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheremis (male person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ramson, bear garlic (<i>Allium ursinum</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čermъša</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ramson, bear garlic (<i>Allium ursinum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>черемша́</i> (čeremšá) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) small handle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>черенъ</i> (čerenŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;černъ</i>. Per Vasmer, probably related to Russian <i>ко́рень</i> (kórenʹ, “root”) as well as Lithuanian <i>kẽras</i> (“bush, root, stump”), Old Prussian <i>kirno</i> (“bush”), Ancient Greek <i>κράνος</i> (krános, “cornelian cherry”), Latin <i>cornus</i> (“cornelian cherry”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle, hilt (<i>of a spade, knife, or similar tool</i>)</li><li>stalk</li><li>petiole</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) solen, razor clam</li><li>(physics,&#32;slang) Cherenkov radiation</li></ol><p><i>че́рен</i> (čéren) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a part of an oven</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) oak forest</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) deceit, deception, trick</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skull, cranium</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) crust</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>черепъ</i> (čerepŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerpъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortoise</li><li>turtle</li><li>tortoise shell</li></ol><p>From <i>че́реп</i> (čérep, “skull, crust”), the earlier term <i>желвь</i> (želvʹ) from Old East Slavic <i>желꙑ</i> (žely) was discontinued. According to the Etymological Dictionary of the Russian Language, черепаха came into use very likely as taboo avoidance of желвь.</p><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>чарапа́ха</i> (čarapáxa), Ukrainian <i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>testūdō</i> (akin to <i>testa</i>). Also compare Bulgarian <i>костену́рка</i> (kostenúrka) (akin to <i>кость</i> (kostʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tortoise, turtle</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) tortoiseshell</li></ol><p><i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa, “tortoise”) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tortoise, turtle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) very slow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa): (small) turtle, tortoise</li><li>shield bug of the genus <i>Eurygaster</i></li></ol><p><i>черепа́ха</i> (čerepáxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) roof tiles (<i>ceramic or clay</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tile; made of tiles, tegular</li></ol><p><i>черепи́ца</i> (čerepíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cranium, neurocranium, braincase (skull enclosing the brain)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cranial</li></ol><p><i>че́реп</i> (čérep) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crock, piece of broken crockery, potsherd</li><li>endearing diminutive of <i>че́реп</i> (čérep): small skull (<i>of toy, jewelry, Jolly Roger etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) head, skull</li><li>earthenware pot or bowl</li></ol><p><i>че́реп</i> (čérep) +&lrm; <i>-у́шка</i> (-úška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>че́реп</i> (čérep).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Adjectival and nominal prefix, used in specialized terminology, slightly productive: trans-, cross-</li><li>Adjectival and nominal prefix, used in specialized terminology, slightly productive: alternating</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colter (a vertical cutting blade fixed in front of a plowshare)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;in the plural,&#32;anatomy) reins, small of the back</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чересло</i> (čereslo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerslo</i>. Doublet of <i>чре́сла</i> (črésla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>too, too much</li></ol><p><i>черес-</i> (čeres-) +&lrm; <i>чур</i> (čur)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) cherry, sweet cherry (relating to <i>Prunus avium</i> and its fruit)</li></ol><p><i>чере́шня</i> (čeréšnja) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherry, sweet cherry (fruit, tree, or wood of the species <i>Prunus avium</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>черешьнѧ</i> (čerešĭnę), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čèršьňa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petiole, scape, (leaf) stalk</li><li>handle (of a tool, e.g. a spade or shuffle)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cross out, to cross off</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrkati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Circassian, Cherkess</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, with suggestions including Turkic, Persian or Ancient Greek. Some of the theories on the origin of the word include:</p><p>See Circassians for more detail.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chokha, cherkeska</li></ol><p>From <i>черке́ска</i> (čerkéska) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to write, to scribble</li></ol><p><i>черкать</i> (čerkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn/become/grow black, to blacken</li><li>to show black</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) bluebill, scaup</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chernihiv/Chernigov (city in Ukraine)</li></ol><p><i>Черни́гов</i> (Černígov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blueberry, bilberry, whortleberry</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ика</i> (-ika)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ink (coloured/colored fluid used for writing)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрнило</i> (čĭrnilo). By surface analysis, <i>чернить</i> (černitʹ, “to blacken”) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo). Akin to Old Church Slavonic <i>чрънило</i> (črŭnilo). Compare Ancient Greek <i>μελάνιον</i> (melánion), Latin <i>ātrāmentum</i>. Also compare <i>бели́ла</i> (belíla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;nonstandard</i>) alternative form of <i>черни́ла</i> (černíla)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bureaucrat, office drudge, office rat, pen-pusher, paper-shuffler</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) inkwell, inkpot (container for ink)</li><li>(animate,&#32;derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism) mud shark, white woman who seeks sexual relationships with black men or men from Caucasus region, Central and West Asia</li></ol><p><i>черни́ла</i> (černíla) +&lrm; <i>-ни́ца</i> (-níca)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ink</li><li>(figurative,&#32;literary) inky, dark black</li></ol><p><i>черни́ла</i> (černíla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blacken</li><li>to slander, to smear</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) blueberry</li></ol><p><i>черни́ка</i> (černíka) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-bearded</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormwood, mugwort (<i>Artemisia vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrnobylь</i>, equivalent to <i>черно-</i> (černo-, “black”) + the base word of <i>были́нка</i> (bylínka, “blade of grass”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Чернобыль)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chernobyl (a small city in Ukraine, the site of a 1986 nuclear disaster)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>Чорно́биль</i> (Čornóbylʹ), from <i>чорно́биль</i> (čornóbylʹ, “wormwood”) (compare native <i>чернобы́ль</i> (černobýlʹ, “wormwood”)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mugwort, felon herb (<i>Artemisia vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чернобы́ль</i> (černobýlʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chernobyl</li></ol><p><i>Черно́быль</i> (Černóbylʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rough copy; draft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) rough</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) draft</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-haired</li></ol><p><i>черно-</i> (černo-) +&lrm; <i>во́лосы</i> (vólosy) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having dark brown eyes</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глаз</i> (glaz) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montenegrin (person from Montenegro)</li></ol><p><i>Черного́рия</i> (Černogórija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Montenegrin</li></ol><p><i>Черного́рия</i> (Černogórija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;vulgar,&#32;ethnic slur) wog, nigger (an offensive term for a dark-skinned person, an immigrant from Central Asia or the Caucasus)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;vulgar,&#32;ethnic slur) Of or related to dark-skinned people, immigrants from Central Asia or the Caucasus</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>жо́па</i> (žópa, “ass”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj). Literally, “black-assed”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chernozem region</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chernozem, black soil</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-зём</i> (-zjóm), combining form of <i>земля́</i> (zemljá, “earth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ornithology</i>) black-billed, black-beaked</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>клюв</i> (kljuv, “beak”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black-billed flycatcher (<i>Aphanotriccus audax</i>)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>черноклю́вый</i> (černokljúvyj, “black-beaked, black-billed”) +&lrm; <i>колумби́йский мухое́д</i> (kolumbíjskij muxojéd, “<i>Aphanotriccus</i>-genus flycatcher”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancer, warlock</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) black (of African descent)</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>ко́жа</i> (kóža, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) wog, nigger (an offensive term for a dark-skinned person, e.g. an African or an immigrant from Central Asia or the Caucasus)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) Of or related to dark-skinned people, e.g., Africans or immigrants from Central Asia or the Caucasus</li><li>A person significantly smeared in black, e.g., a chimney sweep or a soiled child</li></ol><p>черно- ('black') + "-мазый" ('smeared', from "мазать")</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Black Sea</li></ol><p>From <i>черномо́р-</i> (černomór-) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unskilled laborer (<i>especially if performing a physically hard or dirty job</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unskilled (<i>labor or laborer, especially if performing a physically hard or dirty job</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрная рабо́та</i> (čórnaja rabóta, “unskilled job”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij); analyzable as <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>рабо́чий</i> (rabóčij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Eastern Orthodoxy,&#32;dated) monk</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрноризьць</i> (čĭrnorizĭcĭ), from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрьноризьць</i> (črĭnorizĭcĭ); by surface analysis, <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ри́за</i> (ríza, “robe, vestment”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackshirt</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>руба́шка</i> (rubáška) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) member of the Black Hundreds, a reactionary anti-Semitic political movement in the 1900-1910s</li><li>(by extension,&#32;figuratively) vigilante, person voluntarily supporting the Russian government in suppressing protesters or minorities, a member of a pogrom mob</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Black Hundreds</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackness</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрнота</i> (čĭrnota), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrnota</i>, from <i>&#42;čьrnъ</i> (“black”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ota</i> (“-ness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) darkling beetle</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>те́ло</i> (télo, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;collective) the negative aspects of life, e.g. doom, hopelessness, cruelty, poverty, violence</li><li>chernukha (a gloomy genre of Russian horror)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something painted black, e.g. a postage stamp</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate) dark-complexioned girl with black hair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) black cumin herb or flower (<i>Nigella sativa</i>), also called Roman coriander, black caraway, black seed, nutmeg flower or love in the mist</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mushroom of species <i>Russula adusta</i></li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-у́ха</i> (-úxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman or girl with a rich, dark complexion, dark-brown eyes and black hair</li><li>black cumin herb or flower (<i>Nigella sativa</i>), also called Roman coriander, black caraway, black seed, nutmeg flower or love in the mist</li><li>mushroom of <i>Russula adusta</i> species</li></ol><p><i>черну́ха</i> (černúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a rich, dark complexion, dark-brown eyes and black hair (<i>usually said of girls or women</i>)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) black cumin herb or flower (<i>Nigella sativa</i>), also called Roman coriander, black caraway, black seed, nutmeg flower or love in the mist</li></ol><p><i>черну́ха</i> (černúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mob, populace, riffraff, rabble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрнь</i> (čĭrnĭ). By surface analysis, <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dark-haired</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-я́вый</i> (-jávyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cherokee (indigenous North American people)</li><li>Cherokee (member of this indigenous North American tribe)</li><li>Cherokee (the Iroquoian language of this indigenous people)</li><li>Cherokee (the syllabary used to write this language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle (deep-bowled spoon with a long, usually curved, handle)</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) scoop, dipper</li><li>(<i>fishing</i>) skimmer (<i>for clearing slush in ice fishing</i>)</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) bucket, scoop (<i>of an excavator</i>)</li><li>(animate,&#32;military,&#32;slang) a serviceman who has completed a certain period in service, usually six months</li></ol><p><i>черпа́ть</i> (čerpátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ак</i> (-ak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw; to ladle; to scoop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрпати</i> (čĭrpati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrpati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scoop up</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>че́рпать</i> (čérpatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line</li><li>boundary, precinct</li><li>trait, streak, feature</li><li>stroke (particularly of a Chinese character)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрта</i> (čĭrta), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, to draw up, to plot</li><li>(low colloquial&#32;or&#32;dated,&#32;derogatory) to misbehave, to behave disgracefully, to carouse</li></ol><p><i>черта́</i> (čertá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) a she-devil; an annoying woman</li></ol><p><i>чертов</i> (čertov) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>devilishly (in a devilish manner)</li><li>as hell (intensifier)</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov) +&lrm; <i>-ски</i> (-ski)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devil's</li><li>diabolical, fiendish, devilish</li><li>(<i>intensifier</i>) damn, bloody</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-о́вский</i> (-óvskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;collective) devilry</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively,&#32;collective) weirdness, craziness, strange thing(s) (something strange, weird, and&#47;or absurd)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;figuratively,&#32;countable) mysterious thing, dealie (<i>can be used as a placeholder term</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) hall (large, rich room)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) magnificent building, palace</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрътогъ</i> (črŭtogŭ), compare Ukrainian <i>черто́г</i> (čertóh), Old Serbian <i>чртог</i>, <i>чртаг</i>. Per Vasmer, probably borrowed from Persian چارتاق (čârtâq, “four-arch”). See <i>черда́к</i> (čerdák, “attic”), another borrowing of the same term, for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) thistle</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort, “devil”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>поло́х</i> (polóx, “fright”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thistle</li></ol><p><i>чертополо́х</i> (čertopolóx) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to curse, to swear, to cuss (using the word <i>чёрт</i> (čort, “devil”) or related terms)</li></ol><p>From <i>чёрт</i> (čort, “devil”) +&lrm; <i>-ыха́ть</i> (-yxátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to curse, to swear, to cuss (using the word <i>чёрт</i> (čort, “devil”) or related terms)</li></ol><p><i>чертыха́ться</i> (čertyxátʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intensifying) synonym of <i>чёрт</i> (čort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;intensifying) synonym of <i>чёрт</i> (čort)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draft, scheme, plan, diagram</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьртежь</i> (čĭrtežĭ, “mark, verge; plan, drawing”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrtežь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёж</i> (-jóž).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draughtsman</li></ol><p><i>чертёж</i> (čertjóž) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чертёж</i> (čertjóž).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little devil, imp</li><li>hyperactive mischievous child</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing (especially technical), sketching, drafting (act of producing a draft or a picture)</li></ol><p><i>черти́ть</i> (čertítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) belly, womb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>черево</i> (čerevo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;červo</i>. Doublet of <i>чре́во</i> (črévo), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turn, course</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čẽrdъ</i>. Cognate with <i>череда́</i> (čeredá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird cherry (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Until the end of the 19<sup>th</sup> century also черёмха (čerjómxa), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čermъxa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) bird cherry</li></ol><p><i>черёмуха</i> (čerjómuxa) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scratching, carding</li></ol><p><i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li><li>(<i>textiles</i>) to card, to comb</li><li>to scratch (in order to remove itching)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;česàti</i> (“to comb, to scratch”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;késtei</i>. Per Vasmer, cognates include Sanskrit <i>कच्छू</i> (kacchū, “an itch, a scab”), Ancient Greek <i>κεσκέον</i> (keskéon, “oakum, tow”) and Ancient Greek <i>κέωρος</i> (kéōros, “nettle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;figuratively) to chat, to shoot the breeze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch oneself</li><li>to itch</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to comb one's hair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bother, to give oneself the trouble</li></ol><p><i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;collectively) garlic, garlics</li><li>caltrop</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чеснокъ</i> (česnokŭ), <i>&#42;чеснъкъ</i> (&#42;česnŭkŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;česnъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;česnъ</i> (“garlic”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) garlic</li></ol><p><i>чесно́к</i> (česnók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) scabies, mange</li></ol><p>From <i>чеса́ть</i> (česátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ота</i> (-ota) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). For the formation, compare <i>чахо́тка</i> (čaxótka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) scabies, mange</li><li>mangy (afflicted with mange)</li></ol><p><i>чесо́тка</i> (česótka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honoring</li><li>celebration (<i>in someone's honor</i>)</li></ol><p><i>че́ствовать</i> (čéstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honor, to pay tribute to</li></ol><p><i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to curse out</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to honor</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to call (<i>by name</i>)</li></ol><p><i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>че́стно</i> (čéstno)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>honestly</li><li>fair, fairly</li></ol><p><i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word of honor</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>word of honor!</li><li>on my word of honor!</li><li>honestly, frankly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) honored, worthy, honest (not to be confused with <i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honesty (quality of being honest)</li></ol><p><i>че́стный</i> (čéstnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honest, upright, fair (not to be confused with <i>честно́й</i> (čestnój))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambitious (possessing, or controlled by, ambition)</li></ol><p><i>честолю́бие</i> (čestoljúbije) +&lrm; <i>-и́вый</i> (-ívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lust for praises, love to be praised</li></ol><p><i>честь</i> (čestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>люби́ть</i> (ljubítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor</li><li>regard, respect</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to read</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to consider, to think, to suppose</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstь</i></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьсти</i> (čĭsti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьsti</i>, <i>&#42;čisti</i> (“to read, to count”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couple, pair</li><li>match</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чета</i> (četa, “troop, multitude, community”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četa</i>, akin to Czech <i>četa</i> (“platoon, squad”), Serbo-Croatian <i>чета</i>, Bulgarian <i>чета</i> (četa), possibly Latin <i>caterva</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thursday</li></ol><p>Derivation from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četvьrtъkъ</i>, as in other Slavic languages, formally impossible - but cf. Samogitian <i>ketvergs</i> (“Thursday”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (all) fours, hands and knees, hands and feet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a team of four horses</li></ol><p><i>че́тверо</i> (čétvero) +&lrm; <i>-ня</i> (-nja)</p></html>:/ˈt͡ɕetʲvʲɪrə/
<h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective number</i>) four (in a group together), four of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>quadri-, tetra- (four)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-legged (having four legs)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quadruped</li><li>four-footed or four-legged animal</li><li>mammal ambulating on all fours</li></ol><p><i>четверо-</i> (četvero-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) four-handed, quadrumanous</li></ol><p><i>четверо-</i> (četvero-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quatrain</li></ol><p><i>четверо-</i> (četvero-) +&lrm; <i>стих</i> (stix) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije). Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i>, the source of <i>четверо-</i> (četvero-), is also the source of <i>катре́н</i> (katrén, “quatrain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>denotes a relative in the same generation as the head noun but three degrees greater in separation</li></ol><p>From <i>че́тверо</i> (čétvero) +&lrm; <i>-ю-</i> (-ju-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod) +&lrm; <i>ный</i> (nyj), with <i>-ю-</i> (-ju-) by analogy with <i>двою́родный</i> (dvojúrodnyj) (see there for further details).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second cousin twice removed downwards <i>(second cousin's grandson)</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>second cousin three times removed downwards <i>(second cousin's great grandson)</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twenty-five-copeck coin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;dialectal) quarter, fourth part</li><li>quarter-log, quarter-timber</li><li>quarter-carcass</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) container holding a quarter-bucket</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) quantity of liquid in a quarter-bucket</li></ol><p><i>че́тверть</i> (čétvertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́на</i> (-ína)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) endearing diminutive of <i>четверти́на</i> (četvertína, “quarter”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quarter-liter bottle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) quantity of liquid (usually vodka) in a quarter-liter bottle</li></ol><p><i>четверти́на</i> (četvertína) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw and quarter (to execute a person by tying each limb to an animal and driving them in different directions)</li></ol><p>From <i>че́тверть</i> (čétvertʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ). Compare Polish <i>ćwiartować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarter, fourth</li><li>school term, quarter</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) quarter, phase (<i>of the moon</i>)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) crotchet, quarter note</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) quarterfinals</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>quarterfinal</i>, from <i>че́тверть</i> (čétvertʹ, “quarter”) +&lrm; <i>фина́л</i> (finál, “finals”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) four <i>(digit)</i></li><li><i>(group of)</i> four</li><li><i>(mark)</i> four <i>(out of five)</i>, B mark, B grade</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) four</li><li>No. 4 bus (tram, etc)</li><li>four-in-hand, team of four horses</li><li>(<i>rowing</i>) four, quad, quadruple (a boat with four rowers)</li><li>(military,&#32;aviation) flight of four aircraft</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fourth part</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>четвьртъ</i> (četvĭrtŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četvьrtъ</i> (“fourth”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ketwirtas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwr̥tós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four (4)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;kʷetwóres</td></tr></table></p><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>четꙑре</i> (četyre), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četỳre</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ketū́res</i>, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i>. Cognates include Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;čaθwā́rah</i>, Sanskrit <i>चत्वार</i> (catvā́ra-, m.pl.).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>four times, on four occasions</li><li>4×, four times as much</li></ol><p><i>четы́ре</i> (četýre) +&lrm; <i>-жды</i> (-ždy)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four hundred (400)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) tetradecahedron</li></ol><p><i>четырнадцать</i> (četyrnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fourteen-year period</li><li>fourteenth anniversary, fourteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>четы́рнадцать</i> (četýrnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fourteen-year</li><li>fourteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>четы́рнадцать</i> (četýrnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetradecagon</li></ol><p><i>четырна́дцать</i> (četyrnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourteenth</li></ol><p><i>четы́рнадцать</i> (četýrnadcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourteen (14)</li></ol><p>From <i>четы́ре</i> (četýre, “four”) +&lrm; <i>-надцать</i> (-nadcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>14×, fourteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>quadri-, tetra-, four-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) tetrahedron</li></ol><p>From <i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “four-side-er”, a calque of Ancient Greek <i>τετράεδρον</i> (tetráedron). Compare Ukrainian <i>чотиригра́нник</i> (čotyryhránnyk).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) four-day</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having four stars (e.g. star system; decorations)</li><li>(<i>of a hotel</i>) four-star</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>звезда́</i> (zvezdá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-wheeled</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striped cuckoo (<i>Tapera naevia</i>)</li></ol><p><i>четырёхкрылый</i> (četyrjóxkrylyj, “four-winged”) +&lrm; <i>куку́шка</i> (kukúška, “cuckoo”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>four-year period</li><li>fourth anniversary, fourth birthday</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) four-year</li><li>four-year-old</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-dimensional</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-handed (referring to music written for two pianos)</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>рука́</i> (ruká) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-hundredth, 400th</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wounded soldier with a traumatic brain injury or shell concussion</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a lightly wounded soldier</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a drunk soldier</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a prisoner of war</li></ol><p><i>четырёхсо́т</i> (četyrjoxsót) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p><p>Modeled on <i>двухсо́тый</i> (dvuxsótyj) and <i>трёхсо́тый</i> (trjoxsótyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-sided, quadrilateral</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) four-party; quadripartite</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>сторона́</i> (storoná) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-stroke (<i>engine</i>)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) four-beat</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>такт</i> (takt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>four-stroke engine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quadrilateral</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quadrangular</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-storeyed, having four storeys</li></ol><p><i>четырёх-</i> (četyrjóx-) +&lrm; <i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Czech (person)</li><li>(<i>military slang</i>) a Chechen man, especially during the two Russian-Chechen conflicts</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>Čech</i>.</p><p>From чече́нец&nbsp;m (čečénec, “a Chechen man”), which has a similar sound, but has nothing to do with the Czech people.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leapfrog (children's game)</li><li>confusion, mess</li></ol><p>A Kipchak borrowing, in recent Tatar <i>шыгырдий</i> (şığerdiy), derived from <i>шыгырдау</i> (şığırdaw, “to craunch”), after onomatopoeic interjection <i>шыгыр</i> (şığır), <i>шағыр</i> (şağır) akin to Chuvash <i>шӑкӑр</i> (šăk̬ăr), Turkmen <i>шығыр</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chekhov: related to Anton Pavlovich Chekhov or to settlements named Chekhov</li></ol><p><i>Че́хов</i> (Čéxov) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, case, slipcover</li><li>underdress</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) boot, hood, cap</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чехолъ</i> (čexolŭ), <i>чехълъ</i> (čexŭlŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čexъlъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Czechoslovak, Czechoslovakian</li></ol><p><i>Чехослова́кия</i> (Čexoslovákija) +&lrm; <i>-цкий</i> (-ckij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lentil</li><li>rosefinch</li></ol><p>By assimilation from Old East Slavic <i>сочевица</i> (sočevica), from <i>сочиво</i> (sočivo, “lentil seed”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sočivo</i>, probably from <i>&#42;sòkъ</i> (“juice”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sokʷos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) lentil</li></ol><p><i>чечеви́ца</i> (čečevíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chechen (person)</li></ol><p>From East Circassian <i>шашан</i> (šašan) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec); see <i>Чечня</i> (Čečnja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chechen</li></ol><p><i>Чечня́</i> (Čečnjá) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male redpoll (<i>Acanthis</i> spp.)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čečetъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>че́чіт</i> (čéčit), Slovene <i>čečèt</i>. Per Vasmer, of onomatopoeic origin and further related to Lithuanian <i>kekùtis</i> (“type of siskin”), <i>kiki̇̀lis</i> (“linnet”), <i>kė́kštas</i> (“jay”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) A man with uninteresting qualities</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tap dance</li><li>redpoll (<i>Acanthis</i> spp.) (<i>bird</i>)</li></ol><p><i>че́чет</i> (čéčet) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(inanimate,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) a shoe used in gymnastics or ballet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Czech <i>Češka</i> or can be construed as <i>чех</i> (čex) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Czech language</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Czech</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чешьскъ</i> (češĭskŭ). By surface analysis, <i>чех</i> (čex) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scale-winged, scaly-winged</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) lepidopterous</li></ol><p>From <i>чешуя́</i> (češujá, “scales”) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj, adjectival suffix). Calque of translingual <i>Lepidoptera</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legless lizard (Pygopodidae lizard)</li></ol><p><i>чешуя́</i> (češujá, “scales”) + <i>нога́</i> (nogá, “leg”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чешуя́</i> (češujá, “scale (<i>e.g. of a fish</i>)”)</li></ol><p><i>чешуя́</i> (češujá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) Squamata</li></ol><p>Pluralization of Russian <i>чешу́йчатый</i> (češújčatyj, “scaly”), a semantic loan from translingual <i>Squamata</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scaly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scales (<i>of fish, reptiles, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;češuja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Zhuang</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhuyin, Zhuyin fuhao, bopomofo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>注音</i> (Zhùyīn, “sound annotation”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zhuyin fuhao, bopomofo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>注音符號</i>&nbsp;/ <i>注音符号</i> (zhùyīn fúhào).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Jurchen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Jurchen</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jurchen (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>чжурчжэ́нь</i> (čžurčžɛ́nʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lapwing, pewit (bird belonging to the subfamily Vanellinae)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siskin</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čižь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чиж</i> (čiž): siskin</li><li>(youth slang,&#32;dated) a social butterfly, slicker</li></ol><p><i>чиж</i> (čiž) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheeseburger</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) chisel plow (heavy cultivator for deep soil loosening)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chisel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Achnatherum</i>, especially the Jiji grass <i>Achnatherum splendens</i></li><li>a mat of stiff grass traditional to cover the framework of a Kyrgyz, Kazakh etc. yurt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chagatai چیغ (çığ), related to Persian جفت (jaft).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imitative</i>) chirp (sound a bird makes)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chicago, Chicagoan</li></ol><p>From <i>Чика́го</i> (Čikágo) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) (everything's) OK</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>чилл</i> (čill)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chili pepper</li><li>chili con carne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chili</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Чили)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chile (a country in South America)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chilean (person from Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chilean (person from Chile)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chilean</li></ol><p><i>Чи́ли</i> (Číli) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>чи́ллить</i> (číllitʹ); to chill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) relaxation in a pleasant environment; state of being chill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to chill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kimchi (in a style enjoyed by Soviet Koreans)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>짐치</i> (jimchi). Essentially from the Soviet Korean dialect (a variety of the Hamgyeong-do dialect).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rank</li><li>official</li><li>a religious rite</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чинъ</i> (činŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;činъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷey-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane (deciduous tree, <i>Platanus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Persian چنار (čenâr), probably via a Turkic language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane (deciduous tree, <i>Platanus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic, itself from Classical Persian چنار (činār).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repair, to fix</li><li>to sharpen</li><li>to cause, to raise</li><li>to administer</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;činiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sedately, decorously</li></ol><p><i>чи́нный</i> (čínnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sedate, decorous</li></ol><p><i>чин</i> (čin) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>official, functionary, bureaucrat</li><li>(dated,&#32;religion) book on episcopal missals</li></ol><p>From <i>чин</i> (čin) +&lrm; <i>-овник</i> (-ovnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>officialdom, the officials</li></ol><p><i>чино́вник</i> (činóvnik) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) official, functionary, bureaucrat; official's, functionary's, bureaucrat's</li></ol><p><i>чино́вник</i> (činóvnik) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) bureaucrat, red tapist; pedant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veneration towards higher-ups; servility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) apparatchik, bureaucrat (blindly loyal bureaucrat), official</li><li>penpusher</li></ol><p>From <i>чин(о́вник)</i> (čin(óvnik)) +&lrm; <i>-у́ша</i> (-úša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>electronics</i>) chip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chip</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(electronics,&#32;relational) chip</li></ol><p><i>чип</i> (čip) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Russian or Soviet military,&#32;military slang) alternative form of <i>чепок</i> (čepok)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) chip, crisp (thin fried slice of potato)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from American English <i>chips</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whitefish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>че́рва</i> (čérva)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carbuncle, boil, abscess</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čirьjь</i>. Cognate to Bulgarian <i>ци́рей</i> (círej).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>чи́рей</i> (čírej, “boil”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) 10 rubles</li><li>(archaic,&#32;colloquial) 25 rubles</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romani.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chirp, to twitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chirp, to twitter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fwoosh (the sound of a match being struck)</li><li>snick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scratch, to strike</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut, to slash</li></ol><p>From <i>чирк</i> (čirk) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striking surface of a matchbox</li><li>skid mark, smears of excrement on underclothes or another surface</li></ol><p><i>чи́ркать</i> (čírkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-аш</i> (-aš)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scratch, to strike</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to cut, to slash</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>чи́ркать</i> (čírkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cheerleader</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чи́рий</i> (čírij, “boil”).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number, quantity, count</li></ol><p><i>чи́сленный</i> (číslennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>numeral, numerical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) numerator (in a fraction)</li></ol><p><i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) numeral</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive from <i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ, “to count”) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj, verbal adjective suffix): “for counting”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to list, to record</li><li>to consider [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone/something’</small> <i>and</i> instrumental <small>‘as someone/something else’</small>]</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to count, to calculate</li></ol><p><i>число́</i> (čisló) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be recorded [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘under a name’</small>]</li><li>to be listed [<i>with</i> <b>за</b> (za, + instrumental) <small>‘among a group’</small>]</li><li>to number (an amount)</li><li>to be regarded as</li><li>to be attributed to</li></ol><p><i>чи́слить</i> (číslitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number (cardinal)</li><li>date (number of the day of the month)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) number</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>число</i> (čislo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čislo</i>. Related to <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Avogadro's number (number of atoms present in 0.012 kilograms of isotopically pure Carbon-12)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fibonacci number (a number)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arity (number of arguments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arity (number of arguments)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) number; numeric, numerical</li></ol><p><i>число́</i> (čisló) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;gemstone trade) superlative degree of <i>чи́стой воды́</i> (čístoj vodý); first water (of the purest transparency and colour&#47;color)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) superlative degree of <i>чи́стой воды́</i> (čístoj vodý); pure, patent, complete</li></ol><p>Literally, “of the purest water”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj): immaculate, spotless, squeaky-clean</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very clean person</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;derogatory) morally pure person</li></ol><p><i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) purgatory</li></ol><p>Calque of Ecclesiastical Latin <i>pūrgātōrium</i>, from <i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-лище</i> (-lišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, to scour</li><li>to peel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čistiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleaning, clean-up</li><li>peeling, shelling</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) purge, combing-out</li></ol><p><i>чи́стить</i> (čístitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>cleanly, neatly</li><li>purely, merely</li></ol><p><i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fair copy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>gemstone trade</i>) first water (of the purest transparency and colour&#47;color)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) pure, patent, complete</li></ol><p>Literally, “of pure water”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pureblooded; thoroughbred</li></ol><p>From <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кровь</i> (krovʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cleanly (liking/practising cleanliness)</li></ol><p><i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плоть</i> (plotʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) cissy; a fastidious person; a squeamish person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoroughbred</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleanliness, purity</li></ol><p>From <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celandine (<i>Chelidonium</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj, “clean”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>те́л(о)</i> (tél(o), “body”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean, tidy</li><li>clean, clear, pure</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čistъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an excessive lover of cleanliness</li></ol><p><i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj) +&lrm; <i>-ю́ля</i> (-júlja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) readable (enjoyable to read)</li></ol><p>From <i>читать</i> (čitatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-абельный</i> (-abelʹnyj) (from Latin <i>-abilis</i>, French <i>-able</i>, or English <i>-able</i>). Probably a semi-calque from English <i>readable</i>. Attested since 1910.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) reading</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́льный</i> (-álʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading room</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льня</i> (-lʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reader</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ, “to read”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reader's, readers'</li></ol><p><i>чита́тель</i> (čitátelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read</li><li>to recite</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lecture, to give a lecture, to deliver a lecture</li><li>to teach</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive) to tell, to say (for the purpose of a lesson)</li><li>to pronounce, to deliver</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to understand, to interpret, to decipher</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čitati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to read between the lines (to infer a meaning that is not stated explicitly)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to read</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to feel like reading</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;gaming) cheater (a player using software to cheat in a game)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>cheater</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chita (city in Russia)</li></ol><p><i>Чита́</i> (Čitá) +&lrm; <i>-и́нский</i> (-ínskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(action of) reading</li><li>reading aloud</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) stage reading</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to read (<i>repeatedly or frequently</i>); iterative of <i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ)</li></ol><p><i>чита́ть</i> (čitátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ывать</i> (-yvatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sneeze, sneezing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sneezing</li></ol><p><i>чиха́ть</i> (čixátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneeze</li><li>to scorn, to disdain</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čixati</i>, of onomatopoeic origin.</p><p>Cognate with Bulgarian <i>чи́хамь</i> (číhamʹ) (Gerov), Slovene <i>číhati</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>) (1sg. <i>čȋham</i> (<i>tonal orthography</i>)), Upper Sorbian <i>čichać</i>, Lower Sorbian <i>tśichaś</i>. Also found as <i>чхать</i> (čxatʹ), inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьxati</i>, cognate with Ukrainian <i>чха́ти</i> (čxáty), Belarusian <i>чхаць</i> (čxacʹ), Polish <i>czchać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chikhirtma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Georgian <i>ჩიხირთმა</i> (čixirtma), from Azerbaijani <i>çığırtma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of young wine from the Caucasus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneeze</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Chihuahua dog.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чих</i> (čix).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>чи́стый</i> (čístyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limb</li><li>(grammar,&#32;obsolete) article</li><li>part</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) term</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) member of an organization</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) member, penis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>&#42;члѣнъ</i> (&#42;člěnŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čelnъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> / <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> + <i>&#42;-nъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>associate, associate member (member of an institution or society who is granted only partial status or privileges)</li><li>corresponding member</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding member, founder member</li></ol><p><i>член</i> (člen) +&lrm; <i>учреди́тель</i> (učredítelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) segment</li></ol><p><i>член</i> (člen) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arthropod</li></ol><p><i>чле́нистый</i> (člénistyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>нога́</i> (nogá) +&lrm; <i>-ое</i> (-oje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;offensive,&#32;vulgar) shemale, transgender</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>dickgirl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>articulately, distinctly (in a clear or effective manner)</li></ol><p><i>членоразде́льный</i> (členorazdélʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>articulate (speaking in a clear or effective manner)</li></ol><p><i>член</i> (člen, “member, part, constituent”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>разде́льный</i> (razdélʹnyj, “separate, distinct”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;derogatory) cocksucker</li></ol><p>From <i>член</i> (člen, “penis”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-сос</i> (-sos), combining form of <i>соса́ть</i> (sosátʹ, “to suck”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) membership</li></ol><p><i>член</i> (člen) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membership (fact of being a member)</li></ol><p><i>член</i> (člen) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чле́н</i> (člén).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) jerk, schmuck, schmoe, scoundrel, an unpleasant or detestable person, or a person without morals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>שמאָק</i> (shmok, “penis”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(informal,&#32;neologism,&#32;derogatory) synonym of <i>мо́бик</i> (móbik)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>чмо</i> (čmo, “scoundrel”) +&lrm; <i>мо́бик</i> (móbik, “poorly-trained Russian conscript soldier”), as if an abbreviation of <i><b>ч</b>асти́чно <b>моб</b>илизо́ванный</i> (<b>č</b>astíčno <b>mob</b>ilizóvannyj, “partially-mobilized”). In reference to the 2022 Russian mobilization being referred to euphemistically in Russian media as a <i>части́чная мобилиза́ция</i> (častíčnaja mobilizácija, “partial mobilization”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a sound of a kiss</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a sound of a kiss</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) smooch, peck, kiss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peck (to kiss)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack (with the lips)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kiss each other (noisily or casually), to peck each other, to smooch (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чмо́кать</i> (čmókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to peck (to kiss)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to smack (with the lips)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to kiss each other (noisily or casually), to peck each other, to smooch (<i>mutually</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чмо́кнуть</i> (čmóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rude,&#32;derogatory) schmuck, stooge, sleazeball, jerk (contemptible person)</li></ol><p><i>чмо</i> (čmo) +&lrm; <i>-шник</i> (-šnik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard) what, alternative form of <i>что</i> (što), alternative form of <i>чё</i> (čo)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a clinking, such as of glasses (the action or the sound)</li><li>a clatter, a clip-clop, a clop (such as of hooves), a clack</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) pronunciation of the <i>ц</i> (c) sound as <i>ч</i> (č), characteristic of certain Russian dialects</li></ol><p><i>чо́кать</i> (čókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clink glasses <i>(with)</i> <i>(when drinking toasts)</i></li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crazy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) strange, eccentric</li></ol><p>From <i>чокнуться</i> (čoknutʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj), itself from <i>чокаться</i> (čokatʹsja), utimatly of onomatopoeic origin.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>crackpot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go nuts, to get cranky (to become mad)</li><li>to clink glasses (with), to touch glasses (when drinking alcohol)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>чо́каться</i> (čókatʹsja) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prim, prudish</li></ol><p>From an unattested Old East Slavic word + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj); compare Russian Church Slavic <i>чепрь</i> (čeprĭ, “delight, pleasure”). Also cognate with <i>чепори́ться</i> (čeporítʹsja, “to be important”) (also <i>чепури́ться</i> (čepurítʹsja), <i>чепу́риться</i> (čepúritʹsja)), and similarly with Ukrainian <i>чепури́ти</i> (čepurýty, “to dress up, to decorate”) (also <i>чепу́рити</i> (čepúryty)), <i>чепури́тися</i> (čepurýtysja, “to be important”) (also <i>чепу́ритися</i> (čepúrytysja)), Belarusian <i>чепуры́цца</i> (čjepurýcca, “to be important”), Slovene <i>čepériti se</i> (“to bristle one's feathers, to be important”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>), Greek <i>τσοπορός</i> (tsoporós, “cutesy”) (a borrowing from Slavic).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1956 spelling of <i>чёрт</i> (čort).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>чёрт</i> (čort).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) sneeze, sneezing</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьxъ</i>, deverbal from <i>&#42;čьxati</i>; see <i>чиха́ть</i> (čixátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kaftan of blue cloth, with a standing collar and sleeves to the elbow (<i>among some Caucasian peoples</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian چوخا (čôxâ), from Ottoman Turkish چوقه (çoha, “broadcloth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to rump, to swive</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to do some rumpy-pumpy</li></ol><p><i>чпо́кать</i> (čpókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to rump, to swive</li></ol><p><i>чпо́кать</i> (čpókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to perform some rumpy-pumpy</li></ol><p><i>чпо́кнуть</i> (čpóknutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>being fraught with, pregnant with</li></ol><p><i>чрева́тый</i> (črevátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fraught (with)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) pregnant</li></ol><p><i>чре́во</i> (črévo) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) belly, womb</li><li>inside, interior</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрѣво</i> (črěvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;červo</i>. Doublet of <i>черёво</i> (čerjóvo), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ventriloquism (art of projecting one's voice so that it appears to come from another source)</li></ol><p>From <i>чре́во</i> (črévo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ние</i> (veščánije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ventriloquist</li></ol><p><i>чре́во</i> (črévo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gluttony (the vice of eating to excess)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glutton (one who eats voraciously)</li></ol><p><i>чре́во</i> (črévo, “belly”) +&lrm; <i>уго́дник</i> (ugódnik, “pleaser”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gluttonize</li></ol><p>From <i>чре́во</i> (črévo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>уго́дничать</i> (ugódničatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;poetic) succession, turn, sequence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрѣда</i> (črěda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerda</i> (“file, herd”). Doublet of <i>череда́</i> (čeredá), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;poetic) through; across, over</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрѣсъ</i> (črěsŭ) / <i>ⱍⱃⱑⱄⱏ</i> (črěsŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čersъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kert-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>. Doublet of <i>че́рез</i> (čérez), inherited from Old East Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>over-, trans-</li></ol><p>From <i>чрез</i> (črez). Compare <i>через-</i> (čerez-), from <i>через</i> (čerez).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>extremely, utterly</li></ol><p><i>чрезвыча́йный</i> (črezvyčájnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceptional, special, extraordinary</li></ol><p>From <i>чрез-</i> (črez-, “beyond”) + Polish <i>zwyczajny</i> (“usual”). Partial calque of Polish <i>nadzwyczajny</i>. Compare Russian <i>привы́чный</i> (privýčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>excessively (to an excessive degree)</li></ol><p><i>чрезме́рный</i> (črezmérnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excessive</li></ol><p><i>чрез-</i> (črez-) +&lrm; <i>ме́ра</i> (méra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(archaic,&#32;anatomy) reins, small of the back</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чрѣсла</i> (črěsla), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerslo</i>. Displaced the native East Slavic form <i>че́ресла</i> (čéresla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading (the process of interpreting written language)</li><li>reading (legislature&#58; one of several stages a bill passes through before becoming law)</li></ol><p><i>чт-</i> (čt-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elocutionist, reader</li></ol><p><i>чт-</i> (čt-) +&lrm; <i>-е́ц</i> (-éc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) trashy reading material, pulp fiction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьtivo</i>. By surface analysis, <i>чт-</i> (čt-) +&lrm; <i>-иво</i> (-ivo, “(material)”). For the root, compare <i>чту</i> (čtu, “I read”), <i>чти́вший</i> (čtívšij, “having read”), etc. For the ending, compare <i>ме́сиво</i> (mésivo, “jumble, mush”), <i>моло́зиво</i> (molózivo, “colostrum (type of breast milk)”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honor, to revere</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstiti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kʷit-tis</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>чти́ти</i> (čtýty), Polish <i>czcić</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) what</li><li>(literary,&#32;relative) that, which</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьто</i> (čĭto), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьto</i>, a univerbation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷid</i> and <i>&#42;tód</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>ну что ж</i> (nu što ž)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>what, what kind of, what sort of</li><li>what a/an [insert noun]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>as for, as to (regarding&#59; about)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as for (regarding&#59; about)</li><li>with regard to, as regards, in regard to</li><li>with respect to</li><li>in relation to, with reference to</li><li>in the matter of</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) why</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite pronoun</i>) anything, something <i>(implies one does not care what, or does not have any particular thing in mind)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> anything, something <i>(implies one does not care what, or does not have any particular thing in mind)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li><i>(indefinite pronoun)</i> something <i>(implies that one has a certain thing in mind, but does not know exactly what)</i></li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) somewhat, slightly, to some extent</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) for some reason</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) approximately, roughly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>что́бы</i> (štóby)</li></ol><p><i>что</i> (što) +&lrm; <i>б</i> (b)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that...should (indicates the subjunctive)</li><li>so that, in order that; in order to</li><li>lest</li></ol><p><i>что</i> (što) +&lrm; <i>бы</i> (by)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>чего-то</i> (čevo-to, “by some reason”)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) synonym of <i>что это</i> (što eto, “why”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to ask (too many) "what" questions</li></ol><p>From <i>что</i> (što, “what”) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>listen!; be quiet! (<i>interjection used to draw someone's attention to a sound or make someone listen to something</i>)</li><li>(<i>as an introductory word</i>) I can hear</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forelock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čubъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;Internet slang) a Ukrainian person</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur,&#32;Internet slang,&#32;specifically) a Ukrainian soldier who defends their country against the 2022 Russian invasion</li></ol><p><i>чуб</i> (čub, “osedelets forelock used by Ukrainian Cossacks”) +&lrm; <i>гумано́ид</i> (gumanóid, “humanoid”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;of horses</i>) dappled</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language; compare Kazakh <i>шұбар</i> (şūbar), Kyrgyz <i>чыбар</i> (cıbar), Bashkir <i>сыбар</i> (sıbar, “motley, speckled, spotted”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a forelock</li><li>(ethnic slur,&#32;offensive) a Ukrainian</li></ol><p><i>чуба́т</i> (čubát) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj), ultimately from чуб (čub, “a forelock”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>чуб</i> (čub): (small) forelock</li></ol><p><i>чуб</i> (čub) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) dude, guy, dawg</li></ol><p>Possibly a word of Romani origin (compare Romani <i>ćhavo</i>) + <i>-ак</i> (-ak). Compare English <i>chav</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) gunny sack, burlap bag (a large sack holding 80–100 kg of grain or similar commodities)</li><li>open hearth, fireplace, stove, firepit (<i>in the traditional architecture of ethnic groups of the Caucasus, the Volga region, and Siberia</i>)</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing; compare to Azerbaijani <i>çuval</i> (“sack”), Tatar <i>чувал</i> (çuwal, “fireplace”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chuvash (by ethnicity)</li></ol><p>Attested early 16th century in the obsolete form <i>чюваши</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (čjuvaši). Borrowed from Turkic whence modern Chuvash <i>чӑваш</i> (čăvaš, “Chuvash”), Tatar <i>чуаш</i> (çuwaş, “Chuvash”), and Eastern Mari <i>суас</i> (suas, “Tatar”). In the 16th and 17th century the Russian term may have referred to a social estate rather than a particular ethnicity. Further etymology uncertain; there are a number of hypotheses.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chuvash</li></ol><p><i>чува́ш</i> (čuváš) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensuality</li></ol><p><i>чу́вственный</i> (čúvstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perceptional, sensory</li><li>sensual</li></ol><p><i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated&#32;or&#32;ironic) feeling</li></ol><p><i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;dated) senses</li><li>(literary,&#32;dated) the expression of a feeling</li></ol><p><i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-и́лище</i> (-ílišče)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) most sensitively, most sincerely (used in expressions of thanks)</li></ol><p><i>чувстви́тельнейший</i> (čuvstvítelʹnejšij) +&lrm; <i>-е</i> (-e)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>чувстви́тельный</i> (čuvstvítelʹnyj): most sensitive</li></ol><p><i>чувстви́тельный</i> (čuvstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ейший</i> (-ejšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sensitively</li></ol><p><i>чувстви́тельный</i> (čuvstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensitivity</li></ol><p><i>чувстви́тельный</i> (čuvstvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensitive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense, feeling (sensation)</li><li>feeling, emotion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чоувьство</i> (čuvĭstvo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čuvьstvo</i>, from <i>&#42;čuti</i> (“to sense, feel, notice”) + <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i>. Cognate to <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ), <i>чутьё</i> (čutʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) soldier's feeling of being elbow to elbow with the man next to him, that allows him to keep rank</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) feeling of solidarity, of comradeship, of companionship</li></ol><p>Literally, “feeling of elbow”. Compare French <i>se serrer les coudes</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;dated</i>) feeling (<i>action or result</i>), emotion</li></ol><p><i>чу́вствовать</i> (čúvstvovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ание</i> (-anije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel (something)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>чоувьствовати</i> (čuvĭstvovati). By surface analysis, <i>чу́вство</i> (čúvstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>simile</i>) to make oneself at home (make oneself comfortable as if one were in one's own home)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) soft shoe without heels (in the Crimea and the Caucasus)</li></ol><p>From West Circassian <i>цуакъэ</i> (cʷaqɛ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cast iron</li><li>cooking pot (<i>traditionally made of cast iron</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>чюгунъ</i> (čjugun) (att. no later than 1665), likely a back-formation from <i>чюгунной</i> (čjugunnoj) first appearing in the Pskov 3rd Chronicle in the context of a 1615 siege (even though iron shot was cast in Russia already in late 16th century).</p><p>A Turkic borrowing, the most closely fitting are Chuvash <i>чугун</i> (čugun) and Azerbaijani <i>çuqun</i> (“cast iron; iron pot”), but both may be Russian loans. Cf. also Tatar <i>чуен</i> (çuyın), Crimean Tatar <i>çoyun</i>, Ottoman Turkish جويكن (çöygen, “cast iron; teapot”), Kumyk <i>чоюн</i> (çoyun), Southern Altai <i>чӧйгӧн</i> (čöygön, “teapot”), Ukrainian <i>чавун</i> (čavun, “cast iron; iron pot”), Romanian <i>ceaun</i> (“cauldron”).</p><p>Mudrak notes that medieval Turkic cognates meant either 'bronze, copper' as with Karakhanid جُوذِن (čoδïn) or 'iron ore', that in Karluk-Yugur, Altay and Teleut they denote metallic vessels, and suggests that it was derived from Middle Chinese <i>鑄</i> (tsyu<sup>H</sup>, “to cast”).</p><p>Räsänen notes that common turkic cognates (like to Southern Altai <i>чой</i> (čoy), Kumyk <i>чоюн</i> (çoyun), etc) were from Middle Chinese <i>鑄</i> (tsyu<sup>H</sup>, “to cast”). Given that South Siberian Turkic has -y- (like to Shor <i>шойун</i> (šoyun), Tuvan <i>шой</i> (şoy), Khakas <i>сойын</i> (soyın), etc.), ultimately it was borrowed by <i>鑄</i> (tsyu<sup>H</sup>, “to cast”).</p><p>Also, See also Bashkir <i>ҡурғаш</i> (qurğaş), Kyrgyz <i>коргошун</i> (korgoşun), Southern Altai <i>коргожын</i> (korgožïn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) cast iron</li><li>(military slang,&#32;uncountable) iron bombs, dumb bombs</li></ol><p><i>чугу́н</i> (čugún) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), literally, “cast ironium”, humorously modeled on element names such as <i>алюми́ний</i> (aljumínij, “aluminium/aluminum”), <i>ма́гний</i> (mágnij, “magnesium”), etc. Originally, from a Soviet-era military joke; one variation is as follows:</p><p>— На погрузку люминя становись&#33; — Не люминь, а алюминий. — А самый умный идёт грузить чугуний.<dl><dd><i>— Na pogruzku ljuminja stanovisʹ&#33; — Ne ljuminʹ, a aljuminij. — A samyj umnyj idjót gruzitʹ čugunij.</i></dd><dd>“Men, line up to load the luminum!” “Commander, it's not <i>luminum</i>, it's aluminium.” “And the clever fellow will be loading the cast ironium.”</dd></dl></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cast-iron stove</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) railroad</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cast-iron</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) obstinate, stubborn</li></ol><p><i>чугу́н</i> (čugún) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чугу́н</i> (čugún): (small, cast iron) pot, kettle, cauldron</li></ol><p><i>чугу́н</i> (čugún) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p><p>Compare typologically Polish <i>miednica</i>, Slovene <i>medenica</i> (&lt; <i>&#42;mědь</i>, whence also Russian <i>медь</i> (medʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an oddball, eccentric, weirdo</li></ol><p><i>чуди́ть</i> (čudítʹ, “to behave oddly”) +&lrm; <i>-а́к</i> (-ák)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oddity</li></ol><p><i>чудакова́тый</i> (čudakovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>queer, odd, peculiar, unusual</li></ol><p><i>чуда́к</i> (čudák) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strangeness, eccentricity (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чуда́к</i> (čudák) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act strange</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>miraculously, by a miracle</li><li>wonderfully, wonderful</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wonderful!, marvelous!</li></ol><p><i>чуде́сный</i> (čudésnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful, miraculous</li><li>wonderful, lovely, beautiful, marvellous</li></ol><p><i>чудеса́</i> (čudesá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oddball, eccentric, weirdo</li></ol><p><i>чуди́ть</i> (čudítʹ, “to behave oddly”) +&lrm; <i>-ла</i> (-la)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to act weirdly, to fool about</li></ol><p><i>чу́до</i> (čúdo) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to seem <i>(to)</i>, to appear <i>(to)</i></li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čuditi</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čudo</i> + <i>&#42;-išče</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wonderfully, beautifully</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) odd, strange, queer, funny</li></ol><p><i>чу́до</i> (čúdo) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marvelous, wonderful</li><li>beautiful</li><li>excellent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čudьnъ</i>. Equivalent to <i>чу́до</i> (čúdo) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle, marvel</li><li>wonder</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чудо</i> (čudo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čudo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(folklore,&#32;Slavic mythology) monstrous creature with fish-like or dragon-like traits</li><li>(<i>modern usage</i>) fairy-tale monster, bizarre creature</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Bulgarian <i>ю́да</i> (júda), Macedonian <i>јуда</i> (juda).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čudovišče</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ichthyology</i>) silver chimaera (<i>Chimaera phantasma</i>)</li></ol><p><i>чудо́вищный</i> (čudóviščnyj, “monstrous”) +&lrm; <i>химе́ра</i> (ximéra, “chimaera fish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>monstrously</li></ol><p><i>чудо́вищный</i> (čudóviščnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monstrous</li></ol><p><i>чудо́вище</i> (čudóvišče) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miraculous</li><li>amazing, extraordinary</li></ol><p><i>чу́до</i> (čúdo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>де́йствие</i> (déjstvije) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>miraculously</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miracle worker</li></ol><p>чу́до (čúdo) + творе́ц (tvoréc)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) wonder-working, miracle-working; marvellous; thaumaturgical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chud</li></ol><p><i>чудь</i> (čudʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) Chud</li><li>(collective,&#32;mythology,&#32;Urals,&#32;Siberia) a legendary people group (extinct or still living a subterranean life), builders of burial mounds and other prehistoric monuments</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, the term probably originally referred to Germanic people, and derives from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ťuďь</i> (“foreign, strange”), from Gothic <i>𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (þiuda, “people”); compare to <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój). Alternatively, perhaps related to Sami <i>čuđđe</i> (“enemy”), but that would require early contact between East Slavs and Sami, and the Sami word might rather be a loanword from Slavic.</p><p>The Sami word can be explained as a loan from Slavic during the war between Sami and the <i>Čuđek</i> (if this war has any historical evidence).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger, alien (person from elsewhere; also figuratively)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) stranger</li></ol><p><i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-анин</i> (-anin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreign country, strange/foreign land</li></ol><p>From <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-би́на</i> (-bína). For the suffix, compare <i>ложби́на</i> (ložbína, “hollow, depression, shallow ravine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, strange, alien [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>для</b> (dlja, + genitive) <small>‘to someone/something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a stranger to, not possessing, deprived of, without [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>щоуждь</i> (štuždĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ťuďь</i>. Doublet of <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) xenomania</li></ol><p>From <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-бесие</i> (-besije), translating Ancient Greek <i>ξενομανία</i> (xenomanía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stranger, foreigner (person in a foreign land)</li></ol><p><i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) foreign</li></ol><p><i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>земля́</i> (zemljá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alien, foreign, strange</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) alien to the body, rejected by the body</li></ol><p>From <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój, “foreign”) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>род</i> (rod, “origin, kind”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Ultimately a calque of Latin <i>alienigenus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) stranger, foreigner (person in a foreign land)</li></ol><p><i>чужо́й</i> (čužój) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>страна́</i> (straná) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something belonging to someone else</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>someone else’s, another’s, other’s</li><li>alien, strange, foreign</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alien, stranger, foreigner, outsider</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ťuďь</i>. Doublet of <i>чу́ждый</i> (čúždyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chewing gum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chewing gum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chukchi</li><li>(<i>related to</i>) Chukotka</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chukchi (by ethnicity)</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;ethnic slur) a naive and primitive person, especially with a mongoloid phenotype. (in sound analogy of <i>чурка</i> (čurka) and <i>чучмек</i> (čučmek))</li></ol><p>Derived from Chukchi (chauchu), meaning "rich in reindeer".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pantry, storeroom, closet</li></ol><p>A Turkic borrowing. See солан (solan) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>чула́н</i> (čulán): small pantry, small storeroom</li></ol><p><i>чула́н</i> (čulán) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stocking</li><li>(used in the expression <i>си́ний чуло́к</i> (sínij čulók, “bluestocking”))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чулъкъ</i> (čulŭkŭ), from Turkic. See Kazakh <i>шұлғау</i> (şūlğau) for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) stocking</li></ol><p><i>чуло́к</i> (čulók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague, pestilence</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) the plague, the Black Death</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Romanian <i>ciumă</i>, probably from Latin <i>cȳma</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>κῦμα</i> (kûma, “swell, wave, billow”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dirty, stained</li></ol><p>Per Vasmer, from <i>чу-</i> (ču-) (unclear) + the root of <i>ма́зать</i> (mázatʹ, “to soil”) + <i>-ый</i> (-yj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) chumak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) an ancestor</li><li>(<i>paganism</i>) an idol used in native Slavic religious rites</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;in spells) avaunt, away</li><li>(<i>children's talk, expresses leadership, claim to be the first</i>) bags (it)! (British), dibs</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;expresses a condition) mind you, but mind</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čurъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) to shy away from</li></ol><p><i>чур</i> (čur) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block, log</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) blockhead</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block, log</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) blockhead</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) an immigrant from Central Asia or the Caucasus, a wog</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чурба́к</i> (čurbák) block; log</li></ol><p><i>чурба́к</i> (čurbák) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chock, block of wood, a small, severed part of a tree</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dumb, uneducated person, dullard, booby</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) immigrant from Central Asia or the Caucasus, wog</li></ol><p>Related to Lithuanian <i>kiáuras</i>, Latvian <i>саũrs</i>, Lithuanian <i>kiùrstu, kiuraũ, kiùrti</i>, <i>skiaurė̃</i>, Middle High German <i>schore</i>. Less likely Polish <i>szczur</i> (“rat”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čurъ</i> or <i>&#42;ščurъ</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kew-</i>.</p><p>The pejorative sense of "Caucasian or Central Asian immigrant", while being a semantic narrowing of the word used towards dumb people in general, may have been influenced phonetically by words like чёрный (čórnyj, “black”) and -ка (-ka, “little”), referring to the immigrants' darker skin tones, or by черке́с (čerkés).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) alternative form of <i>чуть-чу́ть</i> (čutʹ-čútʹ, “a wee bit”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sensitive (of a person, instrument, etc.)</li><li>sharp, keen (sense of hearing, smell, etc.)</li><li>sympathetic, thoughtful, tactful</li><li>light (sleep)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čùtъkъ</i> (“attentive, careful”). By surface analysis, <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>keenly, sensitively</li><li>tactfully</li></ol><p><i>чу́ткий</i> (čútkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sensitiveness, keenness</li><li>responsiveness, consideration</li></ol><p><i>чу́ткий</i> (čútkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) a little, a bit</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>чут-</i> (čut-) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a (wee) bit, a little, just a bit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tiny</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a little, slightly</li><li>hardly, barely</li><li>(<i>with не</i>) almost, nearly</li></ol><p>From <i>чу́ять</i> (čújatʹ, “to scent, to feel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearly (almost, but not quite), virtually</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a little, a tiny bit, slightly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeling</li><li>sure instinct</li><li>scent (of an animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čutьje</i> (“perception”), from <i>&#42;čùti</i> (“to sense, feel”). Compare Polish <i>czucie</i>, Ukrainian <i>чуття</i> (čuttja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>the sound of a locomotive, choo</li><li>used to drive swine, soo wee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čuxъ</i>. Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>чоха́</i> (čoxá)</li><li>pig</li><li>chock, block of wood</li><li>block of wood in the game of gorodki</li><li>trifle</li></ol><p>See <i>чоха́</i> (čoxá).</p><p>Onomatopoeic in origin; compare <i>чух, чух&#33;</i> (čux, čux&#33;, cry to call a pig), also <i>цух, цух&#33;</i> (cux, cux&#33;), Latvian <i>cūka</i> (“pig”), Lithuanian <i>čiukà</i> (“pig”), Slovene <i>cúka</i> (“pig”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to mess around; to procrastinate</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tinker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) member of the various Finnic peoples living near Saint Petersburg; especially Estonians or Finnish people</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чухно</i> (čuxno), from <i>чудь</i> (čudĭ) with an expressive suffix <i>-хно</i> (-xno). For the suffix compare given names <i>Михно́</i> (Mixnó), <i>Яхно́</i> (Jaxnó), <i>Махно́</i> (Maxnó), <i>Юхно́</i> (Juxnó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drive, to compel, as a pig or horse, particularly as if interjecting <i>чух</i> (čux)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to caress, to pet, to fondle</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to escape, to vamoose, to skedaddle</li><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) to understand</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;rare) to pinch, to take away</li></ol><p><i>чух</i> (čux, “sooey”) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;ethnic slur&#32;in modern usage) a Finnish person, Finn</li></ol><p><i>чухна́</i> (čuxná) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), cognate with <i>чудь</i> (čudʹ, “the Chud people”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) Finnish; Estonian</li></ol><p><i>чухна́</i> (čuxná) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) female sexual organ, pussy</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) bogey, scarecrow</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;cucьkъ</i> (“dog, puppy”). Compare Slovene <i>cúca</i> (“female sexual organ”) (also <i>kúca</i>), <i>cúcati</i> (“to urinate”).</p><p>Probably related to Lithuanian <i>kaũkas</i> (“house spirit, dwarf, gnome”), Old Prussian <i>cawx</i> (“devil”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kuka</i> (see <i>ку́ка</i> (kúka), <i>ку́киш</i> (kúkiš)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stuffed animal (bird), mount</li><li>scarecrow, man of straw, dummy; effigy</li><li>scarecrow, fright, bogeyman</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čučelo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Juche (the state ideology of North Korea)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>주체</i> (juche).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) piglet, young pig</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;inanimate) pig's muzzle</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;animate) dirty person, especially a child</li><li>(criminal slang,&#32;animate) schmoe, schmuck (dirty, degraded person who anyone can humiliate)</li><li>(dialectal,&#32;inanimate) quarter of a pole, for making figures in the game of gorodki</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) ingot of metal, usually of cast iron; compare English <i>pig iron</i></li></ol><p><i>чу́ха</i> (čúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>Alternatively, a Turkic borrowing: compare Azerbaijani <i>çoşqa</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ingot of metal, especially cast iron</li></ol><p><i>чу́шка</i> (čúška) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) nerd, geek, wimp, pussy, patsy, weed (non-chav, non-thug)</li></ol><p>A variant form of <i>чушка́н</i> (čuškán), from <i>чу́шка</i> (čúška, “piglet”).</p><p>Repopularised by the 2023 series <i>Слово пацана. Кровь на асфальте (The Word of a Boy. Blood on the Asphalt)</i>, centred around youth gangs in Kazan in the late 1980s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;often&#32;derogatory) rubbish</li></ol><p>Per Shansky and Tsyganenko akin to <i>чужо́й</i> (čužój). Per Vasmer relation with the term is secondary. Compare typologically <i>дичь</i> (dičʹ) (akin to <i>ди́кий</i> (díkij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell</li><li>to feel, to sense</li><li>(regional,&#32;colloquial) to hear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čùti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewh₁-</i> (“to perceive”), whence English <i>show</i> and <i>caution</i>, as well as German <i>schauen</i> (“to look”), thereby English <i>anschauung</i> and <i>weltanschauung</i> (“sense of the world, worldview”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ǂHoan (Khoisan language of Botswana)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from ǂHoan <i>ǂHoan</i>, with the digraph ⟨чъ⟩ representing the palatal click /ǂ/.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Chengdu</li></ol><p>From <i>Чэнду́</i> (Čɛndú) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chengyu (a kind of Chinese set phrase or idiom)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chinese 成語／成语 (<i>chéngyǔ</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(nonstandard,&#32;colloquial,&#32;proscribed) what, wot (replacing the nominative singular or accusative singular form of the interrogative pronoun <i>что</i> (što))</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>чего́</i> (čevó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringe, bangs (<i>of hair</i>)</li><li><i>(horse's)</i> forelock</li></ol><p><i>чело́</i> (čeló) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canoe, dugout, monoxylon (a traditional boat made from a hollowed out log)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) any boat (<i>usually small</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьlnъ</i>. Cognate with <i>коло́да</i> (kolóda), <i>коло́ть</i> (kolótʹ).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>долблёнка</i> (dolbljónka) (&lt; <i>долби́ть</i> (dolbítʹ), synonymous); English <i>boat</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeyd-</i>; English <i>ship</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skey-</i>. Also compare Ancient Greek <i>σκάφη</i> (skáphē) (whence <i>скафа́ндр</i> (skafándr), <i>батиска́ф</i> (batiskáf)) <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skabʰ-</i> (whence also <i>скобли́ть</i> (skoblítʹ), English <i>shave</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>черка́ть</i> (čerkátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black widow</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black hole</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black magic, black art, witchcraft (magic derived from evil forces)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;uncountable</i>) unskilled job(s), grunt work, rough work (hard work not requiring any specialized skills)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) Black Death</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>substantia nigra (brain structure)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) diminutive of <i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”)</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-енький</i> (-enʹkij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-and-white, monochrome</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>бе́лый</i> (bélyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-brown</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>бу́рый</i> (búryj, “grayish-brown, dark reddish-brown, brown”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) black, gold, and white (the flag of the Russian Empire from 1858 to 1896)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of black and red colours individually</li></ol><p><i>чёрн(ый)</i> (čórn(yj), “black”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj, “red”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black</li><li>rough, coarse</li><li>back <i>(stairs, entrance, etc.)</i></li><li>(<i>labour</i>) unskilled, manual</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) ferrous; iron and steel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) dark, swarthy; dark-haired, black-haired</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) lacking a chimney to exhaust the smoke (<i>of buildings or rooms heated by a fire</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) black (man)</li><li>(offensive,&#32;ethnic slur) a darker-skinned immigrant</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrnъ</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Lithuanian <i>kir̃snas</i> and Sanskrit <i>कृष्ण</i> (kṛṣṇa, “black, dark”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Afro-American (a resident of the United States who is of African heritage)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black coffee (coffee served without cream or milk)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black swan (<i>Cygnus atratus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black pepper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black market</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blacklist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any longhorn beetle of the genus <i>Monochamus</i>.</li></ol><p><i>чёрный</i> (čórnyj, “black”) +&lrm; <i>усач</i> (usač, “longhorn beetle”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back door (a subsidiary entrance to a building, away from the street)</li><li>(<i>computer security</i>) back door (secret, unprotected means of access)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>aviation</i>) black box (recorders in an aircraft)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, explicitly</li></ol><p>Literally, “in black and white”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>callousness, hard-heartedness</li><li>staleness (e.g. of bread)</li></ol><p><i>чёрствый</i> (čórstvyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>about bread and baked goods, more rarely about other food – stale</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hardhearted, callous (emotionally hardened)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>чьрствъ</i> (čĭrstvŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrstvъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil, demon</li><li>Used as an intensifier in phrases grammatically requiring a noun; hell</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>damn!, hell!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) damn!, dammit! (expression of anger or disappointment)</li></ol><p>Literally, “devil, take it”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) damn!, dammit! (expression of anger or disappointment)</li></ol><p>Literally, “devil, pick it up”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) damn!, dammit! (expression of anger or disappointment)</li></ol><p>Literally, “devil, tear it up”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>чёрт</i> (čort): (little) devil, a little depiction of a devil</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possessive</i>) devil’s</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) damn, bloody, fucking</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baker's dozen (set of thirteen units)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ferris wheel (amusement ride: vertical observation wheel)</li><li>Human roulette wheel, Devil's wheel (amusement ride: horizontal rotating disk, which uses centrifugal force to fling people from its surface)</li></ol><p>From <i>чёртово</i> (čórtovo, “devil's”) + <i>колесо́</i> (kolesó, “wheel”). Compare ეშმაკის ბორბალი (ešmaḳis borbali) for a possible calque, as well as Polish <i>diabelski młyn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devil's, devilish</li><li>damn, goddamn, bloody</li></ol><p><i>чёрт</i> (čort) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>черта́</i> (čertá): (small) hyphen, dash; (short) line</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felt boot, a type of valenki</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orthodox Prayer Rope</li><li>rosary, prayer beads, paternoster (Catholic prayer beads)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distinct, clear, clear-cut</li><li>clear (transparent)</li><li>exact, accurate</li><li>legible</li><li>sharp, crisp (picture)</li></ol><p>From <i>честь</i> (čestʹ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьstь</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>distinctly, clearly, audibly, legibly</li></ol><p><i>чёткий</i> (čótkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clearness, legibility</li><li>accuracy, precision, preciseness, efficiency</li></ol><p><i>чёткий</i> (čótkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) even number</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>even (arithmetic: leaving no remainder when divided by 2)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;četьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>чёт</i> (čot, “even number”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>shhh!, shh! (quiet!)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) shush!, shhh! (be quiet!)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) what!? (<i>surprise, indignation, disgust, excitement or just arrogance</i>) (<i>spoken in Odessa, also in multiple other contexts</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Ш, ш.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sha'ban (the eighth month of the Hijri calendar)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturday, observed in Judaism as a day of rest, Sabbath.</li><li>A meeting of witches, presided over by the devil, Sabbath.</li><li>Any merry debauched festival.</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) end of the working day, quitting time (also pronounced šabáš).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szabas</i> from Yiddish <i>שבת</i> (shabes, “Sabbath”), from Hebrew <i>שַׁבָּת</i> (shabát). Doublet of <i>суббо́та</i> (subbóta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technical</i>) hand scraper (cutting tool, designed for leveling the surface of metal products by scraping very thin shavings)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schaber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>template, pattern</li><li>stencil</li><li>mould</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) platitude, cliché</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schablone</i> (“pattern”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) whore, slut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) neighbor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Saint Petersburg</i>) alternative form of <i>шаурма́</i> (šaurmá)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) small dog</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;abuse) cur, insignificant person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) move, step, action, initiative</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) pace, gait, stride (manner/way of walking)</li><li>(metonymic,&#32;locomotion) walking, a walk (a gait where at least one foot is always in contact with the ground)</li><li>(metonymic,&#32;equestrianism,&#32;gaits) walk (a slow, four-beat horse gait)</li><li>distance between evenly spaced objects, pitch</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;сяг</i> (&#42;sjag), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sęgъ</i>.</p><p>For the <i>сяга́ть</i> (sjagátʹ)—<i>сажень</i> (saženʹ) relation, compare typologically Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i>, whence English <i>fathom</i>, Latin <i>passus</i> (whence further <i>пасса́ж</i> (passáž), English <i>pace</i>).</p><p>(<i>move, step, action, initiative</i>): Compare typologically <i>посту́пок</i> (postúpok), <i>вы́ходка</i> (výxodka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>step by step</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to stride, to step (move on the feet)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step (move on the feet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at a walk, at a walking pace</li><li>at a slow pace (not running)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chagrin, shagreen (type of leather or skin)</li><li>(rare,&#32;figurative) chagrin (distress of mind)</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>La Peau de chagrin</i> of Balzac.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шаг</i> (šag)</li></ol><p><i>шаг</i> (šag) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шажо́к</i> (šažók); a little step</li></ol><p><i>шажо́к</i> (šažók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washer (flat disk)</li><li>hockey puck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Scheibe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) saic, a kind of small boat of up to twelve cannons heavily used in the Black Sea, Tisa, Danube and Sava; similar to the ушку́й (uškúj) used in Novgorod</li><li>washtub, tub, pail</li><li>gang of rogues, band of bandits (originally: sea-raiders on saics, like ushkuiniks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish شایقه (şayka), from Bulgarian <i>ша́йка</i> (šájka, “saic”). Doublet of <i>ча́йка</i> (čájka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaitan, Satan; the devil</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَيْطَان (šayṭān, “Shaitan, Satan”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Шайтан)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) shaitan, Satan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَيْطَان (šayṭān, “Satan, devil, shaitan”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schakal</i>, from Ottoman Turkish چقال (çakal), from Persian شغال (šaġâl), from Sanskrit <i>सृगाल</i> (sṛgāla, “jackal”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jackal</li></ol><p><i>шака́л</i> (šakál) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) whore, slut</li></ol><p>Related to шали́ть (šalítʹ, “to be naughty”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a simple wooden hut, hovel</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) the bottom bunk of a bunk bed, with a blanket hanging down from the top bunk as a curtain</li></ol><p>From a Turkic source, ultimately from Hungarian <i>szállás</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hovel</li></ol><p>From <i>шала́ш</i> (šaláš) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive suffix), from Turkish <i>salaş</i> or Azerbaijani <i>şalaş</i> (“hovel, tent”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowerbird</li></ol><p>From <i>шалаш</i> (šalaš, “hut”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, suffix appended to nominal stems to create a masculine noun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) prostitute, loose woman</li></ol><p>From <i>шалаш</i> (šalaš, “hut”) +&lrm; <i>-овка</i> (-ovka, suffix appended to nouns to form related nouns).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to go crazy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šalěti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play pranks, to be naughty</li><li>to play up, to play tricks</li><li>to frolic, to romp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šaliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>playful, mischievous, naughty</li></ol><p><i>шали́ть</i> (šalítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) shalom (Jewish greeting and farewell)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שָׁלוֹם</i> (shalóm). Doublet of <i>сала́м</i> (salám).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) imp, naughty boy (mischievous child)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) idler, loafer</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) scapegrace, scallywag, playboy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prank, mischief, monkey business</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shallot (a vegetable in the onion family)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>échalote</i> or German <i>Schalotte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imp, naughty boy (mischievous child)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sage (plant)</li><li>salvia</li></ol><p>From Middle High German <i>salveie</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>salvia</i>. See German <i>Salbei</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a square rug particularly such as tied up to collect remains of cotton on the field</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Uzbek <i>sholcha</i>, Kyrgyz <i>шалча</i> (şalca, “square rug”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shawl (a square piece of cloth worn as a covering for the head, neck, and shoulders)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Persian شال (šâl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stray bullet (a bullet that, after being fired from a gun, hits an unintended target)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) crazy, mad</li><li>stray (<i>bullet, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ша́лый</i> (šályj) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easy money</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) anyhow, somehow, haphazardly, hit-or-miss, hurry-scurry, hugger-mugger, rough-and-ready, slapdash, half-heartedly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaman</li></ol><p>From Evenki <i>шама̄н</i> (şamān), <i>сама̄н</i> (samān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally&#32;rare</i>) to conjure; to practise witchcraft</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to tinker, to fix something</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to work</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bring</li></ol><p><i>шама́н</i> (šamán) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) fenugreek (plant and produce)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Azerbaijani <i>şəmbəllə</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Шамбала)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shambhala, a mystical kingdom hidden somewhere beyond the snowpeaks of the Himalayas.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mumble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Champenois</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>champagne</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>champignon, field mushroom, meadow mushroom (any mushroom of the genus <i>Agaricus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>champignon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shampoo</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) champagne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>shampooing</i>, ultimately from Hindi <i>चाँपो</i> (cā̃po).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skewer (metal or wooden pin used to secure food during cooking)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Armenian <i>շամփուր</i> (šampʻur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) skewer (metal or wooden pin used to secure food during cooking)</li></ol><p><i>шампу́р</i> (šampúr) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chance, prospect <i>(chance for advancement, progress or profit)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chance</i>, from Latin <i>&#42;cadentia</i> (<i>cadēns</i> (p.a.p. of <i>cadō</i>) + <i>-ia</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) clipping of <i>ру́сский шансо́н</i> (rússkij šansón); Russian criminal song</li><li>chanson (French cabaret-style pop song by a chansonnier or a traditional polyphonic song of the 15th–16th centuries)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chanson</i>&nbsp;f (“song”), ultimately from Latin <i>canō</i>. Emerged as a euphemism in the Russian music industry for Russian criminal songs in the 1990s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chansonnier (singer of chansons)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chansonnier</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>canō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackmail (payment of money exacted by means of intimidation)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chantage</i>, ultimately from Latin <i>canō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blackmail</li></ol><p><i>шанта́ж</i> (šantáž) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackmailer (someone who blackmails)</li></ol><p><i>шанта́ж</i> (šantáž) +&lrm; <i>-ист</i> (-ist)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worthless person, rascal, scoundrel</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Originally a dialectal word.</p><p>Perhaps connected with Old Czech <i>šantrok</i>, <i>šаntrосha</i> (“cheater”), from Middle High German <i>santrocke</i> (“fraud”).</p><p>Alternatively, from French <i>chantera pas</i> (“will not sing”), said about serfs' children which were not chosen for the landowner's choir.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Shanghai; Shanghainese</li></ol><p><i>Шанха́й</i> (Šanxáj) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of pastry likened to a vatrushka but unsweet and often filled with potato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Finnic, compare Karelian <i>šanki</i> (“harvested cereals; stubble”); Komi-Zyrian, Udmurt and Eastern Mari have the word for the pastry from Russian.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Shanxi</li></ol><p>From <i>Шаньси́</i> (Šanʹsí) +&lrm; <i>-ийский</i> (-ijskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warm, soft, usually brimless hat or cap (e.g., beanie)</li><li>heading</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шапка</i> (šapka) attested since late 13th century (birchbark letter #141), ultimately from the Old French <i>chape</i> (see there for further etymology), several steps omitting from Latin <i>cappa</i>. Doublet of <i>капо́т</i> (kapót), <i>капу́т</i> (kapút), <i>капуци́н</i> (kapucín), <i>капучи́но</i> (kapučíno), <i>капюшо́н</i> (kapjušón), and <i>ке́пка</i> (képka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cap of invisibility</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ushanka cap (traditional Russian fur cap with ear flaps)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ша́пка</i> (šápka): (small) usually brimless hat or cap</li></ol><p><i>ша́пка</i> (šápka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball, solid sphere</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) ball (the set of points lying within a given distance from a given point)</li><li>balloon</li></ol><p>Unclear. Perhaps related (although Vasmer found it unconvincing) to Old Church Slavonic <i>шаръ</i> (šarŭ, “color, paint”), Old Church Slavonic <i>шарити</i> (šariti, “to paint”), Old Church Slavonic <i>шаръчи</i> (šarŭči, “painter”), which probably derive from an early Turkic borrowing into Slavic; cf. Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;siarïg</i> (“yellow, white”). In that case, the sense development might have been “color, paint” → “spot” → “round object”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charabanc (a horse-drawn and then, later, motorized omnibus with open sides and, often, no roof)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>char-à-bancs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;Soviet Union) sharashka, sharaga (a secret research and development laboratory in the Soviet gulag)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) any office or business producing low quality or dubious goods or services, especially if involved in a fraud</li><li>(<i>youth&#32;slang</i>) vocational school</li></ol><p>Originally <i>шара́шкина конто́ра</i> (šaráškina kontóra), the possessive form <i>шара́шкин</i> (šaráškin) is possibly from dialectal <i>шара́нь</i> (šaránʹ, “riffraff, fraudsters”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charade</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>charade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шара́га</i> (šarága, “sharashka, sharaga”)</li></ol><p><i>шара́га</i> (šarága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash aside, to shy</li></ol><p><i>шара́хать</i> (šaráxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to hit, to strike, to land a blow <i>(on)</i>, to sock, to paste one</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to boom, to shoot with a boom</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to drink, to gulp down</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>шара́хать</i> (šaráxatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dash aside, to shy</li></ol><p><i>шара́хнуть</i> (šaráxnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>шара́жка</i> (šarážka)</li></ol><p><i>шара́га</i> (šarága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caricature, cartoon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>charge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(usually&#32;uncountable,&#32;Islam) shari'a (Islamic religious law)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَرِيعَة (šarīʕa); the final /t/ is pronounced in very formal settings or in إِضَافَة (ʔiḍāfa, “genitive construction”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шар</i> (šar): small ball, bead, party balloon</li><li>corpuscle (<i>of blood</i>)</li><li>(plural only,&#32;slang) brains, mental ability</li></ol><p><i>шар</i> (šar) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ballpoint pen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) small ball, bead</li></ol><p><i>ша́рик</i> (šárik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fumble, to rummage</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to understand, to have knowledge of, to know something well (<i>about activities</i>)</li><li>(slang,&#32;computing) to share, especially digitally</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ша́ркать</i> (šárkatʹ, “to shuffle”) and <i>шо́рох</i> (šórox, “rustle”).</p><p>Derived from English <i>share</i> + <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shuffling</li></ol><p><i>ша́ркать</i> (šárkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-анье</i> (-anʹje)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shuffle</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Compare <i>шо́ркать</i> (šórkatʹ, “to rustle, to drag one's feet”), <i>шурша́ть</i> (šuršátʹ, “to rustle”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shuffle</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ша́ркать</i> (šárkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charlatan, quack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>charlatan</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charlatanism, charlatanry, quackery</li></ol><p><i>шарлата́н</i> (šarlatán) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charlotte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>charlotte</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charm (quality of inspiring delight or admiration)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>charme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barrel organ</li><li>(<i>informal</i>) repetitious talk</li></ol><p>From French <i>Charmante Catherine</i> (“Charming Catherine”, the name of one of the first songs played by barrel organ) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(street) barrel organ player; barrel organist</li></ol><p><i>шарма́нка</i> (šarmánka) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinge, joint (joint with freedom to rotate)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Scharnier</i>, from French <i>charnière</i>. Further from Latin <i>cardō</i>, whence also <i>кардина́л</i> (kardinál).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shalwar (wide trousers, baggy trousers)</li><li>(computing,&#32;slang) shareware</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>шаровари</i> (šarovary) no later than the 16th century, from Turkish <i>şalvar</i>, from Persian شلوار (šalvâr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ball, globe; globular, spherical</li></ol><p><i>шар</i> (šar) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) crook, swindler, fraud</li></ol><p>From the adverbs ша́ром (šarom), ши́ром (širom), шу́ром (šurom) ‘free of charge’ (from hucksters' slang), plus the suffix <i>-ыга</i> (-yga). The popular version deriving the word from French <i>cher ami</i> (“dear friend”), a term used by soldiers of the retreating army of Napoleon to appeal for help, mercy, etc. nowadays is considered wrong.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) crook, swindler, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>шаромы́га</i> (šaromýga, “crook, swindler”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, “suffix denoting a profession, performer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;used by flat earthers or ironically) globehead</li></ol><p>Literally, “globe fucker”, from <i>шар</i> (šar, “globe”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ). Spread in 2017 from young YouTuber Максим Ожерельев who promoted flat-Earth beliefs in a particularly memeable manner.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go, to walk; (<i>often</i>) to wander (to move without purpose or specified destination)</li></ol><p><i>ша́риться</i> (šáritʹsja, “to walk around, to rummage”) +&lrm; <i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck around”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to abrade</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to rob</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>szarpać</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarf (type of clothing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szarfa</i>, from German <i>Schärpe</i>, from French <i>écharpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шарф</i> (šarf, “scarf”)</li></ol><p><i>ша́рф</i> (šárf) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landing gear</li><li>chassis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>châssis</i>. Ultimately from Latin <i>capsa</i>. Doublet of <i>ка́псула</i> (kápsula), <i>ка́псюль</i> (kápsjulʹ), <i>ка́сса</i> (kássa), and <i>кассе́та</i> (kasséta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;deprecative) to roam, to hang about, to hang around</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šastati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) a kind of outerwear</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) metallic framework of icons</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sway, to rock, to shake</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to sway; to (cause to) stagger, to (cause to) reel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шатати</i> (šatati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šętati</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>шѩтаниѥ</i> (šjętanije), Ukrainian <i>шата́тися</i> (šatátysja, “to sway, to stagger”), Bulgarian <i>ше́там</i> (šétam, “to go back and forth, to manage, to serve”), Serbo-Croatian <i>ше́тати</i> (“to walk”) (1sg. <i>ше̑та̄м</i> or <i>ше̑ће̄м</i>), Slovene <i>šétati se</i> (“to walk”), Czech <i>šátat</i> (“to move”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sway, to rock, to shake; to stagger, to reel; to be unstable</li><li>to lounge, to roam, to loaf about</li></ol><p><i>шата́ть</i> (šatátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) shutdown (action of closing a computer or a program)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shutdown</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man/boy with brown hair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>châtain</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaky, unsteady, rickety</li><li>precarious, wavering, weak, unsteady</li></ol><p><i>шат(а́ть)</i> (šat(átʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shakiness, unsteadiness</li><li>precariousness</li></ol><p><i>ша́ткий</i> (šátkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>château</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>château</i>, from Middle French <i>chasteau</i>, <i>chastel</i>, from Old French <i>chastel</i>, from Latin <i>castellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shatranj</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَطْرَنْج (šaṭranj), from Persian شترنگ (šatrang), from Middle Persian <i>𐭰𐭠𐭲𐭫𐭠𐭭𐭢</i> (ctlng /⁠čatrang⁠/), from Sanskrit <i>चतुरङ्ग</i> (caturaṅga).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shuttle, Space Shuttle</li><li>STS (Space Transportation System)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shuttle</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large tent (portable lodge), marquee</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) hipped roof, tented roof</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) canopy</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;čātïr</i>; compare Albanian <i>çadër</i>, Hungarian <i>sátor</i>, Central Kurdish چادر (çadir), Persian چادر, Serbo-Croatian <i>šator</i>, Urdu چادر (ćādar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>шатёр</i> (šatjór).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shawarma</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَاوِرْمَا (šāwirmā) or شَاوَرْمَا (šāwarmā), from Ottoman Turkish چویرمه (çevirme).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>best man, groomsman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schaffer</i> (&lt; <i>schaffen</i>, literally <i>устрои́тель</i>, akin to English <i>shape</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saffron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish صفران (Turkish <i>şafran</i>), from Arabic زَعْفَرَان (zaʕfarān).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) saffron</li></ol><p><i>шафра́н</i> (šafrán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shah</li><li>(<i>chess</i>) check (Russian notation: +)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian شاه (šāh).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checkmate, check and mate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian شَاهِ‌مَات (šāh-i-māt, “the king is dead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) dishonest deal or trade, machinations, shenanigans</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szacher-macher</i> or Yiddish <i>שאַכער־מאַכער</i> (shakher-makher), from German <i>Schacher-Macher</i>; compare Romanian <i>șahăr-mahăr</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chess player</li></ol><p>From <i>ша́хматы</i> (šáxmaty, “chess”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst, “-ist”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chessboard</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chess</li></ol><p><i>ша́хматы</i> (šáxmaty) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chess (two-player board game)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шахматꙑ</i> (šaxmaty), probably from Middle High German <i>schāch unde mat</i> (compare German <i>Schachmatt</i> (“checkmate”)), from Persian شاه مات (šâh mât, “the king is conquered”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) to check (to make a move that puts an adversary's king in check)</li><li>(<i>poker</i>) to check (to announce that one is remaining in a hand without betting)</li></ol><p><i>шах</i> (šax) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mine, pit (place from which ore is extracted)</li><li>shaft</li><li>(<i>military</i>) silo</li></ol><p>From Middle Low German <i>schacht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mine (underground tunnel for extracting ore)</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) shaft, mine shaft</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) furnished with a shaft, shafted</li></ol><p><i>ша́хта</i> (šáxta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miner, pitman (a person who works in a mine)</li><li>(politics,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;dialect,&#32;slang) someone from the Donbass region.</li></ol><p><i>ша́хта</i> (šáxta, “mine, pit”) +&lrm; <i>-ёр</i> (-jór)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) miner; miner's, miners'</li></ol><p><i>шахтёр</i> (šaxtjór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naval shipworm, turu (<i>Teredo navalis</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) house longhorn beetle, house borer (<i>Hylotrupes bajulus</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps onomatopoeic, referring to the sound of insect larvae boring through wood. Cognate with <i>ша́шель</i> (šášelʹ) and Ukrainian <i>ша́шіль</i> (šášilʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shashiya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>checker, draughtsman (a piece in a game of checkers&#47;draughts)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) checkers/draughts (board game)</li><li>explosive charge, stick (<i>e.g. of dynamite</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) smoke grenade</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) paving block</li><li>Cossack sabre, Circassian sabre, shashka (a type of guardless, lightly-curved sabre)</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) rider, cavalryman (<i>as a counting unit of military strength</i>)</li></ol><p><i>шах</i> (šax, “check”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p><p>A Circassian borrowing, compare to West Circassian <i>сэшхуэ</i> (sɛšxʷɛ, “long knife”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>draughts, checkers (game for two players)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shashlik (skewered dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Crimean Tatar <i>şışlıq</i>/<i>шишлик</i> (“something to be cooked on a skewer”), from <i>şiş</i> (“spit, skewer”) +&lrm; <i>-lik</i> (“-ness, -hood, -ity”; also “for”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shashlik-house, shashlik restaurant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shashlik</li></ol><p><i>шашлы́к</i> (šašlýk) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) love affair</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) intrigues, machinations</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonetics</i>) schwa</li><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>Ə</i>/<i>ə</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swabian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schwabe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mop (implement for washing floors)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) woman, such as seen from an utilitarian point of view</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>zwabber</i> or dialectal German <i>Schwabber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;sometimes&#32;collectively,&#32;animate) scum, dregs, despicable person, riffraff, despicable people</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;usually&#32;collectively,&#32;inanimate) rubbish, useless things</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) tailor</li></ol><p>Unknown. Sometimes compared with German <i>Schwall</i> (“flood”) or French <i>cheval</i> (“horse”).</p><p>From <i>шить</i> (šitʹ, “to sew”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) tailor's</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) mooring line; hawser, (shore) fast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>zwaartouw</i> (literally “heavy cable”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moor, to tie down (to fix or secure, as a vessel, in a particular place by casting anchor, or by fastening with cables or chains)</li></ol><p><i>шварто́в</i> (švartóv, “mooring rope”) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly, poorly</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>schwach</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swede (Swedish person)</li></ol><p>Probably from German <i>Schwede</i>. Doublet of <i>свей</i> (svej).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall bars</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Swedish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swedish (language)</li></ol><p><i>швед</i> (šved) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smorgasbord</li></ol><p>Literally, “a Swedish table”</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) seamstress</li></ol><p><i>швея́</i> (švejá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing machine</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sewing</li></ol><p><i>швея́</i> (švejá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorman (doorkeeper, usher, porter)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) Swiss man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schweizer</i> (“Swiss person; doorman”) (because Swiss people were often employed as doormen). First used in the 18th century. Possibly via Polish <i>szwajcar</i>. Doublet of <i>швейца́рец</i> (švejcárec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Swiss (Swiss person)</li></ol><p><i>Швейца́рия</i> (Švejcárija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Swiss</li></ol><p><i>Швейца́рия</i> (Švejcárija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(construction,&#32;mechanical engineering) channel, C-channel (a structural beam with a &#91;-shaped cross section)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) expression pedal (<i>of a pipe organ</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schweller</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) tailor</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьvьcь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>шить</i> (šitʹ, “to sew”) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>швырну́ть</i> (švyrnútʹ)</li></ol><p><i>швырну́ть</i> (švyrnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss, to hurl, to throw, to chuck</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to put things in disorder, to not where they belong (e.g. clothing)</li><li>to fling (money)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Related to Bulgarian <i>хвъ́рля</i> (hvǎ́rlja), Macedonian <i>фрла</i> (frla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toss, to hurl, to throw, to chuck</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to put things in disorder, to not where they belong (e.g. clothing)</li><li>to fling (money)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Related to Bulgarian <i>хвъ́рля</i> (hvǎ́rlja), Macedonian <i>фрла</i> (frla).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stir, movement</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stir, to turn over</li><li>to move slightly, to budge [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘someone/something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ševeliti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>informal</i>) to consider, to reflect on, to contemplate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move, to stir, to budge</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to awaken, to show signs of life</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) (<i>thoughts, feelings, etc.</i>) to appear</li></ol><p><i>шевели́ть</i> (ševelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move, to stir, to budge</li></ol><p><i>шевели́ть</i> (ševelítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to move</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to awaken, to show signs of life</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) (<i>thoughts, feelings, etc.</i>) to appear</li></ol><p><i>шевельну́ть</i> (ševelʹnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the hair on one's head</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chevelure</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>capillus</i> (whence also Russian <i>капилля́р</i> (kapilljár)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(automotive,&#32;informal) A Chevy.</li></ol><p>From <i>шевроле́</i> (ševrolé, “Chevrolet”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>leather</i>) kid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chevron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chevron</i> (“heraldic chevron”). Further from Latin <i>caper</i>. Doublet of <i>капри́з</i> (kapríz) and <i>шевро́</i> (ševró).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>шевро́н</i> (ševrón).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tinker</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masterpiece (piece of work that has been given much critical praise)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chef-d'œuvre</i>. Displaced previous <i>шедёвр</i> (šedjóvr).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deck chair, beach chair (a folding chair)</li><li>sunlounger</li><li>(<i>obsolete&#32;or&#32;historical</i>) chaise longue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chaise longue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ше́я</i> (šéja)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) narrow part of an object</li><li>(<i>food</i>) neck part of an animal for meat</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) tail (for crustacean animals)</li></ol><p><i>ше́я</i> (šéja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) neck; cervical</li></ol><p><i>ше́я</i> (šéja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fitness</i>) a system of rhythmic gymnastics or fitness exercises for losing weight</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) traffic shaping (bandwidth management technique)</li><li>(<i>psychology</i>) shaping (conditioning paradigm from the school of thought known as experimental analysis of behaviour)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shaping</i>. The fitness meaning in Russian was created at the turn of the 1990s.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Islam</i>) sheik, sheikh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic شَيْخ (šayḵ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheqel, shekel (currency unit in Israel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שֶׁקֶל</i> (shékel).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Shakespeare; (<i>literature</i>) Shakespearean</li></ol><p><i>Шекспи́р</i> (Šekspír) +&lrm; <i>-овский</i> (-ovskij, “-ian; -ish”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustle</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. Cognate of Polish <i>szelest</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle</li></ol><p><i>ше́лест</i> (šélest) +&lrm; <i>-еть</i> (-etʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silky</li></ol><p><i>шёлковый</i> (šólkovyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́стый</i> (-ístyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulberry (<i>the tree or the fruit</i>)</li></ol><p>Possibly by association with silk (шёлк) and the silkworm (Тутовый шелкопряд), for which mulberry leaves are a main source of food.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) mulberry</li></ol><p><i>шелкови́ца</i> (šelkovíca) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folk poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>шёлковый</i> (šólkovyj)</li></ol><p><i>шёлк</i> (šolk) +&lrm; <i>-о́вый</i> (-óvyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silkworm</li></ol><p><i>шёлк</i> (šolk, “silk”) +&lrm; <i>прясть</i> (prjastʹ, “to spin”, verb)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;or&#32;folk poetic</i>) helmet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шеломъ</i> (šelomŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šelmъ</i>. Doublet of <i>шлем</i> (šlem), borrowed from Old Church Slavonic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, to move</li></ol><p><i>шелохну́ть</i> (šeloxnútʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) shelter (an institution that provides temporary housing for homeless people, battered women, etc.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shelter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husk, hull, peel, scale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) rascal, rogue, scoundrel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>schelme</i>, from Polish <i>szelma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shelpek (Central Asian flatbread)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Kazakh <i>шелпек</i> (şelpek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) shelter deck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) shelf, offshore area</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shelf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danube bleak</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sports,&#32;equestrianism) part of a rider's leg from the knee to the ankle facing a horse and used to start the horse's movement or change its pace or direction</li><li>(sports,&#32;equestrianism,&#32;figuratively) leg kick to the croup of the horse by the rider</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lisp, e.g. to mispronounce sibilants [s] and [z]</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) whispering</li></ol><p>From <i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ня́</i> (-njá); compare <i>шёпот</i> (šópot, “whisper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шёпот</i> (šópot, “whisper”)</li></ol><p><i>шёпот</i> (šópot) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sear (part of a firearm that retards the hammer until the trigger is pulled).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper (to talk in a quiet voice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьpъtati</i>, of onomatopoeic origin. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>шепта́ти</i> (šeptáty), Belarusian <i>шапта́ць</i> (šaptácʹ), Polish <i>szeptać</i>, Bulgarian <i>шептя́</i> (šeptjá), etc.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisper to each other</li></ol><p><i>шепта́ть</i> (šeptátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sherbet, sorbet (frozen fruit juice)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military&#32;and&#32;figuratively</i>) rank, row</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szereg</i>, ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;čerig</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheriff</li><li>shrievalty</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rough (not smooth), rugged</li></ol><p>From шо́рох (šórox).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roughness, asperity (as of stone)</li><li>rugosity</li><li>seediness</li><li>shagginess</li></ol><p><i>шерохова́тый</i> (šeroxovátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough (not smooth), rugged, uneven</li></ol><p><i>шеро́хий</i> (šeróxij) +&lrm; <i>-оватый</i> (-ovatyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woollen mill</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fur, hair (animal hair or human body hair; contrast волосы and мех)</li><li>wool (hair of an animal used to make clothing)</li><li>woollen cloth, worsted, wadding</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>сьрсть</i> (sĭrstĭ), (since the 14th century) <i>шьрсть</i> (šĭrstĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrstь</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer(s)-</i> (“rough hair, bristle”) + <i>&#42;-tь</i>, whence also Lithuanian <i>šerys</i>, English <i>hair</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) wool; woolen, woollen</li><li>wooly</li></ol><p><i>шерсть</i> (šerstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-яно́й</i> (-janój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough, coarse</li></ol><p>From <i>шерша́в</i> (šeršáv, “rough”) +&lrm; <i>-вый</i> (-vyj), which per Vasmer comes either from Old East Slavic <i>шьрсть</i> (šĭrstĭ) -and is thus cognate to <i>шерсть</i> (šerstʹ, “wool”)- or from a root related to <i>шо́рох</i> (šórox, “rustle, murmur”). Compare Ukrainian <i>шереха́тий</i> (šerexátyj), Belarusian <i>ше́рхаць</i> (šjérxacʹ), Slovene <i>sŕšati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornet</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьrxy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьstъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sixth (one of six equal parts of a whole)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>procession, demonstration</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>шьствие</i> (šĭstvije), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьstvьje</i>. Constructed from the root of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьdlъ</i> (resultative participle, later past tense, of <i>&#42;jьti</i> (“to go”)) + <i>&#42;-ьstvo</i> + <i>&#42;-ьje</i> (compare Russian <i>идти́</i> (idtí), <i>-ство</i> (-stvo), <i>-ие</i> (-ije)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) procession</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ше́ствие</i> (šéstvije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk (slowly with procession)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>шьствовати</i> (šĭstvovati). By surface analysis, <i>ше́ств(ие)</i> (šéstv(ije)) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>six-in-hand, six horses harnessed together</li><li>cogwheel, gear wheel</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) pinion, smaller of two gear wheels</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) small lantern wheel</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>шестерня</i> (šesternja, “small lantern wheel”), from Middle Russian <i>шесть</i> (šestʹ, “six”) because these wheels were often made with six pins (even though numbers up to ten were also common)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective number</i>) six (in a group together), six of them</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cog, gear wheel</li></ol><p><i>шестерня́</i> (šesternjá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hexahedron</li></ol><p>From <i>шести-</i> (šesti-) +&lrm; <i>-гра́нник</i> (-gránnik), literally “six-side-er”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hexahedral</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allen key (a hex head wrench)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geometry,&#32;rare) hexecontahedron</li></ol><p><i>шести́десяти</i> (šestídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sixty-year period</li><li>sixtieth anniversary, sixtieth birthday</li></ol><p><i>шести́десяти</i> (šestídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sixty-year</li><li>sixty-year-old</li></ol><p><i>шести́десяти</i> (šestídesjati, genitive) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person of the sixties (<i>especially a Russian liberal intellectual of the 1860's, or a Soviet liberal intellectual of the 1960's, during the "Khrushchev Thaw"</i>)</li></ol><p><i>шестидеся́тый</i> (šestidesjátyj) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixtieth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) six-day</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>день</i> (denʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́вный</i> (-évnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>six-winged, hexapterous</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>крыло́</i> (kryló) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>six-year period</li><li>sixth anniversary, sixth birthday</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) six-year</li><li>six-year-old</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>six-fingered</li><li>six-toed</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>па́л(ец)</i> (pál(ec)) +&lrm; <i>-ый</i> (-yj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>шестистру́нная гита́ра</i> (šestistrúnnaja gitára): six-string guitar</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>six-string (of a guitar)</li></ol><p><i>шести</i> (šesti) +&lrm; <i>стру́нный</i> (strúnnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hexagon</li></ol><p><i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hexagonal (shaped like a hexagon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hexadecahedron</li></ol><p><i>шестна́дцать</i> (šestnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>грань</i> (granʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sixteen-year period</li><li>sixteenth anniversary, sixteenth birthday</li></ol><p><i>шестна́дцать</i> (šestnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ие</i> (-ije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sixteen-year</li><li>sixteen-year-old</li></ol><p><i>шестна́дцать</i> (šestnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>ле́то</i> (léto) +&lrm; <i>-ний</i> (-nij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hexadecagon</li></ol><p><i>шестна́дцать</i> (šestnádcatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и-</i> (-i-) +&lrm; <i>у́гол</i> (úgol) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixteenth</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixteen (16)</li></ol><p>From <i>шесть</i> (šestʹ, “six”) +&lrm; <i>-на́дцать</i> (-nádcatʹ, “-teen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>16×, sixteen times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шестъ</i> (šestŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šȅstъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;seśtas</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweḱstós</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шест</i> (šest); a small pole, staff, stick</li><li>roost, perch (a horizontal pole for chickens to perch on)</li><li>the raised and often covered hearth platform in front of the firebox of a traditional Russian stove</li><li>(obsolete,&#32;dialectal) covered hearth (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p><i>шест</i> (šest, “pole”) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók). Dal mentions a hearth with a raised cover on supports (“poles”), providing one possible connection between the “pole” and “hearth” senses.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six (6)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шесть</i> (šestĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šestь</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;šéš</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱs</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty (60)</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>шесть</i> (šestʹ) +&lrm; <i>де́сять</i> (désjatʹ), literally “six tens”. <b>Де́сят</b> was the Old East Slavic genitive plural, which is required by numbers <i>пять</i> (pjatʹ, “5”) and up.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-eight (68)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-two (62)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-nine (69)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-one (61)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-five (65)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-seven (67)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-three (63)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-four (64)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty-six (66)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six hundred (600)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>6×, six times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>60×, sixty times as much</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) six</li><li>group of six</li><li>(<i>card games</i>) six</li><li>No 6 bus tram, etc</li><li>six-in-hand</li><li>six-oar boat</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) underling, minion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boss, chief</li><li>patron, sponsor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chef</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>caput</i> (whence also <i>капита́н</i> (kapitán)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chef (head cook of an establishment such as a restaurant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;neologism,&#32;uncommon) to chef, to juice up, to murk with the rambo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>chef</i> (verb) + <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patronage, sponsorship</li></ol><p><i>шеф</i> (šef) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act as patron, to have the patronage, to patronize, to sponsor, to support, to become a sponsor</li></ol><p>From <i>ше́фство</i> (šéfstvo) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) neck</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šija</i>.</p><p>For <i>шея</i> (šeja) — <i>шить</i> (šitʹ) relation, compare typologically <i>долговя́зый</i> (dolgovjázyj) — <i>вяза́ть</i> (vjazátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shiatsu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>指圧</i> (しあつ, shiatsu).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shibboleth (a word, especially seen as a test, to distinguish someone as belonging to a particular nation, class, profession etc)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>schibboleth</i>&nbsp;/ <i>shibboleth</i>, from Hebrew <i>שִׁבֹּלֶת</i>&nbsp;/ <i>שיבולת</i> (shibólet).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slide valve, gate valve</li><li>damper (<i>for a camera</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schieber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) shorty, a short, unattractive man</li><li>(derogatory,&#32;slang) squirt, a young boy (of short stature)</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly a blend of <i>ши́зик</i> (šízik, “schizo”) +&lrm; <i>бздит</i> (bzdit, “farts”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) fast, quick</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šȋbъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;šibati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>шиба́ть</i> (šibátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) fast, quickly</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) hard, much</li></ol><p><i>ши́бкий</i> (šíbkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o).</p><p>Compare typologically Polish <i>bardzo</i> (akin to <i>борзо́й</i> (borzój)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;Hinduism) Shaivism, the variant of Hinduism that venerates the god Shiva.</li></ol><p><i>Ши́ва</i> (Šíva, “Shiva, the Hindu god of destruction and eternity”) +&lrm; <i>-изм</i> (-izm), from Sanskrit <i>शिव</i> (śiva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) collar, scruff of the neck</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>topsy-turvy (disorderly, chaotically)</li><li>inside out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) schizo</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>шизофре́ник</i> (šizofrénik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) schizo, psycho, crazy, loopy, mental</li></ol><p>Derived from the past passive participle of <i>шизану́ть</i> (šizanútʹ). Ultimately derived from <i>шизофрени́я</i> (šizofreníja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) schizo</li></ol><p>Shortening of <i>шизофре́ник</i> (šizofrénik). Also analysed as <i>шизо-</i> (šizo-) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>schizo-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schizoid</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;nonstandard) schizo</li></ol><p><i>шизо-</i> (šizo-) +&lrm; <i>-ид</i> (-id).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) somebody who signals patriotic attitude while at the same time orienting himself towards a foreign ideology</li></ol><p><i>шизо-</i> (šizo-) +&lrm; <i>патрио́т</i> (patriót)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schizophrenic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>schizophrenic</li></ol><p><i>шизофрени́я</i> (šizofreníja) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) schizophrenia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schizophrenie</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō, “I tear, split, cut”) + <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind, brain”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shi'ism</li></ol><p>Arabic شِيعَة (šīʕa) +&lrm; <i>-и́зм</i> (-ízm)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shiite</li></ol><p>Arabic شِيعَة (šīʕa) +&lrm; <i>-ит</i> (-it)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Shiite</li></ol><p><i>шии́т</i> (šiít) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chic, stylishness, ostentatious smartness, good style</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) chic, smart, splendid, grand, magnificent, stylish (elegant)</li></ol><p><i>шикарный</i> (šikarnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ос</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chic, smart, splendid, grand, magnificent, stylish (elegant)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awl</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шило</i> (šilo), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šidlo</i>, composed of <i>&#42;šiti</i> (“to sew”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-dlo</i> (“(tool suffix)”).</p><p>Cognate to Czech <i>šídlo</i> (“awl”) and Latin <i>sūbula</i> (“awl”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avocet (bird)</li></ol><p><i>ши́ло</i> (šílo) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>клюв</i> (kljuv) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) plate, sign</li></ol><p>Older <i>ши́льда</i> (šílʹda) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik), ultimately from Middle High German <i>schilt</i>. The older terms <i>ши́льда</i> (šílʹda) and <i>шильд</i> (šilʹd) have been discontinued in standard Russian. Compare modern Ukrainian <i>шильд</i> (šylʹd), Belarusian <i>шы́льда</i> (šýlʹda), Polish <i>szyld</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chimpanzee (ape)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>automotive</i>) tire/tyre</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) splint</li><li>bus (electrical conductor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schiene</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shake (type of wooden shingle)</li><li>shingled roof</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schindel</i>, from Middle High German <i>schindel</i>, from Old High German <i>scintula</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;skindulā</i>, from Late Latin <i>scindula</i>, from Latin <i>scandula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pejorative of <i>шине́ль</i> (šinélʹ)</li></ol><p><i>шине́ль</i> (šinélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ишка</i> (-iška)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) uniform overcoat; greatcoat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chenille</i>. Doublet of <i>шени́лл</i> (šeníll).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шине́ль</i> (šinélʹ)</li></ol><p><i>шине́ль</i> (šinélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) coatroom</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) diminutive of <i>шине́ль</i> (šinélʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>regional</i>) ham</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schinken</i> (“ham”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) female innkeeper; female tavernkeeper</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shredded, chopped (<i>of vegetables, especially cabbage</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shred (<i>into thin lines or pieces; especially vegetables</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>Schink(en)</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tyre</li><li>(relational,&#32;medicine) splint</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) tavern where spirits are served (in pre-revolutionary Ukraine, Belarus, Poland)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) bar, pub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szynk</i>, from German <i>Schenke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chinchilla</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn, spine</li><li>stud (small object that protrudes from something)</li><li>cleat (protrusion on the sole of a shoe)</li><li>(<i>construction</i>) tenon, pin</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) journal (part of a shaft or axle that rests on bearings)</li><li>thorn sturgeon, bastard sturgeon (<i>Acipenser nudiventris</i>)</li><li>hissing</li><li>(fandom slang,&#32;neologism) ship</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šipъ</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from English <i>ship</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hissing; sizzling; fizzling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiss</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog rose, dogrose (<i>Rosa canina</i>)</li><li>rosehip</li></ol><p><i>шип</i> (šip) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Austrian briar, <i>Rosa foetida</i>, a species of rose.</li></ol><p><i>шипо́вник</i> (šipóvnik, “dogrose”) +&lrm; <i>злово́нный</i> (zlovónnyj, “fetid, foul-smelling”), a calque of translingual <i>Rosa foetida</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thornbill (<i>Acanthiza</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>From <i>шип</i> (šip, “thorn, spine”) +&lrm; <i>клюв</i> (kljuv, “beak, bill”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka, diminutive nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;fandom slang) to ship</li></ol><p>From English <i>ship</i> (“to create a fictional romantic relationship”) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hissing</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) sibilant, of hushing sounds in Russian orthography (relating to &#47;ʂ&#47;, &#47;ʐ&#47;, &#47;ɕː&#47;, and &#47;ʑː&#47;)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>широ́кий</i> (širókij): wider</li><li>comparative degree of <i>широко́</i> (širokó), comparative degree of <i>широ́ко</i> (širóko)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>width, breadth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;širina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fly (strip that hides the opening of trousers/pants or underpants)</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) towel</li></ol><p><i>ширина́</i> (širiná) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to widen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;širiti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ширь</i> (širʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to widen</li><li>to spread, to expand</li></ol><p><i>ши́рить</i> (šíritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>room divider, divider screen</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) screen, front, facade, smokescreen (something used to conceal true actions, motives, or nature)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schirm</i>. Doublet of <i>экра́н</i> (ekrán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of clothing and shoes</i>) too loose, too wide, loose-fitting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the general public</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide, broad</li><li>broad, extensive</li><li>generous</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;širokъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wide, widely, broadly</li></ol><p>From <i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o) (stem-stressed variant) or from <i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о́</i> (-ó) (ending-stressed variant).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>widish</li></ol><p><i>широ́к(ий)</i> (širók(ij)) +&lrm; <i>-ова́тый</i> (-ovátyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broadcasting</li><li>loud, loud-mouthed; containing large promises; promising</li></ol><p><i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>веща́ть</i> (veščátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rail transport</i>) having a broad gauge</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) concealer moth, oecophorid</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Oecophoridae</li></ol><p>From <i>ширококры́лый</i> (širokokrýlyj, “broad-winged”) +&lrm; <i>моль</i> (molʹ, “moth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide-ranging, large-scale</li></ol><p><i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>масшта́б</i> (masštáb) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad-shouldered</li></ol><p><i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>плечо́</i> (plečó) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broadband; wide-ranged; wide-stripped</li></ol><p><i>широ́кий</i> (širókij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>полоса́</i> (polosá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broadbrimmed</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadth, extent, amplitude, broad scope</li><li>(geology,&#32;geography) latitude</li></ol><p>From <i>ширь</i> (širʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) latitude, latitudinal</li></ol><p>From <i>широта́</i> (širotá) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) very wide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (mass) consumer goods</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>това́ры <b>широ́кого потребле́ния</b></i> (továry <b>širókovo potreblénija</b>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expanse, scope</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;širь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sew</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;only&#32;imperfective) to be occupied with sewing, to tailor</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to order (e.g., a dress)</li><li>(<i>only&#32;imperfective</i>) to embroider</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to nail planking</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šiti</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;sjū́ˀtei</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;syewH-</i>.</p><p>Cognate to Lithuanian <i>siūti</i>, English <i>sew</i>, Latin <i>suō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sewing, needlework</li></ol><p><i>шить</i> (šitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ьё</i> (-ʹjó).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slate (rock or tile)</li><li>asbestos cement, fibro (<i>typically as roofing material</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schiefer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) asbestos cement (typically as roofing material)</li></ol><p><i>ши́фер</i> (šífer) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chiffon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wardrobe, chiffonier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chiffonnier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cipher, code</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chiffre</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>cifra</i>, from Arabic صِفْر (ṣifr). Doublet of <i>цифра</i> (cifra).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encryptor, cipherer, encoder (person engaged in encryption)</li><li>cryptographer, cryptologist</li><li>ransomware</li></ol><p><i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-льщик</i> (-lʹščik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encryption</li></ol><p><i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encrypt, to encode</li></ol><p><i>шифр</i> (šifr) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to hide (especially from the police); avoid meeting anyone; be secretive; conceal any information about oneself</li></ol><p><i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enciphering, encryption</li><li>cipher <i>(message)</i></li></ol><p><i>шифрова́ть</i> (šifrovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shihan, single hill</li><li>sharp hilltop</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furnace charge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schicht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rude gesture in which the hand makes a fist and the thumb is stuck between the index and middle fingers, which indicates "nothing for you, nuts to you"</li><li>(mythology,&#32;somewhat&#32;dated) devil, demon</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) A highwayman</li></ol><p>17th century; perhaps from French <i>chiche</i> (“stingy, niggardly; also "I dare you!"”).</p><p>Etymology uncertain. Sometimes theorized to be of Turkic origin; compare Turkish <i>şişmek</i> (“to swell, to fatten”). This is dismissed by Vasmer as a mere coincidence.</p><p>Etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>folklore</i>) a mythological creature, usually hostile to humans</li></ol><p>According to Vasmer, is related to <i>шиш</i> (šiš, “a devil, a demon”), with uncertain etymology.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cone, strobilus (fruit of conifers)</li><li>bump, bunion</li><li>(slang,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine,&#32;animate) boss</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) medlar tree (<i>Mespilus</i> gen. et spp.; also plurale tantum ши́шки (šíški) and ши́шковое дерево (šíškovoje derevo), and more often чи́шка (číška), чи́шки (číški), чи́шковое дерево (číškovoje derevo))</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шишька</i> (šišĭka), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šišьka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pineal gland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scale (a graph or instrument depicting a sequence of numbers used to measure quantity or quality)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Skala</i>. Further from Latin <i>scāla</i>, from Latin <i>scandō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beaufort scale</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hobble, to totter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>now&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) quarterdeck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>schans</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) cupboard, wardrobe, cabinet, bookcase</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) diminutive of <i>шкап</i> (škap): (small) cupboard, wardrobe, cabinet, bookcase; locker</li></ol><p><i>шкап</i> (škap) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>casket, (small) box, case, jewel-box</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szkatułka</i> (diminutive of <i>szkatuła</i>), from Italian <i>scatola</i>, from Latin <i>castula</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шкату́лка</i> (škatúlka)</li></ol><p><i>шкату́лка</i> (škatúlka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) box</li></ol><p><i>шкату́лка</i> (škatúlka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupboard</li><li>wardrobe, closet</li><li>locker, cabinet</li><li>bookcase, shelves</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skapą</i>. The form <i>шкап</i> (škap) is from Middle Dutch and/or Middle Low German <i>schap</i> (both “cupboard, cabinet, coffer”). The form <i>шкаф</i> (škaf) was likely influenced by the Middle High German cognate <i>schaf</i> (whence Polish <i>szafa</i>). The initial <i>шк-</i> seems to speak against direct borrowing from High German, where <i>sch-</i> was pronounced /ʃ/ early on; on the other hand compare Serbo-Croatian <i>шкаф</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шкаф</i> (škaf): (small) cupboard, wardrobe, cabinet, bookcase; locker</li></ol><p><i>шкаф</i> (škaf) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squall (a sudden gust of wind, often accompanied by precipitation)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) flurry, barrage</li><li>Shkval (Soviet and Russian supercavitating torpedo)</li></ol><p>First attested in 1765. Borrowed from English <i>squall</i>, from Scandinavian language (compare Swedish <i>skval</i>, <i>skvala</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coupling pin</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gut fish</li><li>to remove scales from fish</li><li>to hide something</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;slang</i>) lad, kid, youth, teenager (boy)</li></ol><p>Originally from Saint Petersburg criminal argot. According to Vasmer, perhaps related to Italian <i>schietto</i> (“clear, frank”). According to Trubachyov, borrowed from Czech <i>škеtа</i> (“fool”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulley</li><li>sheave (wheel having a groove)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>schijf</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) skipper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>schipper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) collar; scruff of the neck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>шкі́рка</i> (škírka, “diminutive of <i>шкі́ра</i> (škíra)”). Cognate with Russian <i>шку́ра</i> (škúra, “skin”) (from Polish).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;colloquial,&#32;regional,&#32;Ukraine&#32;or&#32;Belarus,&#32;inanimate) damage, harm; loss</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;inanimate) mischief, annoyance, monkey business</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) mischief-maker, rascal, trickster, scamp, imp</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шкода</i> (škoda, “harm, damage”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li><li>schoolhouse</li><li>academy</li><li>university</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>школа</i> (škola), from Old Polish <i>szkoła</i> (“school”), ultimately from Latin <i>schola</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory,&#32;collective) schoolchildren</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) an annoying online user who behaves in a very immature manner; a noob</li></ol><p><i>шко́ла</i> (škóla, “school”) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school uniform</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schoolboy</li></ol><p><i>шко́ла</i> (škóla) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) school</li></ol><p><i>шко́ла</i> (škóla) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school bus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school student</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szkolarz</i>. By surface analysis, <i>шко́ла</i> (škóla) +&lrm; <i>-я́р</i> (-jár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) guy, guy line, sheet rope (support cable)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>schoot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шкот</i> (škot)</li></ol><p><i>шкот</i> (škot) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) border, edge (of a sail, sewed around with a rope)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) hide, coat, pelt, skin (<i>especially when used as a material</i>)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) bastard, scum</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) whore</li></ol><p>Likely a borrowing from Polish <i>skóra</i>, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skora</i> (“bast, skin”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), whence English <i>carnage</i>, <i>cortex</i>, <i>carrion</i>, <i>corium</i>, <i>scurf</i> and <i>excoriate</i>. Doublet of <i>скора́</i> (skorá), the inherited East Slavic form. Compare typologically with cognate Latin <i>scortum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шку́ра</i> (škúra): skin, fell; rind, peel</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sandpaper</li></ol><p><i>шку́ра</i> (škúra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boom barrier, boom gate, crossing barrier, rising barrier (structure that bars passage)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlagbaum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slag, dross, cinder, scoria</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) bad stuff accumulated in the body</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something unnecessary or useless</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlacke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sludge (waste from industrial processes, or sediment in a steam boiler)</li><li>slime, mud, ooze</li><li>slurry</li><li>sediment, silt, residue, bottoms</li><li>tarry residue (in engines)</li><li>drilling fluid, drilling mud</li><li>(<i>ceramics</i>) slip</li><li>powdered product, formed as a precipitate in the electrolysis of certain metals and usually containing noble metals</li><li>precipitation in the form of fine particles that are released during sedimentation or filtration of a liquid</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlamm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hose</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlange</i> (“snake”).</p><p>Compare typologically Czech <i>hadice</i>, <i>hadička</i>, Slovene <i>hadica</i>, Polish <i>wąż</i>. Also compare typologically German <i>Schlauch</i> (whence Polish <i>szlauch</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>шла̏ух</i>), akin to English <i>slough</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hose davit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) banyan (<i>dressing gown</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from German <i>Schlafrock</i> (“nightgown”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>train, tail (of a dress)</li><li>(trailing) plume (of smoke, gas, pollution)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schleife</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li><li>headpiece (as in a horse's)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>шлѣмъ</i> (šlěmŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šelmъ</i>. Doublet of <i>шело́м</i> (šelóm), the inherited East Slavic form.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) headset, helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>шлем</i> (šlem) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-фо́н</i> (-fón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slap, smack (a blow)</li><li>alternative spelling of <i>шлёпок</i> (šljópok)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) breeching</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(geology,&#32;engineering) thin layer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schliff</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grinding, polishing, filing</li></ol><p><i>шлифова́ть</i> (šlifovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind, to polish, to file</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szlifować</i>, from Middle High German <i>slīfen</i>. Compare German <i>schleifen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) grinding, polishing</li><li>polished surface</li><li>tools for grinding/polishing</li></ol><p><i>шлифова́ть</i> (šlifovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abrasive</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a concentrate of heavy minerals that remain after washing in water natural unconsolidated sediments or rocks crushed specifically for this purpose</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) kerf, spline key, nick, slit, slot, spline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlitz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock, sluice (<i>of a canal or dam</i>)</li><li>airlock</li><li>(<i>mining</i>) sluice</li><li>(telecommunications,&#32;Internet) gateway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>sluis</i> or German <i>Schleuse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sloop (single-masted sailboat)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) sloop-of-war (warship with a single gun deck that carried up to eighteen guns)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) <i>Shoreham</i>-class sloop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>sloep</i>, from Middle French <i>chaloupe</i>, <i>chaloppe</i>, from Old French <i>eschalope, escalope</i> (via PIE related to Old Church Slavonic <i>сколика</i> (skolika, “shell”), English <i>shell</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ship's</i>) boat, yawl, lifeboat</li></ol><p>From <i>шлю́п</i> (šljúp, “sloop”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>highly vulgar</i>) slut, whore, trollop, strumpet, streetwalker</li></ol><p>From <i>шля́ться</i> (šljátʹsja, “to gad about, hang around, gallivant, hang about”).</p><p>For semantic parallels, compare:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a promiscuous or dissolute man; manwhore</li></ol><p><i>шлю́ха</i> (šljúxa) +&lrm; <i>-а́н</i> (-án)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) somewhat whorish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;vulgar) slut, whore</li></ol><p><i>шлю́ха</i> (šljúxa) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hit (success, especially in the entertainment industry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schlager</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brimmed hat</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial,&#32;derogatory) milksop, scatterbrain (an ineffectual person)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;colloquial) a predicament, a real fix (an unpleasant and difficult situation)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шляпа</i> (šljapa), from Bavarian <i>Schlappe</i>.</p><p>The colloquial derogatory sense derives from military slang.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шля́па</i> (šljápa): (small) hat; head (of a nail), cap (of a mushroom)</li></ol><p><i>шля́па</i> (šljápa) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to loaf about, to gad about</li></ol><p>Dialectally also <i>сля́ться</i> (sljátʹsja). Per Vasmer, quoting Sobolevsky, probably from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъljati sę</i>, from <i>&#42;sъlati</i> (“to send”) (whence Russian <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) path, steppe road</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ukrainian <i>шлях</i> (šljax) or Belarusian <i>шлях</i> (šljax), cf. Polish <i>szlach</i> or <i>szlak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) szlachta (nobility in Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шлѧхта</i> (šlęxta), from Polish <i>szlachta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Polish or Lithuanian nobleman.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>splat, flop, smack, plop, splash</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a single) slipper</li></ol><p><i>(от)шлёпанный</i> ((ot)šljópannyj) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>footwear</i>) slides; thongs, flip-flops</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slap</li><li>to spank</li></ol><p>Likely of imitative origin, similar to English <i>slap</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tumble, to fall with a plop, to plop down</li></ol><p><i>шлёпать</i> (šljópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a slap, a smack, a spank</li><li>(<i>vernacular</i>) (a single) slipper</li></ol><p><i>шлёпать</i> (šljópatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slap</li><li>to spank</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to tumble, to fall with a plop, to plop down</li></ol><p><i>шлёпнуть</i> (šljópnutʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a person of little importance</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) narcotic cigarette, marijuana pot, joint</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>шмали́ть</i> (šmalítʹ, “to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) whore</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) piece, bit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>zoology</i>) bumblebee</li><li>Shmel (Soviet and Russian flamethrower)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьmeľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(criminal slang,&#32;prison slang) frisk, shakedown, raid, body search, search (a sudden, unexpected checkup or raid, as a police check); frisker (person doing the frisking)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;slang) a sharp, unpleasant smell</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>שְׁמוֹנֶה</i> (shmone, “eight”), referring to 8:00 a.m. when the cells were searched each morning in the Odessa prison prior to the communist revolution.</p><p>Gračov does not give an etymology. Krysin claims a Yiddish origin, without quoting the Yiddish form. Elistratov compares with dialectal <i>шмон</i> (šmon, “idler, loafer”), <i>шмо́ны</i> (šmóny, “idleness”), on the possible German–Yiddish origin of which see Vasmer.</p><p>Has also been connected with slang <i>ашмала́ш</i> (ašmaláš, “feeling up during a search”), which is of Turkic origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clothes, threads, belongings</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Yiddish.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clothes, threads</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rotgut</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart, to dash, to run quickly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart about</li><li>to sniff noisily</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart, to dash, to run quickly</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to dart about</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>шмы́гать</i> (šmýgatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schnapps</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnaps</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schnauzer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnauzer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Germany</i>) freeway, expressway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnellstraße</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>firearms</i>) set trigger, hair-trigger</li><li>the lock which fixes the belt that attaches the stirrup to the saddle (which belt is called <i>пу́тля</i> (pútlja))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schneller</i>, also a part of guns, from <i>schnellen</i> (“to move into a certain direction while accelerating”) +&lrm; <i>-er</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Germany</i>) freeway, expressway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnellstraße</i> + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chive (<i>Allium schoenoprasum</i>)</li><li>chive (herb)</li></ol><p>Partial calque of German <i>Schnittlauch</i>, from Middle High German <i>snitlouch</i>, from Old High German <i>snitalouh</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schnitzel (a meat dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnitzel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) schnozzle, conk, honker, schnoz, snoot (human nose)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>שנאָבל</i> (shnobl, “beak, nose”), compare German <i>Schnabel</i> (“beak”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord (<i>length of twisted strands</i>)</li><li>(electric) line</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schnur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lace up</li></ol><p><i>шнур</i> (šnur) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin cord</li><li>lace (cord for fastening a shoe or garment)</li></ol><p><i>шнур</i> (šnur) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) thin cord, shoelace</li></ol><p><i>шнуро́к</i> (šnurók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>шныря́ть</i> (šnyrjátʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) a scurrier, a snooper</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) a prisoner with a hustle of performing menial tasks for high-ranking prisoners; a gofer, a dogsbody</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) a prison orderly</li><li>(<i>prison slang</i>) a guard</li></ol><p>Deverbal from <i>шныря́ть</i> (šnyrjátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to snoop around</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scurry, to move quickly</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;idiomatic)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>шны́рить</i> (šnýritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;nonstandard,&#32;proscribed,&#32;Ukraine,&#32;Southern Russia) what, alternative form of <i>что</i> (što)</li></ol><p>Distorted form of <i>что</i> (što), possibly influenced by Ukrainian <i>що</i> (ščo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) company, crowd, group, band, gang</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seam</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) stitch, suture</li><li>joint, junction</li><li>weld, welded joint</li><li>(<i>sewing</i>) stitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьvъ</i>. Compare <i>шить</i> (šitʹ). See Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šiti</i> for more information.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chauvinism, jingoism (excessive patriotism)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chauvinisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chauvinist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chauvinist</li></ol><p><i>шовини́ст</i> (šoviníst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>шо́в</i> (šóv).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shock</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shock</i> or French <i>choc</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shocker (a device for giving electric shocks)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shocker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to horrify, to scandalize, to shock</li></ol><p>From <i>шок</i> (šok) +&lrm; <i>-ировать</i> (-irovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) shock</li></ol><p><i>шок</i> (šok) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chocolate</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) luxury, success, fortune</li></ol><p>Originally <i>чокола́д</i> (čokolád) and <i>чикола́д</i> (čikolád), borrowed from Italian <i>cioccolata</i>. The form <i>шокола́д</i> (šokolád) was borrowed from German <i>Schokolade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of chocolate; a single bar of chocolate</li></ol><p><i>шокола́д</i> (šokolád) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chocolatier</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) cocoa tree</li></ol><p><i>шокола́д</i> (šokolád) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>device/set for making chocolate</li><li>type of utensils for chocolate</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;humorous) chocolate-lover</li><li>(<i>entomology</i>) ellipsis of <i>ба́бочка-шокола́дница</i> (bábočka-šokoládnica); small tortoiseshell (butterfly)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) ellipsis of <i>ви́шня Шокола́дница</i> (víšnja Šokoládnica), a cultivar of cherry</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) ellipsis of <i>хурма́ Шокола́дница</i> (xurmá Šokoládnica), a cultivar of persimmon</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) bottom, rear, behind, butt, buttocks</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) homosexual</li><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) female thief</li></ol><p><i>шокола́д</i> (šokolád) +&lrm; <i>-ница</i> (-nica)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) chocolate</li></ol><p><i>шокола́д</i> (šokolád) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gunstick, ramrod</li><li>metal skewer (<i>for barbecue</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sztępel</i>, from German <i>Stempel</i>. Doublet of <i>ште́мпель</i> (štémpelʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shona (ethnic group)</li><li>Shona (language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(neologism,&#32;animate) shopper (a personal assistant in shopping)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) tote bag</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shopping</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shopping</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1956 spelling of <i>шёпотом</i> (šópotom).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>шо́пинг</i> (šóping)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Someone who makes horse harnesses</li></ol><p><i>шо́р(а)</i> (šór(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) horse harnesses with breeching (without shaft bow and horse collar) and some other types of leather products (belts, suitcase etc.)</li></ol><p><i>шо́р(а)</i> (šór(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) saddlery (a place of business of a saddler)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustle (soft, crackling sound)</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) rough surface</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) thin broken ice; grease ice, pancake ice</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p><p>May be the same as 1. Cognate to Ukrainian <i>ше́рех</i> (šérex, “grease ice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Shor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) short (<i>financial investment</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>short</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shorts (pants worn primarily in the summer that do not go lower than the knees)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shorts</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse harness with breeching (without shaft bow and horse collar)</li><li>blinker (eye shield used for horses)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szory</i>, from Middle High German <i>geschirre</i> (“harness, gear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highway</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chaussée</i>, derived from Latin <i>calciāta</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racing bicycle</li><li>racing cyclist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) highway, road</li></ol><p><i>шоссе́</i> (šossɛ́) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) a shot (alcoholic)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scot, Scotsman</li></ol><p><i>Шотла́ндия</i> (Šotlándija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaid, tartan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collie</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scottish, Scotch</li></ol><p><i>Шотла́ндия</i> (Šotlándija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>show (entertainment)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>show</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>show business (the entertainment industry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>show business</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>showman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>showman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>neologism</i>) showroom</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>showroom</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>business&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) chauffeur, driver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chauffeur</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driver (<i>of an automobile, truck, bus, etc.; usually a paid professional</i>)</li><li>chauffeur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>chauffeur</i> (literally “stoker, fireman”). Several steps omitting from Latin <i>calefaciō</i> + <i>-ātor</i>/<i>-tor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) rapier, smallsword, court sword, officer's sword (any type of 16th–19th century European sword with a straight, narrow blade designed for thrusting)</li><li>(<i>fencing</i>) epee</li><li>(<i>aviation&#32;slang</i>) a variety of potable evaporative coolants used in a multitude of Soviet jet aircraft designs, consisting of distilled water and ethanol</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szpada</i>, from Italian <i>spada</i> (“sword”), from Latin <i>spatha</i> (“sword”), from Ancient Greek <i>σπάθη</i> (spáthē, “broad blade”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, packthread, string, (binder) twine</li><li>(<i>gymnastics</i>) split (the acrobatic feat of spreading the legs flat on the floor 180 degrees apart, either sideways to the body or with one leg in front and one behind, thus lowering the torso completely to the floor in an upright position)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szpagat</i>, from German <i>Spagat</i>, from Italian <i>spaghetto</i> (“thin twine”). Meaning of split apparently influenced by Italian <i>spaccata</i> (“splits”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword swallower</li></ol><p><i>шпа́га</i> (špága, “small sword”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>глота́ть</i> (glotátʹ, “to swallow”) +&lrm; <i>-тель</i> (-telʹ, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шпа́га</i> (špága)</li><li>a wooden or plastic stick with sharp ends used for eating desserts, often made in the shape of an epee</li></ol><p>From <i>шпа́га</i> (špága) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) gladiolus (<i>Gladiolus</i> sp.)</li></ol><p><i>шпа́га</i> (špága, “type of sword”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik, diminutive), calque of translingual <i>Gladiolus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tie, sleeper (railroad tie)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(contemptuous,&#32;colloquial,&#32;jokingly,&#32;collectively) little hooligans, little rascals, punks, young petty criminals or trouble-makers</li></ol><p>Originally prison slang, of unknown derivation.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;education) to cheat during the exam with a cheat sheet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheat sheet, crib (<i>notes concealed by a student to aid them in a test or examination</i>)</li></ol><p>From Polish <i>szpargał</i> +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Per Vasmer, probably from Latin <i>sparganum</i> (“diaper”), from Ancient Greek <i>σπάργανον</i> (spárganon, “diaper”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to scald (one's hands, etc.) (to burn with boiling liquid or steam)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to pour boiling water over (<i>often specifically in order to kill something, e.g. an insect</i>)</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;intransitive,&#32;imperfective only) to do something with hurriedly or with great force, to energy or zeal</li><li>(low colloquial,&#32;figurative,&#32;intransitive,&#32;imperfective only) to be fluent, to speak a language fluently</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>шпа́рити</i> (špáryty, “to scald, to beat”) and Belarusian <i>шпа́рыць</i> (špárycʹ, “to beat”). Possibly derived from Polish <i>sparzyć</i> (“to scald”) (perfective), which is related to <i>пар</i> (par, “steam”) (mentioned by Vasmer, preferred by Shansky). Per Chernykh the relation with <i>пар</i> (par) is secondary. Possible relation with <i>спо́рый</i> (spóryj) is mentioned by Vasmer. In any case compare synonymous <i>шпа́ркий</i> (špárkij), Polish <i>szparki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(dated,&#32;colloquial) to apply clay in the cavities of cracked objects</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szparować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) spar</li><li>(<i>equestrianism</i>) spavin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putty knife</li><li>spatula (a thin hand tool for handling chemicals or other materials)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spatel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putty knife</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small pin, rod, rivet or stud</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatback</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scupper</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>spiegat</i>, <i>spijgat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) spy</li><li>bacon; lard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szpieg</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>spëc</i>, via Polish <i>szpik</i> (“fat (dialectal)”), compare German <i>Speck</i>, Dutch <i>spek</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spire, steeple, spike</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spill</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hairpin</li><li>tack</li><li>spike heel, stiletto heel</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) stud, brad</li></ol><p><i>шпиль</i> (špilʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinach</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spinat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) bolt, catch, latch, fastener</li><li>(animate,&#32;colloquial&#32;or&#32;slang) kid, youth, lad, teenager</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spaniolett</i>, from French <i>espagnolette</i> (“Spanish latch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle (of a machine tool)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spindel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spion</i>. Several steps omitting from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spehōną</i>, further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speḱ-</i>.</p><p>Distant cognate vie PIE with Latin <i>speculātor</i>, Ancient Greek <i>κατάσκοπος</i> (katáskopos).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>согляда́тай</i> (sogljadátaj) (akin to <i>гляде́ть</i> (gljadétʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>espionage, spying (act of learning secret information through clandestine means)</li></ol><p>On the surface, <i>шпио́н</i> (špión) +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž)</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spionage</i>, itself from French <i>espionnage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female spy</li></ol><p><i>шпио́н</i> (špión) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spy mania, spy fever, spy hysteria, spy scare (paranoia about spies, fearmongering about the threat of foreign spies)</li></ol><p><i>шпио́н</i> (špión, “spy”) + <i>-о-</i> + <i>ма́ния</i> (mánija, “mania”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) espionage, spy</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) spyware</li></ol><p><i>шпио́н</i> (špión) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) spyware (harmful software that surreptitiously monitors and reports activity)</li></ol><p><i>шпио́нское</i> (špiónskoje, “spying”) + <i>ПО</i> (PO, “software”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) spire, steeple</li><li>spitz (dog breed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spitz</i>, a variant of <i>Spitze</i> (“tip, peak”).</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spitz</i> (“spitz”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poligraphy</i>) lead</li><li><i>(thin)</i> veneer sheet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spur</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) cheat sheet, crib (from шпаргалка)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>spor</i>; compare German <i>Sporn</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>syringe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Spritze</i>, from <i>spritzen</i> (“spray, inject”). Doublet of <i>спринцовка</i> (sprincovka).</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>stříkačka</i>, Polish <i>strzykawka</i>, Serbo-Croatian <i>бризгалица</i>, Slovene <i>brizgalka</i>, <i>brizga</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spool, bobbin</li></ol><p>From <i>шпу́ля</i> (špúlja) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), from Middle High German <i>spuole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spool, bobbin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>spuole</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scar, cicatrix (a permanent mark on the skin sometimes caused by the healing of a wound)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szram</i>, from Middle High German <i>schramme</i>, <i>schramm</i> (whence also German <i>Schramme</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) shrapnel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shrapnel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shredder (machine that tears up objects into smaller pieces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shredder</i>.</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Шредер)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Шрёдер</i> (Šrjóder)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sri Lankan</li></ol><p>From <i>Шри-Ла́нка</i> (Šri-Lánka) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij) or from <i>Шри-Ланка́</i> (Šri-Lanká) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>economics</i>) shrinkflation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shrinkflation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>typography</i>) type, print, script, font</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Schrift</i> (“writing; font”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state</li><li>staff</li><li>item</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>штат</i> (štat).</p><p>Abbreviation of <i>шту́ка</i> (štúka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>NY, New York State (a state of the United States)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) headquarters, staff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stab</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headquarters</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stack, pile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stapel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;military) staff</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) staff officer</li></ol><p><i>штаб</i> (štab) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;historical) staff-captain (an officer's rank in the infantry, artillery and engineering troops, below lieutenant)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stabskapitän</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff-captain's wife or widow</li></ol><p>From <i>штабс-капита́н</i> (štabs-kapitán) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mainstay on a ship</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stag</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slat, plank</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) joint, spliff</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Staket</i>, the Russian being attested in the early nineteenth century and the German being borrowed in the fifteenth century from Middle French <i>estakette, estachette</i>, derived from Old French <i>estache</i> (“pole, stake”), a Germanic borrowing equalling German <i>Staken</i>, English <i>stake</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stakô</i> (“pole, stick, stake”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teg-</i> (“pole, stick, stake”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picket fence</li></ol><p><i>штаке́т</i> (štakét, “slat, plank”) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) strain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stamm</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stamp, rubber stamp, impress</li><li>die (tool for cutting or shaping material using a press)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stock phrase</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stampfe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stamped (having a stamp, e.g. of a document)</li><li>stamped (made by stamping); embossed</li><li>hackneyed, cliched, trite (<i>of a phrase or word</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metalworking</i>) stamping, punching</li><li>metal part made by stamping</li></ol><p>From <i>штамповать</i> (štampovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, rod, beam, pole (straight round stick, shaft, or bar)</li><li>weight, barbell</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) post</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stange</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slide) calipers, slide gauge (device used to measure thickness between two surfaces)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stangenzirkel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weightlifter</li></ol><p>From <i>шта́нга</i> (štánga) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) standard, ensign, flag</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Standarte</i>. Doublet of <i>стандарт</i> (standart).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouser leg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>штаны́</i> (štaný): short pants or children's pants</li></ol><p><i>штаны́</i> (štaný) +&lrm; <i>-ишки</i> (-iški).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) trousers, pants</li></ol><p>Derived from a Turkic language. Etymologically analyzable as iç ('inside') + ton ('garment').</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state (a political division, e.g. a US state)</li><li>staff (personnel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Staat</i> (“state, country”), from Middle Low German <i>stāt</i>, from Middle Dutch <i>staet</i>, from Latin <i>stātus</i>. The semantic development was influenced by French <i>état</i> from the same Latin source.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand, support, tripod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stativ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>staff, personnel</li><li>staff</li><li>standard, regular, normal</li><li>routine, nonemergency</li></ol><p><i>штат</i> (štat) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civil, civilian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civilian</li></ol><p><i>штат</i> (štat) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civilian clothing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) lining (in boots, cuffs, skirts)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) civilian, civvy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackplug, plug, pin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stecker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>printing</i>) stamp, seal</li><li>impression made with such a stamp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stempel</i>. Doublet of <i>шо́мпол</i> (šómpol).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plug (device for connecting a machine to main power)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stöpsel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sternbergia (<i>Sternbergia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Sternbergia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory&#32;or&#32;facetious</i>) men's shoes</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) leggings fastened with buttons</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stiefelette</i> (“small boot”), from Italian <i>stivaletto</i> (“half-boot”), from Medieval Latin <i>aestivale</i> (“soft summer shoe”), from Latin <i>aestīvus</i> (“of summer”). Doublet of <i>эстива́ция</i> (estivácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) dead calm</li><li>obsolete form of <i>стиль</i> (stilʹ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stille</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stil</i> (“style”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) A small round pin, a dowel installed to keep two pieces in position.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stift</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burin, graver</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stichel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>что</i> (što)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>что</i> (što)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>engineering</i>) rod</li><li>stock (<i>of an anchor</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stock</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dried cod</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stockfisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallery, adit (a horizontal or nearly horizontal passage from the surface into a mine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sztolnia</i>, also <i>stolnia</i>, from German <i>Stollen</i> (“gallery, adit”), from Middle High German <i>stolle</i> (“gallery, adit”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darn (stitch with thread)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stoppen</i> (“to darn”) or from German <i>stopfen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corkscrew (implement for opening bottles sealed by a cork)</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) spin (trajectory)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stopper</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain, shade (for windows)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Store</i>. Further from French <i>store</i> (whence also Bulgarian <i>щора</i> (štora)), from Latin <i>storea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>што́ра</i> (štóra)</li></ol><p><i>што́ра</i> (štóra) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;nautical</i>) storm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>storm</i> or Middle Low German <i>storm</i>. Doublet of <i>штурм</i> (šturm), borrowed from German.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) storm</li><li>stormy</li></ol><p><i>шторм</i> (štorm) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) stoff, shtof (an old unit of liquid measure equal to 1.2299 litres, most often used for alcoholic beverages&#59; comparable to an English quart)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) a one-shtof bottle or flask (<i>for vodka or other distilled liquor</i>)</li><li>damask <i>(fabric)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stoff</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine, penalty, citation</li><li>shtrafbat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strafe</i> (“punishment, penalty, fine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to impose a fine</li></ol><p><i>штрафова́ть</i> (štrafovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ану́ть</i> (-anútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) penalty</li><li>penal</li><li>consisting of penalised soldiers, sportsmen, etc.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(animate,&#32;military) soldier sent to a penal military battalion</li><li>(animate,&#32;sports) a player who has been penalized</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;sports) penalty kick, free throw, etc.</li></ol><p><i>штраф</i> (štraf) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>soccer</i>) direct free kick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fine, to penalize</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>strafen</i> (“to punish”). Composed of <i>штраф</i> (štraf) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strikebreaker</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Streikbrecher</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) drift</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strecke</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stroke, hatch, hachures, dash</li><li>trait, feature, detail</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) A man with violent tendencies</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strich</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barcode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strudel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Strudel</i> (“whirlpool, strudel”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scientific research, scientific work, scientific study</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, each (in counting items)</li><li>thing</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thousand</li><li>Stuka (German Junkers Ju 87 dive bomber)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>sztuka</i> (“piece”), in turn from German <i>Stück</i>. Cognate to English <i>shtick</i>.</p><p>From German <i>Stuka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plastering</li><li>plaster, stucco</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;ironic) heavy make-up, slap</li></ol><p>From <i>штукату́р(ить)</i> (štukatúr(itʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka), ultimately from Italian <i>stuccatura</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) thingamajig, thingumajig, thingummy (something that one does not know the name of)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) widget, gadget, gizmo</li></ol><p><i>шту́ка</i> (štúka, “thing”) +&lrm; <i>-о́вина</i> (-óvina)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helm, steering wheel (of a ship or an airplane)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stuurwiel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military&#32;or&#32;figurative</i>) storm, storming of something, assault (fast, violent, often frontal attack)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Sturm</i> (“fast attack, assault”, literally “storm”). Doublet of <i>шторм</i> (štorm), borrowed from Dutch or Middle Low German.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>navigator (navigation officer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>stuurman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to storm, to assault, to conquer, to attack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>stürmen</i> (“to attack, to storm”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assault rifle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) stormtrooper, assault trooper</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) attack aircraft, ground-attack aircraft</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) a pilot of such an aircraft</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) climber, mountaineer (<i>participating in an ascent</i>)</li></ol><p><i>штурмова́ть</i> (šturmovátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-и́к</i> (-ík).</p><p>Calque of German <i>Stürmer</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) assault</li></ol><p><i>штурм</i> (šturm) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Stuttgart</li></ol><p><i>Шту́тгарт</i> (Štútgart) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mining</i>) a large specimen of ore</li><li>a lump of high-grade ore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stufe</i> (“an aggregation of standalone and well-crystallized mineral”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connecting pipe, coupling</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) muzzle-loading rifle, typically with the shortened length of a carbine</li><li>double rifle (double-barrelled hunting rifle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Stutzer</i>, from <i>stutzen</i> (“to cut short”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шту́ка</i> (štúka): (small) thing</li><li>doodah, doodad; widget</li><li>(idiomatic&#32;colloquial,&#32;possibly&#32;derogatory) calling card</li></ol><p><i>шту́ка</i> (štúka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vernacular,&#32;humorous,&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;derogatory) (little) things, bits and bobs, odds and ends (miscellaneous things)</li><li>(vernacular,&#32;humorous,&#32;ironic&#32;or&#32;derogatory) mumbo-jumbo, (little) tricks (odd and hard-to-understand things)</li></ol><p>Rhyming reduplication of <i>шту́чки</i> (štúčki, “(little) things”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>representing a single piece, not sold by weight (<i>e.g. a garment</i>)</li><li>manufactured individually, one-of-a-kind, not mass-produced</li></ol><p><i>шту́ка</i> (štúka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bayonet</li><li>(military,&#32;dated) foot soldier, infantryman (as a counting unit of military strength; see also <i>са́бля</i> (sáblja) and <i>ша́шка</i> (šáška))</li><li>blade (<i>of a spade</i>)</li><li>(agriculture,&#32;archaeology) a spade's depth (<i>of soil</i>)</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a type of knot</li></ol><p>Attested since ca. 1706. Borrowed from Polish <i>sztych</i> directly and/or through Old Ruthenian <i>штыхъ</i> (štyx, “sharp point”), even though the difference in Auslaut seems unexplained.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>bodák</i> (akin to <i>бода́ть</i> (bodátʹ), note <i>бо́день</i> (bódenʹ), <i>боде́ц</i> (bodéc), listed by Dahl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) to have an deep psychic effect as a drug or like a drug, to make gassed</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to look, to peer</li></ol><p><i>штырь</i> (štyrʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin, pivot, spike</li></ol><p>A nautical term ultimately from Old Norse <i>stýri</i> and from Middle Low German <i>stǖr</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stiuriją</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fur coat (for men or women)</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) bad trip, freakout</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle High German <i>schūbe</i>, from Italian <i>giubba</i>, from Arabic جُبَّة (jubba, “long garment”). Doublet of <i>жупа́н</i> (župán), <i>зипу́н</i> (zipún), <i>ю́бка</i> (júbka), and <i>дже́мпер</i> (džémper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шу́ба</i> (šúba): (small) fur coat</li></ol><p><i>шу́ба</i> (šúba) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slush (spongy white ice lumps a few centimeters across)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth slang</i>) a sugar daddy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sugar daddy</i>. By surface analysis, English <i>sugar</i> (“money”, US slang) +&lrm; <i>дэ́дди</i> (dɛ́ddi), from English <i>daddy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>шу́га дэ́дди</i> (šúga dɛ́ddi, “sugar daddy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmetics</i>) sugaring (a procedure similar to waxing, in which hair is removed from the body by the application and removal of a sugar-based paste)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>sugaring</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sugaring</li></ol><p>From <i>шу́га́ринг</i> (šúgáring) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rook, cardsharper, sharp, blackleg</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szuler</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, radio static</li><li>sound, murmur</li><li>uproar, din, blare, clamour, hubbub</li><li>whirr</li><li>sensation, stir, bustle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šumъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) speedster, leadfoot (a person who drives very fast or recklessly)</li></ol><p>Named after Michael Schumacher&nbsp;(1969–), a former racing driver. He is a seven-time Formula One World Champion and is widely regarded as one of the greatest Formula One drivers of all time.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sumerian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a noise; to be noisy; to rustle (e.g. of leaves)</li><li>to kick up a row, to make a fuss</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šuměti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clamour/clamor, brouhaha, fuss, uproar, racket, ballyhoo, stir</li></ol><p><i>шум</i> (šum) +&lrm; <i>-и́ха</i> (-íxa)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>noisily (in a noisy manner)</li></ol><p><i>шу́мный</i> (šúmnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud, noisy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šumьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>шум</i> (šum) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skimmer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>szumówka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) noise</li></ol><p><i>шум</i> (šum, “noise”) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шум</i> (šum): slight noise</li></ol><p><i>шум</i> (šum) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>шум</i> (šum).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shunting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother-in-law (wife’s brother)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šurь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood screw (fastener)</li></ol><p>From Early Modern Russian <i>шуруб</i> (šurub) (1731), from Old Ruthenian <i>шрубъ</i> (šrub) (1601), probably via Polish <i>śruba, szruba</i> (1630s) from Middle High German <i>schrūbe</i> (1361), further origin uncertain.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power screwdriver</li></ol><p><i>шуруп</i> (šurup) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-вёрт</i> (-vjórt), combining form of <i>верте́ть</i> (vertétʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustling, rustle, swishing, swish</li></ol><p><i>шурша́ть</i> (šuršátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ние</i> (-ánije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rustle (to move (something) with a soft crackling sound)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) flirt, a thing going on, hanky-panky, slap and tickle</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;by extension) secret negotiations</li><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) hodgepodge, whatnot</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) hidden compartment in a shoe or a heel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>cher amour</i>.</p><p>Possibly has some relationship with Dutch <i>schorremorrie</i> (“scums, thugs”), Low German <i>schurrmurr</i> (“chaos, disorder”) Turkish <i>şur mur</i> (“confusion”), Albanian <i>shiri-miri</i>/<i>ciri-miri</i> (“confusion”), Romani <i>ciri-miri</i> (“confusion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, agile</li><li>bright, clever</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šustrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(comedy,&#32;historical) fool, jester (person in a mediaeval royal court)</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) clown, buffoon</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>шутъ</i> (šutŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šutъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>video games</i>) shooter</li><li>(<i>basketball</i>) sharpshooter (a player who specializes in three-point shots)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>shooter</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to joke, to jest</li><li>to trifle, to play (with)</li><li>to make fun (of)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šutiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joke</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>I’m kidding!</li></ol><p><i>шути́ть</i> (šutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(speech act,&#32;idiomatic) all kidding aside (used to attempt to make a serious point in a jocular conversation)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>jocularly, humorously</li></ol><p><i>шутли́вый</i> (šutlívyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jocularity</li></ol><p>From <i>шутлив(ый)</i> (šutliv(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jesting, humorous, playful, flippant</li></ol><p><i>шути́ть</i> (šutítʹ, “to joke”) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joker, jester</li></ol><p><i>шути́ть</i> (šutítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ни́к</i> (-ník)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fool's</li></ol><p><i>шут</i> (šut) +&lrm; <i>-овско́й</i> (-ovskój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fool's cap (a cap, usually brightly coloured&#47;colored with bells and tassels, as worn by a court jester or fool)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jester's occupation, buffoonery</li></ol><p><i>шут</i> (šut) +&lrm; <i>-ов-</i> (-ov-) +&lrm; <i>-ство́</i> (-stvó)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>шу́тка</i> (šútka): joke, jest</li></ol><p><i>шу́тка</i> (šútka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comic, facetious</li><li>trifling</li></ol><p><i>шу́тка</i> (šútka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in jest, for fun</li><li>easily, lightly</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;chiefly&#32;Belarus) drawer (open-topped box in a cabinet used for storing)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Belarusian <i>шуфля́да</i> (šufljáda), from Polish <i>szuflada</i>, from German <i>Schublade</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;chiefly&#32;Belarus) drawer</li></ol><p><i>шуфля́да</i> (šufljáda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>criminal slang</i>) police raid, (house) search</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) turmoil, confusion, noise</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) mess</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Yiddish <i>זוכער</i> (zukher, “searcher”). Perhaps morphologically also influenced by Ukrainian <i>шукати</i> (šukaty), Polish <i>szukać</i>, both meaning "to search".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riffraff, rubbish, rabble</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to whisper to each other</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic; from <i>шу, шу&#33;</i> (šu, šu&#33;, “shhh!”); <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ); <i>-ся</i> (-sja). Cognate with Ukrainian <i>шушу́кати</i> (šušúkaty), Belarusian <i>шушу́каць</i> (šušúkacʹ), <i>шу́шкаць</i> (šúškacʹ), Bulgarian <i>шушу́кам</i> (šušúkam), Serbo-Croatian <i>шу̏шкати</i> (“to rustle, to make noise”) (also <i>шу́штати</i>), Slovene <i>šȗškati</i> (“to rustle, to make noise”) (<i>tonal orthography</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Shuya</li></ol><p><i>Шу́я</i> (Šúja) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skerry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Swedish <i>skär</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>schooner</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>schoener</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Shaanxi</li></ol><p>From <i>Шэньси́</i> (Šɛnʹsí) +&lrm; <i>-ийский</i> (-ijskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silk (fiber and fabric)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) clothes made of silk</li><li>something soft and pleasant for touching</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>шьлкъ</i> (šĭlkŭ), from a Germanic language. Compare Old Norse <i>silki</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) silk; made of silk</li><li>silky, like silk</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) meek, obedient</li></ol><p><i>шёлк</i> (šolk) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silk mill</li></ol><p><i>шёлк</i> (šolk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>пряди́льня</i> (prjadílʹnja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whisper (the act of speaking in a quiet voice)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šьpъtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a whisper</li></ol><p>From <i>шёпот</i> (šópot) +&lrm; <i>-ом</i> (-om).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>шерсть</i> (šerstʹ, “wool”)</li></ol><p><i>шерсть</i> (šerstʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Щ, щ.</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>сейча́с</i> (sejčás, “now”)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hold on, one sec, wait</li></ol><p>From ща &lt; сча &lt; счас &lt; сейчас, combination of /с/ and /ч/ reads as /щ/ [ɕ].</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) oxalic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) sorrel (Rumex acetosa)</li></ol><p><i>щаве́ль</i> (ščavélʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorrel, dock (plant)</li><li>(Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism) a woman's genitals, especially a large labia</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sьčavь, &#42;sьčavьľь</i>.</p><p>Sense 2 was coined on TikTok since 2024.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spare, to have mercy (on)</li><li>to take care of</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>щадѣти</i> (ščaděti, “to keep, spare”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščęditi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skend-</i>. See also <i>ску́дный</i> (skúdnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) contraction of <i>сейча́с</i> (sejčás)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) hold on, one sec, wait</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crushed stone, road metal</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) detritus</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščebьňь</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skabʰ-</i> (“to scratch”).</p><p>Related to <i>ско́бель</i> (skóbelʹ, “shaving/drawing knife, spokeshave”), <i>скобли́ть</i> (skoblítʹ, “to scrape, to scrub”), <i>щебло́</i> (ščebló, “splinter, chip”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twitter, chirrup</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščьbьtъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twitter, to chirp</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to chatter, to prattle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščьbьtati</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ще́бет</i> (ščébet) +&lrm; <i>-а́ть</i> (-átʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chirping bird</li><li>female twitterer, chatterbox</li></ol><p><i>щебету́н</i> (ščebetún) +&lrm; <i>-ья</i> (-ʹja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) gravel</li></ol><p><i>ще́бень</i> (ščébenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) goldfinch (bird)</li></ol><p><i>щего́л</i> (ščegól) +&lrm; <i>-я́чий</i> (-jáčij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldfinch chick</li></ol><p><i>щего́л</i> (ščegól) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goldfinch (bird)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščegъlъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spiffy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flaunt, to show off [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sport [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something, e.g. clothing’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>щеголя́ть</i> (ščegoljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to dress like a dandy, to wear fancy clothes</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to flaunt, to show off [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to sport [<i>with</i> <b>в</b> (v, + prepositional) <small>‘something, e.g. clothing’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>щёголь</i> (ščógolʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>generously</li></ol><p><i>ще́дрый</i> (ščédryj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>generosity, largess (the trait of being willing to give your money and&#47;or time)</li></ol><p><i>ще́дрый</i> (ščédryj, “generous”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness, -ity”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) generosity, largess (the trait of being willing to donate money, resources, time etc.)</li></ol><p><i>ще́др(ый)</i> (ščédr(yj)) +&lrm; <i>-ота́</i> (-otá)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generous (willing to give and share unsparingly)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>щедръ</i> (štedrŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščedrъ</i>. The expected form would be <i>&#42;щёдрый</i> (&#42;ščódryj), compare Belarusian <i>шчо́дры</i> (ščódry). Cognate to Polish <i>szczodry</i> and Ancient Greek <i>σκεδάννῡμι</i> (skedánnūmi, “I split or scatter”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheek</li><li>mouth of animals, jaw</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) side, sidepiece, stock, jaw (of a mechanism)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščeka</i>. Cognate with Belarusian <i>шчака́</i> (ščaká) and Ukrainian <i>щока́</i> (ščoká).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plump-cheeked</li></ol><p><i>щека́</i> (ščeká) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>latch, catch, pawl (fastening for a door or window)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Steckholz</i> (“bolt”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tickle, to titillate (touch repeatedly or stroke delicately)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to chirp, to twitter (<i>said of nightingales, magpies and certain other birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) to twitter, to chirp (especially of nightingales)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščьkъtati</i>. Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>скъкътати</i> (skŭkŭtati) or <i>скоктати</i> (skoktati) (<i>Russian recension</i>), Serbo-Croatian <i>škakljati</i>, Slovene <i>ščegetati</i>, Macedonian <i>скокотка</i> (skokotka).</p><p><i>щёкот</i> (ščókot) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ), Old East Slavic <i>щекотъ</i> (ščekotŭ). Possibly related to etymology 1 ("to tickle") but it is not proven.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) tickling</li><li>tickle</li></ol><p><i>щекота́ть</i> (ščekotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delicate, touchy (requiring great circumspection and tact)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) ticklish</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) extremely scrupulous</li></ol><p><i>щекота́ть</i> (ščekotátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ли́вый</i> (-lívyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scribbler (one who scribbles; hasty or untalented writer)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure nutcracker</li></ol><p><i>щелку́н</i> (ščelkún) +&lrm; <i>-чик</i> (-čik), from <i>щёлкать</i> (ščólkatʹ, “to crack”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(chemistry,&#32;relational) alkaline</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) lye</li></ol><p><i>щёлочь</i> (ščóločʹ) +&lrm; <i>-но́й</i> (-nój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flick, fillip</li><li>click, snap</li><li>slight</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chink, crack; gap, opening</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fissure; cleft</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščelь</i>. Cognates include Ukrainian <i>щіль</i> (ščilʹ), <i>щі́лина</i> (ščílyna), <i>щіли́на</i> (ščilýna), Belarusian <i>шчэ́ль</i> (ščélʹ), <i>шчэ́лка</i> (ščélka), <i>шчы́ліна</i> (ščýlina), Polish <i>szczelina</i>, Slovene <i>ščálja</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch (to cause pain or discomfort by pressing strongly)</li><li>to smart, to ache, to throb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sčemiti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, aching (<i>of pain</i>)</li><li>excruciating, oppressive, distressing (<i>of a feeling or emotion</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>puppy</li><li><i>(wild animals)</i> cub, whelp</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) snot; son of a bitch</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>щенѧ</i> (ščenę) + <i>-ок</i> (-ok), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščenę</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>щено́к</i> (ščenók): (cute little) puppy</li></ol><p><i>щено́к</i> (ščenók) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) puppy; puppyish, puppylike</li></ol><p>From <i>щеня́та</i> (ščenjáta, “puppies”) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij), with irregular iotation (compare <i>ребя́чий</i> (rebjáčij)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) woodchips, kindling wood</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščepa</i>, from <i>&#42;skopiti</i> (“to castrate”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skēp-</i>. Cognate with German <i>Schüppe</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chip, splinter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščepati</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scrupulous, meticulous</li><li>squeamish</li></ol><p>From <i>щепети́ть</i> (ščepetítʹ, “to dress up (obsolete)”) +&lrm; <i>-и́льный</i> (-ílʹnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chip, sliver</li></ol><p><i>щепа́</i> (ščepá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinch</li><li>the first three fingers held together</li></ol><p><i>щепо́ть</i> (ščepótʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dented (of surface)</li><li>pockmarked</li><li>gap-toothed</li></ol><p><i>щерб-</i> (ščerb-) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>шербе́т</i> (šerbét)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indentation; nick (on the surface)</li><li>gap (on the teeth)</li><li>pockmark (on the face)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščьrba</i> + <i>-ина</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bristles (<i>of animal hair or a brush</i>)</li><li>stubble (<i>on a face</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(š)četina</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bristle up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to get angry</li></ol><p><i>щети́нить</i> (ščetínitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silverfish, bristletail (Zygentoma)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Thysanura</i>. By surface analysis, <i>щети́на</i> (ščetína, “bristle”) +&lrm; <i>хвост</i> (xvost, “tail”) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щей</i> (ščej)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>native or inhabitant of Szczecin (<i>usually male</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Szczecin</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Ще́цин</i> (Ščécin) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) shchi</li><li>(slang,&#32;offensive) face, mug</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;сътъ</i> (&#42;sŭtŭ), from Old East Slavic <i>&#42;съто</i> (&#42;sŭto, “nourishment, food”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ankle</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščikokoltъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch</li><li>to pluck</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;also&#32;impersonal) to burn, to sting</li><li>to nibble</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščipati</i>. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>штипати</i>, Czech <i>štípat</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>slang</i>) pickpocket</li></ol><p>From <i>щипа́ть</i> (ščipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-а́ч</i> (-áč).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plucked (instrument)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pinch once</li><li>to pinch off once, to remove (one piece) by pinching</li><li>to sting once, to burn once, to cause one jolt of pain</li></ol><p><i>щипа́ть</i> (ščipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongs, pliers, pincers, nippers</li><li>forceps, tweezers</li><li>nutcrackers</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>щипе́ц</i> (ščipéc). By surface analysis, <i>щипа́ть</i> (ščipátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-цы</i> (-cy).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tweezers</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amaranth (herb)</li></ol><p>From obsolete <i>щир</i> (ščir, “amaranth, mercury”) +&lrm; <i>-и́ца</i> (-íca).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;Ukraine) sincere; frank, honest</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščirъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield</li><li>panel, board (<i>mounting tools, machinery, images, etc.</i>)</li><li>sluice-gate</li><li>(<i>zootomy</i>) carapax or plastron (dorsal or ventral half of a tortoise's or turtle's shell)</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) escutcheon</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) shit!</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>щитъ</i> (ščitŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščitъ</i> (“shield”), from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;skeita</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skey-to-m</i>, from <i>&#42;skey-</i> (“to cut, separate”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>štit</i>, Old Irish <i>scíath</i>, Latin <i>scūtum</i>.</p><p>Phono-semantic matching of English <i>shit</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>control room, switchboard room</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thyroid</li><li>peltate</li></ol><p><i>щит</i> (ščit) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) panel-board, panel-mounted, switchboard</li></ol><p><i>щит</i> (ščit) +&lrm; <i>-овой</i> (-ovoj). Compare Czech <i>štítový</i> and Polish <i>szczytowy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щит</i> (ščit)</li><li>(<i>zootomy</i>) clypeus, scute, mail</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) cyme, corymb</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) dashboard</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) guard</li><li>(<i>automotive</i>) visor, mudguard, splash guard</li><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) inescutcheon</li></ol><p><i>щит</i> (ščit) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>щит</i> (ščit).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet slang,&#32;offensive,&#32;neologism) synonym of <i>щаве́ль</i> (ščavélʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pike (any freshwater fish of the genus Esox, a relatively large, predatory fish)</li><li>KSShch (Soviet anti-ship cruise missile)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščùka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admonish</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>probe, dipstick</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tentacle</li></ol><p><i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ, “to touch”) +&lrm; <i>-ло</i> (-lo) +&lrm; <i>-це</i> (-ce)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel, to touch; to grope</li><li>to probe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>щупати</i> (ščupati), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščupati</i> ; cognate with Russian <i>щуп</i> (ščup, “a probe, a dipstick”). Compare as well Sanskrit <i>छुपति</i> (chupati, “to touch”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) puny, thin and weak</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>щуплъ</i> (ščuplŭ, “weak, pathetic”), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščuplъ</i>. Cognate with Ukrainian <i>щу́плий</i> (ščúplyj, “skinny, unattractive”), also <i>щупля́вий</i> (ščupljávyj); Belarusian <i>шчу́плы</i> (ščúply, “skinny, unattractive”); Czech <i>štíplý</i> (“lean, slender”); Slovak <i>šťúply</i> (“slender, thin”); Polish <i>szczupły</i> (“thin, lean”). Related to <i>щу́пать</i> (ščúpatʹ, “to feel”); original meaning presumably "so skinny that you can feel the bones".</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grosbeak (fringillid)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to squint (one's eye(s))</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čurъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squint one's eye(s)</li></ol><p>From <i>щу́рить</i> (ščúritʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>bird</i>) bee-eater</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) a mischievous child, a brat</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Щусь</i> (Ščusʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok) (referring to Fyodor Shuss). Either from the Soviet cinematography, either popularized by it.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pike's (the fish)</li></ol><p><i>щу́ка</i> (ščúka) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щу́ка</i> (ščúka): little pike (fish)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dandy, fop, beau</li><li>spotted redshank, spotshank (<i>Tringa erythropus</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; perhaps related to <i>щего́л</i> (ščegól, “goldfinch”) or Polish <i>szczególny</i> (“special, particular”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>click (sound of a click)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>click, snap, fillip</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endearing diminutive of <i>щель</i> (ščelʹ): (small) slit, crack</li></ol><p><i>щель</i> (ščelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to click (to make a clicking sound)</li><li>to crack, to click, to snap, etc. [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a whip, one's heels, one's fingers, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to flick, to fillip (someone)</li><li>to crack (nuts)</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to warble (<i>of a bird</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>щёлка</i> (ščólka) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to click (to make a clicking sound)</li><li>to crack, to click, to snap, etc. [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘a whip, one's heels, one's fingers, etc.’</small>]</li><li>to flick, to fillip (someone)</li><li>to crack (nuts)</li></ol><p><i>щёлкать</i> (ščólkatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) lye (a strong caustic alkaline solution of potassium or sodium salts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щёлка</i> (ščólka), diminutive of <i>щель</i> (ščelʹ)</li></ol><p><i>щёлка</i> (ščólka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alkaline earth metal (any element of group II of the periodic table)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>щелочно́й</i> (ščeločnój, “alkaline”)</li></ol><p><i>щёлочь</i> (ščóločʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) alkali</li></ol><p>From <i>щёлок</i> (ščólok) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brush</li><li>fetlock</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(š)četъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyelash brush</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щётка</i> (ščótka, “brush, fetlock”)</li></ol><p><i>щётка</i> (ščótka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>щека́</i> (ščeká): (small) cheek</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>ы</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) lol, used to express laughter. Usually used as "ы-ы-ы", to show the crying sound you make after you were laughing for too long.</li><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) ы? <i>as a closing clause</i>: Understand? Agree?</li></ol><p>Like many Cyrillic letters, originally the letter "y"/"yery" was formed from a ligature: "ꙑ", formed from "ъ" and "і" (formerly written either dotless or with two dots (see "ї")) or "и", whose former letterform resembled "н". In medieval manuscripts, it is almost always found as "ъі" or "ъи". Once the letters "ъ" and "ь" subsequently lost their values as vowels in the Slavic languages, the current simplified form "ы" evolved.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make the sound of the letter <i>ы</i> (y)</li></ol><p><i>ы</i> (y) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a Khakas stringed musical instrument, similar to morin khuur</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Khakas <i>ыых</i> (ııx).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Э</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey; an exclamation to get attention</li><li>to indicate doubt, disgust</li><li>to clearly indicate a protest or reprimand</li><li>well, uh; space filler or pause during conversation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Э</i>/<i>э</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>э</i> (e)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>э</i> (e)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebony (wood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebony (wood)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ebony (wood)</li></ol><p><i>эбе́н</i> (ebén) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebonite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ebonite</li></ol><p><i>эбони́т</i> (ebonít) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tow truck</li></ol><p>From Russian <i>эвакуи́ровать</i> (evakuírovatʹ, “to tow away”), itself from German <i>evakuieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evacuation (leaving a place for protection)</li></ol><p>From French <i>évacuation</i>, from Latin <i>evacuatio</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evacuate</li><li>to tow away (a car)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>evakuieren</i> + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evacuate</li></ol><p><i>эвакуи́ровать</i> (evakuírovatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>formal</i>) evaluation</li></ol><p>Cognate with English <i>evaluation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Even (<i>member of a Tungusic ethnic group</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Evenki</li></ol><p><i>Эве́нки</i> (Evénki, “the Evenki people”) +&lrm; <i>-и́йский</i> (-íjskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eucalyptus</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Eucalyptus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) eucalyptus</li></ol><p>From <i>эвкали́пт</i> (evkalípt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eucryphia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Eucryphia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evolving, evolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evolve</li></ol><p><i>эволюцио́н(ный)</i> (evoljución(nyj)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>evolutionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evolution</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>eureka!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>εὕρηκᾰ</i> (heúrēkă).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) heuristic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>euthanasia, mercy killing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>euphemism</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>εὐφημισμός</i> (euphēmismós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) used to express surprise at something unexpected</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aegis, mythological shield</li><li>auspices (patronage or protection)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>αἰγίς</i> (aigís) (cf. genitive <i>αἰγίδος</i> (aigídos)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) goatgrass (<i>Aegilops</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Aegilops</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ego</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ego</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egoism, egotism, selfishness</li></ol><p>From French <i>égoïsme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egotist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>égoïste</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>egoistical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>selfishly, egotistically</li></ol><p><i>эгоисти́чный</i> (egoistíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selfish, self-centred, egotistic, egotistical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) egocentrism</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from French <i>égocentrisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>occult</i>) an egregore</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>égrégore</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative form of <i>э́так</i> (étak)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>э́так</i> (étak); see <i>э-</i> (e-), <i>дак</i> (dak).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) such, like this</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) what (a)</li></ol><p><i>э́дак</i> (édak) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Edwardian era (a period in British history)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edelweiss (a European perennial alpine plant)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Edelweiß</i>, from <i>edel</i> + <i>Weiß</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aedile (elected official in Ancient Rome)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Oedipean, Oedipal</li></ol><p><i>Эди́п</i> (Edíp, “Oedipus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Эдипов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Oedipus's</li></ol><p><i>Эди́п</i> (Edíp, “Oedipus”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychoanalysis</i>) Oedipus complex</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aesopian, Aesopic, relating to Aesop</li></ol><p><i>Эзо́п</i> (Ezóp, “Aesop”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Эзопов)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aesop's</li></ol><p><i>Эзо́п</i> (Ezóp, “Aesop”) +&lrm; <i>-ов</i> (-ov)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aesopian language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>esoterism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>esoteric</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hey! (you, there!)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> A, a</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>a</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>einsteinium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any snake of the genus <i>Eirenis</i>, certain colubrid snakes found in the Old World.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Eirenis</i>. Likely from Ancient Greek <i>εἰρήνη</i>&nbsp;f (eirḗnē, “peace”, noun).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ace (tennis&#58; point scored without the opponent hitting the ball)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ace</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Eiffel (referring to Gustave Eiffel, French engineer, builder of the Eiffel Tower and Statue of Liberty)</li></ol><p>From <i>Эифе́ль</i> (Eifélʹ, “Eiffel”) +&lrm; <i>-ев</i> (-ev), with stress shift, from Gustave Eiffel (Russian Гюста́в Эйфе́ль).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>euphoria</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> H, h</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>aitch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>Used to express astonishment, mock, annoyance etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>écarté</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>écarté</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ecuadorian</li></ol><p><i>Эквадо́р</i> (Ekvadór) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equator (circle around the earth)</li><li>(<i>university slang</i>) in 5-year college education program, the time after the 3-rd year winter term exams which is considered to be the middle of the entire college education period</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Äquator</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>aequātor</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>еква́тор</i> (ekvátor), Belarusian <i>эква́тар</i> (ekvátar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equatorial</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>équatorial</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>эква́тор</i> (ekvátor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equivalent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Äquivalent</i>, itself from Latin <i>aequivalentem</i>, accusative singular of <i>aequivalēns</i>, present active participle of <i>aequivaleō</i> (“to be equivalent, have equal power”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equivalent</li></ol><p><i>эквивале́нт</i> (ekvivalént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equidistant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) eques (<i>a member of the equites</i>)</li><li>(historical,&#32;in the plural) mounted gladiators</li></ol><p>Derived from Latin <i>eques</i> (“horseman”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, exam, test</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>egzamen</i>, <i>egzamin</i> during the era of Peter the Great, from Latin <i>examen</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examiner (someone who administers an exam)</li></ol><p><i>экза́мен</i> (ekzámen) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) exam, test</li></ol><p><i>экзамена́ция</i> (ekzamenácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) testing (<i>through an exam</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) testing, checking</li></ol><p><i>экзамен(ова́ть)</i> (ekzamen(ovátʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to test (to subject to an exam)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;imperfective only) to test, to check</li></ol><p><i>экза́мен</i> (ekzámen) +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>execution (the act of putting to death or being put to death as a penalty)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) flogging, corporal punishment</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) execution of a judicial sentence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) eczema (acute or chronic inflammation of the skin)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specimen, sample, instance, copy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Exemplar</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) existentialism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>existentialisme</i>, from Latin <i>existentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>existential</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Late Latin <i>existentialis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exoplanet</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exorcist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exotics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exotic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exotic</li></ol><p><i>экзо́т(ика)</i> (ekzót(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) exochorda (<i>Exochorda racemosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Exochorda</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>what a...</li></ol><p>From <i>э́-</i> (é-) +&lrm; <i>кой</i> (koj, “how, which, who, what”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(aviation,&#32;nautical,&#32;sports) crew</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) carriage (a horse-drawn vehicle)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>équipage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equipping</li><li>outfit (a set of clothing)</li><li>outfit, fitting out</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literature,&#32;art,&#32;architecture,&#32;music,&#32;philosophy) eclecticism</li><li>instance of eclecticism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Eklektik</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἐκλεκτικός</i> (eklektikós, “selective”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eclectic</li></ol><p><i>экле́кт(ика)</i> (eklékt(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>éclair</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eclipta (<i>Eclipta</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Eclipta</i>, from Latin <i>eclīpta</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐκλείπω</i> (ekleípō, “to leave out, to pass over”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false daisy (<i>Eclipta prostrata</i>)</li></ol><p>Calque of translingual <i>Eclipta prostrata</i>. By surface analysis, <i>экли́пта</i> (eklípta, “eclipta”) +&lrm; <i>простёртый</i> (prostjórtyj, “prostrated”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) ecliptic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ecliptica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ecologist (a scientist who studies ecology, male or female)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ecologically, environmentally</li></ol><p><i>экологи́ческий</i> (ekologíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ecological (relating to ecology)</li></ol><p><i>эколо́гия</i> (ekológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ecology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) economist</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) steward (one who administers affairs)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>econometrics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>econometric</li></ol><p><i>экономе́трия</i> (ekonométrija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>econometrics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>economy (production and distribution and consumption)</li><li>economics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>economist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save, to spare</li><li>to economize (<i>imperfective only</i>)</li></ol><p><i>эконо́мия</i> (ekonómija) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>economically</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>economic (relating to the economy)</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) economical, thrifty</li></ol><p><i>эконо́м(ика)</i> (ekonóm(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>economicalness</li></ol><p><i>экономи́чный</i> (ekonomíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>economical in terms of efficiency, efficient</li></ol><p><i>эконо́мия</i> (ekonómija) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>economy, saving (frugal use of resources)</li></ol><p>First attested in year 1658. Possibly borrowed from French <i>économie</i>, from Latin <i>oeconomia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>οἰκονομία</i> (oikonomía). See also домостро́й (domostrój), Old East Slavic <i>икономиꙗ</i> (ikonomija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) housekeeper</li></ol><p><i>эконо́мия</i> (ekonómija) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thriftily, economically</li></ol><p><i>эконо́мный</i> (ekonómnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>economical in terms of using less, thrifty</li></ol><p><i>эконо́мия</i> (ekonómija) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ecosystem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ecosystem</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ecocide</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ecoefficient (making efficient, non-wasteful use of environmental resources)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>эко-</i> (eko-) +&lrm; <i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screen (a physical divider)</li><li>screen (theater screen, TV screen)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) cinematography</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>écran</i>. Doublet of <i>ши́рма</i> (šírma).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>film adaptation, screen version</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>film, screen, cinematize</li></ol><p>From <i>экра́н</i> (ekrán) +&lrm; <i>-изи́ровать</i> (-izírovatʹ). Compare Belarusian <i>экранізава́ць</i> (ekranizavácʹ), Ukrainian <i>екранізува́ти</i> (ekranizuváty), Polish <i>ekranizować</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camrip</li></ol><p><i>экра́н</i> (ekrán) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) screen (physical divider or display device)</li><li>on-screen</li></ol><p><i>экра́н</i> (ekrán) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ekranoplan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>(English letter)</i> X, x</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(productive,&#32;in journalistic style,&#32;occasional&#32;or&#32;colloquial,&#32;takes nouns,&#32;produces nouns) ex- (former)</li><li>(unproductive,&#32;takes adjective,&#32;produces adjective) extra- (out of&#59; outside)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ex-Communist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ex-Communist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ex-president</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) exabyte</li></ol><p>From <i>экса-</i> (ɛksa-) +&lrm; <i>байт</i> (bajt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) exabit</li></ol><p>From <i>экса-</i> (ɛksa-) +&lrm; <i>бит</i> (bit).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhibitionism (practice of drawing attention to oneself or displaying one's private bodily parts in public)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhibitionist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disinter, to exhume</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>exhumō</i>, from Latin <i>ex-</i> + <i>humō</i> (“to bury”), + <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vehicles</i>) excavator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>excavator</i>. Several steps omitting from Latin <i>cavus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exclave</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) something that is considered exceptional, exclusive, one of a kind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>exclusif</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exclusive</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>exklusiv</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) excrement, feces (animal solid waste)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>excrēmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excursus, digression</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person on an excursion, outing or tour; sightseer, tripper, excursionist</li></ol><p><i>экску́рсия</i> (ekskúrsija) +&lrm; <i>-а́нт</i> (-ánt)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excursion, trip, visit, journey, sightseeing, hike, outing (brief recreational trip)</li><li>tour, guided tour (of a museum, attraction etc.)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Latin <i>excursiō</i>, through the intermediate of another language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide (a trained specialist who leads guided tours at a museum or other location of interest)</li></ol><p>From <i>экску́рсия</i> (ekskúrsija, “tour”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to guide, to lead”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effusive</li></ol><p>From French <i>expansif</i> + <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) expansionism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(of a state, a tribe, etc) expansion of territory or influence</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forwarding agent, shipping agent, dispatcher, freight forwarder</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) dispatch, forwarding</li><li>expeditionary</li></ol><p><i>экспеди́ция</i> (ekspedícija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dispatch, expedition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Expedition</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) expectoration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experiment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>experīmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>experimentally</li></ol><p><i>эксперимента́льный</i> (eksperimentálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>experimental</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experimenter, experimentalist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experiment <i>(on, with)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>experimentieren</i>, from Latin <i>experimentare</i>. For the suffix, see <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expert</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expert examination</li><li>expert advice, expertise</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) expert</li></ol><p><i>экспе́рт</i> (ekspért) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exploiter</li></ol><p><i>эксплуат(и́ровать)</i> (ekspluat(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́тор</i> (-átor)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) operation</li><li>operational, working</li><li>exploitative</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>эксплуата́ция</i> (ekspluatácija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exploitation</li><li>operation (of machinery, buildings, etc.)</li><li>operation (of land, subsoil, extraction of minerals from deposits)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exploit</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exposition, display</li><li>(<i>photography</i>) exposure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>expositiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhibit (something exhibited)</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from a pseudo-Latin construction Latin <i>&#42;expōnātus</i> (“(something) exhibited, displayed”), from <i>expōnō</i> (the correct past passive participle is <i>expositus</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) exhibitor</li><li>(inanimate,&#32;mathematics,&#32;linguistics,&#32;semiotics) exponent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Exponent</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>export</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to export (to sell (goods) to a foreign country)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) export</li></ol><p><i>э́кспорт</i> (éksport) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>export-oriented, export-dependent</li></ol><p>From <i>экспорт</i> (eksport) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ориентированный</i> (orijentirovannyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exporter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>express (train, bus, etc.)</li><li>(<i>gambling</i>) accumulator</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>near-patient testing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expressionism (movement in the arts)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expression</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>expressiō</i> (“a pressing out”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) express</li></ol><p><i>экспре́сс</i> (ekspréss) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>misspelling of <i>эспре́ссо</i> (esprésso)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impromptu</li><li>improvisation</li><li>extemporization</li><li>ad lib</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ex</i> (“from”) + <i>promptū</i> (“readiness”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hedge</i>) off the top of one's head, Thesaurus:impromptu</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(law,&#32;politics) expropriation</li></ol><p><i>экспропри(и́ровать)</i> (ekspropri(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expropriate</li></ol><p>French <i>exproprier</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ecstasy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ecstatic (feeling or characterized by ecstasy)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exterritorial, extraterritorial</li></ol><p><i>экс-</i> (eks-) +&lrm; <i>территориа́льный</i> (territoriálʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>extra-</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eccentric, extravagant</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>extravagant</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>psychology</i>) extroversion, a personality orientation towards others and things outside oneself; behavior expressing such orientation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extradite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extradition</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extract (something extracted)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>extractum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) extract</li></ol><p><i>экстра́кт</i> (ekstrákt) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) extraordinary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>extrāordinārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extrapolate</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>extrapoler</i> + <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extrapolation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psychic, paranormalist, person with ESP, esper</li></ol><p>Probably back-formed from English <i>extrasensory</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extreme</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) extremism</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extremist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extremist</li></ol><p><i>экстреми́ст</i> (ekstremíst) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>special, extra, extraordinary</li><li>urgent, emergency</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) eccentric (person who does not behave like others)</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) comic (actor)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) cam (part of engine)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) eccentric (off-centre wheel)</li></ol><p>Via French <i>excentrique</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>excentricus</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔκκεντρος</i> (ékkentros, “not having the earth as the center of an orbit”), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) + <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron, “point”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eccentric</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) eccentric (having a different center&#59; not concentric)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔκκεντρος</i> (ékkentros); suffixed with <i>-ический</i> (-ičeskij). By surface analysis, <i>экс-</i> (eks-) +&lrm; <i>центри́ческий</i> (centríčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eccentric</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔκκεντρος</i> (ékkentros); suffixed with <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>ексцентри́чний</i> (ekscentrýčnyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme manifestation of something, incident or abnormality</li><li>excess</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) ectasis (the dilation or distention of a hollow or tubular part of the body, typically due to disease or injury)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἐκτασία</i> (ektasía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>parapsychology</i>) ectoplasm</li><li>(<i>cytology</i>) ectoplasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ectoplasmic</li></ol><p>From <i>эктопла́зма</i> (ektoplázma) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) action movie (a type of motion picture)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>action</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Л, л.</li><li>The Roman letter L, l.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elastic, supple</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elasticity</li></ol><p><i>эласти́чный</i> (elastíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elastic</li><li>flexible</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>élast(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj). Several steps omitting from Ancient Greek <i>ἐλαστός</i> (elastós) (variant of <i>ἐλατός</i> (elatós) &lt; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō)). Akin to <i>ἔλασμα</i> (élasma) (whence <i>эласмоза́вр</i> (elasmozávr), <i>эласмоте́рий</i> (elasmotérij)).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ко́вкий</i> (kóvkij) <~ <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Elaphomyces</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Elaphomyces</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) grain elevator</li><li>(<i>engineering</i>) elevator</li></ol><p>Borrowed from American English <i>elevator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ale</li></ol><p>From <i>эль</i> (elʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj). Compare Ukrainian <i>е́левий</i> (élevyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elegantly</li></ol><p><i>элега́нтный</i> (elegántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elegance</li></ol><p><i>элега́нтный</i> (elegántnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elegant (exhibiting elegance)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elegy</li><li>(figurative,&#32;poetic) sadness, melancholy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐλεγεία</i> (elegeía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electoral</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>électoral</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electorate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrician</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrification (providing of electricity)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>électrification</i> or from a related term in another European language.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to electrify</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>elektrifizieren</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electric</li></ol><p>French <i>électr(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) electricity (form of energy)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (electric) light, lighting</li></ol><p>French <i>électr(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́чество</i> (-íčestvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) (suburban) electric train</li></ol><p><i>электри́ч(еский)</i> (elektríč(eskij)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>electro- (meaning electricity or electrical)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) electro-house, house music combined with electronic music</li></ol><p><i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>ха́ус</i> (xáus)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric razor, electric shaver</li></ol><p>From <i>элѐктро-</i> (ɛlèktro-) +&lrm; <i>бри́тва</i> (brítva).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric locomotive</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>-воз</i> (-voz), combining form of <i>вози́ть</i> (vozítʹ, “to transport”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) electrode</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric engine, electric motor</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>дви́гатель</i> (dvígatelʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) electrodiagnosis, electrodiagnostics</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>диагно́стика</i> (diagnóstika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>technology</i>) electric strike</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>защёлка</i> (zaščólka, “lock mechanism”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) electrolysis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electromagnet</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>магни́т</i> (magnít).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) electromagnetic interaction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electromagnetic</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>магни́тный</i> (magnítnyj) or <i>электромагни́т</i> (elektromagnít) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electromechanical</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>механи́ческий</i> (mexaníčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric car</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>моби́ль</i> (mobílʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electronics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) e-mail (system)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electronic cigarette, e-cigarette</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electron cooling</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>computing</i>) e-mail message</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) electron, electronic</li></ol><p>From <i>электро́н</i> (elektrón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>e-dictionary, electronic dictionary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrical equipment</li></ol><p><i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>обору́дование</i> (oborúdovanije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abstract noun of <i>электроотрица́тельный</i> (elektrootricátelʹnyj): (<i>chemistry</i>) electronegativity</li></ol><p><i>электроотрица́тельный</i> (elektrootricátelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electronegative</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>отрица́тельный</i> (otricátelʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electricity transmission</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>переда́ча</i> (peredáča).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(suburban) electric train</li></ol><p><i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>по́езд</i> (pójezd)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consuming electricity</li></ol><p><i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>потребля́ющий</i> (potrebljájuščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric device; electric appliance; power tool</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>прибо́р</i> (pribór).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motor controller</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>приво́д</i> (privód), or from <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>при́вод</i> (prívod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrical conductivity, electrical conduction</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>провод</i> (provod) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric wiring</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>прово́дка</i> (provódka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrical conductivity, electrical conduction</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>провод</i> (provod) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power grid, electricity network</li></ol><p><i>эле́ктро-</i> (eléktro-) +&lrm; <i>сеть</i> (setʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric power supply</li></ol><p><i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>снабже́ние</i> (snabžénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power station</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-, “electric”) +&lrm; <i>ста́нция</i> (stáncija, “station”).</p><p>Attested since at least 1900 in Robert Klasson's journal writings (see quote).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electrostatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand dryer</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-, “electric”) +&lrm; <i>суши́лка</i> (sušílka, “dryer”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrical engineering</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>те́хника</i> (téxnika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>record player</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>électrophone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;medicine) electrophoresis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Elektrophorese</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electrochemiluminescence</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>хемилюминесценция</i> (xemiljuminescencija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>electrochemical</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>хими́ческий</i> (ximíčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stun gun (a weapon intended to immobilize, usually through delivery of an electric shock)</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-, “electric”) +&lrm; <i>шо́кер</i> (šóker, “shocker”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power industry</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>энерге́тика</i> (enɛrgétika).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric power</li></ol><p>From <i>электро-</i> (elektro-) +&lrm; <i>эне́ргия</i> (ɛnɛ́rgija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>element, component</li><li>(chemistry,&#32;physics) element</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;collective) person (<i>of a specified type</i>); (<i>in the plural</i>) element</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate,&#32;derogatory) bad person, harmful person</li><li>(<i>electricity</i>) electrochemical cell</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>elementum</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Czech <i>prvek</i>, Polish <i>pierwiastek</i> (akin to <i>пе́рвый</i> (pérvyj)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) elementary particle</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an elementary manner</li></ol><p><i>элемента́рный</i> (elementárnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elementary, basic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) element (component or indivisible matter); elemental</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) electrochemical cell</li></ol><p><i>элеме́нт</i> (elemént) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aileron</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) eleutherococcus (<i>Eleutherococcus</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Eleutherococcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elixir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>élixir</i>. Further from Ancient Greek <i>ξηρίον</i> (xēríon) (<i>ξηρός</i> (xērós) + <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), i.e. присы́пка, literally **прису́шка), via Arabic, whence ال (al-).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elimination</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the elite</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elitism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Elitarismus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) elite</li><li>elitist</li></ol><p>From French <i>élitaire</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elitism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>élitisme</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elite</li><li>best, choice</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) luxury, luxurious</li></ol><p><i>эли́та</i> (elíta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) Hellene, Greek</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) ellipse (curve)</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) ellipse</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ale (English beer)</li><li>The Cyrillic letter Л, л.</li><li>The Roman letter L, l.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ale</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mythology,&#32;fantasy) elf</li><li>(uncommon,&#32;slang,&#32;derogatory) a Ukrainian, especially one who serves in the Armed Forces of Ukraine.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>elf</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elbʰós</i>.</p><p>Compare with Ukrainian <i>ельф</i> (elʹf).</p><p>Sense 2 was derived in analogy to the use of орк (ork) referring to Russian soldiers, or Russians in general.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) Related to elves; elven; elvish.</li><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) Related to any fictional language used by elves.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>fantasy</i>) "Elvish" language; any fictional language used by a race or culture of elves.</li></ol><p><i>эльф</i> (elʹf, from English <i>elf</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter М, м.</li><li>The Roman letter M, m.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enameled</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enamel</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>emaillieren</i> +&lrm; <i>-ова́ть</i> (-ovátʹ), or from <i>эма́ль</i> (emálʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ирова́ть</i> (-irovátʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enamel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>émail</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emancipation</li><li>granting of equal rights</li></ol><p><i>эмансип(и́ровать)</i> (emansip(írovatʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-а́ция</i> (-ácija)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emancipate</li><li>to grant equal rights to</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>émanciper</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ровать</i> (-írovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) emo (person)</li></ol><p><i>э́мо</i> (émo) +&lrm; <i>-а́рь</i> (-árʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embargo (an order by the government prohibiting ships from leaving port)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>embargo</i>, from <i>embargar</i>. Several steps omitting from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;barra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambient (type of modern music)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emblem</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>emblēma</i> (“raised ornaments on vessels, tessellated work, mosaic”), from Ancient Greek <i>ἔμβλημα</i> (émblēma, “an insertion”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embryo (an unborn baby that is less developed than a foetus)</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>embryon</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἔμβρυον</i> (émbruon).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Emesene</li></ol><p><i>Эме́са</i> (Emésa) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>эме́сский</i> (emésskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pharmacology</i>) emetine</li></ol><p>From a Western European language (cf. French <i>émétine</i>, English <i>emetine</i>), from Ancient Greek <i>ἔμετος</i> (émetos, “vomiting”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emigrant</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) emigrant, emigre</li></ol><p><i>эмигра́нт</i> (emigránt) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emigration</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emigrate (to leave one's country in order to reside elsewhere)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>émir</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emirate (country ruled by an emir)</li></ol><p><i>эмир</i> (emir) +&lrm; <i>-ат</i> (-at)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) UAE, Emirati</li></ol><p><i>Эмира́ты</i> (Emiráty, “the Emirates”) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emissary</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;finance) issue</li><li>(relational,&#32;physics) emission</li></ol><p><i>эми́ссия</i> (emíssija) +&lrm; <i>-о́нный</i> (-ónnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(physics,&#32;electronics,&#32;finance) emission</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) issuer, issuing bank, bank of issue</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emmentaler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Emmental</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emo (music or subculture)</li><li>emo (person)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>emo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emoji</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>絵文字<rp>(</rp><rt>えもじ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>emoji</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emo skink</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Emoia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>emotionally, with emotion</li></ol><p><i>эмоциона́льный</i> (emocionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emotionality</li></ol><p><i>эмоциона́льный</i> (emocionálʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>emotional</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emotion, feeling</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>émotion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empathy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>empathy</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cosmology</i>) empyrean</li><li>(poetic,&#32;humorous&#32;or&#32;sarcastic) paradise, heaven</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἐμπύριος</i> (empúrios).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empiric, empirical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emu (a large flightless bird)</li></ol><p>Derived from Portuguese <i>ema</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emulsion</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from New Latin <i>ēmulsiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) emphysema</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Н, н.</li><li>The Roman letter N, n.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) endemic (individual or species that is endemic)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biology</i>) endemic (native to a particular area)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endoscope</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Endocellion</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Endocellion</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endoethnonym (an ethnonym used by those who belong to the ethnic group it describes)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>эндо-</i> (endo-) +&lrm; <i>этно́ним</i> (etnónim).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>andrewsarchus (prehistoric mammal)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chess</i>) endgame</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Endspiel</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) power engineering specialist; energeticist</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) energy drink</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power engineering, energetics</li><li>(<i>parapsychology</i>) energy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Energetik</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) energy; energetic</li></ol><p>Perhaps from French <i>énergét(ique)</i> +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;also&#32;pseudo-science) An energy vampire</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) energetic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>energetically, vigorously (in an energetic manner)</li></ol><p><i>энерги́чный</i> (ɛnɛrgíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy, vim</li><li>avidity (eagerness&#59; intenseness of desire)</li><li>boldness</li><li>moxie</li></ol><p><i>энерги́чный</i> (ɛnɛrgíčnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>energetic</li><li>vigorous, forceful</li><li>drastic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy, power</li><li>vitality</li><li>vigor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Energie</i>, from Latin <i>energīa</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐνέργεια</i> (enérgeia).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Энергия)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Energia carrier rocket</li><li>S. P. Korolev Rocket and Space Corporation Energia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind power, wind energy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy resource/carrier, fuel</li></ol><p>Calque of German <i>Energieträger</i>, no later than 1935.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy resource, <i>(plural)</i> energy supply</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy supply</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power saving, energy saving, economical power consumption</li></ol><p><i>энергия</i> (enɛ́rgija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>сбереже́ние</i> (sberežénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power grid</li></ol><p><i>энергия</i> (ɛnɛ́rgija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>систе́ма</i> (sistéma)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>electric power supply</li></ol><p><i>энергия</i> (enɛ́rgija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>снабже́ние</i> (snabžénije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>energy content, energy capacity</li></ol><p><i>энергия</i> (enɛ́rgija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ёмкость</i> (jómkostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) Popular Socialist, enes</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>наро́дный социали́ст</i> (naródnyj socialíst) or from <i>НС</i> (NS).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biochemistry</i>) enzyme</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Enzym</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enzymatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) enzymatic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enzymatic</li></ol><p><i>энзи́м</i> (enzím) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) An employee of the NKVD</li></ol><p>From <i>НКВД</i> (NKVD) (phonetically: <i>эн-ка-вэ-дэ́</i> (en-ka-vɛ-dɛ́)) + <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ordinal number</i>) nth (occurring at position <i>n</i>)</li></ol><p><i>эн</i> (en) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) A plant of the genus <i>Ensete</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Ensete</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Entoloma</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Entoloma</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entomologist</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ἔντομον</i> (éntomon, “insect”) + <i>-лог</i> (-log), from Ancient Greek <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) entomological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entomology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) entropy (term in thermodynamics)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enthusiasm</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enthusiast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encephalitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) encephalartos (<i>Encephalartos</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Encephalartos</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>encyclopaedic, encyclopaedical</li></ol><p><i>энциклопе́д(ия)</i> (enciklopéd(ija)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>энциклопеди́ческий</i> (enciklopedíčeskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>encyclopaedia/encyclopedia</li></ol><p>From New Latin <i>encyclopaedia</i> (“general education”), from a univerbated form of Koine Greek <i>ἐγκύκλιος παιδείᾱ</i> (enkúklios paideíā, “education in the circle of arts and sciences”), from <i>ἐγκύκλιος</i> (enkúklios, “circular”) + <i>παιδείᾱ</i> (paideíā, “child-rearing, education”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) encyrtid</li><li>(taxonomy,&#32;in the plural) Encyrtidae, a family of parasitic wasps.</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Encyrtidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aeolian, eolian (relating to the wind)</li></ol><p><i>Эо́л</i> (Eól) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shocking behavior, outrageousness</li></ol><p>French <i>épater</i> +&lrm; <i>-а́ж</i> (-áž)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) epigaea (<i>Epigaea</i> sp.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Epigaea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epigram</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epigraph</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epigraphist (a person who studies epigraphy (inscriptions))</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epidemiologic, epidemiological</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epidemiology</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>epidemiologia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) + <i>-λογία</i> (-logía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epidemic</li></ol><p><i>эпиде́мия</i> (epidémija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epidemic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) epidermis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;botany) epidermis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>athlete's foot (fungal infection)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) epididymitis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>episode</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épisode</i>. The meaning "episode of a TV/radio/podcast/etc. series" is a semantic loan from English <i>episode</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>episodic</li><li>insignificant</li></ol><p><i>эпизо́д</i> (epizód) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épique</i> (matching the feminine gender) and/or combined with Latin <i>epicus</i> (matching the stress).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epicurean (a follower of Epicurus and Epicureanism)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epilepsy</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιληψίᾱ</i> (epilēpsíā).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epilogue</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épilogue</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any of the genus <i>Epipremnum</i> of climbing vines.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Epipremnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epistemology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithalamium (poetic work in the nature of a panegyric on the occasion of a wedding)</li><li>(<i>Ancient Greece</i>) epithalamium (the wedding song)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>epithalamium</i>, in turn from Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιθαλάμιον</i> (epithalámion), neuter form of Ancient Greek <i>ἐπιθαλάμιος</i> (epithalámios), from <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>θάλαμος</i> (thálamos, “inner chamber, wedding chamber”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epitaph (inscription on a gravestone)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) epithelium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literature</i>) epithet</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἐπίθετον</i> (epítheton, “epithet, adjective”) (which is also calqued into <i>прилага́тельное</i> (prilagátelʹnoje)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epitrochoid (geometric curve)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epiphysis</li><li>pineal gland</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epicentre/epicenter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epic</li></ol><p><i>э́п(ика)</i> (ép(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) alternative form of <i>эполе́та</i> (epoléta, “epaulet”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épaulette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epaulet (shoulder decoration)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épaulette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eponym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epic, epopee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>épopée</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epos, epic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epoch, age, era</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Age of Enlightenment (period of history)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epochal, important, significant</li></ol><p>Perhaps from German <i>epochal</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epsilon (the Greek letter Ε&#47;ε)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἒ ψιλόν</i> (è psilón).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Р, р.</li><li>The Roman letter R, r.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>era, epoch</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erbium</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Airedale Terrier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Airedale Terrier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) erebid</li></ol><p>From translingual <i>Erebidae</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) erection (rigid penis)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ērectiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erzyan</li></ol><p><i>э́рзя</i> (érzja) +&lrm; <i>-янин</i> (-janin)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Erzya</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erica, <i>Erica</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Erica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Eritrean</li></ol><p><i>Эритре́я</i> (Eritréja) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erythrocyte</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) eorl, earl (<i>in Anglo-Saxon England before the Norman conquest</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>eorl, earl</i>. Doublet of <i>ярл</i> (jarl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>airlift (a pipe that is used to suck up objects from the sea bed.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erogenous zone</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erogenous</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erosion</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;philosophy,&#32;psychiatry) eros</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Эрос)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eros</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἔρως</i> (Érōs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eroticism (state of being erotic)</li><li>erotica</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erotic (<i>art, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>эро́т(ика)</i> (erót(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erotic <i>(sexually arousing, sensual)</i></li></ol><p><i>эро́т(ика)</i> (erót(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́чный</i> (-íčnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) oersted (CGS unit of auxiliary magnetic field)</li></ol><p>From Hans Christian Ørsted.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knowledgeable</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erudite, learned person</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Latin <i>ērudītus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erudition, knowledge</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>ērudītiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>erhu</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>二胡</i> (èrhú).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archduke (rank)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Erzherzog</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archduchy (a dominion or region ruled by an archduke or archduchess)</li></ol><p>From <i>эрцге́рцог</i> (ercgércog) +&lrm; <i>-ство</i> (-stvo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter С, с.</li><li>The Roman letter S, s.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>эсце́т</i> (escét)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) Someone who participates in the special military operation (2022 invasion or war) in Ukraine.</li></ol><p>Phonetic spelling of <i>СВО</i> (SVO, as 'эс-вэ-о́') +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;politics) Socialist Revolutionary, Eser</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>социали́ст-революционе́р</i> (socialíst-revoljucionér) or from <i>СР</i> (SR).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;usually navy, rarely air force) squadron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>escadre</i>, itself from Italian <i>squadra</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;navy) related to a squadron, intended to operate in a squadron</li></ol><p><i>эска́дра</i> (eskádra) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;nautical) late 19th or early 20th century pre-dreadnought battleship designed to operate as part of a squadron</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>cuirassé d'escadre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(military,&#32;nautical) destroyer</li></ol><p>Calque of French <i>torpilleur d'escadre</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squadron <i>(Air Force)</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Spanish <i>escuadrilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) squadron, (cavalry) troop</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>escadron</i>, itself from Italian <i>squadrone</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escalator (mechanical device)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>escalator</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escalation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>escalation</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escapism</li></ol><p>From English <i>escapism</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escargot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>escargot</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escargot</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sketch, draft</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>esquisse</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eskimo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>esquimau</i> (“Eskimo”). Presence of final <i>-с</i> (-s) unclear.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort (accompanying group of soldiers, guards, etc.)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from French <i>escorte</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escort</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) destroyer</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>эска́дренный миноно́сец</i> (eskádrennyj minonósec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esperantist (specialist or speaker of Esperanto, the international auxiliary language designed by L. L. Zamenhof)</li></ol><p>From <i>эспера́нто</i> (esperánto) +&lrm; <i>-и́ст</i> (-íst).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esperanto (auxiliary language)</li></ol><p>From Esperanto <i>Esperanto</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>esplanade</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>espresso</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>espresso</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>essay</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>essai</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overpass, trestle, viaduct</li><li>pier (<i>of a bridge</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>estacade</i> (“bridge on stilts”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;uncountable) mounted courier; mail sent by courier</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) relay race</li><li>(<i>sports&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) baton (<i>passed from one runner to another in a relay race</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>estafette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) courier</li><li>(relational,&#32;sports) relay, relay race</li></ol><p><i>эстафе́та</i> (estaféta) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from French <i>estafette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aesthete/esthete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) aesthetics/esthetics (study or philosophy of beauty)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>αἰσθητικός</i> (aisthētikós).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aesthetic (concerned with beauty)</li></ol><p><i>эсте́т(ика)</i> (estɛ́t(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act like an aesthete</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aestivation/estivation (state of inactivity and metabolic depression during summer)</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>штибле́та</i> (štibléta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Estonian</li></ol><p><i>Эсто́ния</i> (Estónija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Estonia</i>) Estonian (any person from Estonia or someone descended from such people)</li></ol><p><i>Эстония</i> (Estonija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>земля</i> (zemlja) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec), probably a calque of Estonian <i>eestimaalane</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Estonian (relating to Estonia, its people or their language)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Estonian language</li></ol><p><i>Эсто́ния</i> (Estónija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) tarragon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>estragon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>platform, stage (for performers)</li><li>variety (art)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>estrade</i>. Further from Spanish <i>estrado</i>, from Latin <i>strātum</i>, akin to <i>strātus</i>, p.p.p. of <i>sternō</i>. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) variety, vaudeville</li></ol><p><i>эстра́да</i> (estráda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estrogen</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eschatology</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Latin script letter <i>ẞ</i>/<i>ß</i>, called <i>eszett</i> in English.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Eszett</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>text message, text <i>(brief electronic message sent using the SMS service for cell phones)</i></li></ol><p>From the letters <i>СМС</i> (SMS) + <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>SS-man, SS-person</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>э́то</i> (éto)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>э́то</i> (éto)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) clipping of <i>э́то</i> (éto)</li></ol><p>Pronunciation spelling of <i>э́то</i> (éto) in fast speech.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eta (the Greek letter Η&#47;η)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἦτα</i> (êta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor, storey</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étage</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookshelf, bookcase, whatnot, etagere (a piece of furniture with open shelves for displaying ornaments or books)</li><li>shoe rack</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étagère</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) floor, level</li></ol><p><i>эта́ж</i> (etáž) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) so, in this manner, thus</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) some, about</li></ol><p><i>э-</i> (e-) +&lrm; <i>так</i> (tak)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) such, like this</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) what (a)</li></ol><p>From <i>э́так</i> (étak) +&lrm; <i>-ий</i> (-ij). By surface analysis, <i>э́-</i> (é-) +&lrm; <i>тако́й</i> (takój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>model, standard, reference, benchmark</li><li>role model, exemplar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étalon</i>, from Old French <i>estalon</i>, from Frankish <i>&#42;stalo</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) model, standard, reference, benchmark</li></ol><p><i>этало́н</i> (etalón) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethanol, ethyl alcohol (simple aliphatic alcohol: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH or CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step, stage</li><li>base</li><li>transport</li><li>étape (a rest point for a group of escorted or transported convicts, e.g. from one prison to another)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étape</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escorting</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethics (study of principles governing right and wrong conduct)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>etiquette</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) synonym of <i>этике́тка</i> (etikétka, “label, tag”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étiquette</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>label, tag, tally</li></ol><p>From <i>этике́т</i> (etikét) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>etymological</li></ol><p><i>этимоло́гия</i> (etimológija) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>etymology</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>etymologia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἐτυμολογία</i> (etumología), from <i>ἔτυμον</i> (étumon, “true sense”) and <i>-λογία</i> (-logía, “study of”) (from <i>λόγος</i> (lógos)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aetiology/etiology (study of causes or origins)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethical (various senses)</li></ol><p><i>э́т(ика)</i> (ét(ika)) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethic, ethical</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethnic</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnographer</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>этногра́фия</i> (etnográfija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethnographic</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) ethnography</li></ol><p><i>этногра́фия</i> (etnográfija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnography</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnoculture, ethnic culture</li></ol><p>Acronym of <i>этни́ческая культу́ра</i> (etníčeskaja kulʹtúra, “ethnic culture”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethnocultural</li></ol><p><i>этнокульту́ра</i> (etnokulʹtúra) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnolect (language variety)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnologist (<i>male or female</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethnological</li></ol><p><i>этноло́гия</i> (etnológija) +&lrm; <i>-и́ческий</i> (-íčeskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnology</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos) + <i>-λογία</i> (-logía).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnonym</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnonymics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnonymics</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnicity, ethnic group, ethnos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swedh-no-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnic slur, ethnophaulism</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>ethnophaulism</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos) + <i>φαῦλος</i> (phaûlos).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Generic demonstrative pronoun. Translated as "this" or "that" (or their plural forms, "these" or "those"), depending on the context.</li><li>Used as a personal pronoun when not referring to a particular noun. Translated as "it".</li><li>(<i>nominative only</i>) used as a personal pronoun to indicate that something at the centre of a matter at hand is a certain thing&#59; can be translated as "it" or "he", "she", "they", as appropriate</li><li>Optionally used after a noun to introduce its definition or explanation. In the most formal texts, separated from the noun by an em dash, or <i>(дли́нное) тире́</i> ((dlínnoje) tirɛ́). In more informal texts, may be separated by a hyphen (<i>дефи́с</i> (defís)), due to typological constraints, or only a space. Often omitted, though usually not when the dash&#47;hyphen is also omitted, to avoid introducing ambiguity.</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Used to emphasise identity, analogously to English "it's me who" etc.</li><li>Used to intensify a question concerning an observation.</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>uh, er, erm, umm (used in hesitant speech)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>A space filler in conversation.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Mostly proximal demonstrative determiner. Usually translated as "this"&#47;"these", but can occasionally be translated as "that"&#47;"those" and, in some cases, the English definite article, "the".</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>this/that one, these/those (ones)</li></ol><p>From <i>тот</i> (tot), with the expressive prefix <i>э-</i> (e-) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁é</i>), winning over <i>сей</i> (sej) in the 19th century. Compare Sanskrit <i>एतद्</i> (etád).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>э́тот</i> (étot).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Etruscan (an inhabitant of ancient Etruria)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Etruscan</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>study, sketch (artwork made in order to practise or demonstrate)</li><li>study, etude, exercise</li><li>problem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>étude</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) eukaryote (any of the single-celled or multicellular organisms of the taxonomic domain <i>Eukaryota</i>, whose cells contain at least one distinct nucleus)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Cyrillic letter Ф, ф.</li><li>The Roman letter F, f.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ephemeral, transitory</li><li>illusory, phantom</li></ol><p>From French <i>éphémère</i> +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj), from Ancient Greek <i>ἐφήμερος</i> (ephḗmeros, “during one day”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effendi (title of respect)</li></ol><p>Via a Turkic source (compare Bashkir <i>әфәнде</i> (əfənde), Turkish <i>efendi</i>), from Greek <i>αφέντη</i> (afénti) (vocative of <i>αφέντης</i> (aféntis)), from Ancient Greek <i>αὐθέντης</i> (authéntēs, “lord, master”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hilt (grip of a sword)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Gefäss</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ephesian</li></ol><p><i>Эфе́с</i> (Efés) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian (a person from Ethiopia or of Ethiopian descent)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian</li></ol><p><i>Эфио́пия</i> (Efiópija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ether (organic chemistry: a compound containing an oxygen atom bonded to two hydrocarbon groups)</li><li>(historical,&#32;physics) luminiferous aether, aether</li><li>(<i>alchemy</i>) ether</li><li>(<i>broadcasting</i>) air, broadcast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>αἰθήρ</i> (aithḗr, “upper air”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Эфир)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aether</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ethereal, aerial</li></ol><p><i>эфи́р</i> (efír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephthalite, Hephthalite</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἐφθαλίτης</i> (Ephthalítēs).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effect</li><li>impression, psychological impact</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) Gluskina effect</li></ol><p>Named after the Soviet linguist Sofya Gluskina.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Streisand effect (phenomenon)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterfly effect</li></ol><p>Calque of English <i>butterfly effect</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>efficiently, effectively (in an efficient manner)</li></ol><p><i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>effectiveness, efficiency (extent to which time is well used)</li><li>efficacy, efficacity</li></ol><p><i>эффекти́вный</i> (effektívnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective, efficacious</li><li>efficient</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>splendidly, impressively, dramatically, spectacularly</li><li>strikingly, impactfully</li></ol><p><i>эффе́ктный</i> (efféktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impressiveness, showiness, flamboyancy; showmanship</li></ol><p><i>эффе́ктный</i> (efféktnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressive, showy, spectacular</li><li>striking, impactful</li></ol><p><i>эффе́кт</i> (effékt) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>эфе́нди</i> (efɛ́ndi)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) an employee of the FSB</li></ol><p>From <i>ФСБ</i> (FSB) (phonetically: <i>эф-эс-бэ́</i> (ef-es-bɛ́)) + <i>-шник</i> (-šnik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alas!, oh!, uh</li><li>oh, what a...</li><li>tut tut, tsk, shame on you!, you of all people!</li><li>ouch, what a shame (self-reproach)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any plant of the genus <i>Echinodorus</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Echinodorus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) aechmea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Aechmea</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echo</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Эхо)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Echo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>Ἠχώ</i> (Ēkhṓ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echolalia</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echolocation</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>öçpoçmaq</i> (Tatar–Bashkir meat pastry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Tatar <i>өчпочмак</i> (öçpoçmaq, “triangle”, literally “three corners”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) scaffold (elevated platform on which a criminal is executed)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>échafaud</i>, ultimately from Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;catafalicum</i> (whence ultimately also <i>катафа́лк</i> (katafálk) (a doublet)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echelon</li><li>special train, troop train</li><li>(<i>aviation</i>) flight level</li></ol><p>Borrowed from French <i>échelon</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>let's get it</li><li>come on</li><li>an expression of joy, hooray</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>let's get it</i>, pronounced by the rapper "Lil Pump" as "esketit". Popularised by the rapper "FACE" since 2017.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ejaculate (semen)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ejaculate</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physiology</i>) ejaculation</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>ēiaculātiō</i>.</p><p>Compare natively formed Polish <i>wytrysk</i> (i.e. **вы́трыск).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>эфи́р</i> (efír).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ю</i>.</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ю)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>юг</i> (jug): south</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of <i>юг</i> (jug).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yuan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>圓</i>&nbsp;/ <i>圆</i> (yuán) or <i>元</i> (yuán).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Юань)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Yuan; the Yuan dynasty (Chinese dynasty that lasted from 1271–1368 <small>C.E.</small>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Chinese surname <i>元</i> (Yuán)</li><li>a surname, Yuan, from Chinese</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin <i>元</i> (Yuán).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yuan</li></ol><p>From <i>Юа́нь</i> (Juánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jubilee, anniversary</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Jubiläum</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anniversary, jubilee</li></ol><p><i>юбиле́й</i> (jubiléj) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>person whose anniversary/jubilee is celebrated</li></ol><p>From <i>юбиле́й</i> (jubiléj) +&lrm; <i>-яр</i> (-jar).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skirt (part of clothing, especially woman's)</li><li>(disparagingly,&#32;animate) woman (<i>as an object of a man's interest</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to Old French <i>jupe</i> and Old Italian <i>jupa</i>, compare French <i>jupe</i>. Ultimately from Arabic جُبَّة (jubba). Doublet of <i>жупа́н</i> (župán), <i>зипу́н</i> (zipún), and <i>шу́ба</i> (šúba). Also doublet of <i>дже́мпер</i> (džémper).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flared skirt, bell-bottomed skirt</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ю́бка</i> (júbka)</li></ol><p><i>ю́бка</i> (júbka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeweler, jeweller (a person whose job is making, repairing or selling jewelry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Juwelier</i> or Dutch <i>juwelier</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jewelry</li><li>jeweler's</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fine, minute, well-made, intricate</li></ol><p><i>ювели́р</i> (juvelír) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jeweller’s wife</li></ol><p>From <i>ювели́р</i> (juvelír) +&lrm; <i>-ша</i> (-ša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>south</li><li>South — southern part of any region</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) southerly wind</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as borrowing from Old Church Slavonic <i>югъ</i> (jugŭ) Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jugъ</i>, compare Old East Slavic <i>угъ</i> (ugŭ). However, self-innovation is also possible, compare the prosthetic /й/ in loanwords or in words that had no iotation in Church Slavonic: dialectal <i>ю́дега</i> (júdega) 'hoarfrost' from Finnish <i>huude</i>, use of a preposition <i>у</i> (u) like /ю/ in some northern Russian dialects, and dialectal <i>юрод</i> (jurod) in the presence of the Church Slavonic <i>Ѫродъ</i> (Ǫrodŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southeast</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southeast, southeastern, southeasterly</li></ol><p><i>ю́го-восто́к</i> (júgo-vostók) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southwest</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>southwest</li><li>southwesterly</li></ol><p><i>ю́го-за́пад</i> (júgo-západ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yugoslav</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yugoslavian, Yugoslav</li></ol><p><i>Югосла́вия</i> (Jugoslávija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yugur (person)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yugur</li></ol><p>From <i>югу́р</i> (jugúr) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>юг</i> (jug).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rime, frost (<i>dialectal</i>)</li></ol><p>From a finnic language, ultimately from Proto-Finnic <i>&#42;huudëh</i>. Compare Finnish <i>huude</i>, Veps <i>hudeg</i>, Ludian <i>huudeh</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Any longhorn beetle of the genus <i>Judolia</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from translingual <i>Judolia</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) valley</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) vale of tears</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) destiny, lot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѫдоль</i> (ǫdolĭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫdolь</i>. Doublet of <i>удо́ль</i> (udólʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anti-Semite</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>юдофо́бия</i> (judofóbija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) anti-Semitism (prejudice or hostility against Jews)</li></ol><p>From German <i>Jude</i> (“Jew”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-фо́бия</i> (-fóbija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southerner</li></ol><p><i>юг</i> (jug, “south”) +&lrm; <i>-а́нин</i> (-ánin).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ю́жный</i> (júžnyj)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Southern Altai</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>South American</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>South African</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Natal red rock hare (<i>Pronolagus crassicaudatus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>южноафрика́нский</i> (južnoafrikánskij, “South African”) +&lrm; <i>кро́лик</i> (królik, “rabbit”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Korean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>South Korean</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>South Ossetian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) South Slavic (relating to the South Slavs)</li></ol><p><i>ю́жный</i> (júžnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>славя́нский</i> (slavjánskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>South Sudanese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>South Sudanese</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>south, southern</li><li>southerly</li></ol><p><i>юг</i> (jug) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skid, skidding, scuffing</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>youth&#32;slang</i>) to use</li></ol><p>Derived from English <i>use</i> + <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(computing,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) luser (a user, especially an incompetent one)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>ю́зер</i> (júzɛr, “user”) +&lrm; <i>зверь</i> (zverʹ, “brute, beast”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Internet slang</i>) user (person who uses a computer)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>user</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Wikimedia jargon</i>) userbox</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>userbox</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Internet,&#32;uncommon) user pic, avatar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>user pic</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yukata</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>浴衣<rp>(</rp><rt>ゆかた</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>yukata</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yukola</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yukhoe (a Korean dish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Korean <i>육회</i> (yukhoe).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinning top (toy)</li><li>woodlark</li></ol><p>Unknown. Theories include:</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Julian</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to bustle, to fuss <i>(about)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to play up <i>(to)</i></li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to wriggle</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>юла́</i> (julá) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humour (something funny)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>humour</i>, further from Latin <i>hūmor</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humoresque</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humorist</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Humorist</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humorous, comic</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>humoristisch</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabin boy, ship's boy (a boy serving on a ship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Junge</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) youth, young boy, lad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;junь̀cь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sports</i>) youth sports participant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>junior</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) junker (landed aristocracy in Prussia)</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) cadet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Junker</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth (<i>age</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;junostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ю́ный</i> (júnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youngster, male youth</li><li>young man</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>юноша</i> (junoša), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;junoša</i>. Compare Old East Slavic <i>уноша</i> (unoša).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>youthful, young</li><li>teenage, teen</li><li>adolescent (characteristic of adolescence)</li><li>junior</li><li>juvenile</li></ol><p><i>ю́ноша</i> (júnoša) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) youths, young adults</li><li>youth, adolescence</li></ol><p><i>ю́ноша</i> (júnoša) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young, youthful (in the early part of life or growth)</li><li>juvenile</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>юнъ</i> (junŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;junъ</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;jauˀnás</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂yuh₁en-</i>. Displaced the inherited Old East Slavic <i>унъ</i> (unŭ), <i>оунъ</i> (unŭ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yunnan</li></ol><p>From <i>Юньна́нь</i> (Junʹnánʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cinematography</i>) projector, a powerful electric arc lamp used in motion-picture filming.</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Юпитер)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Jupiter</li><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Jupiter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttermilk</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anime,&#32;pornography) yuri</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>百合<rp>(</rp><rt>ゆり</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>yuri</i>, “lily”), by analogy to <ruby>薔薇<rp>(</rp><rt>バラ</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>bara</i>, “rose”), indicating love.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>legally, juridically, de jure</li></ol><p><i>юриди́ческий</i> (juridíčeskij) +&lrm; <i>-и</i> (-i)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) law; legal</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>iūridicus</i> +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij)。</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) legal person, legal entity</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judicial cognizance, competence, jurisdiction</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) jurisprudence</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Jurisprudenz</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawyer</li><li>advocate</li></ol><p>First attested in 1704. Borrowed from German <i>Jurist</i>, from Medieval Latin <i>iūrista</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agile, nimble</li><li>quick, brisk (<i>of a vehicle</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>юр</i> (jur, “busy place, market square; whirlpool, strong current”) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whisk, to flit</li></ol><p>From <i>ю́ркий</i> (júrkij) +&lrm; <i>-ну́ть</i> (-nútʹ) or <i>ю́ркий</i> (júrkij) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eccentric, mad, whacky, nutty, mental; crazy, deranged, lunatic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy fool, fool for Christ, yurodivy</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>юродивъ</i> (jurodivŭ), iotacism of earlier <i>уродивъ</i> (urodivŭ) (since 14th century). More at <i>урод</i> (urod).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) yurt (natural resources and arable land over which a Cossack stanitsa had exclusive control in their territory)</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, in the sense of referring to one's native land, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yūrt</i> (“dwelling place, camping site”). Doublet of <i>ю́рта</i> (júrta).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yurt</li></ol><p>From a Turkic language, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yūrt</i> (“dwelling place, camping site”). Doublet of <i>юрт</i> (jurt).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yus, either of two letters, little yus (Ѧ) and big yus (Ѫ), representing nasal vowel sounds in the old Cyrillic alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>ѫсъ</i> (ǫsŭ, “big yus”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adjustment (small change)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) justice</li></ol></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Юстиция)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Roman mythology</i>) Justitia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>iustitia</i> (“Justice”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jute</li><li>quarter-deck</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dutch <i>hut</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to huddle, to huddle together, to take shelter</li></ol><p>A connection with Latvian <i>jumts</i> (“roof”), <i>jùmt</i> (“to thatch”) is one possibility. This etymology conflicts with the doctrine that *<i>jaN</i> became *<i>ję</i> in Proto-Slavic.</p><p>ESSJa reconstructs *<i>jutiti sę</i> instead, allegedly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yewH-</i> (“to blend, mix”) with a t-extension.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>YouTuber (YouTube video creator)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>YouTuber</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yuft, Russia leather</li></ol><p>Unknown, but suspected to be a Turkic borrowing of Classical Persian جفت (juft, “pair”) due to the hides having been tanned pairwise.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broth <i>(the liquid part of soup in which ingredients have been boiled)</i></li><li>(<i>low colloquial</i>) blood</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Я</i>.</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I (first-person singular subject pronoun)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗзъ</i> (jazŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;(j)azъ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;éǵh₂</i>. Doublet of <i>эго</i> (ego).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine,&#32;animate) tattletale</li><li>(feminine,&#32;inanimate,&#32;obsolete) slander, snitching</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѩбедьникъ</i> (jębedĭnikŭ), <i>ѧбетьникъ</i> (jębetĭnikŭ), borrowed from Old Norse <i>embætti</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ambahtiją</i>, derived from <i>&#42;ambahtaz</i> (“servant”), from Gaulish <i>ambaxtos</i> (“servant”), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;ambaxtos</i> (“servant”).</p><p>Compare Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌱𐌰𐌷𐍄𐌹</i> (andbahti, “service, office”) and Old High German <i>ambahti</i> (“position, office”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) to snitch, to inform [<i>with</i> <b>на</b> (na, + accusative) <small>‘on someone’</small>] (<i>especially of children></i>)</li></ol><p><i>я́беда</i> (jábeda) +&lrm; <i>-ничать</i> (-ničatʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple</li><li>eyeball (<i>usually глазно́е я́блоко</i>)</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ꙗблъко</i> (jablŭko), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;àblъko</i>, diminutive of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ablo</i>, equivalent to the non-existent <i>**ябло</i> + <i> -ко</i>. Cognate with Polish <i>jabłko</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple of discord (object which sows dissention and anger)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) apple tree</li></ol><p><i>я́блоня</i> (jáblonja) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) synonym of <i>я́блоневый</i> (jáblonevyj, “pertaining to apple trees”)</li><li>Used in the names of pests that attack apple trees.</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ꙗблоньнꙑи</i> (jablonĭnyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ablonьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́блоня</i> (jáblonja) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ablonь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li><li>apple wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;abloňa</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́блонь</i> (jáblonʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я</i> (-ja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>я́блоко</i> (jábloko): (small) apple</li><li>bullseye</li><li>(<i>dance</i>) yablochko (<i>a Russian folk-dance</i>)</li></ol><p><i>я́блоко</i> (jábloko) +&lrm; <i>-ко</i> (-ko)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>organic chemistry</i>) malic acid</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) apple grower or seller</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) member of an organization or political party using an apple as a symbol</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) insect that feeds exclusively on apples</li><li><i>senses related to Apple Inc.:</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(neologism,&#32;computing,&#32;slang,&#32;animate) avid user of Apple products</li><li>users of Apple's products in general</li></ol><li>(cooking,&#32;inanimate) casserole or cake containing apples</li></ol><p><i>я́блоко</i> (jábloko) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apple-green</li></ol><p><i>я́блочный</i> (jábločnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о-зелёный</i> (-o-zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) apple; made of apples</li></ol><p>From Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ablъčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́блоко</i> (jábloko) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple juice</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(literary,&#32;poetic) to show (to have someone see something)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗвити</i> (javiti), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aviti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew-</i> (“to perceive, see, be aware of”).</p><p>Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;awiz</i> (“obvious”) and Sanskrit <i>आविस्</i> (āvís, “manifestly, evidently”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report (in person), to appear</li><li>to turn up</li></ol><p><i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presence, attendance</li><li>secret address</li><li>turnout</li></ol><p><i>яви́ть(ся)</i> (javítʹ(sja)) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phenomenon</li><li>event, occurrence</li><li>apparition</li><li>scene (in terms of theaters)</li><li>effect</li></ol><p><i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-е́ние</i> (-énije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to show; to display; to present</li></ol><p><i>яви́ть</i> (javítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-я́ть</i> (-játʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to report (in person), to appear</li><li>to turn up</li><li>(<i>imperfective only</i>) to be [<i>with</i> instrumental <small>‘something’</small>] (<i>often used in literary contexts to emphasise being an example of something or holding a certain status</i>)</li></ol><p><i>явля́ть</i> (javljátʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, evidently, obviously</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) explicitly</li></ol><p><i>я́вный</i> (jávnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>open, obvious, evident</li><li>avowed</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) explicit</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;avьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>явь</i> (javʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycamore maple (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;avorъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, distinctly, perceptibly</li></ol><p><i>я́вственный</i> (jávstvennyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear</li><li>distinct, perceptible</li></ol><p>From <i>я́вств-</i> (jávstv-) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj); constructed as if from a nonexistent &#42;я́вство.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literary</i>) to be obvious, to be clear</li></ol><p>From <i>я́вств(енный)</i> (jávstv(ennyj)) +&lrm; <i>-овать</i> (-ovatʹ) or <i>явь</i> (javʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ствовать</i> (-stvovatʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;avь</i> (“obvious, apparent, real”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew-</i> (“apparent”). Cognates include Polish <i>jawa</i>, Ukrainian <i>я́ва</i> (jáva), <i>ява́</i> (javá) or <i>яв</i> (jav).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cladonia rangiferina</i>, reindeer moss, reindeer lichen, caribou moss</li></ol><p>From а Sami language descended from Proto-Samic <i>&#42;jeakēl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lamb (to give birth to a lamb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamb (flesh of a lamb used as food)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agnętina</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ягня́та</i> (jagnjáta, “lambs”) +&lrm; <i>-ина</i> (-ina, suffix denoting a type of meat).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamb</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>агнѧ</i> (agnę), <i>ꙗгнѧ</i> (jagnę) + <i>-ёнок</i> (-jónok), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agnę</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʷn-</i>, the stem of <i>&#42;h₂egʷnós</i>. Compare <i>а́гнец</i> (ágnec).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>berry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗгода</i> (jagoda), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agoda</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ṓˀgāˀ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) buttock</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) cheekbone</li><li>(<i>dialectal</i>) nipple</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agodica</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́года</i> (jágoda, “berry”) +&lrm; <i>-ица</i> (-ica), because of the round shape of buttocks: compare the dialectal senses.</p><p>Compare typologically <i>ты́ква</i> (týkva), <i>ты́ковка</i> (týkovka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) natal cleft (groove between buttocks)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gluteal sulcus, gluteal furrow, sulcus glutealis</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gluteal, pygal</li></ol><p><i>я́годица</i> (jágodica) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>я́года</i> (jágoda); berry</li></ol><p><i>я́года</i> (jágoda) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) berry; made of berries</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;agodьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́года</i> (jágoda) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaguar (<i>Panthera onca</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison, venom</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something or someone that has a harmful effect on someone</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗдъ</i> (jadŭ) / ѣдъ (jědŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ědъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear winter</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear weapon</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nuclear</li></ol><p>From <i>ядро́</i> (jadró, “nucleus”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj). Compare <i>ядрёный</i> (jadrjónyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear reactor</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuclear physicist</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poisonousness, toxicity, venomousness</li></ol><p><i>ядови́тый</i> (jadovítyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonous, venomous, noxious</li></ol><p><i>яд</i> (jad) +&lrm; <i>-ови́тый</i> (-ovítyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) seed, stone (<i>of a fruit</i>)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) cannonball, round shot</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) nucleus (a vowel or syllabic consonant in a syllable)</li><li>(<i>cytology</i>) nucleus (an organelle which contains genetic material)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) nucleus (cluster of nucleons)</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) nucleus (cluster of neurons)</li><li>(astronomy,&#32;geology) core (<i>of a planet, star, or galaxy</i>)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) nucleus (<i>of a comet</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) core (<i>of a processor</i>)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) kernel (<i>of an operating system</i>)</li><li>(<i>physics</i>) cone (<i>of a flame</i>)</li><li>(<i>athletics</i>) shot (heavy iron ball)</li><li>(<i>sociology</i>) core members, activists (<i>in a group</i>)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) core (set of allocations that cannot be improved)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) nullspace, kernel (set of all elements mapped to the zero element by a transformation)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) kernel (a function used to define an integral transform)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) duramen, heartwood (the hard older inner layers of the wood of a tree)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ędro</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ядро́</i> (jadró); a (small) seed, stone, cannonball, shot, nucleus, core, or kernel</li><li>(<i>cytology</i>) nucleolus</li></ol><p><i>ядро́</i> (jadró) +&lrm; <i>-ышко</i> (-yško)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a large kernel (e.g. of nuts)</li><li>juicy, succulent (e.g. of fruit)</li><li>hearty (e.g. of cabbages)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) vigorous; healthy; stalwart</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fresh; bracing</li></ol><p>From <i>ядро́</i> (jadró) +&lrm; <i>-ёный</i> (-jónyj). Compare <i>я́дерный</i> (jádernyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) a mother of the year (a mother who raises her children badly, e.g. by failing to discipline them or endangering their health)</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) an entitled mother (a mother who uses motherhood as an excuse to break rules or seek privileges)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>я</i> (ja) +&lrm; <i>же</i> (že) +&lrm; <i>мать</i> (matʹ) (“I'm a mother, aren't I?”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) weir (wicker fence across a river for catching fish)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ězъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ulcer, sore</li><li>sore, evil</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) pest, viper <i>(malicious person)</i></li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>ꙗзва</i> (jazva), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jazva</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;áiˀźwāˀ</i>. Cognate to Old Prussian <i>eyswo</i> (“wound”), Lithuanian <i>áiža</i>, Latvian <i>aĩza</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sarcastically, caustically</li></ol><p><i>язви́тельный</i> (jazvítelʹnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>caustic, biting, mordant, sarcastic, acrimonious</li></ol><p><i>язви́ть</i> (jazvítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-тельный</i> (-telʹnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite, to sting, to wound</li><li>to taunt, to mock</li></ol><p>Related to <i>я́зва</i> (jázva, “ulcer, sore”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>язь</i> (jazʹ)</li></ol><p>From <i>язь</i> (jazʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) tongue</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) language</li><li>(animate,&#32;military,&#32;slang) prisoner for interrogation, canary, informer</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) clapper (of a bell)</li><li>(animate,&#32;Middle Russian,&#32;obsolete) interpreter</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѧзꙑкъ</i> (językŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ęzykъ</i>, from Early Proto-Slavic <i>*inzū́kus</i>, Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;inźūˀkas</i>, from earlier <i>*dinźūˀ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dn̥ǵʰwéh₂s</i>, whence English <i>tongue</i> and Latin <i>lingua</i>, whence <i>language</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly language (programming language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>programming language</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-tongued (<i>of a person</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-а́стый</i> (-ástyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) sharp-tongued</li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-а́тый</i> (-átyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language family (set of languages)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language school (a school where foreign languages are taught)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguist (one who studies linguistics)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>языкове́дение</i> (jazykovédenije).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguistics</li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>linguistic</li></ol><p>From <i>языкове́д</i> (jazykovéd) +&lrm; <i>-ческий</i> (-českij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tongue</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) language; linguistic</li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language barrier (barrier to communication)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language code (a standard identifier of a language)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>language exchange</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linguistics</li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk, “language”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зна́ние</i> (znánije, “knowledge”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pagan, heathen</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗзꙑческъ</i> (jazyčeskŭ). By surface analysis, <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij). Calque of Ancient Greek <i>ἐθνικός</i> (ethnikós, “of or for a nation, heathen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paganism (local religions practiced before the introduction of Christianity)</li></ol><p><i>язы́ч(ник)</i> (jazýč(nik)) +&lrm; <i>-ество</i> (-estvo)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pagan, heathen (not adhering to an Abrahamic religion)</li><li>polytheist</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѩзꙑчьникъ</i> (języčĭnikŭ), from <i>ѩзꙑкъ</i> (językŭ, “tongue; nation”). Modeled on Ancient Greek <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos, “nation”) : <i>ἐθνικός</i> (ethnikós, “heathen”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) tongue; lingual</li><li>(linguistics,&#32;phonetics) lingual (articulated with the tongue)</li><li>(relational,&#32;dated) language; linguistic</li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>язы́к</i> (jazýk): tongue</li><li>uvula</li><li>reed <i>musical</i></li><li>catch, lug <i>technical</i></li></ol><p><i>язы́к</i> (jazýk) +&lrm; <i>-о́к</i> (-ók)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ide (fish)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;azь</i> or <i>&#42;jazь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>яйцо</i> (jajco), small egg</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) testicle, testis, didymus</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ovary (animal)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ajьčьnikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omelette or otherwise fried egg</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ājьčьnica</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) egg</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ājьčьnъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>egglike, egg-shaped</li></ol><p><i>яйц(о́)</i> (jajc(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>вид</i> (vid) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) oviduct</li></ol><p>From <i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>-вод</i> (-vod), combining form of <i>води́ть</i> (vodítʹ, “to lead”); calque of Latin <i>oviductus</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oviparous</li><li>monotreme</li></ol><p><i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>кладу́щий</i> (kladúščij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cytology</i>) ovum, egg</li><li>ovule</li></ol><p><i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>кле́тка</i> (klétka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oviparous, monotreme</li></ol><p><i>яйцо́</i> (jajcó) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>носи́ть</i> (nosítʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>egglike, egg-shaped</li></ol><p><i>яйц(о́)</i> (jajc(ó)) +&lrm; <i>-е-</i> (-e-) +&lrm; <i>о́браз</i> (óbraz) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg</li><li>ovum</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) testicle, ball, nut, nad, gonad</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗице</i> (jaice), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ājьcè</i>, diminutive of <i>&#42;ȃje</i> (“egg”), ultimately from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ṓˀjan</i> (“egg”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ōwyóm</i> (“egg”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard-boiled egg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft-boiled egg</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yak</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Tibetan <i>གཡག</i> (g.yag), from Proto-Sino-Tibetan <i>&#42;g-jak ~ g-jaŋ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) the pronunciation of unstressed /e/ or /a/ after a palatalised consonant before the stressed syllable as /a/ rather than /i/, e.g. <i>несли́</i> (neslí) is pronounced [nʲas⁽ʲ⁾ˈlʲi], not [nʲɪs⁽ʲ⁾ˈlʲi]. This occurs in some Russian dialects (only where <i>а́канье</i> (ákanʹje) is also present) and in standard Belarusian.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) to pronounce an unstressed /e/ or /a/ after a palatalised consonant before the stressed syllable as /a/ rather than /i/</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to use too much the pronoun <i>я</i> (ja, “I, me”); to talk too much about oneself</li></ol><p><i>я</i> (ja) +&lrm; <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) like, as</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) for, because</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jako</i>. Compare Ukrainian <i>як</i> (jak), Belarusian <i>як</i> (jak), and Polish <i>jak</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>allegedly, supposedly</li></ol><p><i>я́ко</i> (jáko) +&lrm; <i>бы</i> (by)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) anchor</li></ol><p><i>я́корь</i> (jákorʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchor</li><li><i>(electrical)</i> armature core, armature</li><li><i>(acoustic)</i> reed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ѧкорь</i> (jękorĭ), from Latin <i>ancora</i> (“anchor”) via Old Swedish <i>ankari</i> (> Swedish <i>ankare</i>), Old Norse <i>akkeri</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>ἄγκυρα</i> (ánkura, “anchor; hook”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yakuza (Japanese organized crime gang)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>やくざ</i> (yakuza).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yakut (person)</li></ol><p>According to Kulakovskiĭ, from Evenki <i>екэ</i> (jekə), but the Russian word was probably corrupted from the form of another Tungusic language. Possibly ultimately related to Turkish <i>yaka</i> (“collar, edge, border”), referring to the Yakuts' location in Siberia.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Yakut</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yakut (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>яку́т</i> (jakút) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) synonym of <i>обща́ться</i> (obščátʹsja, “to converse”)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;yakšï</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dinghy, yawl, skiff (small boat)</li></ol><p>From <i>ял</i> (jal) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jalovьcь</i> (whence also Czech <i>jalovec</i>), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jalovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barren (of animals)</li><li>made of the hide of a young calf</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗловыи</i> (jalovyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jalovъ</i> (“barren, infertile”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yalta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) mail staging post</li></ol><p>From Turkic, ultimately from Chinese or Mongolian. There is no scholarly consensus regarding the direction of borrowing. Generally, it is believed that Turkic <i>yam</i> and Chinese <i>站</i> (zhàn) are loanwords from Mongolian ǰam, however, some (e.g. Tuymebayev in <i>Казахско-монгольские лексические параллели</i> (Kazaxsko-mongolʹskije leksičeskije paralleli)) believe the directionality is reversed (i.e. Chinese "to stand &gt; stand &gt; station" → Middle Mongol → Turkic → Russian). Whatever the etymology, what is apparent is that the word jam has been around for a long time and was used by Central Asians to designate a key postal relay station or official. Compare Mongolian <i>зам</i> (zam).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit, hole (in the ground)</li><li>dungeon</li><li>(<i>yoga</i>) yama</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗма</i> (jama), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jama</i>.</p><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>यम</i> (yama).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Яма)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yama (a river in Saratov Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Yama (a river in Magadan Oblast, Russia)</li><li>Yama (the previous name of the city of Siversk, Donetsk Oblast, Ukraine)</li><li>Yama (the previous name of the town of Kingisepp, Leningrad Oblast, Russia)</li><li>(<i>Buddhism</i>) Yama (deity in Hinduism, Buddhism)</li></ol><p>From <i>я́ма</i> (jáma, “pit”).</p><p>Short for <i>Я́мбург</i> (Jámburg).</p><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>यम</i> (yama).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Jamaican</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetry</i>) iamb</li><li>(<i>regional</i>) sycee (gold or silver ingot used as currency in imperial China)</li></ol><p>Ultimately borrowed from Ancient Greek <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos).</p><p>Borrowed from Chinese <i>元寶</i>&nbsp;/ <i>元宝</i> (yuánbǎo).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ямб</i> (jamb).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>я́ма</i> (jáma): (small) hole, pit, socket; fossa</li></ol><p><i>я́ма</i> (jáma) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>я́мка</i> (jámka), diminutive of <i>я́ма</i> (jáma): (small) hole, pit, socket; dimple</li></ol><p><i>я́мка</i> (jámka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(relational,&#32;historical) postal horses, carriage</li></ol><p>From <i>ям</i> (jam) +&lrm; <i>-ско́й</i> (-skój).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yum cha (yamcha)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Cantonese 飲茶／饮茶 (<i>jam2 caa4</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driver, coachman, charioteer, cabman, postilion engaged in transporting people and cargo by horse and carriage</li><li>Eurasian nuthatch (<i>Sitta europaea</i>)</li><li>(<i>criminal jargon</i>) fence; brothel keeper</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ямьщикъ</i> (jamĭščikŭ), from Turkic. Compare Tatar <i>ямчы</i> (yamçı), Turkish <i>yamçı</i>. Ultimately from Middle Mongol <i>ᠵᠠᠮᠴᠢ</i> (ǰamči). Ending modified under the influence of <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yamstchik, cabman</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yang</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Mandarin 陽／阳 (<i>yáng</i>).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ян)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Yan, equivalent to English John: (<i>perceived as a West Slavic name</i>)</li><li>a transliteration of the Mandarin surname <i>楊</i>&nbsp;/ <i>杨</i> (Yáng)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Ἰωάννης</i> (Iōánnēs).</p><p>From Chinese, 楊／杨 (<i>Yáng</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>янва́рь</i> (janvárʹ) or <i>янва́ря</i> (janvárja, “January”)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) January</li></ol><p><i>янва́рь</i> (janvárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>January</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>генварь</i> (genvarĭ), <i>генуарии</i> (genuarii), from Byzantine Greek <i>Γενάρης</i> (Genárēs), <i>Ἰανουάριος</i> (Ianouários), from Latin <i>Iānuārius</i>, from Latin <i>Iānus</i> (“god in Rome mythology”), from (perhaps) Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ei-</i> (“to go”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Yankee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>Yankee</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) amber</li><li>amber (<i>colour/color</i>)</li></ol><p><i>янта́рь</i> (jantárʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber (fossil resin)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Middle Russian <i>ꙗнтарь</i> (jantarʹ), from earlier <i>онтарь</i> (ontarʹ), <i>оньтарь</i> (onʹtarʹ). Borrowed from Lithuanian, dialectal <i>jentaras</i>, <i>gẽntaras</i>, literary <i>giñtaras</i>, <i>gintãras</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>janissary (former Turkish soldier)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یڭیچری (<i>yeniçeri</i>), from <i>یڭی</i> (<i>yeni</i>, “new”) + چری (<i>çeri</i>, “soldier”). Per Vasmer, presence of -ы- points to a borrowing through Ukrainian <i>янича́р</i> (janyčár).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yaoi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <i>やおい</i> (yaoi).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fan of yaoi</li></ol><p>From <i>яо́й</i> (jaój) +&lrm; <i>-щик</i> (-ščik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male Japanese person</li></ol><p><i>Япо́ния</i> (Japónija) +&lrm; <i>-ец</i> (-ec)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Japanese studies</li></ol><p><i>Япо́ния</i> (Japónija) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ве́дение</i> (védenije)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Japanese</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Japanese language</li></ol><p><i>Япо́ния</i> (Japónija) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) <i>Taxus cuspidata</i>, Japanese yew</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) the Japanese people</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;animate,&#32;ethnic slur) a Japanese person, a Jap, a Nip</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory,&#32;inanimate) anything from Japan, especially Japanese cars, or Japanese fast food restaurants</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steep bank, ravine</li></ol><p>From Turkic. Per Chernykh akin to <i>я́рус</i> (járus).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yaranga (a rounded or conical tent of reindeer hide, the traditional mobile home of some nomadic indigenous peoples of Russia.)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Chukchi <i>яраӈы</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yarborough</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>yarborough</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yard (three feet)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enrage</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jariti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́рый</i> (járyj) +&lrm; <i>-и́ть</i> (-ítʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chilver (a female lamb)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, brilliant, emitting or reflecting a large amount of light</li><li>(<i>of color</i>) bright, full, saturated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of feelings or thaughts</i>) vivid, memorizable</li></ol><li>gaudy, colorful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗръкꙑи</i> (jarŭkyi), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarъkъ</i>, from <i>&#42;jaro</i>, <i>&#42;jarъ</i> + <i>&#42;-ъkъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>brightly</li></ol><p><i>я́ркий</i> (járkij) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright green</li></ol><p><i>я́ркий</i> (járkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>зелёный</i> (zeljónyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright red, ruddy, vermilion</li></ol><p><i>я́ркий</i> (járkij) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>кра́сный</i> (krásnyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness</li></ol><p><i>я́ркий</i> (járkij, “bright”) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ, “-ness, -ity”)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jarl</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>jarl</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) yarligh (a written decree in the Mongol Empire and its successor states)</li><li>label, tag</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) label, pigeonhole (superficial, stereotypical classification of something or someone)</li><li>(<i>computing</i>) shortcut, quick-access button</li></ol><p>From Middle Mongol <i>ᠵᠠᠷᠯᠢᠭ</i> (ǰarlig).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) fair, market</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Jahrmarkt</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fair (market), funfair, county fair, state fair</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Polish <i>jarmark</i>, from German <i>Jahrmarkt</i> (“fair”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fair (gathering for trade and entertainment), fairground</li></ol><p><i>я́рмарка</i> (jármarka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke (wooden bar)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) burden</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗрьмъ</i> (jarĭmŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;arьmo</i>, <i>&#42;arьmъ</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;agriculture) to vernalize</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) spring (<i>of crops</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarovъ</i>, from <i>&#42;jaro</i> (“spring”). Constructed as if from <i>&#42;яр</i> (&#42;jar) +&lrm; <i>-ово́й</i> (-ovój); compare dialectal <i>яр</i> (jar, “heat, fire”), Old East Slavic <i>ꙗра</i> (jara, “spring”). First attested in the 14th century.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) Yaroslavl</li></ol><p><i>Яросла́в(ль)</i> (Jarosláv(lʹ)) +&lrm; <i>-ский</i> (-skij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>furiously</li><li>violently</li></ol><p><i>я́ростный</i> (járostnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>furious, fierce, violent, frantic</li></ol><p><i>я́рость</i> (járostʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fury, rage, frenzy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarostь</i> (“fury”) (compare Serbo-Croatian <i>járȏst</i>), from <i>&#42;jarъ</i> (“furious”) + <i>&#42;-ostь</i> (abstract nominalizer).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tier (horizontal row)</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) circle (curved upper tier of seats in a theater)</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) geologic stage</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(slang,&#32;humorous,&#32;derogatory) a Russian ultranationalist.</li></ol><p><i>я</i> (ja) +&lrm; <i>ру́сский</i> (rússkij), literally meaning "I am a Russian". Popularised to mock ultranationalists wearing this motto on their shirts as a slogan. Also influenced by a song composed by the Russian nationalist singer Shaman that was mocked as an Internet meme.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>я́ркий</i> (járkij): brightest</li></ol><p><i>я́рче</i> (járče) +&lrm; <i>-а́йший</i> (-ájšij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>я́рко</i> (járko)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>я́ркий</i> (járkij)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ardent</li><li>violent, furious, vehement</li><li>(relational,&#32;obsolete) spring, summer</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) this year's</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarъ</i> (“furious”).</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarъ</i> (“spring”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) yasak</li><li>(<i>archaic</i>) conventional sign, signal</li></ol><p>From Turkic, from Middle Mongol <i>ᠵᠠᠰᠠᠭ</i> (ǰasag). Compare Tatar <i>ясак</i> (yasaq), Turkish <i>yasak</i>. Related to <i>есау́л</i> (jesaúl).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>of course</li></ol><p>Literally, “clear stump”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ash (tree or wood)</li></ol><p><i>я́сень</i> (jásenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-евый</i> (-evyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used in one of the common names of Acer negundo (boxelder)</li></ol><p><i>я́сень</i> (jásenʹ) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>лист</i> (list) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ash (tree or wood)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;asenь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse-ear chickweed (<i>Cerastium</i> gen. et spp.)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nursery, crèche</li><li>crib</li><li>manger</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗсли</i> (jasli), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jasli</i>, from earlier Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ědsli-</i>, from Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ēd-</i>. Related to Russian <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”), Russian <i>еда́</i> (jedá, “food”), Russian <i>яд</i> (jad), Russian <i>я́ство</i> (jástvo), Russian <i>я́стель</i> (jástelʹ, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become clear</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěsněti</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-е́ть</i> (-étʹ); compare with Czech <i>jasnit</i> and Polish <i>jaśnieć</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly</li><li>brightly</li><li>finely</li><li>limpidly</li><li>distinctly, evidently</li></ol><p><i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-о</i> (-o)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clairvoyant</li></ol><p>From <i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj, “clear”) +&lrm; <i>-о-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>ви́дящий</i> (vídjaščij, “seeing”), calque of French <i>clairvoyant</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>obviously, of course, clearly, understandably, needless to say, unsurprisingly</li></ol><p>Literally, “(it's a) clear affair/matter”.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarity, lucidity</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěsnostь</i>. By surface analysis, <i>я́сный</i> (jásnyj) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) The Lamiales.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear</li><li>bright</li><li>fine</li><li>limpid</li><li>discrete</li><li>evident</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ěsnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic</i>) viands, victuals</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;ironic</i>) viand (a choice dish)</li></ol><p>Related to Russian <i>есть</i> (jestʹ, “to eat”), Russian <i>еда́</i> (jedá, “food”), Russian <i>яд</i> (jad), Russian <i>я́сли</i> (jásli).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawk (a predatory bird)</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) hawk, warmonger</li><li>Yastreb (Soviet space suit)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;àstrę̄bъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) hawk</li><li>hawklike</li></ol><p><i>я́стреб</i> (jástreb) +&lrm; <i>-и́ный</i> (-ínyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a prisoner taken by Turks or Crimean Tatars (or, rarely, Cossacks during the colonization of Siberia) after a raid (from the fifteenth up to the eighteenth century)</li></ol><p>Via Crimean Tatar <i>esir</i> (“captive, prisoner”) or Ottoman Turkish اسیر (<i>esir</i>, “captive, prisoner”) from Arabic أَسِير (ʔasīr, “captive, prisoner”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yataghan (type of sword)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یتاغان (yatağan).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(regional,&#32;dated) sister-in-law (husband's brother's wife)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ętry</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) iatrogenesis (adverse effect caused by medical activity)</li><li>(<i>dated</i>) disease caused by the patient's misunderstanding of doctor's advice</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἰατρός</i> (iatrós, “doctor”) + <i>γενεά</i> (geneá, “generation; birth”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) orchid</li></ol><p>From <i>ятры́шник</i> (jatrýšnik) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The archaic letter yat (Cyrillic capital Ѣ, Cyrillic small ѣ, Glagolitic ⱑ).</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to take</li><li>(<i>obsolete</i>) to acquire, to receive</li></ol><p>&nbsp;<b>ять</b> on Russian&nbsp;Wikipedia</p><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ęti</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) ruby; sapphire</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>ὑάκινθος</i> (huákinthos). Doublet of <i>гиаци́нт</i> (giacínt).</p><p>Compare typologically <i>руби́н</i> (rubín) (&lt; Latin <i>rubīnus</i> &lt; <i>rubeus</i> <&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (whence <i>ры́жий</i> (rýžij), <i>ру́дый</i> (rúdyj), <i>ру́сый</i> (rúsyj))).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) ruby or sapphire</li><li>ruby- or sapphire-coloured/colored</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;poetic) dear, precious (<i>as a term of endearment</i>)</li></ol><p><i>я́хонт</i> (jáxont) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yacht (slick and light ship)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from German <i>Jacht</i> or Dutch <i>jacht</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yacht</li></ol><p><i>я́хт(а)</i> (jáxt(a)) +&lrm; <i>-енный</i> (-ennyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yacht</li></ol><p>From <i>я́хт(а)</i> (jáxt(a)) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) yacht</li></ol><p><i>я́хт(а)</i> (jáxt(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yachter, yachtsman (one who sails a yacht)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from English <i>yachtsman</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cell, spot, mesh, nucleus; compartment, cubicle</li></ol><p>From now obsolete <i>ячея́</i> (jačejá) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka). Per Vasmer, possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ęčаjа</i>. Doublet of <i>вечея́</i> (večejá, “hole in a millstone”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archaic form of <i>яче́йка</i> (jačéjka)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley (annual temperate grasses of the species <i>Hordeum vulgare</i> or its grain used in making food and beverages)</li><li>stye (bacterial infection of the eyelash or eyelid)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>ꙗчьмꙑ</i> (jačĭmy), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ęčьmy</i>. Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἀκοστή</i> (akostḗ, “barley”), Latin <i>acus</i> (“needle”) and Old English <i>ahar</i> (“corn-ear”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jasper (<i>a precious stone</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) jasper (a precious stone)</li></ol><p><i>я́шм(а)</i> (jášm(a)) +&lrm; <i>-овый</i> (-ovyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dated</i>) a saurian</li><li>(<i>dated&#32;or&#32;colloquial</i>) an ancient reptile, a dinosaur</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pangolin</li><li>a variety of khorovod (traditional Slavic circle dance) in which a circle of young women dance around a young man</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aščerъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lizard</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aščerica</i>. Most likely related to Russian <i>ско́рый</i> (skóryj, “fast, quick, rapid, speedy, swift”).</p></html>

<h3>* * *</h3><html><h3>Russian (Capitalized: Ящерица)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Lacerta</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial&#32;or&#32;regional</i>) lizard (reptile)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;aščerъka</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, case, chest</li><li>drawer (<i>in a piece of furniture</i>)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;derogatory) idiot box, boob tube (TV set)</li><li>(colloquial,&#32;dated) secret facility, military facility</li><li>(computing,&#32;colloquial) ellipsis of <i>электро́нный почто́вый <b>я́щик</b></i> (elektrónnyj počtóvyj <b>jáščik</b>); (email) inbox</li></ol><p>Inherited from Old East Slavic <i>аскъ</i> (askŭ) or <i>ꙗскъ</i> (jaskŭ), from Old Norse <i>askr</i> (“wooden vessel”) or <i>eski</i> (“ashen box”). See <i>&#42;askaz</i> for cognates.</p><p>In the sense of “secret facility”, from the Soviet Union-era rule of keeping the names of such facilities a secret by addressing mail to a post office box number without a name.</p><p>For the semantic shift from "tree" to "container" compare Latin <i>buxus</i>, <i>buxis</i> (whence English <i>box</i>, <i>boxwood</i>), akin to Ancient Greek <i>πυξίς</i> (puxís) (&lt; <i>πύξος</i> (púxos)) (whence Latin <i>pyxis</i>). Also compare German <i>Truhe</i> (akin to German <i>Trog</i>, further akin to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) Pandora's box</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>я́щик</i> (jáščik): (small) box, crate, drawer, hussy</li></ol><p><i>я́щик</i> (jáščik) +&lrm; <i>-ек</i> (-ek)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot-and-mouth disease</li></ol><p>Cognate with Belarusian <i>я́шчур</i> (jáščur) and Ukrainian <i>я́щур</i> (jáščur).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Cyrillic script letter <i>Ё</i>.</li></ol><p>Stressed [ʲe] regularly developed into [ʲɵ] before hard consonants. The letter <i>ё</i> (jo) was later created to reflect this, the diaeresis probably being inspired by the German use of diaeresis for umlaut. Its first appearance in typography was in 1797.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) alternative spelling of <i>ё-моё</i> (jo-mojó)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial) damn, bloody hell (<i>an expression of disappointment or surprise</i>)</li></ol><p>Formed according to the euphemistic model having the first sound or syllable from the original word (in this case, <i>ёб твою мать</i> (job tvoju matʹ)), but ending it with an unexpected (in this case, phonetically similar) continuation, similarly as <i>ёлки-па́лки</i> (jólki-pálki), <i>ёксель-мо́ксель</i> (jókselʹ-mókselʹ), <i>ёпрст</i> (jopereseté), <i>ёклмн</i> (jokelemené) and <i>ёшкин кот</i> (jóškin kot).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;swear word,&#32;vulgar) motherfuck, fuckshit extremely intense vulgar interjection, more intense than just <i>твою́ ма́ть</i>, used to exclaim peeve, dismay or anger as well as awe or astonishment</li></ol><p>Literally, “fuck your mother”; <i>ёб</i> (job, “fuck”) + <i>твою́</i> (tvojú, “your”) + <i>мать</i> (matʹ, “mother”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;swear word) fucking hell!</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;slang) fucking</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-аный</i> (-anyj). Compare with Ukrainian <i>ї́баний</i> (jíbanyj) and Ukrainian <i>йо́баний</i> (jóbanyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ёбанный в рот</i> (jóbannyj v rot)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) crap, shit (something disgusting)</li></ol><p><i>ёбаный</i> (jóbanyj) +&lrm; <i>-ь</i> (-ʹ).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo) fucker (one who fucks)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo,&#32;slang) stud, cunt-chaser</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;taboo,&#32;slang) boyfriend, lover</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-арь</i> (-arʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) an idiot, a simpleton, a stupid person; a fucker, a dumbfuck.</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) a recruited Russian soldier for the 2022 war against Ukraine.</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik, “a doer, diminutive”)</p><p>Sense 2 was influenced by мо́бик (móbik, “a poorly-trained Russian conscript during the 2022 invasion of Ukraine”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) fucked, fucked up (weird, crazy or insane)</li></ol><p>Compare Ukrainian <i>йо́бнутий</i> (jóbnutyj).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, to bang, to hit</li><li>to kill</li><li>to drink (<i>alcohol</i>)</li><li>to make (<i>unexpectedly or randomly</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ёб-</i> (job-) +&lrm; <i>-ну</i> (-nu) +&lrm; <i>-ть</i> (-tʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to fall</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to hit, to strike <i>(against)</i></li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to go crazy</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ёбарь</i> (jóbarʹ)</li></ol><p><i>еба́ть</i> (jebátʹ, “to fuck”) +&lrm; <i>-ырь</i> (-yrʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) hedgehog (animal)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) hedgehog (military barricade)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ežь</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>ёж</i> (jož)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) crew cut</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) a hot dish, a type of cutlet made from meat and rice</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ežikъ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ёж</i> (jož) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shiver (with/from), shrink (from) shudder (at)</li><li>to be shy</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ežiti (sę)</i>. Synchronically <i>ёжить</i> (jóžitʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja), however the verb <i>ёжить</i> (jóžitʹ) many times less common. <i>ёж</i> (jož) +&lrm; <i>-ить</i> (-itʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ся</i> (-sja).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skip a beat (<i>of the heart</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a sound like a hiccup</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic (see also <i>-кать</i> (-katʹ)).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;minced oath) Expresses disappointment or surprise. euphemistic form of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to skip a beat (<i>of the heart</i>)</li><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to make a sound like a hiccup</li></ol><p><i>ёкать</i> (jókatʹ) +&lrm; <i>-нуть</i> (-nutʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>sumo</i>) yokozuna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Japanese <ruby>横綱<rp>(</rp><rt>よこづな</rt><rp>)</rp></ruby> (<i>yokozuna</i>).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ель</i> (jelʹ): spruce, spruce tree</li><li>Christmas tree; New Year's tree (<i>in Russia</i>)</li><li>New Year's party for children</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;edlъka</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ель</i> (jelʹ) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial) drat!, fiddlesticks! (expression of dismay, annoyance, or surprise)</li></ol><p>Euphemistic alteration of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ), a vulgar curse.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(minced oath,&#32;colloquial) fiddlesticks! (expression of mild dismay or annoyance)</li></ol><p>Euphemistic alteration of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ), a vulgar curse.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ёлка</i> (jólka); little fir tree, little spruce, little Christmas tree (New Year's tree)</li><li>herringbone (<i>pattern</i>)</li><li>(<i>typography</i>) guillemet (type of quotation mark; either « or »)</li></ol><p><i>ёлка</i> (jólka) +&lrm; <i>-ка</i> (-ka)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in herringbone pattern</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) fir tree, Christmas tree</li></ol><p><i>ёлка</i> (jólka) +&lrm; <i>-ный</i> (-nyj)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capacious</li><li>pithy, succinct, laconic, concise (<i>of a speech or written work</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>име́ть</i> (imétʹ) +&lrm; <i>-кий</i> (-kij).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capacitance, capacity, volume</li><li>vessel, bottle (anything that can hold liquid, usually of higher capacity)</li><li>(<i>electronics</i>) capacitor</li></ol><p><i>ёмкий</i> (jómkij) +&lrm; <i>-ость</i> (-ostʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;minced oath) Expresses disappointment or surprise. euphemistic form of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;vulgar) abbreviation of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(idiomatic,&#32;vulgar) abbreviation of <i>ёб твою́ мать</i> (job tvojú matʹ)</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) someone who rudely mocks others</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) dwarf birch</li></ol><p>Most likely related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oriti</i> (“to drag down?”), whence <i>разори́ть</i> (razorítʹ). Not related to Latvian <i>er̃rīgs</i> (“angry, gloomy”), Latin <i>errō</i> (“to wander, get lost, err”), Old High German <i>irri</i> (“confused”) or Russian <i>ёрш</i> (jorš).</p><p>Borrowed from Komi-Zyrian (je̮ra), <small>[script needed]</small> (jör), <small>[script needed]</small> (jörpu, “dwarf birch”). Not related to Sanskrit <i>अरण्य</i> (araṇya, “wilderness, forest”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fidget, to squirm, to move restlessly (to twist in discomfort, especially from shame or embarrassment)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьrzati</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁erǵʰ-</i> (“mount (sexually), cover, copulate”). Cognate to Lithuanian <i>er̃žilas</i>, <i>ar̃žilas</i> (“stallion”), <i>aržùs</i> (“hot-tempered, lustful”), Old Norse <i>argr</i> (“effeminate”), German <i>arg</i> (“bad”), English <i>eerie</i>.</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(colloquial,&#32;animate) someone who rudely mocks others</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) low-growing bush, shrub or birch forest</li><li>(biology,&#32;in the form <i>берёзовый <b>ёрник</b></i> (berjózovyj <b>jórnik</b>) or <i><b>ёрник</b>-сла́нец</i> (<b>jórnik</b>-slánec)) a name for several small species of birch, including dwarf birch (<i>Betula nana</i>), dwarf bog birch (<i>Betula fruticosa</i>) and shrubby birch (<i>Betula humilis</i>)</li><li>(biology,&#32;in the form <i>та́ловый <b>ёрник</b></i> (tálovyj <b>jórnik</b>)) dwarf willow (<i>Salix herbacea</i>)</li><li>(dated,&#32;biology,&#32;in the form <i>обыкнове́нный&#47;чёрный&#47;я́годный <b>ёрник</b></i> (obyknovénnyj&#47;čórnyj&#47;jágodnyj <b>jórnik</b>)) black crowberry (<i>Empetrum nigrum</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ёра</i> (jóra) +&lrm; <i>-ник</i> (-nik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) to behave mockingly/mischievously/frivolously/cynically, to make jabs towards others</li></ol><p><i>ёрник</i> (jórnik) +&lrm; <i>-ать</i> (-atʹ)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>colloquial</i>) characteristic of someone who rudely mocks others</li></ol><p><i>ёрник</i> (jórnik) +&lrm; <i>-еский</i> (-eskij)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruffe (<i>Gymnocephalus cernua</i> syn. <i>Acerina cernua</i>)</li><li>bristle brush, bottle brush</li><li>go-devil</li><li>mixture of vodka and beer</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jerъxь</i> (“something thorny, jagged”), from <i>&#42;(j)erъx-</i>.</p><p>Per Vasmer, the original meaning (still found dialectally) was "jagged nail". In the contemporary sense attested since late 15th century.</p><p>Cognate with Ukrainian <i>йорж</i> (jorž, “ruffe”). Compare also Russian <i>еро́шить</i> (jeróšitʹ, “to ruffle”), Russian <i>еро́ховый</i> (jeróxovyj, “branchy, gnarly”) (about a tree or wood, dialectal), Lithuanian <i>erškė̃tis</i>, Latvian <i>ẽršk̨is</i> (“thorn”).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>animate</i>) diminutive of <i>ёрш</i> (jorš)</li><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) a kind of brush, such as a bottlebrush or toilet brush</li></ol><p><i>ёрш</i> (jorš) +&lrm; <i>-ик</i> (-ik)</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Norse mythology</i>) jotun</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Old Norse <i>jǫtunn</i></p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>inanimate</i>) yoficator, a program, which does the yofication, the process of supplying the Russian letter <i>ё</i> (jo) where the Russian letter <i>е</i> (je) is used instead of it.</li><li>(<i>animate</i>) yoficator, a person, which does the yofication, the process of supplying the Russian letter <i>ё</i> (jo) where the Russian letter <i>е</i> (je) is used instead of it.</li><li>(animate,&#32;by extension) supporter of the yofication of the Russian written language.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) yofication, a process of supplying the Russian letter <i>ё</i> (jo) where the Russian letter <i>е</i> (je) is used instead of it, either by a program or manually.</li></ol><p><i>ё</i> (jo) +&lrm; <i>-фика́ция</i> (-fikácija).</p></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>йод</i> (jod).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>иудаи́зм</i> (iudaízm).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>есть</i> (jestʹ): to eat</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>е́хать</i> (jéxatʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Фале́с</i> (Falés).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Феодо́сия</i> (Feodósija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Фома́</i> (Fomá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Фоми́ч</i> (Fomíč).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>Фёдор</i> (Fjódor).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>феа́тр</i> (feátr).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>феодоси́йский</i> (feodosíjskij).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теокра́тия</i> (teokrátija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>теосо́фия</i> (teosófija).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фессали́ец</i> (fessalíjec).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>фита́</i> (fitá).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ипакои́</i> (ipakoí).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ипа́рх</i> (ipárx).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ипа́т</i> (ipát).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>иподиа́кон</i> (ipodiákon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>ипоста́сь</i> (ipostásʹ).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>и́псилон</i> (ípsilon).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pre-1918 spelling of <i>иссо́п</i> (issóp).</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>Indicates the zero copula (omission of the present tense of быть (bytʹ)).</li><li>Used in — —.</li><li>Replaces ‐ in some appositions, where hyphen would be used to connect the appositive word and the word in apposition if neither of them were a phrase.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>Encloses an embedded (inner) quotation.</li></ol></html><h3>Russian</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>No., number</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of French <i>numéro</i>, from Latin <i>numerō</i> (ablative of <i>numerus</i>).</p></html>